var e=[];e.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-dysphagia-approach`,title:`Approach to dysphagia and odynophagia`,oneLiner:`**Dysphagia** is difficulty in moving a bolus from mouth to stomach and **odynophagia** is pain on swallowing; the history first separates **oropharyngeal (transfer) from oesophageal** dysphagia and then **mechanical (solids, progressive) from motility (solids and liquids from onset)**, and every new oesophageal dysphagia gets an **upper GI endoscopy** before barium or **high-resolution manometry (Chicago 4.0)**.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`dysphagia`,`odynophagia`,`oropharyngeal dysphagia`,`oesophageal dysphagia`,`transfer dysphagia`,`globus`,`Zenker diverticulum`,`Schatzki ring`,`Plummer-Vinson syndrome`,`food bolus impaction`,`videofluoroscopic swallow study`,`VFSS`,`FEES`,`high-resolution manometry`,`HRM`,`Chicago classification 4.0`,`timed barium oesophagram`,`FLIP`,`IDDSI`,`peptic stricture`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Dysphagia** is the sensation that food or liquid is hindered in its passage from mouth to stomach; **odynophagia** is pain on swallowing and points to **mucosal injury** (infection, pill, caustic, radiation, cancer). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Globus** is a painless, persistent sensation of a lump in the throat **between meals**, with normal swallowing; it is not dysphagia and is a **Rome IV functional oesophageal disorder**. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Aphagia** (complete inability to swallow, often with drooling of saliva) means **complete obstruction** - usually food bolus impaction - and is an endoscopic emergency. [ESGE foreign body 2016]`,`Population surveys report swallowing difficulty in about **1 in 6 adults** at some point, rising with age; **oropharyngeal dysphagia** affects a large share of nursing-home residents and **up to half of acute stroke** patients. [Harrison 22e]`,`In India, a **new progressive dysphagia in a middle-aged or elderly smoker** is **oesophageal squamous cell carcinoma** until proved otherwise - the commonest serious cause, with high-incidence belts in the **North-East and Kashmir** (ICMR-NCDIR National Cancer Registry Programme). [ICMR-NCDIR NCRP 2020]`,`**Corrosive (acid and alkali) strictures** from accidental or suicidal ingestion of toilet cleaners are a peculiarly common cause of benign stricture in Indian practice. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Achalasia** is the commonest primary motility disorder seen in Indian tertiary centres; Chagas disease, a cause of secondary achalasia in Latin America, does not occur in India. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`Dysphagia carries real harm: **aspiration pneumonia, malnutrition, dehydration** and social isolation, and it is an independent predictor of death after stroke. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Oropharyngeal - neurological**: stroke, **Parkinson disease**, motor neurone disease (bulbar and pseudobulbar palsy), multiple sclerosis, dementia, cerebral palsy, Guillain-Barre syndrome. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Oropharyngeal - muscular and junctional**: **myasthenia gravis** (fatigable dysphagia), polymyositis and inclusion-body myositis, oculopharyngeal muscular dystrophy, myotonic dystrophy, thyroid myopathy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Oropharyngeal - structural**: **Zenker (pharyngeal pouch) diverticulum**, cricopharyngeal bar, head and neck cancer, post-radiotherapy fibrosis, cervical osteophytes, large goitre, poor dentition and **xerostomia** (Sjogren, anticholinergics, radiotherapy). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Oesophageal - mechanical, intrinsic**: carcinoma, **peptic stricture**, **Schatzki ring**, oesophageal web (**Plummer-Vinson**), **eosinophilic oesophagitis**, corrosive or radiation stricture, anastomotic stricture, foreign body. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Oesophageal - mechanical, extrinsic**: mediastinal lymph nodes (**tuberculosis**, lymphoma, lung cancer), left atrial enlargement in mitral stenosis (**dysphagia megalatriensis**), aortic aneurysm, aberrant right subclavian artery (**dysphagia lusoria**). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Oesophageal - motility**: **achalasia**, distal oesophageal spasm, hypercontractile (jackhammer) oesophagus, ineffective motility and **systemic sclerosis**; opioids cause an **opioid-induced oesophageal dysfunction** pattern. [Chicago 4.0 2021]`,`**Odynophagia causes**: **Candida, CMV, HSV** (HIV, diabetes, steroids, transplant), **pill oesophagitis** (doxycycline, KCl, NSAIDs, bisphosphonates, iron), caustics, radiation, severe reflux oesophagitis and ulcerated cancer. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drugs that cause or worsen dysphagia**: anticholinergics and antihistamines (dry mouth), antipsychotics (extrapyramidal), benzodiazepines and opioids (sedation, motility), and **inhaled steroids** (candidiasis). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology: the three phases of a swallow`,points:[`**Oral phase** (voluntary): the bolus is chewed, mixed with saliva and propelled back by the tongue - lost in facial and lingual weakness and dry mouth. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pharyngeal phase** (reflex, under 1 second, brainstem swallowing centre via cranial nerves **V, VII, IX, X, XII**): soft palate seals the nasopharynx, larynx elevates and the epiglottis tilts, vocal cords close, and the **upper oesophageal sphincter (cricopharyngeus) relaxes and is pulled open**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Failure of the pharyngeal phase produces the signature symptoms: **nasal regurgitation** (palatal failure), **cough and choking during swallowing** (airway protection failure) and **food sticking in the throat** (UOS failure). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Oesophageal phase** (involuntary, about 8-10 seconds): the striated upper third and smooth-muscle lower two-thirds generate a **peristaltic wave** by sequential vagal inhibition then excitation, while the **lower oesophageal sphincter (LOS) relaxes** through nitric oxide and VIP from inhibitory myenteric neurons. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mechanical obstruction** obstructs a solid bolus long before liquids, so dysphagia starts with **meat and bread, then soft food, then liquids** - a narrowing below about **13 mm** almost always causes solid dysphagia, while one above **20 mm** rarely does. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Motility failure** fails to propel liquids and solids alike, so dysphagia to **both from the start** - often with regurgitation, chest pain and relief by manoeuvres (raising the arms, Valsalva, drinking water). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Loss of inhibitory neurons** (achalasia) gives an LOS that will not relax plus aperistalsis; **loss of smooth muscle** (scleroderma) gives a weak, gaping LOS plus aperistalsis - opposite LOS, same failed body, hence obstruction in one and **severe reflux** in the other. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Referred sensation is unreliable for level**: a patient pointing to the suprasternal notch may have a **distal** lesion, but a patient pointing to the lower sternum almost always has a distal one. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification that examiners ask`,points:[`**Step 1 - where**: **oropharyngeal (transfer)** - trouble starting the swallow, within 1 second, cough, nasal regurgitation - versus **oesophageal** - food sticks seconds after swallowing, behind the sternum. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Step 2 - what bolus**: **solids only** suggests mechanical obstruction; **solids and liquids from onset** suggests a motility disorder. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Step 3 - what course**: **intermittent** mechanical dysphagia suggests a ring or web or **EoE**; **progressive** mechanical dysphagia suggests **peptic stricture** (with long heartburn) or **carcinoma** (with weight loss, short history). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Step 4 for motility**: **intermittent** with chest pain suggests distal oesophageal spasm; **progressive** with regurgitation suggests **achalasia**, and with heartburn and Raynaud suggests **scleroderma**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chicago classification v4.0 (2021)** groups HRM findings into **disorders of EGJ outflow** (achalasia types I, II, III and EGJ outflow obstruction) and **disorders of peristalsis** (absent contractility, distal oesophageal spasm, hypercontractile oesophagus, ineffective oesophageal motility). [Chicago 4.0 2021]`,`The **IDDSI framework** grades modified diets on a **0-7 scale**: drinks 0 (thin) to 4 (extremely thick), foods 3 (liquidised) to 7 (regular) - the shared language of speech therapists and dieticians. [IDDSI 2019]`,`The **Eckardt score** (dysphagia, regurgitation, chest pain each 0-3, weight loss 0-3; total 0-12) grades achalasia symptoms; a score **above 3 after treatment** means failure. [ACG achalasia 2020]`]},{heading:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask the patient to **point to where food sticks** and **how many seconds after swallowing** - immediate throat symptoms mean oropharyngeal, delayed retrosternal sticking means oesophageal. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask **solids, liquids or both**, and the order in which they became difficult; ask whether the patient has **changed diet** (soft, liquid) - this reveals severity better than any scale. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask **duration and course**: weeks to a few months with relentless progression and **weight loss** is cancer; years of intermittent symptoms in a young man with **food bolus episodes** is EoE or a ring. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **regurgitation of undigested food** hours after meals, **halitosis** and **gurgling in the neck** - Zenker diverticulum; regurgitation on lying down with nocturnal cough suggests **achalasia**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **heartburn**: long-standing heartburn that **improves** as dysphagia appears suggests a peptic stricture (the stricture blocks reflux) or Barrett with adenocarcinoma. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **pain on swallowing**, HIV risk, steroid or inhaler use, diabetes, chemotherapy, and **pills swallowed at night with little water** (doxycycline, KCl, NSAIDs, alendronate). [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **neurological and muscle symptoms**: dysarthria, diplopia, ptosis worse at day end (myasthenia), tremor, limb weakness, fasciculations; and about **Raynaud phenomenon, skin tightening and heartburn** (systemic sclerosis). [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask the **Indian risk history**: smoking, bidi, gutkha and khaini, alcohol, **very hot tea**, salted tea in Kashmir, smoked or fermented foods in the North-East, previous **corrosive ingestion** and past tuberculosis. [ICMR-NCDIR NCRP 2020]`,`Use the **EAT-10** questionnaire (10 items, 0-4 each) in the elderly and after stroke; a score of **3 or more** suggests swallowing risk and needs formal assessment. [ESSD 2023]`]},{heading:`Examination findings and what they mean`,points:[`**Weight, BMI and hydration** at every visit - documented weight loss of more than 5% is an alarm feature and a nutrition trigger. [NICE NG12 2015]`,`**Pallor, koilonychia, angular cheilitis and glossitis** with dysphagia in a middle-aged woman suggest **Plummer-Vinson (Paterson-Kelly) syndrome**, a premalignant post-cricoid web. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Supraclavicular lymph node (left: Virchow), hepatomegaly** and hoarseness (recurrent laryngeal nerve invasion) mean advanced oesophageal cancer. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Oral cavity**: thrush (candidiasis - look for HIV), poor dentition, dry mouth, tongue fasciculations (MND), and **leukoplakia or submucous fibrosis** in tobacco and areca users. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neck**: goitre, lymph nodes, and a **gurgling swelling** on the left side of the neck after drinking (Zenker diverticulum, Boyce sign). [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Cranial nerve and bulbar examination**: palatal movement, gag, **voice quality (wet or nasal)**, tongue bulk and fasciculations, jaw jerk (brisk in pseudobulbar palsy), fatigable ptosis (myasthenia). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Skin and hands**: sclerodactyly, telangiectasia, calcinosis and digital ulcers (**CREST**), heliotrope rash and Gottron papules (dermatomyositis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bedside water swallow test** (for example, the 3-oz or 100 mL timed test) with observation for **cough, wet voice or fall in oxygen saturation** - a failed test means nil by mouth and formal speech-language assessment. [ESSD 2023]`,`**Cardiac**: the mid-diastolic murmur and loud S1 of **mitral stenosis** (giant left atrium compressing the oesophagus). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis: the discriminating features`,points:[`**Globus versus dysphagia**: globus is felt **between meals**, is relieved by eating, and has no weight loss or bolus hold-up. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Peptic stricture versus carcinoma**: both cause progressive solid dysphagia; stricture has **years of heartburn, little weight loss and smooth tapering** on barium, carcinoma has a **short history, marked weight loss and irregular shouldered narrowing (apple-core)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Achalasia versus pseudoachalasia**: pseudoachalasia (tumour at the cardia) suggests itself with **older age, short history and rapid weight loss** - a careful retroflexed view at endoscopy, CT or EUS is mandatory before treating achalasia. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**EoE versus GERD stricture**: EoE is a young atopic man with **food impactions**, rings, furrows and white exudates, and needs **biopsies even from a normal-looking oesophagus**. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Zenker versus achalasia regurgitation**: Zenker regurgitates **undigested food soon after meals with gurgling in the neck**; achalasia regurgitates at night with a dilated oesophagus on X-ray. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Oesophageal chest pain versus angina**: spasm pain may be relieved by nitrates and mimics angina exactly - **exclude cardiac disease first (ECG, troponin)** before labelling it oesophageal. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Myasthenia versus stroke**: myasthenic dysphagia **worsens through a meal** and through the day, with ptosis and nasal speech; stroke dysphagia is sudden and fixed. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Functional dysphagia** (Rome IV) is a diagnosis of exclusion after normal endoscopy, biopsies and HRM, with symptoms for 3 months. [Rome IV 2016]`]},{heading:`Investigations: first line in OPD and district hospital`,points:[`**Haemoglobin, MCV and ferritin** (iron deficiency in Plummer-Vinson, bleeding cancer), **albumin**, electrolytes and creatinine for nutrition and hydration, **HbA1c and HIV** in odynophagia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Upper GI endoscopy is the first test for oesophageal dysphagia**: it shows the lesion, takes biopsies, and can **dilate** in the same sitting; **NICE NG12** asks for direct-access endoscopy within **2 weeks for dysphagia at any age**. [NICE NG12 2015]`,`**Take oesophageal biopsies even from a normal-looking oesophagus**: at least **6 biopsies from 2 levels** (proximal and distal) to diagnose or exclude EoE. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Barium swallow** is preferred **first** when Zenker diverticulum, a complex or proximal stricture, a post-surgical anatomy or achalasia is suspected, and where endoscopy is unavailable; it shows **bird-beak** (achalasia), **corkscrew** (spasm), **apple-core** (cancer) and **rings or webs**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chest X-ray**: absent gastric air bubble, widened mediastinum and an air-fluid level (achalasia), mediastinal nodes, aspiration pneumonia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Videofluoroscopic swallow study (VFSS)** with a speech-language therapist is the reference test for **oropharyngeal dysphagia** - it shows penetration, aspiration (including **silent aspiration**) and which textures and postures are safe; **FEES** (fibre-optic endoscopic evaluation of swallowing) is the bedside alternative. [ESSD 2023]`,`In oropharyngeal dysphagia also check **ENT examination**, **brain MRI**, **CK**, **acetylcholine receptor antibodies** and thyroid function as the clinical picture dictates. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations: advanced and tertiary - with cut-offs`,points:[`**High-resolution manometry (HRM)** is the gold standard for motility disorders and is done **after endoscopy has excluded obstruction**; Chicago 4.0 requires **10 supine and 5 upright wet swallows** plus provocative tests (multiple rapid swallows, rapid drink challenge). [Chicago 4.0 2021]`,`**Integrated relaxation pressure (IRP)** measures LOS relaxation: a median **supine IRP above 15 mmHg** (upright above 12 mmHg, Medtronic system) is abnormal and defines **EGJ outflow obstruction or achalasia**. [Chicago 4.0 2021]`,`**Distal contractile integral (DCI)** measures contraction vigour: **under 100 mmHg.s.cm = failed**, **100-450 = weak**, **above 8000 = hypercontractile**; **distal latency under 4.5 seconds = premature** (spastic) contraction. [Chicago 4.0 2021]`,`**Timed barium oesophagram (TBE)**: barium column height **above 5 cm at 1 minute and above 2 cm at 5 minutes** supports achalasia and is used to measure **response to treatment**. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**FLIP (functional lumen imaging probe)** measures EGJ distensibility at endoscopy; a **distensibility index under 2 mm2/mmHg** supports obstruction and helps when HRM is inconclusive. [Chicago 4.0 2021]`,`**CECT chest and abdomen and EUS** stage malignancy and find extrinsic compression; **PET-CT** looks for distant spread before surgery. [NCCN Oesophageal 2025]`,`**24-hour pH-impedance** confirms reflux in suspected peptic stricture with atypical features and before anti-reflux surgery; an **acid exposure time above 6%** is conclusive GERD. [Lyon 2.0 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`**Refer every new oesophageal dysphagia for endoscopy** - do not start a PPI trial and wait; a PPI may heal the surface of a cancer and delay diagnosis. [NICE NG12 2015]`,`**Peptic stricture**: endoscopic dilatation plus a **PPI long term** (for example **pantoprazole 40 mg once or twice daily, oral, before meals**) - PPIs halve the need for repeat dilatation. [ACG GERD 2022]`,`**Oesophageal candidiasis**: **fluconazole 200-400 mg orally on day 1, then 200-400 mg daily for 14-21 days**; treat empirically in HIV with thrush and odynophagia and endoscope only if no response in 72 hours. [IDSA Candida 2016]`,`**Pill oesophagitis**: stop or change the drug, advise **at least 200 mL water, stay upright for 30 minutes**, and give a PPI and sucralfate suspension **1 g four times daily** for symptom relief. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Plummer-Vinson**: correct iron deficiency (**ferrous sulphate 200 mg (65 mg elemental iron) once daily or on alternate days**) - the web often softens; dilate if still symptomatic, and keep in endoscopic follow-up for post-cricoid cancer. [BSG IDA 2021]`,`**Oropharyngeal dysphagia**: refer to a **speech-language therapist** for compensatory strategies - **chin tuck, head turn to the weak side, small bolus, slow rate, upright 90 degrees, oral hygiene** - and **IDDSI texture modification**. [ESSD 2023]`,`**Treat the cause**: levodopa optimisation in Parkinson, **pyridostigmine 30-60 mg orally 30-45 minutes before meals** in myasthenia, stopping anticholinergics and sedatives, saliva substitutes in xerostomia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nutrition**: record weight, use energy-dense small meals and oral nutritional supplements; plan **tube feeding** if oral intake is unsafe or inadequate. [ESPEN 2023]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency`,points:[`**Food bolus impaction with inability to swallow saliva** (drooling, complete obstruction) needs endoscopy **within 6 hours**; incomplete obstruction within **24 hours** - aspiration and perforation risk rises with delay. [ESGE foreign body 2016]`,`**Push or retrieve** the bolus endoscopically (gentle push into the stomach is usual), then **biopsy** - EoE is the commonest underlying cause in young adults; **glucagon** is not routinely recommended and **meat tenderisers** are dangerous. [ESGE foreign body 2016]`,`**Acute stroke**: keep **nil by mouth until a bedside swallow screen is passed**, ideally within 4 hours of arrival; a failed screen means formal assessment and **nasogastric feeding within 24-72 hours** if needed. [AHA/ASA stroke 2019]`,`**Nasogastric tube** for short-term feeding; **PEG (percutaneous endoscopic gastrostomy)** if enteral feeding is expected for **more than 4 weeks**. [ESPEN 2023]`,`**PEG is not recommended in advanced dementia** - it does not prevent aspiration or prolong life; **careful hand feeding** is preferred. [ESPEN 2023]`,`**Malignant dysphagia** in a patient not fit for curative treatment: a **self-expanding metal stent (SEMS)** gives rapid relief; **brachytherapy** gives more durable relief in those expected to live longer. [ESGE stenting 2021]`,`**Odynophagia with fever in an immunosuppressed patient**: admit if unable to drink, start IV fluids, endoscope with biopsies for CMV and HSV, and start **IV ganciclovir 5 mg/kg twice daily** for proven CMV. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Suspected oesophageal perforation** after dilatation or vomiting (chest pain, surgical emphysema, fever, Hamman crunch): nil by mouth, **CT with water-soluble oral contrast**, IV antibiotics and urgent surgical opinion. [Bailey and Love 28e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Elderly**: presbyphagia (age-related slowing) is not a diagnosis by itself - look for stroke, Parkinson, drugs and cancer; frailty and sarcopenia worsen swallowing. [ESSD 2023]`,`**Pregnancy**: heartburn-related dysphagia is common; endoscopy is safe in the **second trimester** when indicated, and alarm features must not be attributed to pregnancy. [ASGE pregnancy 2012]`,`**Diabetes and HIV**: odynophagia should prompt a search for **Candida, CMV and HSV**; check CD4 count and start antiretroviral therapy per NACO. [NACO 2021]`,`**Children**: dysphagia raises **foreign body (coins, button batteries)**, caustic ingestion, congenital webs and EoE; a **button battery** in the oesophagus needs removal within 2 hours. [ESGE foreign body 2016]`,`**CKD and liver disease**: check fluconazole and ganciclovir doses against creatinine clearance; avoid NSAID-induced pill injury. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: where endoscopy is not available within 2 weeks, a **barium swallow** and early referral to a district or medical-college endoscopy unit under **PM-JAY** is acceptable - never a long empirical PPI course. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**Head and neck cancer survivors**: radiation fibrosis and stricture cause late dysphagia - prophylactic swallow exercises reduce it. [ESSD 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Aspiration pneumonia** is the main killer in oropharyngeal dysphagia; **silent aspiration** (no cough) occurs in a large share of stroke patients and is detected only by VFSS or FEES. [ESSD 2023]`,`**Malnutrition and dehydration** follow avoidance of eating; weight loss compounds sarcopenia and frailty. [ESPEN 2023]`,`**Bolus impaction, perforation and bleeding** complicate strictures and dilatation; perforation after dilatation occurs in under 1% of benign strictures. [ASGE dilation 2014]`,`**Prognosis follows the cause**: rings, webs and peptic strictures do well with dilatation and PPI; malignant dysphagia signals advanced disease with an overall 5-year oesophageal cancer survival of about **20%**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Refractory benign stricture** is one that fails to reach **14 mm after 5 sessions** at 2-week intervals; **recurrent stricture** fails to maintain 14 mm for 4 weeks. [ASGE dilation 2014]`,`**Psychological burden**: fear of choking leads to social isolation and depression - screen and address it. [ESSD 2023]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up, prevention and programme links`,points:[`**Urgent (within 2 weeks) endoscopy**: any dysphagia, or age 55 and over with weight loss plus upper abdominal pain, reflux or dyspepsia. [NICE NG12 2015]`,`**Same-day referral**: complete obstruction or drooling, odynophagia preventing fluid intake, haematemesis, suspected perforation, aspiration pneumonia. [ESGE foreign body 2016]`,`**Refer to neurology** for new oropharyngeal dysphagia with neurological signs, and to **ENT** for hoarseness, neck mass or suspected Zenker. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Refer for HRM** only when endoscopy and biopsies are normal and dysphagia persists. [Chicago 4.0 2021]`,`After dilatation review at **2-4 weeks** for symptom response; weigh and record diet texture at each visit. [ASGE dilation 2014]`,`**Tobacco and alcohol cessation** is the main prevention of oesophageal squamous cancer - link to the **National Tobacco Control Programme** quitline **1800-11-2356**. [NTCP 2023]`,`Counsel against **very hot beverages** (above 65 degrees C), classified as probably carcinogenic for the oesophagus. [IARC 2016]`,`Teach **pill hygiene**: swallow tablets upright with a full glass of water, never at bedtime lying down. [Harrison 22e]`,`Store **acids and toilet cleaners** away from children and in labelled bottles - corrosive strictures are preventable. [Harrison 22e]`,`Screen for dysphagia in every **stroke** admission and in the elderly at **Ayushman Arogya Mandir** NCD visits; refer to the district NCD clinic under **NP-NCD**. [NP-NCD 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2021-2025)`,points:[`**Chicago classification v4.0 (2021)**: added upright swallows and provocative tests, made **EGJ outflow obstruction a 'clinically relevant' diagnosis only with symptoms plus supportive TBE or FLIP**, and folded fragmented peristalsis into **ineffective oesophageal motility** (above 70% ineffective or at least 50% failed swallows). [Chicago 4.0 2021]`,`**ACG EoE guideline (2025)**: supports routine **biopsies in dysphagia even with normal mucosa**, PPI, swallowed topical steroids, **dupilumab** and diet as first-line options without a mandatory PPI trial. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Lyon consensus 2.0 (2024)**: acid exposure time above 6% and LA B-D oesophagitis are conclusive GERD - relevant to peptic stricture work-up. [Lyon 2.0 2024]`,`**European Society for Swallowing Disorders (ESSD) and ESPEN** guidance favours early **instrumental assessment (VFSS or FEES)** and IDDSI-based texture modification for oropharyngeal dysphagia. [ESSD 2023]`,`**NCCN Oesophageal and Oesophagogastric Junction Cancers (2025)** guides staging of malignant dysphagia; see the Barrett and oesophageal cancer topic for treatment. [NCCN Oesophageal 2025]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **'Approach to a patient with dysphagia'** - draw the algorithm: oropharyngeal versus oesophageal, then solids versus both, then intermittent versus progressive. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: **'Solid then liquid progressive dysphagia with weight loss in a 60-year-old smoker'** - carcinoma oesophagus; first test **endoscopy with biopsy**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: **'Dysphagia more to liquids than solids'** - paradoxical dysphagia classically suggests **achalasia**; oropharyngeal neuromuscular disease also troubles thin liquids most. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake**: giving a 4-8 week PPI trial to new dysphagia instead of endoscopy. [NICE NG12 2015]`,`**Common mistake**: endoscoping a suspected **Zenker diverticulum** blindly - barium first, because the scope enters the pouch and perforates. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Common mistake**: forgetting to biopsy a normal-looking oesophagus in a young man with food impaction - EoE is missed. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`Name the eponyms: **Plummer-Vinson** (web plus iron deficiency), **Schatzki** (lower ring, steakhouse syndrome), **Killian dehiscence** (Zenker), **dysphagia lusoria** (aberrant right subclavian). [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Oropharyngeal versus oesophageal dysphagia`,columns:[`Feature`,`Oropharyngeal (transfer)`,`Oesophageal`],rows:[[`Onset after swallow`,`Immediate, within 1 second`,`A few seconds after swallowing`],[`Site pointed to`,`Throat, neck`,`Retrosternal (distal lesions may refer upward)`],[`Associated`,`Cough, choking, nasal regurgitation, wet voice, drooling`,`Food sticking, regurgitation, heartburn, chest pain`],[`Common causes`,`Stroke, Parkinson, MND, myasthenia, Zenker, head and neck cancer`,`Cancer, peptic stricture, rings, EoE, achalasia, spasm`],[`First test`,`VFSS or FEES with speech therapist`,`Upper GI endoscopy with biopsies`],[`Main risk`,`Aspiration pneumonia`,`Bolus impaction, malnutrition, missed cancer`],[`Treatment lead`,`Speech therapy, texture modification, treat neurological cause`,`Endoscopic dilatation, PPI, cancer pathway, POEM or myotomy`]]},{heading:`Oesophageal dysphagia: bolus and course point to the cause`,columns:[`Pattern`,`Intermittent`,`Progressive`,`Clue that clinches it`],rows:[[`Solids only (mechanical)`,`Schatzki ring, web, EoE`,`Peptic stricture, carcinoma`,`Heartburn for years (stricture) vs weight loss and short history (cancer)`],[`Solids and liquids (motility)`,`Distal oesophageal spasm, hypercontractile oesophagus`,`Achalasia, scleroderma`,`Night regurgitation (achalasia) vs Raynaud and heartburn (scleroderma)`],[`Odynophagia dominant`,`Pill injury`,`Infective oesophagitis, ulcerated cancer`,`Recent doxycycline or KCl vs HIV, steroids, diabetes`],[`Throat lump between meals`,`Globus`,`Not progressive`,`No dysphagia, eased by eating`]]},{heading:`Investigations and interpretation cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`When`,`Key finding`,`Cut-off or interpretation`],rows:[[`Upper GI endoscopy`,`First test for oesophageal dysphagia`,`Stricture, ring, tumour, EoE features`,`6 biopsies from 2 levels even if normal`],[`Barium swallow`,`Zenker, complex stricture, suspected achalasia, no endoscopy`,`Bird-beak, corkscrew, apple-core, pouch`,`Rings narrower than 13 mm almost always symptomatic`],[`Timed barium oesophagram`,`Achalasia diagnosis and follow-up`,`Barium column height`,`Above 5 cm at 1 min, above 2 cm at 5 min abnormal`],[`High-resolution manometry`,`Normal endoscopy with persisting dysphagia`,`IRP, DCI, distal latency`,`Supine IRP above 15 mmHg; DCI above 8000 hypercontractile; DL under 4.5 s premature`],[`FLIP`,`Inconclusive HRM`,`EGJ distensibility index`,`Under 2 mm2/mmHg supports outflow obstruction`],[`VFSS or FEES`,`Oropharyngeal dysphagia`,`Penetration, aspiration, residue`,`Penetration-aspiration scale; silent aspiration`],[`24 h pH-impedance`,`Reflux doubt, before surgery`,`Acid exposure time`,`Above 6% conclusive, under 4% normal`]]},{heading:`Drugs used in common causes of dysphagia and odynophagia`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Pantoprazole`,`40 mg oral once or twice daily before meals`,`Long term after dilatation of peptic stricture`,`Headache, C. difficile, hypomagnesaemia, B12 deficiency on long use`],[`Fluconazole`,`200-400 mg oral day 1, then 200-400 mg daily`,`14-21 days`,`QT prolongation, hepatotoxicity, many CYP interactions`],[`Sucralfate suspension`,`1 g oral four times daily`,`1-2 weeks for pill injury`,`Constipation, aluminium load in CKD, binds other drugs`],[`Ganciclovir`,`5 mg/kg IV twice daily`,`3-6 weeks for CMV oesophagitis`,`Neutropenia, thrombocytopenia, renal dose adjustment`],[`Aciclovir`,`400 mg oral five times daily (or 5 mg/kg IV 8-hourly)`,`7-10 days for HSV (14-21 days if immunocompromised)`,`Renal impairment, hydrate well`],[`Pyridostigmine`,`30-60 mg oral 30-45 min before meals`,`Titrate to 4-6 doses a day`,`Cholinergic diarrhoea, salivation; caution in asthma`],[`Ferrous sulphate`,`200 mg (65 mg elemental iron) oral once daily or alternate days`,`3 months after Hb normalises`,`Constipation, dark stools, GI upset`]]},{heading:`Structural causes worth naming`,columns:[`Lesion`,`Site and mechanism`,`Typical patient`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`Zenker diverticulum`,`Posterior pharyngeal pouch through Killian dehiscence above cricopharyngeus`,`Elderly man, regurgitation of undigested food, halitosis`,`Endoscopic septotomy (Z-POEM) or open myotomy with diverticulectomy`],[`Schatzki ring`,`Mucosal ring at squamocolumnar junction`,`Intermittent steakhouse impaction`,`Single large-bore dilatation plus PPI`],[`Plummer-Vinson web`,`Post-cricoid web with iron deficiency`,`Middle-aged woman, koilonychia`,`Iron replacement, dilatation, cancer surveillance`],[`Peptic stricture`,`Distal, smooth, from chronic acid injury`,`Long heartburn history`,`Dilatation plus long-term PPI`],[`Corrosive stricture`,`Long, often multiple, after acid or alkali`,`Indian young adult after toilet-cleaner ingestion`,`Serial dilatation; surgery (oesophageal replacement) if refractory`]]}],redFlags:[`Progressive dysphagia with weight loss, especially age 55 and over or a smoker - urgent endoscopy within 2 weeks`,`Complete obstruction: unable to swallow saliva, drooling - endoscopy within 6 hours`,`Haematemesis, melaena or iron deficiency anaemia with dysphagia`,`Cough, choking or fever after swallowing - suspected aspiration pneumonia`,`Chest pain, surgical emphysema or fever after dilatation or forceful vomiting - suspected perforation`,`Odynophagia preventing fluid intake, especially in HIV or immunosuppression`,`New bulbar dysphagia with weakness, ptosis or fasciculations - suspected stroke, myasthenic crisis or MND`,`Hoarseness, neck mass or supraclavicular node with dysphagia`],pearls:[`**Where, what, how**: where does it stick (throat vs chest), what bolus (solids vs both), how does it behave (intermittent vs progressive) - three questions give the diagnosis in 80%.`,`**Solids first, then liquids, progressive** = mechanical; **solids and liquids from day one** = motility.`,`**Endoscopy before manometry** - always exclude obstruction and pseudoachalasia before a motility label.`,`**Barium before scope for Zenker** - the scope finds the pouch and perforates it.`,`**Biopsy the normal-looking oesophagus**: 6 biopsies from 2 levels catches EoE.`,`**Odynophagia means mucosal injury** - think Candida, CMV, HSV, pills.`,`**Aphagia with drooling** is an endoscopic emergency within 6 hours.`,`Chicago 4.0 cut-offs to quote: **IRP above 15 mmHg, DCI above 8000, distal latency under 4.5 seconds**.`,`**PEG in advanced dementia** does not prevent aspiration or prolong life - hand feed instead.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-dysphagia-approach-q1`,stem:`A 62-year-old bidi smoker from Assam has 3 months of dysphagia, first to rice and meat and now to soft food, with 7 kg weight loss. What is the most appropriate first investigation?`,options:[`High-resolution oesophageal manometry`,`Upper GI endoscopy with biopsy`,`A 4-week trial of twice-daily PPI`,`24-hour pH-impedance monitoring`],answer:1,explanation:`Progressive solid-then-softer-food dysphagia with weight loss in an older smoker from the North-East cancer belt is oesophageal carcinoma until proved otherwise, and endoscopy with biopsy diagnoses it. Manometry is only for motility disorders after obstruction is excluded, a PPI trial delays diagnosis, and pH monitoring answers a reflux question nobody is asking.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-dysphagia-approach-q2`,stem:`A 74-year-old man regurgitates undigested food eaten several hours earlier, has foul breath and a gurgling sound in the neck on swallowing. Which investigation should be done first?`,options:[`Barium swallow`,`Upper GI endoscopy`,`Videofluoroscopic swallow study only`,`CT neck with contrast`],answer:0,explanation:`The triad of regurgitation of undigested food, halitosis and neck gurgling suggests a Zenker diverticulum, and a barium swallow shows the pouch safely. Blind endoscopy risks entering the pouch and perforating it, VFSS assesses safety of swallowing rather than the anatomy of a pouch, and CT is not the first test for a mucosal pouch.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-dysphagia-approach-q3`,stem:`On high-resolution manometry (Chicago 4.0), which finding defines a premature (spastic) contraction?`,options:[`Distal contractile integral above 8000 mmHg.s.cm`,`Integrated relaxation pressure above 15 mmHg`,`Distal latency under 4.5 seconds`,`Distal contractile integral under 100 mmHg.s.cm`],answer:2,explanation:`A distal latency under 4.5 seconds means the contraction arrives too early - the hallmark of spasm and type III achalasia. A DCI above 8000 defines a hypercontractile swallow, an IRP above 15 mmHg defines impaired EGJ relaxation, and a DCI under 100 marks a failed swallow, so each describes a different abnormality.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-dysphagia-approach-q4`,stem:`A 26-year-old man with asthma and allergic rhinitis presents with a piece of chicken stuck in his oesophagus for 4 hours; he is drooling and cannot swallow saliva. What is the best management?`,options:[`Observe overnight and give IV glucagon`,`Barium swallow to locate the bolus`,`Advise papain meat tenderiser by mouth`,`Urgent endoscopy within 6 hours with biopsies after bolus removal`],answer:3,explanation:`Complete obstruction with inability to handle saliva needs endoscopy within 6 hours, and biopsies afterwards because eosinophilic oesophagitis is the commonest cause of food impaction in a young atopic man. Glucagon is not routinely recommended and delays treatment, barium coats the bolus and raises aspiration risk, and meat tenderisers can perforate the oesophagus.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-dysphagia-approach-q5`,stem:`A 70-year-old woman with advanced dementia has recurrent coughing during meals and has lost weight. The family asks for a feeding tube to prevent pneumonia. What is the best advice?`,options:[`Insert a PEG tube, as it prevents aspiration pneumonia`,`Careful hand feeding with texture modification and speech-therapy advice`,`Long-term nasogastric tube feeding`,`Nil by mouth and intravenous fluids indefinitely`],answer:1,explanation:`In advanced dementia PEG neither prevents aspiration (saliva and refluxed feed are still aspirated) nor prolongs life, so careful hand feeding with IDDSI-modified textures is recommended by ESPEN. Long-term nasogastric feeding causes distress and the same aspiration, and indefinite nil by mouth with drips is not a care plan.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-dysphagia-approach-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Define dysphagia. Classify its causes. Describe the clinical approach and investigation of a 55-year-old man presenting with dysphagia in a district hospital.`,openingLines:[`Dysphagia is difficulty in the passage of a solid or liquid bolus from the mouth to the stomach; odynophagia is pain on swallowing.`,`The approach rests on three questions - where it sticks, what bolus, and how it progresses - and every new oesophageal dysphagia in a 55-year-old needs endoscopy with biopsy within 2 weeks.`],answer:[{heading:`Classification`,points:[`Oropharyngeal: neurological (stroke, Parkinson, MND, myasthenia), muscular (myositis), structural (Zenker, head and neck cancer).`,`Oesophageal mechanical: intrinsic (cancer, peptic stricture, ring, web, EoE, corrosive) and extrinsic (nodes, left atrium, aorta).`,`Oesophageal motility: achalasia, distal oesophageal spasm, hypercontractile oesophagus, scleroderma.`]},{heading:`History`,points:[`Site and timing; solids vs liquids; intermittent vs progressive; weight loss; heartburn; regurgitation; odynophagia.`,`Risk factors: tobacco, alcohol, hot beverages, corrosive ingestion, HIV, drugs.`]},{heading:`Examination`,points:[`Weight, pallor, koilonychia, Virchow node, hepatomegaly, oral thrush, bulbar and cranial nerve signs, sclerodactyly.`,`Bedside water swallow test for aspiration risk.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Hb, ferritin, albumin, electrolytes, HIV, chest X-ray.`,`Upper GI endoscopy with biopsies (6 from 2 levels if normal); barium swallow if Zenker or complex stricture.`,`HRM (Chicago 4.0) if endoscopy normal; TBE, FLIP; CECT, EUS, PET-CT for staging cancer.`,`VFSS or FEES for oropharyngeal dysphagia.`]},{heading:`Initial management and referral`,points:[`Nutrition support, IDDSI diet, speech therapy; dilatation and PPI for peptic stricture.`,`Urgent referral for alarm features; same-day for complete obstruction or perforation.`]}],mustDraw:[`Algorithm: dysphagia, oropharyngeal vs oesophageal, then mechanical vs motility, then intermittent vs progressive with causes`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and classification`,marks:2},{part:`History and examination`,marks:3},{part:`Investigations with interpretation`,marks:3},{part:`Algorithm and referral`,marks:2}],keywords:[`dysphagia`,`oropharyngeal`,`oesophageal`,`endoscopy`,`manometry`,`Chicago 4.0`,`VFSS`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-dysphagia-approach-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate oropharyngeal from oesophageal dysphagia.`,openingLines:[`Oropharyngeal (transfer) dysphagia is difficulty initiating a swallow; oesophageal dysphagia is food sticking seconds after swallowing.`,`The distinction decides the first test: VFSS or FEES for the first, endoscopy for the second.`],answer:[{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Oropharyngeal: immediate, cough, choking, nasal regurgitation, wet voice, drooling.`,`Oesophageal: retrosternal sticking after a few seconds, regurgitation, heartburn, chest pain.`]},{heading:`Causes`,points:[`Oropharyngeal: stroke, Parkinson, MND, myasthenia, Zenker, head and neck cancer.`,`Oesophageal: cancer, peptic stricture, ring, EoE, achalasia, spasm.`]},{heading:`Investigation and treatment`,points:[`Oropharyngeal: VFSS or FEES, neuro work-up; speech therapy, texture modification, PEG if long term.`,`Oesophageal: endoscopy with biopsy, barium, HRM; dilatation, PPI, POEM, cancer pathway.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column comparison table: feature, oropharyngeal, oesophageal`],markSplit:[{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Causes`,marks:1},{part:`Investigation and treatment`,marks:2}],keywords:[`transfer dysphagia`,`aspiration`,`VFSS`,`endoscopy`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Dysphagia; Diseases of the Esophagus`,`Yadlapati R, et al. Esophageal motility disorders on high-resolution manometry: Chicago Classification version 4.0. Neurogastroenterol Motil, 2021`,`Vaezi MF, et al. ACG Clinical Guideline: Diagnosis and Management of Achalasia. Am J Gastroenterol, 2020`,`ACG Clinical Guideline: Diagnosis and Management of Eosinophilic Esophagitis, 2025`,`ESGE Guideline: Removal of foreign bodies in the upper gastrointestinal tract in adults, 2016`,`ESPEN Guideline on Clinical Nutrition and Hydration in Geriatrics / Neurology, 2022-2023`,`International Dysphagia Diet Standardisation Initiative (IDDSI) Framework, 2019`,`NICE NG12: Suspected cancer - recognition and referral (upper GI section)`]}),e.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-achalasia-motility`,title:`Achalasia and oesophageal motility disorders`,oneLiner:`**Achalasia** is a primary oesophageal motility disorder caused by **loss of inhibitory (nitrergic) myenteric neurons**, giving **failed LOS relaxation (raised IRP) and absent peristalsis**; it is diagnosed by **high-resolution manometry (Chicago 4.0, types I-III)** after endoscopy excludes pseudoachalasia, and treated by **POEM, laparoscopic Heller myotomy with partial fundoplication or pneumatic dilatation**.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`achalasia cardia`,`cardiospasm`,`Chicago classification 4.0`,`integrated relaxation pressure`,`IRP`,`POEM`,`peroral endoscopic myotomy`,`Heller myotomy`,`Dor fundoplication`,`pneumatic dilatation`,`botulinum toxin`,`bird-beak sign`,`pseudoachalasia`,`distal oesophageal spasm`,`jackhammer oesophagus`,`hypercontractile oesophagus`,`EGJ outflow obstruction`,`ineffective oesophageal motility`,`scleroderma oesophagus`,`Eckardt score`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Achalasia** (Greek: failure to relax) is a primary motility disorder with **impaired LOS relaxation and absent or spastic peristalsis** of the oesophageal body, in the absence of mechanical obstruction. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`Annual incidence is about **1-2 per 100,000** and prevalence about **10-25 per 100,000**; it affects men and women equally, peaking at **30-60 years**, with a smaller peak in the elderly. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Indian data**: achalasia is the commonest motility disorder in Indian manometry series, and Indian centres (Hyderabad, Lucknow, Mumbai, Delhi) report some of the **largest POEM experiences** in the world. [Indian tertiary series]`,`**Diffuse/distal oesophageal spasm** and **hypercontractile (jackhammer) oesophagus** are much rarer, typically presenting with chest pain and intermittent dysphagia. [Chicago 4.0 2021]`,`**Ineffective oesophageal motility (IEM)** is the commonest manometric abnormality overall, mostly linked with GERD, and often of little clinical consequence. [Chicago 4.0 2021]`,`**Systemic sclerosis** involves the oesophagus in up to **90%** of patients - the commonest visceral involvement of scleroderma. [Harrison 22e]`,`Achalasia is a **chronic, incurable but very treatable** disease: treatments palliate the LOS obstruction but do not restore peristalsis. [ACG achalasia 2020]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Primary (idiopathic) achalasia** is thought to follow a **viral trigger (HSV-1 suggested)** in a genetically susceptible host (HLA-DQ associations), causing **autoimmune ganglionitis** of the myenteric plexus. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Secondary achalasia (Chagas disease)** from *Trypanosoma cruzi* destroys ganglion cells throughout the gut (megaoesophagus, megacolon, cardiomyopathy) - Latin America only, not India. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pseudoachalasia** is mechanical obstruction mimicking achalasia: **adenocarcinoma of the cardia** (commonest), other infiltrating tumours, paraneoplastic anti-Hu syndrome (small-cell lung cancer), and **post-fundoplication or bariatric band** obstruction. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**Associations**: Down syndrome, **Allgrove (triple A) syndrome** - achalasia, alacrima and adrenal insufficiency - and familial visceral neuropathies. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Opioid-induced oesophageal dysfunction**: chronic opioids produce an achalasia type III or EGJ outflow obstruction pattern - review opioid use before labelling. [Chicago 4.0 2021]`,`**Spastic disorders and jackhammer** are associated with **GERD, opioids and anxiety**; jackhammer can also follow EGJ obstruction. [Chicago 4.0 2021]`,`**Scleroderma oesophagus** results from smooth-muscle atrophy and fibrosis; similar weak motility occurs in mixed connective tissue disease, diabetes, amyloidosis and hypothyroidism. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Normal LOS relaxation is mediated by **inhibitory myenteric neurons releasing nitric oxide and VIP**; peristalsis depends on a gradient of inhibition then excitation along the body. [Harrison 22e]`,`In achalasia, **selective loss of inhibitory neurons** leaves cholinergic excitation unopposed: the **LOS fails to relax** (raised IRP) and the body loses its latency gradient, so peristalsis fails or becomes simultaneous. [Harrison 22e]`,`Food and saliva pool above a closed LOS, the oesophagus **dilates and becomes tortuous (sigmoid)**, and the stasis causes **regurgitation, aspiration and chronic stasis oesophagitis** - a substrate for **squamous cancer**. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`Because stasis, not acid, fills the oesophagus, achalasia patients may report **heartburn from fermentation of retained food** (lactic acid), which is wrongly treated as GERD. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Type I** (classic) reflects long-standing neuronal loss with a flaccid, dilated body; **type II** retains enough muscle tone to compress the column and pressurise the whole oesophagus; **type III** retains disordered excitation producing **premature spastic contractions**. [Chicago 4.0 2021]`,`**Distal oesophageal spasm** reflects **impaired inhibitory neurotransmission** in the distal body (premature contractions) with normal LOS relaxation; **jackhammer** reflects excessive cholinergic drive (DCI above 8000). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Scleroderma** destroys smooth muscle: the **LOS becomes hypotensive and the distal two-thirds aperistaltic**, so acid refluxes and is not cleared - severe erosive oesophagitis, stricture and Barrett. [Harrison 22e]`,`**How the treatments map on**: myotomy (POEM, Heller) and dilatation **disrupt the LOS muscle**, botulinum toxin **blocks acetylcholine release**, and nitrates and calcium-channel blockers **relax smooth muscle** briefly. [ACG achalasia 2020]`]},{heading:`Classification: Chicago classification v4.0`,points:[`**Disorders of EGJ outflow**: achalasia type I, II, III and **EGJ outflow obstruction (EGJOO)**; all require an **elevated median IRP** (supine above 15 mmHg on Medtronic systems). [Chicago 4.0 2021]`,`**Type I (classic)**: raised IRP, **100% failed peristalsis**, no pressurisation. [Chicago 4.0 2021]`,`**Type II (with panoesophageal pressurisation)**: raised IRP, 100% failed peristalsis, **panoesophageal pressurisation in at least 20% of swallows** - the best response to any treatment. [Chicago 4.0 2021]`,`**Type III (spastic)**: raised IRP, no normal peristalsis, **premature (spastic) contractions with DL under 4.5 s in at least 20% of swallows** - responds best to **POEM** with a tailored long myotomy. [Chicago 4.0 2021]`,`**EGJOO** (raised IRP with some preserved peristalsis) is diagnosed as **clinically relevant only with dysphagia or chest pain plus supportive TBE or FLIP**; opioids and mechanical causes must be excluded. [Chicago 4.0 2021]`,`**Disorders of peristalsis**: **absent contractility** (normal IRP, 100% failed - e.g. scleroderma), **distal oesophageal spasm** (normal IRP, at least 20% premature), **hypercontractile oesophagus** (at least 20% swallows DCI above 8000) and **IEM** (above 70% ineffective or at least 50% failed). [Chicago 4.0 2021]`,`**Radiological stage** (sigmoid oesophagus, diameter above 6 cm, tortuous) is 'end-stage achalasia' and predicts poorer response and possible oesophagectomy. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**Eckardt score** (0-12) grades symptoms: dysphagia, regurgitation, retrosternal pain each scored 0-3 by frequency, and weight loss 0-3; **above 3 after therapy = treatment failure**. [ACG achalasia 2020]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Dysphagia to solids and liquids** from the start, slowly progressive over months to years - more than 90% of patients. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Paradoxical dysphagia** (liquids as hard as or harder than solids) and use of **manoeuvres** - drinking water, raising the arms, standing up, Valsalva - to force food down. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Regurgitation of undigested food and frothy saliva**, especially at night, with a **wet pillow** and nocturnal cough. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Retrosternal chest pain** is more prominent in **type III** and young patients and may wane as the oesophagus dilates. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**Weight loss** is usually gradual; rapid weight loss over a short history should raise **pseudoachalasia**. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**Aspiration**: recurrent pneumonia, chronic cough, bronchiectasis and lung abscess, sometimes the presenting problem. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Heartburn** in untreated achalasia is from fermentation, and patients are commonly misdiagnosed and treated for **GERD for years** before diagnosis. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**Spasm and jackhammer** present with **intermittent chest pain mimicking angina** and intermittent dysphagia; **scleroderma** presents with heartburn, regurgitation, dysphagia and Raynaud. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Examination in achalasia is usually **normal**; look for **weight loss, cachexia and dehydration**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chest signs** of aspiration: crackles, bronchial breathing, clubbing in long-standing lung suppuration. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Virchow node, hepatomegaly** or an epigastric mass suggest **pseudoachalasia** from a cardia cancer. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**Allgrove syndrome**: absent tears (Schirmer test), pigmentation and hypotension of adrenal insufficiency. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Systemic sclerosis**: sclerodactyly, salt-and-pepper skin, microstomia, **telangiectasia, calcinosis, digital pitting scars**, Raynaud - **CREST**. [Harrison 22e]`,`In suspected spasm presenting with chest pain, do an **ECG and cardiovascular examination first**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Pseudoachalasia** (cardia cancer): older age (above 55-60), short history (under 1 year), weight loss above 10 kg, difficulty passing the scope - needs **retroflexed endoscopic view, EUS or CT**. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**Peptic stricture**: solid-only dysphagia, long heartburn, visible stricture at endoscopy, normal peristalsis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Eosinophilic oesophagitis**: young atopic patient, food impaction, rings and furrows, eosinophils on biopsy - may cause raised IRP and must be biopsied out. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**GERD**: heartburn responding to PPI with normal or IEM manometry; achalasia heartburn does **not** respond to PPI. [Lyon 2.0 2024]`,`**Rumination syndrome**: effortless regurgitation within minutes of every meal, no nocturnal regurgitation, diagnosed by HRM-impedance. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Angina versus spasm**: both can be relieved by nitrates - **exclude coronary disease first** with ECG, troponin and stress testing. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Scleroderma versus achalasia**: both aperistaltic; **scleroderma has a hypotensive LOS (low IRP)** and severe reflux, achalasia a non-relaxing LOS (high IRP). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line`,points:[`**Upper GI endoscopy first** to exclude pseudoachalasia and other obstruction: typical findings are a **dilated oesophagus with retained food and saliva** and a **puckered LOS that 'pops' with gentle pressure**; endoscopy is normal in up to a third of early cases. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**Biopsy** the distal oesophagus to exclude EoE and the cardia if any mucosal abnormality or difficulty passing the scope. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**Chest X-ray**: **absent gastric air bubble**, a widened mediastinum, an **air-fluid level** behind the heart, and aspiration changes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Barium swallow**: dilated oesophagus tapering smoothly to a **'bird-beak'** at the LOS, absent peristalsis, and a **sigmoid** oesophagus in end-stage disease; **corkscrew or rosary-bead** oesophagus suggests spasm. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Timed barium oesophagram (TBE)**: 100-250 mL of low-density barium drunk upright, films at 1, 2 and 5 minutes; a **column above 5 cm at 1 min or above 2 cm at 5 min** supports achalasia and gives an objective baseline. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**Baseline bloods** for nutrition (Hb, albumin), and **ECG** in patients with chest pain. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - advanced and tertiary`,points:[`**High-resolution manometry (HRM)** is the **gold standard**: raised IRP plus 100% failed peristalsis confirms achalasia and the **subtype guides treatment**. [Chicago 4.0 2021]`,`Chicago 4.0 protocol: **10 supine and 5 upright swallows of 5 mL water**, plus **multiple rapid swallows and rapid drink challenge** to unmask obstruction. [Chicago 4.0 2021]`,`**FLIP (EndoFLIP)**: EGJ distensibility index **under 2 mm2/mmHg** supports achalasia when HRM is inconclusive; it can also be used during POEM to judge the adequacy of myotomy. [Chicago 4.0 2021]`,`**CECT chest and abdomen or EUS** when pseudoachalasia is suspected (age above 55, weight loss, short history, asymmetric cardia). [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**Scleroderma work-up**: ANA, anti-Scl-70, anti-centromere antibodies; HRM shows **absent contractility with a hypotensive LOS**; check pH monitoring and lung function (ILD). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Post-treatment assessment**: Eckardt score plus **TBE at 1-3 months**; persisting barium column predicts relapse even if symptoms improve. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**24-hour pH study after POEM** detects asymptomatic reflux oesophagitis, which is common after POEM. [ESGE/ESNM achalasia 2020]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - medical and bridging`,points:[`**No drug cures achalasia**; medical therapy is a **bridge or for patients unfit for definitive therapy**. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**Nifedipine 10-20 mg sublingual 15-30 minutes before meals** relaxes the LOS briefly; headache, hypotension and pedal oedema limit use. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**Isosorbide dinitrate 5 mg sublingual 10-15 minutes before meals** - slightly more effective than nifedipine but causes headache. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**Botulinum toxin**: **100 units injected endoscopically into the LOS (25 units in each of 4 quadrants)**; relief in most patients but effect wanes in **6-12 months** - reserve for the **frail elderly or as a bridge**. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`Repeated botulinum toxin causes **submucosal fibrosis** that makes later myotomy harder - avoid it in surgical candidates. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**Diet advice**: eat upright, chew well, drink water with meals, avoid lying down for 2-3 hours after meals, raise the head end of the bed. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Distal oesophageal spasm and jackhammer**: exclude cardiac disease; treat reflux with PPI; try **nitrates, calcium-channel blockers or low-dose tricyclics/SSRIs** (pain modulation); botulinum toxin or POEM for refractory cases. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Scleroderma oesophagus**: **high-dose PPI** (for example pantoprazole 40 mg twice daily), head-end elevation, small meals; **prokinetics** may help; **avoid full fundoplication** in aperistalsis. [ACG GERD 2022]`]},{heading:`Definitive treatment: POEM vs Heller vs pneumatic dilatation`,points:[`**Pneumatic dilatation (PD)**: graded **30, 35 then 40 mm** Rigiflex balloon under fluoroscopy; success around **90% at 1 year**, perforation about **2%**; best in **type II and older patients**; repeat on relapse. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**Laparoscopic Heller myotomy (LHM)** with **partial fundoplication (Dor anterior or Toupet posterior)**: myotomy at least 6 cm on the oesophagus and 2-3 cm onto the stomach; success about **90%**, and the fundoplication limits reflux. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**POEM (peroral endoscopic myotomy)**: submucosal tunnel and endoscopic myotomy without skin incisions; success about **90-95% at 2 years**, comparable to LHM and better than PD. [ESGE/ESNM achalasia 2020]`,`**POEM vs PD (RCT, 2019)**: treatment success at 2 years **92% vs 54%**, but more reflux oesophagitis after POEM. [Ponds, JAMA 2019]`,`**POEM vs LHM (RCT, 2019)**: POEM non-inferior at 2 years (**83% vs 81%**) but reflux oesophagitis **44% vs 29%** - counsel about long-term PPI after POEM. [Werner, NEJM 2019]`,`**Choose by subtype**: **type III - POEM** (long tailored myotomy reaches the spastic segment); **type I and II - POEM, LHM or PD** are all reasonable, guided by local expertise and patient choice. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**Failed myotomy**: redo POEM, PD or redo surgery; **end-stage sigmoid oesophagus** may need **oesophagectomy**, though POEM is increasingly tried first. [ESGE/ESNM achalasia 2020]`,`In India, POEM is available at major public and private centres and is covered under **PM-JAY** in empanelled hospitals; PD remains a valuable low-cost option. [Indian tertiary series]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Elderly and frail**: botulinum toxin or PD; POEM is safe in the elderly in experienced hands. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**Pregnancy**: defer definitive therapy; use dietary measures and nutritional support; **botulinum toxin** or careful PD have been used in severe malnutrition. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**Children**: rare; POEM and LHM both used; consider **Allgrove syndrome** (check cortisol, tears). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cirrhosis or coagulopathy**: POEM risk rises with varices and bleeding tendency; individualise. [ESGE/ESNM achalasia 2020]`,`**Diabetes**: oesophageal hypomotility is common but true achalasia is not more frequent; check glucose control and gastroparesis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: where HRM is unavailable, **endoscopy plus barium swallow and TBE** suggest the diagnosis; refer for HRM and treatment at a tertiary centre. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Aspiration pneumonia**, lung abscess and bronchiectasis from nocturnal regurgitation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Oesophageal squamous cell carcinoma** risk is increased (estimates up to **several-fold to 30-fold**), mostly after 10-15 years of disease; ACG does not mandate routine surveillance, but long-standing disease justifies a lower threshold for endoscopy. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**Post-treatment GERD**: common after POEM, less after LHM with fundoplication; can lead to erosive oesophagitis and Barrett - long-term PPI and surveillance. [ESGE/ESNM achalasia 2020]`,`**Perforation**: about 2% with PD; managed conservatively or with clips, stents or surgery. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**Megaoesophagus (sigmoid)**: end-stage disease with poor emptying and possible oesophagectomy. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**Prognosis**: life expectancy is near normal with treatment; about 10-20% need re-treatment over 5-10 years. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**Scleroderma oesophagus**: severe reflux, peptic stricture, Barrett and aspiration contributing to **interstitial lung disease**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer** any patient with solid-and-liquid dysphagia, night regurgitation, absent gastric bubble or bird-beak barium to a **gastroenterologist with HRM** facility. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**After treatment**: Eckardt score and **TBE at 1-3 months**, then yearly symptom review; re-treat when Eckardt is above 3 or the barium column persists. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**After POEM**: consider endoscopy or pH monitoring at about **1 year** for silent reflux oesophagitis; PPI if present. [ESGE/ESNM achalasia 2020]`,`**Long-standing achalasia (over 10-15 years)**: low threshold for endoscopy for new symptoms, weight loss or bleeding - squamous cancer risk. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**Scleroderma**: annual review of reflux and nutrition; Barrett surveillance if found; lung function monitoring. [Harrison 22e]`,`Watch **weight and nutrition** at each visit, and check for aspiration (chest infections). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**Chicago classification v4.0 (2021)** remains current: adds upright and provocative swallows, keeps the three achalasia subtypes, requires supportive tests for **clinically relevant EGJOO**, and merges fragmented peristalsis into **IEM**. [Chicago 4.0 2021]`,`**ACG achalasia guideline (2020)**: PD, LHM and POEM are all effective first-line options; **POEM preferred for type III**; botulinum toxin for those unfit for definitive therapy. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**ESNM/UEG achalasia guideline (2020)**: POEM, LHM and PD are equivalent options for type I and II; POEM favoured in type III; counsel about reflux after POEM. [ESGE/ESNM achalasia 2020]`,`**Long-term data (2023-2025)**: 5-year follow-up confirms durable POEM efficacy with persistent reflux in a substantial minority, reinforcing PPI and surveillance after POEM. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lyon 2.0 (2024)** gives the reflux thresholds now used to judge post-POEM reflux (AET above 6% conclusive). [Lyon 2.0 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Viva: **'Name the classic radiological signs of achalasia'** - absent gastric air bubble, mediastinal air-fluid level, **bird-beak** on barium, sigmoid oesophagus. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: **'Gold standard investigation?'** - high-resolution manometry; but **endoscopy comes first** to exclude pseudoachalasia. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`Viva: **'Which subtype responds best, and which needs POEM?'** - type II best overall; type III POEM. [Chicago 4.0 2021]`,`Viva: **'Difference between achalasia and scleroderma on manometry'** - both aperistaltic, but **high IRP in achalasia and low LOS pressure in scleroderma**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake**: diagnosing achalasia without a careful retroflexed view of the cardia - pseudoachalasia is missed. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**Common mistake**: years of PPI for 'refractory GERD' in a young patient with night regurgitation of undigested food. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**Corkscrew oesophagus** = distal oesophageal spasm; **jackhammer** = DCI above 8000 in at least 20% swallows. [Chicago 4.0 2021]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Chicago classification v4.0 - manometric diagnoses`,columns:[`Diagnosis`,`IRP`,`Body findings`,`Clinical note`],rows:[[`Achalasia type I`,`Raised (supine above 15 mmHg)`,`100% failed peristalsis, no pressurisation`,`Dilated flaccid body; POEM, LHM or PD`],[`Achalasia type II`,`Raised`,`100% failed, panoesophageal pressurisation in 20% or more`,`Best response to any therapy`],[`Achalasia type III`,`Raised`,`Premature (DL under 4.5 s) in 20% or more, no normal peristalsis`,`Chest pain; POEM preferred`],[`EGJ outflow obstruction`,`Raised`,`Some preserved peristalsis`,`Needs symptoms plus TBE or FLIP; exclude opioids, mechanical cause`],[`Absent contractility`,`Normal`,`100% failed peristalsis`,`Scleroderma pattern; reflux`],[`Distal oesophageal spasm`,`Normal`,`Premature contractions in 20% or more`,`Chest pain, corkscrew barium`],[`Hypercontractile (jackhammer)`,`Normal`,`DCI above 8000 in 20% or more`,`Chest pain, dysphagia`],[`Ineffective oesophageal motility`,`Normal`,`Above 70% ineffective or 50% or more failed`,`Usually GERD-related`]]},{heading:`Definitive treatments for achalasia compared`,columns:[`Feature`,`Pneumatic dilatation`,`Laparoscopic Heller myotomy + Dor`,`POEM`],rows:[[`Approach`,`Endoscopic balloon 30-35-40 mm`,`Laparoscopic, myotomy plus partial fundoplication`,`Endoscopic submucosal tunnel myotomy`],[`Success`,`About 90% at 1 year; repeat often needed`,`About 90%`,`About 90-95% at 2 years`],[`Best for`,`Type II, older patients, low cost`,`Type I and II, when reflux is a concern`,`Type III (long myotomy), all types, previous failure`],[`Main risk`,`Perforation about 2%`,`Mucosal perforation, dysphagia from wrap`,`Post-POEM reflux oesophagitis (up to 40%+)`],[`Reflux after`,`Low`,`Low (fundoplication)`,`Highest - long-term PPI may be needed`],[`Key trial`,`PD vs POEM (Ponds 2019): 54% vs 92% at 2 years`,`LHM vs POEM (Werner 2019): 81% vs 83%`,`Non-inferior to LHM`]]},{heading:`Drugs in achalasia and oesophageal motility disorders`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Nifedipine`,`10-20 mg sublingual 15-30 min before meals`,`Bridge or unfit for definitive therapy`,`Headache, hypotension, flushing, pedal oedema`],[`Isosorbide dinitrate`,`5 mg sublingual 10-15 min before meals`,`Bridge only`,`Headache, hypotension; avoid with PDE-5 inhibitors`],[`Botulinum toxin A`,`100 units into LOS (25 units x 4 quadrants) endoscopically`,`Effect 6-12 months; repeat`,`Chest pain, fibrosis hampers later myotomy`],[`Pantoprazole`,`40 mg oral once or twice daily`,`Long term after POEM with reflux; scleroderma`,`C. difficile, hypomagnesaemia, fracture risk`],[`Amitriptyline`,`10-25 mg oral at night`,`Titrate for spasm chest pain`,`Anticholinergic effects, sedation, QT; caution in elderly`],[`Sildenafil`,`25-50 mg oral before meals`,`Second line for spasm`,`Hypotension; never with nitrates`]]},{heading:`Achalasia versus look-alikes`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating clinical feature`,`Key test result`],rows:[[`Achalasia`,`Solids and liquids, night regurgitation, slow course`,`Raised IRP, failed peristalsis, bird-beak`],[`Pseudoachalasia`,`Age above 55, weight loss, history under 1 year`,`Cardia tumour on retroflexion, CT or EUS`],[`Scleroderma oesophagus`,`Heartburn, Raynaud, sclerodactyly`,`Absent contractility with low LOS pressure`],[`Peptic stricture`,`Solids only, long heartburn`,`Visible stricture, normal peristalsis`],[`Eosinophilic oesophagitis`,`Young atopic, food impaction`,`15 or more eosinophils per hpf`],[`Rumination syndrome`,`Effortless regurgitation within minutes of every meal`,`HRM-impedance shows gastric pressure spikes`]]}],redFlags:[`Age above 55 with short history (under 1 year) and marked weight loss - suspect pseudoachalasia from cardia cancer`,`Recurrent aspiration pneumonia or lung abscess`,`Chest pain, fever or surgical emphysema after pneumatic dilatation or POEM - perforation`,`Severe malnutrition or dehydration from inability to eat`,`New dysphagia change, bleeding or weight loss in long-standing achalasia - squamous cancer`,`Chest pain thought to be spasm without cardiac evaluation`],pearls:[`**Achalasia = loss of inhibitory (NO/VIP) neurons**: LOS will not relax, body will not squeeze.`,`**Endoscopy first, manometry for proof**: exclude pseudoachalasia before every achalasia label.`,`Chicago 4.0 types: **I = no pressure, II = pan-pressurisation (best outcome), III = spastic (POEM)**.`,`**Bird-beak** barium, **absent gastric bubble** on CXR, **Eckardt above 3** = failure.`,`**POEM beats PD** (92% vs 54% at 2 years) but **more reflux** than Heller with Dor.`,`**Botox for the frail**: 100 units, lasts 6-12 months, scars the plane for later myotomy.`,`**Scleroderma vs achalasia**: both aperistaltic - scleroderma has a weak LOS (reflux), achalasia a tight LOS (obstruction).`,`**Allgrove (triple A)**: achalasia, alacrima, adrenal insufficiency.`,`**Jackhammer** = DCI above 8000 in 20% or more; **spasm** = DL under 4.5 s in 20% or more.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-achalasia-motility-q1`,stem:`A 34-year-old woman has 2 years of dysphagia to both solids and liquids, nocturnal regurgitation of undigested food and an absent gastric air bubble on chest X-ray. Endoscopy shows a dilated oesophagus with retained food and no mass. What is the investigation that confirms the diagnosis and its subtype?`,options:[`Timed barium oesophagram`,`24-hour pH-impedance study`,`High-resolution oesophageal manometry`,`Endoscopic ultrasound`],answer:2,explanation:`High-resolution manometry is the gold standard: a raised IRP with 100% failed peristalsis confirms achalasia and the Chicago 4.0 subtype guides choice of treatment. A timed barium oesophagram supports the diagnosis and measures emptying but cannot subtype it, pH monitoring answers a reflux question, and EUS is for suspected pseudoachalasia.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-achalasia-motility-q2`,stem:`HRM in a 45-year-old man with dysphagia and severe chest pain shows a raised IRP, no normal peristalsis and premature contractions in 40% of swallows. Which treatment is preferred?`,options:[`Pneumatic dilatation with a 30 mm balloon`,`Peroral endoscopic myotomy (POEM)`,`Sublingual nifedipine before meals long term`,`Laparoscopic Nissen fundoplication`],answer:1,explanation:`This is type III (spastic) achalasia, which responds poorly to pneumatic dilatation and standard-length surgery; POEM allows a long myotomy tailored to the spastic segment and is preferred by ACG and European guidelines. Nifedipine is only a bridge, and a full Nissen wrap would worsen obstruction in an aperistaltic oesophagus.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-achalasia-motility-q3`,stem:`A 68-year-old man presents with 4 months of dysphagia to solids and liquids, 12 kg weight loss and a bird-beak appearance on barium swallow. Which is the most important next step?`,options:[`Proceed directly to POEM`,`Botulinum toxin injection as he is elderly`,`Endoscopy with careful retroflexed view of the cardia and CT or EUS`,`Trial of isosorbide dinitrate for 3 months`],answer:2,explanation:`Older age, a short history and heavy weight loss are the classic features of pseudoachalasia from a cardia adenocarcinoma, so the cardia must be examined in retroflexion and imaged with CT or EUS before any achalasia treatment. Treating with POEM, botulinum toxin or nitrates first would delay a cancer diagnosis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-achalasia-motility-q4`,stem:`A woman with limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis has heartburn and dysphagia. Which manometric pattern is expected?`,options:[`Absent contractility with a hypotensive lower oesophageal sphincter`,`Raised IRP with panoesophageal pressurisation`,`Distal contractile integral above 8000 in most swallows`,`Raised IRP with premature contractions`],answer:0,explanation:`Scleroderma destroys oesophageal smooth muscle, so the distal body is aperistaltic and the LOS is weak, causing severe reflux - the Chicago 4.0 'absent contractility' pattern with normal or low IRP. Raised IRP with pressurisation is type II achalasia, raised IRP with premature contractions is type III, and DCI above 8000 is jackhammer oesophagus.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-achalasia-motility-q5`,stem:`In the randomised trial comparing POEM with laparoscopic Heller myotomy plus Dor fundoplication (Werner, NEJM 2019), which outcome was more frequent after POEM?`,options:[`Treatment failure at 2 years`,`Serious adverse events`,`Need for oesophagectomy`,`Reflux oesophagitis on endoscopy`],answer:3,explanation:`POEM was non-inferior to Heller myotomy for symptom control at 2 years (83% vs 81%), with similar serious adverse events, but reflux oesophagitis was seen in 44% after POEM versus 29% after Heller with Dor because POEM has no anti-reflux wrap. Oesophagectomy was not a meaningful outcome in either arm.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-achalasia-motility-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Describe the aetiopathogenesis, clinical features, Chicago classification and management of achalasia cardia.`,openingLines:[`Achalasia is a primary oesophageal motility disorder due to loss of inhibitory myenteric neurons, causing failure of LOS relaxation and absent peristalsis.`,`It is diagnosed by high-resolution manometry after endoscopy excludes pseudoachalasia, and treated by POEM, Heller myotomy or pneumatic dilatation.`],answer:[{heading:`Aetiopathogenesis`,points:[`Viral trigger plus autoimmune ganglionitis destroys NO/VIP inhibitory neurons; unopposed cholinergic tone.`,`Secondary: Chagas; pseudoachalasia: cardia cancer, anti-Hu; associations: Allgrove, Down syndrome.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Dysphagia to solids and liquids, regurgitation at night, chest pain (type III), slow weight loss, aspiration.`,`Examination usually normal; look for pseudoachalasia signs.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Endoscopy first; CXR absent gastric bubble; barium bird-beak; TBE column above 5 cm at 1 min.`,`HRM: raised IRP (above 15 mmHg) plus 100% failed peristalsis; FLIP if inconclusive.`]},{heading:`Chicago 4.0 classification`,points:[`Type I: no pressurisation; type II: panoesophageal pressurisation 20% or more; type III: premature contractions 20% or more.`,`EGJOO; disorders of peristalsis: absent contractility, DES, jackhammer, IEM.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Bridge: nifedipine or nitrates sublingual; botulinum toxin 100 units for frail.`,`Definitive: PD 30-35-40 mm, LHM with Dor, POEM (preferred for type III).`,`Follow-up: Eckardt score, TBE, PPI for post-POEM reflux, cancer vigilance.`]}],mustDraw:[`HRM clouse plot sketches of types I, II and III`,`Barium bird-beak diagram`,`Treatment algorithm by subtype`],markSplit:[{part:`Aetiopathogenesis`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features and investigations`,marks:3},{part:`Chicago classification`,marks:2},{part:`Management`,marks:3}],keywords:[`achalasia`,`IRP`,`Chicago 4.0`,`POEM`,`Heller myotomy`,`pneumatic dilatation`,`bird-beak`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-achalasia-motility-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on peroral endoscopic myotomy (POEM).`,openingLines:[`POEM is an endoscopic myotomy of the LOS and distal oesophagus performed through a submucosal tunnel, without skin incisions.`,`It is a first-line treatment for achalasia and the preferred one for type III.`],answer:[{heading:`Technique`,points:[`Mucosal incision, submucosal tunnel to 2-3 cm beyond the GEJ, circular (or full-thickness) myotomy, clip closure.`]},{heading:`Indications`,points:[`All achalasia subtypes, especially type III; failed Heller or PD; spastic disorders (jackhammer, DES) in selected cases.`]},{heading:`Outcomes and complications`,points:[`Success about 90-95% at 2 years; beats PD (92% vs 54%), non-inferior to LHM.`,`Reflux oesophagitis in up to 40%+; capnoperitoneum, mucosal injury, bleeding.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of submucosal tunnel and myotomy across the GEJ`],markSplit:[{part:`Technique`,marks:2},{part:`Indications`,marks:1},{part:`Outcomes and complications`,marks:2}],keywords:[`POEM`,`submucosal tunnel`,`type III achalasia`,`post-POEM reflux`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Diseases of the Esophagus`,`Yadlapati R, et al. Chicago Classification version 4.0. Neurogastroenterol Motil, 2021`,`Vaezi MF, et al. ACG Clinical Guideline: Diagnosis and Management of Achalasia. Am J Gastroenterol, 2020`,`Oude Nijhuis RAB, et al. European guidelines on achalasia: UEG and ESNM recommendations. United European Gastroenterol J, 2020`,`Werner YB, et al. Endoscopic or surgical myotomy in patients with idiopathic achalasia. N Engl J Med, 2019`,`Ponds FA, et al. Effect of POEM vs pneumatic dilation on symptom severity in achalasia. JAMA, 2019`,`Gyawali CP, et al. Updates to the modern diagnosis of GERD: the Lyon Consensus 2.0. Gut, 2024`]}),e.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-eosinophilic-oesophagitis`,title:`Eosinophilic oesophagitis and pill/infective oesophagitis`,oneLiner:`**Eosinophilic oesophagitis (EoE)** is a chronic, **Th2 (type 2) immune, food-antigen-driven** oesophageal disease defined by **symptoms of oesophageal dysfunction plus at least 15 eosinophils per high-power field** after other causes are excluded, treated with **PPI, swallowed topical steroids, dupilumab or elimination diet**; **pill and infective (Candida, CMV, HSV) oesophagitis** present mainly with **odynophagia** and are diagnosed by endoscopy with biopsy.`,frequency:`less-common`,keywords:[`eosinophilic oesophagitis`,`EoE`,`15 eosinophils per hpf`,`EREFS`,`trachealisation`,`budesonide orodispersible`,`fluticasone swallowed`,`dupilumab`,`six-food elimination diet`,`milk elimination`,`pill oesophagitis`,`doxycycline`,`Candida oesophagitis`,`fluconazole`,`CMV oesophagitis`,`ganciclovir`,`HSV oesophagitis`,`aciclovir`,`odynophagia`,`food bolus impaction`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**EoE** is a chronic, immune/antigen-mediated oesophageal disease characterised clinically by **symptoms of oesophageal dysfunction** and histologically by **eosinophil-predominant inflammation of at least 15 eosinophils per high-power field (about 60 per mm2)**, with other causes excluded. [AGREE consensus 2018]`,`The **AGREE consensus (2018)** removed the requirement for a failed PPI trial - **PPI-responsive oesophageal eosinophilia is now simply EoE** that responds to PPI. [AGREE consensus 2018]`,`Prevalence in Western countries has risen to about **40-60 per 100,000**, now the commonest cause of **food bolus impaction in young adults** and second only to GERD as a cause of chronic oesophagitis. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`It is **3 times commoner in males**, peaks in **childhood and age 20-40**, and **most patients are atopic** (asthma, allergic rhinitis, eczema, IgE-mediated food allergy). [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**India**: EoE is reported less often than in the West, but Indian prospective biopsy series show it is **under-diagnosed** because biopsies are not taken from normal-looking oesophagus in dysphagia. [Indian tertiary series]`,`**Infective oesophagitis** (Candida, CMV, HSV) occurs mainly in **HIV, diabetes, steroid or inhaled-steroid use, chemotherapy and transplant** recipients; **Candida** is by far the commonest. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pill oesophagitis** accounts for a large share of acute odynophagia in otherwise healthy adults - **doxycycline** is the commonest culprit in Indian practice. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**EoE triggers**: dietary antigens - most often **milk, wheat, egg, soy, nuts and seafood** - and probably aeroallergens (seasonal flares). [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Genetic risk**: family clustering; polymorphisms in **TSLP** and **CAPN14** (oesophagus-specific); higher risk in connective tissue disorders (Marfan, Ehlers-Danlos). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Early-life factors** linked to EoE: caesarean birth, early antibiotics, formula feeding, NICU admission - all suggesting a microbiome role. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Oral immunotherapy for peanut** can induce EoE. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Candida risk**: HIV (CD4 below 200), diabetes, **inhaled or systemic steroids**, antibiotics, PPIs, achalasia (stasis), malignancy and chemotherapy. [IDSA Candida 2016]`,`**CMV and HSV risk**: advanced HIV (**CMV with CD4 below 50**), solid organ and stem-cell transplant, high-dose steroids; HSV also occurs in **immunocompetent young adults**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pill culprits**: **doxycycline and tetracycline**, clindamycin, **potassium chloride**, **NSAIDs and aspirin**, **bisphosphonates (alendronate)**, ferrous sulphate, ascorbic acid, quinidine, zidovudine and **dabigatran**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pill risk factors**: taking tablets **lying down, at bedtime, with little water**, older age, left atrial enlargement or achalasia slowing transit. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Food antigens cross a **defective epithelial barrier** (low filaggrin, desmoglein-1) and trigger a **type 2 response**: TSLP from epithelium activates Th2 cells and ILC2s to release **IL-4, IL-5 and IL-13**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**IL-13** induces **eotaxin-3** from epithelium, which recruits eosinophils, and **calpain-14** which further damages the barrier - the reason why **dupilumab (blocks the IL-4 receptor alpha shared by IL-4 and IL-13)** works. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`Eosinophils and mast cells release TGF-beta, causing **subepithelial fibrosis, smooth-muscle dysfunction and remodelling**: inflammation without treatment progresses to **rings and strictures** (about 10% per decade of untreated disease). [ACG EoE 2025]`,`This 'inflammatory to fibrostenotic' progression explains why **children present with feeding difficulty, vomiting and pain**, while **adults present with dysphagia and impaction**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PPIs** work in EoE partly by **anti-inflammatory effects independent of acid** - they reduce IL-13-stimulated eotaxin-3 expression - not only by treating coexistent reflux. [AGREE consensus 2018]`,`**Pill injury**: an acidic (doxycycline, ascorbic acid), alkaline (alendronate) or hyperosmolar (KCl) tablet lodges where the oesophagus is narrowed - **the aortic arch and left main bronchus (mid-oesophagus)** - and causes a local discrete ulcer. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Infective oesophagitis**: Candida forms adherent plaques; **HSV infects squamous epithelium** (so biopsy the ulcer **edge**); **CMV infects endothelial and stromal cells** in the ulcer **base** (so biopsy the base). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and grading`,points:[`**EREFS score** grades endoscopic EoE: **E**dema (loss of vascular pattern), **R**ings (trachealisation), **E**xudates (white plaques), **F**urrows (linear), **S**tricture. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Phenotypes**: **inflammatory** (oedema, furrows, exudates), **fibrostenotic** (rings, narrow-calibre oesophagus, stricture) and mixed. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Histological remission**: **under 15 eos/hpf** is the usual threshold; **6 or fewer eos/hpf** is deep remission, used in trials. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**EoE Histology Scoring System (EoEHSS)** grades eight features beyond peak count - basal zone hyperplasia, eosinophil abscesses, surface layering, dilated intercellular spaces, lamina propria fibrosis. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Kodsi classification** grades Candida oesophagitis **I-IV**: from a few plaques under 2 mm (I) to confluent plaques with ulceration and luminal narrowing (IV). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Odynophagia classification by cause**: infective, pill, caustic, radiation, reflux, malignant - each has a characteristic endoscopic picture. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Adult EoE**: **intermittent solid-food dysphagia**, often for years, and **food bolus impaction** - the presenting event in about a third to a half. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`Ask about **adaptive eating behaviours (IMPACT)**: Imbibe fluids with meals, Modify food (cut small, puree), Prolong meal times, Avoid hard foods, Chew excessively, Turn away tablets. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Children with EoE**: feeding refusal, vomiting, abdominal pain, failure to thrive; adolescents: heartburn and dysphagia. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Heartburn and chest pain** not responding to PPI may be EoE. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Infective oesophagitis**: **odynophagia** (often severe, worse with each swallow), dysphagia, retrosternal pain; ask for HIV risk, recent steroids or inhalers, diabetes, chemotherapy, transplant. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pill oesophagitis**: **sudden retrosternal pain and odynophagia** hours after taking a tablet at bedtime with little water; often a young person on **doxycycline for acne** or an older person on **alendronate or KCl**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HSV** in immunocompetent patients can follow a prodrome of fever and **herpes labialis**; **CMV** has a more gradual onset with fever and weight loss. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**EoE**: usually normal; look for **atopy** - wheeze, eczema, allergic rhinitis. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Oral thrush** (white plaques that scrape off leaving a raw base) in a patient with odynophagia strongly suggests **Candida oesophagitis** - but its absence does not exclude it. [IDSA Candida 2016]`,`**Herpes labialis or oral ulcers** may accompany HSV oesophagitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HIV stigmata**: oral hairy leukoplakia, generalised lymphadenopathy, seborrhoeic dermatitis, wasting. [NACO 2021]`,`**CMV**: look for retinitis (fundoscopy) and colitis in advanced HIV. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dehydration and weight loss** when odynophagia prevents oral intake. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Oesophageal eosinophilia is not always EoE**: GERD, **eosinophilic gastritis/gastroenteritis**, hypereosinophilic syndrome, Crohn disease, achalasia, drug hypersensitivity, parasitic infection, connective tissue disease and graft-versus-host disease. [AGREE consensus 2018]`,`**GERD versus EoE**: GERD eosinophils are usually few and **distal only**; EoE is often **proximal and distal** with furrows and rings, and in a younger atopic patient - but the two coexist and interact. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Candida versus EoE exudates**: Candida plaques are larger, adherent and show **hyphae on brushing**; EoE exudates are tiny white specks (eosinophil micro-abscesses). [Harrison 22e]`,`**HSV versus CMV**: HSV gives **small, shallow 'volcano' ulcers** with raised edges in the mid-oesophagus; CMV gives **large, deep, linear or longitudinal ulcers** in the distal oesophagus. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HIV idiopathic oesophageal ulcer**: large ulcers with negative viral studies - responds to **prednisolone** (or thalidomide). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pill ulcer versus cancer**: pill ulcers are **discrete, well-demarcated, often 'kissing' ulcers** at the aortic arch level and heal in days; biopsy any ulcer that persists. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cardiac pain**: severe retrosternal pain of pill oesophagitis can mimic acute coronary syndrome - **ECG first**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line`,points:[`**Upper GI endoscopy with biopsy** is the diagnostic test for EoE: **at least 6 biopsies from at least 2 levels** (proximal and distal oesophagus), targeting exudates and furrows, **even if the mucosa looks normal**. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`Biopsy the **stomach and duodenum** when there are GI symptoms beyond the oesophagus or abnormal mucosa, to look for **eosinophilic gastritis or duodenitis**. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`Endoscopy is normal in about **10-20%** of active EoE - one reason biopsies are mandatory in dysphagia. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Peripheral eosinophilia** is present in only a minority and total IgE may be raised; neither is diagnostic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Odynophagia**: check **HIV, CD4 count, blood sugar/HbA1c**, and drug history; in HIV with oral thrush, **empirical fluconazole** is reasonable before endoscopy. [IDSA Candida 2016]`,`**Endoscopy with brushings and biopsies** if odynophagia persists after 72 hours of empirical antifungal therapy, or in non-HIV patients. [IDSA Candida 2016]`,`**Biopsy technique** in ulcers: **edge for HSV** (multinucleated giant cells, Cowdry type A inclusions), **base for CMV** (enlarged cells with **owl's-eye** inclusions); send tissue for **immunohistochemistry and PCR**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - advanced`,points:[`**Allergy skin-prick tests and serum specific IgE** do **not** reliably identify EoE food triggers and are **not recommended** to guide elimination diets. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Barium oesophagram** shows a **narrow-calibre oesophagus** and long strictures that endoscopy underestimates - useful before dilatation in fibrostenotic disease. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**FLIP** measures oesophageal distensibility and predicts impaction risk in fibrostenotic EoE (research and tertiary use). [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Cytosponge and transnasal endoscopy** are emerging less-invasive monitoring tools for EoE. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Fungal culture and susceptibility** for Candida not responding to fluconazole (C. glabrata, C. krusei). [IDSA Candida 2016]`,`**CMV viral load** in blood supports but cannot replace tissue diagnosis of CMV oesophagitis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management of EoE in OPD`,points:[`Choose with the patient among the **'3 Ds'**: **drugs (PPI, topical steroid, dupilumab), diet, dilatation** - all are first-line options; repeat endoscopy with biopsies **8-12 weeks** after starting to confirm histological response. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**PPI**: **omeprazole 20 mg twice daily** (or equivalent; children 1-2 mg/kg/day) for **8-12 weeks**; histological response in about **40-50%**; if in remission, step down to the lowest effective dose. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Swallowed topical steroids**: **budesonide orodispersible tablet 1 mg twice daily** (Europe) or **budesonide oral suspension 2 mg twice daily** (FDA 2024) for 12 weeks, or **fluticasone MDI 440-880 mcg twice daily swallowed** (not inhaled); response about **60-90%**. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`Teach the patient to **swallow, not inhale**, and take **nothing by mouth for 30-60 minutes** afterward; in India, **budesonide respules mixed into a viscous slurry** (honey or sucralose) is a practical low-cost option. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Dupilumab** (anti-IL-4R alpha): **300 mg subcutaneously weekly** for ages 12 and above weighing 40 kg or more; weight-based dosing from **age 1 year (15 kg)** since 2024; used for steroid-refractory disease or atopic multimorbidity. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Elimination diet**: start with **one-food (milk) or two-food (milk, wheat) elimination** - a step-up approach - rather than six-food elimination; reintroduce foods one at a time with endoscopy after each. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Elemental (amino-acid formula) diet** gives the highest remission (above 90%) but is costly and poorly tolerated - mainly for children. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Maintenance therapy** is recommended for most patients in remission, because **EoE relapses in most when treatment stops** and fibrosis progresses silently. [ACG EoE 2025]`]},{heading:`Management of pill and infective oesophagitis; IPD and emergency`,points:[`**Pill oesophagitis**: stop or switch the drug (e.g. **doxycycline to a liquid or different agent**), sucralfate suspension **1 g four times daily** and PPI for 1-2 weeks; most heal within days. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Candida oesophagitis**: **fluconazole 200-400 mg (3-6 mg/kg) orally daily for 14-21 days**; IV fluconazole or an **echinocandin** if unable to swallow; refractory disease: **itraconazole solution 200 mg daily, posaconazole or voriconazole**. [IDSA Candida 2016]`,`**CMV oesophagitis**: **ganciclovir 5 mg/kg IV 12-hourly**, switching to **valganciclovir 900 mg orally twice daily** when swallowing improves, for **3-6 weeks**; foscarnet for resistance. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HSV oesophagitis**: **aciclovir 400 mg orally five times daily** (or **5 mg/kg IV 8-hourly** if unable to swallow) - **7-10 days** in immunocompetent and **14-21 days** in immunocompromised patients; valaciclovir 1 g three times daily is an alternative. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HIV**: start or optimise **antiretroviral therapy** as per NACO - immune recovery is the best prevention of recurrence; secondary fluconazole prophylaxis only for frequent recurrences. [NACO 2021]`,`**Food bolus impaction (EoE)**: endoscopic removal **within 6 hours** if complete obstruction, within 24 hours otherwise; take biopsies at the same procedure. [ESGE foreign body 2016]`,`**Dilatation in EoE**: safe with modern technique (perforation under 1%), relieves dysphagia in fibrostenotic disease, but **does not treat inflammation** - combine with medical or diet therapy. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Admit** when odynophagia prevents fluids, for IV antivirals or antifungals, and for impaction or suspected perforation. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Children**: EoE presents with feeding problems and vomiting; PPI and swallowed steroids are dosed by weight; **diet therapy** with a dietician is used more often; monitor growth. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Pregnancy**: PPIs and budesonide are generally considered acceptable when needed; **avoid fluconazole in high doses in the first trimester** (teratogenicity) - topical or alternative agents where possible. [IDSA Candida 2016]`,`**Asthma patients on inhaled steroids**: advise **mouth rinsing and spacer use** to prevent oral and oesophageal candidiasis. [GINA 2024]`,`**Elderly on alendronate, KCl or NSAIDs**: high pill-injury risk with reduced oesophageal motility - consider liquid forms or alternatives. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CKD**: adjust fluconazole, ganciclovir, valganciclovir and aciclovir doses for creatinine clearance; avoid KCl tablets where possible. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Transplant and chemotherapy**: low threshold for endoscopy and empirical cover; check drug interactions of azoles with tacrolimus and cyclosporine. [IDSA Candida 2016]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: where endoscopy is delayed, treat **thrush-associated odynophagia empirically with fluconazole** and refer if no response in 3 days. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**EoE**: strictures, narrow-calibre oesophagus, food impaction and **perforation (Boerhaave-type)** during impaction or forceful vomiting. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**EoE is chronic**: it is **not premalignant**, but relapse after stopping therapy is the rule and quality of life is impaired. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Topical steroid side effect**: **oesophageal candidiasis in about 5-15%**, usually asymptomatic; adrenal suppression is uncommon at standard doses. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Pill oesophagitis complications**: stricture (KCl, NSAIDs), bleeding, rarely perforation or fistula. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CMV and HSV**: bleeding, perforation, tracheo-oesophageal fistula; CMV recurrence when immunosuppression persists. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Candida**: dissemination in neutropenic patients; **azole resistance** with repeated courses. [IDSA Candida 2016]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and prevention`,points:[`**Refer** suspected EoE (young atopic patient with dysphagia or impaction) for endoscopy with biopsies; refer infective oesophagitis not responding to 72 hours of fluconazole. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Re-endoscope with biopsies 8-12 weeks** after starting or changing EoE therapy - symptoms do not reliably reflect histology. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Long-term EoE follow-up**: symptom review and periodic endoscopy (for example yearly or when symptoms change) on maintenance therapy. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Involve an allergist and dietician** for elimination diets and coexisting atopy. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**HIV**: link to the ART centre; monitor CD4 count and viral load. [NACO 2021]`,`**Pill hygiene**: take tablets **upright with at least 200 mL water**, remain upright for **30 minutes** (alendronate: fasting, upright 30 minutes), and never at bedtime lying down. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Inhaled steroids**: rinse and spit after each dose and use a spacer. [GINA 2024]`,`**EoE**: explain it is a **chronic allergic disease** like asthma; adherence to maintenance prevents strictures; chew well and avoid dry meat and bread during flares. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**HIV prevention and early ART** under NACO prevent opportunistic oesophagitis. [NACO 2021]`,`**Diabetes control** under **NP-NCD** reduces Candida infections. [NP-NCD 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ACG EoE guideline (2025)**: PPI, swallowed topical steroids, dupilumab and diet are all first-line options; **step-up (one- or two-food) elimination** preferred to six-food; maintenance therapy recommended. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Dupilumab**: FDA approval for EoE in **ages 12 and above (2022)** and **ages 1-11 weighing at least 15 kg (2024)** - the first biologic for EoE. [FDA 2024]`,`**Budesonide oral suspension** approved by FDA in **2024** for ages 11 and above (12 weeks); **budesonide orodispersible tablet** approved by EMA for induction and maintenance in adults. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**AGA-JTF EoE guideline (2020)** conditionally recommends PPI and strongly recommends topical steroids; ACG 2025 expands this with biologics. [AGA EoE 2020]`,`**SOFEED trial (2023)**: **one-food (milk) elimination was similar to six-food elimination** in adults for histological remission, supporting a simpler start. [Kliewer, Lancet Gastroenterol Hepatol 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Viva: **'Diagnostic criteria for EoE'** - symptoms of oesophageal dysfunction, **15 or more eos/hpf**, other causes excluded; PPI trial no longer required. [AGREE consensus 2018]`,`Viva: **'Where do you biopsy HSV and CMV ulcers?'** - **HSV at the edge, CMV at the base**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: **'Commonest drug causing pill oesophagitis in India?'** - doxycycline. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: **'Trachealisation'** (ringed oesophagus) and **'crêpe-paper oesophagus'** (mucosal tearing during endoscopy) are classic EoE signs. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Common mistake**: not taking biopsies from a normal-looking oesophagus in dysphagia or after food impaction. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Common mistake**: using allergy skin tests to plan EoE diets - they are not reliable. [ACG EoE 2025]`,`**Common mistake**: inhaling rather than swallowing topical steroid. [ACG EoE 2025]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for EoE and infective oesophagitis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Omeprazole`,`20 mg oral twice daily (children 1-2 mg/kg/day)`,`8-12 weeks, then lowest effective maintenance`,`Headache, C. difficile, hypomagnesaemia`],[`Budesonide orodispersible tablet`,`1 mg twice daily dissolved on tongue`,`6-12 weeks induction; 0.5-1 mg twice daily maintenance`,`Oesophageal candidiasis; nil by mouth 30 min after`],[`Budesonide oral suspension`,`2 mg oral twice daily`,`12 weeks`,`Candidiasis, rarely adrenal suppression`],[`Fluticasone (swallowed MDI)`,`440-880 mcg twice daily sprayed into mouth and swallowed`,`8-12 weeks`,`Candidiasis; must not be inhaled`],[`Dupilumab`,`300 mg SC weekly (12 years and over, 40 kg or more)`,`Long term; reassess at 24 weeks`,`Injection reactions, conjunctivitis, eosinophilia`],[`Fluconazole`,`200-400 mg (3-6 mg/kg) oral daily`,`14-21 days`,`QT prolongation, hepatotoxicity, interactions (tacrolimus, warfarin)`],[`Ganciclovir / valganciclovir`,`5 mg/kg IV 12-hourly, then valganciclovir 900 mg oral twice daily`,`3-6 weeks`,`Neutropenia, thrombocytopenia, renal dosing`],[`Aciclovir`,`400 mg oral five times daily or 5 mg/kg IV 8-hourly`,`7-10 days (14-21 days if immunocompromised)`,`Renal impairment, crystalluria - hydrate`]]},{heading:`Infective and pill oesophagitis compared`,columns:[`Cause`,`Typical host`,`Endoscopic picture`,`Diagnosis`],rows:[[`Candida`,`HIV, diabetes, steroids, inhaled steroids`,`White adherent plaques, Kodsi I-IV`,`Brushings: hyphae and yeast`],[`HSV`,`Immunocompromised or healthy young adults`,`Small shallow volcano ulcers, mid-oesophagus`,`Biopsy edge: giant cells, Cowdry A, PCR`],[`CMV`,`Advanced HIV (CD4 below 50), transplant`,`Large deep linear ulcers, distal`,`Biopsy base: owl's-eye inclusions, IHC`],[`HIV idiopathic ulcer`,`Advanced HIV`,`Large ulcers, viral studies negative`,`Exclusion; responds to prednisolone`],[`Pill injury`,`Doxycycline, KCl, NSAID, alendronate users`,`Discrete or kissing ulcers at aortic arch level`,`History plus endoscopy`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of oesophageal eosinophilia`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating feature`],rows:[[`Eosinophilic oesophagitis`,`15 or more eos/hpf proximal and distal, rings and furrows, atopy`],[`GERD`,`Fewer eosinophils, distal only, erosions, abnormal pH study`],[`Eosinophilic gastritis or gastroenteritis`,`Eosinophils in stomach or duodenum, abdominal pain, diarrhoea`],[`Hypereosinophilic syndrome`,`Blood eosinophils above 1500/microL with organ damage`],[`Crohn disease`,`Granulomas, ileocolonic disease`],[`Drug hypersensitivity`,`Temporal link to drug, resolves on stopping`],[`Achalasia`,`Raised IRP and aperistalsis on HRM`]]},{heading:`EoE endoscopy and histology`,columns:[`Item`,`What it means`,`Practical point`],rows:[[`Oedema`,`Loss of vascular markings`,`Inflammatory phenotype`],[`Rings (trachealisation)`,`Fixed or feline rings`,`Fibrostenotic; dilatation may be needed`],[`Exudates`,`White specks of eosinophil micro-abscess`,`Target for biopsy`],[`Furrows`,`Linear vertical lines`,`Commonest finding`],[`Stricture`,`Focal or narrow-calibre oesophagus`,`Barium shows length better`],[`Peak count 15 or more eos/hpf`,`Diagnostic threshold`,`6 biopsies from 2 levels`],[`Under 15 eos/hpf on treatment`,`Histological remission`,`6 or fewer = deep remission`]]}],redFlags:[`Food bolus impaction with inability to swallow saliva - endoscopy within 6 hours`,`Odynophagia preventing fluid intake or causing dehydration`,`Chest pain with fever or surgical emphysema after impaction or vomiting - perforation`,`Haematemesis from an oesophageal ulcer`,`Odynophagia in advanced HIV, neutropenia or transplant - needs urgent endoscopy and IV therapy`,`Persistent ulcer after stopping the culprit pill - biopsy to exclude cancer`],pearls:[`**EoE = 15**: 15 or more eosinophils per hpf, plus symptoms, minus other causes.`,`**Six from two**: at least 6 biopsies from 2 levels, even from a normal-looking oesophagus.`,`**3 Ds of EoE**: Drugs (PPI, steroid, dupilumab), Diet, Dilatation.`,`**Swallow, don't inhale**: topical steroid in EoE.`,`**HSV - edge, CMV - centre (base)**: where to biopsy the ulcer.`,`**Doxycycline at bedtime** is the classic pill oesophagitis - 200 mL water, upright 30 minutes.`,`**Thrush plus odynophagia in HIV** - fluconazole first, endoscope if no response in 72 hours.`,`**Dupilumab blocks IL-4R alpha** - shuts both IL-4 and IL-13 pathways that drive EoE.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-eosinophilic-oesophagitis-q1`,stem:`A 24-year-old man with asthma has had intermittent dysphagia for meat for 3 years and two episodes of food bolus impaction. Endoscopy shows rings and linear furrows. What histological finding confirms the likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Intestinal metaplasia with goblet cells`,`15 or more eosinophils per high-power field`,`Multinucleated giant cells at the ulcer edge`,`Fungal hyphae on brushing`],answer:1,explanation:`A young atopic man with intermittent solid dysphagia, impactions, rings and furrows has eosinophilic oesophagitis, defined histologically by at least 15 eosinophils per high-power field once other causes are excluded. Goblet-cell metaplasia defines Barrett oesophagus, giant cells point to HSV, and hyphae indicate Candida.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-eosinophilic-oesophagitis-q2`,stem:`A patient with advanced HIV (CD4 count 30 cells/microL) has severe odynophagia. Endoscopy shows large, deep, linear ulcers in the distal oesophagus. Where should biopsies be taken and what is the likely organism?`,options:[`Ulcer edge; herpes simplex virus`,`Normal mucosa; Candida albicans`,`Ulcer base; cytomegalovirus`,`Ulcer edge; Mycobacterium tuberculosis`],answer:2,explanation:`Large, deep, linear distal ulcers in a patient with CD4 below 50 are typical of CMV, which infects endothelial and stromal cells, so the base yields the owl's-eye inclusions. HSV produces small volcano ulcers biopsied at the edge, Candida forms plaques rather than deep ulcers, and tuberculous oesophagitis is rare and usually secondary to nodes.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-eosinophilic-oesophagitis-q3`,stem:`A 19-year-old student taking doxycycline for acne wakes with severe retrosternal pain on swallowing. She took her tablet at bedtime with a sip of water. ECG is normal. What is the best management?`,options:[`Urgent CT angiogram of the aorta`,`Start fluconazole for presumed Candida`,`Swallowed fluticasone for eosinophilic oesophagitis`,`Stop or change the doxycycline, give sucralfate and PPI, and teach pill hygiene`],answer:3,explanation:`Acute odynophagia after a bedtime doxycycline taken with little water is classic pill oesophagitis; it heals within days once the drug is stopped or changed, with sucralfate and PPI for comfort and advice to take tablets upright with 200 mL water. Aortic imaging is not indicated with a normal ECG and typical history, and nothing suggests Candida or EoE.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-eosinophilic-oesophagitis-q4`,stem:`Which statement about the treatment of eosinophilic oesophagitis is correct according to current guidelines?`,options:[`Allergy skin-prick testing should guide which foods to eliminate`,`A failed 8-week PPI trial is required before EoE can be diagnosed`,`Dupilumab blocks the IL-4 receptor alpha and is approved for EoE from age 1 year`,`Topical steroids should be inhaled deeply to reach the oesophagus`],answer:2,explanation:`Dupilumab blocks IL-4 receptor alpha, interrupting both IL-4 and IL-13 signalling, and has been approved for EoE in adolescents and adults since 2022 and in children from age 1 (15 kg or more) since 2024. Skin tests do not predict EoE triggers, the PPI-trial requirement was dropped by the AGREE consensus in 2018, and topical steroids must be swallowed, not inhaled.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-eosinophilic-oesophagitis-q5`,stem:`An adult with newly diagnosed EoE prefers dietary therapy over drugs. According to recent evidence, which dietary approach is the most reasonable first step?`,options:[`Six-food elimination diet from the start`,`Milk elimination (one-food) diet with repeat biopsies after 6-12 weeks`,`Elimination of all foods positive on serum specific IgE`,`Elemental amino-acid formula as the sole diet for 6 months`],answer:1,explanation:`The SOFEED trial showed that eliminating milk alone gave histological remission comparable to six-food elimination in adults, so a step-up approach starting with one- or two-food elimination is preferred, followed by endoscopy with biopsies. Six-food elimination is burdensome as a start, IgE-guided diets are unreliable, and elemental diets are effective but impractical for most adults.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-eosinophilic-oesophagitis-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Discuss the pathogenesis, diagnosis and management of eosinophilic oesophagitis. Add a note on infective causes of odynophagia.`,openingLines:[`Eosinophilic oesophagitis is a chronic, type 2 immune, food-antigen-driven disease defined by oesophageal dysfunction and at least 15 eosinophils per high-power field after excluding other causes.`,`It is treated with PPI, swallowed topical steroids, dupilumab or elimination diet, with dilatation for strictures; infective oesophagitis presents with odynophagia and needs biopsy-guided therapy.`],answer:[{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Barrier defect, TSLP, Th2 and ILC2 cells, IL-4, IL-5, IL-13, eotaxin-3; remodelling and fibrosis over time.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Young atopic male, intermittent solid dysphagia, impaction, IMPACT eating behaviours; children with feeding problems and vomiting.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Endoscopy: EREFS (oedema, rings, exudates, furrows, stricture); 6 biopsies from 2 levels; 15 or more eos/hpf; exclude GERD and other causes.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`PPI omeprazole 20 mg twice daily 8-12 weeks; budesonide 1 mg twice daily or fluticasone 880 mcg twice daily swallowed.`,`Dupilumab 300 mg SC weekly; milk or two-food elimination; dilatation for strictures; maintenance and repeat biopsy at 8-12 weeks.`]},{heading:`Infective odynophagia`,points:[`Candida: plaques, fluconazole 200-400 mg 14-21 days.`,`HSV: volcano ulcers, biopsy edge, aciclovir; CMV: deep linear ulcers, biopsy base, ganciclovir 3-6 weeks.`]}],mustDraw:[`Pathogenesis flow: antigen, barrier, Th2 cytokines, eosinophils, fibrosis`,`Table of Candida vs HSV vs CMV`],markSplit:[{part:`Pathogenesis`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features and diagnosis`,marks:3},{part:`Treatment of EoE`,marks:3},{part:`Infective odynophagia`,marks:2}],keywords:[`EoE`,`15 eos/hpf`,`EREFS`,`budesonide`,`dupilumab`,`elimination diet`,`Candida`,`CMV`,`HSV`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-eosinophilic-oesophagitis-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on pill-induced oesophagitis.`,openingLines:[`Pill oesophagitis is direct mucosal injury from a tablet that lodges in the oesophagus, usually at the level of the aortic arch.`,`It presents with sudden odynophagia and retrosternal pain and heals within days once the drug is stopped.`],answer:[{heading:`Culprit drugs and risk factors`,points:[`Doxycycline, tetracycline, KCl, NSAIDs, alendronate, iron, ascorbic acid, clindamycin, dabigatran.`,`Bedtime dosing, lying down, little water, elderly, slow oesophageal transit.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Typical history is enough; endoscopy if severe, bleeding or persistent: discrete or kissing ulcers mid-oesophagus.`]},{heading:`Management and prevention`,points:[`Stop or change drug, sucralfate 1 g four times daily, PPI, analgesia; liquid formulations.`,`200 mL water, upright 30 minutes, never at bedtime.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of oesophagus showing aortic arch and left main bronchus narrowing where pills lodge`],markSplit:[{part:`Drugs and risk factors`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:1},{part:`Management and prevention`,marks:2}],keywords:[`pill oesophagitis`,`doxycycline`,`alendronate`,`KCl`,`odynophagia`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Diseases of the Esophagus`,`Dellon ES, et al. ACG Clinical Guideline: Diagnosis and Management of Eosinophilic Esophagitis. Am J Gastroenterol, 2025`,`Dellon ES, et al. Updated international consensus diagnostic criteria for eosinophilic esophagitis (AGREE). Gastroenterology, 2018`,`Hirano I, et al. AGA Institute and Joint Task Force on Allergy-Immunology Practice Parameters Clinical Guidelines for the Management of Eosinophilic Esophagitis, 2020`,`Kliewer KL, et al. One-food versus six-food elimination diet therapy for adults with EoE (SOFEED). Lancet Gastroenterol Hepatol, 2023`,`Pappas PG, et al. IDSA Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Candidiasis, 2016`,`NACO National Guidelines for HIV Care and Treatment, 2021`]}),e.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-barrett-oesophageal-cancer`,title:`Barrett oesophagus and oesophageal cancer`,oneLiner:`**Barrett oesophagus** is replacement of distal squamous epithelium by **intestinal metaplasia (goblet cells) extending at least 1 cm above the gastro-oesophageal junction** - the precursor of **oesophageal adenocarcinoma** - described by the **Prague C and M criteria**, sampled by the **Seattle protocol** and managed by **dysplasia-graded surveillance and endoscopic eradication**; in India most oesophageal cancer is **squamous cell carcinoma** of the tobacco-alcohol-hot-beverage belts, staged by **AJCC 8th edition** and treated with **neoadjuvant CROSS chemoradiation or perioperative FLOT plus surgery**.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`Barrett oesophagus`,`intestinal metaplasia`,`Prague C and M`,`Seattle protocol`,`low-grade dysplasia`,`high-grade dysplasia`,`radiofrequency ablation`,`endoscopic mucosal resection`,`ESD`,`oesophageal adenocarcinoma`,`oesophageal squamous cell carcinoma`,`Siewert classification`,`CROSS`,`FLOT`,`ESOPEC`,`nivolumab adjuvant`,`Ivor Lewis oesophagectomy`,`SEMS`,`Lugol chromoendoscopy`,`AJCC 8th edition`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Barrett oesophagus (BE)** per ACG 2022: **salmon-coloured columnar mucosa extending at least 1 cm proximal to the GEJ** (top of the gastric folds) with **intestinal metaplasia** on biopsy; BSG accepts columnar mucosa without goblet cells, a difference examiners like. [ACG Barrett 2022]`,`**An irregular Z-line (under 1 cm)** with intestinal metaplasia is **not Barrett** and needs **no biopsies or surveillance**. [ESGE Barrett 2023]`,`BE is found in about **5-15%** of Western patients with chronic GERD and about **1-2%** of the general Western population; **Indian series report BE much less often**, and adenocarcinoma is far rarer than squamous cancer in India. [ACG Barrett 2022]`,`Annual progression of **non-dysplastic BE to adenocarcinoma is only about 0.2-0.5%**, so most patients with BE will never develop cancer; the risk rises steeply with **dysplasia** and **segment length**. [ACG Barrett 2022]`,`**Oesophageal cancer worldwide**: about **5.1 lakh new cases and 4.4 lakh deaths (GLOBOCAN 2022)**, with very high case fatality; **squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) is about 85%** of cases globally, and **adenocarcinoma (EAC)** dominates in Western countries. [GLOBOCAN 2022]`,`**India**: oesophageal cancer is among the leading cancers in both sexes, overwhelmingly **SCC**; the highest age-adjusted rates are in the **North-East (East Khasi Hills of Meghalaya, Assam, Mizoram)** and **Kashmir**. [ICMR-NCDIR NCRP 2020]`,`Overall **5-year survival is about 20%**, because most patients present with dysphagia only after the tumour has narrowed the lumen and spread. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Barrett risk factors**: chronic GERD, **male sex, age above 50, white race, central obesity, smoking**, hiatus hernia and a **first-degree relative with BE or EAC**. [ACG Barrett 2022]`,`**Adenocarcinoma risk** follows Barrett: GERD, obesity, smoking; **H. pylori infection is inversely associated** (it lowers acid), as are NSAIDs and statins in observational data. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Squamous cancer risk - tobacco and alcohol** (synergistic), plus **betel quid and areca**, **very hot beverages above 65 degrees C**, low fruit and vegetable intake, nitrosamines and poor oral hygiene. [IARC 2016]`,`**Indian regional risks**: **noon chai (salted hot tea), hookah and sun-dried, smoked foods in Kashmir**; **smoked meat and fish, fermented soybean, tuibur (tobacco smoke-infused water) and kwai (betel quid) in the North-East**. [ICMR-NCDIR NCRP 2020]`,`**Premalignant conditions for SCC**: **achalasia**, **corrosive (lye) stricture** (after 20-40 years), **Plummer-Vinson syndrome**, **tylosis (focal non-epidermolytic palmoplantar keratoderma, RHBDF2)** and prior head and neck squamous cancer. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Field cancerisation**: patients with head and neck SCC carry a high risk of a **synchronous or metachronous oesophageal SCC** - screen with Lugol chromoendoscopy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HPV** has an uncertain role in oesophageal SCC in high-incidence areas. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Chronic exposure of the distal oesophagus to **acid and bile** injures squamous epithelium; healing occurs by **columnar metaplasia** from stem cells at the GEJ and oesophageal glands - a more acid-resistant lining. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CDX2** activation drives the **intestinal phenotype with goblet cells** - the hallmark that defines Barrett in ACG criteria. [Harrison 22e]`,`Progression runs **metaplasia, low-grade dysplasia, high-grade dysplasia, intramucosal carcinoma, invasive adenocarcinoma**, with early **TP53** loss and **CDKN2A (p16)** inactivation; **aberrant p53 immunostaining** helps grade dysplasia. [ACG Barrett 2022]`,`**SCC** arises from squamous dysplasia in a field damaged by carcinogens; tobacco nitrosamines and acetaldehyde from alcohol (worse in **ALDH2-deficient** people) produce DNA adducts; heat injury promotes proliferation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Site**: SCC is mostly **mid-oesophagus** (and upper third), EAC is **distal oesophagus and GEJ** - the reason SCC more often invades the **airway (tracheo-oesophageal fistula)** and recurrent laryngeal nerve. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rich submucosal lymphatics** allow early nodal spread: nodal metastasis occurs in **under 5% of T1a but 20-40% of T1b** tumours, the logic behind endoscopic versus surgical treatment of early cancer. [NCCN Oesophageal 2025]`,`**Why dysphagia comes late**: the oesophagus has no serosa and is distensible; solid dysphagia appears when the lumen narrows below about **13 mm (more than half to two-thirds of the circumference)**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and staging`,points:[`**Prague C and M**: **C** = circumferential extent, **M** = maximum extent of Barrett above the GEJ, in cm (e.g. C2M5); **long-segment** BE is 3 cm or more, **short-segment** under 3 cm. [ACG Barrett 2022]`,`**Dysplasia grades**: **non-dysplastic, indefinite, low-grade (LGD), high-grade (HGD)** and intramucosal carcinoma; any dysplasia must be **confirmed by a second expert GI pathologist**. [ACG Barrett 2022]`,`**Paris classification** describes visible lesions: 0-I (polypoid), 0-IIa/b/c (flat elevated, flat, depressed), 0-III (excavated) - type 0-IIc and 0-III predict submucosal invasion. [ESGE Barrett 2023]`,`**AJCC 8th edition TNM**: **T1a** lamina propria or muscularis mucosae, **T1b** submucosa, **T2** muscularis propria, **T3** adventitia, **T4a** resectable (pleura, pericardium, azygos, diaphragm, peritoneum), **T4b** unresectable (aorta, vertebra, trachea). [AJCC 8e 2017]`,`**N staging** counts nodes: **N1 = 1-2, N2 = 3-6, N3 = 7 or more** regional nodes; supraclavicular nodes in lower-third tumours count as M1. [AJCC 8e 2017]`,`AJCC 8th has **separate clinical (cTNM), pathological (pTNM) and post-neoadjuvant (ypTNM)** stage groups, and different grouping for SCC and adenocarcinoma. [AJCC 8e 2017]`,`**Siewert classification of GEJ adenocarcinoma**: **type I** centre 1-5 cm above the GEJ (treat as oesophageal), **type II** 1 cm above to 2 cm below (true cardia), **type III** 2-5 cm below (treat as gastric). [NCCN Oesophageal 2025]`,`**Submucosal depth** for endoscopic decisions: **sm1 (under 500 micrometres in EAC, under 200 in SCC)** with good differentiation and no lymphovascular invasion is low risk. [ESGE Barrett 2023]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history and examination`,points:[`**Barrett itself is asymptomatic**; patients have the symptoms of GERD, and up to **40% of EAC patients report no prior heartburn**. [ACG Barrett 2022]`,`**Progressive solid-food dysphagia** over weeks to months, progressing to soft food and liquids, is the classic cancer presentation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Weight loss** (often above 10%) from dysphagia and cancer cachexia - the strongest adverse prognostic symptom. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Odynophagia** (ulceration), **regurgitation**, retrosternal pain, **iron deficiency anaemia** or overt bleeding. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Signs of local invasion**: **hoarseness** (left recurrent laryngeal nerve), **cough on swallowing** (tracheo-oesophageal fistula), **hiccups** (phrenic nerve), Horner syndrome, back pain (prevertebral invasion). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Metastatic symptoms**: jaundice and hepatomegaly, bone pain, neck nodes; **hypercalcaemia** (PTHrP) in SCC. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cachexia, dehydration and pallor** - assess nutritional status with weight, BMI and the PG-SGA. [ESPEN 2021]`,`**Supraclavicular lymph nodes** (Virchow node on the left) mean metastatic or N3/M1 disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hepatomegaly, ascites** and jaundice mean liver or peritoneal spread. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hoarse voice** with vocal cord palsy on indirect laryngoscopy - mediastinal invasion. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Peptic stricture**: long heartburn history, smooth tapering stricture, benign biopsies - **biopsy every stricture**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Achalasia vs pseudoachalasia**: a cardia adenocarcinoma can mimic achalasia - suspect with older age, short history and weight loss. [ACG achalasia 2020]`,`**Benign tumours (leiomyoma)**: smooth submucosal bulge with normal mucosa; EUS shows origin from the muscularis propria. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Extrinsic compression**: mediastinal nodes (TB, lymphoma, lung cancer), left atrium, aorta - CT distinguishes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Gastric cardia cancer extending up (Siewert III)** - treat as gastric cancer; see the gastric cancer topic. [NCCN Oesophageal 2025]`,`**Corrosive stricture with a new change in dysphagia** after many years - suspect superimposed SCC. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Gastric heterotopia (inlet patch)** in the upper oesophagus is benign and is not Barrett. [ACG Barrett 2022]`]},{heading:`Investigations - screening and Barrett work-up`,points:[`**ACG screening**: a single screening endoscopy for patients with **chronic GERD plus 3 or more risk factors** (male, age above 50, white race, tobacco, obesity, family history of BE or EAC). [ACG Barrett 2022]`,`A **non-endoscopic swallowable capsule-sponge device with a biomarker (e.g. Cytosponge with TFF3)** is an acceptable alternative to endoscopy for BE screening. [ACG Barrett 2022]`,`**High-definition white-light endoscopy with chromoendoscopy (NBI or acetic acid)**, a careful inspection time, and **Prague C and M** documentation are the standard. [ACG Barrett 2022]`,`**Seattle protocol**: **targeted biopsies of any visible lesion first**, then **4-quadrant random biopsies every 2 cm** of Barrett (every **1 cm** in known or suspected dysplasia). [ACG Barrett 2022]`,`**Do not biopsy for dysplasia during active erosive oesophagitis** - inflammation mimics dysplasia; give **twice-daily PPI for 8-12 weeks** and repeat. [ACG Barrett 2022]`,`**Squamous cancer screening in high-risk areas**: **Lugol iodine chromoendoscopy** (dysplasia stains poorly, 'pink colour sign') or NBI; no national oesophageal screening programme exists in India. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - diagnosis and staging of cancer`,points:[`**Endoscopy with multiple biopsies** (at least 6-8) is the diagnostic test; a **barium swallow** shows an irregular **'apple-core' or shouldered stricture** and its length. [NCCN Oesophageal 2025]`,`**CECT chest, abdomen (and pelvis)** first, for metastases and local invasion. [NCCN Oesophageal 2025]`,`**PET-CT** for patients being considered for curative treatment - it upstages about **15-20%** by finding occult metastases. [NCCN Oesophageal 2025]`,`**EUS** gives the best **T and N staging** (with FNA of suspicious nodes) once metastases are excluded; it decides **T1a versus T1b** together with **endoscopic resection as a staging procedure**. [NCCN Oesophageal 2025]`,`**Bronchoscopy** for tumours at or above the carina (airway invasion); **staging laparoscopy** for Siewert II-III and T3 or higher GEJ adenocarcinoma (peritoneal disease). [NCCN Oesophageal 2025]`,`**Biomarkers**: **HER2** (adenocarcinoma), **PD-L1 (CPS and TPS)**, **MMR/MSI** and, for GEJ adenocarcinoma, **claudin 18.2** - all decide systemic therapy in advanced disease. [NCCN Oesophageal 2025]`,`**Fitness**: ECOG performance status, lung function, echocardiography, nutritional screening and **frailty assessment** before oesophagectomy. [NCCN Oesophageal 2025]`]},{heading:`Management of Barrett oesophagus`,points:[`**All patients with BE get a PPI once daily** (twice daily if symptoms or oesophagitis persist), e.g. **pantoprazole 40 mg or esomeprazole 40 mg orally before breakfast** long term. [ACG Barrett 2022]`,`**Aspirin should not be used solely for chemoprevention**, and **anti-reflux surgery is not a cancer-prevention procedure** - it is offered for reflux control. [ACG Barrett 2022]`,`**Surveillance of non-dysplastic BE** (ACG 2022): **every 5 years if under 3 cm, every 3 years if 3 cm or longer**; ESGE 2023: 1-3 cm every 5 years, 3-10 cm every 3 years, **10 cm or more at an expert centre**. [ACG Barrett 2022]`,`**Indefinite for dysplasia**: optimise PPI and **repeat endoscopy in about 6 months**. [ACG Barrett 2022]`,`**Confirmed LGD**: **endoscopic eradication therapy (EET)** is preferred; surveillance at **6 months, 12 months and then yearly** is an acceptable alternative for those who decline. [ACG Barrett 2022]`,`**HGD or intramucosal carcinoma (T1a)**: **EET** - **endoscopic resection (EMR or ESD) of every visible lesion**, followed by **ablation (radiofrequency ablation, cryotherapy or APC)** of the remaining Barrett; surgery is no longer first-line. [ACG Barrett 2022]`,`**After complete eradication of intestinal metaplasia**: surveillance for previous **HGD/IMC at 3, 6 and 12 months, then yearly**; for previous **LGD at 1 and 3 years, then every 2 years**. [ACG Barrett 2022]`,`**Stop surveillance** when the patient is no longer fit for EET or surgery, or life expectancy is limited. [ESGE Barrett 2023]`]},{heading:`Management of oesophageal cancer`,points:[`**Early cancer (T1a, and low-risk T1b sm1)**: **endoscopic resection** - **ESD preferred for squamous** cancer, EMR or ESD for adenocarcinoma; oesophagectomy if deep submucosal invasion, poor differentiation or lymphovascular invasion. [ESGE Barrett 2023]`,`**Locally advanced (cT2 N+ or cT3-4a any N), resectable SCC**: **neoadjuvant chemoradiotherapy (CROSS) followed by oesophagectomy**, or **definitive chemoradiotherapy** with salvage surgery - definitive CRT is standard for **cervical oesophageal** SCC. [NCCN Oesophageal 2025]`,`**CROSS regimen**: weekly **carboplatin AUC 2 plus paclitaxel 50 mg/m2 for 5 weeks with 41.4 Gy in 23 fractions**; it doubled median survival (49 vs 24 months) with **pathological complete response in 49% of SCC vs 23% of adenocarcinoma**. [van Hagen, NEJM 2012]`,`**Locally advanced adenocarcinoma**: **perioperative FLOT** (5-FU 2600 mg/m2 24-hour infusion, leucovorin 200 mg/m2, oxaliplatin 85 mg/m2, docetaxel 50 mg/m2, every 2 weeks, **4 cycles before and 4 after surgery**) is now preferred after **ESOPEC (2024)**: 3-year survival **57% vs 51%** over CROSS. [Hoeppner, NEJM 2025]`,`**Adjuvant nivolumab** (240 mg IV every 2 weeks or 480 mg every 4 weeks for **1 year**) for **residual pathological disease after CROSS and R0 resection** doubled disease-free survival (22.4 vs 11.0 months) in CheckMate 577. [Kelly, NEJM 2021]`,`**Surgery**: **Ivor Lewis** (laparotomy plus right thoracotomy, intrathoracic anastomosis) for lower-third tumours, **McKeown three-stage** (neck anastomosis) for mid-third, **transhiatal** for selected; **minimally invasive oesophagectomy** reduces pulmonary complications; do it in **high-volume centres**. [NCCN Oesophageal 2025]`,`**Metastatic disease, first line**: **platinum plus fluoropyrimidine with a PD-1 inhibitor (nivolumab, pembrolizumab, tislelizumab)**, the benefit concentrated in **PD-L1-positive** tumours; **trastuzumab** added for HER2-positive adenocarcinoma; **zolbetuximab** for claudin 18.2-positive, HER2-negative GEJ adenocarcinoma. [NCCN Oesophageal 2025]`,`**Palliation of dysphagia**: **self-expanding metal stent** for rapid relief or fistula, **brachytherapy or external beam radiotherapy** for durable relief, and a **feeding jejunostomy** rather than gastrostomy if surgery is still possible. [ESGE stenting 2021]`]},{heading:`Inpatient and emergency issues`,points:[`**Complete dysphagia**: admit, IV fluids, correct electrolytes, and arrange **urgent stenting or feeding access**; beware **refeeding syndrome** - start feeds slowly with **thiamine** and phosphate monitoring. [NICE CG32 2017]`,`**Tracheo-oesophageal fistula** (coughing on swallowing, recurrent pneumonia): nil by mouth, antibiotics, **covered oesophageal (with or without airway) stent**. [ESGE stenting 2021]`,`**Bleeding from tumour**: resuscitate, endoscopic haemostasis, then **palliative radiotherapy**; massive bleeding may signal **aorto-oesophageal fistula**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Post-oesophagectomy complications**: **anastomotic leak** (fever, tachycardia, pleural collection on day 5-7), **chylothorax** (milky drain), recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy, pneumonia; operative mortality under 5% in high-volume centres. [NCCN Oesophageal 2025]`,`**Perforation after dilatation, EMR or stenting**: nil by mouth, CT with water-soluble contrast, IV antibiotics, endoscopic clip or stent, surgery if contained management fails. [ESGE Barrett 2023]`,`**Hypercalcaemia** in SCC: IV saline and **zoledronic acid 4 mg IV** over 15 minutes (dose-adjust in renal impairment). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Elderly and frail**: definitive chemoradiation or endoscopic therapy may replace oesophagectomy; prioritise swallowing and nutrition. [NCCN Oesophageal 2025]`,`**Pregnancy**: rare; endoscopy is safe in the second trimester; treatment needs multidisciplinary planning. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diabetes and CKD**: cisplatin is nephrotoxic - carboplatin preferred in CKD; steroids with docetaxel raise glucose. [NCCN Oesophageal 2025]`,`**Cirrhosis**: oesophagectomy carries prohibitive risk in Child-Pugh B and C; consider non-surgical options. [NCCN Oesophageal 2025]`,`**Resource-limited settings in India**: barium swallow and CECT at the district hospital, biopsy at the medical college, treatment at **regional cancer centres** with **PM-JAY** cover; palliative stenting and radiotherapy are cost-effective. [NCG 2020]`,`**Achalasia, corrosive stricture and tylosis**: lifelong low threshold for endoscopy; tylosis families need surveillance from early adulthood. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Barrett prognosis**: most patients die of other causes; NDBE cancer risk about **0.2-0.5% per year**, much higher with HGD. [ACG Barrett 2022]`,`**EET complications**: **post-ablation stricture (about 5%)**, bleeding and perforation after resection; buried Barrett glands. [ACG Barrett 2022]`,`**Recurrence after eradication** of intestinal metaplasia occurs in about **10% per year** of patients initially, mostly non-dysplastic - the reason for continued surveillance. [ACG Barrett 2022]`,`**Cancer prognosis by stage**: T1a after endoscopic resection more than 90% 5-year survival; locally advanced after trimodality treatment about 40-50%; metastatic disease median survival about **1 year**. [NCCN Oesophageal 2025]`,`**Complications of disease**: malnutrition, aspiration, tracheo-oesophageal fistula, bleeding, hypercalcaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Late effects after oesophagectomy**: dumping, reflux into the conduit, anastomotic stricture, weight loss. [NCCN Oesophageal 2025]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up, prevention and programme links`,points:[`**Urgent (2-week) endoscopy**: any dysphagia, or age 55 and above with weight loss and dyspepsia or reflux. [NICE NG12 2015]`,`**Barrett with any dysplasia** or a visible lesion: refer to an **expert Barrett centre** for EET. [ACG Barrett 2022]`,`**All oesophageal cancer** goes to a **multidisciplinary tumour board** (surgeon, medical and radiation oncologist, gastroenterologist, radiologist, dietician). [NCCN Oesophageal 2025]`,`**After curative treatment**: clinical review every 3-6 months for 2 years, then 6-12 monthly to 5 years; imaging as symptoms dictate; endoscopy after endoscopic or definitive CRT treatment. [NCCN Oesophageal 2025]`,`**Nutrition**: weigh at every visit, dietician input, oral supplements; B12, iron and vitamin D after oesophagectomy. [ESPEN 2021]`,`**Tobacco cessation** (all forms including bidi, gutkha, khaini and tuibur) under the **National Tobacco Control Programme** - quitline **1800-11-2356**. [NTCP 2023]`,`**Alcohol reduction**, avoiding **very hot beverages** and promoting fruit and vegetables cut SCC risk. [IARC 2016]`,`**Weight control and GERD treatment** for adenocarcinoma risk; PPI does not need to be stopped in Barrett for 'side-effect' fears. [ACG Barrett 2022]`,`**NP-NCD** screens for oral, breast and cervical cancer - oral cancer screening at **Ayushman Arogya Mandir** is the opportunity to counsel tobacco users about oesophageal cancer too. [NP-NCD 2023]`,`**Corrosive prevention**: regulate acid sales and store cleaners safely. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ACG Barrett guideline (2022)**: screening for chronic GERD with 3 or more risk factors, **capsule-sponge non-endoscopic screening accepted**, surveillance 5 years (under 3 cm) or 3 years (3 cm or more), **EET for confirmed LGD**. [ACG Barrett 2022]`,`**ESGE Barrett guideline (2023)**: no surveillance for irregular Z-line under 1 cm; 10 cm or longer BE managed at expert centres; ablation recommended for confirmed LGD. [ESGE Barrett 2023]`,`**ESOPEC trial (2024-2025)**: perioperative **FLOT beat CROSS** for resectable oesophageal adenocarcinoma (3-year OS 57% vs 51%) - FLOT now preferred for adenocarcinoma. [Hoeppner, NEJM 2025]`,`**Checkpoint inhibitors**: first-line PD-1 inhibitors with chemotherapy are standard in advanced disease; regulators in **2024-2025 narrowed use to PD-L1-expressing tumours** because benefit in PD-L1-negative disease is minimal. [NCCN Oesophageal 2025]`,`**Zolbetuximab** (anti-claudin 18.2) approved in **2024** for HER2-negative, claudin 18.2-positive gastric and GEJ adenocarcinoma. [NCCN Oesophageal 2025]`,`**Squamous neoadjuvant therapy**: Japanese **JCOG1109 NExT** data support a **docetaxel-cisplatin-5FU (DCF)** triplet before surgery; CROSS remains the Western standard. [NCCN Oesophageal 2025]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Viva: **'Define Barrett oesophagus'** - 1 cm or more of columnar mucosa above the GEJ with intestinal metaplasia (ACG); mention BSG difference. [ACG Barrett 2022]`,`Viva: **'What is the Seattle protocol?'** - targeted biopsies first, then 4-quadrant biopsies every 2 cm (1 cm if dysplasia). [ACG Barrett 2022]`,`Viva: **'Commonest type and site of oesophageal cancer in India?'** - squamous cell carcinoma, mid-oesophagus. [ICMR-NCDIR NCRP 2020]`,`Viva: **'CROSS vs FLOT'** - CROSS (carboplatin-paclitaxel plus 41.4 Gy) for SCC; FLOT for adenocarcinoma after ESOPEC. [NCCN Oesophageal 2025]`,`**Common mistake**: grading dysplasia during active oesophagitis - treat with PPI first. [ACG Barrett 2022]`,`**Common mistake**: using 'rule of thumb' annual surveillance for non-dysplastic Barrett - intervals are 3-5 years. [ACG Barrett 2022]`,`**Mnemonic for SCC risks - 'ABCDEFG'**: Achalasia, Betel and bidi, Corrosive stricture, Drinks very hot and alcohol, Esophageal web (Plummer-Vinson), Fruit and vegetables lacking, Genetic tylosis.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Barrett surveillance and treatment by histology (ACG 2022, ESGE 2023)`,columns:[`Histology`,`ACG 2022`,`ESGE 2023`,`Comment`],rows:[[`Irregular Z-line under 1 cm`,`Not Barrett`,`No biopsy or surveillance`,`Do not label as Barrett`],[`NDBE under 3 cm`,`Endoscopy every 5 years`,`1-3 cm every 5 years`,`PPI once daily`],[`NDBE 3 cm or more`,`Endoscopy every 3 years`,`3-10 cm every 3 years; 10 cm or more at expert centre`,`Seattle protocol biopsies`],[`Indefinite for dysplasia`,`Optimise PPI, repeat in 6 months`,`Repeat after PPI optimisation`,`Inflammation mimics dysplasia`],[`Confirmed LGD`,`EET preferred; or surveillance 6 months, 12 months, then yearly`,`Ablation recommended`,`Second expert pathologist`],[`HGD or T1a cancer`,`EMR or ESD of lesions plus ablation`,`Endoscopic resection plus ablation`,`Surgery no longer first-line`],[`After eradication (prior HGD/IMC)`,`3, 6, 12 months, then yearly`,`Expert centre follow-up`,`Recurrence possible`],[`After eradication (prior LGD)`,`1 and 3 years, then every 2 years`,`Expert centre follow-up`,`Biopsy GEJ and neosquamous mucosa`]]},{heading:`Squamous cell carcinoma vs adenocarcinoma of the oesophagus`,columns:[`Feature`,`Squamous cell carcinoma`,`Adenocarcinoma`],rows:[[`Share in India`,`Large majority`,`Minority, rising in cities`],[`Site`,`Mid and upper third`,`Distal oesophagus and GEJ`],[`Precursor`,`Squamous dysplasia`,`Barrett oesophagus`],[`Risk factors`,`Tobacco, alcohol, betel, hot drinks, achalasia, corrosive, tylosis`,`GERD, obesity, male, white, smoking`],[`H. pylori`,`No clear link`,`Inverse association`],[`Early treatment`,`ESD preferred`,`EMR or ESD plus ablation`],[`Neoadjuvant`,`CROSS chemoradiation or definitive CRT`,`Perioperative FLOT (ESOPEC)`],[`Response to CRT`,`pCR about 49%`,`pCR about 23%`]]},{heading:`AJCC 8th edition TNM essentials`,columns:[`Category`,`Definition`,`Treatment implication`],rows:[[`T1a`,`Lamina propria or muscularis mucosae`,`Endoscopic resection`],[`T1b`,`Submucosa`,`Surgery unless low-risk sm1`],[`T2`,`Muscularis propria`,`Neoadjuvant therapy if node-positive; surgery`],[`T3`,`Adventitia`,`Neoadjuvant therapy plus surgery`],[`T4a`,`Pleura, pericardium, azygos, diaphragm, peritoneum`,`Resectable after neoadjuvant therapy`],[`T4b`,`Aorta, vertebral body, trachea`,`Unresectable: definitive CRT`],[`N1 / N2 / N3`,`1-2 / 3-6 / 7 or more regional nodes`,`Prognosis worsens with count`],[`M1`,`Distant metastasis`,`Systemic therapy and palliation`]]},{heading:`Drugs and regimens in Barrett and oesophageal cancer`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Pantoprazole or esomeprazole`,`40 mg oral once daily before breakfast (twice if symptoms)`,`Lifelong in Barrett`,`Hypomagnesaemia, C. difficile, B12 deficiency`],[`CROSS (carboplatin + paclitaxel)`,`Carboplatin AUC 2 plus paclitaxel 50 mg/m2 IV weekly with 41.4 Gy`,`5 weeks, surgery 4-8 weeks later`,`Neutropenia, oesophagitis, neuropathy, paclitaxel hypersensitivity`],[`FLOT`,`5-FU 2600 mg/m2 24 h, leucovorin 200 mg/m2, oxaliplatin 85 mg/m2, docetaxel 50 mg/m2 IV`,`Every 2 weeks: 4 cycles before, 4 after surgery`,`Neutropenia, diarrhoea, neuropathy; DPD deficiency risk with 5-FU`],[`Nivolumab (adjuvant)`,`240 mg IV every 2 weeks or 480 mg every 4 weeks`,`1 year for residual disease after CROSS and surgery`,`Immune colitis, pneumonitis, hepatitis, thyroiditis`],[`Pembrolizumab (advanced)`,`200 mg IV every 3 weeks with platinum-fluoropyrimidine`,`Up to 2 years`,`Immune-related adverse events; benefit mainly PD-L1 positive`],[`Trastuzumab`,`8 mg/kg IV load, then 6 mg/kg every 3 weeks`,`Until progression (HER2+ adenocarcinoma)`,`Cardiotoxicity - echo every 3 months`],[`Zoledronic acid`,`4 mg IV over 15 minutes`,`Single dose for hypercalcaemia`,`Renal toxicity, osteonecrosis of jaw`]]},{heading:`Staging investigations and their role`,columns:[`Test`,`Role`,`Key point`],rows:[[`Endoscopy with biopsy`,`Diagnosis`,`6-8 biopsies; HER2, PD-L1, MMR on tissue`],[`CECT chest and abdomen`,`First staging test`,`Metastases, local invasion`],[`PET-CT`,`Before curative treatment`,`Upstages 15-20% with occult metastases`],[`EUS with FNA`,`T and N stage`,`After metastases excluded; T1a vs T1b`],[`Bronchoscopy`,`Tumours at or above carina`,`Airway invasion, fistula`],[`Staging laparoscopy`,`Siewert II-III, T3 or higher GEJ adenocarcinoma`,`Peritoneal disease`]]}],redFlags:[`Progressive dysphagia with weight loss - endoscopy within 2 weeks`,`Complete dysphagia or inability to swallow saliva`,`Coughing on swallowing or recurrent pneumonia - tracheo-oesophageal fistula`,`Haematemesis or iron deficiency anaemia in a patient with Barrett or dysphagia`,`Hoarseness, supraclavicular node or hepatomegaly with dysphagia - advanced disease`,`Barrett with a visible nodule or any dysplasia - expert centre referral`,`Fever and chest pain after dilatation, resection or stenting - perforation`],pearls:[`**Barrett = 1 cm + goblet cells** (ACG); **irregular Z-line under 1 cm is not Barrett**.`,`**Prague C and M** describes it; **Seattle protocol** samples it - 4 quadrants every 2 cm.`,`**Surveillance 5-3**: under 3 cm every 5 years, 3 cm or more every 3 years.`,`**Dysplasia needs two pathologists** and **no oesophagitis** at the time of biopsy.`,`**Resect the bump, burn the flat**: EMR/ESD for visible lesions, RFA for the rest.`,`**India = squamous, mid-oesophagus**; **West = adenocarcinoma, distal and GEJ**.`,`**CROSS for squamous, FLOT for adeno** (ESOPEC 2024); **nivolumab for residual disease** (CheckMate 577).`,`**N1 = 1-2, N2 = 3-6, N3 = 7+** nodes.`,`**Siewert I, II, III**: 1-5 cm above, 1 above to 2 below, 2-5 cm below the GEJ.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-barrett-oesophageal-cancer-q1`,stem:`A 56-year-old obese man with 15 years of reflux has a 4 cm segment (C3M4) of salmon-coloured mucosa; Seattle-protocol biopsies show intestinal metaplasia without dysplasia. When should the next surveillance endoscopy be done according to ACG 2022?`,options:[`In 6 months`,`In 1 year`,`In 3 years`,`In 5 years`],answer:2,explanation:`Non-dysplastic Barrett of 3 cm or more is surveyed every 3 years under ACG 2022, while segments under 3 cm are surveyed every 5 years. A 6-month repeat is for an indefinite-for-dysplasia result after PPI optimisation, and yearly endoscopy is the alternative pathway for confirmed low-grade dysplasia, not for non-dysplastic disease.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-barrett-oesophageal-cancer-q2`,stem:`In a patient with Barrett oesophagus and a 1.5 cm flat-elevated nodule, biopsies show high-grade dysplasia. CT is normal. What is the recommended management?`,options:[`Endoscopic resection of the nodule followed by ablation of the remaining Barrett`,`Oesophagectomy`,`Double-dose PPI and surveillance in 1 year`,`Radiofrequency ablation of the entire segment including the nodule`],answer:0,explanation:`Visible lesions in Barrett must be removed by endoscopic resection (EMR or ESD), which both treats and stages them, and the residual flat Barrett is then ablated to prevent metachronous neoplasia. Oesophagectomy is no longer first-line for HGD or T1a disease, surveillance alone is unsafe with HGD, and ablation alone cannot assess depth in a nodule.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-barrett-oesophageal-cancer-q3`,stem:`Which combination is most characteristic of oesophageal cancer as seen in the North-East of India?`,options:[`Adenocarcinoma of the distal oesophagus arising in Barrett`,`Squamous cell carcinoma of the middle third linked to tobacco, betel quid and smoked foods`,`Gastrointestinal stromal tumour of the oesophagus`,`Small-cell carcinoma of the upper third`],answer:1,explanation:`The North-East cancer belt (Meghalaya, Assam, Mizoram) has some of the highest rates of oesophageal squamous cell carcinoma in the world, mostly in the middle third and driven by tobacco, betel quid, alcohol, tuibur and smoked or fermented foods. Barrett adenocarcinoma is a Western pattern, and GIST and small-cell carcinoma are rare oesophageal tumours.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-barrett-oesophageal-cancer-q4`,stem:`A fit 60-year-old has a cT3N1M0 adenocarcinoma of the distal oesophagus. Based on the ESOPEC trial, which treatment is preferred?`,options:[`Upfront oesophagectomy alone`,`Definitive chemoradiotherapy without surgery`,`Perioperative FLOT chemotherapy with oesophagectomy`,`Self-expanding metal stent and palliative radiotherapy`],answer:2,explanation:`ESOPEC showed perioperative FLOT plus surgery improved 3-year overall survival over CROSS chemoradiation plus surgery in resectable oesophageal adenocarcinoma (about 57% vs 51%), so FLOT is now preferred. Surgery alone undertreats node-positive disease, definitive CRT is mainly for squamous or unfit patients, and stenting is palliative.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-barrett-oesophageal-cancer-q5`,stem:`In the Seattle biopsy protocol for Barrett oesophagus without known dysplasia, how are random biopsies taken?`,options:[`Single biopsy from the most proximal extent only`,`Biopsies every 5 cm from two quadrants`,`Only targeted biopsies of visible lesions`,`Four-quadrant biopsies every 2 cm, after targeted biopsies of any visible lesion`],answer:3,explanation:`The Seattle protocol takes targeted biopsies of visible abnormalities first and then four-quadrant random biopsies every 2 cm along the Barrett segment (every 1 cm when dysplasia is known or suspected), because dysplasia is patchy and often invisible. A single biopsy, sparse two-quadrant sampling or targeted biopsies alone miss most dysplasia.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-barrett-oesophageal-cancer-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the risk factors, clinical features, staging and management of carcinoma oesophagus with special reference to India.`,openingLines:[`Oesophageal cancer is a malignant epithelial tumour of the oesophagus - squamous cell carcinoma, the dominant type in India, or adenocarcinoma arising from Barrett oesophagus.`,`It presents late with progressive dysphagia and weight loss; management depends on AJCC stage and histology, from endoscopic resection to neoadjuvant CROSS or FLOT with surgery, or palliation.`],answer:[{heading:`Epidemiology and risk factors`,points:[`India: SCC, mid-oesophagus; North-East and Kashmir belts; tobacco, alcohol, betel, hot beverages, smoked foods, salted tea.`,`Premalignant: achalasia, corrosive stricture, Plummer-Vinson, tylosis; adenocarcinoma: GERD, Barrett, obesity.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Progressive dysphagia solids to liquids, weight loss, odynophagia, regurgitation; hoarseness, TEF cough, Virchow node.`]},{heading:`Investigations and staging`,points:[`Endoscopy with biopsy, barium apple-core, CECT, PET-CT, EUS, bronchoscopy, staging laparoscopy; HER2, PD-L1, MMR.`,`AJCC 8th TNM: T1a, T1b, T2, T3, T4a, T4b; N1 1-2, N2 3-6, N3 7 or more; Siewert for GEJ.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`T1a: endoscopic resection (ESD for SCC).`,`Locally advanced SCC: CROSS then surgery or definitive CRT; adenocarcinoma: perioperative FLOT (ESOPEC).`,`Adjuvant nivolumab for residual disease; metastatic: chemotherapy plus PD-1 inhibitor, trastuzumab if HER2+.`,`Palliation: SEMS, brachytherapy, radiotherapy, nutrition, palliative care.`]},{heading:`Prevention`,points:[`Tobacco and alcohol cessation, avoid very hot drinks, NTCP and NP-NCD links.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of oesophagus with thirds and Siewert types`,`Stage-wise treatment flow chart`,`Table SCC vs adenocarcinoma`],markSplit:[{part:`Risk factors with Indian context`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:1},{part:`Investigations and staging`,marks:3},{part:`Management`,marks:3},{part:`Prevention`,marks:1}],keywords:[`carcinoma oesophagus`,`squamous`,`adenocarcinoma`,`AJCC 8th`,`CROSS`,`FLOT`,`ESOPEC`,`SEMS`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-barrett-oesophageal-cancer-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on Barrett oesophagus.`,openingLines:[`Barrett oesophagus is replacement of distal oesophageal squamous epithelium by columnar epithelium with intestinal metaplasia extending at least 1 cm above the GEJ.`,`It is the precursor of oesophageal adenocarcinoma, with a cancer risk of about 0.2-0.5% per year without dysplasia.`],answer:[{heading:`Risk factors and pathogenesis`,points:[`Chronic GERD, male, age above 50, obesity, smoking, family history; acid and bile, CDX2, metaplasia-dysplasia-carcinoma sequence.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Endoscopy with Prague C and M; Seattle 4-quadrant biopsies every 2 cm; second pathologist for dysplasia.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`PPI once daily for all; surveillance every 5 years (under 3 cm) or 3 years (3 cm or more).`,`LGD: EET preferred; HGD/T1a: EMR or ESD plus RFA; surveillance after eradication.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of Prague C and M measurement`,`Surveillance ladder by dysplasia grade`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and risk factors`,marks:1},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Management and surveillance`,marks:2}],keywords:[`Barrett`,`Prague C and M`,`Seattle protocol`,`dysplasia`,`RFA`,`EMR`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Cancers of the Upper Gastrointestinal Tract; Diseases of the Esophagus`,`Shaheen NJ, et al. ACG Clinical Guideline: Diagnosis and Management of Barrett's Esophagus. Am J Gastroenterol, 2022`,`Weusten BLAM, et al. Diagnosis and management of Barrett esophagus: ESGE Guideline, 2023`,`NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Esophageal and Esophagogastric Junction Cancers, version 2025`,`Hoeppner J, et al. Perioperative chemotherapy or preoperative chemoradiotherapy in esophageal cancer (ESOPEC). N Engl J Med, 2025`,`van Hagen P, et al. Preoperative chemoradiotherapy for esophageal or junctional cancer (CROSS). N Engl J Med, 2012`,`Kelly RJ, et al. Adjuvant nivolumab in resected esophageal or GEJ cancer (CheckMate 577). N Engl J Med, 2021`,`AJCC Cancer Staging Manual, 8th edition, 2017; ICMR-NCDIR Report of National Cancer Registry Programme, 2020`]});var t=[];t.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-dyspepsia-functional`,title:`Uninvestigated and functional dyspepsia`,oneLiner:`**Dyspepsia** is bothersome **epigastric pain or burning, postprandial fullness or early satiation**; it is **uninvestigated** until endoscopy, and **functional dyspepsia (Rome IV)** is the same symptoms for **3 months (onset 6 months earlier) with a normal endoscopy** - a **disorder of gut-brain interaction** managed by **H. pylori test-and-treat, PPI, then low-dose tricyclic or prokinetic**, with endoscopy reserved for **alarm features or age above about 45 years in India**.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`dyspepsia`,`functional dyspepsia`,`uninvestigated dyspepsia`,`Rome IV`,`postprandial distress syndrome`,`PDS`,`epigastric pain syndrome`,`EPS`,`disorder of gut-brain interaction`,`DGBI`,`non-ulcer dyspepsia`,`test and treat`,`H. pylori-associated dyspepsia`,`itopride`,`levosulpiride`,`domperidone`,`acotiamide`,`amitriptyline`,`buspirone`,`fundic accommodation`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Dyspepsia** is one or more of **bothersome postprandial fullness, early satiation, epigastric pain or epigastric burning**; heartburn and regurgitation alone are reflux, not dyspepsia. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Uninvestigated dyspepsia** is the symptom before any endoscopy; **investigated dyspepsia** is classified after endoscopy as organic (ulcer, cancer, oesophagitis) or **functional**. [ACG/CAG 2017]`,`**Functional dyspepsia (FD)** needs the symptoms for the **last 3 months with onset at least 6 months before diagnosis**, and **no structural disease on upper endoscopy** that explains them. [Rome IV 2016]`,`Uninvestigated dyspepsia affects about **20% of adults worldwide**; Rome IV FD affects about **7% in internet surveys and 5% in household surveys** across 26 countries. [Rome Foundation Global Epidemiology Study 2021]`,`Of dyspeptic patients who are scoped, **about 70-80% have no relevant structural lesion** - FD is the commonest final diagnosis, peptic ulcer about 5-10%, cancer under 1%. [BSG FD 2022]`,`In India, community surveys report dyspepsia in about **10-30%** of adults, and the majority of endoscoped Indian dyspeptics have functional dyspepsia; **overlap with IBS and GERD is frequent**. [Harrison 22e]`,`FD is now called a **disorder of gut-brain interaction (DGBI)**, replacing 'non-ulcer dyspepsia' - the term signals a real disorder of motility, sensation and central processing, not 'nothing wrong'. [Rome IV 2016]`,`Dyspepsia is a leading cause of **PPI over-prescription** and repeat endoscopy in India; a planned, stepwise approach saves cost and harm. [ACG/CAG 2017]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Organic causes** of dyspepsia: peptic ulcer, **NSAID or aspirin gastropathy**, oesophagitis, gastric cancer, coeliac disease, and drugs - these must be excluded before labelling FD. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drugs that cause dyspepsia**: NSAIDs, aspirin, **metformin**, iron, potassium chloride, bisphosphonates, doxycycline, steroids, theophylline, **GLP-1 receptor agonists** and SGLT2 inhibitor-associated nausea. [Harrison 22e]`,`**H. pylori** causes a subgroup called **H. pylori-associated dyspepsia** - symptoms that resolve and stay resolved 6-12 months after successful eradication. [Kyoto Consensus 2015]`,`**Post-infectious FD** follows acute gastroenteritis (Salmonella, Giardia, norovirus) in about **10%** - relevant in India where enteric infection is common. [BSG FD 2022]`,`Risk factors for FD: **female sex, anxiety, depression, somatisation, childhood adversity, smoking, NSAID use** and coexisting IBS. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Gallstones do not cause dyspepsia** - biliary pain is severe, episodic and lasts over 30 minutes; cholecystectomy for dyspepsia with incidental stones does not cure it. [BSG FD 2022]`,`Other mimics: **chronic pancreatitis, pancreatic cancer, gastroparesis, mesenteric ischaemia, hepatic congestion, hypercalcaemia, hypothyroidism, CKD and inferior wall ischaemia**. [Harrison 22e]`,`In India, consider **giardiasis and strongyloidiasis** in persistent dyspepsia with diarrhoea, eosinophilia or immunosuppression. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**Impaired fundic accommodation** (the proximal stomach fails to relax after a meal) occurs in about **40%** and causes **early satiation and weight loss** - the target of buspirone and acotiamide. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Delayed gastric emptying** occurs in about **25-35%** and correlates with postprandial fullness and nausea - the rationale for prokinetics; rapid emptying occurs in a minority. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Visceral hypersensitivity** to gastric distension, acid and lipids lowers the pain threshold - explaining pain after small fatty meals and the benefit of **neuromodulators (TCAs)**. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Duodenal low-grade inflammation**: increased **duodenal eosinophils and mast cells** and raised mucosal permeability, often after infection or stress, sensitise local afferent nerves. [BSG FD 2022]`,`**Duodenal acid and lipid sensitivity** reduces antral motility and triggers nausea - one reason PPIs help a subset with **epigastric pain syndrome**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Central processing**: anxiety, depression and abnormal brain-gut signalling amplify normal gut sensations; the gut-to-brain direction also exists, as FD precedes anxiety in some patients. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**H. pylori gastritis** alters gastrin, somatostatin and acid output and causes mucosal inflammation; eradication cures dyspepsia in about **1 in 12-15** patients. [Maastricht VI 2022]`,`How the drugs map on: **PPI** for acid sensitivity, **prokinetic** for delayed emptying, **buspirone/acotiamide** for accommodation, **TCA** for hypersensitivity and central amplification, **eradication** for H. pylori gastritis. [BSG FD 2022]`]},{heading:`Classification - Rome IV subtypes`,points:[`**Postprandial distress syndrome (PDS)**: bothersome **postprandial fullness or early satiation** severe enough to stop finishing a regular meal, on **at least 3 days a week**. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Epigastric pain syndrome (EPS)**: bothersome **epigastric pain or burning** severe enough to impact usual activities, on **at least 1 day a week**; pain may be induced or relieved by meals or occur fasting. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**PDS-EPS overlap** is common (about one-third); treat the dominant symptom first. [Rome IV 2016]`,`Supportive features of PDS: postprandial epigastric pain or burning, bloating, excessive belching and nausea; **vomiting is unusual** and should prompt another diagnosis. [Rome IV 2016]`,`Pain relieved by defaecation or passage of flatus is **IBS**, not FD; heartburn is GERD - but both may coexist with FD. [Rome IV 2016]`,`Related Rome IV gastroduodenal disorders: **belching disorders, chronic nausea and vomiting syndrome, cyclic vomiting syndrome, cannabinoid hyperemesis and rumination syndrome** (see the nausea, vomiting and gastroparesis topic). [Rome IV 2016]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Ask the **four cardinal symptoms**: postprandial fullness, early satiation, epigastric pain, epigastric burning - and which one bothers most, since this picks the drug. [Rome IV 2016]`,`Ask the **alarm questions**: **weight loss, dysphagia, odynophagia, persistent vomiting, haematemesis or melaena, iron deficiency anaemia, epigastric mass, family history of gastric cancer**. [ACG/CAG 2017]`,`Take a **complete drug history**, including **over-the-counter NSAIDs, ayurvedic pain powders, metformin, iron and GLP-1 agonists** - stopping the drug may be the whole treatment. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **meal pattern**: large, late, fatty or spicy meals, skipped meals, tea or coffee excess, alcohol, smoking and gutkha. [BSG FD 2022]`,`Screen for **anxiety and depression** (PHQ-4 or PHQ-9, GAD-7) and life stressors - they predict severity and guide neuromodulator choice. [Rome IV 2016]`,`Ask about **bowel habit** (IBS overlap), **heartburn** (GERD overlap), diarrhoea (giardiasis, coeliac) and **cannabis use** (hyperemesis). [Rome IV 2016]`,`In a **diabetic, elderly or smoker**, epigastric discomfort on exertion with sweating or breathlessness is **angina until proved otherwise** - do an ECG. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about previous endoscopies, H. pylori tests and treatments, and how many PPI courses have been taken - repeated scoping of FD should be avoided. [BSG FD 2022]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Examination in FD is **normal apart from mild epigastric tenderness**; any other finding suggests organic disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`Record **weight and BMI** at each visit - **documented weight loss** is an alarm feature; stable weight reassures. [ACG/CAG 2017]`,`Look for **pallor, koilonychia and angular cheilitis** (iron deficiency from occult bleeding or coeliac disease). [Harrison 22e]`,`Feel for **Virchow node (Troisier sign)**, an **epigastric mass**, nodular liver and ascites - features of gastric cancer. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`A **succussion splash** more than 3 hours after a meal means gastric outlet obstruction, not FD. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Carnett sign** (pain unchanged or worse when tensing the abdominal wall) suggests **abdominal wall pain** such as anterior cutaneous nerve entrapment, not visceral pain. [Harrison 22e]`,`Check for **jaundice, signs of chronic liver disease and thyroid status**, and do a cardiovascular examination in those at risk. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Peptic ulcer**: night pain relieved by food, NSAID or H. pylori history, possible bleeding - distinguished only by endoscopy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**GERD**: heartburn and regurgitation dominate, worse lying or bending; responds well to PPI (see the acid peptic disease and GERD topic). [ACG GERD 2022]`,`**Gastric cancer**: age over 40-45, weight loss, anaemia, short history, family history - endoscopy with biopsy is mandatory. [ACG/CAG 2017]`,`**Gastroparesis**: nausea, vomiting and fullness dominate, often in long-standing diabetes; **delayed emptying on a 4-hour scintigraphy** with vomiting distinguishes it from PDS. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**Biliary pain**: severe, steady right upper quadrant or epigastric pain lasting **over 30 minutes to hours**, episodic, often nocturnal - not daily postprandial discomfort. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chronic pancreatitis or pancreatic cancer**: pain radiating to the back, steatorrhoea, diabetes, weight loss, alcohol history. [Harrison 22e]`,`**IBS**: pain related to defaecation with change in stool form or frequency. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Coronary ischaemia, abdominal wall pain, coeliac disease and drug-induced dyspepsia** complete the list. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Baseline in every patient: **haemoglobin, MCV and ferritin** (occult blood loss) and, if indicated, blood sugar, LFT, calcium and TSH. [BSG FD 2022]`,`**Non-invasive H. pylori test** - **urea breath test or stool antigen** - is the test of choice for test-and-treat; **serology is not recommended** in high-prevalence India. [Maastricht VI 2022]`,`**Stop PPI 2 weeks and antibiotics or bismuth 4 weeks** before UBT, stool antigen or rapid urease test to avoid false negatives. [Maastricht VI 2022]`,`**Upper GI endoscopy** for **any alarm feature or new dyspepsia from about 45 years in India** (ACG/CAG uses 60 for North America, where gastric cancer is rarer); take **gastric biopsies by the updated Sydney protocol** for H. pylori and atrophy. [ACG/CAG 2017]`,`During endoscopy for dyspepsia, take **duodenal biopsies if coeliac disease is suspected**; check **IgA tTG with total IgA** in FD with IBS-type or anaemic overlap. [BSG FD 2022]`,`**Ultrasound abdomen is not routine** in FD; use it when biliary pain, pancreatic or hepatic disease is suspected. [BSG FD 2022]`,`**Gastric emptying study** only when vomiting is prominent or symptoms are refractory - not for typical PDS. [BSG FD 2022]`,`In endemic areas, **stool microscopy for Giardia and Strongyloides** (or serology before steroids) when diarrhoea or eosinophilia coexist. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Avoid repeat endoscopy** in FD unless the symptom pattern or alarm status changes - it reinforces illness belief and adds cost. [BSG FD 2022]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - first line`,points:[`**Explain the diagnosis positively**: a real disorder of gut-brain communication, not cancer, not 'gas'; a good **therapeutic relationship** is itself therapeutic and lowers health-care use. [BSG FD 2022]`,`**Diet**: small frequent meals, reduce fatty and spicy foods, avoid late heavy dinners; **regular aerobic exercise** helps; evidence for low-FODMAP diet in FD is limited. [BSG FD 2022]`,`**Stop or replace culprit drugs**: NSAIDs to paracetamol; metformin to extended-release with meals; review iron and bisphosphonates. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Test and treat H. pylori** in all with dyspepsia and no alarm features below the endoscopy age; treat with **bismuth quadruple therapy for 14 days** and **confirm eradication at least 4 weeks after** therapy. [Maastricht VI 2022]`,`**PPI for 8 weeks** if H. pylori negative or symptoms persist after eradication: **omeprazole 20 mg or pantoprazole 40 mg once daily 30 minutes before breakfast** - standard dose is as good as double dose; most effective in EPS. [ACG/CAG 2017]`,`**H2 receptor antagonist** (famotidine 20 mg twice daily) is an alternative first-line acid suppressant. [BSG FD 2022]`,`If PPI works, **stop it after 8 weeks** and use **on-demand** therapy for recurrence; do not continue long-term without an indication. [BSG FD 2022]`,`**Prokinetic** first in PDS or as add-on: **itopride 50 mg three times daily before meals** or **levosulpiride 25 mg three times daily** for 4-8 weeks; **domperidone 10 mg up to three times daily for the shortest time (about 1 week)** because of QT risk. [BSG FD 2022]`]},{heading:`Management - second line and refractory FD`,points:[`**Low-dose tricyclic antidepressant** is the best-evidenced second-line drug: **amitriptyline 10 mg at night, titrate by 10 mg every 1-2 weeks to 30-50 mg**, continue **6-12 months** if helpful; works best in EPS. [BSG FD 2022]`,`Explain that the TCA is a **gut-brain neuromodulator**, used at doses below antidepressant doses, to improve adherence; warn of dry mouth, constipation and drowsiness. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**SSRIs and SNRIs are not effective for FD** symptoms themselves, though they treat comorbid anxiety or depression. [BSG FD 2022]`,`**Mirtazapine 15 mg at night** may help FD with **early satiation, nausea and weight loss**; **buspirone 10 mg three times daily before meals** improves fundic accommodation in PDS. [BSG FD 2022]`,`**Acotiamide 100 mg three times daily before meals** (acetylcholinesterase inhibitor; licensed in Japan and India) improves PDS symptoms. [JSGE FD 2021]`,`**Antipsychotics (low-dose sulpiride, levosulpiride or quetiapine)** may be tried by specialists in refractory FD. [BSG FD 2022]`,`**Brain-gut behavioural therapies** - **CBT and gut-directed hypnotherapy** - are recommended for refractory FD where available. [BSG FD 2022]`,`Herbal: **STW-5 (Iberogast)** and **peppermint-caraway oil** have modest evidence; avoid unregulated powders. [BSG FD 2022]`]},{heading:`Emergency care and special situations`,points:[`FD itself **never needs admission**; admit for **alarm features**: haematemesis, melaena, persistent vomiting with dehydration, suspected perforation or obstruction. [ACG/CAG 2017]`,`Rule out **acute coronary syndrome** with ECG and troponin in any acute epigastric pain with cardiovascular risk. [Harrison 22e]`,`Rule out **acute pancreatitis** (serum lipase 3 times upper limit) and **biliary colic** before diagnosing 'acute gastritis' in the emergency room. [Harrison 22e]`,`Avoid **repeated IV PPI and opioids** for FD flares; opioids worsen emptying and lead to **narcotic bowel syndrome**. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Pregnancy**: dyspepsia affects up to 80%; use lifestyle advice, **antacids or alginates** first, then **famotidine** or a PPI (omeprazole has most data); defer H. pylori eradication until after delivery. [ACG GERD 2022]`,`**Elderly**: lower the threshold for endoscopy, review polypharmacy, avoid TCAs with anticholinergic burden (prefer nortriptyline at low dose), check ECG before domperidone. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diabetes**: think of **gastroparesis, metformin, GLP-1 agonists, silent ischaemia and diabetic radiculopathy** before FD. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**CKD**: avoid magnesium antacids and bismuth in advanced CKD; reduce domperidone and itopride doses cautiously; famotidine needs dose reduction. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Children and adolescents**: Rome IV paediatric FD criteria require symptoms for **2 months**; screen for anxiety, school stress, coeliac disease and Giardia. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: if UBT or stool antigen is unavailable, a **4-8 week PPI trial** is reasonable in the young without alarm features; refer for endoscopy if symptoms persist. [ACG/CAG 2017]`]},{heading:`Complications, prognosis, referral and follow-up`,points:[`FD **does not shorten life** or progress to ulcer or cancer, but it has a **chronic relapsing course**; about **half remain symptomatic** at long-term follow-up. [Rome IV 2016]`,`Quality of life is impaired as much as in mild organic disease; **health-care use and work absence** are high. [BSG FD 2022]`,`**Iatrogenic harm** is the main complication: long-term unnecessary PPI, domperidone-related arrhythmia, repeated endoscopy and unnecessary cholecystectomy. [BSG FD 2022]`,`On a TCA, check **ECG at baseline in those over 50 or with cardiac disease**, and review at 2-4 weeks for side effects. [BSG FD 2022]`,`Response rates: PPI **NNT about 10**, TCA **NNT about 6**, prokinetics modest; placebo response is about 30-40%. [ACG/CAG 2017]`,`Deprescribe PPI at each review if not clearly helping - see the PPI deprescribing topic. [AGA PPI deprescribing 2022]`,`**Review at 4-8 weeks** after each therapeutic step; change only one drug at a time. [BSG FD 2022]`,`**Confirm H. pylori eradication** with UBT or stool antigen **at least 4 weeks after antibiotics and 2 weeks off PPI**. [Maastricht VI 2022]`,`**Refer for endoscopy** for alarm features, age above about 45, failed empirical therapy or family history of gastric cancer. [ACG/CAG 2017]`,`**Refer to gastroenterology** for refractory FD after PPI, prokinetic and TCA trials, weight loss, or diagnostic doubt; **psychology referral** for CBT or hypnotherapy. [BSG FD 2022]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`Counsel: **regular small meals, stop smoking, limit alcohol, manage stress, sleep hygiene and exercise**. [BSG FD 2022]`,`**Rational NSAID use** - community education and pharmacist awareness about over-the-counter analgesics and pain powders. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Food and water safety** reduces post-infectious FD and H. pylori transmission (faeco-oral and oro-oral) - links to Swachh Bharat and Jal Jeevan Mission. [Maastricht VI 2022]`,`**Ayushman Arogya Mandir** (health and wellness centres) handle dyspepsia with lifestyle advice and refer alarm features to the CHC or district hospital for endoscopy. [MoHFW 2023]`,`Screen and treat comorbid **depression and anxiety** under the **National Mental Health Programme / Tele-MANAS** where needed. [MoHFW 2023]`,`Omeprazole, famotidine and amitriptyline are on the **NLEM** and in **Jan Aushadhi** stores, keeping treatment cheap. [NLEM 2022]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2026)`,points:[`**BSG 2022 FD guideline**: positive diagnosis without endoscopy in most under 55 (UK cut-off), TCA as second line, **SSRIs not recommended**, mirtazapine and antipsychotics as options, brain-gut psychotherapy for refractory cases. [BSG FD 2022]`,`**Maastricht VI/Florence 2022**: H. pylori gastritis is an **infectious disease regardless of symptoms**; test-and-treat remains first step in uninvestigated dyspepsia; eradication is the first-line therapy for H. pylori-positive dyspepsia. [Maastricht VI 2022]`,`**ACG 2024 H. pylori guideline**: **14-day bismuth quadruple** is preferred when susceptibility is unknown; **vonoprazan-based dual or triple** therapy is an alternative; clarithromycin triple only if susceptibility is proven. [ACG H. pylori 2024]`,`**Ranitidine was withdrawn in the US in 2020** (NDMA contamination) and **dropped from India's NLEM 2022** in favour of **famotidine**. [NLEM 2022]`,`**Domperidone**: maximum **10 mg up to three times daily for no more than 1 week** (EMA); avoid with QT-prolonging drugs and in cardiac disease. [EMA 2014]`,`ACG/CAG 2017 age cut-off of **60** applies to low-incidence regions; Asian and Indian practice keep a **lower cut-off of about 40-45** because of regional gastric cancer risk. [Asian FD Consensus 2012]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **'Define functional dyspepsia and outline its management'** - write Rome IV with both subtypes, alarm features, the stepwise ladder and a flow chart. [Rome IV 2016]`,`Common mistake: writing **gallstones** or **'gastritis' on endoscopy** as the cause of dyspepsia - neither correlates with symptoms. [BSG FD 2022]`,`Know the **PDS (3 days a week) versus EPS (1 day a week)** frequency criteria. [Rome IV 2016]`,`Know why India uses a **lower endoscopy age (about 45)** than ACG/CAG (60): earlier onset of gastric cancer in Asia. [Asian FD Consensus 2012]`,`Viva: 'Which antidepressant class works in FD?' - **TCAs, not SSRIs**. [BSG FD 2022]`,`Viva: 'What is H. pylori-associated dyspepsia?' - dyspepsia that **resolves 6-12 months after eradication**; it is excluded from FD by Kyoto. [Kyoto Consensus 2015]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for functional dyspepsia`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Omeprazole / pantoprazole`,`20 mg / 40 mg orally once daily, 30 minutes before breakfast`,`8 weeks, then stop or on-demand`,`Headache, diarrhoea; long-term: C. difficile, hypomagnesaemia, B12 deficiency, fractures`],[`Famotidine`,`20 mg orally twice daily`,`4-8 weeks`,`Headache; reduce dose in CKD (eGFR under 50)`],[`Itopride`,`50 mg orally three times daily before meals`,`4-8 weeks`,`Diarrhoea, raised prolactin (rare); no significant QT effect`],[`Levosulpiride`,`25 mg orally three times daily before meals`,`4-8 weeks; avoid long-term`,`Hyperprolactinaemia, galactorrhoea, extrapyramidal effects, tardive dyskinesia in elderly`],[`Domperidone`,`10 mg orally up to three times daily`,`Shortest course, about 1 week`,`QT prolongation, sudden cardiac death; avoid with macrolides, azoles, cardiac disease`],[`Acotiamide`,`100 mg orally three times daily before meals`,`4 weeks, extend if responding`,`Constipation, diarrhoea; mainly for PDS`],[`Amitriptyline`,`10 mg at night, titrate to 30-50 mg`,`Increase every 1-2 weeks; continue 6-12 months`,`Dry mouth, constipation, drowsiness, urinary retention, QT; avoid in glaucoma, BPH, recent MI`],[`Mirtazapine`,`7.5-15 mg at night`,`8-12 weeks`,`Weight gain (useful if underweight), sedation`],[`Buspirone`,`10 mg orally three times daily, 15 minutes before meals`,`4 weeks`,`Dizziness, headache; helps fundic accommodation in PDS`]]},{heading:`Rome IV classification of functional dyspepsia`,columns:[`Feature`,`Postprandial distress syndrome (PDS)`,`Epigastric pain syndrome (EPS)`],rows:[[`Core symptom`,`Postprandial fullness or early satiation`,`Epigastric pain or burning`],[`Minimum frequency`,`At least 3 days a week`,`At least 1 day a week`],[`Severity`,`Stops finishing a regular-sized meal`,`Impacts usual activities`],[`Relation to meals`,`Triggered by meals`,`Meal-induced, meal-relieved or fasting`],[`Dominant mechanism`,`Impaired accommodation, delayed emptying`,`Visceral hypersensitivity, acid sensitivity`],[`First drug`,`Prokinetic (itopride, acotiamide), buspirone`,`PPI or H2RA`],[`Second line`,`TCA, mirtazapine`,`TCA (amitriptyline)`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of dyspepsia`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating clinical clue`,`Confirming test`],rows:[[`Functional dyspepsia`,`Chronic, fluctuating, no alarm features, IBS overlap`,`Normal endoscopy, Rome IV criteria`],[`Peptic ulcer`,`Night pain relieved by food, NSAID or H. pylori`,`Endoscopy`],[`GERD`,`Heartburn and regurgitation dominate`,`PPI response, endoscopy or pH study`],[`Gastric cancer`,`Age over 45, weight loss, anaemia, short history`,`Endoscopy with biopsy`],[`Gastroparesis`,`Vomiting of food eaten hours earlier, diabetes`,`4-hour gastric emptying scintigraphy`],[`Biliary pain`,`Severe episodic pain over 30 minutes, often at night`,`Ultrasound abdomen`],[`Chronic pancreatitis`,`Pain radiating to back, steatorrhoea, alcohol`,`CT, faecal elastase`],[`Coronary ischaemia`,`Exertional, sweating, risk factors`,`ECG, troponin, stress test`]]},{heading:`Investigations and interpretation in dyspepsia`,columns:[`Test`,`When`,`Interpretation / cut-off`],rows:[[`Haemoglobin, ferritin`,`All patients`,`Iron deficiency (ferritin under 30 ng/mL) is an alarm feature`],[`Urea breath test / stool antigen`,`Under endoscopy age, no alarm`,`Positive: eradicate; stop PPI 2 weeks, antibiotics 4 weeks before`],[`Upper GI endoscopy`,`Alarm features or age about 45 or more (India)`,`Normal in FD; biopsy by Sydney protocol`],[`IgA tTG with total IgA`,`IBS overlap, anaemia, diarrhoea`,`Positive: duodenal biopsy for coeliac disease`],[`Ultrasound abdomen`,`Suspected biliary or pancreatic disease`,`Incidental gallstones do not explain dyspepsia`],[`Gastric emptying scintigraphy`,`Prominent vomiting, refractory symptoms`,`More than 10% retention at 4 hours is delayed`],[`ECG`,`Cardiovascular risk, exertional symptoms`,`Exclude inferior wall ischaemia`]]},{heading:`Endoscopy age thresholds across guidelines`,columns:[`Guideline`,`Endoscopy age threshold`,`Below threshold`],rows:[[`ACG/CAG 2017`,`60 years or more`,`H. pylori test-and-treat, then PPI, TCA, prokinetic`],[`BSG 2022 / NICE`,`55 years or more with weight loss (UK cancer pathway)`,`Test-and-treat or PPI trial`],[`Asian FD Consensus 2012`,`40-45 years depending on local gastric cancer incidence`,`Test-and-treat`],[`Indian practice`,`About 45 years, lower with family history`,`Test-and-treat, PPI trial`]]}],redFlags:[`Unintentional weight loss of more than 5% in 6 months`,`Progressive dysphagia or odynophagia`,`Persistent vomiting, especially vomiting of food eaten hours earlier`,`Haematemesis, melaena or iron deficiency anaemia`,`Epigastric mass, Virchow node, jaundice or ascites`,`New-onset dyspepsia after about 45 years in India, or family history of gastric cancer`,`Epigastric pain with sweating, breathlessness or ECG changes (acute coronary syndrome)`],pearls:[`**Rome IV FD = 3 months of symptoms, onset 6 months ago, normal endoscopy** - the '3-6' rule.`,`**PDS is 3 days a week; EPS is 1 day a week**.`,`**Test-and-treat before PPI** in the young without alarm features - the cheapest cure for H. pylori dyspepsia.`,`**TCA, not SSRI**, is the neuromodulator for FD.`,`**Gallstones and endoscopic 'gastritis' do not explain dyspepsia** - do not operate on incidental stones.`,`**Domperidone: 10 mg up to three times daily, 1 week** - QT risk.`,`Indian endoscopy age is **about 45**, not the ACG 60.`,`Mnemonic for alarm features - **VBAD + family history**: Vomiting, Bleeding/anaemia, Abdominal mass/weight loss, Dysphagia.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-dyspepsia-functional-q1`,stem:`According to Rome IV, the postprandial distress syndrome subtype of functional dyspepsia requires bothersome postprandial fullness or early satiation on at least how many days a week?`,options:[`1 day a week`,`2 days a week`,`3 days a week`,`Daily`],answer:2,explanation:`PDS needs postprandial fullness or early satiation on at least 3 days a week, over the last 3 months with onset 6 months earlier. The 1-day-a-week threshold belongs to epigastric pain syndrome, and 2 days a week is the GERD threshold for troublesome heartburn.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-dyspepsia-functional-q2`,stem:`A 28-year-old woman has 4 months of epigastric burning without weight loss, vomiting, bleeding or anaemia. She takes no NSAIDs. What is the most appropriate first step in an Indian primary care clinic?`,options:[`Urea breath test or stool antigen for H. pylori and eradicate if positive`,`Upper GI endoscopy`,`H. pylori serology`,`Ultrasound abdomen for gallstones`],answer:0,explanation:`A young patient without alarm features is managed by non-invasive H. pylori test-and-treat with a breath test or stool antigen. Endoscopy is reserved for alarm features or age about 45 or more in India; serology cannot separate past from current infection in a high-prevalence country; gallstones do not cause dyspepsia.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-dyspepsia-functional-q3`,stem:`A 35-year-old man with functional dyspepsia (epigastric pain syndrome), H. pylori negative, has not improved after 8 weeks of a once-daily PPI. Which is the best-evidenced next drug?`,options:[`Sertraline 50 mg daily`,`Amitriptyline 10 mg at night titrated up`,`Double-dose PPI for 6 months`,`Long-term domperidone 10 mg three times daily`],answer:1,explanation:`Low-dose tricyclic antidepressants are the recommended second-line gut-brain neuromodulator in FD (BSG 2022, ACG/CAG 2017), with an NNT of about 6. SSRIs such as sertraline do not improve FD symptoms; double-dose PPI is no better than standard dose; domperidone should be used only short term because of QT prolongation.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-dyspepsia-functional-q4`,stem:`Which mechanism best explains early satiation and weight loss in postprandial distress syndrome, and is the target of buspirone?`,options:[`Excess gastric acid secretion`,`Rapid gastric emptying`,`Duodenal acid hypersensitivity`,`Impaired gastric fundic accommodation`],answer:3,explanation:`Failure of the proximal stomach to relax after a meal (impaired fundic accommodation) occurs in about 40% of FD and causes early satiation; buspirone and acotiamide improve accommodation. Acid secretion is normal in FD, rapid emptying is uncommon, and duodenal acid sensitivity relates more to nausea and EPS pain.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-dyspepsia-functional-q5`,stem:`A 30-year-old with dyspepsia becomes symptom-free 9 months after successful H. pylori eradication. How is this condition classified?`,options:[`Functional dyspepsia, epigastric pain syndrome`,`H. pylori-associated dyspepsia`,`Placebo response in functional dyspepsia`,`Non-erosive reflux disease`],answer:1,explanation:`The Kyoto consensus defines H. pylori-associated dyspepsia as symptoms that resolve and stay resolved 6-12 months after eradication; it is an organic cause and is excluded from functional dyspepsia. A placebo response would not be sustained this long, and NERD presents with heartburn and regurgitation.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-dyspepsia-functional-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Define functional dyspepsia. Discuss its pathophysiology and outline a stepwise approach to a patient presenting with uninvestigated dyspepsia in Indian primary care.`,openingLines:[`Functional dyspepsia (Rome IV) is bothersome postprandial fullness, early satiation, epigastric pain or burning for the last 3 months with onset at least 6 months earlier and no structural disease on endoscopy.`,`It is a disorder of gut-brain interaction with two subtypes - postprandial distress syndrome and epigastric pain syndrome - and is the commonest final diagnosis in dyspepsia.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and subtypes`,points:[`Rome IV criteria; PDS at least 3 days a week, EPS at least 1 day a week; overlap common.`,`Uninvestigated versus investigated dyspepsia; FD in 70-80% of those scoped.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Impaired fundic accommodation (40%), delayed emptying (25-35%), visceral hypersensitivity.`,`Duodenal eosinophils and mast cells, increased permeability, post-infectious FD.`,`H. pylori gastritis, psychological comorbidity and abnormal brain-gut signalling.`]},{heading:`Initial assessment`,points:[`History of four cardinal symptoms, drugs (NSAIDs, metformin), alarm features, anxiety and depression.`,`Examination: weight, pallor, mass, Virchow node, succussion splash.`,`Haemoglobin, ferritin; ECG if cardiac risk.`]},{heading:`Stepwise approach`,points:[`Alarm features or age about 45 or more: upper GI endoscopy with biopsies.`,`Others: urea breath test or stool antigen; eradicate with 14-day bismuth quadruple and confirm cure.`,`H. pylori negative or persistent: PPI once daily for 8 weeks (EPS) or prokinetic (PDS).`,`Refractory: amitriptyline 10 mg titrated to 30-50 mg; then mirtazapine, buspirone, CBT or hypnotherapy.`]},{heading:`General measures and follow-up`,points:[`Positive explanation, small meals, exercise, stop smoking and NSAIDs.`,`Review every 4-8 weeks; one change at a time; deprescribe PPI; avoid repeat endoscopy.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: uninvestigated dyspepsia - alarm/age to endoscopy, else test-and-treat, PPI, prokinetic, TCA`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and Rome IV subtypes`,marks:2},{part:`Pathophysiology`,marks:2},{part:`Assessment and alarm features`,marks:2},{part:`Stepwise management with doses and flow chart`,marks:4}],keywords:[`Rome IV`,`PDS`,`EPS`,`test and treat`,`PPI`,`amitriptyline`,`alarm features`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-dyspepsia-functional-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate postprandial distress syndrome from epigastric pain syndrome of functional dyspepsia.`,openingLines:[`Rome IV divides functional dyspepsia into postprandial distress syndrome (meal-related fullness and early satiation) and epigastric pain syndrome (epigastric pain or burning).`,`The distinction matters because it picks the first drug: prokinetic for PDS, acid suppression for EPS.`],answer:[{heading:`Symptoms and frequency`,points:[`PDS: postprandial fullness or early satiation, at least 3 days a week.`,`EPS: epigastric pain or burning, at least 1 day a week, not necessarily meal-related.`]},{heading:`Mechanism`,points:[`PDS: impaired accommodation, delayed emptying.`,`EPS: visceral hypersensitivity, acid and duodenal sensitivity.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`PDS: itopride, acotiamide, buspirone; then TCA or mirtazapine.`,`EPS: PPI or H2RA for 8 weeks; then amitriptyline.`,`Both: H. pylori test-and-treat, reassurance, diet and psychotherapy.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column table: PDS versus EPS (symptoms, frequency, mechanism, first drug)`],markSplit:[{part:`Criteria and frequency`,marks:2},{part:`Mechanism`,marks:1},{part:`Treatment`,marks:2}],keywords:[`postprandial distress syndrome`,`epigastric pain syndrome`,`Rome IV`,`prokinetic`,`PPI`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Dyspepsia and peptic ulcer disease chapters`,`Stanghellini V, et al. Rome IV - Gastroduodenal disorders. Gastroenterology 2016`,`Moayyedi P, et al. ACG and CAG Clinical Guideline: Management of Dyspepsia. Am J Gastroenterol 2017`,`Black CJ, et al. British Society of Gastroenterology guidelines on the management of functional dyspepsia. Gut 2022`,`Malfertheiner P, et al. Management of Helicobacter pylori infection: the Maastricht VI/Florence consensus report. Gut 2022`,`Chey WD, et al. ACG Clinical Guideline: Treatment of Helicobacter pylori Infection. Am J Gastroenterol 2024`,`Miwa H, et al. Asian consensus report on functional dyspepsia. J Neurogastroenterol Motil 2012`,`National List of Essential Medicines (NLEM), MoHFW, 2022`]}),t.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-peptic-ulcer-complications`,title:`Peptic ulcer complications: perforation, obstruction, NSAID ulcer prevention`,oneLiner:`Peptic ulcer disease is complicated by **bleeding (commonest), perforation, penetration and gastric outlet obstruction**; perforation is a **surgical emergency (free gas, omental patch repair, then H. pylori eradication)**, obstruction causes **hypochloraemic hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis with paradoxical aciduria**, and NSAID ulcers are prevented by matching **GI and cardiovascular risk** to **PPI co-therapy, a COX-2 inhibitor or avoidance**, with **Zollinger-Ellison syndrome** sought in multiple, distal or refractory ulcers.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`perforated duodenal ulcer`,`perforated peptic ulcer`,`Graham patch`,`omental patch`,`Boey score`,`gastric outlet obstruction`,`pyloric stenosis`,`succussion splash`,`paradoxical aciduria`,`Forrest classification`,`NSAID gastroprotection`,`misoprostol`,`celecoxib`,`etoricoxib`,`PPI co-therapy`,`Zollinger-Ellison syndrome`,`gastrinoma`,`MEN1`,`refractory ulcer`,`penetrating ulcer`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`A **peptic ulcer** is a mucosal break of **5 mm or more through the muscularis mucosae**; complications occur when it erodes a vessel (bleeding), the full wall (perforation), an adjacent organ (penetration) or scars the pylorus (obstruction). [Harrison 22e]`,`Lifetime risk of a complication in untreated PUD is about **1-2% per year**; **bleeding is commonest (about 70% of complications)**, followed by **perforation (about 20%)** and **obstruction (under 5%)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Perforated peptic ulcer** carries **mortality of about 10-25%**, rising to 30-50% in the elderly with delayed presentation - it is the most lethal ulcer complication. [WSES Perforated Peptic Ulcer 2020]`,`Worldwide, ulcer incidence is falling with H. pylori eradication, but complications are **shifting to the elderly on NSAIDs, aspirin, antiplatelets and anticoagulants**. [Harrison 22e]`,`In India, **duodenal ulcer perforation** is among the commonest causes of **secondary peritonitis**, typically in **young and middle-aged men who smoke**, often presenting late. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Gastric outlet obstruction** in adults is now more often **malignant (gastric antral or pancreatic cancer)** than peptic in many series; peptic GOO is commonest in long-standing untreated duodenal ulcer. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Zollinger-Ellison syndrome** causes under 1% of ulcers; about **25% are part of MEN1**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors for complications`,points:[`**H. pylori** and **NSAIDs including low-dose aspirin** are the two dominant causes; they act synergistically, and smoking adds independent risk. [Maastricht VI 2022]`,`**NSAID GI risk factors**: **age over 65, previous ulcer (especially complicated), high-dose or multiple NSAIDs, concurrent aspirin, corticosteroids, SSRIs or anticoagulants**, and H. pylori infection. [ACG NSAID 2009]`,`**Relative bleeding risk by NSAID**: ibuprofen lowest, naproxen and diclofenac intermediate, **ketorolac and piroxicam highest**; **COX-2 selective drugs** have about half the GI risk of non-selective NSAIDs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dual antiplatelet therapy** and **anticoagulant plus antiplatelet** combinations raise ulcer bleeding risk 2-6 fold; DOACs (especially dabigatran and rivaroxaban) carry more GI bleeding than apixaban. [ESC ACS 2023]`,`**Corticosteroids alone** have little ulcer risk, but **steroid plus NSAID** multiplies it. [Harrison 22e]`,`**H. pylori-negative, NSAID-negative (idiopathic) ulcers** are increasing and have a higher rebleeding and mortality rate; consider ZES, Crohn disease, CMV, TB, ischaemia and cocaine or crack use. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Perforation triggers**: NSAIDs, smoking, crack cocaine, steroids, fasting and physiological stress; **Curling ulcer** follows burns and **Cushing ulcer** follows head injury. [Bailey and Love 28e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology and anatomy of complications`,points:[`**Posterior duodenal ulcer erodes the gastroduodenal artery** (massive bleeding) or penetrates the **pancreas** (constant back pain, raised amylase); **lesser-curve gastric ulcer** erodes the **left gastric artery**. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Anterior duodenal ulcer perforates** into the free peritoneal cavity; acid and bile cause **chemical peritonitis** first, then **bacterial peritonitis after 6-12 hours**, explaining the early board-like rigidity and later sepsis. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Gastric outlet obstruction** arises from **acute oedema and spasm** (reversible with PPI and decompression) or **chronic fibrotic scarring** (needs dilatation or surgery). [Harrison 22e]`,`Vomiting gastric juice loses **H+, Cl- and K+**; the kidney reabsorbs sodium with bicarbonate, and when K+ is depleted it exchanges **H+ for Na+ in the distal tubule**, giving **paradoxical aciduria** that perpetuates alkalosis. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**NSAIDs** inhibit **COX-1**, removing prostaglandins that maintain mucus, bicarbonate and mucosal blood flow; they also cause topical injury by ion trapping - so rectal or parenteral NSAIDs still cause ulcers. [Harrison 22e]`,`**COX-2 selective inhibitors** spare gastric COX-1 (less ulcer) but reduce endothelial **prostacyclin** while sparing platelet thromboxane - the basis of their **thrombotic cardiovascular risk**. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Gastrinoma** secretes gastrin autonomously; gastrin drives parietal cell hyperplasia and **massive acid output** that overwhelms the duodenum and jejunum, causing **multiple and distal ulcers**, and acid **inactivates pancreatic lipase** causing diarrhoea and steatorrhoea. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PPIs** raise intragastric pH above 6, which **stabilises the clot** (pepsin is inactive and platelet aggregation works) - the rationale for high-dose PPI after endoscopic haemostasis. [ACG UGIB 2021]`]},{heading:`Classification and scores examiners ask`,points:[`**Forrest classification** of bleeding ulcers: **Ia spurting, Ib oozing, IIa non-bleeding visible vessel, IIb adherent clot, IIc flat pigmented spot, III clean base**. [ACG UGIB 2021]`,`**Rebleeding risk without endoscopic therapy**: Ia/Ib about **55%**, IIa about **43%**, IIb about **22%**, IIc about 10%, III under 5%; endoscopic therapy for **Ia, Ib and IIa**, consider for IIb after clot removal. [ACG UGIB 2021]`,`**Glasgow-Blatchford score** decides who needs intervention (**score 0-1 can be managed as outpatient**); Rockall score predicts mortality - detailed in the GI bleeding topic. [ACG UGIB 2021]`,`**Boey score** for perforation: **shock on admission (SBP under 100 mmHg), delay over 24 hours, major medical comorbidity** - one point each; mortality rises steeply from 0 to 3 points. [WSES Perforated Peptic Ulcer 2020]`,`**PULP score** (Peptic Ulcer Perforation score) combines age, comorbidity, malignancy, AIDS, shock, delay, creatinine and ASA class and outperforms Boey in some cohorts. [WSES Perforated Peptic Ulcer 2020]`,`**Johnson classification of gastric ulcers**: type I lesser curve (normal acid), II gastric with duodenal ulcer (high acid), III prepyloric (high acid), IV high lesser curve near GOJ, V NSAID-induced anywhere. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**ACG NSAID GI risk grading**: **high** = previous complicated ulcer or more than 2 risk factors; **moderate** = 1-2 risk factors; **low** = none. [ACG NSAID 2009]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Perforation**: **sudden severe epigastric pain** spreading over the whole abdomen, often with **shoulder-tip pain** (diaphragmatic irritation); about one-third have no prior ulcer history. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`Classic three phases of perforation: **prostration** (first 2 hours), **reaction or illusory improvement** (2-6 hours), then **peritonitis and sepsis** (after 6-12 hours) - the 'improvement' phase delays diagnosis. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Gastric outlet obstruction**: **projectile, non-bilious vomiting of stale undigested food eaten hours or days earlier**, early satiety, weight loss and relief after vomiting. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Penetration**: change from episodic to **constant, severe pain radiating to the back**, no longer relieved by food or antacids. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bleeding**: haematemesis, coffee-ground vomitus, melaena or syncope; the elderly on NSAIDs often have **no preceding pain**. [ACG UGIB 2021]`,`**ZES clues**: **multiple ulcers, ulcers beyond the first part of the duodenum or in the jejunum, refractory or recurrent ulcers, ulcer with chronic diarrhoea**, ulcer without H. pylori or NSAID, and family history of MEN1 (hypercalcaemia, pituitary tumour). [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **NSAIDs, aspirin, antiplatelets, anticoagulants, steroids, SSRIs, smoking and crack cocaine**, and previous eradication therapy. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Perforation**: patient lies still, shallow breathing, **board-like rigidity**, **obliteration of liver dullness** (free gas) and absent bowel sounds; tachycardia and fever appear later. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Elderly, steroid-treated and immunosuppressed** patients may have **minimal rigidity** despite perforation - rely on imaging. [WSES Perforated Peptic Ulcer 2020]`,`**Gastric outlet obstruction**: **succussion splash** 3-4 hours after a meal, **visible gastric peristalsis moving left to right**, dehydration and weight loss. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Tetany** (Trousseau and Chvostek signs) can occur in GOO because **alkalosis lowers ionised calcium**. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`Feel for an **epigastric mass and Virchow node** in GOO - they point to malignant obstruction. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`In bleeding, record **pulse, BP, postural drop, shock index and per-rectal melaena**. [ACG UGIB 2021]`,`Look for features of **MEN1** (hypercalcaemia signs, visual field defects, galactorrhoea) when ZES is suspected. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Acute pancreatitis**: pain radiates to the back, lipase over 3 times normal, no free gas; perforation can raise amylase mildly - always check for free gas. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute cholecystitis**: right upper quadrant pain, **Murphy sign**, fever; ultrasound shows a thickened gallbladder wall and stones. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Inferior wall MI**: epigastric pain with sweating, ECG changes and troponin rise - do an ECG before laparotomy in the elderly. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mesenteric ischaemia**: pain out of proportion to signs, AF, raised lactate; CT angiography. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Other causes of free gas**: perforated colon, typhoid ileal perforation (India), recent laparotomy or laparoscopy, and peritoneal dialysis. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**GOO differentials**: **antral carcinoma, pancreatic head cancer, duodenal TB, Crohn disease, caustic stricture, gastroparesis** (no mechanical block on endoscopy) and, in infants, hypertrophic pyloric stenosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypergastrinaemia differentials**: **PPI use (commonest), atrophic gastritis or pernicious anaemia, H. pylori, CKD, retained antrum, vagotomy** - in all of these gastric pH is high except retained antrum and G-cell hyperplasia. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Erect chest X-ray** (patient upright for 10 minutes) shows **free gas under the diaphragm in about 75-80%** of perforations; a left lateral decubitus film helps the very ill. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**CT abdomen** is the most sensitive test for perforation (**over 95%**), shows the site, fluid and alternative diagnoses; oral water-soluble contrast shows leak and whether it has sealed. [WSES Perforated Peptic Ulcer 2020]`,`Perforation bloods: **CBC, urea, creatinine, electrolytes, lactate, lipase, blood gas, blood culture, grouping and cross-matching**; check **HIV, HBsAg and HCV** before surgery. [WSES Perforated Peptic Ulcer 2020]`,`**GOO**: serum **Na, K, Cl, bicarbonate, urea, creatinine and urine pH and chloride**; expect **hypochloraemic hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis** with urine chloride under 20 mmol/L. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Endoscopy after decompression** in GOO with **biopsy of every narrowing** to exclude cancer; CT for extrinsic or malignant causes; test H. pylori. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ZES**: **fasting serum gastrin over 10 times the upper limit (about 1000 pg/mL) with gastric pH under 2 is diagnostic**; if gastrin is lower, a **secretin stimulation test** (rise of 120 pg/mL or more) confirms. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Do not stop PPIs abruptly** to test gastrin in suspected ZES - acid hypersecretion can cause perforation; switch to an H2RA under specialist supervision or test in a referral centre. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Localisation of gastrinoma**: **Ga-68 DOTATATE PET-CT** (most sensitive), EUS for pancreatic lesions, and serum calcium and PTH for MEN1. [ENETS 2023]`,`**Biopsy every gastric ulcer** (malignancy) and re-scope at **8-12 weeks** to confirm healing; refractory duodenal ulcers also need biopsy for TB, Crohn disease and CMV. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - NSAID and aspirin gastroprotection`,points:[`**Test and treat H. pylori before starting long-term NSAIDs or aspirin** in those with ulcer history; eradication alone is not enough for continuing NSAID users - add a PPI. [Maastricht VI 2022]`,`**Low GI and low CV risk**: least ulcerogenic NSAID (ibuprofen) at the **lowest dose for the shortest time**; prefer paracetamol or topical NSAIDs. [ACG NSAID 2009]`,`**Moderate GI risk**: **NSAID plus PPI** (omeprazole 20 mg or pantoprazole 40 mg once daily) or misoprostol. [ACG NSAID 2009]`,`**High GI risk** (previous complicated ulcer): **avoid NSAIDs if possible**; if essential, **COX-2 inhibitor plus PPI** - in the CONCERN trial celecoxib plus esomeprazole rebleed **5.6% versus 12.3%** with naproxen plus esomeprazole. [CONCERN trial 2017]`,`**High CV risk needing low-dose aspirin**: naproxen plus PPI if NSAID essential; **avoid COX-2 inhibitors and NSAIDs altogether when both GI and CV risk are high**. [ACG NSAID 2009]`,`**Misoprostol 200 microgram four times daily** reduces NSAID ulcer complications by about 40% but causes **diarrhoea and cramps** and is **contraindicated in pregnancy** (abortifacient); 400-600 microgram/day is better tolerated. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Standard-dose H2RAs do not prevent NSAID gastric ulcers**; **double-dose famotidine (40 mg twice daily)** offers partial protection - a PPI is preferred. [Harrison 22e]`,`On **DAPT, aspirin plus anticoagulant or triple therapy**, give a **PPI to those at high GI bleeding risk**; with clopidogrel prefer **pantoprazole** over omeprazole or esomeprazole (CYP2C19 interaction). [ESC ACS 2023]`,`**Celecoxib 100-200 mg twice daily** or **etoricoxib 60-90 mg once daily** (available in India); in PRECISION celecoxib was **non-inferior for CV safety** to ibuprofen and naproxen at moderate doses with fewer GI events. [PRECISION 2016]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency`,points:[`**Perforation - resuscitate first**: IV crystalloid, **nil by mouth, nasogastric decompression, urinary catheter**, analgesia, **IV PPI (pantoprazole 80 mg bolus then 40 mg twice daily)** and early **broad-spectrum antibiotics** (e.g. ceftriaxone plus metronidazole or piperacillin-tazobactam; add antifungal if critically ill). [WSES Perforated Peptic Ulcer 2020]`,`**Source control within 12 hours (ideally sooner)**: **laparoscopic or open simple closure with omental patch (Graham or Cellan-Jones)** and thorough peritoneal lavage; definitive acid-reducing surgery is rarely needed now. [WSES Perforated Peptic Ulcer 2020]`,`**Perforated gastric ulcer**: biopsy the edges or excise the ulcer to exclude cancer; giant (over 2 cm) perforations may need distal gastrectomy or controlled tube duodenostomy. [WSES Perforated Peptic Ulcer 2020]`,`**Non-operative (Taylor) approach** - NG suction, IV fluids, PPI, antibiotics - only for **young stable patients with a sealed leak on water-soluble contrast**, under surgical observation; operate if no improvement in 12-24 hours. [WSES Perforated Peptic Ulcer 2020]`,`After repair, **test for H. pylori and eradicate**, continue PPI for **8 weeks**, stop NSAIDs and smoking - this prevents recurrence. [WSES Perforated Peptic Ulcer 2020]`,`**GOO**: NG decompression for **72 hours**, IV **0.9% saline with potassium chloride** (correct chloride first - alkalosis will not correct without it), IV PPI, nutrition support; then endoscopy with biopsy. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Benign GOO**: **endoscopic balloon dilatation** (graded, repeated sessions; perforation risk a few percent) plus eradication; **surgery (vagotomy with gastrojejunostomy or antrectomy)** if dilatation fails. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Malignant GOO**: **enteral (duodenal) stent** for short life expectancy, **surgical gastrojejunostomy** or **EUS-guided gastroenterostomy** for longer survival in expert centres. [ESGE 2022]`,`**Bleeding ulcer**: resuscitate, restrictive transfusion (Hb threshold 7 g/dL, 8 with cardiovascular disease), endoscopy within 24 hours, dual endoscopic therapy for Forrest Ia-IIa, then **high-dose PPI for 72 hours** - details in the GI bleeding topic. [ACG UGIB 2021]`]},{heading:`Zollinger-Ellison syndrome management`,points:[`**High-dose PPI is first line**: start **omeprazole 60 mg/day** (or equivalent), titrate to keep basal acid output under 10 mmol/h; many need 60-120 mg/day in divided doses. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sporadic localised gastrinoma**: **surgical resection** (duodenotomy and lymphadenectomy) offers about 30-40% cure; about **60-90% lie in the gastrinoma triangle**, mostly in the duodenum. [ENETS 2023]`,`**MEN1 gastrinomas** are usually multiple and small - surgery is reserved for tumours over 2 cm; **correct hyperparathyroidism first** because hypercalcaemia stimulates gastrin. [ENETS 2023]`,`**Metastatic disease**: somatostatin analogues (octreotide LAR, lanreotide), PRRT with Lu-177 DOTATATE, everolimus or sunitinib. [ENETS 2023]`,`Long-term PPI in ZES needs monitoring of **vitamin B12** and attention to gastric neuroendocrine tumours in MEN1. [Harrison 22e]`,`Screen first-degree relatives for **MEN1** (genetic testing) when MEN1 is confirmed. [ENETS 2023]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Elderly**: perforation and bleeding are often painless; mortality is higher; avoid NSAIDs, use paracetamol or topical diclofenac; give PPI with antiplatelets. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pregnancy**: **misoprostol is contraindicated**; PPIs (omeprazole) are acceptable; perforation in pregnancy is rare but delayed diagnosis is common. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**CKD**: avoid NSAIDs and COX-2 inhibitors entirely (nephrotoxicity); famotidine needs dose reduction; magnesium antacids are avoided. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Cirrhosis**: NSAIDs precipitate renal failure, ascites and variceal bleeding - avoid; paracetamol up to 2 g/day is safer. [Harrison 22e]`,`**After PCI or ACS**: never stop antiplatelets on your own because of dyspepsia; add PPI and discuss with cardiology; resume aspirin as soon as haemostasis is secured after ulcer bleeding. [ESC ACS 2023]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: if CT is unavailable, an erect chest X-ray plus clinical rigidity is enough to refer for laparotomy; start fluids, NG tube, antibiotics and PPI before transfer. [Bailey and Love 28e]`]},{heading:`Complications, prognosis, referral and follow-up`,points:[`Perforation mortality is predicted by **age, shock, delay over 24 hours, comorbidity and perforation size**; most deaths are from sepsis and multi-organ failure. [WSES Perforated Peptic Ulcer 2020]`,`**Postoperative complications**: leak from patch, intra-abdominal abscess, wound infection, burst abdomen, pneumonia, and recurrent ulcer if H. pylori is not eradicated. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**After gastric surgery**: **dumping syndrome** (early - osmotic, within 30 minutes; late - reactive hypoglycaemia at 1-3 hours), afferent loop syndrome, B12 and iron deficiency, bone disease and gastric stump cancer. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**GOO** causes malnutrition, aspiration pneumonia, refeeding syndrome on re-feeding, and tetany from alkalosis. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Ulcer recurrence after eradication** is under 10% at 1 year versus over 60% without eradication. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ZES**: with PPI control, prognosis depends on **liver metastases**; 10-year survival exceeds 90% without liver metastases. [ENETS 2023]`,`**Same-day surgical referral** for sudden severe abdominal pain with rigidity or free gas, and for persistent vomiting with succussion splash. [WSES Perforated Peptic Ulcer 2020]`,`**Confirm H. pylori eradication** at least 4 weeks after therapy in every complicated ulcer. [Maastricht VI 2022]`,`**Re-scope gastric ulcers at 8-12 weeks** for healing and biopsy; refer refractory ulcers for ZES and other rare causes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Review gastroprotection at every prescription** of NSAIDs, aspirin, antiplatelets or anticoagulants; document GI and CV risk. [ACG NSAID 2009]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`**Smoking cessation** is the strongest modifiable factor for perforation and ulcer recurrence - offer brief advice and NRT under the **National Tobacco Control Programme**. [NTCP 2023]`,`Educate on the danger of **over-the-counter NSAIDs and 'pain powders'** and on taking analgesics with food and PPI where needed. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Aspirin for primary prevention** is not recommended in most older adults because bleeding risk outweighs benefit. [USPSTF 2022]`,`Patients with **arthritis on long-term NSAIDs** seen under **NP-NCD** should have their GI and CV risk documented. [MoHFW 2023]`,`Teach **warning symptoms** - black stools, vomiting blood, sudden severe pain, persistent vomiting - and to seek care immediately. [ACG UGIB 2021]`,`Pantoprazole, omeprazole and famotidine are on the **NLEM 2022** and in **Jan Aushadhi** stores. [NLEM 2022]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2020-2026)`,points:[`**WSES 2020 perforated peptic ulcer guideline**: CT as the imaging of choice, laparoscopic repair where expertise exists, non-operative management only in selected sealed perforations, eradication after repair. [WSES Perforated Peptic Ulcer 2020]`,`**ACG 2021 upper GI and ulcer bleeding guideline**: GBS 0-1 outpatient care, endoscopy within 24 hours, **high-dose PPI (continuous or intermittent) for 3 days** after endoscopic therapy, then twice daily to 2 weeks. [ACG UGIB 2021]`,`**ESC 2023 ACS guideline**: **PPI recommended (class I) with antithrombotic therapy in patients at high GI bleeding risk**. [ESC ACS 2023]`,`**Maastricht VI 2022 and ACG 2024**: test-and-treat H. pylori in **long-term aspirin and NSAID users with ulcer history**; bismuth quadruple 14 days preferred. [ACG H. pylori 2024]`,`**ENETS 2023 guidance on duodenal-pancreatic NETs**: Ga-68 DOTATATE PET-CT for localisation; surgery for sporadic gastrinoma; PRRT for progressive metastatic disease. [ENETS 2023]`,`**Vonoprazan** (potassium-competitive acid blocker) is licensed in India and Japan; it is used in H. pylori regimens and for NSAID or aspirin ulcer prevention in Japan, but PPI remains standard after ulcer bleeding. [ACG H. pylori 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic: **'Discuss the management of a perforated duodenal ulcer'** - resuscitation, imaging, Graham patch, eradication, and Boey score. [WSES Perforated Peptic Ulcer 2020]`,`Classic: **'Metabolic changes in gastric outlet obstruction'** - hypochloraemic hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis with **paradoxical aciduria**; treat with **normal saline and KCl**. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`Viva: **Forrest classes and which need endoscopic therapy** (Ia, Ib, IIa). [ACG UGIB 2021]`,`Viva: **'When do you suspect ZES?'** - multiple, distal, refractory ulcers, ulcer with diarrhoea, no H. pylori or NSAID, MEN1 family history. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: giving **Ringer lactate or dextrose alone** in GOO - chloride and potassium are what correct the alkalosis. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`Common mistake: stopping aspirin indefinitely after an ulcer bleed in a patient with a coronary stent - **resume early with PPI**. [ACG UGIB 2021]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for complicated ulcer and gastroprotection`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Pantoprazole IV`,`80 mg bolus, then 8 mg/h infusion or 40 mg twice daily IV`,`72 hours after endoscopic therapy, then oral twice daily to 2 weeks`,`Headache, C. difficile; generally safe`],[`Omeprazole / pantoprazole oral`,`20 mg / 40 mg once daily before breakfast`,`8 weeks for healing; long-term with NSAID or DAPT if high risk`,`Hypomagnesaemia, B12 deficiency, fractures, C. difficile with long use`],[`Omeprazole (ZES)`,`60 mg/day orally, titrate to 60-120 mg/day in divided doses`,`Lifelong unless tumour resected`,`Do not stop abruptly; B12 deficiency`],[`Misoprostol`,`200 microgram orally 2-4 times daily with NSAID`,`As long as NSAID continues`,`Diarrhoea, cramps; contraindicated in pregnancy`],[`Famotidine`,`40 mg orally twice daily (gastroprotection)`,`With NSAID`,`Less effective than PPI; reduce in CKD`],[`Celecoxib`,`100-200 mg orally twice daily`,`Lowest dose, shortest time`,`CV thrombosis, renal impairment, oedema; avoid in IHD, stroke, CKD; sulfonamide allergy`],[`Etoricoxib`,`60-90 mg orally once daily (120 mg short-term in gout)`,`Short courses`,`Hypertension, CV events; avoid in uncontrolled hypertension and IHD`],[`Bismuth quadruple (eradication)`,`PPI twice daily + bismuth + tetracycline 500 mg four times daily + metronidazole 400 mg three times daily`,`14 days`,`Black stools, nausea; tetracycline avoided in pregnancy and children`]]},{heading:`Forrest classification and rebleeding risk`,columns:[`Forrest class`,`Endoscopic finding`,`Rebleed without therapy`,`Endoscopic therapy`],rows:[[`Ia`,`Active spurting`,`About 55% (up to 100%)`,`Yes - dual therapy (adrenaline plus thermal or clips)`],[`Ib`,`Active oozing`,`About 55%`,`Yes`],[`IIa`,`Non-bleeding visible vessel`,`About 43%`,`Yes`],[`IIb`,`Adherent clot`,`About 22%`,`Consider clot removal and treat the underlying lesion`],[`IIc`,`Flat pigmented spot`,`About 10%`,`No; oral PPI`],[`III`,`Clean base`,`Under 5%`,`No; early feeding and discharge`]]},{heading:`NSAID prescribing by GI and cardiovascular risk`,columns:[`CV risk`,`Low GI risk (no factors)`,`Moderate GI risk (1-2 factors)`,`High GI risk (prior complicated ulcer or more than 2 factors)`],rows:[[`Low CV risk (no aspirin)`,`NSAID alone, lowest dose`,`NSAID plus PPI or misoprostol`,`Avoid; if essential COX-2 inhibitor plus PPI`],[`High CV risk (on low-dose aspirin)`,`Naproxen plus PPI`,`Naproxen plus PPI`,`Avoid NSAIDs and COX-2 inhibitors`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of acute epigastric catastrophe`,columns:[`Condition`,`Key clinical feature`,`Key test`],rows:[[`Perforated peptic ulcer`,`Sudden pain, board-like rigidity, absent liver dullness`,`Erect CXR free gas; CT`],[`Acute pancreatitis`,`Pain to back, vomiting, no free gas`,`Lipase over 3 times normal; CT after 72 hours if needed`],[`Acute cholecystitis`,`RUQ pain, Murphy sign, fever`,`Ultrasound`],[`Inferior wall MI`,`Sweating, risk factors, epigastric pain`,`ECG, troponin`],[`Mesenteric ischaemia`,`Pain out of proportion to signs, AF`,`Lactate, CT angiography`],[`Ruptured AAA`,`Pulsatile mass, shock, back pain`,`Bedside ultrasound, CT`]]},{heading:`Investigations and cut-offs in ulcer complications`,columns:[`Situation`,`Test`,`Interpretation`],rows:[[`Suspected perforation`,`Erect chest X-ray`,`Free gas in 75-80%; normal film does not exclude`],[`Suspected perforation`,`CT abdomen`,`Over 95% sensitive; shows site and collections`],[`Gastric outlet obstruction`,`Electrolytes, blood gas, urine chloride`,`Low Cl, low K, high HCO3; urine Cl under 20 mmol/L; paradoxical aciduria`],[`Gastric outlet obstruction`,`Endoscopy with biopsy after decompression`,`Benign scar versus malignancy`],[`Suspected ZES`,`Fasting gastrin with gastric pH`,`Gastrin over 10 times ULN with pH under 2 is diagnostic`],[`Equivocal ZES`,`Secretin stimulation test`,`Rise of 120 pg/mL or more confirms gastrinoma`],[`Gastrinoma localisation`,`Ga-68 DOTATATE PET-CT, EUS`,`Most sensitive for primary and metastases`]]}],redFlags:[`Sudden severe epigastric pain with board-like rigidity or absent liver dullness (perforation)`,`Free gas under the diaphragm on erect chest X-ray`,`Haematemesis or melaena with tachycardia, hypotension or syncope`,`Persistent projectile vomiting of stale food with succussion splash (gastric outlet obstruction)`,`Severe hypokalaemia or alkalosis with tetany or arrhythmia`,`Constant back pain replacing episodic ulcer pain (penetration)`,`Multiple, distal or refractory ulcers with diarrhoea (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome)`],pearls:[`**Bleeding is the commonest, perforation the deadliest** ulcer complication.`,`**GOO = hypochloraemic hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis with paradoxical aciduria**; treat with **saline plus KCl**.`,`**Forrest Ia, Ib, IIa get endoscopic therapy**; IIc and III do not.`,`**Boey score: Shock, Delay over 24 hours, Comorbidity** - one point each.`,`**Graham patch plus eradication** has replaced vagotomy for perforated duodenal ulcer.`,`**High GI plus high CV risk = no NSAID, no COX-2** - use paracetamol, topical NSAIDs or non-drug therapy.`,`With clopidogrel, choose **pantoprazole**, not omeprazole.`,`**ZES: gastrin over 1000 pg/mL plus pH under 2**; never stop the PPI abruptly to test.`,`Every gastric ulcer is **biopsied and re-scoped at 8-12 weeks**.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-peptic-ulcer-complications-q1`,stem:`A 45-year-old man has vomited stale food daily for 2 weeks. A succussion splash is present. Which set of laboratory findings is most likely?`,options:[`Hyperchloraemic metabolic acidosis with hyperkalaemia`,`Hypochloraemic hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis with paradoxical aciduria`,`Normal anion gap acidosis with alkaline urine`,`Respiratory alkalosis with hypocalcaemia`],answer:1,explanation:`Gastric outlet obstruction loses HCl and potassium, producing hypochloraemic hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis; potassium depletion makes the distal tubule secrete H+ in exchange for Na+, so the urine is paradoxically acidic. Acidosis patterns occur with diarrhoea, not vomiting gastric juice, and respiratory alkalosis is not the primary disturbance.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-peptic-ulcer-complications-q2`,stem:`At endoscopy for melaena, a duodenal ulcer shows a non-bleeding visible vessel. What is its Forrest class and the correct action?`,options:[`Forrest IIc; oral PPI only`,`Forrest Ib; no endoscopic therapy`,`Forrest IIa; endoscopic haemostasis followed by high-dose PPI`,`Forrest III; discharge the same day`],answer:2,explanation:`A non-bleeding visible vessel is Forrest IIa, with about 43% rebleeding risk, so it needs endoscopic therapy followed by high-dose PPI for 72 hours. Forrest IIc is a flat pigmented spot and Forrest III a clean base, both low risk and managed without endoscopic therapy; Forrest Ib is active oozing, which does need therapy.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-peptic-ulcer-complications-q3`,stem:`A 68-year-old with osteoarthritis had a bleeding gastric ulcer 2 years ago and takes low-dose aspirin after a coronary stent. He asks for a stronger painkiller than paracetamol. What is the best advice?`,options:[`Etoricoxib 90 mg daily`,`Diclofenac with famotidine 20 mg daily`,`Naproxen alone`,`Avoid NSAIDs and COX-2 inhibitors; use topical NSAID, non-drug therapy and continue PPI with aspirin`],answer:3,explanation:`He has high GI risk (previous complicated ulcer) and high CV risk (aspirin after a stent), so both NSAIDs and COX-2 inhibitors should be avoided; topical NSAIDs and physiotherapy are safer, and aspirin should continue with PPI cover. Etoricoxib raises CV risk, standard-dose famotidine does not protect against gastric ulcer, and naproxen alone gives no GI protection.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-peptic-ulcer-complications-q4`,stem:`A 40-year-old has recurrent jejunal ulcers, chronic diarrhoea, negative H. pylori tests and no NSAID use. On PPI therapy, fasting gastrin is raised. What is the most appropriate next step?`,options:[`Stop the PPI abruptly for 1 week and repeat gastrin`,`Measure gastric pH with gastrin, and if equivocal a secretin stimulation test at a referral centre`,`Start octreotide immediately`,`Proceed directly to Whipple surgery`],answer:1,explanation:`Gastrin must be interpreted with gastric pH because PPIs themselves raise gastrin; gastrin over 10 times normal with pH under 2 is diagnostic, and the secretin test resolves equivocal cases. Abrupt PPI withdrawal in suspected ZES risks perforation, octreotide is for metastatic disease, and surgery follows localisation with DOTATATE PET-CT.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-peptic-ulcer-complications-q5`,stem:`Which of the following is a component of the Boey score for perforated peptic ulcer?`,options:[`Age over 70 years`,`Serum albumin under 3 g/dL`,`Delay of more than 24 hours from perforation to presentation`,`Perforation size over 1 cm`],answer:2,explanation:`The Boey score has three items - shock on admission, major medical comorbidity and delay over 24 hours - each scoring one point. Age is part of the PULP score, not Boey; albumin and perforation size influence outcome but are not Boey components.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-peptic-ulcer-complications-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Enumerate the complications of peptic ulcer disease. Discuss the clinical features, investigations and management of a perforated duodenal ulcer, and add a note on prevention of NSAID-induced ulcers.`,openingLines:[`Peptic ulcer complications are bleeding (commonest), perforation (most lethal), penetration and gastric outlet obstruction, increasingly seen in elderly NSAID, aspirin and anticoagulant users.`,`Perforated duodenal ulcer is a surgical emergency with 10-25% mortality; resuscitation, early source control with omental patch and H. pylori eradication are the pillars.`],answer:[{heading:`Complications enumerated`,points:[`Bleeding (70%), perforation (20%), gastric outlet obstruction (under 5%), penetration, malignancy in gastric ulcer, recurrence.`]},{heading:`Clinical features of perforation`,points:[`Sudden severe epigastric pain, shoulder-tip pain; phases of prostration, reaction and peritonitis.`,`Board-like rigidity, obliterated liver dullness, absent bowel sounds; minimal signs in the elderly.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Erect chest X-ray (free gas 75-80%); CT abdomen most sensitive.`,`CBC, electrolytes, creatinine, lactate, lipase, ABG, cultures, cross-match, ECG.`,`Boey score: shock, delay over 24 hours, comorbidity.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Resuscitate, NG tube, catheter, IV PPI, broad-spectrum antibiotics.`,`Laparoscopic or open Graham omental patch with lavage within 12 hours.`,`Non-operative Taylor approach only for sealed perforation in stable young patients.`,`Postoperatively: H. pylori eradication, PPI 8 weeks, stop NSAIDs and smoking, confirm cure.`]},{heading:`Prevention of NSAID ulcers`,points:[`Assess GI risk (age over 65, previous ulcer, steroids, anticoagulants, aspirin) and CV risk.`,`Moderate GI risk: NSAID plus PPI; high GI risk: COX-2 plus PPI or avoid; high GI plus CV risk: avoid both.`,`Test and treat H. pylori before long-term NSAIDs; misoprostol is an alternative except in pregnancy.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: perforation - resuscitation, imaging, surgery or non-operative, eradication`,`Matrix of GI versus CV risk for NSAID choice`],markSplit:[{part:`Complications enumerated`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical features and investigations of perforation`,marks:3},{part:`Management of perforation`,marks:3},{part:`NSAID ulcer prevention`,marks:3}],keywords:[`perforation`,`Graham patch`,`Boey score`,`free gas`,`NSAID`,`COX-2`,`PPI`,`H. pylori`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-peptic-ulcer-complications-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on gastric outlet obstruction due to peptic ulcer, including the metabolic derangement and its correction.`,openingLines:[`Gastric outlet obstruction is mechanical blockage at the pylorus or duodenum; peptic GOO results from oedema or fibrotic scarring of a pyloric or duodenal ulcer.`,`Its hallmark is hypochloraemic hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis with paradoxical aciduria.`],answer:[{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Projectile non-bilious vomiting of stale food, early satiety, weight loss.`,`Succussion splash, visible peristalsis left to right, dehydration, tetany.`]},{heading:`Metabolic derangement`,points:[`Loss of HCl and K+; renal bicarbonate retention; distal H+ secretion gives paradoxical aciduria.`,`Urine chloride under 20 mmol/L; low ionised calcium causes tetany.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`NG decompression 72 hours; IV 0.9% saline with KCl; IV PPI; nutrition.`,`Endoscopy and biopsy to exclude cancer; H. pylori eradication.`,`Balloon dilatation; surgery (vagotomy plus gastrojejunostomy or antrectomy) if it fails.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of the vomiting-alkalosis-paradoxical aciduria sequence`],markSplit:[{part:`Clinical features`,marks:1},{part:`Metabolic changes`,marks:2},{part:`Management`,marks:2}],keywords:[`gastric outlet obstruction`,`paradoxical aciduria`,`succussion splash`,`balloon dilatation`,`normal saline`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Peptic ulcer disease and related disorders`,`Bailey and Love's Short Practice of Surgery, 28th edition, 2023`,`Tarasconi A, et al. Perforated and bleeding peptic ulcer: WSES guidelines. World J Emerg Surg 2020`,`Laine L, et al. ACG Clinical Guideline: Upper Gastrointestinal and Ulcer Bleeding. Am J Gastroenterol 2021`,`Lanza FL, et al. ACG Guidelines for prevention of NSAID-related ulcer complications. Am J Gastroenterol 2009`,`Byrne RA, et al. 2023 ESC Guidelines for the management of acute coronary syndromes. Eur Heart J 2023`,`Malfertheiner P, et al. Maastricht VI/Florence consensus report. Gut 2022`,`ENETS 2023 guidance paper for gastroduodenal and pancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasms (including gastrinoma). J Neuroendocrinol 2023`]}),t.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-gastroparesis-vomiting`,title:`Approach to nausea, vomiting and gastroparesis`,oneLiner:`**Nausea and vomiting** are symptoms with GI, CNS, vestibular, metabolic, drug and pregnancy causes that are sorted by **timing, content and associated features**, while **gastroparesis** is **objectively delayed gastric emptying without mechanical obstruction (over 10% retention at 4 hours on solid scintigraphy)** - commonest in **long-standing diabetes, idiopathic and post-surgical** forms - treated with **small-particle low-fat diet, glucose control, metoclopramide or domperidone and a cause-matched antiemetic ladder**; **cyclic vomiting** and **cannabinoid hyperemesis** are episodic Rome IV disorders not to be missed.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`nausea`,`vomiting`,`gastroparesis`,`diabetic gastroparesis`,`gastric emptying scintigraphy`,`cyclic vomiting syndrome`,`cannabinoid hyperemesis syndrome`,`chronic nausea and vomiting syndrome`,`rumination syndrome`,`chemoreceptor trigger zone`,`metoclopramide`,`domperidone`,`ondansetron`,`erythromycin`,`aprepitant`,`haloperidol`,`G-POEM`,`gastric electrical stimulation`,`antiemetic ladder`,`hyperemesis`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Nausea** is the unpleasant sensation of an imminent need to vomit; **vomiting** is forceful oral expulsion of gastric contents; **regurgitation** is effortless return without retching; **rumination** is effortless regurgitation of recently eaten food that is re-chewed or spat. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Gastroparesis** is **symptomatic, objectively delayed gastric emptying of solids in the absence of mechanical obstruction**. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`Definite gastroparesis affects about **10-25 per 100,000** people (higher in women); symptoms suggestive of it are far commoner. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`Cumulative 10-year incidence is about **5% in type 1 diabetes** and **1% in type 2 diabetes** (Olmsted County); in absolute numbers, type 2 contributes more cases. [Harrison 22e]`,`India has about **101 million people with diabetes** (ICMR-INDIAB 2023), so **diabetic gastroparesis** and drug-induced delayed emptying (GLP-1 agonists) are increasingly seen in primary care. [ICMR-INDIAB 2023]`,`Aetiology split of gastroparesis: **idiopathic about 35-50%**, **diabetic about 25-30%**, **post-surgical about 10-15%**, with drug, neurological and connective-tissue causes making up the rest. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**Cyclic vomiting syndrome** affects about **2% of adults** in surveys and is strongly linked to **migraine**; **cannabinoid hyperemesis** is rising wherever cannabis use rises. [Rome IV 2016]`]},{heading:`Aetiology - causes of nausea and vomiting`,points:[`**GI obstruction and inflammation**: gastric outlet obstruction, small-bowel obstruction, gastroparesis, pancreatitis, cholecystitis, hepatitis, appendicitis, peptic ulcer, gastroenteritis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CNS**: **raised intracranial pressure** (tumour, haemorrhage, meningitis), migraine, seizures, and **vestibular** causes (labyrinthitis, BPPV, Meniere disease, motion sickness). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Metabolic and endocrine**: **pregnancy, DKA, uraemia, hypercalcaemia, hyponatraemia, adrenal insufficiency, hyperthyroidism and hypothyroidism**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drugs and toxins**: chemotherapy, **opioids, digoxin, metformin, GLP-1 agonists, antibiotics (erythromycin, metronidazole), iron, NSAIDs, alcohol, cannabis** and organophosphate poisoning. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cardiac**: **inferior wall MI** and heart failure with hepatic congestion. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Psychiatric and functional**: anorexia nervosa, bulimia, anxiety, **chronic nausea and vomiting syndrome**, cyclic vomiting syndrome, rumination. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Causes of gastroparesis**: diabetes, idiopathic or **post-viral**, post-surgical (vagotomy, fundoplication, bariatric, lung or heart transplant), Parkinson disease, **systemic sclerosis**, amyloidosis, hypothyroidism, paraneoplastic and drugs. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - vomiting reflex and gastric motor failure`,points:[`Vomiting is coordinated by the **central pattern generator in the brainstem (nucleus tractus solitarius and dorsal vagal complex)**, which receives four inputs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chemoreceptor trigger zone (area postrema)** lies outside the blood-brain barrier and senses drugs and toxins via **D2, 5-HT3, NK1 and opioid receptors** - why opioids, digoxin, uraemia and chemotherapy cause nausea. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Vagal afferents from the gut** fire through **5-HT3 receptors** when enterochromaffin cells release serotonin (chemotherapy, gastroenteritis) - the target of **ondansetron**. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Vestibular input** acts through **H1 and muscarinic M1 receptors** - so motion sickness responds to **promethazine, cinnarizine and hyoscine**, not ondansetron. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Cortical and limbic inputs** explain anticipatory and anxiety-related nausea - helped by **benzodiazepines**; **raised ICP** acts directly on the brainstem. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Gastroparesis mechanisms**: loss of **interstitial cells of Cajal (pacemaker)**, loss of **neuronal nitric oxide synthase** neurons, loss of anti-inflammatory **M2 macrophages**, vagal neuropathy, antral hypomotility and **pylorospasm**. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**Acute hyperglycaemia (glucose over 200 mg/dL) itself slows emptying** and blunts prokinetic response; erratic emptying causes **unpredictable postprandial hypoglycaemia** on insulin. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**Drug mapping**: metoclopramide = central D2 block plus **5-HT4 agonist prokinetic**; **domperidone = peripheral D2 block** (does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so few extrapyramidal effects); erythromycin = **motilin agonist**; aprepitant = **NK1 antagonist**. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`]},{heading:`Classification - Rome IV nausea and vomiting disorders and gastroparesis severity`,points:[`**Chronic nausea and vomiting syndrome (CNVS)**: bothersome nausea on at least **1 day a week** and/or vomiting at least **once a week**, without eating disorder, rumination or self-induced vomiting, for 3 months with onset 6 months earlier. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Cyclic vomiting syndrome (CVS)**: **stereotyped episodes of acute-onset vomiting lasting under 1 week**, at least **3 episodes in the past year and 2 in the past 6 months**, at least 1 week apart, with **no vomiting between episodes**. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Cannabinoid hyperemesis syndrome (CHS)**: CVS-like episodes after **prolonged excessive cannabis use**, relieved by **sustained cessation**; pathological hot bathing is supportive. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Rumination syndrome**: persistent effortless regurgitation of recently ingested food, **not preceded by retching**, re-chewed and swallowed or spat. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Gastroparesis severity by 4-hour scintigraphy retention**: **mild 10-15%**, **moderate 15-35%**, **severe over 35%** - severity of delay correlates only loosely with symptoms. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**Clinical severity (Abell grading)**: grade 1 mild - controlled with diet; grade 2 compensated - needs drugs, rarely admitted; grade 3 refractory - frequent admissions, nutritional failure. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**CVS phases**: **prodrome, emetic, recovery and inter-episodic (well)** - treatment differs for each phase. [ANMS-CVSA CVS 2019]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Timing**: early-morning vomiting suggests **pregnancy, uraemia, alcohol or raised ICP**; vomiting **1 hour or more after meals** suggests gastroparesis or outlet obstruction; vomiting soon after eating suggests ulcer near the pylorus or psychogenic cause. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Content**: undigested food hours later = **gastroparesis or GOO**; bile = obstruction beyond the ampulla; **faeculent = distal bowel obstruction or gastrocolic fistula**; blood or coffee grounds = upper GI bleed. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Gastroparesis symptoms**: nausea, vomiting, **early satiety, postprandial fullness, bloating** and upper abdominal pain; weight loss and erratic glucose control in diabetes. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**Associated features**: headache with vomiting (raised ICP, migraine), vertigo (vestibular), chest pain (MI), polyuria and abdominal pain (DKA), amenorrhoea (pregnancy). [Harrison 22e]`,`**CVS clue**: identical attacks that start often in the early morning, last hours to days, with **complete wellness in between**, often with migraine or family history of migraine. [ANMS-CVSA CVS 2019]`,`**CHS clue**: daily cannabis use (ganja, charas, bhang) for months to years, episodes of intractable vomiting with **relief from hot showers or baths**. [AGA CHS 2024]`,`Ask about **medications** including opioids, GLP-1 agonists, metformin and digoxin, and about diet, weight, eating attitudes and stress. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`In diabetics, ask for **other autonomic neuropathy** - postural dizziness, erectile dysfunction, diarrhoea, gustatory sweating - that points to diabetic gastroparesis. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Assess **hydration**: pulse, BP, postural drop, capillary refill, mucous membranes, urine output. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Succussion splash** 3-4 hours after a meal suggests retained gastric contents - gastroparesis or outlet obstruction. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`Look for **abdominal distension, visible peristalsis, hernial orifices and surgical scars** (adhesive obstruction). [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Neurological examination with fundoscopy** for papilloedema, neck stiffness, nystagmus and Dix-Hallpike testing for BPPV. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for **Russell sign** (knuckle calluses), parotid enlargement and dental erosion - **self-induced vomiting** in bulimia. [Harrison 22e]`,`In diabetes, examine for **peripheral neuropathy and postural hypotension** (autonomic neuropathy) and look for **retinopathy** - microvascular disease travels together. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`Look for **sclerodactyly, Raynaud phenomenon and telangiectasia** (systemic sclerosis), hyperpigmentation (Addison disease) and goitre. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Mechanical gastric outlet obstruction** must be excluded by **endoscopy (or CT) before diagnosing gastroparesis** - both give vomiting of food and splash. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**Functional dyspepsia (PDS)**: fullness and early satiation dominate, **vomiting is unusual**, emptying is often normal - there is large overlap with idiopathic gastroparesis. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**CVS versus CHS**: identical episodic pattern; **cannabis history and relief with hot bathing** point to CHS, and only sustained abstinence for at least 6 months (or 3 typical cycle lengths) proves it. [AGA CHS 2024]`,`**Rumination versus vomiting**: rumination is **effortless, without nausea or retching**, within minutes of eating, and the food tastes normal. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Eating disorders**: concealed self-induced vomiting, low BMI, fear of weight gain - screen sensitively. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Intestinal pseudo-obstruction**: dilated small bowel without obstruction, often with systemic sclerosis or amyloidosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Opioid-induced and GLP-1 agonist-induced delayed emptying** mimic gastroparesis and reverse on stopping the drug. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**First line for acute vomiting**: electrolytes (**K+, Na+, Cl-, bicarbonate**), urea, creatinine, glucose (and ketones in diabetics), LFT, lipase, calcium, **urine pregnancy test** in women of child-bearing age, and ECG in those over 40 or with risk factors. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chronic vomiting**: add **TSH, HbA1c, morning cortisol**, CBC, and drug levels (digoxin, lithium) where relevant. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**Upper GI endoscopy** to exclude obstruction, ulcer and malignancy; **retained food after an overnight fast without obstruction suggests gastroparesis** but is not diagnostic. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**Gastric emptying scintigraphy (gold standard)**: **4-hour solid-phase study** with a standard low-fat egg-white meal; **retention over 10% at 4 hours (or over 60% at 2 hours)** = delayed; 1- or 2-hour studies alone miss cases. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`Before the scan: **stop opioids, anticholinergics, prokinetics and GLP-1 agonists** for an appropriate period, and check **glucose is under 275 mg/dL**. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**Gastric emptying breath test (13C-Spirulina)** is a validated non-radioactive alternative; wireless motility capsule is used in some centres. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**CT or MRI brain** when vomiting comes with headache, focal signs or papilloedema; **CT abdomen** for suspected obstruction; **urine cannabinoid screen** when CHS is suspected. [Harrison 22e]`,`In resource-limited settings, a **barium meal with food retained beyond 4-6 hours** or ultrasound antral measurement gives indirect evidence; prefer referral for scintigraphy when management depends on it. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - general and gastroparesis`,points:[`**Treat the cause** (stop the drug, correct calcium, treat pregnancy, vestibular disorder or DKA) and **choose the antiemetic by receptor** that drives the nausea. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Gastroparesis diet**: **small, frequent (4-6) meals, low fat and low insoluble fibre, small-particle (mashed or blended) food**, more liquid calories, chew well, stay upright 1-2 hours after meals. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**Diabetic gastroparesis**: optimise glucose (target near-normal), consider **insulin pump or CGM**, match bolus insulin to delayed absorption (dose after the meal), and **stop GLP-1 agonists and pramlintide** if symptomatic. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**Metoclopramide** - the only FDA-approved drug for gastroparesis: **5-10 mg orally three times daily 15-30 minutes before meals** (liquid form absorbs better), maximum 40 mg/day; ACG advises **use for up to 12 weeks** with review, EMA limits routine use to **5 days at 30 mg/day**. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`Warn about **tardive dyskinesia** (risk rises with duration, dose, age and female sex), acute dystonia, akathisia and depression; stop at the first involuntary movement. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**Domperidone 10 mg three times daily** (available in India; restricted access in the US) is the alternative with fewer CNS effects; check **ECG for QTc** (avoid if QTc over 470 ms in men, 450 ms in women) and avoid CYP3A4 inhibitors. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**Erythromycin 125-250 mg orally three times daily** (or IV 3 mg/kg for acute exacerbation) is a potent short-term prokinetic, but **tachyphylaxis** develops within about 4 weeks; avoid with QT-prolonging drugs. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**Symptomatic antiemetics**: **ondansetron 4-8 mg two or three times daily**, prochlorperazine 5-10 mg, promethazine 12.5-25 mg; **avoid opioids** for pain because they worsen emptying. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**Neuromodulators**: **nortriptyline did not improve overall symptoms of idiopathic gastroparesis** (NORIG trial); mirtazapine may help nausea and appetite. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency`,points:[`**Admit** for dehydration not corrected orally, **AKI, K+ under 3 mmol/L**, haematemesis, suspected obstruction, raised ICP, DKA, pregnancy with ketosis, or inability to keep any oral intake. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fluids**: IV **0.9% saline with potassium chloride** guided by electrolytes; give **thiamine 100-300 mg IV before dextrose** in prolonged vomiting, alcohol use or malnutrition (**Wernicke encephalopathy**). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Suspected obstruction**: nil by mouth, **nasogastric decompression**, surgical review, CT; do not give prokinetics. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Severe gastroparesis flare**: IV fluids, glucose control, **IV erythromycin 3 mg/kg every 8 hours** or **IV metoclopramide 10 mg**, IV antiemetic, NG decompression if needed; **enteral feeding via nasojejunal tube** if weight loss over 10% in 3-6 months. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**CVS emetic phase**: dark quiet room, **IV fluids with dextrose**, **ondansetron 8 mg IV**, **lorazepam 1-2 mg IV** for sedation, consider **sumatriptan 20 mg intranasal** early in the episode, and **aprepitant** for ongoing vomiting. [ANMS-CVSA CVS 2019]`,`**CHS acute episode**: conventional antiemetics often fail; use **haloperidol 0.05-0.1 mg/kg IV** (HaVOC trial: better than ondansetron), **topical capsaicin 0.075% cream to the abdomen**, benzodiazepines and fluids; avoid opioids. [AGA CHS 2024]`,`**Monitor** fluid balance, electrolytes, glucose, ECG (QTc on ondansetron, haloperidol, domperidone) and watch for **Mallory-Weiss bleeding or Boerhaave rupture** (chest pain, surgical emphysema). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy (nausea and vomiting, hyperemesis)**: first line **doxylamine-pyridoxine** (10/10 mg, up to 4 tablets a day), then promethazine or cyclizine, then **metoclopramide or ondansetron**, then steroids for refractory hyperemesis; give **thiamine**. [RCOG Hyperemesis 2024]`,`**Chemotherapy-induced**: highly emetogenic regimens need **5-HT3 antagonist + NK1 antagonist + dexamethasone + olanzapine**; delayed nausea responds best to olanzapine. [MASCC/ESMO 2023]`,`**Palliative care**: match cause - **haloperidol 0.5-1.5 mg** for chemical (opioid, uraemia), **cyclizine** for raised ICP or vestibular, **metoclopramide** for gastric stasis, **levomepromazine** as broad-spectrum; dexamethasone for raised ICP or bowel obstruction. [Oxford Palliative Medicine 6e]`,`**Elderly and Parkinson disease**: **avoid metoclopramide, prochlorperazine and haloperidol** (extrapyramidal); domperidone is preferred in Parkinson disease with ECG check. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**CKD**: reduce metoclopramide dose by half when CrCl is under 40-60 mL/min; uraemic vomiting improves with dialysis. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Children**: single-dose **oral ondansetron 0.15 mg/kg** in gastroenteritis aids ORS success; metoclopramide is avoided under 1 year and used cautiously under 18 years. [EMA 2013]`,`**GLP-1 agonists before endoscopy or anaesthesia**: most patients continue them, with a **liquid diet for 24 hours before** procedures, and hold if GI symptoms are present (multisociety 2024). [ASA-AGA multisociety 2024]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Vomiting complications: **dehydration, AKI, hypokalaemic hypochloraemic metabolic alkalosis, Mallory-Weiss tear, Boerhaave syndrome, aspiration pneumonia, Wernicke encephalopathy** and dental erosion. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Gastroparesis complications**: malnutrition, weight loss, **bezoar formation**, erratic glycaemia with hypoglycaemia, Candida oesophagitis and frequent admissions. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**Diabetic gastroparesis** has higher mortality, mainly from associated cardiovascular and renal disease, not the gastroparesis itself. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**Post-viral gastroparesis** often improves over 1-2 years; idiopathic and diabetic forms tend to be chronic and fluctuating. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**CVS** may cause **dehydration, AKI and missed school or work**; with prophylaxis most patients have fewer and shorter episodes. [ANMS-CVSA CVS 2019]`,`**CHS** relapses with resumed cannabis use; deaths from severe dehydration and electrolyte disturbance have been reported. [AGA CHS 2024]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer urgently** for vomiting with headache and neurological signs, suspected obstruction, haematemesis, severe dehydration or pregnancy with ketosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Refer to gastroenterology** for gastric emptying testing, refractory gastroparesis, weight loss over 10% or need for enteral feeding. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**Refractory gastroparesis options in specialist centres**: **gastric peroral endoscopic myotomy (G-POEM)** when pyloric dysfunction is suspected, **gastric electrical stimulation** for refractory diabetic nausea and vomiting, and **jejunostomy feeding**; **pyloric botulinum toxin is not recommended** (negative RCTs). [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`On metoclopramide, **review every 4-12 weeks** for movement disorders; on domperidone, **repeat ECG** after dose changes or new interacting drugs. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`Monitor **weight, HbA1c, hypoglycaemia logs, potassium and magnesium** at each visit in gastroparesis. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**CVS prophylaxis**: **amitriptyline starting 25 mg at night, titrated to about 75-100 mg (1-1.5 mg/kg)** for at least 3 months, with ECG; alternatives topiramate, aprepitant, coenzyme Q10 and riboflavin. [ANMS-CVSA CVS 2019]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`**Good glycaemic control from diagnosis** prevents autonomic neuropathy and gastroparesis - reinforce under **NP-NCD** diabetes care. [ADA Standards of Care 2026]`,`Counsel CHS patients that **cannabis is the cause** and that **cessation for at least 6 months** is the only cure; refer to de-addiction services (Drug De-addiction Programme, NMHP). [AGA CHS 2024]`,`Teach **ORS and early fluid intake** for acute vomiting illness, and danger signs needing hospital care. [WHO 2023]`,`Identify **CVS triggers** - sleep deprivation, fasting, infection, stress, menstruation - and give a written **rescue plan** for the prodrome. [ANMS-CVSA CVS 2019]`,`Review prescriptions of **opioids and GLP-1 agonists** in patients with new vomiting before labelling gastroparesis. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`Ondansetron, metoclopramide, domperidone and promethazine are available through **Jan Aushadhi**; ondansetron, metoclopramide and promethazine are on the **NLEM 2022**. [NLEM 2022]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2026)`,points:[`**ACG 2022 gastroparesis guideline**: 4-hour scintigraphy or breath test required; metoclopramide first line (liquid form, up to 12 weeks); domperidone where available with ECG; G-POEM for selected refractory cases; **botulinum toxin and opioids not recommended**. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**AGA 2024 clinical practice update on CHS**: diagnosis by Rome IV, sustained cessation as definitive treatment, **haloperidol and topical capsaicin** for acute episodes, and tricyclic prophylaxis while abstaining. [AGA CHS 2024]`,`**GLP-1 receptor agonist era**: delayed emptying is now a common iatrogenic cause; **2024 multisociety guidance** favours continuing them before procedures with a 24-hour liquid diet rather than routine 1-week holds. [ASA-AGA multisociety 2024]`,`**Tradipitant (NK1 antagonist) was not approved by the FDA for gastroparesis in 2024**; no new drug has replaced metoclopramide. [FDA 2024]`,`**RCOG 2024 hyperemesis guideline**: earlier step-up to ondansetron and metoclopramide, outpatient day-case IV fluids, and **thiamine** for prolonged vomiting. [RCOG Hyperemesis 2024]`,`**MASCC/ESMO 2023 antiemetic guideline**: **olanzapine** added to the four-drug regimen for highly emetogenic chemotherapy. [MASCC/ESMO 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic: **'Approach to a patient with recurrent vomiting'** - write causes by system, timing and content clues, investigations and an antiemetic table by receptor. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: **'Gold standard for gastroparesis?'** - 4-hour solid-phase gastric emptying scintigraphy, **retention over 10% at 4 hours**. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`Viva: **'Why is domperidone preferred in Parkinson disease?'** - it does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so no extrapyramidal effects. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`Viva: **'What is the hot-shower sign?'** - compulsive hot bathing relieving vomiting in cannabinoid hyperemesis syndrome. [AGA CHS 2024]`,`Common mistake: diagnosing gastroparesis **without first excluding obstruction by endoscopy** or testing while on opioids or with glucose over 275 mg/dL. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`Common mistake: giving **dextrose before thiamine** in a malnourished, alcohol-using vomiting patient. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Antiemetic and prokinetic drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Metoclopramide (D2 antagonist, 5-HT4 agonist)`,`5-10 mg orally or IV three times daily before meals; max 40 mg/day (EMA 30 mg/day)`,`ACG up to 12 weeks; EMA 5 days for acute use`,`Tardive dyskinesia, dystonia, akathisia, depression; avoid in Parkinson disease, epilepsy, obstruction`],[`Domperidone (peripheral D2)`,`10 mg orally up to three times daily`,`Shortest course; longer only with ECG monitoring`,`QT prolongation, arrhythmia, hyperprolactinaemia; avoid with CYP3A4 inhibitors`],[`Erythromycin (motilin agonist)`,`125-250 mg orally three times daily; IV 3 mg/kg 8-hourly`,`Up to 4 weeks (tachyphylaxis)`,`QT prolongation, drug interactions, abdominal cramps`],[`Ondansetron (5-HT3)`,`4-8 mg orally or IV 8-12 hourly; max single IV dose 16 mg`,`As needed`,`Constipation, headache, QT prolongation`],[`Prochlorperazine (D2)`,`5-10 mg orally 8-hourly; 12.5 mg IM`,`Short courses`,`Extrapyramidal effects, sedation, hypotension`],[`Promethazine (H1)`,`12.5-25 mg orally or IM 6-8 hourly`,`Short courses`,`Sedation, anticholinergic effects; tissue injury if extravasated IV`],[`Haloperidol (D2)`,`0.5-1.5 mg orally or SC; 0.05-0.1 mg/kg IV in CHS`,`As needed`,`QT prolongation, extrapyramidal effects`],[`Aprepitant (NK1)`,`125 mg day 1, 80 mg days 2-3 orally (chemotherapy, CVS)`,`3 days per cycle or episode`,`Fatigue, hiccups; CYP3A4 interactions`],[`Doxylamine-pyridoxine`,`10/10 mg, 2 tablets at night, up to 4 a day`,`Through pregnancy nausea`,`Drowsiness; safe in pregnancy`],[`Amitriptyline (CVS prophylaxis)`,`25 mg at night, titrate to 75-100 mg (1-1.5 mg/kg)`,`At least 3 months`,`Sedation, dry mouth, QT; ECG before high doses`]]},{heading:`Rome IV episodic and chronic vomiting disorders`,columns:[`Disorder`,`Core criteria`,`Clue`,`Specific treatment`],rows:[[`Chronic nausea and vomiting syndrome`,`Nausea 1 day/week and/or vomiting 1/week`,`Continuous, no eating disorder`,`Neuromodulators (TCA, mirtazapine), antiemetics`],[`Cyclic vomiting syndrome`,`Stereotyped episodes under 1 week; 3 in a year, 2 in 6 months; well between`,`Migraine, early-morning onset`,`Abortive triptan, ondansetron; amitriptyline prophylaxis`],[`Cannabinoid hyperemesis syndrome`,`CVS-like episodes with prolonged excessive cannabis use`,`Hot bathing relief`,`Cessation; haloperidol, capsaicin in attack`],[`Rumination syndrome`,`Effortless regurgitation within minutes of meals, no retching`,`Food re-chewed, tastes normal`,`Diaphragmatic breathing, behavioural therapy`]]},{heading:`Gastroparesis severity`,columns:[`Grade`,`4-hour retention on scintigraphy`,`Clinical (Abell) description`,`Typical management`],rows:[[`Mild`,`10-15%`,`Symptoms controlled by diet`,`Diet, glucose control, avoid culprit drugs`],[`Moderate`,`15-35%`,`Needs prokinetic and antiemetic, few admissions`,`Metoclopramide or domperidone, antiemetics`],[`Severe`,`Over 35%`,`Refractory, admissions, nutritional failure`,`Nasojejunal feeding, G-POEM, gastric electrical stimulation`]]},{heading:`Clues to the cause of vomiting`,columns:[`Clue`,`Suggests`,`Next test`],rows:[[`Early-morning vomiting`,`Pregnancy, uraemia, alcohol, raised ICP`,`Pregnancy test, creatinine, fundoscopy`],[`Undigested food hours after meals`,`Gastroparesis or gastric outlet obstruction`,`Endoscopy, then emptying study`],[`Bilious vomiting`,`Obstruction beyond the ampulla`,`X-ray or CT abdomen`],[`Faeculent vomiting`,`Distal bowel obstruction, gastrocolic fistula`,`CT abdomen`],[`Headache, papilloedema`,`Raised intracranial pressure`,`CT or MRI brain`],[`Vertigo, nystagmus`,`Vestibular cause`,`Dix-Hallpike, ENT review`],[`Episodic attacks, well between`,`CVS or CHS`,`Cannabis history, urine screen`],[`Effortless regurgitation after meals`,`Rumination syndrome`,`Clinical; high-resolution manometry if doubt`]]},{heading:`Antiemetic choice by mechanism`,columns:[`Mechanism of nausea`,`Receptor`,`First choice`],rows:[[`Drugs, toxins, uraemia, hypercalcaemia`,`D2 at chemoreceptor trigger zone`,`Haloperidol, metoclopramide`],[`Gastroenteritis, chemotherapy, gut serotonin`,`5-HT3 vagal`,`Ondansetron`],[`Gastric stasis`,`D2 and 5-HT4`,`Metoclopramide, domperidone`],[`Motion sickness, vestibular`,`H1 and M1`,`Promethazine, cinnarizine, hyoscine`],[`Raised ICP`,`Direct brainstem`,`Dexamethasone, cyclizine`],[`Anticipatory or anxiety`,`Cortical`,`Lorazepam`]]}],redFlags:[`Vomiting with severe headache, papilloedema, neck stiffness or focal neurological signs`,`Bilious or faeculent vomiting with distension or absent bowel sounds (obstruction)`,`Haematemesis or coffee-ground vomitus`,`Severe dehydration, AKI or potassium under 3 mmol/L`,`Vomiting in diabetes with ketones or glucose over 250 mg/dL (DKA)`,`Chest pain or surgical emphysema after forceful vomiting (Boerhaave)`,`Confusion, ataxia or ophthalmoplegia after prolonged vomiting (Wernicke)`,`Weight loss over 10% in 3-6 months in gastroparesis (needs enteral feeding)`],pearls:[`**Gastroparesis = over 10% retention at 4 hours** - and only after obstruction is excluded.`,`Test emptying **off opioids and GLP-1 agonists with glucose under 275 mg/dL**.`,`**Domperidone does not cross the blood-brain barrier** - preferred in Parkinson disease.`,`**Metoclopramide**: only FDA-approved gastroparesis drug; tardive dyskinesia limits it.`,`**Erythromycin works fast but fades fast** (tachyphylaxis).`,`**Hot-shower relief = cannabinoid hyperemesis**; cure is stopping cannabis.`,`**CVS = well between stereotyped attacks**, migraine link, amitriptyline prophylaxis.`,`**Thiamine before dextrose** in prolonged vomiting.`,`Antiemetic by receptor: **D2 drugs, 5-HT3 gut, H1/M1 vestibular, NK1 chemotherapy**.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-gastroparesis-vomiting-q1`,stem:`Which result on a standard 4-hour solid-phase gastric emptying scintigraphy confirms delayed gastric emptying?`,options:[`Retention over 10% at 4 hours`,`Retention over 10% at 1 hour`,`Retention over 30% at 4 hours only`,`Retention under 60% at 2 hours`],answer:0,explanation:`Gastric retention over 10% at 4 hours (or over 60% at 2 hours) defines delayed emptying, and the 4-hour reading is the most reliable. Retention at 1 hour is normally high and does not define delay; a 30% threshold would miss mild gastroparesis; under 60% retention at 2 hours is normal.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gastroparesis-vomiting-q2`,stem:`A 62-year-old man with Parkinson disease and diabetes has postprandial fullness and vomiting due to gastroparesis. Which prokinetic is most suitable?`,options:[`Metoclopramide`,`Domperidone with baseline ECG`,`Prochlorperazine`,`Haloperidol`],answer:1,explanation:`Domperidone blocks peripheral D2 receptors without crossing the blood-brain barrier, so it does not worsen parkinsonism; an ECG is needed for QT. Metoclopramide, prochlorperazine and haloperidol are central dopamine antagonists that worsen Parkinson disease, and the last two are not prokinetics.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gastroparesis-vomiting-q3`,stem:`A 24-year-old man has had four episodes of intractable vomiting lasting 2-3 days over the past year, completely well between them. He smokes ganja daily and feels better under hot showers. What is the definitive treatment?`,options:[`Long-term ondansetron`,`Laparoscopic cholecystectomy`,`Sustained cessation of cannabis`,`Long-term metoclopramide`],answer:2,explanation:`This is cannabinoid hyperemesis syndrome - CVS-like episodes with chronic heavy cannabis use and relief from hot bathing; only sustained abstinence cures it. Ondansetron often fails in CHS, the gallbladder is not involved, and metoclopramide has no role in prevention and carries tardive dyskinesia risk.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gastroparesis-vomiting-q4`,stem:`Motion sickness is best treated with a drug acting on which receptor?`,options:[`5-HT3 receptor`,`NK1 receptor`,`Motilin receptor`,`H1 and muscarinic receptors`],answer:3,explanation:`Vestibular input to the vomiting centre runs through histamine H1 and muscarinic M1 receptors, so promethazine, cinnarizine and hyoscine work. 5-HT3 antagonists suit chemotherapy and gastroenteritis, NK1 antagonists suit chemotherapy, and motilin agonists such as erythromycin are prokinetics.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gastroparesis-vomiting-q5`,stem:`Which intervention for refractory gastroparesis is NOT recommended by the ACG 2022 guideline because randomised trials showed no benefit over placebo?`,options:[`Intrapyloric botulinum toxin injection`,`Gastric peroral endoscopic myotomy in selected patients`,`Nasojejunal feeding for malnutrition`,`Liquid metoclopramide`],answer:0,explanation:`Randomised trials of intrapyloric botulinum toxin showed no benefit over placebo, so ACG 2022 advises against it. G-POEM is an option for selected refractory patients with pyloric dysfunction, nasojejunal feeding treats malnutrition, and liquid metoclopramide is first-line drug therapy.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-gastroparesis-vomiting-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Describe the approach to a patient with chronic recurrent vomiting. Discuss the diagnosis and management of diabetic gastroparesis.`,openingLines:[`Vomiting is a coordinated brainstem reflex triggered by the chemoreceptor trigger zone, gut vagal afferents, the vestibular system and the cortex; its cause is found by timing, content and associated features.`,`Gastroparesis is symptomatic objectively delayed gastric emptying without mechanical obstruction; diabetes is a leading cause, especially long-standing type 1.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes of chronic vomiting`,points:[`GI: obstruction, gastroparesis, ulcer, pancreatobiliary disease.`,`CNS and vestibular; metabolic (pregnancy, uraemia, hypercalcaemia, Addison); drugs (opioids, GLP-1 RA, digoxin).`,`Functional: CNVS, CVS, CHS, rumination; eating disorders.`]},{heading:`Clinical approach`,points:[`Timing, content, relation to meals, associated headache, vertigo, weight loss, drugs, cannabis.`,`Hydration, succussion splash, fundoscopy, autonomic neuropathy, Russell sign.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Electrolytes, glucose, creatinine, calcium, LFT, lipase, TSH, cortisol, pregnancy test, ECG.`,`Endoscopy to exclude obstruction; 4-hour gastric emptying scintigraphy (over 10% retention).`,`Brain imaging if neurological features.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology of diabetic gastroparesis`,points:[`Vagal autonomic neuropathy, loss of interstitial cells of Cajal and nNOS neurons, pylorospasm; hyperglycaemia slows emptying.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Small frequent low-fat low-fibre small-particle meals; glucose optimisation with CGM; post-meal bolus insulin.`,`Stop GLP-1 agonists and opioids.`,`Metoclopramide 5-10 mg three times daily up to 12 weeks, or domperidone 10 mg three times daily with ECG.`,`Erythromycin short-term; ondansetron or prochlorperazine for nausea.`,`Refractory: nasojejunal feeding, gastric electrical stimulation, G-POEM.`]},{heading:`Complications and follow-up`,points:[`Dehydration, hypokalaemia, bezoar, malnutrition, hypoglycaemia; monitor weight, HbA1c, electrolytes and movement disorders.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of the four inputs to the vomiting centre with receptors`,`Flow chart: chronic vomiting - exclude obstruction - emptying study - treatment ladder`],markSplit:[{part:`Causes and clinical approach`,marks:3},{part:`Investigations`,marks:2},{part:`Pathophysiology of diabetic gastroparesis`,marks:1},{part:`Management and follow-up`,marks:4}],keywords:[`vomiting centre`,`gastroparesis`,`scintigraphy`,`metoclopramide`,`domperidone`,`diabetes`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gastroparesis-vomiting-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Differentiate cyclic vomiting syndrome from cannabinoid hyperemesis syndrome.`,openingLines:[`Both are Rome IV episodic vomiting disorders with stereotyped attacks and well intervals.`,`Cannabinoid hyperemesis is distinguished by prolonged excessive cannabis use and resolution with sustained abstinence.`],answer:[{heading:`Similarities`,points:[`Stereotyped episodes under 1 week, prodrome, emetic, recovery and well phases.`,`Dehydration and AKI in attacks.`]},{heading:`Differences`,points:[`CVS: migraine association, triggers like stress and sleep loss, any age; CHS: daily cannabis for months to years.`,`CHS: compulsive hot bathing; resolves after at least 6 months of abstinence.`,`CVS acute: ondansetron, sumatriptan, lorazepam; CHS acute: haloperidol, topical capsaicin.`,`CVS prophylaxis: amitriptyline, topiramate; CHS: cannabis cessation.`]}],mustDraw:[`Comparison table: CVS versus CHS`],markSplit:[{part:`Definitions and similarities`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical differences`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment differences`,marks:2}],keywords:[`cyclic vomiting syndrome`,`cannabinoid hyperemesis`,`hot bathing`,`haloperidol`,`amitriptyline`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Nausea, vomiting and indigestion; Gastroparesis`,`Camilleri M, et al. ACG Clinical Guideline: Gastroparesis. Am J Gastroenterol 2022`,`Stanghellini V, et al. Rome IV - Gastroduodenal disorders. Gastroenterology 2016`,`Venkatesan T, et al. Guidelines on management of cyclic vomiting syndrome in adults (ANMS and CVSA). Neurogastroenterol Motil 2019`,`AGA Clinical Practice Update on Diagnosis and Management of Cannabinoid Hyperemesis Syndrome. Gastroenterology 2024`,`RCOG Green-top Guideline No. 69: Management of Nausea and Vomiting in Pregnancy and Hyperemesis Gravidarum, 2024`,`MASCC/ESMO Antiemetic Guideline update, 2023`,`ICMR-INDIAB study: metabolic non-communicable disease health report of India. Lancet Diabetes Endocrinol 2023`]}),t.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-gastric-cancer`,title:`Gastric cancer and gastric premalignant lesions`,oneLiner:`**Gastric adenocarcinoma** (about 90% of gastric cancers) arises mostly through the **H. pylori-driven Correa cascade - chronic gastritis, atrophy, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia, cancer** - so prevention rests on **H. pylori eradication** and **OLGA/OLGIM-staged endoscopic surveillance (about every 3 years for extensive or incomplete intestinal metaplasia)**; early cancer is cured by **endoscopic submucosal dissection**, locally advanced cancer by **perioperative FLOT with D2 gastrectomy**, advanced disease by **biomarker-guided chemo-immunotherapy (HER2, PD-L1 CPS, MSI, Claudin 18.2)**, and localised **gastric MALT lymphoma** often by eradication alone.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`gastric cancer`,`stomach cancer`,`gastric adenocarcinoma`,`Correa cascade`,`intestinal metaplasia`,`atrophic gastritis`,`OLGA`,`OLGIM`,`MAPS II`,`updated Sydney protocol`,`early gastric cancer`,`endoscopic submucosal dissection`,`Lauren classification`,`linitis plastica`,`FLOT`,`D2 gastrectomy`,`Virchow node`,`Krukenberg tumour`,`MALT lymphoma`,`Claudin 18.2`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Gastric cancer** is a malignant tumour of the stomach; **adenocarcinoma makes up about 90%**, the rest are lymphoma (MALT, DLBCL), GIST and neuroendocrine tumours. [Harrison 22e]`,`Worldwide there were about **9.7 lakh new cases and 6.6 lakh deaths in 2022** - the **fifth commonest cancer and fifth commonest cause of cancer death**; incidence is highest in East Asia, Eastern Europe and the Andes. [GLOBOCAN 2022]`,`In India, gastric cancer is among the **ten commonest cancers in men**; the **north-east (Mizoram, Aizawl district) has some of the highest rates in the world**, with higher rates also in parts of south India. [ICMR-NCRP 2020]`,`Despite **H. pylori prevalence of 60-80%** in Indian adults, overall gastric cancer incidence is moderate - the **'Indian enigma'**, attributed to host genetics, strain virulence and diet. [Maastricht VI 2022]`,`Most Indian patients present with **advanced disease (stage III-IV)**, so 5-year survival is poor, in contrast to Japan and Korea where screening detects over half as **early gastric cancer**. [ESMO Gastric 2022]`,`**Non-cardia cancer** (H. pylori-related) is falling worldwide, while **cardia and gastro-oesophageal junction cancer** (obesity, GERD) is rising in the West; incidence peaks in the **6th-7th decade**, men about twice women. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Early-onset gastric cancer (under 40-45)** is often diffuse type, commoner in women, and should prompt evaluation for **hereditary diffuse gastric cancer**. [IGCLC HDGC 2020]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**H. pylori** is an IARC **group 1 carcinogen** and causes about **90% of non-cardia gastric cancer**; CagA-positive strains carry greater risk. [Maastricht VI 2022]`,`**Dietary**: high salt, salted and smoked fish or meat, pickled foods, nitrates and N-nitroso compounds, low fresh fruit and vegetable intake; refrigeration has reduced incidence. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Smoking** (about 1.5-fold), heavy alcohol, **EBV infection** (about 10% of cancers), **obesity** (cardia cancer), and occupational exposures. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Precursor conditions**: **atrophic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia, autoimmune (pernicious anaemia) gastritis, adenomatous polyps, Menetrier disease** and **partial gastrectomy over 15-20 years earlier** (gastric stump cancer). [ESGE MAPS II 2019]`,`**Family history** in a first-degree relative raises risk 2-3 fold; eradicating H. pylori in such relatives **halves their cancer risk**. [Choi NEJM 2020]`,`**Hereditary syndromes**: **hereditary diffuse gastric cancer (CDH1, CTNNA1)** with lifetime risk up to about 40-70% in older series, **Lynch syndrome**, **FAP**, Peutz-Jeghers and **GAPPS**. [IGCLC HDGC 2020]`,`**Blood group A** is associated with diffuse-type cancer; **aspirin and statin use** are associated with lower risk in observational studies. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - Correa cascade and molecular types`,points:[`**Correa cascade**: **chronic non-atrophic gastritis, multifocal atrophic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia (complete then incomplete), dysplasia (low then high grade) and intestinal-type adenocarcinoma** - over decades. [ESGE MAPS II 2019]`,`**Atrophy** removes acid-secreting glands, causing **hypochlorhydria**, bacterial overgrowth that converts nitrates to carcinogenic nitrosamines, and raised gastrin - fuelling the cascade. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Point of no return**: eradication before extensive atrophy and metaplasia gives the greatest cancer reduction; after metaplasia it still slows progression and reduces **metachronous cancer after endoscopic resection by about half**. [Maastricht VI 2022]`,`**Incomplete (colonic, type III) intestinal metaplasia** carries higher cancer risk than complete (small-intestinal, type I) metaplasia. [AGA GIM 2020]`,`**Diffuse-type cancer** follows a different path - **loss of E-cadherin (CDH1)**, so cells lose adhesion and infiltrate singly as **signet-ring cells**, thickening the wall into **linitis plastica** ('leather-bottle stomach'). [Harrison 22e]`,`**TCGA molecular subtypes**: **EBV-positive** (PD-L1 high), **microsatellite unstable (MSI-H)**, **genomically stable** (diffuse, CDH1/RHOA) and **chromosomal instability** (intestinal, HER2 amplification). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Actionable biomarkers**: **HER2** positive in about **15-20%**, **Claudin 18.2** positive in about **35-40%**, **MSI-H/dMMR** in about **5-10%** of advanced cases, and **PD-L1 CPS** - each matched to a targeted drug. [ESMO Gastric 2022]`,`**Spread**: direct to pancreas, colon and liver; lymphatic (perigastric, coeliac, Virchow node); haematogenous (liver, lung); **transcoelomic to the peritoneum and ovaries (Krukenberg tumour)**. [Bailey and Love 28e]`]},{heading:`Classification and staging`,points:[`**Lauren classification**: **intestinal type** (gland-forming, older men, H. pylori, haematogenous spread, better prognosis) versus **diffuse type** (poorly cohesive, signet-ring, younger, women, peritoneal spread, worse prognosis); mixed type also exists. [Harrison 22e]`,`**WHO 2019 histological types**: tubular, papillary, mucinous, **poorly cohesive (including signet-ring)** and mixed adenocarcinoma. [WHO Digestive Tumours 2019]`,`**Early gastric cancer**: tumour **confined to mucosa or submucosa (T1), regardless of lymph node status**; macroscopic **Paris classification 0-I (protruded), 0-II a/b/c (superficial) and 0-III (excavated)**. [JGCA 2021]`,`**Advanced gastric cancer - Borrmann types**: I polypoid, II fungating (ulcerated with sharp margins), III ulcerated infiltrative, **IV diffusely infiltrative (linitis plastica)**. [JGCA 2021]`,`**AJCC 8th TNM T stage**: T1a lamina propria or muscularis mucosae, T1b submucosa, T2 muscularis propria, T3 subserosa, **T4a serosa**, T4b adjacent structures. [AJCC 8e]`,`**N stage**: N1 1-2 nodes, N2 3-6, **N3a 7-15, N3b 16 or more**; at least **16 nodes (ideally 30 or more)** must be examined for accurate staging. [AJCC 8e]`,`**OLGA (atrophy) and OLGIM (intestinal metaplasia)** stage the gastritis 0-IV from Sydney-protocol biopsies by extent in antrum and corpus; **stage III-IV is high risk** for cancer. [ESGE MAPS II 2019]`,`**Siewert classification** of gastro-oesophageal junction tumours (types I-III) decides whether they are treated as oesophageal or gastric - covered in the Barrett and oesophageal cancer topic. [ESMO Gastric 2022]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Early gastric cancer is usually asymptomatic** or causes non-specific dyspepsia - hence dyspepsia after about 45 years in India needs endoscopy. [ACG/CAG 2017]`,`**Advanced disease**: **weight loss (commonest), epigastric pain, early satiety, anorexia**, nausea, and fatigue from anaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Site-specific symptoms**: **dysphagia** with cardia tumours, **vomiting of stale food** with antral tumours (outlet obstruction), and early satiety with linitis plastica (non-distensible stomach). [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Bleeding**: occult (iron deficiency anaemia) is common; overt haematemesis or melaena occurs in a minority. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Paraneoplastic features**: **acanthosis nigricans (sudden, extensive), Leser-Trelat sign** (eruptive seborrhoeic keratoses), **Trousseau syndrome** (migratory thrombophlebitis), dermatomyositis, membranous nephropathy and microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **family history of gastric, colorectal, endometrial or lobular breast cancer** (Lynch, HDGC), prior gastric surgery, pernicious anaemia and H. pylori treatment. [IGCLC HDGC 2020]`,`**Gastric MALT lymphoma** presents like dyspepsia or ulcer; B symptoms are uncommon in localised disease. [ESMO MZL 2020]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Pallor, cachexia** and an **epigastric mass** (a palpable mass usually means advanced disease). [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Virchow node** - left supraclavicular node; its presence is **Troisier sign** (the thoracic duct drains into the left subclavian vein). [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Sister Mary Joseph nodule** - periumbilical metastasis spreading along the falciform ligament or umbilical lymphatics. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Irish node** - left axillary node; **Blumer shelf** - rectal shelf on per-rectal examination from pouch of Douglas deposits. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Krukenberg tumour** - bilateral ovarian metastases with signet-ring cells; examine the pelvis in women. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Hepatomegaly (nodular), ascites, jaundice and succussion splash** indicate metastatic or obstructive disease. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`Look for **acanthosis nigricans, seborrhoeic keratoses and superficial thrombophlebitis** (paraneoplastic). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Benign gastric ulcer**: smooth, regular folds radiating to the edge - but **every gastric ulcer is biopsied and re-scoped** because malignant ulcers can heal transiently with PPI. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Gastric lymphoma (MALT or DLBCL)**: thickened folds or ulcers; diagnosis by deep biopsies with immunohistochemistry. [ESMO MZL 2020]`,`**GIST**: submucosal mass with central ulceration and bleeding; **CD117 (KIT) and DOG1** positive; treated by resection and **imatinib**. [ESMO GIST 2022]`,`**Gastric neuroendocrine tumours**: **type 1 (autoimmune atrophic gastritis, high gastrin, small multiple, benign)**, type 2 (gastrinoma with MEN1), **type 3 (sporadic, normal gastrin, aggressive)**. [ENETS 2023]`,`**Linitis plastica mimics**: **gastric TB**, Crohn disease, syphilis, Menetrier disease, metastatic lobular breast cancer and corrosive stricture. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Functional dyspepsia and peptic ulcer** - excluded only by endoscopy when alarm features or age criteria are present. [ACG/CAG 2017]`,`**Pancreatic cancer** with gastric invasion or outlet obstruction - CT distinguishes the origin. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Upper GI endoscopy with multiple biopsies (at least 6-8 from an ulcer edge and base)** is the diagnostic test; high-definition white light with **image-enhanced endoscopy (NBI, chromoendoscopy)** detects early lesions. [ESMO Gastric 2022]`,`**Updated Sydney protocol** for gastritis staging: **5 biopsies - 2 antrum, 1 incisura, 2 corpus - in separate labelled jars**; plus targeted biopsies of any visible lesion. [ESGE MAPS II 2019]`,`**Baseline**: CBC, iron studies, LFT, renal function, albumin, nutritional assessment; **CEA and CA 19-9 are not diagnostic** but help in follow-up. [ESMO Gastric 2022]`,`**Staging**: **contrast CT of chest, abdomen and pelvis** for all; **EUS** for T and N in early tumours being considered for endoscopic resection. [ESMO Gastric 2022]`,`**Staging laparoscopy with peritoneal washings** for **cT2 or higher (or cT1b) resectable tumours** before neoadjuvant therapy - CT misses peritoneal disease in about 20-30%. [NCCN Gastric 2025]`,`**PET-CT** is selective (not useful in diffuse or mucinous types); **MRI liver** for indeterminate liver lesions. [ESMO Gastric 2022]`,`**Biomarkers on the biopsy**: **HER2 (IHC, then FISH if 2+), MMR/MSI, PD-L1 CPS and Claudin 18.2** for every advanced case; MMR status also for localised cancer. [ESMO Gastric 2022]`,`**H. pylori testing** on biopsies (histology, rapid urease) or by stool antigen, and in first-degree relatives. [Maastricht VI 2022]`,`**MALT lymphoma work-up**: deep biopsies, H. pylori status, **t(11;18) by FISH**, EUS for depth and nodes, CT chest-abdomen-pelvis, and bone marrow in selected cases; staged by **Lugano or Paris** systems. [ESMO MZL 2020]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care - premalignant lesions and prevention`,points:[`**Eradicate H. pylori in everyone with atrophy, metaplasia or dysplasia** and confirm cure; bismuth quadruple 14 days is first line. [Maastricht VI 2022]`,`**Mild atrophy or IM limited to the antrum (OLGA/OLGIM 0-II)**: **no routine surveillance**, unless family history of gastric cancer, incomplete IM or persistent H. pylori - then endoscopy in **3 years**. [ESGE MAPS II 2019]`,`**Extensive atrophy or IM (antrum and corpus, OLGA/OLGIM III-IV)**: **high-quality endoscopy every 3 years**; with a first-degree relative with gastric cancer, **every 1-2 years**. [ESGE MAPS II 2019]`,`**Low-grade dysplasia without a visible lesion**: repeat endoscopy with image enhancement **within 12 months**; **high-grade dysplasia without a visible lesion**: within **6 months**, and **any visible dysplastic lesion is resected** endoscopically. [ESGE MAPS II 2019]`,`**Autoimmune atrophic gastritis**: check **B12 and iron**, screen for thyroid disease, and endoscope every **3-5 years** for adenocarcinoma and type 1 NET. [ESGE MAPS II 2019]`,`**Primary-care roles**: endoscopy referral for alarm features or age about 45 or more, **test-and-treat H. pylori in first-degree relatives** of gastric cancer patients, and dietary counselling. [Maastricht VI 2022]`,`**CDH1 pathogenic variant carriers**: refer for **prophylactic total gastrectomy** (typically in early adulthood) or annual expert endoscopic surveillance (Cambridge protocol) if surgery is declined; women also need breast screening for lobular cancer. [IGCLC HDGC 2020]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD - curative and palliative treatment`,points:[`**Endoscopic submucosal dissection (ESD)** for early cancer: **absolute indication - differentiated intramucosal (cT1a) cancer without ulceration of any size, or with ulceration 3 cm or less; undifferentiated cT1a non-ulcerated 2 cm or less**; cure judged by the **eCura** system. [JGES ESD 2020]`,`**Surgery for resectable disease**: **subtotal distal gastrectomy** for distal tumours (macroscopic margin about **3 cm for expansive and 5 cm for infiltrative** types), **total gastrectomy** for proximal or diffuse tumours, with **D2 lymphadenectomy**. [JGCA 2021]`,`**Perioperative FLOT** for **cT2 or higher, or node-positive** disease: **docetaxel 50 mg/m2, oxaliplatin 85 mg/m2, leucovorin 200 mg/m2 and 5-FU 2600 mg/m2 by 24-hour infusion, every 2 weeks - 4 cycles before and 4 after surgery**; median survival **50 versus 35 months** with ECF/ECX (FLOT4). [ESMO Gastric 2022]`,`**Asian adjuvant alternative** after upfront D2 gastrectomy: **CAPOX for 6 months (CLASSIC)** or **S-1 for 1 year (ACTS-GC)**. [JGCA 2021]`,`**Advanced HER2-negative**: **fluoropyrimidine plus oxaliplatin with nivolumab** (CheckMate 649) or **pembrolizumab** (KEYNOTE-859), benefit greatest with **PD-L1 CPS 5 or more (or 10 or more)**; **Claudin 18.2-positive**: **zolbetuximab** plus chemotherapy (SPOTLIGHT, GLOW). [ESMO Gastric 2024 update]`,`**Advanced HER2-positive**: **trastuzumab plus chemotherapy** (ToGA) with **pembrolizumab for PD-L1 CPS 1 or more** (KEYNOTE-811); second line **trastuzumab deruxtecan** (DESTINY-Gastric). [ESMO Gastric 2024 update]`,`**Second and third line**: **ramucirumab plus paclitaxel** (RAINBOW), then **trifluridine-tipiracil** (TAGS); **MSI-H/dMMR** tumours respond well to immune checkpoint inhibitors. [ESMO Gastric 2022]`,`**Palliation**: bleeding - **endoscopic haemostasis or palliative radiotherapy**; outlet obstruction - **duodenal stent, gastrojejunostomy or EUS-guided gastroenterostomy**; malignant ascites - paracentesis; nutrition and pain control with early palliative care. [ESMO Gastric 2022]`,`**Gastric MALT lymphoma**: **H. pylori eradication is first-line for localised (stage I-II1) disease**, with **remission in about 75%**; **t(11;18) predicts non-response**; **involved-site radiotherapy 24 Gy** for non-responders or H. pylori-negative disease; **rituximab-based therapy** for advanced disease; gastric DLBCL needs **R-CHOP**. [ESMO MZL 2020]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Elderly and frail**: use **geriatric assessment**; modified (doublet) chemotherapy and less radical surgery; ESD remains suitable for early lesions. [ESMO Gastric 2022]`,`**Pregnancy-associated gastric cancer** is often diffuse and diagnosed late because symptoms are attributed to pregnancy - **endoscopy is safe** in pregnancy when alarm features persist. [Harrison 22e]`,`**MSI-H/dMMR localised tumours**: derive little benefit from perioperative chemotherapy; neoadjuvant immunotherapy is being studied - discuss in a tumour board. [ESMO Gastric 2024 update]`,`**After gastrectomy**: lifelong **vitamin B12 (1 mg IM every 3 months)**, iron, calcium and vitamin D; watch for **dumping syndrome**, weight loss and bone disease. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: CAPOX or FOLFOX-based regimens are cheaper than FLOT; trastuzumab biosimilars are available in India; **PM-JAY** covers gastrectomy and chemotherapy packages. [NHA PM-JAY HBP 2022]`,`**Diabetes and CKD**: dose-adjust capecitabine for creatinine clearance; monitor for oxaliplatin neuropathy, which overlaps with diabetic neuropathy. [ESMO Gastric 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Disease complications**: bleeding, perforation, gastric outlet obstruction, malignant ascites, obstructive jaundice and cachexia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Five-year survival**: **early gastric cancer over 90%**, stage II about 50-70%, stage III about 20-40%, **stage IV under 10%** (median survival with modern chemo-immunotherapy about 13-15 months). [ESMO Gastric 2022]`,`**Surgical complications**: anastomotic leak, duodenal stump blowout, pancreatic fistula after D2, dumping, afferent loop syndrome, and nutritional deficiencies. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Chemotherapy toxicity**: FLOT - neutropenia and diarrhoea; oxaliplatin - **cold-induced and cumulative neuropathy**; trastuzumab - cardiotoxicity (echo every 3 months); immunotherapy - immune-related colitis, hepatitis, thyroiditis. [ESMO Gastric 2022]`,`**MALT lymphoma** has an excellent prognosis - **10-year survival about 90%** - and histological regression after eradication may take 12-24 months. [ESMO MZL 2020]`,`**Diffuse type, linitis plastica, peritoneal disease and R1 resection** predict poor outcome. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Urgent (2-week) endoscopy referral** for dyspepsia with weight loss, dysphagia, persistent vomiting, iron deficiency anaemia or an epigastric mass. [ACG/CAG 2017]`,`**After ESD**: endoscopy at **3-6 months then yearly** for metachronous lesions, with H. pylori eradication; non-curative resection (eCura C-2) needs surgery. [JGES ESD 2020]`,`**After gastrectomy**: clinical review every **3-6 months for 2-3 years**, then 6-12 monthly to 5 years; imaging guided by symptoms in most guidelines. [ESMO Gastric 2022]`,`**MALT lymphoma after eradication**: confirm H. pylori cure at 4-6 weeks, then **endoscopy with biopsies every 3-6 months for 2 years**, then yearly (GELA histological grading). [ESMO MZL 2020]`,`**Premalignant lesions**: follow the MAPS II interval - **3 years for extensive IM, 1-2 years with family history, 6-12 months for dysplasia without a visible lesion**. [ESGE MAPS II 2019]`,`**Family members**: H. pylori test-and-treat for first-degree relatives; **genetic counselling** if criteria for HDGC or Lynch are met. [IGCLC HDGC 2020]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`**H. pylori eradication is primary prevention**; Maastricht VI and the Taipei consensus support **population screen-and-treat in high-incidence regions** (in India, worth considering for high-incidence areas such as the north-east). [Maastricht VI 2022]`,`**Diet**: less salt and salted, smoked or pickled food (e.g. smoked meat and fermented fish in the north-east), more fresh fruit and vegetables, and refrigerated storage. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Stop smoking and limit alcohol** - tobacco cessation through NTCP and quit-lines. [NTCP 2023]`,`**NP-NCD** screens for oral, breast and cervical cancer, not gastric cancer; Indian prevention therefore relies on **alarm-feature recognition and endoscopy referral** from Ayushman Arogya Mandirs. [MoHFW 2023]`,`**Japan and Korea** screen by endoscopy (Korea every 2 years from age 40) - not cost-effective for India's overall incidence. [ESMO Gastric 2022]`,`**Counsel survivors** about B12 injections, small frequent meals, and early reporting of weight loss or new symptoms. [Bailey and Love 28e]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2026)`,points:[`**ESMO 2022 gastric cancer guideline and 2024 living update**: biomarker testing (HER2, MSI, PD-L1 CPS, **Claudin 18.2**) for all advanced cases; nivolumab or pembrolizumab with chemotherapy by CPS; **zolbetuximab** for Claudin 18.2-positive HER2-negative disease. [ESMO Gastric 2024 update]`,`**Zolbetuximab** (anti-Claudin 18.2) was approved by the EMA, Japan and the US FDA in **2024** - the first new target in gastric cancer since HER2. [FDA 2024]`,`**MATTERHORN trial (2025)**: adding **perioperative durvalumab to FLOT** improved event-free survival in resectable gastric and GOJ cancer and is being adopted into guidelines. [MATTERHORN NEJM 2025]`,`**ACG 2025 guideline on gastric premalignant conditions** endorses H. pylori eradication, high-quality endoscopy with Sydney biopsies, and **surveillance about every 3 years for high-risk intestinal metaplasia** - converging with MAPS II. [ACG Gastric Premalignant 2025]`,`**Maastricht VI 2022**: eradication in first-degree relatives and after endoscopic resection of early cancer; screen-and-treat in high-risk populations. [Maastricht VI 2022]`,`**KEYNOTE-811 (2023-2024)**: pembrolizumab added to trastuzumab-chemotherapy in HER2-positive disease, now restricted to **PD-L1 CPS 1 or more**. [ESMO Gastric 2024 update]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic: **'Discuss the aetiopathogenesis, staging and management of carcinoma stomach'** - write Correa cascade, Lauren types, TNM, signs of spread, FLOT plus D2 gastrectomy. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: **name the eponymous signs** - Virchow node (Troisier sign), Sister Mary Joseph nodule, Irish node, Blumer shelf, Krukenberg tumour. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`Viva: **'Definition of early gastric cancer?'** - confined to mucosa or submucosa **regardless of nodal status**. [JGCA 2021]`,`Viva: **'Which MALT lymphoma will not respond to eradication?'** - one with **t(11;18)(q21;q21) API2-MALT1**, or deep or nodal disease. [ESMO MZL 2020]`,`Common mistake: treating a gastric ulcer with PPI **without biopsy or follow-up endoscopy**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: ordering **CEA or CA 19-9 to diagnose** gastric cancer - they are for follow-up only. [ESMO Gastric 2022]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs and regimens in gastric cancer and MALT lymphoma`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`FLOT (perioperative)`,`Docetaxel 50 mg/m2, oxaliplatin 85 mg/m2, leucovorin 200 mg/m2 IV, 5-FU 2600 mg/m2 IV over 24 h`,`Every 2 weeks, 4 cycles before and 4 after surgery`,`Neutropenia, diarrhoea, neuropathy, mucositis; needs good performance status`],[`CAPOX (adjuvant or palliative)`,`Oxaliplatin 130 mg/m2 IV day 1 + capecitabine 1000 mg/m2 orally twice daily days 1-14`,`Every 3 weeks; 8 cycles adjuvant`,`Hand-foot syndrome, neuropathy, diarrhoea; reduce capecitabine in CKD; DPD deficiency`],[`Nivolumab with chemotherapy`,`240 mg IV every 2 weeks or 360 mg every 3 weeks`,`Up to 2 years or progression`,`Immune colitis, hepatitis, pneumonitis, thyroiditis`],[`Trastuzumab with chemotherapy`,`8 mg/kg IV loading then 6 mg/kg every 3 weeks`,`Until progression`,`Cardiotoxicity - echo every 3 months; infusion reactions`],[`Zolbetuximab with chemotherapy`,`800 mg/m2 IV loading then 600 mg/m2 every 3 weeks`,`Until progression`,`Severe nausea and vomiting (premedicate), hypoalbuminaemia`],[`Ramucirumab + paclitaxel`,`Ramucirumab 8 mg/kg IV days 1 and 15; paclitaxel 80 mg/m2 days 1, 8, 15`,`28-day cycles, second line`,`Hypertension, bleeding, proteinuria, neutropenia`],[`H. pylori eradication (MALT lymphoma, premalignant lesions)`,`PPI twice daily + bismuth + tetracycline 500 mg four times daily + metronidazole 400 mg three times daily`,`14 days; confirm cure at 4-6 weeks`,`Black stools, nausea, metallic taste; avoid tetracycline in pregnancy`],[`Vitamin B12 after gastrectomy`,`1 mg IM every 3 months (or high-dose oral 1-2 mg daily)`,`Lifelong`,`Safe`]]},{heading:`Lauren classification`,columns:[`Feature`,`Intestinal type`,`Diffuse type`],rows:[[`Histology`,`Gland-forming, cohesive`,`Poorly cohesive, signet-ring cells`],[`Precursor`,`Correa cascade (atrophy, IM)`,`No clear precursor; E-cadherin loss`],[`Patient`,`Older, men`,`Younger, women`],[`Risk factors`,`H. pylori, diet`,`CDH1, blood group A`],[`Spread`,`Haematogenous (liver)`,`Peritoneal, transmural (linitis plastica)`],[`Prognosis`,`Better`,`Worse`]]},{heading:`MAPS II surveillance of gastric premalignant conditions`,columns:[`Finding`,`Risk`,`Surveillance`],rows:[[`Mild atrophy or IM in antrum only (OLGA/OLGIM 0-II)`,`Low`,`None after eradication, unless risk factors`],[`Antral IM plus family history, incomplete IM or persistent H. pylori`,`Moderate`,`Endoscopy in 3 years`],[`Extensive atrophy or IM in antrum and corpus (OLGA/OLGIM III-IV)`,`High`,`Endoscopy every 3 years`],[`Extensive IM with first-degree relative with gastric cancer`,`Higher`,`Endoscopy every 1-2 years`],[`Low-grade dysplasia, no visible lesion`,`High`,`Repeat within 12 months`],[`High-grade dysplasia, no visible lesion`,`Very high`,`Repeat within 6 months; resect any lesion`],[`Autoimmune atrophic gastritis`,`Moderate`,`Every 3-5 years`]]},{heading:`AJCC 8th edition TNM for gastric cancer`,columns:[`Category`,`Definition`,`Practical meaning`],rows:[[`T1a`,`Lamina propria or muscularis mucosae`,`ESD candidate if criteria met`],[`T1b`,`Submucosa`,`Usually surgery`],[`T2`,`Muscularis propria`,`Perioperative chemotherapy plus surgery`],[`T3`,`Subserosa`,`Perioperative chemotherapy plus surgery`],[`T4a / T4b`,`Serosa / adjacent organs`,`Staging laparoscopy; multivisceral resection if feasible`],[`N1 / N2`,`1-2 / 3-6 regional nodes`,`Node-positive: perioperative therapy`],[`N3a / N3b`,`7-15 / 16 or more nodes`,`Poor prognosis`],[`M1`,`Distant metastasis or positive cytology`,`Systemic biomarker-guided therapy`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of a gastric mass or ulcer`,columns:[`Lesion`,`Endoscopic clue`,`Confirmation`],rows:[[`Adenocarcinoma`,`Irregular heaped edge, folds clubbed or stop short`,`Multiple biopsies`],[`Benign gastric ulcer`,`Smooth edge, folds radiate to crater`,`Biopsy and re-scope at 8-12 weeks`],[`MALT lymphoma`,`Erythema, erosions, thick folds`,`Deep biopsy, IHC, t(11;18)`],[`GIST`,`Submucosal mass, central ulcer`,`EUS-FNB, CD117 and DOG1`],[`Neuroendocrine tumour`,`Small polyps in atrophic corpus (type 1)`,`Biopsy, chromogranin, gastrin`],[`Gastric TB`,`Ulcer or stricture, antral`,`Biopsy, AFB, GeneXpert`]]},{heading:`Eponymous signs of gastric cancer spread`,columns:[`Sign`,`Site`,`Route`],rows:[[`Virchow node (Troisier sign)`,`Left supraclavicular node`,`Lymphatic via thoracic duct`],[`Sister Mary Joseph nodule`,`Umbilicus`,`Lymphatic or falciform ligament`],[`Irish node`,`Left axillary node`,`Lymphatic`],[`Blumer shelf`,`Rectovesical or rectouterine pouch`,`Transcoelomic`],[`Krukenberg tumour`,`Ovaries (bilateral)`,`Transcoelomic or lymphatic`]]}],redFlags:[`Dyspepsia with weight loss, dysphagia or persistent vomiting`,`Iron deficiency anaemia or melaena with upper GI symptoms`,`Palpable epigastric mass or left supraclavicular node`,`New dyspepsia after about 45 years in India, or with a first-degree relative with gastric cancer`,`Gastric ulcer that fails to heal at 8-12 weeks`,`Vomiting of stale food (malignant gastric outlet obstruction)`,`Sudden extensive acanthosis nigricans or migratory thrombophlebitis`],pearls:[`**Correa cascade**: gastritis, atrophy, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia, cancer.`,`**Updated Sydney protocol = 5 biopsies** (2 antrum, 1 incisura, 2 corpus).`,`**OLGA/OLGIM III-IV = high risk = endoscopy every 3 years**.`,`**Early gastric cancer** = mucosa or submucosa, **regardless of nodes**.`,`**FLOT 4 + 4** around D2 gastrectomy for cT2+ or node-positive disease.`,`**Biomarkers in advanced disease: HER2, MSI, PD-L1 CPS, Claudin 18.2**.`,`**MALT lymphoma: eradicate first**; t(11;18) means it will not respond.`,`Signs of spread - **'VIS-BK'**: Virchow, Irish, Sister Mary Joseph, Blumer, Krukenberg.`,`**Every gastric ulcer is biopsied and re-scoped**.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-gastric-cancer-q1`,stem:`Endoscopic biopsies by the updated Sydney protocol show intestinal metaplasia in both antrum and corpus (OLGIM stage III) in a 55-year-old after H. pylori eradication. No dysplasia is seen. What surveillance does MAPS II recommend?`,options:[`No surveillance`,`Endoscopy every 3 years`,`Endoscopy every 6 months`,`Annual CT abdomen`],answer:1,explanation:`Extensive atrophy or intestinal metaplasia involving antrum and corpus (OLGA/OLGIM III-IV) is high risk, and MAPS II recommends high-quality endoscopy every 3 years. No surveillance applies to mild antrum-limited change, 6-monthly endoscopy is for high-grade dysplasia without a visible lesion, and CT has no role in surveillance.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gastric-cancer-q2`,stem:`Which statement correctly defines early gastric cancer?`,options:[`Tumour under 2 cm in diameter`,`Tumour without lymph node metastasis`,`Tumour confined to the mucosa or submucosa, regardless of lymph node status`,`Tumour confined to the mucosa only`],answer:2,explanation:`Early gastric cancer is defined by depth - confined to mucosa or submucosa (T1) - irrespective of nodal status. Size is relevant to ESD criteria, not to the definition; node-negative status is not required; and limiting it to mucosa alone would exclude T1b submucosal cancers.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gastric-cancer-q3`,stem:`A 50-year-old with stage I gastric MALT lymphoma is H. pylori positive. Which finding best predicts failure of response to H. pylori eradication?`,options:[`t(11;18)(q21;q21) API2-MALT1 translocation`,`CagA-positive H. pylori strain`,`Lesion limited to the antrum`,`Age over 45 years`],answer:0,explanation:`The t(11;18) translocation produces an API2-MALT1 fusion that makes the lymphoma independent of H. pylori-driven stimulation, so it rarely regresses after eradication and needs radiotherapy. CagA strain status, antral location and age do not predict non-response; superficial antral disease actually responds well.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gastric-cancer-q4`,stem:`A 58-year-old man has a cT3N1M0 distal gastric adenocarcinoma with negative staging laparoscopy and good performance status. What is the standard of care?`,options:[`Upfront total gastrectomy with D1 lymphadenectomy and no chemotherapy`,`Palliative chemotherapy only`,`Endoscopic submucosal dissection`,`Perioperative FLOT chemotherapy with subtotal gastrectomy and D2 lymphadenectomy`],answer:3,explanation:`Locally advanced resectable gastric cancer (cT2 or more, or node-positive) is treated with perioperative FLOT (4 cycles before and after) and gastrectomy with D2 lymphadenectomy; a distal tumour allows subtotal gastrectomy. Surgery alone with D1 dissection undertreats, palliative chemotherapy is for metastatic disease, and ESD is only for early mucosal cancers.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gastric-cancer-q5`,stem:`A woman with signet-ring gastric adenocarcinoma has bilateral ovarian masses. What is this ovarian lesion called?`,options:[`Sister Mary Joseph nodule`,`Krukenberg tumour`,`Blumer shelf`,`Brenner tumour`],answer:1,explanation:`Bilateral ovarian metastases containing mucin-filled signet-ring cells, usually from gastric cancer, are Krukenberg tumours. A Sister Mary Joseph nodule is an umbilical metastasis, Blumer shelf is a pelvic peritoneal deposit felt rectally, and a Brenner tumour is a primary ovarian transitional-cell neoplasm.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-gastric-cancer-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the aetiopathogenesis, clinical features, staging and management of carcinoma stomach. Add a note on the role of H. pylori in its prevention.`,openingLines:[`Gastric adenocarcinoma, about 90% of gastric malignancies, is the fifth commonest cancer worldwide and arises mostly through the H. pylori-driven Correa cascade of gastritis, atrophy, intestinal metaplasia and dysplasia.`,`In India most patients present late; cure depends on detecting early cancer for endoscopic resection or treating locally advanced disease with perioperative FLOT and D2 gastrectomy.`],answer:[{heading:`Aetiology and pathogenesis`,points:[`H. pylori (group 1 carcinogen), salt, smoked and pickled foods, smoking, EBV, family history, CDH1, Lynch, pernicious anaemia, prior gastrectomy.`,`Correa cascade; diffuse type through E-cadherin loss; TCGA subtypes; biomarkers HER2, MSI, PD-L1, Claudin 18.2.`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`Lauren intestinal versus diffuse; Borrmann I-IV; early gastric cancer and Paris types.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Weight loss, epigastric pain, early satiety, dysphagia (cardia), vomiting (antrum), anaemia.`,`Virchow node, Sister Mary Joseph nodule, Irish node, Blumer shelf, Krukenberg tumour; paraneoplastic signs.`]},{heading:`Investigations and staging`,points:[`Endoscopy with biopsies; CECT chest-abdomen-pelvis; EUS for early lesions; staging laparoscopy with washings.`,`AJCC 8th TNM; at least 16 nodes examined; biomarker testing.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Early: ESD for differentiated intramucosal lesions meeting criteria.`,`Resectable cT2+ or N+: FLOT 4 cycles, D2 gastrectomy, FLOT 4 cycles.`,`Advanced: chemotherapy with nivolumab or pembrolizumab, trastuzumab if HER2-positive, zolbetuximab if Claudin 18.2-positive; second line ramucirumab-paclitaxel.`,`Palliation: stent, radiotherapy for bleeding, nutrition, palliative care.`]},{heading:`Role of H. pylori in prevention`,points:[`Eradication before atrophy gives greatest benefit; halves risk in first-degree relatives and metachronous cancer after ESD.`,`Screen-and-treat in high-incidence regions; MAPS II surveillance for extensive intestinal metaplasia.`]}],mustDraw:[`Correa cascade flow chart`,`Diagram of lymphatic and transcoelomic spread with eponymous signs`],markSplit:[{part:`Aetiopathogenesis and classification`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations and staging`,marks:2},{part:`Management`,marks:3},{part:`H. pylori in prevention`,marks:1}],keywords:[`Correa cascade`,`Lauren`,`TNM`,`FLOT`,`D2 gastrectomy`,`ESD`,`H. pylori`,`Virchow node`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gastric-cancer-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on gastric MALT lymphoma.`,openingLines:[`Gastric MALT lymphoma is an extranodal marginal zone B-cell lymphoma arising in mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue acquired in response to chronic H. pylori gastritis.`,`It is the only lymphoma that can be cured with antibiotics: H. pylori eradication induces remission in about 75% of localised cases.`],answer:[{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`H. pylori drives T-cell-dependent B-cell proliferation; t(11;18) API2-MALT1 makes it antigen-independent.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis and staging`,points:[`Endoscopy with deep biopsies, IHC (CD20+, CD5-, CD10-), lymphoepithelial lesions; H. pylori status; t(11;18) FISH.`,`EUS for depth and nodes; CT; Lugano or Paris staging.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Localised H. pylori-positive: 14-day eradication, confirm cure, endoscopic biopsies every 3-6 months for 2 years.`,`Non-response, t(11;18) or H. pylori-negative: involved-site radiotherapy 24 Gy.`,`Advanced: rituximab-based therapy; transformation to DLBCL: R-CHOP.`]},{heading:`Prognosis`,points:[`10-year survival about 90%; regression may take 12-24 months.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: MALT lymphoma - H. pylori status and t(11;18) - eradication or radiotherapy - follow-up`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and pathogenesis`,marks:1},{part:`Diagnosis and staging`,marks:1},{part:`Treatment`,marks:2},{part:`Follow-up and prognosis`,marks:1}],keywords:[`MALT lymphoma`,`H. pylori`,`t(11;18)`,`radiotherapy`,`rituximab`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Upper gastrointestinal tract cancers`,`Pimentel-Nunes P, et al. Management of epithelial precancerous conditions and lesions in the stomach (MAPS II). Endoscopy 2019`,`Lordick F, et al. Gastric cancer: ESMO Clinical Practice Guideline for diagnosis, treatment and follow-up. Ann Oncol 2022, with 2024 living guideline update`,`Japanese Gastric Cancer Treatment Guidelines 2021 (6th edition). Gastric Cancer 2023`,`Malfertheiner P, et al. Maastricht VI/Florence consensus report. Gut 2022`,`Zucca E, et al. Marginal zone lymphomas: ESMO Clinical Practice Guidelines. Ann Oncol 2020`,`Blair VR, et al. Hereditary diffuse gastric cancer: updated clinical practice guidelines (IGCLC). Lancet Oncol 2020`,`ICMR-National Centre for Disease Informatics and Research. Report of National Cancer Registry Programme, 2020`]});var n=[];n.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-chronic-diarrhoea-approach`,title:`Approach to chronic diarrhoea`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Chronic diarrhoea** is the passage of **loose stools (Bristol 5-7) with or without increased frequency for 4 weeks or more**; the approach sorts it into **osmotic, secretory, inflammatory or fatty (steatorrhoea)** by history, stool tests (**faecal calprotectin, elastase, osmotic gap**) and **colonoscopy with ileal and segmental biopsies**, remembering **coeliac disease, bile acid diarrhoea, microscopic colitis** and, in India, **intestinal TB, giardiasis and tropical sprue**.`,keywords:[`chronic diarrhea`,`osmotic gap`,`secretory diarrhoea`,`steatorrhoea`,`faecal calprotectin`,`faecal elastase`,`bile acid diarrhoea`,`SeHCAT`,`serum C4`,`microscopic colitis`,`collagenous colitis`,`lymphocytic colitis`,`budesonide`,`cholestyramine`,`loperamide`,`factitious diarrhoea`,`VIPoma`,`BSG chronic diarrhoea guideline`,`functional diarrhoea`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Chronic diarrhoea** is persistent alteration from the norm with stool consistency **Bristol type 5-7** and usually increased frequency, lasting **more than 4 weeks**; stool weight above 200 g/day is a research definition, not a bedside one. [BSG 2018]`,`**Persistent diarrhoea** (WHO usage, mainly children) lasts **14-29 days**; acute diarrhoea is under 14 days and is covered in the Acute diarrhoea and dysentery topic. [WHO 2017]`,`Chronic diarrhoea affects an estimated **3-5% of adults** in Western population surveys and is a common reason for gastroenterology referral. [BSG 2018]`,`Patients often mean **urgency, incontinence or increased frequency of formed stools** when they say diarrhoea - pin down consistency with the **Bristol stool chart** before investigating. [Harrison 22e]`,`In high-income countries the commonest causes are **IBS-D/functional diarrhoea, bile acid diarrhoea, microscopic colitis, coeliac disease, IBD, drugs and colorectal cancer**. [BSG 2018]`,`In India, **infective causes (giardiasis, amoebiasis, cryptosporidium and isospora in HIV), intestinal tuberculosis, tropical sprue, IBD and coeliac disease (north India)** remain prominent in hospital series. [ISG/Harrison 22e]`,`**Bile acid diarrhoea** is under-recognised: about **25-33% of patients labelled IBS-D** have it on SeHCAT testing. [BSG 2018]`,`**Microscopic colitis** accounts for about **10% of chronic watery diarrhoea**, rising to about 20% in those over 70, and is commoner in women. [AGA 2022]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors - the four mechanisms`,points:[`**Osmotic**: poorly absorbed solutes hold water in the lumen - **lactose (lactase deficiency), magnesium antacids and laxatives, lactulose, sorbitol, mannitol and fructose**; diarrhoea stops with fasting. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Secretory**: active chloride secretion or blocked sodium absorption - **bile acid diarrhoea, microscopic colitis, drugs, stimulant laxative abuse, VIPoma, carcinoid, gastrinoma, medullary thyroid carcinoma, villous adenoma, mastocytosis and cholera-like toxins**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Inflammatory**: mucosal damage with blood, pus or protein loss - **ulcerative colitis, Crohn disease, intestinal TB, amoebic colitis, CMV, radiation colitis, ischaemic colitis and colorectal cancer**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fatty (steatorrhoea)**: maldigestion (**pancreatic exocrine insufficiency**, bile salt deficiency) or mucosal malabsorption (**coeliac disease, tropical sprue, giardiasis, SIBO, Whipple disease, short bowel**). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drugs** cause about 4% of chronic diarrhoea: **metformin, PPIs, NSAIDs, SSRIs, olmesartan, colchicine, mycophenolate, magnesium, antibiotics, immune checkpoint inhibitors and sugar-free sweeteners**. [BSG 2018]`,`**Post-surgical causes**: cholecystectomy (bile acid), ileal resection under 100 cm (bile acid) or over 100 cm (fat), vagotomy, gastrectomy (dumping), and short bowel. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Endocrine causes**: **hyperthyroidism, diabetic autonomic neuropathy, Addison disease** and the neuroendocrine tumours. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HIV** must be considered in every Indian patient with chronic diarrhoea and weight loss - cryptosporidium, cystoisospora, microsporidia, CMV and MAC are the usual agents. [NACO 2021]`,`**Overflow diarrhoea** around faecal impaction in the elderly and **faecal incontinence** mislabelled as diarrhoea must be excluded by rectal examination. [BSG 2018]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`About **9 litres** of fluid enter the small bowel daily; the small bowel absorbs about 7 litres and the colon about 1.9 litres, so a small loss of colonic reserve (maximum about 4-5 litres/day) causes diarrhoea. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Osmotic gap**: stool osmolality is assumed to be 290 mOsm/kg; gap = 290 - 2 x (stool Na + K); a gap **above 125** means osmotic, **below 50** means secretory diarrhoea. [Harrison 22e]`,`A **measured stool osmolality below 290** means the sample has been diluted with water or hypotonic urine - think **factitious diarrhoea**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Secretory diarrhoea** comes from raised cAMP, cGMP or calcium in enterocytes opening the **CFTR chloride channel**; it persists during a 48-72 hour fast and at night. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bile acid diarrhoea**: unabsorbed bile acids reach the colon and stimulate secretion and motility; in primary (type 2) disease the ileal hormone **FGF19 is low**, so hepatic bile acid synthesis (measured by **serum C4**) runs unchecked. [BSG 2018]`,`Ileal resection **under 100 cm** causes bile acid diarrhoea (liver compensates for fat) - treat with sequestrants; resection **over 100 cm** exhausts the bile acid pool, causing **steatorrhoea** - sequestrants worsen it. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Steatorrhoea** occurs only after more than **90% of pancreatic lipase output is lost**, which is why pancreatic insufficiency is late and why faecal elastase is the screen. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Inflammatory diarrhoea** combines exudation of blood, mucus and protein, loss of absorptive surface and cytokine-driven secretion; **calprotectin** is a neutrophil cytosolic protein shed into the lumen, so it tracks neutrophil infiltration. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Microscopic colitis** causes secretory diarrhoea with a normal-looking colon because the lesion is a **thickened subepithelial collagen band** or **intraepithelial lymphocytosis** that impairs sodium and chloride absorption. [AGA 2022]`]},{heading:`Classification that examiners ask`,points:[`**Clinical classification**: watery (osmotic or secretory), inflammatory (blood, pus) and fatty (bulky, pale, greasy, floating, hard to flush). [AGA 1999/Harrison 22e]`,`**Anatomical localisation**: small-bowel diarrhoea is **large volume, infrequent, periumbilical cramps, undigested food, weight loss**; colonic diarrhoea is **small volume, frequent, urgency, tenesmus, blood and mucus**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Functional versus organic**: functional diarrhoea (Rome IV) is loose stools in more than 25% of bowel movements for 3 months without predominant pain; IBS-D adds recurrent pain related to defecation. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Bile acid diarrhoea types**: type 1 secondary to ileal disease or resection, type 2 primary (idiopathic), type 3 other (post-cholecystectomy, vagotomy, SIBO, coeliac, pancreatic). [BSG 2018]`,`**Microscopic colitis subtypes**: **collagenous colitis** (subepithelial collagen band **above 10 micrometres**) and **lymphocytic colitis** (**above 20 intraepithelial lymphocytes per 100 epithelial cells**); incomplete forms fall short of these cut-offs. [AGA 2022]`,`**SeHCAT 7-day retention grading**: under 15% abnormal, **under 10% moderate, under 5% severe** bile acid diarrhoea; lower retention predicts a better response to sequestrants. [BSG 2018]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Clarify **true diarrhoea** with the Bristol chart, number of stools a day, **nocturnal stools** (organic until proved otherwise) and incontinence. [BSG 2018]`,`**Duration and onset**: sudden onset after travel or a meal suggests post-infective causes (giardia, post-infective IBS or bile acid diarrhoea); a continuous course over years without weight loss suggests functional disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Blood and mucus with tenesmus** point to colitis (IBD, amoebic colitis, TB, cancer); **pale, oily, foul stools that float** point to steatorrhoea. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Weight loss, fever, night sweats and anorexia** in an Indian patient raise intestinal TB, HIV, lymphoma and IBD. [ISG/Harrison 22e]`,`**Drug history is decisive**: list every drug started in the last 6 months, including metformin, PPIs, NSAIDs, SSRIs, olmesartan, magnesium and laxatives; ask directly about **sorbitol-containing sugar-free gum and sweets**. [BSG 2018]`,`Ask about **diet** - milk and lactose, fructose, caffeine, alcohol (alcohol causes diarrhoea by direct toxicity and rapid transit). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Past surgery**: cholecystectomy, ileal resection, gastrectomy, bariatric surgery, pelvic radiotherapy. [BSG 2018]`,`**Systemic clues**: heat intolerance and palpitations (thyrotoxicosis), flushing and wheeze (carcinoid), peptic ulcers (gastrinoma), autonomic symptoms in long-standing diabetes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Family history** of coeliac disease, IBD or colorectal cancer, and **personal autoimmunity** (type 1 diabetes, thyroid disease - associated with coeliac disease and microscopic colitis). [BSG 2018]`,`**Psychosocial history**: healthcare workers and patients with secondary gain may abuse laxatives or dilute stool samples - think of it when tests are inconsistent. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**General**: weight and BMI against previous records, pallor, **dehydration**, oedema from protein-losing enteropathy, and lymphadenopathy (TB, lymphoma, HIV). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nutritional signs**: angular cheilitis and glossitis (B12, folate, iron), **koilonychia**, bruising (vitamin K), **Chvostek and Trousseau signs** (low calcium and magnesium), night blindness (vitamin A). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Thyroid signs**: goitre, tremor, tachycardia, lid lag. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Skin**: **dermatitis herpetiformis** (coeliac), erythema nodosum and pyoderma gangrenosum (IBD), flushing and telangiectasia (carcinoid), urticaria pigmentosa (mastocytosis), hyperpigmentation (Addison, Whipple). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Abdomen**: right iliac fossa mass (Crohn disease, ileocaecal TB, caecal cancer), hepatomegaly (metastatic carcinoid), ascites (TB), surgical scars. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Digital rectal examination** in every patient: faecal impaction, rectal mass, anal fistula (Crohn disease) and sphincter tone (incontinence). [BSG 2018]`,`**Postural hypotension** and peripheral neuropathy in a diabetic suggest autonomic neuropathy as the cause. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**IBS-D/functional diarrhoea versus organic disease**: functional disease has no nocturnal stools, no weight loss, no blood, normal Hb, CRP and calprotectin; the label is safe only after these are checked. [AGA 2019]`,`**Bile acid diarrhoea versus IBS-D**: urgent watery stools, often after cholecystectomy or ileal disease, abnormal SeHCAT or high C4, and a dramatic response to cholestyramine. [BSG 2018]`,`**Microscopic colitis versus IBS-D**: older woman, nocturnal watery stools, recent PPI/NSAID/SSRI, autoimmune disease, **normal colonoscopy but abnormal biopsies**. [AGA 2022]`,`**IBD versus intestinal TB versus amoebic colitis** - the Indian triad for chronic bloody diarrhoea; stool for trophozoites and serology, ileocolonoscopy with biopsy for TB culture and GeneXpert. [ISG]`,`**Coeliac disease versus tropical sprue**: both cause villous atrophy; coeliac has **positive tTG-IgA and responds to a gluten-free diet**, sprue follows residence in the tropics and responds to **folate plus tetracycline**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pancreatic insufficiency versus mucosal malabsorption**: low faecal elastase and normal **D-xylose absorption** in pancreatic disease; abnormal D-xylose in mucosal disease (test now rarely used). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Colorectal cancer** must be excluded in anyone over 40-50 with a new change in bowel habit, iron deficiency or rectal bleeding - covered in the Colorectal polyps, cancer and screening topic. [NICE DG56]`,`**Factitious diarrhoea**: very high stool output, low stool osmolality, high magnesium, **positive laxative screen (bisacodyl, senna)** and melanosis coli on colonoscopy. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line (OPD/district hospital)`,points:[`**Blood tests in all**: full blood count, ESR or CRP, urea, electrolytes, liver function tests including albumin, **ferritin, B12, folate, calcium, TSH**, and HbA1c where relevant. [BSG 2018]`,`**Coeliac serology in all**: **tTG-IgA with total IgA**, on a gluten-containing diet. [BSG 2018]`,`**HIV test** (with consent) in every Indian adult with chronic diarrhoea and weight loss. [NACO 2021]`,`**Stool microscopy**: three samples for ova, cysts and parasites, **modified acid-fast stain** for cryptosporidium and cystoisospora in HIV, and **Giardia stool antigen** where available; stool culture has low yield in chronic diarrhoea. [BSG 2018]`,`**Faecal calprotectin** separates IBD from IBS in those **under 40**: **below 50 micrograms/g** makes IBD unlikely; **50-200** repeat in 4-6 weeks; **above 200-250** refer for colonoscopy. [NICE DG11/BSG 2018]`,`Calprotectin is also raised by **NSAIDs, PPIs, infection, colorectal cancer, intestinal TB and microscopic colitis**, so a high value is not specific for IBD. [BSG 2018]`,`**FIT** (faecal immunochemical test) **10 micrograms Hb/g or more** in a patient with a change in bowel habit triggers urgent colonoscopy for colorectal cancer. [NICE DG56/BSG-ACPGBI 2022]`,`**Faecal elastase-1**: **below 200 micrograms/g** suggests pancreatic exocrine insufficiency and **below 100** confirms severe insufficiency; watery stool falsely lowers it, so use a formed sample. [UEG 2017/Harrison 22e]`,`**Stool electrolytes, osmolality and osmotic gap**, plus a **laxative screen and stool magnesium**, when the cause is still obscure. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - advanced and tertiary`,points:[`**Ileocolonoscopy** for chronic diarrhoea with alarm features, raised calprotectin, or age over 50 when non-invasive tests are unhelpful; intubate the **terminal ileum**. [BSG 2018]`,`**Segmental colonic biopsies even when the mucosa looks normal**: at least **2 from the right and 2 from the left colon** in separate pots, to diagnose microscopic colitis. [BSG 2018/AGA 2022]`,`**Upper GI endoscopy with at least 4 duodenal biopsies** (2 from the bulb) for positive coeliac serology, iron deficiency or suspected tropical sprue, giardiasis or Whipple disease. [ACG 2023]`,`**Bile acid diarrhoea testing**: **SeHCAT** (75Se-homocholic acid taurine retention at 7 days) where available, or **serum C4** (7-alpha-hydroxy-4-cholesten-3-one, raised in BAD) or FGF19; most Indian centres use an **empirical trial of cholestyramine**. [BSG 2018]`,`**Glucose or lactulose hydrogen breath test** for SIBO (rise of **20 ppm or more** within 90 minutes) and lactose hydrogen breath test for lactase deficiency. [North American Consensus 2017]`,`**CT or MR enterography** for small-bowel Crohn disease, TB, lymphoma and fistulae; **capsule endoscopy** when obstruction is excluded. [ECCO-ESGAR 2019]`,`**Neuroendocrine tumour work-up only for high-volume secretory diarrhoea** (over 1 litre/day) without another cause: plasma **VIP, gastrin, calcitonin, chromogranin A** and urinary **5-HIAA**; random gut hormone panels mostly give false positives. [BSG 2018]`,`**Quantitative faecal fat** (over **7 g/day** on a 100 g fat diet over 72 hours) confirms steatorrhoea but is rarely done now. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Treat the cause** once found: stop the culprit drug (switch metformin to an extended-release form or another agent, stop PPI or olmesartan), gluten-free diet for coeliac disease, antiparasitic therapy for giardia. [BSG 2018]`,`**Giardiasis**: **tinidazole 2 g orally single dose** or **metronidazole 400 mg three times daily for 5-7 days**; tinidazole is better tolerated. [ICMR STW/Harrison 22e]`,`**Bile acid diarrhoea**: **cholestyramine 4 g once daily, titrated to 4 g up to four times daily**, taken at least 4 hours apart from other drugs; colesevelam 625 mg tablets (1.875-3.75 g/day) is better tolerated but not widely available in India. [BSG 2018]`,`**Microscopic colitis**: stop PPIs, NSAIDs and SSRIs where possible and give **budesonide 9 mg once daily for 6-8 weeks**; loperamide alone is acceptable only for mild disease. [AGA 2022]`,`**Symptomatic loperamide**: **4 mg then 2 mg after each loose stool, maximum 16 mg/day** - only after infection and colitis are excluded, never in bloody diarrhoea. [BNF/ICMR STW]`,`**Pancreatic exocrine insufficiency**: pancreatin with **40,000-50,000 lipase units with each main meal and 25,000 with snacks**, taken with food, plus a PPI if response is poor - details in the Chronic pancreatitis topic. [UEG 2017]`,`**Functional diarrhoea/IBS-D**: soluble fibre, low FODMAP diet under a dietitian, loperamide, low-dose amitriptyline 10-30 mg at night, and rifaximin for IBS-D - see the IBS and chronic constipation topic. [BSG IBS 2021]`,`**Rehydration and nutrition**: ORS for daily losses, and replace **iron, B12, folate, vitamin D, calcium, zinc and magnesium** according to tests. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Admit** for dehydration with acute kidney injury, **hypokalaemia below 3 mmol/L**, hypomagnesaemia, severe hypoalbuminaemia, bloody diarrhoea with systemic toxicity, or weight loss with inability to eat. [BSG 2018]`,`**Fluids**: Ringer lactate or normal saline with **potassium replacement**; check magnesium, phosphate and bicarbonate (secretory diarrhoea loses bicarbonate - normal anion gap acidosis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Refeeding risk**: in a cachectic patient start feeding at **10 kcal/kg/day** with thiamine, potassium, phosphate and magnesium supplements. [NICE CG32]`,`**Inpatient 48-72 hour fast with measured stool volume** separates osmotic (stops) from secretory (continues) diarrhoea when outpatient tests are inconclusive. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Suspected acute severe colitis** (6 or more bloody stools with tachycardia or fever) is managed as acute severe UC after excluding infection - see the Ulcerative colitis topic. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Octreotide 50-100 micrograms subcutaneously three times daily** controls high-output secretory diarrhoea from VIPoma or carcinoid syndrome and chemotherapy-induced diarrhoea. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Elderly**: think of **microscopic colitis, faecal impaction with overflow, drugs, ischaemic colitis and colorectal cancer** first; avoid long-term loperamide when constipation alternates. [BSG 2018]`,`**Diabetes**: metformin intolerance, **diabetic autonomic neuropathy**, SIBO from dysmotility, coeliac disease (type 1 diabetes) and exocrine pancreatic insufficiency all cause diarrhoea. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HIV**: cryptosporidium (control with ART; nitazoxanide 500 mg twice daily 14 days as adjunct), **cystoisospora (co-trimoxazole 960 mg four times daily for 10 days)**, microsporidia (albendazole) and CMV colitis (ganciclovir). [NACO 2021]`,`**Pregnancy**: most investigations can wait; flexible sigmoidoscopy is safe if needed; avoid SeHCAT and use loperamide sparingly. [BSG 2018]`,`**Post-cholecystectomy diarrhoea** occurs in about 5-10% and is usually bile acid diarrhoea - try cholestyramine. [BSG 2018]`,`**Cancer patients**: immune checkpoint inhibitor colitis needs steroids and oncology input; pelvic radiotherapy causes bile acid malabsorption, SIBO and radiation proctitis. [ESMO 2022]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: blood counts, stool microscopy (3 samples), HIV, tTG-IgA and a therapeutic trial (tinidazole for giardia, cholestyramine for BAD) answer most questions before endoscopy. [ICMR STW]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Dehydration, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia and metabolic acidosis** in high-output secretory diarrhoea. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Malnutrition and micronutrient deficiency**: iron deficiency anaemia, megaloblastic anaemia, osteomalacia and osteoporosis, night blindness, coagulopathy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Oxalate kidney stones** after ileal resection with intact colon - unabsorbed fat binds calcium, freeing oxalate for absorption; treat with a low-fat, low-oxalate diet and oral calcium. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cholesterol gallstones** after ileal resection because the bile acid pool shrinks. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Microscopic colitis relapses in about 60-80% after stopping budesonide**, so many need low-dose maintenance; it does not increase colorectal cancer risk. [AGA 2022]`,`**Functional diarrhoea** has an excellent prognosis for life but a fluctuating course; a confident positive diagnosis reduces repeated investigation. [Rome IV 2016]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer urgently (2-week pathway)** for age over 40 with unexplained weight loss and abdominal pain, age over 50 with unexplained rectal bleeding, iron deficiency anaemia, abdominal or rectal mass, or **FIT 10 or more**. [NICE NG12/DG56]`,`**Refer routinely to gastroenterology** for calprotectin above 200-250, positive coeliac serology, suspected BAD, microscopic colitis or chronic diarrhoea unexplained after first-line tests. [BSG 2018]`,`**Review in 4-6 weeks** after any therapeutic trial (cholestyramine, budesonide, gluten-free diet, drug withdrawal) and document stool frequency and weight. [BSG 2018]`,`**Repeat calprotectin in 4-6 weeks** for a grey-zone result (50-200) before deciding on colonoscopy. [NICE DG11]`,`**On cholestyramine**, monitor fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K) and drug interactions (warfarin, levothyroxine, digoxin) every 6-12 months. [BNF]`,`**On budesonide maintenance**, check bone health (DXA) if used beyond 6-12 months and use the lowest effective dose (6 mg or less). [AGA 2022]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme links`,points:[`**Safe water and sanitation (Swachh Bharat, Jal Jeevan Mission)** prevent the parasitic and infective causes that dominate Indian chronic diarrhoea. [MoHFW]`,`**Drug review** at every NCD clinic visit - metformin and PPI-related diarrhoea is common among NP-NCD clinic attenders. [NP-NCD 2023]`,`**HIV testing** (ICTC under NACP) is part of the work-up; a positive result links the patient to ART, which itself cures most cryptosporidial diarrhoea. [NACO 2021]`,`**Counsel** on a food and stool diary, avoiding sorbitol sweeteners, reducing caffeine and alcohol, and gradual introduction of soluble fibre. [BSG 2018]`,`**Explain the plan** when the diagnosis is functional: name the condition, explain gut-brain interaction and discourage repeated tests. [Rome IV 2016]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2026)`,points:[`**AGA Clinical Practice Guideline on microscopic colitis (2022)**: budesonide is first line for induction and maintenance; recommends against mesalamine and bismuth as first choice and against probiotics; immunomodulators or biologics for budesonide-refractory disease. [AGA 2022]`,`**BSG/ACPGBI FIT guideline (2022)** endorsed **FIT 10 micrograms Hb/g or more** in symptomatic patients to prioritise colonoscopy, with safety-netting for FIT-negative patients with ongoing symptoms. [BSG-ACPGBI 2022]`,`**AGA 2019 guideline on functional diarrhoea/IBS-D** advises **calprotectin or lactoferrin, Giardia testing and coeliac serology**, and recommends against ESR or CRP alone and against routine ova and parasite testing in low-prevalence settings - not applicable to India, where parasite testing stays first line. [AGA 2019]`,`**BSG chronic diarrhoea guideline (2018)** remains the framework: test for BAD (SeHCAT or C4) rather than treat empirically where tests exist; empirical cholestyramine is acceptable where they do not. [BSG 2018]`,`**ACG coeliac guideline (2023)** reaffirms tTG-IgA with total IgA as the first test in chronic diarrhoea. [ACG 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic viva: **calculate the stool osmotic gap** and interpret it - 290 minus twice (Na + K); above 125 osmotic, below 50 secretory. [Harrison 22e]`,`Classic viva: **diarrhoea that persists on fasting** is secretory; diarrhoea that stops on fasting is osmotic (or the patient stopped the laxative). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake**: not taking colonic biopsies from a normal-looking colon, which misses microscopic colitis. [BSG 2018]`,`**Common mistake**: giving loperamide before excluding colitis and infection, risking toxic megacolon. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake**: labelling IBS-D without coeliac serology, calprotectin and blood counts. [AGA 2019]`,`Know the **small-bowel versus large-bowel diarrhoea** table and the four-mechanism classification - the answer frame for every long question. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Mechanistic classification of chronic diarrhoea`,columns:[`Type`,`Key clue`,`Stool tests`,`Main causes`],rows:[[`Osmotic`,`Stops on fasting; bloating, flatus`,`Osmotic gap above 125; pH below 6 in carbohydrate malabsorption`,`Lactase deficiency, Mg laxatives, lactulose, sorbitol, fructose`],[`Secretory`,`Large volume, watery, continues on fasting and at night`,`Osmotic gap below 50`,`Bile acid diarrhoea, microscopic colitis, drugs, stimulant laxatives, VIPoma, carcinoid, villous adenoma`],[`Inflammatory`,`Blood, mucus, tenesmus, fever, weight loss`,`Calprotectin high; RBC and pus cells`,`UC, Crohn disease, intestinal TB, amoebic colitis, CMV, radiation, cancer`],[`Fatty (steatorrhoea)`,`Pale, bulky, greasy, floating, weight loss`,`Faecal fat above 7 g/day; low elastase in pancreatic disease`,`Pancreatic insufficiency, coeliac disease, tropical sprue, giardia, SIBO, Whipple, short bowel`]]},{heading:`Small-bowel versus large-bowel diarrhoea`,columns:[`Feature`,`Small bowel`,`Large bowel`],rows:[[`Volume`,`Large, watery or fatty`,`Small, frequent`],[`Pain`,`Periumbilical cramps`,`Lower abdominal, relieved by defecation`],[`Urgency and tenesmus`,`Absent`,`Present`],[`Blood and mucus`,`Rare`,`Common`],[`Undigested food or oil`,`Common`,`Rare`],[`Weight loss and deficiencies`,`Prominent`,`Less prominent unless severe colitis`],[`Typical causes`,`Coeliac, giardia, sprue, Crohn ileitis`,`UC, Crohn colitis, amoebic colitis, cancer, microscopic colitis`]]},{heading:`Investigations and interpretation cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`Cut-off`,`Interpretation`,`Pitfall`],rows:[[`Faecal calprotectin`,`Below 50 / 50-200 / above 200-250 micrograms/g`,`IBD unlikely / repeat in 4-6 weeks / colonoscopy`,`Raised by NSAIDs, PPIs, infection, TB, cancer`],[`Faecal elastase-1`,`Below 200 / below 100 micrograms/g`,`Pancreatic insufficiency / severe insufficiency`,`Falsely low in watery stool`],[`Stool osmotic gap`,`Above 125 / below 50 mOsm/kg`,`Osmotic / secretory`,`Measured osmolality below 290 means diluted sample`],[`SeHCAT 7-day retention`,`Below 15% / 10% / 5%`,`Mild / moderate / severe BAD`,`Limited availability in India`],[`tTG-IgA with total IgA`,`Above laboratory ULN`,`Coeliac disease likely; confirm with duodenal biopsy`,`False negative in IgA deficiency or on gluten-free diet`],[`FIT`,`10 micrograms Hb/g or more`,`Urgent colonoscopy for cancer`,`Not a test for IBD`],[`Glucose hydrogen breath test`,`Rise of 20 ppm or more within 90 minutes`,`SIBO`,`Rapid transit gives false positives`]]},{heading:`Drugs used in chronic diarrhoea`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Loperamide`,`4 mg orally, then 2 mg after each loose stool; max 16 mg/day`,`As needed; lowest effective dose`,`Avoid in bloody diarrhoea, colitis, C. difficile; QT prolongation in overdose`],[`Cholestyramine`,`4 g orally once daily, up to 4 g four times daily`,`Titrate to response; long term if effective`,`Bloating, constipation; binds levothyroxine, warfarin, digoxin, fat-soluble vitamins - separate by 4 hours`],[`Budesonide (microscopic colitis)`,`9 mg orally once daily`,`6-8 weeks, then taper or 6 mg/day or less maintenance`,`Low systemic effect; watch bone density, glucose on long-term use`],[`Tinidazole / metronidazole (giardia)`,`Tinidazole 2 g single dose; metronidazole 400 mg three times daily`,`Single dose / 5-7 days`,`Metallic taste, disulfiram reaction with alcohol, avoid first trimester`],[`Pancreatin (PERT)`,`40,000-50,000 lipase units with meals, 25,000 with snacks`,`Lifelong; titrate to stool and weight`,`Take with food; fibrosing colonopathy at very high doses`],[`Octreotide`,`50-100 micrograms subcutaneously three times daily`,`Specialist; depot forms monthly`,`Gallstones, steatorrhoea, hyperglycaemia`]]},{heading:`Microscopic colitis subtypes`,columns:[`Feature`,`Collagenous colitis`,`Lymphocytic colitis`],rows:[[`Histology`,`Subepithelial collagen band above 10 micrometres`,`Above 20 intraepithelial lymphocytes per 100 epithelial cells`],[`Typical patient`,`Woman over 50`,`Woman over 50; associated with coeliac disease`],[`Drug triggers`,`PPIs, NSAIDs, SSRIs, statins`,`PPIs, NSAIDs, SSRIs, ranitidine`],[`Colonoscopy`,`Usually normal`,`Usually normal`],[`Treatment`,`Budesonide 9 mg/day 6-8 weeks`,`Budesonide 9 mg/day 6-8 weeks`]]}],redFlags:[`Age over 50 with new change in bowel habit, or FIT 10 micrograms Hb/g or more - urgent colonoscopy`,`Rectal bleeding with anaemia or a palpable abdominal or rectal mass`,`Unintentional weight loss over 5% in 6 months, fever or night sweats - TB, lymphoma, HIV, IBD`,`Nocturnal diarrhoea waking the patient from sleep`,`Dehydration with acute kidney injury or potassium below 3 mmol/L`,`Six or more bloody stools a day with tachycardia or fever - acute severe colitis`,`Family history of colorectal cancer or IBD with new symptoms`],pearls:[`Diarrhoea that persists on a 48-72 hour fast is secretory; diarrhoea that stops is osmotic.`,`Osmotic gap = 290 - 2 (Na + K): above 125 osmotic, below 50 secretory; measured osmolality below 290 means a diluted, factitious sample.`,`A normal-looking colon still needs biopsies: 2 right plus 2 left - or you miss microscopic colitis.`,`One-third of 'IBS-D' is bile acid diarrhoea - try cholestyramine if SeHCAT is not available.`,`Ileal resection under 100 cm gives bile acid diarrhoea (cholestyramine helps); over 100 cm gives fatty diarrhoea (cholestyramine worsens).`,`Calprotectin below 50 virtually excludes IBD in under-40s, but in India a high value can be TB or infection.`,`Microscopic colitis: older woman, PPI or NSAID or SSRI, normal colonoscopy, budesonide 9 mg.`,`Every Indian with chronic diarrhoea and weight loss gets an HIV test and three stool samples.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-chronic-diarrhoea-approach-q1`,stem:`A 45-year-old man has 3 months of watery diarrhoea. Stool Na is 40 mmol/L and K is 25 mmol/L. Measured stool osmolality is 295 mOsm/kg. What does this suggest?`,options:[`Secretory diarrhoea`,`Osmotic diarrhoea from magnesium laxatives`,`Factitious dilution of the stool sample with water`,`Steatorrhoea from pancreatic insufficiency`],answer:1,explanation:`The osmotic gap is 290 - 2 x (40 + 25) = 160 mOsm/kg, which is above 125 and indicates an osmotic diarrhoea such as magnesium or lactulose intake. A secretory diarrhoea would give a gap below 50 because electrolytes account for the osmolality. Factitious water dilution would make measured osmolality fall below 290, which it has not. Steatorrhoea is a fat-based diagnosis made on faecal fat or elastase, not on the gap.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-chronic-diarrhoea-approach-q2`,stem:`A 64-year-old woman on omeprazole and sertraline has 8 weeks of nocturnal watery diarrhoea. Hb, CRP and tTG-IgA are normal and colonoscopy is macroscopically normal. What is the most useful next step?`,options:[`Label as IBS-D and start amitriptyline`,`CT abdomen for neuroendocrine tumour`,`Start mesalamine 2.4 g/day empirically`,`Review random biopsies from the right and left colon`],answer:3,explanation:`An older woman on a PPI and an SSRI with nocturnal watery diarrhoea and a normal-looking colon has microscopic colitis until biopsies say otherwise, so the right and left colonic biopsies are the key. Nocturnal stools argue against IBS-D. A neuroendocrine tumour work-up is reserved for very high-volume secretory diarrhoea after common causes are excluded. AGA 2022 advises against mesalamine as first choice; budesonide is the drug once the diagnosis is made.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-chronic-diarrhoea-approach-q3`,stem:`A man had 60 cm of terminal ileum resected for Crohn disease and now has watery diarrhoea. Which treatment is most likely to help?`,options:[`Cholestyramine`,`A strict low-fat diet with medium-chain triglycerides alone`,`Pancreatic enzyme replacement`,`Long-term ciprofloxacin`],answer:0,explanation:`Resection of less than 100 cm of ileum causes bile acid diarrhoea: the liver compensates for lost bile acids so fat absorption is preserved, and unabsorbed bile acids drive colonic secretion, which cholestyramine binds. A low-fat MCT diet is the strategy for resections over 100 cm with steatorrhoea. Pancreatic enzymes treat exocrine insufficiency, which is not present. Antibiotics are for SIBO, not the primary problem here.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-chronic-diarrhoea-approach-q4`,stem:`A 28-year-old woman has 6 months of loose stools without blood or weight loss. Hb, CRP and tTG-IgA are normal. Faecal calprotectin is 32 micrograms/g. What is the best interpretation?`,options:[`Colonoscopy is needed to exclude IBD`,`Inflammatory bowel disease is unlikely; a functional diagnosis is reasonable`,`Repeat calprotectin in 4-6 weeks before deciding`,`Start budesonide for microscopic colitis`],answer:1,explanation:`Calprotectin below 50 micrograms/g in a patient under 40 has a high negative predictive value for IBD, so with normal bloods and serology a positive diagnosis of IBS-D or functional diarrhoea is appropriate. Colonoscopy is for values above 200-250 or alarm features. Repeat testing is reserved for the 50-200 grey zone. Microscopic colitis is unusual at 28 and is diagnosed on biopsy, not treated blindly.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-chronic-diarrhoea-approach-q5`,stem:`A nurse has large-volume watery diarrhoea with hypokalaemia. Colonoscopy shows brown pigmentation of the colonic mucosa with a pale-spotted pattern. Which test is most likely to confirm the diagnosis?`,options:[`Plasma VIP level`,`SeHCAT retention`,`Urinary laxative screen for anthraquinones and bisacodyl`,`Serum chromogranin A`],answer:2,explanation:`Melanosis coli (brown pigmentation from apoptotic cells in lamina propria macrophages) points to chronic anthraquinone laxative use, and a healthcare worker with unexplained secretory diarrhoea and hypokalaemia should have a urine and stool laxative screen. VIP and chromogranin testing for neuroendocrine tumours gives false positives and is low-yield before excluding laxatives. SeHCAT tests bile acid diarrhoea, which does not cause melanosis coli.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-chronic-diarrhoea-approach-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Define chronic diarrhoea. Classify it by mechanism and describe a stepwise approach to its evaluation and management in an adult attending a family medicine clinic in India.`,openingLines:[`Chronic diarrhoea is the passage of loose stools (Bristol 5-7), usually with increased frequency, for more than 4 weeks.`,`It is classified by mechanism into osmotic, secretory, inflammatory and fatty, and in India infections, intestinal TB, tropical sprue, coeliac disease and IBD must be considered alongside drugs, bile acid diarrhoea and microscopic colitis.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and classification`,points:[`Over 4 weeks; Bristol 5-7; exclude incontinence and overflow.`,`Osmotic, secretory, inflammatory, fatty - with examples of each.`,`Small-bowel versus large-bowel features.`]},{heading:`History and examination`,points:[`Nocturnal stools, blood, weight loss, fever, drugs, surgery, diet, travel, HIV risk.`,`Pallor, nutrition, thyroid, skin clues, abdominal mass, rectal examination.`,`Red flags: age over 50, bleeding, anaemia, mass, weight loss, family history.`]},{heading:`First-line investigations`,points:[`FBC, CRP, LFT, albumin, ferritin, B12, folate, TSH, tTG-IgA with total IgA, HIV.`,`Stool microscopy x 3 with modified ZN, Giardia antigen.`,`Faecal calprotectin (cut-offs 50 and 200), faecal elastase, FIT.`]},{heading:`Second-line investigations`,points:[`Ileocolonoscopy with ileal intubation and right and left colonic biopsies.`,`OGD with 4 duodenal biopsies.`,`SeHCAT or C4, breath tests, CT or MR enterography, osmotic gap, laxative screen, gut hormones only for high-volume secretory diarrhoea.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Treat cause: stop drug, gluten-free diet, tinidazole, cholestyramine, budesonide, PERT, ATT.`,`Supportive: ORS, micronutrient replacement, loperamide only when colitis excluded.`,`Admit for dehydration, AKI, hypokalaemia, severe colitis; refer for red flags.`]},{heading:`Prevention and follow-up`,points:[`Safe water and sanitation; drug review in NCD clinics; HIV linkage to ART.`,`Review at 4-6 weeks after any trial; monitor weight and stool frequency.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: chronic diarrhoea - red flags? - first-line bloods and stool tests - calprotectin branch - colonoscopy with biopsies / OGD with duodenal biopsies - specific diagnosis`,`Table of osmotic versus secretory versus inflammatory versus fatty diarrhoea`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and classification`,marks:2},{part:`History, examination and red flags`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations with cut-offs`,marks:3},{part:`Management and referral`,marks:3}],keywords:[`osmotic gap`,`calprotectin`,`elastase`,`colonoscopy biopsies`,`bile acid diarrhoea`,`microscopic colitis`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-chronic-diarrhoea-approach-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on bile acid diarrhoea.`,openingLines:[`Bile acid diarrhoea is chronic watery diarrhoea caused by excess bile acids entering the colon, where they stimulate secretion and motility.`,`It underlies about a quarter to a third of cases labelled IBS-D and responds to bile acid sequestrants.`],answer:[{heading:`Types and mechanism`,points:[`Type 1 ileal resection or Crohn ileitis; type 2 primary with low FGF19; type 3 post-cholecystectomy, SIBO, coeliac, pancreatic.`,`Excess hepatic synthesis because FGF19 feedback is lost.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Urgent watery diarrhoea, often early morning, after cholecystectomy or ileal disease.`,`No blood; normal colonoscopy; often mislabelled IBS-D.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`SeHCAT 7-day retention below 15% (below 10% moderate, below 5% severe).`,`Serum C4 raised, FGF19 low; empirical cholestyramine trial where tests are unavailable.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Cholestyramine 4 g once daily up to four times daily; colesevelam if tolerated and available.`,`Separate other drugs by 4 hours; monitor fat-soluble vitamins.`,`Resection over 100 cm: steatorrhoea, so sequestrants are avoided.`]}],mustDraw:[`Enterohepatic circulation of bile acids with FGF19 feedback loop`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and mechanism`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:1.5},{part:`Treatment`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`SeHCAT`,`C4`,`FGF19`,`cholestyramine`,`ileal resection`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - chapters on diarrhoea and malabsorption`,`Arasaradnam RP et al. Guidelines for the investigation of chronic diarrhoea in adults: British Society of Gastroenterology, 3rd edition, Gut 2018`,`AGA Clinical Practice Guideline on the Management of Microscopic Colitis, Gastroenterology 2022`,`AGA Clinical Practice Guideline on the Laboratory Evaluation of Functional Diarrhea and Diarrhea-Predominant IBS in Adults, Gastroenterology 2019`,`Monahan KJ et al. Faecal immunochemical testing (FIT) in patients with signs or symptoms of suspected colorectal cancer: BSG/ACPGBI guideline, Gut 2022`,`NICE Diagnostics Guidance DG11 (faecal calprotectin) and DG56 (FIT), updated 2023`,`ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows - Gastroenterology, 2023`]}),n.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-malabsorption-coeliac`,title:`Malabsorption and coeliac disease`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Malabsorption** is defective digestion or absorption of nutrients, and **coeliac disease** is a **gluten-triggered, HLA-DQ2/DQ8-restricted immune enteropathy** diagnosed by **tTG-IgA (with total IgA) plus duodenal villous atrophy (Marsh 3)** on a gluten-containing diet and treated with a **lifelong strict gluten-free diet**; in India it is common in the **wheat-eating north**, and must be told apart from **tropical sprue, SIBO, giardiasis and Whipple disease**.`,keywords:[`celiac disease`,`gluten-sensitive enteropathy`,`tTG-IgA`,`anti-tissue transglutaminase`,`endomysial antibody`,`deamidated gliadin peptide`,`HLA-DQ2`,`HLA-DQ8`,`Marsh-Oberhuber`,`gluten-free diet`,`refractory coeliac disease`,`dermatitis herpetiformis`,`tropical sprue`,`SIBO`,`hydrogen breath test`,`rifaximin`,`Whipple disease`,`Tropheryma whipplei`,`IgA deficiency`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Malabsorption** covers **maldigestion** (luminal failure - pancreatic enzymes, bile salts) and **mucosal malabsorption** (enterocyte failure) and **post-absorptive** failure (lymphatic obstruction). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Coeliac disease** is a chronic immune-mediated enteropathy of the small intestine triggered by dietary **gluten (gliadin of wheat, secalin of rye, hordein of barley)** in genetically predisposed people. [ACG 2023]`,`Global seroprevalence is about **1.4%** and biopsy-confirmed prevalence about **0.7%**; most cases remain undiagnosed - the coeliac iceberg. [Singh, Clin Gastroenterol Hepatol 2018]`,`In India, the multicentre ICMR-supported study found coeliac autoimmunity in about **1.23% in the north, 0.87% in the north-east and 0.10% in the south**, tracking wheat consumption and HLA-DQ2 frequency. [Ramakrishna, Am J Gastroenterol 2016]`,`An estimated **6-8 million Indians** have coeliac disease; the **Indian Task Force for Celiac Disease** consensus (2016) guides local practice. [ISG Task Force 2016]`,`Coeliac disease is about **1.5-2 times commoner in women** and can present at any age; in India the typical child presents with diarrhoea and short stature after wheat is introduced. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tropical sprue** is a post-infective malabsorption of residents of or visitors to the tropics (India, South-East Asia, Caribbean); its incidence in India has declined but it is still seen. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**HLA-DQ2 (about 90%) and HLA-DQ8 (about 5-10%)** are necessary but not sufficient - about 30-40% of the general population carry them. [ACG 2023]`,`**First-degree relatives** have a **10-15% risk**; monozygotic twin concordance is about 75%. [ACG 2023]`,`**Associated conditions** with higher prevalence: **type 1 diabetes (3-8%)**, autoimmune thyroid disease, **Down syndrome, Turner syndrome, Williams syndrome**, selective IgA deficiency, autoimmune hepatitis, PBC and microscopic colitis. [ACG 2023]`,`**Triggers for onset**: timing and quantity of gluten in infancy, enteric viral infections (rotavirus, reovirus) and possibly antibiotics; early or delayed gluten introduction does not prevent it. [ESPGHAN 2016]`,`**Tropical sprue** probably follows enteric infection with persistent bacterial overgrowth (coliforms) and folate deficiency in residents of endemic regions. [Harrison 22e]`,`**SIBO risk factors**: blind loops and surgery, strictures (Crohn disease), diverticula, **dysmotility (diabetes, scleroderma, opioids)**, hypochlorhydria (PPIs, atrophic gastritis), cirrhosis, CKD and immunodeficiency. [ACG SIBO 2020]`,`**Whipple disease** is caused by **Tropheryma whipplei**, typically in middle-aged white men, often with HLA-B27 and impaired macrophage function; it is very rare in India. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Gliadin is rich in **proline and glutamine**, which resist digestion and leave immunogenic peptides such as the **33-mer** in the lumen. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tissue transglutaminase (tTG, TG2)** deamidates glutamine to glutamate, making gliadin peptides bind **HLA-DQ2/DQ8 more tightly**, which activates CD4 T cells in the lamina propria. [Harrison 22e]`,`Activated T cells release **interferon-gamma**, and epithelial stress drives **IL-15**, which turns intraepithelial **CD8 lymphocytes into killers** of enterocytes - giving IEL rise, villous atrophy and crypt hyperplasia. [Harrison 22e]`,`B cells make antibodies against tTG itself (the autoantigen) and against deamidated gliadin - the basis of **tTG-IgA, EMA and DGP serology**, which fall when gluten is withdrawn. [ACG 2023]`,`Disease is **most severe proximally (duodenum and jejunum)**, so **iron, folate and calcium** - absorbed there - are the first deficiencies; B12 (ileum) is affected late. [Harrison 22e]`,`Loss of brush-border enzymes causes **secondary lactase deficiency**, which is why some patients need lactose restriction initially. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tropical sprue** affects the whole small bowel including the ileum, so **B12 and folate deficiency (megaloblastic anaemia)** are typical. [Harrison 22e]`,`**SIBO** damages by bacterial **deconjugation of bile acids (steatorrhoea), B12 consumption (low B12) and folate production (high folate)** plus mucosal injury. [ACG SIBO 2020]`,`**Whipple disease**: PAS-positive foamy macrophages packed with bacilli infiltrate the lamina propria and obstruct lymphatics, causing steatorrhoea, and spread to joints, heart and brain. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and grading`,points:[`**Marsh-Oberhuber**: **type 0** normal; **type 1** IEL above 25 per 100 enterocytes with normal villi; **type 2** IEL plus crypt hyperplasia; **type 3a/3b/3c** partial, subtotal and total villous atrophy. [Oberhuber 1999]`,`Simplified **Corazza-Villanacci** grading: grade A (non-atrophic, IEL raised), grade B1 (villus:crypt ratio below 3:1) and B2 (no detectable villi). [Corazza 2007]`,`**Clinical forms**: classical (malabsorption - diarrhoea, weight loss, growth failure), **non-classical** (anaemia, osteoporosis, raised transaminases, infertility, neuropathy), **subclinical** (screen-detected) and **potential** coeliac disease (positive serology, normal histology). [Oslo definitions 2013]`,`**Refractory coeliac disease (RCD)**: persistent or recurrent malabsorption and villous atrophy despite a **strict gluten-free diet for 12 months**, other causes excluded; **type 1** has normal IEL phenotype, **type 2** has **aberrant clonal IEL (loss of surface CD3 and CD8, above 20-25% by flow)**. [ESsCD 2019]`,`**Malabsorption by site**: luminal (pancreas, bile, SIBO), mucosal (coeliac, sprue, Whipple, giardia, Crohn, amyloid, lymphoma), post-mucosal (lymphangiectasia, lymphoma, Whipple). [Harrison 22e]`,`**SIBO breath test criteria (North American consensus)**: hydrogen rise of **20 ppm or more above baseline within 90 minutes**; methane of **10 ppm or more** at any time defines **intestinal methanogen overgrowth (IMO)**. [North American Consensus 2017/ACG 2020]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Classical malabsorption**: chronic diarrhoea, steatorrhoea, weight loss, abdominal distension and flatulence; in children **failure to thrive and short stature** after weaning to wheat. [Harrison 22e]`,`Many adults now present **without diarrhoea**: **iron-deficiency anaemia unresponsive to oral iron**, bloating mimicking IBS, fatigue or constipation. [ACG 2023]`,`**Extra-intestinal presentations**: osteoporosis or fractures in young adults, **dental enamel defects, aphthous ulcers, unexplained raised transaminases, infertility, recurrent miscarriage, delayed puberty, ataxia and peripheral neuropathy**. [ACG 2023]`,`**Dermatitis herpetiformis**: intensely itchy vesicles on **extensor surfaces (elbows, knees, buttocks, scalp)** - the skin form of coeliac disease. [ACG 2023]`,`**Symptoms by deficiency**: tetany and paraesthesia (calcium, magnesium), bone pain (vitamin D), easy bruising (vitamin K), night blindness (vitamin A), glossitis and neuropathy (B12, folate). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tropical sprue**: an Indian or traveller with diarrhoea, weight loss and **megaloblastic anaemia** beginning after an acute diarrhoeal illness. [Harrison 22e]`,`**SIBO**: bloating, flatulence and diarrhoea in someone with diabetes, scleroderma, previous surgery or long-term PPI; **Whipple disease**: arthralgia for years before diarrhoea, weight loss, fever and later dementia, ophthalmoplegia. [ACG 2020/Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **diet before testing** - many patients have already cut wheat, which makes serology and biopsy falsely normal. [ACG 2023]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Anthropometry**: low BMI, wasting, short stature and delayed puberty in adolescents. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pallor, koilonychia, angular stomatitis, glossitis** - iron, folate and B12 deficiency. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pedal oedema** from hypoalbuminaemia (protein-losing enteropathy or malnutrition). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chvostek and Trousseau signs**, proximal myopathy and bone tenderness from hypocalcaemia and osteomalacia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bruising and purpura** from vitamin K deficiency; **follicular hyperkeratosis and Bitot spots** from vitamin A deficiency. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dermatitis herpetiformis** and **dental enamel hypoplasia**; **Howell-Jolly bodies** on blood film signal hyposplenism. [ACG 2023]`,`**Whipple disease**: lymphadenopathy, skin hyperpigmentation, arthritis, and **oculomasticatory myorhythmia** (pathognomonic). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Other causes of villous atrophy (seronegative)**: tropical sprue, **giardiasis**, **olmesartan enteropathy**, common variable immunodeficiency, autoimmune enteropathy, Crohn disease, intestinal TB, HIV enteropathy, lymphoma, mycophenolate and checkpoint inhibitors. [ACG 2023]`,`**Tropical sprue versus coeliac disease**: sprue involves the ileum (low B12), serology is negative, and it responds to **folate plus tetracycline** rather than a gluten-free diet. [Harrison 22e]`,`**IBS versus coeliac disease**: test every patient with IBS-type symptoms for coeliac disease before labelling; coeliac prevalence among IBS patients is about 4-fold higher. [BSG IBS 2021]`,`**Non-coeliac gluten (wheat) sensitivity**: symptoms on gluten with **negative serology and normal histology**; often FODMAP (fructan) sensitivity - see the Food intolerance topic. [ACG 2023]`,`**Wheat allergy**: IgE-mediated, immediate urticaria or anaphylaxis, positive specific IgE - an allergy, not an enteropathy. [ACG 2023]`,`**Pancreatic exocrine insufficiency**: steatorrhoea with **low faecal elastase**, normal duodenal histology - see the Chronic pancreatitis topic. [UEG 2017]`,`**Intestinal lymphangiectasia**: lymphopenia, hypoalbuminaemia, chylous ascites and dilated lacteals on biopsy. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line`,points:[`**tTG-IgA plus total serum IgA** on a gluten-containing diet is the single best first test (sensitivity and specificity above 90%). [ACG 2023]`,`**Selective IgA deficiency** is 10-15 times commoner in coeliac disease (about 2-3% of patients); if IgA is low, use **IgG-based tests - DGP-IgG or tTG-IgG**. [ACG 2023]`,`Patients already on a gluten-free diet need a **gluten challenge of at least 3 g (about 2 slices of bread) a day for 6-8 weeks** before testing (2 weeks may suffice for biopsy). [ACG 2023]`,`**EMA-IgA** (endomysial antibody by immunofluorescence) is nearly 100% specific and is used to confirm high tTG values. [ACG 2023]`,`**Baseline tests at diagnosis**: FBC, ferritin, folate, B12, calcium, vitamin D, albumin, LFT, TSH, and blood film for hyposplenism. [ACG 2023]`,`**Faecal elastase** and stool for **Giardia** when steatorrhoea is present; stool microscopy is mandatory in India. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HLA-DQ2/DQ8 typing** is useful only to **exclude** coeliac disease (negative predictive value close to 100%), e.g. a patient already on a gluten-free diet or with equivocal histology. [ACG 2023]`]},{heading:`Investigations - advanced`,points:[`**Upper GI endoscopy with at least 4 biopsies from the second part of the duodenum and 1-2 from the bulb**, as changes are patchy. [ACG 2023]`,`Endoscopic clues - **scalloping, mosaic pattern, reduced folds** - are supportive but insensitive; biopsy regardless of appearance. [ACG 2023]`,`**No-biopsy diagnosis in children**: **tTG-IgA 10 times ULN or more plus positive EMA in a second sample** (ESPGHAN 2020); in adults most guidelines still advise biopsy, though selected adults may be diagnosed without it in some centres. [ESPGHAN 2020/ACG 2023]`,`**Glucose hydrogen breath test** for SIBO (more specific than lactulose); **jejunal aspirate culture above 10^3 CFU/mL** is the reference standard (ACG 2020 threshold). [ACG SIBO 2020]`,`**Whipple disease**: duodenal biopsy with **PAS-positive, diastase-resistant macrophages** and **T. whipplei PCR**; CSF PCR in all because CNS involvement may be silent. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Refractory coeliac disease work-up**: review diet with a dietitian, repeat serology and biopsy, **flow cytometry or TCR gene rearrangement** for aberrant IEL, **capsule endoscopy and CT or MR enterography** for ulcerative jejunitis and lymphoma. [ESsCD 2019]`,`**DXA** at diagnosis in adults with classical disease, older age or other risk factors for osteoporosis. [ACG 2023]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Lifelong strict gluten-free diet (GFD)**: avoid wheat (atta, maida, suji, dalia), barley and rye; **rice, maize, jowar, bajra, ragi, millets, pulses, potato and pure oats** are safe. [ACG 2023]`,`Safe gluten intake is **below 10 mg/day**; 'gluten-free' labelling means **below 20 ppm (20 mg/kg)** under Codex and **FSSAI** rules. [Codex/FSSAI]`,`**Oats** are uncontaminated in most coeliac patients but commonly cross-contaminated; introduce certified gluten-free oats after remission and watch for symptoms. [ACG 2023]`,`**Referral to an expert dietitian** is the most important intervention; teach label reading, hidden gluten (soy sauce, malt, medicines, chapati at functions) and cross-contamination in shared kitchens. [ACG 2023]`,`**Correct deficiencies**: oral iron (ferrous sulfate 200 mg once daily or alternate days), folic acid 5 mg daily, vitamin B12, calcium 1,000-1,200 mg/day and vitamin D; temporary lactose restriction if needed. [ACG 2023]`,`**Vaccinate** for pneumococcus (and consider Hib, meningococcus, influenza) because of functional **hyposplenism**. [BSG 2014]`,`**Dermatitis herpetiformis**: GFD plus **dapsone 50-100 mg daily** (check G6PD first) for rapid itch control. [ACG 2023]`,`**Tropical sprue**: **folic acid 5 mg daily plus tetracycline 250 mg four times daily (or doxycycline 100 mg twice daily) for 3-6 months**, with B12 replacement. [Harrison 22e]`,`**SIBO**: treat the cause and give **rifaximin 400-550 mg three times daily for 14 days** (alternatives: amoxicillin-clavulanate, metronidazole, ciprofloxacin, doxycycline); IMO may need rifaximin plus neomycin. [ACG SIBO 2020]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / specialist care`,points:[`**Coeliac crisis** (rare): severe diarrhoea, dehydration, hypokalaemia, hypoalbuminaemia and acidosis - admit for IV fluids, electrolytes and nutrition; steroids may be needed. [ACG 2023]`,`**Refeeding syndrome** risk is high in malnourished patients - start at **10-15 kcal/kg/day** with thiamine, phosphate, potassium and magnesium. [NICE CG32]`,`**RCD type 1**: strict dietary review, then **open-capsule budesonide 9 mg/day** (or prednisolone), with azathioprine for maintenance. [ESsCD 2019]`,`**RCD type 2**: specialist centre - cladribine, autologous stem cell transplantation or trials; it is a premalignant condition for **enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma (EATL)**. [ESsCD 2019]`,`**Whipple disease**: **ceftriaxone 2 g IV daily for 2 weeks**, then **co-trimoxazole 960 mg twice daily for 12 months**; alternative doxycycline plus hydroxychloroquine; watch for **IRIS**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Short bowel or intestinal failure**: parenteral nutrition and specialist rehabilitation (teduglutide where available). [ESPEN 2016]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Children**: screen with tTG-IgA; no-biopsy diagnosis allowed when tTG is 10 times ULN or more and EMA positive; growth monitoring on a GFD. [ESPGHAN 2020]`,`**Type 1 diabetes**: screen at diagnosis and periodically (e.g. every 1-2 years in children) because prevalence is 3-8%; unexplained hypoglycaemia may be coeliac disease. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Pregnancy**: untreated coeliac disease causes **infertility, miscarriage, low birth weight**; a GFD improves outcomes; give folic acid 5 mg periconceptionally. [ACG 2023]`,`**Elderly**: often presents with anaemia, osteoporosis or neuropathy; new symptoms despite a GFD raise RCD or lymphoma. [ACG 2023]`,`**Screen high-risk groups** (first-degree relatives, type 1 diabetes, autoimmune thyroid disease, Down and Turner syndromes, unexplained iron deficiency or raised transaminases) with tTG-IgA. [ACG 2023]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: tTG-IgA ELISA is widely available in Indian district laboratories; when endoscopy is not, refer seropositive patients rather than starting a GFD without confirmation. [ISG Task Force 2016]`,`**Cost and culture**: Indian staples outside the north (rice, millets) are naturally gluten-free - use them to make the diet affordable. [ISG Task Force 2016]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Osteopenia and osteoporosis** in up to half of adults at diagnosis; most improve within 1-2 years of a GFD. [ACG 2023]`,`**Malignancy**: **EATL**, small-bowel adenocarcinoma and oesophageal cancer are increased; risk falls toward normal after about 5 years of strict GFD. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hyposplenism** with risk of pneumococcal sepsis. [BSG 2014]`,`**Ulcerative jejunitis** and **collagenous sprue** are serious complications of refractory disease. [ESsCD 2019]`,`**RCD type 2** has a **5-year survival of about 40-58%**, with EATL developing in about a third to a half. [ESsCD 2019]`,`Most patients improve symptomatically within weeks of a GFD; **serology normalises in 6-12 months** but histological healing in adults may take **2 years or more**. [ACG 2023]`,`**Commonest cause of non-response is continued gluten exposure** (deliberate or inadvertent) - about 40-50% of non-responders. [ACG 2023]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer** positive serology for endoscopy before a GFD, and every patient to a dietitian. [ACG 2023]`,`**Follow-up at 3-6 months, then annually**: symptoms, weight, diet adherence, tTG-IgA, FBC, ferritin, LFT, TSH, vitamin D. [ACG 2023]`,`**tTG-IgA should fall by 6-12 months** and normalise; persistent positivity means ongoing gluten exposure. [ACG 2023]`,`Normal serology does not guarantee mucosal healing; **repeat biopsy** is advised for persistent symptoms or after about 2 years in selected adults. [ACG 2023]`,`**Non-responsive coeliac disease** (symptoms after 6-12 months GFD): check gluten exposure, then microscopic colitis, SIBO, lactose intolerance, pancreatic insufficiency, IBS, then RCD. [ACG 2023]`,`**Screen first-degree relatives** with tTG-IgA. [ACG 2023]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme links`,points:[`No primary prevention exists; **early or late gluten introduction and breastfeeding do not prevent coeliac disease** - introduce gluten between 4 and 12 months normally. [ESPGHAN 2016]`,`Counsel that the disease is **lifelong and the GFD is the treatment**; 'a little gluten' still damages the mucosa. [ACG 2023]`,`Link to **anaemia programmes (Anaemia Mukt Bharat)** - unexplained or refractory iron deficiency in adolescents and women should prompt coeliac testing. [MoHFW]`,`Under **RBSK**, children with growth faltering and chronic diarrhoea are referred to District Early Intervention Centres, where coeliac testing should be part of the work-up. [RBSK]`,`Encourage patient support groups (e.g. Celiac Society of India) and **FSSAI gluten-free labelled** products. [FSSAI]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**ACG Clinical Guideline on Celiac Disease (2023)**: tTG-IgA with total IgA first; biopsy recommended in adults; recommends against routine follow-up biopsy in all but suggests it for selected patients; does not recommend gluten-detection devices or enzyme supplements. [ACG 2023]`,`**ESPGHAN 2020** extended the **no-biopsy pathway** to asymptomatic children with tTG 10 times ULN and positive EMA, without needing HLA typing. [ESPGHAN 2020]`,`**European Society for the Study of Coeliac Disease (ESsCD) 2019** guideline defines refractory coeliac disease types and their work-up. [ESsCD 2019]`,`**Drug pipeline**: larazotide failed in phase 3 (2022); glutenases (latiglutenase, TAK-062), the TG2 inhibitor **ZED1227** and tolerance therapies are investigational - none replaces a GFD. [ACG 2023]`,`**ACG SIBO guideline (2020)** with the 2017 North American breath-test consensus remains current: glucose or lactulose breath test, rifaximin first line, methane-dominant IMO as a separate entity. [ACG 2020]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **why measure total IgA with tTG-IgA?** - IgA deficiency (2-3% of coeliacs) gives a false-negative tTG-IgA. [ACG 2023]`,`Classic question: **Marsh classification** - draw it with IEL counts and villous changes. [Oberhuber 1999]`,`**Common mistake**: starting a gluten-free diet before serology and biopsy, which ruins the diagnosis. [ACG 2023]`,`**Common mistake**: treating villous atrophy as coeliac disease without serology - in India think of tropical sprue, giardia and TB. [Harrison 22e]`,`**High folate with low B12** suggests SIBO; **low folate with low B12** suggests tropical sprue. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Whipple disease** - PAS-positive macrophages, arthralgia, dementia, oculomasticatory myorhythmia, 1 year of co-trimoxazole. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Marsh-Oberhuber classification of coeliac histology`,columns:[`Marsh type`,`Intraepithelial lymphocytes`,`Crypts`,`Villi`],rows:[[`0`,`Normal (under 25 per 100)`,`Normal`,`Normal`],[`1 (infiltrative)`,`Above 25 per 100 enterocytes`,`Normal`,`Normal`],[`2 (hyperplastic)`,`Raised`,`Hyperplastic`,`Normal`],[`3a`,`Raised`,`Hyperplastic`,`Partial villous atrophy`],[`3b`,`Raised`,`Hyperplastic`,`Subtotal villous atrophy`],[`3c`,`Raised`,`Hyperplastic`,`Total villous atrophy (flat)`]]},{heading:`Serological and genetic tests in coeliac disease`,columns:[`Test`,`Use`,`Strength`,`Limitation`],rows:[[`tTG-IgA`,`First-line screen`,`Sensitivity and specificity above 90%; cheap ELISA`,`False negative in IgA deficiency and on GFD`],[`Total serum IgA`,`Always with tTG-IgA`,`Detects IgA deficiency`,`Not diagnostic itself`],[`EMA-IgA`,`Confirm high tTG; no-biopsy pathway`,`Specificity near 100%`,`Operator-dependent immunofluorescence`],[`DGP-IgG / tTG-IgG`,`IgA-deficient patients`,`Works without IgA`,`Lower specificity`],[`HLA-DQ2/DQ8`,`Exclude coeliac disease`,`Negative predictive value near 100%`,`30-40% of population positive, so useless to confirm`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of malabsorption with villous changes`,columns:[`Condition`,`Key clue`,`Diagnostic test`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`Coeliac disease`,`Wheat-eating north Indian, iron deficiency, autoimmunity`,`tTG-IgA and duodenal biopsy Marsh 3`,`Lifelong gluten-free diet`],[`Tropical sprue`,`Onset after acute diarrhoea in tropics; megaloblastic anaemia`,`Seronegative villous atrophy, low B12 and folate`,`Folate 5 mg + tetracycline 3-6 months`],[`SIBO`,`Diabetes, scleroderma, surgery, PPI; low B12, high folate`,`Glucose breath test rise 20 ppm or more`,`Rifaximin 14 days; treat cause`],[`Giardiasis`,`Bloating, foul flatus, steatorrhoea`,`Stool antigen, trophozoites on biopsy`,`Tinidazole 2 g single dose`],[`Whipple disease`,`Middle-aged man, arthralgia, fever, CNS signs`,`PAS-positive macrophages, T. whipplei PCR`,`Ceftriaxone then co-trimoxazole 12 months`],[`Olmesartan enteropathy`,`Severe diarrhoea on olmesartan`,`Seronegative villous atrophy`,`Stop olmesartan`]]},{heading:`Drugs in malabsorptive disorders`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Folic acid (sprue, coeliac)`,`5 mg orally once daily`,`3-6 months in sprue; until replete`,`Give B12 first if deficient to avoid neurological worsening`],[`Tetracycline / doxycycline (sprue)`,`250 mg orally four times daily / 100 mg twice daily`,`3-6 months`,`Avoid in pregnancy and under 8 years; photosensitivity`],[`Rifaximin (SIBO)`,`400-550 mg orally three times daily`,`14 days; repeat for relapse`,`Minimal absorption; costly`],[`Dapsone (dermatitis herpetiformis)`,`50-100 mg orally daily`,`Until GFD controls rash (1-2 years)`,`Haemolysis in G6PD deficiency, methaemoglobinaemia, agranulocytosis`],[`Budesonide (RCD type 1)`,`9 mg orally daily, capsule opened`,`Months; specialist`,`Adrenal suppression, bone loss`],[`Ceftriaxone then co-trimoxazole (Whipple)`,`2 g IV daily; then 960 mg orally twice daily`,`2 weeks; then 12 months`,`Allergy, cytopenias, hyperkalaemia; IRIS`]]}],redFlags:[`Weight loss, fever or abdominal pain despite strict gluten-free diet - refractory coeliac disease or lymphoma`,`Severe diarrhoea with dehydration, hypokalaemia or hypoalbuminaemia - coeliac crisis`,`Tetany, seizures or symptomatic hypocalcaemia`,`GI bleeding, obstruction or perforation in known coeliac disease - ulcerative jejunitis or EATL`,`Neurological signs (ataxia, dementia, ophthalmoplegia) with malabsorption - Whipple disease or severe deficiency`,`BMI below 16 or rapid weight loss - risk of refeeding syndrome`],pearls:[`Always order total IgA with tTG-IgA - IgA deficiency is 10-15 times commoner in coeliac disease.`,`Test before gluten-free diet: on GFD, serology and biopsy turn normal; gluten challenge 3 g/day for 6-8 weeks.`,`HLA-DQ2/DQ8 is for ruling out, never ruling in.`,`4 biopsies from D2 plus 1-2 from the bulb - the lesion is patchy.`,`Non-response to GFD is gluten exposure until proved otherwise.`,`SIBO: low B12, high folate. Tropical sprue: low B12, low folate. Coeliac: low iron and folate first.`,`Safe Indian cereals: rice, jowar, bajra, ragi, maize, millets; avoid atta, maida, suji, dalia.`,`Whipple: PAS-positive macrophages and oculomasticatory myorhythmia.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-malabsorption-coeliac-q1`,stem:`A 30-year-old woman from Punjab has iron deficiency anaemia not responding to oral iron. tTG-IgA is negative. Which additional test is most important before excluding coeliac disease?`,options:[`HLA-DQ2/DQ8 typing`,`Serum total IgA level`,`Anti-gliadin IgA antibody`,`Stool for occult blood`],answer:1,explanation:`Selective IgA deficiency is 10-15 times commoner in coeliac disease and makes tTG-IgA falsely negative, so total IgA must be measured; if low, IgG-based DGP or tTG tests are used. HLA typing helps only to exclude coeliac disease and is not the next routine step. Native anti-gliadin antibodies are obsolete because of poor accuracy. Occult blood testing addresses a different cause of anaemia, not the reliability of serology.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-malabsorption-coeliac-q2`,stem:`A patient with biopsy-proven coeliac disease has persistent diarrhoea after 9 months of a 'gluten-free diet'. tTG-IgA remains raised. What is the most likely cause?`,options:[`Refractory coeliac disease type 2`,`Enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma`,`Ongoing gluten ingestion`,`Microscopic colitis`],answer:2,explanation:`Persistently raised tTG-IgA on a supposed GFD signals continued gluten exposure, which is the commonest cause of non-responsive coeliac disease (about 40-50%). Refractory coeliac disease requires 12 months of a strict diet with exclusion of gluten exposure, and serology is usually negative. Lymphoma is rare and comes after excluding common causes. Microscopic colitis causes non-response but would not keep tTG raised.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-malabsorption-coeliac-q3`,stem:`A 55-year-old man with long-standing diabetic autonomic neuropathy has bloating and diarrhoea. Serum B12 is low and serum folate is high. Which test is most appropriate?`,options:[`Glucose hydrogen breath test`,`Duodenal biopsy for Marsh grading`,`Faecal elastase`,`Schilling test`],answer:0,explanation:`Low B12 with high folate in a patient with dysmotility suggests small intestinal bacterial overgrowth: bacteria consume B12 and synthesise folate. The glucose hydrogen breath test (rise of 20 ppm or more within 90 minutes) is the practical diagnostic test. Coeliac disease and pancreatic insufficiency do not produce raised folate. The Schilling test is obsolete and not available.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-malabsorption-coeliac-q4`,stem:`An 8-year-old child has growth faltering. tTG-IgA is 14 times the upper limit of normal and EMA is positive on a second sample. According to ESPGHAN 2020, what is the next step?`,options:[`Duodenal biopsy to confirm Marsh 3 before diet`,`HLA typing before diagnosis`,`Repeat tTG-IgA after 3 months on normal diet`,`Diagnose coeliac disease and start a gluten-free diet without biopsy`],answer:3,explanation:`ESPGHAN 2020 permits a no-biopsy diagnosis in children when tTG-IgA is at least 10 times ULN and EMA is positive in a separate sample, after discussion with the family; HLA typing is no longer required. Biopsy remains necessary only when these criteria are not met. Repeating serology on gluten delays treatment without adding certainty.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-malabsorption-coeliac-q5`,stem:`A 50-year-old man has arthralgia for 6 years, weight loss, diarrhoea, fever and now memory loss. Duodenal biopsy shows foamy macrophages that are PAS-positive. What is the recommended treatment?`,options:[`Gluten-free diet`,`Rifaximin for 14 days`,`IV ceftriaxone for 2 weeks followed by co-trimoxazole for 12 months`,`Tetracycline with folic acid for 6 months`],answer:2,explanation:`Arthralgia preceding diarrhoea, weight loss, fever, CNS involvement and PAS-positive macrophages define Whipple disease due to Tropheryma whipplei, treated with a blood-brain-barrier-penetrating induction (ceftriaxone 2 g daily for 2 weeks) and then co-trimoxazole for a year. A GFD treats coeliac disease, rifaximin treats SIBO and tetracycline plus folate treats tropical sprue, none of which explain PAS-positive macrophages.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-malabsorption-coeliac-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Describe the pathogenesis, clinical presentation, diagnosis and management of coeliac disease. Add a note on refractory coeliac disease.`,openingLines:[`Coeliac disease is a chronic immune-mediated enteropathy triggered by dietary gluten in HLA-DQ2/DQ8-positive individuals, causing villous atrophy of the proximal small bowel.`,`It affects about 1% of north Indians, is diagnosed by tTG-IgA with total IgA plus duodenal biopsy on a gluten diet, and is treated by a lifelong gluten-free diet.`],answer:[{heading:`Epidemiology and genetics`,points:[`Global 0.7-1.4%; India 1.23% north, 0.1% south.`,`HLA-DQ2/DQ8; first-degree relatives 10-15%; associations T1DM, thyroid, Down, IgA deficiency.`]},{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Gliadin peptides resist digestion; tTG deamidates them; bind DQ2/DQ8; CD4 T-cell IFN-gamma.`,`IL-15 activates IEL; villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia; tTG autoantibodies.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Classical: diarrhoea, weight loss, failure to thrive.`,`Non-classical: iron deficiency, osteoporosis, raised ALT, infertility, neuropathy, DH.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`tTG-IgA + total IgA on gluten; EMA confirms; DGP-IgG in IgA deficiency.`,`OGD with 4 D2 + 1-2 bulb biopsies; Marsh classification.`,`No-biopsy pathway in children (ESPGHAN 2020); HLA to exclude.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Lifelong GFD below 10 mg gluten/day; dietitian; safe Indian cereals.`,`Iron, folate, calcium, vitamin D; pneumococcal vaccine; DXA; dapsone for DH.`,`Follow-up tTG at 6-12 months then yearly.`]},{heading:`Refractory coeliac disease`,points:[`Villous atrophy despite 12 months strict GFD; exclude gluten exposure first.`,`Type 1 normal IEL - budesonide; type 2 aberrant clonal IEL - EATL risk, specialist therapy.`]}],mustDraw:[`Marsh-Oberhuber classification table`,`Diagnostic flow: tTG-IgA + total IgA - biopsy - GFD - follow-up`],markSplit:[{part:`Pathogenesis`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:3},{part:`Management and RCD`,marks:3}],keywords:[`tTG-IgA`,`Marsh`,`HLA-DQ2`,`gluten-free diet`,`refractory coeliac`,`EATL`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-malabsorption-coeliac-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Differentiate coeliac disease from tropical sprue.`,openingLines:[`Both cause malabsorption with villous atrophy, but coeliac disease is a gluten-driven autoimmune enteropathy while tropical sprue is a post-infective enteropathy of the tropics.`,`The discriminators are serology, the extent of small-bowel involvement and the response to diet versus antibiotics.`],answer:[{heading:`Cause and epidemiology`,points:[`Coeliac: gluten in HLA-DQ2/DQ8 carriers; north India, wheat eaters, any age.`,`Tropical sprue: post-infective bacterial overgrowth in residents of or visitors to the tropics.`]},{heading:`Site and deficiencies`,points:[`Coeliac: proximal small bowel - iron, folate and calcium deficiency first.`,`Sprue: whole small bowel including ileum - B12 and folate deficiency, megaloblastic anaemia.`]},{heading:`Serology and histology`,points:[`Coeliac: tTG-IgA and EMA positive; Marsh 3 with IEL above 25 per 100.`,`Sprue: serology negative; milder partial villous atrophy along the whole small bowel.`]},{heading:`Treatment and associations`,points:[`Coeliac: lifelong gluten-free diet; associations autoimmunity, dermatitis herpetiformis, EATL.`,`Sprue: folic acid 5 mg plus tetracycline 3-6 months; no specific associations; cure expected.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column comparison table`],markSplit:[{part:`Cause and epidemiology`,marks:1},{part:`Site and deficiencies`,marks:1},{part:`Serology and histology`,marks:1.5},{part:`Treatment and associations`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`tropical sprue`,`tTG-IgA`,`folate`,`tetracycline`,`villous atrophy`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Disorders of absorption`,`Rubio-Tapia A et al. ACG Clinical Guidelines: Diagnosis and Management of Celiac Disease, Am J Gastroenterol 2023`,`Husby S et al. ESPGHAN Guidelines for Diagnosing Coeliac Disease 2020, J Pediatr Gastroenterol Nutr 2020`,`Al-Toma A et al. European Society for the Study of Coeliac Disease (ESsCD) guideline, United European Gastroenterol J 2019`,`Pimentel M et al. ACG Clinical Guideline: Small Intestinal Bacterial Overgrowth, Am J Gastroenterol 2020`,`Makharia GK et al. Indian Task Force for Celiac Disease: current status of celiac disease in India, 2016`,`Ramakrishna BS et al. Prevalence of adult celiac disease in India: regional variations, Am J Gastroenterol 2016`]}),n.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-ulcerative-colitis`,title:`Ulcerative colitis`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Ulcerative colitis (UC)** is a **chronic relapsing, continuous mucosal inflammation that begins in the rectum and extends proximally**, presenting with **bloody diarrhoea, urgency and tenesmus**; it is staged by **Montreal extent (E1-E3)** and graded by **Mayo/Truelove-Witts**, treated **5-ASA first for mild-moderate disease, steroids for flares, and thiopurines, biologics, JAK or S1P modulators for steroid-dependent or moderate-severe disease**, and **acute severe UC is an emergency managed with IV steroids, day-3 assessment and rescue infliximab, ciclosporin or colectomy**.`,keywords:[`UC`,`inflammatory bowel disease`,`IBD`,`Montreal classification`,`Mayo score`,`Truelove and Witts`,`UCEIS`,`mesalamine`,`mesalazine`,`sulfasalazine`,`azathioprine`,`infliximab`,`vedolizumab`,`tofacitinib`,`upadacitinib`,`ozanimod`,`acute severe ulcerative colitis`,`toxic megacolon`,`proctocolectomy ileal pouch`,`ECCO 2022`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**UC** is chronic idiopathic inflammation confined to the **colonic mucosa**, starting in the **rectum** and extending proximally in a **continuous, circumferential** pattern without skip lesions. [ECCO 2022]`,`Incidence peaks at **15-30 years** with a smaller second peak at 50-70 years; men and women are affected about equally. [Harrison 22e]`,`IBD affects about **0.3% of people in Western countries** and is rising rapidly in newly industrialised countries including India. [Ng, Lancet 2017]`,`India is estimated to have the **second-largest IBD population in the world (over 1 million patients)**, with UC more common than Crohn disease. [Kedia and Ahuja, Inflamm Intest Dis 2017]`,`A population study from **Punjab** found a UC prevalence of **44.3 per 100,000**, comparable to some Western figures. [Sood, Gut 2003]`,`Indian UC is predominantly **left-sided or extensive**, often diagnosed after a long delay because bloody diarrhoea is first treated as amoebic or bacillary dysentery. [ISG]`,`About **10-15% of UC patients need colectomy within 10 years**, and roughly 15-25% experience an episode of acute severe UC. [ECCO 2022]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Genetics**: about 8-14% have a family history; over 240 IBD loci are known, many shared with Crohn disease (IL23R, HLA region); UC-specific loci affect epithelial barrier function. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Smoking is protective in UC** (ex-smokers have higher risk and more severe disease), whereas it worsens Crohn disease - but never advise smoking. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Appendicectomy before age 20** for appendicitis lowers UC risk. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Environmental triggers**: westernised diet (high fat and processed food, low fibre), urbanisation, antibiotics in childhood, and NSAIDs (trigger flares). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Gut microbiome**: reduced diversity with fewer butyrate-producing Firmicutes - the basis of the hygiene hypothesis and of faecal transplant trials. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Flare triggers**: NSAIDs, infections (C. difficile, CMV, amoebiasis), stopping maintenance 5-ASA, stress and antibiotics. [ECCO 2022]`,`**PSC association**: 70-80% of patients with PSC have IBD, usually UC; PSC-UC is often extensive, rectal-sparing and carries high colorectal cancer risk. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`A **defective epithelial barrier and mucus layer** lets luminal bacteria contact the mucosal immune system, triggering an exaggerated response in genetically susceptible hosts. [Harrison 22e]`,`UC has an **atypical Th2 profile with IL-13 from NK-T cells** that damages epithelium, plus IL-23/Th17 and **TNF-alpha** driven inflammation - the targets of anti-TNF and anti-IL-23 drugs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Leukocyte trafficking** to the gut needs alpha4-beta7 integrin binding MAdCAM-1 - blocked by **vedolizumab**; lymphocyte exit from lymph nodes needs S1P receptors - blocked by **ozanimod and etrasimod**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Cytokine signalling runs through the **JAK-STAT pathway**, explaining why **tofacitinib and upadacitinib** work across several cytokines. [Harrison 22e]`,`Inflammation is **mucosal and submucosal only** - so fistulae, strictures and granulomas are not features; **crypt abscesses, crypt distortion and basal plasmacytosis** are. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bloody diarrhoea and urgency** come from friable ulcerated mucosa and a **non-compliant inflamed rectum**; tenesmus from rectal inflammation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**5-ASA** acts topically on the mucosa (PPAR-gamma activation, inhibition of NF-kappa B and prostaglandins), so delivery to the inflamed segment matters - suppositories for rectum, enemas to the splenic flexure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Toxic megacolon** occurs when transmural extension of severe inflammation paralyses the muscle layer - hypokalaemia, opioids and anticholinergics precipitate it. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pseudopolyps** are islands of regenerating mucosa between ulcers; long-standing inflammation drives **dysplasia and colitis-associated cancer** without the classic adenoma sequence. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and severity grading`,points:[`**Montreal extent**: **E1 proctitis** (rectum only), **E2 left-sided** (distal to the splenic flexure), **E3 extensive** (proximal to the splenic flexure, pancolitis). [Montreal 2005]`,`**Montreal severity**: S0 remission, S1 mild (4 or fewer stools/day), S2 moderate (more than 4, minimal systemic toxicity), S3 severe (6 or more bloody stools with systemic toxicity). [Montreal 2005]`,`**Truelove and Witts acute severe UC**: **6 or more bloody stools per day plus at least one of pulse above 90/min, temperature above 37.8 C, Hb below 10.5 g/dL, ESR above 30 mm/h (or CRP above 30 mg/L)**. [Truelove-Witts 1955/ECCO 2022]`,`**Mayo score (0-12)**: stool frequency, rectal bleeding, endoscopic findings and physician global assessment, each 0-3; **remission is 2 or less with no subscore above 1**. [Schroeder 1987]`,`**Endoscopic Mayo subscore**: 0 normal; 1 erythema, decreased vascular pattern, mild friability; 2 marked erythema, absent vascular pattern, friability, erosions; 3 spontaneous bleeding, ulceration. [Schroeder 1987]`,`**UCEIS (0-8)** scores vascular pattern, bleeding and erosions/ulcers and is more reproducible; the modified Mayo score (0-9, without physician assessment) is used in regulatory trials. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Disease course types**: steroid-dependent (cannot taper prednisolone below 10 mg within 3 months, or relapse within 3 months of stopping), steroid-refractory (active despite 0.75-1 mg/kg/day for 4 weeks). [ECCO 2022]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Bloody diarrhoea with mucus** is the hallmark; **urgency, tenesmus and nocturnal defecation** reflect rectal inflammation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Proctitis** may present with **rectal bleeding and constipation** (proximal constipation) - often misdiagnosed as haemorrhoids. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Severe colitis**: fever, weight loss, anorexia, abdominal pain and more than 6 bloody stools a day. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Extra-intestinal manifestations**: peripheral arthritis, sacroiliitis, ankylosing spondylitis, **episcleritis, uveitis, erythema nodosum, pyoderma gangrenosum**, PSC, and venous thromboembolism. [ECCO EIM 2024]`,`**Type 1 peripheral arthropathy** (pauciarticular, large joints) **parallels bowel activity**; type 2 (polyarticular, small joints), uveitis, axial disease and PSC run independently. [ECCO 2022]`,`Ask about **recent antibiotics, travel, NSAIDs and sick contacts** - infection must be excluded at every flare. [ECCO 2022]`,`Ask **how long the bloody diarrhoea has lasted** - over 4-6 weeks makes infection unlikely and IBD likely. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Vital signs** define severity: tachycardia above 90, fever above 37.8 C and postural hypotension mark acute severe UC. [Truelove-Witts]`,`**Pallor** (iron deficiency and chronic disease anaemia), weight loss and **oedema** (hypoalbuminaemia). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Abdominal tenderness and distension** with reduced bowel sounds in severe disease suggest **toxic megacolon or perforation** - steroids can mask peritonism. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Rectal examination**: blood and mucus; perianal disease is unusual in UC and points to Crohn disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Eyes**: red eye with pain and photophobia (uveitis - urgent ophthalmology) versus painless red eye (episcleritis). [ECCO 2022]`,`**Skin**: tender red nodules on the shins (**erythema nodosum**, follows activity); painful ulcer with undermined violaceous edge (**pyoderma gangrenosum**, may follow trauma - pathergy). [ECCO 2022]`,`**Mouth and joints**: aphthous ulcers, swollen knees or ankles, sacroiliac tenderness. [ECCO 2022]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Infective colitis** - shigella, campylobacter, salmonella, **C. difficile**, **amoebic colitis**, **CMV** - must be excluded at diagnosis and at every flare. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Amoebic colitis** in India mimics UC (flask-shaped ulcers, often caecal); **steroids given for presumed UC can cause fulminant amoebic colitis** - always do stool microscopy and, where possible, biopsy and serology. [ISG]`,`**Crohn colitis**: rectal sparing, skip lesions, deep linear ulcers, perianal disease, ileal involvement and granulomas. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Intestinal TB**: ileocaecal predominance, transverse ulcers, necrotic nodes, caseating granulomas - see the Crohn disease topic for the ITB-CD discriminators. [ISG]`,`**Ischaemic colitis**: older patient, sudden onset, watershed (splenic flexure) and rectal sparing. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Radiation proctitis, NSAID colitis, diverticular colitis, solitary rectal ulcer and colorectal cancer**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Haemorrhoids** - commonly blamed for the rectal bleeding of proctitis; examine and do sigmoidoscopy if bleeding is with mucus or diarrhoea. [ECCO 2022]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Bloods**: FBC, CRP, ESR, albumin, urea, electrolytes, LFT (PSC), ferritin; CRP is often normal in mild UC. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Stool**: culture, **C. difficile toxin/PCR**, microscopy for **E. histolytica** trophozoites (stool antigen or PCR where available), and **faecal calprotectin** as a baseline marker. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Ileocolonoscopy with biopsies** (at least 2 from each of 5 sites plus ileum and rectum) confirms diagnosis and extent; in severe colitis do an **unprepared flexible sigmoidoscopy** only. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Histology**: **crypt architectural distortion, basal plasmacytosis, crypt abscesses**, mucin depletion, Paneth cell metaplasia distal to the hepatic flexure; no granulomas. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Plain abdominal X-ray** in severe colitis: **transverse colon above 5.5 cm means toxic megacolon**; mucosal islands and small-bowel gas predict colectomy. [ECCO 2022]`,`**CMV**: immunohistochemistry or PCR on colonic biopsies in steroid-refractory severe colitis. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Pre-advanced-therapy screen (mandatory in India)**: **IGRA or tuberculin test plus chest X-ray**, HBsAg, anti-HBc, anti-HCV, HIV, varicella IgG; lipids and LFT before JAK; ECG and eye check before S1P modulators. [ECCO 2022/ISG]`,`**Calprotectin** tracks response: values **below about 150-250 micrograms/g** suggest mucosal healing; a rise predicts relapse. [STRIDE-II 2021]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - mild to moderate disease`,points:[`**Proctitis (E1)**: **mesalamine 1 g suppository once daily** at night - more effective than oral 5-ASA; add oral 5-ASA or topical steroid if not settling. [ECCO 2022/ACG 2025]`,`**Left-sided (E2) and extensive (E3) mild-moderate**: **oral mesalamine 2.4-4.8 g/day** (once-daily dosing works as well as divided) **plus mesalamine enema 1 g daily**; combination beats either alone. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Sulfasalazine 2-4 g/day** in divided doses is a cheap alternative (useful with arthropathy); give **folic acid** and check FBC - causes reversible oligospermia, rash, haemolysis. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Steroids if 5-ASA fails in 2-4 weeks**: **prednisolone 40 mg once daily, tapering by 5 mg a week over about 8 weeks**; or **budesonide MMX 9 mg daily for 8 weeks** for mild-moderate disease. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Maintenance**: **mesalamine at least 2 g/day orally** (or 1 g suppository three times a week for proctitis) lifelong - also lowers colorectal cancer risk. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Thiopurines for steroid dependence**: **azathioprine 2-2.5 mg/kg/day** or 6-mercaptopurine 1-1.5 mg/kg/day after **TPMT (and NUDT15 in South Asians)** testing; they take 12-16 weeks to work and are for maintenance, not induction. [ECCO 2022/ACG 2025]`,`**Thiopurine monitoring**: FBC and LFT weekly for 4 weeks, then monthly for 3 months, then every 3 months - myelosuppression, hepatotoxicity, pancreatitis (early, idiosyncratic), and lymphoma and non-melanoma skin cancer long term. [BSG 2019]`,`**Supportive**: iron replacement (IV ferric carboxymaltose if Hb below 10 g/dL or intolerance), calcium and vitamin D with steroids, **avoid NSAIDs, opioids and antidiarrhoeals in active colitis**. [ECCO 2022]`]},{heading:`Moderate to severe disease - advanced therapies`,points:[`**Anti-TNF**: **infliximab 5 mg/kg IV at weeks 0, 2, 6 then every 8 weeks** (biosimilars make it affordable in India), adalimumab 160/80 mg SC then 40 mg every 2 weeks, golimumab; combination with azathioprine improves response (UC SUCCESS). [ECCO 2022]`,`**Anti-integrin**: **vedolizumab 300 mg IV at weeks 0, 2, 6 then every 8 weeks** - gut-selective, so safest for infection and TB risk; slower onset. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Anti-IL-12/23 and anti-IL-23**: **ustekinumab 6 mg/kg IV then 90 mg SC every 8-12 weeks**; mirikizumab, risankizumab and guselkumab (IL-23 p19) approved for UC 2023-2025. [ACG 2025]`,`**JAK inhibitors**: **tofacitinib 10 mg twice daily for 8 weeks then 5-10 mg twice daily**; **upadacitinib 45 mg once daily for 8 weeks then 15-30 mg daily** - rapid oral options; risks of **herpes zoster, VTE, MACE, lipid rise**; avoid in over-65s, smokers and CV risk unless no alternative. [ECCO 2022/FDA 2021]`,`**S1P receptor modulators**: **ozanimod 0.23 mg days 1-4, 0.46 mg days 5-7, then 0.92 mg daily**; **etrasimod 2 mg daily** - bradycardia and heart block (baseline ECG), macular oedema (eye check in diabetics, uveitis), raised liver enzymes. [ACG 2025]`,`**Generic tofacitinib** is far cheaper than biologics in India and is widely used as first advanced therapy after TB and hepatitis B screening. [ISG]`,`**Latent TB** found on screening: start **isoniazid-rifapentine (3HP) or isoniazid 6 months** and delay anti-TNF or JAK for **at least 3-4 weeks**; vedolizumab and ustekinumab carry lower TB risk. [ECCO 2021 infections/ISG]`,`**Treat-to-target (STRIDE-II)**: clinical response short term, clinical remission, normal CRP and calprotectin intermediate term, and **endoscopic healing (Mayo 0-1)** long term. [STRIDE-II 2021]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD - acute severe UC`,points:[`**Admit every patient meeting Truelove-Witts criteria**; joint care by physician and colorectal surgeon from day 1. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Day 1 bundle**: stool C. difficile and amoeba, **unprepared flexible sigmoidoscopy with biopsy (CMV)**, AXR, FBC, CRP, albumin, electrolytes, **TB and HBV screen sent early** for possible rescue. [ECCO 2022]`,`**IV corticosteroids**: **hydrocortisone 100 mg four times daily or methylprednisolone 60 mg once daily**; higher doses add nothing. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Supportive**: IV fluids, **potassium above 4 mmol/L and magnesium above 1 mmol/L**, **LMWH thromboprophylaxis despite rectal bleeding**, nutrition, stop NSAIDs, opioids and anticholinergics. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Day 3 assessment (Oxford/Travis criteria)**: **stool frequency above 8 a day, or 3-8 a day with CRP above 45 mg/L, predicts about 85% colectomy** - start rescue therapy. [Travis, Gut 1996]`,`**Rescue**: **infliximab 5 mg/kg** (accelerated or 10 mg/kg dosing for low albumin or high CRP is used by many) or **ciclosporin 2 mg/kg/day IV** (check Mg and cholesterol; seizure risk) - equal efficacy (CONSTRUCT, CYSIF). [ECCO 2022]`,`**Tofacitinib** is emerging as rescue or add-on: an Indian randomised trial (**TACOS, 2024**) found adding tofacitinib to IV steroids reduced the need for rescue therapy. [TACOS 2024]`,`**Colectomy (subtotal colectomy with end ileostomy)** for no response **5-7 days after rescue**, toxic megacolon not improving within 24-72 hours, perforation or massive bleeding; delay increases mortality. [ECCO 2022]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: conceive in remission; **continue 5-ASA, thiopurines and anti-TNF**; stop methotrexate 3-6 months before conception; avoid JAK inhibitors and S1P modulators. [ECCO 2023 pregnancy]`,`**Sulfasalazine in pregnancy** needs **folic acid 2-5 mg daily**; mesalamine is safe in breastfeeding. [ECCO 2023]`,`**Infants exposed to anti-TNF in late pregnancy** should not receive live vaccines - in India **BCG and rotavirus** - for at least the first 6 months. [ECCO 2023]`,`**Elderly**: more infections and VTE; prefer vedolizumab or ustekinumab; avoid JAK inhibitors where cardiovascular risk is high; steroids cause delirium and fractures. [ECCO 2022]`,`**CKD**: mesalamine causes **interstitial nephritis** (rare) - check creatinine before and every 6-12 months. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Children**: higher rates of extensive disease and ASUC; use PUCAI for severity; growth and bone health monitoring. [ECCO-ESPGHAN 2018]`,`**Vaccinate before immunosuppression**: hepatitis B, pneumococcal, influenza, HPV, and **recombinant zoster vaccine** before JAK inhibitors; avoid live vaccines on immunosuppressants. [ECCO 2021]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Toxic megacolon** (transverse colon above 5.5 cm with systemic toxicity), **perforation** and **massive haemorrhage** are surgical emergencies. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Colorectal cancer** risk rises with duration, extent, severity of inflammation, PSC and family history; modern cumulative risk is about **1-2% at 10 years and 5% at 20-30 years**. [ECCO 2023 surveillance]`,`**Venous thromboembolism** risk is 2-3 times higher, especially during flares and admissions. [ECCO 2022]`,`**PSC** and **cholangiocarcinoma**; osteoporosis from steroids; anaemia; growth failure in children. [Harrison 22e]`,`**After restorative proctocolectomy with ileal pouch-anal anastomosis (IPAA)**: **pouchitis** in about half - treat with **ciprofloxacin 500 mg twice daily or metronidazole for 2 weeks**. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Prognosis**: life expectancy is near normal; proctitis extends proximally in about a third over 10 years. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer every suspected UC** for colonoscopic confirmation before long-term treatment. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Review flares within 2 weeks**; assess with stool frequency, bleeding, CRP and calprotectin. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Surveillance colonoscopy from 8 years after symptom onset** in E2/E3 disease, then every 1, 3 or 5 years by risk; **annually from diagnosis in PSC**; dye-spray chromoendoscopy or high-definition with targeted biopsies. [ECCO 2023/BSG 2019]`,`**Monitor drugs**: creatinine yearly on 5-ASA; FBC and LFT on thiopurines; lipids at 8-12 weeks on JAK; skin checks yearly on thiopurines. [BSG 2019]`,`**Bone health**: DXA after 3 months of steroids or repeated courses; calcium and vitamin D during steroid courses. [BSG 2019]`,`**Steroid stewardship**: more than one course of steroids a year means the maintenance plan has failed and needs escalation. [BSG 2019]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme links`,points:[`Counsel that UC is **chronic but controllable** and that **adherence to maintenance 5-ASA** halves relapse and reduces cancer risk. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Smoking**: do not advise starting; for smokers, cessation is still recommended for overall health. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Avoid NSAIDs**; paracetamol is the preferred analgesic. [ECCO 2022]`,`**TB vigilance in India**: report persistent fever, cough or weight loss on biologics or JAK inhibitors. [ISG]`,`**PM-JAY and state schemes** cover colectomy and some infusion packages; Jan Aushadhi supplies generic mesalamine and azathioprine. [NHA/PMBJP]`,`Encourage IBD support groups and IBD nurse helplines where available. [ECCO 2022]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2026)`,points:[`**ACG Clinical Guideline on UC (2025)**: endorses treat-to-target with calprotectin and endoscopic healing, positions JAK inhibitors, S1P modulators and IL-23 antibodies among advanced therapies, and recommends against thiopurine monotherapy for induction. [ACG 2025]`,`**ECCO UC guideline (2022)**: once-daily oral 5-ASA at 2 g or more for maintenance, budesonide MMX for mild-moderate disease, tofacitinib, ustekinumab and ozanimod for moderate-severe, and infliximab or ciclosporin rescue in ASUC. [ECCO 2022]`,`**AGA living guideline (2024) for moderate-severe UC** suggests **early advanced therapy rather than gradual step-up** from 5-ASA, and combination with an immunomodulator for infliximab. [AGA 2024]`,`**New approvals 2023-2025**: etrasimod (S1P), mirikizumab, risankizumab and guselkumab (IL-23 p19) for UC; availability and cost in India still limit use. [ACG 2025]`,`**FDA 2021 boxed warning** for JAK inhibitors (MACE, malignancy, thrombosis, death) from the ORAL Surveillance trial shapes patient selection. [FDA 2021]`,`**ECCO 2023 surveillance guidance** and the **SCENIC consensus** favour high-definition colonoscopy with targeted biopsies over random biopsies. [ECCO 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **Truelove and Witts criteria** and the **day-3 Oxford criteria** - learn the numbers 6, 90, 37.8, 10.5, 30 and 8 / 45. [ECCO 2022]`,`Classic question: **UC versus Crohn disease** - continuous versus skip, mucosal versus transmural, crypt abscesses versus granulomas, smoking protective versus harmful. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake**: starting steroids for bloody diarrhoea without excluding amoebiasis and C. difficile. [ISG]`,`**Common mistake**: withholding LMWH in ASUC because of rectal bleeding - VTE risk outweighs bleeding risk. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Common mistake**: oral 5-ASA alone for proctitis - suppositories are the first-line treatment. [ECCO 2022]`,`Viva: **why test NUDT15 in Indians?** - NUDT15 variants causing thiopurine myelotoxicity are commoner in Asians than TPMT variants. [ACG 2025]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Montreal classification and Truelove-Witts severity`,columns:[`System`,`Category`,`Definition`],rows:[[`Montreal extent`,`E1 proctitis`,`Rectum only`],[`Montreal extent`,`E2 left-sided`,`Distal to splenic flexure`],[`Montreal extent`,`E3 extensive`,`Proximal to splenic flexure`],[`Truelove-Witts`,`Mild`,`Fewer than 4 stools/day, small blood, no systemic toxicity, normal ESR`],[`Truelove-Witts`,`Moderate`,`4-6 stools/day, minimal toxicity`],[`Truelove-Witts`,`Severe`,`6 or more bloody stools plus pulse above 90, temp above 37.8 C, Hb below 10.5 g/dL or ESR above 30`]]},{heading:`Mayo score`,columns:[`Component`,`0`,`1`,`2`,`3`],rows:[[`Stool frequency`,`Normal`,`1-2 above normal`,`3-4 above normal`,`5 or more above normal`],[`Rectal bleeding`,`None`,`Streaks less than half the time`,`Obvious blood most of the time`,`Blood alone passed`],[`Endoscopy`,`Normal or inactive`,`Erythema, reduced vascular pattern, mild friability`,`Marked erythema, absent vascular pattern, erosions`,`Spontaneous bleeding, ulceration`],[`Physician global`,`Normal`,`Mild`,`Moderate`,`Severe`]]},{heading:`Drugs in ulcerative colitis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Mesalamine`,`Oral 2.4-4.8 g/day induction, 2 g or more maintenance; 1 g suppository; 1 g enema`,`Lifelong maintenance`,`Interstitial nephritis (check creatinine), diarrhoea, pancreatitis, rare myocarditis`],[`Sulfasalazine`,`2-4 g/day orally in divided doses`,`Lifelong; with folic acid`,`Rash, haemolysis, agranulocytosis, oligospermia, sulfa allergy`],[`Prednisolone`,`40 mg/day orally`,`Taper 5 mg/week over about 8 weeks`,`Hyperglycaemia, osteoporosis, infection, mood change; not for maintenance`],[`Budesonide MMX`,`9 mg orally once daily`,`8 weeks`,`Fewer systemic effects; less effective in severe disease`],[`Azathioprine`,`2-2.5 mg/kg/day orally`,`Effect at 12-16 weeks; long term`,`Myelosuppression (TPMT/NUDT15), hepatitis, pancreatitis, lymphoma, skin cancer`],[`Infliximab`,`5 mg/kg IV weeks 0, 2, 6 then every 8 weeks`,`Long term; trough-guided`,`TB reactivation, HBV, infusion reactions, lymphoma; screen first`],[`Vedolizumab`,`300 mg IV weeks 0, 2, 6 then every 8 weeks`,`Assess at 14 weeks`,`Gut-selective; nasopharyngitis; low systemic infection risk`],[`Tofacitinib`,`10 mg orally twice daily x 8 weeks, then 5-10 mg twice daily`,`Long term`,`Zoster, VTE, MACE, lipids; caution age over 65, smokers; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Ozanimod`,`0.23 mg days 1-4, 0.46 mg days 5-7, then 0.92 mg orally daily`,`Long term`,`Bradycardia, AV block, macular oedema, raised ALT; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Ciclosporin (ASUC)`,`2 mg/kg/day IV infusion`,`Bridge to thiopurine`,`Nephrotoxicity, hypertension, seizures with low Mg or cholesterol`]]},{heading:`Ulcerative colitis versus Crohn disease`,columns:[`Feature`,`Ulcerative colitis`,`Crohn disease`],rows:[[`Distribution`,`Rectum upward, continuous`,`Mouth to anus, skip lesions, ileocaecal commonest`],[`Depth`,`Mucosal`,`Transmural`],[`Rectal involvement`,`Almost always`,`Often spared`],[`Perianal disease, fistula, stricture`,`Rare`,`Common`],[`Histology`,`Crypt abscesses, no granulomas`,`Non-caseating granulomas (30-50%)`],[`Smoking`,`Protective`,`Worsens disease`],[`Serology`,`p-ANCA often positive`,`ASCA often positive`],[`Surgery`,`Curative (proctocolectomy)`,`Not curative; recurrence common`]]},{heading:`Acute severe UC - day-by-day protocol`,columns:[`Day`,`Action`,`Threshold or target`],rows:[[`Day 0-1`,`Admit, stool tests, unprepared sigmoidoscopy with CMV biopsy, AXR, TB/HBV screen`,`Truelove-Witts severe`],[`Day 1`,`IV hydrocortisone 100 mg 6-hourly or methylprednisolone 60 mg daily; LMWH; K and Mg`,`K above 4, Mg above 1 mmol/L`],[`Day 3`,`Oxford/Travis assessment`,`Stools above 8, or 3-8 with CRP above 45: rescue`],[`Day 3-5`,`Rescue infliximab 5 mg/kg or ciclosporin 2 mg/kg/day`,`Response in 5-7 days`],[`Any day`,`Colectomy`,`Toxic megacolon above 5.5 cm not improving, perforation, haemorrhage, rescue failure`]]}],redFlags:[`Six or more bloody stools a day with pulse above 90, fever above 37.8 C, Hb below 10.5 or ESR above 30 - acute severe UC, admit`,`Abdominal distension, tenderness and reduced bowel sounds - toxic megacolon or perforation`,`Transverse colon above 5.5 cm on plain X-ray`,`Massive rectal bleeding with haemodynamic instability`,`Painful red eye with photophobia - uveitis`,`Fever, cough or weight loss on biologic or JAK therapy - TB reactivation`,`Calf swelling or breathlessness during a flare - venous thromboembolism`],pearls:[`UC starts in the rectum and spreads up continuously; Crohn skips and goes deep.`,`Truelove-Witts: 6 bloody stools plus 90 (pulse), 37.8 (temp), 10.5 (Hb), 30 (ESR).`,`Day 3 of IV steroids: stools above 8, or 3-8 with CRP above 45 - 85% colectomy, so rescue now.`,`Proctitis is treated from below: mesalamine 1 g suppository at night.`,`LMWH in every admitted flare, even with rectal bleeding.`,`Exclude amoeba, C. difficile and CMV before calling it a steroid-refractory flare.`,`Thiopurines maintain, they do not induce; check TPMT and NUDT15 in Indians.`,`Screen for TB (IGRA plus chest X-ray) and hepatitis B before any biologic or JAK inhibitor.`,`Surveillance colonoscopy at 8 years from symptoms; from diagnosis if PSC.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-ulcerative-colitis-q1`,stem:`A 26-year-old man with UC has 8 bloody stools a day, pulse 104/min, temperature 38.1 C and Hb 9.8 g/dL. After 3 days of IV hydrocortisone he still has 6 stools a day and CRP 62 mg/L. What is the next step?`,options:[`Continue IV steroids for another week`,`Rescue therapy with infliximab or ciclosporin`,`Switch to oral prednisolone and discharge`,`Add oral mesalamine 4.8 g/day`],answer:1,explanation:`By the Oxford (Travis) criteria, 3-8 stools a day with CRP above 45 mg/L on day 3 of IV steroids predicts about 85% colectomy, so rescue with infliximab 5 mg/kg or ciclosporin 2 mg/kg/day is indicated. Prolonging IV steroids without response delays rescue and raises surgical mortality. Oral prednisolone and discharge are for responders. Adding mesalamine has no role in acute severe UC.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-ulcerative-colitis-q2`,stem:`A 30-year-old woman has rectal bleeding with mucus and urgency. Sigmoidoscopy shows continuous inflammation limited to the distal 12 cm of rectum. What is the most effective first-line treatment?`,options:[`Oral prednisolone 40 mg daily`,`Oral mesalamine 2.4 g/day alone`,`Mesalamine 1 g suppository once daily`,`Azathioprine 2 mg/kg/day`],answer:2,explanation:`Ulcerative proctitis (Montreal E1) is best treated with topical mesalamine 1 g suppositories, which deliver drug to the inflamed rectum and outperform oral 5-ASA and topical steroids. Oral mesalamine alone is less effective for proctitis. Oral steroids are reserved for failure of 5-ASA. Azathioprine is a maintenance drug for steroid dependence and does not induce remission.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-ulcerative-colitis-q3`,stem:`Before starting infliximab in an Indian patient with steroid-dependent UC, which screening is essential?`,options:[`IGRA or tuberculin test, chest X-ray and hepatitis B serology`,`Echocardiography and pulmonary function tests`,`TPMT genotype and NUDT15`,`Ophthalmological examination for macular oedema`],answer:0,explanation:`Anti-TNF therapy can reactivate latent tuberculosis and hepatitis B, so IGRA or tuberculin skin test with chest X-ray and HBsAg/anti-HBc are mandatory, especially in India; latent TB is treated before starting. TPMT and NUDT15 testing applies to thiopurines. Eye examination for macular oedema is needed before S1P modulators such as ozanimod. Echocardiography is only relevant if heart failure is suspected.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-ulcerative-colitis-q4`,stem:`A 68-year-old smoker with prior myocardial infarction has moderate-severe UC refractory to steroids and azathioprine. Which advanced therapy is least appropriate?`,options:[`Vedolizumab`,`Infliximab`,`Ustekinumab`,`Tofacitinib`],answer:3,explanation:`After the ORAL Surveillance trial, the 2021 FDA boxed warning restricts JAK inhibitors such as tofacitinib in people over 65, current or past smokers and those with cardiovascular disease because of excess MACE, thrombosis and malignancy. Vedolizumab is gut-selective and safe in the elderly, ustekinumab has a favourable safety profile, and infliximab is acceptable without heart failure after TB screening.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-ulcerative-colitis-q5`,stem:`A patient with acute severe UC has abdominal distension and a transverse colon of 7 cm on plain X-ray, with tachycardia and fever. Which drug should be stopped immediately?`,options:[`Low molecular weight heparin`,`Intravenous hydrocortisone`,`Codeine phosphate`,`Intravenous potassium chloride`],answer:2,explanation:`Toxic megacolon is precipitated by opioids and anticholinergics, which further paralyse the inflamed colon, so codeine must be stopped. LMWH is continued because VTE risk is high. IV steroids are the core medical treatment while surgical review proceeds. Potassium replacement corrects hypokalaemia, which itself worsens colonic dilatation.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-ulcerative-colitis-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Classify ulcerative colitis. Describe the assessment and management of acute severe ulcerative colitis.`,openingLines:[`Ulcerative colitis is a chronic relapsing continuous mucosal inflammation of the colon starting in the rectum, classified by Montreal extent (E1-E3) and severity.`,`Acute severe UC, defined by Truelove and Witts criteria, is a medical emergency with a colectomy rate of about 30%, managed with IV steroids, day-3 assessment and timely rescue or surgery.`],answer:[{heading:`Classification`,points:[`Montreal E1, E2, E3 and S0-S3.`,`Mayo score 0-12 and endoscopic Mayo 0-3.`,`Truelove and Witts mild, moderate, severe.`]},{heading:`Definition of ASUC`,points:[`6 or more bloody stools a day plus pulse above 90, temperature above 37.8 C, Hb below 10.5 or ESR above 30.`]},{heading:`Initial assessment`,points:[`Stool C. difficile, amoeba, culture; unprepared sigmoidoscopy with CMV biopsy.`,`FBC, CRP, albumin, electrolytes, AXR (colon above 5.5 cm), TB and HBV screen.`]},{heading:`Medical management`,points:[`IV hydrocortisone 100 mg 6-hourly or methylprednisolone 60 mg daily.`,`Fluids, K and Mg, LMWH, nutrition; stop NSAIDs, opioids, anticholinergics.`,`Daily stool chart, vitals, abdominal girth; joint medical-surgical care.`]},{heading:`Day 3 and rescue`,points:[`Oxford criteria: stools above 8, or 3-8 with CRP above 45.`,`Infliximab 5 mg/kg or ciclosporin 2 mg/kg/day; tofacitinib emerging.`]},{heading:`Surgery and follow-up`,points:[`Subtotal colectomy with ileostomy for failure, toxic megacolon, perforation, haemorrhage.`,`Later IPAA; maintenance therapy, surveillance colonoscopy.`]}],mustDraw:[`ASUC day-0 to day-7 flow chart with day-3 decision`,`Montreal extent diagram of the colon`],markSplit:[{part:`Classification`,marks:2},{part:`Assessment of ASUC`,marks:3},{part:`Medical management and rescue`,marks:3},{part:`Surgery and follow-up`,marks:2}],keywords:[`Truelove and Witts`,`Oxford criteria`,`infliximab rescue`,`ciclosporin`,`toxic megacolon`,`colectomy`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-ulcerative-colitis-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on 5-aminosalicylates in ulcerative colitis.`,openingLines:[`5-aminosalicylates (mesalamine, sulfasalazine) are topically acting anti-inflammatory drugs that are first-line for inducing and maintaining remission in mild to moderate UC.`,`Their effect depends on delivering the drug to the inflamed segment, so formulation and route are chosen by disease extent.`],answer:[{heading:`Mechanism and formulations`,points:[`PPAR-gamma activation, NF-kappa B and prostaglandin inhibition at the mucosa.`,`Sulfasalazine (sulfapyridine carrier, colonic azo-reduction), pH-dependent and MMX mesalamine, suppositories and enemas.`]},{heading:`Dosing by extent`,points:[`Proctitis: 1 g suppository daily.`,`Left-sided or extensive: oral 2.4-4.8 g/day plus 1 g enema.`,`Maintenance: 2 g or more daily; once-daily dosing.`]},{heading:`Adverse effects and monitoring`,points:[`Interstitial nephritis - creatinine yearly; paradoxical diarrhoea, pancreatitis.`,`Sulfasalazine: rash, haemolysis, agranulocytosis, oligospermia; give folic acid.`,`Safe in pregnancy and breastfeeding; may reduce colorectal cancer risk.`]},{heading:`Place in therapy`,points:[`First line for mild-moderate UC induction and maintenance; escalate if no response in 2-4 weeks.`,`Not recommended in Crohn disease (ECCO 2024); no role in acute severe UC.`]}],mustDraw:[`Colon diagram showing reach of suppository (rectum), enema (to splenic flexure) and oral drug`],markSplit:[{part:`Mechanism and formulations`,marks:1.5},{part:`Dosing`,marks:2},{part:`Adverse effects and monitoring`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`mesalamine`,`sulfasalazine`,`suppository`,`interstitial nephritis`,`maintenance`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Inflammatory bowel disease`,`Raine T et al. ECCO Guidelines on Therapeutics in Ulcerative Colitis: Medical Treatment, J Crohns Colitis 2022`,`Rubin DT et al. ACG Clinical Guideline Update: Ulcerative Colitis in Adults, Am J Gastroenterol 2025`,`Singh S et al. AGA Living Clinical Practice Guideline on Pharmacological Management of Moderate-to-Severe Ulcerative Colitis, Gastroenterology 2024`,`Lamb CA et al. British Society of Gastroenterology consensus guidelines on the management of IBD in adults, Gut 2019`,`Turner D et al. STRIDE-II: an update on the Selecting Therapeutic Targets in IBD initiative, Gastroenterology 2021`,`Kedia S, Ahuja V. Epidemiology of inflammatory bowel disease in India: the great shift east, Inflamm Intest Dis 2017`]}),n.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-crohns-disease`,title:`Crohn disease and intestinal TB differentiation`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Crohn disease (CD)** is a **chronic, transmural, granulomatous inflammation that can affect any part of the gut from mouth to anus with skip lesions** (commonest ileocaecal), causing **pain, diarrhoea, weight loss, strictures, fistulae and perianal disease**; it is staged by **Montreal (A, L, B, p)** and graded by **CDAI/HBI**, and in India must be carefully separated from **intestinal tuberculosis (ITB)** - by endoscopy, CT, histology, microbiology and, when doubt remains, an **empirical ATT trial with endoscopic reassessment at 2-3 months** - before **steroids, anti-TNF, ustekinumab, vedolizumab, risankizumab or upadacitinib**.`,keywords:[`Crohn's disease`,`regional enteritis`,`intestinal tuberculosis`,`ITB versus CD`,`ileocaecal TB`,`empirical ATT trial`,`Montreal classification`,`CDAI`,`Harvey-Bradshaw index`,`SES-CD`,`Rutgeerts score`,`adalimumab`,`infliximab`,`ustekinumab`,`risankizumab`,`upadacitinib`,`perianal fistula`,`seton`,`exclusive enteral nutrition`,`ECCO 2024`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Crohn disease** is chronic relapsing **transmural** inflammation that may involve any segment of the gastrointestinal tract, characteristically **discontinuous (skip lesions)**, with **non-caseating granulomas** in 30-50% of biopsies. [Harrison 22e]`,`Sites: **ileocolonic about 40-50%, ileal alone about 30%, colonic alone about 20%**, upper GI 5-15%; perianal disease affects up to a third. [Harrison 22e]`,`Peak onset **15-30 years**, with a smaller peak at 60-70 years; slight female predominance in the West. [Harrison 22e]`,`Incidence is rising fastest in **newly industrialised countries**; India, with the second-largest IBD population, has a rising CD:UC ratio, with CD now about a third or more of new IBD cases in tertiary centres. [Kedia and Ahuja, Inflamm Intest Dis 2017]`,`India carries about a quarter of the world's TB burden, so **intestinal tuberculosis is the main mimic** of CD; both commonly affect the ileocaecal region. [India TB Report 2024]`,`In Indian series a large proportion of CD patients (around half in several tertiary cohorts) **receive ATT before the correct diagnosis**, delaying CD treatment by 1-2 years. [Kedia 2019]`,`Conversely, labelling ITB as CD and starting steroids or anti-TNF can cause **disseminated TB** - so the differentiation is a core Indian exam theme. [ISG]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Genetics**: about 15% have an affected relative; **NOD2/CARD15** (bacterial sensing - ileal disease, strictures), **ATG16L1 and IRGM** (autophagy), **IL23R** and HLA loci. NOD2 variants are rare in Indians. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Smoking doubles the risk** of CD, worsens its course, increases post-operative recurrence and reduces drug response - cessation is as effective as an immunosuppressant. [ECCO 2024]`,`**Environment**: westernised diet, ultra-processed food, early-life antibiotics, urban living; NSAIDs and the oral contraceptive pill may increase risk. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Microbiome**: reduced Faecalibacterium prausnitzii and increased adherent-invasive E. coli in ileal disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ITB risk factors**: past or contact TB, HIV, diabetes, malnutrition, immunosuppression, poverty and crowding; spread is by swallowed sputum, haematogenous seeding or contiguous nodes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mycobacterium tuberculosis** is the cause of ITB in India; **M. bovis** from unpasteurised milk is now rare. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Defective **innate sensing and autophagy** (NOD2, ATG16L1) let bacteria persist in the mucosa, driving a **Th1/Th17 response via IL-12 and IL-23** with TNF-alpha and IFN-gamma. [Harrison 22e]`,`This explains the drug targets: **anti-TNF** (infliximab, adalimumab), **anti-IL-12/23 p40** (ustekinumab), **anti-IL-23 p19** (risankizumab, guselkumab, mirikizumab), **anti-integrin** (vedolizumab) and **JAK1 inhibition** (upadacitinib). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Transmural inflammation** leads to **fibrosis and stricture** (obstruction), **sinus tracts and fistulae** (to bowel, bladder, vagina, skin) and **abscesses**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Aphthous ulcers over lymphoid follicles** are the earliest lesion; they coalesce into **deep longitudinal (linear) ulcers**, and oedematous mucosa between them gives **cobblestoning**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Creeping fat (fat wrapping)** around inflamed bowel and the **'comb sign'** of engorged vasa recta on CT reflect transmural mesenteric involvement. [ECCO-ESGAR 2019]`,`**ITB** involves the ileocaecal region because of **abundant lymphoid tissue (Peyer patches), stasis and high absorption**; TB spreads along **circumferential lymphatics**, producing **transverse ulcers** and strictures, plus **caseating, confluent granulomas** and necrotic nodes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ileal disease** causes **B12 deficiency, bile acid diarrhoea, gallstones and oxalate stones**; ileal resection over 100 cm causes steatorrhoea. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and activity scores`,points:[`**Montreal Age**: **A1 16 years or under, A2 17-40, A3 over 40**. [Montreal 2005]`,`**Montreal Location**: **L1 ileal, L2 colonic, L3 ileocolonic, L4 isolated upper GI** (L4 can be added to L1-L3 as a modifier). [Montreal 2005]`,`**Montreal Behaviour**: **B1 non-stricturing non-penetrating, B2 stricturing, B3 penetrating**, with **p** added for perianal disease. [Montreal 2005]`,`**CDAI**: below 150 remission, 150-220 mild, 220-450 moderate, above 450 severe; response is a fall of 70-100 points (needs a 7-day diary, used in trials). [Best 1976]`,`**Harvey-Bradshaw Index** (bedside): **below 5 remission, 5-7 mild, 8-16 moderate, above 16 severe**. [Harvey-Bradshaw 1980]`,`**SES-CD** (simple endoscopic score) grades ulcer size, ulcerated surface, affected surface and narrowing in 5 segments; **Rutgeerts score i0-i4** grades post-operative recurrence at the neo-terminal ileum (**i2 or more** predicts clinical recurrence). [ECCO 2024]`,`**Perianal fistula - Parks classification**: superficial, intersphincteric, transsphincteric, suprasphincteric, extrasphincteric; simple versus complex (ECCO/AGA). [ECCO 2024]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Ileocaecal CD**: right iliac fossa pain (post-prandial, colicky), non-bloody diarrhoea, weight loss, low-grade fever - may mimic appendicitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Colonic CD**: bloody diarrhoea like UC but with rectal sparing and perianal disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Stricturing disease**: recurrent subacute intestinal obstruction - colic, distension, vomiting and borborygmi after meals; patients eat less to avoid pain. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Penetrating disease**: enterovesical fistula (**pneumaturia, faecaluria, recurrent UTI**), enterovaginal fistula, enterocutaneous fistula, psoas abscess (hip flexion). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Perianal disease**: recurrent abscesses, discharging fistulae, fissures in atypical position, skin tags - may precede gut symptoms by years. [ECCO 2024]`,`**Children**: **growth failure and delayed puberty** may be the only presentation. [ECCO-ESPGHAN 2020]`,`**ITB history clues**: shorter duration (usually under 1 year), **fever and night sweats**, past or contact TB, ascites, and fewer perianal and extra-intestinal features. [Kedia 2019]`,`**Extra-intestinal**: arthritis, sacroiliitis, uveitis, erythema nodosum, pyoderma gangrenosum, oral aphthae, gallstones, renal stones, VTE. [ECCO EIM 2024]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Nutrition**: low BMI, pallor, clubbing (active small-bowel CD), oedema, short stature. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mouth**: aphthous ulcers, **cobblestoned buccal mucosa, lip swelling (orofacial granulomatosis)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Abdomen**: tender **right iliac fossa mass** (inflamed matted loops or abscess), visible peristalsis in obstruction, enterocutaneous fistula openings, surgical scars. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Perianal inspection in every patient**: oedematous skin tags, fissures, fistula openings, abscesses - **Goodsall rule** helps predict internal openings of simple fistulae. [ECCO 2024]`,`**ITB clues on examination**: **ascites (doughy abdomen)**, lymphadenopathy, and **signs of pulmonary TB**; a 'doughy' abdomen suggests peritoneal TB. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Skin and eyes**: erythema nodosum, pyoderma gangrenosum, episcleritis, uveitis. [ECCO EIM 2024]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis - ITB versus CD (Indian context)`,points:[`**Favour ITB**: fever, night sweats, **ascites**, short history, **transverse ulcers**, **patulous ileocaecal valve**, involvement of **fewer than 4 segments**, **necrotic lymph nodes** on CT, concurrent pulmonary TB. [Kedia 2019]`,`**Favour CD**: long history, **perianal disease**, extra-intestinal manifestations, **longitudinal ulcers, aphthous ulcers, cobblestoning, skip lesions**, **long-segment involvement, comb sign, fibrofatty proliferation** and **high visceral-to-subcutaneous fat ratio**. [Kedia 2019]`,`**Histology**: **caseation** is diagnostic of TB but seen in only a minority; **large (over 200-400 micrometres), confluent, numerous submucosal granulomas** and ulcers lined by epithelioid histiocytes favour TB; small, sparse, discrete granulomas favour CD. [Kedia 2019]`,`**Microbiology**: AFB smear, **Xpert MTB/RIF (GeneXpert) and MGIT culture** on biopsies are highly specific but **positive in only about 10-30%** of ITB - a negative result does not exclude TB. [Kedia 2019]`,`**IGRA** has modest discrimination in India (many have latent TB): a negative IGRA favours CD, a positive one is weakly suggestive of ITB. [Kedia 2019]`,`**Bayesian and multiparameter models** combining these features (e.g. Limsrivilai 2017) improve accuracy beyond any single feature. [Limsrivilai, Am J Gastroenterol 2017]`,`Other differentials: **Yersinia ileitis, lymphoma, NSAID enteropathy, Behcet disease, carcinoma caecum, amoeboma, appendicular mass** and **UC** (see the Ulcerative colitis topic). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Bloods**: FBC, CRP, ESR, albumin, ferritin, B12, folate, vitamin D, LFT, renal function; **HIV** test. [ECCO 2024]`,`**Stool**: calprotectin (raised in both CD and ITB), infection screen including C. difficile. [ECCO 2024]`,`**Ileocolonoscopy with biopsies from every segment and the terminal ileum**, sending samples for **histology, AFB, Xpert MTB/RIF and mycobacterial culture**. [ECCO 2024/ISG]`,`**CT or MR enterography**: extent, skip segments, strictures, fistulae, abscess, comb sign, fat wrapping, necrotic nodes; **MR is preferred in the young** to avoid radiation. [ECCO-ESGAR 2019]`,`**Chest X-ray in all**, and sputum testing if abnormal - pulmonary TB in an ileocaecal ulcer patient makes ITB likely. [NTEP 2023]`,`**Upper GI endoscopy** for upper symptoms (L4); **capsule endoscopy** for suspected small-bowel CD after excluding stricture (patency capsule). [ECCO-ESGAR 2019]`,`**MRI pelvis** is the investigation of choice for **perianal fistulae**, with EUA by a surgeon. [ECCO 2024]`,`**Pre-treatment screening**: IGRA or TST, chest X-ray, HBsAg, anti-HBc, HCV, HIV, varicella status; **TPMT and NUDT15** before thiopurines. [ECCO 2024]`,`**Intestinal ultrasound** is a cheap, radiation-free tool for monitoring bowel wall thickness and response (increasingly used in Indian centres). [ECCO-ESGAR 2019]`]},{heading:`The empirical ATT trial`,points:[`When ITB and CD cannot be separated after full work-up, Indian practice is a **therapeutic trial of standard ATT** (2HRZE + 4HRE) under NTEP, rather than empirical immunosuppression. [ISG/NTEP 2023]`,`**Reassess at 2-3 months**: **objective response - mucosal healing or marked improvement of ulcers on colonoscopy** - supports ITB; clinical response alone is unreliable because CD may improve transiently on ATT. [Pratap Mouli, Aliment Pharmacol Ther 2017]`,`**Non-response at 2-3 months** (persistent ulcers, rising calprotectin, new fistula) after checking adherence and drug resistance favours CD - stop ATT and treat as CD. [Kedia 2019]`,`**Responders** complete **6 months of ATT**, which is adequate for abdominal TB in most patients. [INDEX-TB 2016/Cochrane 2016]`,`**Never start steroids or anti-TNF during an unresolved ITB-CD dilemma** without TB cover; steroid exposure in ITB worsens outcome. [ISG]`,`Paradoxical worsening or new strictures can occur in true ITB on ATT; obstruction may need **endoscopic dilatation or surgery** even with cure. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - induction and maintenance`,points:[`**Stop smoking** - the single most effective non-drug measure; also avoid NSAIDs. [ECCO 2024]`,`**Mild ileocaecal CD**: **budesonide 9 mg once daily for 8 weeks** then taper; **5-ASA is not recommended** for inducing or maintaining remission in CD. [ECCO 2024]`,`**Moderate-severe CD**: **prednisolone 40 mg/day (0.5-0.75 mg/kg)** tapering over 8-12 weeks for induction only; steroids do not maintain remission. [ECCO 2024]`,`**Thiopurines (azathioprine 2-2.5 mg/kg/day)** maintain steroid-induced remission and reduce anti-drug antibodies with infliximab; not for induction. [ECCO 2024]`,`**Methotrexate 25 mg IM or SC weekly for 16 weeks then 15 mg weekly**, with **folic acid 5 mg once weekly** on another day - for steroid-dependent CD; contraindicated in pregnancy. [ECCO 2024]`,`**Anti-TNF**: **infliximab 5 mg/kg IV at weeks 0, 2, 6 then every 8 weeks** or **adalimumab 160 mg, 80 mg at week 2, then 40 mg every 2 weeks** - for moderate-severe, fistulising and high-risk disease; **combination with azathioprine** (SONIC) is superior to monotherapy. [ECCO 2024]`,`**Ustekinumab 6 mg/kg IV then 90 mg SC every 8 weeks**; **risankizumab 600 mg IV at weeks 0, 4, 8 then 180-360 mg SC every 8 weeks**; **vedolizumab 300 mg IV at 0, 2, 6 then every 8 weeks**; **upadacitinib 45 mg daily for 12 weeks then 15-30 mg daily**. [ECCO 2024]`,`**Early (top-down) advanced therapy** for high-risk features: young age, extensive small-bowel disease, perianal disease, deep ulcers, stricturing or penetrating behaviour, need for early steroids. [ECCO 2024]`,`**Limited (under 40 cm) terminal ileal disease** not responding to conventional therapy: **laparoscopic ileocaecal resection is a reasonable alternative to anti-TNF** (LIR!C trial). [ECCO 2024]`,`**Nutrition**: **exclusive enteral nutrition for 6-8 weeks** is first-line induction in children; correct iron (IV if needed), B12 (ileal disease/resection), vitamin D and zinc. [ECCO-ESPGHAN 2020]`]},{heading:`Perianal and complicated disease`,points:[`**Perianal abscess**: drain surgically first - never start anti-TNF with an undrained abscess. [ECCO 2024]`,`**Complex perianal fistula**: MRI pelvis plus EUA with **loose draining seton**, then **antibiotics (ciprofloxacin 500 mg twice daily and/or metronidazole) for several weeks** as a bridge to **anti-TNF (infliximab preferred), ideally with a thiopurine**. [ECCO 2024]`,`**Refractory perianal disease**: ustekinumab or vedolizumab as alternatives; diverting stoma or proctectomy for severe disease. Darvadstrocel (stem cells) was withdrawn in Europe in 2024 after a negative phase 3 trial. [ECCO 2024]`,`**Short inflammatory or fibrotic strictures (under 5 cm)**: **endoscopic balloon dilatation**; longer or multiple strictures need **strictureplasty or resection**. [ECCO 2024]`,`**Intra-abdominal abscess**: antibiotics plus **percutaneous drainage**, followed by elective resection or medical therapy once sepsis is controlled. [ECCO 2024]`,`**Post-operative recurrence**: stop smoking; **prophylaxis with anti-TNF (with or without thiopurine) for high-risk patients** (smokers, penetrating disease, prior resection, perianal disease); **ileocolonoscopy at 6-12 months** and escalate for **Rutgeerts i2 or more**. [ECCO 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Admit** for severe flare (HBI above 16 or systemic toxicity), **intestinal obstruction, abscess, perforation, GI bleeding**, severe malnutrition or dehydration. [ECCO 2024]`,`**Severe flare**: exclude infection and abscess (CT), then **IV hydrocortisone 100 mg four times daily**, LMWH prophylaxis, nutrition and early surgical opinion. [ECCO 2024]`,`**Subacute obstruction**: nil by mouth, nasogastric decompression, IV fluids; CT or MR to separate inflammatory (steroid-responsive) from fibrotic (surgical) stricture. [ECCO 2024]`,`**Nutritional support**: enteral feeding preferred; parenteral nutrition for intestinal failure or high-output fistula; screen for **refeeding syndrome**. [ESPEN 2023]`,`**Free perforation, massive bleeding and obstruction not settling** need surgery; resections should be **bowel-sparing** to avoid short bowel syndrome. [ECCO 2024]`,`**ITB with obstruction** is common in Indian surgical wards: many strictures resolve on ATT; endoscopic dilatation or surgery for persistent ones. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: conceive in remission; **continue anti-TNF and thiopurines**; stop methotrexate 3-6 months before conception; avoid upadacitinib; **caesarean section for active perianal disease**. [ECCO 2023 pregnancy]`,`**Infants exposed to anti-TNF in utero**: delay **BCG** and rotavirus for at least 6 months. [ECCO 2023]`,`**Children**: exclusive enteral nutrition first; monitor growth and puberty; early anti-TNF for high-risk disease. [ECCO-ESPGHAN 2020]`,`**Elderly**: more colonic disease; infection and malignancy risk on immunosuppression - prefer vedolizumab or ustekinumab. [ECCO 2024]`,`**Latent TB on screening (common in India)**: complete preventive therapy (**3HP or 6H**) and start anti-TNF after at least **3-4 weeks**; monitor for active TB throughout. [ECCO 2021 infections/NTEP 2023]`,`**Active TB during anti-TNF**: stop the biologic, give full ATT, and consider resuming after 2 months of ATT with specialist advice. [ECCO 2021]`,`**HIV**: CD and ITB both occur; CD4 count and ART are essential before immunosuppression. [NACO 2021]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Strictures, fistulae and abscesses**: about **half of patients develop B2 or B3 behaviour within 10-20 years**, and about half need surgery within 10 years of diagnosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Post-operative recurrence**: endoscopic recurrence in **70-90% at 1 year** without prophylaxis and clinical recurrence in about 30% at 3 years. [ECCO 2024]`,`**Malnutrition, anaemia, B12 deficiency, osteoporosis, growth failure**, gallstones and oxalate kidney stones. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cancer**: colorectal cancer in Crohn colitis (surveillance as for UC), small-bowel adenocarcinoma, anal cancer in chronic fistulae, and lymphoma on thiopurines plus anti-TNF (hepatosplenic T-cell lymphoma in young men). [ECCO 2023]`,`**Short bowel syndrome** after repeated resections. [ESPEN 2023]`,`**ITB prognosis**: excellent with 6 months of ATT; strictures and adhesions may still cause obstruction. [INDEX-TB 2016]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer every suspected CD or ITB-CD dilemma** to a gastroenterologist; do not start steroids in primary care for undiagnosed ileocaecal disease in India. [ISG]`,`**Monitor activity** with HBI, CRP and calprotectin every 3-6 months, and **endoscopy or cross-sectional imaging 6-12 months** after starting a new therapy - **treat to target (STRIDE-II)**. [STRIDE-II 2021]`,`**Drug monitoring**: FBC and LFT on thiopurines and methotrexate; **therapeutic drug monitoring** of anti-TNF troughs for loss of response. [ECCO 2024]`,`**Bone health**: DXA for steroid exposure, low BMI or long-standing disease; calcium and vitamin D. [ECCO 2024]`,`**Colonoscopic surveillance** for Crohn colitis affecting a third or more of the colon, from 8 years after onset. [ECCO 2023]`,`**Vaccinations**: hepatitis B, pneumococcal, influenza, HPV, recombinant zoster; no live vaccines on immunosuppressants. [ECCO 2021]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme links`,points:[`**Smoking cessation support** (NTCP quitline 1800-11-2356) is part of CD treatment. [NTCP]`,`Notify **ITB under NTEP (Ni-kshay)**; patients get free ATT and **Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana** nutrition support. [NTEP 2023]`,`**TB contact screening** and preventive therapy for household contacts reduces ITB at source. [NTEP 2023]`,`Counsel about **adherence, flare warning signs, infection risk on immunosuppressants** and the need to report fever or cough. [ECCO 2024]`,`**Biosimilar infliximab and adalimumab** and PM-JAY surgical packages improve access in India. [NHA]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**ECCO Crohn disease guideline (2024)**: 5-ASA not recommended; budesonide for mild ileocaecal disease; anti-TNF, ustekinumab, vedolizumab, **risankizumab and upadacitinib** for moderate-severe disease; ileocaecal resection as an alternative for limited ileal disease. [ECCO 2024]`,`**PROFILE trial (2024)**: **top-down infliximab plus immunomodulator** at diagnosis gave far higher sustained remission than accelerated step-up. [PROFILE, Lancet Gastroenterol Hepatol 2024]`,`**SEQUENCE trial (2024)**: after anti-TNF failure, **risankizumab was superior to ustekinumab** for endoscopic remission at week 48. [SEQUENCE, NEJM 2024]`,`**New approvals 2024-2025**: **guselkumab and mirikizumab** (IL-23 p19) for CD; availability in India is limited. [ECCO 2024]`,`**Darvadstrocel** was withdrawn in the EU (2024) after the ADMIRE-CD II trial missed its endpoint. [EMA 2024]`,`**Indian practice**: an objective (endoscopic) response at 2-3 months of ATT remains the recommended way to resolve an ITB-CD dilemma. [ISG/Kedia 2019]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **differentiate ITB from CD** - draw the table of clinical, endoscopic, CT, histological and microbiological features. [Kedia 2019]`,`Classic question: **Montreal classification** of CD (A1-3, L1-4, B1-3, p). [Montreal 2005]`,`**Common mistake**: giving steroids or anti-TNF to an ileocaecal ulcer without excluding TB. [ISG]`,`**Common mistake**: calling ATT 'successful' on symptom improvement alone - insist on colonoscopic response. [Pratap Mouli 2017]`,`**Common mistake**: prescribing 5-ASA for CD - ECCO 2024 recommends against it. [ECCO 2024]`,`Viva: **why does smoking matter?** - it doubles CD risk and post-operative recurrence; it protects in UC. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Montreal classification of Crohn disease`,columns:[`Domain`,`Code`,`Definition`],rows:[[`Age at diagnosis`,`A1 / A2 / A3`,`16 or under / 17-40 / over 40`],[`Location`,`L1`,`Terminal ileum`],[`Location`,`L2`,`Colon`],[`Location`,`L3`,`Ileocolon`],[`Location modifier`,`L4`,`Isolated upper GI (added to L1-L3)`],[`Behaviour`,`B1 / B2 / B3`,`Non-stricturing non-penetrating / stricturing / penetrating`],[`Modifier`,`p`,`Perianal disease`]]},{heading:`Intestinal TB versus Crohn disease`,columns:[`Feature`,`Intestinal tuberculosis`,`Crohn disease`],rows:[[`History`,`Short (under 1 year), fever, night sweats, TB contact`,`Longer, relapsing course`],[`Perianal disease, EIMs`,`Rare`,`Common`],[`Ulcers`,`Transverse, circumferential`,`Longitudinal, aphthous, cobblestoning`],[`Ileocaecal valve`,`Patulous (gaping)`,`Narrowed or ulcerated`],[`Segments involved`,`Fewer than 4`,`4 or more, skip lesions`],[`CT`,`Necrotic nodes, ascites, short segment`,`Comb sign, fat wrapping, long segment, high visceral fat`],[`Histology`,`Caseating, large confluent granulomas`,`Small, sparse, non-caseating granulomas`],[`Microbiology`,`AFB, Xpert, culture positive (10-30%)`,`Negative`],[`Response to ATT at 2-3 months`,`Mucosal healing`,`No endoscopic healing`]]},{heading:`Activity scores`,columns:[`Score`,`Remission`,`Mild`,`Moderate`,`Severe`],rows:[[`CDAI`,`Below 150`,`150-220`,`220-450`,`Above 450`],[`Harvey-Bradshaw Index`,`Below 5`,`5-7`,`8-16`,`Above 16`],[`Rutgeerts (post-op)`,`i0-i1 (low risk)`,`i2`,`i3`,`i4`]]},{heading:`Drugs in Crohn disease`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Budesonide`,`9 mg orally once daily`,`8 weeks then taper`,`Mild ileocaecal only; less effective in colonic disease`],[`Prednisolone`,`40 mg/day orally`,`Taper over 8-12 weeks`,`Induction only; exclude TB and abscess first`],[`Azathioprine`,`2-2.5 mg/kg/day orally`,`Long-term maintenance`,`Myelosuppression (TPMT/NUDT15), pancreatitis, lymphoma, NMSC`],[`Methotrexate`,`25 mg IM/SC weekly x 16 weeks, then 15 mg weekly; folic acid 5 mg weekly`,`Long term`,`Teratogenic, hepatotoxic, pneumonitis, cytopenias`],[`Infliximab`,`5 mg/kg IV weeks 0, 2, 6 then every 8 weeks`,`Long term; trough-guided`,`TB and HBV reactivation, infusion reaction, lymphoma; not with undrained abscess`],[`Adalimumab`,`160 mg SC, 80 mg at week 2, then 40 mg every 2 weeks`,`Long term`,`Same as infliximab; injection-site reactions`],[`Ustekinumab`,`About 6 mg/kg IV once, then 90 mg SC every 8 weeks`,`Long term`,`Low infection risk; safe in elderly`],[`Risankizumab`,`600 mg IV weeks 0, 4, 8, then 180-360 mg SC every 8 weeks`,`Long term`,`Raised liver enzymes; infections`],[`Upadacitinib`,`45 mg orally daily x 12 weeks, then 15-30 mg daily`,`Long term`,`Zoster, VTE, MACE, lipids; avoid in pregnancy`],[`ATT (ITB)`,`2HRZE + 4HRE by weight band under NTEP`,`6 months`,`Hepatotoxicity, optic neuritis (E), peripheral neuropathy (H - give pyridoxine)`]]}],redFlags:[`Colicky pain, distension and vomiting - intestinal obstruction`,`Fever with a tender right iliac fossa mass - intra-abdominal abscess`,`Peritonitis or free gas - perforation`,`Perianal pain and swelling - perianal abscess needing drainage`,`Severe flare: HBI above 16, tachycardia, fever, hypoalbuminaemia`,`Fever, cough, night sweats on anti-TNF or JAK therapy - TB reactivation`,`Pneumaturia or faecaluria - enterovesical fistula`],pearls:[`Crohn: transmural, skip lesions, granulomas, mouth to anus; smoking makes it worse.`,`Montreal: A (age) L (location) B (behaviour) p (perianal).`,`ITB: transverse ulcers, patulous valve, fewer than 4 segments, necrotic nodes, caseation. CD: longitudinal ulcers, perianal disease, comb sign, fat wrapping.`,`In doubt in India, ATT first - and judge it by colonoscopy at 2-3 months, not by symptoms.`,`No 5-ASA in Crohn disease (ECCO 2024).`,`Drain the abscess before anti-TNF; seton plus antibiotics plus infliximab for complex perianal fistula.`,`Steroids induce, never maintain; thiopurines and methotrexate maintain, not induce.`,`Ileocolonoscopy 6-12 months after resection; Rutgeerts i2 or more means escalate.`,`Smoking cessation is as powerful as an immunosuppressant in Crohn disease.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-crohns-disease-q1`,stem:`A 32-year-old man from Bihar has 5 months of right iliac fossa pain, fever, night sweats and weight loss. Colonoscopy shows transverse ulcers in the caecum with a gaping ileocaecal valve. CT shows necrotic mesenteric nodes. Biopsy shows large confluent granulomas; Xpert MTB/RIF is negative. What is the best next step?`,options:[`Start prednisolone 40 mg/day for Crohn disease`,`Start infliximab with isoniazid prophylaxis`,`Repeat colonoscopy after 6 months without treatment`,`Start standard ATT and reassess endoscopically at 2-3 months`],answer:3,explanation:`Short history, fever, transverse ulcers, patulous ileocaecal valve, necrotic nodes and large confluent granulomas strongly favour intestinal TB; a negative Xpert does not exclude it because sensitivity on biopsies is only about 10-30%. Standard ATT with endoscopic reassessment at 2-3 months is correct. Steroids or infliximab in untreated TB risk dissemination, and waiting without treatment delays care for a likely curable infection.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-crohns-disease-q2`,stem:`In the Montreal classification, a 25-year-old with ileocolonic Crohn disease and an enterovesical fistula plus a perianal fistula is classified as:`,options:[`A2 L3 B3p`,`A1 L3 B2p`,`A2 L2 B3`,`A3 L1 B3p`],answer:0,explanation:`Age 17-40 is A2, ileocolonic disease is L3, fistulising (penetrating) behaviour is B3, and perianal disease adds the modifier p, giving A2 L3 B3p. A1 is 16 or under and B2 denotes stricturing disease. L2 is colonic only, and A3 is over 40 with L1 ileal only, so the other codes misstate age, site or behaviour.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-crohns-disease-q3`,stem:`A patient with Crohn disease has a painful fluctuant perianal swelling and fever. MRI shows a transsphincteric fistula with an associated abscess. What should be done first?`,options:[`Start infliximab immediately`,`Examination under anaesthesia with abscess drainage and loose seton`,`Fistulotomy of the transsphincteric tract`,`Oral budesonide 9 mg daily`],answer:1,explanation:`Sepsis must be controlled before immunosuppression, so EUA with drainage and a loose draining seton comes first, followed by antibiotics and then anti-TNF. Starting infliximab with an undrained abscess risks severe sepsis. Fistulotomy of a transsphincteric tract risks incontinence in Crohn disease. Budesonide acts on ileocaecal mucosal disease and has no role in perianal fistula.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-crohns-disease-q4`,stem:`Which drug is NOT recommended by ECCO 2024 for induction or maintenance of remission in Crohn disease?`,options:[`Ustekinumab`,`Upadacitinib`,`Oral mesalamine`,`Risankizumab`],answer:2,explanation:`ECCO 2024 recommends against 5-ASA (mesalamine) for inducing or maintaining remission in Crohn disease because trials show no meaningful benefit over placebo. Ustekinumab (anti-IL-12/23), risankizumab (anti-IL-23 p19) and upadacitinib (JAK1 inhibitor) are all recommended for moderate-to-severe Crohn disease, including after anti-TNF failure.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-crohns-disease-q5`,stem:`A smoker had an ileocaecal resection for penetrating Crohn disease. Which strategy best reduces post-operative recurrence?`,options:[`Mesalamine 4 g/day and colonoscopy only if symptoms recur`,`Long-term oral prednisolone 10 mg daily`,`No treatment because resection is curative`,`Smoking cessation, early anti-TNF prophylaxis and ileocolonoscopy at 6-12 months`],answer:3,explanation:`Smoking and penetrating disease make him high risk, so ECCO 2024 advises smoking cessation, early post-operative prophylaxis with anti-TNF (with or without a thiopurine) and ileocolonoscopy at 6-12 months, escalating for Rutgeerts i2 or more. Surgery is not curative in Crohn disease, steroids do not maintain remission, and waiting for symptoms misses endoscopic recurrence, which is present in most patients at one year.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-crohns-disease-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Describe the clinical features, classification and management of Crohn disease. How will you differentiate it from intestinal tuberculosis in India?`,openingLines:[`Crohn disease is chronic relapsing transmural granulomatous inflammation that can affect any part of the gut from mouth to anus with skip lesions, most often the terminal ileum and caecum.`,`In India the ileocaecal region is also the commonest site of intestinal tuberculosis, so ITB must be excluded before immunosuppression, if necessary by an ATT trial with endoscopic reassessment.`],answer:[{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Pain, diarrhoea, weight loss, fever; RIF mass; obstruction; fistulae; perianal disease; growth failure.`,`EIMs: arthritis, uveitis, EN, PG, gallstones, oxalate stones.`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`Montreal A1-3, L1-4, B1-3, p.`,`Activity: CDAI, HBI; endoscopic SES-CD; Rutgeerts post-op.`]},{heading:`ITB versus CD`,points:[`Clinical: fever, short history, ascites (ITB) versus perianal, EIMs (CD).`,`Endoscopy: transverse ulcers, patulous valve, under 4 segments versus longitudinal ulcers, cobblestoning, skip lesions.`,`CT: necrotic nodes versus comb sign, fat wrapping.`,`Histology and microbiology: caseation, confluent granulomas, AFB, Xpert, culture.`,`Empirical ATT with endoscopic response at 2-3 months.`]},{heading:`Medical management`,points:[`Stop smoking; budesonide for mild ileocaecal; prednisolone for moderate-severe induction.`,`Maintenance: azathioprine or methotrexate.`,`Advanced: anti-TNF (with azathioprine), ustekinumab, vedolizumab, risankizumab, upadacitinib after TB and HBV screen.`,`Nutrition: EEN in children; micronutrients.`]},{heading:`Surgery and complications`,points:[`Abscess drainage, seton for fistula, stricture dilatation or strictureplasty, bowel-sparing resection.`,`Post-op prophylaxis in high risk; colonoscopy at 6-12 months.`]}],mustDraw:[`ITB versus CD comparison table`,`Flow chart for an ileocaecal ulcer: work-up, ATT trial, reassessment at 2-3 months`],markSplit:[{part:`Clinical features and classification`,marks:3},{part:`ITB versus CD`,marks:3},{part:`Management`,marks:4}],keywords:[`Montreal`,`ITB`,`ATT trial`,`anti-TNF`,`perianal fistula`,`ECCO 2024`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-crohns-disease-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`chart-flow`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Draw a flow chart for the evaluation of a patient with ileocaecal ulcers in India.`,openingLines:[`Ileocaecal ulcers in India are most often due to intestinal tuberculosis or Crohn disease, with lymphoma, amoebiasis, Yersinia and NSAIDs as less common causes.`,`The evaluation combines clinical, endoscopic, radiological, histological and microbiological features, with an ATT trial as the arbiter when doubt remains.`],answer:[{heading:`Initial clinical evaluation`,points:[`History: fever, night sweats, TB contact, duration, perianal disease, extra-intestinal manifestations.`,`Chest X-ray, HIV test, IGRA, CRP, albumin.`]},{heading:`Endoscopy and imaging`,points:[`Ileocolonoscopy with biopsies: histology, AFB, Xpert MTB/RIF, MGIT culture.`,`CT or MR enterography: necrotic nodes and short segment versus comb sign, fat wrapping, skip lesions.`]},{heading:`Decision branches`,points:[`Definite TB (caseation, AFB, Xpert or culture positive): ATT for 6 months.`,`Definite CD (typical features, no TB evidence): CD treatment after latent TB screen.`]},{heading:`Indeterminate: ATT trial`,points:[`Start standard ATT under NTEP; check adherence.`,`Colonoscopy at 2-3 months: mucosal healing means ITB - complete 6 months.`,`No healing means CD - stop ATT and start CD therapy.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart from ileocaecal ulcer to ITB or CD with the ATT-trial branch`],markSplit:[{part:`Initial evaluation`,marks:2},{part:`Decision branches`,marks:2},{part:`ATT trial and reassessment`,marks:1}],keywords:[`ileocaecal ulcer`,`Xpert`,`IGRA`,`ATT trial`,`mucosal healing`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Inflammatory bowel disease`,`Gordon H et al. ECCO Guidelines on Therapeutics in Crohn's Disease: Medical Treatment, J Crohns Colitis 2024`,`Adamina M et al. ECCO Guidelines on Therapeutics in Crohn's Disease: Surgical Treatment, J Crohns Colitis 2024`,`Kedia S et al. Differentiating Crohn's disease from intestinal tuberculosis, World J Gastroenterol 2019`,`Pratap Mouli V et al. Endoscopic and clinical responses to anti-tubercular therapy can differentiate intestinal tuberculosis from Crohn's disease, Aliment Pharmacol Ther 2017`,`Index-TB Guidelines: Guidelines on extrapulmonary tuberculosis for India, ICMR 2016`,`National TB Elimination Programme (NTEP) Technical and Operational Guidelines, MoHFW 2023`,`Turner D et al. STRIDE-II, Gastroenterology 2021`]});var r=[];r.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-colorectal-cancer-screening`,title:`Colorectal polyps, cancer and screening`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Colorectal cancer (CRC)** is an adenocarcinoma of the colon or rectum that arises over **10-15 years** from a precursor **adenoma or serrated polyp** (adenoma-carcinoma sequence), so it is preventable by **screening from age 45** (annual **FIT** or **colonoscopy every 10 years**), polyp removal and risk-stratified **surveillance**, with **Lynch syndrome and FAP** as the key hereditary forms.`,keywords:[`CRC`,`colon cancer`,`rectal cancer`,`adenomatous polyp`,`sessile serrated lesion`,`adenoma-carcinoma sequence`,`FIT`,`faecal immunochemical test`,`gFOBT`,`colonoscopy`,`CT colonography`,`multitarget stool DNA`,`Lynch syndrome`,`HNPCC`,`FAP`,`MUTYH`,`CEA`,`TNM`,`Dukes`,`CAPOX`,`FOLFOX`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology (global and Indian)`,points:[`**Colorectal cancer** is a malignant epithelial tumour of the colon or rectum; over **95% are adenocarcinomas**, the rest neuroendocrine, lymphoma, GIST or squamous (anal canal). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Global burden**: about **1.9 million new cases and 0.9 million deaths** a year - **third commonest cancer and second commonest cause of cancer death** worldwide. [GLOBOCAN 2022]`,`**India**: age-standardised incidence is low (roughly a tenth of Western rates), but absolute numbers are large and rising with urbanisation, and colorectal cancer is among the leading cancers in **urban registries**. [ICMR-NCRP 2020]`,`**Young-onset disease** is a distinctive Indian pattern: hospital series show a much larger share of patients **under 40-45 years** than in the West, more **rectal** primaries, signet-ring/mucinous histology and **later stage** at presentation. [Indian hospital series; Harrison 22e]`,`**Early-onset CRC (under 50)** is rising globally by about **1-2% a year** - this drove the lowering of the screening start age to **45** in the USA. [USPSTF 2021]`,`**Right-sided (proximal) cancers** rise with age and in women, are more often **MSI-high, BRAF-mutant** and serrated-pathway; **left-sided** cancers are commoner overall and in the young. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Stage drives survival**: 5-year relative survival is about **90% localised, 70-75% regional, 13-15% distant** - which is why detecting polyps and early cancer saves lives. [SEER 2024]`,`**India has no organised CRC screening programme**; NP-NCD population screening covers oral, breast and cervical cancer only, so CRC detection is opportunistic and symptom-driven. [MoHFW NP-NCD 2023]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Age** is the strongest risk factor: over 85% of cases occur after 50, but incidence in 45-49-year-olds now approaches that of 50-54-year-olds in the 1990s. [USPSTF 2021]`,`**Family history**: one first-degree relative (FDR) with CRC doubles risk; risk is higher if the relative was **under 60** or there are **two or more FDRs**. [USMSTF 2017 family risk]`,`**Hereditary syndromes** cause about **5%**: **Lynch syndrome** (about 3%), **FAP** (under 1%), **MUTYH-associated polyposis**, Peutz-Jeghers, juvenile polyposis and serrated polyposis. [NCCN 2024]`,`**Inflammatory bowel disease**: risk rises with **extent and duration (8-10 years)** of ulcerative or Crohn colitis, and is highest with **primary sclerosing cholangitis**. [ACG 2025 UC]`,`**Diet and lifestyle**: **processed meat (IARC group 1)**, red meat (group 2A), alcohol, smoking, **obesity**, physical inactivity and **type 2 diabetes** each raise risk modestly. [IARC 2015; WCRF 2018]`,`**Protective**: dietary **fibre**, whole grains, calcium, physical activity and **aspirin** (long-term use lowers CRC incidence after about 10 years). [WCRF 2018; Harrison 22e]`,`**Other associations**: **Streptococcus gallolyticus (bovis) bacteraemia or endocarditis**, acromegaly, ureterosigmoidostomy, prior **pelvic radiotherapy** and childhood abdominal radiation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Personal history** of advanced adenoma, sessile serrated lesion or prior CRC places the patient in **surveillance**, not screening. [USMSTF 2020]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology: the three molecular pathways`,points:[`**Chromosomal instability (CIN) pathway** - about **70-85%**: **APC** loss (Wnt/beta-catenin activation) starts the adenoma, then **KRAS** activation, 18q/SMAD4 loss and **TP53** loss give carcinoma (**Vogelstein sequence**). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Microsatellite instability (MSI) pathway** - about **15%**: loss of **mismatch repair** (MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2) leaves thousands of replication errors; in Lynch it is germline, in sporadic cases it is **MLH1 promoter hypermethylation**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Serrated pathway** - about **15-30%**: **BRAF V600E** and **CpG island methylator phenotype (CIMP)** in **sessile serrated lesions**, mostly right colon, flat and easily missed. [WHO GI tumours 5e 2019]`,`**Dwell time**: an adenoma takes on average **10-15 years** to become cancer - the window that makes screening effective and justifies a **10-year** interval after a normal colonoscopy. [USPSTF 2021]`,`**Advanced adenoma** = **10 mm or more**, **villous component** (tubulovillous or villous) or **high-grade dysplasia**; these carry the highest cancer risk and set the shortest surveillance intervals. [USMSTF 2020]`,`**MSI-high tumours** are right-sided, mucinous, with **tumour-infiltrating lymphocytes**, better stage-for-stage prognosis, **no benefit from 5-FU alone in stage II**, and dramatic response to **immune checkpoint inhibitors**. [NCCN 2024]`,`**Why symptoms differ by side**: the right colon is wide with liquid stool, so cancers grow silently and bleed occultly (**iron-deficiency anaemia**); the left colon is narrow with formed stool, so cancers cause **obstruction and altered bowel habit**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Spread**: lymphatic to mesenteric nodes, portal venous to the **liver (commonest metastatic site)**; low rectal cancers drain via systemic veins and can metastasise first to the **lung**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification: polyps and TNM staging`,points:[`**Polyp types**: **neoplastic** (tubular, tubulovillous, villous adenoma; sessile serrated lesion; traditional serrated adenoma) versus **non-neoplastic** (hyperplastic, inflammatory, hamartomatous). [WHO GI tumours 5e 2019]`,`**Paris classification** describes shape - **0-Ip** pedunculated, **0-Is** sessile, **0-IIa/b/c** flat or depressed; depressed (0-IIc) lesions have the highest submucosal invasion risk. [Paris endoscopic classification 2003]`,`**NICE (NBI International Colorectal Endoscopic) classification**: type 1 hyperplastic, **type 2 adenoma**, **type 3 deep submucosal invasion** - type 3 goes to surgery, not snare. [ESGE 2017]`,`**Malignant polyp**: cancer invading the submucosa (pT1); endoscopic resection is curative only with **clear margin (over 1 mm), grade 1-2, no lymphovascular invasion, no tumour budding** and **sm1 depth under 1000 micrometres** in sessile lesions. [ESGE 2017; NCCN 2024]`,`**TNM (AJCC 8th)**: **T1** submucosa, **T2** muscularis propria, **T3** into pericolorectal tissue, **T4a** visceral peritoneum, **T4b** adjacent organ; **N1** 1-3 nodes (N1c tumour deposits), **N2** 4 or more; **M1a** one organ, **M1b** more than one, **M1c** peritoneum. [AJCC 8e]`,`**Stage groups**: **I** T1-2 N0; **II** T3-4 N0 (IIA T3, IIB T4a, IIC T4b); **III** any node-positive; **IV** distant metastasis. [AJCC 8e]`,`**Modified Dukes** (still asked in viva): **A** confined to wall, **B** through wall, **C** nodes, **D** distant metastasis. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**At least 12 lymph nodes** must be examined for accurate N staging; fewer than 12 counts as a high-risk feature in stage II. [NCCN 2024]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Most polyps and early cancers are asymptomatic** - symptoms usually mean established cancer, which is the case for screening. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Right-sided cancer**: fatigue and breathlessness from **iron-deficiency anaemia**, vague right-sided pain, weight loss, a mass; bowel habit often normal. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Left-sided cancer**: **change in bowel habit** (constipation or alternating), colicky pain, **blood mixed with stool**, narrowing of stool calibre and subacute obstruction. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rectal cancer**: **fresh bleeding per rectum**, **tenesmus**, sense of incomplete evacuation, mucus discharge - commonly mislabelled as **haemorrhoids** in Indian OPDs. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**Emergency presentations** (about 20%): **large-bowel obstruction**, perforation with peritonitis, or massive bleeding - these carry worse survival. [NICE NG151 2020]`,`**Unexplained iron-deficiency anaemia** in a man or post-menopausal woman needs **both upper endoscopy and colonoscopy** whatever the bowel symptoms. [BSG 2021 IDA]`,`**Young-onset red flags**: any adult under 50 with **rectal bleeding, change in bowel habit, abdominal pain or IDA** must be investigated - age is not reassurance. [ACG 2021 CRC screening]`,`Ask about **family history** (age at diagnosis of each relative, endometrial, ovarian, gastric, urinary tract cancers for Lynch), IBD, prior polyps and previous colonoscopy. [NCCN 2024]`,`**Urgent (2-week) colonoscopy referral**: **FIT 10 micrograms Hb/g or more**, rectal or abdominal mass, unexplained IDA, or rectal bleeding with change in bowel habit over 40-50. [NICE DG56 2023; NICE NG12 2015 updated 2023]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Pallor**, koilonychia and angular stomatitis point to chronic occult blood loss from a right-sided lesion. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Palpable mass**: right iliac fossa mass (caecal cancer, differential ileocaecal TB, appendicular mass, Crohn) or left iliac fossa mass (sigmoid cancer, diverticular phlegmon). [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Hepatomegaly** with a hard, irregular, **nodular liver** suggests metastases; jaundice is late. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Digital rectal examination** reaches about **7-8 cm** and detects many low rectal cancers: note distance from anal verge, fixity, circumferential extent and blood on the glove. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Distension with tinkling bowel sounds** and an empty rectum indicates large-bowel obstruction; a **competent ileocaecal valve** gives a closed loop with caecal tenderness - risk of caecal perforation. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Signs of spread**: **Virchow node** (left supraclavicular), **Sister Mary Joseph nodule** (umbilical), ascites, **Blumer shelf** on rectal examination (pelvic peritoneal deposit). [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Syndromic clues**: **perioral pigmentation** (Peutz-Jeghers), **CHRPE** on fundoscopy, osteomas and epidermoid cysts (**Gardner variant of FAP**), sebaceous tumours (**Muir-Torre variant of Lynch**). [NCCN 2024]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Haemorrhoids and fissure**: bright red blood on the paper or dripping after stool, no change in bowel habit; still needs examination and, over 40-45 or with risk factors, colonoscopy. [ACG 2021]`,`**Ileocaecal tuberculosis** (common in India): right iliac fossa mass, fever, weight loss, subacute obstruction - colonoscopic biopsy with **histology, AFB, GeneXpert and culture** separates it from caecal cancer. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**Crohn disease / ulcerative colitis**: younger patient, chronic bloody diarrhoea, extra-intestinal features, continuous or skip inflammation on endoscopy - see the Ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease topics. [ECCO 2024]`,`**Amoebic colitis and amoeboma**: a caecal mass with dysentery that resolves on metronidazole - biopsy any persistent mass. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diverticular disease**: left iliac fossa pain, fever, CT shows diverticula and pericolic fat stranding; colonoscopy after complicated diverticulitis excludes a masquerading cancer - see the Diverticular disease topic. [AGA 2021]`,`**Irritable bowel syndrome**: long history, no weight loss, no bleeding, no anaemia, normal faecal calprotectin - new symptoms after 45-50 are not IBS until colonoscopy is normal. [Rome IV]`,`**Ischaemic colitis**: older patient with sudden crampy left-sided pain followed within 24 hours by bloody stool; segmental mucosal oedema on colonoscopy - see the Mesenteric ischaemia topic. [ACG 2015 ischaemic colitis]`]},{heading:`Investigations: diagnosis, staging and molecular testing`,points:[`**First line in OPD**: CBC (**microcytic anaemia**), **ferritin under 30 ng/mL** confirms iron deficiency (under 45 is used by some guidelines), LFT, creatinine and DRE. [BSG 2021 IDA]`,`**Faecal immunochemical test (FIT)** detects human globin, needs **one sample**, no diet restriction; positive threshold commonly **20 micrograms Hb/g faeces** for screening (UK programme uses higher, 80-120). [USPSTF 2021; NHS BCSP]`,`**FIT in symptomatic patients**: a value of **10 micrograms Hb/g or more** triggers urgent suspected-cancer referral; a negative FIT with no anaemia or mass lowers risk to under 1%. [NICE DG56 2023; BSG/ACPGBI 2022]`,`**Colonoscopy with biopsy** is the diagnostic gold standard - it localises, biopsies, tattoos and removes synchronous polyps (**synchronous cancers in 3-5%**). [Harrison 22e]`,`**CT colonography** is the alternative when colonoscopy is incomplete or unsafe; polyps of **6 mm or more** need follow-up colonoscopy. [ACR 2023]`,`**Staging**: **contrast CT chest-abdomen-pelvis** for all; **MRI pelvis** for rectal cancer (T stage, **circumferential resection margin**, extramural venous invasion, nodes); **PET-CT** only for equivocal or potentially resectable metastases. [NCCN 2024; ESMO 2017 rectal]`,`**CEA** is a **baseline and follow-up marker, not a diagnostic or screening test** - it is raised in smokers, cirrhosis, IBD and pancreatitis. [ASCO 2013 surveillance]`,`**Mismatch repair IHC (MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2) or MSI testing on every CRC** (universal tumour testing) screens for Lynch and guides immunotherapy; loss of MLH1 is followed by **BRAF V600E or MLH1 methylation testing** to separate sporadic from Lynch. [NCCN 2024]`,`**Metastatic disease**: test **KRAS/NRAS (extended RAS), BRAF V600E, HER2 and MSI**; RAS-mutant tumours do not respond to anti-EGFR antibodies. [ESMO 2023 mCRC]`,`**Pre-chemotherapy DPYD genotyping** (or DPD phenotyping) before 5-FU/capecitabine prevents fatal toxicity in DPD-deficient patients. [EMA 2020; NCCN 2024]`]},{heading:`Screening: who, when and with what`,points:[`**Average risk**: screen from **45 to 75 years** (grade B for 45-49, grade A for 50-75); **76-85** selectively by health and prior screening; **not after 85**. [USPSTF 2021]`,`**Stool-based options**: **annual FIT** (preferred, cheap, sensitivity about 74-79% for cancer), annual high-sensitivity guaiac FOBT, or **multitarget stool DNA-FIT every 1-3 years**. [USPSTF 2021]`,`**Direct visualisation options**: **colonoscopy every 10 years**, **CT colonography every 5 years**, **flexible sigmoidoscopy every 5 years** (or every 10 years plus annual FIT). [USPSTF 2021]`,`**Any positive non-colonoscopy test must be followed by colonoscopy** - ideally within **6-9 months**; delays beyond this raise the risk of advanced cancer. [USMSTF 2017; ACG 2021]`,`**One FDR with CRC or advanced adenoma under 60, or two FDRs at any age**: colonoscopy from **age 40 or 10 years before the youngest affected relative**, every **5 years**. [USMSTF 2017 family risk]`,`**One FDR diagnosed at 60 or older**: start at **45** as average risk, any modality. [ACG 2021]`,`**IBD (extensive or left-sided colitis, Crohn colitis over a third of colon)**: surveillance colonoscopy from **8 years** after symptom onset, every 1-5 years by risk; **annually from diagnosis if PSC**. [ACG 2025 UC; ECCO 2023]`,`**Indian practical approach**: no national programme, so offer **FIT annually from 45-50** or colonoscopy where available, prioritise high-risk families, and **investigate every symptomatic young patient** rather than wait for screening age. [ICMR STW 2023; expert consensus]`,`**Colonoscopy quality** determines protection: **adenoma detection rate at least 35%** (40% men, 30% women), withdrawal time **8 minutes or more**, caecal intubation **at least 95%**, adequate bowel prep **at least 90%**; each 1% rise in ADR lowers interval cancer risk by about 3%. [ASGE/ACG quality indicators 2024; Corley NEJM 2014]`,`**Counsel on screening choice**: "the best test is the one that gets done" - FIT is home-based and annual, colonoscopy is one-step but invasive; both reduce mortality. [USPSTF 2021; ACG 2021]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Bowel preparation**: low-residue diet the day before; **split-dose PEG-electrolyte 4 L** (2 L the evening before, 2 L 4-6 hours before procedure, finishing 2 hours before); low-volume PEG-ascorbate 2 L is an alternative. [ESGE 2019 bowel prep; USMSTF 2025 bowel prep]`,`**Avoid sodium phosphate** preparations in **CKD, heart failure, elderly and on ACE inhibitors/diuretics** - risk of acute phosphate nephropathy. [ESGE 2019]`,`**Diabetes on prep day**: halve basal insulin the evening before, omit prandial insulin and oral agents on the clear-liquid day, stop **SGLT2 inhibitors 3 days before** (euglycaemic DKA). [ADA 2026; ESGE 2019]`,`**Antithrombotics**: continue **aspirin**; for high-risk polypectomy stop **clopidogrel 5-7 days**, **warfarin 5 days** (INR check), **DOACs 2-3 days** (longer in renal impairment); cold-snare polypectomy of small polyps is safe on most antiplatelets. [BSG/ESGE 2021 antithrombotics]`,`**Polypectomy**: **cold snare** for polyps **under 10 mm** (and 10-19 mm non-pedunculated in many centres); EMR or ESD for large flat lesions; send all for histology. [USMSTF 2020 polypectomy; ESGE 2024]`,`**Iron-deficiency anaemia**: oral **ferrous sulphate 200 mg (65 mg elemental iron) once daily or on alternate days** while the cause is found; IV ferric carboxymaltose if intolerant or before surgery. [BSG 2021 IDA]`,`**Lifestyle counselling**: stop smoking, limit alcohol, avoid processed meat, aim for **150 minutes of moderate activity a week**, maintain BMI under 23 (Asian cut-off), increase fibre to 25-30 g/day. [WCRF 2018; ICMR-NIN 2024]`,`**Aspirin for primary prevention of CRC is no longer recommended** for the general population (bleeding risk); it is reserved for **Lynch syndrome** after specialist discussion. [USPSTF 2022; NCCN 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD: surgery, oncology and emergencies`,points:[`**Colon cancer stage I-III**: **segmental colectomy with en bloc lymphadenectomy** (right hemicolectomy, left hemicolectomy, sigmoid colectomy); complete mesocolic excision with central vascular ligation in specialist centres. [NCCN 2024]`,`**Adjuvant chemotherapy stage III**: **CAPOX for 3 months** (low-risk T1-3 N1) or **CAPOX 3-6 months / FOLFOX 6 months** (high-risk T4 or N2) - the **IDEA** collaboration. [IDEA NEJM 2018; ESMO 2020 colon]`,`**Stage II**: chemotherapy only if high-risk (**T4, obstruction/perforation, under 12 nodes, LVI, poorly differentiated**) and **not for dMMR/MSI-H** tumours. [NCCN 2024]`,`**Rectal cancer**: **total mesorectal excision (TME)**; locally advanced (T3-4 or N+) gets **total neoadjuvant therapy** (chemoradiation or short-course radiotherapy plus chemotherapy) before surgery; **watch-and-wait** for complete clinical response. [NCCN 2024; ESMO 2017 rectal]`,`**dMMR locally advanced rectal cancer**: PD-1 blockade with **dostarlimab** achieved **100% clinical complete response** in the first cohort, allowing organ preservation; checkpoint inhibitor is now an NCCN option. [Cercek NEJM 2022; NCCN 2024]`,`**Metastatic CRC**: **FOLFOX or FOLFIRI (or CAPOX)** plus **bevacizumab**, or plus **cetuximab/panitumumab** for **RAS/BRAF wild-type left-sided** tumours; **resect liver/lung metastases** when feasible (5-year survival 40-50%). [ESMO 2023 mCRC]`,`**MSI-H/dMMR metastatic CRC**: first-line **pembrolizumab** (KEYNOTE-177) or **nivolumab plus ipilimumab** (CheckMate 8HW). [ESMO 2023 mCRC; NCCN 2025]`,`**Malignant large-bowel obstruction**: resuscitate, CT, then resection (Hartmann procedure or primary anastomosis) or **self-expanding metal stent** as bridge to surgery or palliation in left-sided disease. [ESGE 2020 stenting; WSES 2018]`,`**Monitor during chemotherapy**: CBC before each cycle, **oxaliplatin neuropathy** (cold-induced), hand-foot syndrome with capecitabine, diarrhoea and neutropenic fever (admit if temperature 38.3 C or more with ANC under 500). [NCCN 2024]`,`**Insurance link**: colonoscopy, surgery and chemotherapy are covered under **Ayushman Bharat PM-JAY** packages for eligible families; cancer care at **Tertiary Cancer Care Centres** and **State Cancer Institutes**. [NHA PM-JAY HBP 2022]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: colonoscopy is acceptable in the **second trimester** when results change management; CRC in pregnancy presents late because symptoms are blamed on pregnancy; CEA is unreliable. [ASGE 2012 pregnancy; Harrison 22e]`,`**Elderly (76-85)**: screen only if fit, life expectancy over 10 years and not previously screened; **stop surveillance** when risk of the procedure outweighs benefit. [USPSTF 2021; USMSTF 2020]`,`**CKD**: use **PEG preparations**; avoid sodium phosphate and magnesium-based preps; dose-adjust capecitabine (reduce to 75% if CrCl 30-50, avoid if under 30). [ESGE 2019; capecitabine SmPC]`,`**Diabetes**: higher CRC risk and higher risk of poor prep; prescribe extended prep and adjust hypoglycaemics as above. [ADA 2026]`,`**Children and adolescents**: begin colonoscopy in **FAP at 10-12 years**, juvenile polyposis at 12-15 years and Peutz-Jeghers at around 8 years. [NCCN 2024; ESPGHAN 2019 polyposis]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: one-time **FIT** with colonoscopy referral for positives is the most cost-effective model; prioritise colonoscopy for symptomatic and high-risk patients. [WHO 2023 cancer screening; expert consensus]`,`**Women with Lynch syndrome**: offer **risk-reducing hysterectomy and bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy** after childbearing (endometrial cancer risk up to 40-60% in MLH1/MSH2 carriers). [NCCN 2024]`]},{heading:`Hereditary syndromes: Lynch and FAP`,points:[`**Lynch syndrome**: autosomal dominant germline mutation in **MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2 or EPCAM**; lifetime CRC risk up to **50-60%** (MLH1/MSH2), mostly right-sided, at a mean age of 45; also endometrial, ovarian, gastric, small bowel, urothelial and biliary cancers. [NCCN 2024]`,`**Amsterdam II criteria (3-2-1-1-0)**: **3** relatives with a Lynch cancer (one an FDR of the other two), **2** successive generations, **1** diagnosed under 50, FAP excluded, tumours verified. [Amsterdam II 1999]`,`**Revised Bethesda criteria** identify tumours to test (CRC under 50, synchronous/metachronous Lynch cancers, MSI histology under 60) - largely replaced by **universal MMR testing of all CRC**. [NCCN 2024]`,`**Lynch surveillance**: colonoscopy **every 1-2 years** from **20-25 years** (MLH1, MSH2, EPCAM) or **30-35 years** (MSH6, PMS2); consider upper endoscopy every 2-4 years from 30-40 years. [NCCN 2024]`,`**Aspirin in Lynch**: **CAPP2** showed about **35-50% fewer CRCs** with aspirin 600 mg daily after 2 or more years; lower doses are being compared (CAPP3), so discuss dose individually. [CAPP2 Lancet 2020; NCCN 2024]`,`**FAP**: germline **APC** mutation, **100 to thousands of adenomas**, near **100% cancer by age 40** without colectomy; flexible sigmoidoscopy or colonoscopy **annually from 10-12 years**; **prophylactic colectomy** (IPAA or IRA) in late teens to mid-20s. [NCCN 2024]`,`**FAP extracolonic**: **duodenal/periampullary adenomas** (leading cause of death after colectomy; Spigelman staging guides upper endoscopy), **desmoid tumours**, thyroid cancer, CHRPE, osteomas (**Gardner**) and medulloblastoma (**Turcot**). [NCCN 2024]`,`**Attenuated FAP** (10-99 adenomas, later onset) and **MUTYH-associated polyposis** (**autosomal recessive**, 10-100s of adenomas) - test patients with **10 or more cumulative adenomas** for a polyposis gene panel. [NCCN 2024; ACG 2015 hereditary]`,`**Genetics referral**: dMMR/MSI-H tumour without MLH1 methylation or BRAF mutation, Amsterdam criteria, **10 or more adenomas**, CRC under 50, or known familial mutation - **cascade testing of relatives**. [NCCN 2024]`]},{heading:`Complications, prognosis and post-polypectomy surveillance`,points:[`**Disease complications**: obstruction, perforation (proximal caecal blow-out from closed loop), bleeding, fistula (colovesical), and liver/lung/peritoneal metastases. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Colonoscopy complications**: perforation about **1 in 2000-3000 diagnostic** (higher with therapy), **post-polypectomy bleeding 1-2%** (up to 2 weeks later), post-polypectomy coagulation syndrome. [ASGE 2019 adverse events]`,`**USMSTF 2020 intervals after high-quality colonoscopy**: normal - **10 years**; **1-2 tubular adenomas under 10 mm - 7-10 years**; 3-4 adenomas under 10 mm - 3-5 years; **5-10 adenomas or any advanced adenoma - 3 years**; **more than 10 adenomas - 1 year** (genetic testing). [USMSTF 2020]`,`**Serrated lesions**: hyperplastic under 10 mm in rectosigmoid - 10 years; **1-2 SSLs under 10 mm - 5-10 years**; 3-4 SSLs - 3-5 years; **SSL 10 mm or more, SSL with dysplasia or traditional serrated adenoma - 3 years**. [USMSTF 2020]`,`**Piecemeal EMR of a lesion 20 mm or more**: check the scar at **6 months**, then 1 year, then 3 years. [USMSTF 2020]`,`**After curative CRC resection**: colonoscopy at **1 year** (or 3-6 months if the colon was not cleared pre-operatively), then **3 years**, then every **5 years**. [USMSTF 2016 post-resection]`,`**Stage II-III follow-up**: history, examination and **CEA every 3-6 months for 2 years, then 6-monthly to 5 years**; **CT chest-abdomen-pelvis every 6-12 months for up to 5 years**. [NCCN 2024; ASCO 2013]`,`**Prognosis**: by stage, 5-year survival about **I over 90%, II 80%, III 60-70%, IV 10-15%**; adverse factors are T4, N2, obstruction/perforation, LVI, high CEA and BRAF V600E. [AJCC 8e; SEER 2024]`,`**Survivorship care**: monitor CEA and imaging schedule, manage **oxaliplatin neuropathy**, bowel dysfunction after anterior resection (**LARS**), stoma care, sexual and urinary function, and psychological support. [ASCO 2019 survivorship]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2021-2026)`,points:[`**USPSTF 2021** and **ACG 2021** lowered the average-risk start age from 50 to **45**; ACS had done so in 2018. [USPSTF 2021; ACG 2021]`,`**USMSTF 2020** lengthened the interval for **1-2 small tubular adenomas to 7-10 years** (from 5-10) and added interval rules for **serrated lesions** and piecemeal EMR. [USMSTF 2020]`,`**USPSTF 2022** withdrew the recommendation to start **low-dose aspirin** for primary prevention of CVD and CRC in 50-59-year-olds - net benefit too small. [USPSTF 2022]`,`**Blood-based screening**: a cell-free DNA test (Shield) was **FDA-approved in 2024** for average-risk adults 45+, but its sensitivity for **advanced adenomas is only about 13%**, so it is for people who decline FIT or colonoscopy, not a replacement. [ECLIPSE NEJM 2024; FDA 2024]`,`**Next-generation multitarget stool DNA test** (higher specificity) was FDA-approved in 2024; intervals remain **1-3 years**. [FDA 2024]`,`**Colonoscopy quality indicators 2024** raised the **adenoma detection rate target to 35%** (from 25%) and added a **sessile serrated detection rate of at least 6%**. [ASGE/ACG 2024]`,`**Immunotherapy for dMMR disease**: **nivolumab plus ipilimumab** first-line in MSI-H metastatic CRC (CheckMate 8HW 2024) and **PD-1 blockade for dMMR rectal cancer** (organ preservation) have entered NCCN 2024-2025. [NCCN 2025]`,`**BRAF V600E metastatic CRC**: first-line **encorafenib + cetuximab + mFOLFOX6** improved response and survival (BREAKWATER 2025). [BREAKWATER NEJM 2025]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic long question: "**Screening for colorectal cancer**" - write risk stratification, screening modalities with intervals, USMSTF surveillance table and Indian context. [DNB pattern]`,`**Commonest mistake**: labelling rectal bleeding as piles without a **DRE and proctoscopy** - a missed low rectal cancer in a 35-year-old is the classic Indian scenario. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**CEA is never a screening test** and a normal CEA does not exclude cancer. [ASCO 2013]`,`**Adenoma versus hyperplastic**: only adenomas (and SSLs) are precancerous; small rectosigmoid hyperplastic polyps carry no excess risk. [USMSTF 2020]`,`**Strep bovis (gallolyticus) endocarditis** demands colonoscopy - a favourite viva link. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lynch is the commonest hereditary CRC** and is detected by **universal MMR IHC**, not by family history alone (Amsterdam misses about half). [NCCN 2024]`,`**Positive FIT means colonoscopy** - repeating FIT after a positive result is wrong. [USMSTF 2017]`,`Know **FAP vs Lynch**: polyps hundreds vs few; APC vs MMR genes; left/diffuse vs right; prophylactic colectomy vs colonoscopic surveillance. [NCCN 2024]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Screening tests for average-risk adults (45-75 years)`,columns:[`Test`,`Interval`,`Strengths`,`Limitations`],rows:[[`FIT (faecal immunochemical test)`,`Every year`,`Single home sample, no diet change, cheap; cancer sensitivity about 74-79%`,`Misses many advanced adenomas; must be repeated yearly; positive needs colonoscopy`],[`High-sensitivity guaiac FOBT`,`Every year`,`Cheap and widely known`,`Diet and drug restrictions (red meat, vitamin C, NSAIDs); three samples; lower sensitivity`],[`Multitarget stool DNA-FIT`,`Every 1-3 years`,`Higher cancer sensitivity (about 92%) than FIT`,`More false positives; costly; limited availability in India`],[`Colonoscopy`,`Every 10 years`,`Diagnostic and therapeutic in one step; reference standard`,`Bowel prep, sedation, perforation about 1 in 2000-3000, operator-dependent`],[`CT colonography`,`Every 5 years`,`No sedation; also images extracolonic organs`,`Radiation; prep still needed; polyps 6 mm or more need colonoscopy`],[`Flexible sigmoidoscopy`,`Every 5 years (or 10 years plus annual FIT)`,`Short prep (enema), no sedation`,`Misses proximal (right-sided) lesions`],[`Blood cell-free DNA test`,`Every 3 years (manufacturer)`,`Simple blood draw`,`Advanced adenoma sensitivity about 13%; second line only`]]},{heading:`Post-polypectomy surveillance intervals (USMSTF 2020)`,columns:[`Finding at baseline colonoscopy`,`Next colonoscopy`],rows:[[`Normal colonoscopy`,`10 years`],[`Fewer than 20 hyperplastic polyps under 10 mm`,`10 years`],[`1-2 tubular adenomas under 10 mm`,`7-10 years`],[`3-4 tubular adenomas under 10 mm`,`3-5 years`],[`5-10 tubular adenomas under 10 mm`,`3 years`],[`Adenoma 10 mm or more, villous component or high-grade dysplasia`,`3 years`],[`More than 10 adenomas`,`1 year, and refer for genetic testing`],[`1-2 sessile serrated lesions under 10 mm`,`5-10 years`],[`SSL 10 mm or more, SSL with dysplasia, or traditional serrated adenoma`,`3 years`],[`Piecemeal resection of adenoma or SSL 20 mm or more`,`6 months, then 1 year, then 3 years`]]},{heading:`TNM stage grouping and treatment outline (AJCC 8th)`,columns:[`Stage`,`TNM`,`Primary treatment`,`Approximate 5-year survival`],rows:[[`0`,`Tis N0 M0`,`Endoscopic resection`,`Near 100%`],[`I`,`T1-T2 N0 M0`,`Surgery alone (local excision for selected T1)`,`Over 90%`],[`IIA-IIC`,`T3-T4 N0 M0`,`Surgery; adjuvant chemotherapy only if high-risk and pMMR`,`About 80%`],[`IIIA-IIIC`,`Any T, N1-N2, M0`,`Surgery plus CAPOX 3-6 months or FOLFOX 6 months; rectal - total neoadjuvant therapy then TME`,`About 60-70%`],[`IVA-IVC`,`Any T, any N, M1a-c`,`Systemic therapy by RAS/BRAF/MSI status; resect oligometastases`,`About 10-15% (40-50% after liver resection)`]]},{heading:`Lynch syndrome vs FAP vs MUTYH-associated polyposis`,columns:[`Feature`,`Lynch syndrome`,`FAP`,`MUTYH-associated polyposis`],rows:[[`Gene`,`MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2, EPCAM`,`APC`,`MUTYH (biallelic)`],[`Inheritance`,`Autosomal dominant`,`Autosomal dominant (25% de novo)`,`Autosomal recessive`],[`Polyp number`,`Few adenomas, accelerated progression`,`100 to thousands`,`10 to a few hundred`],[`Lifetime CRC risk`,`Up to 50-60% (MLH1/MSH2)`,`Near 100% by 40 without colectomy`,`About 40-90%`],[`Extracolonic`,`Endometrium, ovary, stomach, urothelium, small bowel`,`Duodenal adenoma, desmoid, thyroid, CHRPE, osteoma`,`Duodenal adenoma`],[`Surveillance start`,`Colonoscopy every 1-2 years from 20-25 (MLH1/MSH2) or 30-35 (MSH6/PMS2)`,`Annual sigmoidoscopy/colonoscopy from 10-12 years`,`Colonoscopy every 1-2 years from 25-30 years`],[`Definitive step`,`Surveillance; aspirin; hysterectomy/BSO after family complete`,`Prophylactic colectomy late teens to mid-20s`,`Colectomy if polyps uncontrollable`]]},{heading:`Drugs used in colorectal polyps and cancer`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`PEG-electrolyte bowel preparation`,`4 L oral split dose: 2 L evening before, 2 L 4-6 hours before procedure`,`Single preparation; finish 2 hours before colonoscopy`,`Nausea, bloating; aspiration risk in dysphagia; caution in obstruction`],[`Capecitabine (oral 5-FU prodrug)`,`1000 mg/m2 orally twice daily days 1-14 every 21 days with oxaliplatin (CAPOX); 1250 mg/m2 BD as monotherapy`,`Adjuvant 3-6 months`,`Hand-foot syndrome, diarrhoea; fatal toxicity in DPD deficiency; reduce dose if CrCl 30-50, avoid under 30; warfarin interaction`],[`Oxaliplatin`,`130 mg/m2 IV every 3 weeks (CAPOX) or 85 mg/m2 IV every 2 weeks (FOLFOX)`,`Adjuvant 3-6 months; stop if grade 2-3 neuropathy`,`Cold-induced and cumulative sensory neuropathy, allergy, myelosuppression`],[`5-fluorouracil with leucovorin (FOLFOX/FOLFIRI)`,`Leucovorin 400 mg/m2 IV, 5-FU 400 mg/m2 IV bolus then 2400 mg/m2 over 46 hours, every 2 weeks`,`12 cycles adjuvant (6 months); until progression in metastatic`,`Mucositis, diarrhoea, neutropenia, coronary vasospasm; DPD deficiency`],[`Irinotecan (FOLFIRI)`,`180 mg/m2 IV every 2 weeks`,`Metastatic disease, until progression`,`Early cholinergic diarrhoea (atropine), late diarrhoea (loperamide), neutropenia; reduce in UGT1A1*28 homozygotes`],[`Bevacizumab (anti-VEGF)`,`5 mg/kg IV every 2 weeks or 7.5 mg/kg every 3 weeks`,`With chemotherapy in metastatic disease`,`Hypertension, proteinuria, bleeding, bowel perforation, poor wound healing - stop 6 weeks before surgery`],[`Cetuximab / panitumumab (anti-EGFR)`,`Cetuximab 500 mg/m2 IV every 2 weeks; panitumumab 6 mg/kg IV every 2 weeks`,`RAS and BRAF wild-type, left-sided metastatic`,`Acneiform rash, hypomagnesaemia, infusion reaction; useless if RAS mutant`],[`Pembrolizumab (anti-PD-1)`,`200 mg IV every 3 weeks or 400 mg every 6 weeks`,`MSI-H/dMMR metastatic, up to 2 years`,`Immune-related colitis, hepatitis, thyroiditis, pneumonitis`],[`Aspirin (Lynch syndrome)`,`Oral daily; 600 mg used in CAPP2, lower doses under study`,`Long term, at least 2 years, specialist decision`,`GI bleeding, peptic ulcer; avoid in active ulcer or bleeding disorder`]]}],redFlags:[`Rectal bleeding with change in bowel habit, weight loss or a mass at any age`,`Unexplained iron-deficiency anaemia in a man or post-menopausal woman`,`FIT 10 micrograms Hb/g or more in a symptomatic patient`,`Abdominal distension, colicky pain and absolute constipation - large-bowel obstruction`,`Caecal tenderness on a distended abdomen - impending caecal perforation`,`Hard nodular liver or jaundice in a patient with bowel symptoms`,`Ten or more adenomas, or CRC under 50 years - refer to genetics`,`Streptococcus gallolyticus (bovis) bacteraemia or endocarditis`],pearls:[`Screen average risk from **45 to 75**: **FIT yearly or colonoscopy every 10 years** [USPSTF 2021].`,`**Positive FIT = colonoscopy within 6-9 months**, never a repeat FIT.`,`**Right side bleeds (anaemia), left side blocks (obstruction), rectum bleeds red with tenesmus.**`,`**Advanced adenoma = 10 mm, villous or high-grade dysplasia** - 3-year surveillance.`,`**Lynch 3-2-1** (Amsterdam II): 3 relatives, 2 generations, 1 under 50, FAP excluded.`,`**Every CRC gets MMR IHC** - universal testing finds Lynch and selects immunotherapy.`,`**CEA is for follow-up, not diagnosis.**`,`**Strep bovis endocarditis means colonoscopy.**`,`**dMMR tumours: no 5-FU alone in stage II; checkpoint inhibitors work dramatically.**`,`In India, **young age is not reassurance** - investigate rectal bleeding in the 30s.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-colorectal-cancer-screening-q1`,stem:`A 46-year-old man with no symptoms and no family history asks about bowel cancer screening. According to USPSTF 2021, which is the most appropriate advice?`,options:[`Screening is not needed until age 50`,`Annual FIT or colonoscopy every 10 years starting now`,`Serum CEA every year`,`Colonoscopy every 3 years starting now`],answer:1,explanation:`USPSTF 2021 recommends screening average-risk adults from 45 to 75, with annual FIT or colonoscopy every 10 years among the accepted options. Waiting until 50 reflects the outdated 2016 advice. CEA is a follow-up marker, not a screening test. A 3-year colonoscopy interval is used only after advanced adenomas, not for average-risk screening.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-colorectal-cancer-screening-q2`,stem:`A screening colonoscopy in a 55-year-old woman removes two tubular adenomas, 4 mm and 6 mm, with low-grade dysplasia. Bowel preparation was excellent and the caecum was reached. When should she have her next colonoscopy (USMSTF 2020)?`,options:[`1 year`,`3 years`,`7-10 years`,`No further colonoscopy`],answer:2,explanation:`USMSTF 2020 set 7-10 years for one or two tubular adenomas under 10 mm, lengthened from the earlier 5-10 years. One year is for more than 10 adenomas or piecemeal resection follow-up. Three years is for advanced adenomas (10 mm or more, villous or high-grade dysplasia) or 5-10 adenomas. Stopping surveillance is wrong because she still carries an elevated risk.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-colorectal-cancer-screening-q3`,stem:`A 38-year-old woman has a right-sided colon cancer. Immunohistochemistry shows loss of MSH2 and MSH6 with intact MLH1 and PMS2. Her mother had endometrial cancer at 49. What is the most appropriate next step?`,options:[`BRAF V600E testing of the tumour`,`Reassure that this is sporadic microsatellite instability`,`APC gene testing for familial adenomatous polyposis`,`Germline genetic testing for Lynch syndrome with genetic counselling`],answer:3,explanation:`Loss of MSH2/MSH6 is almost always germline, so the next step is germline Lynch testing and cascade testing of relatives; the family history of early endometrial cancer supports it. BRAF or MLH1 methylation testing is used only when MLH1/PMS2 is lost, to identify sporadic hypermethylated tumours. Calling it sporadic ignores the MSH2 pattern. APC testing is for polyposis with hundreds of adenomas.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-colorectal-cancer-screening-q4`,stem:`Which feature is most typical of a caecal carcinoma compared with a sigmoid carcinoma?`,options:[`Iron-deficiency anaemia without change in bowel habit`,`Early large-bowel obstruction`,`Fresh blood coating the stool with tenesmus`,`Pencil-thin stools`],answer:0,explanation:`The caecum is wide and its contents liquid, so cancers grow large and bleed occultly, presenting with iron-deficiency anaemia, fatigue or a mass. Early obstruction and narrowed stool calibre are features of the narrow left colon with formed stool. Fresh blood with tenesmus suggests a rectal or low sigmoid lesion.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-colorectal-cancer-screening-q5`,stem:`A patient with resected stage III colon cancer has a tumour that is RAS wild-type but microsatellite-stable. Two years later liver metastases appear. Which statement about systemic therapy is correct?`,options:[`Pembrolizumab monotherapy is the preferred first-line treatment`,`Anti-EGFR antibodies are ineffective because the tumour is microsatellite-stable`,`Chemotherapy with an anti-EGFR antibody is an option, especially if the primary was left-sided`,`Systemic therapy has no role once liver metastases appear`],answer:2,explanation:`In RAS (and BRAF) wild-type metastatic CRC, FOLFOX or FOLFIRI plus cetuximab or panitumumab is a standard option, with the best benefit in left-sided primaries; resection of liver metastases may follow. Pembrolizumab first line is for MSI-H/dMMR tumours, not microsatellite-stable ones. Anti-EGFR response depends on RAS status, not MSI status. Saying there is no role ignores conversion chemotherapy and 40-50% 5-year survival after liver resection.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-colorectal-cancer-screening-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the adenoma-carcinoma sequence. Describe the methods of colorectal cancer screening, risk stratification and post-polypectomy surveillance, with reference to the Indian context.`,openingLines:[`Colorectal cancer arises over 10-15 years from adenomatous or serrated polyps through defined molecular pathways, which makes it one of the few cancers that screening can prevent rather than merely detect early.`,`Current guidelines (USPSTF 2021, ACG 2021) recommend screening average-risk adults from age 45 to 75, with intervals after polypectomy set by USMSTF 2020.`],answer:[{heading:`Adenoma-carcinoma sequence and pathways`,points:[`**CIN pathway (70-85%)**: APC loss, KRAS activation, 18q loss, TP53 loss - normal mucosa to adenoma to carcinoma.`,`**MSI pathway (15%)**: mismatch repair failure - germline (Lynch) or MLH1 hypermethylation (sporadic).`,`**Serrated pathway**: BRAF V600E, CIMP; sessile serrated lesions in the right colon.`,`**Dwell time 10-15 years** gives the screening window; advanced adenoma = 10 mm, villous or HGD.`]},{heading:`Risk stratification`,points:[`**Average risk**: age 45+, no personal or family history.`,`**Increased risk**: FDR with CRC/advanced adenoma under 60 or two FDRs - colonoscopy from 40 (or 10 years before youngest case) every 5 years.`,`**High risk**: Lynch (colonoscopy 1-2 yearly from 20-25), FAP (annual from 10-12, colectomy), IBD over 8 years, PSC (annual).`]},{heading:`Screening tests and intervals`,points:[`**FIT annually**; stool DNA-FIT 1-3 yearly; gFOBT annually.`,`**Colonoscopy 10 yearly**; CT colonography or flexible sigmoidoscopy 5 yearly.`,`**Positive non-invasive test = colonoscopy** within 6-9 months.`,`**Quality**: ADR 35% or more, withdrawal 8 minutes, caecal intubation 95%.`]},{heading:`Post-polypectomy surveillance (USMSTF 2020)`,points:[`Normal: 10 years; 1-2 small tubular adenomas: 7-10 years; 3-4: 3-5 years.`,`5-10 adenomas or advanced adenoma: 3 years; more than 10: 1 year plus genetics.`,`SSL 10 mm+, with dysplasia or TSA: 3 years; piecemeal EMR 20 mm+: 6 months.`,`After cancer resection: 1 year, then 3, then 5 years.`]},{heading:`Indian context`,points:[`Lower incidence but **rising young-onset rectal cancer** with late presentation.`,`No organised programme under NP-NCD; opportunistic **FIT from 45-50** and colonoscopy for high-risk families.`,`**Investigate symptomatic young patients** (bleeding, IDA) rather than wait for screening age; exclude ileocaecal TB on biopsy.`]},{heading:`Prevention and counselling`,points:[`Lifestyle: fibre, exercise, avoid processed meat, stop smoking, limit alcohol.`,`Aspirin only in Lynch syndrome; USPSTF 2022 withdrew routine aspirin for prevention.`,`Universal MMR testing of every CRC and cascade testing of relatives.`]}],mustDraw:[`Adenoma-carcinoma sequence diagram with APC, KRAS, TP53 at each step`,`Screening and surveillance flow chart by risk category`,`USMSTF 2020 surveillance interval table`],markSplit:[{part:`Adenoma-carcinoma sequence and pathways`,marks:2},{part:`Risk stratification`,marks:2},{part:`Screening methods and intervals`,marks:2},{part:`Surveillance intervals`,marks:2},{part:`Indian context and prevention`,marks:2}],keywords:[`adenoma-carcinoma sequence`,`FIT`,`colonoscopy`,`USPSTF 2021`,`USMSTF 2020`,`Lynch`,`FAP`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-colorectal-cancer-screening-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Differentiate between Lynch syndrome and familial adenomatous polyposis.`,openingLines:[`Lynch syndrome and FAP are the two commonest inherited colorectal cancer syndromes; both are autosomal dominant but differ in the gene, polyp burden and management.`,`Lynch causes about 3% of all CRC through mismatch repair loss; FAP causes under 1% through APC loss.`],answer:[{heading:`Genetics and mechanism`,points:[`**Lynch**: MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2, EPCAM - mismatch repair loss, MSI-high tumours.`,`**FAP**: APC on chromosome 5q21 - Wnt pathway activation, chromosomal instability.`]},{heading:`Phenotype`,points:[`**Lynch**: few adenomas, accelerated progression, right-sided CRC around 45 years; endometrial, ovarian, gastric, urothelial cancers.`,`**FAP**: 100 to thousands of adenomas from adolescence; CRC near 100% by 40; duodenal adenomas, desmoids, CHRPE, osteomas.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`**Lynch**: universal tumour MMR IHC/MSI, Amsterdam II, revised Bethesda, germline testing.`,`**FAP**: colonoscopy count of polyps, APC germline testing; attenuated FAP 10-99 adenomas.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`**Lynch**: colonoscopy every 1-2 years from 20-25; aspirin; hysterectomy and BSO after childbearing.`,`**FAP**: annual endoscopy from 10-12 years; prophylactic colectomy late teens to mid-20s; upper GI surveillance (Spigelman).`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column comparison table: gene, polyps, CRC risk, extracolonic, surveillance, surgery`],markSplit:[{part:`Genetics`,marks:1},{part:`Phenotype and extracolonic features`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis and management`,marks:2}],keywords:[`Lynch`,`HNPCC`,`FAP`,`APC`,`MMR`,`Amsterdam II`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Chapter on lower gastrointestinal cancers`,`US Preventive Services Task Force. Screening for Colorectal Cancer: Recommendation Statement. JAMA 2021`,`Gupta S et al. Recommendations for Follow-Up After Colonoscopy and Polypectomy: US Multi-Society Task Force on Colorectal Cancer. Gastroenterology 2020`,`Shaukat A et al. ACG Clinical Guidelines: Colorectal Cancer Screening 2021. Am J Gastroenterol 2021`,`NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines: Colon Cancer, Rectal Cancer and Genetic/Familial High-Risk Assessment: Colorectal, 2024-2025`,`ESMO Clinical Practice Guideline: Metastatic Colorectal Cancer, 2023`,`NICE Diagnostics Guidance DG56: Quantitative faecal immunochemical testing to guide colorectal cancer pathway referral in primary care, 2023`,`ICMR-National Centre for Disease Informatics and Research. Report of National Cancer Registry Programme, 2020; ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows, 2023`]}),r.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-acute-gi-infections-adult`,title:`Infective colitis: C. difficile, amoebic, shigella and antibiotic-associated diarrhoea`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Infective colitis** is inflammation of the colonic mucosa by invasive or toxin-producing organisms - **Clostridioides difficile** (the leading cause of **antibiotic-associated** and hospital diarrhoea, treated with **fidaxomicin or oral vancomycin**), **Entamoeba histolytica** (amoebic colitis, treated with a **nitroimidazole plus a luminal agent**), **Shigella**, Campylobacter, non-typhoidal Salmonella and **Shiga toxin-producing E. coli** - presenting as diarrhoea with **blood, mucus, fever and tenesmus**.`,keywords:[`Clostridioides difficile`,`CDI`,`pseudomembranous colitis`,`fidaxomicin`,`oral vancomycin`,`faecal microbiota transplantation`,`FMT`,`amoebic colitis`,`Entamoeba histolytica`,`amoeboma`,`shigellosis`,`bacillary dysentery`,`Campylobacter`,`STEC`,`haemolytic uraemic syndrome`,`traveller's diarrhoea`,`antibiotic-associated diarrhoea`,`stool multiplex PCR`,`GDH`,`Klebsiella oxytoca`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology (global and Indian)`,points:[`**Infective colitis** = colonic inflammation from a pathogen, clinically **dysentery** (frequent small stools with blood and mucus, tenesmus, fever) as opposed to the watery diarrhoea of small-bowel infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Antibiotic-associated diarrhoea (AAD)** occurs in **5-35%** of antibiotic courses; only about **10-25% of AAD is C. difficile**, but CDI causes nearly all antibiotic-associated **colitis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**C. difficile infection (CDI)** is the commonest **healthcare-associated infection** in US hospitals (about half a million cases a year); about a third of cases are now **community-onset**. [IDSA/SHEA 2021]`,`**India**: hospital studies detect C. difficile toxin in roughly **7-20%** of patients with nosocomial diarrhoea - under-recognised because few labs test routinely. [Indian hospital series; ICMR AMR 2022]`,`**Amoebiasis**: **E. histolytica** infects tens of millions and causes an estimated **50 million invasive cases and up to 100,000 deaths** a year, concentrated in India, Bangladesh, Mexico and Africa. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Shigellosis** causes around **200 million episodes and over 200,000 deaths** a year, mostly in children under 5 in South Asia and sub-Saharan Africa; **fluoroquinolone-resistant Shigella** is a WHO high-priority pathogen. [WHO BPPL 2024]`,`**Traveller's diarrhoea** affects **30-70%** of travellers to high-risk regions (including South Asia) within the first 2 weeks; **ETEC** is the commonest cause, with Campylobacter frequent from South and South-East Asia. [ISTM 2017]`,`Acute watery diarrhoea and its rehydration are covered in the **Acute diarrhoea and dysentery** topic; this topic focuses on the **colitic and antibiotic-associated** syndromes in adults.`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**CDI antibiotics**: highest risk with **clindamycin, fluoroquinolones, third/fourth-generation cephalosporins and carbapenems**; any antibiotic, even one dose of surgical prophylaxis, can trigger it; risk persists for **3 months**. [IDSA/SHEA 2021]`,`**CDI host risks**: **age over 65**, hospitalisation or long-term care, **PPI use**, IBD, chemotherapy, solid-organ transplant, CKD and enteral feeding. [ACG 2021 CDI]`,`**Hypervirulent strain** **ribotype 027 (NAP1/BI)** produces more toxin and binary toxin and is fluoroquinolone-resistant - associated with severe outbreaks. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Amoebiasis**: faeco-oral via **cysts in contaminated water and food**; risks are poor sanitation, travel, institutional living, men who have sex with men, and **corticosteroid use** (precipitates fulminant colitis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Shigella**: infective dose only **10-100 organisms**, so person-to-person spread in households, crèches and camps; **S. dysenteriae type 1** produces **Shiga toxin**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Campylobacter jejuni**: undercooked poultry, unpasteurised milk; **Salmonella (non-typhoidal)**: eggs, poultry; **STEC O157:H7**: undercooked beef, raw vegetables, petting farms. [IDSA 2017 diarrhoea]`,`**Other AAD causes**: osmotic diarrhoea from loss of colonic fermentation, **amoxicillin-clavulanate** (direct prokinetic effect), **erythromycin** (motilin agonist), and **Klebsiella oxytoca** haemorrhagic colitis after penicillins. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Immunocompromised**: **CMV colitis** (transplant, HIV with CD4 under 50, steroid-refractory UC), cryptosporidium, microsporidia; in **MSM**, gonococcal, chlamydial (**LGV**) and herpes proctitis. [IDSA 2017 diarrhoea]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**C. difficile**: spore-forming anaerobic Gram-positive bacillus; antibiotics deplete the commensal microbiota and **secondary bile acids**, allowing spores to germinate. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Toxins A (TcdA) and B (TcdB)** glucosylate **Rho GTPases**, collapse the actin cytoskeleton, open tight junctions and trigger neutrophil influx - producing **pseudomembranes** (yellow-white plaques of fibrin, mucus and neutrophils; 'volcano' lesions). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Spores** resist heat, acid and **alcohol hand rubs**, survive months on surfaces - hence **soap-and-water** hand washing and sporicidal (chlorine) cleaning. [IDSA/SHEA 2018]`,`**Recurrence** (20-25% after a first episode, 40-60% after a second) occurs because spores persist while the microbiome remains disrupted - the rationale for **fidaxomicin** (narrow spectrum, spares Bacteroides) and **FMT** (restores microbiota). [IDSA/SHEA 2021]`,`**E. histolytica** trophozoites adhere via a **Gal/GalNAc lectin**, kill cells by 'trogocytosis' and amoebapores, and invade beneath the mucosa to form **flask-shaped ulcers** with normal mucosa in between; spread via portal vein gives **liver abscess**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Shigella** invades M cells and colonocytes, spreads cell-to-cell by actin polymerisation, causing **mucosal ulceration** mainly in the rectosigmoid; **Shiga toxin** damages glomerular endothelium, causing **HUS**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**STEC** is not invasive; its Shiga toxins cause **haemorrhagic colitis** and in 5-15% (more in children) **HUS** - antibiotics may increase toxin release, so they are avoided. [IDSA 2017 diarrhoea]`,`**Campylobacter** cross-reacting lipo-oligosaccharide mimics gangliosides, explaining post-infective **Guillain-Barre syndrome** (about 1 in 1000 infections). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification: CDI severity and episode type`,points:[`**Non-severe CDI**: **WBC 15,000/microL or less** and **serum creatinine under 1.5 mg/dL**. [IDSA/SHEA 2017]`,`**Severe CDI**: **WBC over 15,000/microL** or **creatinine over 1.5 mg/dL**. [IDSA/SHEA 2017]`,`**Fulminant CDI**: **hypotension or shock, ileus or toxic megacolon**; mortality up to **30-50%**. [IDSA/SHEA 2017]`,`**Recurrent CDI**: symptoms return **within 8 weeks** of a completed, successful treatment of a previous episode. [IDSA/SHEA 2021]`,`**Onset classification**: **healthcare-facility onset** (more than 3 days after admission), **community-onset healthcare-associated** (within 12 weeks of discharge), and **community-associated**. [CDC/SHEA surveillance definitions]`,`**Amoebiasis spectrum**: asymptomatic cyst passage (90%), **amoebic colitis**, **fulminant necrotising colitis**, **amoeboma** and extra-intestinal (liver abscess). [WHO; Harrison 22e]`,`**Diarrhoea syndromes** by site: **small-bowel (watery, large volume, no blood)** vs **large-bowel (small frequent stools, blood, mucus, tenesmus)** - the bedside guide to the organism. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**CDI**: **3 or more unformed stools in 24 hours**, usually starting **5-10 days into antibiotics** (range 1 day to 10 weeks after), crampy lower abdominal pain, low-grade fever; blood is uncommon. [IDSA/SHEA 2017]`,`**Clues to severe CDI**: **diarrhoea that stops** while the abdomen distends (ileus, megacolon), high WBC and rising creatinine - not improvement. [ACG 2021 CDI]`,`**Amoebic colitis**: **gradual onset over 1-3 weeks**, bloody mucoid stools, abdominal pain, weight loss; fever in only a third - slower than bacillary dysentery. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Shigellosis**: abrupt fever, cramps and watery diarrhoea, then within 1-2 days **small-volume bloody mucoid stools** 10-30 times a day with tenesmus. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Campylobacter**: prodrome of fever and myalgia, severe periumbilical pain (may mimic **appendicitis**), then bloody diarrhoea. [Harrison 22e]`,`**STEC**: bloody diarrhoea with **little or no fever** and severe pain; **HUS** follows 5-10 days later (oliguria, pallor, petechiae). [IDSA 2017 diarrhoea]`,`**Traveller's diarrhoea**: onset in the first week of travel, 3 or more unformed stools a day, cramps; usually self-limiting in **3-5 days**. [ISTM 2017]`,`**Essential history**: antibiotics in the last 3 months, PPI, hospitalisation, travel, food and water source, sexual history, HIV/immunosuppression, steroids, similar illness in contacts. [IDSA 2017 diarrhoea]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Hydration status**: pulse, postural BP, mucous membranes, capillary refill, urine output - dysentery can still dehydrate. [WHO]`,`**Lower abdominal tenderness** is common; **rebound, guarding or distension with absent bowel sounds** suggests fulminant colitis, toxic megacolon or perforation. [ACG 2021 CDI]`,`**Toxic megacolon**: fever, tachycardia over 120, distension, colonic dilatation **over 6 cm** on X-ray - from CDI, amoebiasis, Shigella or IBD. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Right upper quadrant tenderness and tender hepatomegaly** indicate **amoebic liver abscess** (present in a minority with concurrent colitis) - see the Liver abscess topic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pallor, petechiae, oliguria and hypertension** after bloody diarrhoea point to **HUS**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Reactive arthritis** (knees, ankles), conjunctivitis and urethritis may follow Shigella, Salmonella, Campylobacter or Yersinia 1-4 weeks later, especially in **HLA-B27** carriers. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypoalbuminaemic oedema** in prolonged CDI reflects protein-losing enteropathy and predicts severity. [ACG 2021 CDI]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Ulcerative colitis / Crohn colitis**: chronic (over 4-6 weeks), relapsing, extra-intestinal features; **always exclude infection (stool C. difficile, amoeba) before steroids** - see the Ulcerative colitis topic. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Ischaemic colitis**: older patient, sudden pain then bleeding within 24 hours, splenic flexure/sigmoid; vascular risk factors - see the Mesenteric ischaemia topic. [ACG 2015 ischaemic colitis]`,`**Intestinal tuberculosis**: chronic symptoms, ileocaecal involvement, transverse ulcers, weight loss, fever - biopsy with GeneXpert. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**Colorectal cancer**: older patient, iron deficiency, change in bowel habit, no fever - colonoscopy. [ACG 2021]`,`**Drug-induced colitis**: NSAIDs, **immune checkpoint inhibitors**, mycophenolate, chemotherapy (neutropenic enterocolitis/typhlitis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Microscopic colitis**: chronic watery (non-bloody) diarrhoea in older women on PPI/NSAID/SSRI; normal-looking colonoscopy, diagnosis on biopsy. [AGA 2016]`,`**Diverticulitis**: left iliac fossa pain and fever with little diarrhoea; CT confirms - see the Diverticular disease topic. [AGA 2021]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Who needs stool testing**: fever, **bloody stool**, severe dehydration, symptoms **over 7 days**, immunocompromise, hospital-acquired diarrhoea, food handlers, outbreaks; most mild watery diarrhoea needs none. [IDSA 2017 diarrhoea; ACG 2016]`,`**CDI testing**: only **unformed stool** in patients with **3 or more unformed stools in 24 hours** and **no laxative in the previous 48 hours**; never test formed stool. [IDSA/SHEA 2017]`,`**CDI algorithm**: **GDH antigen plus toxin A/B EIA** (with NAAT to arbitrate discordance) or **NAAT followed by toxin EIA**; NAAT alone over-diagnoses **colonisation** (5-15% of inpatients carry C. difficile). [IDSA/SHEA 2017; ESCMID 2016]`,`**No test of cure** for CDI - toxin and NAAT stay positive for weeks after clinical recovery. [IDSA/SHEA 2017]`,`**Do not test infants under 12 months** for CDI (asymptomatic carriage up to 40-70%); test children 1-2 years only after excluding other causes. [IDSA/SHEA 2017]`,`**Amoebiasis**: microscopy showing **trophozoites with ingested RBCs** is suggestive; it cannot separate E. histolytica from **non-pathogenic E. dispar**, so use **stool antigen (ELISA) or PCR**; serology positive in over 90% of invasive disease (stays positive for years). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Stool multiplex PCR panels** (about 20-25 targets) are sensitive and fast but detect **colonisation and dead organisms**, give no susceptibility, and multiple positives are common - interpret clinically and **send culture** for Shigella/Salmonella/Campylobacter sensitivity. [IDSA 2017 diarrhoea; ACG 2016]`,`**Bloods**: CBC (WBC over 15,000 = severe CDI; leucocytosis up to 50,000 in fulminant), creatinine, electrolytes, albumin, **lactate**, blood cultures if febrile; in bloody diarrhoea check **platelets, LDH, smear for schistocytes** (HUS). [IDSA/SHEA 2017]`,`**Imaging and endoscopy**: abdominal X-ray for megacolon (over 6 cm); **CT** for severe/fulminant CDI (wall thickening, 'accordion sign'); **flexible sigmoidoscopy** with biopsy when diagnosis unclear, avoiding full colonoscopy in severe colitis. [ACG 2021 CDI]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**General**: oral rehydration, continue feeding, hand hygiene; **stop the inciting antibiotic** if possible and **review PPI** in CDI. [IDSA/SHEA 2021]`,`**CDI initial episode**: **fidaxomicin 200 mg orally twice daily for 10 days** (preferred) or **vancomycin 125 mg orally four times daily for 10 days**. [IDSA/SHEA 2021]`,`**Metronidazole 500 mg orally three times daily for 10-14 days** only for **non-severe CDI when fidaxomicin and vancomycin are unavailable** - it is no longer first line. [IDSA/SHEA 2021]`,`**Indian practice**: fidaxomicin is costly or unavailable in many centres, so **oral vancomycin** (capsules, or the IV vial reconstituted and given by mouth) is the practical first line. [ICMR AMR 2022; expert practice]`,`**Amoebic colitis**: **metronidazole 500-750 mg orally three times daily for 7-10 days** or **tinidazole 2 g orally once daily for 3 days**, **followed by a luminal agent** - **paromomycin 25-35 mg/kg/day in 3 divided doses for 7 days** or **diloxanide furoate 500 mg three times daily for 10 days**. [Harrison 22e; CDC 2024]`,`**Shigellosis**: treat all proven cases to shorten illness and spread; in India use **azithromycin 500 mg once daily for 3 days** (or 1 g single dose); **avoid ciprofloxacin empirically** because of high resistance. [ICMR AMR 2022; WHO 2024]`,`**Campylobacter**: most are self-limiting; for severe or immunocompromised cases **azithromycin 500 mg daily for 3 days** (fluoroquinolone resistance over 70% in South Asia). [IDSA 2017 diarrhoea]`,`**Suspected STEC** (bloody diarrhoea without fever): **no antibiotics and no antimotility agents**; monitor CBC and creatinine for HUS. [IDSA 2017 diarrhoea]`,`**Traveller's diarrhoea**: mild - fluids plus **loperamide** (4 mg then 2 mg per stool, max 16 mg/day) or bismuth; moderate - **azithromycin 1 g single dose** or **rifaximin 200 mg three times daily for 3 days** (not for dysentery); severe or dysentery - azithromycin. [ISTM 2017]`,`**Loperamide**: avoid in **bloody diarrhoea, fever, suspected CDI or STEC** - risk of toxic megacolon and HUS. [IDSA 2017 diarrhoea]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Admit**: severe or fulminant CDI, dehydration not corrected orally, sepsis, elderly with comorbidity, AKI, suspected HUS, toxic megacolon, amoebic perforation or fulminant colitis. [IDSA/SHEA 2017]`,`**Infection control for CDI**: single room with **contact precautions** (gloves, gown), **soap and water hand washing**, dedicated equipment, sporicidal (chlorine 1000 ppm) cleaning; continue until **48 hours after diarrhoea resolves**. [IDSA/SHEA 2018]`,`**Severe CDI**: **fidaxomicin 200 mg BD or vancomycin 125 mg QID orally for 10 days**; monitor WBC, creatinine, lactate and abdominal girth daily. [IDSA/SHEA 2021]`,`**Fulminant CDI**: **vancomycin 500 mg orally or by nasogastric tube four times daily** plus **metronidazole 500 mg IV every 8 hours**; add **rectal vancomycin 500 mg in 100 mL saline every 6 hours as retention enema** if ileus. [IDSA/SHEA 2017]`,`**Early surgical consult** in fulminant CDI: **subtotal colectomy** or **diverting loop ileostomy with colonic vancomycin lavage**; triggers include shock, peritonitis, megacolon, **lactate over 5 mmol/L** or **WBC over 50,000**. [IDSA/SHEA 2017; ACG 2021]`,`**FMT for fulminant CDI** refractory to antibiotics is an option in non-surgical candidates. [AGA 2024 FMT; ACG 2021]`,`**Fulminant amoebic colitis**: IV **metronidazole 500 mg every 8 hours** plus broad-spectrum cover for bowel flora (perforation risk); surgery for perforation or toxic megacolon; **never give steroids**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Severe shigellosis / sepsis**: **ceftriaxone 1-2 g IV once daily for 3-5 days**; watch for HUS, seizures, rectal prolapse and hypoglycaemia. [ICMR AMR 2022; WHO 2024]`,`**HUS**: supportive care, careful fluids, **dialysis** for AKI; avoid antibiotics and platelet transfusion unless bleeding. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Recurrent CDI and microbiome therapies`,points:[`**First recurrence**: **fidaxomicin 200 mg BD for 10 days**, or **extended-pulsed fidaxomicin** (200 mg BD days 1-5, then 200 mg once on alternate days days 7-25), or **vancomycin tapered-pulsed** if vancomycin was used first. [IDSA/SHEA 2021]`,`**Vancomycin taper-pulse example**: 125 mg QID 10-14 days, BD 7 days, OD 7 days, then every 2-3 days for 2-8 weeks. [IDSA/SHEA 2017]`,`**Second or later recurrence**: tapered vancomycin, vancomycin followed by **rifaximin 400 mg TDS for 20 days**, or fidaxomicin - then **faecal microbiota transplantation (FMT)**. [IDSA/SHEA 2021]`,`**FMT** cures about **80-90%** of multiply recurrent CDI; given by colonoscopy, enema or capsules after a course of anti-CDI antibiotics; donors are screened for infections including **multidrug-resistant organisms**. [AGA 2024 FMT; ACG 2021]`,`**Licensed microbiome products**: rectal **faecal microbiota live-jslm (2022)** and oral **faecal microbiota spores live-brpk (2023)** are FDA-approved to prevent recurrence after antibiotic treatment of recurrent CDI - not yet routinely available in India. [FDA 2022; FDA 2023]`,`**Bezlotoxumab** (anti-toxin B monoclonal) was recommended as an adjunct for high-risk recurrence in 2021 but the manufacturer **discontinued it in 2025**. [IDSA/SHEA 2021; manufacturer notice 2025]`,`**Probiotics**: evidence for preventing CDI is inconsistent - **ACG 2021 and IDSA/SHEA advise against routine use**, whereas AGA 2020 conditionally suggested specific strains for adults on antibiotics; avoid in the immunocompromised (fungaemia, bacteraemia). [ACG 2021; AGA 2020]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: **oral vancomycin** is safe for CDI (minimal absorption); for amoebiasis, metronidazole is acceptable (avoid high single doses in the first trimester); **paromomycin** is the preferred luminal agent; avoid fluoroquinolones. [CDC 2024; Harrison 22e]`,`**Elderly**: higher CDI incidence, severity and recurrence; review every antibiotic and PPI; watch for dehydration-induced AKI. [ACG 2021 CDI]`,`**CKD**: oral vancomycin and fidaxomicin need **no dose adjustment**; creatinine over 1.5 mg/dL already signals severe CDI in the classification. [IDSA/SHEA 2017]`,`**IBD patients**: CDI is commoner and mimics a flare - **test every flare** for C. difficile (and CMV in steroid-refractory UC); treat with vancomycin or fidaxomicin and continue IBD therapy. [ACG 2021 CDI; ECCO 2022]`,`**Immunocompromised / HIV**: broaden testing (CMV, cryptosporidium, microsporidia, MAC); treat Salmonella, Shigella and Campylobacter with antibiotics; FMT is used cautiously. [IDSA 2017 diarrhoea]`,`**Children**: CDI testing only over 12 months; shigellosis in children - **azithromycin 10-12 mg/kg day 1 then 5-6 mg/kg/day for 4 days** or ceftriaxone - see the paediatric diarrhoea topic. [IAP 2022; WHO]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: no PCR - rely on **stool microscopy (pus cells, RBCs, trophozoites)**, empirical azithromycin for bacillary dysentery and a nitroimidazole plus luminal agent for proven amoebiasis. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**CDI**: dehydration, AKI, hypoalbuminaemia, **toxic megacolon, perforation**, sepsis; 30-day mortality about **5-10%**, up to **30-50% in fulminant disease**. [ACG 2021 CDI]`,`**Recurrence**: about **20-25%** after a first episode, **40-60%** after a second; risk factors are age over 65, continued antibiotics, PPI, severe disease and ribotype 027. [IDSA/SHEA 2021]`,`**Amoebiasis**: fulminant necrotising colitis (mortality over 40%), perforation, **amoeboma** (mimics cancer), stricture, rectovaginal fistula, **liver abscess**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Shigellosis**: **HUS** (S. dysenteriae 1), seizures in children, toxic megacolon, rectal prolapse, hypoglycaemia, **reactive arthritis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Campylobacter**: **Guillain-Barre syndrome** (Miller Fisher variant), reactive arthritis, bacteraemia in the immunocompromised. [Harrison 22e]`,`**STEC**: **HUS in 5-15%** (higher in children under 5 and the elderly); mortality 3-5%; long-term CKD in a quarter. [IDSA 2017 diarrhoea]`,`**Post-infection IBS** follows in about **10%** of bacterial gastroenteritis episodes. [Rome IV]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and prevention`,points:[`**Refer**: fulminant or recurrent CDI (FMT centre), suspected amoebic liver abscess, HUS, colitis persisting beyond 2-4 weeks (colonoscopy to exclude IBD, TB, cancer), immunocompromised with unexplained colitis. [ACG 2021 CDI]`,`**Follow-up CDI**: review at **48-72 hours** for response, then counsel on recurrence (8-week window); **no test of cure**. [IDSA/SHEA 2021]`,`**Antimicrobial stewardship** is the single most effective CDI prevention: shorter courses, avoid unnecessary fluoroquinolones and clindamycin - India's **Kerala and national AMR action plans** and ICMR AMRSN promote this. [ICMR AMR 2022; NAP-AMR 2017]`,`**Deprescribe PPIs** without a clear indication - PPI use raises CDI risk and recurrence. [ACG 2021 CDI]`,`**Amoebiasis prevention**: safe drinking water (boiling kills cysts; chlorination does not reliably), sanitation, hand washing - **Swachh Bharat Mission** and **Jal Jeevan Mission** are the programme links; screen household contacts if invasive disease. [MoHFW; Harrison 22e]`,`**Food handlers** with Shigella, STEC or Salmonella need exclusion until stool clearance per local public health rules; report outbreaks under **IDSP**. [IDSP]`,`**Travellers**: boil it, cook it, peel it; carry azithromycin for self-treatment of moderate-severe traveller's diarrhoea; prophylactic antibiotics only for high-risk travellers. [ISTM 2017]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2021-2026)`,points:[`**IDSA/SHEA 2021 focused update**: **fidaxomicin preferred** over vancomycin for initial and recurrent CDI; vancomycin remains acceptable; metronidazole only when neither is available. [IDSA/SHEA 2021]`,`**ACG 2021 CDI guideline**: same preference for fidaxomicin or vancomycin, FMT after second recurrence and for refractory fulminant CDI, and **against routine probiotics**. [ACG 2021]`,`**ESCMID 2021** CDI update also places fidaxomicin first line and endorses FMT for multiple recurrences. [ESCMID 2021]`,`**FDA 2022-2023** approved the first standardised microbiome therapeutics (rectal and oral) for preventing recurrent CDI. [FDA 2022; FDA 2023]`,`**AGA 2024 guideline on faecal microbiota-based therapies**: recommends them after antibiotics in recurrent CDI and suggests FMT for severe or fulminant CDI not responding to standard therapy. [AGA 2024]`,`**Bezlotoxumab discontinued (2025)** - remove it from recurrence-prevention plans. [Manufacturer notice 2025]`,`**WHO Bacterial Priority Pathogens List 2024** lists **fluoroquinolone-resistant Shigella** as high priority - reinforcing azithromycin or ceftriaxone in India. [WHO BPPL 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**"Diarrhoea that stops while the abdomen distends"** in CDI is ileus or megacolon, not cure. [ACG 2021]`,`**Alcohol gel does not kill C. difficile spores** - soap and water. [IDSA/SHEA 2018]`,`**Metronidazole is no longer first line for CDI** - a common outdated answer. [IDSA/SHEA 2021]`,`**Oral vancomycin, not IV**, treats CDI - IV vancomycin does not reach the colon lumen. [IDSA/SHEA 2017]`,`**Always add a luminal agent** after metronidazole in amoebiasis - otherwise cysts persist and relapse occurs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Steroids for 'IBD' in undiagnosed amoebic colitis** can cause fatal fulminant colitis - test before steroids. [Harrison 22e]`,`**No antibiotics, no loperamide in STEC** - HUS risk. [IDSA 2017]`,`**Flask-shaped ulcers** (amoebic) vs **transverse ulcers** (TB) vs **pseudomembranes** (CDI) vs **longitudinal ulcers** (Crohn) - classic viva. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for infective colitis in adults`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Fidaxomicin`,`200 mg orally twice daily; extended-pulsed: 200 mg BD days 1-5 then 200 mg alternate days days 7-25`,`10 days (or 25-day extended regimen)`,`Nausea, minimal absorption; costly and limited availability in India`],[`Vancomycin (oral)`,`125 mg orally four times daily; fulminant 500 mg QID oral/NG plus rectal 500 mg in 100 mL every 6 hours if ileus`,`10 days; taper-pulse over 6-12 weeks for recurrence`,`Poorly absorbed; IV route useless for CDI; VRE selection`],[`Metronidazole`,`CDI: 500 mg orally TDS (non-severe, only if others unavailable); fulminant CDI and amoebic colitis: 500 mg IV 8-hourly; amoebic colitis oral 500-750 mg TDS`,`CDI 10-14 days; amoebic colitis 7-10 days`,`Metallic taste, disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol, peripheral neuropathy with repeated courses`],[`Tinidazole`,`2 g orally once daily`,`3 days (amoebic colitis)`,`As metronidazole; avoid first trimester`],[`Paromomycin (luminal)`,`25-35 mg/kg/day orally in 3 divided doses`,`7 days after the tissue agent`,`Mild diarrhoea, nausea; minimal absorption; preferred in pregnancy`],[`Diloxanide furoate (luminal)`,`500 mg orally three times daily`,`10 days`,`Flatulence; avoid in first trimester`],[`Azithromycin`,`Shigella/Campylobacter: 500 mg orally once daily for 3 days; traveller's diarrhoea: 1 g single dose`,`1-3 days`,`GI upset, QT prolongation; preferred in India due to fluoroquinolone resistance`],[`Ceftriaxone`,`1-2 g IV once daily`,`3-5 days (severe shigellosis, sepsis)`,`Biliary sludge, C. difficile risk, allergy`],[`Rifaximin`,`200 mg orally three times daily (traveller's diarrhoea); 400 mg TDS as CDI 'chaser'`,`3 days; 20 days after vancomycin in recurrent CDI`,`Not for invasive or bloody diarrhoea; resistance`],[`Loperamide`,`4 mg orally then 2 mg after each loose stool, maximum 16 mg/day`,`Up to 48 hours`,`Contraindicated in bloody diarrhoea, fever, CDI, STEC; cardiac toxicity in overdose`]]},{heading:`CDI severity and treatment (IDSA/SHEA 2017, 2021)`,columns:[`Category`,`Criteria`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`Initial, non-severe`,`WBC 15,000/microL or less and creatinine under 1.5 mg/dL`,`Fidaxomicin 200 mg BD 10 days or vancomycin 125 mg QID 10 days; metronidazole only if neither available`],[`Initial, severe`,`WBC over 15,000/microL or creatinine over 1.5 mg/dL`,`Fidaxomicin or vancomycin 125 mg QID for 10 days`],[`Fulminant`,`Hypotension/shock, ileus or megacolon`,`Vancomycin 500 mg QID oral/NG plus IV metronidazole 500 mg 8-hourly; rectal vancomycin if ileus; surgery consult`],[`First recurrence`,`Return within 8 weeks`,`Fidaxomicin standard or extended-pulsed, or vancomycin taper-pulse`],[`Second or later recurrence`,`Two or more recurrences`,`Fidaxomicin, vancomycin taper-pulse or vancomycin then rifaximin; then FMT`]]},{heading:`Organisms causing infective colitis: discriminating features`,columns:[`Organism`,`Typical setting`,`Key clinical clue`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`C. difficile`,`Antibiotics within 3 months, hospital, PPI, elderly`,`Watery diarrhoea, leucocytosis, pseudomembranes`,`Fidaxomicin or oral vancomycin`],[`E. histolytica`,`Contaminated water, endemic India`,`Gradual bloody diarrhoea, little fever, flask ulcers, liver abscess`,`Metronidazole or tinidazole then paromomycin/diloxanide`],[`Shigella`,`Crowding, households, very low infective dose`,`High fever, tenesmus, many tiny bloody stools; HUS (S. dysenteriae 1)`,`Azithromycin; ceftriaxone if severe`],[`Campylobacter jejuni`,`Poultry, unpasteurised milk`,`Prodrome, pseudo-appendicitis pain; Guillain-Barre later`,`Usually none; azithromycin if severe`],[`Non-typhoidal Salmonella`,`Eggs, poultry`,`Fever, diarrhoea; bacteraemia in elderly, HIV, sickle cell`,`Usually none; ceftriaxone or azithromycin if high-risk`],[`STEC (E. coli O157)`,`Undercooked beef, raw produce`,`Bloody diarrhoea with little fever; HUS after 5-10 days`,`No antibiotics, no loperamide; supportive`],[`CMV`,`Transplant, HIV CD4 under 50, steroid-refractory UC`,`Deep ulcers, inclusion bodies on biopsy`,`Ganciclovir IV then valganciclovir`]]},{heading:`Stool tests and how to read them`,columns:[`Test`,`What it detects`,`Strength`,`Pitfall`],rows:[[`GDH antigen EIA`,`C. difficile common antigen (toxigenic and non-toxigenic)`,`Very sensitive screen; negative rules out`,`Positive does not prove toxin`],[`Toxin A/B EIA`,`Free toxin in stool`,`Specific for active disease`,`Less sensitive; pair with GDH or NAAT`],[`C. difficile NAAT (PCR)`,`Toxin genes`,`Very sensitive`,`Detects colonisation; test only unformed stool, no laxatives`],[`Stool microscopy`,`Pus cells, RBCs, trophozoites, cysts`,`Cheap, available at PHC`,`Cannot distinguish E. histolytica from E. dispar`],[`E. histolytica antigen or PCR`,`Pathogenic species specifically`,`Sensitive and specific`,`Limited availability`],[`Stool culture`,`Shigella, Salmonella, Campylobacter`,`Gives antibiotic susceptibility`,`Slow (48-72 hours)`],[`Multiplex GI PCR panel`,`About 20-25 bacteria, viruses, parasites`,`Rapid, broad`,`Colonisation, multiple positives, no susceptibility, costly`]]}],redFlags:[`Hypotension, shock or lactate over 5 mmol/L with colitis`,`Abdominal distension with diarrhoea stopping - ileus or toxic megacolon`,`Colonic diameter over 6 cm on X-ray`,`WBC over 15,000/microL or rising creatinine in CDI`,`Peritonism - perforation from amoebic or C. difficile colitis`,`Oliguria, pallor, petechiae after bloody diarrhoea - haemolytic uraemic syndrome`,`Tender hepatomegaly with dysentery - amoebic liver abscess`,`Bloody diarrhoea in an immunocompromised patient`],pearls:[`**CDI first line = fidaxomicin or oral vancomycin**; metronidazole is now a fallback [IDSA/SHEA 2021].`,`**Test only unformed stool**, never for test of cure, never in infants under 12 months.`,`**Soap and water**, not alcohol gel - spores survive alcohol.`,`**Amoebiasis = tissue agent then luminal agent** (metronidazole then paromomycin/diloxanide).`,`**Steroids in undiagnosed amoebic colitis can kill** - test before treating 'IBD'.`,`**STEC: no antibiotics, no antimotility** - HUS.`,`**Fluoroquinolone-resistant Shigella** - use azithromycin in India.`,`**Campylobacter is the commonest antecedent of Guillain-Barre syndrome.**`,`**FMT cures 80-90%** of multiply recurrent CDI.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-acute-gi-infections-adult-q1`,stem:`A 68-year-old woman develops 6 watery stools a day on day 7 of ceftriaxone for pyelonephritis. WBC is 11,000/microL and creatinine 0.9 mg/dL. Stool GDH and toxin EIA are positive. Which is the preferred treatment per IDSA/SHEA 2021?`,options:[`Metronidazole 500 mg orally three times daily for 14 days`,`IV vancomycin 1 g twice daily for 10 days`,`Loperamide and probiotics`,`Fidaxomicin 200 mg orally twice daily for 10 days`],answer:3,explanation:`This is a non-severe initial CDI episode; IDSA/SHEA 2021 prefers fidaxomicin 200 mg BD for 10 days, with oral vancomycin 125 mg QID as an acceptable alternative. Metronidazole is reserved for when neither is available. IV vancomycin does not reach the colonic lumen. Loperamide risks toxic megacolon and probiotics are not recommended treatment.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-acute-gi-infections-adult-q2`,stem:`A 30-year-old man is treated with metronidazole for 10 days for amoebic colitis confirmed by stool antigen. What should be given next?`,options:[`A luminal agent such as paromomycin or diloxanide furoate`,`Nothing further; metronidazole eradicates cysts`,`Prednisolone to settle residual colitis`,`Ciprofloxacin for 5 days`],answer:0,explanation:`Nitroimidazoles kill tissue trophozoites but leave luminal cysts in many patients, so a luminal agent (paromomycin 25-35 mg/kg/day for 7 days or diloxanide 500 mg TDS for 10 days) prevents relapse and transmission. Stopping after metronidazole leaves cysts. Steroids can precipitate fulminant amoebic colitis. Ciprofloxacin has no anti-amoebic activity.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-acute-gi-infections-adult-q3`,stem:`A 72-year-old inpatient with C. difficile infection has blood pressure 82/50 mmHg, a distended silent abdomen, and diarrhoea that has stopped. X-ray shows the transverse colon at 8 cm. Which regimen is most appropriate while surgeons review him?`,options:[`Oral fidaxomicin 200 mg twice daily alone`,`Oral or nasogastric vancomycin 500 mg QID plus IV metronidazole 500 mg 8-hourly and rectal vancomycin`,`Oral metronidazole 500 mg three times daily`,`Faecal microbiota transplantation by colonoscopy immediately`],answer:1,explanation:`Shock, ileus and megacolon define fulminant CDI: high-dose enteral vancomycin plus IV metronidazole, with rectal vancomycin because ileus stops oral drug reaching the colon, and an early surgical opinion. Fidaxomicin alone has not been studied in fulminant disease and cannot reach the colon in ileus. Oral metronidazole is inadequate. Colonoscopy in megacolon risks perforation; FMT is reserved for refractory non-surgical candidates.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-acute-gi-infections-adult-q4`,stem:`A 25-year-old has bloody diarrhoea and severe cramps but no fever, 3 days after eating at a barbecue. Stool PCR detects Shiga toxin genes. What is the best management?`,options:[`Ciprofloxacin 500 mg BD for 3 days`,`Azithromycin 1 g single dose and loperamide`,`Oral rehydration, no antibiotics or antimotility agents, and monitoring of CBC and creatinine`,`Immediate colonoscopy and IV steroids`],answer:2,explanation:`Shiga toxin-producing E. coli is treated supportively; antibiotics may increase toxin release and antimotility agents prolong toxin exposure, both linked to haemolytic uraemic syndrome, so CBC, platelets and creatinine are monitored for 5-10 days. Ciprofloxacin or azithromycin with loperamide therefore raise HUS risk. Colonoscopy and steroids have no role in infective colitis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-acute-gi-infections-adult-q5`,stem:`A ward wants to reduce C. difficile infections. Which measure is LEAST effective against spore transmission?`,options:[`Hand washing with soap and water`,`Chlorine-based cleaning of rooms`,`Contact precautions with gloves and gowns`,`Alcohol-based hand rub after patient contact`],answer:3,explanation:`C. difficile spores are resistant to alcohol, so alcohol hand rub does not remove them; soap-and-water washing mechanically removes spores. Sporicidal chlorine cleaning, gloves and gowns, and single-room isolation are all effective parts of the IDSA/SHEA infection-control bundle, along with antimicrobial stewardship.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-acute-gi-infections-adult-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the risk factors, pathogenesis, diagnosis and management of Clostridioides difficile infection, including recurrent disease, as per current guidelines.`,openingLines:[`Clostridioides difficile infection is a toxin-mediated colitis caused by a spore-forming anaerobe after antibiotics disrupt the colonic microbiota; it is the leading cause of healthcare-associated diarrhoea.`,`IDSA/SHEA 2021 made fidaxomicin the preferred treatment, relegated metronidazole to a fallback, and positioned faecal microbiota transplantation for multiple recurrences.`],answer:[{heading:`Risk factors`,points:[`Antibiotics (clindamycin, fluoroquinolones, cephalosporins, carbapenems) within 3 months.`,`Age over 65, hospital or long-term care, PPI, IBD, chemotherapy, CKD.`,`Hypervirulent ribotype 027.`]},{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Loss of commensals and secondary bile acids allows spore germination.`,`Toxins A and B inactivate Rho GTPases; neutrophil influx forms pseudomembranes.`,`Spores persist, explaining recurrence and environmental spread.`]},{heading:`Clinical features and severity`,points:[`Three or more unformed stools in 24 hours, cramps, fever, leucocytosis.`,`Non-severe, severe (WBC over 15,000 or creatinine over 1.5) and fulminant (shock, ileus, megacolon).`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Test only unformed stool without recent laxatives.`,`Two-step: GDH plus toxin EIA, or NAAT plus toxin; no test of cure.`,`CBC, creatinine, lactate, albumin; X-ray/CT for megacolon.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Stop inciting antibiotic and PPI; contact precautions, soap and water.`,`Initial: fidaxomicin 200 mg BD 10 days or vancomycin 125 mg QID 10 days.`,`Fulminant: vancomycin 500 mg QID enteral plus IV metronidazole, rectal vancomycin if ileus; surgery (colectomy or loop ileostomy with lavage).`]},{heading:`Recurrent CDI`,points:[`First recurrence: fidaxomicin (standard or extended-pulsed) or vancomycin taper-pulse.`,`Second or later: antibiotics followed by FMT (80-90% cure) or licensed microbiome product.`,`Bezlotoxumab discontinued 2025; probiotics not routinely recommended.`]},{heading:`Prevention`,points:[`Antimicrobial stewardship, PPI deprescribing, isolation, sporicidal cleaning.`]}],mustDraw:[`CDI severity-based treatment flow chart`,`Diagram of pseudomembrane formation (toxin A/B on colonocyte)`],markSplit:[{part:`Risk factors and pathogenesis`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features and severity`,marks:1},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment including fulminant`,marks:3},{part:`Recurrence and prevention`,marks:2}],keywords:[`Clostridioides difficile`,`fidaxomicin`,`vancomycin`,`FMT`,`pseudomembranous colitis`,`IDSA/SHEA 2021`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-acute-gi-infections-adult-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate amoebic dysentery from bacillary dysentery.`,openingLines:[`Both present with blood and mucus in stool, but amoebic dysentery (Entamoeba histolytica) is gradual and afebrile while bacillary dysentery (Shigella) is acute and toxic.`,`Treatment differs: a nitroimidazole plus luminal agent versus azithromycin or ceftriaxone.`],answer:[{heading:`Clinical`,points:[`**Onset**: amoebic gradual over 1-3 weeks; bacillary abrupt.`,`**Fever and toxicity**: amoebic often absent; bacillary high fever, prostration.`,`**Stools**: amoebic fewer, offensive, blood mixed with stool; bacillary 10-30 small stools of blood and mucus, little faecal matter, tenesmus.`]},{heading:`Pathology and investigations`,points:[`**Ulcers**: amoebic flask-shaped with normal intervening mucosa (caecum, rectosigmoid); bacillary diffuse superficial ulceration, rectosigmoid.`,`**Microscopy**: amoebic trophozoites with ingested RBCs, few pus cells; bacillary many pus cells and RBCs, macrophages.`,`Amoebic antigen/PCR, serology; bacillary stool culture.`]},{heading:`Complications`,points:[`Amoebic: liver abscess, amoeboma, perforation.`,`Bacillary: HUS, seizures, reactive arthritis, rectal prolapse.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Amoebic: metronidazole 500-750 mg TDS 7-10 days or tinidazole 2 g 3 days, then paromomycin or diloxanide.`,`Bacillary: azithromycin 500 mg OD 3 days; ceftriaxone if severe; avoid empirical ciprofloxacin in India.`]}],mustDraw:[`Comparison table: onset, fever, stool, microscopy, ulcer, complications, treatment`],markSplit:[{part:`Clinical differences`,marks:2},{part:`Pathology and laboratory`,marks:1.5},{part:`Complications and treatment`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`amoebic dysentery`,`bacillary dysentery`,`Shigella`,`Entamoeba histolytica`,`flask-shaped ulcer`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - chapters on Clostridioides difficile infection, shigellosis and amoebiasis`,`Johnson S et al. Clinical Practice Guideline by IDSA and SHEA: 2021 Focused Update Guidelines on Management of Clostridioides difficile Infection in Adults. Clin Infect Dis 2021`,`McDonald LC et al. Clinical Practice Guidelines for Clostridium difficile Infection in Adults and Children: 2017 Update by IDSA and SHEA. Clin Infect Dis 2018`,`Kelly CR et al. ACG Clinical Guidelines: Prevention, Diagnosis, and Treatment of Clostridioides difficile Infections. Am J Gastroenterol 2021`,`AGA Clinical Practice Guideline on Fecal Microbiota-Based Therapies for Select Gastrointestinal Diseases, Gastroenterology 2024`,`Shane AL et al. 2017 IDSA Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Management of Infectious Diarrhea. Clin Infect Dis 2017`,`Riddle MS et al. Guidelines for the prevention and treatment of travelers' diarrhea (ISTM). J Travel Med 2017`,`ICMR Treatment Guidelines for Antimicrobial Use in Common Syndromes, 2nd edition, 2022; ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows, 2023`]}),r.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-intestinal-ischaemia`,title:`Mesenteric ischaemia and ischaemic colitis`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`**Intestinal ischaemia** is injury from inadequate splanchnic blood flow - **acute mesenteric ischaemia** (arterial embolism, arterial thrombosis, **non-occlusive mesenteric ischaemia** or **mesenteric venous thrombosis**) presenting as **pain out of proportion to signs** and needing immediate **CT angiography, heparin and revascularisation**; **ischaemic colitis**, the commonest and usually self-limiting form; and **chronic mesenteric ischaemia** ('intestinal angina' with food fear and weight loss).`,keywords:[`acute mesenteric ischaemia`,`AMI`,`SMA embolism`,`SMA thrombosis`,`NOMI`,`mesenteric venous thrombosis`,`MVT`,`ischaemic colitis`,`colon ischaemia`,`chronic mesenteric ischaemia`,`intestinal angina`,`pain out of proportion`,`CT angiography`,`thumbprinting`,`pneumatosis intestinalis`,`portal venous gas`,`Griffiths point`,`Sudeck point`,`median arcuate ligament syndrome`,`papaverine`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Mesenteric ischaemia** = hypoperfusion of the small and/or large intestine; it is classified as **acute (AMI)**, **chronic (CMI)** or **colonic (ischaemic colitis)**. [ESVS 2017]`,`**Acute mesenteric ischaemia** accounts for about **1 in 1000 acute hospital admissions** but carries **in-hospital mortality of 50-70%**, mainly because of delayed diagnosis. [WSES 2022 AMI]`,`**Colon ischaemia** is the **commonest form of intestinal ischaemia** (about 60-70%), mostly in people over 60 and more often women; most cases are mild and transient. [ACG 2015 colon ischaemia]`,`**Chronic mesenteric ischaemia** is rare; atherosclerotic stenosis of mesenteric arteries is common in the elderly (up to 15-20% on imaging) but few become symptomatic thanks to collaterals. [UEG 2020 CMI]`,`**Incidence rises steeply with age** - AMI is about 10 times commoner over 70 than at 50, tracking with **atrial fibrillation and atherosclerosis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**India**: published series are small but show a relatively high proportion of **mesenteric venous thrombosis** in younger patients (prothrombotic states, pancreatitis, alcohol, post-COVID), and frequent **late presentation with gangrene**. [Indian surgical series; Harrison 22e]`,`**Time is bowel**: survival is about 50% higher when revascularisation occurs **within 6-12 hours** of symptom onset compared with later. [ESVS 2017]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**SMA embolism (about 40-50% of AMI)**: from **atrial fibrillation**, recent MI with mural thrombus, valve disease, endocarditis or cardioversion; lodges **3-10 cm distal to the SMA origin**, often beyond the middle colic artery. [ESVS 2017]`,`**SMA thrombosis (about 20-35%)**: acute occlusion of an atherosclerotic stenosis at the **ostium**; often preceded by **chronic postprandial pain and weight loss**; ischaemia involves the whole midgut. [ESVS 2017]`,`**Non-occlusive mesenteric ischaemia (NOMI, about 10-20%)**: splanchnic vasoconstriction from **low cardiac output, shock, vasopressors, cardiac surgery, dialysis, digoxin, cocaine** - common in ICU. [WSES 2022]`,`**Mesenteric venous thrombosis (MVT, about 5-15%)**: **thrombophilia** (factor V Leiden, protein C/S deficiency, **JAK2 myeloproliferative neoplasm**, PNH, antiphospholipid syndrome), **cirrhosis/portal hypertension**, pancreatitis, abdominal surgery, malignancy, **oral contraceptives**, COVID-19. [ESVS 2021 venous thrombosis]`,`**Ischaemic colitis causes**: transient hypotension, **dehydration**, aortic surgery with IMA ligation, vasculitis, **drugs** (vasopressors, alosetron, NSAIDs, oestrogens, cocaine, chemotherapy, immune checkpoint inhibitors), severe constipation, long-distance running. [ACG 2015]`,`**Chronic mesenteric ischaemia**: atherosclerosis in **over 90%**; also **median arcuate ligament compression** of the coeliac axis, vasculitis (Takayasu, polyarteritis nodosa), fibromuscular dysplasia. [UEG 2020 CMI]`,`**Shared risk factors**: age, smoking, hypertension, diabetes, dyslipidaemia, CKD, prior cardiovascular or peripheral arterial disease. [ESVS 2017]`,`**Indian-specific**: **hyperhomocysteinaemia** (often from B12 deficiency in vegetarians) and **Takayasu arteritis** in young women are worth checking. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**Blood supply**: the **coeliac axis** (foregut), **SMA** (midgut - duodenum to two-thirds of transverse colon) and **IMA** (hindgut); collaterals via the **pancreaticoduodenal arcades** and the **marginal artery of Drummond / arc of Riolan**. [Gray's Anatomy 42e]`,`**Watershed areas** have poor collaterals: **splenic flexure (Griffiths point, SMA-IMA)** and **rectosigmoid junction (Sudeck point, IMA-internal iliac)** - hence the sites of ischaemic colitis. [ACG 2015]`,`**Mucosa dies first**: villus tips receive blood last (countercurrent exchange), so **mucosal necrosis begins within 3-6 hours**; transmural infarction and perforation follow over 6-12 hours. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pain out of proportion to signs**: early ischaemia stimulates visceral afferents and spasm (severe pain) while the peritoneum is still uninvolved (soft abdomen); **peritonism appears only after transmural necrosis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Reperfusion injury**: restored flow generates oxygen free radicals and neutrophil activation, worsening mucosal damage and causing **bacterial translocation, sepsis and multi-organ failure**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lactate rises late**: the liver clears portal lactate efficiently, so **systemic lactate is often normal until infarction** - a normal lactate does not exclude AMI. [ESVS 2017; WSES 2022]`,`**MVT** causes venous congestion, bowel wall oedema and haemorrhagic infarction with a more **gradual onset (days)** and a longer diagnostic window. [ESVS 2021]`,`**Chronic mesenteric ischaemia**: post-prandial demand exceeds supply through stenosed vessels - a 'steal' of blood that produces **intestinal angina 15-60 minutes after meals**, food fear and weight loss. [UEG 2020 CMI]`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`**Acute mesenteric ischaemia**: **arterial occlusive** (embolic, thrombotic), **non-occlusive (NOMI)** and **venous (MVT)**. [ESVS 2017]`,`**Colon ischaemia patterns**: **reversible colopathy** (submucosal haemorrhage), **transient colitis**, **chronic ischaemic colitis**, **stricture**, **gangrene** and **fulminant universal colitis**. [ACG 2015]`,`**Isolated right colon ischaemia (IRCI)** is a distinct high-risk pattern - it may herald **SMA occlusion** and has higher mortality, so it needs CT angiography. [ACG 2015]`,`**ACG severity of colon ischaemia**: **mild** (typical symptoms, segmental, no high-risk factors); **moderate** (up to 3 of: male sex, SBP under 90, HR over 100, pain without bleeding, BUN over 20 mg/dL, Hb under 12 g/dL, LDH over 350 U/L, Na under 136, WBC over 15,000, mucosal ulceration); **severe** (more than 3 of these, or peritonism, pneumatosis/portal venous gas, gangrene, pancolonic or IRCI). [ACG 2015]`,`**Chronic mesenteric ischaemia**: atherosclerotic (single or multivessel) vs non-atherosclerotic (MALS, vasculitis); **acute-on-chronic** when a stenosis thromboses. [UEG 2020 CMI]`,`**Timeline of AMI**: reversible ischaemia, then transmural infarction, then peritonitis and sepsis - management differs (revascularise vs resect). [WSES 2022]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Embolic AMI**: **sudden severe periumbilical pain**, often with **forceful bowel emptying** (vomiting, diarrhoea) in a patient with **AF** - the classic triad of pain, gut emptying and cardiac source. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Thrombotic AMI**: more insidious, on a background of **post-prandial pain, food fear and weight loss** (acute-on-chronic). [ESVS 2017]`,`**NOMI**: ICU patient on vasopressors or after cardiac surgery with **unexplained abdominal distension, feed intolerance, rising lactate or GI bleeding**; pain may be absent in a sedated patient. [WSES 2022]`,`**MVT**: vague abdominal pain over **days to 1-2 weeks**, nausea, anorexia, sometimes diarrhoea; ask about prior DVT, family history, OCP, cirrhosis, pancreatitis. [ESVS 2021]`,`**Ischaemic colitis**: older patient with **sudden mild crampy left lower abdominal pain**, urge to defecate, then **bright red or maroon blood mixed with stool within 24 hours**. [ACG 2015]`,`**Chronic mesenteric ischaemia**: **post-prandial pain 15-60 minutes after eating**, lasting 1-2 hours, **food fear (sitophobia)**, unintended **weight loss**, often a smoker woman over 60 with vascular disease elsewhere. [UEG 2020 CMI]`,`**Late features of AMI**: bloody stool, distension, fever, confusion and shock - signs that infarction has already occurred. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Pain out of proportion to physical findings** - a writhing patient with a **soft, mildly tender abdomen** - is the hallmark of early AMI. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Irregularly irregular pulse** (AF) or signs of recent MI, valve disease or peripheral embolism (cold limb) support embolism. [ESVS 2017]`,`**Bowel sounds** are hyperactive early and **absent** after infarction; distension, guarding, rigidity and rebound indicate **transmural necrosis or perforation**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Shock, tachycardia, fever and altered sensorium** reflect sepsis from gangrenous bowel. [WSES 2022]`,`**Abdominal bruit** in a thin patient with weight loss suggests chronic mesenteric stenosis (also heard in MALS); absence does not exclude it. [UEG 2020 CMI]`,`**PR examination**: occult or frank blood is common in colon ischaemia and late AMI. [ACG 2015]`,`**Signs of the cause**: peripheral arterial disease, carotid bruits, xanthelasma; stigmata of chronic liver disease or splenomegaly (MVT with portal hypertension or myeloproliferative disease). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Acute pancreatitis**: epigastric pain radiating to back, **lipase over 3 times normal**; note pancreatitis can itself cause MVT - see the Acute abdomen and pancreatitis topic. [ACG 2024 pancreatitis]`,`**Perforated peptic ulcer**: sudden pain with early **board-like rigidity** and free gas - signs match pain, unlike early AMI. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Small-bowel obstruction**: colicky pain, vomiting, distension, prior surgery; CT shows transition point; strangulation can itself cause ischaemia. [WSES 2017 ASBO]`,`**Ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm** and **aortic dissection** (which may extend into the SMA): back pain, pulsatile mass, pulse deficit. [ESVS 2024 AAA]`,`**Infective colitis and IBD** vs ischaemic colitis: ischaemic colitis is segmental, sudden, older patient, **rectum spared**, resolves in days - see the Infective colitis topic. [ACG 2015]`,`**Diverticular bleeding** vs ischaemic colitis: diverticular bleeding is **painless** - see the Diverticular disease topic. [ACG 2023 LGIB]`,`**Chronic mesenteric ischaemia** mimics **gastric/pancreatic cancer, peptic ulcer and gallstone disease** (post-prandial pain plus weight loss) - endoscopy and CT should exclude these first. [UEG 2020 CMI]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Biphasic CT angiography (arterial and portal venous phase)** is the first and key test for suspected AMI - sensitivity and specificity over **90%**; do it **immediately, without waiting for creatinine**. [ESVS 2017; WSES 2022]`,`**CT findings**: SMA filling defect (embolus) or ostial occlusion with calcification (thrombosis); **SMV thrombus** (MVT); bowel wall thickening or **non-enhancement**, dilatation, **pneumatosis intestinalis, portal venous gas** and free fluid (late, infarction). [ESVS 2017]`,`**Lactate pitfalls**: lactate is **insensitive early and non-specific** (raised in sepsis, shock, metformin, seizures); **a normal lactate must never delay CT angiography**; a high lactate usually means advanced necrosis. [ESVS 2017; WSES 2022]`,`**D-dimer** is sensitive: a **normal D-dimer makes AMI unlikely**, but a raised value is non-specific. [ESVS 2017]`,`**Other bloods**: leucocytosis, metabolic acidosis, raised amylase, LDH, CK, phosphate - all non-specific and late. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Plain X-ray** is normal early; later shows ileus, **thumbprinting**, pneumatosis or free gas - not useful for diagnosis. [WSES 2022]`,`**Ischaemic colitis**: CT with contrast (segmental wall thickening, thumbprinting) then **colonoscopy within 48 hours** with minimal insufflation (preferably CO2) - findings are oedema, petechiae, **single-stripe sign**, ulcers; biopsy. Avoid colonoscopy if peritonitis. [ACG 2015]`,`**Chronic mesenteric ischaemia**: **duplex ultrasound** screen (SMA peak systolic velocity over 275 cm/s or coeliac over 200 cm/s suggests 70% stenosis), confirmed by **CT angiography**; functional tests (tonometry, visible-light spectroscopy) in expert centres. [UEG 2020 CMI]`,`**MVT work-up**: thrombophilia screen, **JAK2 V617F** (even with normal counts), PNH flow cytometry, antiphospholipid antibodies, liver assessment. [ESVS 2021]`,`**Catheter angiography** is now mainly therapeutic (embolectomy, stenting, intra-arterial vasodilators for NOMI). [ESVS 2017]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Suspect AMI in any older patient with AF and sudden severe abdominal pain with a soft abdomen** - refer immediately to a centre with CT, vascular and surgical services; do not discharge with antispasmodics. [WSES 2022]`,`**Before transfer**: IV access, oxygen, IV crystalloid, analgesia (**opioids are appropriate**), nil by mouth, ECG; note AF and anticoagulant status. [WSES 2022]`,`**Mild ischaemic colitis** (stable, tolerating fluids): may be managed with fluids, bowel rest, review of causative drugs, and early colonoscopy; antibiotics are not needed for mild disease. [ACG 2015]`,`**Secondary prevention after arterial disease**: **aspirin 75-150 mg once daily** or **clopidogrel 75 mg once daily**, **high-intensity statin** (atorvastatin 40-80 mg), smoking cessation, BP and diabetes control. [ESVS 2017; ESC 2024 PAD]`,`**Atrial fibrillation**: anticoagulate according to CHA2DS2-VA (**DOAC preferred**, e.g. apixaban 5 mg BD) - the key preventive step against mesenteric embolism. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Chronic mesenteric ischaemia**: refer to a vascular centre; meanwhile small frequent low-fat meals, nutritional support, antiplatelet and statin; avoid prolonged delay because acute thrombosis can follow. [UEG 2020 CMI]`,`**Stop offending drugs** in colon ischaemia - vasoconstrictors, alosetron, oestrogens, cocaine - and correct dehydration and constipation. [ACG 2015]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Resuscitate**: crystalloids guided by lactate and urine output, avoid vasoconstrictors where possible (if needed prefer **low-dose dobutamine or milrinone** over high-dose noradrenaline); NG decompression; correct acidosis and electrolytes. [WSES 2022]`,`**Anticoagulate immediately**: **IV unfractionated heparin 80 U/kg bolus then 18 U/kg/h** (aPTT 1.5-2.5 times control) unless contraindicated - for all occlusive AMI and MVT. [ESVS 2017]`,`**Broad-spectrum antibiotics** covering gut flora (e.g. **piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g IV every 6-8 hours**) because of bacterial translocation. [WSES 2022]`,`**Arterial AMI without peritonitis**: **endovascular revascularisation** (aspiration embolectomy, thrombolysis, stenting) is preferred where available; with peritonitis go to **laparotomy**, revascularise first (embolectomy, bypass or **retrograde open mesenteric stenting**), then resect only clearly dead bowel. [ESVS 2017; WSES 2022]`,`**Damage-control surgery** with temporary abdominal closure and a **planned second-look laparotomy at 24-48 hours** to reassess bowel of doubtful viability. [WSES 2022]`,`**NOMI**: treat the cause (improve cardiac output, reduce vasopressors); **intra-arterial papaverine** infusion via SMA catheter in selected patients; laparotomy only for peritonitis. [ESVS 2017]`,`**MVT**: **anticoagulation alone** cures most; catheter-directed thrombolysis or thrombectomy if deteriorating; surgery only for peritonitis or infarcted bowel. [ESVS 2021]`,`**Moderate to severe ischaemic colitis**: IV fluids, bowel rest, **empirical antibiotics**, serial abdominal examination, lactate; **surgery** for peritonitis, pneumatosis/portal venous gas, gangrene on colonoscopy, massive bleeding, or failure to improve in 2-3 days. [ACG 2015]`,`**Monitoring**: hourly vitals and urine, serial lactate and abdominal examination, ICU care for shock; plan for **intestinal failure** and parenteral nutrition after massive resection. [WSES 2022]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Elderly**: atypical presentation, sedation and dementia mask pain - maintain a low threshold for CT angiography. [WSES 2022]`,`**CKD**: contrast nephropathy risk is outweighed by the mortality of missed AMI - **do not withhold CT angiography** for creatinine. [ESVS 2017]`,`**Diabetes**: metformin raises lactate (confusing interpretation) and should be stopped in shock; diabetic autonomic neuropathy may blunt pain. [ADA 2026]`,`**Pregnancy and OCP users**: oestrogen-related MVT; use **LMWH** for treatment in pregnancy, avoid warfarin and DOACs. [ESVS 2021]`,`**Cirrhosis**: portal vein thrombosis extending to the SMV causes MVT; anticoagulation (LMWH, then DOAC in Child-Pugh A-B) under hepatology guidance - see the Portal hypertension topic. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Post-aortic surgery**: colon ischaemia after AAA repair (IMA sacrifice) - watch for **bloody diarrhoea in the first 48 hours**; sigmoidoscopy confirms. [ESVS 2024 AAA]`,`**ICU / dialysis**: NOMI in patients with intradialytic hypotension or on high vasopressors - unexplained acidosis or distension should prompt CT. [WSES 2022]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: if CT angiography is unavailable, do not delay **laparotomy** in a patient with suspected AMI and peritonism; start heparin and antibiotics before transfer. [WSES 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**AMI mortality 50-70% overall**, highest in NOMI and thrombosis; lower (about 20%) in MVT; delay beyond 24 hours sharply worsens survival. [WSES 2022]`,`**Short bowel syndrome / intestinal failure** after extensive resection - less than **100-150 cm small bowel** without colon usually needs parenteral nutrition; GLP-2 analogue **teduglutide** reduces dependence. [ESPEN 2023 intestinal failure]`,`**Sepsis, multi-organ failure and reperfusion injury** cause most early deaths. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ischaemic colitis**: most resolve in **1-2 weeks**; about **20%** need surgery; mortality about 10% overall but much higher with gangrene or IRCI; late **stricture** or chronic segmental colitis can follow. [ACG 2015]`,`**Recurrence**: AF without anticoagulation leads to repeat emboli; stent restenosis in CMI is 20-40% at 3 years. [ESVS 2017; UEG 2020]`,`**Chronic mesenteric ischaemia untreated** progresses to malnutrition and acute thrombosis with high mortality. [UEG 2020 CMI]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and prevention`,points:[`**Same-hour referral** for suspected AMI to a centre with CT angiography, interventional radiology and emergency surgery; **'time is bowel'**. [WSES 2022]`,`**After MVT**: anticoagulate for **3-6 months if provoked**; **long-term** if unprovoked, thrombophilia or myeloproliferative neoplasm (with haematology). [ESVS 2021]`,`**After arterial AMI or CMI stenting**: lifelong antiplatelet and statin, **duplex surveillance** of stents (e.g. at 1, 6 and 12 months then yearly), and vigilance for recurrent post-prandial pain. [UEG 2020 CMI; ESVS 2017]`,`**After ischaemic colitis**: colonoscopy at 6-8 weeks if initial evaluation was incomplete or symptoms persist (stricture, cancer); evaluate for cardiac source only in IRCI or embolic features. [ACG 2015]`,`**Short bowel**: refer to an **intestinal rehabilitation** unit; monitor weight, electrolytes, B12, fat-soluble vitamins, line infections. [ESPEN 2023]`,`**Prevention**: anticoagulate AF, control vascular risk factors under **NP-NCD**, stop smoking, treat hyperhomocysteinaemia with B12/folate, avoid dehydration in the elderly. [ESC 2024 AF; MoHFW NP-NCD]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**WSES 2022 AMI update**: CT angiography immediately in all suspected AMI regardless of renal function; **endovascular-first** for occlusive arterial AMI without peritonitis; damage control and second look. [WSES 2022]`,`**ESVS 2017** remains the principal vascular guideline: D-dimer useful to exclude, **lactate not useful to diagnose**; hybrid retrograde open mesenteric stenting. [ESVS 2017]`,`**UEG/ESGAR/CIRSE 2020 European guideline on CMI**: CMI can occur with **single-vessel** disease when symptoms fit; endovascular stenting (often covered stents) is first line; MALS treated only after multidisciplinary review. [UEG 2020 CMI]`,`**ESVS 2021 venous thrombosis guideline** covers splanchnic vein thrombosis: anticoagulation 3-6 months for provoked MVT, longer for unprovoked or thrombophilia; DOACs are increasingly used outside cirrhosis. [ESVS 2021]`,`**ESC 2024 AF guideline** uses **CHA2DS2-VA** (sex removed) and prefers DOACs - better anticoagulation is the main way to prevent embolic AMI. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Baveno VII (2022)** recommends anticoagulation for recent splanchnic/portal vein thrombosis in cirrhosis, extending to SMV. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**ACG 2015 colon ischaemia** severity scoring and colonoscopy-within-48-hours advice remain current. [ACG 2015]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Pain out of proportion to signs** in an elderly patient with AF = AMI until proven otherwise. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Normal lactate does not exclude AMI** - the commonest examination trap. [ESVS 2017]`,`**Embolus spares proximal jejunum** (lodges beyond middle colic); **thrombosis infarcts the whole midgut** (ostial occlusion). [ESVS 2017]`,`**Watershed areas**: Griffiths point (splenic flexure) and Sudeck point (rectosigmoid); the **rectum is spared** by its dual supply. [ACG 2015]`,`**Isolated right colon ischaemia** points to SMA disease - get CT angiography. [ACG 2015]`,`**Single-stripe sign** on colonoscopy = ischaemic colitis. [ACG 2015]`,`**CMI triad**: post-prandial pain, food fear, weight loss. [UEG 2020]`,`**Dunbar syndrome** = median arcuate ligament compressing the coeliac axis, worse in expiration. [UEG 2020]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Types of acute mesenteric ischaemia`,columns:[`Type`,`Share of AMI`,`Typical patient and cause`,`CT angiography finding`,`Key treatment`],rows:[[`Arterial embolism`,`40-50%`,`AF, recent MI, valve disease; sudden pain with gut emptying`,`Filling defect in SMA 3-10 cm beyond origin; proximal jejunum spared`,`Heparin, endovascular aspiration or open embolectomy, resect dead bowel`],[`Arterial thrombosis`,`20-35%`,`Atherosclerosis; prior post-prandial pain and weight loss`,`Ostial SMA occlusion with calcification; collaterals`,`Heparin, stenting (including retrograde open), bypass`],[`Non-occlusive (NOMI)`,`10-20%`,`ICU, shock, vasopressors, cardiac surgery, dialysis`,`Diffuse narrowing and spasm of SMA branches, no occlusion`,`Treat cause, reduce vasopressors, intra-arterial papaverine`],[`Mesenteric venous thrombosis`,`5-15%`,`Thrombophilia, JAK2, cirrhosis, pancreatitis, OCP; younger; subacute`,`SMV thrombus, bowel wall thickening, mesenteric oedema`,`Anticoagulation; thrombolysis if worsening; surgery for peritonitis`]]},{heading:`ACG 2015 severity of colon ischaemia`,columns:[`Severity`,`Criteria`,`Management`],rows:[[`Mild`,`Typical symptoms, segmental non-right-sided colitis, none of the risk factors below`,`Supportive care, fluids, stop offending drugs; antibiotics not needed`],[`Moderate`,`Up to 3 of: male sex, SBP under 90 mmHg, HR over 100, pain without bleeding, BUN over 20 mg/dL, Hb under 12 g/dL, LDH over 350 U/L, Na under 136 mmol/L, WBC over 15,000/microL, mucosal ulceration`,`Admit, IV fluids, antibiotics, serial examination, colonoscopy within 48 h`],[`Severe`,`More than 3 of the above, or any of: peritoneal signs, pneumatosis or portal venous gas, gangrene on colonoscopy, pancolonic or isolated right colon ischaemia on imaging`,`ICU, antibiotics, early surgical consult, CT angiography for IRCI; surgery if gangrene or deterioration`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of severe acute abdominal pain in an older adult`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating clinical feature`,`Key test`],rows:[[`Acute mesenteric ischaemia`,`Pain out of proportion to signs, AF, gut emptying`,`Biphasic CT angiography`],[`Perforated peptic ulcer`,`Board-like rigidity early, free gas`,`Erect chest X-ray, CT`],[`Acute pancreatitis`,`Epigastric pain to back, vomiting`,`Lipase over 3 times normal, CT if unclear`],[`Ruptured AAA`,`Back pain, pulsatile mass, shock`,`Bedside ultrasound, CT angiography`],[`Small-bowel obstruction`,`Colicky pain, distension, vomiting, prior surgery`,`CT with transition point`],[`Inferior myocardial infarction`,`Epigastric pain with sweating, ECG changes`,`ECG, troponin`],[`Ischaemic colitis`,`Left-sided cramps then bloody stool within 24 h`,`CT, colonoscopy within 48 h`]]},{heading:`Investigations and cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`Finding / cut-off`,`Interpretation`],rows:[[`Biphasic CT angiography`,`Vessel occlusion, non-enhancing bowel, pneumatosis, portal venous gas`,`Diagnostic test of choice; do not delay for creatinine`],[`Serum lactate`,`Over 2 mmol/L (often normal early)`,`Late, non-specific; normal value does not exclude AMI`],[`D-dimer`,`Normal value`,`Makes AMI unlikely; raised value non-specific`],[`WBC`,`Over 15,000/microL`,`Common but non-specific; severity marker in colitis`],[`Duplex ultrasound (CMI)`,`SMA PSV over 275 cm/s; coeliac PSV over 200 cm/s`,`Suggests 70% or greater stenosis; confirm with CTA`],[`Colonoscopy (colon ischaemia)`,`Segmental oedema, petechiae, single-stripe sign, rectum spared`,`Within 48 h; avoid if peritonitis`],[`Thrombophilia screen (MVT)`,`JAK2 V617F, protein C/S, antithrombin, APLA, PNH`,`Guides duration of anticoagulation`]]},{heading:`Drugs used in intestinal ischaemia`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Unfractionated heparin`,`80 U/kg IV bolus then 18 U/kg/h infusion`,`Titrate to aPTT 1.5-2.5 times control; switch to oral agent when stable`,`Bleeding, HIT; reversible with protamine - preferred peri-operatively`],[`Enoxaparin (LMWH)`,`1 mg/kg SC every 12 hours`,`Bridge to oral anticoagulant; drug of choice in pregnancy`,`Bleeding; reduce to once daily if CrCl under 30`],[`Apixaban / rivaroxaban (DOAC)`,`Apixaban 5 mg orally BD (10 mg BD first 7 days for VTE); rivaroxaban 15 mg BD 21 days then 20 mg OD`,`3-6 months for provoked MVT; long term if unprovoked or AF`,`Bleeding; avoid in Child-Pugh C, antiphospholipid syndrome, pregnancy`],[`Warfarin`,`Dose to INR 2-3`,`3-6 months or long term`,`Bleeding, interactions; teratogenic`],[`Piperacillin-tazobactam`,`4.5 g IV every 6-8 hours`,`Until bowel viability settled and source controlled`,`Allergy, C. difficile; adjust in renal impairment`],[`Papaverine (intra-arterial)`,`30-60 mg/h infusion via SMA catheter`,`24 hours or until angiographic improvement (NOMI)`,`Hypotension; incompatible with heparin in same line`],[`Aspirin`,`75-150 mg orally once daily`,`Lifelong after arterial disease or stent`,`GI bleeding, peptic ulcer`],[`Clopidogrel`,`75 mg orally once daily`,`With aspirin after stenting for a period set by the vascular team, then single agent`,`Bleeding; interaction with omeprazole - use pantoprazole`],[`Atorvastatin`,`40-80 mg orally once daily`,`Lifelong`,`Myalgia, raised transaminases`]]}],redFlags:[`Severe abdominal pain with a soft abdomen in a patient with atrial fibrillation`,`Rising lactate or metabolic acidosis with abdominal pain`,`Peritonism, absent bowel sounds or distension after initial pain`,`Pneumatosis intestinalis or portal venous gas on imaging`,`Bloody diarrhoea with shock, or gangrene seen at colonoscopy`,`Isolated right-sided colon ischaemia`,`Unexplained feed intolerance, distension or acidosis in an ICU patient on vasopressors`,`Post-prandial pain with progressive weight loss suddenly becoming constant`],pearls:[`**Pain out of proportion to signs = AMI** until CT angiography says otherwise.`,`**Normal lactate never excludes AMI**; D-dimer normal makes it unlikely.`,`**CT angiography first, creatinine later.**`,`**Embolus: AF, sudden, spares proximal jejunum; thrombus: ostial, whole midgut, prior intestinal angina.**`,`**Griffiths (splenic flexure) and Sudeck (rectosigmoid)** are the watershed points; rectum spared.`,`**Heparin immediately** for occlusive AMI and MVT.`,`**Revascularise first, then resect**, then second look at 24-48 hours.`,`**CMI triad**: post-prandial pain, sitophobia, weight loss.`,`**MVT: check JAK2** even with normal blood counts.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-intestinal-ischaemia-q1`,stem:`A 74-year-old woman with untreated atrial fibrillation has sudden severe periumbilical pain for 3 hours and has vomited and passed loose stool. She is writhing, but the abdomen is soft with mild tenderness. Lactate is 1.6 mmol/L. What is the next step?`,options:[`Reassure because the lactate is normal and prescribe an antispasmodic`,`Erect chest X-ray and observe`,`Immediate biphasic CT angiography and IV heparin`,`Colonoscopy within 48 hours`],answer:2,explanation:`Sudden pain out of proportion to signs with gut emptying in a patient with AF is embolic acute mesenteric ischaemia until proven otherwise; CT angiography and heparin must not be delayed. Lactate is often normal early because the liver clears portal lactate, so it cannot reassure. A chest X-ray detects perforation, not ischaemia. Colonoscopy is for ischaemic colitis and would waste time.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-intestinal-ischaemia-q2`,stem:`Ischaemic colitis most commonly affects which region of the colon?`,options:[`Rectum`,`Splenic flexure and rectosigmoid junction`,`Caecum and appendix`,`Hepatic flexure`],answer:1,explanation:`The splenic flexure (Griffiths point, between SMA and IMA territories) and rectosigmoid junction (Sudeck point) are watershed zones with poor collaterals, so they suffer first in low-flow states. The rectum has a dual blood supply from IMA and internal iliac arteries and is usually spared. Right-sided ischaemia occurs but is less common and suggests SMA disease.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-intestinal-ischaemia-q3`,stem:`A 34-year-old man presents with 6 days of vague abdominal pain and nausea. CT shows superior mesenteric vein thrombosis with bowel wall thickening but no peritonism. Blood counts are normal. Which investigation is most important to find the cause?`,options:[`JAK2 V617F mutation testing`,`Serum amylase`,`Coeliac axis duplex ultrasound`,`Stool C. difficile toxin`],answer:0,explanation:`Mesenteric venous thrombosis in a young adult demands a search for prothrombotic causes; JAK2 V617F identifies a latent myeloproliferative neoplasm even with normal blood counts and changes anticoagulation to long-term. Amylase is non-specific. Duplex of the coeliac axis evaluates arterial stenosis, not venous thrombosis. C. difficile testing is irrelevant.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-intestinal-ischaemia-q4`,stem:`A 66-year-old smoker has 8 months of abdominal pain beginning 30 minutes after meals, fear of eating and 10 kg weight loss. Endoscopy and CT for malignancy are normal. Which is the most appropriate next investigation?`,options:[`Gastric emptying scintigraphy`,`Mesenteric duplex ultrasound followed by CT angiography`,`Barium follow-through`,`Trial of a proton pump inhibitor for 8 weeks`],answer:1,explanation:`Post-prandial pain, food fear and weight loss in a vascular patient is the triad of chronic mesenteric ischaemia; duplex screening of the coeliac axis and SMA followed by CT angiography confirms the stenosis. Gastric emptying studies and barium follow-through look for motility or mucosal disease, and a PPI trial would delay diagnosis while risking acute thrombosis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-intestinal-ischaemia-q5`,stem:`At laparotomy for embolic SMA occlusion, the small bowel shows segments of doubtful viability. What is the recommended approach?`,options:[`Resect all bowel of doubtful viability before revascularisation`,`Close the abdomen and start thrombolysis only`,`Revascularise first, resect only clearly necrotic bowel, and plan a second-look laparotomy in 24-48 hours`,`Perform primary anastomosis after extensive resection and avoid re-operation`],answer:2,explanation:`Restoring flow first often rescues bowel that looked doubtful; only frankly dead bowel is resected, with damage-control temporary closure and a second look at 24-48 hours, as recommended by ESVS 2017 and WSES 2022. Resecting doubtful bowel first risks short bowel syndrome. Thrombolysis alone with peritonitis ignores dead bowel. Primary anastomosis without second look in an unstable patient risks leak.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-intestinal-ischaemia-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Describe the aetiology, clinical features, investigations and management of acute mesenteric ischaemia. Add a note on the pitfalls of serum lactate.`,openingLines:[`Acute mesenteric ischaemia is a sudden interruption of intestinal blood flow - by arterial embolism, arterial thrombosis, non-occlusive vasospasm or venous thrombosis - that progresses from reversible mucosal ischaemia to transmural infarction within hours.`,`Mortality remains 50-70% mainly because of delayed diagnosis; ESVS 2017 and WSES 2022 stress immediate CT angiography, heparin and early revascularisation.`],answer:[{heading:`Aetiology`,points:[`Embolism (40-50%): AF, MI, valve disease.`,`Thrombosis (20-35%): atherosclerotic ostial stenosis.`,`NOMI (10-20%): shock, vasopressors, cardiac surgery.`,`MVT (5-15%): thrombophilia, JAK2, cirrhosis, pancreatitis, OCP.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Mucosa dies first (3-6 h); transmural infarction by 6-12 h.`,`Pain out of proportion because peritoneum is spared early.`,`Reperfusion injury and bacterial translocation cause sepsis.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Sudden severe periumbilical pain, gut emptying, AF; soft abdomen early.`,`Late: distension, peritonism, bloody stool, shock.`,`MVT subacute over days; NOMI silent in ICU.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Biphasic CT angiography immediately, regardless of creatinine.`,`Lactate, ABG, CBC, D-dimer, renal function, ECG.`,`Thrombophilia and JAK2 testing in MVT.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Resuscitation, NG tube, broad-spectrum antibiotics, heparin infusion.`,`Endovascular revascularisation if no peritonitis; otherwise laparotomy - revascularise, resect dead bowel, second look at 24-48 h.`,`NOMI: treat cause, intra-arterial papaverine; MVT: anticoagulation.`,`Long term: anticoagulate AF, antiplatelet and statin, manage short bowel.`]},{heading:`Lactate pitfalls`,points:[`Liver clears portal lactate, so normal early.`,`Raised in sepsis, shock, metformin, seizures - non-specific.`,`High lactate usually means infarction already present - never wait for it.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of coeliac, SMA and IMA territories with Griffiths and Sudeck points`,`Management flow chart for suspected AMI`],markSplit:[{part:`Aetiology and pathophysiology`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations`,marks:2},{part:`Management`,marks:3},{part:`Lactate pitfalls`,marks:1}],keywords:[`acute mesenteric ischaemia`,`SMA embolism`,`NOMI`,`MVT`,`CT angiography`,`lactate`,`second look`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-intestinal-ischaemia-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on ischaemic colitis.`,openingLines:[`Ischaemic colitis is the commonest form of intestinal ischaemia, caused by transient hypoperfusion of the colon, typically at the splenic flexure and rectosigmoid watershed zones.`,`Most cases are mild and resolve within 1-2 weeks; ACG 2015 criteria identify the minority needing surgery.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes`,points:[`Hypotension, dehydration, aortic surgery, drugs (vasopressors, alosetron, oestrogens, cocaine), constipation, vasculitis.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Older adult, sudden mild left lower abdominal cramps then bloody stool within 24 h.`,`Isolated right colon ischaemia is high-risk (SMA disease).`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`CT with contrast: segmental wall thickening, thumbprinting.`,`Colonoscopy within 48 h: oedema, petechiae, single-stripe sign, rectum spared; biopsy.`,`Stool tests to exclude infection.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Mild: fluids, bowel rest, stop offending drugs.`,`Moderate-severe: antibiotics, monitoring, surgery for gangrene, peritonitis or no improvement.`,`Follow-up colonoscopy for stricture; CT angiography for IRCI.`]}],mustDraw:[`Colon blood supply showing watershed points`,`ACG severity table`],markSplit:[{part:`Causes and sites`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:1},{part:`Investigations`,marks:1.5},{part:`Management`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`ischaemic colitis`,`Griffiths point`,`Sudeck point`,`single-stripe sign`,`thumbprinting`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - chapter on intestinal ischaemia`,`Björck M et al. Editor's Choice - Management of the Diseases of Mesenteric Arteries and Veins: Clinical Practice Guidelines of the European Society for Vascular Surgery (ESVS). Eur J Vasc Endovasc Surg 2017`,`Bala M et al. Acute mesenteric ischemia: updated guidelines of the World Society of Emergency Surgery. World J Emerg Surg 2022`,`Terlouw LG et al. European guidelines on chronic mesenteric ischaemia - joint UEG, ESGAR, CIRSE and partner societies. United European Gastroenterol J 2020`,`Brandt LJ et al. ACG Clinical Guideline: Epidemiology, Risk Factors, Patterns of Presentation, Diagnosis, and Management of Colon Ischemia. Am J Gastroenterol 2015`,`Kakkos SK et al. ESVS 2021 Clinical Practice Guidelines on the Management of Venous Thrombosis. Eur J Vasc Endovasc Surg 2021`,`Van Gelder IC et al. 2024 ESC Guidelines for the management of atrial fibrillation. Eur Heart J 2024`]}),r.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-diverticular-disease`,title:`Diverticular disease and diverticulitis`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`**Diverticulosis** is the presence of acquired **pulsion pseudodiverticula** of the colon (mucosa and submucosa herniating through the muscle where the **vasa recta** penetrate); **diverticular disease** is when they cause symptoms, **diverticulitis** is their inflammation or perforation - graded on CT by the **Hinchey/WSES** systems and, if uncomplicated in a well immunocompetent patient, managed as an **outpatient without routine antibiotics** - and **diverticular bleeding** is painless arterial haemorrhage from a vasa recta.`,keywords:[`diverticulosis`,`diverticulitis`,`SUDD`,`Hinchey classification`,`modified Hinchey`,`WSES classification`,`pericolic abscess`,`percutaneous drainage`,`Hartmann procedure`,`sigmoid colectomy`,`colovesical fistula`,`pneumaturia`,`diverticular bleeding`,`lower GI bleeding`,`Oakland score`,`SCAD`,`high-fibre diet`,`left iliac fossa pain`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Diverticulosis**: the presence of colonic diverticula; **diverticular disease**: clinically significant, symptomatic diverticulosis (SUDD, diverticulitis, bleeding); **diverticulitis**: inflammation, usually from micro-perforation. [AGA 2021]`,`**Western prevalence** rises with age: about **5% at 40**, **over 50% by 60** and **over 60% by 80**; sigmoid-predominant (over 90%). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lifetime risk of diverticulitis** among people with diverticulosis is under **5%** (older estimates of 10-25% were overestimates); about **15%** of episodes are complicated. [AGA 2021]`,`**Asia and India**: prevalence in colonoscopy series is lower (single-digit percentages) but rising with urbanisation and lower fibre intake; **right-sided diverticula** are proportionally commoner, mimicking appendicitis. [Asian colonoscopy series; Harrison 22e]`,`**Incidence in younger adults (under 50)** is rising, especially with obesity; younger age is no longer thought to mean a more aggressive course. [ASCRS 2020]`,`**Diverticular bleeding** is the **commonest cause of lower GI bleeding in adults**, accounting for about **30-40%**; it is painless and stops spontaneously in about **75-80%**. [ACG 2023 LGIB]`,`Diverticular disease causes substantial admissions, costs and emergency colectomies in high-income countries and is increasingly seen in Indian urban surgical practice. [WSES 2020]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Low-fibre diet** (Painter and Burkitt hypothesis): small, hard stools need high segmental pressures to propel, causing herniation - supported by population data, weaker in cohort studies. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Obesity** (especially central), **physical inactivity**, **smoking** and high **red meat** intake raise the risk of diverticulitis and its complications. [AGA 2021]`,`**Drugs**: **NSAIDs and aspirin** increase both diverticulitis and **bleeding**; **opioids and corticosteroids** increase **perforation** risk. [AGA 2021; ASCRS 2020]`,`**Genetics**: twin studies suggest about 40-50% heritability; connective tissue disorders (**Ehlers-Danlos, Marfan, polycystic kidney disease**) predispose to early, extensive diverticulosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ageing**: altered collagen cross-linking and elastin deposition in the taenia coli stiffen and shorten the colon wall. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nuts, seeds, corn and popcorn do not increase risk** - avoiding them is not recommended. [AGA 2021]`,`**Immunosuppression** (transplant, steroids, chemotherapy) increases the risk of complicated and perforated diverticulitis and masks signs. [ASCRS 2020]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**False (pulsion) diverticula**: mucosa and submucosa herniate through the circular muscle at weak points where the **vasa recta penetrate**, between the mesenteric and antimesenteric taeniae. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Segmentation**: high intraluminal pressure in closed segments of the narrow sigmoid (law of Laplace - smallest radius, highest pressure) explains **sigmoid predominance**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Right-sided (caecal) diverticula** in Asians are often **true congenital diverticula** containing all layers, presenting in younger patients. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diverticulitis** results from **micro-perforation** of a diverticulum (via inspissated faecalith or erosion), causing pericolic inflammation, phlegmon, abscess, or free perforation; the old 'obstructed diverticulum' theory is less favoured. [AGA 2021]`,`**Low-grade chronic inflammation, altered microbiota and visceral hypersensitivity** explain **SUDD** (symptomatic uncomplicated diverticular disease) - overlapping with IBS. [AGA 2021]`,`**Bleeding**: the vasa recta is stretched over the dome of the diverticulum and separated from the lumen only by mucosa; **asymmetric intimal thickening and rupture** causes brisk arterial bleeding without inflammation - hence **painless**. [ACG 2023 LGIB]`,`**Right-sided diverticula bleed disproportionately** (wider necks and domes expose more vasa recta) despite being fewer. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fistula** forms when an inflamed segment adheres to an adjacent organ and the abscess decompresses into it - most often the **bladder** (colovesical), then vagina (after hysterectomy), small bowel and skin. [ASCRS 2020]`]},{heading:`Classification and staging`,points:[`**Clinical spectrum**: asymptomatic diverticulosis; **SUDD**; **uncomplicated diverticulitis**; **complicated diverticulitis** (abscess, perforation, fistula, obstruction, stricture); **diverticular bleeding**; **SCAD** (segmental colitis associated with diverticulosis). [AGA 2021]`,`**Original Hinchey (operative)**: **I** pericolic or mesenteric abscess; **II** pelvic, distant intra-abdominal or retroperitoneal abscess; **III** generalised **purulent** peritonitis; **IV** generalised **faecal** peritonitis. [Hinchey 1978]`,`**Modified Hinchey (Wasvary, CT-based)**: **0** mild clinical diverticulitis; **Ia** confined pericolic inflammation/phlegmon; **Ib** pericolic or mesocolic abscess; **II** pelvic, distant or retroperitoneal abscess; **III** purulent; **IV** faecal peritonitis. [Wasvary 1999]`,`**WSES 2020 CT classification**: **stage 0** uncomplicated; **1A** pericolic air bubbles or little fluid without abscess; **1B** abscess **4 cm or smaller**; **2A** abscess **over 4 cm**; **2B** distant air (over 5 cm from the segment); **3** diffuse fluid without distant free air; **4** diffuse fluid with distant free air. [WSES 2020]`,`**Hansen-Stock** and the German **CDD classification** are used in Europe; for Indian exams **Hinchey (modified)** is the one asked. [German S3 2021]`,`**Uncomplicated** = localised inflammation (wall thickening, fat stranding) only; **complicated** = abscess, free perforation, fistula, obstruction or stricture. [AGA 2021]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Acute diverticulitis**: constant **left lower quadrant pain** for 1-2 days or more, fever, anorexia, nausea, change in bowel habit (constipation or diarrhoea). [AGA 2021]`,`**Clinical triad** - **left iliac fossa tenderness, no vomiting and CRP over 50 mg/L** - has high predictive value, but CT is still needed for a first episode. [Lameris 2010; ASCRS 2020]`,`**Right-sided diverticulitis** (common in Asians) causes **right lower quadrant pain mimicking appendicitis**; a redundant sigmoid can also cause suprapubic or right-sided pain. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Urinary symptoms** (dysuria, frequency) occur when the inflamed colon lies on the bladder; **pneumaturia, faecaluria and recurrent polymicrobial UTI** mean **colovesical fistula**. [ASCRS 2020]`,`**SUDD**: recurrent lower abdominal pain and bloating without inflammation markers - overlaps IBS. [AGA 2021]`,`**Diverticular bleeding**: sudden **painless** passage of large-volume bright red or maroon blood, often in an elderly patient on **aspirin, NSAIDs or anticoagulants**; may cause syncope. [ACG 2023 LGIB]`,`**Stricture/obstruction**: progressive constipation, distension, narrowing of stools - must be distinguished from carcinoma. [ASCRS 2020]`,`**Smouldering diverticulitis**: persistent low-grade pain for weeks after an attack. [ASCRS 2020]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Left iliac fossa tenderness** with or without guarding is the key sign; a **tender mass** suggests phlegmon or abscess. [AGA 2021]`,`**Fever and tachycardia** indicate inflammation; **hypotension, generalised rigidity and absent bowel sounds** indicate **perforation and peritonitis** (Hinchey III-IV). [WSES 2020]`,`**DRE / pelvic examination**: tender pelvic mass (pelvic abscess); in women examine for adnexal tenderness to exclude gynaecological causes. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Elderly and immunosuppressed** may have minimal tenderness and no fever despite perforation - a well-appearing patient on steroids can have free perforation. [ASCRS 2020]`,`**Faeculent vaginal discharge or air per vagina** indicates colovaginal fistula (usually after hysterectomy). [ASCRS 2020]`,`**Diverticular bleeding**: pallor, tachycardia, **postural hypotension**, shock index (HR/SBP) over 1 means significant blood loss; abdomen non-tender. [ACG 2023 LGIB]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Colorectal cancer**: can perforate or obstruct and look identical on CT - hence **colonoscopy after recovery** from complicated diverticulitis; see the Colorectal cancer topic. [AGA 2021]`,`**Acute appendicitis**: periumbilical pain shifting to right iliac fossa; right-sided diverticulitis is the classic mimic in Asians - CT separates them. [WSES 2020 appendicitis]`,`**Gynaecological**: **ectopic pregnancy** (always do a **pregnancy test**), ovarian torsion, tubo-ovarian abscess, endometriosis. [RCOG]`,`**Urological**: ureteric colic, pyelonephritis - urinalysis and CT. [EAU 2024]`,`**IBD and infective colitis**: diarrhoea predominant, longer segments, younger patients; **SCAD** looks like UC but is confined to the diverticular segment with rectal sparing. [AGA 2021]`,`**Ischaemic colitis**: sudden pain then bloody stool within 24 hours in an elderly patient - see the Mesenteric ischaemia topic. [ACG 2015]`,`**IBS vs SUDD**: both recurrent pain and bloating; SUDD patients are older and may have raised faecal calprotectin. [AGA 2021]`,`**Bleeding mimics**: angiodysplasia, colitis, haemorrhoids, post-polypectomy bleeding, cancer, and a **brisk upper GI bleed** (check urea/BUN ratio, consider upper endoscopy) - see the GI bleeding topic. [ACG 2023 LGIB]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Bloods**: CBC (leucocytosis), **CRP** (over 50 mg/L supports diverticulitis; **over 150-170 mg/L predicts complicated disease**), creatinine, electrolytes, urinalysis, **pregnancy test** in women of childbearing age. [ASCRS 2020; WSES 2020]`,`**CT abdomen and pelvis with IV contrast** is the test of choice (sensitivity and specificity over **95%**): confirms diagnosis, grades severity (abscess, free air), and finds alternative diagnoses. [AGA 2021; WSES 2020]`,`**Graded-compression ultrasound** in experienced hands is an alternative first test (pregnancy, young patients, no CT), with CT if unclear or complicated. [WSES 2020]`,`**Imaging can be omitted** in a patient with a **known previous CT-proven episode**, typical recurrence and mild symptoms. [AGA 2021]`,`**Avoid colonoscopy and contrast enema during the acute attack** - risk of perforation; water-soluble contrast only if obstruction is in question. [ASCRS 2020]`,`**Colonoscopy 6-8 weeks after recovery** for **complicated diverticulitis** or a first uncomplicated episode **without a high-quality colonoscopy in the past year**; cancer is found in about **8% after complicated** versus about **1% after uncomplicated** episodes. [AGA 2021]`,`**Colovesical fistula**: **CT** (air in bladder, thickened adjacent colon) is the most sensitive; cystoscopy shows bullous oedema; colonoscopy excludes cancer or Crohn disease. [ASCRS 2020]`,`**Diverticular bleeding**: **CT angiography first in haemodynamically unstable** patients (localises bleeding at 0.3-0.5 mL/min, then **embolisation**); otherwise **colonoscopy after rapid bowel preparation** (4-6 L PEG) with endoscopic haemostasis. [ACG 2023 LGIB; BSG 2019 LGIB]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Outpatient care** is appropriate for **CT-confirmed uncomplicated diverticulitis** in a patient who tolerates oral fluids, has pain controlled with oral analgesia, no significant comorbidity, and reliable follow-up. [AGA 2021; DINAMO 2021]`,`**Antibiotics selectively, not routinely**: immunocompetent patients with mild uncomplicated diverticulitis recover equally well without antibiotics (**AVOD, DIABOLO** trials). [AGA 2021; ASCRS 2020]`,`**Give antibiotics** if: **immunocompromised**, frail or significant comorbidity, **CRP over 140 mg/L**, **WBC over 15,000/microL**, fluid collection or long inflamed segment on CT, symptoms **over 5 days**, vomiting, or failure to improve. [AGA 2021]`,`**Oral antibiotic regimens**: **amoxicillin-clavulanate 875/125 mg twice daily** (or 625 mg three times daily) **or ciprofloxacin 500 mg twice daily plus metronidazole 400-500 mg three times daily** for **4-7 days**. [ASCRS 2020; ICMR AMR 2022]`,`**Analgesia**: **paracetamol 1 g four to six-hourly** (maximum 4 g/day); **avoid NSAIDs** (perforation risk) and minimise opioids. [NICE NG147 2019]`,`**Diet**: clear fluids for 2-3 days then advance as tolerated - supported by experience rather than trials. [AGA 2021]`,`**Safety-net**: return immediately for worsening pain, fever, vomiting, inability to drink or rectal bleeding; **review at 2-3 days** (most improve within 48-72 hours). [NICE NG147 2019]`,`**SUDD**: high-fibre diet, **ispaghula husk 3.5 g sachet one to three times daily**, antispasmodics; treat overlapping IBS. [AGA 2021]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Admit**: complicated diverticulitis, sepsis, inability to tolerate oral intake, uncontrolled pain, significant comorbidity, immunosuppression, elderly frail, failed outpatient therapy. [AGA 2021; WSES 2020]`,`**IV antibiotics**: **ceftriaxone 1-2 g once daily plus metronidazole 500 mg every 8 hours**, or **piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g every 6-8 hours** if severe; switch to oral when improving; total **4-7 days after source control**. [WSES 2020; STOP-IT 2015]`,`**Small abscess (under 3-4 cm)**: antibiotics alone; **larger abscess (about 4 cm or more)**: **CT- or ultrasound-guided percutaneous drainage** plus antibiotics. [WSES 2020; ASCRS 2020]`,`**Purulent peritonitis (Hinchey III)**: **resection with primary anastomosis and diverting ileostomy** in stable patients (lower permanent stoma rate than Hartmann), **Hartmann procedure** if unstable; laparoscopic lavage is an option in selected centres. [WSES 2020; DIVERTI 2019; LADIES 2019]`,`**Faecal peritonitis (Hinchey IV) or septic shock**: emergency **Hartmann procedure** (sigmoid resection, end colostomy) or damage-control surgery. [WSES 2020]`,`**Diverticular bleeding**: two large-bore cannulae, crystalloid, **restrictive transfusion (Hb trigger 7 g/dL, 8 g/dL in cardiovascular disease)**, hold anticoagulants, reverse if life-threatening; most stop spontaneously. [ACG 2023 LGIB; BSG 2019]`,`**Endoscopic haemostasis** of a stigmata (active bleeding, visible vessel, adherent clot): **clips, band ligation**, or adrenaline plus clips; **angiographic embolisation** if CTA-positive; surgery (segmental colectomy) for persistent or recurrent bleeding. [ACG 2023 LGIB]`,`**Oakland score 8 or less** identifies low-risk lower GI bleeding suitable for early discharge and outpatient investigation. [BSG 2019 LGIB]`,`**Monitor**: vitals, pain, CRP trend at 48-72 hours, abdominal signs; **repeat CT** if not improving (missed abscess or perforation). [WSES 2020]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Immunocompromised** (transplant, steroids, chemotherapy, HIV): always antibiotics, lower threshold for admission, CT and surgery; consider **elective colectomy after one episode**. [ASCRS 2020; WSES 2020]`,`**Pregnancy**: rare; use **ultrasound or MRI** instead of CT; **amoxicillin-clavulanate or cephalosporin plus metronidazole**, avoid fluoroquinolones. [WSES 2020]`,`**Elderly**: atypical presentation, delirium, minimal tenderness; polypharmacy (NSAIDs, anticoagulants) raises bleeding and perforation risk. [AGA 2021]`,`**CKD**: CT contrast decisions individualised (do not withhold if peritonitis suspected); dose-adjust antibiotics (e.g. ciprofloxacin, piperacillin-tazobactam). [WSES 2020]`,`**Diabetes**: higher risk of complicated disease and sepsis; tighter glucose control during infection. [ADA 2026]`,`**Antithrombotics after a diverticular bleed**: **continue aspirin for secondary cardiovascular prevention** (restart once haemostasis is achieved); **stop aspirin used for primary prevention**; **stop NSAIDs**. [ACG 2023 LGIB; BSG 2019]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: if CT is unavailable, use clinical triad plus ultrasound; give antibiotics more liberally because complicated disease cannot be excluded; refer peritonitis early. [WSES 2020]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Abscess** (about 15-20% of acute diverticulitis admissions), **free perforation** (mortality up to **20%** with faecal peritonitis), **fistula**, **obstruction** and **stricture**. [WSES 2020]`,`**Recurrence**: about **10-35%** after a first uncomplicated episode, mostly within 1-2 years; most recurrences are uncomplicated, and **free perforation is commonest with the first episode**. [ASCRS 2020]`,`**Persistent symptoms** (smouldering disease or post-diverticulitis pain) affect up to a third and impair quality of life. [AGA 2021]`,`**Bleeding** recurs in about **10-40%**; risk is higher on NSAIDs and anticoagulants. [ACG 2023 LGIB]`,`**Hartmann procedure**: about a third of stomas are never reversed, especially in the elderly. [WSES 2020]`,`**SCAD** usually responds to mesalamine and rarely progresses to IBD. [AGA 2021]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and prevention`,points:[`**Refer urgently**: peritonitis, sepsis, abscess, obstruction, uncontrolled pain, significant rectal bleeding, failure of outpatient treatment at 48-72 hours. [NICE NG147 2019]`,`**Elective sigmoid colectomy** is **individualised** - **not based on the number of attacks** (the old 'two-episode rule' is abandoned); consider for **fistula, stricture, persistent symptoms, recurrent attacks affecting quality of life, immunosuppression, or after a drained abscess**. [ASCRS 2020; AGA 2021; DIRECT 2018]`,`**Colonoscopy at 6-8 weeks** after complicated disease or a first episode without recent quality colonoscopy, and when symptoms persist. [AGA 2021]`,`**Prevention of recurrence**: **high-fibre diet (25-30 g/day)** or fibre supplement, **regular vigorous physical activity**, **weight loss**, **stop smoking**, avoid NSAIDs and unnecessary opioids. [AGA 2021]`,`**Not recommended** for prevention: **mesalamine**, **rifaximin** and **probiotics** (no benefit in trials); avoiding nuts and seeds. [AGA 2021]`,`**Programme link**: obesity, physical activity and diet counselling under **NP-NCD and Fit India**; manage at Ayushman Arogya Mandir level with referral pathways for complicated cases. [MoHFW NP-NCD]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**AGA 2021 clinical practice update**: antibiotics **selectively rather than routinely** in immunocompetent uncomplicated diverticulitis; outpatient care for most; no avoidance of nuts/seeds; colonoscopy only if not recently done or complicated. [AGA 2021]`,`**ASCRS 2020**: elective colectomy decision individualised, not by number of episodes; non-operative management of uncomplicated attacks. [ASCRS 2020]`,`**WSES 2020 update**: CT staging 0-4, drainage of abscesses over 4 cm, resection with primary anastomosis plus stoma preferred over Hartmann in stable patients. [WSES 2020]`,`**DINAMO 2021 trial**: outpatient management without antibiotics was non-inferior to antibiotic treatment for mild uncomplicated diverticulitis, supporting the AGA approach. [DINAMO Ann Surg 2021]`,`**ACG 2023 lower GI bleeding guideline**: CTA first in unstable patients, colonoscopy for most others but **no benefit from urgent (under 24 hours) colonoscopy**, restart aspirin for secondary prevention, stop NSAIDs. [ACG 2023 LGIB]`,`**German S3 2021 / ESCP** guidance also supports antibiotic-free treatment of uncomplicated disease in low-risk patients. [German S3 2021; ESCP 2020]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Diverticula are false (pulsion) diverticula** - only mucosa and submucosa, at vasa recta entry points. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diverticulitis hurts, diverticular bleeding does not** - painful bleeding suggests ischaemic colitis or IBD. [ACG 2023]`,`**Hinchey I-IV**: pericolic abscess, distant abscess, purulent peritonitis, faecal peritonitis. [Hinchey 1978]`,`**Never do colonoscopy during acute diverticulitis**; do it 6-8 weeks later to exclude cancer. [AGA 2021]`,`**Antibiotics are no longer mandatory** for mild uncomplicated diverticulitis in the immunocompetent - an outdated answer loses marks. [AGA 2021]`,`**Pneumaturia = colovesical fistula**, commonest cause diverticular disease (then cancer, Crohn). [ASCRS 2020]`,`**Nuts and seeds are allowed.** [AGA 2021]`,`**Two-attack rule for colectomy is obsolete.** [ASCRS 2020]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Hinchey, modified Hinchey and WSES staging`,columns:[`Stage`,`Modified Hinchey (CT)`,`WSES 2020 CT stage`,`Usual management`],rows:[[`Uncomplicated`,`0 - mild clinical diverticulitis; Ia - confined pericolic inflammation/phlegmon`,`0 - wall thickening, fat stranding`,`Outpatient, selective antibiotics`],[`Localised air or small abscess`,`Ib - pericolic or mesocolic abscess`,`1A - pericolic air or fluid; 1B - abscess 4 cm or smaller`,`Antibiotics, admit if unwell`],[`Large or distant abscess`,`II - pelvic, distant or retroperitoneal abscess`,`2A - abscess over 4 cm; 2B - distant air over 5 cm away`,`Percutaneous drainage plus antibiotics`],[`Purulent peritonitis`,`III - generalised purulent peritonitis`,`3 - diffuse fluid without distant free air`,`Resection with primary anastomosis plus stoma, or Hartmann; lavage in selected`],[`Faecal peritonitis`,`IV - generalised faecal peritonitis`,`4 - diffuse fluid with distant free air`,`Emergency Hartmann procedure or damage control`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of left (or right) iliac fossa pain with fever`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating feature`,`Key test`],rows:[[`Acute diverticulitis`,`Older, constant LIF pain, fever, CRP over 50, no vomiting`,`CT with contrast`],[`Perforated colorectal cancer`,`Weight loss, anaemia, change in habit, shouldered stricture`,`CT, colonoscopy after recovery`],[`Acute appendicitis (vs right-sided diverticulitis)`,`Migratory pain to RIF, anorexia, younger`,`CT or ultrasound`],[`Ectopic pregnancy / tubo-ovarian abscess`,`Missed period, adnexal tenderness, vaginal discharge`,`Urine hCG, pelvic ultrasound`],[`Ureteric colic / pyelonephritis`,`Loin-to-groin colic, haematuria, renal angle tenderness`,`Urinalysis, non-contrast CT KUB`],[`Ischaemic colitis`,`Sudden cramps then bloody stool within 24 h`,`CT, colonoscopy within 48 h`],[`Crohn disease / infective colitis`,`Diarrhoea, younger, longer segment`,`Stool tests, calprotectin, colonoscopy`]]},{heading:`Investigations and interpretation`,columns:[`Test`,`Cut-off / finding`,`Interpretation`],rows:[[`CRP`,`Over 50 mg/L; over 150-170 mg/L`,`Supports diverticulitis; predicts complicated disease`],[`WBC`,`Over 15,000/microL`,`Favours antibiotics, consider complicated disease`],[`CT with IV contrast`,`Diverticula, wall thickening, fat stranding, abscess, free air`,`Diagnostic test of choice; stages disease`],[`Ultrasound (graded compression)`,`Thickened segment, inflamed diverticulum, abscess`,`Alternative in pregnancy or young; operator-dependent`],[`Colonoscopy (6-8 weeks later)`,`Cancer in about 8% after complicated, about 1% after uncomplicated`,`Exclude malignancy; never in acute phase`],[`CT angiography (bleeding)`,`Contrast extravasation (bleeding over 0.3-0.5 mL/min)`,`Localises source in unstable patients; guides embolisation`],[`Oakland score (bleeding)`,`8 or less`,`Low risk; safe for discharge and outpatient investigation`]]},{heading:`Drugs used in diverticular disease`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Amoxicillin-clavulanate`,`875/125 mg orally twice daily or 625 mg three times daily; 1.2 g IV 8-hourly`,`4-7 days (selective use)`,`Diarrhoea, C. difficile, cholestatic hepatitis; penicillin allergy`],[`Ciprofloxacin plus metronidazole`,`Ciprofloxacin 500 mg orally BD plus metronidazole 400-500 mg orally TDS`,`4-7 days`,`Tendinopathy, QT prolongation, aortic aneurysm risk; disulfiram reaction; avoid ciprofloxacin in pregnancy`],[`Ceftriaxone plus metronidazole`,`Ceftriaxone 1-2 g IV once daily plus metronidazole 500 mg IV every 8 hours`,`Until improving, then oral; total 4-7 days after source control`,`Allergy, biliary sludge, C. difficile`],[`Piperacillin-tazobactam`,`4.5 g IV every 6-8 hours`,`Severe or complicated disease, 4-7 days after source control`,`Allergy, hypokalaemia; renal dose adjustment`],[`Paracetamol`,`1 g orally every 4-6 hours, maximum 4 g/day (2-3 g in liver disease or low weight)`,`During acute attack`,`Hepatotoxicity in overdose; NSAIDs avoided`],[`Ispaghula husk (psyllium)`,`3.5 g sachet in water one to three times daily`,`Long term for prevention and SUDD`,`Bloating; take with plenty of fluid; avoid in obstruction`],[`Polyethylene glycol bowel preparation (bleeding)`,`4-6 L orally over 3-4 hours (via NG tube if needed)`,`Before colonoscopy for diverticular bleeding`,`Aspiration risk; volume load in heart failure`],[`Mesalamine (SCAD only)`,`2.4 g orally daily`,`Weeks to months`,`Not effective for preventing diverticulitis recurrence; rare nephritis`]]}],redFlags:[`Generalised peritonitis, rigidity or free air on imaging`,`Sepsis or hypotension with left iliac fossa pain`,`Pelvic or large (over 4 cm) abscess on CT`,`Immunocompromised patient with diverticulitis`,`Failure to improve after 48-72 hours of outpatient treatment`,`Large-volume rectal bleeding with shock index over 1`,`Pneumaturia or faecaluria - colovesical fistula`,`Absolute constipation and distension - obstruction from stricture or cancer`],pearls:[`**Diverticula are false pulsion diverticula at vasa recta entry points.**`,`**Diverticulitis hurts; diverticular bleeding is painless.**`,`**Antibiotics selectively, not routinely**, for mild uncomplicated diverticulitis [AGA 2021].`,`**Hinchey: I pericolic abscess, II distant abscess, III pus, IV faeces.**`,`**Drain abscesses about 4 cm or larger**; smaller ones get antibiotics.`,`**Colonoscopy 6-8 weeks later, never during the attack.**`,`**Nuts and seeds are fine; mesalamine and rifaximin do not prevent recurrence.**`,`**No two-attack rule** - colectomy is individualised.`,`**Pneumaturia = colovesical fistula.**`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-diverticular-disease-q1`,stem:`A 58-year-old healthy man has 2 days of left iliac fossa pain and temperature 37.9 C. He is tolerating fluids. CRP is 70 mg/L, WBC 11,000/microL. CT shows sigmoid diverticulitis with fat stranding and no abscess or free air. Which management is most appropriate per AGA 2021?`,options:[`Admit for IV ceftriaxone and metronidazole`,`Emergency sigmoid colectomy`,`Urgent colonoscopy to exclude cancer`,`Outpatient care with analgesia, clear fluids and observation without routine antibiotics`],answer:3,explanation:`CT-proven uncomplicated diverticulitis in an immunocompetent patient tolerating oral intake can be managed as an outpatient, with antibiotics used selectively rather than routinely (AVOD, DIABOLO, DINAMO). Admission and IV antibiotics are for complicated disease, sepsis, comorbidity or immunosuppression. Colectomy is not indicated. Colonoscopy during the acute attack risks perforation and is deferred 6-8 weeks.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-diverticular-disease-q2`,stem:`A CT scan in a febrile patient with diverticulitis shows a 6 cm pelvic abscess without generalised peritonitis. What is the modified Hinchey stage and the best management?`,options:[`Stage II; percutaneous drainage plus antibiotics`,`Stage Ia; oral antibiotics at home`,`Stage III; emergency Hartmann procedure`,`Stage IV; laparoscopic lavage`],answer:0,explanation:`A pelvic, distant or retroperitoneal abscess is modified Hinchey II, and an abscess of about 4 cm or more is drained percutaneously under CT or ultrasound guidance with IV antibiotics. Stage Ia is phlegmon without abscess. Stages III and IV are generalised purulent and faecal peritonitis, which need surgery; this patient has no peritonitis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-diverticular-disease-q3`,stem:`A 76-year-old woman on aspirin passes a large volume of maroon blood painlessly. Pulse is 118/min and BP 95/60 mmHg after initial fluids. What is the best next investigation?`,options:[`Barium enema`,`Colonoscopy without bowel preparation within 2 hours`,`CT angiography`,`Capsule endoscopy`],answer:2,explanation:`In haemodynamically unstable lower GI bleeding (shock index over 1), ACG 2023 and BSG 2019 recommend CT angiography first to localise active bleeding and guide catheter embolisation, after considering a brisk upper GI source. Barium obscures later imaging and cannot treat. Unprepared colonoscopy has poor views. Capsule endoscopy is for suspected small-bowel bleeding in stable patients.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-diverticular-disease-q4`,stem:`A 64-year-old man has recurrent urinary tract infections with mixed enteric organisms and notices air bubbles in his urine. He had an episode of diverticulitis a year ago. What is the most likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Emphysematous cystitis from diabetes`,`Colovesical fistula`,`Bladder carcinoma`,`Chronic prostatitis`],answer:1,explanation:`Pneumaturia and polymicrobial UTIs after diverticulitis indicate a colovesical fistula, the commonest diverticular fistula; CT shows air in the bladder adjacent to thickened sigmoid, and colonoscopy excludes cancer or Crohn disease. Emphysematous cystitis is gas-forming infection in diabetics without enteric organisms mixture. Bladder cancer and prostatitis do not cause pneumaturia.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-diverticular-disease-q5`,stem:`After recovery from a second episode of uncomplicated diverticulitis, a 50-year-old asks how to prevent further attacks. Which advice is supported by current guidelines?`,options:[`Avoid nuts, seeds and popcorn`,`Take mesalamine long term`,`Take rifaximin for one week each month`,`High-fibre diet, regular physical activity, weight loss and smoking cessation`],answer:3,explanation:`AGA 2021 recommends fibre, vigorous physical activity, weight reduction, smoking cessation and avoiding NSAIDs to lower recurrence risk. Avoiding nuts and seeds is not supported - they are not associated with diverticulitis. Mesalamine (PREVENT trials) and cyclic rifaximin did not prevent recurrence and are not recommended.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-diverticular-disease-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Discuss the pathogenesis, classification, clinical features and management of acute diverticulitis, highlighting recent changes in practice.`,openingLines:[`Acute diverticulitis is inflammation of colonic pulsion diverticula, usually from micro-perforation, ranging from mild uncomplicated disease to abscess, fistula and faecal peritonitis.`,`Current guidelines (AGA 2021, ASCRS 2020, WSES 2020) favour outpatient care without routine antibiotics for uncomplicated disease and individualised, not episode-count-based, surgery.`],answer:[{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Low fibre, high segmental pressure, weak points at vasa recta entry - false diverticula, sigmoid predominance.`,`Micro-perforation leads to pericolic inflammation, abscess, perforation or fistula.`,`Risk factors: obesity, smoking, inactivity, NSAIDs, steroids, opioids.`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`Uncomplicated vs complicated (abscess, perforation, fistula, obstruction, stricture).`,`Modified Hinchey 0, Ia, Ib, II, III, IV; WSES CT stages 0-4.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`LIF pain, fever, change in bowel habit; tender mass in abscess.`,`Peritonitis and shock in perforation; pneumaturia in colovesical fistula.`,`Right-sided disease in Asians mimics appendicitis.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`CBC, CRP (over 150 suggests complicated), urinalysis, pregnancy test.`,`CT abdomen-pelvis with contrast; ultrasound alternative.`,`Colonoscopy 6-8 weeks after recovery when indicated.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Uncomplicated, immunocompetent: outpatient, analgesia, fluids, selective antibiotics.`,`Antibiotics if immunocompromised, comorbid, CRP over 140, WBC over 15,000, collection on CT.`,`Abscess about 4 cm or more: percutaneous drainage; Hinchey III: resection with primary anastomosis plus stoma or Hartmann; Hinchey IV: Hartmann.`,`Elective colectomy individualised: fistula, stricture, persistent symptoms, immunosuppression.`]},{heading:`Prevention and recent changes`,points:[`Fibre, exercise, weight loss, no smoking, avoid NSAIDs; nuts and seeds allowed.`,`Mesalamine, rifaximin and probiotics not recommended; two-attack rule abandoned.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of a false diverticulum at the vasa recta entry point`,`Hinchey staging diagram with management for each stage`],markSplit:[{part:`Pathogenesis`,marks:2},{part:`Classification`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features and investigations`,marks:2},{part:`Management`,marks:3},{part:`Prevention and recent changes`,marks:1}],keywords:[`diverticulitis`,`Hinchey`,`WSES`,`AGA 2021`,`percutaneous drainage`,`Hartmann procedure`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-diverticular-disease-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on diverticular bleeding.`,openingLines:[`Diverticular bleeding is painless arterial haemorrhage from a vasa recta stretched over a colonic diverticulum, and is the commonest cause of lower GI bleeding in adults.`,`It stops spontaneously in about 75-80%, but rebleeding is common, especially on NSAIDs and anticoagulants.`],answer:[{heading:`Pathogenesis and risk factors`,points:[`Asymmetric rupture of the vasa recta at the diverticular dome; right-sided diverticula bleed more.`,`Risk: age, NSAIDs, aspirin, anticoagulants, obesity, hypertension.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Sudden painless bright red or maroon blood; syncope, tachycardia in large bleeds.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`CBC, coagulation, group and crossmatch; Oakland score.`,`Unstable: CT angiography then embolisation; stable: colonoscopy after bowel preparation.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Resuscitation, restrictive transfusion (Hb 7, or 8 in cardiac disease), hold anticoagulants.`,`Endoscopic clips or band ligation; embolisation; surgery for refractory bleeding.`,`Stop NSAIDs; continue aspirin for secondary prevention; stop aspirin for primary prevention.`]}],mustDraw:[`Lower GI bleeding flow chart: stable vs unstable pathway`],markSplit:[{part:`Pathogenesis and risk`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:1},{part:`Investigations`,marks:1.5},{part:`Management`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`diverticular bleeding`,`lower GI bleeding`,`CT angiography`,`Oakland score`,`embolisation`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - chapter on diverticular disease`,`Peery AF, Shaukat A, Strate LL. AGA Clinical Practice Update on Medical Management of Colonic Diverticulitis: Expert Review. Gastroenterology 2021`,`Hall J et al. The American Society of Colon and Rectal Surgeons Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Management of Left-sided Colonic Diverticulitis. Dis Colon Rectum 2020`,`Sartelli M et al. 2020 update of the WSES guidelines for the management of acute colonic diverticulitis in the emergency setting. World J Emerg Surg 2020`,`Sengupta N et al. ACG Clinical Guideline: Management of Patients With Acute Lower Gastrointestinal Bleeding. Am J Gastroenterol 2023`,`Oakland K et al. Diagnosis and management of acute lower gastrointestinal bleeding: guidelines from the British Society of Gastroenterology. Gut 2019`,`NICE Guideline NG147: Diverticular disease - diagnosis and management, 2019`,`ICMR Treatment Guidelines for Antimicrobial Use in Common Syndromes, 2nd edition, 2022`]});var i=[];i.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-lft-interpretation`,title:`Interpreting abnormal liver function tests`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`Abnormal liver tests are interpreted by **pattern (hepatocellular, cholestatic or mixed by the R ratio), magnitude (fold rise over the upper limit of normal) and true synthetic function (albumin, INR, bilirubin)**; per **ACG 2017** an ALT above **33 IU/L in men or 25 IU/L in women** deserves a structured aetiology screen rather than repeated reassurance.`,keywords:[`LFT`,`liver function tests`,`liver blood tests`,`ALT`,`AST`,`SGPT`,`SGOT`,`alkaline phosphatase`,`GGT`,`R ratio`,`hepatocellular pattern`,`cholestatic pattern`,`Gilbert syndrome`,`unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia`,`Hy's law`,`FIB-4`,`INR`,`albumin`,`macro-AST`,`transaminitis`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Liver function tests** is a misnomer: ALT, AST, ALP and GGT are **markers of injury or cholestasis**, while only **albumin, INR/prothrombin time and bilirubin** reflect true hepatic function. [Harrison 22e]`,`The **ACG 2017** guideline defines the healthy upper limit of **ALT as 29-33 IU/L in men and 19-25 IU/L in women**; values above this should be evaluated, not ignored. [ACG 2017]`,`Laboratory reference ranges are usually set at **mean plus 2 SD of a local population**, so about **2.5% of healthy people** fall outside the range on any single test, and a panel of 6 tests flags many normal people. [Harrison 22e]`,`Abnormal liver tests are found in **10-20% of adults** tested in primary care, and most are **mild (under 3 times ULN)**. [BSG 2018]`,`In India the commonest cause of a mildly raised ALT is **MASLD**, with a pooled adult prevalence of about **38%** in an Indian meta-analysis; alcohol and hepatitis B follow. [Shalimar 2022 meta-analysis]`,`**Hepatitis B surface antigen** is positive in about **2-4% of the Indian population** and anti-HCV in about **0.5-1%**, so viral serology is part of every first-line screen in India. [NVHCP 2018]`,`A persistently abnormal ALT, even mildly, predicts **higher all-cause and liver-related mortality**, which is why the modern guidelines say investigate rather than repeat indefinitely. [ACG 2017]`]},{heading:`Aetiology - what causes which pattern`,points:[`**Hepatocellular pattern** (ALT/AST dominant): MASLD, alcohol, viral hepatitis A-E, drugs and herbals, autoimmune hepatitis, Wilson disease, haemochromatosis, alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, ischaemic hepatitis. [ACG 2017]`,`**Cholestatic pattern** (ALP and GGT dominant): CBD stones, malignant obstruction (periampullary, pancreatic head, cholangiocarcinoma), PBC, PSC, drug cholestasis, infiltration (TB, sarcoid, lymphoma, metastases). [ACG 2017]`,`**Non-hepatic causes of raised transaminases**: muscle injury or myositis (check **CK**), heavy exercise, haemolysis, hypothyroidism, adrenal insufficiency, **coeliac disease** and anorexia nervosa. [ACG 2017]`,`**Non-hepatic ALP**: bone (growth, Paget disease, fractures, osteomalacia, bony metastases, hyperparathyroidism), **placenta in the third trimester**, intestine (blood group O and B after a fatty meal). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Isolated GGT rise**: alcohol, enzyme-inducing drugs (phenytoin, carbamazepine, barbiturates), obesity and MASLD, diabetes, smoking - it is sensitive but **not specific**. [BSG 2018]`,`**Drug patterns worth memorising**: paracetamol, isoniazid, NSAIDs, valproate - hepatocellular; **amoxicillin-clavulanate**, chlorpromazine, anabolic steroids, oestrogens - cholestatic or mixed. [AASLD DILI 2023]`,`In India, **anti-tubercular therapy** is the commonest cause of drug-induced liver injury, and herbal and dietary supplements are rising causes. [INASL DILI 2022]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why each test behaves as it does`,points:[`**ALT** is cytosolic and largely liver-specific; **AST** is cytosolic and mitochondrial and is also abundant in heart, muscle, kidney and red cells - so ALT is the better liver marker. [Harrison 22e]`,`The **plasma half-life of AST is about 17 hours and ALT about 47 hours**, so after an acute insult AST falls first and the AST:ALT ratio drops. [Harrison 22e]`,`In alcohol injury, **pyridoxal-5-phosphate deficiency** lowers ALT synthesis more than AST and alcohol damages mitochondria (mitochondrial AST), giving an **AST:ALT ratio above 2**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ALP** is a canalicular membrane enzyme; bile acids retained in cholestasis **induce new ALP synthesis**, so ALP rises over days, not hours, and can lag behind acute obstruction. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bilirubin** is made from haem, carried bound to albumin as unconjugated bilirubin, conjugated by **UGT1A1** in the hepatocyte and excreted into bile by **MRP2** - a defect at each step gives a distinct syndrome. [Harrison 22e]`,`Only **conjugated bilirubin is water-soluble** and appears in urine; dark urine therefore means conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia and **excludes pure haemolysis or Gilbert syndrome**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Delta bilirubin** is conjugated bilirubin covalently bound to albumin with a half-life of about **3 weeks**, which is why jaundice resolves slowly after an obstruction is relieved. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Albumin** has a half-life of about **3 weeks**, so it falls only in chronic disease; clotting factor VII has a half-life of about **6 hours**, so the **INR is the earliest marker of acute liver failure**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Low platelets** in liver disease mean **portal hypertension with hypersplenism** and reduced thrombopoietin - a platelet count under 150,000 is often the first clue to cirrhosis. [Baveno VII 2022]`]},{heading:`Classification - pattern and magnitude`,points:[`**R ratio = (ALT / ULN of ALT) divided by (ALP / ULN of ALP)**, using values taken at the same time. [ACG 2017]`,`**R of 5 or more = hepatocellular; R of 2 or less = cholestatic; R between 2 and 5 = mixed** - the same R value is used to classify drug-induced liver injury. [AASLD DILI 2023]`,`**ACG magnitude grading of ALT**: borderline under 2 times ULN, mild 2-5 times, moderate 5-15 times, severe over 15 times, and massive over 10,000 IU/L. [ACG 2017]`,`**Transaminases above 1000 IU/L** narrow the field to **ischaemic hepatitis, acute viral hepatitis, toxin or drug (paracetamol), acute CBD stone, autoimmune hepatitis and acute Wilson disease**. [ACG 2017]`,`**Bilirubin pattern**: unconjugated when the direct fraction is under about **15-20% of total**; conjugated when it is higher - the first branch point in any jaundice work-up. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Isolated ALP rise** with normal GGT points to bone or placenta; ALP rise with **raised GGT or 5-prime nucleotidase** confirms a hepatic source. [ACG 2017]`,`Classify by **duration** too: abnormal tests persisting **over 6 months** are chronic and need a full aetiology screen even if mild. [BSG 2018]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history that discriminates`,points:[`Most patients are **asymptomatic** and the abnormal LFT is found on a health check, pre-operative test or insurance screen. [ACG 2017]`,`Take a quantified **alcohol history in units or grams per week** with AUDIT-C, since self-report underestimates intake. [ACG 2024]`,`Take a complete **drug and supplement history**: ATT, statins, antiepileptics, methotrexate, amoxicillin-clavulanate, anabolic steroids, gym supplements, ayurvedic and herbal products, over the preceding **6 months**. [AASLD DILI 2023]`,`Ask about **metabolic risk**: weight gain, diabetes, hypertension, dyslipidaemia, sleep apnoea - the MASLD profile. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`,`Ask about **viral risk**: transfusion, injections, dialysis, tattoos, unsafe sex, household contact with hepatitis B, and contaminated water for hepatitis A and E. [NVHCP 2018]`,`**Cholestatic symptoms**: itching, pale stools, dark urine, fever with rigors (cholangitis), biliary colic and weight loss (malignancy). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Intermittent mild jaundice during fasting, illness or exertion** in a young person with normal enzymes is the classic history of **Gilbert syndrome**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask for **family history** of liver disease, diabetes, early neuropsychiatric illness (Wilson) or emphysema (alpha-1 antitrypsin). [ACG 2017]`]},{heading:`Examination - signs and what they mean`,points:[`**Jaundice** is visible in the sclera when bilirubin exceeds about **2.5-3 mg/dL**; look in natural light. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Stigmata of chronic liver disease** - spider naevi, palmar erythema, gynaecomastia, testicular atrophy, Dupuytren contracture, leuconychia - mean established cirrhosis rather than an acute insult. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Splenomegaly with dilated abdominal veins and ascites** indicates **portal hypertension**, which changes the work-up from aetiology alone to staging and variceal screening. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Scratch marks and xanthelasma** suggest chronic cholestasis such as PBC. [EASL PBC 2017]`,`**Kayser-Fleischer rings** on slit lamp in a young patient with hepatitis or neuropsychiatric signs point to **Wilson disease**. [AASLD Wilson 2022]`,`A **palpable gallbladder in a jaundiced patient** suggests malignant distal obstruction (**Courvoisier law**), not stones. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`Record **BMI and waist circumference** (Asian cut-offs: waist **90 cm men, 80 cm women**), acanthosis nigricans and BP - the metabolic signs of MASLD. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`,`**Asterixis, fetor hepaticus and confusion** with abnormal LFTs mean hepatic encephalopathy and need urgent admission. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis - reading the clues`,points:[`**AST:ALT ratio above 2** with GGT rise and macrocytosis suggests **alcohol**; AST above 300-400 IU/L is unusual in alcohol alone. [ACG 2024]`,`**AST:ALT ratio above 1** in a non-drinker suggests **advanced fibrosis or cirrhosis** from any cause. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ischaemic hepatitis** shows transaminases in the thousands within 24-48 hours of hypotension with very high **LDH (ALT:LDH ratio under 1.5)** and a fall of about 50% within 72 hours. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute CBD stone** can cause a brief ALT spike above 1000 IU/L with pain, settling within 24-72 hours as ALP rises. [ACG 2017]`,`**Acute Wilson disease** gives an **ALP:bilirubin ratio under 4 and AST:ALT ratio above 2.2**, with Coombs-negative haemolysis. [AASLD Wilson 2022]`,`**Isolated raised AST** with normal ALT suggests muscle, haemolysis or **macro-AST** (AST bound to immunoglobulin, benign). [ACG 2017]`,`**Isolated unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia** with normal Hb, reticulocytes and enzymes is **Gilbert syndrome**; with anaemia and high reticulocytes it is **haemolysis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Isolated conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia** with normal enzymes suggests **Dubin-Johnson** (black liver, MRP2 defect) or **Rotor syndrome** - both benign. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line in OPD or district hospital`,points:[`Repeat the test, confirm it is real, and order a **full liver panel: bilirubin (total and direct), ALT, AST, ALP, GGT, albumin, PT/INR** with a complete blood count and platelets. [BSG 2018]`,`First-line **aetiology screen for raised ALT**: **HBsAg, anti-HCV, ultrasound abdomen**, fasting glucose or HbA1c, lipids, ferritin with **transferrin saturation**. [ACG 2017]`,`Add **anti-HAV IgM and anti-HEV IgM** when the picture is acute, and **HCV RNA** if anti-HCV is positive. [NVHCP 2018]`,`**Ultrasound** shows fatty liver, cirrhotic nodularity, splenomegaly, ascites, focal lesions and - in cholestasis - **dilated ducts (CBD over 6 mm)**, which separates obstructive from intrahepatic causes. [ACG 2017]`,`In a cholestatic pattern with **non-dilated ducts**, check **anti-mitochondrial antibody (AMA)** for PBC; if negative, proceed to **MRCP** for PSC. [ACG 2017]`,`Calculate **FIB-4 = (age x AST) / (platelets in 10^9/L x square root of ALT)**; under **1.3 is low risk** and over **2.67 is high risk** of advanced fibrosis. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`,`Check **CK** for an isolated AST rise or a muscular young man, and **reticulocytes, LDH, haptoglobin and peripheral smear** for unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia. [ACG 2017]`,`**Hy's law**: ALT over **3 times ULN plus bilirubin over 2 times ULN** without cholestasis on a drug predicts about **10% mortality** - stop the drug and refer. [AASLD DILI 2023]`]},{heading:`Investigations - second line and tertiary`,points:[`**Second-line screen** if the first is negative: **ANA, anti-smooth muscle antibody, serum IgG**, ceruloplasmin (under age 55), **alpha-1 antitrypsin level and phenotype**, anti-tTG IgA with total IgA, and TSH. [ACG 2017]`,`**Transient elastography (FibroScan)**: liver stiffness under **8 kPa** largely excludes and over **12-15 kPa** suggests advanced fibrosis; **CAP** quantifies steatosis. [EASL 2024]`,`**Haemochromatosis** is suspected with **transferrin saturation over 45%** and raised ferritin, and confirmed by **HFE C282Y genotyping** - though C282Y is rare in Indians. [ACG 2019 haemochromatosis]`,`**Wilson screen**: ceruloplasmin under **20 mg/dL**, 24-hour urinary copper over **100 microgram** (lower thresholds in children), slit lamp for KF rings. [AASLD Wilson 2022]`,`**MRCP** images the biliary tree non-invasively; **EUS** detects small CBD stones and periampullary tumours; **ERCP** is reserved for therapy. [ASGE 2019]`,`**Liver biopsy** is indicated when the non-invasive work-up is inconclusive, to confirm autoimmune hepatitis, grade suspected MASH, or explain persistent unexplained abnormal tests. [ACG 2017]`,`**Vitamin K challenge** (10 mg IV or SC): correction of a prolonged INR within 24-48 hours suggests **vitamin K malabsorption from cholestasis**, while failure to correct means **hepatocellular synthetic failure**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - acting on the result`,points:[`**Treat the cause, not the number** - there is no drug that 'treats raised enzymes'; avoid prescribing liver tonics, silymarin or ursodeoxycholic acid without a diagnosis. [ACG 2017]`,`**Stop the likely culprit drug or supplement** and recheck LFTs in **2-4 weeks**; most DILI resolves on withdrawal. [AASLD DILI 2023]`,`**Alcohol**: advise abstinence or within low-risk limits; brief intervention in the OPD halves heavy drinking in many patients. [ACG 2024]`,`**MASLD**: target **7-10% weight loss**, Mediterranean-pattern diet and **150 minutes a week** of moderate exercise; see the MASLD topic. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`,`**Gilbert syndrome** needs only reassurance and an explanation that it is benign; no follow-up, no diet change, but warn about **irinotecan and atazanavir** toxicity. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Statins are safe** in raised transaminases from MASLD and compensated cirrhosis; do not stop a statin for ALT under **3 times ULN**. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`,`If the first-line screen is negative and ALT stays raised for **over 6 months**, proceed to the second-line screen and elastography rather than repeating LFTs indefinitely. [BSG 2018]`,`**Vaccinate** non-immune patients with chronic liver disease against **hepatitis A and B**. [ACG 2017]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD - when abnormal LFTs mean admission`,points:[`Admit when **INR is 1.5 or more**, there is any encephalopathy, bilirubin is rising rapidly, or there is vomiting with inability to take oral fluids - these define possible **acute liver failure**. [AASLD ALF 2011]`,`**Paracetamol overdose** with deranged LFTs: start **IV N-acetylcysteine** (150 mg/kg over 1 hour then 50 mg/kg over 4 h, then 100 mg/kg over 16 h, or the 2-bag regimen) regardless of time since ingestion. [AASLD ALF 2011]`,`**Cholestasis with fever, rigors and jaundice** is **acute cholangitis** (Charcot triad): blood cultures, IV antibiotics and ERCP drainage within 24-48 hours, urgently if severe. [Tokyo Guidelines 2018]`,`In **ischaemic hepatitis**, treat the circulatory cause (heart failure, sepsis, hypotension); the transaminases follow the haemodynamics. [Harrison 22e]`,`Monitor **INR, bilirubin, glucose, creatinine and mental status daily** in severe acute hepatitis; hypoglycaemia signals synthetic failure. [AASLD ALF 2011]`,`**Do not correct INR with FFP** unless bleeding or before a procedure, because INR is the key prognostic marker in acute liver failure. [AASLD ALF 2011]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: ALP rises **2-4 times** from the placenta and albumin falls by dilution, but **ALT, AST, bilirubin and bile acids should stay normal** - any rise is pathological. [ACG Liver Pregnancy 2016]`,`In pregnancy think of **hyperemesis, intrahepatic cholestasis (bile acids 10 micromol/L or more), pre-eclampsia, HELLP and acute fatty liver**, by trimester. [ACG Liver Pregnancy 2016]`,`**Children and adolescents**: ALP is normally up to **3 times adult values** during growth spurts and needs no work-up if GGT is normal. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Elderly**: cholestatic DILI and malignant obstruction are more common; isolated ALP rise often reflects **Paget disease or bony metastases**. [ACG 2017]`,`**CKD**: transaminases run **lower** in dialysis patients (pyridoxine deficiency), so a 'normal' ALT may hide hepatitis; screen HBV and HCV routinely. [KDIGO HCV 2022]`,`**Diabetes**: raised ALT is MASLD until proven otherwise, but compute FIB-4 because diabetics progress to fibrosis faster. [ADA 2025]`,`**Patients on ATT**: stop isoniazid, rifampicin and pyrazinamide if ALT is over **3 times ULN with symptoms or 5 times ULN without**, or bilirubin is raised. [NTEP Guidelines 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`The prognosis depends on the cause and stage, not the height of the ALT: a **massive ALT rise in hepatitis A** usually recovers fully, while **a normal ALT in cirrhosis** can coexist with decompensation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Low albumin, high INR, high bilirubin and low platelets** are the markers of decompensation, and feed into the **Child-Pugh and MELD** scores. [AASLD 2023]`,`**Hy's law** cases carry **about 10% mortality or transplant**. [AASLD DILI 2023]`,`Untreated causes progress: MASLD and alcohol to cirrhosis, HBV and HCV to cirrhosis and **hepatocellular carcinoma**. [Harrison 22e]`,`A **falling transaminase with a rising bilirubin and INR** signals massive necrosis (few hepatocytes left), not recovery - an ominous sign. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Gilbert, Dubin-Johnson and Rotor syndromes** have a normal life expectancy. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer urgently** for INR 1.5 or more, encephalopathy, bilirubin over 5 mg/dL with rising trend, ALT over 1000 IU/L, cholangitis, or a liver mass. [ACG 2017]`,`**Refer to hepatology** when the aetiology screen identifies chronic viral hepatitis, autoimmune, metabolic or genetic disease, or when **FIB-4 is over 2.67 or elastography over 12 kPa**. [EASL 2024]`,`Refer **dilated ducts or a suspected mass on ultrasound** to a surgical gastroenterology unit for MRCP, EUS or ERCP. [ACG 2017]`,`Recheck **mild unexplained elevations at 3-6 months** after lifestyle change or drug withdrawal; refer if still abnormal. [BSG 2018]`,`In MASLD with low FIB-4, **repeat FIB-4 every 2-3 years**; in patients on hepatotoxic drugs (methotrexate, ATT) follow the drug-specific monitoring schedule. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`,`In cirrhosis from any cause, arrange **ultrasound with or without AFP every 6 months** for HCC surveillance. [AASLD HCC 2023]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`Under **NP-NCD**, the population-based screening at **Ayushman Arogya Mandir** for diabetes and hypertension is the entry point to identify MASLD risk. [NP-NCD 2023]`,`**NVHCP** provides free testing for hepatitis B and C and free treatment for hepatitis C and eligible hepatitis B at model treatment centres. [NVHCP 2018]`,`**Hepatitis B vaccination**: birth dose within 24 hours under UIP, and adult catch-up for health workers, dialysis patients and chronic liver disease. [UIP 2023]`,`Counsel about **avoiding unprescribed herbal products and gym supplements**, which are a leading cause of liver injury in Indian referral centres. [INASL DILI 2022]`,`Counsel safe drinking water and food hygiene to prevent **hepatitis A and E**, which cause most acute hepatitis in India. [NVHCP 2018]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates and exam angle`,points:[`**ACG 2017 (Kwo et al)** remains the current ACG guideline on abnormal liver chemistries, with the sex-specific ALT ULN of 33 and 25 IU/L. [ACG 2017]`,`The **2023 multisociety nomenclature** replaced NAFLD with **MASLD**, so a fatty liver on ultrasound now needs at least one cardiometabolic risk factor for that label. [AASLD-EASL-ALEH 2023]`,`**AASLD 2023 and EASL-EASD-EASO 2024** put **FIB-4 first, then elastography** as the triage pathway for anyone with raised enzymes and metabolic risk. [EASL 2024]`,`**AASLD 2023 DILI practice guidance** endorses **RECAM** (the revised electronic causality method) over the older RUCAM score. [AASLD DILI 2023]`,`**Viva classics**: R ratio formula, causes of ALT over 1000, AST:ALT over 2, how to confirm ALP is hepatic, vitamin K test, why INR beats albumin in acute disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistakes**: calling ALT under 40 'normal' in a woman, ignoring isolated low platelets, and labelling raised ALT as fatty liver without excluding HBV and HCV. [ACG 2017]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Pattern of liver test abnormality and likely causes`,columns:[`Pattern`,`Definition`,`Commonest causes`,`Next test`],rows:[[`Hepatocellular`,`R ratio 5 or more; ALT/AST dominant`,`MASLD, alcohol, viral hepatitis, drugs, AIH, Wilson`,`HBsAg, anti-HCV, ultrasound, iron studies`],[`Cholestatic`,`R ratio 2 or less; ALP and GGT dominant`,`CBD stone, malignancy, PBC, PSC, drugs, infiltration`,`Ultrasound for duct dilatation, then AMA or MRCP`],[`Mixed`,`R ratio between 2 and 5`,`Drugs (amoxicillin-clavulanate), infiltrative disease, hepatitis with cholestasis`,`Both screens; drug history`],[`Isolated unconjugated bilirubin`,`Direct fraction under 15-20% of total`,`Gilbert syndrome, haemolysis, drugs (rifampicin, atazanavir)`,`CBC, reticulocytes, LDH, smear`],[`Isolated conjugated bilirubin`,`Direct fraction high, enzymes normal`,`Dubin-Johnson, Rotor, early obstruction`,`Ultrasound; urinary coproporphyrin if needed`],[`Isolated ALP`,`ALP raised, GGT normal`,`Bone growth, Paget, metastases, pregnancy`,`Calcium, vitamin D, PTH, bone imaging`],[`Isolated GGT`,`GGT raised, others normal`,`Alcohol, enzyme inducers, obesity`,`No work-up beyond history and metabolic risk`]]},{heading:`ACG 2017 magnitude grading and very high ALT`,columns:[`Grade`,`ALT level`,`Typical causes`,`Action`],rows:[[`Borderline`,`Under 2 times ULN`,`MASLD, alcohol, drugs, chronic HBV/HCV`,`First-line screen; lifestyle`],[`Mild`,`2-5 times ULN`,`Chronic hepatitis of any cause`,`First-line screen; second-line if negative`],[`Moderate`,`5-15 times ULN`,`Acute viral, DILI, AIH flare`,`Full screen within days`],[`Severe`,`Over 15 times ULN`,`Acute viral, paracetamol, ischaemic, acute CBD stone`,`Check INR and mental status today`],[`Massive`,`Over 10,000 IU/L`,`Ischaemic hepatitis, paracetamol`,`Admit; NAC for paracetamol`]]},{heading:`Tests of injury versus tests of function`,columns:[`Test`,`What it reflects`,`Half-life or kinetics`,`Interpretation pearl`],rows:[[`ALT`,`Hepatocyte injury (liver-specific)`,`About 47 hours`,`ULN 33 men, 25 women (ACG 2017)`],[`AST`,`Hepatocyte, muscle, heart, red cell injury`,`About 17 hours`,`AST:ALT over 2 suggests alcohol`],[`ALP`,`Canalicular cholestasis, bone, placenta`,`Rises over days`,`Confirm hepatic source with GGT`],[`GGT`,`Microsomal induction, cholestasis`,`About 2-4 weeks`,`Sensitive, non-specific`],[`Bilirubin`,`Uptake, conjugation, excretion`,`Delta fraction about 3 weeks`,`Dark urine means conjugated`],[`Albumin`,`Chronic synthetic function`,`About 3 weeks`,`Also low in sepsis, nephrotic, malnutrition`],[`PT / INR`,`Acute synthetic function`,`Factor VII about 6 hours`,`Vitamin K corrects cholestatic, not hepatocellular, INR`],[`Platelets`,`Portal hypertension, hypersplenism`,`Not applicable`,`Under 150,000 suggests cirrhosis`]]},{heading:`Differentiating the benign hyperbilirubinaemias`,columns:[`Feature`,`Gilbert`,`Crigler-Najjar type 1/2`,`Dubin-Johnson`,`Rotor`],rows:[[`Defect`,`Reduced UGT1A1 (promoter TA repeat)`,`Absent (type 1) or severely reduced (type 2) UGT1A1`,`MRP2 canalicular export`,`OATP1B1/1B3 uptake and storage`],[`Bilirubin type`,`Unconjugated, usually under 4 mg/dL`,`Unconjugated, 6-45 mg/dL`,`Conjugated`,`Conjugated`],[`Liver appearance`,`Normal`,`Normal`,`Black pigmented liver`,`Normal`],[`Treatment`,`Reassurance`,`Phototherapy, phenobarbital (type 2), transplant (type 1)`,`None`,`None`],[`Prognosis`,`Normal`,`Kernicterus risk in type 1`,`Normal`,`Normal`]]},{heading:`Drugs and measures used after the diagnosis is made`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`N-acetylcysteine (paracetamol toxicity)`,`150 mg/kg IV over 1 h, then 50 mg/kg over 4 h, then 100 mg/kg over 16 h`,`Continue until INR under 2 and ALT falling`,`Anaphylactoid reaction - slow, do not stop`],[`Vitamin K (phytomenadione)`,`10 mg IV slowly or SC`,`Single dose, recheck INR at 24-48 h`,`Anaphylactoid reaction with fast IV push`],[`Ursodeoxycholic acid (PBC)`,`13-15 mg/kg/day oral in 2 divided doses`,`Lifelong; response assessed at 12 months`,`Diarrhoea, weight gain; not for undiagnosed raised LFTs`],[`Hepatitis B vaccine`,`1 mL (20 microgram) IM deltoid`,`0, 1, 6 months; double dose in dialysis`,`Local soreness; check anti-HBs in high risk`],[`Hepatitis A vaccine (inactivated)`,`1 mL IM`,`2 doses 6-12 months apart`,`Local reaction; give in non-immune CLD`]]}],redFlags:[`INR 1.5 or more, or any confusion or drowsiness, with abnormal LFTs - possible acute liver failure`,`ALT over 1000 IU/L or over 15 times ULN, especially after a paracetamol ingestion`,`Jaundice with fever and rigors - acute cholangitis`,`Hy's law on a drug: ALT over 3 times ULN plus bilirubin over 2 times ULN`,`Painless progressive jaundice with weight loss or a palpable gallbladder - malignant obstruction`,`Hypoglycaemia, ascites or GI bleeding with abnormal LFTs - decompensation`,`Raised ALT with Coombs-negative haemolysis or neuropsychiatric signs in a young patient - acute Wilson disease`],pearls:[`**LFTs measure injury; albumin, INR and bilirubin measure function.**`,`R ratio = (ALT/ULN) / (ALP/ULN): **5 or more hepatocellular, 2 or less cholestatic.**`,`**ALT over 1000**: think 'I VIT-AW' - Ischaemic, Viral, Ingested toxin (paracetamol), Tract stone, Autoimmune, Wilson.`,`**AST:ALT over 2 = alcohol** (low pyridoxal phosphate); AST over 400 is rarely alcohol alone.`,`**ACG 2017 ALT ULN: 33 men, 25 women** - '40 is normal' is outdated.`,`**Dark urine excludes Gilbert and pure haemolysis**: only conjugated bilirubin enters urine.`,`**INR is the earliest sign of failure** (factor VII half-life 6 hours); albumin takes weeks.`,`**Vitamin K corrects the INR of cholestasis, not of cirrhosis.**`,`Isolated ALP rise with **normal GGT = bone or placenta**.`,`**Falling ALT with rising INR and bilirubin is bad news**, not recovery.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-lft-interpretation-q1`,stem:`A 45-year-old man has ALT 240 IU/L (ULN 40) and ALP 260 IU/L (ULN 120). What is the R ratio and pattern?`,options:[`R = 6.0, hepatocellular`,`R = 2.8, mixed`,`R = 0.9, cholestatic`,`R = 1.9, cholestatic`],answer:1,explanation:`R = (ALT/ULN) / (ALP/ULN) = (240/40) / (260/120) = 6 / 2.17 = about 2.8, which lies between 2 and 5 and is therefore a mixed pattern. Writing 6.0 forgets to divide by the ALP fold-rise; 0.9 uses raw values without normalising to the ULN; and a cholestatic pattern needs R of 2 or less.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-lft-interpretation-q2`,stem:`A 22-year-old student notices yellow eyes during exam week fasting. Total bilirubin 3.2 mg/dL, direct 0.3 mg/dL, ALT, ALP, haemoglobin and reticulocyte count normal. Urine is pale. The best next step is:`,options:[`MRCP to look for a choledochal cyst`,`Liver biopsy`,`Reassurance - this is Gilbert syndrome`,`UGT1A1 genotyping before any diagnosis can be made`],answer:2,explanation:`Isolated unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia under 4 mg/dL, worse with fasting, with normal enzymes, Hb and reticulocytes is Gilbert syndrome, and the diagnosis is clinical - reassurance is the management. Genotyping is not required for diagnosis, MRCP addresses conjugated cholestatic jaundice, and a biopsy is unjustified for a benign condition.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-lft-interpretation-q3`,stem:`A 68-year-old man admitted with cardiogenic shock has ALT 4800 IU/L and LDH 6200 IU/L on day 2; by day 5 ALT is 1100 IU/L. The most likely diagnosis is:`,options:[`Acute hepatitis E`,`Drug-induced liver injury from amiodarone`,`Autoimmune hepatitis`,`Ischaemic hepatitis`],answer:3,explanation:`Ischaemic hepatitis produces transaminases in the thousands within 24-48 hours of hypotension, a very high LDH (ALT:LDH ratio under 1.5) and a rapid fall of more than half within 72 hours as perfusion is restored. Viral hepatitis has a slower peak and fall with a lower LDH; autoimmune hepatitis rarely falls this fast; amiodarone injury lacks this kinetic shape.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-lft-interpretation-q4`,stem:`A 50-year-old woman has ALP 3 times ULN, raised GGT, normal bilirubin and ALT, and a normal ultrasound with non-dilated ducts. The next best test is:`,options:[`Anti-mitochondrial antibody`,`ERCP`,`Bone scan`,`Ceruloplasmin`],answer:0,explanation:`Raised ALP with raised GGT confirms a hepatic source; non-dilated ducts on ultrasound point to intrahepatic cholestasis, and in a middle-aged woman the first test is AMA for primary biliary cholangitis (MRCP follows if negative). ERCP is therapeutic and carries pancreatitis risk; a bone scan is for ALP with normal GGT; ceruloplasmin is for hepatocellular injury in young patients.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-lft-interpretation-q5`,stem:`In a patient with prolonged jaundice and INR 2.0, the INR falls to 1.1 within 24 hours of 10 mg parenteral vitamin K. This indicates:`,options:[`Severe hepatocellular synthetic failure`,`Disseminated intravascular coagulation`,`Vitamin K malabsorption due to cholestasis`,`Warfarin resistance`],answer:2,explanation:`Bile salts are needed to absorb fat-soluble vitamin K; in cholestasis the liver can still make clotting factors once vitamin K is given parenterally, so the INR corrects within 24-48 hours. In hepatocellular failure the liver cannot synthesise the factors and the INR does not correct; DIC consumes factors and is not corrected by vitamin K; warfarin resistance is unrelated.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-lft-interpretation-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Describe the interpretation of abnormal liver function tests and outline the approach to an asymptomatic adult with a raised ALT detected on a health check.`,openingLines:[`Liver function tests are markers of hepatocyte injury (ALT, AST), cholestasis (ALP, GGT) and true synthetic function (albumin, INR, bilirubin); they are interpreted by pattern, magnitude and duration.`,`ACG 2017 sets the healthy ALT ULN at 29-33 IU/L in men and 19-25 IU/L in women, and persistent elevation above this deserves a structured aetiology screen.`],answer:[{heading:`Components of the liver panel`,points:[`ALT - liver-specific; AST - also muscle, heart, red cells; ALP - canalicular, bone, placenta; GGT - confirms hepatic ALP.`,`Bilirubin total and direct; albumin (half-life 3 weeks); INR (factor VII half-life 6 hours); platelets for portal hypertension.`]},{heading:`Pattern recognition`,points:[`R ratio = (ALT/ULN)/(ALP/ULN): 5 or more hepatocellular, 2 or less cholestatic, 2-5 mixed.`,`AST:ALT over 2 - alcohol; over 1 in non-drinker - cirrhosis; ALT over 1000 - ischaemic, viral, toxin, stone, AIH, Wilson.`,`Isolated bilirubin: unconjugated (Gilbert, haemolysis) vs conjugated (Dubin-Johnson, Rotor).`]},{heading:`Approach to asymptomatic raised ALT`,points:[`Confirm on repeat; quantify alcohol; full drug and herbal history; metabolic risk; viral risk.`,`First line: HBsAg, anti-HCV, ultrasound, HbA1c, lipids, ferritin and transferrin saturation; FIB-4.`,`Second line if negative and persistent: ANA, ASMA, IgG, ceruloplasmin, alpha-1 antitrypsin, anti-tTG, TSH, CK.`,`Elastography if FIB-4 1.3 or more; biopsy if still unexplained.`]},{heading:`Management principles`,points:[`Treat the cause; stop culprit drugs; alcohol abstinence; 7-10% weight loss for MASLD.`,`Vaccinate against hepatitis A and B; no liver tonics.`]},{heading:`When to refer or admit`,points:[`INR 1.5 or more, encephalopathy, ALT over 1000, cholangitis, Hy's law, mass or dilated ducts.`,`FIB-4 over 2.67 or LSM over 12 kPa - hepatology referral.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: raised ALT - repeat - first-line screen - second-line screen - FIB-4/elastography - biopsy`,`Table: hepatocellular vs cholestatic vs mixed with R ratio`],markSplit:[{part:`Components and physiology`,marks:2},{part:`Patterns and R ratio`,marks:3},{part:`Stepwise approach with flow chart`,marks:3},{part:`Management and referral`,marks:2}],keywords:[`R ratio`,`ACG 2017`,`FIB-4`,`Hy's law`,`ALT ULN 33/25`,`vitamin K test`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-lft-interpretation-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate Gilbert syndrome from haemolytic jaundice and from Dubin-Johnson syndrome.`,openingLines:[`All three cause jaundice with normal liver enzymes, but Gilbert and haemolysis give unconjugated and Dubin-Johnson gives conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia.`,`Gilbert syndrome is a benign reduction of UGT1A1 activity seen in 3-10% of people.`],answer:[{heading:`Mechanism`,points:[`Gilbert - reduced conjugation (UGT1A1 promoter TA repeat); haemolysis - overproduction of bilirubin; Dubin-Johnson - defective canalicular export (MRP2).`]},{heading:`Clinical and laboratory`,points:[`Gilbert - bilirubin under 4 mg/dL, worse on fasting, normal Hb and reticulocytes, no bilirubinuria.`,`Haemolysis - anaemia, high reticulocytes, LDH, low haptoglobin, splenomegaly, pigment gallstones.`,`Dubin-Johnson - conjugated bilirubin, bilirubinuria, black liver, urinary coproporphyrin I over 80%.`]},{heading:`Management and prognosis`,points:[`Gilbert and Dubin-Johnson - reassurance, normal life expectancy; haemolysis - treat cause, folic acid.`]}],mustDraw:[`Bilirubin metabolism diagram showing the step affected in each`],markSplit:[{part:`Mechanism`,marks:1},{part:`Comparison table`,marks:3},{part:`Management`,marks:1}],keywords:[`UGT1A1`,`MRP2`,`unconjugated`,`conjugated`,`reticulocytes`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Evaluation of liver function; Approach to the patient with liver disease`,`Kwo PY, Cohen SM, Lim JK. ACG Clinical Guideline: Evaluation of Abnormal Liver Chemistries. Am J Gastroenterol 2017`,`Newsome PN et al. Guidelines on the management of abnormal liver blood tests (BSG). Gut 2018`,`AASLD Practice Guidance on the clinical assessment and management of MASLD, 2023`,`EASL-EASD-EASO Clinical Practice Guidelines on MASLD, 2024`,`AASLD Practice Guidance: Drug, herbal and dietary supplement-induced liver injury, 2023`,`National Viral Hepatitis Control Programme (NVHCP) operational and technical guidelines, MoHFW 2018`]}),i.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-masld`,title:`MASLD/MASH (fatty liver disease)`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD)** is hepatic steatosis with **at least one of five cardiometabolic risk factors** and no other cause, renamed from NAFLD in **2023**; its outcome is set by **fibrosis stage**, triaged by **FIB-4 then elastography**, and treated by **7-10% weight loss**, with **resmetirom (2024)** and **semaglutide 2.4 mg (2025)** approved for non-cirrhotic MASH with F2-F3 fibrosis.`,keywords:[`MASLD`,`MASH`,`NAFLD`,`NASH`,`MAFLD`,`MetALD`,`steatotic liver disease`,`fatty liver`,`FIB-4`,`FibroScan`,`transient elastography`,`CAP`,`resmetirom`,`semaglutide`,`saroglitazar`,`pioglitazone`,`vitamin E`,`PNPLA3`,`lean NAFLD`,`bariatric surgery`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Steatotic liver disease (SLD)** is the 2023 umbrella term for hepatic fat of **5% or more**; it is split into **MASLD, MetALD, ALD, specific aetiology SLD and cryptogenic SLD**. [AASLD-EASL-ALEH 2023]`,`**MASLD** = steatosis on imaging or biopsy plus **at least one of five cardiometabolic criteria** (BMI or waist, glucose, BP, triglycerides, HDL) and alcohol under **140 g/week in women and 210 g/week in men**. [AASLD-EASL-ALEH 2023]`,`**MetALD** is MASLD with alcohol intake of **140-350 g/week in women or 210-420 g/week in men**; above that it is alcohol-related liver disease. [AASLD-EASL-ALEH 2023]`,`**MASH** (formerly NASH) is MASLD with **lobular inflammation and hepatocyte ballooning** on histology, the progressive form. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`,`Global prevalence is about **30-38% of adults** and rising, making MASLD the commonest chronic liver disease worldwide. [Younossi 2023]`,`In India a meta-analysis found a pooled prevalence of about **38.6% in adults** and about one-third in children, with higher rates in urban and diabetic populations. [Shalimar 2022 meta-analysis]`,`About **55-70% of people with type 2 diabetes** have MASLD, and **10-20%** of them have advanced fibrosis. [ADA 2025]`,`**Lean MASLD** (BMI under 23 kg/m2 in Asians) is common in India, reflecting visceral adiposity and genetic risk at lower BMI. [INASL 2023]`,`MASLD is becoming a leading indication for **liver transplantation and a growing cause of HCC**. [EASL 2024]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**The five cardiometabolic criteria**: BMI 23 or more (Asian) or waist over **90 cm men / 80 cm women**; fasting glucose 100 mg/dL or more, HbA1c 5.7% or more, or diabetes; BP 130/85 or more or treated; triglycerides 150 mg/dL or more; HDL under 40 (men) or 50 mg/dL (women). [AASLD-EASL-ALEH 2023]`,`**Insulin resistance and visceral adiposity** are the core drivers; type 2 diabetes is the strongest predictor of progression to advanced fibrosis. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`,`**Diet**: excess calories, **fructose and sugar-sweetened beverages**, refined carbohydrates, saturated fat and ultra-processed food. [EASL 2024]`,`**Genetics**: **PNPLA3 I148M** (common in South Asians), TM6SF2 and MBOAT7 raise risk; **HSD17B13** loss-of-function variants protect. [Harrison 22e]`,`Associations: **obstructive sleep apnoea, PCOS, hypothyroidism, hypogonadism, growth hormone deficiency**. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`,`**Specific aetiology SLD** must be excluded: drugs (amiodarone, methotrexate, tamoxifen, steroids, valproate), HCV genotype 3, Wilson disease, starvation and total parenteral nutrition. [AASLD-EASL-ALEH 2023]`,`**Alcohol** interacts with metabolic risk - even moderate drinking accelerates fibrosis in MASLD, which is why MetALD was created. [EASL 2024]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**Insulin resistance in adipose tissue** fails to suppress lipolysis, so **free fatty acid flux** to the liver rises and provides about 60% of hepatic triglyceride. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hyperinsulinaemia** activates **SREBP-1c** and fructose activates **ChREBP**, driving **de novo lipogenesis**, which contributes about 25% of liver fat. [Harrison 22e]`,`Stored triglyceride itself is relatively inert; **lipotoxic species** (diacylglycerol, ceramides, free cholesterol) cause **mitochondrial dysfunction, oxidative stress and ER stress**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Injured hepatocytes balloon and die, releasing danger signals that activate **Kupffer cells and the inflammasome**, producing MASH. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`,`Activated **hepatic stellate cells** become myofibroblasts and lay down collagen around sinusoids (**zone 3 perisinusoidal 'chicken-wire' fibrosis**) - the stage that predicts death. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Gut dysbiosis**, increased intestinal permeability and endotoxin delivered by the portal vein amplify inflammation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Thyroid hormone receptor-beta** in the liver promotes fatty acid oxidation; intrahepatic hypothyroidism in MASH explains why the THR-beta agonist **resmetirom** reduces liver fat. [MAESTRO-NASH 2024]`,`**GLP-1 agonists** act mainly indirectly - weight loss, lower insulin resistance and reduced lipotoxicity - with resolution of MASH as a downstream effect. [ESSENCE 2025]`,`MASLD is a **multisystem disease**: the same insulin resistance raises risk of **cardiovascular disease, type 2 diabetes and CKD**, and **cardiovascular disease is the commonest cause of death**. [EASL 2024]`]},{heading:`Classification and staging`,points:[`**Fibrosis stage (NASH CRN / Kleiner)**: F0 none; F1 perisinusoidal or periportal; F2 perisinusoidal plus periportal; **F3 bridging**; **F4 cirrhosis**. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`,`**'At-risk MASH'** = MASH with **F2 or more** fibrosis - the target group for drug therapy. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`,`**Advanced fibrosis** = F3-F4, and **compensated advanced chronic liver disease (cACLD)** is suspected with liver stiffness over **10-15 kPa**. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**NAFLD activity score (NAS)**: steatosis 0-3, lobular inflammation 0-3, ballooning 0-2 (total 0-8) - a research grading of activity, not diagnosis. [Kleiner 2005]`,`**Steatosis grade on ultrasound or CAP**: mild, moderate, severe; ultrasound detects steatosis reliably only above **about 20% fat**. [EASL 2024]`,`**Fibrosis stage, not inflammation or steatosis**, independently predicts liver-related and overall mortality. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Most patients are **asymptomatic** and present with a fatty liver on ultrasound done for another reason or with mildly raised ALT. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`,`Some report **fatigue or right upper quadrant discomfort** from capsular stretch. [Harrison 22e]`,`Take a **quantified alcohol history** (grams per week) to separate MASLD, MetALD and ALD. [AASLD-EASL-ALEH 2023]`,`Ask about **weight trajectory, diet (sweet drinks, fried snacks), physical activity, sleep and snoring**. [EASL 2024]`,`Ask about **diabetes, hypertension, dyslipidaemia, PCOS, family history of cirrhosis or diabetes**. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`,`Ask about **steatogenic drugs**: amiodarone, methotrexate, tamoxifen, corticosteroids, valproate, antiretrovirals. [AASLD-EASL-ALEH 2023]`,`Late presentations are with **decompensated cirrhosis or HCC**, sometimes in patients never told they had fatty liver. [EASL 2024]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Record **weight, BMI, waist circumference** (Asian cut-offs 90/80 cm) and BP at every visit. [AASLD-EASL-ALEH 2023]`,`**Acanthosis nigricans** marks insulin resistance; **xanthelasma** and arcus suggest dyslipidaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Smooth, mildly enlarged, non-tender liver** is typical of steatosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Spider naevi, palmar erythema, splenomegaly and ascites** indicate cirrhosis and portal hypertension. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for features of **hypothyroidism, PCOS (hirsutism)** and sleep apnoea (large neck circumference). [AASLD MASLD 2023]`,`**Sarcopenic obesity** - low muscle mass with high fat - is common in Indian patients and predicts poor outcome. [EASL 2024]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Alcohol-related liver disease and MetALD**: distinguished only by quantified alcohol intake, AUDIT and AST:ALT over 2. [AASLD-EASL-ALEH 2023]`,`**Chronic hepatitis B and C**: always test HBsAg and anti-HCV, as both coexist with fatty liver; HCV genotype 3 itself causes steatosis. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`,`**Drug-induced steatosis**: amiodarone, methotrexate, tamoxifen, valproate, steroids - classified as specific aetiology SLD. [AASLD-EASL-ALEH 2023]`,`**Wilson disease** in anyone under 40 with fatty liver and raised ALT - check ceruloplasmin. [AASLD Wilson 2022]`,`**Autoimmune hepatitis**: autoantibodies are positive at low titre in up to 20% of MASLD; check IgG and biopsy if high. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`,`**Haemochromatosis**: raised ferritin in MASLD usually reflects inflammation and insulin resistance (**dysmetabolic iron overload**), not iron overload; check transferrin saturation. [EASL 2024]`,`**Coeliac disease, hypothyroidism and malnutrition** (kwashiorkor) can also cause steatosis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - OPD and district level`,points:[`Baseline: **LFT, CBC with platelets, HbA1c or fasting glucose, lipid profile, creatinine, urine albumin, HBsAg and anti-HCV**. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`,`**Ultrasound** diagnoses moderate-severe steatosis (bright liver, posterior attenuation) and shows cirrhosis, splenomegaly and focal lesions. [EASL 2024]`,`**ALT is normal in many patients with MASH and advanced fibrosis**, so a normal ALT does not exclude disease. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`,`**FIB-4** is the first-line fibrosis test: **under 1.3 low risk**, **1.3-2.67 indeterminate**, **over 2.67 high risk**; use **under 2.0 as the low cut-off at age 65 or over**. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`,`FIB-4 is unreliable **under age 35**, so younger patients with metabolic risk go straight to elastography if available. [EASL 2024]`,`**Screen for MASLD fibrosis** in everyone with **type 2 diabetes, abdominal obesity with another metabolic risk factor, or abnormal liver enzymes**. [EASL 2024]`,`Treat **MASLD as a cardiovascular risk state**: calculate 10-year CV risk and screen for diabetes and CKD. [ADA 2025]`]},{heading:`Investigations - advanced and tertiary`,points:[`**Vibration-controlled transient elastography (FibroScan)**: LSM **under 8 kPa rules out** advanced fibrosis; **8-12 kPa indeterminate**; **over 12 kPa high risk**. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`,`**CAP** (controlled attenuation parameter) of about **275 dB/m or more** indicates steatosis. [EASL 2024]`,`**ELF test**: under 7.7 low risk, **9.8 or more high risk** - an alternative second-line test where elastography is unavailable. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`,`**MR elastography and MRI-PDFF** are the most accurate non-invasive measures of fibrosis and fat, used in trials and tertiary centres. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`,`**Baveno VII rule of five**: LSM **under 15 kPa with platelets over 150,000 excludes** clinically significant portal hypertension; **25 kPa or more** confirms it. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`Endoscopy for varices can be avoided if **LSM under 20 kPa and platelets over 150,000**. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Liver biopsy** is reserved for diagnostic doubt, discordant non-invasive tests, or suspected competing aetiology; no longer needed to start resmetirom or semaglutide when NITs agree. [EASL 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - lifestyle first`,points:[`**Weight-loss targets**: **5% or more** reduces liver fat, **7-10%** resolves MASH, **10% or more** can regress fibrosis. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`,`Advise a **calorie deficit of 500-1000 kcal/day**, a **Mediterranean-pattern diet**, and avoid sugar-sweetened beverages, fructose and ultra-processed foods. [EASL 2024]`,`Prescribe **150-300 minutes a week of moderate or 75-150 minutes of vigorous aerobic exercise** plus resistance training twice a week; exercise helps even without weight loss. [EASL 2024]`,`**Alcohol**: advise **complete abstinence in advanced fibrosis or cirrhosis**, and minimise intake in everyone else. [EASL 2024]`,`**Coffee 3 or more cups a day** is associated with less fibrosis and may be encouraged. [EASL 2024]`,`**Statins are safe and should be used** for CV risk in MASLD, including compensated cirrhosis; do not withhold them for raised ALT. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`,`In type 2 diabetes with MASLD, prefer **GLP-1 RA (semaglutide), dual GIP/GLP-1 agonist (tirzepatide), SGLT2 inhibitors or pioglitazone**; metformin is safe but does not improve histology. [ADA 2025]`,`Vaccinate against **hepatitis A and B** if non-immune. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`]},{heading:`Pharmacotherapy for MASH`,points:[`**Resmetirom** (oral THR-beta agonist) received **FDA accelerated approval in March 2024** for non-cirrhotic MASH with **F2-F3** fibrosis: **80 mg once daily if under 100 kg, 100 mg if 100 kg or more**. [FDA 2024]`,`In **MAESTRO-NASH**, resmetirom achieved MASH resolution in **about 26-30% vs 10%** and fibrosis improvement in **about 24-26% vs 14%** with placebo at 52 weeks. [MAESTRO-NASH 2024]`,`**Semaglutide 2.4 mg SC weekly** received **FDA accelerated approval in August 2025** for non-cirrhotic MASH with F2-F3 after the **ESSENCE** trial (MASH resolution about 63% vs 34%; fibrosis improvement about 37% vs 22%). [ESSENCE 2025]`,`**Pioglitazone 30 mg daily** improves MASH histology and may be used in MASH, particularly with type 2 diabetes; weight gain and oedema limit it. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`,`**Vitamin E 800 IU/day** may be considered in non-diabetic biopsy-proven MASH per AASLD, while **EASL 2024 does not recommend it** as MASH-targeted therapy - an acknowledged guideline difference. [AASLD MASLD 2023; EASL 2024]`,`**Saroglitazar 4 mg daily** (dual PPAR alpha/gamma agonist) is approved in **India** for non-cirrhotic NASH and NAFLD with comorbidities; it lowers ALT and triglycerides but long-term histological outcome data are limited. [DCGI 2020]`,`**Ursodeoxycholic acid, silymarin and other 'liver tonics' have no proven benefit** in MASLD and should not be prescribed. [INASL 2023]`,`**Bariatric (metabolic) surgery** is considered for BMI **35 or more, or 30-34.9 with metabolic disease**, with lower thresholds (about 27.5) in Asians; it resolves MASH in most patients. [IFSO-ASMBS 2022]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / complicated disease`,points:[`MASH cirrhosis is managed like any cirrhosis: **variceal screening, ascites, encephalopathy and HCC surveillance** - see the Cirrhosis topic. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Resmetirom and semaglutide are not approved in decompensated cirrhosis**; resmetirom is also not approved in compensated cirrhosis (F4) pending outcome trials. [FDA 2024]`,`Before bariatric surgery in cirrhosis, confirm **compensated disease without clinically significant portal hypertension**; sleeve gastrectomy is preferred. [EASL 2024]`,`In **hospitalised diabetics with MASH cirrhosis**, use insulin for glycaemic control; avoid metformin in decompensation and renal impairment. [ADA 2025]`,`Rapid weight loss over **1.5 kg/week** (crash diets, surgery) can transiently worsen inflammation - aim for gradual loss. [EASL 2024]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Children**: screen obese children (and overweight with risk factors) from **age 9-11** with ALT; ALT over **2 times the sex-specific ULN (26 boys, 22 girls)** persisting warrants evaluation. [NASPGHAN 2017]`,`**Pregnancy**: MASLD raises the risk of **gestational diabetes, pre-eclampsia and preterm birth**; GLP-1 agonists, resmetirom and statins are avoided in pregnancy. [EASL 2024]`,`**Elderly**: FIB-4 low cut-off rises to **2.0 at age 65 or over**; sarcopenia means weight loss should protect muscle with protein and resistance training. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`,`**Lean MASLD**: aim for **3-5% weight loss**, which is enough to reduce fat in lean patients; still screen fibrosis. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`,`**CKD**: MASLD doubles CKD risk; SGLT2 inhibitors and GLP-1 RA serve both organs. [ADA 2025]`,`**Resource-limited settings**: ultrasound plus FIB-4 from routine CBC and LFT is enough to triage most patients without elastography. [INASL 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Cardiovascular disease is the leading cause of death**, then extrahepatic cancers, then liver-related deaths. [EASL 2024]`,`Fibrosis progresses about **one stage every 7 years in MASLD and every 14 years** in simple steatosis, but faster with diabetes. [Singh 2015 meta-analysis]`,`Liver-related mortality rises steeply with stage: risk is **many-fold higher at F3-F4** than F0-F1. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`,`**HCC** can arise in non-cirrhotic MASLD, though surveillance is recommended only in cirrhosis. [AASLD HCC 2023]`,`MASLD increases incidence of **type 2 diabetes, CKD, atrial fibrillation and colorectal cancer**. [EASL 2024]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and prevention`,points:[`**Low risk (FIB-4 under 1.3 or LSM under 8 kPa)**: manage in primary care with lifestyle and CV risk control; **repeat FIB-4 every 1-2 years in diabetes, 2-3 years otherwise**. [EASL 2024]`,`**Indeterminate or high risk**: refer for elastography and hepatology review; **LSM over 12 kPa** or FIB-4 over 2.67 needs specialist care. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`,`**Cirrhosis**: ultrasound with or without AFP **every 6 months**, variceal screening per Baveno VII. [AASLD HCC 2023]`,`India became the first country to integrate **NAFLD into the national NCD programme (NPCDCS, now NP-NCD) in 2021**, with screening at Ayushman Arogya Mandir. [MoHFW 2021]`,`Counsel **families**: shared diet and genetic risk mean relatives, especially children, benefit from screening and lifestyle change. [INASL 2023]`,`Use plain, non-stigmatising language - the 2023 rename dropped 'non-alcoholic' and 'fatty' partly to reduce stigma. [AASLD-EASL-ALEH 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates and exam angle`,points:[`**2023 multisociety Delphi consensus**: NAFLD to **MASLD**, NASH to **MASH**, new **MetALD** category; about 98-99% of NAFLD cohorts meet MASLD criteria, so older data still apply. [AASLD-EASL-ALEH 2023]`,`**AASLD 2023 practice guidance**: FIB-4 then VCTE or ELF; 'at-risk MASH' F2 or more; statins safe. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`,`**EASL-EASD-EASO 2024 CPG**: resmetirom where approved for F2-F3; no recommendation for vitamin E or pioglitazone as MASH-targeted drugs. [EASL 2024]`,`**FDA 2024 resmetirom** and **FDA 2025 semaglutide 2.4 mg** approvals - the first two drugs specifically licensed for MASH. [FDA 2025]`,`**Tirzepatide** (SYNERGY-NASH phase 2, 2024) and other incretin agents improved MASH histology but are **not yet licensed for MASH**; use them for diabetes or obesity indications. [SYNERGY-NASH 2024]`,`**Viva classics**: five cardiometabolic criteria, MetALD alcohol thresholds, FIB-4 formula and cut-offs, weight loss targets, why fibrosis not ALT predicts outcome, commonest cause of death (CVD). [EASL 2024]`,`**Common mistakes**: stopping statins for raised ALT, reassuring because ALT is normal, prescribing UDCA or liver tonics, and forgetting HBsAg in Indian patients. [AASLD MASLD 2023]`]}],tables:[{heading:`2023 steatotic liver disease nomenclature`,columns:[`Category`,`Criteria`,`Alcohol intake`,`Comment`],rows:[[`MASLD`,`Steatosis plus 1 or more of 5 cardiometabolic criteria`,`Under 140 g/week (women), 210 g/week (men)`,`Replaces NAFLD`],[`MASH`,`MASLD with inflammation and ballooning on histology`,`As MASLD`,`Replaces NASH; progressive form`],[`MetALD`,`MASLD criteria plus higher alcohol`,`140-350 g/week (women), 210-420 g/week (men)`,`Continuum of metabolic and alcohol injury`],[`ALD`,`Alcohol-related`,`Over 350 g/week (women), 420 g/week (men)`,`See alcohol-associated liver disease`],[`Specific aetiology SLD`,`Drugs, HCV-3, Wilson, starvation, TPN`,`Any`,`Treat the cause`],[`Cryptogenic SLD`,`Steatosis, no metabolic criteria, no other cause`,`Low`,`Re-evaluate over time`]]},{heading:`Non-invasive fibrosis tests and cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`Low risk (rule out)`,`Indeterminate`,`High risk (rule in)`],rows:[[`FIB-4 (age 35-64)`,`Under 1.3`,`1.3-2.67`,`Over 2.67`],[`FIB-4 (age 65 or over)`,`Under 2.0`,`2.0-2.67`,`Over 2.67`],[`VCTE liver stiffness`,`Under 8 kPa`,`8-12 kPa`,`Over 12 kPa`],[`ELF`,`Under 7.7`,`7.7-9.8`,`9.8 or more`],[`Baveno VII CSPH`,`LSM under 15 kPa and platelets over 150,000`,`15-25 kPa`,`25 kPa or more`]]},{heading:`Drug treatment in MASLD/MASH`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Resmetirom`,`80 mg oral OD (under 100 kg); 100 mg OD (100 kg or more)`,`Long-term; reassess at 12 months`,`Diarrhoea, nausea; avoid strong CYP2C8 inhibitors (gemfibrozil); not in decompensated cirrhosis`],[`Semaglutide`,`0.25 mg SC weekly, step up every 4 weeks to 2.4 mg weekly`,`Long-term`,`Nausea, vomiting, gallstones, pancreatitis; avoid in MTC/MEN2 and pregnancy`],[`Pioglitazone`,`15-30 mg oral OD (up to 45 mg)`,`Long-term`,`Weight gain, oedema, fractures; avoid in heart failure, bladder cancer`],[`Vitamin E`,`800 IU oral OD`,`Up to 2 years, reassess`,`Non-diabetic biopsy-proven MASH only; possible prostate cancer and haemorrhagic stroke signal`],[`Saroglitazar (India)`,`4 mg oral OD before breakfast`,`Long-term with monitoring`,`Avoid in pregnancy; monitor creatinine, weight`],[`Atorvastatin`,`10-40 mg oral OD per CV risk`,`Lifelong`,`Safe in MASLD and compensated cirrhosis; myalgia`]]},{heading:`MASLD vs MetALD vs alcohol-related liver disease`,columns:[`Feature`,`MASLD`,`MetALD`,`ALD`],rows:[[`Alcohol (men)`,`Under 210 g/week`,`210-420 g/week`,`Over 420 g/week`],[`AST:ALT`,`Usually under 1`,`Variable`,`Often over 2`],[`GGT and MCV`,`Mildly raised GGT`,`Raised`,`High GGT, macrocytosis`],[`Main treatment`,`Weight loss, exercise`,`Both alcohol reduction and weight loss`,`Abstinence, AUD treatment`]]},{heading:`Weight loss and histological effect`,columns:[`Weight loss`,`Effect on liver`,`Practical target`],rows:[[`3-5%`,`Reduces liver fat`,`Enough for lean MASLD`],[`7-10%`,`Resolves MASH in most`,`Standard target for overweight`],[`10% or more`,`Fibrosis regression in about 45%`,`Target with F2 or more`],[`Bariatric surgery (20-30%)`,`MASH resolution in about 80%`,`BMI 35 or more, Asian thresholds lower`]]}],redFlags:[`Jaundice, ascites, encephalopathy or GI bleed - decompensated MASH cirrhosis`,`FIB-4 over 2.67 or liver stiffness over 12 kPa - advanced fibrosis needing hepatology`,`Platelets under 150,000 or splenomegaly in a fatty liver patient - possible portal hypertension`,`New focal lesion on ultrasound in a MASLD patient - exclude HCC`,`Rapid weight loss over 1.5 kg/week with rising bilirubin - decompensation risk`,`ALT over 5 times ULN - look for another cause (viral, drug, autoimmune)`],pearls:[`**MASLD = steatosis + 1 of 5 cardiometabolic criteria** (waist/BMI, glucose, BP, TG, HDL).`,`**Fibrosis, not ALT or fat, predicts death**; ALT is normal in many with advanced fibrosis.`,`**FIB-4 under 1.3 rules out, over 2.67 rules in**; use 2.0 as the low cut-off at 65+.`,`**FibroScan 8 and 12 kPa** are the rule-out and rule-in numbers.`,`Weight loss **5-7-10**: fat, MASH, fibrosis.`,`**CVD is the commonest cause of death in MASLD** - treat it as a CV risk state.`,`**Statins are safe** - stopping them for raised ALT is a classic error.`,`**Resmetirom (2024) and semaglutide 2.4 mg (2025)**: the only drugs licensed for F2-F3 MASH.`,`India integrated NAFLD into **NPCDCS in 2021** - first country to do so.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-masld-q1`,stem:`A 52-year-old diabetic man has fatty liver on ultrasound, AST 38, ALT 42 IU/L and platelets 1.9 lakh/microL. FIB-4 is 1.8. What is the next step?`,options:[`Reassure, as ALT is barely raised`,`Liver biopsy`,`Repeat FIB-4 after 3 years`,`Vibration-controlled transient elastography`],answer:3,explanation:`A FIB-4 of 1.3-2.67 is indeterminate, and AASLD 2023 and EASL 2024 recommend a second-tier test - elastography (or ELF) - next. Reassurance ignores that ALT is a poor marker of fibrosis; repeating after 3 years is for low-risk FIB-4 under 1.3; biopsy is reserved for discordant or doubtful non-invasive results.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-masld-q2`,stem:`Under the 2023 nomenclature, a woman with steatosis, BMI 27 kg/m2 and alcohol intake of 200 g/week is classified as:`,options:[`MASLD`,`MetALD`,`Alcohol-related liver disease`,`Cryptogenic steatotic liver disease`],answer:1,explanation:`She meets a cardiometabolic criterion (BMI 23 or more in Asians) and drinks 140-350 g/week, which for women defines MetALD. MASLD requires intake under 140 g/week in women; ALD requires over 350 g/week; cryptogenic SLD has no metabolic criteria.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-masld-q3`,stem:`The commonest cause of death in patients with MASLD is:`,options:[`Hepatocellular carcinoma`,`Cardiovascular disease`,`Variceal haemorrhage`,`Liver failure`],answer:1,explanation:`MASLD shares insulin resistance with atherosclerosis, and cardiovascular disease is the leading cause of death, followed by extrahepatic cancers and only then liver-related causes. HCC, variceal bleeding and liver failure matter in the minority with advanced fibrosis but do not dominate overall mortality.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-masld-q4`,stem:`Resmetirom, approved by the FDA in 2024 for non-cirrhotic MASH with F2-F3 fibrosis, acts by:`,options:[`Agonism of thyroid hormone receptor-beta in the liver`,`PPAR-gamma agonism improving adipose insulin sensitivity`,`GLP-1 receptor agonism causing weight loss`,`Farnesoid X receptor agonism`],answer:0,explanation:`Resmetirom is a liver-directed THR-beta agonist that increases hepatic fatty acid oxidation and lowers liver fat, without the cardiac and bone effects of THR-alpha. PPAR-gamma agonism describes pioglitazone; GLP-1 agonism describes semaglutide (approved in 2025); FXR agonism describes obeticholic acid, which was not approved for MASH.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-masld-q5`,stem:`A 40-year-old overweight man with biopsy-proven MASH and F2 fibrosis asks how much weight he must lose for a chance of fibrosis regression. The best answer is:`,options:[`3% of body weight`,`5% of body weight`,`10% or more of body weight`,`Weight loss does not affect fibrosis`],answer:2,explanation:`Weight loss of 10% or more produces fibrosis regression in a substantial proportion and MASH resolution in most. Around 3-5% reduces steatosis only, 7-10% resolves MASH, and the claim that weight loss does not affect fibrosis contradicts the lifestyle trial data underlying AASLD and EASL guidance.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-masld-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Discuss the new nomenclature, pathogenesis, non-invasive assessment and management of metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD).`,openingLines:[`MASLD is hepatic steatosis of 5% or more with at least one of five cardiometabolic risk factors and no other cause, renamed from NAFLD in the 2023 multisociety Delphi consensus.`,`It affects about 38% of Indian adults, and fibrosis stage - not ALT - determines prognosis.`],answer:[{heading:`Nomenclature 2023`,points:[`SLD umbrella: MASLD, MetALD, ALD, specific aetiology, cryptogenic.`,`Five criteria: BMI 23+/waist 90/80, glucose/HbA1c 5.7%, BP 130/85, TG 150, low HDL.`,`MetALD: 140-350 g/week women, 210-420 g/week men.`]},{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Insulin resistance, FFA flux, de novo lipogenesis (SREBP-1c, fructose), lipotoxicity, ER and oxidative stress.`,`Stellate cell activation with zone 3 perisinusoidal fibrosis; PNPLA3 I148M genetic risk.`]},{heading:`Non-invasive assessment`,points:[`Ultrasound for steatosis; FIB-4 under 1.3 / over 2.67 (2.0 at 65+).`,`VCTE under 8 / over 12 kPa; ELF 9.8; Baveno VII 15/25 kPa for CSPH.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Weight loss 5/7-10/10%+; Mediterranean diet; 150-300 min exercise; alcohol minimise.`,`Statins safe; GLP-1 RA, tirzepatide, SGLT2i or pioglitazone in diabetes.`,`Resmetirom 80/100 mg OD and semaglutide 2.4 mg weekly for F2-F3; vitamin E 800 IU non-diabetic (AASLD); saroglitazar 4 mg in India.`,`Bariatric surgery for BMI thresholds; cirrhosis care and HCC surveillance.`]},{heading:`Programme and follow-up`,points:[`NAFLD in NPCDCS (2021); screen at Ayushman Arogya Mandir; repeat FIB-4 1-3 yearly.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: risk group - FIB-4 - elastography - primary care vs hepatology`,`Diagram: pathogenesis from insulin resistance to fibrosis`],markSplit:[{part:`Nomenclature and epidemiology`,marks:2},{part:`Pathogenesis`,marks:2},{part:`Non-invasive assessment with flow chart`,marks:3},{part:`Management`,marks:3}],keywords:[`MASLD`,`MetALD`,`FIB-4`,`VCTE`,`resmetirom`,`semaglutide`,`weight loss`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-masld-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`chart-flow`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Draw a flow chart for fibrosis risk stratification of a patient with type 2 diabetes found to have fatty liver in primary care.`,openingLines:[`Every patient with type 2 diabetes and steatosis should be risk-stratified for advanced fibrosis using a two-step non-invasive pathway.`,`Step one is FIB-4 from routine tests; step two is elastography or ELF.`],answer:[{heading:`Step 1 - FIB-4`,points:[`Under 1.3 (under 2.0 at 65+): low risk - lifestyle, CV risk control, repeat in 1-2 years.`,`1.3-2.67: indeterminate - elastography.`,`Over 2.67: high risk - hepatology referral.`]},{heading:`Step 2 - Elastography`,points:[`Under 8 kPa: low risk, primary care.`,`8-12 kPa: hepatology, consider biopsy or ELF.`,`Over 12 kPa: advanced fibrosis - drug therapy, Baveno VII variceal assessment.`]},{heading:`Treatment link`,points:[`Prefer GLP-1 RA or pioglitazone for glycaemia; resmetirom or semaglutide 2.4 mg for F2-F3.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-step flow chart with FIB-4 and VCTE cut-offs`],markSplit:[{part:`FIB-4 step with cut-offs`,marks:2},{part:`Elastography step`,marks:2},{part:`Action at each level`,marks:1}],keywords:[`FIB-4`,`VCTE`,`type 2 diabetes`,`two-step pathway`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Nonalcoholic (metabolic dysfunction-associated) fatty liver diseases`,`Rinella ME et al. A multisociety Delphi consensus statement on new fatty liver disease nomenclature. Hepatology / J Hepatol 2023`,`Rinella ME et al. AASLD Practice Guidance on the clinical assessment and management of NAFLD/MASLD. Hepatology 2023`,`EASL-EASD-EASO Clinical Practice Guidelines on the management of MASLD. J Hepatol 2024`,`Harrison SA et al. A phase 3 trial of resmetirom in NASH with liver fibrosis (MAESTRO-NASH). N Engl J Med 2024`,`Sanyal AJ et al. Phase 3 trial of semaglutide in metabolic dysfunction-associated steatohepatitis (ESSENCE). N Engl J Med 2025`,`INASL guidance on nomenclature, diagnosis and treatment of NAFLD/MASLD. J Clin Exp Hepatol 2023`,`MoHFW Operational guidelines for integration of NAFLD with NPCDCS, 2021; Baveno VII consensus 2022`]}),i.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-alcohol-liver-disease`,title:`Alcohol-associated liver disease and alcoholic hepatitis`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Alcohol-associated liver disease (ALD)** is a spectrum of **steatosis, steatohepatitis, fibrosis and cirrhosis** caused by sustained heavy drinking; **alcohol-associated hepatitis** is recent-onset jaundice (bilirubin over 3 mg/dL, AST over 50, AST:ALT over 1.5, both under 400) graded by **Maddrey 32 or more / MELD over 20**, treated with **prednisolone 40 mg for 28 days with Lille stopping at day 7**, while **abstinence with AUD treatment (baclofen, acamprosate)** decides long-term survival.`,keywords:[`ALD`,`alcoholic liver disease`,`alcohol-associated hepatitis`,`alcoholic hepatitis`,`Maddrey discriminant function`,`MELD`,`Lille score`,`Glasgow alcoholic hepatitis score`,`prednisolone`,`pentoxifylline`,`N-acetylcysteine`,`AUD`,`baclofen`,`acamprosate`,`naltrexone`,`AUDIT-C`,`PEth`,`early liver transplantation`,`Mallory-Denk bodies`,`alcohol withdrawal`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**ALD** is liver injury caused by alcohol, ranging from **steatosis (in 90% of heavy drinkers)** to steatohepatitis (10-35%) and **cirrhosis (10-20%)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Alcohol-associated hepatitis (AH)** is a clinical syndrome of **jaundice onset within 8 weeks of heavy drinking**, often superimposed on cirrhosis, with high short-term mortality. [ACG 2024]`,`Under the 2023 nomenclature, liver disease with intake over **350 g/week in women or 420 g/week in men** is ALD, while lower but significant intake with metabolic risk is **MetALD**. [AASLD-EASL-ALEH 2023]`,`A **standard drink** contains **10 g of alcohol (WHO)**; in the US it is 14 g - convert every history to **grams per day or week**. [WHO 2024]`,`Alcohol causes about **2.6 million deaths a year worldwide**, and ALD accounts for about half of all cirrhosis deaths. [WHO Global Status Report 2024]`,`In India, the **Magnitude of Substance Use survey (2019)** found about **14.6% of people aged 10-75 years use alcohol**, and about **5% have harmful use or dependence**. [MoSJE-AIIMS 2019]`,`ALD is the **commonest cause of cirrhosis in many Indian tertiary centres** and presents a decade earlier than in the West, often with AH or ACLF. [INASL 2019]`,`The risk of cirrhosis rises steeply above **about 30-40 g/day in men and 20 g/day in women** sustained for 10 years or more. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Amount and duration** of drinking are the main determinants; pattern matters too - **daily drinking and binge drinking** are both harmful. [ACG 2024]`,`**Women** develop ALD at lower doses because of lower gastric alcohol dehydrogenase, smaller volume of distribution and oestrogen effects. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Genetics**: **PNPLA3 I148M**, TM6SF2 and MBOAT7 variants increase the risk of alcohol cirrhosis; HSD17B13 variants protect. [ACG 2024]`,`**Obesity and diabetes** act synergistically with alcohol, multiplying the risk of fibrosis and HCC. [EASL 2024]`,`**Coexisting HBV or HCV infection** sharply accelerates cirrhosis and HCC. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Smoking** increases fibrosis progression and HCC risk; coffee appears protective. [ACG 2024]`,`Malnutrition, which is very common in Indian drinkers, worsens prognosis and infection risk. [ACG 2024]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Alcohol is oxidised to **acetaldehyde** by **alcohol dehydrogenase (ADH)** and, at high or chronic intake, by **CYP2E1** (the inducible microsomal ethanol-oxidising system), then to acetate by **ALDH2**. [Harrison 22e]`,`These reactions raise the **NADH:NAD+ ratio**, which suppresses fatty acid oxidation and gluconeogenesis - explaining **steatosis, fasting hypoglycaemia, lactic acidosis and hyperuricaemia** in drinkers. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acetaldehyde** forms protein adducts that are directly toxic and immunogenic; **CYP2E1** generates reactive oxygen species and explains increased **paracetamol toxicity** in drinkers. [Harrison 22e]`,`Alcohol increases gut permeability, so **endotoxin (LPS) reaches Kupffer cells via TLR4**, releasing **TNF-alpha, IL-1 and IL-8** and recruiting neutrophils - the inflammatory engine of AH. [ACG 2024]`,`Histology of AH shows **macrovesicular steatosis, ballooning, Mallory-Denk bodies, neutrophilic infiltration and pericellular 'chicken-wire' fibrosis** in **zone 3 (perivenular)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Zone 3 is most vulnerable** because it has the lowest oxygen tension and the highest CYP2E1 activity. [Harrison 22e]`,`In severe AH the liver fails to regenerate (impaired hepatocyte proliferation), which is why **steroids reduce inflammation** but survival depends on regeneration and abstinence. [ACG 2024]`,`**Immune paralysis** after the initial inflammatory burst makes **infections** the commonest cause of death in AH, especially in steroid non-responders. [EASL 2018]`,`Alcohol also causes **thiamine and folate deficiency, cardiomyopathy, pancreatitis and neuropathy**, which coexist with liver disease. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and severity scores`,points:[`**Clinical spectrum**: alcohol-associated steatosis, steatohepatitis, fibrosis, cirrhosis (compensated or decompensated), and **AH superimposed at any stage**. [ACG 2024]`,`**NIAAA criteria for probable AH**: jaundice within 60 days of heavy drinking (over **40 g/day women, 60 g/day men** for 6 months or more), **bilirubin over 3 mg/dL, AST over 50 IU/L, AST:ALT over 1.5, both AST and ALT under 400 IU/L**. [NIAAA 2016]`,`**Maddrey discriminant function = 4.6 x (patient PT minus control PT in seconds) + bilirubin (mg/dL)**; **32 or more = severe AH**. [ACG 2024]`,`**MELD over 20** identifies severe AH and is now preferred by ACG 2024 because it is more accurate and uses the INR. [ACG 2024]`,`**Lille score** combines age, albumin, creatinine, PT and the **change in bilirubin from day 0 to day 7 (or day 4)**; **over 0.45 = non-response** to steroids. [ACG 2024]`,`**Glasgow AH score** (age, WBC, urea, INR, bilirubin) of **9 or more** identifies patients who benefit from steroids; **ABIC** is another prognostic score. [EASL 2018]`,`**MELD-Na and Child-Pugh** stage alcohol cirrhosis for prognosis and transplant listing. [AASLD 2023]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Take a **quantified alcohol history**: beverage, volume, strength, days per week, years, last drink, binges - and convert to grams. [ACG 2024]`,`Screen everyone with **AUDIT-C** (positive at **4 or more in men, 3 or more in women**) and assess with full **AUDIT (8 or more hazardous; 20 or more dependence)**. [ACG 2024]`,`Ask about **dependence**: morning drinking, withdrawal tremor, seizures, delirium tremens in the past, and failed attempts to quit. [DSM-5-TR 2022]`,`Steatosis is usually **asymptomatic** or causes vague RUQ discomfort. [Harrison 22e]`,`**AH presents with rapid-onset jaundice, fever, anorexia, RUQ pain and tender hepatomegaly**, often after a binge, and may have **ascites and encephalopathy**. [ACG 2024]`,`Ask about **GI bleeding, confusion, abdominal swelling and reduced urine output** - markers of decompensation and AKI. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **family, work, legal and financial consequences** - they gauge AUD severity and motivation and guide counselling. [ACG 2024]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Tender hepatomegaly with a hepatic bruit**, fever and jaundice are classic of AH. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bilateral parotid enlargement, Dupuytren contracture and gynaecomastia** are associated with alcohol-related chronic liver disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Spider naevi, palmar erythema, testicular atrophy, loss of body hair** reflect hyperoestrogenism of cirrhosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ascites, splenomegaly and caput medusae** indicate portal hypertension. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Asterixis and confusion** suggest hepatic encephalopathy; **ophthalmoplegia, ataxia and confusion** suggest **Wernicke encephalopathy** - give thiamine. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for **muscle wasting (sarcopenia)**, peripheral neuropathy, cerebellar signs and signs of withdrawal (tremor, sweating, tachycardia). [ACG 2024]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Acute viral hepatitis (A, E, B)**: transaminases usually **over 1000 IU/L**, whereas AH rarely exceeds 400. [ACG 2024]`,`**Drug-induced liver injury**, especially **paracetamol in a drinker** (therapeutic misadventure), causes very high transaminases. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sepsis, cholangitis or liver abscess**: fever and leucocytosis overlap with AH; do blood cultures and ultrasound. [ACG 2024]`,`**MASLD/MetALD**: decided by grams of alcohol per week and metabolic features. [AASLD-EASL-ALEH 2023]`,`**Autoimmune hepatitis and Wilson disease** in young patients with atypical features. [ACG 2024]`,`**Ischaemic hepatitis** in alcohol cardiomyopathy or shock. [Harrison 22e]`,`When the diagnosis is uncertain (confounders like recent drugs, atypical labs, unclear alcohol use), **transjugular liver biopsy** confirms AH. [ACG 2024]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**LFT**: AST:ALT over 2 in alcohol injury; **GGT** raised; in AH bilirubin over 3 mg/dL with AST 50-400 IU/L. [ACG 2024]`,`**CBC**: **macrocytosis (MCV over 100 fL)**, leucocytosis in AH, thrombocytopenia from alcohol or portal hypertension. [Harrison 22e]`,`**INR, creatinine, sodium, albumin** to calculate MELD and Maddrey; glucose for hypoglycaemia. [ACG 2024]`,`**Infection screen before steroids**: blood and urine cultures, chest X-ray, **diagnostic ascitic tap**; in India also screen for **TB** and consider **strongyloides** treatment. [ACG 2024; INASL 2019]`,`**Ultrasound with Doppler** to exclude biliary obstruction, HCC and portal vein thrombosis. [ACG 2024]`,`**Alcohol biomarkers**: **phosphatidylethanol (PEth)** in blood (about 2-4 weeks) and **urine ethyl glucuronide** (about 3-5 days) monitor abstinence objectively; CDT and GGT are less specific. [ACG 2024]`,`**Fibrosis staging** in non-AH ALD: FIB-4 and elastography, remembering liver stiffness is falsely high with active drinking and hepatitis - repeat after a week of abstinence. [EASL 2024]`,`Check **HBsAg, anti-HCV, HIV**, lipase if abdominal pain, and echocardiography if cardiomyopathy is suspected. [ACG 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - ALD and AUD`,points:[`**Abstinence is the single most important treatment** and the strongest predictor of survival at every stage of ALD. [ACG 2024]`,`Deliver a **brief intervention (FRAMES)** to every hazardous drinker and refer dependence to integrated hepatology-addiction care. [ACG 2024]`,`**Baclofen** 5 mg TDS for 3 days then **10 mg TDS (30 mg/day)**, titrated up to 75 mg/day - the AUD drug with the best safety data **in cirrhosis**. [ACG 2024]`,`**Acamprosate 666 mg TDS** (two 333 mg tablets TDS; lower dose under 60 kg) maintains abstinence; avoid if **CrCl under 30 mL/min**. [ACG 2024]`,`**Naltrexone 50 mg OD** reduces heavy drinking but is **avoided in decompensated cirrhosis** and in patients on opioids. [ACG 2024]`,`**Disulfiram is contraindicated in ALD** because of hepatotoxicity; **topiramate and gabapentin** are off-label options. [ACG 2024]`,`Prescribe **thiamine 100 mg daily orally** and folic acid, with a nutrition plan of **35-40 kcal/kg/day and 1.2-1.5 g/kg/day protein** plus a late-evening snack. [ACG 2024]`,`Treat **alcohol withdrawal** with symptom-triggered benzodiazepines guided by **CIWA-Ar**; in liver disease prefer **lorazepam or oxazepam** over long-acting chlordiazepoxide or diazepam. [ACG 2024]`,`Vaccinate against **hepatitis A and B, pneumococcus and influenza**; avoid NSAIDs; limit paracetamol to **2 g/day**. [ACG 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD - severe alcohol-associated hepatitis`,points:[`**Admit** AH with MELD over 20 or Maddrey 32 or more, encephalopathy, GI bleed, AKI, infection or inability to eat. [ACG 2024]`,`**Prednisolone 40 mg orally once daily for 28 days** (then stop or taper over 2-3 weeks) for severe AH without contraindications; **methylprednisolone 32 mg IV** if unable to take oral. [ACG 2024]`,`In **STOPAH (2015)**, prednisolone reduced 28-day mortality modestly but **not 90-day or 1-year mortality**; **pentoxifylline had no benefit** and is no longer recommended. [STOPAH 2015]`,`**Contraindications to steroids**: uncontrolled infection, active GI bleeding, AKI or hepatorenal syndrome, **MELD over about 50** (futility), uncontrolled diabetes, active TB. [ACG 2024]`,`**Calculate Lille at day 4 or 7**: over 0.45 means **non-response - stop steroids** because continuing only adds infection risk. [ACG 2024]`,`**IV N-acetylcysteine** for 5 days combined with prednisolone improved 1-month survival in one RCT and may be added. [ACG 2024]`,`**Nutrition**: enteral feeding if oral intake under the target; malnutrition independently predicts death. [ACG 2024]`,`**Thiamine 200-500 mg IV TDS before glucose** if Wernicke is suspected; correct hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia and hypoglycaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**G-CSF and faecal microbiota transplantation** showed benefit in small Indian studies but are **not recommended outside trials** by ACG 2024. [ACG 2024]`,`**Early liver transplantation** (without a 6-month abstinence rule) is an option for carefully selected **first-presentation severe AH non-responders** with strong psychosocial support. [ACG 2024]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: no safe level of alcohol; risk of **fetal alcohol spectrum disorder**; prefer psychosocial treatment; benzodiazepines for withdrawal under specialist care. [WHO 2024]`,`**Elderly**: lower tolerance, more falls and drug interactions; use lower doses of baclofen and short-acting benzodiazepines. [ACG 2024]`,`**CKD**: avoid acamprosate if CrCl under 30; reduce baclofen dose (renally excreted, risk of encephalopathy-like toxicity). [ACG 2024]`,`**Diabetes**: steroids for AH cause marked hyperglycaemia - start insulin; alcohol plus sulfonylurea risks hypoglycaemia. [ADA 2025]`,`**Concomitant TB**: screen before steroids; ATT is more hepatotoxic in ALD - choose regimen with specialist input. [NTEP Guidelines 2022]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: Maddrey uses PT and bilirubin, which most district labs can provide, and prednisolone is cheap and on NLEM. [NLEM 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Severe AH** has about **20-30% mortality at 1 month** and up to **30-50% at 3 months**; Lille non-responders have about **25% 6-month survival**. [ACG 2024]`,`**Infection** (spontaneous bacterial peritonitis, pneumonia, fungal) and **AKI or hepatorenal syndrome** are the commonest causes of death in AH. [EASL 2018]`,`Severe AH frequently evolves into **acute-on-chronic liver failure (ACLF)**, which is particularly common in India. [APASL ACLF 2019]`,`**Alcohol cirrhosis** has a 5-year survival of about **60-70% with abstinence** versus under 40% with continued drinking. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HCC** risk is about **1-2% per year** in alcohol cirrhosis. [AASLD HCC 2023]`,`Extrahepatic: **Wernicke-Korsakoff, cardiomyopathy, pancreatitis, oropharyngeal and oesophageal cancers**, trauma and suicide. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer urgently** any drinker with jaundice, encephalopathy, GI bleed, ascites or INR over 1.5. [ACG 2024]`,`**Integrated care**: hepatology plus addiction psychiatry or a de-addiction centre doubles abstinence rates. [ACG 2024]`,`**Monitor abstinence** with history, AUDIT-C and **PEth or urine EtG** at each visit, framed supportively, not punitively. [ACG 2024]`,`On steroids: **weekly bilirubin, creatinine, glucose and infection screen**, Lille at day 4-7. [ACG 2024]`,`**Cirrhosis**: 6-monthly ultrasound for HCC, variceal screening per Baveno VII, MELD for transplant referral at **MELD 15 or more**. [AASLD 2023]`,`Relapse is common - treat it as part of a chronic disease, not a reason to withdraw care. [ACG 2024]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`**WHO SAFER**: strengthen restrictions on availability, enforce drink-driving laws, screening and brief interventions, ban advertising, and raise prices through taxation. [WHO SAFER 2018]`,`**National Action Plan for Drug Demand Reduction (NAPDDR)** funds de-addiction centres, and **Tele-MANAS (14416)** offers free mental health support. [MoSJE 2018; MoHFW 2022]`,`Screen for hazardous drinking at **NP-NCD** and **Ayushman Arogya Mandir** visits, and deliver brief advice. [NP-NCD 2023]`,`Counsel families; involve them in treatment and recognise domestic violence and child neglect. [ACG 2024]`,`There is **no safe level of alcohol for the liver** in established liver disease of any cause. [EASL 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates and exam angle`,points:[`**ACG 2024 ALD guideline**: universal AUDIT-C screening, MELD over 20 for severe AH, prednisolone 40 mg for 28 days, Lille at day 4 or 7, **against pentoxifylline**, consider NAC, PEth for monitoring, and early transplant in selected cases. [ACG 2024]`,`**AASLD 2019 practice guidance** and **EASL 2018 CPG** remain consistent on steroids and Lille, but older guidance used Maddrey alone. [AASLD 2019]`,`**2023 nomenclature** introduced **MetALD** for patients with both metabolic risk and moderate drinking. [AASLD-EASL-ALEH 2023]`,`The **6-month abstinence rule** before transplant has been abandoned in favour of individual psychosocial assessment. [ACG 2024]`,`**Viva classics**: Maddrey formula, NIAAA criteria, Lille, why AST:ALT exceeds 2, steroid contraindications, why disulfiram is avoided and baclofen is preferred in cirrhosis. [ACG 2024]`,`**Common mistakes**: starting steroids without an infection screen, continuing steroids beyond day 7 in non-responders, giving glucose before thiamine, and using chlordiazepoxide in decompensated cirrhosis. [ACG 2024]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Severity scores in alcohol-associated hepatitis`,columns:[`Score`,`Components`,`Threshold`,`Use`],rows:[[`Maddrey DF`,`4.6 x (PT minus control) + bilirubin mg/dL`,`32 or more = severe`,`Start steroids if no contraindication`],[`MELD`,`Bilirubin, INR, creatinine (+/- sodium)`,`Over 20 = severe`,`Preferred by ACG 2024`],[`Glasgow AH score`,`Age, WBC, urea, INR, bilirubin`,`9 or more = poor prognosis`,`Identifies steroid benefit`],[`Lille`,`Age, albumin, creatinine, PT, bilirubin day 0 and day 7`,`Over 0.45 = non-response`,`Stop steroids at day 4-7`],[`ABIC`,`Age, bilirubin, INR, creatinine`,`Over 9 = high risk`,`Prognosis at 90 days`]]},{heading:`Drugs in alcohol-associated liver disease and AUD`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Prednisolone`,`40 mg oral OD`,`28 days; stop if Lille over 0.45 at day 7`,`Infection, hyperglycaemia, GI bleed; not with sepsis, AKI, bleeding`],[`N-acetylcysteine (adjunct)`,`IV infusion over 5 days with prednisolone`,`5 days`,`Anaphylactoid reaction`],[`Baclofen`,`5 mg TDS for 3 days, then 10 mg TDS`,`Up to 75 mg/day; long-term`,`Drowsiness, confusion; reduce in CKD; safe in cirrhosis`],[`Acamprosate`,`666 mg oral TDS (lower under 60 kg)`,`12 months, continue during relapse`,`Diarrhoea; avoid CrCl under 30`],[`Naltrexone`,`50 mg oral OD`,`3-12 months`,`Avoid in decompensated cirrhosis and opioid use`],[`Lorazepam (withdrawal)`,`1-2 mg oral or IV, symptom-triggered by CIWA-Ar`,`Taper over 5-7 days`,`Sedation, respiratory depression; preferred in liver disease`],[`Thiamine`,`100 mg oral OD; 200-500 mg IV TDS if Wernicke`,`3-5 days IV, then oral`,`Give before glucose`]]},{heading:`Alcohol-associated hepatitis vs acute viral hepatitis vs DILI`,columns:[`Feature`,`Alcohol-associated hepatitis`,`Acute viral hepatitis`,`Paracetamol DILI in a drinker`],rows:[[`AST / ALT`,`AST 50-400, AST:ALT over 1.5`,`ALT often over 1000`,`AST and ALT often over 3000`],[`Fever, leucocytosis`,`Common`,`Prodrome, lymphocytosis`,`Not typical`],[`Liver`,`Tender, enlarged, bruit`,`Tender, mildly enlarged`,`Variable`],[`Key test`,`MELD, Maddrey, exclusion of infection`,`IgM anti-HAV, anti-HEV, HBsAg`,`Paracetamol level, history`],[`Treatment`,`Prednisolone if severe, abstinence`,`Supportive`,`N-acetylcysteine`]]},{heading:`Spectrum of alcohol-associated liver disease`,columns:[`Stage`,`Frequency in heavy drinkers`,`Histology`,`Reversibility`],rows:[[`Steatosis`,`90-100%`,`Macrovesicular fat, zone 3`,`Fully reversible in 4-6 weeks of abstinence`],[`Steatohepatitis`,`10-35%`,`Ballooning, Mallory-Denk bodies, neutrophils`,`Reversible`],[`Fibrosis`,`Variable`,`Pericellular chicken-wire fibrosis`,`Partly reversible`],[`Cirrhosis`,`10-20%`,`Micronodular cirrhosis`,`Not reversible; decompensation can recompensate with abstinence`]]},{heading:`Steroid decision checklist in severe AH`,columns:[`Step`,`What to check`,`Action`],rows:[[`Day 0`,`MELD over 20 or Maddrey 32 or more`,`Candidate for steroids`],[`Day 0`,`Cultures, chest X-ray, ascitic tap, TB screen`,`Treat infection first, then start`],[`Day 0`,`GI bleed, AKI, MELD over about 50`,`Withhold steroids`],[`Day 4-7`,`Lille score`,`Over 0.45 stop; 0.45 or less continue to 28 days`],[`Day 28`,`Bilirubin trend`,`Stop or taper over 2-3 weeks`]]}],redFlags:[`Jaundice in a heavy drinker with MELD over 20 or Maddrey 32 or more - severe alcohol-associated hepatitis`,`Confusion, ataxia or ophthalmoplegia - hepatic or Wernicke encephalopathy`,`Haematemesis or melaena - variceal bleed`,`Falling urine output or rising creatinine - AKI or hepatorenal syndrome`,`Fever or abdominal pain with ascites - spontaneous bacterial peritonitis`,`Withdrawal seizures, hallucinations or delirium tremens`,`Hypoglycaemia in a drinker - synthetic failure or starvation`],pearls:[`**AST:ALT over 2 with AST under 400** = alcohol; ALT over 1000 is not alcohol alone.`,`**Maddrey = 4.6 x PT prolongation + bilirubin; 32 or more severe.**`,`**MELD over 20** is the ACG 2024 threshold for severe AH.`,`**Prednisolone 40 mg x 28 days**, Lille at day 7 - **over 0.45 stop**.`,`**Pentoxifylline is out** (STOPAH).`,`**Baclofen is the AUD drug of choice in cirrhosis**; disulfiram is contraindicated.`,`**Thiamine before glucose** in every malnourished drinker.`,`**Lorazepam or oxazepam (LOT)** for withdrawal in liver disease.`,`**Abstinence is the best predictor of survival** at every stage.`,`**PEth** is the most specific blood marker of recent drinking.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-alcohol-liver-disease-q1`,stem:`A 38-year-old man who drinks 150 g/day presents with 3 weeks of jaundice. Bilirubin 12 mg/dL, AST 210, ALT 80 IU/L, PT 22 s (control 13 s). His Maddrey discriminant function is:`,options:[`About 29`,`About 53`,`About 41`,`About 22`],answer:1,explanation:`Maddrey = 4.6 x (22 - 13) + 12 = 4.6 x 9 + 12 = 41.4 + 12 = about 53, which is severe (32 or more). About 41 forgets to add the bilirubin; about 22 adds PT prolongation and bilirubin without the 4.6 multiplier; about 29 uses no valid arithmetic. Severe AH qualifies for prednisolone after an infection screen.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-alcohol-liver-disease-q2`,stem:`A patient with severe alcohol-associated hepatitis on prednisolone 40 mg/day has a Lille score of 0.62 on day 7. The correct action is:`,options:[`Stop prednisolone`,`Continue prednisolone for 28 days`,`Switch to pentoxifylline`,`Double the prednisolone dose`],answer:0,explanation:`A Lille score over 0.45 at day 4-7 indicates non-response; continuing steroids adds infection risk without survival benefit, so they are stopped and the patient is assessed for early transplant and supportive care. Pentoxifylline had no benefit in STOPAH and is not a rescue; higher doses are not evidence-based; continuing the full 28 days is only for responders.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-alcohol-liver-disease-q3`,stem:`For a man with decompensated alcohol cirrhosis (ascites, Child-Pugh C) and alcohol use disorder, the most appropriate drug to help maintain abstinence is:`,options:[`Disulfiram`,`Naltrexone`,`Chlordiazepoxide long-term`,`Baclofen`],answer:3,explanation:`Baclofen, a GABA-B agonist, has the best safety and efficacy data in cirrhosis and is recommended by ACG 2024. Disulfiram is hepatotoxic and contraindicated; naltrexone is avoided in decompensated cirrhosis; long-term benzodiazepines are not AUD treatment and precipitate encephalopathy.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-alcohol-liver-disease-q4`,stem:`Which finding argues most strongly against a diagnosis of alcohol-associated hepatitis in a jaundiced heavy drinker?`,options:[`AST:ALT ratio of 2.5`,`Leucocytosis of 16,000/microL`,`ALT of 1850 IU/L`,`Tender hepatomegaly with a bruit`],answer:2,explanation:`NIAAA criteria require AST and ALT under 400 IU/L; an ALT of 1850 points to viral hepatitis, ischaemia or paracetamol toxicity instead. An AST:ALT ratio over 1.5, neutrophil leucocytosis and a tender enlarged liver with bruit all support alcohol-associated hepatitis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-alcohol-liver-disease-q5`,stem:`The biochemical basis of fasting hypoglycaemia and lactic acidosis after a binge is:`,options:[`Induction of CYP2E1`,`Increased NADH:NAD+ ratio from ethanol oxidation`,`Acetaldehyde-protein adduct formation`,`Endotoxin-mediated TNF release`],answer:1,explanation:`Oxidation of ethanol by ADH and ALDH generates NADH; the high NADH:NAD+ ratio diverts pyruvate to lactate and blocks gluconeogenesis, causing hypoglycaemia and lactic acidosis (and steatosis). CYP2E1 induction explains paracetamol toxicity, adducts explain immune injury, and endotoxin-TNF explains the inflammation of AH.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-alcohol-liver-disease-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Describe the diagnosis, severity assessment and management of severe alcohol-associated hepatitis. Add a note on treatment of alcohol use disorder in liver disease.`,openingLines:[`Alcohol-associated hepatitis is a syndrome of jaundice within 8 weeks of heavy drinking with bilirubin over 3 mg/dL, AST over 50 IU/L, AST:ALT over 1.5 and both under 400 IU/L.`,`Severe disease (Maddrey 32 or more or MELD over 20) carries 20-30% one-month mortality, and abstinence decides long-term survival.`],answer:[{heading:`Pathogenesis in brief`,points:[`ADH/CYP2E1 to acetaldehyde, NADH excess, ROS; endotoxin via TLR4 to TNF, neutrophils; Mallory-Denk bodies in zone 3.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`NIAAA criteria; exclude viral, DILI, sepsis, obstruction; transjugular biopsy if doubt.`,`Infection screen: cultures, chest X-ray, ascitic tap, TB.`]},{heading:`Severity`,points:[`Maddrey 4.6 x PT prolongation + bilirubin, 32 or more; MELD over 20; Glasgow 9 or more; Lille over 0.45 at day 7.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Abstinence, thiamine before glucose, nutrition 35-40 kcal/kg and 1.2-1.5 g/kg protein.`,`Prednisolone 40 mg x 28 days if no contraindication; stop if Lille over 0.45; NAC adjunct; no pentoxifylline.`,`Manage AKI, infection, encephalopathy, withdrawal (lorazepam by CIWA-Ar).`,`Early transplant for selected non-responders.`]},{heading:`AUD treatment`,points:[`Brief intervention, integrated care; baclofen 10 mg TDS, acamprosate 666 mg TDS; naltrexone only if compensated; no disulfiram.`,`Monitor with PEth or urine EtG; NAPDDR de-addiction centres, Tele-MANAS.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: jaundiced drinker - NIAAA - MELD/Maddrey - infection screen - prednisolone - Lille day 7 - continue or stop`,`Table of severity scores`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and pathogenesis`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis and severity scores`,marks:3},{part:`Treatment with flow chart`,marks:3},{part:`AUD pharmacotherapy`,marks:2}],keywords:[`NIAAA`,`Maddrey`,`MELD`,`Lille`,`prednisolone`,`baclofen`,`acamprosate`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-alcohol-liver-disease-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on the Lille model and the use of corticosteroids in alcohol-associated hepatitis.`,openingLines:[`The Lille model predicts 6-month survival in steroid-treated alcohol-associated hepatitis from the early change in bilirubin.`,`A score over 0.45 at day 4-7 identifies non-responders in whom steroids should be stopped.`],answer:[{heading:`Components`,points:[`Age, albumin, creatinine (renal insufficiency), PT, bilirubin day 0 and day 7.`]},{heading:`Steroid indications and regimen`,points:[`MELD over 20 or Maddrey 32 or more; prednisolone 40 mg OD x 28 days.`,`Contraindications: infection, GI bleed, AKI/HRS, MELD over about 50.`]},{heading:`Interpretation`,points:[`Lille 0.45 or less: continue; over 0.45: stop, consider transplant; non-responders have about 25% 6-month survival.`]}],mustDraw:[`Decision flow at day 7 by Lille score`],markSplit:[{part:`Components`,marks:1},{part:`Steroid indications and regimen`,marks:2},{part:`Interpretation and action`,marks:2}],keywords:[`Lille`,`0.45`,`prednisolone`,`STOPAH`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Alcohol-associated liver disease`,`Jophlin LL et al. ACG Clinical Guideline: Alcohol-Associated Liver Disease. Am J Gastroenterol 2024`,`Crabb DW et al. AASLD Practice Guidance: Diagnosis and treatment of alcohol-associated liver diseases. Hepatology 2020 (2019 guidance)`,`EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines: Management of alcohol-related liver disease. J Hepatol 2018`,`Thursz MR et al. Prednisolone or pentoxifylline for alcoholic hepatitis (STOPAH). N Engl J Med 2015`,`Ambekar A et al. Magnitude of Substance Use in India. Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment / NDDTC AIIMS, 2019`,`WHO Global status report on alcohol and health and treatment of substance use disorders, 2024`]}),i.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatitis-b-chronic`,title:`Chronic hepatitis B: phases and treatment`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Chronic hepatitis B** is persistence of **HBsAg for over 6 months**; it passes through **HBeAg-positive and HBeAg-negative infection and hepatitis phases** defined by HBV DNA, ALT and fibrosis, and under the **WHO 2024** guideline treatment is broadened to anyone with **significant fibrosis (APRI over 0.5 or stiffness over 7 kPa)**, or **HBV DNA over 2000 IU/mL with raised ALT**, or key co-factors, using **tenofovir or entecavir** lifelong in most, with **TDF in pregnancy and birth-dose vaccine plus HBIG** to stop mother-to-child transmission.`,keywords:[`HBV`,`chronic hepatitis B`,`HBsAg`,`HBeAg`,`anti-HBc`,`HBV DNA`,`immune tolerant`,`inactive carrier`,`tenofovir`,`TDF`,`TAF`,`entecavir`,`peginterferon`,`mother-to-child transmission`,`HBIG`,`HBV reactivation`,`rituximab`,`hepatitis D`,`APRI`,`NVHCP`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Chronic HBV infection** = **HBsAg positive for more than 6 months**; it is not a single disease but a dynamic interaction between virus and host immunity. [EASL 2017]`,`WHO estimates **254 million people** were living with chronic HBV in 2022, with about **1.1 million deaths** a year, mostly from cirrhosis and HCC. [WHO Global Hepatitis Report 2024]`,`India has **intermediate endemicity (HBsAg 2-4%)**, with about **2.9 crore people** living with HBV - one of the largest national burdens; prevalence is higher in some tribal and north-eastern populations. [WHO Global Hepatitis Report 2024]`,`**Age at infection decides chronicity**: about **90% after perinatal infection**, 20-30% at age 1-5 years, and **under 5% in adults**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mother-to-child (perinatal) transmission** is the main route in India, followed by unsafe injections, transfusion, dialysis, household and sexual contact. [NVHCP 2018]`,`Only a minority of infected Indians are **diagnosed**, and fewer are on treatment - the main gap in the WHO 2030 elimination target. [WHO Global Hepatitis Report 2024]`,`HBV causes about **40-50% of HCC** worldwide, and HCC can develop **without cirrhosis** because HBV DNA integrates into the host genome. [AASLD HCC 2023]`]},{heading:`Virology and transmission`,points:[`HBV is a **partially double-stranded DNA hepadnavirus** that replicates through an **RNA intermediate using reverse transcriptase** - why nucleoside analogues (reverse transcriptase inhibitors) work. [Harrison 22e]`,`In the hepatocyte nucleus the genome forms **covalently closed circular DNA (cccDNA)**, a stable minichromosome that current drugs cannot eliminate - why therapy is usually lifelong. [EASL 2017]`,`**Integrated HBV DNA** in the host genome drives HBsAg production and oncogenesis even when replication is suppressed. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Precore and basal core promoter mutants** stop or reduce HBeAg production while replication continues, producing **HBeAg-negative chronic hepatitis**, common in India. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HBV is 50-100 times more infectious than HIV**; a needlestick from an HBeAg-positive source carries about **30% transmission risk**. [CDC 2023]`,`**Genotypes A-J** exist; genotypes **D and A** predominate in India, and genotype affects interferon response. [INASL 2018]`,`**Hepatitis D (delta) virus** needs HBsAg to replicate and causes the most severe form of viral hepatitis; superinfection of a carrier often becomes chronic. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - immune-mediated injury`,points:[`**HBV is not directly cytopathic**; liver injury is caused by **cytotoxic T cells** attacking infected hepatocytes - so ALT reflects immune activity, not viral load. [Harrison 22e]`,`In perinatal infection, **neonatal immune tolerance** (partly from transplacental HBeAg) allows very high viral loads with normal ALT - the 'immune-tolerant' phase. [Harrison 22e]`,`Immune activation causes **flares of ALT**, and successful clearance leads to **HBeAg seroconversion to anti-HBe**, often with a flare. [EASL 2017]`,`**Immune complex deposition** (HBsAg-antibody) explains extrahepatic disease: **polyarteritis nodosa, membranous nephropathy and serum-sickness-like prodrome**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Repeated cycles of necroinflammation and regeneration cause **fibrosis, cirrhosis and HCC**; high HBV DNA (the REVEAL cohort) independently predicts cirrhosis and HCC. [REVEAL-HBV 2006]`,`**Immunosuppression removes immune control**, allowing explosive replication (**reactivation**); when immunity returns, the immune attack can cause **fulminant hepatitis**. [AGA 2014]`,`**Nucleos(t)ide analogues** block reverse transcription and suppress DNA but do not clear cccDNA; **interferon** acts immunologically and can achieve finite therapy and HBsAg loss in a minority. [EASL 2017]`]},{heading:`Classification - the phases of chronic HBV`,points:[`**Phase 1 - HBeAg-positive chronic infection** (formerly immune tolerant): HBeAg+, **HBV DNA very high (over 10^7 IU/mL)**, **normal ALT**, minimal inflammation or fibrosis. [EASL 2017]`,`**Phase 2 - HBeAg-positive chronic hepatitis** (immune active): HBeAg+, DNA high (10^4-10^7 IU/mL), **raised ALT**, moderate to severe inflammation and progressing fibrosis. [EASL 2017]`,`**Phase 3 - HBeAg-negative chronic infection** (formerly inactive carrier): anti-HBe+, **HBV DNA under 2000 IU/mL, normal ALT**, low HBsAg (under 1000 IU/mL), low risk. [EASL 2017]`,`**Phase 4 - HBeAg-negative chronic hepatitis**: anti-HBe+, **HBV DNA over 2000 IU/mL with fluctuating raised ALT** - precore mutant disease, the commonest treated phase in India. [EASL 2017]`,`**Phase 5 - HBsAg-negative phase** (occult or resolved infection): HBsAg negative, **anti-HBc positive**, DNA usually undetectable in serum - but cccDNA persists and can **reactivate with immunosuppression**. [EASL 2017]`,`Phases are **not always sequential** and one measurement is unreliable - classify after **serial ALT and DNA over 6-12 months**. [EASL 2017]`,`**Grey-zone** patients (not fitting any phase) form up to a third of cases; WHO 2024 favours treating many of them. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Most chronic carriers are **asymptomatic**, diagnosed on screening (antenatal, blood donation, pre-operative, pre-employment). [NVHCP 2018]`,`Symptoms are non-specific: **fatigue, RUQ discomfort, anorexia**; jaundice suggests a flare, superinfection (HDV, HEV, HAV) or decompensation. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **family history of HBV, cirrhosis or HCC** - it changes both treatment and surveillance thresholds. [WHO 2024]`,`Ask about **mother's HBsAg status, transfusion, surgery, dental procedures, injections, tattoos, dialysis, multiple sexual partners and injecting drug use**. [NVHCP 2018]`,`Ask about **alcohol, metabolic risk and other drugs** - co-factors that accelerate fibrosis. [WHO 2024]`,`Ask about **planned chemotherapy, rituximab, biologics or long-term steroids** - triggers for reactivation prophylaxis. [AGA 2025]`,`Ask about **pregnancy plans** - TDF timing and newborn prophylaxis depend on it. [WHO 2024]`,`Ask about **extrahepatic symptoms**: rash and arthralgia (serum-sickness), oedema and proteinuria (membranous GN), mononeuritis and hypertension (PAN). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Examination is often **normal** in chronic infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Stigmata of chronic liver disease** (spider naevi, palmar erythema, splenomegaly, ascites) mean cirrhosis and change management - treat all cirrhotics with detectable DNA. [WHO 2024]`,`**A hard, nodular liver or hepatic bruit** suggests HCC. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Jaundice, asterixis or ascites** indicate decompensation needing urgent hepatology care. [EASL 2017]`,`Look for **oedema, hypertension and palpable purpura** as clues to glomerulonephritis or vasculitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Check for **injection marks, tattoos** and signs of HIV co-infection (oral candida, lymphadenopathy). [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis and serology interpretation`,points:[`**HBsAg+, IgM anti-HBc+** = acute hepatitis B (or a severe flare of chronic HBV); **HBsAg+, IgG anti-HBc+ over 6 months** = chronic infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HBsAg-, anti-HBc+, anti-HBs+** = resolved infection; **HBsAg-, anti-HBc-, anti-HBs+** = vaccinated. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Isolated anti-HBc+** = resolved infection with waned anti-HBs, occult HBV, window period or false positive - check HBV DNA. [EASL 2017]`,`**HBeAg-negative with high DNA** is precore mutant hepatitis, not an inactive carrier - DNA, not HBeAg, decides. [EASL 2017]`,`**Acute flare** of chronic HBV must be distinguished from **superinfection with HDV, HEV or HAV**, drug injury and HCC. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Co-existing MASLD or alcohol** may explain a raised ALT in a low-DNA carrier; use DNA and elastography to decide. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Baseline panel**: HBeAg, anti-HBe, **quantitative HBV DNA**, LFT, CBC with platelets, INR, creatinine, ultrasound. [WHO 2024]`,`**APRI = (AST/ULN x 100) / platelets (10^9/L)**; WHO 2024 uses **APRI over 0.5** as significant fibrosis and over 1.5 as cirrhosis; FIB-4 is an alternative. [WHO 2024]`,`**Transient elastography**: WHO 2024 uses **over 7 kPa for significant fibrosis** and **over 12.5 kPa for cirrhosis**; inflammation (ALT flare) overestimates stiffness. [WHO 2024]`,`Test **anti-HDV in all HBsAg-positive people**, and HDV RNA if positive. [WHO 2024]`,`Test for **HIV and HCV co-infection**, and **anti-HAV IgG** to vaccinate non-immune patients. [WHO 2024]`,`**Quantitative HBsAg** helps phase classification (under 1000 IU/mL in inactive carriers) and predicts HBsAg loss. [EASL 2017]`,`**Liver biopsy** is rarely needed now - only when non-invasive tests are discordant or another cause is suspected. [WHO 2024]`,`Before therapy check **creatinine clearance, phosphate and urine protein** (TDF), and a **pregnancy test** in women. [EASL 2017]`]},{heading:`Who to treat - WHO 2024 broadened criteria`,points:[`Treat all adults and adolescents with **significant fibrosis or cirrhosis (APRI over 0.5 or TE over 7 kPa)**, regardless of HBV DNA or ALT. [WHO 2024]`,`Treat those with **HBV DNA over 2000 IU/mL and ALT above ULN** (**ALT ULN 30 U/L men, 19 U/L women** for this purpose). [WHO 2024]`,`Treat regardless of DNA or ALT if there is **HIV, HDV or HCV co-infection, family history of HCC or cirrhosis, immunosuppression, comorbidity such as diabetes or MASLD, or extrahepatic manifestations**. [WHO 2024]`,`Where **HBV DNA is unavailable**, treat those with **persistently abnormal ALT** (two values over 6-12 months) after excluding other causes. [WHO 2024]`,`WHO estimates these criteria make about **half of all people with chronic HBV eligible**, compared with about 10-20% before. [WHO 2024]`,`**AASLD 2018** thresholds were narrower: HBeAg+ DNA over 20,000 and HBeAg- over 2000 IU/mL, both with ALT over 2 times ULN (35 men, 25 women), or any cirrhosis with detectable DNA. [AASLD 2018]`,`**EASL 2017**: treat DNA over 2000 with ALT above ULN and/or at least moderate fibrosis; DNA over 20,000 with ALT over 2 times ULN regardless; HBeAg+ chronic infection over age 30. [EASL 2017]`,`**Always treat decompensated cirrhosis** with any detectable DNA and refer for transplant evaluation. [EASL 2017]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - drugs and monitoring`,points:[`**First-line drugs**: **tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF) 300 mg OD**, **entecavir 0.5 mg OD on an empty stomach**, or **tenofovir alafenamide (TAF) 25 mg OD with food** - all with a high genetic barrier to resistance. [WHO 2024]`,`**Entecavir 1 mg OD** is used in **lamivudine-experienced or decompensated** patients, though TDF is preferred after lamivudine resistance. [EASL 2017]`,`**TAF** (or entecavir) is preferred over TDF in **age over 60, eGFR under 60, albuminuria, low phosphate or osteoporosis**, because of TDF's renal tubular and bone toxicity. [EASL 2017]`,`**Lamivudine, adefovir and telbivudine** are no longer recommended as monotherapy because of high resistance rates. [WHO 2024]`,`**Peginterferon alfa-2a 180 microgram SC weekly for 48 weeks** offers finite therapy in young HBeAg+ patients with high ALT and low DNA; contraindicated in decompensated cirrhosis, pregnancy, autoimmune and severe psychiatric disease. [EASL 2017]`,`**Monitor on NA**: HBV DNA at **3 months**, then every **6-12 months**; ALT every 6 months; creatinine and phosphate yearly (more often on TDF with risk); HBsAg yearly once DNA is undetectable. [EASL 2017]`,`**Untreated carriers** need ALT every **6-12 months** and DNA yearly (HBeAg-negative infection), as phases change. [EASL 2017]`,`**Stopping NA**: HBeAg+ non-cirrhotics may stop after **HBeAg seroconversion plus at least 12 months of consolidation**; selected HBeAg- non-cirrhotics after long suppression with close monitoring; **never stop in cirrhosis**. [EASL 2017]`,`**HBsAg loss (functional cure)** is the ideal endpoint but occurs in only about **1% a year** on NA. [EASL 2017]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD - flares, decompensation and reactivation`,points:[`**Severe acute flare or reactivation** with jaundice or INR rise: admit, start **entecavir or tenofovir immediately** without waiting for DNA, monitor for acute liver failure. [EASL 2017]`,`**Decompensated HBV cirrhosis**: entecavir or TDF (TAF if renal risk), never interferon; many patients **recompensate** with viral suppression; refer for transplant. [EASL 2017]`,`**Withdrawal flares** after stopping NA can be fatal in cirrhosis - never stop without hepatology supervision. [AASLD 2018]`,`**Reactivation prophylaxis** - screen **HBsAg, anti-HBc (total) and anti-HBs** before chemotherapy, rituximab, biologics, transplant or long-term steroids. [AGA 2025]`,`**High risk** (prophylaxis mandatory): **anti-CD20 (rituximab, obinutuzumab) in HBsAg+ or anti-HBc+ patients**, stem-cell transplant, anthracyclines in HBsAg+, and **prednisolone 20 mg/day or more for 4 weeks or more in HBsAg+**. [AGA 2025]`,`Use **entecavir, TDF or TAF** (not lamivudine), started before or with immunosuppression and continued **6 months after (12 months after anti-CD20 therapy)**. [AGA 2025]`,`**HCV direct-acting antiviral therapy** can reactivate HBV in HBsAg+ patients - give NA prophylaxis or monitor DNA during DAA therapy. [AASLD-IDSA HCV 2023]`]},{heading:`Special situations - pregnancy, children, CKD, co-infection`,points:[`**Universal antenatal HBsAg screening** in every pregnancy. [WHO 2024]`,`**TDF prophylaxis** for pregnant women with **HBV DNA 200,000 IU/mL or more (5.3 log) or HBeAg positive**, from the **second trimester (ideally 24-28 weeks)** until at least delivery; where DNA and HBeAg tests are unavailable WHO 2024 allows TDF for all HBsAg+ pregnant women. [WHO 2024]`,`Every infant of an HBsAg+ mother gets **hepatitis B birth-dose vaccine within 24 hours (ideally 12 hours)** plus **HBIG 100-200 IU IM** at a separate site, followed by the full vaccine series. [UIP 2023; WHO 2024]`,`**Breastfeeding is allowed**, including on TDF; caesarean section is not indicated for HBV alone. [WHO 2024]`,`**Post-vaccination testing** of infants of HBsAg+ mothers: **HBsAg and anti-HBs at 9-12 months**. [CDC 2023]`,`**Children and adolescents**: TDF or entecavir from age 2 years and TAF in adolescents (12 years or more) under the WHO 2024 recommendations. [WHO 2024]`,`**CKD and dialysis**: adjust TDF and entecavir doses to creatinine clearance; prefer **entecavir or TAF**; dialysis patients need double-dose HBV vaccine. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**HIV co-infection**: give ART containing **TDF or TAF plus lamivudine or emtricitabine** (e.g. TLD), never lamivudine monotherapy. [NACO 2021]`,`**HDV co-infection**: peginterferon for 48 weeks; **bulevirtide** is approved in Europe but not widely available in India. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Without treatment, **15-40%** of chronic carriers develop cirrhosis, liver failure or HCC over their lifetime. [Harrison 22e]`,`Annual **cirrhosis incidence** is about **2-6% in HBeAg+ and 8-10% in HBeAg- chronic hepatitis**. [EASL 2017]`,`**HCC incidence** is about **2-5% per year in cirrhosis**, lower but not zero without cirrhosis. [AASLD HCC 2023]`,`Long-term NA therapy suppresses DNA in **over 95%**, reverses fibrosis and **reduces HCC risk**, but does not abolish it. [EASL 2017]`,`**PAGE-B score** (age, sex, platelets) estimates HCC risk in Caucasians on NA; **REACH-B** and CU-HCC are Asian scores. [EASL 2017]`,`Extrahepatic: **membranous nephropathy, PAN, cryoglobulinaemia, aplastic anaemia**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and HCC surveillance`,points:[`**HCC surveillance with ultrasound with or without AFP every 6 months** in all with cirrhosis, and in non-cirrhotic **Asian men over 40, Asian women over 50, Africans over 20, and those with a family history of HCC**. [AASLD HCC 2023]`,`**Refer to a model treatment centre or hepatologist** all HBsAg+ patients for phase assessment; urgent referral for jaundice, decompensation or pregnancy with high DNA. [NVHCP 2018]`,`Screen and vaccinate **household and sexual contacts**: HBsAg, anti-HBs, and vaccine if non-immune. [NVHCP 2018]`,`Check **adherence** at each visit - virological breakthrough on entecavir or TDF is almost always non-adherence, not resistance. [EASL 2017]`,`Counsel to **avoid alcohol**, control weight and diabetes, and avoid unprescribed herbal medicines. [WHO 2024]`,`**Variceal screening** per Baveno VII in cirrhosis. [Baveno VII 2022]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`**Universal Immunisation Programme**: HBV birth dose within 24 hours, then pentavalent vaccine at **6, 10 and 14 weeks**. [UIP 2023]`,`**NVHCP (launched 2018)** provides free HBsAg testing, free TDF/entecavir at model treatment centres and treatment sites, and free HBIG for newborns of HBsAg+ mothers at government facilities. [NVHCP 2018]`,`Adult vaccination - **20 microgram IM at 0, 1 and 6 months** - for health workers, dialysis patients, people with CLD, HIV, contacts of carriers and high-risk groups. [NVHCP 2018]`,`**Safe injection and blood safety** practices: single-use syringes, NAT-screened blood, universal precautions. [NVHCP 2018]`,`**Needlestick from HBsAg+ source** in a non-immune worker: **HBIG plus vaccine within 24 hours** (up to 7 days). [CDC 2023]`,`Counsel that HBV is **not spread by casual contact, food or sharing utensils** - stigma reduces testing and job security. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates and exam angle`,points:[`**WHO 2024 HBV guideline** (first update since 2015): simplified, broadened treatment criteria; APRI over 0.5 or TE over 7 kPa; DNA over 2000 with ALT above 30/19; co-factor-based treatment; ALT-only criteria where DNA unavailable. [WHO 2024]`,`**WHO 2024**: TDF for all HBsAg+ pregnant women where DNA/HBeAg testing is unavailable; adolescents included; **universal anti-HDV testing** recommended. [WHO 2024]`,`**AGA 2025** updated HBV reactivation guidance: universal screening with HBsAg and anti-HBc before immunosuppression and prophylaxis for high- and moderate-risk patients. [AGA 2025]`,`**AASLD HCC 2023** guidance retains 6-monthly ultrasound with or without AFP for HBV risk groups. [AASLD HCC 2023]`,`**Viva classics**: phases with DNA and ALT, serology interpretation table, WHO 2024 treatment criteria, TDF vs TAF vs entecavir, MTCT prophylaxis, reactivation risk groups. [WHO 2024]`,`**Common mistakes**: calling every HBeAg-negative patient an inactive carrier without DNA, using lamivudine for prophylaxis, stopping NA in cirrhosis, and forgetting the birth dose within 24 hours. [EASL 2017]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Phases of chronic HBV infection (EASL 2017)`,columns:[`Phase`,`HBeAg`,`HBV DNA (IU/mL)`,`ALT`,`Liver disease`,`Treat?`],rows:[[`HBeAg+ chronic infection (immune tolerant)`,`Positive`,`Over 10^7`,`Normal`,`None or minimal`,`Usually monitor; treat if over 30 years, family HCC, or fibrosis`],[`HBeAg+ chronic hepatitis`,`Positive`,`10^4-10^7`,`Raised`,`Moderate to severe`,`Yes`],[`HBeAg- chronic infection (inactive carrier)`,`Negative`,`Under 2000`,`Normal`,`None`,`No; monitor`],[`HBeAg- chronic hepatitis (precore mutant)`,`Negative`,`Over 2000`,`Raised or fluctuating`,`Moderate to severe`,`Yes`],[`HBsAg-negative phase (occult/resolved)`,`Negative`,`Usually undetectable`,`Normal`,`None (risk of reactivation)`,`Prophylaxis if high-risk immunosuppression`]]},{heading:`Interpretation of HBV serology`,columns:[`HBsAg`,`Anti-HBc`,`Anti-HBs`,`Interpretation`],rows:[[`Negative`,`Negative`,`Negative`,`Susceptible - vaccinate`],[`Negative`,`Negative`,`Positive`,`Immune by vaccination`],[`Negative`,`Positive (IgG)`,`Positive`,`Immune after natural infection`],[`Positive`,`Positive (IgM)`,`Negative`,`Acute infection (or severe flare)`],[`Positive`,`Positive (IgG)`,`Negative`,`Chronic infection (over 6 months)`],[`Negative`,`Positive`,`Negative`,`Isolated anti-HBc: resolved, occult, window or false positive - check DNA`]]},{heading:`Antiviral drugs for chronic hepatitis B`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF)`,`300 mg oral OD`,`Long-term, usually lifelong; adjust for CrCl under 50`,`Proximal tubulopathy, Fanconi, low phosphate, bone loss; drug of choice in pregnancy`],[`Tenofovir alafenamide (TAF)`,`25 mg oral OD with food`,`Long-term; no adjustment if CrCl 15 or more`,`Less renal and bone toxicity; lipid rise; preferred over 60, CKD, osteoporosis`],[`Entecavir`,`0.5 mg oral OD empty stomach (1 mg if decompensated or lamivudine-exposed)`,`Long-term; adjust for CrCl under 50`,`Lactic acidosis in decompensated cirrhosis; avoid after lamivudine resistance`],[`Peginterferon alfa-2a`,`180 microgram SC weekly`,`48 weeks (finite)`,`Flu-like illness, cytopenias, depression, thyroid disease; not in decompensation or pregnancy`],[`Hepatitis B immunoglobulin (HBIG)`,`100-200 IU IM in newborn; 0.06 mL/kg after exposure`,`Single dose within 12-24 h`,`Give at a different site from vaccine`],[`Hepatitis B vaccine`,`10 microgram (child) or 20 microgram (adult) IM`,`Birth, 6, 10, 14 weeks (UIP); adults 0, 1, 6 months`,`Double dose in dialysis; check anti-HBs over 10 mIU/mL in high risk`]]},{heading:`Treatment criteria compared`,columns:[`Criterion`,`WHO 2024`,`EASL 2017`,`AASLD 2018`],rows:[[`Fibrosis`,`APRI over 0.5 or TE over 7 kPa: treat regardless`,`At least moderate fibrosis with DNA over 2000`,`Cirrhosis with any detectable DNA`],[`DNA plus ALT`,`DNA over 2000 and ALT above ULN (30/19)`,`DNA over 2000 and ALT above ULN`,`HBeAg+ over 20,000; HBeAg- over 2000; ALT over 2x ULN (35/25)`],[`Co-factors`,`HIV, HDV, HCV, family HCC, immunosuppression, diabetes, MASLD, extrahepatic disease`,`Family HCC, extrahepatic manifestations`,`Selected cases`],[`No DNA test`,`Persistently abnormal ALT`,`Not addressed`,`Not addressed`]]},{heading:`HBV reactivation risk with immunosuppression`,columns:[`Risk level`,`Examples`,`Action`],rows:[[`High (over 10%)`,`Anti-CD20 (rituximab) in HBsAg+ or anti-HBc+; HSCT; anthracyclines or prednisolone 20 mg/day for 4 weeks or more in HBsAg+`,`Prophylaxis with entecavir/TDF/TAF`],[`Moderate (1-10%)`,`TNF inhibitors, tyrosine kinase inhibitors, moderate-dose steroids in HBsAg+; anthracyclines in anti-HBc+`,`Prophylaxis generally recommended`],[`Low (under 1%)`,`Methotrexate, azathioprine, low-dose steroids under 4 weeks`,`Monitor ALT and DNA; no routine prophylaxis`],[`Duration`,`After stopping immunosuppression`,`Continue 6 months (12 months after anti-CD20)`]]}],redFlags:[`Jaundice, rising INR or confusion in a known HBV carrier - severe flare or acute liver failure`,`Ascites, variceal bleeding or encephalopathy - decompensated HBV cirrhosis`,`New liver mass or rising AFP - HCC`,`HBsAg+ or anti-HBc+ patient about to start rituximab, chemotherapy or high-dose steroids without prophylaxis`,`Pregnant woman with HBV DNA 200,000 IU/mL or more or HBeAg positive not on TDF by 28 weeks`,`Self-discontinuation of entecavir or tenofovir in a cirrhotic patient - risk of fatal withdrawal flare`,`HBsAg+ newborn without birth dose and HBIG within 24 hours`],pearls:[`**Chronic = HBsAg over 6 months**; IgM anti-HBc = acute.`,`**Age at infection decides chronicity**: 90% neonates, under 5% adults.`,`**HBeAg-negative is not inactive** - HBV DNA over 2000 means precore mutant hepatitis.`,`**WHO 2024**: treat **APRI over 0.5 / TE over 7 kPa**, or **DNA over 2000 + ALT over 30/19**, or co-factors.`,`**TDF, TAF, entecavir** - high barrier; **lamivudine is obsolete** for monotherapy and prophylaxis.`,`**TAF over TDF** if over 60, CKD or osteoporosis.`,`**Pregnancy: TDF from 24-28 weeks if DNA 200,000 or more; birth dose + HBIG within 24 h; breastfeeding allowed.**`,`**Rituximab + anti-HBc+ = prophylaxis**, continued 12 months after the last dose.`,`**Never stop NA in cirrhosis.**`,`**HCC can occur without cirrhosis** in HBV - surveil Asian men over 40.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatitis-b-chronic-q1`,stem:`A 35-year-old man is HBsAg positive for 2 years, HBeAg negative, anti-HBe positive, HBV DNA 85,000 IU/mL and ALT 78 IU/L. Elastography is 6.5 kPa. His phase is:`,options:[`HBeAg-negative chronic infection (inactive carrier)`,`HBeAg-positive chronic infection`,`HBeAg-negative chronic hepatitis`,`Occult HBV infection`],answer:2,explanation:`HBeAg negativity with HBV DNA over 2000 IU/mL and raised ALT defines HBeAg-negative chronic hepatitis (precore or basal core promoter mutant), which needs treatment. An inactive carrier has DNA under 2000 and normal ALT; HBeAg-positive phases require a positive HBeAg; occult infection is HBsAg negative.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatitis-b-chronic-q2`,stem:`A 28-year-old primigravida is HBsAg positive with HBV DNA 3 x 10^7 IU/mL at 16 weeks. The best plan to prevent mother-to-child transmission is:`,options:[`Elective caesarean section and avoid breastfeeding`,`Tenofovir disoproxil from about 24-28 weeks plus birth-dose vaccine and HBIG within 24 hours`,`Entecavir from 12 weeks`,`HBIG to the mother monthly from 28 weeks`],answer:1,explanation:`With DNA of 200,000 IU/mL or more, WHO 2024 recommends TDF from the second trimester (ideally 24-28 weeks) to delivery, plus hepatitis B vaccine birth dose and HBIG to the newborn within 24 hours. Caesarean section and avoiding breastfeeding are not indicated; entecavir lacks pregnancy safety data; maternal HBIG is ineffective.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatitis-b-chronic-q3`,stem:`A 60-year-old woman with lymphoma is to receive R-CHOP. She is HBsAg negative, anti-HBc positive and anti-HBs positive. The correct approach is:`,options:[`No action as she has protective anti-HBs`,`Lamivudine during chemotherapy only`,`Hepatitis B vaccine booster before chemotherapy`,`Entecavir or tenofovir prophylaxis continued for 12 months after rituximab`],answer:3,explanation:`Rituximab (anti-CD20) carries a high reactivation risk even in HBsAg-negative, anti-HBc-positive patients, and guidelines recommend a high-barrier NA (entecavir, TDF or TAF) started before therapy and continued 12 months after the last dose. Anti-HBs does not protect against reactivation from cccDNA; lamivudine has high resistance; a vaccine booster does not prevent reactivation.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatitis-b-chronic-q4`,stem:`A 66-year-old man with chronic hepatitis B needing treatment has eGFR 48 mL/min and osteoporosis. The preferred antiviral is:`,options:[`Tenofovir alafenamide`,`Tenofovir disoproxil fumarate`,`Lamivudine`,`Adefovir`],answer:0,explanation:`TAF delivers tenofovir into hepatocytes at a much lower plasma tenofovir level, causing less proximal tubular and bone toxicity, so it (or entecavir) is preferred with age over 60, CKD or osteoporosis. TDF worsens renal and bone disease; lamivudine and adefovir have high resistance rates and adefovir is nephrotoxic.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatitis-b-chronic-q5`,stem:`Under the WHO 2024 hepatitis B guideline, which HBsAg-positive adult qualifies for treatment regardless of HBV DNA and ALT?`,options:[`A 25-year-old with HBV DNA 1500 IU/mL and ALT 22 U/L`,`A 40-year-old with APRI 0.3 and no co-factors`,`A 30-year-old with HBV DNA 1000 IU/mL whose father died of HCC`,`A 35-year-old woman with ALT 18 U/L and HBV DNA undetectable, no co-factors`],answer:2,explanation:`WHO 2024 recommends treatment, regardless of DNA or ALT, for anyone with a family history of liver cancer or cirrhosis, co-infections, immunosuppression, comorbidities or extrahepatic disease. The other three have low DNA, normal ALT (below 30 men, 19 women), APRI under 0.5 and no co-factors, so they are monitored rather than treated.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatitis-b-chronic-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Describe the natural history and phases of chronic hepatitis B infection. Discuss the indications for treatment and the drugs used, with reference to the WHO 2024 guideline.`,openingLines:[`Chronic hepatitis B is persistence of HBsAg for more than 6 months; India has about 2.9 crore infected people and perinatal transmission is the main route.`,`Disease is immune-mediated, passes through HBeAg-positive and HBeAg-negative phases, and treatment with tenofovir or entecavir prevents cirrhosis and HCC.`],answer:[{heading:`Virology and natural history`,points:[`Hepadnavirus with reverse transcriptase; cccDNA persists; integration causes HCC.`,`Chronicity 90% perinatal, 20-30% at 1-5 years, under 5% adults.`]},{heading:`Phases (EASL 2017)`,points:[`HBeAg+ infection, HBeAg+ hepatitis, HBeAg- infection, HBeAg- hepatitis, HBsAg-negative phase - with DNA and ALT for each.`]},{heading:`Evaluation`,points:[`HBeAg, DNA, LFT, platelets, APRI/FIB-4, elastography, ultrasound; anti-HDV, HIV, HCV.`]},{heading:`Treatment indications (WHO 2024)`,points:[`APRI over 0.5 or TE over 7 kPa; DNA over 2000 + ALT over 30/19; co-factors; persistently abnormal ALT if no DNA test.`,`Contrast with AASLD 2018 (2x ULN) and EASL 2017.`]},{heading:`Drugs and monitoring`,points:[`TDF 300 mg, TAF 25 mg, entecavir 0.5 mg; peginterferon 48 weeks in selected; no lamivudine.`,`DNA at 3 months then 6-12 monthly; renal monitoring; HBsAg loss rare; never stop in cirrhosis.`]},{heading:`Prevention`,points:[`Birth dose within 24 h + HBIG; TDF in pregnancy if DNA 200,000 or more; NVHCP free testing and treatment; HCC surveillance 6-monthly.`]}],mustDraw:[`Graph of the phases: HBV DNA and ALT over time with HBeAg seroconversion`,`Table: phases with HBeAg, DNA, ALT and treatment`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition, epidemiology and virology`,marks:2},{part:`Phases with diagram`,marks:3},{part:`Treatment indications`,marks:2},{part:`Drugs, monitoring and prevention`,marks:3}],keywords:[`HBeAg`,`cccDNA`,`WHO 2024`,`APRI`,`tenofovir`,`entecavir`,`birth dose`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatitis-b-chronic-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on prevention of mother-to-child transmission of hepatitis B.`,openingLines:[`Perinatal transmission is the main route of HBV in India and leads to chronic infection in about 90% of infected neonates.`,`Prevention combines antenatal screening, maternal tenofovir for high viral load, and newborn birth-dose vaccine with HBIG.`],answer:[{heading:`Screening`,points:[`Universal HBsAg in every pregnancy; if positive, HBV DNA and HBeAg.`]},{heading:`Maternal antiviral`,points:[`TDF 300 mg OD from 24-28 weeks if DNA 200,000 IU/mL or more or HBeAg+; all HBsAg+ if testing unavailable (WHO 2024); continue to delivery or longer.`]},{heading:`Newborn prophylaxis`,points:[`Birth-dose vaccine within 24 h (ideally 12 h) plus HBIG 100-200 IU IM; complete series at 6, 10, 14 weeks.`,`Test HBsAg and anti-HBs at 9-12 months.`]},{heading:`Delivery and feeding`,points:[`No caesarean for HBV alone; breastfeeding allowed; NVHCP supplies free HBIG.`]}],mustDraw:[`Antenatal-to-infancy timeline of MTCT prevention`],markSplit:[{part:`Screening`,marks:1},{part:`Maternal TDF`,marks:2},{part:`Newborn prophylaxis and follow-up`,marks:2}],keywords:[`MTCT`,`TDF`,`HBIG`,`birth dose`,`200,000 IU/mL`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Chronic hepatitis`,`WHO Guidelines for the prevention, diagnosis, care and treatment for people with chronic hepatitis B infection, March 2024`,`EASL 2017 Clinical Practice Guidelines on the management of hepatitis B virus infection. J Hepatol 2017`,`Terrault NA et al. Update on prevention, diagnosis and treatment of chronic hepatitis B: AASLD 2018 hepatitis B guidance. Hepatology 2018`,`AGA Clinical Practice Guideline on prevention and treatment of hepatitis B virus reactivation in at-risk individuals, 2025`,`WHO Global Hepatitis Report, 2024`,`National Viral Hepatitis Control Programme (NVHCP) National guidelines for diagnosis and management of viral hepatitis, MoHFW 2018`]});var a=[];a.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatitis-c-chronic`,title:`Chronic hepatitis C: pan-genotypic DAA therapy`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Chronic hepatitis C** is infection with the **hepatitis C virus (HCV RNA) persisting beyond 6 months**, a silent cause of **cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma**, now **curable in over 95%** with 8-12 weeks of oral **pan-genotypic direct-acting antivirals (sofosbuvir/velpatasvir or sofosbuvir + daclatasvir, free under NVHCP)**, cure being defined as **SVR12** - undetectable HCV RNA 12 weeks after the end of treatment.`,keywords:[`HCV`,`hepatitis C`,`DAA`,`direct-acting antivirals`,`sofosbuvir`,`velpatasvir`,`daclatasvir`,`glecaprevir/pibrentasvir`,`voxilaprevir`,`SVR12`,`sustained virological response`,`NVHCP`,`APRI`,`FIB-4`,`transient elastography`,`cryoglobulinaemia`,`ribavirin`,`simplified HCV treatment`,`anti-HCV`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Chronic HCV infection** is detectable **HCV RNA for more than 6 months**; a positive anti-HCV antibody alone means only exposure, past or present. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`WHO estimates about **50 million people live with chronic HCV**, with about **1 million new infections and 2.4 lakh deaths a year** (2022 data), mostly from cirrhosis and HCC. [WHO Global Hepatitis Report 2024]`,`India carries an estimated **6-12 million viraemic people (anti-HCV about 0.5-1% of adults)**, with pockets of much higher prevalence in **Punjab, Haryana and the north-eastern states**. [NVHCP Technical Guidelines 2018]`,`High-prevalence Indian groups are **people who inject drugs (PWID, 30-60% in Punjab and Manipur), patients on haemodialysis, multi-transfused thalassaemics and people with HIV**. [NVHCP Technical Guidelines 2018]`,`**Genotype 3 is the commonest genotype in India (about 60%), followed by genotype 1**; genotype 3 with cirrhosis is the hardest group to cure, which is why pan-genotypic regimens matter. [NVHCP Technical Guidelines 2018]`,`India's **National Viral Hepatitis Control Programme (NVHCP)**, launched on **28 July 2018**, gives free anti-HCV testing, HCV RNA and DAA treatment through Model Treatment Centres and Treatment Centres. [MoHFW NVHCP 2018]`,`The programme aims at **elimination of hepatitis C as a public health threat by 2030** (SDG 3.3), the WHO target being a **90% fall in incidence and 65% fall in mortality** versus 2015. [WHO GHSS 2022-2030]`]},{heading:`Virology, transmission and risk factors`,points:[`HCV is a **positive-sense single-stranded RNA virus of the Flaviviridae (genus Hepacivirus)**, about 9.6 kb, with **8 genotypes** and many subtypes; it never integrates into the host genome, which is why it can be cured. [Harrison 22e]`,`Its polyprotein yields the three drug targets: **NS3/4A protease (-previr drugs), NS5A replication complex (-asvir drugs) and NS5B RNA polymerase (-buvir drugs)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Error-prone replication creates a **quasispecies** that escapes antibody - there is **no vaccine**, and cure does not protect against reinfection. [Harrison 22e]`,`In India the main route is **unsafe therapeutic injections and reuse of needles**, followed by **blood transfusion before 2002 screening, haemodialysis, injecting drug use and unsterile dental or surgical procedures**. [NVHCP Technical Guidelines 2018]`,`Other routes: **tattooing, shared razors, needle-stick injury (risk about 1.8% per exposure)**, sexual (low, higher in MSM with HIV) and **vertical (about 5%, double with HIV co-infection)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Factors that speed fibrosis: **alcohol, age over 40 at infection, male sex, obesity/MASLD, diabetes, HIV or HBV co-infection** and genotype 3 (steatosis). [EASL HCV 2020]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology and natural history`,points:[`HCV is **not directly cytopathic**; liver injury comes from **CD8 T-cell-mediated killing of infected hepatocytes**, which drives stellate-cell activation and fibrosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`About **15-45% clear acute infection spontaneously** (more in the young, women and those with favourable IL28B/IFNL3 genotype); **55-85% become chronic**. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`Over **20-30 years, 15-30% of chronically infected people develop cirrhosis**; once cirrhotic, **HCC develops at 1-4% a year** and decompensation at about 3-6% a year. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Genotype 3 deposits fat in hepatocytes (viral steatosis)** and is associated with faster fibrosis and higher HCC risk. [EASL HCV 2020]`,`**Extrahepatic disease** arises from chronic B-cell stimulation: **mixed (type II) cryoglobulinaemia**, membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis, B-cell non-Hodgkin lymphoma; plus insulin resistance and type 2 diabetes. [Harrison 22e]`,`DAAs cure because HCV has **no latent reservoir** (unlike HBV cccDNA or HIV proviral DNA): blocking replication for 8-12 weeks lets infected cells turn over until the virus is gone. [Harrison 22e]`,`Combining a **sofosbuvir backbone (high barrier to resistance) with an NS5A inhibitor** prevents emergence of resistant variants - the logic of every pan-genotypic regimen. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`]},{heading:`Classification and staging`,points:[`Classify by **phase**: acute (within 6 months of exposure) versus chronic (RNA beyond 6 months); AASLD now advises **treating acute HCV at once without waiting for spontaneous clearance**. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`Classify by **liver stage**, which decides regimen and follow-up: **no cirrhosis, compensated cirrhosis (Child-Pugh A) or decompensated cirrhosis (Child-Pugh B/C)**. [NVHCP Technical Guidelines 2018]`,`Fibrosis is staged **METAVIR F0-F4** (F4 = cirrhosis); in practice it is estimated by **APRI, FIB-4 or transient elastography**, not biopsy. [EASL HCV 2020]`,`Classify by **treatment history**: treatment-naive, interferon-experienced, or **DAA-experienced (NS5A failure)** - the last needs a different regimen. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`Genotype matters now only for **genotype 3 with cirrhosis** (Y93H resistance testing in some guidelines) and when non-pan-genotypic drugs are used. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`**Special populations**: HBV or HIV co-infection, CKD/dialysis, pregnancy, children, HCC, post-transplant - excluded from simplified treatment. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Most people are **asymptomatic for decades**; HCV is usually found on **screening before surgery, blood donation, dialysis or antenatal care**, or on a raised ALT. [Harrison 22e]`,`The commonest symptom is **fatigue**; others are vague right upper quadrant discomfort, poor concentration and depression. [Harrison 22e]`,`Acute HCV is **icteric in only 20-30%** and rarely fulminant; a history of jaundice is therefore usually absent. [Harrison 22e]`,`Take a **risk history**: injections from unqualified practitioners, transfusion, dialysis, surgery or dental work, injecting drugs, tattoos, shared razors, HIV, and family members with HCV. [NVHCP Technical Guidelines 2018]`,`Ask about **alcohol** (in units/week), **diabetes and weight** - they accelerate fibrosis - and about all medicines and herbal products for **drug interactions** with DAAs. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`Ask for **decompensation**: jaundice, abdominal distension, confusion or sleep reversal, haematemesis or melaena - they change the regimen and need referral. [NVHCP Technical Guidelines 2018]`,`Atypical presentations: **purpura and arthralgia (cryoglobulinaemia), nephrotic syndrome, blistering of sun-exposed skin (porphyria cutanea tarda), lichen planus**, peripheral neuropathy. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Examination is **often normal** in the non-cirrhotic patient - a normal liver on palpation does not exclude advanced fibrosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for **stigmata of chronic liver disease**: palmar erythema, spider naevi, gynaecomastia, Dupuytren contracture, parotid enlargement and loss of axillary hair. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Splenomegaly** and a low platelet count point to portal hypertension and probable cirrhosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for **decompensation**: jaundice, ascites (shifting dullness), flapping tremor (asterixis), fetor hepaticus and pedal oedema. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for extrahepatic signs: **palpable purpura on the legs (cryoglobulin vasculitis), oral or skin lichen planus, PCT blisters and scarring on the hands**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for **needle tracks, tattoos and thrombophlebitis** (PWID) and record **BMI and waist** for metabolic co-factors. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Chronic hepatitis B** - HBsAg positive; may co-exist with HCV, and HBV can **reactivate during DAA therapy**. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`**MASLD/MASH** - metabolic risk factors, steatosis on ultrasound, anti-HCV negative; HCV genotype 3 also causes steatosis, so test anti-HCV in every fatty liver with raised ALT (see the MASLD topic). [EASL HCV 2020]`,`**Alcohol-related liver disease** - AST:ALT over 2, raised GGT and MCV; often coexists with HCV and multiplies cirrhosis risk. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Autoimmune hepatitis** - raised IgG, ANA/ASMA; note that HCV itself can produce low-titre autoantibodies and anti-LKM1. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Wilson disease, haemochromatosis and drug-induced liver injury** in the young or with no risk factor. [Harrison 22e]`,`**False-positive anti-HCV** - low signal-to-cutoff ratio, negative RNA; and **resolved infection** - antibody positive, RNA negative on two occasions. [CDC HCV Testing 2020]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Step 1 - screen with anti-HCV antibody** (ELISA/CLIA or WHO-prequalified rapid diagnostic test); it turns positive about **8-12 weeks** after exposure. [NVHCP Technical Guidelines 2018]`,`**Step 2 - confirm viraemia with HCV RNA (quantitative PCR) or HCV core antigen**; a positive antibody with negative RNA means cleared infection or false positive. [WHO HCV Guidelines 2022]`,`**Reflex testing** (RNA run on the same sample when antibody is positive) and **point-of-care RNA (GeneXpert/Truenat)** reduce loss to follow-up; WHO 2022 endorses both. [WHO HCV Guidelines 2022]`,`**Assess fibrosis**: **APRI = (AST/AST upper limit) x 100 / platelets (10^9/L)**; **APRI over 2 suggests cirrhosis** (NVHCP and WHO cut-off), under 0.5 makes significant fibrosis unlikely. [NVHCP Technical Guidelines 2018]`,`**FIB-4 = age x AST / (platelets x square root of ALT)**; **over 3.25 suggests advanced fibrosis/cirrhosis**, under 1.45 excludes it; **transient elastography over 12.5 kPa** indicates cirrhosis. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`Pre-treatment baseline: CBC, LFT, INR, creatinine with eGFR, **HBsAg and anti-HBc, HIV**, pregnancy test in women, and an **ultrasound** for cirrhosis/HCC. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`**Genotyping is not needed** before a pan-genotypic regimen under NVHCP or the AASLD simplified pathway. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`In cirrhosis, calculate **Child-Pugh and MELD** and arrange **endoscopy for varices** if platelets are under 1.5 lakh or stiffness over 20 kPa (Baveno VII). [Baveno VII 2022]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - simplified pan-genotypic treatment`,points:[`**Treat everyone with HCV viraemia** aged 3 years and over, whatever the fibrosis stage, except those with short life expectancy from other illness. [WHO HCV Guidelines 2022]`,`**NVHCP, no cirrhosis**: **sofosbuvir 400 mg + daclatasvir 60 mg orally once daily for 12 weeks**, or sofosbuvir/velpatasvir 400/100 mg once daily for 12 weeks. [NVHCP Technical Guidelines 2018]`,`**NVHCP, compensated cirrhosis**: **sofosbuvir/velpatasvir 400/100 mg OD for 12 weeks**, or sofosbuvir + daclatasvir for **24 weeks**. [NVHCP Technical Guidelines 2018]`,`**AASLD simplified (naive, no cirrhosis)**: **glecaprevir/pibrentasvir 300/120 mg (three tablets) once daily with food for 8 weeks**, or sofosbuvir/velpatasvir for 12 weeks. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`**AASLD simplified, compensated cirrhosis**: glecaprevir/pibrentasvir 8 weeks, or sofosbuvir/velpatasvir 12 weeks (genotype 3: test for **NS5A Y93H** - if present add weight-based ribavirin or use SOF/VEL/VOX). [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`**Check drug interactions before prescribing**: rifampicin, carbamazepine, phenytoin and St John's wort are **contraindicated** (they cut DAA levels); **amiodarone with sofosbuvir causes fatal bradycardia**. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`**Acid suppressants and velpatasvir**: PPIs reduce velpatasvir absorption - avoid, or give omeprazole up to 20 mg 4 hours after SOF/VEL taken with food; space antacids 4 hours. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`**No on-treatment monitoring is needed** in the simplified pathway apart from adherence and glucose/INR checks in diabetics or those on warfarin, since clearing HCV can lower glucose and change INR. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`Counsel: **daily adherence, no alcohol, avoid new medicines without checking**, safe sex and no sharing of razors or needles; dispense monthly under NVHCP. [NVHCP Technical Guidelines 2018]`]},{heading:`Management of difficult groups and in hospital`,points:[`**Decompensated cirrhosis (Child-Pugh B/C)**: **sofosbuvir/velpatasvir + ribavirin for 12 weeks** (ribavirin 1000 mg if under 75 kg, 1200 mg if 75 kg or more, or start at 600 mg and titrate), or SOF/VEL for 24 weeks if ribavirin is not tolerated. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`**Protease inhibitors (glecaprevir, voxilaprevir, grazoprevir) are contraindicated in Child-Pugh B/C** - they are hepatically cleared and can precipitate liver failure. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`Decompensated patients are treated at a **Model Treatment Centre or liver unit** with liver transplant assessment; those with **MELD 20 or more** may be better treated after transplant. [EASL HCV 2020]`,`**DAA failure (NS5A-experienced)**: **sofosbuvir/velpatasvir/voxilaprevir 400/100/100 mg once daily for 12 weeks** (compensated liver only); refer to a specialist centre. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`**HBV co-infection**: HBV can flare when HCV is cleared - if **HBsAg positive**, start tenofovir/entecavir prophylaxis until 12 weeks after DAAs or monitor HBV DNA; if only anti-HBc positive, check ALT if it rises. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`**Admission** is for complications rather than for HCV itself: variceal bleed, encephalopathy, SBP, HCC, or **severe cryoglobulinaemic vasculitis** (rituximab or plasmapheresis plus DAA). [EASL HCV 2020]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: test every pregnant woman (AASLD/CDC); DAAs are not yet licensed in pregnancy, so treatment is usually **deferred until after delivery**, though AASLD 2023 allows individualised treatment. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`**Ribavirin is teratogenic** - contraindicated in pregnancy, with **contraception for 6 months after the last dose in both partners**. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`Mode of delivery is decided on obstetric grounds; **caesarean section does not prevent transmission**, and **breastfeeding is allowed** unless nipples are cracked or bleeding. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`**Perinatally exposed infants**: test **HCV RNA at 2-6 months** (CDC 2023), not antibody, which is maternal until 18 months. [CDC Perinatal HCV 2023]`,`**Children 3 years and over** are treated with weight-based pan-genotypic DAAs (SOF/VEL or G/P granules). [WHO HCV Guidelines 2022]`,`**CKD and dialysis**: sofosbuvir-based regimens and glecaprevir/pibrentasvir need **no dose change at any eGFR including dialysis**; screen all dialysis patients every 6 months. [KDIGO HCV in CKD 2022]`,`**HIV co-infection**: same DAA regimens, but check ART interactions (efavirenz lowers velpatasvir; boosted protease inhibitors with glecaprevir). [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`**PWID and people on opioid substitution**: treat - active drug use is **not** a reason to withhold DAAs; combine with needle-syringe and OST services. [WHO HCV Guidelines 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Untreated: **cirrhosis (15-30% over 20-30 years), decompensation, HCC (1-4%/year in cirrhosis)** and liver-related death. [Harrison 22e]`,`**SVR12 is durable in over 99%** and equals cure; it reduces all-cause mortality, decompensation and HCC by about 70-80%. [EASL HCV 2020]`,`**HCC risk persists after SVR in cirrhosis** (about 1-2% a year), so surveillance does not stop with cure. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`Pan-genotypic DAAs achieve **SVR above 95% in all genotypes**; the lowest rates are in genotype 3 with cirrhosis and decompensated disease. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`Side effects are minor: **headache, fatigue, nausea**; ribavirin adds **haemolytic anaemia, cough, rash and teratogenicity**. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`Extrahepatic complications - cryoglobulinaemic vasculitis, MPGN, lymphoma - often **improve after SVR**. [EASL HCV 2020]`]},{heading:`Follow-up and referral`,points:[`Check **HCV RNA 12 weeks after the end of treatment (SVR12)**; undetectable RNA means cure. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`**Anti-HCV stays positive for life after cure**, so reinfection is diagnosed only by **HCV RNA** - test yearly in PWID and other ongoing-risk groups. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`**No cirrhosis + SVR**: discharge from liver follow-up with normal ALT; advise alcohol limits and metabolic control. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`**Cirrhosis + SVR**: **ultrasound with or without AFP every 6 months for life** and variceal surveillance as per Baveno VII. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`Refer to a **Model Treatment Centre/hepatologist**: decompensated cirrhosis, DAA failure, HCC, HBV/HIV co-infection, pregnancy, children, transplant, eGFR under 30 needing ribavirin. [NVHCP Technical Guidelines 2018]`,`Detectable RNA at SVR12 means **relapse or reinfection** - repeat adherence history and refer for retreatment. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and NVHCP link`,points:[`**Universal one-time screening of all adults 18 years and over**, every pregnancy, and periodic screening for ongoing risk is the AASLD/CDC strategy; NVHCP targets high-risk groups. [CDC HCV Screening 2020]`,`Injection safety: **auto-disable syringes, single-use devices and fewer unnecessary injections** - the key prevention in India. [NVHCP Technical Guidelines 2018]`,`**Safe blood**: NAT screening of donated blood where available and voluntary non-remunerated donation. [NVHCP Technical Guidelines 2018]`,`Harm reduction for PWID: **needle-syringe programmes and opioid substitution therapy** (Targeted Interventions under NACO). [WHO HCV Guidelines 2022]`,`Counsel that HCV **is not spread by hugging, sharing food or utensils, or breastfeeding**; household members should not share razors or toothbrushes. [CDC HCV 2020]`,`**Vaccinate against hepatitis A and B** if non-immune, as superinfection worsens outcome; there is no HCV vaccine. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2026)`,points:[`**AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance (2023 update)**: universal screening, simplified treatment pathways for non-cirrhotic and compensated cirrhotic adults, and **treat acute HCV immediately**. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`**CDC 2023**: perinatally exposed infants are tested by **HCV RNA at 2-6 months** instead of antibody at 18 months. [CDC Perinatal HCV 2023]`,`**WHO 2022 HCV guidelines**: treat children and adolescents 3 years and over with pan-genotypic DAAs, **point-of-care RNA testing, same-visit test and treat**, and task-sharing to non-specialists. [WHO HCV Guidelines 2022]`,`**WHO Global Hepatitis Report 2024** places India among the countries carrying most of the global viral hepatitis burden, re-emphasising decentralised testing. [WHO Global Hepatitis Report 2024]`,`**NVHCP** now delivers testing and treatment through decentralised Treatment Centres and **Ayushman Arogya Mandirs** screening high-risk groups. [MoHFW NVHCP 2023]`,`Older regimens - **interferon, sofosbuvir + ribavirin alone, genotype-specific ledipasvir or elbasvir/grazoprevir** - are no longer first-line where pan-genotypic DAAs are available. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic viva: **'Anti-HCV positive - what next?'** Answer: HCV RNA; antibody alone never starts treatment. [NVHCP Technical Guidelines 2018]`,`Know the **three DAA classes by suffix**: -previr (NS3/4A protease), -asvir (NS5A), -buvir (NS5B polymerase). [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: **forgetting HBsAg/anti-HBc before DAAs** - HBV reactivation (FDA boxed warning 2016) can be fatal. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`Common mistake: **stopping HCC surveillance after SVR in a cirrhotic**. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`Common mistake: prescribing **glecaprevir/pibrentasvir in decompensated cirrhosis** - protease inhibitors are contraindicated in Child-Pugh B/C. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`,`Remember **amiodarone + sofosbuvir = symptomatic bradycardia** and **rifampicin + any DAA = treatment failure** - both high-yield in India where TB co-exists. [AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance 2023]`]}],tables:[{heading:`DAA and adjunct drugs for chronic hepatitis C`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Sofosbuvir (NS5B nucleotide inhibitor)`,`400 mg orally once daily, with or without food`,`12 weeks (24 weeks with daclatasvir in cirrhosis)`,`Bradycardia with amiodarone (contraindicated); rifampicin, carbamazepine, phenytoin reduce levels; no dose change in CKD/dialysis`],[`Daclatasvir (NS5A inhibitor)`,`60 mg orally once daily; 30 mg with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors; 90 mg with moderate inducers (efavirenz)`,`12 weeks (no cirrhosis); 24 weeks (cirrhosis)`,`Headache, fatigue; avoid strong CYP3A inducers`],[`Sofosbuvir/velpatasvir (SOF/VEL)`,`400/100 mg one tablet orally once daily`,`12 weeks; + ribavirin 12 weeks or alone 24 weeks in decompensated`,`PPIs and antacids lower velpatasvir; efavirenz, rifampicin contraindicated; amiodarone`],[`Glecaprevir/pibrentasvir (G/P)`,`300/120 mg (three 100/40 mg tablets) orally once daily with food`,`8 weeks naive (with or without compensated cirrhosis)`,`Contraindicated in Child-Pugh B/C; avoid with atazanavir, rifampicin, ethinylestradiol pills (ALT rise)`],[`Sofosbuvir/velpatasvir/voxilaprevir`,`400/100/100 mg orally once daily with food`,`12 weeks (DAA failure)`,`Contraindicated in Child-Pugh B/C; protease-inhibitor interactions`],[`Ribavirin`,`1000 mg/day (under 75 kg) or 1200 mg/day (75 kg or more) in two divided doses with food; start 600 mg/day in decompensated`,`12 weeks with SOF/VEL`,`Haemolytic anaemia, teratogenic (contraception 6 months both partners), cough, rash; reduce in CKD`],[`Tenofovir disoproxil (HBV prophylaxis)`,`300 mg orally once daily`,`From DAA start until 12 weeks after the end (HBsAg positive)`,`Renal tubular toxicity, bone loss; use TAF or entecavir in CKD`]]},{heading:`Which regimen for whom (NVHCP 2018 and AASLD-IDSA 2023)`,columns:[`Patient group`,`NVHCP regimen`,`AASLD-IDSA alternative`,`Note`],rows:[[`Treatment-naive, no cirrhosis`,`SOF 400 + DCV 60 mg OD x 12 weeks or SOF/VEL x 12 weeks`,`G/P x 8 weeks or SOF/VEL x 12 weeks`,`Simplified pathway; no genotype needed`],[`Compensated cirrhosis (Child A)`,`SOF/VEL x 12 weeks or SOF + DCV x 24 weeks`,`G/P x 8 weeks or SOF/VEL x 12 weeks`,`Genotype 3: Y93H test; add ribavirin if present`],[`Decompensated cirrhosis (Child B/C)`,`SOF/VEL + ribavirin x 12 weeks (SOF/VEL x 24 weeks if no ribavirin)`,`Same; no protease inhibitor`,`Treat at MTC/transplant centre`],[`DAA failure (NS5A-experienced)`,`Refer to MTC`,`SOF/VEL/VOX x 12 weeks`,`Compensated liver only`],[`CKD stage 4-5 / dialysis`,`SOF-based, no dose change`,`G/P x 8 weeks or SOF/VEL x 12 weeks`,`Avoid ribavirin or dose-reduce`],[`Children 3 years and over`,`Per national paediatric guidance`,`Weight-based SOF/VEL or G/P`,`WHO 2022 recommends treating`]]},{heading:`HCV test interpretation and fibrosis cut-offs`,columns:[`Test / result`,`Interpretation`,`Action`],rows:[[`Anti-HCV positive, HCV RNA detectable`,`Current (active) infection`,`Stage fibrosis and treat`],[`Anti-HCV positive, HCV RNA not detected`,`Past cleared infection or false-positive antibody`,`Repeat RNA if recent exposure; reassure`],[`Anti-HCV negative, RNA detectable`,`Very early acute infection (window) or immunosuppressed/dialysis`,`Treat as acute HCV; retest antibody later`],[`APRI over 2 / under 0.5`,`Cirrhosis likely / significant fibrosis unlikely`,`Over 2: cirrhosis regimen, ultrasound, endoscopy`],[`FIB-4 over 3.25 / under 1.45`,`Advanced fibrosis likely / excluded`,`Over 3.25: treat as cirrhosis, confirm with elastography`],[`Liver stiffness over 12.5 kPa`,`Cirrhosis`,`HCC surveillance for life even after SVR`],[`HCV RNA undetectable 12 weeks after treatment`,`SVR12 - cure`,`Discharge if no cirrhosis; antibody stays positive`]]},{heading:`Chronic HCV versus other causes of chronic hepatitis`,columns:[`Feature`,`Chronic HCV`,`Chronic HBV`,`MASLD`,`Autoimmune hepatitis`],rows:[[`Key marker`,`Anti-HCV + HCV RNA`,`HBsAg > 6 months, HBV DNA`,`Steatosis + metabolic risk`,`Raised IgG, ANA/ASMA, interface hepatitis`],[`Typical ALT`,`Mild, fluctuating, often normal`,`Variable by phase`,`Mild, ALT > AST`,`Moderate-high`],[`Extrahepatic`,`Cryoglobulinaemia, MPGN, PCT, lichen planus`,`Polyarteritis nodosa, membranous GN`,`Diabetes, CVD`,`Thyroiditis, other autoimmunity`],[`Treatment`,`DAA 8-12 weeks - cure`,`Long-term tenofovir/entecavir - suppression`,`Weight loss 7-10%, resmetirom`,`Prednisolone + azathioprine`],[`Cure possible?`,`Yes (SVR over 95%)`,`Functional cure rare`,`Reversible with weight loss`,`Remission, rarely off-drug`]]},{heading:`High-yield DAA drug interactions`,columns:[`Co-medication`,`Interaction`,`What to do`],rows:[[`Rifampicin, rifabutin`,`Strong induction - DAA levels fall, failure`,`Complete TB treatment first or defer DAA`],[`Carbamazepine, phenytoin, phenobarbitone`,`Strong induction`,`Switch to levetiracetam or valproate before DAA`],[`Amiodarone`,`Severe bradycardia/asystole with sofosbuvir`,`Contraindicated; stop and wait or use alternative`],[`PPIs, H2 blockers, antacids`,`Lower velpatasvir and ledipasvir absorption`,`Avoid PPI; omeprazole max 20 mg 4 hours after SOF/VEL; antacid 4 hours apart`],[`Statins (rosuvastatin, atorvastatin)`,`Levels rise with velpatasvir and G/P`,`Lowest dose; rosuvastatin max 10 mg with SOF/VEL`],[`Ethinylestradiol pills`,`ALT rise with glecaprevir/pibrentasvir`,`Use progestogen-only or non-hormonal method`],[`Efavirenz`,`Lowers velpatasvir and daclatasvir`,`Switch to dolutegravir or raise daclatasvir to 90 mg`]]}],redFlags:[`Jaundice, ascites, encephalopathy or variceal bleed in a known HCV patient - decompensation, needs a liver unit and transplant assessment.`,`Liver mass on ultrasound or rising AFP in HCV cirrhosis - suspect hepatocellular carcinoma.`,`ALT flare or jaundice during DAA therapy in an HBsAg or anti-HBc positive patient - HBV reactivation.`,`Bradycardia, syncope or dizziness in a patient on sofosbuvir with amiodarone.`,`Palpable purpura, rising creatinine, haematuria or neuropathy - cryoglobulinaemic vasculitis or MPGN.`,`Pregnancy discovered while on ribavirin - stop at once and refer.`,`Detectable HCV RNA at SVR12 - treatment failure or reinfection, refer for retreatment.`],pearls:[`**Antibody says exposure, RNA says infection** - never treat on anti-HCV alone.`,`**Previr-asvir-buvir**: NS3/4A protease, NS5A, NS5B polymerase.`,`**SVR12 = cure**, but **cirrhosis + SVR = ultrasound every 6 months for life**.`,`**No protease inhibitor in Child B/C** - use SOF/VEL + ribavirin.`,`Test **HBsAg and anti-HBc before every DAA course** (HBV reactivation boxed warning).`,`**APRI over 2 = cirrhosis** (NVHCP); **FIB-4 over 3.25 = advanced fibrosis**.`,`**Genotype 3 is India's commonest** and the hardest to cure in cirrhosis.`,`**Rifampicin and amiodarone** are the two interactions every examiner asks.`,`Anti-HCV stays positive after cure - **check reinfection by RNA**.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatitis-c-chronic-q1`,stem:`A 34-year-old man found anti-HCV positive on pre-operative screening has HCV RNA detectable, AST 48 U/L (ULN 40), ALT 62 U/L and platelets 2.4 lakh/mm3. He has no cirrhosis on ultrasound. What is the best NVHCP regimen?`,options:[`Sofosbuvir 400 mg + daclatasvir 60 mg once daily for 12 weeks`,`Pegylated interferon with ribavirin for 48 weeks`,`Sofosbuvir/velpatasvir with ribavirin for 24 weeks`,`Wait for genotype result before starting any therapy`],answer:0,explanation:`His APRI is about 0.5, so he is non-cirrhotic; NVHCP treats non-cirrhotics with pan-genotypic sofosbuvir + daclatasvir (or SOF/VEL) for 12 weeks without genotyping. Interferon is obsolete; ribavirin and 24-week courses are reserved for decompensated disease; genotyping is unnecessary with pan-genotypic drugs.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatitis-c-chronic-q2`,stem:`A 52-year-old woman with HCV cirrhosis has ascites, bilirubin 3.2 mg/dL and albumin 2.7 g/dL (Child-Pugh C). Which DAA regimen must be avoided?`,options:[`Sofosbuvir/velpatasvir with ribavirin for 12 weeks`,`Sofosbuvir/velpatasvir for 24 weeks`,`Glecaprevir/pibrentasvir for 8 weeks`,`Sofosbuvir/velpatasvir with low-dose ribavirin titrated up`],answer:2,explanation:`Glecaprevir is an NS3/4A protease inhibitor, hepatically cleared, and protease inhibitors are contraindicated in Child-Pugh B/C because of hepatotoxicity and decompensation. SOF/VEL plus ribavirin for 12 weeks, ribavirin started low, or SOF/VEL alone for 24 weeks when ribavirin cannot be used are the recommended decompensated regimens.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatitis-c-chronic-q3`,stem:`Before starting direct-acting antivirals, which test is mandatory because of a boxed warning about a potentially fatal complication?`,options:[`Serum alpha-fetoprotein`,`HBsAg and anti-HBc`,`HCV genotype`,`Serum cryoglobulins`],answer:1,explanation:`Rapid clearance of HCV by DAAs removes viral interference and can reactivate co-existing hepatitis B, sometimes fatally (FDA boxed warning 2016), so HBsAg and anti-HBc are checked and HBsAg-positive patients get prophylaxis or monitoring. Genotype is no longer needed with pan-genotypic drugs; AFP and cryoglobulins are not required to start treatment.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatitis-c-chronic-q4`,stem:`A 45-year-old man with pulmonary tuberculosis on HRZE is found to have chronic HCV without cirrhosis. What is the most appropriate plan?`,options:[`Start sofosbuvir/velpatasvir now alongside anti-TB treatment`,`Switch rifampicin to rifabutin and start glecaprevir/pibrentasvir`,`Start sofosbuvir + daclatasvir with daclatasvir increased to 90 mg`,`Complete anti-TB treatment first, then start DAAs`],answer:3,explanation:`Rifampicin, and also rifabutin, are strong inducers of P-glycoprotein and CYP3A that lower DAA levels and cause treatment failure, so co-administration is contraindicated. In a non-cirrhotic, delaying HCV therapy for a few months is safe, so finish TB treatment first; raising daclatasvir to 90 mg applies to moderate inducers such as efavirenz, not rifampicin.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatitis-c-chronic-q5`,stem:`A 60-year-old man with compensated HCV cirrhosis achieved SVR12 after sofosbuvir/velpatasvir. Which follow-up is correct?`,options:[`Repeat anti-HCV yearly to confirm continued cure`,`Discharge from follow-up as he is cured`,`Ultrasound with or without AFP every 6 months for life`,`Liver biopsy at 1 year to document fibrosis regression`],answer:2,explanation:`SVR lowers but does not abolish HCC risk in cirrhosis, so 6-monthly ultrasound surveillance continues for life, with variceal surveillance as indicated. Discharge is appropriate only for non-cirrhotics; anti-HCV remains positive for life after cure so it cannot monitor anything, and biopsy is not needed.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatitis-c-chronic-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the diagnosis, pre-treatment assessment and management of chronic hepatitis C in primary care, with reference to pan-genotypic direct-acting antivirals and the National Viral Hepatitis Control Programme.`,openingLines:[`Chronic hepatitis C is persistence of HCV RNA beyond 6 months, a silent cause of cirrhosis and HCC that affects an estimated 6-12 million Indians.`,`It is now curable in over 95% with 8-12 weeks of oral pan-genotypic DAAs, given free under NVHCP, with cure defined as SVR12.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition, burden and virology`,points:[`HCV - Flaviviridae RNA virus, 8 genotypes; genotype 3 commonest in India (about 60%).`,`WHO 2024: about 50 million chronic infections worldwide; India 6-12 million; hotspots Punjab, north-east, PWID, dialysis.`,`Natural history: 55-85% chronic, 15-30% cirrhosis in 20-30 years, HCC 1-4%/year in cirrhosis.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis - two-step testing`,points:[`Screen with anti-HCV (ELISA or rapid test).`,`Confirm viraemia with HCV RNA (or core antigen); antibody alone is not an indication to treat.`,`Reflex and point-of-care RNA testing reduce loss to follow-up (WHO 2022).`]},{heading:`Pre-treatment assessment`,points:[`Fibrosis: APRI (over 2 = cirrhosis), FIB-4 (over 3.25 advanced), elastography over 12.5 kPa.`,`Child-Pugh status - compensated vs decompensated.`,`HBsAg, anti-HBc, HIV, eGFR, pregnancy test, drug interaction check (rifampicin, amiodarone, antiepileptics, PPIs).`]},{heading:`Treatment regimens`,points:[`No cirrhosis: SOF 400 + DCV 60 mg OD 12 weeks or SOF/VEL 12 weeks (AASLD: G/P 8 weeks).`,`Compensated cirrhosis: SOF/VEL 12 weeks or SOF + DCV 24 weeks.`,`Decompensated: SOF/VEL + ribavirin 12 weeks; no protease inhibitors.`,`DAA failure: SOF/VEL/VOX 12 weeks at a specialist centre.`]},{heading:`Monitoring and follow-up`,points:[`Adherence counselling; monthly dispensing under NVHCP.`,`HCV RNA at 12 weeks after the end - SVR12 = cure.`,`Cirrhosis: 6-monthly ultrasound for HCC for life; varices per Baveno VII.`,`Reinfection checked by RNA, not antibody.`]},{heading:`Special groups and prevention`,points:[`Pregnancy: screen every pregnancy, usually treat after delivery; ribavirin teratogenic.`,`Children 3 years and over, CKD/dialysis, PWID and HIV - all treatable.`,`Prevention: injection safety, NAT-screened blood, harm reduction; no vaccine.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: anti-HCV test, HCV RNA, fibrosis assessment (APRI/FIB-4), regimen by liver stage, SVR12`,`Table of regimens by patient group (NVHCP)`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition, burden, virology`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnostic algorithm`,marks:2},{part:`Pre-treatment assessment`,marks:2},{part:`Regimens by group`,marks:2},{part:`Follow-up, special groups, prevention`,marks:2}],keywords:[`anti-HCV`,`HCV RNA`,`APRI`,`FIB-4`,`sofosbuvir`,`daclatasvir`,`velpatasvir`,`SVR12`,`NVHCP`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatitis-c-chronic-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on direct-acting antiviral drugs for hepatitis C.`,openingLines:[`Direct-acting antivirals (DAAs) inhibit specific HCV non-structural proteins - NS3/4A protease, NS5A and NS5B polymerase.`,`Pan-genotypic combinations cure over 95% of chronic HCV in 8-12 weeks.`],answer:[{heading:`Classes and examples`,points:[`NS3/4A protease inhibitors (-previr): glecaprevir, voxilaprevir, grazoprevir.`,`NS5A inhibitors (-asvir): daclatasvir, velpatasvir, pibrentasvir, ledipasvir.`,`NS5B polymerase inhibitor (-buvir): sofosbuvir - nucleotide, high barrier to resistance.`]},{heading:`Pan-genotypic regimens`,points:[`SOF/VEL 400/100 mg OD 12 weeks; SOF 400 + DCV 60 mg OD 12 weeks.`,`G/P 300/120 mg OD with food 8 weeks.`,`SOF/VEL/VOX 12 weeks for DAA failure.`]},{heading:`Cautions`,points:[`HBV reactivation - test HBsAg and anti-HBc first.`,`Amiodarone with sofosbuvir - bradycardia; rifampicin and antiepileptic inducers - failure.`,`Protease inhibitors contraindicated in Child-Pugh B/C.`,`PPIs reduce velpatasvir absorption.`]},{heading:`End-point`,points:[`SVR12 - undetectable RNA 12 weeks after treatment = cure; HCC surveillance continues in cirrhosis.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of HCV polyprotein with NS3/4A, NS5A, NS5B and the drug class acting on each`],markSplit:[{part:`Classes with mechanism`,marks:2},{part:`Regimens and duration`,marks:1.5},{part:`Cautions and end-point`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`NS5A`,`NS5B`,`NS3/4A`,`pan-genotypic`,`SVR12`,`HBV reactivation`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Chronic hepatitis`,`AASLD-IDSA. HCV Guidance: Recommendations for Testing, Managing, and Treating Hepatitis C, 2023 update`,`WHO. Updated recommendations on treatment of adolescents and children with chronic HCV infection, and HCV simplified service delivery and diagnostics, 2022`,`WHO. Global Hepatitis Report 2024`,`MoHFW, Government of India. National Viral Hepatitis Control Programme - National Guidelines for Diagnosis and Management of Viral Hepatitis, 2018`,`EASL Recommendations on Treatment of Hepatitis C: Final update of the series, 2020`,`CDC. Recommendations for Hepatitis C Testing Among Perinatally Exposed Infants and Children, 2023`,`KDIGO 2022 Clinical Practice Guideline for the Prevention, Diagnosis, Evaluation, and Treatment of Hepatitis C in CKD`]}),a.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-autoimmune-liver`,title:`Autoimmune hepatitis, PBC and PSC`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`The **autoimmune liver diseases** are three immune-mediated disorders - **autoimmune hepatitis** (hepatocyte injury with **raised IgG, ANA/ASMA or anti-LKM1 and interface hepatitis**, treated with **steroids plus azathioprine**), **primary biliary cholangitis** (destruction of small bile ducts in middle-aged women with **AMA and raised ALP**, treated with **ursodeoxycholic acid 13-15 mg/kg/day**) and **primary sclerosing cholangitis** (**beaded strictures of large ducts on MRCP**, usually with **ulcerative colitis**, with no proven drug and a high **cholangiocarcinoma** risk).`,keywords:[`AIH`,`autoimmune hepatitis`,`PBC`,`primary biliary cholangitis`,`primary biliary cirrhosis`,`PSC`,`primary sclerosing cholangitis`,`ANA`,`ASMA`,`anti-LKM1`,`anti-SLA`,`AMA`,`ursodeoxycholic acid`,`obeticholic acid`,`elafibranor`,`seladelpar`,`budesonide`,`azathioprine`,`IgG4 cholangitis`,`overlap syndrome`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Autoimmune hepatitis (AIH)** is a chronic immune-mediated hepatitis with **interface hepatitis, raised IgG and circulating autoantibodies**, responding to immunosuppression. [EASL AIH 2025]`,`AIH has a prevalence of about **10-25 per 100,000** in Europe, a **female:male ratio of about 4:1** and bimodal peaks in adolescence and at 40-60 years; it occurs in all ethnic groups. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Primary biliary cholangitis (PBC)** is a chronic **non-suppurative destructive cholangitis of small intrahepatic bile ducts**; about **90% of patients are women**, typically 40-60 years old. [EASL PBC 2025]`,`**Primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC)** is chronic inflammation and fibrosis of **intra- and extrahepatic large bile ducts** causing multifocal strictures; **two-thirds are men** aged 30-40. [AASLD PSC 2022]`,`**60-80% of PSC patients in the West have inflammatory bowel disease (mainly ulcerative colitis)**, while only about 5% of UC patients develop PSC. [AASLD PSC 2022]`,`Indian data are from single-centre series: **AIH accounts for a few per cent of chronic liver disease** and often presents late with cirrhosis or acute severe hepatitis; PBC and PSC appear less common than in the West. [INASL AIH data / Harrison 22e]`,`PBC was renamed from 'primary biliary cirrhosis' in **2015** because most patients now are diagnosed before cirrhosis. [EASL PBC 2017]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`AIH arises from loss of tolerance in genetically susceptible people: **HLA-DR3 (DRB1*03:01) and DR4** in Europeans, with triggers such as viruses and drugs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drug-induced AIH-like hepatitis** follows **nitrofurantoin, minocycline, methyldopa, statins and checkpoint inhibitors**; stopping the drug and short steroids usually suffices (see the DILI topic). [EASL AIH 2025]`,`AIH clusters with **autoimmune thyroiditis, type 1 diabetes, coeliac disease, vitiligo and UC**; type 2 AIH occurs in APECED (AIRE mutation). [Harrison 22e]`,`PBC risk factors: **female sex, first-degree relative with PBC (about 100-fold risk), smoking, recurrent urinary infection** and molecular mimicry with bacterial PDC-E2. [EASL PBC 2025]`,`PBC associates with **Sjogren syndrome, autoimmune thyroid disease, limited systemic sclerosis (CREST) and Raynaud phenomenon**. [Harrison 22e]`,`PSC pathogenesis links the **gut-liver axis** - leaky gut, bacterial products and gut-homing lymphocytes reaching bile ducts - explaining its tie to colitis. [AASLD PSC 2022]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`In AIH, CD4 T cells recognise liver autoantigens (**CYP2D6 in type 2, SLA/SEPSECS**) with failed regulatory T-cell control; CD8 cells and plasma cells cause **interface hepatitis** - the reason IgG rises. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Typical AIH histology**: interface hepatitis with **lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate, hepatocyte rosettes and emperipolesis** (lymphocytes inside hepatocytes). [IAIHG 2008]`,`In PBC, T cells and AMA target the **E2 subunit of pyruvate dehydrogenase complex (PDC-E2)** expressed on biliary epithelium, which undergoes apoptosis - the **florid duct lesion**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Loss of the **biliary bicarbonate umbrella** exposes cholangiocytes to toxic bile acids; UDCA works partly by restoring it and by making bile more hydrophilic. [EASL PBC 2025]`,`Retained bile acids cause **pruritus** (via autotaxin-LPA and bile acids) and **fatigue**; fat malabsorption causes deficiency of **vitamins A, D, E and K** and osteoporosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`In PSC, concentric **'onion-skin' periductal fibrosis** obliterates ducts; strictures cause stasis, cholangitis, stones and secondary biliary cirrhosis. [AASLD PSC 2022]`,`**FXR agonists (obeticholic acid)** suppress bile acid synthesis; **PPAR agonists (elafibranor, seladelpar, bezafibrate)** reduce bile acid synthesis and inflammation and relieve itch - the basis of second-line PBC therapy. [EASL PBC 2025]`]},{heading:`Classification and diagnostic scores`,points:[`**AIH type 1** - ANA and/or anti-smooth muscle (anti-actin) antibody; adults; about 80-90% of cases. [Harrison 22e]`,`**AIH type 2** - **anti-LKM1 and/or anti-LC1**; mainly children; more often acute and severe, and relapses more. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Simplified IAIHG score (2008)**: autoantibodies (ANA/ASMA 1:40 = 1, 1:80 or more/LKM1/SLA = 2), IgG (over ULN = 1, over 1.1 x ULN = 2), histology (compatible 1, typical 2), viral hepatitis absent (2); **6 = probable, 7 or more = definite AIH**. [IAIHG 2008]`,`**Anti-SLA/LP** is the most specific AIH antibody and marks more severe disease and relapse. [EASL AIH 2025]`,`**PBC diagnosis**: raised ALP (**1.5 x ULN or more**) with **AMA titre over 1:40** or PBC-specific ANA (**anti-sp100, anti-gp210**); biopsy only if antibodies are negative or overlap is suspected. [EASL PBC 2025]`,`**UDCA response** is judged at **12 months**: Paris II (ALP and AST under 1.5 x ULN, normal bilirubin), Toronto (ALP under 1.67 x ULN), or GLOBE/UK-PBC risk scores. [EASL PBC 2025]`,`**PSC** is classified as **large-duct (classic, MRCP-visible), small-duct (normal MRCP, typical biopsy) and PSC with features of AIH**. [AASLD PSC 2022]`,`**Overlap (variant) syndromes**: AIH-PBC by the **Paris criteria** (2 of 3 features of each) and AIH-PSC, commonest in children (autoimmune sclerosing cholangitis). [EASL AIH 2025]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`AIH presents in four ways: **asymptomatic raised ALT, insidious fatigue and arthralgia, acute hepatitis with jaundice (about 25-40%), or acute severe/fulminant hepatitis**. [EASL AIH 2025]`,`About **one-third of adults already have cirrhosis at diagnosis** - more in Indian series because of late presentation. [Harrison 22e]`,`AIH may flare in the **post-partum period** or after stopping treatment; ask about previous 'jaundice that settled'. [EASL AIH 2025]`,`PBC: **fatigue (up to 80%) and pruritus (up to 70%)** are typical, itch often worse at night and starting on palms and soles; many are diagnosed from a raised ALP. [EASL PBC 2025]`,`PBC itch starting or worsening in **late pregnancy or on oral contraceptives** is a clue; ask about dry eyes and mouth (Sjogren). [Harrison 22e]`,`PSC: often **asymptomatic with raised ALP in a patient with UC**; symptoms are itch, fatigue, RUQ pain and **episodes of cholangitis (fever, rigors, jaundice)**. [AASLD PSC 2022]`,`Ask a full **drug history** (nitrofurantoin, minocycline, statins, herbs) and about alcohol, IBD symptoms and autoimmune family history. [EASL AIH 2025]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`AIH: jaundice, **hepatomegaly**, stigmata of chronic liver disease, and associated signs such as **vitiligo, goitre or cushingoid features** on treatment. [Harrison 22e]`,`PBC: **excoriations from scratching, xanthelasma and xanthomas** (cholesterol), skin hyperpigmentation and hepatomegaly. [Harrison 22e]`,`PBC with CREST: **sclerodactyly, telangiectasia, calcinosis**; check for **dry eyes (Schirmer test)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Late cholestatic disease: **jaundice, splenomegaly, ascites**, and signs of **vitamin deficiency** (night blindness - A, bruising - K, osteomalacia - D). [Harrison 22e]`,`PSC: jaundice, **hepatosplenomegaly**, signs of colitis or a stoma; fever with jaundice suggests **bacterial cholangitis**. [AASLD PSC 2022]`,`Bone examination - **kyphosis or vertebral tenderness** - is relevant because cholestasis and steroids both cause osteoporosis. [EASL PBC 2025]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Viral hepatitis (HBV, HCV, HEV)** - must be excluded; low-titre ANA occurs in viral hepatitis and MASLD. [EASL AIH 2025]`,`**Drug-induced liver injury** - temporal link to drug; resolves on withdrawal and does not relapse after steroid withdrawal. [EASL AIH 2025]`,`**Wilson disease** - young patient, low ceruloplasmin, KF ring; must be excluded in AIH-like presentation under 40. [EASL Wilson 2025]`,`**IgG4-related sclerosing cholangitis** - raised serum IgG4, autoimmune pancreatitis, older men; **dramatic steroid response**, unlike PSC. [AASLD PSC 2022]`,`**Secondary sclerosing cholangitis** - stones, previous biliary surgery, ischaemia (critical illness), HIV cholangiopathy, cholangiocarcinoma. [AASLD PSC 2022]`,`**Other cholestasis**: extrahepatic obstruction (stones, tumour - dilated ducts on ultrasound), drug cholestasis, infiltration (sarcoid, TB, lymphoma), MASLD. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`LFT pattern: **AIH hepatitic** (ALT/AST high, often 5-10 x ULN); **PBC and PSC cholestatic** (ALP and GGT high, transaminases modest). [Harrison 22e]`,`AIH: **IgG raised in about 85%**, ANA, ASMA (anti-actin), anti-LKM1, anti-LC1, anti-SLA by indirect immunofluorescence on rodent tissue (titre 1:40 or more in adults). [EASL AIH 2025]`,`**Liver biopsy is required to diagnose AIH** and before starting treatment, unless contraindicated (severe coagulopathy - use transjugular route). [EASL AIH 2025]`,`PBC: **AMA (M2) positive in about 95%**; raised IgM and cholesterol; AMA-negative PBC may have anti-sp100/gp210. [EASL PBC 2025]`,`PSC: **MRCP is the first test** - multifocal strictures and dilatations ('beaded' ducts); ERCP is for therapy or brushings; **p-ANCA** is non-specific. [AASLD PSC 2022]`,`In PSC, measure **serum IgG4** once (exclude IgG4-SC) and do a **colonoscopy with biopsies at diagnosis** even without bowel symptoms. [AASLD PSC 2022]`,`Baseline for all: **ultrasound, elastography for fibrosis, TSH, DEXA**, fat-soluble vitamins in cholestasis, and HBV/HCV/HIV screening before immunosuppression. [EASL PBC 2025]`,`Before azathioprine, measure **TPMT activity or genotype** (and NUDT15 in Asians), since deficiency causes life-threatening myelosuppression. [EASL AIH 2025]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD`,points:[`**AIH induction**: **prednisolone 0.5-1 mg/kg/day (usually 30-60 mg)**, tapering as transaminases fall to about 5-10 mg/day; add **azathioprine 50 mg after 2 weeks**, increasing to **1-2 mg/kg/day**. [EASL AIH 2025]`,`**Budesonide 9 mg/day (3 mg three times a day)** with azathioprine is an alternative in **non-cirrhotic AIH**, with fewer steroid side effects; it is ineffective in cirrhosis because of portosystemic shunting. [EASL AIH 2025]`,`Target **complete biochemical response: normal ALT/AST and IgG within 6 months**; continue maintenance azathioprine for **at least 3 years and 2 years after biochemical remission** before considering withdrawal. [EASL AIH 2025]`,`Second line in AIH: **mycophenolate mofetil 1-2 g/day** (contraindicated in pregnancy), tacrolimus, or infliximab/rituximab in specialist centres. [EASL AIH 2025]`,`**PBC first line: ursodeoxycholic acid 13-15 mg/kg/day orally, lifelong** (single or divided doses); it improves transplant-free survival. [EASL PBC 2025]`,`**PBC second line** (inadequate UDCA response or intolerance): **elafibranor 80 mg OD or seladelpar 10 mg OD** (PPAR agonists, approved 2024), or **bezafibrate 400 mg/day** off-label; obeticholic acid 5-10 mg was the earlier choice but is contraindicated in decompensated cirrhosis. [EASL PBC 2025]`,`**Pruritus ladder**: cholestyramine 4-16 g/day (4 hours apart from other drugs), then **rifampicin 150-300 mg/day** (watch LFT), naltrexone 25-50 mg/day, sertraline 75-100 mg; bezafibrate also relieves itch. [EASL PBC 2025]`,`**PSC**: no drug proven to alter outcome; moderate-dose UDCA (15-20 mg/kg) may improve ALP, but **high-dose UDCA 28-30 mg/kg is harmful**. [AASLD PSC 2022]`,`Bone protection: **calcium 1000-1200 mg and vitamin D 800-1000 IU daily**, DEXA every 1-2 years, bisphosphonate if osteoporotic; bone protection for every patient on long-term steroids. [EASL PBC 2025]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Acute severe AIH** (INR 1.5 or more without encephalopathy): admit, start **IV methylprednisolone or prednisolone 1 mg/kg**, and refer to a transplant centre if no improvement in **1-2 weeks** (falling bilirubin/INR). [EASL AIH 2025]`,`AIH presenting as **acute liver failure with encephalopathy** responds poorly to steroids and carries sepsis risk - list for transplant (see the Acute liver failure topic). [EASL AIH 2025]`,`**Acute cholangitis in PSC**: blood cultures, IV antibiotics (e.g. piperacillin-tazobactam), and ERCP for a dominant stricture. [AASLD PSC 2022]`,`**Dominant stricture** (stenosis of 1.5 mm or less in CBD or 1 mm in hepatic duct) needs **ERCP with brushing cytology and FISH** to exclude cholangiocarcinoma, then balloon dilatation. [EASL PSC 2022]`,`Give **antibiotic prophylaxis before ERCP** in PSC because of high cholangitis risk. [EASL PSC 2022]`,`Manage decompensation (ascites, variceal bleeding, encephalopathy) as in cirrhosis of any cause (see the Cirrhosis topic). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy in AIH**: continue **prednisolone and azathioprine** (safe); stop mycophenolate at least 6 weeks before conception; watch for **post-partum flare**. [EASL AIH 2025]`,`**Pregnancy in PBC**: UDCA is considered safe, especially in the second and third trimesters; itch may worsen. [EASL PBC 2025]`,`**Children**: AIH type 2 and autoimmune sclerosing cholangitis are commoner; growth and bone health are key, and budesonide or lower steroid doses are preferred where suitable. [ESPGHAN AIH 2018]`,`**Elderly AIH**: often cirrhotic at diagnosis, but responds well; use lower steroid doses and protect bone. [EASL AIH 2025]`,`**Diabetes**: steroids worsen control - consider budesonide in non-cirrhotics and intensify glucose monitoring. [EASL AIH 2025]`,`**TB-endemic settings**: screen for latent TB (chest X-ray, IGRA/Mantoux) and HBV before long-term immunosuppression. [EASL AIH 2025]`,`**PSC with UC** has a higher risk of **pouchitis after colectomy** and right-sided colon cancer; colonoscopy surveillance continues after liver transplant. [AASLD PSC 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Untreated severe AIH has a **10-year mortality up to 90%**; treated AIH has **10-year survival over 80-90%** with near-normal life expectancy in responders. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Relapse after drug withdrawal in 50-90%**, usually within 12 months; most need lifelong maintenance. [EASL AIH 2025]`,`PBC: UDCA responders have survival close to the general population; **non-responders progress to cirrhosis**; HCC risk mainly in cirrhosis and men. [EASL PBC 2025]`,`PSC: median transplant-free survival **15-20 years** in tertiary series; **cholangiocarcinoma lifetime risk 10-20% (0.5-1.5% per year)**, often within the first year of diagnosis. [AASLD PSC 2022]`,`PSC-IBD: **colorectal cancer risk about 4-5 times that of UC alone**; gallbladder carcinoma risk also high. [AASLD PSC 2022]`,`Drug complications: **steroid diabetes, osteoporosis, cataract**, azathioprine **myelosuppression, pancreatitis**, non-melanoma skin cancer. [EASL AIH 2025]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Refer every suspected AIH, PBC or PSC to a **hepatologist for diagnosis (biopsy, MRCP)**; primary care continues drugs and monitoring. [EASL AIH 2025]`,`AIH: **LFT and IgG every 2 weeks during induction, then every 3-6 months**; **CBC weekly for 4 weeks, then every 3 months on azathioprine**. [EASL AIH 2025]`,`PBC: **LFT every 3-6 months**, response assessment at 12 months, elastography yearly, TSH yearly, DEXA every 2-3 years. [EASL PBC 2025]`,`PSC: **MRI/MRCP or ultrasound with or without CA 19-9 every 6-12 months** for cholangiocarcinoma and gallbladder lesions. [AASLD PSC 2022]`,`PSC with IBD: **colonoscopy every year** from PSC diagnosis; without IBD, colonoscopy every 5 years. [AASLD PSC 2022]`,`**Gallbladder polyp 8 mm or more in PSC** - cholecystectomy because of malignancy risk. [AASLD PSC 2022]`,`**Cirrhosis of any of the three**: 6-monthly ultrasound for HCC (AIH, PBC) and transplant referral when MELD 15 or more or intractable itch. [EASL PBC 2025]`]},{heading:`Prevention and counselling`,points:[`Explain these are **chronic, controllable but not contagious** diseases; adherence prevents relapse and cirrhosis. [EASL AIH 2025]`,`**Vaccinate against hepatitis A and B, pneumococcus, influenza and herpes zoster (recombinant)** before immunosuppression; avoid live vaccines on high-dose steroids or azathioprine. [EASL AIH 2025]`,`Advise **alcohol avoidance**, weight control, stopping smoking (PBC progression) and sun protection on azathioprine. [EASL PBC 2025]`,`Counsel about **steroid sick-day rules** and not stopping steroids abruptly (adrenal suppression). [EASL AIH 2025]`,`Screen first-degree female relatives of PBC with **ALP** if symptomatic or opportunistically. [EASL PBC 2025]`,`Medicines for these diseases (prednisolone, azathioprine, UDCA) are on the **NLEM/Jan Aushadhi**, keeping long-term care affordable. [NLEM 2022]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2026)`,points:[`**EASL AIH Clinical Practice Guidelines 2025**: aims at **complete biochemical response (normal ALT and IgG) by 6 months**, keeps budesonide for non-cirrhotics and refines second-line options. [EASL AIH 2025]`,`**Elafibranor (June 2024) and seladelpar (August 2024)** received FDA accelerated approval for PBC with inadequate response or intolerance to UDCA. [FDA 2024]`,`**EASL PBC Clinical Practice Guidelines 2025**: earlier second-line therapy, with the aim of **normal ALP** (and bilirubin 0.6 x ULN or less) rather than the older Paris/Toronto thresholds. [EASL PBC 2025]`,`**Obeticholic acid**: EMA revoked conditional authorisation in 2024 and it was withdrawn from the US market in 2025 after failure to confirm benefit; it was already contraindicated in decompensated cirrhosis. [EMA 2024 / FDA 2025]`,`**AASLD PSC Practice Guidance 2022 and EASL PSC 2022**: MRI-based cholangiocarcinoma surveillance, annual colonoscopy with IBD, avoid high-dose UDCA. [AASLD PSC 2022]`,`**IBAT inhibitors (linerixibat)** are under evaluation for cholestatic itch in PBC; maralixibat/odevixibat are licensed for paediatric cholestasis. [EASL PBC 2025]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Viva: **'Young woman, ALT 600, IgG high, ANA positive'** - think AIH, exclude Wilson and viral hepatitis, biopsy, then prednisolone plus azathioprine. [EASL AIH 2025]`,`Viva: **'Middle-aged woman with itch and raised ALP'** - PBC; AMA, ultrasound to exclude obstruction, UDCA 13-15 mg/kg. [EASL PBC 2025]`,`Viva: **'UC patient with raised ALP'** - PSC; MRCP, IgG4, colonoscopy surveillance. [AASLD PSC 2022]`,`Common mistake: **budesonide in cirrhotic AIH** - ineffective and risky because of shunting. [EASL AIH 2025]`,`Common mistake: **starting azathioprine without TPMT/CBC** monitoring. [EASL AIH 2025]`,`Common mistake: **high-dose UDCA in PSC** - increases serious adverse events. [AASLD PSC 2022]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in autoimmune liver disease`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Prednisolone (AIH)`,`0.5-1 mg/kg/day orally (30-60 mg)`,`Taper over 6-12 weeks to 5-10 mg; stop if azathioprine maintenance adequate`,`Diabetes, osteoporosis, cataract, infection, adrenal suppression`],[`Azathioprine (AIH)`,`50 mg/day from week 2, up to 1-2 mg/kg/day orally`,`At least 3 years and 2 years after remission; often lifelong`,`Myelosuppression (TPMT/NUDT15), pancreatitis, hepatotoxicity, skin cancer; avoid allopurinol`],[`Budesonide (AIH)`,`3 mg orally three times daily (9 mg/day)`,`Induction, taper to 6 mg`,`Not in cirrhosis or portosystemic shunting`],[`Mycophenolate mofetil`,`500 mg BD, up to 1 g BD orally`,`Second line, long-term`,`Teratogenic (stop 6 weeks before conception), diarrhoea, cytopenia`],[`Ursodeoxycholic acid (PBC)`,`13-15 mg/kg/day orally`,`Lifelong; assess at 12 months`,`Weight gain, loose stools; high dose (28-30 mg/kg) harmful in PSC`],[`Elafibranor (PBC)`,`80 mg orally once daily`,`Add to UDCA if inadequate response`,`Myalgia, rhabdomyolysis with statins, fractures; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Seladelpar (PBC)`,`10 mg orally once daily`,`Add to UDCA if inadequate response`,`Headache, abdominal pain; avoid in decompensated cirrhosis`],[`Bezafibrate (PBC, off-label)`,`400 mg/day orally`,`Add to UDCA`,`Myalgia, creatinine rise; caution in CKD`],[`Obeticholic acid (PBC)`,`5 mg OD, increase to 10 mg at 6 months`,`Second line where still available`,`Severe pruritus; contraindicated in decompensated cirrhosis; withdrawn US 2025`],[`Cholestyramine / rifampicin (itch)`,`4-16 g/day / 150-300 mg/day orally`,`Long-term as needed`,`Separate other drugs 4 hours / hepatotoxicity, orange urine`]]},{heading:`AIH vs PBC vs PSC at a glance`,columns:[`Feature`,`Autoimmune hepatitis`,`Primary biliary cholangitis`,`Primary sclerosing cholangitis`],rows:[[`Typical patient`,`Woman, any age (type 2 in children)`,`Woman 40-60 years`,`Man 30-40 years with UC`],[`LFT pattern`,`Hepatitic (ALT/AST high)`,`Cholestatic (ALP, GGT high)`,`Cholestatic`],[`Antibody`,`ANA, ASMA, anti-LKM1, anti-SLA`,`AMA (95%), anti-sp100, anti-gp210`,`p-ANCA (non-specific)`],[`Immunoglobulin`,`IgG high`,`IgM high`,`IgG4 normal (exclude IgG4-SC)`],[`Key test`,`Liver biopsy - interface hepatitis`,`AMA + ALP; biopsy rarely`,`MRCP - beaded ducts`],[`Treatment`,`Prednisolone + azathioprine`,`UDCA, then PPAR agonist`,`No proven drug; ERCP for strictures; transplant`],[`Cancer risk`,`HCC in cirrhosis`,`HCC in cirrhosis`,`Cholangiocarcinoma, colorectal, gallbladder`]]},{heading:`Simplified IAIHG diagnostic score for AIH (2008)`,columns:[`Variable`,`Finding`,`Points`],rows:[[`ANA or ASMA`,`1:40`,`1`],[`ANA or ASMA / anti-LKM1 / anti-SLA`,`1:80 or more / 1:40 or more / positive`,`2 (maximum 2 for all antibodies)`],[`IgG`,`Above ULN / above 1.1 x ULN`,`1 / 2`],[`Liver histology`,`Compatible / typical`,`1 / 2`],[`Absence of viral hepatitis`,`Yes`,`2`],[`Total`,`6 = probable; 7 or more = definite AIH`,`-`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of an autoimmune-looking liver profile`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating feature`,`Test`],rows:[[`Drug-induced AIH-like injury`,`Nitrofurantoin, minocycline, statin; no relapse after steroids stop`,`Drug history, RUCAM`],[`Wilson disease`,`Age under 40, haemolysis, neuro-psychiatric signs`,`Ceruloplasmin, 24-h urine copper, KF ring`],[`Chronic viral hepatitis`,`Risk factors; low-titre ANA possible`,`HBsAg, anti-HCV/RNA, HEV RNA if immunosuppressed`],[`IgG4-related sclerosing cholangitis`,`Older man, autoimmune pancreatitis, steroid-responsive`,`Serum IgG4, imaging of pancreas`],[`Extrahepatic biliary obstruction`,`Dilated ducts, pain, fever`,`Ultrasound, MRCP`],[`MASLD with autoantibodies`,`Metabolic risk, low-titre ANA, normal IgG`,`Ultrasound, biopsy if doubt`]]},{heading:`Monitoring schedule`,columns:[`Disease`,`What to check`,`How often`],rows:[[`AIH on azathioprine`,`CBC, LFT, IgG`,`CBC weekly x 4, then 3-monthly; LFT 2-weekly in induction, then 3-6 monthly`],[`PBC on UDCA`,`LFT (ALP, bilirubin), TSH, lipids, elastography`,`LFT 3-6 monthly; response at 12 months; elastography yearly`],[`PSC`,`MRCP/ultrasound with or without CA 19-9`,`Every 6-12 months`],[`PSC with IBD`,`Colonoscopy with biopsies`,`Yearly from diagnosis`],[`All on steroids or cholestasis`,`DEXA, vitamin D`,`Every 1-3 years`],[`Any cirrhosis`,`Ultrasound with or without AFP; endoscopy per Baveno VII`,`6-monthly`]]}],redFlags:[`AIH with INR 1.5 or more, rising bilirubin or any encephalopathy - acute severe AIH, admit and refer to a transplant centre.`,`Fever, rigors and jaundice in PSC - acute cholangitis needing IV antibiotics and ERCP.`,`New dominant stricture, weight loss or rising CA 19-9 in PSC - suspect cholangiocarcinoma.`,`Fever, sore throat or mouth ulcers on azathioprine - check CBC for neutropenia.`,`Severe epigastric pain on azathioprine - drug-induced pancreatitis.`,`Intractable pruritus, jaundice (bilirubin rising) or decompensation in PBC - transplant referral.`,`Gallbladder polyp 8 mm or more in PSC - surgical referral for cholecystectomy.`],pearls:[`**AIH = hepatitic + IgG; PBC = cholestatic + AMA + IgM; PSC = cholestatic + MRCP beading + UC.**`,`Simplified IAIHG: **6 probable, 7 definite**.`,`**Emperipolesis and rosettes** - histological hallmarks of AIH.`,`**Budesonide only if no cirrhosis.**`,`**UDCA 13-15 mg/kg for PBC; never high-dose (28-30 mg/kg) in PSC.**`,`**Elafibranor and seladelpar** - PPAR agonists approved 2024 for PBC.`,`**Every new PSC gets a colonoscopy; every UC with raised ALP gets an MRCP.**`,`Exclude **IgG4 cholangitis** - it melts with steroids, PSC does not.`,`**Check TPMT before azathioprine.**`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-autoimmune-liver-q1`,stem:`A 48-year-old woman has generalised itching for 6 months. ALP is 3 times ULN, GGT raised, ALT mildly raised, bilirubin normal and ultrasound shows a normal biliary tree. Which test best confirms the likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Anti-smooth muscle antibody`,`Serum IgG4`,`Anti-mitochondrial antibody`,`MRCP`],answer:2,explanation:`Cholestatic LFTs with itch in a middle-aged woman and non-dilated ducts suggest primary biliary cholangitis, and AMA is positive in about 95%; ALP plus AMA is diagnostic without biopsy. ASMA supports autoimmune hepatitis; IgG4 is for IgG4-related cholangitis; MRCP is the key test for PSC, which is commoner in men with colitis.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-autoimmune-liver-q2`,stem:`A 30-year-old woman with biopsy-proven autoimmune hepatitis and compensated cirrhosis is to start treatment. Which induction regimen is most appropriate?`,options:[`Prednisolone with azathioprine added after 2 weeks`,`Budesonide 9 mg/day with azathioprine`,`Ursodeoxycholic acid 13-15 mg/kg/day`,`Mycophenolate mofetil alone as first line`],answer:0,explanation:`Prednisolone 0.5-1 mg/kg with azathioprine added after about 2 weeks is standard induction. Budesonide depends on first-pass hepatic metabolism, so in cirrhosis with portosystemic shunting it is less effective and causes systemic steroid effects. UDCA treats PBC, not AIH, and mycophenolate is second line.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-autoimmune-liver-q3`,stem:`A 28-year-old man with ulcerative colitis has ALP twice ULN. MRCP shows multifocal strictures with beading of intra- and extrahepatic ducts. Which statement about his care is correct?`,options:[`High-dose UDCA 28-30 mg/kg/day slows progression`,`Colonoscopy surveillance is needed yearly from diagnosis`,`Prednisolone is first-line therapy`,`Cholangiocarcinoma surveillance is not needed before cirrhosis`],answer:1,explanation:`PSC with IBD carries a much higher colorectal cancer risk than UC alone, so annual colonoscopy starts at PSC diagnosis. High-dose UDCA increased serious events in trials, steroids help IgG4 cholangitis but not PSC, and cholangiocarcinoma can occur early, so imaging surveillance every 6-12 months is advised whatever the fibrosis stage.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-autoimmune-liver-q4`,stem:`A woman with PBC has taken UDCA 15 mg/kg/day for 12 months. ALP remains 2.2 times ULN and bilirubin is normal; she has no cirrhosis. What is the best next step?`,options:[`Stop UDCA and start prednisolone`,`Double the UDCA dose to 30 mg/kg/day`,`Continue UDCA alone and recheck in 2 years`,`Add a PPAR agonist such as elafibranor or seladelpar`],answer:3,explanation:`ALP above 1.67 x ULN at 12 months is an inadequate UDCA response, which predicts progression; current guidance adds second-line therapy - elafibranor, seladelpar or bezafibrate - while continuing UDCA. Stopping UDCA loses its benefit, doubling the dose adds nothing, steroids are not PBC treatment, and waiting 2 years accepts preventable progression.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-autoimmune-liver-q5`,stem:`Before starting azathioprine for autoimmune hepatitis, which test helps most to prevent life-threatening toxicity?`,options:[`Serum amylase`,`Thiopurine methyltransferase (TPMT) activity`,`HLA-DR3 typing`,`Anti-LKM1 antibody`],answer:1,explanation:`Patients with absent TPMT (and NUDT15 variants, common in Asians) accumulate thioguanine nucleotides and develop severe myelosuppression, so TPMT is checked and CBC monitored. Amylase is checked only if pancreatitis is suspected; HLA-DR3 typing and anti-LKM1 relate to diagnosis or subtype, not to drug toxicity.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-autoimmune-liver-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Describe the clinical features, diagnosis and management of autoimmune hepatitis. How will you differentiate it from primary biliary cholangitis and primary sclerosing cholangitis?`,openingLines:[`Autoimmune hepatitis is a chronic immune-mediated hepatitis with interface hepatitis, raised IgG and autoantibodies (ANA/ASMA or anti-LKM1), responding to immunosuppression.`,`It mainly affects women, may present as acute severe hepatitis or silent cirrhosis, and is controlled with prednisolone plus azathioprine.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition, epidemiology and types`,points:[`Female:male 4:1; bimodal age; HLA-DR3/DR4.`,`Type 1 - ANA/ASMA (adults); type 2 - anti-LKM1/anti-LC1 (children).`,`Triggers - viruses, drugs (nitrofurantoin, minocycline).`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Asymptomatic raised ALT, fatigue/arthralgia, acute hepatitis, or acute severe/fulminant presentation.`,`One-third cirrhotic at diagnosis; associated thyroiditis, diabetes, coeliac, UC.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Raised ALT/AST, IgG; autoantibodies; exclude viral, Wilson, DILI.`,`Biopsy - interface hepatitis, plasma cells, rosettes, emperipolesis.`,`Simplified IAIHG score - 6 probable, 7 or more definite.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Prednisolone 0.5-1 mg/kg/day; azathioprine from week 2 at 1-2 mg/kg (TPMT first).`,`Budesonide 9 mg/day if no cirrhosis.`,`Target normal ALT and IgG by 6 months (EASL 2025); maintenance at least 3 years.`,`Second line - mycophenolate, tacrolimus; acute severe AIH - IV steroids and transplant referral.`]},{heading:`Monitoring and special groups`,points:[`CBC and LFT schedule; bone protection; vaccinate before immunosuppression.`,`Pregnancy - continue prednisolone and azathioprine; post-partum flare.`]},{heading:`Differentiation from PBC and PSC`,points:[`PBC - cholestatic, AMA, IgM, middle-aged women, UDCA.`,`PSC - cholestatic, MRCP beading, UC, men, cholangiocarcinoma risk, no proven drug.`]}],mustDraw:[`Comparison table AIH vs PBC vs PSC (patient, LFT pattern, antibody, Ig, key test, treatment, cancer risk)`,`Simplified IAIHG score table`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition, types, clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis and score`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment and monitoring`,marks:3},{part:`Differentiation table`,marks:3}],keywords:[`interface hepatitis`,`IgG`,`ANA`,`anti-LKM1`,`IAIHG`,`prednisolone`,`azathioprine`,`budesonide`,`AMA`,`MRCP`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-autoimmune-liver-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on primary biliary cholangitis, including newer therapies.`,openingLines:[`Primary biliary cholangitis is a chronic autoimmune destruction of small intrahepatic bile ducts, mostly in women aged 40-60, marked by AMA and raised ALP.`,`UDCA 13-15 mg/kg/day is first line; PPAR agonists elafibranor and seladelpar (2024) are second line.`],answer:[{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Fatigue, pruritus, sicca, xanthelasma; associated Sjogren, thyroid disease, CREST.`,`Osteoporosis and fat-soluble vitamin deficiency.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`ALP 1.5 x ULN or more plus AMA over 1:40 (or anti-sp100/gp210).`,`Ultrasound to exclude obstruction; biopsy rarely.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`UDCA 13-15 mg/kg/day lifelong; response assessed at 12 months.`,`Second line: elafibranor 80 mg, seladelpar 10 mg, bezafibrate 400 mg; obeticholic acid withdrawn in many markets.`,`Itch: cholestyramine, rifampicin, naltrexone, sertraline.`]},{heading:`Follow-up`,points:[`LFT 3-6 monthly, DEXA, TSH; HCC surveillance in cirrhosis; transplant for liver failure or intractable itch.`]}],mustDraw:[`PBC treatment ladder: UDCA, response check at 12 months, second-line agent, transplant`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and features`,marks:1.5},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:1},{part:`Treatment incl. new drugs`,marks:2},{part:`Follow-up`,marks:.5}],keywords:[`AMA`,`ALP`,`UDCA`,`elafibranor`,`seladelpar`,`obeticholic acid`,`pruritus`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Autoimmune hepatitis; Primary biliary cholangitis; Primary sclerosing cholangitis`,`EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines on the management of autoimmune hepatitis, 2025`,`EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines on the management of primary biliary cholangitis, 2025`,`AASLD Practice Guidance on primary sclerosing cholangitis and cholangiocarcinoma, 2022`,`EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines on sclerosing cholangitis, 2022`,`International Autoimmune Hepatitis Group simplified diagnostic criteria (Hennes et al.), 2008`,`US FDA accelerated approvals: elafibranor (June 2024) and seladelpar (August 2024) for PBC`,`National List of Essential Medicines (NLEM), MoHFW, 2022`]}),a.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-wilson-haemochromatosis`,title:`Wilson disease, haemochromatosis and alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`The three **inherited metabolic liver diseases** a physician must not miss are **Wilson disease** (autosomal recessive **ATP7B** defect causing **copper overload** of liver and brain - **low ceruloplasmin, Kayser-Fleischer rings, high 24-hour urine copper**, diagnosed by the **Leipzig score** and treated lifelong with **penicillamine, trientine or zinc**), **hereditary haemochromatosis** (**HFE C282Y** hepcidin deficiency causing **iron overload with TSAT over 45% and raised ferritin**, treated by **venesection**) and **alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency** (**PiZZ** misfolded protein causing **neonatal cholestasis, cirrhosis and basal panacinar emphysema**).`,keywords:[`Wilson disease`,`hepatolenticular degeneration`,`ATP7B`,`Kayser-Fleischer ring`,`ceruloplasmin`,`Leipzig score`,`penicillamine`,`trientine`,`zinc acetate`,`haemochromatosis`,`hemochromatosis`,`HFE C282Y`,`hepcidin`,`transferrin saturation`,`ferritin`,`phlebotomy`,`venesection`,`deferasirox`,`alpha-1 antitrypsin`,`PiZZ`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Wilson disease** is an **autosomal recessive** disorder of the **ATP7B copper-transporting ATPase (chromosome 13q14)** that impairs biliary copper excretion, causing copper accumulation in liver, brain, cornea and kidney. [Harrison 22e]`,`Wilson disease affects about **1 in 30,000** worldwide with a carrier rate of about **1 in 90**; it is relatively more frequent where consanguinity is common, and in Indian paediatric series it is a leading metabolic cause of chronic liver disease and acute liver failure in children over 5. [EASL-ERN Wilson 2025]`,`**Hereditary haemochromatosis (HH)** is inherited **iron overload from inappropriately low hepcidin**; type 1 (**HFE C282Y homozygosity**) occurs in about **1 in 200-300 northern Europeans**. [EASL Haemochromatosis 2022]`,`**HFE C282Y is very rare in Indians**; iron overload in India is mainly **secondary** - transfusion in **thalassaemia major (India has about 1 lakh patients, the world's largest number)**, sickle cell disease and chronic haemolysis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency (AATD)** is a **codominant** disorder of the **SERPINA1 gene (chromosome 14)**; the severe **PiZZ** genotype affects about **1 in 2000-5000 Europeans** and is uncommon in South Asians. [ATS/ERS AATD]`,`AATD is the commonest **genetic cause of liver disease in children** in Western series and causes **early-onset emphysema** in adults - a single gene with lung and liver faces. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and pathophysiology`,points:[`**ATP7B** normally loads copper onto **apoceruloplasmin** and excretes excess copper into bile; when it fails, copper builds up in hepatocytes and **unbound ceruloplasmin is degraded - hence low serum ceruloplasmin**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Hepatocyte copper causes **oxidative injury, steatosis, hepatitis and cirrhosis**; when hepatocytes die, free copper floods the blood, causing **Coombs-negative haemolysis** and spill-over to brain (**basal ganglia - lenticular nuclei**), cornea and renal tubules. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Kayser-Fleischer ring** is copper deposited in **Descemet membrane** of the cornea; **sunflower cataract** is copper in the lens capsule. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hepcidin**, made in the liver, blocks the iron exporter **ferroportin** on enterocytes and macrophages; in HH low hepcidin lets iron be absorbed unchecked, saturating transferrin and depositing **non-transferrin-bound iron** in liver, pancreas, heart, pituitary and joints. [EASL Haemochromatosis 2022]`,`Non-HFE HH: **haemojuvelin and hepcidin (HAMP) mutations - juvenile HH (type 2)** with cardiac and endocrine failure before 30; **TfR2 (type 3)**; **ferroportin disease (type 4)** with high ferritin but often normal TSAT. [EASL Haemochromatosis 2022]`,`In AATD the **Z protein misfolds and polymerises in the hepatocyte endoplasmic reticulum** (gain-of-function liver toxicity - **PAS-positive, diastase-resistant globules**), while low circulating AAT leaves lung elastin unprotected from neutrophil elastase (loss-of-function lung damage). [Harrison 22e]`,`This explains why **augmentation therapy helps the lung but not the liver**, and why **null genotypes** (no protein made) cause emphysema without liver disease. [ATS/ERS AATD]`]},{heading:`Classification and diagnostic scores`,points:[`Wilson phenotypes: **hepatic (H1 acute, H2 chronic), neurological/psychiatric (N1 with liver disease, N2 without), and presymptomatic** (found by sibling screening). [Leipzig 2001]`,`**Leipzig score**: KF rings (2), neuro symptoms (mild 1, severe 2), ceruloplasmin (0.1-0.2 g/L = 1, under 0.1 = 2), Coombs-negative haemolysis (1), liver copper (over 250 ug/g = 2), urine copper (1-2 x ULN = 1, over 2 x ULN = 2), ATP7B mutations (two = 4, one = 1); **4 or more = diagnosis established, 3 = probable, 2 or less = unlikely**. [EASL-ERN Wilson 2025]`,`**Wilson acute liver failure** is suggested by **ALP:bilirubin ratio under 4 and AST:ALT ratio over 2.2**, with haemolysis and relatively modest transaminases. [AASLD Wilson 2022]`,`**New Wilson Index (King's)** - bilirubin, INR, AST, WBC, albumin; **score 11 or more predicts death without transplant**. [AASLD Wilson 2022]`,`HH stages (EASL): **genetic predisposition only, biochemical iron overload (TSAT over 45%, ferritin raised), and iron overload with organ damage**. [EASL Haemochromatosis 2022]`,`HH genotypes: **C282Y/C282Y** is the main disease genotype (penetrance for organ disease about 10-25% in men, lower in women); C282Y/H63D and H63D homozygosity rarely cause overload without co-factors. [EASL Haemochromatosis 2022]`,`AATD protease inhibitor (Pi) phenotypes: **MM normal; MZ and SZ intermediate; ZZ severe deficiency**; null-null no protein. [ATS/ERS AATD]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Wilson **hepatic presentation peaks at 5-35 years**: asymptomatic raised ALT, fatty liver, chronic hepatitis, cirrhosis, or **acute liver failure with haemolysis** (often female adolescents). [EASL-ERN Wilson 2025]`,`Wilson **neuropsychiatric presentation peaks in the second-third decades**: tremor (wing-beating), **dysarthria, drooling, dystonia, clumsy handwriting, parkinsonism**, and personality change, depression or declining school performance. [Harrison 22e]`,`Other Wilson clues: **unexplained haemolytic anaemia, renal tubular acidosis/Fanconi, early osteoarthritis, amenorrhoea or recurrent miscarriage**, and a sibling with liver disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`HH is usually found from **raised ferritin or TSAT on routine tests**; symptoms are **fatigue, arthralgia (2nd and 3rd MCP joints), erectile dysfunction/amenorrhoea** and later diabetes. [EASL Haemochromatosis 2022]`,`Late HH: the triad of **cirrhosis, diabetes and skin pigmentation ('bronze diabetes')**, cardiomyopathy with arrhythmias, and hypogonadotropic hypogonadism. [Harrison 22e]`,`AATD: **prolonged neonatal jaundice (cholestasis)** in infants; **dyspnoea and emphysema before 45, often in a light or never smoker**; chronic liver disease in adults; rarely **necrotising panniculitis** and c-ANCA vasculitis. [ATS/ERS AATD]`,`Take a **family history** (siblings for Wilson, first-degree relatives for HH and AATD) and a transfusion, alcohol and iron/vitamin C supplement history. [EASL Haemochromatosis 2022]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Kayser-Fleischer ring** - golden-brown ring at the corneal limbus, starting at 12 and 6 o'clock; confirm on **slit lamp**; present in about **95% of neurological** but only **40-50% of hepatic** Wilson patients. [EASL-ERN Wilson 2025]`,`Wilson neuro signs: **wing-beating tremor, dystonia, 'risus sardonicus', dysarthria, rigidity** and ataxia; psychiatric changes may come first. [Harrison 22e]`,`Wilson: jaundice, hepatosplenomegaly, ascites and **pallor from haemolysis**; **azure lunulae** of the nails are a rare sign. [Harrison 22e]`,`HH: **slate-grey/bronze skin pigmentation**, hepatomegaly, testicular atrophy, loss of body hair, and **'iron fist'** - tender swollen 2nd and 3rd MCP joints. [EASL Haemochromatosis 2022]`,`HH heart: signs of **dilated cardiomyopathy** - raised JVP, S3, arrhythmia; a key cause of death in juvenile HH. [Harrison 22e]`,`AATD: **barrel chest, hyperinflation and basal emphysema signs**, clubbing absent; jaundiced infant with hepatomegaly; tender red nodules (panniculitis). [ATS/ERS AATD]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Low ceruloplasmin without Wilson**: heterozygous carriers (about 20%), protein-losing states, malnutrition, severe liver failure of any cause, aceruloplasminaemia, Menkes disease. [AASLD Wilson 2022]`,`**Normal ceruloplasmin does not exclude Wilson** - it is an acute-phase reactant and rises with inflammation, pregnancy and oestrogens. [EASL-ERN Wilson 2025]`,`**KF-like rings** occur in prolonged **cholestasis (PBC, neonatal cholestasis)**; raised urine copper also occurs in cholestasis and AIH. [AASLD Wilson 2022]`,`Wilson vs **autoimmune hepatitis** in a young person: both may have raised IgG; check ceruloplasmin and urine copper in every AIH under 40. [EASL AIH 2025]`,`**Raised ferritin is usually not haemochromatosis**: inflammation, **MASLD/metabolic syndrome (dysmetabolic hyperferritinaemia), alcohol, CKD, malignancy, Still disease** - TSAT is usually normal in these. [EASL Haemochromatosis 2022]`,`**Secondary iron overload**: thalassaemia, sideroblastic and dyserythropoietic anaemia, repeated transfusion, porphyria cutanea tarda; ferroportin disease has high ferritin with normal TSAT. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Early emphysema** differential: smoking COPD (upper-lobe centrilobular, older), AATD (basal panacinar, younger) - test AAT in every COPD patient once. [ATS/ERS AATD]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Wilson first line**: **serum ceruloplasmin (under 20 mg/dL suggestive, under 10 strongly suggestive)**, **24-hour urine copper (over 100 ug/day diagnostic; over 40 ug/day suspicious - AASLD 2022)**, slit-lamp for KF ring, CBC and Coombs test. [AASLD Wilson 2022]`,`Collect 24-hour urine in **copper-free (acid-washed or plastic) containers**; the penicillamine challenge test is now rarely used. [EASL-ERN Wilson 2025]`,`**Hepatic copper over 250 ug/g dry weight** (biopsy) is the best biochemical evidence; **ATP7B genetic testing** confirms and screens siblings. [EASL-ERN Wilson 2025]`,`**Relative exchangeable copper (REC) over 18.5%** is a newer serum test with high accuracy where available. [EASL-ERN Wilson 2025]`,`**MRI brain** in neurological Wilson: T2 hyperintensity of putamen, thalamus and brainstem - the **'face of the giant panda' sign** in the midbrain. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HH**: fasting **TSAT over 45% and ferritin over 200 ug/L (women) or 300 ug/L (men)** - then **HFE genotyping**; if C282Y homozygous with **ferritin over 1000 ug/L or raised ALT**, assess fibrosis (elastography/biopsy). [EASL Haemochromatosis 2022]`,`**MRI liver iron (R2/T2*)** quantifies iron non-invasively; **cardiac T2* under 20 ms** indicates myocardial iron in transfusional overload. [EASL Haemochromatosis 2022]`,`**AATD**: **serum AAT level** (low if under about 1.1 g/L; severe deficiency under 0.5 g/L/11 uM), then **phenotype (isoelectric focusing) or genotype**; liver biopsy shows PAS-positive diastase-resistant globules. [ATS/ERS AATD]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD`,points:[`Wilson treatment is **lifelong**: **chelator (penicillamine or trientine) for symptomatic disease, zinc for maintenance or presymptomatic disease** and as an option in neurological disease. [EASL-ERN Wilson 2025]`,`**D-penicillamine**: start **250-500 mg/day and increase by 250 mg every 4-7 days to 750-1500 mg/day in 2-4 divided doses** (children 20 mg/kg/day), 1 hour before or 2 hours after meals, with **pyridoxine 25-50 mg/day**; maintenance 750-1000 mg/day. [AASLD Wilson 2022]`,`**Trientine** is better tolerated and preferred by many as first line, especially if penicillamine intolerant or neurological disease: about **900-1500 mg/day (dihydrochloride salt) in 2-3 doses**; dose depends on the salt. [EASL-ERN Wilson 2025]`,`**Zinc acetate/sulphate**: **50 mg elemental zinc three times a day** (25 mg TDS in children under 50 kg), away from food; it induces enterocyte metallothionein that traps dietary copper. [AASLD Wilson 2022]`,`**Diet**: avoid high-copper foods in the first year - **shellfish, liver, nuts, chocolate, mushrooms**; check copper content of drinking water if from copper pipes. [AASLD Wilson 2022]`,`**Haemochromatosis**: **venesection of 400-500 mL (one unit) weekly or fortnightly** as tolerated, with Hb checked before each, until **ferritin about 50-100 ug/L**; then maintenance venesection every 1-4 months to keep **ferritin under 100**. [EASL Haemochromatosis 2022]`,`HH advice: **no iron or vitamin C supplements**, limit alcohol, **avoid raw shellfish (Vibrio vulnificus)**; strict dietary iron restriction is not needed. [EASL Haemochromatosis 2022]`,`**Transfusional overload (thalassaemia)**: chelate when ferritin over 1000 ug/L or after 10-20 transfusions - **deferasirox 14-28 mg/kg/day (film-coated) orally**, deferiprone 75-100 mg/kg/day in 3 doses, or desferrioxamine 20-60 mg/kg SC over 8-12 hours, 5-7 nights a week. [TIF Thalassaemia 2021]`,`**AATD**: **stop smoking absolutely**, vaccinate (influenza, pneumococcal, hepatitis A/B), treat COPD per GOLD; **IV augmentation (pooled human AAT 60 mg/kg weekly)** for emphysema with severe deficiency - not available in public sector in India. [GOLD 2025]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Wilsonian acute liver failure** is almost uniformly fatal without **liver transplantation** - transfer early to a transplant centre; transplant corrects the defect. [AASLD Wilson 2022]`,`Bridging in Wilson ALF: **plasma exchange, albumin dialysis (MARS) or continuous renal replacement** lower serum copper and haemolysis; chelators act too slowly. [EASL-ERN Wilson 2025]`,`**Neurological worsening after starting penicillamine (10-50%)** - reduce dose, switch to trientine or zinc, and admit if severe dystonia or dysphagia. [EASL-ERN Wilson 2025]`,`**Severe HH with cardiomyopathy or arrhythmia** - admit; combine venesection (if Hb allows) with chelation, and treat heart failure. [EASL Haemochromatosis 2022]`,`Decompensated cirrhosis from any of the three is managed as cirrhosis (see the Cirrhosis topic); transplant is curative for Wilson and AATD liver disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`Neonatal cholestasis from AATD: rule out **biliary atresia urgently** (needs Kasai before 60 days), supplement fat-soluble vitamins and MCT feeds. [ESPGHAN Neonatal Cholestasis 2017]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy in Wilson**: **never stop treatment** (risk of fulminant failure); reduce chelator dose by **25-50% in the last trimester** for wound healing; zinc unchanged. [EASL-ERN Wilson 2025]`,`Breastfeeding on penicillamine or trientine is generally discouraged in some guidance; zinc is considered compatible - individualise. [AASLD Wilson 2022]`,`**Children**: screen siblings from age 1-3 years; presymptomatic children are usually treated with **zinc**. [EASL-ERN Wilson 2025]`,`**Women with HH** present 10-20 years later than men because of menstrual and pregnancy iron losses. [EASL Haemochromatosis 2022]`,`**Diabetes in HH** may improve partly with venesection; HbA1c is falsely low during venesection - use glucose readings. [EASL Haemochromatosis 2022]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: Wilson can be suspected on **ceruloplasmin, 24-hour urine copper, slit lamp and Coombs** without biopsy or genetics; penicillamine and zinc are cheap and widely available in India. [AASLD Wilson 2022]`,`**Venesected blood** from HH patients can be used for donation in many countries if donor criteria are met. [EASL Haemochromatosis 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Untreated Wilson is **fatal**; with adherent lifelong therapy survival approaches normal, and neurological signs improve over **6-24 months** but may persist. [EASL-ERN Wilson 2025]`,`**Stopping Wilson treatment can cause fulminant liver failure** within months - non-adherence is the commonest cause of deterioration. [AASLD Wilson 2022]`,`**Penicillamine toxicity**: early fever, rash, lymphadenopathy; later **proteinuria/nephrotic syndrome, lupus-like syndrome, marrow suppression, myasthenia, elastosis perforans serpiginosa**. [AASLD Wilson 2022]`,`**Over-treatment** causes copper deficiency - anaemia, neutropenia and myeloneuropathy. [EASL-ERN Wilson 2025]`,`HH treated **before cirrhosis or diabetes gives normal life expectancy**; with cirrhosis, **HCC risk is about 20-fold** and continues after iron depletion. [EASL Haemochromatosis 2022]`,`Venesection improves fatigue, skin pigment, liver tests and cardiac function, but **not arthropathy, hypogonadism or established cirrhosis**. [EASL Haemochromatosis 2022]`,`AATD PiZZ: about **10% of infants have clinically significant liver disease**; adults risk cirrhosis and HCC; emphysema is the main cause of death in smokers. [ATS/ERS AATD]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Refer all suspected cases to a **hepatologist/metabolic centre**; neurological Wilson also to neurology. [EASL-ERN Wilson 2025]`,`Wilson monitoring: **LFT, CBC, urinalysis (protein) every 3-6 months**, and **24-hour urine copper** - **200-500 ug/day on chelators**, **under 75 ug/day on zinc** (24 h after a missed dose, a level under 100 on chelator suggests over-treatment or non-adherence). [AASLD Wilson 2022]`,`**Non-ceruloplasmin-bound copper** should fall below about 15 ug/dL on adequate therapy; KF rings fade with treatment and reappear with non-adherence. [AASLD Wilson 2022]`,`HH: **Hb before each venesection** (do not bleed if Hb under 12 g/dL), **ferritin every 4-12 venesections** during induction and every 6-12 months in maintenance. [EASL Haemochromatosis 2022]`,`HH with **cirrhosis** (or ferritin over 1000 with fibrosis): **ultrasound every 6 months for HCC** for life. [EASL Haemochromatosis 2022]`,`AATD: **spirometry and LFT/elastography yearly**; HCC surveillance in cirrhosis. [ATS/ERS AATD]`]},{heading:`Prevention, family screening and counselling`,points:[`**Screen all first-degree relatives** of a Wilson patient (siblings have a **25% risk**) with ceruloplasmin, urine copper, slit lamp, LFT and **ATP7B mutation analysis**. [EASL-ERN Wilson 2025]`,`**HH**: screen adult first-degree relatives with **TSAT, ferritin and HFE genotype**. [EASL Haemochromatosis 2022]`,`**AATD**: test **siblings and children**, everyone with COPD, adult-onset asthma with fixed obstruction, unexplained liver disease, panniculitis or c-ANCA vasculitis. [ATS/ERS AATD]`,`Genetic counselling for consanguineous families (Wilson, autosomal recessive) and for carrier couples. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Thalassaemia prevention**: carrier screening (HbA2 by HPLC) and antenatal diagnosis under **NHM haemoglobinopathy guidelines** prevents transfusional iron overload. [NHM Haemoglobinopathies 2016]`,`Counsel that Wilson treatment is **lifelong and never to be stopped**, even in pregnancy. [AASLD Wilson 2022]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2026)`,points:[`**AASLD Wilson Practice Guidance 2022**: lower 24-hour urine copper threshold (**over 40 ug/day suspicious**), genetic testing earlier, New Wilson Index for transplant. [AASLD Wilson 2022]`,`**EASL-ERN Clinical Practice Guidelines on Wilson disease 2025**: endorses **relative exchangeable copper**, trientine as an alternative first line, and revisits the Leipzig score with genetics. [EASL-ERN Wilson 2025]`,`**Trientine tetrahydrochloride (Cuvrior)** licensed (EMA 2017, FDA 2022) - room-temperature stable, unlike the dihydrochloride. [FDA 2022]`,`**EASL Haemochromatosis CPG 2022**: diagnosis now rests on **C282Y homozygosity plus raised TSAT and ferritin**; ferritin target 50-100 ug/L. [EASL Haemochromatosis 2022]`,`**AATD liver disease**: RNA-interference drug **fazirsiran** reduced liver Z-AAT in phase 2 and is in phase 3 trials - not yet licensed. [Harrison 22e]`,`**GOLD 2025** continues to recommend **testing every COPD patient once for AATD**. [GOLD 2025]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Viva: **'Young person with liver disease and a movement disorder'** - Wilson until proven otherwise; examine eyes with a slit lamp. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: **'Coombs-negative haemolysis with acute liver failure and low ALP'** - Wilson ALF; ALP:bilirubin under 4. [AASLD Wilson 2022]`,`Viva: **'Diabetes, pigmentation and cirrhosis'** - haemochromatosis; first test is **fasting TSAT**. [EASL Haemochromatosis 2022]`,`Common mistake: **excluding Wilson with a normal ceruloplasmin** - it is normal in up to 15-50% of hepatic cases. [EASL-ERN Wilson 2025]`,`Common mistake: **ordering HFE testing for every raised ferritin** - most raised ferritin in India is inflammatory or metabolic; check TSAT first. [EASL Haemochromatosis 2022]`,`Common mistake: forgetting **pyridoxine with penicillamine** and **sibling screening** in Wilson. [AASLD Wilson 2022]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for Wilson disease and iron overload`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`D-penicillamine`,`Start 250-500 mg/day, raise by 250 mg every 4-7 days to 750-1500 mg/day orally in 2-4 doses; child 20 mg/kg/day; with pyridoxine 25-50 mg/day`,`Lifelong; maintenance 750-1000 mg/day`,`Hypersensitivity, neurological worsening, proteinuria, lupus-like, marrow suppression, EPS skin`],[`Trientine (dihydrochloride)`,`900-1500 mg/day orally in 2-3 doses, empty stomach (child 20 mg/kg/day)`,`Lifelong`,`Better tolerated; sideroblastic anaemia, colitis; neurological worsening less common`],[`Zinc acetate/sulphate`,`50 mg elemental zinc orally TDS (25 mg TDS if under 50 kg), away from food`,`Lifelong maintenance or presymptomatic`,`Gastric irritation, raised amylase/lipase; slow onset`],[`Venesection (HH)`,`400-500 mL weekly or fortnightly; Hb 12 g/dL or more before each`,`Until ferritin 50-100 ug/L, then every 1-4 months`,`Hypotension, fatigue; avoid if severe anaemia or cardiac failure`],[`Deferasirox`,`14-28 mg/kg/day orally once daily (film-coated tablet)`,`Long-term; adjust to ferritin and LIC`,`Renal and hepatic toxicity, GI upset; check creatinine monthly`],[`Deferiprone`,`75-100 mg/kg/day orally in 3 doses`,`Long-term`,`Agranulocytosis (weekly ANC), arthropathy`],[`Desferrioxamine`,`20-60 mg/kg SC over 8-12 hours, 5-7 nights/week`,`Long-term`,`Ototoxicity, retinopathy, Yersinia infection, local reactions`],[`AAT augmentation`,`60 mg/kg IV weekly`,`Lifelong for emphysema`,`Expensive; no benefit to liver disease; IgA deficiency caution`]]},{heading:`Leipzig scoring system for Wilson disease`,columns:[`Feature`,`Finding`,`Score`],rows:[[`Kayser-Fleischer ring`,`Present / absent`,`2 / 0`],[`Neuropsychiatric symptoms or typical brain MRI`,`Severe / mild / absent`,`2 / 1 / 0`],[`Serum ceruloplasmin`,`Over 0.2 g/L / 0.1-0.2 g/L / under 0.1 g/L`,`0 / 1 / 2`],[`Coombs-negative haemolytic anaemia`,`Present / absent`,`1 / 0`],[`Liver copper (no cholestasis)`,`Over 250 ug/g / 50-250 ug/g / normal under 50`,`2 / 1 / -1`],[`Rhodanine-positive hepatocytes (if no copper measurement)`,`Present`,`1`],[`24-hour urinary copper (no acute hepatitis)`,`Over 2 x ULN / 1-2 x ULN / normal`,`2 / 1 / 0`],[`ATP7B mutations`,`Two / one / none`,`4 / 1 / 0`],[`Interpretation`,`4 or more established; 3 probable; 2 or less unlikely`,`-`]]},{heading:`Wilson vs haemochromatosis vs AATD`,columns:[`Feature`,`Wilson disease`,`Hereditary haemochromatosis`,`AAT deficiency`],rows:[[`Gene / inheritance`,`ATP7B, chromosome 13; autosomal recessive`,`HFE C282Y, chromosome 6; autosomal recessive`,`SERPINA1, chromosome 14; codominant`],[`Metal / protein`,`Copper excess`,`Iron excess (low hepcidin)`,`Misfolded Z-AAT polymers`],[`Age at presentation`,`5-35 years`,`Men 40-60; women later`,`Neonate (cholestasis), adult (emphysema, cirrhosis)`],[`Other organs`,`Brain (basal ganglia), cornea, kidney, RBC`,`Pancreas, heart, pituitary, joints, skin`,`Lung (basal panacinar emphysema), skin`],[`Key test`,`Low ceruloplasmin, high urine copper, KF ring`,`TSAT over 45%, ferritin, HFE genotype`,`Low serum AAT, Pi phenotype`],[`Treatment`,`Penicillamine, trientine, zinc; transplant`,`Venesection`,`Stop smoking, augmentation, transplant`]]},{heading:`Investigation cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`Cut-off`,`Interpretation`],rows:[[`Serum ceruloplasmin`,`Under 20 mg/dL (under 10 strongly)`,`Suggests Wilson; normal does not exclude`],[`24-hour urine copper (untreated)`,`Over 100 ug/day (over 40 suspicious)`,`Supports Wilson`],[`24-hour urine copper on chelator / zinc`,`200-500 ug/day / under 75 ug/day`,`Adequate treatment and adherence`],[`Hepatic copper`,`Over 250 ug/g dry weight`,`Diagnostic of Wilson`],[`ALP:bilirubin and AST:ALT in ALF`,`Under 4 and over 2.2`,`Wilsonian acute liver failure`],[`Transferrin saturation (fasting)`,`Over 45%`,`Screen positive for HH`],[`Ferritin`,`Over 1000 ug/L in HH`,`Risk of cirrhosis - assess fibrosis`],[`Serum AAT`,`Under 0.5 g/L (11 uM)`,`Severe deficiency (usually ZZ)`]]}],redFlags:[`Jaundice, encephalopathy or INR rise with haemolysis in a young patient - Wilsonian acute liver failure, transfer to a transplant centre.`,`New or worsening dystonia, dysarthria or dysphagia after starting penicillamine - paradoxical neurological deterioration.`,`Wilson patient who has stopped therapy or is pregnant and stopped drugs - risk of fulminant failure.`,`Proteinuria, rash, fever or cytopenia on penicillamine - drug toxicity.`,`Breathlessness, arrhythmia or heart failure in haemochromatosis - iron cardiomyopathy.`,`Ferritin over 1000 ug/L with raised ALT or low platelets in HH - probable cirrhosis, needs fibrosis staging and HCC surveillance.`,`Neonatal jaundice beyond 2 weeks with pale stools - urgent cholestasis work-up including AATD and biliary atresia.`],pearls:[`**Wilson: think of it in anyone under 40 with unexplained liver disease, haemolysis or a movement disorder.**`,`**KF ring in 95% of neuro-Wilson, only half of hepatic Wilson.**`,`**Leipzig 4 or more = Wilson.**`,`**ALP low, bilirubin high, AST over ALT, Coombs-negative haemolysis = Wilson ALF.**`,`**Penicillamine always with pyridoxine; never stop Wilson treatment, even in pregnancy.**`,`**HH screen = fasting TSAT over 45%**; ferritin alone misleads.`,`**Iron fist** (2nd-3rd MCP arthropathy) is the classic HH joint.`,`**AATD: emphysema at the bases in a young non-smoker; PAS-positive diastase-resistant globules in the liver.**`,`**Augmentation helps lungs, not liver** - liver disease is gain-of-function.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-wilson-haemochromatosis-q1`,stem:`A 16-year-old girl presents with jaundice, confusion and INR 3.1. Hb is 7.8 g/dL with a negative Coombs test, ALP 40 U/L, bilirubin 18 mg/dL, AST 210 U/L and ALT 80 U/L. What is the most likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Acute hepatitis E with liver failure`,`Paracetamol-induced acute liver failure`,`Autoimmune hepatitis type 2`,`Wilsonian acute liver failure`],answer:3,explanation:`Coombs-negative haemolysis, a very low ALP with ALP:bilirubin ratio under 4 and AST:ALT over 2.2 with only modest transaminases is the classic pattern of Wilsonian acute liver failure, which needs urgent transplant referral. Paracetamol and viral ALF give transaminases in the thousands without haemolysis, and AIH lacks haemolysis with a low ALP.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-wilson-haemochromatosis-q2`,stem:`In the Leipzig scoring system for Wilson disease, which single finding scores 4 points?`,options:[`Kayser-Fleischer ring`,`Two disease-causing ATP7B mutations`,`Serum ceruloplasmin under 0.1 g/L`,`Hepatic copper over 250 ug/g dry weight`],answer:1,explanation:`Two ATP7B mutations score 4 and alone establish the diagnosis (4 or more). A KF ring, ceruloplasmin under 0.1 g/L and hepatic copper over 250 ug/g each score 2, so they need another feature to reach the diagnostic threshold.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-wilson-haemochromatosis-q3`,stem:`A 50-year-old man of European descent has fatigue, arthralgia of the 2nd and 3rd MCP joints and newly diagnosed diabetes. Which is the best initial screening test for haemochromatosis?`,options:[`Fasting transferrin saturation with serum ferritin`,`Liver biopsy with Perls stain`,`HFE genotyping alone`,`Serum iron alone`],answer:0,explanation:`Fasting transferrin saturation over 45% with raised ferritin is the screening step; HFE genotyping follows a raised TSAT. Ferritin alone is non-specific, serum iron alone varies with meals and time of day, and biopsy is now reserved for fibrosis staging or diagnostic doubt.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-wilson-haemochromatosis-q4`,stem:`A 24-year-old woman with Wilson disease well controlled on penicillamine becomes pregnant. What is the correct advice?`,options:[`Stop penicillamine for the first trimester to avoid teratogenicity`,`Switch to oral iron chelation for the pregnancy`,`Continue therapy throughout, reducing the chelator dose by 25-50% in the last trimester`,`Stop all treatment and restart after delivery`],answer:2,explanation:`Stopping Wilson treatment risks fulminant hepatic failure, which is far more dangerous than drug exposure, so therapy continues throughout pregnancy, with the chelator dose reduced by 25-50% in the third trimester to aid wound healing. Interrupting therapy for a trimester or until delivery is wrong, and iron chelators have no role in copper overload.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-wilson-haemochromatosis-q5`,stem:`A 38-year-old never-smoker has breathlessness with basal panacinar emphysema on CT and mildly raised liver enzymes. Liver biopsy would most likely show which feature?`,options:[`Copper-associated protein on rhodanine stain`,`Prussian blue-positive iron in periportal hepatocytes`,`Mallory-Denk bodies with neutrophils`,`PAS-positive, diastase-resistant globules in hepatocytes`],answer:3,explanation:`Basal panacinar emphysema in a young non-smoker with liver involvement suggests PiZZ alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, in which misfolded Z protein accumulates in the endoplasmic reticulum as PAS-positive, diastase-resistant globules. Rhodanine staining points to Wilson disease, Perls iron to haemochromatosis, and Mallory-Denk bodies to alcohol-related hepatitis.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-wilson-haemochromatosis-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Discuss the pathogenesis, clinical features, diagnosis and management of Wilson disease. Add a note on family screening.`,openingLines:[`Wilson disease is an autosomal recessive disorder of the ATP7B copper-transporting ATPase on chromosome 13 that impairs biliary copper excretion and ceruloplasmin loading.`,`Copper accumulates in liver, brain, cornea and kidney, causing hepatic, neuropsychiatric and haemolytic disease that is fatal if untreated but controllable with lifelong chelation or zinc.`],answer:[{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`ATP7B defect - copper not excreted in bile or loaded on ceruloplasmin; apoceruloplasmin degraded.`,`Hepatocyte copper - oxidative injury, hepatitis, cirrhosis; release causes haemolysis and spill-over to basal ganglia, Descemet membrane, renal tubules.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Hepatic (5-35 years): raised ALT, chronic hepatitis, cirrhosis, ALF with Coombs-negative haemolysis.`,`Neuropsychiatric: tremor, dystonia, dysarthria, drooling, parkinsonism, behaviour change.`,`Eyes: KF ring, sunflower cataract; others - RTA, arthropathy, amenorrhoea.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Ceruloplasmin under 20 mg/dL; 24-h urine copper over 100 ug (over 40 suspicious); slit lamp.`,`Hepatic copper over 250 ug/g; ATP7B genetics; MRI brain (face of giant panda).`,`Leipzig score 4 or more = diagnosis; ALF pattern ALP:bilirubin under 4, AST:ALT over 2.2.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Penicillamine 750-1500 mg/day with pyridoxine, or trientine; zinc 50 mg TDS for maintenance and presymptomatic.`,`Low-copper diet first year.`,`ALF - transplant (New Wilson Index 11 or more); bridge with plasma exchange.`,`Pregnancy - continue; reduce chelator 25-50% in last trimester.`]},{heading:`Monitoring`,points:[`24-h urine copper 200-500 ug on chelator, under 75 on zinc; LFT, CBC, urine protein 3-6 monthly.`,`Watch for penicillamine toxicity and neurological worsening.`]},{heading:`Family screening`,points:[`All siblings (25% risk) and first-degree relatives: ceruloplasmin, urine copper, slit lamp, LFT, ATP7B mutation.`,`Presymptomatic cases treated with zinc; genetic counselling in consanguinity.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of hepatocyte copper handling: ATP7B, ceruloplasmin, bile excretion, spill-over to brain/cornea/RBC`,`Leipzig score table`],markSplit:[{part:`Pathogenesis`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis and Leipzig score`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment and monitoring`,marks:3},{part:`Family screening`,marks:1}],keywords:[`ATP7B`,`ceruloplasmin`,`Kayser-Fleischer ring`,`Leipzig score`,`penicillamine`,`trientine`,`zinc`,`liver transplant`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-wilson-haemochromatosis-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Differentiate Wilson disease from hereditary haemochromatosis.`,openingLines:[`Both are autosomal recessive metal-overload disorders of the liver, but Wilson disease is copper overload from ATP7B failure and haemochromatosis is iron overload from hepcidin deficiency (HFE C282Y).`,`They differ in age, organs affected, tests and treatment.`],answer:[{heading:`Genetics and mechanism`,points:[`Wilson - ATP7B (chr 13), failed biliary copper excretion.`,`HH - HFE (chr 6), low hepcidin, excess iron absorption.`]},{heading:`Clinical`,points:[`Wilson - 5-35 years; liver, basal ganglia, KF ring, haemolysis.`,`HH - men 40-60; liver, diabetes, bronze skin, cardiomyopathy, hypogonadism, 2nd/3rd MCP arthropathy.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Wilson - low ceruloplasmin, high urine copper, hepatic copper over 250 ug/g.`,`HH - TSAT over 45%, raised ferritin, HFE C282Y homozygous; MRI liver iron.`]},{heading:`Treatment and prognosis`,points:[`Wilson - penicillamine/trientine/zinc lifelong; transplant for ALF.`,`HH - venesection to ferritin 50-100 ug/L; HCC surveillance if cirrhotic.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column comparison table: Wilson vs HH (gene, metal, age, organs, tests, treatment)`],markSplit:[{part:`Mechanism`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:1.5},{part:`Investigations`,marks:1.5},{part:`Treatment`,marks:1}],keywords:[`ATP7B`,`HFE`,`hepcidin`,`ceruloplasmin`,`transferrin saturation`,`venesection`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Wilson disease; Hemochromatosis; Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency`,`AASLD Practice Guidance: A multidisciplinary approach to the diagnosis and management of Wilson disease, 2022`,`EASL-ERN Clinical Practice Guidelines on Wilson disease, 2025`,`EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines on haemochromatosis, 2022`,`American Thoracic Society/European Respiratory Society statement: standards for the diagnosis and management of individuals with alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency`,`Global Initiative for Chronic Obstructive Lung Disease (GOLD) Report, 2025`,`Thalassaemia International Federation Guidelines for the management of transfusion-dependent thalassaemia, 4th edition, 2021`,`NHM, MoHFW. Prevention and Control of Haemoglobinopathies in India, 2016`]}),a.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-acute-liver-failure`,title:`Acute liver failure and acute-on-chronic liver failure`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Acute liver failure (ALF)** is **coagulopathy (INR 1.5 or more) with hepatic encephalopathy** developing **within 26 weeks of the first symptom in a patient without pre-existing liver disease** - in India usually from **hepatitis E, hepatitis B, anti-TB drugs or yellow phosphorus**, killing through **cerebral oedema, sepsis and multi-organ failure**, and judged for transplant by the **King's College criteria**; **acute-on-chronic liver failure (ACLF)** is an acute insult on chronic liver disease or cirrhosis causing **jaundice and coagulopathy with ascites/encephalopathy (APASL-AARC)** or **organ failures (EASL-CLIF)** with high 28-day mortality.`,keywords:[`ALF`,`fulminant hepatic failure`,`ACLF`,`acute-on-chronic liver failure`,`King's College criteria`,`N-acetylcysteine`,`NAC`,`cerebral oedema`,`intracranial hypertension`,`hepatic encephalopathy`,`APASL`,`AARC score`,`EASL-CLIF`,`CLIF-C ACLF`,`ALFED`,`plasma exchange`,`liver transplantation`,`hepatitis E`,`yellow phosphorus`,`paracetamol overdose`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**ALF (AASLD)**: **INR 1.5 or more plus any degree of encephalopathy** in a patient **without pre-existing cirrhosis**, with illness under **26 weeks**; Wilson disease, vertically acquired HBV and AIH qualify even with underlying cirrhosis if recognised for under 26 weeks. [AASLD ALF Position Paper 2011 / Harrison 22e]`,`**O'Grady (King's) subtypes by jaundice-to-encephalopathy interval**: **hyperacute under 7 days** (paracetamol, HAV, HEV - most cerebral oedema but best survival), **acute 8-28 days**, **subacute 5-26 weeks** (drugs, indeterminate - least oedema, worst spontaneous survival). [EASL ALF 2017]`,`ALF is rare - about **1-6 per million per year** in the West, where **paracetamol causes about 40-50%** of cases in the UK and USA. [Harrison 22e]`,`**In India, viral hepatitis dominates**: **hepatitis E (about 30-45%), hepatitis B, hepatitis A (children)**, then **anti-TB drug-induced liver injury**; paracetamol is uncommon. [INASL ALF Consensus 2019]`,`**Yellow phosphorus (rat-killer paste)** is a leading cause of toxic ALF in **Tamil Nadu and south India**; and **HEV in pregnancy** carries maternal mortality of 20-25% in the third trimester. [INASL ALF Consensus 2019]`,`**ACLF (APASL-AARC 2019)**: an acute hepatic insult causing **jaundice (bilirubin 5 mg/dL or more) and coagulopathy (INR 1.5 or more)**, complicated **within 4 weeks by ascites and/or encephalopathy**, in a patient with known or unknown chronic liver disease or cirrhosis. [APASL ACLF 2019]`,`**ACLF (EASL-CLIF)**: **acute decompensation of cirrhosis with organ failure(s)** (liver, kidney, brain, coagulation, circulation, lungs) and **28-day mortality of 20-80%** by grade; it affects about **30% of patients admitted with decompensated cirrhosis**. [EASL ACLF 2023]`,`In Asia, the **AARC registry** shows **alcohol as the commonest acute insult and underlying disease**, followed by **HBV reactivation, HEV and drug/herbal injury**. [APASL ACLF 2019]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and precipitants`,points:[`**Viral ALF**: HEV, HBV (acute or reactivation, often with HDV), HAV; rarely **HSV, CMV, EBV, dengue** - HSV ALF may lack jaundice and needs **acyclovir**. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`**Drug/toxin ALF**: **paracetamol (dose-dependent), anti-TB drugs (isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide), valproate, NSAIDs, antiepileptics, herbal and dietary supplements**, yellow phosphorus, **Amanita phalloides** mushroom. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`**Other ALF causes**: **Wilson disease, autoimmune hepatitis, Budd-Chiari syndrome, ischaemic hepatitis (shock), acute fatty liver of pregnancy/HELLP**, malignant infiltration (lymphoma), heat stroke. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Indeterminate ALF** - no cause found in about 10-15%; it has the poorest transplant-free survival. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`**ACLF hepatic precipitants (APASL)**: **alcoholic hepatitis, HBV flare, HEV or HAV superinfection, DILI including CAM/herbal products**, autoimmune flare. [APASL ACLF 2019]`,`**ACLF extrahepatic precipitants (EASL)**: **bacterial infection (the commonest, about one-third), GI bleeding**, alcohol, surgery; in about 40% no precipitant is found. [EASL ACLF 2023]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Massive hepatocyte necrosis or apoptosis removes **synthetic, detoxifying and metabolic** function: **INR rises (factor VII has a 6-hour half-life), glucose falls, lactate rises and ammonia accumulates**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ammonia** crosses the blood-brain barrier and is converted by **astrocytes into glutamine**, an osmolyte that swells astrocytes - **cytotoxic cerebral oedema and intracranial hypertension**, the main cause of death in hyperacute ALF. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`**Arterial ammonia over 150-200 umol/L** predicts cerebral herniation; loss of cerebral autoregulation and systemic inflammation add to brain swelling. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`Dying hepatocytes release **DAMPs**, provoking a **systemic inflammatory response** with vasodilatation, low SVR, high cardiac output (a 'septic' haemodynamic picture), **AKI** and immune paralysis - hence high infection rates. [Harrison 22e]`,`INR overstates bleeding risk: pro- and anticoagulant factors fall together (**rebalanced haemostasis**), so routine FFP is not needed. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`**ACLF** is driven by **systemic inflammation** (bacterial PAMPs or sterile DAMPs) on a cirrhotic background, causing **organ failure via immunopathology, mitochondrial dysfunction and microcirculatory failure** - explains why infection is both cause and consequence. [EASL ACLF 2023]`,`**Paracetamol toxicity**: excess drug is shunted to **CYP2E1 producing NAPQI**, normally neutralised by glutathione; when glutathione falls under 30%, NAPQI binds hepatocyte proteins - **NAC works by replenishing glutathione**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and prognostic scores`,points:[`**King's College criteria - paracetamol ALF**: **arterial pH under 7.3 (or lactate over 3.0 mmol/L) after fluid resuscitation**, OR all three of **INR over 6.5, creatinine over 3.4 mg/dL (300 umol/L) and grade III-IV encephalopathy** within 24 hours. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`**King's College criteria - non-paracetamol ALF**: **INR over 6.5** alone, OR any 3 of: **age under 10 or over 40, aetiology indeterminate or drug-induced, jaundice to encephalopathy over 7 days, INR over 3.5, bilirubin over 17.5 mg/dL (300 umol/L)**. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`King's criteria have **high specificity (about 90%) but modest sensitivity (about 60%)**, so not meeting them does not mean safety. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`**ALFED model (Indian, AIIMS)**: **arterial ammonia 123 umol/L or more, bilirubin 15 mg/dL or more, INR 5 or more and encephalopathy grade over II** - scored dynamically over 3 days; **4 or more predicts high mortality**. [INASL ALF Consensus 2019]`,`**AARC score (APASL)**: bilirubin, HE grade, INR, lactate and creatinine scored 1-3 each; **grade I (5-7), II (8-10), III (11-15)**; the first **7 days are the 'golden window'** for reversal. [APASL ACLF 2019]`,`**EASL-CLIF grades**: **ACLF-1** - single kidney failure, or single other organ failure with creatinine 1.5-1.9 mg/dL and/or HE grade I-II; **ACLF-2** - two organ failures; **ACLF-3** - three or more; 28-day mortality about **22%, 32% and 77%**. [EASL ACLF 2023]`,`**NACSELD ACLF** (North America): two or more extrahepatic organ failures - shock, grade III-IV HE, dialysis or mechanical ventilation. [ACG ACLF 2022]`,`**West Haven grading** of encephalopathy (I-IV) is applied to both; grade III-IV means **intubation for airway protection**. [AASLD-EASL HE 2014]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`ALF: a **previously well patient** with a prodrome of malaise, nausea and anorexia, then **jaundice followed by drowsiness, confusion or agitation**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Take a meticulous **drug history**: paracetamol (including staggered overdose), **ATT, antiepileptics, antibiotics, herbal or ayurvedic medicines, supplements, rat poison, mushrooms**. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`Ask **travel, water source and local outbreaks** (HEV/HAV), sexual and injection risk (HBV), and **pregnancy status and gestation** (HEV, AFLP, HELLP). [INASL ALF Consensus 2019]`,`**Yellow phosphorus**: a 'symptom-free' interval of 1-3 days after ingestion of **rat-kill paste**, then jaundice, ALF and arrhythmia; garlic odour of breath and vomit. [INASL ALF Consensus 2019]`,`ACLF: a **known or unrecognised cirrhotic** who deteriorates over days with **jaundice, ascites, confusion, oliguria or bleeding**, often after **binge drinking, a new infection or a herbal remedy**. [APASL ACLF 2019]`,`Ask about previous liver disease, alcohol quantity and stopping HBV antivirals - **stopping tenofovir/entecavir causes HBV flare ACLF**. [APASL ACLF 2019]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`ALF: **jaundice with a small or shrinking liver** (loss of dullness - a bad sign); absence of stigmata of chronic liver disease points to ALF rather than ACLF. [Harrison 22e]`,`Grade encephalopathy: **asterixis (grade II), somnolence with confusion (III), coma (IV)**; check **GCS, pupils and fundi** every 1-2 hours. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`**Signs of raised ICP**: systemic hypertension with bradycardia (Cushing reflex), **sluggish or dilated pupils, decerebrate posturing, hyperventilation** - papilloedema is often absent. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`Look for **hepatomegaly with ascites and abdominal pain** - Budd-Chiari, malignant infiltration or acute fatty liver of pregnancy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**KF rings** (Wilson), **herpetic lesions** (HSV), **needle marks** and signs of sepsis or bleeding. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`ACLF: **stigmata of cirrhosis** (spider naevi, palmar erythema, splenomegaly, ascites) with deep jaundice, encephalopathy and haemodynamic instability. [APASL ACLF 2019]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**ALF vs ACLF vs decompensated cirrhosis**: ALF has no prior liver disease; ACLF has an acute insult on chronic disease with organ failure or jaundice-coagulopathy; plain decompensation lacks organ failure. [EASL ACLF 2023]`,`**Sepsis with cholestatic jaundice and DIC** - high INR, but **factor VIII is low in DIC and normal/high in liver failure**; platelets and fibrinogen fall more in DIC. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Severe falciparum malaria, leptospirosis (Weil disease), dengue and scrub typhus** - fever, renal failure, thrombocytopenia; jaundice is often from haemolysis/cholestasis with modest INR. [ICMR Acute Febrile Illness 2023]`,`**Severe acute hepatitis without encephalopathy** (INR 1.5 or more, no HE) - by definition not yet ALF but must be watched as it may progress. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`**Other causes of coma** in a jaundiced patient: hypoglycaemia, intracranial bleed, meningitis, alcohol withdrawal, Wernicke encephalopathy, drug overdose. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pregnancy-specific**: AFLP (hypoglycaemia, raised uric acid, Swansea criteria) and HELLP (hypertension, haemolysis, low platelets) - both cured by delivery, unlike HEV. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**First line at the district hospital**: **PT/INR, bilirubin, AST, ALT, ALP, albumin, blood glucose (2-hourly), CBC, creatinine, electrolytes, ABG with lactate**, blood group and cross-match. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`**Aetiology screen**: **IgM anti-HEV, IgM anti-HAV, HBsAg and IgM anti-HBc**, anti-HCV, HSV PCR, **paracetamol level**, toxicology, **ceruloplasmin and urine copper**, ANA, ASMA, IgG, pregnancy test. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`**Arterial (or venous) ammonia** - over 150-200 umol/L means high risk of cerebral oedema; a persistent rise over 3 days predicts poor outcome. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`**Ultrasound with Doppler**: liver size, ascites, hepatic and portal vein patency (Budd-Chiari, portal vein thrombosis), signs of cirrhosis (to separate ACLF). [EASL ALF 2017]`,`**Infection screen** - blood and urine cultures, chest X-ray, **ascitic tap** in ACLF (SBP if PMN 250/mm3 or more), procalcitonin. [EASL ACLF 2023]`,`**CT brain** if focal signs or doubt; **ICP monitoring** is used selectively in transplant candidates; **optic nerve sheath diameter on ultrasound** is a bedside surrogate. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`In ACLF, calculate **MELD-Na, CLIF-C ACLF and AARC** scores at admission and **day 3-7** - dynamic change matters more than the baseline. [EASL ACLF 2023]`,`**Transjugular liver biopsy** only when aetiology changes management (AIH, malignancy, Wilson) - percutaneous biopsy is unsafe with coagulopathy. [EASL ALF 2017]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / first contact`,points:[`**Any patient with acute hepatitis and INR 1.5 or more, or any confusion, needs same-day admission** - ALF cannot be managed as an outpatient. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`**Stop all potentially hepatotoxic drugs** including ATT and herbal products; do not give sedatives, NSAIDs or aminoglycosides. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`**Paracetamol overdose**: start **IV NAC** if the 4-hour level is above the treatment line, the timing is unknown or staggered, or there is liver injury - do not wait for the level if more than 8 hours have passed. [UK MHRA / EASL ALF 2017]`,`**Contact a liver transplant centre early** - before grade III encephalopathy - because transfer becomes unsafe once the patient is comatose. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`Check **capillary glucose** at first contact and correct hypoglycaemia with **IV 10-25% dextrose**; give **IV thiamine** in alcohol users. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`Counsel family on the **seriousness, need for transfer and possibility of transplant**; collect a full drug and toxin history from relatives. [EASL ALF 2017]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / ICU`,points:[`**Admit to ICU/HDU**; **intubate at grade III-IV encephalopathy**; nurse **head-end up 30 degrees, neck neutral**, minimise stimulation. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`**NAC in all ALF**: paracetamol - **150 mg/kg IV over 1 hour, 50 mg/kg over 4 hours, 100 mg/kg over 16 hours** (or the 2-bag SNAP regimen) continued until INR improves; **non-paracetamol ALF - NAC improves transplant-free survival in coma grade I-II** (adults, not children). [AASLD / EASL ALF 2017]`,`**Cerebral oedema prevention** (ammonia over 150 or grade III-IV): **hypertonic saline to keep serum sodium 145-155 mmol/L**, avoid hypotonic fluids, fever and hypercapnia; **early continuous renal replacement therapy** lowers ammonia. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`**Surges of ICP**: **mannitol 0.5-1 g/kg IV bolus** (if serum osmolality under 320 mOsm/kg) or 3% / 30% hypertonic saline, brief hyperventilation to PaCO2 30-35 mmHg as a bridge; indomethacin and hypothermia only in specialist units. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`**Haemodynamics**: crystalloid to euvolaemia, then **noradrenaline for MAP 65 mmHg or more** (or CPP 50-80 mmHg); hydrocortisone for refractory shock. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`**High-volume plasma exchange** (8-12% of body weight per day for 3 days) improves transplant-free survival in ALF and is used as a bridge in Indian centres. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`**Coagulopathy**: give vitamin K 10 mg IV once; **FFP or platelets only for bleeding or procedures**; **PPI** for stress ulcer prophylaxis. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`**Specific therapy**: **tenofovir/entecavir for HBV, acyclovir for HSV, steroids for AIH, delivery for AFLP/HELLP, plasma exchange for Wilson, silibinin/penicillin for Amanita**, anticoagulation/TIPS for Budd-Chiari. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`**ACLF**: treat the precipitant - **antibiotics for infection, prednisolone 40 mg/day for severe alcoholic hepatitis (Maddrey DF 32 or more, judged by Lille at day 7), tenofovir for HBV flare**, albumin and terlipressin for HRS-AKI, organ support, and **early transplant assessment**. [EASL ACLF 2023]`,`**Liver transplantation** is the definitive therapy for ALF meeting King's criteria and for selected ACLF (including ACLF-3 within 28 days); **CLIF-C ACLF score over 70 with 4 or more organ failures at day 3-7** suggests futility without transplant. [EASL ACLF 2023]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: **HEV ALF** has very high maternal and fetal mortality - supportive ICU care (delivery does not clearly improve outcome); **AFLP and HELLP need prompt delivery**. [INASL ALF Consensus 2019]`,`**Children**: paediatric ALF is defined without the encephalopathy requirement (INR 2 or more, or 1.5 with HE); HAV, Wilson and metabolic diseases dominate; NAC is not of proven benefit in non-paracetamol paediatric ALF. [PALF / ESPGHAN]`,`**Elderly**: worse prognosis (age over 40 is a King's criterion) and more drug-induced and indeterminate ALF. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`**Anti-TB DILI ALF**: stop all hepatotoxic ATT; use a **non-hepatotoxic regimen (streptomycin, ethambutol, levofloxacin)** until recovery, then reintroduce sequentially (see the DILI topic). [NTEP Guidelines 2022]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: early recognition, **glucose, NAC, avoiding sedatives, head-up nursing, hypertonic saline and transfer** save lives even without ICP monitoring. [INASL ALF Consensus 2019]`,`**CKD/diabetes**: diabetics on insulin or sulfonylureas risk severe hypoglycaemia as gluconeogenesis fails - stop sulfonylureas and use dextrose infusions. [EASL ALF 2017]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Causes of death in ALF: **cerebral oedema and herniation, sepsis and multi-organ failure, bleeding**; infection develops in up to 80%. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`**Spontaneous survival** is best in **paracetamol, HAV, ischaemic and pregnancy-related ALF (over 50-60%)** and worst in **drug-induced, indeterminate and Wilson ALF (under 25-30%)**. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`Overall ALF survival in Western centres is now **over 70%** with transplant; Indian series report **mortality of 40-60%** because transplant access is limited. [INASL ALF Consensus 2019]`,`**Post-transplant 1-year survival for ALF** is about 80-85%, lower than for elective transplant early on. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`ACLF 28-day mortality: **about 22% (ACLF-1), 32% (ACLF-2), 77% (ACLF-3)**; ACLF can resolve - about half of grade 1-2 improve with treatment of the precipitant. [EASL ACLF 2023]`,`Other complications: **AKI (up to 50-70%), hypoglycaemia, hyponatraemia, pancreatitis, ARDS, seizures**, and adrenal insufficiency. [EASL ALF 2017]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer to a transplant centre** if: **any encephalopathy, INR over 2 (rising), hypoglycaemia, acidosis, lactate over 3, creatinine rise, or King's/ALFED criteria approaching**. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`Monitor in ICU: **GCS and pupils hourly, glucose 2-hourly, INR, lactate and ammonia 6-12 hourly**, electrolytes and urine output. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`Reassess **King's criteria daily** and **ACLF scores at day 3-7** to decide transplant or palliation. [EASL ACLF 2023]`,`After recovery: **LFT weekly until normal**, aetiology-specific follow-up (HBV DNA, copper studies, autoantibodies), and abstinence support after alcohol-related ACLF. [EASL ACLF 2023]`,`ACLF survivors are at high risk of readmission - **3-monthly hepatology review**, transplant listing, and management of cirrhosis complications. [EASL ACLF 2023]`,`Report suspected drug-induced ALF to **PvPI (Pharmacovigilance Programme of India)**. [PvPI 2023]`]},{heading:`Prevention and counselling`,points:[`**Safe water and sanitation** prevent HEV and HAV; **hepatitis A vaccine** for children and non-immune adults with chronic liver disease. [NVHCP Technical Guidelines 2018]`,`**Hepatitis B vaccination** (birth dose within 24 hours), and **never stopping HBV antivirals** without specialist advice. [NVHCP Technical Guidelines 2018]`,`**Monitor LFT in high-risk ATT patients** (elderly, alcohol, HBV/HCV, HIV, malnutrition); stop drugs if ALT over 3 x ULN with symptoms or over 5 x ULN. [NTEP Guidelines 2022]`,`Paracetamol: maximum **4 g/day (2-3 g/day in alcohol users, low weight or malnutrition)**; restricted pack sizes and safe storage of **rat poison and pesticides**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Counsel against **unregulated herbal and dietary supplements** - an important cause of DILI and ACLF in India. [APASL ACLF 2019]`,`**Alcohol cessation** is the most effective prevention of alcohol-related ACLF; link to de-addiction services. [EASL ACLF 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2026)`,points:[`**EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines on ACLF 2023**: CLIF-C definitions retained; **transplant should be considered in ACLF-3** within the window; futility criteria at day 3-7. [EASL ACLF 2023]`,`**ACG Clinical Guideline on ACLF 2022**: acknowledges **APASL, EASL-CLIF and NACSELD** definitions; prioritises infection control, organ support and early transplant evaluation. [ACG ACLF 2022]`,`**APASL ACLF consensus (AARC 2019) remains the Asian standard**; G-CSF is not supported by later randomised trials and is not recommended by EASL. [APASL ACLF 2019 / EASL ACLF 2023]`,`**INASL consensus on ALF (2019)** highlights Indian aetiology (HEV, HBV, ATT, yellow phosphorus) and the **ALFED model** for transplant decisions. [INASL ALF Consensus 2019]`,`**Plasma exchange** is gaining ground in ALF (EASL) and is being studied in ACLF (APACHE trial); it is widely used as a bridge in India. [EASL ALF 2017 / EASL ACLF 2023]`,`**UK 2-bag (SNAP) NAC regimen** (100 mg/kg over 2 hours, then 200 mg/kg over 10 hours) reduces anaphylactoid reactions and is being adopted. [UK MHRA 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Viva: **'Define ALF'** - INR 1.5 or more plus encephalopathy, no prior liver disease, under 26 weeks; name the O'Grady subtypes. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`Viva: **'King's College criteria'** - paracetamol vs non-paracetamol; the number examiners want is **INR over 6.5**. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`Viva: **'Commonest cause of ALF in India?'** - hepatitis E (especially in pregnancy); in the West, paracetamol. [INASL ALF Consensus 2019]`,`Viva: **'ACLF definitions'** - APASL (bilirubin 5, INR 1.5, ascites/HE within 4 weeks) vs EASL-CLIF (organ failures, cirrhosis). [APASL ACLF 2019]`,`Common mistake: **correcting INR with FFP routinely** - it masks the prognostic marker and adds volume; give only for bleeding. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`Common mistake: **sedating an agitated jaundiced patient with benzodiazepines** and **giving lactulose in ALF** (bowel distension hinders transplant surgery). [EASL ALF 2017]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in ALF and ACLF`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`N-acetylcysteine (paracetamol)`,`150 mg/kg IV over 1 h, 50 mg/kg over 4 h, 100 mg/kg over 16 h (or 100 mg/kg over 2 h then 200 mg/kg over 10 h)`,`Continue 100 mg/kg/16 h bags until INR improving and ALT falling`,`Anaphylactoid reaction (flushing, wheeze) - pause, antihistamine, restart slower`],[`N-acetylcysteine (non-paracetamol)`,`Same 3-bag IV regimen, continued up to 72 hours`,`HE grade I-II in adults`,`Benefit not shown in children or grade III-IV`],[`Hypertonic saline (3% or 30%)`,`Infusion to keep Na 145-155 mmol/L; bolus for ICP surges`,`While ICP risk persists`,`Hypernatraemia over 160, osmotic demyelination if overcorrected chronic hyponatraemia`],[`Mannitol 20%`,`0.5-1 g/kg IV bolus over 15-20 min`,`Repeat if osmolality under 320 mOsm/kg`,`Volume overload in AKI, hyperosmolality`],[`Noradrenaline`,`0.05-1 ug/kg/min IV infusion`,`Titrate to MAP 65 mmHg or more`,`Arrhythmia, ischaemia; central line`],[`Tenofovir disoproxil / entecavir`,`300 mg / 0.5-1 mg orally daily`,`Indefinite in HBV ALF/ACLF`,`Lactic acidosis rare; renal dose adjustment`],[`Acyclovir`,`10 mg/kg IV 8-hourly`,`Until HSV excluded or 14-21 days`,`Nephrotoxicity - hydrate, renal dose`],[`Prednisolone (alcoholic hepatitis ACLF)`,`40 mg orally daily`,`28 days if Lille under 0.45 at day 7; stop if 0.45 or more`,`Sepsis, GI bleed; exclude infection first`],[`Terlipressin + albumin (HRS-AKI)`,`1 mg IV 4-6 hourly or 2 mg/day infusion up to 12 mg; albumin 20-40 g/day`,`Up to 14 days`,`Ischaemia, respiratory failure - avoid if SpO2 under 90% or ACLF-3 with hypoxia`]]},{heading:`King's College criteria for liver transplant in ALF`,columns:[`Paracetamol-induced ALF`,`Non-paracetamol ALF`],rows:[[`Arterial pH under 7.3 (or lactate over 3.0 mmol/L) after fluid resuscitation`,`INR over 6.5 (PT over 100 s) irrespective of encephalopathy grade`],[`OR all three within 24 h: INR over 6.5`,`OR any three of: age under 10 or over 40`],[`Creatinine over 3.4 mg/dL (300 umol/L)`,`Aetiology: indeterminate or drug-induced`],[`Grade III-IV encephalopathy`,`Jaundice to encephalopathy over 7 days; INR over 3.5; bilirubin over 17.5 mg/dL`]]},{heading:`ACLF definitions compared`,columns:[`Feature`,`APASL-AARC (2019)`,`EASL-CLIF (2023)`,`NACSELD`],rows:[[`Underlying disease`,`Chronic liver disease or cirrhosis, known or unknown`,`Cirrhosis (compensated or decompensated)`,`Cirrhosis`],[`Acute insult`,`Hepatic (alcohol, HBV, HEV, DILI)`,`Hepatic or extrahepatic (infection, bleed)`,`Mainly infection`],[`Defining criteria`,`Bilirubin 5 mg/dL or more + INR 1.5 or more + ascites/HE within 4 weeks`,`Organ failures by CLIF-C OF score`,`2 or more extrahepatic organ failures`],[`Grading`,`AARC score I (5-7), II (8-10), III (11-15)`,`ACLF-1, 2, 3`,`Not graded`],[`Key window`,`Golden window - first 7 days`,`Reassess day 3-7`,`-`]]},{heading:`Causes of ALF - India vs West and specific treatment`,columns:[`Cause`,`Clue`,`Specific treatment`],rows:[[`Hepatitis E`,`Outbreak, contaminated water, pregnancy`,`Supportive; ICU; transplant if criteria met`],[`Hepatitis B (acute/reactivation)`,`HBsAg, IgM anti-HBc, immunosuppression`,`Tenofovir or entecavir`],[`Anti-TB drugs`,`On ATT for 2-8 weeks`,`Stop ATT; non-hepatotoxic regimen; NAC`],[`Paracetamol`,`Overdose, very high ALT, acidosis`,`IV NAC`],[`Yellow phosphorus`,`Rat-kill paste, garlic odour, arrhythmia`,`NAC, plasma exchange, early transplant referral`],[`Wilson disease`,`Haemolysis, low ALP, KF ring`,`Plasma exchange; transplant`],[`Autoimmune hepatitis`,`High IgG, autoantibodies`,`Prednisolone; transplant if no response`],[`HSV`,`Fever, high ALT, often anicteric`,`IV acyclovir`],[`AFLP / HELLP`,`Third trimester, hypoglycaemia / hypertension`,`Delivery`]]},{heading:`Monitoring and cut-offs in ALF`,columns:[`Parameter`,`Target / danger level`,`Action`],rows:[[`Arterial ammonia`,`Over 150-200 umol/L`,`Cerebral oedema prophylaxis, CRRT`],[`Serum sodium`,`Target 145-155 mmol/L`,`Hypertonic saline`],[`Blood glucose`,`Keep over 70 mg/dL (2-hourly checks)`,`IV 10-25% dextrose`],[`Mean arterial pressure`,`65 mmHg or more (CPP 50-80)`,`Fluids then noradrenaline`],[`Lactate`,`Over 3 mmol/L after fluids`,`Poor prognosis - transplant listing (paracetamol)`],[`INR`,`Over 6.5 / rising after day 3-4`,`King's criterion - transplant`]]}],redFlags:[`Any confusion, drowsiness or asterixis in a patient with acute jaundice - ALF until proven otherwise, admit and contact a transplant centre.`,`INR 1.5 or more in acute hepatitis, or a rising INR on serial tests.`,`Hypoglycaemia, metabolic acidosis or lactate over 3 mmol/L in acute liver injury.`,`Hypertension with bradycardia, sluggish pupils or posturing - raised intracranial pressure.`,`Jaundice with encephalopathy in a pregnant woman - HEV ALF, AFLP or HELLP.`,`Known cirrhotic with new organ failure (oliguria, shock, hypoxia, grade III-IV HE) - ACLF, ICU care.`,`Ingestion of rat-kill paste even if asymptomatic - yellow phosphorus, admit and observe.`],pearls:[`**ALF = INR 1.5 + any encephalopathy + no prior liver disease + under 26 weeks.**`,`**Hyperacute ALF has the most cerebral oedema but the best survival.**`,`**King's: paracetamol - pH under 7.3; non-paracetamol - INR over 6.5.**`,`**India: HEV commonest; West: paracetamol.**`,`**NAC for all ALF in adults** with early encephalopathy, not just paracetamol.`,`**Ammonia to glutamine in astrocytes = brain swelling**; keep sodium 145-155.`,`**No routine FFP, no sedatives, no lactulose** in ALF.`,`**APASL ACLF = 5-1.5-4**: bilirubin 5, INR 1.5, ascites/HE within 4 weeks.`,`**EASL-CLIF ACLF-3 = 3 organ failures, about 77% 28-day mortality** - consider transplant.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-acute-liver-failure-q1`,stem:`A 22-year-old woman, 32 weeks pregnant, presents during a monsoon jaundice outbreak with 6 days of jaundice and new confusion. INR is 2.4. What is the most likely cause?`,options:[`Hepatitis E`,`Hepatitis C`,`Paracetamol toxicity`,`Autoimmune hepatitis`],answer:0,explanation:`Hepatitis E spreads by contaminated water, causes outbreaks after the monsoon, and in the third trimester causes acute liver failure with high maternal mortality - it is the commonest cause of ALF in India. Hepatitis C almost never causes ALF, paracetamol ALF is uncommon in India and lacks an outbreak link, and autoimmune hepatitis does not cluster in outbreaks.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-acute-liver-failure-q2`,stem:`In a patient with paracetamol-induced acute liver failure, which single finding, after adequate fluid resuscitation, meets the King's College criteria for transplant listing?`,options:[`INR 3.8`,`Arterial pH 7.25`,`Bilirubin 20 mg/dL`,`ALT 8000 U/L`],answer:1,explanation:`In paracetamol ALF an arterial pH below 7.3 after resuscitation is a stand-alone King's criterion. The alternative requires all three of INR over 6.5, creatinine over 3.4 mg/dL and grade III-IV encephalopathy, so INR 3.8 alone is insufficient; bilirubin is a criterion only in non-paracetamol ALF, and ALT level has no prognostic role.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-acute-liver-failure-q3`,stem:`A 30-year-old man with ALF from acute hepatitis B is in grade III encephalopathy with arterial ammonia 210 umol/L. Which measure best reduces the risk of cerebral oedema?`,options:[`Lactulose 30 mL every 2 hours`,`Fresh frozen plasma to bring INR below 1.5`,`Hypertonic saline to keep serum sodium 145-155 mmol/L`,`Lorazepam for agitation`],answer:2,explanation:`Induced hypernatraemia with hypertonic saline (sodium 145-155) reduces astrocyte swelling and intracranial hypertension in high-ammonia ALF; early CRRT also lowers ammonia. Lactulose causes bowel distension without proven benefit in ALF, routine FFP only masks the INR, and benzodiazepines worsen encephalopathy and obscure neurological assessment.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-acute-liver-failure-q4`,stem:`A 48-year-old man with known alcohol-related cirrhosis develops pneumonia, then shock needing noradrenaline, creatinine 2.6 mg/dL needing dialysis, and grade III encephalopathy. By EASL-CLIF, how is he classified?`,options:[`Acute decompensation without ACLF`,`ACLF grade 1`,`ACLF grade 2`,`ACLF grade 3`],answer:3,explanation:`He has three organ failures - circulatory (vasopressor), kidney (creatinine 2 mg/dL or more or dialysis) and brain (HE grade III-IV) - which is ACLF-3, with 28-day mortality around 77% and an indication for urgent transplant evaluation. ACLF-1 is a single kidney failure or single other failure with mild renal or brain dysfunction, ACLF-2 needs exactly two organ failures, and acute decompensation without ACLF has no organ failure.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-acute-liver-failure-q5`,stem:`A 35-year-old woman with drug-induced acute liver failure from anti-TB drugs has grade I encephalopathy. Besides stopping the drugs, which therapy has shown improved transplant-free survival in this situation?`,options:[`Oral rifaximin`,`Intravenous N-acetylcysteine`,`High-dose corticosteroids`,`Ursodeoxycholic acid`],answer:1,explanation:`IV N-acetylcysteine improved transplant-free survival in adult non-paracetamol ALF with early (grade I-II) encephalopathy, especially drug-induced cases. Steroids are for autoimmune ALF or severe alcoholic hepatitis, rifaximin is for chronic encephalopathy in cirrhosis, and ursodeoxycholic acid has no role in ALF.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-acute-liver-failure-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Define acute liver failure. Discuss its aetiology in India, assessment of prognosis and management, including the King's College criteria.`,openingLines:[`Acute liver failure is coagulopathy (INR 1.5 or more) with hepatic encephalopathy developing within 26 weeks of symptom onset in a patient without pre-existing liver disease.`,`In India it is caused mainly by hepatitis E, hepatitis B and anti-TB drugs, and it kills through cerebral oedema, sepsis and multi-organ failure unless the liver recovers or is transplanted.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and classification`,points:[`AASLD definition; exceptions - Wilson, vertical HBV, AIH.`,`O'Grady: hyperacute (under 7 days), acute (8-28 days), subacute (5-26 weeks) - with prognosis and oedema risk.`]},{heading:`Aetiology (India vs West)`,points:[`Viral - HEV (commonest, pregnancy), HBV, HAV (children), HSV.`,`Drugs/toxins - ATT, paracetamol, herbals, yellow phosphorus, Amanita.`,`Others - Wilson, AIH, Budd-Chiari, ischaemic, AFLP/HELLP, indeterminate.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology and clinical features`,points:[`Ammonia to glutamine in astrocytes - cerebral oedema.`,`SIRS, vasodilatation, AKI, infection, hypoglycaemia, coagulopathy.`,`Jaundice, shrinking liver, encephalopathy grade, Cushing reflex.`]},{heading:`Investigations and prognosis`,points:[`INR, glucose, ABG/lactate, ammonia, creatinine; viral markers, paracetamol level, copper studies, autoantibodies, Doppler.`,`King's College criteria - paracetamol and non-paracetamol.`,`ALFED model (Indian): ammonia, bilirubin, INR, HE grade.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`ICU, intubate at grade III-IV, head-up 30 degrees.`,`NAC for all adults (paracetamol and early non-paracetamol).`,`Cerebral oedema: hypertonic saline Na 145-155, mannitol for surges, CRRT; avoid sedatives, lactulose.`,`Noradrenaline for MAP 65; glucose 2-hourly; FFP only for bleeding; infection surveillance.`,`Specific - antivirals, acyclovir, steroids, delivery; plasma exchange; liver transplant.`]},{heading:`Prevention`,points:[`Safe water, HAV and HBV vaccines, ATT monitoring, paracetamol limits, rat-poison safety.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table of King's College criteria (paracetamol vs non-paracetamol)`,`Flow chart of ALF management from first contact to transplant decision`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and classification`,marks:1.5},{part:`Aetiology in India`,marks:2},{part:`Pathophysiology and features`,marks:1.5},{part:`Investigations and King's criteria`,marks:2},{part:`Management`,marks:3}],keywords:[`INR 1.5`,`encephalopathy`,`26 weeks`,`hepatitis E`,`King's College criteria`,`N-acetylcysteine`,`cerebral oedema`,`hypertonic saline`,`liver transplant`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-acute-liver-failure-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate acute liver failure from acute-on-chronic liver failure, and compare the APASL and EASL-CLIF definitions of ACLF.`,openingLines:[`ALF occurs in a previously normal liver, whereas ACLF is an acute deterioration on chronic liver disease or cirrhosis with organ failure and high short-term mortality.`,`APASL defines ACLF by jaundice, coagulopathy and ascites/HE within 4 weeks; EASL-CLIF defines it by organ failures in cirrhosis.`],answer:[{heading:`ALF vs ACLF`,points:[`Background liver: normal vs chronic liver disease/cirrhosis.`,`Causes: viral, drugs, toxins vs alcohol, HBV flare, HEV, infection, bleed.`,`Main killer: cerebral oedema vs sepsis and multi-organ failure.`,`Prognostic tools: King's criteria, ALFED vs AARC, CLIF-C ACLF, MELD.`]},{heading:`APASL-AARC definition`,points:[`Bilirubin 5 mg/dL or more + INR 1.5 or more + ascites and/or HE within 4 weeks.`,`Hepatic insult only; AARC grades I-III; golden window 7 days.`]},{heading:`EASL-CLIF definition`,points:[`Acute decompensation of cirrhosis + organ failure (CLIF-C OF).`,`ACLF-1, 2, 3 with 28-day mortality about 22%, 32%, 77%.`]},{heading:`Management common ground`,points:[`Treat precipitant, organ support, infection control, early transplant evaluation.`]}],mustDraw:[`Comparison table ALF vs ACLF and APASL vs EASL-CLIF`],markSplit:[{part:`ALF vs ACLF`,marks:2},{part:`APASL definition`,marks:1.5},{part:`EASL-CLIF definition and grades`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`ACLF`,`APASL`,`AARC`,`EASL-CLIF`,`organ failure`,`golden window`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Acute liver failure`,`EASL Clinical Practical Guidelines on the management of acute (fulminant) liver failure, 2017`,`EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines on acute-on-chronic liver failure, 2023`,`APASL ACLF Research Consortium (AARC) consensus recommendations on acute-on-chronic liver failure, 2019`,`ACG Clinical Guideline: Acute-on-Chronic Liver Failure, 2022`,`INASL position statement/consensus on acute liver failure in India, 2019`,`AASLD Position Paper: The Management of Acute Liver Failure, 2011 update`,`Central TB Division, MoHFW. National TB Elimination Programme (NTEP) guidance on management of anti-TB drug-induced hepatotoxicity, 2022`]});var o=[];o.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-portal-hypertension-varices`,title:`Portal hypertension and variceal prophylaxis`,oneLiner:`**Portal hypertension** is a **hepatic venous pressure gradient (HVPG) above 5 mmHg**, becoming **clinically significant (CSPH) at 10 mmHg or more**, when varices, ascites and decompensation appear; Baveno VII lets you diagnose **compensated advanced chronic liver disease (cACLD)** and CSPH non-invasively with **liver stiffness and platelets**, and treat CSPH with a **non-selective beta-blocker, preferably carvedilol**, to prevent decompensation, reserving **band ligation (EVL)** for those who cannot take one and **TIPS** for bleeding that recurs or carries high risk.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`portal hypertension`,`HVPG`,`CSPH`,`cACLD`,`Baveno VII`,`oesophageal varices`,`gastric varices`,`carvedilol`,`propranolol`,`non-selective beta-blocker`,`NSBB`,`endoscopic variceal ligation`,`EVL`,`TIPS`,`transient elastography`,`Fibroscan`,`NCPF`,`EHPVO`,`porto-sinusoidal vascular disorder`,`Budd-Chiari syndrome`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Portal hypertension** is a pathological rise in the pressure gradient between the portal vein and the inferior vena cava; normal **HVPG is 1-5 mmHg**, and **above 5 mmHg** is portal hypertension. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Clinically significant portal hypertension (CSPH)** is **HVPG 10 mmHg or more** - the threshold at which varices, ascites, decompensation and HCC risk rise sharply. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**HVPG 12 mmHg or more** is the threshold for variceal bleeding and ascites, and **HVPG 20 mmHg or more** during an acute bleed predicts failure to control bleeding and high early mortality. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Varices are present in about 30-40% of compensated and 60-85% of decompensated cirrhosis**; new varices form at about **7-8% per year** in those without them at baseline. [AASLD Portal Hypertension 2024]`,`Bleeding risk is about **5% a year for small and 15% a year for large varices**; each variceal bleed carries a **6-week mortality of about 15-20%**. [AASLD Portal Hypertension 2024]`,`India carries roughly **one-fifth of global cirrhosis deaths**, driven by alcohol, hepatitis B and C and rising **MASLD**; cirrhosis is the commonest cause of portal hypertension in Indian adults. [GBD 2019]`,`**Non-cirrhotic portal hypertension** is disproportionately common in India: **extrahepatic portal vein obstruction (EHPVO)** is the leading cause of portal hypertension and upper GI bleeding in **Indian children**, and non-cirrhotic portal fibrosis (NCPF) historically accounted for a sizeable share of adult cases. [Harrison 22e]`,`Budd-Chiari syndrome in India and Nepal more often involves the **IVC (membranous obstruction or combined hepatic vein-IVC block)**, whereas in the West pure hepatic vein thrombosis is typical. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and site of block`,points:[`**Prehepatic**: portal or splenic vein thrombosis, **EHPVO** (umbilical sepsis, neonatal catheterisation, dehydration, prothrombotic states), splenic arteriovenous fistula. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Intrahepatic presinusoidal**: **schistosomiasis**, NCPF/**porto-sinusoidal vascular disorder (PSVD)**, early PBC, sarcoidosis, congenital hepatic fibrosis - HVPG is normal or only mildly raised. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sinusoidal**: **cirrhosis of any cause** (alcohol, HBV, HCV, MASLD), alcoholic hepatitis, massive fatty infiltration - accounts for about **90% of portal hypertension** in adults. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Postsinusoidal**: **sinusoidal obstruction syndrome (veno-occlusive disease)** after stem-cell transplant, pyrrolizidine alkaloid herbal teas and some chemotherapy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Posthepatic**: **Budd-Chiari syndrome**, IVC web, **constrictive pericarditis**, severe tricuspid regurgitation and right heart failure - always check the JVP. [Harrison 22e]`,`Budd-Chiari risk factors: **myeloproliferative neoplasm (JAK2 V617F in about 30-40%)**, PNH, antiphospholipid syndrome, factor V Leiden, protein C/S deficiency, oral contraceptives, pregnancy; more than one factor is present in about 25%. [EASL Vascular 2016]`,`Risk of variceal bleeding rises with **variceal size, red wale marks and Child-Pugh B/C** - the three features of the NIEC index. [Baveno VII 2022]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why the drugs work`,points:[`Portal pressure obeys **Ohm's law: pressure gradient = flow x resistance**; portal hypertension starts with **increased intrahepatic resistance** and is sustained by **increased portal inflow**. [Harrison 22e]`,`About **70% of the raised resistance is structural** (fibrosis, regenerative nodules, sinusoidal capillarisation) and **30% is dynamic** - activated **hepatic stellate cells** contract because sinusoidal **nitric oxide is deficient** and endothelin, angiotensin II and thromboxane are in excess. [Harrison 22e]`,`In the splanchnic bed the opposite happens: **excess NO, carbon monoxide, glucagon and prostacyclin** cause arteriolar vasodilatation, raising portal inflow and creating the **hyperdynamic circulation** (high cardiac output, low SVR, low BP). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Non-selective beta-blockers** act on both limbs: **beta-1 blockade lowers cardiac output** and **beta-2 blockade leaves alpha tone unopposed**, constricting splanchnic arterioles and cutting portal inflow. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Carvedilol** adds **alpha-1 blockade**, which relaxes stellate cells and lowers intrahepatic resistance - so it lowers HVPG more than propranolol and achieves a haemodynamic response in more patients. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`Collaterals open at **porto-systemic junctions**: gastro-oesophageal (left gastric and short gastric to azygos - **varices**), rectal (superior to middle/inferior rectal), umbilical (**caput medusae**) and retroperitoneal; spontaneous splenorenal shunts also drive encephalopathy. [Harrison 22e]`,`Variceal rupture follows **Laplace's law: wall tension = transmural pressure x radius / wall thickness** - large varices with thin walls (red wale marks) rupture first. [Harrison 22e]`,`Splanchnic vasodilatation underfills the effective arterial volume, activating **RAAS, sympathetic system and ADH** - the root of ascites, hyponatraemia and hepatorenal syndrome. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`Portal hypertension in cirrhosis also causes **hypersplenism (low platelets)**, **portal hypertensive gastropathy** and, through shunts and gut translocation, encephalopathy and infections. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and staging that examiners ask`,points:[`**Baveno VII cACLD**: liver stiffness by transient elastography **under 10 kPa rules out**, **10-15 kPa is suggestive** and **over 15 kPa is highly suggestive** of compensated advanced chronic liver disease - the rule of five (10-15-20-25). [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**CSPH ruled in** (alcohol, viral, non-obese MASLD) by **LSM 25 kPa or more**, or high probability with **LSM 20-25 kPa and platelets under 150,000** or **LSM 15-20 kPa and platelets under 110,000**. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**CSPH ruled out** by **LSM 15 kPa or less with platelets 150,000 or more**; spleen stiffness **21 kPa or less** also helps rule out CSPH in viral cACLD. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Varices by size**: small (under 5 mm, straighten on insufflation) versus **medium/large (over 5 mm)**; high-risk varices are **medium/large, or small with red wale marks, or small in Child-Pugh C**. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Sarin classification of gastric varices** (Indian, used worldwide): **GOV1** (extend along lesser curve - most common), **GOV2** (extend towards fundus), **IGV1** (isolated fundal) and **IGV2** (isolated elsewhere in stomach). [Sarin 1992]`,`**Decompensation** (Baveno VII) means **ascites, variceal bleeding or overt hepatic encephalopathy**; jaundice alone is not counted. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Recompensation** (new in Baveno VII): cause removed or suppressed, **ascites resolved off diuretics, no encephalopathy off lactulose/rifaximin, no bleed for 12 months**, and stable improvement in albumin, INR and bilirubin. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Child-Pugh** (bilirubin, albumin, INR, ascites, encephalopathy) and **MELD** grade liver function and guide pre-emptive TIPS and transplant referral. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Most patients with compensated cirrhosis and varices are **asymptomatic**; the first event is often **haematemesis or melaena**, abdominal distension or confusion. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask the cause-finding questions: **alcohol (units and duration)**, blood transfusion, injections, tattoos, HBV/HCV status, diabetes and obesity (MASLD), herbal medicines and family history. [AASLD Portal Hypertension 2024]`,`A young Indian adult with **repeated well-tolerated bleeds, massive splenomegaly and no jaundice or ascites** suggests **NCPF/PSVD**. [Harrison 22e]`,`A child or adolescent with **haematemesis after a febrile illness**, splenomegaly, growth failure and a history of **umbilical sepsis or neonatal umbilical catheter** suggests **EHPVO**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Budd-Chiari** presents as the triad **abdominal pain, ascites and hepatomegaly**; acute forms mimic acute hepatitis, chronic forms present with leg oedema and back veins from IVC obstruction. [EASL Vascular 2016]`,`Symptoms of portal hypertensive complications: **abdominal swelling** (ascites), **sleep reversal and forgetfulness** (covert encephalopathy), left upper quadrant dragging pain (splenomegaly), dyspnoea (hepatopulmonary syndrome or portopulmonary hypertension). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Splenomegaly** is the single most consistent sign of portal hypertension; massive spleen with normal liver function favours NCPF or EHPVO. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Caput medusae** - flow away from the umbilicus; a **venous hum (Cruveilhier-Baumgarten murmur)** over a recanalised umbilical vein confirms portal hypertension. [Harrison 22e]`,`**IVC obstruction** gives dilated veins on the flanks and back with **flow upward from below the umbilicus**, distinguishing it from portal collaterals. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Stigmata of chronic liver disease** - spider naevi, palmar erythema, gynaecomastia, Dupuytren contracture, parotid enlargement, testicular atrophy - point to cirrhosis rather than non-cirrhotic portal hypertension. [Harrison 22e]`,`Check for **shifting dullness/fluid thrill**, **asterixis**, jaundice, pallor and postural BP drop (recent bleed). [Harrison 22e]`,`Raised **JVP with Kussmaul sign** and pericardial knock point to **constrictive pericarditis** - a posthepatic cause easily missed. [Harrison 22e]`,`Per-rectal examination: melaena, and **anorectal varices** (distinct from haemorrhoids). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Cirrhosis vs NCPF/PSVD**: NCPF has normal or near-normal LFTs, LSM usually under 12-15 kPa, normal HVPG despite varices, and no regenerative nodules on biopsy. [VALDIG/Baveno VII 2022]`,`**EHPVO vs cirrhosis**: Doppler shows **portal cavernoma** with a normal liver; synthetic function preserved. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Budd-Chiari vs cirrhosis**: Doppler shows absent/reversed hepatic vein flow, intrahepatic collaterals, **caudate lobe hypertrophy** (separate drainage into IVC). [EASL Vascular 2016]`,`**Splenomegaly with pancytopenia**: think of **chronic malaria, kala-azar, haematological malignancy (CML, myelofibrosis)** and thalassaemia before portal hypertension. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Haematemesis**: peptic ulcer is still the commonest cause overall - in a cirrhotic, about **70% of upper GI bleeds are variceal** but the rest are ulcers, gastropathy or Mallory-Weiss tears (see the Upper and lower GI bleeding topic). [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Ascites with high SAAG (1.1 g/dL or more)** means portal hypertension; with **total protein over 2.5 g/dL** it suggests a cardiac or Budd-Chiari cause rather than cirrhosis. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`]},{heading:`Investigations - OPD to tertiary`,points:[`First line: **CBC (platelets under 150,000 is the cheapest marker of CSPH)**, LFT, albumin, INR, creatinine, electrolytes, HBsAg, anti-HCV, and abdominal **ultrasound with Doppler**. [AASLD Portal Hypertension 2024]`,`Doppler signs of portal hypertension: **portal vein diameter over 13 mm**, reduced or **hepatofugal portal flow**, splenomegaly, collaterals and recanalised paraumbilical vein; Doppler also detects **portal vein thrombosis, cavernoma and Budd-Chiari**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Transient elastography (Fibroscan)** is the key non-invasive test: interpret only when fasting, without acute hepatitis, cholestasis, heart failure or alcohol binge; IQR/median should be 30% or less. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Screening endoscopy can be avoided** in cACLD with **LSM under 20 kPa and platelets over 150,000** (very low risk of varices needing treatment); repeat LSM and platelets yearly. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`Patients started on an NSBB for CSPH **do not need a screening endoscopy**; those who cannot take an NSBB need **upper GI endoscopy** to look for varices needing EVL. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**HVPG** (wedged minus free hepatic vein pressure via jugular catheter) is the gold standard but is a tertiary test; it is **normal in presinusoidal disease** (NCPF, EHPVO), which is itself a clue. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Contrast CT/MRI** maps collaterals, portal and hepatic veins, IVC web and HCC; triple-phase imaging is needed before TIPS. [Harrison 22e]`,`Budd-Chiari work-up: **JAK2 V617F (and CALR if negative)**, flow cytometry for PNH, antiphospholipid antibodies, protein C/S, antithrombin, factor V Leiden. [EASL Vascular 2016]`,`Liver biopsy is needed to diagnose **PSVD** (obliterative portal venopathy, nodular regenerative hyperplasia, incomplete septal fibrosis) and to exclude cirrhosis. [VALDIG/Baveno VII 2022]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`Treat the cause first - **alcohol abstinence, HBV/HCV antiviral therapy, weight loss in MASLD** - which lowers portal pressure and can produce recompensation. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**CSPH in compensated cirrhosis**: start an **NSBB to prevent decompensation** (PREDESCI trial), **carvedilol preferred**, regardless of whether varices are present. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Carvedilol**: **6.25 mg once daily**, increase after 3 days to **6.25 mg twice daily**; maximum **12.5 mg/day**; keep **systolic BP 90 mmHg or above**. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Propranolol**: **20-40 mg twice daily**, titrate every 2-3 days to resting **heart rate 55-60/min**; maximum **320 mg/day without ascites, 160 mg/day with ascites**. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`No need to check heart-rate response for carvedilol; with propranolol, titrate to heart rate and BP; **do not use atenolol or metoprolol** (cardioselective drugs do not cut splanchnic inflow). [AASLD Portal Hypertension 2024]`,`**EVL** is the primary prophylaxis for **high-risk varices only when NSBB is contraindicated or not tolerated** (asthma, bradycardia, refractory hypotension): band every **2-4 weeks until eradication**, endoscopy at **6 months** then every **12 months**. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Small varices without red signs** in Child A: NSBB if CSPH (it is present by definition); otherwise repeat endoscopy in 1-2 years (yearly if ongoing injury). [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Secondary prophylaxis** after a variceal bleed: **NSBB plus EVL** combined, started once vasoactive drugs stop (day 3-5); TIPS for rebleeding despite this. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`Avoid **NSAIDs, aminoglycosides and nephrotoxins**; give HAV/HBV, pneumococcal and influenza vaccines; stop alcohol completely. [AASLD Portal Hypertension 2024]`,`**Statins** are safe for their usual indication in Child A/B (simvastatin at most 20 mg/day in Child B) but are not yet recommended solely to lower portal pressure. [Baveno VII 2022]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency`,points:[`Acute variceal bleed: admit to HDU/ICU, **restrictive transfusion (threshold Hb 7 g/dL, target 7-9 g/dL)**, avoid over-expanding volume, protect the airway before endoscopy in encephalopathy or massive bleed. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`Start a **vasoactive drug as soon as bleeding is suspected**: **terlipressin 2 mg IV every 4 h** (reduce to 1 mg every 4 h once bleeding is controlled) or **octreotide 50 microgram bolus then 50 microgram/h**, for **2-5 days**. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Antibiotic prophylaxis for every cirrhotic with GI bleed**: **ceftriaxone 1 g IV daily for up to 7 days** - reduces infection, rebleeding and death. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Erythromycin 250 mg IV** 30-120 min before endoscopy improves visibility; **endoscopy within 12 h**, with EVL for oesophageal and **cyanoacrylate glue for GOV2/IGV1**. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Pre-emptive TIPS within 72 h (ideally 24 h)** for **Child-Pugh C 10-13** or **Child-Pugh B over 7 with active bleeding at endoscopy** (or HVPG over 20 mmHg) improves survival. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`Uncontrolled bleeding: **balloon tamponade (Sengstaken-Blakemore, maximum 24 h)** or preferably a **self-expanding oesophageal metal stent** as a bridge to **rescue TIPS**. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Lactulose** (or rapid bowel cleansing) after a bleed helps prevent encephalopathy; **hold the NSBB during the acute bleed** and in shock. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Portal vein thrombosis in cirrhosis**: anticoagulate recent occlusive or progressive PVT, especially in transplant candidates - **LMWH or VKA**; **DOACs acceptable in Child A and selected B**, avoid in Child C. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Budd-Chiari stepwise ladder**: **anticoagulation for all**, treat the cause, then **angioplasty/stenting** of short-segment stenosis or IVC web, then **TIPS (DIPS)**, then liver transplant. [EASL Vascular 2016]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**NCPF/PSVD**: bleeds managed with EVL and NSBB as in cirrhosis; **shunt surgery or TIPS** for refractory bleeding or symptomatic hypersplenism; prognosis is good because liver function is preserved. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**EHPVO in children**: EVL or sclerotherapy for bleeding; the curative operation is the **meso-Rex bypass** (superior mesenteric vein to left portal vein) where anatomy allows; growth failure and **portal biliopathy** are later problems. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Recent (acute) portal vein thrombosis** without cirrhosis: **anticoagulate at once for at least 6 months** (lifelong if a permanent prothrombotic cause); intestinal infarction signs need surgery. [AASLD Vascular 2020]`,`**Pregnancy** in a woman with portal hypertension: screen for varices in the second trimester; **propranolol** is used (carvedilol has less data); avoid vaginal straining in large varices - shortened second stage. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Ascites and NSBB**: not an absolute contraindication; **reduce or stop if systolic BP under 90 mmHg, serum sodium under 125 mmol/L or AKI**, and restart when stable. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Elderly and comorbid**: carvedilol may unmask heart failure or bradycardia; check orthostatic BP; in COPD with reversible airway disease prefer EVL. [AASLD Portal Hypertension 2024]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: where Fibroscan is unavailable, **platelets under 150,000 with splenomegaly and collaterals on ultrasound** identify likely CSPH; the APRI and FIB-4 are rule-out tools, not CSPH tests. [Baveno VII 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Compensated cirrhosis has a **median survival over 12 years**; after decompensation it falls to about **2 years**. [D'Amico/Harrison 22e]`,`Complications of portal hypertension: **variceal bleeding, ascites, SBP, hepatorenal syndrome, hepatic encephalopathy, portal hypertensive gastropathy, hypersplenism, HPS and portopulmonary hypertension**. [Harrison 22e]`,`After a first variceal bleed, rebleeding without prophylaxis is about **60% within 1-2 years**, with about **33% mortality** per rebleed. [AASLD Portal Hypertension 2024]`,`TIPS complications: **hepatic encephalopathy (about 30%)**, shunt dysfunction (much less with **covered PTFE stents**), heart failure, liver failure, haemolysis. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`EVL complications: **post-banding ulcer bleeding** (day 5-14), chest pain, transient dysphagia, stricture (more with sclerotherapy). [Harrison 22e]`,`NSBB adverse effects: fatigue, bradycardia, hypotension, erectile dysfunction, bronchospasm - about **15% stop the drug**. [AASLD Portal Hypertension 2024]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer to a hepatologist** every new cACLD with LSM over 15 kPa, any decompensation, suspected PSVD/EHPVO/Budd-Chiari, PVT, and every patient for TIPS or transplant assessment. [AASLD Portal Hypertension 2024]`,`On NSBB: check **heart rate, BP, creatinine and sodium at each visit** (every 3 months); reduce dose if systolic BP under 90 mmHg. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**LSM and platelets every 12 months** in cACLD not on NSBB; a **fall of LSM to under 12 kPa with platelets over 150,000** after cause removal means CSPH is unlikely. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`After EVL eradication: endoscopy **at 3-6 months, then every 6-12 months** to band recurrences. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**HCC surveillance** - ultrasound with or without AFP every 6 months - is part of every cirrhosis follow-up visit (see the Hepatocellular carcinoma topic). [AASLD HCC 2023]`,`Indications for liver transplant referral: **any decompensation or MELD 15 or more** (see the Liver transplantation topic). [AASLD Transplant Evaluation 2013]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`Primary prevention is prevention of cirrhosis: **hepatitis B birth-dose and universal immunisation**, **NVHCP** testing and free treatment for HBV and HCV, and alcohol control. [NVHCP 2018]`,`Screen **diabetes, obesity and hypertension** clinics for MASLD fibrosis with **FIB-4** under **NP-NCD** at Ayushman Arogya Mandir level; refer FIB-4 1.3 or more for elastography. [MoHFW NAFLD Guidelines 2021]`,`Counsel to **carry a list of medicines**, avoid NSAIDs and herbal remedies, and report black stools, vomiting of blood or confusion immediately. [AASLD Portal Hypertension 2024]`,`Teach not to stop the beta-blocker abruptly (rebound portal pressure rise) and to hold it during vomiting, diarrhoea or dizziness and seek advice. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Haemoglobin and iron**: chronic gastropathy bleeding causes iron deficiency - treat it and recheck. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2026)`,points:[`**Baveno VII 2022**: introduced the **rule of five** for LSM, non-invasive diagnosis of CSPH, **NSBB (carvedilol) for CSPH to prevent decompensation** rather than only to prevent first bleed, and the concept of **recompensation**. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**AASLD 2024 Practice Guidance** on portal hypertension endorses LSM 25 kPa or more as CSPH, **carvedilol as the preferred NSBB**, and NSBB rather than EVL for primary prophylaxis when tolerated. [AASLD Portal Hypertension 2024]`,`**PSVD (VALDIG 2019, Baveno VII)** replaces idiopathic non-cirrhotic portal hypertension and allows diagnosis even without portal hypertension if specific histology is present. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`Pre-emptive TIPS criteria were widened to **Child B over 7 with active bleeding**, and **covered stents** are now standard. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**DOACs** are now accepted for PVT in Child A and selected Child B cirrhosis. [Baveno VII 2022]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **Define CSPH and how it is diagnosed without HVPG** - answer with the 10/15/20/25 kPa rule and platelet cut-offs.`,`Common mistake: writing **propranolol to a target heart rate as first choice** - current guidance prefers **carvedilol 12.5 mg/day**, which needs no heart-rate target.`,`Common mistake: offering **both NSBB and EVL** for primary prophylaxis - combination is for **secondary** prophylaxis only.`,`Viva: why is HVPG **normal in EHPVO and NCPF**? Because the block is **presinusoidal**, and wedged pressure reflects sinusoidal pressure.`,`Viva: name the **Sarin classification** of gastric varices and which ones get glue (**GOV2 and IGV1**).`,`Viva: why does the **caudate lobe hypertrophy in Budd-Chiari**? It drains directly into the IVC through its own veins.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs and procedures for portal hypertension`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Carvedilol (preferred NSBB)`,`6.25 mg oral once daily, then 6.25 mg twice daily`,`Increase after 3 days; maximum 12.5 mg/day; lifelong`,`Hypotension, fatigue; reduce or stop if SBP under 90, Na under 125 or AKI; avoid in asthma, heart block`],[`Propranolol`,`20-40 mg oral twice daily`,`Titrate every 2-3 days to HR 55-60; max 320 mg/day (160 with ascites)`,`Bradycardia, bronchospasm, erectile dysfunction, masks hypoglycaemia; same hold rules`],[`Terlipressin`,`2 mg IV every 4 h, then 1 mg every 4 h once bleeding controlled`,`2-5 days in acute variceal bleed`,`Ischaemia (cardiac, mesenteric, digital), hyponatraemia, pulmonary oedema; avoid in IHD, PVD`],[`Octreotide`,`50 microgram IV bolus, then 50 microgram/h infusion`,`2-5 days`,`Hyperglycaemia, abdominal cramps, bradycardia`],[`Ceftriaxone`,`1 g IV once daily`,`Up to 7 days in any cirrhotic GI bleed`,`Allergy, biliary sludge; use local resistance data`],[`Endoscopic variceal ligation`,`Bands every 2-4 weeks`,`Until eradication; surveillance at 3-6 months then 6-12 monthly`,`Post-banding ulcer bleed, chest pain, dysphagia`],[`Cyanoacrylate glue`,`Endoscopic injection`,`For GOV2 and IGV1 bleeding`,`Glue embolism (pulmonary, splenic), ulceration`],[`Anticoagulation (LMWH, VKA, DOAC)`,`Enoxaparin 1 mg/kg SC twice daily; warfarin INR 2-3`,`At least 6 months for PVT; lifelong in Budd-Chiari`,`Bleeding; band varices first; DOACs avoided in Child C`]]},{heading:`Baveno VII non-invasive staging`,columns:[`Finding`,`Cut-off`,`Meaning`,`Action`],rows:[[`Liver stiffness`,`Under 10 kPa`,`cACLD ruled out (without other signs)`,`Repeat in 3 years if risk persists`],[`Liver stiffness`,`10-15 kPa`,`cACLD suggestive`,`Refer; repeat LSM yearly; treat cause`],[`Liver stiffness`,`Over 15 kPa`,`cACLD highly suggestive`,`Hepatology referral; HCC surveillance`],[`LSM and platelets`,`LSM 15 kPa or less and platelets 150,000 or more`,`CSPH ruled out`,`No NSBB; monitor yearly`],[`LSM and platelets`,`LSM under 20 kPa and platelets over 150,000`,`Varices needing treatment unlikely`,`Skip screening endoscopy`],[`Liver stiffness`,`25 kPa or more`,`CSPH ruled in`,`Start carvedilol; no screening endoscopy needed`],[`HVPG`,`10 mmHg or more`,`CSPH (gold standard)`,`NSBB; decompensation risk`]]},{heading:`Causes of portal hypertension by site`,columns:[`Site`,`Examples`,`HVPG`,`Liver function`],rows:[[`Prehepatic`,`EHPVO, portal/splenic vein thrombosis`,`Normal`,`Preserved`],[`Intrahepatic presinusoidal`,`NCPF/PSVD, schistosomiasis, early PBC`,`Normal or mildly raised`,`Preserved`],[`Sinusoidal`,`Cirrhosis (alcohol, HBV, HCV, MASLD)`,`Raised`,`Impaired`],[`Postsinusoidal`,`Sinusoidal obstruction syndrome`,`Raised`,`Variable`],[`Posthepatic`,`Budd-Chiari, IVC web, constrictive pericarditis, right heart failure`,`Raised (free pressure also high in cardiac causes)`,`Variable; acute forms may fail`]]},{heading:`Cirrhosis vs NCPF vs EHPVO vs Budd-Chiari`,columns:[`Feature`,`Cirrhosis`,`NCPF/PSVD`,`EHPVO`,`Budd-Chiari`],rows:[[`Typical patient`,`Middle-aged, risk factor present`,`Young adult, low socio-economic`,`Child/adolescent`,`Young adult, prothrombotic state`],[`Spleen`,`Moderate`,`Massive`,`Large`,`Variable`],[`Ascites`,`Common`,`Rare (transient after bleed)`,`Rare`,`Prominent`],[`LFT/synthetic function`,`Impaired`,`Normal`,`Normal`,`Deranged in acute forms`],[`Doppler/imaging`,`Nodular liver, hepatopetal or reversed flow`,`Patent portal vein, thick walls`,`Portal cavernoma`,`Absent hepatic vein flow, caudate hypertrophy, IVC web`],[`Liver stiffness`,`High (often over 15 kPa)`,`Normal or mildly high`,`Normal`,`High (congestion)`],[`Mainstay of treatment`,`NSBB, EVL, TIPS, transplant`,`EVL, NSBB, shunt for refractory`,`EVL, meso-Rex bypass`,`Anticoagulation, angioplasty, TIPS, transplant`]]},{heading:`Primary vs secondary prophylaxis and acute bleed`,columns:[`Setting`,`First choice`,`Alternative`,`Do not do`],rows:[[`CSPH, no or small varices`,`Carvedilol to prevent decompensation`,`Propranolol`,`Routine screening endoscopy if on NSBB`],[`High-risk varices, never bled`,`NSBB (carvedilol)`,`EVL if NSBB not tolerated`,`Combine NSBB and EVL; use sclerotherapy or TIPS`],[`Acute variceal bleed`,`Vasoactive + ceftriaxone + EVL within 12 h`,`Glue for GOV2/IGV1; tamponade or stent bridge`,`Liberal transfusion; delay antibiotics`],[`High-risk bleeders (Child C 10-13 or B over 7 active bleed)`,`Pre-emptive TIPS within 72 h`,`Rescue TIPS if uncontrolled`,`Wait for rebleeding`],[`After a bleed`,`NSBB plus EVL`,`TIPS for failure`,`EVL alone if NSBB tolerated`]]}],redFlags:[`Haematemesis or melaena in a known or suspected cirrhotic - admit, vasoactive drug and ceftriaxone before endoscopy`,`Systolic BP under 90 mmHg, heart rate over 100/min or Hb fall with signs of shock`,`New confusion, drowsiness or asterixis after a bleed or infection`,`New or rapidly increasing ascites with abdominal pain or fever (SBP, PVT or Budd-Chiari)`,`Acute abdominal pain with ascites and tender hepatomegaly in a young woman on OCP or with polycythaemia (acute Budd-Chiari)`,`Rising creatinine or sodium under 125 mmol/L on a beta-blocker or diuretic`,`Severe abdominal pain with portal vein thrombosis - suspect mesenteric venous infarction`],pearls:[`Normal HVPG is 1-5 mmHg; portal hypertension above 5, CSPH 10 or more, bleeding risk 12 or more, poor outcome 20 or more.`,`Rule of five: LSM 10, 15, 20, 25 kPa - rule out cACLD, suggest cACLD, skip endoscopy (with platelets over 150,000), rule in CSPH.`,`Carvedilol 6.25 mg once daily to twice daily, max 12.5 mg/day - the preferred NSBB because alpha-1 blockade lowers intrahepatic resistance.`,`Primary prophylaxis is NSBB OR EVL; secondary prophylaxis is NSBB AND EVL.`,`Every cirrhotic GI bleed gets ceftriaxone 1 g/day and a vasoactive drug before the endoscope.`,`Sarin classification - GOV1 is banded, GOV2 and IGV1 are glued.`,`Massive spleen, normal liver, well-tolerated bleeds in a young Indian adult - NCPF/PSVD.`,`Portal cavernoma in a child with haematemesis - EHPVO; curative surgery is the meso-Rex bypass.`,`Caudate lobe hypertrophy plus ascites and pain - Budd-Chiari; check JAK2 V617F.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-portal-hypertension-varices-q1`,stem:`A 52-year-old man with hepatitis C cirrhosis (Child-Pugh A, never decompensated) has liver stiffness 27 kPa and platelets 98,000/microlitre. He has no asthma and BP 128/80 mmHg. What is the best next step according to Baveno VII?`,options:[`Screening endoscopy and band ligation only if large varices are seen`,`Start carvedilol and titrate to 12.5 mg/day`,`Measure HVPG before any treatment`,`Repeat elastography in 12 months`],answer:1,explanation:`LSM of 25 kPa or more rules in clinically significant portal hypertension, and Baveno VII recommends a non-selective beta-blocker (carvedilol preferred) to prevent decompensation, without the need for screening endoscopy. Endoscopy-guided banding is for those who cannot take an NSBB; HVPG is not needed when non-invasive tests already rule in CSPH; simply repeating elastography leaves a high-risk patient untreated.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-portal-hypertension-varices-q2`,stem:`Which mechanism best explains why carvedilol lowers portal pressure more than propranolol?`,options:[`Greater beta-1 blockade reducing cardiac output`,`Stronger beta-2 blockade causing splanchnic vasoconstriction`,`Additional alpha-1 blockade reducing intrahepatic vascular resistance`,`Direct inhibition of hepatic stellate cell collagen synthesis`],answer:2,explanation:`Both drugs block beta-1 (lower cardiac output) and beta-2 (unopposed alpha tone constricts splanchnic arterioles, cutting portal inflow). Carvedilol also blocks alpha-1 receptors, relaxing activated stellate cells and lowering the dynamic component of intrahepatic resistance, so HVPG falls more. It does not have a meaningful antifibrotic effect on collagen synthesis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-portal-hypertension-varices-q3`,stem:`A 22-year-old man from rural Uttar Pradesh has had three episodes of haematemesis over 4 years, each settling quickly. He has a spleen palpable 12 cm below the costal margin, no jaundice, no ascites and normal LFTs. Doppler shows a patent, thick-walled portal vein and liver stiffness is 8 kPa. The most likely diagnosis is`,options:[`Non-cirrhotic portal fibrosis (porto-sinusoidal vascular disorder)`,`Extrahepatic portal vein obstruction`,`Compensated alcohol-related cirrhosis`,`Chronic Budd-Chiari syndrome`],answer:0,explanation:`Massive splenomegaly, repeated well-tolerated variceal bleeds, normal liver function, low liver stiffness and a patent portal vein in a young Indian adult typify NCPF, now called PSVD. EHPVO would show a portal cavernoma rather than a patent vein; cirrhosis would give a high LSM and stigmata; Budd-Chiari causes ascites and abnormal hepatic venous flow.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-portal-hypertension-varices-q4`,stem:`A Child-Pugh C (score 11) cirrhotic has an acute oesophageal variceal bleed controlled with terlipressin, ceftriaxone and band ligation. What further step improves his survival?`,options:[`Add propranolol on day 1 in addition to terlipressin`,`Balloon tamponade for 24 hours to prevent rebleeding`,`Pre-emptive covered TIPS within 72 hours`,`Continue terlipressin for 14 days`],answer:2,explanation:`Pre-emptive TIPS within 72 hours (ideally 24) in Child-Pugh C 10-13 or Child B over 7 with active bleeding reduces rebleeding and improves survival. NSBB is started only after vasoactive drugs stop; tamponade is a rescue bridge for uncontrolled bleeding, not prophylaxis; vasoactive therapy runs 2-5 days, not 14.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-portal-hypertension-varices-q5`,stem:`In which condition is the hepatic venous pressure gradient typically normal despite oesophageal varices?`,options:[`Alcohol-related cirrhosis`,`Sinusoidal obstruction syndrome`,`Hepatitis B cirrhosis`,`Extrahepatic portal vein obstruction`],answer:3,explanation:`HVPG is wedged minus free hepatic venous pressure and reflects sinusoidal pressure. In presinusoidal and prehepatic block such as EHPVO and NCPF, the sinusoids are not under high pressure, so HVPG is normal even with varices. Cirrhosis of any cause is sinusoidal and sinusoidal obstruction syndrome is postsinusoidal, so both raise HVPG.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-portal-hypertension-varices-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Define portal hypertension. Discuss its pathophysiology, non-invasive assessment according to Baveno VII and the prevention of variceal bleeding.`,openingLines:[`Portal hypertension is a hepatic venous pressure gradient above 5 mmHg; it becomes clinically significant (CSPH) at 10 mmHg or more, when varices, ascites and decompensation appear.`,`In India cirrhosis is the commonest cause in adults, but non-cirrhotic causes - EHPVO in children and NCPF in young adults - are disproportionately frequent.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and classification`,points:[`HVPG normal 1-5 mmHg; portal hypertension above 5; CSPH 10 or more; bleeding 12 or more.`,`Classify by site: prehepatic (EHPVO), presinusoidal (NCPF, schistosomiasis), sinusoidal (cirrhosis), postsinusoidal (SOS), posthepatic (Budd-Chiari, constrictive pericarditis).`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Ohm's law: pressure = flow x resistance.`,`Raised intrahepatic resistance: 70% structural, 30% dynamic (stellate cell contraction from low NO).`,`Splanchnic vasodilatation (NO, glucagon) raises inflow - hyperdynamic circulation.`,`Collaterals at porto-systemic junctions; Laplace's law explains rupture.`]},{heading:`Clinical features and investigations`,points:[`Splenomegaly, caput medusae, ascites, haematemesis, encephalopathy.`,`CBC (platelets), LFT, INR, viral markers, Doppler ultrasound, endoscopy.`,`HVPG gold standard; normal in presinusoidal disease.`]},{heading:`Baveno VII non-invasive assessment`,points:[`LSM under 10 kPa rules out cACLD; over 15 kPa highly suggestive.`,`LSM 25 kPa or more rules in CSPH; LSM 15 or less with platelets 150,000 or more rules it out.`,`LSM under 20 and platelets over 150,000 - skip screening endoscopy.`]},{heading:`Prevention of variceal bleeding`,points:[`Treat cause; NSBB (carvedilol 6.25-12.5 mg/day) for CSPH to prevent decompensation.`,`High-risk varices: NSBB, or EVL every 2-4 weeks if NSBB not tolerated.`,`Secondary prophylaxis: NSBB plus EVL; TIPS for failure.`,`Acute bleed: vasoactive drug, ceftriaxone, EVL within 12 h, pre-emptive TIPS in high risk.`]},{heading:`Follow-up`,points:[`Heart rate, BP, creatinine and sodium every 3 months on NSBB.`,`Six-monthly ultrasound for HCC; refer for transplant at decompensation or MELD 15.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of porto-systemic collateral sites`,`Baveno VII flow: LSM and platelets to cACLD, CSPH and NSBB`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and classification`,marks:2},{part:`Pathophysiology`,marks:2},{part:`Baveno VII non-invasive assessment`,marks:2},{part:`Prevention of variceal bleeding`,marks:3},{part:`Follow-up`,marks:1}],keywords:[`HVPG`,`CSPH`,`Baveno VII`,`carvedilol`,`EVL`,`TIPS`,`liver stiffness`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-portal-hypertension-varices-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate non-cirrhotic portal fibrosis from extrahepatic portal vein obstruction.`,openingLines:[`Both are non-cirrhotic, presinusoidal or prehepatic causes of portal hypertension common in India, with normal HVPG and preserved liver function.`,`NCPF is a disease of young adults with a patent portal vein; EHPVO is a disease of children with a portal cavernoma.`],answer:[{heading:`Epidemiology and cause`,points:[`NCPF: young adults, low socio-economic status, possibly infection, arsenic, immune causes; now PSVD.`,`EHPVO: children; umbilical sepsis, neonatal catheterisation, dehydration, thrombophilia.`]},{heading:`Clinical`,points:[`Both: well-tolerated variceal bleeds, splenomegaly, rare ascites, no jaundice.`,`NCPF: massive spleen, hypersplenism; EHPVO: growth failure, portal biliopathy.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`NCPF: patent thick-walled portal vein on Doppler; biopsy shows obliterative portal venopathy.`,`EHPVO: portal cavernoma on Doppler; normal liver.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Both: EVL and NSBB for bleeding.`,`NCPF: proximal splenorenal shunt or TIPS for refractory bleeding; EHPVO: meso-Rex bypass.`]}],mustDraw:[`Comparison table NCPF vs EHPVO`],markSplit:[{part:`Epidemiology and cause`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:1},{part:`Investigations`,marks:1.5},{part:`Treatment`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`NCPF`,`PSVD`,`EHPVO`,`portal cavernoma`,`meso-Rex bypass`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Portal hypertension chapter`,`de Franchis R et al. Baveno VII - Renewing consensus in portal hypertension. J Hepatol 2022`,`Kaplan DE et al. AASLD Practice Guidance on risk stratification and management of portal hypertension and varices in cirrhosis. Hepatology 2024`,`Northup PG et al. Vascular liver disorders, portal vein thrombosis and procedural bleeding: 2020 Practice Guidance by AASLD. Hepatology 2021`,`EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines: Vascular diseases of the liver. J Hepatol 2016`,`Sarin SK et al. Prevalence, classification and natural history of gastric varices. Hepatology 1992`,`MoHFW Operational Guidelines, National Viral Hepatitis Control Programme, 2018`]}),o.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatorenal-hyponatraemia`,title:`Hepatorenal syndrome, refractory ascites and hyponatraemia in cirrhosis`,oneLiner:`**Hepatorenal syndrome-acute kidney injury (HRS-AKI)** is a **functional, potentially reversible AKI in cirrhosis with ascites** caused by **splanchnic vasodilatation and intense renal vasoconstriction**, diagnosed by the **ICA criteria** after **48 h of diuretic withdrawal and albumin 1 g/kg** fail and structural kidney disease and shock are excluded, and treated with **terlipressin plus albumin**; **refractory ascites** (unresponsive to or intolerant of maximal diuretics) is managed with **large-volume paracentesis plus albumin or TIPS**, and **hypervolaemic hyponatraemia** with diuretic withdrawal and fluid restriction, correcting **no faster than 8 mmol/L in 24 h**.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`hepatorenal syndrome`,`HRS-AKI`,`HRS-NAKI`,`ICA criteria`,`ADQI-ICA 2024`,`terlipressin`,`albumin`,`noradrenaline`,`midodrine`,`octreotide`,`refractory ascites`,`large-volume paracentesis`,`post-paracentesis circulatory dysfunction`,`TIPS`,`dilutional hyponatraemia`,`tolvaptan`,`hypertonic saline`,`osmotic demyelination`,`AKI in cirrhosis`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**AKI in cirrhosis (ICA)** is a rise in serum creatinine of **0.3 mg/dL or more within 48 h**, or **50% or more from a baseline** known or presumed within the previous 7 days; the ADQI-ICA 2024 update also accepts **urine output under 0.5 mL/kg/h for 6 h or more**. [ADQI-ICA 2024]`,`**HRS-AKI** is AKI in a patient with cirrhosis and ascites that is **functional (no structural injury)** and does not respond to volume expansion; it replaces the old **type 1 HRS**. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`**HRS-NAKI** (non-AKI) replaces type 2 HRS: **HRS-AKD** (eGFR under 60 for under 3 months) and **HRS-CKD** (eGFR under 60 for 3 months or more) - usually accompanies refractory ascites. [ICA 2019]`,`AKI occurs in about **20% of hospitalised cirrhotics**; HRS accounts for about **15-20% of AKI** in cirrhosis, with prerenal AKI and ATN making up most of the rest. [EASL Decompensated Cirrhosis 2018]`,`Among patients with ascites, HRS develops in about **18% at 1 year and 39% at 5 years**; untreated HRS-AKI has a **median survival of weeks**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Refractory ascites** develops in about **5-10% of patients with ascites per year**, and its 1-year survival is about **50%**. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`**Hyponatraemia (serum Na under 135 mmol/L)** occurs in about **half of patients with ascites**, and Na under 130 in about 20%; it independently predicts death, which is why sodium is built into **MELD-Na and MELD 3.0**. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`Indian ACLF data (APASL AARC registry) show **kidney failure is among the commonest organ failures** in acute-on-chronic liver failure, with alcohol the leading precipitant. [APASL ACLF 2019]`]},{heading:`Precipitants and risk factors`,points:[`**Infection - especially SBP** - is the commonest trigger of HRS-AKI; about **30% of SBP** episodes lead to AKI without albumin. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`**Over-diuresis, diarrhoea from lactulose, vomiting and GI bleeding** cause prerenal AKI that may progress to HRS. [EASL Decompensated Cirrhosis 2018]`,`**Large-volume paracentesis without albumin** causes post-paracentesis circulatory dysfunction and HRS. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`**Nephrotoxins**: NSAIDs (block renal prostaglandins that sustain GFR), aminoglycosides, IV contrast; ACE inhibitors and ARBs cause hypotension and should be avoided in ascites. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`**Alcoholic hepatitis** and ACLF are strong precipitants in Indian practice. [APASL ACLF 2019]`,`Risk factors for hyponatraemia: **high diuretic doses, free water intake, refractory ascites**, and use of hypotonic fluids in hospital. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - the underfill story`,points:[`Portal hypertension triggers **splanchnic arterial vasodilatation** (NO, CO, endocannabinoids, bacterial products), lowering **effective arterial blood volume**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Baroreceptors activate the **RAAS, sympathetic nervous system and ADH (vasopressin)** - sodium and water retention cause ascites; free water retention causes **dilutional hyponatraemia**. [Harrison 22e]`,`As disease advances, renal vasoconstriction overwhelms renal prostaglandins and **GFR falls** - HRS - while the kidney itself is histologically normal (it works when transplanted). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Systemic inflammation** (PAMPs from gut translocation, DAMPs from dying hepatocytes) worsens vasodilatation and directly injures tubules - the modern explanation for AKI in ACLF. [ADQI-ICA 2024]`,`**Cirrhotic cardiomyopathy** blunts cardiac output so it cannot compensate for low SVR; **raised intra-abdominal pressure** from tense ascites reduces renal perfusion. [EASL Decompensated Cirrhosis 2018]`,`**Terlipressin** (V1 agonist) constricts the splanchnic bed, restoring effective volume; **albumin** expands plasma volume and binds inflammatory mediators - together they reverse the haemodynamic defect. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`Hyponatraemia in cirrhosis is **hypervolaemic**: total body sodium and water are both increased, water more than sodium - giving saline worsens ascites. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`Brain cells adapt to chronic hyponatraemia by losing osmolytes; **hepatic encephalopathy, malnutrition and hypokalaemia** further deplete them, so cirrhotics are at **high risk of osmotic demyelination** with rapid correction. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and diagnostic criteria`,points:[`**ICA-AKI stages**: **stage 1** creatinine rise 0.3 mg/dL or more or 1.5-2 times baseline (**1A** peak under 1.5 mg/dL, **1B** 1.5 mg/dL or more); **stage 2** 2-3 times; **stage 3** over 3 times, or 4 mg/dL or more with an acute rise of 0.3, or dialysis. [ICA 2015]`,`**HRS-AKI criteria**: cirrhosis with ascites; AKI by ICA criteria; **no response after 2 consecutive days of diuretic withdrawal and albumin 1 g/kg/day** (maximum 100 g/day). [ICA 2015]`,`Plus: **no shock**; **no current or recent nephrotoxic drugs**; and **no structural kidney injury** - proteinuria under 500 mg/day, **under 50 RBC per high-power field**, and normal renal ultrasound. [ICA 2015]`,`**ADQI-ICA 2024** keeps this framework but allows the volume challenge to be **tailored to the patient's volume status** and adds urine output; a **FENa under 0.2%** supports HRS (under 0.1% is more specific). [ADQI-ICA 2024]`,`**Refractory ascites (ICA)**: diuretic-resistant (no response to **spironolactone 400 mg and furosemide 160 mg/day for 1 week** with Na under 90 mmol/day) or diuretic-intractable (complications prevent these doses). [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`Non-response is **mean weight loss under 0.8 kg over 4 days** with urinary sodium below intake; **early recurrence** is grade 2-3 ascites within 4 weeks of mobilisation. [EASL Decompensated Cirrhosis 2018]`,`Diuretic-induced complications that define intractability: **encephalopathy, creatinine doubling to over 2 mg/dL, Na fall by over 10 to under 125 mmol/L, K under 3 or over 6 mmol/L**, and incapacitating cramps. [EASL Decompensated Cirrhosis 2018]`,`**Hyponatraemia grades in cirrhosis**: mild 126-135, moderate 120-125, severe under 120 mmol/L. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`HRS-AKI is usually discovered on **routine creatinine**; symptoms are those of the trigger - fever, abdominal pain, bleeding, confusion. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Oliguria** with weight gain and tense ascites despite diuretics; refractory ascites patients report breathlessness, early satiety and repeated tapping. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`Ask about **diuretic dose changes, NSAIDs, lactulose-induced diarrhoea, recent paracentesis without albumin, contrast and alcohol binge**. [EASL Decompensated Cirrhosis 2018]`,`Hyponatraemia is mostly asymptomatic above 125 mmol/L; lower levels cause **nausea, headache, lethargy, falls, confusion and seizures** - easily mistaken for hepatic encephalopathy. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`Muscle cramps are common with diuretics and hyponatraemia and impair quality of life. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Assess **volume status**: postural BP drop, dry tongue (prerenal) versus tense ascites and oedema (overload). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mean arterial pressure** is often low (under 80 mmHg); a MAP rise of 10 mmHg on terlipressin predicts response. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`Look for **SBP clues**: fever, abdominal tenderness, worsening encephalopathy - tap every patient with ascites and AKI. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`Tense ascites with **umbilical hernia** threatens rupture (Flood syndrome). [Harrison 22e]`,`Asterixis, jaundice and stigmata of CLD; signs of **pleural effusion (hepatic hydrothorax)**, usually right-sided. [Harrison 22e]`,`Check for **leg ischaemia and chest pain** before terlipressin; **SpO2 and lung crackles** for volume overload. [FDA Terlipressin label 2022]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of AKI in cirrhosis`,points:[`**Prerenal (hypovolaemic) AKI** - commonest; creatinine improves within 48 h of fluid/albumin and diuretic withdrawal. [EASL Decompensated Cirrhosis 2018]`,`**Acute tubular necrosis** - after shock, sepsis, contrast or aminoglycoside; **FENa over 2%**, granular casts, raised urinary **NGAL**. [ADQI-ICA 2024]`,`**Glomerular disease** - HBV/HCV-related membranous or MPGN, IgA nephropathy in alcohol cirrhosis: proteinuria over 500 mg/day, haematuria. [ICA 2015]`,`**Obstructive** - rare; renal ultrasound shows hydronephrosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bile cast nephropathy** - deep jaundice (bilirubin over 20 mg/dL). [ADQI-ICA 2024]`,`**Hyponatraemia differential**: hypovolaemic (diuretics, diarrhoea), hypervolaemic (dilutional), **pseudohyponatraemia** (hyperlipidaemia in cholestasis), hyperglycaemia, SIADH from drugs. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Serum creatinine, urea, sodium, potassium** daily in admitted cirrhotics; remember creatinine **overestimates GFR** in sarcopenic cirrhotics. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`**Urinalysis and urine protein:creatinine ratio**, urine microscopy, **urine sodium and FENa**; a urine sodium under 10 mmol/L fits HRS or prerenal. [ICA 2015]`,`**Diagnostic paracentesis** in every patient with ascites and AKI: ascitic **PMN 250/mm3 or more = SBP**. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`**Renal ultrasound** for size, echotexture and obstruction; blood and urine cultures, chest X-ray. [ICA 2015]`,`**24-h urinary sodium** (or spot urine Na:K ratio over 1) checks diuretic adherence in apparent refractory ascites; if Na excretion exceeds 78 mmol/day yet weight rises, the patient is eating too much salt. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`Hyponatraemia work-up: serum osmolality, glucose, lipids, urine osmolality and urine sodium; review all drugs. [Harrison 22e]`,`Pre-TIPS work-up: **echocardiography**, MELD, bilirubin, platelets, Doppler of portal vein, encephalopathy history. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`Sodium restriction **2 g/day (88 mmol)**; do not restrict fluids unless Na under 125 mmol/L; avoid very low-salt diets that cause malnutrition. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`Standard diuretics: **spironolactone 100 mg with furosemide 40 mg** once daily, raised together every 3-5 days to **400 mg and 160 mg** maximum; target weight loss **0.5 kg/day without oedema, 1 kg/day with oedema**. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`**Stop NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors, ARBs and aminoglycosides**; use paracetamol up to **2 g/day** for pain. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`Check **creatinine, sodium and potassium within 1 week** of each diuretic change and every 1-3 months when stable. [EASL Decompensated Cirrhosis 2018]`,`Na 126-135 mmol/L with normal creatinine: **continue diuretics, no water restriction**, monitor. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`Na 121-125 mmol/L: **reduce or stop diuretics** (always stop if creatinine is rising), **fluid restriction 1-1.5 L/day**; refer if falling. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`Recognise refractory ascites and **refer for LVP/TIPS and transplant assessment** - its 1-year survival is about 50%. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`Muscle cramps: correct sodium, potassium and magnesium; **baclofen 5-10 mg** at night or albumin may help. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency`,points:[`**Any AKI**: stop diuretics, NSBB if hypotensive, nephrotoxins and lactulose excess; treat infection; replace volume (crystalloid for GI losses, **blood for bleeding**). [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`**Stage 1B or higher** (or 1A not resolving): **albumin 1 g/kg/day IV for 2 days** (maximum 100 g/day); reassess. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`**HRS-AKI**: **terlipressin 1 mg IV every 4-6 h**, or **continuous infusion 2 mg/day** (fewer side effects); if creatinine has not fallen by **25% by day 3**, raise to **2 mg every 4-6 h or up to 12 mg/day infusion**. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`Give **albumin 20-40 g/day** with terlipressin; stop albumin if pulmonary oedema; continue until creatinine is **under 1.5 mg/dL or within 0.3 of baseline**, maximum **14 days**. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`**Terlipressin cautions** (US FDA 2022 boxed warning): serious or fatal **respiratory failure** - do not start if SpO2 under 90%, in volume overload, or with **ACLF grade 3 and creatinine 5 mg/dL or more**; avoid in coronary, mesenteric or peripheral ischaemia. [FDA Terlipressin label 2022]`,`If terlipressin is unavailable or contraindicated: **noradrenaline 0.5-3 mg/h IV** in ICU to raise MAP by 10 mmHg; **midodrine 7.5-15 mg TDS plus octreotide 100-200 microgram SC TDS** only when neither is possible (much less effective). [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`**Renal replacement therapy** only as a bridge to transplant or for reversible ACLF; **liver transplant** (often simultaneous liver-kidney if prolonged dialysis) is the definitive treatment. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`**Large-volume paracentesis**: remove as needed; give **albumin 6-8 g per litre removed** when over 5 L. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`**TIPS for refractory ascites** (covered stent) improves transplant-free survival in selected patients: bilirubin under 3 mg/dL, platelets over 75,000, no recurrent overt HE, no heart failure or severe pulmonary hypertension. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`**Severe symptomatic hyponatraemia** (seizure, coma): **3% saline 100-150 mL IV bolus**, repeat to raise Na by 4-6 mmol/L, then limit total rise to **4-6 and never over 8 mmol/L in 24 h**. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Tolvaptan** can be used only short term (**30 days or less**), starting at **15 mg** in hospital, with daily Na checks; the US FDA advises against it in liver disease because of hepatotoxicity. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`**Before liver transplant**, aim for Na over 125 mmol/L to limit osmotic demyelination at surgery; hypertonic saline is acceptable in the hours before transplant. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`**NSBB in refractory ascites**: not banned, but **reduce or stop with SBP under 90, Na under 125 or AKI**; carvedilol at most 12.5 mg/day. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Hepatic hydrothorax**: treat like ascites; **no chest drain** (protein and electrolyte loss, infection); therapeutic thoracentesis and TIPS. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`**Long-term albumin** (ANSWER trial, 40 g twice weekly then weekly) improved survival in uncomplicated ascites, and EASL supports it; AASLD 2021 found evidence insufficient for routine use - a guideline difference. [EASL 2018 vs AASLD 2021]`,`**Midodrine 7.5 mg TDS** added to diuretics can improve refractory ascites control and sodium. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`Resource-limited setting: where terlipressin cannot be used, **noradrenaline** in an HDU is as effective; albumin cost is a real barrier - prioritise **SBP, LVP over 5 L and HRS**. [EASL Decompensated Cirrhosis 2018]`,`**Elderly and CKD**: creatinine is low from sarcopenia - small rises matter; cystatin C helps estimate GFR. [ADQI-ICA 2024]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Untreated HRS-AKI: **median survival about 2 weeks to 3 months**; terlipressin plus albumin reverses HRS in about **40%**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Response predictors: **low baseline creatinine** (under 5 mg/dL), bilirubin under 10 mg/dL and a **MAP rise of 5-10 mmHg** by day 3. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`Terlipressin harms: **pulmonary oedema**, myocardial and **mesenteric ischaemia, digital necrosis**, hyponatraemia, diarrhoea, bradycardia. [FDA Terlipressin label 2022]`,`**Post-paracentesis circulatory dysfunction** - renin rise, rapid ascites reaccumulation, hyponatraemia, AKI and death - is prevented by albumin. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`**Osmotic demyelination syndrome** follows over-rapid sodium correction: dysarthria, dysphagia, quadriparesis, locked-in state 2-6 days later. [Harrison 22e]`,`TIPS complications: **hepatic encephalopathy (about 30%)**, heart failure, liver failure, shunt dysfunction. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`HRS recurs after stopping terlipressin in about 20%; retreatment usually works. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer every HRS, refractory ascites and Na under 125 mmol/L** to a hepatology centre; all are indications for **liver transplant evaluation**. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`During terlipressin: creatinine, Na, K daily; **SpO2, fluid balance and chest exam** every shift; ECG if chest pain. [FDA Terlipressin label 2022]`,`During hyponatraemia correction: **Na every 4-6 h**; if overshoot, give 5% dextrose and desmopressin to re-lower. [Harrison 22e]`,`After discharge: weight daily at home, creatinine and electrolytes in **1 week**, then monthly. [EASL Decompensated Cirrhosis 2018]`,`Patients with refractory ascites on serial LVP: plan **every 2-3 weeks**, with albumin; record volume removed to judge salt adherence. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`]},{heading:`Prevention and counselling`,points:[`**Albumin with SBP**: **1.5 g/kg on day 1 and 1 g/kg on day 3** cuts HRS and mortality (see the Cirrhosis topic). [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`**Norfloxacin 400 mg daily** (or ciprofloxacin) prophylaxis after SBP, and primary prophylaxis when ascitic protein is low with advanced disease, reduces HRS. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`Counsel: **no NSAIDs or over-the-counter pain balms with NSAIDs**, report reduced urine, diarrhoea or vomiting; hold diuretics during vomiting or diarrhoea. [EASL Decompensated Cirrhosis 2018]`,`Teach **salt, not water, restriction**: avoid pickles, papad, namkeen, processed food and added salt at the table. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`Avoid **IV contrast** where possible; if needed, ensure euvolaemia. [ADQI-ICA 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2021-2026)`,points:[`**AASLD 2021 Practice Guidance** (ascites, SBP and HRS) adopted HRS-AKI and HRS-NAKI terminology and terlipressin plus albumin as first line. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`**US FDA approved terlipressin (2022)** for HRS with rapid kidney decline, with a boxed warning on respiratory failure; it has long been available in India. [FDA Terlipressin label 2022]`,`**ADQI-ICA 2024** joint consensus: adds urine output to AKI criteria, allows **individualised volume challenge**, endorses FENa and biomarkers to separate HRS from ATN, and frames AKI-CKD overlap. [ADQI-ICA 2024]`,`**Continuous terlipressin infusion** is now preferred where feasible because it causes fewer adverse events than boluses. [AASLD Ascites 2021]`,`**MELD 3.0** (US allocation from 2023) adds albumin and sex to MELD-Na, recognising hyponatraemia and renal dysfunction as transplant priorities. [OPTN 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **ICA criteria for HRS-AKI** - list all six, including the 2-day albumin challenge and the absence of shock and nephrotoxins.`,`Common mistake: giving **normal saline to a hyponatraemic cirrhotic with ascites** - this is hypervolaemic hyponatraemia; restrict water and stop diuretics.`,`Common mistake: **correcting sodium over 8 mmol/L in 24 h** - cirrhotics are the highest-risk group for osmotic demyelination.`,`Viva: why do **kidneys from HRS donors work in a recipient**? HRS is functional vasoconstriction, not structural injury.`,`Viva: name the **two types of refractory ascites** and their numeric definitions.`,`Viva: albumin doses - **1 g/kg** for AKI challenge, **6-8 g/L** for LVP over 5 L, **1.5 then 1 g/kg** for SBP, **20-40 g/day** with terlipressin.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in HRS, refractory ascites and hyponatraemia`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Terlipressin`,`1 mg IV every 4-6 h, or infusion 2 mg/day`,`Raise to 2 mg 4-6 hourly (max 12 mg/day) if creatinine not down 25% by day 3; max 14 days`,`Respiratory failure, ischaemia, hyponatraemia; avoid if SpO2 under 90%, ACLF-3 with creatinine 5 or more`],[`Albumin 20%`,`1 g/kg/day (max 100 g) x 2 days; then 20-40 g/day with vasoconstrictor`,`Until HRS resolves`,`Pulmonary oedema; cost`],[`Albumin for LVP`,`6-8 g per litre removed`,`When over 5 L removed`,`Volume overload`],[`Noradrenaline`,`0.5-3 mg/h IV infusion`,`Titrate to MAP rise of 10 mmHg; ICU only`,`Arrhythmia, ischaemia; needs central line`],[`Midodrine + octreotide`,`Midodrine 7.5-15 mg oral TDS; octreotide 100-200 microgram SC TDS`,`Only if terlipressin and noradrenaline not possible`,`Supine hypertension, bradycardia; low efficacy`],[`Spironolactone + furosemide`,`100 mg + 40 mg oral daily, up to 400 mg + 160 mg`,`Raise every 3-5 days`,`Hyperkalaemia, gynaecomastia (amiloride alternative), AKI, hyponatraemia`],[`Tolvaptan`,`15 mg oral daily, up to 60 mg`,`30 days or less, start in hospital`,`Hepatotoxicity, over-rapid Na rise, thirst; FDA advises against in liver disease`],[`3% saline`,`100-150 mL IV bolus over 10-20 min`,`Repeat to raise Na 4-6; max 8 mmol/L in 24 h`,`Osmotic demyelination, volume overload`]]},{heading:`ICA-AKI staging and action`,columns:[`Stage`,`Creatinine criterion`,`Action`],rows:[[`1A`,`Rise 0.3 or more or 1.5-2x baseline; peak under 1.5 mg/dL`,`Remove precipitants, close monitoring`],[`1B`,`As stage 1, peak 1.5 mg/dL or more`,`Withdraw diuretics, albumin 1 g/kg x 2 days`],[`2`,`2-3 times baseline`,`As 1B; if no response and criteria met, terlipressin + albumin`],[`3`,`Over 3 times, or 4 mg/dL or more with acute rise 0.3, or RRT`,`As 2; ICU; consider RRT as bridge`]]},{heading:`ICA diagnostic criteria for HRS-AKI`,columns:[`Criterion`,`Detail`],rows:[[`Cirrhosis with ascites`,`Clinical, imaging or biopsy`],[`AKI by ICA criteria`,`Creatinine rise 0.3 mg/dL in 48 h or 50% in 7 days`],[`No response to volume challenge`,`2 days of diuretic withdrawal and albumin 1 g/kg/day`],[`No shock`,`Including septic shock`],[`No nephrotoxins`,`NSAIDs, aminoglycosides, contrast`],[`No structural injury`,`Proteinuria under 500 mg/day, RBC under 50/hpf, normal renal ultrasound`]]},{heading:`Differentiating causes of AKI in cirrhosis`,columns:[`Feature`,`Prerenal`,`HRS-AKI`,`ATN`],rows:[[`Trigger`,`Diuretics, diarrhoea, bleeding`,`Infection, LVP without albumin, none`,`Shock, sepsis, contrast, aminoglycosides`],[`Response to albumin`,`Yes, within 48 h`,`No`,`No`],[`Urine sodium`,`Under 10 mmol/L`,`Under 10 mmol/L`,`Over 40 mmol/L`],[`FENa`,`Under 1%`,`Under 0.2%`,`Over 2%`],[`Urine sediment`,`Bland`,`Bland`,`Muddy brown granular casts`],[`Urinary NGAL`,`Low`,`Low`,`High`]]},{heading:`Hyponatraemia in cirrhosis - stepwise management`,columns:[`Serum Na (mmol/L)`,`Diuretics`,`Fluids`,`Other`],rows:[[`126-135`,`Continue`,`No restriction`,`Monitor`],[`121-125, normal creatinine`,`Reduce or stop`,`Restrict 1-1.5 L/day`,`Albumin may help`],[`121-125, raised creatinine`,`Stop`,`Volume expansion with albumin`,`Look for AKI cause`],[`120 or less, asymptomatic`,`Stop`,`Restrict 1 L/day`,`Albumin; short-term tolvaptan in hospital`],[`Severe symptoms`,`Stop`,`3% saline bolus`,`Max correction 8 mmol/L in 24 h; ICU`]]}],redFlags:[`Creatinine rise of 0.3 mg/dL or more in a cirrhotic with ascites - admit and look for infection`,`Oliguria or anuria in decompensated cirrhosis`,`Serum sodium under 125 mmol/L, or any sodium with confusion or seizure`,`Fever or abdominal pain with ascites (SBP until proved otherwise)`,`Tense ascites with breathlessness or a leaking/ulcerated umbilical hernia`,`SpO2 under 90% or crackles in a patient on terlipressin or albumin`,`Chest pain, abdominal pain or cold blue fingers on terlipressin`],pearls:[`HRS is a functional kidney: no proteinuria, no haematuria, normal ultrasound, and it works after transplant.`,`Albumin numbers: 1 g/kg for AKI challenge, 6-8 g/L for LVP over 5 L, 1.5 and 1 g/kg for SBP, 20-40 g/day with terlipressin.`,`Terlipressin plus albumin first; noradrenaline in ICU second; midodrine plus octreotide last.`,`Do not start terlipressin with SpO2 under 90% or ACLF grade 3 with creatinine 5 or more.`,`Refractory ascites = spironolactone 400 + furosemide 160 for a week without response, or complications preventing those doses.`,`Spot urine Na:K over 1 with no weight loss means salt in the diet, not diuretic failure.`,`Hyponatraemia in cirrhosis is hypervolaemic: restrict water, stop diuretics, never normal saline.`,`Correct sodium by 4-6, never over 8 mmol/L in 24 h - cirrhotics demyelinate easily.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatorenal-hyponatraemia-q1`,stem:`A 50-year-old with alcohol-related cirrhosis and ascites has creatinine rising from 0.9 to 2.1 mg/dL over 3 days. Diuretics were stopped and albumin 1 g/kg was given for 48 hours without improvement. BP is 98/60 mmHg, urine is bland, proteinuria is 120 mg/day and renal ultrasound is normal. Ascitic PMN count is 80/mm3 and SpO2 is 96%. What is the next step?`,options:[`Furosemide infusion to promote diuresis`,`Haemodialysis`,`Terlipressin with albumin 20-40 g/day`,`Normal saline 2 L over 4 hours`],answer:2,explanation:`He meets ICA criteria for HRS-AKI: cirrhosis with ascites, AKI, no response to 2 days of diuretic withdrawal and albumin, no shock, no nephrotoxins and no structural injury. Terlipressin plus albumin is first-line. Furosemide worsens underfilling, saline adds sodium and ascites without fixing vasodilatation, and dialysis is only a bridge to transplant.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatorenal-hyponatraemia-q2`,stem:`Which finding argues most strongly against hepatorenal syndrome in a cirrhotic with AKI?`,options:[`Urine sodium under 10 mmol/L`,`Urine microscopy showing 80 red cells per high-power field`,`Absence of response to albumin for 2 days`,`Low mean arterial pressure`],answer:1,explanation:`The ICA criteria require no evidence of structural kidney injury: proteinuria under 500 mg/day, fewer than 50 RBC/hpf and a normal ultrasound. Haematuria of 80 RBC/hpf suggests glomerular disease such as HBV- or HCV-related GN. Low urine sodium, lack of albumin response and low MAP are all typical of HRS.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatorenal-hyponatraemia-q3`,stem:`A cirrhotic with ascites has serum sodium 118 mmol/L, is drowsy but has no seizures, and has no hepatic encephalopathy precipitant. Sodium 24 hours later must not rise above which value?`,options:[`126 mmol/L`,`130 mmol/L`,`135 mmol/L`,`132 mmol/L`],answer:0,explanation:`Cirrhotics are at highest risk of osmotic demyelination because of malnutrition, hypokalaemia and depleted brain osmolytes. The aim is a rise of 4-6 mmol/L, never more than 8 mmol/L in 24 hours, so from 118 the ceiling is 126. Targets of 130-135 would over-correct and risk central pontine myelinolysis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatorenal-hyponatraemia-q4`,stem:`A patient with ascites is on spironolactone 400 mg and furosemide 160 mg daily, keeps gaining weight, and has a spot urine Na:K ratio of 2.5. What is the most likely explanation?`,options:[`Diuretic-resistant refractory ascites`,`Hepatorenal syndrome`,`Non-adherence to sodium restriction`,`Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis`],answer:2,explanation:`A spot urine Na:K ratio over 1 corresponds to urinary sodium excretion above 78 mmol/day, showing that the diuretics are working; continued weight gain therefore means dietary sodium intake is too high. Diuretic resistance would show low urinary sodium below intake. HRS causes avid sodium retention with low urine sodium, and SBP needs a tap, not a urine test.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatorenal-hyponatraemia-q5`,stem:`Which is a contraindication to starting terlipressin for HRS-AKI?`,options:[`Serum sodium of 131 mmol/L`,`Mean arterial pressure of 70 mmHg`,`Ascites requiring paracentesis`,`SpO2 of 88% on room air`],answer:3,explanation:`Terlipressin carries a boxed warning for serious or fatal respiratory failure; it should not be started with SpO2 under 90% or volume overload until oxygenation improves. Mild hyponatraemia, low-normal MAP and ascites are the usual setting of HRS, and a low MAP actually predicts benefit from the vasoconstrictor.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatorenal-hyponatraemia-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Define hepatorenal syndrome. Describe its pathogenesis, diagnostic criteria and management. Add a note on refractory ascites.`,openingLines:[`Hepatorenal syndrome-AKI is a functional, potentially reversible acute kidney injury in cirrhosis with ascites, caused by splanchnic vasodilatation and intense renal vasoconstriction, in the absence of shock, nephrotoxins or structural kidney disease.`,`It is diagnosed by ICA criteria and treated with terlipressin plus albumin, with liver transplantation as the definitive cure.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and types`,points:[`HRS-AKI (old type 1) and HRS-NAKI: HRS-AKD and HRS-CKD (old type 2).`,`ICA-AKI: creatinine rise 0.3 mg/dL in 48 h or 50% in 7 days; stages 1A, 1B, 2, 3.`]},{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Portal hypertension, splanchnic vasodilatation (NO), effective arterial underfilling.`,`RAAS, SNS and ADH activation; renal vasoconstriction; falling GFR.`,`Systemic inflammation, cirrhotic cardiomyopathy and intra-abdominal pressure add to injury.`]},{heading:`Precipitants`,points:[`SBP and other infections, over-diuresis, GI bleed, LVP without albumin, NSAIDs, alcoholic hepatitis.`]},{heading:`ICA diagnostic criteria`,points:[`Cirrhosis with ascites; AKI by ICA; no response to 2 days of diuretic withdrawal and albumin 1 g/kg.`,`No shock, no nephrotoxins, no structural injury (protein under 500 mg/day, RBC under 50/hpf, normal USG).`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Stop diuretics, NSAIDs, NSBB if hypotensive; treat infection; tap ascites.`,`Terlipressin 1 mg 4-6 hourly or 2 mg/day infusion, up to 12 mg/day, with albumin 20-40 g/day, max 14 days.`,`Noradrenaline in ICU; midodrine plus octreotide if nothing else; RRT as bridge; liver transplant.`]},{heading:`Refractory ascites`,points:[`Diuretic-resistant or diuretic-intractable at spironolactone 400 and furosemide 160 mg.`,`LVP with albumin 6-8 g/L over 5 L; covered TIPS in selected patients; transplant referral.`]},{heading:`Prevention`,points:[`Albumin with SBP, norfloxacin prophylaxis, avoid NSAIDs, albumin with LVP.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart of the peripheral arterial vasodilatation hypothesis`,`AKI-in-cirrhosis algorithm (ICA)`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and types`,marks:1},{part:`Pathogenesis`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnostic criteria`,marks:2},{part:`Management`,marks:3},{part:`Refractory ascites`,marks:2}],keywords:[`HRS-AKI`,`ICA criteria`,`terlipressin`,`albumin`,`refractory ascites`,`TIPS`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatorenal-hyponatraemia-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on hyponatraemia in cirrhosis.`,openingLines:[`Hyponatraemia (Na under 135 mmol/L) occurs in about half of cirrhotics with ascites, is usually hypervolaemic (dilutional), and independently predicts death.`,`Treatment is diuretic withdrawal and water restriction, with correction never exceeding 8 mmol/L in 24 hours.`],answer:[{heading:`Mechanism`,points:[`Effective arterial underfilling drives non-osmotic ADH release and free water retention.`,`Hypovolaemic type from over-diuresis or diarrhoea.`]},{heading:`Clinical significance`,points:[`Symptoms mimic encephalopathy; predicts mortality; part of MELD-Na and MELD 3.0.`,`High risk of osmotic demyelination.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`126-135: continue diuretics; 121-125: reduce/stop diuretics, restrict fluid 1-1.5 L.`,`120 or less: stop diuretics, restrict 1 L, albumin; short-term tolvaptan in hospital.`,`Severe symptoms: 3% saline 100-150 mL bolus; limit rise to 8 mmol/L in 24 h.`]},{heading:`Definitive`,points:[`Liver transplant referral; Na over 125 before transplant.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table of sodium bands and actions`],markSplit:[{part:`Mechanism`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical significance`,marks:1},{part:`Management`,marks:2.5},{part:`Definitive care`,marks:.5}],keywords:[`dilutional hyponatraemia`,`fluid restriction`,`tolvaptan`,`osmotic demyelination`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Cirrhosis and its complications`,`Biggins SW et al. Diagnosis, evaluation and management of ascites, SBP and hepatorenal syndrome: 2021 Practice Guidance by AASLD. Hepatology 2021`,`EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines for the management of patients with decompensated cirrhosis. J Hepatol 2018`,`Angeli P et al. Diagnosis and management of AKI in cirrhosis: revised ICA consensus. J Hepatol 2015`,`Nadim MK et al. Acute kidney injury in patients with cirrhosis: ADQI-ICA joint multidisciplinary consensus. J Hepatol 2024`,`US FDA prescribing information, Terlivaz (terlipressin), 2022`,`Sarin SK et al. APASL ACLF Research Consortium (AARC) consensus recommendations. Hepatol Int 2019`]}),o.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatocellular-carcinoma`,title:`Hepatocellular carcinoma: surveillance, diagnosis and treatment`,oneLiner:`**Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC)** is the **primary malignancy of hepatocytes**, arising in **cirrhosis or chronic hepatitis B** in about 90% of cases; it is caught early by **6-monthly ultrasound with or without AFP** in at-risk patients, diagnosed **non-invasively in cirrhosis by LI-RADS 5 features on multiphase CT/MRI (arterial hyperenhancement with washout)**, staged by **BCLC 2022** and treated by stage - **ablation, resection or transplant (Milan criteria)** for early disease, **TACE** for intermediate, and **atezolizumab-bevacizumab or durvalumab-tremelimumab** for advanced disease.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`HCC`,`hepatoma`,`liver cancer`,`AFP`,`alpha-fetoprotein`,`PIVKA-II`,`GALAD`,`LI-RADS`,`BCLC`,`Milan criteria`,`TACE`,`TARE`,`radiofrequency ablation`,`atezolizumab`,`bevacizumab`,`durvalumab`,`tremelimumab`,`lenvatinib`,`sorafenib`,`fibrolamellar carcinoma`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**HCC** is a malignant tumour of hepatocytes and accounts for **75-85% of primary liver cancers**; intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma makes up most of the rest. [Harrison 22e]`,`Liver cancer is the **6th commonest cancer and the 3rd leading cause of cancer death** worldwide, with more than 750,000 deaths a year. [GLOBOCAN 2022]`,`About **80-90% of HCC arises in cirrhosis**; the annual incidence in cirrhosis is **1-8%** depending on cause (highest in HBV and HCV cirrhosis). [AASLD HCC 2023]`,`India has a **lower age-adjusted incidence than East Asia** but large absolute numbers; registries under the **ICMR National Cancer Registry Programme** report the highest rates in the **north-east**. [ICMR NCRP 2020]`,`In India **hepatitis B is the leading cause**, followed by HCV, alcohol and rapidly rising **MASLD**; most Indian patients present at **BCLC B or C**, beyond curative treatment. [INASL HCC 2019]`,`Men are affected **2-4 times** more often than women; median age at diagnosis in India is in the 50s, younger in HBV-related disease. [INASL HCC 2019]`,`Without treatment, advanced HCC has a median survival of **under 1 year**; early HCC treated curatively achieves **5-year survival of 60-80%**. [BCLC 2022]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Cirrhosis of any cause** is the dominant risk factor; the risk persists after HCV cure (SVR) and after alcohol abstinence in established cirrhosis. [AASLD HCC 2023]`,`**Chronic hepatitis B** causes HCC even without cirrhosis, through **HBV DNA integration** into the host genome; high HBV DNA, HBeAg positivity, genotype C and family history add risk. [EASL HCC 2025]`,`**HCV, alcohol and MASLD** cause HCC mainly through cirrhosis, but **MASLD-HCC can arise without cirrhosis** (in up to a third). [EASL HCC 2025]`,`**Aflatoxin B1** from mould on stored groundnuts and maize causes a characteristic **TP53 codon 249 mutation** and synergises with HBV. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diabetes, obesity, smoking** and male sex add risk; coffee, statins and metformin are associated with lower risk in observational data. [EASL HCC 2025]`,`Metabolic and genetic diseases: **haemochromatosis, alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, tyrosinaemia, glycogen storage disease, Wilson disease** (rarer). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hepatic adenoma** with beta-catenin mutation, especially in men and with anabolic steroids, can transform to HCC. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why it looks the way it does`,points:[`Chronic inflammation drives cycles of **hepatocyte necrosis and regeneration**, telomere shortening and accumulation of mutations - the dysplastic nodule to HCC sequence. [Harrison 22e]`,`Commonest mutations: **TERT promoter (about 60%)**, **TP53** and **CTNNB1 (beta-catenin)**; WNT/beta-catenin-active tumours are immune-cold and respond less to immunotherapy. [Harrison 22e]`,`During carcinogenesis the nodule loses its **portal venous supply** and gains **unpaired hepatic arteries** (neoangiogenesis driven by **VEGF**) - this is why HCC enhances brightly in the **arterial phase** and **washes out** in the portal venous phase. [LI-RADS 2018]`,`The arterial supply is also why **TACE** works (chemotherapy and embolic delivered into the feeding artery while normal liver survives on the portal vein). [BCLC 2022]`,`**VEGF overexpression** explains the efficacy of **bevacizumab and multikinase inhibitors** (sorafenib, lenvatinib), and the immunosuppressive tumour milieu explains the benefit of **PD-L1/PD-1 and CTLA-4 blockade**. [EASL HCC 2025]`,`HCC has a strong tendency to **invade the portal vein** (tumour thrombus) - which upstages to BCLC C and can precipitate variceal bleeding and ascites. [Harrison 22e]`,`**AFP** is an oncofetal protein re-expressed by dedifferentiated hepatocytes; only about **60% of HCCs secrete it**, which is why AFP alone is a poor test. [AASLD HCC 2023]`]},{heading:`Staging and classification - BCLC 2022`,points:[`**BCLC 0 (very early)**: single tumour **2 cm or less**, ECOG PS 0, preserved liver function - ablation, or resection if suitable. [BCLC 2022]`,`**BCLC A (early)**: **single tumour of any size, or up to 3 nodules each 3 cm or less**, PS 0, preserved liver function - resection, **transplant if within Milan**, or ablation. [BCLC 2022]`,`**BCLC B (intermediate)**: **multinodular**, PS 0, preserved function - three subgroups: transplant if within extended criteria, **TACE** if well-defined nodules with preserved portal flow, or **systemic therapy** if diffuse or extensive bilobar. [BCLC 2022]`,`**BCLC C (advanced)**: **portal invasion and/or extrahepatic spread, or PS 1-2** - systemic therapy. [BCLC 2022]`,`**BCLC D (terminal)**: **end-stage liver function (non-transplantable) or PS 3-4** - best supportive care. [BCLC 2022]`,`BCLC 2022 added **expert clinical decision-making** and **treatment stage migration** - offering the next stage's treatment when the stage-specific option is unsuitable. [BCLC 2022]`,`**Liver function** is graded by Child-Pugh and, increasingly, the **ALBI grade** (albumin-bilirubin), which separates Child A patients more finely. [EASL HCC 2025]`,`**Milan criteria** for transplant: **single tumour 5 cm or less, or 2-3 tumours each 3 cm or less**, no macrovascular invasion and no extrahepatic spread - 5-year survival about **70%**. [Mazzaferro 1996]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Most surveillance-detected HCC is **asymptomatic** - the reason to do surveillance. [AASLD HCC 2023]`,`Symptomatic HCC: **right upper quadrant pain, weight loss, anorexia, early satiety and fever**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Suspect HCC whenever **stable cirrhosis suddenly decompensates** - new ascites, variceal bleed, encephalopathy or jaundice - especially with portal vein thrombosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tumour rupture** presents as sudden abdominal pain, shock and **haemoperitoneum** - commoner in Asia and Africa. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Paraneoplastic syndromes**: **hypoglycaemia** (IGF-2), **erythrocytosis** (erythropoietin), **hypercalcaemia** (PTHrP), hypercholesterolaemia, watery diarrhoea and feminisation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fibrolamellar carcinoma** occurs in **adolescents and young adults without cirrhosis**, with normal AFP and a **DNAJB1-PRKACA fusion**; prognosis is better after resection. [Harrison 22e]`,`Bone pain or breathlessness suggests metastasis - **lung, regional nodes, bone and adrenal** are the usual sites. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Hard, irregular, nodular hepatomegaly** with tenderness in a cirrhotic liver. [Harrison 22e]`,`A **hepatic arterial bruit** over the liver suggests HCC (also heard in alcoholic hepatitis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Friction rub** over the liver suggests a subcapsular tumour or infarct. [Harrison 22e]`,`Signs of cirrhosis and portal hypertension: ascites (may be **blood-stained**), splenomegaly, jaundice, asterixis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for metastases: **supraclavicular nodes**, bony tenderness, pleural effusion. [Harrison 22e]`,`Record **ECOG performance status** - it changes BCLC stage and every treatment decision. [BCLC 2022]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of a focal liver lesion in cirrhosis`,points:[`**Regenerative or dysplastic nodule** - no arterial hyperenhancement or washout; LR-2/LR-3; follow with imaging. [LI-RADS 2018]`,`**Haemangioma** - peripheral nodular discontinuous enhancement with centripetal fill-in; commonest benign lesion in non-cirrhotic liver. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma** - rim arterial enhancement, progressive central enhancement, raised **CA 19-9**; LR-M category; biopsy needed. [LI-RADS 2018]`,`**Metastasis** - usually non-cirrhotic liver, multiple, rim enhancement; look for primary (colon, stomach, pancreas, breast, lung). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Liver abscess** - fever, target appearance (see the Amoebic and pyogenic liver abscess topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Arterioportal shunt (perfusion pseudolesion)** - wedge-shaped arterial enhancement without washout, no mass on other phases. [LI-RADS 2018]`,`Detailed approach to a mass in a non-cirrhotic liver is covered in the Approach to a liver mass/space-occupying lesion topic.`]},{heading:`Surveillance and investigations`,points:[`Surveillance population: **all cirrhosis** (Child A/B, and Child C if listed for transplant), and **non-cirrhotic chronic HBV at high risk** - Asian men over 40, Asian women over 50, family history of HCC, Africans from early adulthood, or a high PAGE-B score. [AASLD HCC 2023]`,`Surveillance method: **ultrasound with or without AFP every 6 months**; the combination raises early-stage sensitivity from about **45% to 63%**. [AASLD HCC 2023]`,`If ultrasound visualisation is poor (**LI-RADS US visualisation score C** - obesity, MASLD, nodular liver), use **abbreviated MRI or multiphase CT** for surveillance. [AASLD HCC 2023]`,`**Ultrasound result**: nodule under 10 mm (US-2) - repeat ultrasound in 3 months; **nodule 10 mm or more, new thrombus or rising AFP (US-3)** - multiphase CT or MRI. [LI-RADS US 2017]`,`**LI-RADS 5 (definitely HCC)** on multiphase CT/MRI: observation **10 mm or more with non-rim arterial phase hyperenhancement** plus **non-peripheral washout, enhancing capsule or threshold growth** (number needed depends on size). [LI-RADS 2018]`,`In cirrhosis (and high-risk HBV), **LR-5 is diagnostic without biopsy** - one of the few cancers treated on imaging alone; biopsy is needed for **LR-4 when it changes management, LR-M and any lesion in a non-cirrhotic liver**. [AASLD HCC 2023]`,`**AFP over 400 ng/mL** in a cirrhotic with a mass is highly suggestive, but a normal AFP does not exclude HCC; AFP is also high in acute flares, pregnancy and germ cell tumours. [Harrison 22e]`,`Adjunct markers: **PIVKA-II (DCP)**, **AFP-L3** and the **GALAD score** (gender, age, AFP-L3, AFP, DCP) improve early detection but are not yet standard in India. [EASL HCC 2025]`,`Staging work-up: **CT chest**, liver function (Child-Pugh, ALBI, MELD), **endoscopy for varices**, platelets, HVPG or LSM before resection; bone scan only if symptomatic. [EASL HCC 2025]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`The family physician's job is **surveillance**: enrol every cirrhotic and high-risk HBV patient, book the **6-monthly ultrasound and AFP**, and chase missed appointments - adherence is under 50% in most cohorts. [AASLD HCC 2023]`,`**Treat the underlying liver disease**: **tenofovir or entecavir** for HBV, **pan-genotypic DAAs** for HCV, alcohol abstinence and weight loss for MASLD - all lower HCC risk. [EASL HCC 2025]`,`Every new HCC goes to a **multidisciplinary tumour board** (hepatologist, surgeon, interventional radiologist, oncologist); do not start any treatment in isolation. [EASL HCC 2025]`,`Before systemic therapy with **bevacizumab**, the patient needs an **upper endoscopy within 6 months** and treatment of high-risk varices (bleeding risk). [IMbrave150/EASL HCC 2025]`,`Primary care during treatment: watch for **immune-related adverse events** - hepatitis, colitis (diarrhoea), thyroiditis, adrenalitis, pneumonitis, rash - and report early. [EASL HCC 2025]`,`Manage **pain** with paracetamol up to 2 g/day and opioids with laxatives; **avoid NSAIDs** in cirrhosis. [EASL HCC 2025]`,`Palliative care referral early for BCLC C/D; address nutrition, ascites, encephalopathy and family counselling. [EASL HCC 2025]`]},{heading:`Definitive treatment by stage`,points:[`**Resection**: preferred for a **single tumour in Child-Pugh A without CSPH** (HVPG under 10 mmHg or platelets over 100,000), normal bilirubin and adequate future liver remnant (**40% or more in cirrhosis**). [BCLC 2022]`,`**Ablation** (radiofrequency or microwave): first-line for **BCLC 0** and for tumours **3 cm or less** when resection is not possible; comparable survival to resection in tumours under 2-3 cm. [EASL HCC 2025]`,`**Liver transplant**: best for early HCC with portal hypertension or poor function - treats tumour and cirrhosis; Milan, UCSF (single 6.5 cm or less, or 3 nodules largest 4.5 cm, total 8 cm) and **AFP-based models**; **downstaging** with TACE can bring patients within criteria. [EASL HCC 2025]`,`**TACE (conventional lipiodol or drug-eluting beads)**: for BCLC B with preserved liver function; **contraindicated in decompensated cirrhosis, main portal vein tumour thrombus, and creatinine clearance under 30 mL/min**. [BCLC 2022]`,`**TARE/SIRT (yttrium-90)**: alternative for selected early/intermediate tumours and segmental portal vein invasion; **SBRT** for small tumours unsuitable for ablation. [EASL HCC 2025]`,`**First-line systemic therapy (BCLC C and TACE-unsuitable B)**: **atezolizumab 1200 mg IV plus bevacizumab 15 mg/kg IV every 3 weeks** (IMbrave150: median OS **19.2 vs 13.4 months** with sorafenib), or **durvalumab 1500 mg every 4 weeks plus a single dose of tremelimumab 300 mg (STRIDE)**. [EASL HCC 2025]`,`Other first-line options: **nivolumab plus ipilimumab** (CheckMate 9DW), durvalumab or tislelizumab monotherapy, and the TKIs **lenvatinib (12 mg daily if 60 kg or more, 8 mg if under 60 kg)** or **sorafenib 400 mg twice daily** when immunotherapy is contraindicated. [EASL HCC 2025]`,`**Second line**: **regorafenib 160 mg daily (days 1-21 of 28)**, **cabozantinib 60 mg daily**, or **ramucirumab if AFP is 400 ng/mL or more**; or a TKI after first-line immunotherapy. [EASL HCC 2025]`,`Immunotherapy contraindications: **active autoimmune disease, organ transplant recipients** (risk of graft rejection) and Child-Pugh C. [EASL HCC 2025]`,`**Ruptured HCC**: resuscitate, **emergency transarterial embolisation**, then staged definitive treatment. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**HCC in non-cirrhotic MASLD or HBV**: tumours are often large at diagnosis but resection is better tolerated because the liver is healthy. [EASL HCC 2025]`,`**Elderly**: age alone does not bar resection, ablation or systemic therapy; decide on performance status and liver function. [EASL HCC 2025]`,`**HBV patients on chemotherapy, TACE or immunotherapy** must stay on **antiviral therapy** to prevent HBV reactivation. [AASLD HCC 2023]`,`**Child-Pugh B** advanced HCC: limited trial data; selected Child B7 patients may receive systemic therapy, Child C gets best supportive care. [BCLC 2022]`,`**Pregnancy**: rare; AFP is physiologically raised, so rely on imaging; MRI without gadolinium where possible. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: where TACE and immunotherapy are unaffordable, **ablation for small tumours and lenvatinib or sorafenib** (generic in India) remain options; **PM-JAY and state schemes** cover selected cancer packages. [INASL HCC 2019]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Tumour complications: **portal vein tumour thrombus**, rupture with haemoperitoneum, obstructive jaundice, liver failure, paraneoplastic hypoglycaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`Treatment complications: **post-embolisation syndrome** after TACE (fever, pain, nausea - self-limiting), liver decompensation, abscess; **bleeding, hypertension and proteinuria** with bevacizumab; **hand-foot skin reaction, diarrhoea, hypertension** with TKIs. [EASL HCC 2025]`,`Recurrence after resection or ablation is about **70% at 5 years** - mostly de novo tumours in the diseased liver - so surveillance continues for life. [EASL HCC 2025]`,`Median survival by stage: **BCLC 0/A over 5 years** with curative treatment, **B over 2.5 years** with TACE, **C about 2 years** with modern systemic therapy, **D about 3 months**. [BCLC 2022]`,`Adjuvant atezolizumab-bevacizumab after resection or ablation (IMbrave050) showed early recurrence benefit that was **not sustained** on longer follow-up, so it is not standard. [EASL HCC 2025]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer urgently** any liver nodule 10 mm or more on surveillance ultrasound, rising AFP, or new portal vein thrombus in cirrhosis. [AASLD HCC 2023]`,`After curative treatment: **multiphase CT/MRI and AFP every 3-4 months in year 1-2**, then every 6 months. [EASL HCC 2025]`,`Response assessment uses **mRECIST** (viable arterially enhancing tumour) after locoregional therapy. [EASL HCC 2025]`,`On immunotherapy: **LFT, thyroid function, cortisol, glucose and creatinine** before each cycle. [EASL HCC 2025]`,`On TKIs: **BP weekly at first**, skin checks, diarrhoea and weight; dose-reduce rather than stop for grade 2 toxicity. [EASL HCC 2025]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`**Hepatitis B vaccination including the birth dose** within 24 h is the most effective HCC prevention - proven by Taiwan's universal vaccination programme. [WHO 2024]`,`**NVHCP** provides free testing and treatment for HBV and HCV; antiviral therapy lowers HCC risk but does not remove it in cirrhosis. [NVHCP 2018]`,`**Store grains and groundnuts dry** to prevent aflatoxin; avoid mouldy nuts. [Harrison 22e]`,`Counsel cirrhotic patients that **surveillance only works if it is regular** - 6 months, not 'when symptoms come'. [AASLD HCC 2023]`,`Alcohol cessation, weight and diabetes control under **NP-NCD**; screen high-risk families of HBV-HCC patients. [MoHFW NAFLD Guidelines 2021]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2026)`,points:[`**BCLC 2022** update: subgroups of stage B, treatment stage migration, and **atezolizumab-bevacizumab or durvalumab-tremelimumab** as first-line systemic therapy. [BCLC 2022]`,`**AASLD 2023 HCC Practice Guidance**: ultrasound plus AFP every 6 months; **abbreviated MRI when ultrasound visualisation is poor**; surveillance not recommended for non-cirrhotic MASLD. [AASLD HCC 2023]`,`**EASL 2025 HCC Clinical Practice Guidelines**: immunotherapy combinations first line, **nivolumab-ipilimumab** added, refined transplant criteria using AFP, and a stronger role for multidisciplinary decision-making. [EASL HCC 2025]`,`Combining **TACE with systemic therapy** (EMERALD-1, LEAP-012) improved progression-free survival in intermediate HCC, but overall survival benefit is not yet established. [EASL HCC 2025]`,`**LI-RADS v2018** remains the CT/MRI diagnostic standard; contrast-enhanced ultrasound LI-RADS (CEUS) is an accepted alternative. [LI-RADS 2018]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **surveillance of HCC - who, how and how often**: cirrhosis and high-risk HBV, ultrasound with or without AFP, every 6 months.`,`Common mistake: insisting on **biopsy** for an LR-5 lesion in cirrhosis - imaging is diagnostic, and biopsy risks seeding.`,`Common mistake: offering **TACE to a Child C patient** or one with **main portal vein thrombus**.`,`Viva: why does HCC **wash out**? It loses portal supply and is fed only by arteries, so contrast leaves early.`,`Viva: state the **Milan criteria** and name one expanded criterion (UCSF).`,`Viva: list **paraneoplastic syndromes** of HCC - hypoglycaemia, erythrocytosis, hypercalcaemia.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for advanced HCC`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Atezolizumab + bevacizumab`,`Atezolizumab 1200 mg IV + bevacizumab 15 mg/kg IV every 3 weeks`,`Until progression or toxicity`,`Immune hepatitis, colitis, thyroiditis; bleeding, hypertension, proteinuria; treat varices first; avoid in autoimmune disease, transplant`],[`Durvalumab + tremelimumab (STRIDE)`,`Tremelimumab 300 mg IV once + durvalumab 1500 mg IV every 4 weeks`,`Durvalumab until progression`,`Immune-related events; no bleeding risk - choice when varices or bleeding risk`],[`Nivolumab + ipilimumab`,`Nivolumab 1 mg/kg + ipilimumab 3 mg/kg every 3 weeks x 4, then nivolumab maintenance`,`Until progression`,`Higher immune-related toxicity`],[`Lenvatinib`,`12 mg oral daily (60 kg or more) or 8 mg (under 60 kg)`,`Until progression; dose-reduce for toxicity`,`Hypertension, proteinuria, fatigue, hand-foot reaction`],[`Sorafenib`,`400 mg oral twice daily`,`Until progression`,`Hand-foot skin reaction, diarrhoea, hypertension`],[`Regorafenib (second line)`,`160 mg oral daily, days 1-21 of 28`,`After sorafenib tolerance`,`Hand-foot reaction, hepatotoxicity`],[`Cabozantinib (second line)`,`60 mg oral daily`,`Until progression`,`Diarrhoea, hand-foot reaction, hypertension`],[`Ramucirumab (second line)`,`8 mg/kg IV every 2 weeks`,`Only if AFP 400 ng/mL or more`,`Hypertension, bleeding`]]},{heading:`BCLC 2022 staging and treatment`,columns:[`Stage`,`Tumour burden`,`Liver function and PS`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`0 Very early`,`Single 2 cm or less`,`Preserved, PS 0`,`Ablation; resection if suitable`],[`A Early`,`Single any size, or up to 3 nodules each 3 cm or less`,`Preserved, PS 0`,`Resection, transplant (Milan), ablation`],[`B Intermediate`,`Multinodular`,`Preserved, PS 0`,`Transplant (extended), TACE, or systemic therapy`],[`C Advanced`,`Portal invasion and/or extrahepatic spread`,`Preserved, PS 1-2`,`Atezo-bev, STRIDE, other systemic`],[`D Terminal`,`Any`,`End-stage function or PS 3-4`,`Best supportive care`]]},{heading:`LI-RADS categories on CT/MRI`,columns:[`Category`,`Meaning`,`Action`],rows:[[`LR-1`,`Definitely benign`,`Routine surveillance`],[`LR-2`,`Probably benign`,`Routine surveillance; consider repeat imaging`],[`LR-3`,`Intermediate probability`,`Repeat imaging in 3-6 months`],[`LR-4`,`Probably HCC`,`Multidisciplinary discussion; repeat imaging in 3 months or biopsy`],[`LR-5`,`Definitely HCC (10 mm or more, APHE plus washout/capsule/growth)`,`Treat without biopsy`],[`LR-M`,`Probably malignant, not HCC-specific`,`Biopsy (cholangiocarcinoma, mixed tumours)`],[`LR-TIV`,`Tumour in vein`,`Treat as advanced (BCLC C)`]]},{heading:`Focal liver lesions - differential`,columns:[`Lesion`,`Background liver`,`Arterial phase`,`Portal/delayed phase`,`Clue`],rows:[[`HCC`,`Cirrhosis or HBV`,`Non-rim hyperenhancement`,`Washout, capsule`,`AFP may be high`],[`Haemangioma`,`Normal`,`Peripheral nodular`,`Centripetal fill-in`,`Commonest benign lesion`],[`Cholangiocarcinoma`,`Normal or cirrhosis`,`Rim enhancement`,`Progressive central enhancement`,`CA 19-9, capsular retraction`],[`Metastasis`,`Usually normal`,`Rim or hypovascular`,`Hypodense`,`Known primary, multiple`],[`FNH`,`Normal, young woman`,`Intense homogeneous`,`Isointense; central scar`,`No malignant potential`],[`Dysplastic nodule`,`Cirrhosis`,`No hyperenhancement`,`No washout`,`LR-2/3`]]},{heading:`Surveillance - who, how, how often`,columns:[`Group`,`Surveil?`,`Method`,`Interval`],rows:[[`Cirrhosis Child A/B (any cause)`,`Yes`,`Ultrasound +/- AFP`,`6 months`],[`Cirrhosis Child C`,`Only if transplant candidate`,`Ultrasound +/- AFP`,`6 months`],[`HBV non-cirrhotic: Asian men over 40, women over 50, family history`,`Yes`,`Ultrasound +/- AFP`,`6 months`],[`HCV cirrhosis after SVR`,`Yes, continue`,`Ultrasound +/- AFP`,`6 months`],[`Poor ultrasound visualisation (score C)`,`Yes`,`Abbreviated MRI or CT`,`6 months`],[`Non-cirrhotic MASLD`,`No (insufficient benefit)`,`Not applicable`,`Not applicable`]]}],redFlags:[`New liver nodule 10 mm or more on surveillance ultrasound`,`Sudden decompensation (ascites, bleed, jaundice, encephalopathy) in previously stable cirrhosis`,`New portal vein thrombosis in a cirrhotic`,`Rising AFP on serial tests`,`Sudden abdominal pain with shock in a known HCC (rupture)`,`Recurrent hypoglycaemia in a cirrhotic not on insulin or sulfonylurea`,`Diarrhoea, jaundice or breathlessness on immunotherapy (immune-related events)`],pearls:[`Surveil every cirrhotic and high-risk HBV carrier with ultrasound +/- AFP every 6 months.`,`HCC is the cancer diagnosed on imaging: LR-5 in cirrhosis needs no biopsy.`,`Arterial hyperenhancement with washout - because HCC loses portal supply and lives on arteries.`,`Milan: one up to 5 cm, or up to three each up to 3 cm, no vascular invasion, no spread.`,`BCLC: 0 ablate, A resect/transplant/ablate, B TACE, C atezo-bev, D support.`,`Scope for varices before bevacizumab; STRIDE avoids the bleeding risk.`,`No TACE in Child C, main portal vein thrombus or creatinine clearance under 30.`,`Young patient, normal liver, normal AFP, central scar on imaging - fibrolamellar HCC.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatocellular-carcinoma-q1`,stem:`A 58-year-old with HCV cirrhosis (Child A) has a 2.4 cm nodule on surveillance ultrasound. Multiphase MRI shows non-rim arterial phase hyperenhancement and non-peripheral washout. What is the next step in diagnosis?`,options:[`Percutaneous biopsy to confirm HCC`,`Repeat MRI in 3 months`,`Accept the diagnosis of HCC (LI-RADS 5) and stage it`,`Measure AFP; diagnose HCC only if over 400 ng/mL`],answer:2,explanation:`In cirrhosis an observation of 10 mm or more with non-rim arterial hyperenhancement and washout is LI-RADS 5, which is diagnostic of HCC without histology; the next step is staging (CT chest, liver function, endoscopy). Biopsy is reserved for LR-4, LR-M or non-cirrhotic livers. Waiting 3 months is for LR-3/4, and AFP is not needed for diagnosis because many HCCs do not secrete it.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatocellular-carcinoma-q2`,stem:`Which patient does NOT need routine HCC surveillance according to AASLD 2023 guidance?`,options:[`A 45-year-old Indian man with chronic hepatitis B, no cirrhosis`,`A 60-year-old with alcohol-related Child-Pugh B cirrhosis`,`A 50-year-old with HCV cirrhosis who achieved SVR 2 years ago`,`A 48-year-old with non-cirrhotic MASLD and F2 fibrosis`],answer:3,explanation:`Surveillance is cost-effective when annual HCC risk is high: all cirrhosis including after HCV cure, and non-cirrhotic HBV in Asian men over 40. Non-cirrhotic MASLD has a low absolute HCC incidence, so surveillance is not recommended even though MASLD-HCC can occur without cirrhosis.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatocellular-carcinoma-q3`,stem:`A 62-year-old with HBV cirrhosis (Child-Pugh A, ECOG 1) has a 6 cm HCC with tumour thrombus in the right portal vein and no extrahepatic disease. Upper endoscopy shows small varices without red signs. What is the preferred first-line treatment?`,options:[`Atezolizumab plus bevacizumab`,`Conventional TACE`,`Liver transplantation`,`Radiofrequency ablation`],answer:0,explanation:`Portal vein invasion makes this BCLC C, for which systemic therapy is indicated, and atezolizumab-bevacizumab is a preferred first-line regimen in Child A; varices without high-risk features have been assessed, as required before bevacizumab. TACE suits BCLC B with preserved portal flow, transplantation is excluded by macrovascular invasion, and ablation is for small early tumours.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatocellular-carcinoma-q4`,stem:`The characteristic arterial-phase hyperenhancement with portal venous washout of HCC is explained by`,options:[`Increased Kupffer cell density within the tumour`,`Loss of portal venous supply with neoangiogenic unpaired arteries`,`Biliary obstruction by the tumour`,`High fat content of tumour cells`],answer:1,explanation:`As dysplastic nodules become HCC they lose portal tracts and are fed by VEGF-driven unpaired arteries, so contrast arrives early (arterial hyperenhancement) and leaves early relative to the portal-fed liver (washout). HCC actually has fewer Kupffer cells, biliary obstruction does not create this pattern, and fat content affects MRI signal rather than enhancement kinetics.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatocellular-carcinoma-q5`,stem:`Under the Milan criteria, which patient with cirrhosis qualifies for liver transplantation for HCC?`,options:[`Single tumour of 6 cm`,`Four tumours each 2 cm`,`Two tumours of 3 cm and 4 cm`,`Three tumours each 2.5 cm without vascular invasion`],answer:3,explanation:`Milan criteria allow a single tumour up to 5 cm, or 2-3 tumours each up to 3 cm, without macrovascular invasion or extrahepatic spread. Three nodules of 2.5 cm fit. A 6 cm single tumour exceeds 5 cm, four nodules exceeds three, and a 4 cm nodule in a multifocal tumour exceeds 3 cm (though some fall within expanded UCSF criteria).`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatocellular-carcinoma-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Discuss the risk factors, surveillance, diagnosis and stage-wise management of hepatocellular carcinoma.`,openingLines:[`Hepatocellular carcinoma is the primary malignancy of hepatocytes, arising in cirrhosis or chronic hepatitis B in about 90% of cases, and is the third leading cause of cancer death worldwide.`,`It is detected early by 6-monthly ultrasound with or without AFP, diagnosed non-invasively by LI-RADS in cirrhosis, and treated according to BCLC 2022.`],answer:[{heading:`Risk factors`,points:[`Cirrhosis of any cause; HBV even without cirrhosis; HCV, alcohol, MASLD.`,`Aflatoxin (TP53 codon 249), diabetes, obesity, smoking, haemochromatosis.`]},{heading:`Surveillance`,points:[`Cirrhosis (Child A/B, C if listed) and high-risk HBV (Asian men over 40, women over 50, family history).`,`Ultrasound +/- AFP every 6 months; abbreviated MRI if visualisation poor.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Often silent; RUQ pain, weight loss, sudden decompensation, rupture.`,`Paraneoplastic: hypoglycaemia, erythrocytosis, hypercalcaemia.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Nodule 10 mm or more: multiphase CT/MRI; LR-5 = APHE plus washout/capsule/growth.`,`Biopsy for LR-4, LR-M, non-cirrhotic liver; AFP, PIVKA-II, GALAD as adjuncts.`,`Staging: CT chest, Child-Pugh/ALBI, endoscopy, PS.`]},{heading:`Stage-wise management (BCLC 2022)`,points:[`0: ablation; A: resection (no CSPH), transplant (Milan), ablation.`,`B: TACE, or transplant/systemic therapy by subgroup.`,`C: atezolizumab-bevacizumab or durvalumab-tremelimumab; TKIs if immunotherapy contraindicated.`,`D: best supportive care.`]},{heading:`Prevention and follow-up`,points:[`HBV birth-dose vaccination, antivirals, DAA for HCV, alcohol and weight control.`,`Imaging and AFP every 3-4 months after treatment; lifelong surveillance.`]}],mustDraw:[`BCLC 2022 staging and treatment flow chart`,`Surveillance recall flow from ultrasound to LI-RADS`],markSplit:[{part:`Risk factors`,marks:1.5},{part:`Surveillance`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Stage-wise management`,marks:3.5},{part:`Prevention and follow-up`,marks:1}],keywords:[`HCC`,`surveillance`,`AFP`,`LI-RADS`,`BCLC`,`TACE`,`atezolizumab-bevacizumab`,`Milan`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatocellular-carcinoma-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on surveillance for hepatocellular carcinoma.`,openingLines:[`HCC surveillance is the periodic testing of at-risk patients to detect HCC at a curable stage; it is recommended when annual HCC risk exceeds about 1.5% in cirrhosis.`,`The standard is ultrasound with or without AFP every 6 months.`],answer:[{heading:`Who`,points:[`All cirrhosis (Child A/B, C if transplant candidate), including after HCV SVR.`,`Non-cirrhotic HBV: Asian men over 40, women over 50, family history.`]},{heading:`How and how often`,points:[`Ultrasound +/- AFP every 6 months (doubling time of HCC).`,`Abbreviated MRI or CT if ultrasound visualisation is poor.`]},{heading:`Recall policy`,points:[`Nodule under 10 mm: repeat ultrasound in 3 months.`,`Nodule 10 mm or more or rising AFP: multiphase CT/MRI, LI-RADS.`]},{heading:`Pitfalls`,points:[`Poor adherence; obesity limits ultrasound; AFP alone misses 40%.`]}],mustDraw:[`Surveillance recall algorithm`],markSplit:[{part:`Who`,marks:1.5},{part:`How and how often`,marks:1.5},{part:`Recall policy`,marks:1.5},{part:`Pitfalls`,marks:.5}],keywords:[`surveillance`,`ultrasound`,`AFP`,`abbreviated MRI`,`LI-RADS`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Tumours of the liver and biliary tree`,`Reig M et al. BCLC strategy for prognosis prediction and treatment recommendation: the 2022 update. J Hepatol 2022`,`Singal AG et al. AASLD Practice Guidance on prevention, diagnosis and treatment of hepatocellular carcinoma. Hepatology 2023`,`EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines on the management of hepatocellular carcinoma. J Hepatol 2025`,`American College of Radiology. LI-RADS v2018 CT/MRI and US LI-RADS v2017`,`Kumar A et al. INASL Puri III consensus guidelines on management of hepatocellular carcinoma in India. J Clin Exp Hepatol 2019-2020`,`Mazzaferro V et al. Liver transplantation for small hepatocellular carcinomas in cirrhosis (Milan criteria). N Engl J Med 1996`]}),o.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-liver-transplant-referral`,title:`Liver transplantation: when to refer and post-transplant primary care`,oneLiner:`**Liver transplantation** is the only cure for **end-stage liver disease, acute liver failure and early HCC beyond local therapy**; refer for evaluation at the **first decompensation (ascites, variceal bleed, encephalopathy, jaundice) or a MELD of 15 or more**, and afterwards the family physician co-manages **lifelong immunosuppression (tacrolimus-based), its drug interactions, metabolic and renal toxicity, infection, cancer screening and inactivated vaccines** - in India about **four in five liver transplants are from living donors** under the **THOTA framework and NOTTO**.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`liver transplantation`,`LDLT`,`DDLT`,`MELD`,`MELD-Na`,`MELD 3.0`,`Child-Pugh`,`King's College criteria`,`tacrolimus`,`mycophenolate`,`everolimus`,`calcineurin inhibitor`,`CYP3A4 interactions`,`graft rejection`,`PTLD`,`NOTTO`,`THOTA`,`GRWR`,`post-transplant diabetes`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Orthotopic liver transplantation (LT)** replaces the diseased liver with a whole graft from a **deceased (brain-dead) donor (DDLT)** or a partial graft from a **living donor (LDLT)**; 1-year survival is about **90%** and 5-year survival about **75-80%**. [EASL Transplant 2024]`,`India performs the **third-highest number of organ transplants** in the world; about **4,500 liver transplants were done in 2023**, and about **80% of Indian LTs are living donor**, the reverse of Western practice. [NOTTO 2023]`,`India's deceased-donation rate remains **under 1 per million population**, so waiting-list mortality is high and LDLT expertise is among the most advanced globally. [NOTTO 2023]`,`Commonest indications in India: **alcohol-related cirrhosis, HBV and HCV cirrhosis, MASLD cirrhosis, HCC, acute liver failure** (often hepatitis A/E or drug-induced) and paediatric **biliary atresia** and metabolic disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`Globally, **MASLD and alcohol** have overtaken HCV as the leading indications since DAAs. [EASL Transplant 2024]`,`Transplant is governed by the **Transplantation of Human Organs and Tissues Act (THOTA) 1994**, amended 2011, with **Rules 2014**; **NOTTO** runs the national registry, with regional (ROTTO) and state (SOTTO) organisations allocating deceased-donor organs. [MoHFW THOTA Rules 2014]`]},{heading:`Indications`,points:[`**Decompensated cirrhosis**: ascites, variceal bleeding, overt hepatic encephalopathy, jaundice, or **refractory ascites, HRS, SBP** - each is a referral trigger. [AASLD Transplant Evaluation 2013]`,`**MELD 15 or more** - the point at which transplant survival benefit exceeds waiting-list risk. [AASLD Transplant Evaluation 2013]`,`**Hepatocellular carcinoma within Milan (or accepted expanded) criteria** in cirrhosis, including after downstaging. [EASL Transplant 2024]`,`**Acute liver failure** meeting **King's College or Clichy criteria** - e.g. paracetamol: arterial pH under 7.3 after resuscitation, or INR over 6.5 with creatinine over 3.4 mg/dL and grade 3-4 encephalopathy. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`**ACLF** (selected grade 2-3 patients) and **severe alcoholic hepatitis not responding to steroids** in highly selected patients with good psychosocial profile. [EASL Transplant 2024]`,`Indications independent of MELD: **hepatopulmonary syndrome (PaO2 under 60 mmHg)**, controlled **portopulmonary hypertension**, intractable pruritus in **PBC/PSC**, recurrent cholangitis in PSC, **polycystic liver disease**, familial amyloidosis, and metabolic defects (Wilson acute presentation, urea cycle defects, primary hyperoxaluria). [EASL Transplant 2024]`,`Paediatric: **biliary atresia after failed Kasai** is the commonest indication in children. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology and transplant immunology`,points:[`The liver is relatively **immunologically privileged** - lower rejection rates than kidney or heart, ABO-incompatible LDLT is possible with desensitisation, and **HLA matching is not required**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute cellular rejection** is T-cell mediated (portal inflammation, bile duct injury, endotheliitis), occurs in about **10-20%**, mostly in the first 3 months, and responds to steroids. [EASL Transplant 2024]`,`**Calcineurin inhibitors (tacrolimus, ciclosporin)** block calcineurin, preventing NFAT dephosphorylation and **IL-2 transcription**, so T cells cannot proliferate. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`Tacrolimus is metabolised by **CYP3A4/5 and transported by P-glycoprotein** - every inhibitor (macrolides, azoles, diltiazem) raises levels and every inducer (rifampicin, carbamazepine, phenytoin) lowers them, explaining most post-transplant drug disasters. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Mycophenolate** inhibits inosine monophosphate dehydrogenase, starving lymphocytes of guanosine; **mTOR inhibitors (everolimus, sirolimus)** block IL-2 signalling downstream and spare the kidney. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`CNI **afferent arteriolar vasoconstriction** explains nephrotoxicity, hypertension and hyperkalaemia; tacrolimus toxicity to beta cells causes **post-transplant diabetes**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Chronic immunosuppression impairs **T-cell surveillance of oncogenic viruses** (EBV, HPV, HHV-8) - hence PTLD, skin and anogenital cancers. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Scores and allocation - MELD, MELD-Na and MELD 3.0`,points:[`**MELD** = 3.78 ln(bilirubin mg/dL) + 11.2 ln(INR) + 9.57 ln(creatinine mg/dL) + 6.43; predicts **90-day mortality** and ranks the waiting list (range 6-40). [Harrison 22e]`,`**MELD-Na** added serum sodium (bounded 125-137 mmol/L) because hyponatraemia predicts death independently. [OPTN 2016]`,`**MELD 3.0** (adopted by OPTN in July 2023) adds **female sex** and **serum albumin**, caps creatinine at **3.0 mg/dL**, and includes bilirubin-sodium and albumin-creatinine interactions - it reduces the disadvantage women faced. [OPTN 2023]`,`**MELD exception points** are given where MELD underestimates risk: **HCC within criteria, hepatopulmonary syndrome, portopulmonary hypertension, cholangiocarcinoma protocols, recurrent cholangitis**. [OPTN 2023]`,`**Child-Pugh score** (A 5-6, B 7-9, C 10-15) remains a bedside prognostic tool; **Child-Pugh 7 or more** is another referral trigger. [AASLD Transplant Evaluation 2013]`,`In India, deceased-donor allocation follows **state-level MELD/PELD-based lists**; since **2023 NOTTO removed the domicile requirement** and the **upper age limit of 65 years** for deceased-donor registration. [NOTTO 2023]`,`**Living donor criteria**: graft-to-recipient weight ratio (**GRWR**) of **0.8% or more**, donor remnant liver **30-35% or more**, donor age usually 18-55, BMI under 30, minimal steatosis. [EASL Transplant 2024]`,`**Donor relationship under THOTA**: near relatives (spouse, parents, children, siblings, grandparents, grandchildren) donate after hospital committee review; unrelated donors need **Authorisation Committee approval** to prevent commerce. [MoHFW THOTA Rules 2014]`]},{heading:`Contraindications`,points:[`**Absolute**: active extrahepatic malignancy, HCC with macrovascular invasion or extrahepatic spread, **uncontrolled sepsis**, severe cardiopulmonary disease, **ongoing alcohol or drug use without engagement in treatment**, and lack of adequate social support. [AASLD Transplant Evaluation 2013]`,`**Portopulmonary hypertension** with **mean PAP 50 mmHg or more** despite therapy is an absolute contraindication; 35-50 mmHg requires treatment and reassessment. [AASLD Transplant Evaluation 2013]`,`**Anatomical**: complete porto-mesenteric thrombosis without a reconstruction option. [EASL Transplant 2024]`,`**Relative**: advanced age (over 70), **BMI over 40**, severe sarcopenia and frailty, uncontrolled diabetes, CKD, prior major abdominal surgery, psychiatric illness. [AASLD Transplant Evaluation 2013]`,`**HIV with controlled viral load and CD4 count over 100-200** is no longer a contraindication. [EASL Transplant 2024]`,`The rigid **6-month abstinence rule** has been dropped; candidacy rests on psychosocial assessment, insight and **relapse risk tools (SALT, HRAR)**. [ACG Alcohol 2024]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history in the OPD (pre- and post-transplant)`,points:[`Pre-transplant: ask about **decompensating events, hospitalisations, alcohol and substance use, smoking, family support and finances** - these drive referral and listing. [AASLD Transplant Evaluation 2013]`,`Post-transplant: ask about **adherence (missed tacrolimus doses)**, new medicines including **over-the-counter and ayurvedic products**, fever, diarrhoea, jaundice, pruritus and dark urine. [AASLD Long-term LT 2013]`,`**Acute rejection** is usually silent - a rise in ALT/ALP/bilirubin; fever and graft tenderness are late. [EASL Transplant 2024]`,`**Biliary complications** (anastomotic stricture, leak) present with cholestatic LFTs, cholangitis or pruritus; more common after LDLT. [EASL Transplant 2024]`,`**Hepatic artery thrombosis** presents early with graft failure or later with **ischaemic cholangiopathy, biliary abscess and sepsis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Tacrolimus toxicity: **tremor, headache, paraesthesia**, insomnia, diarrhoea, hyperglycaemia, and rarely **PRES** with seizures and visual loss. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Pre-transplant: grade **frailty (Liver Frailty Index, gait speed, grip strength)** and **sarcopenia** - both predict waiting-list and post-LT mortality. [AASLD Frailty 2021]`,`Look for signs of decompensation: ascites, asterixis, jaundice, muscle wasting, **clubbing and orthodeoxia** (hepatopulmonary syndrome). [Harrison 22e]`,`Post-transplant: **blood pressure** (CNI hypertension in up to 70%), weight, BMI, waist, **gum hypertrophy and hirsutism (ciclosporin)**, tremor. [AASLD Long-term LT 2013]`,`**Skin examination yearly** for squamous cell carcinoma, basal cell carcinoma and actinic keratoses. [AASLD Long-term LT 2013]`,`**Lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly or tonsillar enlargement** raise concern for **PTLD**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Inspect the **Mercedes-Benz or J-shaped incision** for incisional hernia, a common late surgical problem. [EASL Transplant 2024]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of abnormal LFTs after transplant`,points:[`**Acute cellular rejection**: early (weeks to months), raised transaminases, often with low tacrolimus levels; confirmed by biopsy (Banff criteria). [EASL Transplant 2024]`,`**Biliary stricture or leak**: cholestatic pattern, dilated ducts on ultrasound or MRCP. [EASL Transplant 2024]`,`**Hepatic artery thrombosis or stenosis**: Doppler shows absent or low-resistance arterial flow. [EASL Transplant 2024]`,`**CMV hepatitis** (1-6 months): fever, leucopenia, raised transaminases; CMV PCR. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**Recurrent disease**: HBV, HCV, PBC, PSC, autoimmune hepatitis, MASLD, alcohol relapse. [EASL Transplant 2024]`,`**Drug-induced liver injury**: azoles, antitubercular drugs, statins, herbal products. [EASL Transplant 2024]`,`**Hepatitis E** (chronic in the immunosuppressed) - relevant in India; test HEV RNA in unexplained transaminase rise. [EASL HEV 2018]`]},{heading:`Evaluation work-up (what the transplant centre does)`,points:[`**Liver**: MELD/Child-Pugh, triple-phase CT or MRI (HCC, vascular anatomy, PVT), endoscopy. [AASLD Transplant Evaluation 2013]`,`**Cardiac**: ECG, **echocardiography with bubble study** (HPS) and RVSP; RVSP over 45-50 mmHg needs **right heart catheterisation**; stress testing or CT coronary angiography if age over 50 or risk factors. [AASLD Transplant Evaluation 2013]`,`**Pulmonary**: ABG (PaO2 under 80 mmHg with A-a gradient 15 or more suggests HPS), pulmonary function tests. [AASLD Transplant Evaluation 2013]`,`**Infection screen**: HIV, HBV (HBsAg, anti-HBc, anti-HBs), HCV, CMV, EBV, VZV, toxoplasma, syphilis; **IGRA or TST for latent TB** and stool for **Strongyloides** in Indian candidates. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**Renal**: eGFR, urine protein; prolonged dialysis or eGFR under 30 for 90 days may need **simultaneous liver-kidney transplant**. [OPTN 2023]`,`**Cancer screening** by age (cervical, breast, colon), **dental review**, bone density, and **psychosocial and addiction assessment**. [AASLD Transplant Evaluation 2013]`,`**Donor work-up (LDLT)**: blood group, liver volumetry by CT, MRCP for biliary anatomy, steatosis assessment, cardiac and psychological evaluation, and independent donor advocate. [EASL Transplant 2024]`]},{heading:`Post-transplant primary care - immunosuppression and interactions`,points:[`Typical regimen: **tacrolimus** (trough about **6-10 ng/mL in the first months, 4-8 ng/mL after 1 year**, lower in renal impairment) plus **mycophenolate mofetil 500-1000 mg twice daily**, with **steroids tapered off by 3-6 months**. [EASL Transplant 2024]`,`**Tacrolimus** is taken **12-hourly at the same times, consistently with or without food**; **never switch brands** without level monitoring (narrow therapeutic index). [EASL Transplant 2024]`,`**Drugs that raise tacrolimus** (toxicity, AKI): **clarithromycin, erythromycin, fluconazole, voriconazole, posaconazole, diltiazem, verapamil, nirmatrelvir-ritonavir, grapefruit**; prefer **azithromycin** and **amlodipine**. [EASL Transplant 2024]`,`**Drugs that lower tacrolimus** (rejection): **rifampicin, carbamazepine, phenytoin, phenobarbital, St John's wort**; for TB use a **rifabutin-based** or rifamycin-free regimen with close level checks. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**Additive toxicity**: NSAIDs and aminoglycosides (nephrotoxic), **co-trimoxazole, ACE inhibitors and potassium-sparing diuretics** (hyperkalaemia), **azathioprine with allopurinol** (fatal myelosuppression), statins with ciclosporin (myopathy - use low-dose pravastatin, fluvastatin or rosuvastatin). [EASL Transplant 2024]`,`**Ritonavir-boosted nirmatrelvir** can raise tacrolimus many-fold - use **molnupiravir or remdesivir**, or hold tacrolimus only under specialist guidance. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`Infection prophylaxis: **co-trimoxazole for PCP for 6-12 months**, **valganciclovir 900 mg daily** for 3-6 months in CMV donor-positive/recipient-negative, and lifelong **antiviral (with or without HBIG) for HBV**. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**Renal sparing**: if eGFR falls, the centre lowers tacrolimus and adds **everolimus** (not before 30 days post-LT because of hepatic artery thrombosis and wound risk). [EASL Transplant 2024]`]},{heading:`Inpatient and emergency issues`,points:[`**Fever in a recent transplant recipient** is an emergency: timeline - under 1 month nosocomial and surgical; **1-6 months opportunistic (CMV, PCP, nocardia, listeria, fungi, TB)**; over 6 months community pathogens. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**Vomiting or diarrhoea** stops tacrolimus absorption (rejection) or causes dehydration with high levels (AKI) - admit for IV fluids and **tacrolimus levels**. [EASL Transplant 2024]`,`**Rejection** is treated with **methylprednisolone 500-1000 mg IV daily for 3 days** and optimised baseline immunosuppression; steroid-resistant rejection needs thymoglobulin. [EASL Transplant 2024]`,`**Hyperkalaemia, AKI, seizures (PRES) or new neurological signs** on tacrolimus need same-day review and a trough level. [Harrison 22e]`,`Never stop immunosuppression for intercurrent illness without speaking to the transplant team, and **stress-dose steroids** are needed if the patient is still on prednisolone during surgery or sepsis. [AASLD Long-term LT 2013]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Vaccines before transplant**: complete **hepatitis A and B**, pneumococcal, Tdap, HPV, and give **live vaccines (MMR, varicella) at least 4 weeks before transplant**. [IDSA Vaccination 2013]`,`**Vaccines after transplant**: **inactivated vaccines only**, restarting **3-6 months after LT** (influenza from 1 month); **annual inactivated influenza**, pneumococcal (PCV20, or PCV15 followed by PPSV23), **recombinant zoster vaccine (2 doses)**, COVID-19 and HBV boosters by anti-HBs. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Live vaccines are contraindicated** after transplant (MMR, varicella, live zoster, oral typhoid, BCG, yellow fever, oral polio); household contacts may take MMR and varicella but should receive **IPV rather than OPV**, and avoid nappy handling for **4 weeks after rotavirus vaccine**. [IDSA Vaccination 2013]`,`**Pregnancy after LT**: wait **at least 1 year** with stable graft; **stop mycophenolate (teratogenic) at least 6 weeks before conception**, switching to azathioprine; tacrolimus and prednisolone continue; manage as high-risk (pre-eclampsia, diabetes). [AASLD Long-term LT 2013]`,`**Children**: monitor growth, school, adherence in adolescence (commonest cause of late graft loss) and avoid live vaccines. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Travel**: no live yellow fever vaccine - letter of exemption; food and water hygiene; carry extra immunosuppression. [IDSA Vaccination 2013]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: tacrolimus levels and specialist follow-up are often far away - the GP keeps a **medicine list, interaction checker and contact for the transplant coordinator**; PM-JAY and state schemes' coverage of LT varies by state. [EASL Transplant 2024]`]},{heading:`Long-term complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Chronic kidney disease**: about **18% of liver recipients reach CKD stage 4-5 by 5 years**, mainly from CNIs, diabetes and hypertension. [Ojo NEJM 2003]`,`**Metabolic syndrome**: post-transplant **diabetes in 10-30%**, hypertension in up to 70%, dyslipidaemia, obesity - cardiovascular disease is a leading cause of late death. [AASLD Long-term LT 2013]`,`**De novo malignancy** is a leading cause of death after 1 year: **skin cancer**, **PTLD** (EBV-driven), oropharyngeal and lung cancer in smokers, colorectal cancer in PSC. [EASL Transplant 2024]`,`**Bone disease**: osteoporosis and fractures, worst in the first year (steroids, pre-existing cholestatic disease). [AASLD Long-term LT 2013]`,`**Recurrence**: HCV recurs universally if untreated (now cured by DAAs), HBV controlled by lifelong nucleos(t)ides, **alcohol relapse in 10-20% (harmful)**, PSC and AIH recur in 10-30%. [EASL Transplant 2024]`,`Neurological: tremor, neuropathy, PRES; psychological: depression, anxiety and return to work issues. [EASL Transplant 2024]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring schedule`,points:[`**Refer for transplant evaluation**: first decompensation, MELD 15 or more, Child-Pugh 7 or more, HCC, hepatopulmonary syndrome, refractory ascites or HRS - **early, not when moribund**. [AASLD Transplant Evaluation 2013]`,`**Acute liver failure** (encephalopathy with INR 1.5 or more, no prior liver disease) - **transfer at once** to a transplant centre. [EASL ALF 2017]`,`Post-LT labs: **CBC, LFT, creatinine, electrolytes, magnesium and tacrolimus trough** weekly in the first months, then monthly to every 3 months for life. [EASL Transplant 2024]`,`**Annually**: BP, **HbA1c, lipids, eGFR and urine albumin**, weight, **skin check**, dental check; **DEXA** at baseline and every 2-3 years. [AASLD Long-term LT 2013]`,`Age-appropriate cancer screening (**cervical cytology yearly**, breast, colorectal; **yearly colonoscopy in PSC with IBD**). [AASLD Long-term LT 2013]`,`Targets: **BP under 130/80 mmHg** (amlodipine first; ACE inhibitor/ARB if proteinuria, watch potassium), LDL by CV risk, avoid NSAIDs. [AASLD Long-term LT 2013]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`**Organ donation**: counsel families, register pledges on the **NOTTO portal**; brain-death certification under THOTA by a board of four doctors, done twice six hours apart. [MoHFW THOTA Rules 2014]`,`**Adherence counselling**: missed tacrolimus doses are the commonest preventable cause of late rejection; use alarms and pill boxes. [EASL Transplant 2024]`,`**Alcohol relapse prevention**: regular screening, **PEth or urine ethyl glucuronide** testing where available, and addiction support - baclofen or acamprosate are liver-safe. [ACG Alcohol 2024]`,`**Sun protection** (hat, sunscreen), **no smoking**, food hygiene (no raw eggs, unpasteurised milk) and safe water. [AASLD Long-term LT 2013]`,`Living donors need follow-up too - **LFTs, liver volume regeneration** and psychological support; donor mortality is about **0.1-0.5%** for right-lobe donation. [EASL Transplant 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**MELD 3.0** replaced MELD-Na for US allocation in **July 2023**, adding sex and albumin. [OPTN 2023]`,`**NOTTO 2023 reforms**: removal of the **domicile requirement** for deceased-donor registration and of the **65-year age limit**, and a 'One Nation One Policy' approach to allocation. [NOTTO 2023]`,`**EASL 2024 Clinical Practice Guidelines on liver transplantation**: support transplant for selected **ACLF grade 3** and **severe alcoholic hepatitis**, abandon fixed abstinence periods, and promote **renal-sparing CNI minimisation**. [EASL Transplant 2024]`,`**ACG 2024 alcohol-associated liver disease guideline** endorses early LT in carefully selected steroid-non-responsive alcoholic hepatitis. [ACG Alcohol 2024]`,`**ACIP** recommends **recombinant zoster vaccine** for immunocompromised adults aged 19 and over, including transplant recipients, and **PCV20 or PCV21** as single-dose pneumococcal options. [ACIP 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **When will you refer a cirrhotic for liver transplant?** - first decompensation, MELD 15 or more, HCC within criteria, HPS, acute liver failure.`,`Common mistake: prescribing **clarithromycin or fluconazole** to a transplant recipient without adjusting tacrolimus - use azithromycin and call the transplant team.`,`Common mistake: giving **rifampicin-based ATT** without a plan - rifampicin can make tacrolimus undetectable and cause rejection.`,`Viva: list the **components of MELD 3.0** - bilirubin, INR, creatinine, sodium, albumin and sex.`,`Viva: why is **mycophenolate stopped before pregnancy**? It is teratogenic (ear, facial and limb malformations, miscarriage).`,`Viva: name **live vaccines to avoid** after transplant and what household contacts may receive.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Immunosuppressants used after liver transplant`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Tacrolimus`,`0.05-0.1 mg/kg oral 12-hourly (or once-daily extended release)`,`Trough 6-10 ng/mL early, 4-8 after 1 year; lifelong`,`Nephrotoxicity, hypertension, diabetes, hyperkalaemia, low Mg, tremor, PRES; CYP3A4 interactions`],[`Ciclosporin`,`Weight-based oral 12-hourly, level-guided`,`Lifelong if used`,`Nephrotoxicity, gum hypertrophy, hirsutism, hyperlipidaemia; statin interaction`],[`Mycophenolate mofetil`,`500-1000 mg oral twice daily`,`Often long term; stop 6 weeks before conception`,`Leucopenia, diarrhoea, teratogenic`],[`Prednisolone`,`Tapering from IV methylprednisolone at surgery`,`Usually stopped by 3-6 months (kept in AIH)`,`Diabetes, bone loss, infection, weight gain`],[`Everolimus`,`0.75-1 mg oral twice daily, trough 3-8 ng/mL`,`Start after 30 days to spare kidney`,`Delayed wound healing, mouth ulcers, proteinuria, dyslipidaemia, pneumonitis`],[`Co-trimoxazole (prophylaxis)`,`480 mg daily or 960 mg three times a week`,`6-12 months`,`Hyperkalaemia, marrow suppression, rash`],[`Valganciclovir (prophylaxis)`,`900 mg oral daily (renally adjusted)`,`3-6 months in CMV D+/R-`,`Leucopenia`]]},{heading:`Common drug interactions with tacrolimus`,columns:[`Effect`,`Drugs`,`Safer alternative`,`Action`],rows:[[`Raise level (toxicity)`,`Clarithromycin, erythromycin`,`Azithromycin`,`Avoid; if essential, reduce dose and check level`],[`Raise level (toxicity)`,`Fluconazole, voriconazole, posaconazole`,`Topical antifungal; echinocandin`,`Specialist dose reduction`],[`Raise level (toxicity)`,`Diltiazem, verapamil`,`Amlodipine`,`Avoid`],[`Raise level (toxicity)`,`Nirmatrelvir-ritonavir`,`Molnupiravir, remdesivir`,`Avoid without transplant team`],[`Lower level (rejection)`,`Rifampicin, carbamazepine, phenytoin, St John's wort`,`Rifabutin; levetiracetam`,`Check level within days`],[`Additive nephrotoxicity`,`NSAIDs, aminoglycosides, IV contrast`,`Paracetamol`,`Avoid NSAIDs entirely`],[`Hyperkalaemia`,`Co-trimoxazole, ACE inhibitors, ARBs, spironolactone`,`Monitor`,`Check potassium`]]},{heading:`MELD versions`,columns:[`Score`,`Variables`,`Use`,`Note`],rows:[[`MELD`,`Bilirubin, INR, creatinine`,`90-day mortality; allocation (2002)`,`Range 6-40; dialysis counts as creatinine 4`],[`MELD-Na`,`MELD + sodium (125-137)`,`US allocation 2016-2023`,`Captures hyponatraemia`],[`MELD 3.0`,`Bilirubin, INR, creatinine (cap 3), sodium, albumin, female sex`,`US allocation from July 2023`,`Reduces sex disparity`],[`Child-Pugh`,`Bilirubin, albumin, INR, ascites, encephalopathy`,`Bedside prognosis; 7 or more triggers referral`,`Subjective elements`],[`PELD`,`Bilirubin, INR, albumin, age, growth failure`,`Children under 12`,`Paediatric allocation`]]},{heading:`Infection timeline after solid organ transplant`,columns:[`Period`,`Typical infections`,`Prophylaxis`,`Clue`],rows:[[`Under 1 month`,`Surgical site, line, pneumonia, C. difficile, biliary leak infection`,`Peri-operative antibiotics`,`Nosocomial pattern`],[`1-6 months`,`CMV, PCP, nocardia, listeria, aspergillus, TB reactivation, HBV/HCV recurrence`,`Co-trimoxazole, valganciclovir, antivirals`,`Peak immunosuppression`],[`Over 6 months`,`Community pneumonia, UTI, influenza; late CMV, PTLD (EBV)`,`Vaccines`,`Like general population unless rejection treated`]]},{heading:`Vaccines around liver transplantation`,columns:[`Vaccine`,`Before transplant`,`After transplant`,`Note`],rows:[[`Hepatitis A and B`,`Complete series early (better response before decompensation)`,`Booster if anti-HBs under 10`,`Check titres`],[`Influenza (inactivated)`,`Annually`,`Annually, from 1 month`,`Household contacts too`],[`Pneumococcal (PCV20 or PCV15 + PPSV23)`,`Yes`,`Yes, if not given`,`Single PCV20 simplest`],[`Recombinant zoster`,`Yes (age 19 and over immunocompromised)`,`Yes, 2 doses`,`Non-live, safe`],[`MMR, varicella (live)`,`At least 4 weeks before LT if non-immune`,`Contraindicated`,`Contacts may receive`],[`Oral polio, BCG, oral typhoid, yellow fever`,`Avoid near LT`,`Contraindicated`,`Contacts: IPV, not OPV`]]}],redFlags:[`Encephalopathy with INR 1.5 or more and no prior liver disease (acute liver failure) - transfer to a transplant centre now`,`Fever in a transplant recipient within the first 6 months`,`Rising bilirubin or transaminases after transplant (rejection, biliary or vascular complication)`,`Vomiting or diarrhoea preventing tacrolimus intake`,`Rising creatinine or potassium over 6 mmol/L on tacrolimus`,`Seizure, headache with visual loss or confusion on tacrolimus (PRES)`,`New lymphadenopathy, weight loss or night sweats after transplant (PTLD)`,`Return to drinking in a transplant candidate or recipient`],pearls:[`Refer at the first decompensation or MELD 15 - not when the patient is moribund.`,`MELD 3.0 = bilirubin, INR, creatinine, sodium, albumin and sex.`,`In India about four in five liver transplants are living donor; GRWR must be 0.8% or more.`,`Tacrolimus is a CYP3A4 drug: macrolides, azoles, diltiazem and ritonavir raise it; rifampicin and anticonvulsants drop it.`,`Azithromycin, amlodipine and paracetamol are the safe defaults in a transplant recipient.`,`No live vaccines after transplant; give MMR and varicella at least 4 weeks before.`,`Household contacts of a recipient get IPV, not OPV.`,`Stop mycophenolate 6 weeks before conception; tacrolimus continues in pregnancy.`,`Late deaths after LT are cardiovascular, cancer and kidney - the GP's territory.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-liver-transplant-referral-q1`,stem:`A 49-year-old man with alcohol-related cirrhosis, abstinent for 2 months, presents with his first episode of ascites. MELD is 13. What is the most appropriate action regarding liver transplantation?`,options:[`Refer for transplant evaluation now`,`Refer only when MELD reaches 25`,`Defer referral until 6 months of abstinence`,`Transplant is not an option in alcohol-related disease`],answer:0,explanation:`The first decompensating event (ascites, variceal bleed, encephalopathy or jaundice) is an indication for transplant evaluation regardless of MELD, as is MELD 15 or more. The fixed 6-month abstinence rule has been abandoned in favour of psychosocial assessment, and alcohol-related cirrhosis is now one of the commonest indications. Waiting for MELD 25 risks death on the list.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-liver-transplant-referral-q2`,stem:`A liver transplant recipient on tacrolimus develops community-acquired pneumonia. Which antibiotic is least likely to cause tacrolimus toxicity?`,options:[`Clarithromycin`,`Erythromycin`,`Azithromycin`,`Ciprofloxacin with fluconazole`],answer:2,explanation:`Clarithromycin and erythromycin are strong CYP3A4 inhibitors and can raise tacrolimus levels several-fold, causing AKI, hyperkalaemia and neurotoxicity; fluconazole also inhibits CYP3A4. Azithromycin has minimal CYP3A4 effect and is the macrolide of choice in transplant recipients, though a level check is still sensible.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-liver-transplant-referral-q3`,stem:`Which variables make up MELD 3.0?`,options:[`Bilirubin, INR, creatinine only`,`Bilirubin, albumin, INR, ascites and encephalopathy`,`Bilirubin, INR, albumin, age and growth failure`,`Bilirubin, INR, creatinine, sodium, albumin and female sex`],answer:3,explanation:`MELD 3.0, adopted for US allocation in July 2023, adds serum albumin and female sex to MELD-Na (bilirubin, INR, creatinine, sodium) and caps creatinine at 3.0 mg/dL. Bilirubin, INR and creatinine alone is the original MELD; bilirubin, albumin, INR, ascites and encephalopathy is Child-Pugh; age and growth failure belong to PELD for children.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-liver-transplant-referral-q4`,stem:`A 30-year-old woman, 3 years after liver transplant with stable graft function on tacrolimus and mycophenolate, wishes to conceive. What advice is correct?`,options:[`Stop tacrolimus and mycophenolate before conception`,`Switch mycophenolate to azathioprine at least 6 weeks before conception and continue tacrolimus`,`Pregnancy is contraindicated after liver transplant`,`Continue both drugs unchanged; they are safe in pregnancy`],answer:1,explanation:`Pregnancy is feasible after 1 year of stable graft function. Mycophenolate is teratogenic (miscarriage, ear, facial and limb defects) and should be replaced, usually by azathioprine, at least 6 weeks before conception; tacrolimus continues with level monitoring. Stopping all immunosuppression risks rejection, and pregnancy is not contraindicated.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-liver-transplant-referral-q5`,stem:`A liver transplant recipient is diagnosed with pulmonary tuberculosis. Why must standard rifampicin-based therapy be avoided or used with great care?`,options:[`Rifampicin induces CYP3A4, sharply lowering tacrolimus levels and risking rejection`,`Rifampicin inhibits CYP3A4, causing tacrolimus toxicity`,`Rifampicin is directly toxic to the graft biliary epithelium`,`Rifampicin is contraindicated with mycophenolate because of marrow suppression`],answer:0,explanation:`Rifampicin is a potent CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein inducer that can make tacrolimus levels undetectable, leading to acute rejection; a rifabutin-based or rifamycin-free regimen with close level monitoring is preferred. It induces rather than inhibits CYP3A4, its hepatotoxicity is not biliary-specific, and its interaction with mycophenolate is minor compared with the CNI effect.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-liver-transplant-referral-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the indications, contraindications and timing of referral for liver transplantation. Outline the role of the family physician in the long-term care of a liver transplant recipient.`,openingLines:[`Liver transplantation is the only definitive treatment for end-stage liver disease, acute liver failure and selected HCC, with 1-year survival about 90% and 5-year survival about 75-80%.`,`Referral should occur at the first decompensation or MELD 15 or more; afterwards the family physician co-manages immunosuppression, interactions, metabolic disease, infection, cancer screening and vaccination.`],answer:[{heading:`Indications`,points:[`Decompensated cirrhosis (alcohol, HBV, HCV, MASLD), HCC within Milan, acute liver failure (King's College criteria), ACLF, severe alcoholic hepatitis (selected).`,`MELD-independent: HPS, portopulmonary hypertension, PBC/PSC pruritus or cholangitis, metabolic disease, biliary atresia.`]},{heading:`Timing of referral`,points:[`First decompensation, MELD 15 or more, Child-Pugh 7 or more, HCC, refractory ascites, HRS.`,`MELD and MELD 3.0 variables; exception points.`]},{heading:`Contraindications`,points:[`Absolute: extrahepatic malignancy, HCC beyond criteria, uncontrolled sepsis, severe cardiopulmonary disease, mPAP 50 or more, active substance use, no support.`,`Relative: age over 70, BMI over 40, frailty; HIV controlled is not a contraindication.`]},{heading:`Indian context`,points:[`About 80% LDLT; THOTA 1994/2011, Rules 2014; NOTTO; GRWR 0.8% or more; 2023 domicile and age reforms.`]},{heading:`Immunosuppression and interactions`,points:[`Tacrolimus + MMF, steroid withdrawal; trough monitoring.`,`CYP3A4 inhibitors raise, inducers lower tacrolimus; azithromycin, amlodipine, paracetamol safe.`]},{heading:`Long-term primary care`,points:[`BP, diabetes, lipids, eGFR, bone, skin cancer, PTLD, cancer screening.`,`Inactivated vaccines only; contacts get IPV; pregnancy after 1 year with MMF switched.`,`Adherence and alcohol relapse prevention; infection timeline.`]}],mustDraw:[`Referral flow chart from cirrhosis to transplant listing`,`Table of tacrolimus drug interactions`],markSplit:[{part:`Indications`,marks:2},{part:`Timing of referral and MELD`,marks:2},{part:`Contraindications`,marks:1.5},{part:`Immunosuppression and interactions`,marks:2},{part:`Long-term primary care`,marks:2.5}],keywords:[`liver transplant`,`MELD`,`MELD 3.0`,`tacrolimus`,`LDLT`,`THOTA`,`vaccines`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-liver-transplant-referral-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on MELD score and its versions.`,openingLines:[`MELD (Model for End-stage Liver Disease) is an objective score of bilirubin, INR and creatinine predicting 90-day mortality in cirrhosis and used to prioritise the transplant waiting list.`,`It evolved into MELD-Na and, in 2023, MELD 3.0, which adds albumin and female sex.`],answer:[{heading:`Original MELD`,points:[`3.78 ln bilirubin + 11.2 ln INR + 9.57 ln creatinine + 6.43; range 6-40.`,`Developed for TIPS outcome, adopted for allocation in 2002.`]},{heading:`MELD-Na and MELD 3.0`,points:[`MELD-Na adds sodium (125-137).`,`MELD 3.0 adds albumin and female sex, caps creatinine at 3.0 (OPTN July 2023).`]},{heading:`Uses`,points:[`Transplant referral at 15 or more; allocation; TIPS and surgical risk; alcoholic hepatitis prognosis (over 20 severe).`]},{heading:`Limitations`,points:[`Underestimates HCC, HPS, refractory ascites; INR and creatinine lab variation; sarcopenia lowers creatinine - hence exception points.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table comparing MELD, MELD-Na, MELD 3.0 and Child-Pugh`],markSplit:[{part:`Original MELD`,marks:1.5},{part:`MELD-Na and MELD 3.0`,marks:1.5},{part:`Uses`,marks:1},{part:`Limitations`,marks:1}],keywords:[`MELD`,`MELD-Na`,`MELD 3.0`,`exception points`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Liver transplantation chapter`,`EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines on liver transplantation. J Hepatol 2024`,`Martin P et al. Evaluation for liver transplantation in adults: 2013 Practice Guideline by AASLD and AST. Hepatology 2014`,`Lucey MR et al. Long-term management of the successful adult liver transplant: 2012 Practice Guideline by AASLD and AST. Liver Transpl 2013`,`Kim WR et al. MELD 3.0: the Model for End-Stage Liver Disease updated for the modern era. Gastroenterology 2021; OPTN policy 2023`,`Rubin LG et al. 2013 IDSA clinical practice guideline for vaccination of the immunocompromised host. Clin Infect Dis 2014; ACIP adult schedule 2024`,`MoHFW. Transplantation of Human Organs and Tissues Act 1994 (amended 2011) and Rules 2014; NOTTO annual report 2023`,`AST Infectious Diseases Community of Practice guidelines. Clin Transplant 2019`]});var s=[];s.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-gallstones-biliary`,title:`Gallstone disease, cholecystitis and cholangitis`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Cholelithiasis** is stone formation in the gallbladder from **cholesterol supersaturation, nucleation and gallbladder stasis**; it presents as **biliary colic**, **acute cholecystitis** (graded by **Tokyo 2018**), **choledocholithiasis** (risk-stratified by **ASGE 2019**) and **acute cholangitis**, which needs **antibiotics plus timely biliary drainage by ERCP** - and in North India long-standing stones carry the added shadow of **gallbladder cancer**.`,keywords:[`cholelithiasis`,`gallstones`,`biliary colic`,`acute calculous cholecystitis`,`acalculous cholecystitis`,`choledocholithiasis`,`CBD stone`,`acute cholangitis`,`Charcot triad`,`Reynolds pentad`,`Murphy sign`,`Tokyo guidelines TG18`,`ASGE 2019`,`ERCP`,`MRCP`,`EUS`,`HIDA scan`,`Mirizzi syndrome`,`gallstone ileus`,`gallbladder cancer`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Cholelithiasis** means stones in the gallbladder; **choledocholithiasis** means stones in the common bile duct (CBD), which are secondary (migrated from the gallbladder) in most adults and primary (formed in the duct) behind strictures or in recurrent pyogenic cholangitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Gallstones are found in **10-15% of adults in Western countries**; about **80% remain asymptomatic** for life, and only **1-4% per year** develop biliary colic or a complication. [Harrison 22e]`,`India has a marked **north-south gradient**: community ultrasound surveys found gallstones in about **6% of adults in Kashmir and the North** against about **1-2% in South India**, with the highest load along the **Gangetic belt** (Uttar Pradesh, Bihar, West Bengal, Assam). [Khuroo, Gut 1989; ISG]`,`**Cholesterol (mixed) stones** predominate in North India and the West (about 80%), whereas **pigment stones** are relatively commoner in South India and in haemolytic disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Laparoscopic cholecystectomy** is one of the commonest elective operations in Indian district hospitals and is covered under **PM-JAY** packages. [NHA PM-JAY HBP 2022]`,`**Acute cholecystitis** develops in about **10-20%** of untreated symptomatic patients, and **CBD stones** coexist in about **10-15%** of patients undergoing cholecystectomy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute cholangitis** carries a mortality of under 5% when drained in time but **20-30% or more** in severe (Grade III) disease with delayed drainage. [TG18]`,`**Gallbladder cancer (GBC)** incidence in women of North and North-East India is **among the highest in the world**, and **gallstones are present in 70-90%** of patients with GBC. [ICMR-NCDIR PBCR 2020]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`The classic **'5 F' profile - female, fertile, fat, forty, fair** - reflects oestrogen raising biliary cholesterol secretion and progesterone slowing gallbladder emptying. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pregnancy and oral oestrogen** (combined pill, HRT) raise risk; sludge appears in up to **30% of pregnancies** and often clears post-partum. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Obesity, metabolic syndrome, diabetes and MASLD** raise risk, but so does **rapid weight loss** (very-low-calorie diets, bariatric surgery) - up to **30% form stones** within 6 months of bariatric surgery. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drugs**: ceftriaxone (calcium-ceftriaxone sludge, 'pseudolithiasis'), octreotide, fibrates (clofibrate), oestrogens and long-term **total parenteral nutrition** (gallbladder stasis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Black pigment stones** form with **chronic haemolysis** (sickle cell disease, thalassaemia, hereditary spherocytosis), **cirrhosis** and **ileal disease or resection** (enterohepatic cycling of unconjugated bilirubin). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Brown pigment stones** form in the ducts with **biliary infection and stasis** - parasites (Ascaris, Clonorchis), strictures and recurrent pyogenic cholangitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Genetic factors**: variants in the hepatic cholesterol transporter **ABCG5/ABCG8** and a strong family history; high-risk ethnic groups include Native Americans and North Indians. [Harrison 22e]`,`Indian dietary hypotheses for North India (high-fat ghee diet, low fibre, contaminated water) are plausible but unproven; age and female sex remain the strongest risks. [ISG]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Cholesterol is held in bile by **bile salts and phospholipid (lecithin) micelles**; when cholesterol exceeds their carrying capacity, bile becomes **supersaturated (lithogenic)** - the Admirand-Small triangle. [Harrison 22e]`,`Stone formation needs **three defects together**: cholesterol supersaturation, accelerated **nucleation** (mucin glycoprotein from the gallbladder wall acts as a scaffold) and **gallbladder hypomotility** (stasis allows crystals to grow). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Biliary colic** is not colic: a stone impacts in the **cystic duct or Hartmann pouch** after a fatty meal (cholecystokinin-driven contraction), giving **steady visceral pain** that ends when the stone falls back. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute cholecystitis** follows **persistent cystic duct obstruction**: trapped bile, lysolecithin (from phospholipase A) and prostaglandins inflame the wall; bacterial infection (E. coli, Klebsiella, Enterococcus, anaerobes) is **secondary**. [Harrison 22e]`,`When inflammation touches the **parietal peritoneum**, pain moves from the epigastrium to the **right upper quadrant** and **Murphy sign** appears; wall ischaemia leads to **gangrene, empyema and perforation**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute cholangitis** = **obstruction plus infection** of the bile duct: raised intraductal pressure (above about **20-25 cm H2O**) drives bacteria and endotoxin across hepatocyte tight junctions into blood (**cholangiovenous reflux**), causing rapid sepsis - hence 'drain the duct' is the cure. [TG18]`,`A stone at the **ampulla** can block the pancreatic duct as well, so the same CBD stone causes **jaundice, cholangitis and biliary pancreatitis** (see the acute pancreatitis topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chronic inflammation from long-standing large stones** drives metaplasia-dysplasia-carcinoma in the gallbladder wall - the link to **gallbladder cancer**. [ICMR GBC consensus]`]},{heading:`Classification and grading - Tokyo 2018 (TG18)`,points:[`**TG18 acute cholecystitis diagnosis**: **A** local inflammation (Murphy sign, RUQ mass/pain/tenderness), **B** systemic inflammation (fever, raised CRP, raised WBC), **C** imaging findings; **suspected = A + B, definite = A + B + C**. [TG18]`,`**Cholecystitis Grade III (severe)**: dysfunction of any organ - hypotension needing noradrenaline or dopamine 5 mcg/kg/min or more, reduced consciousness, **PaO2/FiO2 under 300**, oliguria or **creatinine over 2.0 mg/dL**, **PT-INR over 1.5**, **platelets under 100,000/mm3**. [TG18]`,`**Cholecystitis Grade II (moderate)**: any one of **WBC over 18,000/mm3**, palpable tender RUQ mass, **symptoms for more than 72 hours**, or marked local inflammation (gangrenous or emphysematous cholecystitis, pericholecystic or hepatic abscess, biliary peritonitis). [TG18]`,`**Cholecystitis Grade I (mild)**: neither Grade II nor III - healthy patient, mild local inflammation, cholecystectomy safe. [TG18]`,`**TG18 cholangitis diagnosis**: **A** systemic inflammation (fever over 38 °C or rigors; WBC under 4,000 or over 10,000, CRP 1 mg/dL or more), **B** cholestasis (bilirubin 2 mg/dL or more; liver enzymes over 1.5 times ULN), **C** imaging (biliary dilatation or a cause - stone, stricture, stent); **definite = A + B + C**. [TG18]`,`**Cholangitis Grade II (moderate)**: any **two** of WBC over 12,000 or under 4,000, fever 39 °C or more, **age 75 years or more**, **bilirubin 5 mg/dL or more**, albumin under 0.7 times the lower limit of normal; **Grade III** uses the same organ-dysfunction list as cholecystitis. [TG18]`,`**Charcot triad** (fever, RUQ pain, jaundice) has high specificity but **only about 26% sensitivity**, which is why TG13/TG18 replaced it with the A-B-C criteria; **Reynolds pentad** adds shock and confusion. [TG18]`,`**Mirizzi syndrome** (stone in Hartmann pouch or cystic duct compressing the common hepatic duct) is graded by **Csendes types I-V** by the size of the cholecystobiliary fistula. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Stone types**: cholesterol (yellow, radiolucent), black pigment (calcium bilirubinate, often radio-opaque, haemolysis/cirrhosis) and brown pigment (soft, ductal, infection); only **10-15% of gallstones are radio-opaque** on plain X-ray. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Biliary colic**: steady epigastric or RUQ pain building over minutes, lasting **30 minutes to 6 hours**, often 1-3 hours after a fatty or heavy meal or at night, radiating to the **right scapula or shoulder tip**, with nausea and vomiting. [Harrison 22e]`,`Pain lasting **more than 6 hours**, or with fever, suggests **acute cholecystitis** rather than simple colic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Flatulence, bloating, fatty food intolerance and belching** are not gallstone symptoms - they persist after cholecystectomy, so stones found while investigating dyspepsia are usually incidental. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Choledocholithiasis** presents with colic plus **fluctuating jaundice, dark urine and pale stools**; pain with intermittent jaundice favours stone, **painless progressive jaundice** favours malignancy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cholangitis** presents with fever with **rigors**, RUQ pain and jaundice; elderly patients may present with only **confusion, hypotension or falls**. [TG18]`,`**Elderly and diabetic patients** may have minimal pain and no fever even with **gangrenous or emphysematous cholecystitis** - a low threshold for imaging is needed. [WSES 2020]`,`In a North Indian woman over 50 with long-standing stones, **new constant pain, anorexia, weight loss or jaundice** must raise suspicion of **gallbladder cancer**. [ICMR GBC consensus]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Murphy sign**: inspiratory arrest when the examiner's fingers press under the right costal margin during deep inspiration - sensitivity about 65%, lower in the elderly. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sonographic Murphy sign** (maximal tenderness over the gallbladder under the probe) is more reliable than the bedside sign. [TG18]`,`A **tender RUQ mass** suggests a distended inflamed gallbladder, empyema or an omental phlegmon - a Grade II feature. [TG18]`,`**Courvoisier law**: a palpable, non-tender gallbladder in a jaundiced patient is **unlikely to be due to stones** (a chronically fibrosed stone-bearing gallbladder cannot distend) - think periampullary or pancreatic head cancer. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Boas sign** (hyperaesthesia below the right scapula) is a classical but rarely useful sign of cholecystitis. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Jaundice, fever and hypotension** together mean cholangitis with sepsis until proved otherwise; check capillary refill, urine output and sensorium (qSOFA). [TG18]`,`**Generalised peritonitis** suggests gallbladder perforation; a **hard, irregular RUQ mass with hepatomegaly** suggests gallbladder cancer. [Bailey and Love 28e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Peptic ulcer disease and functional dyspepsia** - pain related to hunger or meals, relieved by antacids, normal ultrasound (see the acid peptic disease topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute pancreatitis** - pain radiating to the back, **lipase over 3 times ULN**; but gallstones are its commonest cause in India, so both may coexist. [ACG 2024]`,`**Inferior wall myocardial infarction** - always take an **ECG** in a diabetic or elderly patient with epigastric pain. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Right lower lobe pneumonia, acute hepatitis, liver abscess and Fitz-Hugh-Curtis perihepatitis** all give RUQ pain and fever. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Right renal colic and pyelonephritis** - loin pain radiating to the groin, haematuria, renal angle tenderness. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute appendicitis (high retrocaecal or in pregnancy)** can mimic cholecystitis. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`For obstructive jaundice: **periampullary or pancreatic cancer, cholangiocarcinoma, GBC, benign stricture, PSC and choledochal cyst** - imaging decides. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Abdominal ultrasound** is the first test: sensitivity **over 95% for gallbladder stones** (mobile echogenic foci with posterior acoustic shadowing); cholecystitis signs are **wall over 4 mm**, distension, pericholecystic fluid and sonographic Murphy sign. [TG18]`,`**CBD diameter**: normal up to about **6 mm** with the gallbladder in situ (slightly more in the elderly) and up to **8-10 mm after cholecystectomy**; ultrasound sees CBD stones in only about **50%**. [ASGE 2019]`,`**Blood tests**: CBC, CRP, bilirubin (direct), ALP, GGT, ALT/AST, **lipase**, creatinine, INR, lactate and **blood cultures before antibiotics** in suspected cholangitis. [TG18]`,`A transient **ALT/AST rise to several hundred IU/L** within 24-48 hours can occur with a passing CBD stone and may mimic hepatitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HIDA (cholescintigraphy)** is the most accurate test for acute cholecystitis (sensitivity about 96%) - non-filling of the gallbladder at 4 hours (or 30 minutes after morphine) confirms cystic duct obstruction; used when ultrasound is equivocal. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CECT abdomen** is for complications (gangrene, emphysema, perforation, abscess) and for suspected malignancy; it misses many cholesterol stones. [TG18]`,`**MRCP** (sensitivity about 90% for CBD stones, poorer under 5 mm) and **EUS** (the most sensitive test for small CBD stones and microlithiasis) are used for **intermediate-risk** choledocholithiasis. [ASGE 2019]`,`**ERCP** is now a **therapeutic, not diagnostic, test** - reserve it for high-probability CBD stones or cholangitis because of its **post-ERCP pancreatitis risk of about 3-10%**. [ASGE 2019]`,`**CA 19-9 and CECT/MRI with staging** are needed when imaging shows gallbladder wall thickening, a mass or a polyp over 10 mm; CA 19-9 is also raised by cholangitis, so interpret after drainage. [ICMR GBC consensus]`]},{heading:`Choledocholithiasis risk - ASGE 2019`,points:[`**High probability** (any one): **CBD stone seen on ultrasound or cross-sectional imaging**, **total bilirubin over 4 mg/dL plus a dilated CBD**, or **ascending cholangitis** - go straight to **ERCP** (pre-operative ERCP or laparoscopic CBD exploration). [ASGE 2019]`,`**Intermediate probability** (any one): **abnormal liver biochemistry**, **age over 55 years**, or a **dilated CBD** on imaging - confirm with **EUS or MRCP** (or intra-operative cholangiography/laparoscopic ultrasound) before ERCP. [ASGE 2019]`,`**Low probability**: none of the above - proceed to **cholecystectomy** without further ductal imaging. [ASGE 2019]`,`ASGE 2019 dropped the 2010 use of **gallstone pancreatitis** and bilirubin 1.8-4 mg/dL as predictors, which over-referred patients to ERCP. [ASGE 2019]`,`**ESGE 2019** is similar: ERCP for a CBD stone seen on ultrasound or cholangitis; EUS/MRCP for persistent suspicion; and **removal of all CBD stones, even asymptomatic ones**. [ESGE 2019]`,`**Single-stage laparoscopic cholecystectomy with CBD exploration** is as effective as two-stage ERCP then cholecystectomy, with shorter stay, where surgical expertise exists. [ESGE 2019]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Asymptomatic gallstones**: reassure and **do not operate routinely** - lifetime complication risk is low and surgical risk exceeds benefit. [EASL 2016]`,`Exceptions where **prophylactic cholecystectomy** is advised: **porcelain gallbladder**, **stones over 3 cm**, **gallbladder polyp 10 mm or more**, coexisting polyp with stones, haemolytic anaemia (sickle cell), and planned bariatric or other upper-abdominal surgery. [EASL 2016; ESGAR 2022]`,`**In high-GBC regions of North and North-East India**, many Indian surgeons offer cholecystectomy for long-standing or large asymptomatic stones in women; this is a regional practice argued from GBC risk, not a universal guideline recommendation. [ICMR GBC consensus]`,`**Biliary colic** - analgesia with an NSAID: **diclofenac 75 mg IM** once (or 50 mg oral 8-hourly) or **ketorolac 30 mg IV/IM**; NSAIDs relieve pain as well as opioids and **reduce progression to cholecystitis**. [EASL 2016]`,`Avoid **antispasmodics alone** (hyoscine is less effective than NSAIDs) and avoid NSAIDs in CKD, active peptic ulcer, late pregnancy and dehydration. [EASL 2016]`,`After **one attack of uncomplicated biliary colic**, refer for **elective laparoscopic cholecystectomy** - about 50-70% have recurrent pain within 2 years. [EASL 2016]`,`**Ursodeoxycholic acid (UDCA) dissolution**, 10-15 mg/kg/day orally for 6-24 months, works only for **small (under 5-10 mm) radiolucent cholesterol stones in a functioning gallbladder**; about **50% recur within 5 years**, so it is rarely used. [EASL 2016]`,`**UDCA 500-600 mg/day for 6 months after bariatric surgery** reduces new stone formation. [EASL 2016]`,`**Diet**: no specific diet dissolves stones; small low-fat meals may reduce colic while awaiting surgery; regular meals and gradual weight loss reduce stone formation. [EASL 2016]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Admit** all suspected acute cholecystitis and cholangitis: nil by mouth or clear fluids, **IV crystalloid**, analgesia, blood cultures, and **antibiotics within 1 hour if septic** (within 6 hours otherwise). [TG18]`,`**Grade I cholecystitis**: **early laparoscopic cholecystectomy** - ideally within **72 hours**, acceptable up to **7 days of admission and 10 days from onset**; delayed (interval) surgery at 6 weeks has higher readmission rates. [TG18; WSES 2020]`,`**Grade II**: early cholecystectomy in an **experienced centre** if CCI and ASA allow; otherwise antibiotics and **percutaneous transhepatic gallbladder drainage (PTGBD)** with later surgery. [TG18]`,`**Grade III**: organ support and antibiotics; early cholecystectomy only at an advanced centre if organ dysfunction (cardiovascular or renal) reverses rapidly, and no jaundice, neurological or respiratory failure, **CCI 3 or less and ASA 2 or less**; otherwise urgent gallbladder drainage. [TG18]`,`**Endoscopic ultrasound-guided gallbladder drainage (EUS-GBD)** with a lumen-apposing stent is an alternative to percutaneous drainage in high-risk patients unfit for surgery. [TG18]`,`**Cholangitis drainage timing**: Grade I - drain if no response in 24 hours; **Grade II - early drainage (within 24-48 hours)**; **Grade III - urgent drainage** once resuscitated; **ERCP with sphincterotomy and stone extraction or stent** is first choice, PTBD if ERCP fails. [TG18]`,`**Antibiotic duration**: cholecystitis Grade I-II - stop **within 24 hours of cholecystectomy**; Grade III or cholangitis - **4-7 days after source control**; **2 weeks** for Enterococcus or Streptococcus bacteraemia. [TG18]`,`**Post-ERCP pancreatitis prophylaxis**: **rectal diclofenac or indomethacin 100 mg** just before or after ERCP plus aggressive Ringer lactate in average-risk patients. [ESGE 2020]`,`**Acalculous cholecystitis** (ICU, burns, sepsis, TPN, post-op) - antibiotics and **percutaneous cholecystostomy**; mortality is high. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: gallstone disease is the second commonest non-obstetric surgical problem; **laparoscopic cholecystectomy is safe in any trimester**, ideally the second, and **ERCP with fetal shielding** is safe for CBD stones or cholangitis. [SAGES 2017]`,`Conservative management of symptomatic gallstones in pregnancy carries **recurrence in up to 50%** and higher fetal loss with complicated disease - so operate rather than wait. [SAGES 2017]`,`**Elderly (75 or more)**: higher rates of gangrenous cholecystitis and cholangitis Grade II by age alone; **frailty and CCI**, not age, decide surgery versus drainage. [TG18]`,`**Diabetes**: higher risk of **emphysematous cholecystitis** (gas in gallbladder wall, Clostridium; commoner in men) - needs urgent cholecystectomy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cirrhosis**: laparoscopic cholecystectomy is acceptable in **Child-Pugh A and selected B**; avoid in Child C - percutaneous drainage instead. [WSES 2020]`,`**Children**: gallstones suggest **haemolytic disease** (thalassaemia, sickle cell, hereditary spherocytosis) or ceftriaxone use; screen the haemogram. [Nelson 22e]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: where ERCP is unavailable, stabilise, start antibiotics (ceftriaxone plus metronidazole) and transfer Grade II-III cholangitis early; percutaneous drainage is the bridge. [TG18]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Gallbladder**: empyema, **gangrene, perforation** (localised abscess or bile peritonitis), **emphysematous cholecystitis**, Mirizzi syndrome and cholecystoenteric fistula. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Gallstone ileus**: a stone over 2.5 cm erodes into the duodenum and impacts at the **ileocaecal valve**; **Rigler triad** - pneumobilia, small-bowel obstruction and an ectopic gallstone; **Bouveret syndrome** is gastric outlet obstruction by a duodenal stone. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Duct**: obstructive jaundice, cholangitis, **biliary pancreatitis**, secondary biliary cirrhosis and liver abscess (see the liver abscess topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`**After cholecystectomy**: **bile duct injury** (about 0.3-0.5% laparoscopic - prevented by the **critical view of safety**), bile leak, retained CBD stone and **post-cholecystectomy syndrome**. [SAGES 2020]`,`**Gallbladder cancer**: risk factors are **long-standing large stones, porcelain gallbladder, polyp 10 mm or more, anomalous pancreaticobiliary ductal junction, chronic typhoid carrier state and obesity**; median survival in advanced disease is about **12 months**. [ICMR GBC consensus]`,`**Incidental GBC** is found in about **1% of cholecystectomy specimens** in North India - so every gallbladder removed in a high-incidence region should go for **histopathology**. [ICMR GBC consensus]`,`Prognosis of uncomplicated gallstone disease after cholecystectomy is excellent; mortality of elective laparoscopic cholecystectomy is **under 0.1%**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and gallbladder polyps`,points:[`**Refer same day**: fever with jaundice, sepsis, persistent pain over 6 hours, peritonism, pancreatitis, or jaundice with CBD dilatation. [TG18]`,`**Refer electively**: symptomatic gallstones for cholecystectomy; incidental stones with prophylactic indications; any gallbladder wall thickening or mass. [EASL 2016]`,`**Gallbladder polyps (ESGAR/EAES/EFISDS/ESGE 2022)**: **10 mm or more - cholecystectomy**; **6-9 mm with a risk factor** (age over 60, PSC, **Asian ethnicity**, sessile polyp or focal wall thickening over 4 mm) - cholecystectomy; 6-9 mm without risk factors or 5 mm or less with risk factors - ultrasound at **6 months, 1 year and 2 years**; 5 mm or less without risk factors - no follow-up. [ESGAR 2022]`,`Growth of a polyp by **2 mm or more within 2 years** or to 10 mm means cholecystectomy. [ESGAR 2022]`,`After ERCP clearance of CBD stones, do **cholecystectomy during the same admission or within 2 weeks** - delay increases recurrent biliary events. [ESGE 2019]`,`**After cholecystectomy**, check the histopathology report; if T1b or higher GBC, refer to a hepatobiliary oncology centre for **revision radical cholecystectomy**. [ICMR GBC consensus]`]},{heading:`Gallbladder cancer in North India`,points:[`GBC is the commonest biliary tract cancer in India and a leading digestive cancer in **women of UP, Bihar, West Bengal, Assam and the North-East**. [ICMR-NCDIR PBCR 2020]`,`Proposed Indian contributors besides stones: **heavy metals and pesticides in Gangetic water**, mustard oil adulterants, chronic **Salmonella typhi carriage**, and genetic susceptibility. [ICMR GBC consensus]`,`Presentation is late: **pain, anorexia, weight loss, jaundice** (hilar invasion), palpable mass; **fewer than 20-30% are resectable** at diagnosis. [ICMR GBC consensus]`,`**T1a (lamina propria)**: simple cholecystectomy is enough; **T1b and above**: **extended (radical) cholecystectomy** - gallbladder with a 2 cm wedge of segments IVb/V and regional lymphadenectomy. [NCCN 2024]`,`**Adjuvant capecitabine** for 6 months after resection (BILCAP) is standard. [ESMO 2023]`,`**Advanced GBC**: **gemcitabine-cisplatin plus durvalumab** (TOPAZ-1, 2022) or **plus pembrolizumab** (KEYNOTE-966, 2023) is the new first-line standard; palliative biliary stenting for jaundice. [ESMO 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates and exam angle`,points:[`**TG18** remains the current Tokyo guideline: it validated TG13 diagnostic and severity criteria and **allowed early cholecystectomy in selected Grade III cholecystitis** at advanced centres. [TG18]`,`**ASGE 2019** replaced the 2010 choledocholithiasis criteria with **stricter high-risk criteria** (stone on imaging, bilirubin over 4 with dilated duct, or cholangitis), sparing unnecessary ERCP. [ASGE 2019]`,`**ESGAR 2022** gallbladder polyp guideline added **Asian ethnicity** as a risk factor, lowering the threshold for surgery in Indian patients with 6-9 mm polyps. [ESGAR 2022]`,`**ACG 2024 acute pancreatitis** guideline: in gallstone pancreatitis, **urgent ERCP (within 24 hours) only with cholangitis**, not for pancreatitis alone; same-admission cholecystectomy for mild disease. [ACG 2024]`,`**Biliary tract cancer**: addition of immunotherapy (durvalumab or pembrolizumab) to gemcitabine-cisplatin is now first-line in ESMO 2023 and NCCN 2024. [ESMO 2023]`,`Viva favourite: **state TG18 grading of cholangitis and when to drain** - drainage timing follows the grade. [TG18]`,`Common mistake: ordering **diagnostic ERCP** in an intermediate-risk patient - EUS or MRCP first. [ASGE 2019]`,`Common mistake: treating dyspepsia with incidental gallstones by cholecystectomy - **bloating and belching persist** afterwards. [EASL 2016]`,`Know **Courvoisier law**, **Rigler triad**, **Charcot triad and Reynolds pentad**, and **Mirizzi syndrome** - all one-line eponym questions. [Bailey and Love 28e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Tokyo 2018 severity grading - cholecystitis versus cholangitis`,columns:[`Grade`,`Acute cholecystitis`,`Acute cholangitis`,`Action`],rows:[[`Grade III (severe)`,`Any organ dysfunction: hypotension on vasopressor, reduced consciousness, PaO2/FiO2 < 300, creatinine > 2.0 mg/dL or oliguria, INR > 1.5, platelets < 100,000`,`Same organ dysfunction list`,`ICU support; urgent drainage (cholangitis) or PTGBD; early LC only at advanced centre with favourable predictors`],[`Grade II (moderate)`,`Any one: WBC > 18,000; tender RUQ mass; symptoms > 72 h; marked local inflammation (gangrenous, emphysematous, abscess, biliary peritonitis)`,`Any two: WBC > 12,000 or < 4,000; fever >= 39 C; age >= 75; bilirubin >= 5 mg/dL; hypoalbuminaemia`,`Early LC in experienced centre or PTGBD; early ERCP drainage for cholangitis`],[`Grade I (mild)`,`None of the above; healthy patient with mild inflammation`,`None of the above`,`Early LC; antibiotics and drainage if no response in 24 h (cholangitis)`]]},{heading:`ASGE 2019 risk of choledocholithiasis`,columns:[`Risk`,`Predictors (any one)`,`Next step`],rows:[[`High`,`CBD stone on ultrasound or cross-sectional imaging; total bilirubin > 4 mg/dL plus dilated CBD; ascending cholangitis`,`ERCP (pre-operative) or laparoscopic CBD exploration at cholecystectomy`],[`Intermediate`,`Abnormal liver biochemistry; age > 55 years; dilated CBD on imaging`,`EUS or MRCP (or intra-operative cholangiography / laparoscopic ultrasound)`],[`Low`,`None of the above`,`Cholecystectomy without further ductal imaging`]]},{heading:`Drugs in gallstone disease`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Diclofenac`,`75 mg IM once, or 50 mg orally 8-hourly; or 100 mg rectally`,`Single dose to 48 h for colic; rectal 100 mg peri-ERCP`,`Peptic ulcer, AKI, avoid in CKD, third trimester, asthma with NSAID sensitivity`],[`Ketorolac`,`30 mg IV/IM (15 mg if over 65 years or under 50 kg) 6-hourly`,`Maximum 5 days`,`GI bleeding, renal impairment`],[`Ceftriaxone + metronidazole`,`Ceftriaxone 2 g IV once daily + metronidazole 500 mg IV 8-hourly`,`Grade I-II; stop within 24 h of cholecystectomy; 4-7 days after drainage in cholangitis`,`Biliary sludge with ceftriaxone, disulfiram reaction with metronidazole`],[`Piperacillin-tazobactam`,`4.5 g IV 6- to 8-hourly (extended infusion in sepsis)`,`Grade II-III cholangitis or healthcare-associated infection; 4-7 days after source control`,`Penicillin allergy, hypokalaemia, adjust in renal failure`],[`Meropenem`,`1 g IV 8-hourly`,`Grade III or known ESBL; de-escalate by culture`,`Seizures (rare), C. difficile, reserve drug - antimicrobial stewardship`],[`Ursodeoxycholic acid (UDCA)`,`10-15 mg/kg/day orally in divided doses; 500-600 mg/day for prophylaxis`,`6-24 months for dissolution; 6 months after bariatric surgery`,`Diarrhoea; useless for pigment, calcified or large stones and non-functioning gallbladder`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of RUQ pain with or without jaundice`,columns:[`Condition`,`Clinical clue`,`Discriminating test`],rows:[[`Biliary colic`,`Steady RUQ pain 30 min to 6 h after fatty meal, afebrile`,`USG: stones, normal wall`],[`Acute cholecystitis`,`Pain > 6 h, fever, Murphy sign`,`USG: wall > 4 mm, pericholecystic fluid; HIDA non-filling`],[`Acute cholangitis`,`Fever with rigors, jaundice, RUQ pain`,`Raised bilirubin and ALP, dilated CBD`],[`Acute pancreatitis`,`Epigastric pain to back, vomiting`,`Lipase > 3 times ULN`],[`Acute viral hepatitis`,`Prodrome, tender liver, jaundice`,`ALT in thousands, IgM anti-HAV / anti-HEV`],[`Liver abscess`,`Fever, tender hepatomegaly, intercostal tenderness`,`USG: hypoechoic lesion`],[`Inferior wall MI`,`Diabetic or elderly, sweating`,`ECG, troponin`],[`Gallbladder cancer`,`Older woman, weight loss, hard mass, painless jaundice`,`CECT: mass, CA 19-9 raised`]]},{heading:`Key investigations and cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`Use`,`Cut-off / finding`],rows:[[`Ultrasound abdomen`,`First test for stones and cholecystitis`,`Sensitivity > 95% for GB stones; wall > 4 mm; CBD > 6 mm dilated`],[`HIDA scan`,`Equivocal cholecystitis`,`Non-visualisation of gallbladder at 4 h`],[`MRCP`,`Intermediate-risk CBD stone`,`Sensitivity about 90%; poorer for stones < 5 mm`],[`EUS`,`Intermediate risk, microlithiasis`,`Most sensitive for small CBD stones`],[`ERCP`,`Therapy: high-risk CBD stone, cholangitis`,`Post-ERCP pancreatitis 3-10%`],[`CECT abdomen`,`Complications, suspected GBC`,`Gas in wall, perforation, mass, nodes`],[`Bilirubin`,`ASGE risk and TG18 grading`,`> 4 mg/dL plus dilated duct = high risk; >= 5 mg/dL = cholangitis Grade II factor`]]}],redFlags:[`Fever with rigors plus jaundice - acute cholangitis needing antibiotics and biliary drainage`,`Hypotension, confusion, oliguria or INR > 1.5 with biliary sepsis - TG18 Grade III`,`RUQ pain lasting more than 6 hours or a tender RUQ mass - acute cholecystitis or empyema`,`Generalised peritonitis or gas in the gallbladder wall - perforation or emphysematous cholecystitis`,`Biliary pain with lipase over 3 times ULN - gallstone pancreatitis`,`Painless progressive jaundice, weight loss or a hard RUQ mass - gallbladder or periampullary cancer`,`Vomiting and distension in an elderly woman with known stones - gallstone ileus`],pearls:[`Biliary colic is a misnomer - the pain is **steady**, lasting 30 minutes to 6 hours.`,`**Charcot triad** is specific but insensitive (about 26%) - use TG18 A + B + C criteria.`,`Cholangitis is cured by **drainage**, not by antibiotics alone.`,`ASGE high risk: **stone seen, bilirubin over 4 with dilated duct, or cholangitis** - go to ERCP.`,`**Courvoisier law**: palpable non-tender gallbladder with jaundice is unlikely to be stones.`,`**Rigler triad** of gallstone ileus: pneumobilia, small-bowel obstruction, ectopic stone.`,`Asymptomatic stones are left alone - except porcelain gallbladder, stones over 3 cm, polyp 10 mm or more, haemolytic disease.`,`In North India, send **every cholecystectomy specimen for histopathology** - incidental GBC is not rare.`,`**Critical view of safety** prevents bile duct injury in laparoscopic cholecystectomy.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-gallstones-biliary-q1`,stem:`A 62-year-old man has fever 39.4 C with rigors, jaundice and RUQ pain. WBC 14,800/mm3, bilirubin 6.2 mg/dL, BP 118/74 mmHg, sensorium normal, creatinine 1.0 mg/dL, INR 1.1. Ultrasound shows a dilated CBD with a distal stone. According to TG18, what is the severity grade?`,options:[`Grade I (mild) cholangitis`,`Grade II (moderate) cholangitis`,`Grade III (severe) cholangitis`,`Suspected, not definite, cholangitis`],answer:1,explanation:`He meets definite cholangitis (systemic inflammation, cholestasis and imaging). Grade II needs any two of WBC over 12,000, fever 39 C or more, age 75 or more, bilirubin 5 mg/dL or more or hypoalbuminaemia - he has three. Grade III needs organ dysfunction, which he does not have, and Grade I is excluded because Grade II factors are present. Grade II needs early ERCP drainage.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gallstones-biliary-q2`,stem:`A 48-year-old woman with symptomatic gallstones has ALT 110 IU/L, bilirubin 1.6 mg/dL and a CBD of 7 mm on ultrasound with no stone seen. She has no fever. Per ASGE 2019, the best next step is:`,options:[`Direct ERCP with sphincterotomy`,`Laparoscopic cholecystectomy without further imaging`,`EUS or MRCP to look for a CBD stone`,`Oral ursodeoxycholic acid for 6 months`],answer:2,explanation:`Abnormal liver tests and a dilated CBD are intermediate-risk predictors, so a CBD stone should be confirmed by EUS or MRCP before ERCP. Direct ERCP is reserved for high risk (stone on imaging, bilirubin over 4 with dilated duct, or cholangitis). Cholecystectomy without ductal imaging is for low risk only. UDCA does not clear CBD stones.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gallstones-biliary-q3`,stem:`Which finding in an asymptomatic patient with gallstones is an accepted indication for prophylactic cholecystectomy?`,options:[`Postprandial bloating and belching`,`Multiple 4 mm stones in a normal gallbladder`,`Type 2 diabetes mellitus`,`A 12 mm gallbladder polyp alongside the stones`],answer:3,explanation:`A gallbladder polyp of 10 mm or more carries malignant potential and is an indication for cholecystectomy (ESGAR 2022). Bloating and belching are not biliary symptoms and persist after surgery. Small stones alone do not justify surgery, and diabetes is no longer considered an indication for prophylactic cholecystectomy.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gallstones-biliary-q4`,stem:`A 74-year-old woman has colicky abdominal pain, distension and bilious vomiting. Plain X-ray shows dilated small-bowel loops, air in the biliary tree and a rounded opacity in the right iliac fossa. The diagnosis is:`,options:[`Gallstone ileus`,`Mirizzi syndrome`,`Emphysematous cholecystitis`,`Bouveret syndrome`],answer:0,explanation:`Rigler triad - pneumobilia, small-bowel obstruction and an ectopic gallstone (usually at the ileocaecal valve) - defines gallstone ileus through a cholecystoduodenal fistula. Bouveret syndrome is gastric outlet obstruction from a duodenal stone, not distal small-bowel obstruction. Mirizzi syndrome causes jaundice by extrinsic hepatic duct compression, and emphysematous cholecystitis shows gas in the gallbladder wall, not obstruction.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gallstones-biliary-q5`,stem:`A 40-year-old woman with acute calculous cholecystitis, Grade I by TG18, was admitted 2 days after pain began. She is otherwise fit. What does TG18 recommend?`,options:[`Antibiotics now and interval cholecystectomy after 6-8 weeks`,`Percutaneous gallbladder drainage followed by antibiotics`,`Early laparoscopic cholecystectomy during this admission`,`Oral UDCA and dietary fat restriction`],answer:2,explanation:`TG18 recommends early laparoscopic cholecystectomy for Grade I cholecystitis, ideally within 72 hours and acceptable within 7 days of admission. Interval surgery after 6-8 weeks has higher readmission and recurrence rates. Percutaneous drainage is for high-risk patients with Grade II-III disease who cannot tolerate surgery, and UDCA has no role in acute cholecystitis.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-gallstones-biliary-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Discuss the aetiopathogenesis, clinical features, diagnosis (Tokyo 2018 criteria) and management of acute cholangitis.`,openingLines:[`Acute cholangitis is infection of an obstructed biliary tree, most often by a CBD stone, in which raised intraductal pressure drives bacteria and endotoxin into the blood.`,`It is diagnosed and graded by the Tokyo 2018 (TG18) criteria, and its cure is antibiotics plus timely biliary drainage, usually by ERCP.`],answer:[{heading:`Aetiology`,points:[`CBD stones (commonest), benign stricture, malignant obstruction (periampullary, cholangiocarcinoma, GBC), blocked stent, post-ERCP, parasites (Ascaris), recurrent pyogenic cholangitis.`,`Organisms: E. coli, Klebsiella, Enterococcus, Enterobacter, anaerobes (Bacteroides) in elderly or post-anastomosis.`]},{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Obstruction plus bacterial colonisation; intraductal pressure above 20-25 cm H2O opens tight junctions and causes cholangiovenous and cholangiolymphatic reflux.`,`Bacteraemia and endotoxaemia lead to sepsis and organ failure - the reason drainage is curative.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Charcot triad (fever with rigors, RUQ pain, jaundice) in only about a quarter; Reynolds pentad adds shock and confusion.`,`Elderly may present with confusion or hypotension alone.`]},{heading:`TG18 diagnostic criteria`,points:[`A: fever > 38 C or rigors; WBC < 4,000 or > 10,000, CRP >= 1 mg/dL.`,`B: bilirubin >= 2 mg/dL; ALP, GGT, AST, ALT > 1.5 times ULN.`,`C: biliary dilatation or evidence of cause on imaging. Suspected = A + B or C; definite = A + B + C.`]},{heading:`Severity grading`,points:[`Grade III: any organ dysfunction (vasopressor, confusion, PaO2/FiO2 < 300, creatinine > 2, INR > 1.5, platelets < 1 lakh).`,`Grade II: two of WBC > 12,000 or < 4,000, fever >= 39 C, age >= 75, bilirubin >= 5 mg/dL, hypoalbuminaemia.`,`Grade I: neither.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`CBC, CRP, LFT, lipase, creatinine, INR, lactate, blood cultures; ultrasound; MRCP or CECT if cause unclear.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Resuscitation, blood cultures, antibiotics within 1 hour if septic: ceftriaxone + metronidazole (Grade I-II) or piperacillin-tazobactam / meropenem (Grade III).`,`Drainage: Grade I if no response in 24 h; Grade II early (24-48 h); Grade III urgent after stabilisation - ERCP with sphincterotomy and stone extraction or stent; PTBD if ERCP fails.`,`Antibiotics 4-7 days after source control; cholecystectomy in same admission once stable.`]}],mustDraw:[`TG18 diagnostic criteria A-B-C box and severity grading table`,`Flow chart: suspected cholangitis to grade to timing of drainage`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition, aetiology and pathogenesis`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features and TG18 criteria`,marks:3},{part:`Investigations`,marks:1},{part:`Management including drainage timing`,marks:4}],keywords:[`Charcot triad`,`Reynolds pentad`,`TG18`,`ERCP`,`PTBD`,`biliary drainage`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gallstones-biliary-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on management of asymptomatic gallstones and choledocholithiasis risk stratification.`,openingLines:[`Asymptomatic gallstones develop symptoms at only 1-4% per year, so expectant management is the rule.`,`The ASGE 2019 guideline stratifies the probability of a CBD stone into high, intermediate and low risk to decide between ERCP, EUS/MRCP and direct cholecystectomy.`],answer:[{heading:`Asymptomatic stones`,points:[`Reassure; no routine cholecystectomy.`,`Exceptions: porcelain gallbladder, stones > 3 cm, polyp >= 10 mm, haemolytic anaemia, during bariatric surgery; in high-GBC regions of North India surgery is often offered for large long-standing stones.`]},{heading:`ASGE 2019 risk`,points:[`High: CBD stone on imaging, bilirubin > 4 mg/dL with dilated duct, or cholangitis - ERCP.`,`Intermediate: abnormal LFT, age > 55, dilated CBD - EUS or MRCP.`,`Low: none - cholecystectomy.`]},{heading:`After ductal clearance`,points:[`Cholecystectomy in the same admission or within 2 weeks; send specimen for histopathology.`]}],mustDraw:[`ASGE 2019 three-tier flow chart`],markSplit:[{part:`Asymptomatic stones and exceptions`,marks:2},{part:`ASGE risk stratification flow`,marks:3}],keywords:[`asymptomatic gallstones`,`porcelain gallbladder`,`ASGE 2019`,`MRCP`,`EUS`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Diseases of the gallbladder and bile ducts`,`Tokyo Guidelines 2018 (TG18): diagnostic criteria, severity grading and management of acute cholangitis and cholecystitis, J Hepatobiliary Pancreat Sci 2018`,`ASGE guideline on the role of endoscopy in the evaluation and management of choledocholithiasis, Gastrointest Endosc 2019`,`ESGE guideline: Endoscopic management of common bile duct stones, update 2019`,`EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines on the prevention, diagnosis and treatment of gallstones, J Hepatol 2016`,`ESGAR/EAES/EFISDS/ESGE joint guideline on gallbladder polyps, Eur Radiol 2022`,`WSES guidelines on acute calculous cholecystitis, World J Emerg Surg 2020`,`ICMR Consensus Document for Management of Gallbladder Cancer, ICMR; ICMR-NCDIR Report of National Cancer Registry Programme 2020`]}),s.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-chronic-pancreatitis`,title:`Chronic pancreatitis and pancreatic insufficiency`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Chronic pancreatitis** is a **progressive fibro-inflammatory disease** of the pancreas with **irreversible loss of acinar and islet tissue**, producing the triad of **pain, exocrine insufficiency (steatorrhoea) and diabetes (type 3c)**; in India the commonest form is **idiopathic/tropical calcific pancreatitis** of young non-drinkers, often linked to **SPINK1** mutations, and treatment rests on **abstinence, a pain ladder, pancreatic enzyme replacement and endotherapy or surgery for ductal obstruction**.`,keywords:[`chronic pancreatitis`,`tropical calcific pancreatitis`,`TCP`,`fibrocalculous pancreatic diabetes`,`FCPD`,`exocrine pancreatic insufficiency`,`EPI`,`steatorrhoea`,`faecal elastase`,`PERT`,`pancreatin`,`Creon`,`type 3c diabetes`,`TIGAR-O`,`M-ANNHEIM`,`SPINK1`,`ESWL`,`Frey procedure`,`Rosemont criteria`,`Cambridge classification`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`The international mechanistic definition calls chronic pancreatitis a **pathological fibro-inflammatory syndrome** in individuals with genetic, environmental or other risk factors who develop **persistent pathological responses to parenchymal injury or stress**. [IAP/APA/JPS/EPC consensus 2016]`,`End-stage features are **atrophy, fibrosis, pain, duct distortion and strictures, calcifications, exocrine and endocrine dysfunction and dysplasia**. [IAP/APA/JPS/EPC consensus 2016]`,`Global incidence is about **5-14 per 100,000 per year** with prevalence of about **30-50 per 100,000** in Western countries. [Harrison 22e]`,`**South India, especially Kerala and Tamil Nadu**, has some of the highest prevalence recorded - about **114-200 per 100,000** in community surveys. [Balaji, Chennai 1994]`,`In a large Indian multicentre series of over 1,000 patients, **idiopathic (including tropical) pancreatitis was about 60%** and **alcohol about 38%** - the reverse of the Western pattern. [Balakrishnan, JOP 2008]`,`**Tropical calcific pancreatitis (TCP)** classically affects **children and young adults (onset under 30 years)**, non-drinkers, often poor, with large ductal calculi and early diabetes. [Harrison 22e]`,`The Indian phenotype has changed: today's 'idiopathic' patients are **older at onset, better nourished and diabetes appears later** than the classic Kerala TCP of the 1960s-80s. [Balakrishnan, JOP 2008]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors - TIGAR-O version 2`,points:[`**T - Toxic-metabolic**: **alcohol** (usually over 5 drinks a day for years), **smoking** (independent, dose-dependent, and synergistic with alcohol), hypercalcaemia, hypertriglyceridaemia, CKD, drugs. [TIGAR-O v2 2019]`,`**I - Idiopathic**: early-onset, late-onset and **tropical** forms; many 'idiopathic' Indian cases carry genetic variants. [TIGAR-O v2 2019]`,`**G - Genetic**: **PRSS1** (autosomal dominant hereditary pancreatitis), **SPINK1 N34S** (found in about **40-50% of Indian TCP**), **CFTR**, **CTRC** and **CPA1** variants. [TIGAR-O v2 2019]`,`**A - Autoimmune**: **type 1 AIP (IgG4-related disease)** and type 2 AIP (associated with IBD) - steroid-responsive and important not to miss. [TIGAR-O v2 2019]`,`**R - Recurrent and severe acute pancreatitis**: the **sentinel acute pancreatitis event (SAPE)** model - repeated attacks activate stellate cells and lead to fibrosis. [TIGAR-O v2 2019]`,`**O - Obstructive**: pancreas divisum (debated), ampullary or ductal tumours (including **IPMN**), post-traumatic duct stricture. [TIGAR-O v2 2019]`,`**The cassava (cyanogen) hypothesis** for TCP has been largely discounted; **malnutrition is now viewed as a result, not a cause**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**M-ANNHEIM** risk factor letters: **Multiple** factors, **A**lcohol, **N**icotine, **N**utrition, **H**ereditary, **E**fferent duct, **I**mmunological, **M**iscellaneous. [M-ANNHEIM 2007]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Injury leads to premature **trypsinogen activation**; SPINK1 (trypsin inhibitor) and CTRC normally degrade active trypsin, so their loss-of-function lets repeated autodigestion occur. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pancreatic stellate cells**, activated by alcohol, cytokines (TGF-beta) and oxidative stress, lay down collagen - fibrosis replaces acini and later islets. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Protein plugs** of lithostathine and calcium carbonate form in the ducts and calcify into **intraductal stones**, causing ductal hypertension. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pain** has three drivers: **ductal and parenchymal hypertension** (target of drainage), **inflammation**, and **neuropathic pain with peripheral and central sensitisation** - which is why some patients still hurt after a perfect drainage procedure. [Drewes, Pancreatology 2017]`,`**Steatorrhoea appears only when lipase output falls below about 10% of normal** - the pancreas has a huge reserve, so EPI is a late feature. [Harrison 22e]`,`Fat malabsorption comes before protein malabsorption because **lipase is destroyed by acid and trypsin faster** and has no extra-pancreatic back-up; bicarbonate deficiency also lowers duodenal pH and inactivates enzymes - the reason a PPI is added. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Type 3c diabetes** results from loss of **beta cells and also alpha cells (glucagon) and PP cells**: insulin deficiency with **impaired counter-regulation**, so hypoglycaemia is frequent and brittle control is common. [ADA Standards 2025]`,`**Fat-soluble vitamin (A, D, E, K) and B12 deficiency** (failure to cleave R-protein-B12 complex) follow EPI; **vitamin D deficiency and osteoporosis** affect about two-thirds. [AGA 2023]`]},{heading:`Classification and staging`,points:[`**TIGAR-O v2 (2019)** classifies aetiology and is the classification examiners expect for 'causes'. [TIGAR-O v2 2019]`,`**M-ANNHEIM clinical stages**: **0** subclinical; **I** pain or recurrent attacks without pancreatic insufficiency; **II** exocrine or endocrine insufficiency; **III** both exocrine and endocrine insufficiency with pain; **IV** 'burnt-out' insufficiency with secondary loss of pain. [M-ANNHEIM 2007]`,`**Cambridge classification** grades imaging (CT/MRCP/ERCP) duct changes: normal, equivocal, mild, moderate and marked (main duct over 10 mm, large cavities, stones, strictures). [Cambridge 1984]`,`**Rosemont criteria** grade EUS findings (major: hyperechoic foci with shadowing, lobularity with honeycombing); 'consistent with', 'suggestive of', 'indeterminate' or 'normal'. [Rosemont 2009]`,`**Early chronic pancreatitis** remains hard to diagnose; the 2016 mechanistic definition separates 'at-risk', 'early' and 'established' disease. [IAP 2016]`,`**Fibrocalculous pancreatic diabetes (FCPD)** is the diabetic form of TCP: young onset, **ketosis-resistant** despite insulin need, with pancreatic calculi. [WHO diabetes classification 2019]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Pain** (in about 80-90%): epigastric, boring, **radiating to the back**, worse after meals and relieved by **sitting forward**; either recurrent attacks or constant daily pain. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Steatorrhoea**: bulky, pale, foul, oily, floating stools that are hard to flush, with oil droplets; weight loss despite good appetite (sitophobia - fear of eating because of pain - adds to it). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diabetes**: polyuria, weight loss, often insulin-requiring, with **unexplained hypoglycaemia** on insulin. [ADA Standards 2025]`,`**TCP triad**: abdominal pain in childhood, steatorrhoea and diabetes in a young non-drinker from South India. [Harrison 22e]`,`About **10-20% have painless disease** and present first with diabetes or steatorrhoea - especially elderly and late-onset idiopathic patients. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **alcohol (amount and duration), smoking, family history** of pancreatitis or early diabetes, hyperlipidaemia, hypercalcaemia, and previous acute attacks. [ACG 2020]`,`**New jaundice, weight loss and loss of previously controlled diabetes** in a known patient suggest a **CBD stricture or pancreatic cancer**. [ACG 2020]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Often **thin, malnourished, with muscle wasting** (sarcopenia) and low BMI. [AGA 2023]`,`**Epigastric tenderness**; a **palpable epigastric mass** suggests a pseudocyst. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Erythema ab igne** - brown reticulated pigmentation over the back or abdomen from repeated hot-water bottle use - indicates chronic severe pain. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Jaundice** suggests distal CBD stricture or cancer; **splenomegaly** suggests **splenic vein thrombosis** with left-sided (sinistral) portal hypertension. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for **features of deficiency**: night blindness (vit A), bruising (vit K), bone pain and proximal myopathy (vit D), glossitis and neuropathy (B12). [AGA 2023]`,`**Parotid enlargement, cataract** (in TCP young diabetics) and signs of chronic liver disease in alcohol-associated disease. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Pancreatic cancer** - older patient, painless jaundice, new diabetes, a mass rather than diffuse calcification; can coexist, so a focal mass in CP needs EUS-FNB. [ACG 2020]`,`**Autoimmune pancreatitis** - 'sausage-shaped' pancreas, capsule-like rim, raised IgG4, other organ involvement; responds to steroids. [ICDC 2011]`,`**Peptic ulcer disease and biliary colic** - other causes of chronic epigastric pain; endoscopy and ultrasound discriminate. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Coeliac disease, small-intestinal bacterial overgrowth and giardiasis** cause malabsorption but have normal faecal elastase. [AGA 2023]`,`**Type 1 or type 2 diabetes** - type 3c is often misclassified; check pancreatic imaging in young lean diabetics with steatorrhoea. [ADA Standards 2025]`,`**Abdominal tuberculosis and mesenteric ischaemia** (post-prandial pain, weight loss) in the right setting. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Plain X-ray abdomen** showing **pancreatic calcification** at L1-L2 is diagnostic in TCP and advanced alcoholic CP - a cheap district-hospital test. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Transabdominal ultrasound** shows calculi and a dilated main duct, but sensitivity for early disease is poor. [ACG 2020]`,`**CECT abdomen (pancreatic protocol)** is the **first-line cross-sectional test**: calcifications, main duct dilatation (over 3-4 mm), atrophy, pseudocysts, splenic vein thrombosis, pseudoaneurysm and mass. [ACG 2020]`,`**MRI with secretin-enhanced MRCP** is the next step if CT is non-diagnostic; it shows side-branch ectasia, duct strictures and exocrine reserve. [ACG 2020]`,`**EUS** (Rosemont criteria) is reserved for equivocal cases and for **EUS-FNB of a mass**; it over-diagnoses CP in the elderly, smokers and drinkers. [ACG 2020]`,`**Faecal elastase-1** (FE-1): **under 200 mcg/g suggests EPI, under 100 mcg/g indicates severe EPI**; false lows with watery stool. [AGA 2023]`,`**72-hour faecal fat** (over 7 g/day on a 100 g fat diet) is the reference but impractical; a **therapeutic trial of PERT is not diagnostic** of EPI. [AGA 2023]`,`**Baseline and annual**: HbA1c and fasting glucose, **25-OH vitamin D**, fat-soluble vitamins, B12, albumin, prealbumin, magnesium, and **DEXA every 2 years** in those at risk. [ACG 2020; AGA 2023]`,`**Aetiology work-up**: serum calcium, triglycerides, IgG4; **genetic testing (PRSS1, SPINK1, CFTR, CTRC)** in young patients with idiopathic disease or family history. [ACG 2020]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Stop alcohol and smoking completely** - abstinence slows progression and reduces pain; offer brief intervention and refer to de-addiction (Tele-MANAS 14416). [ACG 2020]`,`**Diet**: small frequent meals, normal fat (about 30% of energy) **with PERT rather than fat restriction**; avoid very high-fibre diets that inhibit enzymes; refer to a dietitian. [ESPEN 2020]`,`**PERT (pancreatin, enteric-coated microspheres)**: start **40,000-50,000 lipase units with each main meal and 20,000-25,000 with snacks**, taken **with the first bites** of food. [UEG HaPanEU 2017]`,`**AGA 2023 weight-based dosing**: **500 lipase units/kg per meal**, half with snacks, up to **2,500 units/kg per meal or 10,000 units/kg/day**. [AGA 2023]`,`**If symptoms persist**, check timing and adherence, increase the dose, **add a PPI** (pantoprazole 40 mg once daily) and look for SIBO. [AGA 2023]`,`Judge PERT response by **weight, stool frequency and consistency and nutrition markers**, not by repeating FE-1 (exogenous enzymes do not change FE-1). [AGA 2023]`,`**Supplement** vitamin D (cholecalciferol 60,000 IU weekly for 8 weeks, then monthly) and calcium, other fat-soluble vitamins as needed. [ESPEN 2020]`,`**Type 3c diabetes**: **metformin** is reasonable first-line if hyperglycaemia is mild (and may lower cancer risk); **insulin** for most with significant disease; avoid GLP-1 RAs and DPP-4 inhibitors where pancreatitis risk is a concern; teach hypoglycaemia awareness. [ADA Standards 2025]`]},{heading:`Pain ladder`,points:[`**Step 1**: abstinence, **paracetamol 1 g up to 4 times a day** and NSAIDs for short periods. [ACG 2020]`,`**Step 2**: **tramadol 50-100 mg orally 6- to 8-hourly (max 400 mg/day)**, a weak opioid with SNRI action. [Drewes, Pancreatology 2017]`,`**Adjuvants**: **pregabalin 75 mg twice daily titrated to 150-300 mg twice daily** reduces pain (Olesen RCT 2011); amitriptyline, duloxetine or nortriptyline for neuropathic pain. [Drewes, Pancreatology 2017]`,`**Antioxidants** (selenium, vitamin C, vitamin E, beta-carotene, methionine) reduced pain in an AIIMS RCT (Bhardwaj 2009) but not in a later UK trial; ACG 2020 does not recommend them routinely. [ACG 2020]`,`**PERT is not recommended for pain** (only for EPI); octreotide does not help. [ACG 2020]`,`**Step 3 - endotherapy**: for an obstructed main duct in the head with stones over 5 mm - **ESWL followed by ERCP and pancreatic stenting** for strictures; assess at 6-8 weeks. [ESGE 2018]`,`**Step 4 - surgery**: **Frey** (local head coring plus lateral pancreaticojejunostomy), **Beger** (duodenum-preserving head resection), **Partington-Rochelle/Puestow** for a dilated duct; **early surgery beat endoscopy-first** for pain relief in the ESCAPE trial (2020). [ESCAPE, JAMA 2020]`,`**Strong opioids** (morphine) only after the above, with an opioid agreement, because of addiction and narcotic bowel syndrome. [Drewes, Pancreatology 2017]`,`**Coeliac plexus block** (EUS-guided) gives short-lived relief; **total pancreatectomy with islet autotransplantation (TPIAT)** is a last resort at specialised centres. [ACG 2020]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Admit** for acute-on-chronic flare with uncontrolled pain, vomiting, inability to eat, or suspected complication. [ACG 2020]`,`**Infected or symptomatic pseudocyst** (over 6 cm causing gastric outlet obstruction, pain, biliary obstruction) - **EUS-guided cystogastrostomy** is preferred over surgery. [ESGE 2018]`,`**Haemorrhage from a pseudoaneurysm** (splenic, gastroduodenal artery) presents as GI bleed or 'haemosuccus pancreaticus' - **CT angiography and angioembolisation**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Biliary stricture** with persistent cholestasis (ALP over 2 times ULN for over a month) or cholangitis - ERCP with **fully covered metal stent** or surgery. [ESGE 2018]`,`**Pancreatic ascites or pleural effusion** (fluid amylase over 1,000 IU/L) - from a ductal leak; treat with ERCP stenting across the leak, octreotide and drainage. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Gastric variceal bleed** from **splenic vein thrombosis** - **splenectomy** is curative (left-sided portal hypertension). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Children and adolescents**: think genetic (**PRSS1, SPINK1, CFTR**) and TCP; screen family members and counsel. [ACG 2020]`,`**Pregnancy**: PERT is safe (not absorbed); insulin for type 3c diabetes; avoid ESWL. [AGA 2023]`,`**Elderly**: often painless, presenting with EPI or diabetes; higher pancreatic cancer risk - lower threshold for EUS. [ACG 2020]`,`**Diabetes**: hypoglycaemia risk is higher (glucagon deficiency); avoid tight targets in brittle cases. [ADA Standards 2025]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: plain X-ray for calcification, ultrasound, and a trial of PERT with weight monitoring if FE-1 unavailable; refer for ESWL/ERCP to tertiary centres (AIG Hyderabad, PGI, AIIMS, CMC). [ISG]`,`**PERT availability**: pancreatin preparations (10,000, 25,000 and 40,000 unit capsules) are available in India but costly; cheap non-enteric-coated tablets need a PPI. [AGA 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Local**: pseudocyst (about 20-40%), duodenal and CBD stricture, pancreatic fistula, ascites, pleural effusion, pseudoaneurysm. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Splenic vein thrombosis** causes **isolated gastric fundal varices** with a normal liver. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma**: risk is about **13-16 times** that of the general population, and much higher in **hereditary pancreatitis (PRSS1)** and TCP - cumulative risk in hereditary pancreatitis reaches about 40% by age 70. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Metabolic**: malnutrition, osteoporosis and fractures, sarcopenia; **cardiovascular events** rise with untreated EPI. [AGA 2023]`,`**Mortality** is about **3-4 times** that of the general population, mostly from alcohol, smoking-related disease, cancer and diabetes complications, not from pancreatitis itself. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Burn-out hypothesis**: pain may lessen as the gland calcifies and fails (M-ANNHEIM stage IV), but this is inconsistent. [M-ANNHEIM 2007]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer** at diagnosis to a gastroenterologist for aetiology, staging and endotherapy assessment. [ACG 2020]`,`**Every 6-12 months**: weight, BMI, stool pattern, pain score, alcohol and smoking status, HbA1c. [ACG 2020]`,`**Annually**: fasting glucose/HbA1c (diabetes screening), vitamin D, fat-soluble vitamins, albumin; **DEXA every 2 years**. [ACG 2020; AGA 2023]`,`**Red flags for cancer**: new weight loss, jaundice, new or worsening diabetes, a new focal mass - **CECT and CA 19-9, EUS-FNB**. [ACG 2020]`,`**Hereditary pancreatitis (PRSS1)**: pancreatic cancer surveillance with EUS/MRI from about **age 40**. [CAPS 2020]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`**Alcohol and tobacco cessation** are the only disease-modifying interventions; link to **NTCP quitlines (1800-11-2356)** and de-addiction centres. [NTCP]`,`**Screening for diabetes** under **NP-NCD at Ayushman Arogya Mandir** picks up type 3c diabetes - flag young lean diabetics with abdominal pain for imaging. [NP-NCD 2023]`,`**Genetic counselling** for families with PRSS1 hereditary pancreatitis. [ACG 2020]`,`Explain to patients that **enzymes are taken with food, not before or after** - the commonest reason PERT 'fails'. [AGA 2023]`,`Treat **hypertriglyceridaemia (over 500 mg/dL)** and **hyperparathyroidism** to prevent recurrent attacks. [ACG 2020]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates and exam angle`,points:[`**TIGAR-O version 2 (2019)** reorganised aetiology into a risk/modifier checklist and added **CTRC, CPA1** and other genetic variants. [TIGAR-O v2 2019]`,`**AGA Clinical Practice Update on EPI (2023)**: FE-1 as initial test, **weight-based PERT 500 units/kg/meal**, judge response clinically, add PPI if needed. [AGA 2023]`,`**ADA Standards of Care 2025** recognise **pancreatogenic (type 3c) diabetes** as a distinct category and advise screening of CP patients annually. [ADA Standards 2025]`,`**ESCAPE trial (2020)**: early surgery gave better pain relief than an endoscopy-first approach - shifting practice towards earlier surgical referral. [ESCAPE, JAMA 2020]`,`Viva: **TCP vs alcoholic CP**, **TIGAR-O**, **PERT dosing**, **why add PPI**, **why type 3c diabetes is brittle**, and **splenic vein thrombosis with isolated gastric varices**. [Examiner practice]`,`Common mistake: **stopping fat in the diet** instead of giving adequate PERT, or giving enzymes after meals. [AGA 2023]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Tropical calcific vs alcoholic chronic pancreatitis`,columns:[`Feature`,`Tropical calcific (idiopathic)`,`Alcoholic`],rows:[[`Age at onset`,`Childhood to 30s`,`35-50 years`],[`Sex`,`Both equally`,`Mostly men`],[`Alcohol`,`None`,`Heavy, long duration`],[`Genetics`,`SPINK1 N34S in about 40-50%`,`Less important; smoking co-factor`],[`Calculi`,`Large, dense, in main duct`,`Smaller, speckled`],[`Diabetes`,`Early, common (FCPD), ketosis-resistant`,`Later`],[`Cancer risk`,`High`,`Increased`],[`Pseudocyst`,`Less common`,`More common`]]},{heading:`TIGAR-O version 2 and M-ANNHEIM stages`,columns:[`Category`,`Content`,`Example`],rows:[[`T - Toxic-metabolic`,`Alcohol, smoking, hypercalcaemia, hypertriglyceridaemia, CKD, drugs`,`Alcohol over 5 drinks/day`],[`I - Idiopathic`,`Early-onset, late-onset, tropical`,`TCP in Kerala`],[`G - Genetic`,`PRSS1, SPINK1, CFTR, CTRC, CPA1`,`Hereditary pancreatitis`],[`A - Autoimmune`,`Type 1 AIP (IgG4), type 2 AIP`,`Sausage pancreas`],[`R - Recurrent/severe AP`,`Sentinel acute pancreatitis event`,`Post-necrotic CP`],[`O - Obstructive`,`Divisum, tumour, stricture`,`IPMN`],[`M-ANNHEIM stage I-IV`,`I no insufficiency; II exocrine or endocrine; III both with pain; IV burnt-out painless`,`Staging for prognosis`]]},{heading:`Drugs in chronic pancreatitis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Pancreatin (PERT)`,`40,000-50,000 lipase units with each meal, half with snacks; or 500 U/kg/meal`,`Lifelong; titrate to 2,500 U/kg/meal or 10,000 U/kg/day max`,`Bloating; fibrosing colonopathy at very high doses; take with food`],[`Pantoprazole`,`40 mg orally once daily before breakfast`,`Add if PERT response poor`,`Hypomagnesaemia, C. difficile, B12 deficiency on long use`],[`Paracetamol`,`1 g orally 6-hourly (max 3 g/day if alcohol use)`,`Step 1 analgesic`,`Hepatotoxicity in alcohol users`],[`Tramadol`,`50-100 mg orally 6- to 8-hourly, max 400 mg/day`,`Step 2; review dependence`,`Seizures, serotonin syndrome, constipation`],[`Pregabalin`,`75 mg twice daily, titrate to 150-300 mg twice daily`,`Adjuvant for neuropathic pain`,`Dizziness, sedation, weight gain; reduce in CKD`],[`Insulin (basal-bolus)`,`Individualised; start basal 0.1-0.2 U/kg/day`,`Lifelong in most type 3c`,`Hypoglycaemia (glucagon deficiency)`],[`Cholecalciferol`,`60,000 IU orally weekly for 8 weeks, then monthly`,`Maintain 25-OH D above 30 ng/mL`,`Hypercalcaemia if excess`]]},{heading:`Investigations and cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`Finding`,`Interpretation`],rows:[[`Plain X-ray abdomen`,`Calcification at L1-L2`,`Diagnostic of calcific CP`],[`CECT pancreatic protocol`,`Calculi, MPD > 4 mm, atrophy`,`First-line imaging`],[`Secretin MRCP`,`Duct strictures, side-branch ectasia`,`If CT non-diagnostic`],[`EUS (Rosemont)`,`Hyperechoic foci with shadowing, honeycomb lobularity`,`Equivocal cases; FNB for mass`],[`Faecal elastase-1`,`< 200 mcg/g EPI; < 100 mcg/g severe`,`Invalid with watery stool`],[`72-h faecal fat`,`> 7 g/day`,`Reference test for steatorrhoea`],[`HbA1c / FPG`,`>= 6.5% / >= 126 mg/dL`,`Type 3c diabetes`]]},{heading:`Type 3c vs type 1 vs type 2 diabetes`,columns:[`Feature`,`Type 3c (pancreatogenic)`,`Type 1`,`Type 2`],rows:[[`Islet loss`,`Beta, alpha and PP cells`,`Beta cells (autoimmune)`,`Insulin resistance and beta-cell failure`],[`Glucagon`,`Low`,`Normal or high`,`High`],[`Hypoglycaemia risk`,`High, brittle`,`Moderate`,`Low`],[`Ketosis`,`Uncommon`,`Common`,`Rare`],[`Exocrine function`,`Impaired (FE-1 low)`,`Usually normal`,`Usually normal`],[`First drug`,`Metformin if mild; insulin`,`Insulin`,`Metformin`]]}],redFlags:[`New jaundice or weight loss in known chronic pancreatitis - CBD stricture or pancreatic cancer`,`Haematemesis or melaena with a pseudocyst - pseudoaneurysm bleed needing angioembolisation`,`Fever, pain and an enlarging epigastric mass - infected pseudocyst`,`Persistent vomiting and early satiety - duodenal obstruction or pseudocyst compression`,`Recurrent hypoglycaemia on insulin - brittle type 3c diabetes`,`Gastric fundal varices with splenomegaly and normal liver - splenic vein thrombosis`,`Uncontrolled pain escalating to strong opioids - refer for endotherapy or surgery`],pearls:[`Triad of chronic pancreatitis: **pain, steatorrhoea, diabetes**.`,`Steatorrhoea needs **over 90% loss** of lipase output.`,`In India **idiopathic/tropical** beats alcohol; **SPINK1 N34S** is the key gene.`,`**TIGAR-O**: Toxic, Idiopathic, Genetic, Autoimmune, Recurrent, Obstructive.`,`PERT goes **with the first bite** of every meal - start 40,000-50,000 units.`,`Add a **PPI** when PERT fails - acid inactivates lipase.`,`Type 3c diabetes is **brittle** because glucagon is lost too.`,`**Isolated gastric varices** with a normal liver = splenic vein thrombosis; cure is splenectomy.`,`**Erythema ab igne** on the back tells of chronic pain treated with hot-water bottles.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-chronic-pancreatitis-q1`,stem:`A 22-year-old non-drinking man from Kerala has recurrent abdominal pain since childhood, oily stools and diabetes requiring insulin without ketosis. X-ray shows large calcific densities at L1-L2. Which genetic variant is most often associated?`,options:[`PRSS1 R122H`,`SPINK1 N34S`,`CFTR delta F508`,`HLA-DR3/DR4`],answer:1,explanation:`This is tropical calcific pancreatitis with fibrocalculous pancreatic diabetes, and SPINK1 N34S is found in about 40-50% of Indian TCP patients. PRSS1 causes autosomal dominant hereditary pancreatitis, which is uncommon in India. CFTR variants contribute in a minority, and HLA-DR3/DR4 relates to autoimmune type 1 diabetes, which is ketosis-prone.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-chronic-pancreatitis-q2`,stem:`A 45-year-old with chronic pancreatitis and faecal elastase 60 mcg/g is on pancreatin 10,000 units taken after each meal and still has steatorrhoea. The best next step is:`,options:[`Restrict dietary fat to under 20 g/day`,`Add octreotide`,`Increase to 40,000-50,000 units taken with the first bites of each meal`,`Repeat faecal elastase to judge response`],answer:2,explanation:`The dose is too low and taken at the wrong time; PERT should start at 40,000-50,000 lipase units with meals, taken with food, and a PPI can be added if response remains poor. Fat restriction worsens malnutrition. Octreotide has no role, and faecal elastase is not altered by exogenous enzymes, so it cannot monitor treatment.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-chronic-pancreatitis-q3`,stem:`A man with alcoholic chronic pancreatitis has haematemesis. Endoscopy shows isolated fundal gastric varices; liver function is normal and the spleen is enlarged. The definitive treatment is:`,options:[`Splenectomy`,`Non-selective beta-blocker`,`TIPS`,`Endoscopic band ligation of oesophagus`],answer:0,explanation:`Isolated gastric varices with splenomegaly and a normal liver point to splenic vein thrombosis causing left-sided (sinistral) portal hypertension, cured by splenectomy. TIPS and beta-blockers treat cirrhotic portal hypertension where portal pressure is raised, which is not the case here, and band ligation targets oesophageal varices, which are absent.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-chronic-pancreatitis-q4`,stem:`Which feature most distinguishes type 3c (pancreatogenic) diabetes from type 2 diabetes?`,options:[`High fasting C-peptide`,`Acanthosis nigricans`,`Raised glucagon levels`,`Low glucagon response with frequent hypoglycaemia`],answer:3,explanation:`Type 3c diabetes destroys alpha cells as well as beta cells, so glucagon is low and counter-regulation fails, making hypoglycaemia frequent and control brittle. Raised glucagon, acanthosis nigricans and high C-peptide all reflect insulin resistance of type 2 diabetes.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-chronic-pancreatitis-q5`,stem:`In chronic pancreatitis with a dilated main pancreatic duct and pain uncontrolled by medical therapy, the ESCAPE randomised trial (2020) showed that:`,options:[`Endoscopy-first was superior to surgery for pain relief`,`Early surgery gave better pain relief than an endoscopy-first approach`,`Antioxidants abolished the need for drainage`,`Coeliac plexus block gave durable relief beyond 2 years`],answer:1,explanation:`ESCAPE randomised patients to early surgery or an endoscopy-first step-up approach and found better pain scores with early surgery, with fewer interventions. Endoscopy is not superior. Antioxidant evidence is inconsistent and does not replace drainage, and coeliac plexus block gives only short-term relief.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-chronic-pancreatitis-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Describe the aetiology, clinical features, investigations and management of chronic pancreatitis, with special reference to tropical calcific pancreatitis.`,openingLines:[`Chronic pancreatitis is a progressive fibro-inflammatory disease causing irreversible loss of pancreatic tissue, with pain, exocrine insufficiency and diabetes.`,`In India the idiopathic/tropical form predominates over alcohol, often associated with SPINK1 mutations and fibrocalculous pancreatic diabetes.`],answer:[{heading:`Aetiology (TIGAR-O v2)`,points:[`Toxic-metabolic (alcohol, smoking, hypercalcaemia, hypertriglyceridaemia), Idiopathic (tropical), Genetic (PRSS1, SPINK1, CFTR, CTRC), Autoimmune, Recurrent AP, Obstructive.`]},{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Trypsin activation, stellate cell fibrosis, protein plugs and calculi, ductal hypertension and neuropathic pain; EPI when lipase < 10%.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Epigastric pain to back relieved by leaning forward; steatorrhoea; weight loss; diabetes; TCP triad in young non-drinkers.`]},{heading:`Tropical calcific pancreatitis`,points:[`Onset < 30 years, South India, large ductal calculi, FCPD (ketosis-resistant), SPINK1 N34S, high cancer risk.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`X-ray calcification, CECT first line, secretin MRCP, EUS; FE-1 < 200 mcg/g; HbA1c, vitamin D, DEXA.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Abstinence; PERT 40,000-50,000 U/meal + PPI; pain ladder (paracetamol, tramadol, pregabalin); ESWL + ERCP for head stones; Frey/Beger surgery; insulin or metformin for type 3c; vitamins.`]},{heading:`Complications`,points:[`Pseudocyst, CBD and duodenal stricture, splenic vein thrombosis, pseudoaneurysm, pancreatic cancer, osteoporosis.`]}],mustDraw:[`Pain ladder for chronic pancreatitis`,`TCP vs alcoholic CP comparison table`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and aetiology`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features and TCP`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations`,marks:2},{part:`Management and complications`,marks:4}],keywords:[`TIGAR-O`,`tropical calcific pancreatitis`,`FCPD`,`PERT`,`ESWL`,`Frey procedure`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-chronic-pancreatitis-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on pancreatic enzyme replacement therapy.`,openingLines:[`PERT replaces lipase, protease and amylase in exocrine pancreatic insufficiency, diagnosed by faecal elastase-1 under 200 mcg/g.`,`It is given as enteric-coated pancreatin microspheres taken with meals, dosed by lipase units.`],answer:[{heading:`Indications`,points:[`EPI from chronic pancreatitis, pancreatic cancer, pancreatic resection, cystic fibrosis, severe acute pancreatitis.`]},{heading:`Dose`,points:[`40,000-50,000 lipase units per main meal, 20,000-25,000 per snack; or 500 U/kg/meal up to 2,500 U/kg/meal.`]},{heading:`Administration`,points:[`With the first bites, spread through the meal; do not crush microspheres; add PPI if response poor.`]},{heading:`Monitoring`,points:[`Weight, stool, fat-soluble vitamins, albumin; not FE-1.`]},{heading:`Adverse effects`,points:[`Bloating, abdominal pain; fibrosing colonopathy at very high doses (CF).`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow: EPI suspected to FE-1 to PERT to titrate to add PPI`],markSplit:[{part:`Indications and diagnosis of EPI`,marks:1},{part:`Dose and administration`,marks:2},{part:`Failure and monitoring`,marks:2}],keywords:[`PERT`,`pancreatin`,`lipase units`,`faecal elastase`,`PPI`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Chronic pancreatitis and pancreatic exocrine insufficiency`,`ACG Clinical Guideline: Chronic Pancreatitis, Am J Gastroenterol 2020`,`AGA Clinical Practice Update on the Epidemiology, Evaluation and Management of Exocrine Pancreatic Insufficiency, Gastroenterology 2023`,`Whitcomb DC et al. Chronic pancreatitis: TIGAR-O version 2 risk/aetiology checklist, Clin Transl Gastroenterol 2019`,`ESGE Guideline: Endoscopic treatment of chronic pancreatitis, updated 2018`,`United European Gastroenterology evidence-based guidelines for chronic pancreatitis (HaPanEU), 2017`,`American Diabetes Association Standards of Care in Diabetes 2025 - Classification (pancreatogenic diabetes)`,`Issa Y et al. ESCAPE trial: early surgery vs endoscopy-first for chronic pancreatitis, JAMA 2020; Balakrishnan V et al. Indian multicentre CP study, JOP 2008`]}),s.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-pancreatic-cancer-cystic`,title:`Pancreatic cancer and pancreatic cystic lesions`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`**Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC)** is a **KRAS-driven, desmoplastic cancer** that presents late with **painless obstructive jaundice, weight loss, back pain or new-onset diabetes after 50**; it is staged by **pancreatic-protocol CT** into **resectable, borderline, locally advanced or metastatic** disease, with **CA 19-9** as a prognostic (not screening) marker - while **pancreatic cysts** are triaged by type, and **IPMNs** by **Fukuoka 2017/Kyoto 2023 high-risk stigmata and worrisome features**.`,keywords:[`pancreatic adenocarcinoma`,`PDAC`,`carcinoma head of pancreas`,`periampullary carcinoma`,`CA 19-9`,`double duct sign`,`Whipple procedure`,`pancreaticoduodenectomy`,`FOLFIRINOX`,`NALIRIFOX`,`gemcitabine nab-paclitaxel`,`Trousseau sign`,`new-onset diabetes`,`IPMN`,`mucinous cystic neoplasm`,`serous cystadenoma`,`Fukuoka criteria`,`Kyoto guidelines 2023`,`worrisome features`,`high-risk stigmata`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**PDAC** arises from ductal epithelium and makes up about **90% of pancreatic malignancies**; the rest are neuroendocrine tumours, acinar cell carcinoma and cystic neoplasm-derived cancers. [Harrison 22e]`,`It is the **third leading cause of cancer death in the USA** and is projected to become second by 2030; overall **5-year survival is only about 13%**. [ACS Cancer Statistics 2025]`,`About **80-85% are unresectable at diagnosis** (about 50% metastatic, 30-35% locally advanced); even after resection, 5-year survival is only **20-30%** (higher with modern adjuvant chemotherapy). [NCCN 2025]`,`**India** has a low age-standardised incidence (roughly **1-2 per 100,000**), higher in urban and North-East registries, but cases are rising with ageing, diabetes and obesity. [ICMR-NCRP 2020]`,`Peak age is **60-80 years**; it is rare before 40 except in hereditary syndromes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pancreatic cysts** are found incidentally in about **2-3% of adults on CT and up to 15-20% on MRI**, rising with age; most are small branch-duct IPMNs with low malignant risk. [Kyoto 2023]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Smoking** is the strongest modifiable risk (about **2-fold**), responsible for about 20-25% of cases; risk falls to baseline 10-20 years after quitting. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Long-standing diabetes** (about 1.5-2 fold), **obesity**, heavy alcohol (via chronic pancreatitis) and **chronic pancreatitis** (about **13-16 fold**, higher in hereditary and tropical pancreatitis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hereditary syndromes**: **BRCA2, BRCA1, PALB2, ATM**, Lynch syndrome (mismatch repair genes), **Peutz-Jeghers (STK11)** and **FAMMM (CDKN2A)**; and **hereditary pancreatitis (PRSS1)**. [NCCN 2025]`,`**Familial pancreatic cancer**: two or more first-degree relatives affected without a known gene raises risk about **6-fold** (3 relatives: about 30-fold). [CAPS 2020]`,`**Precursor lesions**: **PanIN** (microscopic, commonest), **IPMN** and **MCN** (macroscopic cystic). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Non-O blood group** and *H. pylori* have weak associations; coffee does not cause pancreatic cancer. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**KRAS** activating mutations occur in **over 90%** of PDAC as the initiating event, followed by loss of **CDKN2A (p16)**, **TP53** and **SMAD4 (DPC4)** in the PanIN-to-cancer sequence. [Harrison 22e]`,`**SMAD4 loss** is associated with widespread metastatic spread; germline **BRCA/PALB2** defects create sensitivity to **platinum and PARP inhibitors**. [NCCN 2025]`,`A dense **desmoplastic stroma** (activated stellate cells) makes the tumour hypovascular (hypoattenuating on CT) and limits drug delivery. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Perineural invasion** along the coeliac and superior mesenteric plexus causes the **boring back pain** and explains why back pain implies unresectability. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Head tumours (60-70%)** compress the intrapancreatic CBD early, giving painless jaundice; **body and tail tumours** grow silently and present with pain, weight loss or metastases. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Paraneoplastic diabetes**: tumour-derived factors (e.g. adrenomedullin, exosomes) cause beta-cell dysfunction and insulin resistance, so **new-onset diabetes often precedes diagnosis by 1-3 years** - with weight loss instead of the usual gain. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypercoagulability** from tumour mucin and tissue factor gives **Trousseau syndrome** (migratory thrombophlebitis) and high VTE rates. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Staging and resectability`,points:[`**AJCC 8th edition**: T1 2 cm or less, T2 over 2 to 4 cm, T3 over 4 cm, **T4 involves coeliac axis, SMA or common hepatic artery** regardless of size; N1 1-3 nodes, N2 4 or more nodes. [AJCC 8e]`,`**Resectable (NCCN)**: **no arterial contact** (coeliac, SMA, CHA) and **no SMV/portal vein contact or contact of 180 degrees or less without contour irregularity**. [NCCN 2025]`,`**Borderline resectable**: head - contact with **CHA** without extension to the coeliac or hepatic artery bifurcation, **SMA contact 180 degrees or less**, or SMV/PV involvement that is **reconstructible**; body/tail - coeliac contact 180 degrees or less. [NCCN 2025]`,`**Locally advanced (unresectable)**: **SMA or coeliac encasement over 180 degrees** or **unreconstructible SMV/PV**. [NCCN 2025]`,`**Metastatic**: liver, peritoneum, lung, distant nodes - including **ascites with positive cytology**. [NCCN 2025]`,`The **IAP 2017 ABC** concept adds **biological (CA 19-9 over 500 U/mL)** and **conditional (ECOG 2 or more)** criteria to anatomical resectability. [IAP 2017]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Painless progressive jaundice** with dark urine, pale stools and **pruritus** - the classic head-of-pancreas presentation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Weight loss and anorexia** in over 80%; **epigastric pain radiating to the back**, worse lying flat and eased by sitting forward. [Harrison 22e]`,`**New-onset diabetes after age 50 with weight loss** is the most important early clue - about **1% of such patients** have pancreatic cancer within 3 years. [Chari, Gastroenterology 2005]`,`The **ENDPAC score** (age at diabetes onset, weight change, glucose change) identifies new diabetics at higher risk who merit imaging. [Sharma, Gastroenterology 2018]`,`**Steatorrhoea** (duct obstruction), unexplained **acute pancreatitis** in an older non-drinker, **depression** and fatigue may precede diagnosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Unexplained DVT or migratory thrombophlebitis** (Trousseau) should prompt a search for occult cancer. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cystic lesions** are usually asymptomatic and incidental; symptoms (pancreatitis, jaundice, pain) are themselves worrisome features. [Kyoto 2023]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Jaundice with scratch marks**; **Courvoisier sign** - a palpable non-tender gallbladder in a jaundiced patient suggests malignant distal CBD obstruction. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Hepatomegaly** (metastases or cholestasis), **epigastric mass**, **ascites** (peritoneal disease). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Virchow node** (left supraclavicular), **Sister Mary Joseph nodule** (periumbilical) and **Blumer shelf** on rectal exam signal metastatic spread. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cachexia and temporal wasting**; **migratory superficial thrombophlebitis** (Trousseau sign of malignancy). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Splenomegaly** suggests splenic vein invasion with sinistral portal hypertension. [Harrison 22e]`,`Record **ECOG performance status** - it decides fitness for surgery and FOLFIRINOX. [NCCN 2025]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Other periampullary cancers** (ampullary, distal cholangiocarcinoma, duodenal) - better prognosis; same Whipple operation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Choledocholithiasis** - painful, fluctuating jaundice, fever; non-palpable gallbladder. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Autoimmune pancreatitis** - diffuse 'sausage' gland or focal mass, **IgG4 raised**, other organ involvement, responds to steroids; biopsy before labelling as cancer. [ICDC 2011]`,`**Mass-forming chronic pancreatitis** - calcification, duct-penetrating sign; needs EUS-FNB. [ACG 2020]`,`**Pancreatic neuroendocrine tumour** - well-defined, **hyperenhancing** in arterial phase, often functional (insulinoma, gastrinoma). [NCCN 2025]`,`**Pseudocyst vs cystic neoplasm** - history of pancreatitis, no septations, high cyst amylase, low CEA favour pseudocyst. [European cyst guideline 2018]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Ultrasound** first in jaundice: dilated intrahepatic ducts and CBD with a distended gallbladder; a head mass may be obscured by bowel gas. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pancreatic-protocol multiphasic CT**: thin slices (3 mm or less, ideally under 1 mm), **pancreatic parenchymal (late arterial, about 40-50 s)** and **portal venous (about 65-70 s)** phases - done **before any biliary stent**, which causes artefact and inflammation. [NCCN 2025]`,`Typical CT: **hypoattenuating mass**, **double duct sign** (dilated CBD and pancreatic duct), upstream atrophy, vascular contact measured in degrees; sensitivity about 90%. [NCCN 2025]`,`**MRI with MRCP** for liver metastases (more sensitive than CT for small lesions) and for cysts; **PET-CT** in high-risk features (CA 19-9 very high) to detect occult metastases. [NCCN 2025]`,`**EUS-FNB** is the preferred tissue diagnosis; **tissue is mandatory before neoadjuvant or palliative chemotherapy**, but a resectable typical mass in a fit patient can go straight to surgery. [NCCN 2025]`,`**CA 19-9**: ULN 37 U/mL; sensitivity about 80%, specificity about 80-90%; **falsely raised in cholestasis and cholangitis** (recheck after biliary drainage) and **undetectable in Lewis-antigen-negative people (5-10%)**. [ASCO 2006; NCCN 2025]`,`CA 19-9 is used for **prognosis, response and recurrence monitoring - never for screening**; a pre-operative value **over 500 U/mL** signals likely occult metastasis (biological borderline). [IAP 2017]`,`**Staging laparoscopy** for high-risk features (large tumour, high CA 19-9, equivocal imaging) detects peritoneal disease missed on CT. [NCCN 2025]`,`**Germline testing for all PDAC patients** and somatic tumour profiling (KRAS wild-type, MSI-high, NTRK, BRCA) in advanced disease. [NCCN 2025]`]},{heading:`Pancreatic cystic lesions - types and Fukuoka 2017 / Kyoto 2023`,points:[`**Four main cyst types**: **IPMN** (communicates with duct, mucin; main-duct, branch-duct or mixed), **MCN** (women, body/tail, ovarian-type stroma, no duct communication), **serous cystadenoma** (elderly women, microcystic honeycomb with central scar, benign) and **solid pseudopapillary neoplasm** (young women, malignant potential). [European cyst guideline 2018]`,`**Kyoto 2023 high-risk stigmata** (resect if fit): **obstructive jaundice** with a head cyst, **enhancing mural nodule 5 mm or more**, **main pancreatic duct 10 mm or more**, and **suspicious or positive cytology** (newly added). [Kyoto 2023]`,`**Kyoto 2023 worrisome features** (EUS ± FNA): acute pancreatitis, raised CA 19-9, **new-onset or worsening diabetes within 1 year**, cyst **30 mm or more**, enhancing mural nodule under 5 mm, thickened enhancing wall, **MPD 5-9 mm**, abrupt duct calibre change with distal atrophy, lymphadenopathy, **cyst growth 2.5 mm or more per year**. [Kyoto 2023]`,`**Fukuoka 2017** used similar features but defined growth as **5 mm or more over 2 years** and did not include new-onset diabetes or cytology among the criteria. [Fukuoka 2017]`,`**Main-duct IPMN** carries about **60% risk of high-grade dysplasia or cancer** - resect fit patients with MPD 10 mm or more; branch-duct IPMN is much lower risk (about 1-2% per year progression in larger cysts). [Kyoto 2023]`,`**Cyst fluid**: **CEA over 192 ng/mL** suggests mucinous cyst; **glucose under 50 mg/dL** is more sensitive for mucinous; high amylase suggests pseudocyst or IPMN; **KRAS/GNAS mutations** support IPMN. [Kyoto 2023]`,`**Surveillance of branch-duct IPMN** without worrisome features is by **MRI/MRCP** (or CT/EUS) at intervals set by cyst size, lengthened for small stable cysts; surveillance stops when the patient is no longer fit for surgery. [Kyoto 2023]`,`**Serous cystadenoma** needs no surveillance once confirmed unless symptomatic; **MCN 4 cm or more, symptomatic or with risk features, and all SPN** should be resected. [European cyst guideline 2018]`]},{heading:`Management - resectable and borderline disease`,points:[`**Discuss every case in a multidisciplinary tumour board** at a high-volume pancreatic centre - surgical mortality is lower (under 3-5%) where volumes are high. [NCCN 2025]`,`**Head tumours**: **pancreaticoduodenectomy (Whipple)** or pylorus-preserving PD; **body/tail**: **distal pancreatectomy with splenectomy** (radical antegrade modular, RAMPS). [NCCN 2025]`,`**Adjuvant chemotherapy for 6 months** in all fit resected patients: **modified FOLFIRINOX** (PRODIGE 24 - median survival about 54 months) or **gemcitabine plus capecitabine** (ESPAC-4) if less fit. [NCCN 2025; ESMO 2023]`,`**Borderline resectable**: **neoadjuvant chemotherapy** (mFOLFIRINOX or gemcitabine-nab-paclitaxel) ± chemoradiation, then re-stage and resect. [NCCN 2025]`,`**Pre-operative biliary drainage** is avoided if surgery is prompt (DROP trial - more complications); drain with a **short self-expanding metal stent** if there is cholangitis, severe pruritus, or neoadjuvant therapy is planned. [ESGE 2018]`,`**Neoadjuvant therapy for resectable disease** is increasingly used for high-risk features (CA 19-9 very high, large nodes, borderline performance). [NCCN 2025]`]},{heading:`Management - advanced disease and palliation`,points:[`**Metastatic, ECOG 0-1**: **FOLFIRINOX**, **NALIRIFOX** (liposomal irinotecan + 5-FU + leucovorin + oxaliplatin; NAPOLI-3 2023) or **gemcitabine plus nab-paclitaxel**. [NCCN 2025]`,`**ECOG 2**: gemcitabine alone or gemcitabine-nab-paclitaxel; **ECOG 3-4**: best supportive care. [ESMO 2023]`,`**Germline BRCA**: platinum-based first line and **olaparib maintenance** (POLO trial); **MSI-high**: pembrolizumab. [NCCN 2025]`,`**Biliary obstruction**: **endoscopic self-expanding metal stent (SEMS)** via ERCP (or EUS-guided drainage) - better patency than plastic stents. [ESGE 2018]`,`**Gastric outlet obstruction**: **duodenal stent** or **EUS-guided gastroenterostomy** / surgical gastrojejunostomy. [ESGE 2022]`,`**Pain**: WHO ladder with early **morphine**; **EUS-guided coeliac plexus neurolysis** reduces opioid needs; palliative radiotherapy. [ESMO 2023]`,`**PERT** for steatorrhoea and weight loss (40,000-50,000 units per meal), **LMWH** for VTE (rather than warfarin), **mirtazapine/olanzapine** and dietitian support for cachexia. [ESMO 2023]`,`**Early palliative care referral** at diagnosis of advanced disease improves quality of life; enrol in **PM-JAY** oncology packages for chemotherapy cost. [ESMO 2023]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Elderly**: decide by frailty and ECOG, not age; gemcitabine-based regimens are better tolerated than FOLFIRINOX. [ESMO 2023]`,`**Diabetes**: a long-standing diabetic whose control suddenly worsens with weight loss needs pancreatic imaging. [ADA Standards 2025]`,`**Chronic pancreatitis**: a new focal mass, jaundice or weight loss needs EUS-FNB to exclude cancer. [ACG 2020]`,`**Pregnancy**: MRI without gadolinium for cysts; MCNs may enlarge in pregnancy (ovarian stroma, hormone-responsive). [European cyst guideline 2018]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: ultrasound and a contrast CT before stenting, then early referral; avoid inserting plastic stents that complicate staging. [NCCN 2025]`,`**Liver disease or obstructive jaundice** limits chemotherapy (irinotecan and nab-paclitaxel need dose reduction) - drain first. [NCCN 2025]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Post-Whipple complications**: **postoperative pancreatic fistula** (ISGPS grade B/C), delayed gastric emptying, post-pancreatectomy haemorrhage, new diabetes and EPI. [ISGPS 2016]`,`**Disease complications**: biliary obstruction, cholangitis, duodenal obstruction, VTE (up to 20%), cachexia, malignant ascites. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Median survival**: metastatic about **11 months with FOLFIRINOX/NALIRIFOX** versus about 6 months with gemcitabine alone; resected with mFOLFIRINOX about **54 months**. [NCCN 2025]`,`**Best prognostic factors**: R0 resection, node-negative disease, CA 19-9 normalisation after surgery, and completion of adjuvant therapy. [NCCN 2025]`,`**Branch-duct IPMN without worrisome features** has an annual malignancy risk of well under 1%; most never need surgery. [Kyoto 2023]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and surveillance`,points:[`**Urgent referral**: painless jaundice in anyone over 40; weight loss with new diabetes, back pain, or steatorrhoea in those over 60. [NICE NG12]`,`**After resection**: history, examination, **CA 19-9 and CT every 3-6 months for 2 years**, then every 6-12 months. [NCCN 2025]`,`**High-risk individual surveillance (CAPS 2020)**: annual **EUS and/or MRI/MRCP** for familial pancreatic cancer and gene carriers, starting around **age 50** (or 10 years before the youngest affected relative), **age 40 for CDKN2A and Peutz-Jeghers** and PRSS1 hereditary pancreatitis. [CAPS 2020]`,`**USPSTF 2019** recommends **against screening average-risk adults** (grade D). [USPSTF 2019]`,`**Incidental cyst**: refer to a gastroenterologist for MRI/MRCP characterisation; urgent referral if any high-risk stigma. [Kyoto 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**Kyoto 2023 IPMN guideline** replaced Fukuoka 2017: added **positive cytology** to high-risk stigmata, **new-onset/worsening diabetes** to worrisome features, redefined growth as **2.5 mm or more per year**, and lengthened intervals for small stable cysts. [Kyoto 2023]`,`**NAPOLI-3 (2023)**: **NALIRIFOX** improved survival over gemcitabine-nab-paclitaxel and became an approved first-line regimen (US FDA 2024). [NCCN 2025]`,`**NCCN 2024-2025**: **universal germline testing** for every PDAC patient and somatic profiling in advanced disease. [NCCN 2025]`,`**CAPS 2020** consensus defined who to screen, from what age, and with which modality (EUS/MRI). [CAPS 2020]`,`**ESMO 2023** pancreatic cancer guideline endorses mFOLFIRINOX adjuvant and neoadjuvant therapy for borderline disease. [ESMO 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Viva: **'What is the double duct sign?'** - simultaneous dilatation of CBD and pancreatic duct from a head mass. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: **why not screen with CA 19-9?** - low positive predictive value (under 1%) in an average-risk population. [ASCO 2006]`,`Viva: **Courvoisier law, Trousseau sign, Virchow node** - classical eponyms. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`Common mistake: **stenting before CT** - obtain pancreatic-protocol CT first. [NCCN 2025]`,`Common mistake: interpreting a raised CA 19-9 in cholangitis as cancer - **recheck after drainage**. [NCCN 2025]`,`Know **IPMN vs MCN vs SCA** in one table and the **Kyoto high-risk stigmata** - frequent short-note topics. [Examiner practice]`]}],tables:[{heading:`NCCN resectability of pancreatic cancer`,columns:[`Category`,`Arterial (coeliac, SMA, CHA)`,`Venous (SMV, PV)`,`Plan`],rows:[[`Resectable`,`No contact`,`No contact or <= 180 degrees without contour irregularity`,`Upfront surgery then adjuvant mFOLFIRINOX 6 months`],[`Borderline resectable`,`CHA contact without coeliac/HA bifurcation; SMA <= 180 degrees`,`> 180 degrees or thrombosis but reconstructible`,`Neoadjuvant chemotherapy then surgery`],[`Locally advanced`,`SMA or coeliac > 180 degrees`,`Unreconstructible`,`Chemotherapy ± chemoradiation; re-stage`],[`Metastatic`,`Any`,`Any`,`FOLFIRINOX / NALIRIFOX / gem-nab-paclitaxel; palliation`]]},{heading:`Pancreatic cystic lesions compared`,columns:[`Lesion`,`Patient`,`Imaging`,`Cyst fluid`,`Malignant risk / action`],rows:[[`Branch-duct IPMN`,`Men and women, 60s-70s`,`Communicates with duct, grape-like`,`CEA high, KRAS/GNAS`,`Low; surveillance unless WF/HRS`],[`Main-duct IPMN`,`Elderly`,`MPD dilated >= 5 mm, fish-mouth papilla`,`Mucin`,`High (about 60%); resect if MPD >= 10 mm`],[`Mucinous cystic neoplasm`,`Women 40-50s`,`Body/tail, unilocular, peripheral calcification, no duct communication`,`CEA high`,`Moderate; resect if >= 4 cm or risk features`],[`Serous cystadenoma`,`Women 60s ('grandmother')`,`Microcystic honeycomb, central scar`,`CEA low, glucose normal`,`Benign; leave unless symptomatic`],[`Solid pseudopapillary neoplasm`,`Young women ('daughter')`,`Solid-cystic, haemorrhage`,`Bloody`,`Low-grade malignant; resect`],[`Pseudocyst`,`History of pancreatitis`,`Unilocular, no septa or nodule`,`Amylase very high, CEA low`,`None; drain if symptomatic`]]},{heading:`Kyoto 2023 IPMN criteria (changes from Fukuoka 2017)`,columns:[`Category`,`Criteria`,`Change from Fukuoka 2017`],rows:[[`High-risk stigmata`,`Obstructive jaundice (head lesion); enhancing mural nodule >= 5 mm; MPD >= 10 mm; suspicious/positive cytology`,`Cytology added`],[`Worrisome features - clinical`,`Acute pancreatitis; raised CA 19-9; new-onset or worsening diabetes within 1 year`,`Diabetes added`],[`Worrisome features - imaging`,`Cyst >= 30 mm; mural nodule < 5 mm; thick enhancing wall; MPD 5-9 mm; abrupt calibre change with atrophy; nodes`,`Unchanged`],[`Growth`,`>= 2.5 mm per year`,`Was >= 5 mm over 2 years`],[`Action`,`HRS: surgery if fit; WF: EUS ± FNA then decide`,`Same principle`]]},{heading:`Drugs in pancreatic cancer`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Modified FOLFIRINOX`,`Oxaliplatin 85 mg/m2, irinotecan 150 mg/m2, leucovorin 400 mg/m2, 5-FU 2,400 mg/m2 46-h infusion, IV every 2 weeks`,`12 cycles (6 months) adjuvant`,`Neutropenia, diarrhoea, neuropathy; needs ECOG 0-1 and bilirubin near normal`],[`Gemcitabine + capecitabine`,`Gemcitabine 1,000 mg/m2 IV days 1, 8, 15 + capecitabine 830 mg/m2 orally twice daily days 1-21, 28-day cycle`,`6 cycles adjuvant`,`Myelosuppression, hand-foot syndrome`],[`Gemcitabine + nab-paclitaxel`,`Nab-paclitaxel 125 mg/m2 + gemcitabine 1,000 mg/m2 IV days 1, 8, 15 every 28 days`,`Until progression`,`Neuropathy, neutropenia, fatigue`],[`NALIRIFOX`,`Liposomal irinotecan 50 mg/m2, oxaliplatin 60 mg/m2, leucovorin 400 mg/m2, 5-FU 2,400 mg/m2 IV every 2 weeks`,`Until progression`,`Diarrhoea, nausea, neutropenia`],[`Olaparib`,`300 mg orally twice daily`,`Maintenance after platinum in germline BRCA`,`Anaemia, nausea, MDS rarely`],[`Pancreatin (PERT)`,`40,000-50,000 lipase units orally with meals`,`Throughout illness`,`Bloating`],[`Enoxaparin`,`1 mg/kg subcutaneously 12-hourly or 1.5 mg/kg daily`,`At least 6 months for cancer VTE`,`Bleeding; reduce in CKD`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of a pancreatic head mass with jaundice`,columns:[`Condition`,`Clue`,`Discriminating test`],rows:[[`PDAC`,`Older smoker, weight loss, new diabetes`,`Hypoattenuating mass, double duct, CA 19-9, EUS-FNB`],[`Ampullary carcinoma`,`Intermittent jaundice, occult bleed`,`Side-viewing endoscopy and biopsy`],[`Distal cholangiocarcinoma`,`Jaundice, no pancreatic mass`,`MRCP, brush cytology`],[`Autoimmune pancreatitis`,`Diffuse enlargement, other organs`,`IgG4 > 2 times ULN, steroid response`],[`Mass-forming chronic pancreatitis`,`Long history, calcification`,`Duct-penetrating sign, EUS-FNB`],[`Neuroendocrine tumour`,`Hormonal syndrome or incidental`,`Arterial hyperenhancement, chromogranin, Ga-68 DOTATATE`]]}],redFlags:[`Painless progressive jaundice in an adult over 40`,`New-onset diabetes after 50 with unintentional weight loss`,`Epigastric pain radiating to the back with weight loss`,`Unexplained migratory thrombophlebitis or unprovoked DVT with weight loss`,`Pancreatic cyst with jaundice, enhancing mural nodule >= 5 mm or main duct >= 10 mm`,`Fever with rigors in a stented or jaundiced patient - cholangitis`,`Persistent vomiting in known pancreatic cancer - gastric outlet obstruction`],pearls:[`**KRAS** is mutated in over 90% of PDAC.`,`**New diabetes after 50 with weight loss** - image the pancreas.`,`**Double duct sign** = dilated CBD and pancreatic duct from a head mass.`,`**CT before stent** - a stent spoils staging.`,`**CA 19-9** is for prognosis and follow-up, never screening; false high in cholestasis, absent in Lewis-negative.`,`Resectable = **no arterial contact**; SMA over 180 degrees = locally advanced.`,`Adjuvant **mFOLFIRINOX for 6 months** after resection in fit patients.`,`Kyoto high-risk stigmata: **jaundice, nodule 5 mm or more, MPD 10 mm or more, positive cytology**.`,`**Serous cystadenoma** = grandmother lesion, honeycomb, benign; **SPN** = daughter lesion.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-pancreatic-cancer-cystic-q1`,stem:`A 64-year-old man has painless jaundice and weight loss of 7 kg. Ultrasound shows dilated CBD and intrahepatic ducts. Bilirubin is 9 mg/dL; there is no fever. What is the most appropriate next step?`,options:[`ERCP with plastic stent before any further imaging`,`Serum CA 19-9 alone to confirm the diagnosis`,`Pancreatic-protocol multiphasic CT before any biliary stenting`,`Percutaneous liver biopsy`],answer:2,explanation:`Pancreatic-protocol CT should be done before stenting because a stent causes artefact and inflammation that impair staging of vascular contact. Early ERCP stenting is not needed without cholangitis and may increase surgical complications. CA 19-9 is falsely raised in cholestasis and cannot confirm the diagnosis, and liver biopsy has no role in a suspected head mass.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-pancreatic-cancer-cystic-q2`,stem:`On CT, a pancreatic head adenocarcinoma abuts the superior mesenteric artery over 120 degrees of its circumference, with no venous or distant disease. Under NCCN criteria this is:`,options:[`Resectable`,`Borderline resectable`,`Locally advanced`,`Metastatic`],answer:1,explanation:`SMA contact of 180 degrees or less is borderline resectable, treated with neoadjuvant chemotherapy then surgery. Resectable disease requires no arterial contact at all. Locally advanced disease needs encasement over 180 degrees of the SMA or coeliac axis, and there is no distant spread to call it metastatic.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-pancreatic-cancer-cystic-q3`,stem:`A 68-year-old woman has a 22 mm branch-duct IPMN on MRCP. Which finding is a high-risk stigma under the Kyoto 2023 guideline?`,options:[`Cyst size of 32 mm`,`Main pancreatic duct of 7 mm`,`New-onset diabetes in the past year`,`Enhancing mural nodule of 6 mm`],answer:3,explanation:`An enhancing mural nodule of 5 mm or more is a high-risk stigma, along with obstructive jaundice, main duct of 10 mm or more and positive cytology, and indicates surgery if fit. Cyst size of 30 mm or more, MPD 5-9 mm and new-onset diabetes are worrisome features that prompt EUS rather than immediate surgery.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-pancreatic-cancer-cystic-q4`,stem:`Regarding CA 19-9 in pancreatic cancer, which statement is correct?`,options:[`It may be undetectable in Lewis-antigen-negative patients despite advanced cancer`,`It is recommended for population screening above age 50`,`A normal value excludes pancreatic cancer`,`It is unaffected by biliary obstruction`],answer:0,explanation:`About 5-10% of people are Lewis-antigen negative and cannot synthesise CA 19-9, so the marker stays low even in advanced disease. It is not used for screening because its positive predictive value in average-risk people is under 1%. A normal value does not exclude cancer, and cholestasis or cholangitis falsely raises it.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-pancreatic-cancer-cystic-q5`,stem:`A 70-year-old woman has an incidental 4 cm cyst in the pancreatic head with a microcystic honeycomb pattern and a central calcified scar. Cyst fluid CEA is low. Best management if asymptomatic:`,options:[`Whipple pancreaticoduodenectomy`,`Reassurance without routine surveillance once the diagnosis is confident`,`Six-monthly EUS-FNA for life`,`Cystogastrostomy`],answer:1,explanation:`The honeycomb pattern with central scar and low CEA indicates serous cystadenoma, which is benign; asymptomatic lesions need no resection or routine surveillance. Whipple is for malignant or high-risk lesions and carries substantial morbidity. Lifelong EUS-FNA is unnecessary, and cystogastrostomy is for symptomatic pseudocysts, not neoplasms.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-pancreatic-cancer-cystic-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Discuss the risk factors, clinical features, staging and management of carcinoma of the head of the pancreas. Add a note on the role of CA 19-9.`,openingLines:[`Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma is a KRAS-driven cancer, 60-70% in the head, presenting with painless progressive jaundice and weight loss, and 80% are unresectable at diagnosis.`,`Management depends on resectability on pancreatic-protocol CT: resectable, borderline, locally advanced or metastatic.`],answer:[{heading:`Risk factors`,points:[`Smoking, diabetes, obesity, chronic pancreatitis, family history, BRCA2/PALB2, Lynch, Peutz-Jeghers, CDKN2A, IPMN.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Painless jaundice, pruritus, weight loss, back pain, new-onset diabetes after 50, Courvoisier sign, Trousseau sign, Virchow node.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`USG; pancreatic-protocol CT before stenting (double duct sign); MRI for liver; EUS-FNB; CA 19-9; staging laparoscopy; germline testing.`]},{heading:`Staging and resectability`,points:[`AJCC 8e TNM; NCCN resectable (no arterial contact), borderline (SMA <= 180 degrees, CHA, reconstructible vein), locally advanced, metastatic.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Resectable: Whipple then mFOLFIRINOX 6 months; borderline: neoadjuvant then surgery; metastatic: FOLFIRINOX/NALIRIFOX/gem-nab-paclitaxel; olaparib for BRCA.`]},{heading:`Palliation`,points:[`SEMS for jaundice, duodenal stent or GJ, coeliac plexus neurolysis, PERT, LMWH, palliative care.`]},{heading:`CA 19-9`,points:[`ULN 37 U/mL; prognosis and follow-up, not screening; false high in cholestasis; absent in Lewis-negative; > 500 U/mL suggests occult metastasis.`]}],mustDraw:[`Resectability flow chart from CT to treatment`,`Diagram of Whipple resection`],markSplit:[{part:`Risk factors and clinical features`,marks:3},{part:`Investigations and staging`,marks:3},{part:`Management and palliation`,marks:3},{part:`CA 19-9 note`,marks:1}],keywords:[`PDAC`,`Whipple`,`FOLFIRINOX`,`resectability`,`CA 19-9`,`double duct sign`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-pancreatic-cancer-cystic-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`uncommon`,question:`Differentiate the common pancreatic cystic lesions and list the Kyoto 2023 high-risk stigmata for IPMN.`,openingLines:[`Pancreatic cysts are found incidentally in up to 15-20% of adults on MRI; the key task is to separate mucinous (premalignant) from non-mucinous cysts.`,`IPMNs are triaged by the Kyoto 2023 high-risk stigmata and worrisome features.`],answer:[{heading:`Differentiating table`,points:[`IPMN (duct communication, mucin, elderly), MCN (women, body/tail, ovarian stroma), serous cystadenoma (honeycomb, central scar, benign), SPN (young women), pseudocyst (pancreatitis history, high amylase).`]},{heading:`Cyst fluid`,points:[`CEA > 192 ng/mL and glucose < 50 mg/dL suggest mucinous cyst.`]},{heading:`Kyoto high-risk stigmata`,points:[`Obstructive jaundice, enhancing mural nodule >= 5 mm, MPD >= 10 mm, positive cytology.`]},{heading:`Worrisome features`,points:[`Pancreatitis, CA 19-9, new diabetes, cyst >= 30 mm, MPD 5-9 mm, nodule < 5 mm, growth >= 2.5 mm/year.`]}],mustDraw:[`Comparison table of five cyst types`],markSplit:[{part:`Comparison table`,marks:3},{part:`High-risk stigmata and worrisome features`,marks:2}],keywords:[`IPMN`,`MCN`,`serous cystadenoma`,`Kyoto 2023`,`Fukuoka 2017`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Pancreatic cancer`,`NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Pancreatic Adenocarcinoma, version 2025`,`ESMO Clinical Practice Guideline: Pancreatic cancer, Ann Oncol 2023`,`Ohtsuka T et al. International evidence-based Kyoto guidelines for the management of IPMN of the pancreas, Pancreatology 2024 (Kyoto 2023)`,`Tanaka M et al. Revisions of international consensus Fukuoka guidelines for IPMN, Pancreatology 2017`,`European evidence-based guidelines on pancreatic cystic neoplasms, Gut 2018`,`Goggins M et al. International Cancer of the Pancreas Screening (CAPS) Consortium consensus, Gut 2020`,`ICMR-NCDIR Report of National Cancer Registry Programme 2020`]}),s.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-liver-in-pregnancy`,title:`Liver disease in pregnancy`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`**Liver disease in pregnancy** (abnormal liver tests in about **3-5% of pregnancies**) is either **pregnancy-specific** - **hyperemesis gravidarum, intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy, pre-eclampsia/HELLP and acute fatty liver of pregnancy (Swansea criteria)** - or **intercurrent/pre-existing** disease such as **hepatitis E (high maternal mortality in India)** and **hepatitis B (prevent vertical transmission with tenofovir, birth-dose vaccine and HBIG)**; the **trimester** is the first diagnostic clue and **delivery** is the definitive treatment for AFLP and HELLP.`,keywords:[`hyperemesis gravidarum`,`intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy`,`obstetric cholestasis`,`ICP`,`bile acids`,`ursodeoxycholic acid`,`acute fatty liver of pregnancy`,`AFLP`,`Swansea criteria`,`LCHAD deficiency`,`HELLP syndrome`,`pre-eclampsia`,`hepatic rupture`,`hepatitis E in pregnancy`,`HBV mother-to-child transmission`,`tenofovir in pregnancy`,`HBIG`,`Budd-Chiari syndrome`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Abnormal liver tests occur in about 3-5% of pregnancies**; pregnancy-specific causes account for most in the West, whereas **viral hepatitis (especially E)** dominates acute liver failure in pregnancy in India. [EASL 2023]`,`**Hyperemesis gravidarum** affects about **0.3-2%** of pregnancies (first trimester); **ICP** about **0.5-1.5%** worldwide (higher in South Asia, Chile and Bolivia); **HELLP** about **0.5-0.9%** of all pregnancies and **10-20% of severe pre-eclampsia**; **AFLP** about **1 in 7,000-20,000**. [EASL 2023]`,`**Normal pregnancy changes**: **ALP rises 2-4 times** (placental isoenzyme), **albumin falls** (haemodilution), AFP, fibrinogen, cholesterol and triglycerides rise; **ALT, AST, GGT, bilirubin and bile acids stay normal** - any rise in these is pathological. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Spider naevi and palmar erythema** appear in up to 60% of normal pregnancies (oestrogen) and do not mean liver disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hepatitis E** causes acute liver failure in pregnancy with maternal mortality of about **15-25%, highest in the third trimester**, in India and South Asia (genotype 1). [EASL 2023]`,`**HBsAg prevalence in pregnant women in India is about 1%** (range 0.5-3%); without prophylaxis, transmission is up to **70-90% from HBeAg-positive mothers**. [NVHCP 2018]`,`India's maternal mortality ratio is about **97 per 100,000 live births (SRS 2018-20)**; liver disease and hepatitis contribute to indirect maternal deaths. [SRS 2018-20]`]},{heading:`Aetiology - classification by cause and trimester`,points:[`**First trimester**: **hyperemesis gravidarum** (transaminases up in about 50% of admitted cases). [EASL 2023]`,`**Second-third trimester**: **intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy** (from about 25-30 weeks onwards). [RCOG GTG 43 2022]`,`**Third trimester (and post-partum)**: **pre-eclampsia with liver involvement, HELLP syndrome, AFLP**, hepatic haematoma and rupture. [EASL 2023]`,`**Intercurrent at any time**: viral hepatitis (A, B, E, HSV, CMV), **drug-induced liver injury**, gallstones and cholangitis, **Budd-Chiari** (pregnancy is prothrombotic), sepsis. [EASL 2023]`,`**Pre-existing**: chronic hepatitis B/C, **cirrhosis and portal hypertension**, autoimmune hepatitis, Wilson disease, **liver transplant recipients**, MASLD. [EASL 2023]`,`**ICP risk factors**: personal or family history, **multiple pregnancy, IVF**, hepatitis C, ABCB4 (MDR3) and ABCB11 (BSEP) variants. [RCOG GTG 43 2022]`,`**AFLP risk factors**: **fetal LCHAD deficiency**, **twin pregnancy, male fetus, low BMI, primigravida**, pre-eclampsia. [EASL 2023]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**ICP**: high **oestrogen and progesterone sulphated metabolites** inhibit the bile salt export pump (BSEP) in genetically susceptible women; bile acids cross the placenta and are **cardiotoxic to the fetus** (arrhythmia) and cause meconium passage - explaining **stillbirth risk at very high bile acids**. [RCOG GTG 43 2022]`,`**AFLP**: a fetus homozygous for **LCHAD deficiency (G1528C)** cannot oxidise long-chain fatty acids; toxic metabolites return to the heterozygous mother, causing **microvesicular steatosis** and mitochondrial liver failure - hypoglycaemia, coagulopathy, encephalopathy. [EASL 2023]`,`**HELLP**: abnormal placentation releases anti-angiogenic factors (**sFlt-1, soluble endoglin**) causing endothelial injury, **microangiopathic haemolysis**, platelet consumption and **periportal hepatic necrosis** with fibrin deposition - the source of RUQ pain and subcapsular haematoma. [ACOG PB 222 2020]`,`**Hyperemesis**: high **hCG** and **GDF15** drive vomiting; dehydration and starvation raise transaminases, which normalise with rehydration. [RCOG GTG 69 2024]`,`**Why HEV is lethal in pregnancy**: altered T-helper (Th2-dominant) immunity, high oestrogen/progesterone enhancing viral replication, and placental HEV replication. [EASL 2023]`,`**Pregnancy hyperdynamic circulation** raises portal pressure - **variceal bleeding risk peaks in the second trimester and labour** (Valsalva) in women with portal hypertension. [EASL 2023]`]},{heading:`Diagnostic criteria and classification`,points:[`**ICP (RCOG 2022)**: **pruritus plus random (non-fasting) total bile acids of 19 micromol/L or more**, with no alternative cause; **severe at 40 or more**, and **very high stillbirth risk at 100 or more micromol/L**. [RCOG GTG 43 2022]`,`Other guidelines (SMFM 2021, EASL 2023) use a **fasting bile acid cut-off of 10 micromol/L or more** for diagnosis - note the difference when reading Indian lab reports. [EASL 2023]`,`**AFLP - Swansea criteria (6 or more of 14)**: vomiting, abdominal pain, polydipsia/polyuria, encephalopathy, raised bilirubin, **hypoglycaemia**, raised urate, leucocytosis, ascites or bright liver on USG, raised transaminases, raised ammonia, renal impairment, **coagulopathy**, microvesicular steatosis on biopsy. [Swansea, Gut 2002]`,`**HELLP (Tennessee)**: **haemolysis** (schistocytes, LDH 600 IU/L or more, bilirubin 1.2 mg/dL or more), **AST 70 IU/L or more**, **platelets under 100,000/mm3**; partial HELLP has one or two features. [ACOG PB 222 2020]`,`**Mississippi classification of HELLP** by platelet nadir: class 1 under 50,000, class 2 50,000-100,000, class 3 100,000-150,000 - class 1 has the highest morbidity. [ACOG PB 222 2020]`,`**Hyperemesis gravidarum (RCOG 2024)**: severe nausea and vomiting with inability to eat or drink normally, strongly limiting daily activities; severity scored by **PUQE-24** (13 or more = severe). [RCOG GTG 69 2024]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**ICP**: **pruritus of palms and soles, worse at night**, without rash (only excoriations), from late second trimester; jaundice in only about 10-15%. [RCOG GTG 43 2022]`,`**AFLP**: 1-2 weeks of **malaise, nausea, vomiting, epigastric pain, polydipsia** (transient diabetes insipidus) in the third trimester, then jaundice, confusion and bleeding. [EASL 2023]`,`**HELLP**: **epigastric or RUQ pain, nausea, malaise** in the third trimester or up to 48 hours post-partum; hypertension may be mild or absent in about 15%. [ACOG PB 222 2020]`,`**Hepatic rupture**: sudden severe RUQ pain, shoulder-tip pain and shock in a pre-eclamptic woman - a surgical emergency. [ACOG PB 222 2020]`,`**Acute viral hepatitis**: prodrome of fever, anorexia, vomiting, then jaundice; **HSV hepatitis** gives fever, very high transaminases and often **no jaundice** - start acyclovir empirically. [EASL 2023]`,`**Hepatitis E** is suspected in an outbreak or monsoon setting, with contaminated water; progression to **encephalopathy within days** signals ALF. [EASL 2023]`,`Ask about **drugs** (antithyroid, antiepileptics, antibiotics, herbal remedies, high-dose paracetamol) and **previous ICP** (recurrence 45-90%). [RCOG GTG 43 2022]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**BP and urine protein** at every visit - hypertension and proteinuria point to pre-eclampsia/HELLP. [ACOG PB 222 2020]`,`**Jaundice, flapping tremor and confusion** indicate hepatic encephalopathy - AFLP or ALF from viral hepatitis. [EASL 2023]`,`**Excoriations without primary rash** suggest ICP; a rash suggests a pregnancy dermatosis (polymorphic eruption, pemphigoid gestationis). [RCOG GTG 43 2022]`,`**Liver size**: AFLP has a small or normal liver; a tender enlarged liver suggests HELLP with haematoma or Budd-Chiari (with ascites). [EASL 2023]`,`**Dehydration and ketosis** (dry mucosa, tachycardia, weight loss over 5%) in hyperemesis. [RCOG GTG 69 2024]`,`**Fetal assessment**: fetal heart rate, growth and liquor; **fetal distress** often prompts delivery in AFLP and HELLP. [EASL 2023]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis - AFLP versus HELLP and others`,points:[`**AFLP vs HELLP**: both occur in the third trimester; **hypoglycaemia, coagulopathy, encephalopathy, high ammonia and marked renal failure favour AFLP**; **hypertension, proteinuria and marked thrombocytopenia with haemolysis favour HELLP** - about 20-40% of AFLP overlap with pre-eclampsia. [EASL 2023]`,`**HELLP vs TTP/aHUS**: ADAMTS13 under 10% (TTP), severe renal failure persisting post-partum (aHUS); HELLP improves within 48-72 hours of delivery. [ACOG PB 222 2020]`,`**ICP vs viral hepatitis**: ICP has itch first and modest ALT; viral hepatitis has prodrome, ALT often over 1,000 IU/L and positive serology. [EASL 2023]`,`**Hepatitis E ALF vs AFLP**: HEV - IgM anti-HEV positive, any trimester (worst in third), often epidemic; AFLP - Swansea features with hypoglycaemia, serology negative. [EASL 2023]`,`**Biliary disease**: choledocholithiasis or cholangitis - colicky pain, dilated CBD on ultrasound. [EASL 2023]`,`**Budd-Chiari syndrome**: pain, ascites, hepatomegaly, Doppler shows hepatic vein occlusion - commoner post-partum. [EASL 2023]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**First line**: CBC with smear (schistocytes), LFT (ALT, AST, bilirubin, ALP, GGT), **random total bile acids**, PT/INR, glucose, creatinine, uric acid, LDH, urine protein-creatinine ratio. [EASL 2023]`,`**Viral serology in every jaundiced pregnant woman**: **IgM anti-HAV, HBsAg and IgM anti-HBc, IgM anti-HEV**, anti-HCV, and HSV PCR if fever with high transaminases. [EASL 2023]`,`**Ultrasound** (safe) for fatty liver, haematoma, biliary obstruction and **Doppler** for Budd-Chiari; **MRI without gadolinium** if needed. [EASL 2023]`,`**Bile acids** rise after meals, so RCOG uses **non-fasting** values with a 19 micromol/L cut-off; repeat **weekly** once ICP is diagnosed to guide delivery timing. [RCOG GTG 43 2022]`,`**AFLP labs**: hypoglycaemia (under 72 mg/dL), raised urate, **prolonged PT and low fibrinogen**, leucocytosis, raised ammonia; liver biopsy is rarely needed (coagulopathy). [Swansea, Gut 2002]`,`**HELLP labs**: LDH 600 or more, AST 70 or more, platelets under 100,000; **CT or MRI** if hepatic haematoma or rupture is suspected. [ACOG PB 222 2020]`,`**HBsAg-positive mother**: HBeAg, **HBV DNA at 24-28 weeks**, ALT - decides tenofovir prophylaxis. [EASL 2023]`,`**Hyperemesis**: electrolytes (hypokalaemia), ketones, TSH/FT4 (transient gestational thyrotoxicosis), ultrasound for molar or multiple pregnancy. [RCOG GTG 69 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / antenatal clinic`,points:[`**Hyperemesis - first line**: **doxylamine-pyridoxine 10/10 mg**, 2 tablets at bedtime up to 4 a day, or promethazine/cyclizine; **second line ondansetron 4-8 mg 8-hourly** or metoclopramide 10 mg 8-hourly (max 5 days); **thiamine 100 mg daily** for prolonged vomiting. [RCOG GTG 69 2024]`,`Ondansetron carries a **very small increase in orofacial cleft risk** if used in the first trimester - discuss but do not withhold when needed. [RCOG GTG 69 2024]`,`**ICP**: **UDCA 10-15 mg/kg/day** (e.g. 300 mg 2-3 times daily, up to 21 mg/kg/day) improves itch and is recommended by EASL 2023 and SMFM; **RCOG 2022 does not recommend UDCA to improve perinatal outcome** (PITCHES trial) but allows it for maternal itch. [EASL 2023; RCOG GTG 43 2022]`,`**ICP symptom care**: emollients, **chlorphenamine** at night for sleep; **vitamin K** only if steatorrhoea or prolonged PT. [RCOG GTG 43 2022]`,`**ICP delivery timing (RCOG 2022)**: peak bile acids **19-39 micromol/L - planned birth by 40 weeks**; **40-99 - consider birth at 38-39 weeks**; **100 or more - consider birth at 35-36 weeks**. [RCOG GTG 43 2022]`,`**HBV**: **tenofovir disoproxil fumarate 300 mg once daily from 24-28 weeks** if HBV DNA is **200,000 IU/mL or more** (or HBeAg positive where DNA is unavailable), continued to at least delivery and usually 4-12 weeks post-partum. [EASL 2023; WHO HBV 2024]`,`**Newborn of HBsAg-positive mother**: **hepatitis B vaccine birth dose within 24 hours (ideally 12 hours) plus HBIG 0.5 mL (100 IU) IM** at a different site, then complete the schedule; test infant HBsAg and anti-HBs at 9-12 months. [NVHCP 2018; IAP 2023]`,`**Breastfeeding is allowed** with HBV (including on tenofovir) and HCV; caesarean section is **not indicated** solely to prevent HBV transmission. [EASL 2023]`,`**Chronic HCV**: no DAAs in pregnancy (insufficient data); treat after delivery and breastfeeding; vertical transmission about 5%; test infant (HCV RNA at 2-6 months or antibody after 18 months). [AASLD-IDSA 2023]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Admit hyperemesis** if unable to keep down oral fluids or antiemetics, ketonuria with weight loss over 5%, or electrolyte disturbance: **IV normal saline with potassium**, **thiamine before dextrose** (prevents Wernicke encephalopathy), IV antiemetics, thromboprophylaxis. [RCOG GTG 69 2024]`,`**AFLP**: obstetric emergency - **stabilise and deliver promptly** regardless of gestation; **IV 10-50% dextrose** for hypoglycaemia, FFP/cryoprecipitate for coagulopathy, ICU care; **plasma exchange** and liver transplant for refractory failure. [EASL 2023]`,`**HELLP**: stabilise BP (**labetalol 20 mg IV**, escalating, or **nifedipine 10 mg orally**), **magnesium sulphate** (Pritchard or Zuspan) for seizure prophylaxis, and **deliver if 34 weeks or more**, or at any gestation with deterioration; **betamethasone 12 mg IM 2 doses 24 hours apart** before 34 weeks if delay is safe. [ACOG PB 222 2020; FOGSI 2019]`,`**Platelet transfusion** in HELLP: to **50,000 or more before caesarean**; transfuse if under 20,000 for vaginal birth or with bleeding. [ACOG PB 222 2020]`,`**Hepatic haematoma**: conservative if contained and stable; **rupture** - massive transfusion, laparotomy with packing or angioembolisation, liver transplant in extremis. [EASL 2023]`,`**Hepatitis E ALF**: ICU at a liver centre, treat cerebral oedema, avoid sedatives; **delivery does not improve maternal outcome** - obstetric management as usual; **ribavirin is contraindicated** (teratogenic). [EASL 2023]`,`**HSV hepatitis**: **IV acyclovir 10 mg/kg 8-hourly** empirically - mortality is high without it. [EASL 2023]`,`**Varices in pregnancy**: screen by **endoscopy in the second trimester**; **propranolol** (safe) or band ligation for large varices; acute bleed - octreotide, endoscopic band ligation, ceftriaxone; vaginal delivery with a shortened second stage. [EASL 2023]`]},{heading:`Safe and unsafe drugs in pregnancy liver disease`,points:[`**Safe/acceptable**: paracetamol (up to 3 g/day in liver disease), UDCA, tenofovir disoproxil, lactulose, propranolol, prednisolone, **azathioprine**, tacrolimus, cyclosporine, PPIs, doxylamine-pyridoxine, metoclopramide, labetalol, nifedipine, magnesium sulphate. [EASL 2023]`,`**Contraindicated**: **ribavirin** (teratogenic, also for 6 months before conception in either partner), **mycophenolate** (embryopathy - stop 6 weeks before conception), **methotrexate**, warfarin (switch to LMWH). [EASL 2023]`,`**Avoid**: **NSAIDs after 20 weeks** (fetal renal dysfunction, oligohydramnios, ductus closure), tetracyclines, and terlipressin (uterine ischaemia). [US FDA 2020; EASL 2023]`,`**Wilson disease**: continue therapy - **zinc** or **reduced-dose** penicillamine/trientine (25-50% dose reduction in the third trimester); stopping risks fulminant decompensation. [EASL 2023]`,`**Autoimmune hepatitis**: continue prednisolone and azathioprine - flares are commonest **post-partum**. [EASL 2023]`,`**Entecavir** and DAAs lack safety data; switch entecavir to **tenofovir** in women planning pregnancy. [EASL 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**ICP**: preterm birth, meconium-stained liquor and **stillbirth - risk rises mainly at bile acids 100 micromol/L or more** (about 3.4% versus background); maternal outcome excellent and itch resolves within days of delivery. [Ovadia, Lancet 2019]`,`**ICP recurrence** in **45-90%** of subsequent pregnancies; later higher risk of gallstones, hepatitis C detection and immune-mediated disease. [RCOG GTG 43 2022]`,`**AFLP**: maternal mortality has fallen from about 70% to **under 10-20%** with early delivery; perinatal mortality about 10-20%; **screen the baby (and mother) for LCHAD** - neonatal hypoglycaemia and cardiomyopathy risk. [EASL 2023]`,`**HELLP**: DIC, abruption, AKI, pulmonary oedema, **subcapsular haematoma and rupture**, eclampsia; recurrence about 5-20%. [ACOG PB 222 2020]`,`**Hepatitis E** in pregnancy: maternal mortality 15-25%, preterm labour, fetal loss and **vertical transmission** with neonatal hepatitis. [EASL 2023]`,`**Hyperemesis**: Wernicke encephalopathy, hyponatraemia (central pontine myelinolysis if corrected too fast), oesophageal tear, VTE, low birth weight. [RCOG GTG 69 2024]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer immediately** to a tertiary obstetric-hepatology unit: jaundice with confusion, INR over 1.5, hypoglycaemia, HELLP, suspected AFLP or hepatitis E with coagulopathy. [EASL 2023]`,`**ICP**: weekly LFT and bile acids, fetal monitoring per local protocol; **recheck LFT 4-6 weeks post-partum** to confirm resolution (otherwise look for PBC, PSC, ABCB4 disease). [RCOG GTG 43 2022]`,`**After AFLP**: LCHAD testing of mother and baby; counsel about recurrence (higher if LCHAD mutation). [EASL 2023]`,`**HBV**: refer mother for long-term HBV assessment after delivery; **monitor ALT every 3 months for 6 months post-partum** for flares (especially after stopping tenofovir). [EASL 2023]`,`**Combined oral contraceptives** should be avoided in women with past ICP if they cause itch; progestogen-only methods are preferred. [RCOG GTG 43 2022]`,`**Pre-conception counselling** for chronic liver disease, cirrhosis, Wilson and transplant recipients: optimise disease, switch teratogens, screen varices. [EASL 2023]`]},{heading:`Prevention and national programme link`,points:[`**Universal HBsAg screening** of every pregnant woman at the first antenatal visit is part of the **National Viral Hepatitis Control Programme (NVHCP)** and the **triple elimination** (HIV, syphilis, HBV) agenda. [NVHCP 2018; WHO 2024]`,`**Hepatitis B birth dose** is part of the **Universal Immunisation Programme**; institutional delivery (JSY, JSSK) improves birth-dose coverage. [MoHFW UIP]`,`**Safe drinking water and sanitation** (Jal Jeevan Mission, Swachh Bharat) prevent hepatitis E and A; boil or chlorinate water during monsoon outbreaks. [NVHCP 2018]`,`**HEV vaccine (HEV 239, Hecolin)** is licensed in China and was used in a pregnancy trial in Bangladesh; it is **not available in India's programme**. [WHO HEV position 2015]`,`**Antenatal care under PMSMA** (Pradhan Mantri Surakshit Matritva Abhiyan): BP, urine protein and LFT for symptomatic women detect pre-eclampsia and HELLP early. [MoHFW PMSMA]`,`**Low-dose aspirin 75-150 mg at night from 12 weeks** for women at high risk of pre-eclampsia reduces pre-eclampsia (and HELLP) risk. [ACOG 2021; FOGSI 2019]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines on liver diseases in pregnancy (2023)** - first EASL CPG on the subject: UDCA for ICP, Swansea for AFLP, tenofovir at DNA 200,000 IU/mL or more. [EASL 2023]`,`**RCOG Green-top 43 (2022)** on ICP: bile acid cut-off 19 micromol/L non-fasting, delivery timing by peak bile acids, **UDCA not routinely for perinatal benefit** after PITCHES. [RCOG GTG 43 2022]`,`**RCOG Green-top 69 (2024)** on nausea, vomiting and hyperemesis: updated stepwise antiemetic use and day-case rehydration. [RCOG GTG 69 2024]`,`**WHO hepatitis B guidelines (2024)**: tenofovir prophylaxis for HBsAg-positive pregnant women with HBV DNA 200,000 IU/mL or more or HBeAg positive - and for **all HBsAg-positive pregnant women where DNA/HBeAg testing is unavailable**. [WHO HBV 2024]`,`**SMFM 2021** ICP guidance: diagnose with bile acids 10 micromol/L or more; deliver at **36 weeks if 100 or more**; UDCA first line - the main ICP guideline conflict with RCOG. [SMFM 2021]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Viva: **'Classify liver disease in pregnancy by trimester'** - HG first, ICP second-third, HELLP/AFLP third. [EASL 2023]`,`Viva: **AFLP versus HELLP** - hypoglycaemia and coagulopathy in AFLP; hypertension and haemolysis in HELLP. [EASL 2023]`,`Viva: **Why is hepatitis E dangerous in pregnancy?** - Th2 shift, hormones, placental replication; mortality 15-25% in the third trimester. [EASL 2023]`,`Common mistake: treating **raised ALP** in the third trimester as liver disease - it is placental. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: **giving dextrose before thiamine** in hyperemesis - precipitates Wernicke encephalopathy. [RCOG GTG 69 2024]`,`Common mistake: **forgetting HBIG and birth-dose vaccine within 12-24 hours** for babies of HBsAg-positive mothers. [NVHCP 2018]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Pregnancy-specific liver diseases by trimester`,columns:[`Disease`,`Trimester`,`Key features`,`Key lab`,`Management`],rows:[[`Hyperemesis gravidarum`,`First (peak 9-13 weeks)`,`Vomiting, weight loss > 5%, ketosis`,`ALT usually < 200 IU/L, hypokalaemia`,`Fluids, thiamine, antiemetic ladder`],[`Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy`,`Second-third (> 25-30 weeks)`,`Itch palms and soles, worse at night`,`Bile acids >= 19 micromol/L (RCOG), ALT mildly raised`,`UDCA; deliver by bile acid level`],[`Pre-eclampsia / HELLP`,`Third, or post-partum`,`Hypertension, RUQ pain, proteinuria`,`LDH >= 600, AST >= 70, platelets < 100,000`,`MgSO4, BP control, deliver`],[`AFLP`,`Third`,`Vomiting, polydipsia, encephalopathy`,`Hypoglycaemia, high INR, urate, ammonia`,`Stabilise and deliver promptly`],[`Hepatic rupture`,`Third`,`Sudden pain, shock`,`Falling Hb; CT haematoma`,`Resuscitate, embolise or laparotomy`]]},{heading:`AFLP versus HELLP`,columns:[`Feature`,`AFLP`,`HELLP`],rows:[[`Incidence`,`1 in 7,000-20,000`,`0.5-0.9% of pregnancies`],[`Hypertension and proteinuria`,`In about half`,`Usual (absent in about 15%)`],[`Hypoglycaemia`,`Common, marked`,`Rare`],[`Coagulopathy (INR, fibrinogen)`,`Prolonged INR, low fibrinogen early`,`Usually normal unless DIC`],[`Platelets`,`Mildly low`,`Markedly low (< 100,000)`],[`Haemolysis`,`Mild`,`Prominent (schistocytes, LDH)`],[`Encephalopathy, ammonia`,`Common`,`Rare`],[`Mechanism`,`Fetal LCHAD deficiency, microvesicular fat`,`Placental anti-angiogenic factors, periportal necrosis`],[`Treatment`,`Prompt delivery, glucose, FFP`,`Delivery if >= 34 weeks, MgSO4, BP control`]]},{heading:`Swansea criteria for AFLP (6 or more of 14)`,columns:[`Domain`,`Criterion`],rows:[[`Symptoms`,`Vomiting`],[`Symptoms`,`Abdominal pain`],[`Symptoms`,`Polydipsia / polyuria`],[`Symptoms`,`Encephalopathy`],[`Biochemistry`,`Raised bilirubin (> 14 micromol/L)`],[`Biochemistry`,`Hypoglycaemia (< 4 mmol/L)`],[`Biochemistry`,`Raised urate (> 340 micromol/L)`],[`Haematology`,`Leucocytosis (> 11 x 10^9/L)`],[`Imaging`,`Ascites or bright liver on ultrasound`],[`Biochemistry`,`Raised transaminases (> 42 IU/L)`],[`Biochemistry`,`Raised ammonia (> 47 micromol/L)`],[`Renal`,`Renal impairment (creatinine > 150 micromol/L)`],[`Coagulation`,`Coagulopathy (PT > 14 s or APTT > 34 s)`],[`Histology`,`Microvesicular steatosis on liver biopsy`]]},{heading:`Drugs for liver disease in pregnancy`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Ursodeoxycholic acid`,`10-15 mg/kg/day orally in 2-3 doses (up to 21 mg/kg/day)`,`Until delivery`,`Diarrhoea; RCOG 2022: no proven perinatal benefit`],[`Doxylamine-pyridoxine`,`10/10 mg, 2 tablets at bedtime, up to 4 per day orally`,`Through first trimester as needed`,`Drowsiness`],[`Ondansetron`,`4-8 mg orally or IV 8-hourly`,`Second line for HG`,`Constipation, QT prolongation; small cleft risk in first trimester`],[`Thiamine`,`100 mg orally daily, or 100 mg IV before dextrose`,`While vomiting persists`,`Very rare anaphylaxis IV`],[`Tenofovir disoproxil fumarate`,`300 mg orally once daily`,`From 24-28 weeks to delivery, usually 4-12 weeks post-partum`,`Renal tubular toxicity, bone loss; monitor ALT flare after stopping`],[`Hepatitis B immunoglobulin`,`0.5 mL (100 IU) IM to newborn`,`Single dose within 12 hours of birth with vaccine`,`Local pain`],[`Magnesium sulphate`,`Pritchard: 4 g IV + 10 g IM loading, then 5 g IM 4-hourly; or Zuspan 4 g IV then 1 g/h`,`24 hours after delivery or last fit`,`Respiratory depression - monitor reflexes, urine output; calcium gluconate antidote`],[`Labetalol`,`20 mg IV, then 40, 80 mg every 10 minutes (max 300 mg); 100-400 mg orally 8-12 hourly`,`To BP < 160/110 then < 140/90`,`Asthma contraindication, neonatal bradycardia`],[`Acyclovir`,`10 mg/kg IV 8-hourly`,`14-21 days for HSV hepatitis`,`Nephrotoxicity - hydrate`]]},{heading:`Viral hepatitis in pregnancy`,columns:[`Virus`,`Maternal course`,`Vertical transmission`,`Key action`],rows:[[`Hepatitis A`,`Usually mild; rare preterm labour`,`Rare`,`Supportive; vaccine safe`],[`Hepatitis B`,`Acute usually mild; chronic flares post-partum`,`70-90% if HBeAg positive without prophylaxis`,`TDF if DNA >= 200,000 IU/mL; birth-dose vaccine + HBIG`],[`Hepatitis C`,`Usually stable`,`About 5%`,`Treat after delivery; no DAAs in pregnancy`],[`Hepatitis E`,`ALF, mortality 15-25% in third trimester`,`Common, neonatal hepatitis`,`Supportive ICU care; no ribavirin; safe water`],[`Herpes simplex`,`Fulminant anicteric hepatitis`,`Possible`,`IV acyclovir empirically`]]}],redFlags:[`Jaundice with confusion, INR > 1.5 or hypoglycaemia in the third trimester - AFLP or ALF`,`RUQ or epigastric pain with hypertension and platelets < 100,000 - HELLP`,`Sudden severe RUQ pain with shock in pre-eclampsia - hepatic rupture`,`Jaundice in a pregnant woman during a hepatitis E outbreak - risk of fulminant failure`,`Bile acids >= 100 micromol/L - high stillbirth risk, plan early delivery`,`Persistent vomiting with ketosis, hypokalaemia or confusion - admit, thiamine before dextrose`,`Fever with very high transaminases and no jaundice - HSV hepatitis, start acyclovir`],pearls:[`**ALP up, albumin down** is normal in pregnancy; ALT, AST, bilirubin and bile acids are not.`,`Trimester tells the diagnosis: **HG first, ICP second-third, HELLP and AFLP third**.`,`ICP: **itch of palms and soles at night** with bile acids 19 or more (RCOG) - deliver by bile acid level.`,`AFLP: **hypoglycaemia and coagulopathy**; HELLP: **haemolysis and low platelets**.`,`**Swansea criteria**: 6 or more of 14 for AFLP.`,`Test the baby of an AFLP mother for **LCHAD deficiency**.`,`**Hepatitis E** kills 15-25% of pregnant women in the third trimester in India - give safe water.`,`HBV: **tenofovir from 24-28 weeks if DNA 200,000 IU/mL or more**, plus vaccine and HBIG within 12 hours of birth.`,`Thiamine **before** dextrose in hyperemesis.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-liver-in-pregnancy-q1`,stem:`A primigravida at 35 weeks with twins has 1 week of nausea, vomiting and polydipsia, then jaundice. Glucose 52 mg/dL, INR 2.1, fibrinogen 110 mg/dL, uric acid 9 mg/dL, platelets 1.4 lakh/mm3, BP 136/88 mmHg. The most likely diagnosis is:`,options:[`HELLP syndrome`,`Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy`,`Hyperemesis gravidarum`,`Acute fatty liver of pregnancy`],answer:3,explanation:`Third-trimester vomiting and polydipsia with hypoglycaemia, coagulopathy, low fibrinogen and high urate meet the Swansea criteria for acute fatty liver of pregnancy; twins and primigravidity are risk factors. HELLP would show marked thrombocytopenia and haemolysis with hypertension and rarely hypoglycaemia. ICP causes itch without liver failure, and hyperemesis is a first-trimester condition.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-liver-in-pregnancy-q2`,stem:`A woman at 33 weeks has generalised itching worse on the palms and soles at night, no rash, ALT 98 IU/L and random total bile acids of 112 micromol/L. Per RCOG 2022, planned birth should be considered at:`,options:[`35-36 weeks`,`38-39 weeks`,`40 weeks`,`Immediately, regardless of gestation`],answer:0,explanation:`RCOG 2022 links stillbirth risk in ICP mainly to peak bile acids of 100 micromol/L or more and advises considering birth at 35-36 weeks. Birth at 38-39 weeks is for peak bile acids of 40-99, and by 40 weeks for 19-39. Immediate delivery at 33 weeks is not indicated without other obstetric reasons.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-liver-in-pregnancy-q3`,stem:`An HBsAg-positive woman at 26 weeks is HBeAg positive with HBV DNA 8 x 10^7 IU/mL and normal ALT. The best plan to prevent mother-to-child transmission is:`,options:[`Elective caesarean section and no breastfeeding`,`Tenofovir disoproxil 300 mg daily from now, plus birth-dose vaccine and HBIG to the baby within 12 hours`,`Entecavir 0.5 mg daily from now`,`Newborn vaccine at 6 weeks only`],answer:1,explanation:`With HBV DNA above 200,000 IU/mL, tenofovir from 24-28 weeks plus birth-dose vaccine and HBIG reduces transmission to near zero. Caesarean section and avoidance of breastfeeding do not reduce HBV transmission. Entecavir lacks pregnancy safety data, and delaying the vaccine to 6 weeks misses the birth dose that prevents perinatal infection.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-liver-in-pregnancy-q4`,stem:`Which liver test change is physiological in normal third-trimester pregnancy?`,options:[`ALT rise to twice the upper limit`,`Raised serum bilirubin`,`ALP rise to three times the non-pregnant upper limit`,`Raised fasting bile acids`],answer:2,explanation:`ALP rises 2-4 times in late pregnancy because of the placental isoenzyme, and albumin falls from haemodilution. ALT, AST, bilirubin and bile acids remain normal (or slightly lower) in normal pregnancy, so any rise in these indicates disease such as ICP, pre-eclampsia, AFLP or hepatitis.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-liver-in-pregnancy-q5`,stem:`A 24-year-old at 30 weeks in Uttar Pradesh during a monsoon jaundice outbreak develops jaundice, confusion and INR 3.0. IgM anti-HEV is positive. Which statement is correct?`,options:[`Ribavirin should be started immediately`,`Urgent delivery improves maternal survival`,`Hepatitis E rarely affects pregnant women more severely than others`,`Intensive supportive care at a liver unit is the mainstay; ribavirin is contraindicated`],answer:3,explanation:`Hepatitis E acute liver failure in pregnancy is managed with intensive supportive care at a liver centre; ribavirin is teratogenic and contraindicated. Termination or early delivery has not been shown to improve maternal outcome, unlike AFLP or HELLP. Hepatitis E is far more severe in pregnancy, with maternal mortality of 15-25% in the third trimester.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-liver-in-pregnancy-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Classify liver diseases in pregnancy. Discuss the diagnosis and management of acute fatty liver of pregnancy and HELLP syndrome.`,openingLines:[`Abnormal liver tests occur in 3-5% of pregnancies and are classified as pregnancy-specific (by trimester) or coincidental/pre-existing liver disease.`,`AFLP and HELLP are third-trimester emergencies whose definitive treatment is stabilisation and delivery.`],answer:[{heading:`Classification`,points:[`Pregnancy-specific: HG (first), ICP (second-third), pre-eclampsia/HELLP, AFLP, hepatic rupture (third).`,`Coincidental: viral hepatitis (A, B, E, HSV), DILI, gallstones, Budd-Chiari.`,`Pre-existing: chronic HBV/HCV, cirrhosis, AIH, Wilson, transplant.`]},{heading:`Normal pregnancy changes`,points:[`ALP up 2-4 times, albumin down; ALT, AST, bilirubin, bile acids normal.`]},{heading:`AFLP - pathogenesis and diagnosis`,points:[`Fetal LCHAD deficiency; microvesicular steatosis.`,`Swansea criteria: 6 or more of 14 - vomiting, pain, polydipsia, encephalopathy, bilirubin, hypoglycaemia, urate, leucocytosis, bright liver/ascites, transaminases, ammonia, renal impairment, coagulopathy, biopsy.`]},{heading:`AFLP - management`,points:[`ICU, glucose infusion, FFP/cryoprecipitate, prompt delivery, plasma exchange or transplant if refractory; LCHAD testing of baby.`]},{heading:`HELLP - diagnosis`,points:[`Tennessee: LDH >= 600, AST >= 70, platelets < 100,000; Mississippi classes by platelets.`]},{heading:`HELLP - management`,points:[`MgSO4, labetalol/nifedipine, deliver if >= 34 weeks or deteriorating; steroids for lung maturity < 34 weeks; platelets >= 50,000 before caesarean; watch for haematoma/rupture.`]},{heading:`AFLP vs HELLP`,points:[`Hypoglycaemia, coagulopathy, encephalopathy - AFLP; haemolysis, low platelets, hypertension - HELLP.`]}],mustDraw:[`Trimester-wise classification chart`,`AFLP vs HELLP comparison table`],markSplit:[{part:`Classification by trimester`,marks:2},{part:`AFLP diagnosis and management`,marks:4},{part:`HELLP diagnosis and management`,marks:4}],keywords:[`AFLP`,`Swansea criteria`,`HELLP`,`LCHAD`,`magnesium sulphate`,`delivery`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-liver-in-pregnancy-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on prevention of mother-to-child transmission of hepatitis B.`,openingLines:[`Without prophylaxis, 70-90% of babies of HBeAg-positive mothers become infected, and 90% of infected infants develop chronic hepatitis B.`,`Prevention combines universal antenatal HBsAg screening, maternal tenofovir for high viral load, and newborn birth-dose vaccine plus HBIG.`],answer:[{heading:`Screening`,points:[`Universal HBsAg at first antenatal visit (NVHCP); if positive - HBeAg, HBV DNA, ALT at 24-28 weeks.`]},{heading:`Maternal antiviral`,points:[`Tenofovir disoproxil 300 mg daily from 24-28 weeks if HBV DNA >= 200,000 IU/mL or HBeAg positive; WHO 2024 - all HBsAg-positive where testing unavailable; continue to delivery and up to 12 weeks post-partum.`]},{heading:`Newborn`,points:[`Hepatitis B vaccine birth dose within 24 h (ideally 12 h) + HBIG 0.5 mL IM within 12 h; complete schedule; test at 9-12 months.`]},{heading:`Delivery and feeding`,points:[`Mode of delivery by obstetric indication; breastfeeding allowed.`]},{heading:`Post-partum`,points:[`ALT every 3 months for 6 months for flares; long-term hepatology follow-up.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: HBsAg screen to HBV DNA to tenofovir to newborn prophylaxis`],markSplit:[{part:`Screening and risk assessment`,marks:1},{part:`Maternal tenofovir`,marks:2},{part:`Newborn prophylaxis and follow-up`,marks:2}],keywords:[`PMTCT`,`HBsAg`,`tenofovir`,`HBIG`,`birth dose`,`NVHCP`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Liver disease in pregnancy`,`EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines on the management of liver diseases in pregnancy, J Hepatol 2023`,`RCOG Green-top Guideline No. 43: Intrahepatic Cholestasis of Pregnancy, 2022`,`RCOG Green-top Guideline No. 69: Management of Nausea and Vomiting of Pregnancy and Hyperemesis Gravidarum, 2024`,`ACOG Practice Bulletin No. 222: Gestational Hypertension and Preeclampsia, 2020`,`WHO Guidelines for the prevention, diagnosis, care and treatment for people with chronic hepatitis B infection, 2024`,`Ch'ng CL et al. Prospective study of liver dysfunction in pregnancy in Southwest Wales (Swansea criteria), Gut 2002`,`MoHFW National Viral Hepatitis Control Programme: Operational and Technical Guidelines, 2018; SMFM Consult Series No. 53: ICP, 2021`]});var c=[];c.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-upper-gi-endoscopy-indications`,title:`Endoscopy for the family physician: indications, preparation and findings`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`**GI endoscopy** (oesophagogastroduodenoscopy, colonoscopy and their advanced relatives ERCP, EUS and capsule) is the **diagnostic and therapeutic look-and-biopsy test of the gut**; the family physician's job is to **refer the right patient at the right urgency** (alarm features, positive FIT, iron deficiency), **prepare them safely** (fasting, split-dose bowel prep, antithrombotic plan per **BSG/ESGE 2021**, diabetes drugs) and **read the report** (LA grade, Forrest, Prague, Boston score, polyp histology) to plan follow-up.`,keywords:[`OGD`,`EGD`,`UGIE`,`upper GI endoscopy`,`colonoscopy`,`sigmoidoscopy`,`ERCP`,`EUS`,`capsule endoscopy`,`bowel preparation`,`split-dose PEG`,`Boston Bowel Preparation Scale`,`BSG/ESGE antithrombotic 2021`,`DOAC interruption`,`conscious sedation midazolam`,`Forrest classification`,`Los Angeles classification`,`Prague C and M`,`adenoma detection rate`,`polyp surveillance`],sections:[{heading:`Definition, scope and epidemiology`,points:[`**Oesophagogastroduodenoscopy (OGD/EGD)** examines the oesophagus, stomach and duodenum to the second part (D2) with a flexible video endoscope; **colonoscopy** examines the rectum to caecum and terminal ileum, and **flexible sigmoidoscopy** the rectum to the splenic flexure. [Harrison 22e]`,`Endoscopy is both **diagnostic** (inspection, biopsy, rapid urease test) and **therapeutic** (haemostasis, variceal banding, polypectomy, dilatation, stenting, foreign-body removal, PEG). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Advanced procedures** are tertiary: **ERCP** (therapeutic biliary/pancreatic access), **EUS** (ultrasound from inside the gut with FNA/FNB), **capsule endoscopy** and **device-assisted enteroscopy** (small bowel). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Globally**, stomach cancer (about 0.97 million new cases) and colorectal cancer (about 1.9 million) are among the commonest cancers, and most are diagnosed endoscopically. [GLOBOCAN 2022]`,`**In India**, oesophageal and gastric cancers cluster in the **North-East (Mizoram, Meghalaya, Assam)** and the South, and most present late with dysphagia or weight loss - early referral of alarm features is the main lever a family physician has. [ICMR-NCRP 2020]`,`Indian H. pylori prevalence of **60-80% in adults** and a large burden of **cirrhosis (varices), peptic ulcer bleeding and iron deficiency anaemia (NFHS-5: 57% of women 15-49 anaemic)** make OGD one of the commonest specialist tests requested from primary care. [NFHS-5 2021]`,`Endoscopy is now offered in **district hospitals** in many states and is covered under **PM-JAY** packages for therapeutic procedures; diagnostic OGD costs little but waiting lists make **correct triage** important. [NHA PM-JAY HBP 2022]`,`The family physician owns **four tasks**: decide the indication and urgency, prepare the patient (consent, fasting, bowel prep, drugs), manage after-care and complications, and **act on the report and histology**. [ESGE 2019]`]},{heading:`When to refer for upper GI endoscopy (OGD)`,points:[`**Dysphagia at any age** is an absolute indication for OGD - true dysphagia is structural (stricture, cancer, ring, eosinophilic oesophagitis) until proven otherwise; see the Dysphagia topic. [ACG 2022]`,`**Dyspepsia with alarm features** - unintended weight loss, progressive dysphagia, persistent vomiting, GI bleeding, iron deficiency anaemia, palpable mass, or family history of upper GI cancer - needs OGD whatever the age. [ACG-CAG 2017]`,`**Age threshold for new dyspepsia**: ACG-CAG uses **60 years** for Western populations, but because gastric cancer occurs younger in Asia, Indian practice is to scope new-onset dyspepsia from **about 40-45 years**. [ISG 2021]`,`**Upper GI bleeding** (haematemesis, melaena) needs OGD **within 24 hours** after resuscitation, and within 12 hours for suspected variceal bleeding; see the GI bleeding topic. [ESGE 2021]`,`**Unexplained iron deficiency anaemia** in men and post-menopausal women needs **bidirectional endoscopy** (OGD with duodenal biopsies plus colonoscopy); premenopausal women need it if there are GI symptoms, a strong family history or age over 50. [BSG 2021]`,`**Cirrhosis variceal screening**: OGD can be avoided if **liver stiffness below 20 kPa and platelets above 150 x 10^9/L** (Baveno VI/VII 'favourable' criteria); otherwise screen, unless already on a non-selective beta-blocker. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Suspected coeliac disease** with positive tTG-IgA needs OGD with **4 biopsies from D2 and 1-2 from the bulb** while still on gluten; in children, tTG over 10x upper limit with positive EMA allows a no-biopsy diagnosis. [ESsCD 2019]`,`**Persistent reflux despite 8 weeks of PPI**, suspected **Barrett oesophagus** (long-standing GERD plus risk factors), caustic ingestion within 12-48 hours, food bolus or sharp foreign body, and **gastric ulcer re-check at 8-12 weeks** are other common indications. [ACG GERD 2022]`,`**Not indications**: uncomplicated dyspepsia under the age threshold (test-and-treat H. pylori first), functional symptoms already investigated, and routine surveillance of healed duodenal ulcer. [ACG-CAG 2017]`]},{heading:`When to refer for colonoscopy and lower GI endoscopy`,points:[`**Change in bowel habit with rectal bleeding, iron deficiency anaemia, weight loss or an abdominal/rectal mass** - the classic lower GI alarm set - needs urgent colonoscopy. [NICE NG12 2023]`,`**Faecal immunochemical test (FIT)**: in symptomatic primary-care patients a **FIT of 10 micrograms Hb/g faeces or more** triggers an urgent suspected-cancer referral; a negative FIT with normal Hb has a very high negative predictive value. [BSG/ACPGBI 2022]`,`**Average-risk screening**: from **age 45 to 75** by colonoscopy every 10 years or annual FIT; India has **no national CRC screening programme**, so screening is opportunistic and FIT-based where offered. [USPSTF 2021]`,`**Family history**: a first-degree relative with CRC under 60 means colonoscopy from age 40 (or 10 years before the relative's age at diagnosis), repeated every 5 years. [USMSTF 2017]`,`**Chronic diarrhoea** (over 4 weeks) with negative stool tests, raised faecal calprotectin, or suspected IBD needs ileocolonoscopy with **segmental biopsies even from normal-looking mucosa** (microscopic colitis). [BSG 2018]`,`**Distinguishing intestinal TB from Crohn disease** needs ileocolonoscopy with biopsies for histology, **CBNAAT/Xpert, AFB culture and caseating granuloma** - see the Abdominal TB topic. [ISG Crohn consensus 2015]`,`**Flexible sigmoidoscopy** is enough for bright-red outlet bleeding in a young patient with a visible anorectal cause, after a proctoscopy, and for acute colitis where full colonoscopy risks perforation. [BSG 2019]`,`**CT colonography** is the alternative when colonoscopy is incomplete, unsafe or refused; it cannot biopsy, so any polyp of 6 mm or more still goes to colonoscopy. [ESGAR/ESGE 2020]`]},{heading:`How the procedures work and what each can do`,points:[`A video endoscope carries a **CCD chip, light guide, air/CO2 insufflation, water jet and a 2.8-3.8 mm working channel** through which biopsy forceps, snares, clips, injection needles and bands pass. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CO2 insufflation** is absorbed about 150 times faster than air, so it causes less post-colonoscopy bloating and pain; it is preferred for colonoscopy and long therapeutic procedures. [ESGE 2019]`,`**Image-enhanced endoscopy** - narrow band imaging (NBI), blue-light and linked-colour imaging, chromoendoscopy with indigo carmine or acetic acid - highlights vascular and pit patterns of dysplasia, Barrett and early gastric cancer. [ESGE 2023]`,`**Rapid urease test (CLO test)** on antral and body biopsies detects H. pylori in about an hour; PPIs must be stopped **2 weeks** and antibiotics/bismuth **4 weeks** before, or it gives a false negative. [Maastricht VI 2022]`,`**ERCP** is now almost entirely therapeutic (stone extraction, biliary stenting, sphincterotomy) because **MRCP and EUS** diagnose biliary disease without the **3-10% risk of post-ERCP pancreatitis**. [ESGE 2019]`,`**EUS** stages oesophageal, gastric, rectal and pancreatic cancer, characterises submucosal lesions and pancreatic cysts, finds small CBD stones, and samples lesions by **FNA/FNB**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Capsule endoscopy** is the first test for **suspected small-bowel bleeding after a normal OGD and colonoscopy**; it is contraindicated with known or suspected stricture (retention) unless a patency capsule passes. [ESGE 2022]`,`**Device-assisted enteroscopy** (single/double balloon) reaches the small bowel for biopsy and therapy after capsule localises a lesion. [ESGE 2022]`]},{heading:`Contraindications, risk assessment and consent`,points:[`**Absolute contraindications**: suspected **perforation** (perforated viscus is a CT and surgical diagnosis), haemodynamic instability not yet resuscitated, and a patient who refuses or cannot cooperate without safe sedation. [ASGE 2015]`,`**Relative contraindications**: recent MI (under 30 days) unless life-threatening bleeding, severe hypoxaemia, acute fulminant colitis or toxic megacolon (colonoscopy), and recent bowel surgery anastomosis. [ASGE 2015]`,`Assess fitness with the **ASA physical status class**: ASA I-II are suitable for day-care sedation; ASA III or more, OSA, BMI over 35, difficult airway or significant cardiorespiratory disease need anaesthetist-supported sedation. [ASGE 2018]`,`**Informed consent** must name the purpose, alternatives, sedation, and **risks with numbers**: diagnostic OGD perforation about **1 in 10,000**, colonoscopy perforation about **1 in 1,000-3,000** and post-polypectomy bleeding about **1 in 100-300**. [BSG 2016]`,`Explain that small lesions can be missed (**miss rate for adenomas about 20%**, for cancer about 5% within 3 years - post-colonoscopy CRC) and that biopsy results take 5-10 days. [WEO 2018]`,`Check **allergies** (latex, lignocaine, egg/soy for propofol is no longer a contraindication), **implanted devices** (pacemaker/ICD with diathermy) and **pregnancy status** in women of reproductive age. [ASGE 2018]`,`In India, consent should be in the **patient's language**, with a witness, and documented for the specific therapeutic step if one is anticipated (banding, dilatation, polypectomy). [ASGE 2015]`]},{heading:`Preparing the patient: fasting and routine drugs`,points:[`**Fasting**: no solid food for **6 hours** and clear fluids allowed until **2 hours** before sedated endoscopy; unsedated OGD under throat spray needs the same fasting to protect the airway. [ASA 2023]`,`**Gastric outlet obstruction or achalasia** needs a liquid diet for 24-48 hours and longer fasting, as food residue causes aspiration; consider a nasogastric tube first. [ASGE 2015]`,`Continue **antihypertensives, antiepileptics, thyroxine, inhalers and cardiac drugs** with a sip of water on the morning of the procedure. [ASGE 2015]`,`**Diabetes - oral drugs**: omit sulfonylureas and other oral agents on the morning of the procedure (and on the bowel-prep clear-fluid day); restart with the first meal. [JBDS 2023]`,`**Diabetes - insulin**: reduce **basal insulin by about 20-25%** the evening before (and on clear-fluid days), omit prandial insulin while fasting, check capillary glucose on arrival and book the patient **first on the list**. [JBDS 2023]`,`**SGLT2 inhibitors** (dapagliflozin, empagliflozin, canagliflozin) should be stopped **3 days before** (ertugliflozin 4 days) any procedure needing fasting or bowel prep, because of **euglycaemic diabetic ketoacidosis**. [FDA 2020]`,`**GLP-1 receptor agonists** (semaglutide, liraglutide, dulaglutide, tirzepatide): the 2024 multisociety guidance says most patients can **continue them**, but should take a **liquid-only diet for 24 hours before**; hold one dose (weekly agent) only if in dose escalation or with GI symptoms - retained gastric content is the risk. [AGA/ASA multisociety 2024]`,`**Oral iron** should be stopped **about 5-7 days before colonoscopy**, as black sticky iron residue impairs the view. [ESGE 2019]`]},{heading:`Bowel preparation for colonoscopy`,points:[`Bowel preparation quality is the single biggest determinant of adenoma detection; the target is **adequate preparation in at least 90% of colonoscopies**. [ESGE 2019]`,`**Low-fibre (low-residue) diet the day before** (white rice, idli, curd, white bread, eggs, clear soups; avoid fruit with seeds, leafy vegetables, whole grains), with clear fluids only after lunch. [ESGE 2019]`,`**Split-dose regimen** is the standard: half the preparation the **evening before** and half on the **morning of the procedure**; for an afternoon list a **same-day regimen** is acceptable. [ESGE 2019]`,`The second dose should **start within 5 hours of the colonoscopy and finish at least 2 hours before** it, to avoid both aspiration and fresh proximal colon effluent. [ESGE 2019]`,`**High-volume (4 L) PEG-electrolyte** or validated **low-volume (2 L PEG-ascorbate, sodium picosulfate-magnesium citrate)** preparations are equally effective in fit patients; low-volume improves tolerance. [ESGE 2019]`,`**PEG-electrolyte is iso-osmotic** and passes through without net fluid shift, so it is the **safest choice in CKD, heart failure, cirrhosis with ascites, elderly and pregnancy**. [ESGE 2019]`,`**Oral sodium phosphate is not recommended** routinely because it causes **acute phosphate nephropathy**, hyperphosphataemia and hypocalcaemia, especially in CKD, elderly, and patients on ACE inhibitors, ARBs or diuretics. [ESGE 2019]`,`**Simethicone** added to the preparation reduces bubbles and may improve detection; patients with prior inadequate prep, constipation, diabetes or opioid use benefit from an **extra day of low-residue diet or added laxative**. [ESGE 2019]`,`**Boston Bowel Preparation Scale (BBPS)** scores right, transverse and left colon 0-3 each (total 0-9); **adequate = total 6 or more with every segment 2 or more**; an inadequate prep should be **repeated within 1 year**. [ESGE 2019]`]},{heading:`Antithrombotic management (BSG/ESGE 2021)`,points:[`Two questions decide the plan: **procedure bleeding risk** (low or high) and **patient thrombotic risk** (low or high) - draw a 2 x 2 grid. [BSG/ESGE 2021]`,`**Low-risk procedures**: diagnostic OGD/colonoscopy **with or without biopsy**, biliary or pancreatic stenting, device-assisted enteroscopy without polypectomy, and EUS without FNA; **high-risk**: polypectomy, ERCP with sphincterotomy, EMR/ESD, dilatation, variceal therapy, PEG, EUS-FNA and stenting. [BSG/ESGE 2021]`,`**Aspirin**: continue for **all** procedures except ESD, large colonic EMR (over 2 cm), upper GI EMR and ampullectomy, where stopping is individualised for low thrombotic risk patients. [BSG/ESGE 2021]`,`**P2Y12 inhibitors (clopidogrel, prasugrel, ticagrelor)**: continue for low-risk procedures; for high-risk procedures in low thrombotic risk patients, **stop 7 days before** (continue aspirin); in high thrombotic risk (recent coronary stent), discuss with the cardiologist and continue aspirin. [BSG/ESGE 2021]`,`**Warfarin, low-risk procedure**: continue, and check that INR is not above the therapeutic range in the week before; **high-risk procedure**: stop 5 days before and check **INR below 1.5**; high thrombotic risk (mechanical mitral valve, AF with mitral stenosis, VTE within 3 months) needs **LMWH bridging** starting 2 days after stopping warfarin, last dose at least 24 hours before. [BSG/ESGE 2021]`,`**DOACs, low-risk procedure**: **omit only the morning dose** on the day of the procedure - a 2021 change from the earlier 'continue' advice. [BSG/ESGE 2021]`,`**DOACs, high-risk procedure**: take the **last dose 3 days before** (2 full days off) for apixaban, rivaroxaban, edoxaban and dabigatran with CrCl 50 mL/min or more; dabigatran with **CrCl 30-49: last dose 5 days before**; apixaban/rivaroxaban/edoxaban with CrCl 15-29: last dose 3 days before; CrCl under 15: discuss with haematology. **No bridging for DOACs**. [BSG/ESGE 2021]`,`**Restarting**: warfarin the evening of the procedure at the usual dose; DOACs and P2Y12 inhibitors usually **2-3 days after** a high-risk procedure, depending on haemostasis and thrombotic risk. [BSG/ESGE 2021]`,`**Emergency endoscopy on anticoagulants**: do not delay endoscopy for INR correction once resuscitated; reverse warfarin with **vitamin K 5-10 mg IV plus prothrombin complex concentrate** for life-threatening bleeding, dabigatran with **idarucizumab 5 g IV**, and factor Xa inhibitors with andexanet alfa where available or PCC. [BSG/ESGE 2021]`]},{heading:`Sedation, monitoring and recovery`,points:[`Options range from **topical pharyngeal lignocaine 10% spray** (unsedated OGD - quicker recovery and can drive home) to **conscious sedation** (midazolam with or without opioid) to **propofol deep sedation**. [ESGE 2015]`,`**Midazolam**: start **1-2 mg IV** (0.5-1 mg if over 70, frail or with COPD) and titrate in 0.5-1 mg steps every 2 minutes; most OGDs need under 3-5 mg - the goal is a patient who responds to voice. [ASGE 2018]`,`**Opioid add-on** for colonoscopy: **fentanyl 25-50 micrograms IV** or pethidine 25-50 mg IV; the benzodiazepine-opioid combination is synergistic, so reduce both doses. [ASGE 2018]`,`**Propofol** (0.5-1 mg/kg then 10-20 mg boluses) gives faster recovery and better tolerance but has **no reversal agent**; it needs a dedicated trained person and airway skills. [ESGE 2015]`,`**Reversal**: **flumazenil 0.2 mg IV** over 15 seconds, then 0.1 mg every 60 seconds to a maximum of 1 mg; **naloxone 0.1-0.4 mg IV** repeated every 2-3 minutes; both act shorter than the drug reversed, so observe for re-sedation. [BNF 2024]`,`**Monitoring**: continuous **pulse oximetry, ECG for cardiac patients, BP every 5 minutes**, supplemental oxygen and an assistant whose only job is the patient; **capnography** is recommended for deep sedation. [ESGE 2015]`,`**Discharge**: after sedation the patient must go home **with an escort**, not drive, operate machinery, sign legal documents or drink alcohol for **24 hours**. [BSG 2016]`]},{heading:`Reading an upper GI endoscopy report`,points:[`**Los Angeles classification of oesophagitis**: A - mucosal breaks 5 mm or less; B - over 5 mm but not continuous between folds; C - continuous across 2 or more folds but under 75% of circumference; D - 75% or more; **LA B-D is conclusive GERD** under Lyon 2.0. [Lyon 2.0 2024]`,`**Prague C and M criteria** describe Barrett oesophagus: **C** = circumferential extent and **M** = maximum extent above the gastro-oesophageal junction in cm (e.g. C2M5); **1 cm or more** is needed to call Barrett, with intestinal metaplasia on biopsy. [ESGE 2023]`,`**Forrest classification** of ulcer stigmata predicts rebleeding without therapy: **Ia spurting (about 55%), Ib oozing (about 55%), IIa non-bleeding visible vessel (about 43%), IIb adherent clot (about 22%), IIc flat pigmented spot (about 10%), III clean base (about 5%)**; Ia-IIa get endoscopic therapy. [ESGE 2021]`,`**Varices** are reported as **small (under 5 mm) or large (5 mm or more)** with **red signs (red wale marks, cherry-red spots)**; large or red-sign varices need NSBB or banding - see the Portal hypertension topic. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Gastric ulcer** reports must state that **biopsies were taken** (malignant ulcers can look benign) and should be re-scoped at 8-12 weeks to confirm healing; duodenal ulcers are not biopsied routinely. [ACG 2017]`,`**Updated Sydney protocol**: 5 biopsies (2 antrum, 1 incisura, 2 body) for H. pylori and atrophy; **OLGA/OLGIM stage III-IV** (extensive atrophy or intestinal metaplasia) means **endoscopic surveillance every 3 years**. [MAPS III 2025]`,`**Duodenal histology for coeliac** is reported with **Marsh-Oberhuber** grades: 1 raised intraepithelial lymphocytes, 2 crypt hyperplasia, **3a-3c villous atrophy** (partial, subtotal, total); Marsh 3 with positive serology confirms coeliac disease. [ESsCD 2019]`,`Phrases a family physician should act on: '**suspicious of malignancy**' (chase histology within a week), '**intestinal metaplasia**' (eradicate H. pylori, plan surveillance), '**candida plaques**' (check HbA1c, HIV, inhaled steroid use), '**food residue in stomach**' (gastroparesis or outlet obstruction). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Reading a colonoscopy report and polyp histology`,points:[`The report should state **caecal intubation** (photo of appendiceal orifice and ileocaecal valve), **withdrawal time** and **BBPS**; an incomplete or poorly prepared examination is not a negative examination. [ESGE 2017]`,`Quality standards: **caecal intubation 90% or more** (95% in screening), **withdrawal time 6 minutes or more**, and **adenoma detection rate (ADR) 25% or more** in ESGE; the 2024 US quality indicators raised the ADR target to **35% overall (40% men, 30% women)**. [ACG/ASGE 2024]`,`**Paris classification** describes polyp shape: **0-Ip pedunculated, 0-Is sessile, 0-IIa slightly elevated, 0-IIb flat, 0-IIc depressed, 0-III excavated**; depressed (0-IIc) lesions carry the highest risk of submucosal invasion. [Paris 2003]`,`**Histology that matters**: **tubular, tubulovillous and villous adenomas** (villous carries higher cancer risk), **sessile serrated lesions** (right colon, flat, pathway to CIMP-high cancer), **hyperplastic polyps** (small rectosigmoid ones are harmless) and **high-grade dysplasia**. [WHO 2019]`,`A **malignant polyp** (cancer invading submucosa - pT1) needs MDT review; **Haggitt and Kikuchi levels**, depth over 1 mm, lymphovascular invasion, poor differentiation, tumour budding or margin under 1 mm favour **surgical resection**. [ESGE 2022]`,`**Mayo endoscopic score** for ulcerative colitis: 0 normal, 1 erythema and decreased vascular pattern, 2 marked erythema and erosions, 3 spontaneous bleeding and ulceration; **mucosal healing (Mayo 0-1)** is the treatment target. [STRIDE-II 2021]`,`Report clues to aetiology: **transverse ulcers, patulous ileocaecal valve and caecal deformity** suggest intestinal TB; **longitudinal ulcers, cobblestoning and skip lesions** suggest Crohn disease; **pseudomembranes** suggest C. difficile. [ISG Crohn consensus 2015]`,`**Diverticula** reported incidentally need only fibre and no follow-up; **melanosis coli** is benign pigmentation from anthraquinone laxatives (senna). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Complications and post-procedure care`,points:[`**Cardiopulmonary events** (hypoxia, hypotension, arrhythmia, aspiration) cause about half of serious endoscopy complications and are mostly sedation-related. [ASGE 2015]`,`**Perforation** presents with **chest or abdominal pain, fever, subcutaneous emphysema (crepitus in the neck) or distension** within hours; it needs erect chest X-ray or CT, nil by mouth, IV antibiotics and surgical/endoscopic closure. [ASGE 2015]`,`**Post-polypectomy bleeding** can be **immediate or delayed up to 14 days**; passage of fresh blood after a polypectomy is a reason to return - most stop with clipping. [ESGE 2017]`,`**Post-polypectomy coagulation syndrome** (transmural burn): localised pain, fever and tenderness **without free air**, usually 1-5 days after hot snare - manage with fluids and antibiotics. [ESGE 2017]`,`**Post-ERCP pancreatitis** occurs in about **3-10%**; prophylactic **rectal diclofenac or indomethacin 100 mg** and aggressive lactated Ringer's reduce it. [ESGE 2020]`,`**Bowel prep complications**: dehydration, **hyponatraemia** (elderly, thiazides, SSRIs), hypokalaemia, and acute phosphate nephropathy with sodium phosphate. [ESGE 2019]`,`**Infection** transmission is rare with proper reprocessing; antibiotic prophylaxis is **not needed for endocarditis prevention** in routine endoscopy, but is given for **PEG insertion (cefazolin 1-2 g IV), cirrhosis with GI bleeding (ceftriaxone 1 g IV daily up to 7 days)** and ERCP with incomplete biliary drainage. [ESC 2023]`,`Mild **bloating and sore throat** for a day are normal; **severe or worsening pain, black stools, fever or breathlessness** within 2 weeks are red flags for return. [BSG 2016]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: endoscopy only for a strong indication (significant bleeding, suspected obstruction); prefer the **second trimester**, use minimal sedation (pethidine or propofol; midazolam acceptable in low doses), left lateral tilt to avoid aortocaval compression, and **fetal heart monitoring** after 24 weeks. [ASGE 2012]`,`**PEG solutions** are the bowel prep of choice in pregnancy; avoid sodium phosphate; flexible sigmoidoscopy unprepared or with enema is often enough. [ASGE 2012]`,`**Elderly (over 75)**: use lower sedative doses, PEG-based prep with extra fluids, check sodium after prep in those on diuretics; colonoscopy screening beyond 75 is individualised by life expectancy. [USPSTF 2021]`,`**CKD**: use PEG preparation, avoid sodium phosphate and magnesium-based preps in eGFR under 30, and adjust DOAC interruption to creatinine clearance. [ESGE 2019]`,`**Cirrhosis**: correct only severe abnormalities; INR does not predict bleeding risk in cirrhosis, so **do not give FFP to 'correct' INR** before endoscopy; give antibiotic prophylaxis if bleeding. [AASLD 2024]`,`**Children**: paediatric OGD is done under general anaesthesia or deep sedation in a paediatric unit; common indications are foreign body (button battery is an emergency), caustic injury, coeliac, and portal hypertension (EHPVO). [ESPGHAN 2017]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: an unsedated OGD with lignocaine spray is safe and cheap; **rapid urease test kits** can be made in-house; FIT can triage which symptomatic patients travel for colonoscopy. [WGO 2023]`,`**Implanted cardiac devices**: use bipolar diathermy where possible; ICD therapy may need suspension with a magnet during electrosurgery near the device. [ASGE 2015]`]},{heading:`Follow-up, surveillance intervals and referral`,points:[`**Chase histology**: book a review 7-14 days after biopsy, and keep a register of pending results - a missed malignant biopsy result is a leading cause of litigation. [BSG 2016]`,`**After polypectomy (ESGE 2020)**: 1-4 adenomas under 10 mm with low-grade dysplasia need **no surveillance** - return to screening; **high-risk** (5 or more adenomas, any adenoma 10 mm or more or with high-grade dysplasia, serrated polyp 10 mm or more or with dysplasia) need colonoscopy at **3 years**. [ESGE 2020]`,`**US interval (USMSTF 2020)**: 1-2 tubular adenomas under 10 mm at **7-10 years**, 3-4 at **3-5 years**, 5-10 or any 10 mm or more at **3 years**, over 10 adenomas at 1 year; small rectosigmoid hyperplastic polyps at 10 years. [USMSTF 2020]`,`**Piecemeal resection of a polyp 20 mm or more** needs a **scar check at 3-6 months** for residual adenoma. [ESGE 2020]`,`**Barrett oesophagus without dysplasia**: surveillance every **5 years if 1-3 cm and every 3 years if 3-10 cm**; 10 cm or more goes to an expert centre; under 1 cm needs no surveillance. [ESGE 2023]`,`**Gastric intestinal metaplasia**: extensive (both antrum and body, OLGIM III-IV) or with family history of gastric cancer needs surveillance every 3 years; limited antral IM with no risk factors needs none. [MAPS III 2025]`,`**Varices**: no varices on screening - repeat every 2 years if liver injury is ongoing (3 years if cured); small varices - every 1 year (2 years if aetiology removed), unless on NSBB. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Referral letters** should state the question, urgency, anticoagulant/antiplatelet and its indication, diabetes drugs, renal function, and allergies - these allow same-day procedure without a separate visit. [BSG 2016]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2021-2026)`,points:[`**BSG/ESGE 2021 antithrombotics**: DOACs are omitted on the **morning of low-risk procedures** (previously continued); dabigatran with CrCl 30-49 needs 5 days off; prasugrel and ticagrelor treated like clopidogrel (7 days). [BSG/ESGE 2021]`,`**AGA/ASA multisociety 2024 GLP-1 RA guidance** replaced the 2023 ASA 'hold for a week' advice: most patients continue, with a **24-hour liquid diet**; holding is individualised. [AGA/ASA multisociety 2024]`,`**ACG/ASGE 2024 colonoscopy quality indicators** raised ADR to **35%** (men 40%, women 30%) and introduced **sessile serrated lesion detection rate (6% or more)**. [ACG/ASGE 2024]`,`**ESGE 2023 Barrett guideline** changed surveillance to **5 years (1-3 cm) and 3 years (3-10 cm)**, with expert-centre referral for 10 cm or more. [ESGE 2023]`,`**Lyon Consensus 2.0 (2024)** made **LA grade B** (not only C/D) conclusive evidence of GERD. [Lyon 2.0 2024]`,`**MAPS III (2025)** recommends **OLGA/OLGIM staging** and risk-based gastric surveillance, and opportunistic screening for H. pylori-related precancer in high-incidence regions. [MAPS III 2025]`,`**USPSTF 2021** lowered average-risk CRC screening start age to **45 years**. [USPSTF 2021]`,`**ESGE 2022 small-bowel guideline** keeps capsule endoscopy first-line for suspected small-bowel bleeding, ideally **within 48 hours** of the bleed for best yield. [ESGE 2022]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic viva**: 'Patient on apixaban for AF needs polypectomy - what will you do?' Answer: high-risk procedure, last dose 3 days before (CrCl 50 or more), no bridging, restart 2-3 days after. [BSG/ESGE 2021]`,`'**Patient on clopidogrel after a DES 3 months ago needs ERCP sphincterotomy**' - high thrombotic risk: do not stop on your own; continue aspirin and liaise with the cardiologist; consider delaying if elective. [BSG/ESGE 2021]`,`**Common mistake**: stopping aspirin for a diagnostic OGD with biopsy - it is a low-risk procedure and aspirin is continued. [BSG/ESGE 2021]`,`**Common mistake**: prescribing sodium phosphate prep to an elderly hypertensive on an ACE inhibitor - risk of acute phosphate nephropathy. [ESGE 2019]`,`Know the four classifications by heart: **Los Angeles, Prague C and M, Forrest and Boston Bowel Preparation Scale**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**H. pylori trap**: a rapid urease test done on PPI can be falsely negative - stop PPI 2 weeks before. [Maastricht VI 2022]`,`**Theory favourite**: 'Indications and preparation for upper GI endoscopy' or 'Role of the family physician before and after colonoscopy' - write indications, preparation (fasting, drugs, prep, consent), sedation, complications, follow-up. [DNB FM papers]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs used around endoscopy`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`PEG-electrolyte (high volume)`,`4 L orally: 2 L the evening before, 2 L on the morning`,`Second half starts within 5 h and ends at least 2 h before procedure`,`Nausea, bloating; safest in CKD, HF, cirrhosis, pregnancy; avoid in obstruction`],[`PEG-ascorbate (low volume)`,`2 L orally split with 1 L extra clear fluid`,`Split dose as above`,`Avoid in G6PD deficiency and phenylketonuria (aspartame)`],[`Sodium picosulfate with magnesium citrate`,`2 sachets orally, split`,`Split dose with plenty of clear fluid`,`Hyponatraemia, hypermagnesaemia; avoid eGFR under 30, heart failure`],[`Midazolam`,`1-2 mg IV (0.5-1 mg elderly)`,`Titrate 0.5-1 mg every 2 min; usually under 5 mg`,`Respiratory depression, paradoxical agitation; reduce with opioids, COPD, OSA`],[`Fentanyl`,`25-50 micrograms IV`,`Repeat 25 micrograms every 5 min as needed`,`Apnoea, chest wall rigidity, synergy with benzodiazepines`],[`Propofol`,`0.5-1 mg/kg IV, then 10-20 mg boluses`,`Titrated by trained personnel throughout`,`Hypotension, apnoea; no reversal agent; needs airway skills`],[`Flumazenil`,`0.2 mg IV over 15 s`,`0.1 mg every 60 s to max 1 mg`,`Re-sedation, seizures in benzodiazepine-dependent patients`],[`Naloxone`,`0.1-0.4 mg IV`,`Repeat every 2-3 min`,`Short action - re-narcotisation; acute withdrawal, pain, hypertension`],[`Lignocaine 10% spray`,`Pharyngeal spray before unsedated OGD`,`Single application`,`Aspiration if eating within 1 hour; rare methaemoglobinaemia`],[`Ceftriaxone (cirrhosis with GI bleed)`,`1 g IV once daily`,`Up to 7 days`,`Biliary sludge; check allergy`]]},{heading:`Antithrombotic plan by procedure risk (BSG/ESGE 2021)`,columns:[`Drug`,`Low-risk procedure`,`High-risk procedure, low thrombotic risk`,`High-risk procedure, high thrombotic risk`],rows:[[`Aspirin`,`Continue`,`Continue (individualise for ESD, large EMR, ampullectomy)`,`Continue`],[`Clopidogrel, prasugrel, ticagrelor`,`Continue`,`Stop 7 days before; continue aspirin`,`Discuss with cardiologist; continue aspirin; stop P2Y12 only if stent beyond 12 months (DES) or 1 month (BMS)`],[`Warfarin`,`Continue; check INR not above range in the week before`,`Stop 5 days before; INR under 1.5 on the day`,`Stop 5 days before; LMWH bridging from day 3; last LMWH 24 h before`],[`Apixaban, rivaroxaban, edoxaban`,`Omit morning dose on the day`,`Last dose 3 days before (CrCl 15 or more); no bridging`,`Same; no bridging`],[`Dabigatran`,`Omit morning dose on the day`,`Last dose 3 days before (CrCl 50 or more) or 5 days before (CrCl 30-49)`,`Same; no bridging`],[`Restart`,`Same day`,`Warfarin same evening; DOAC and P2Y12 at 2-3 days`,`Warfarin same evening with LMWH until INR in range`]]},{heading:`Procedure bleeding risk and patient thrombotic risk`,columns:[`Category`,`Low`,`High`],rows:[[`Procedure bleeding risk`,`Diagnostic OGD, colonoscopy, flexible sigmoidoscopy with or without biopsy; biliary/pancreatic stenting; EUS without FNA; enteroscopy without polypectomy`,`Polypectomy, ERCP with sphincterotomy, EMR, ESD, ampullectomy, dilatation, variceal banding, PEG, EUS-FNA, enteral stenting`],[`Thrombotic risk - warfarin users`,`Prosthetic aortic valve, AF without valve disease, VTE more than 3 months ago`,`Prosthetic mitral valve, prosthetic valve with AF, AF with mitral stenosis, VTE within 3 months, thrombophilia`],[`Thrombotic risk - P2Y12 users`,`Ischaemic heart disease without stent, cerebrovascular or peripheral disease`,`Drug-eluting stent within 12 months, bare-metal stent within 1 month`]]},{heading:`Report classifications the family physician must interpret`,columns:[`Classification`,`Used for`,`Grades`,`What it means for you`],rows:[[`Los Angeles`,`Reflux oesophagitis`,`A (5 mm or less) to D (75% or more of circumference)`,`LA B-D is proven GERD; C-D need twice-daily PPI and maintenance`],[`Prague C and M`,`Barrett oesophagus`,`C = circumferential length, M = maximum length (cm)`,`Sets surveillance interval: 5 y (1-3 cm), 3 y (3-10 cm)`],[`Forrest`,`Peptic ulcer bleeding`,`Ia spurting, Ib oozing, IIa visible vessel, IIb clot, IIc spot, III clean base`,`Ia-IIa get endoscopic therapy and high-dose PPI; IIc-III early feeding and discharge`],[`Boston Bowel Preparation Scale`,`Colonoscopy prep`,`0-3 per segment, total 0-9`,`Adequate if 6 or more with each segment 2 or more; else repeat within 1 year`],[`Paris`,`Polyp morphology`,`0-Ip, 0-Is, 0-IIa, 0-IIb, 0-IIc, 0-III`,`Depressed (0-IIc) lesions carry the highest invasion risk`],[`Marsh-Oberhuber`,`Duodenal biopsy in coeliac`,`1 (IEL), 2 (crypt hyperplasia), 3a-c (villous atrophy)`,`Marsh 3 with positive tTG confirms coeliac disease`],[`Mayo endoscopic score`,`Ulcerative colitis`,`0 normal to 3 spontaneous bleeding, ulcers`,`Mayo 0-1 is the mucosal healing target`],[`OLGA/OLGIM`,`Gastric atrophy and intestinal metaplasia`,`Stage 0-IV`,`Stage III-IV: surveillance every 3 years`]]},{heading:`Who to refer and how urgently`,columns:[`Clinical situation`,`Test`,`Urgency`],rows:[[`Haematemesis or melaena`,`OGD`,`Within 24 h after resuscitation; within 12 h if variceal suspected`],[`Dysphagia at any age`,`OGD`,`Urgent (within 2 weeks)`],[`Dyspepsia with alarm feature or new onset over about 45 years (India)`,`OGD with biopsy and RUT`,`Urgent (within 2 weeks)`],[`Iron deficiency anaemia (men, post-menopausal women)`,`OGD with duodenal biopsy plus colonoscopy`,`Urgent`],[`Positive FIT (10 micrograms/g or more) or rectal bleeding with change in habit`,`Colonoscopy`,`Urgent suspected-cancer pathway`],[`Cirrhosis not meeting Baveno favourable criteria`,`Screening OGD for varices`,`Routine`],[`Positive tTG-IgA`,`OGD with 4 D2 + 1-2 bulb biopsies on gluten`,`Routine`],[`Button battery or sharp object in oesophagus`,`Emergency OGD`,`Within 2-6 hours`]]},{heading:`Post-polypectomy surveillance`,columns:[`Finding at index colonoscopy`,`ESGE 2020`,`USMSTF 2020`],rows:[[`1-2 tubular adenomas under 10 mm, low-grade dysplasia`,`No surveillance; return to screening`,`7-10 years`],[`3-4 adenomas under 10 mm`,`No surveillance; return to screening`,`3-5 years`],[`5 or more adenomas`,`3 years`,`3 years (5-10); 1 year if over 10`],[`Any adenoma 10 mm or more, or high-grade dysplasia`,`3 years`,`3 years`],[`Serrated polyp 10 mm or more or with dysplasia`,`3 years`,`3 years`],[`Piecemeal resection 20 mm or more`,`Scar check at 3-6 months`,`6 months`],[`Small hyperplastic polyps in rectosigmoid`,`No surveillance`,`10 years`]]}],redFlags:[`**Haematemesis or melaena** with tachycardia or hypotension - resuscitate and arrange OGD within 24 hours.`,`**Chest or neck pain, crepitus or fever after OGD or dilatation** - suspect oesophageal perforation.`,`**Abdominal pain, distension and guarding after colonoscopy** - suspect colonic perforation; erect chest X-ray or CT.`,`**Fresh rectal bleeding up to 14 days after polypectomy** - delayed post-polypectomy bleed.`,`**Button battery or sharp object in the oesophagus**, or complete food bolus obstruction (drooling) - emergency endoscopy.`,`**Progressive dysphagia with weight loss** - urgent OGD for oesophageal or gastric cancer.`,`**Histology report of carcinoma or high-grade dysplasia** - same-week specialist referral.`,`**Severe vomiting, confusion or seizures during bowel prep** - hyponatraemia or dehydration.`],pearls:[`Two questions decide every antithrombotic plan: **procedure bleeding risk and patient thrombotic risk** - draw the 2 x 2 grid.`,`**Aspirin is almost never stopped** for endoscopy; DOACs need **no bridging**.`,`Low-risk procedure on a DOAC: **skip only the morning dose** (BSG/ESGE 2021).`,`Split-dose prep: second dose **starts within 5 h, finishes at least 2 h** before colonoscopy.`,`**BBPS 6 or more with each segment 2 or more** = adequate; otherwise repeat within a year.`,`**Stop SGLT2 inhibitors 3 days** before endoscopy - euglycaemic DKA.`,`Forrest rebleeding: **'Spurt 55, Vessel 43, Clot 22, Spot 10, Clean 5'**.`,`Every gastric ulcer is **biopsied and re-scoped at 8-12 weeks**; duodenal ulcers are not.`,`**INR does not predict bleeding in cirrhosis** - do not give FFP before endoscopy.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-upper-gi-endoscopy-indications-q1`,stem:`A 68-year-old man on apixaban 5 mg twice daily for non-valvular atrial fibrillation (creatinine clearance 70 mL/min) is booked for colonoscopy with removal of a 15 mm polyp. Per BSG/ESGE 2021, what is the correct plan?`,options:[`Continue apixaban and omit only the morning dose on the day`,`Take the last dose 3 days before the procedure, with no heparin bridging`,`Stop apixaban 5 days before and bridge with enoxaparin`,`Switch to warfarin a week before and check INR on the day`],answer:1,explanation:`Polypectomy is a high-risk procedure, so a factor Xa inhibitor with CrCl 50 or more is stopped with the last dose 3 days before (two full days off) and bridging is never used for DOACs. Omitting only the morning dose is the plan for a low-risk diagnostic procedure, enoxaparin bridging adds bleeding without benefit, and switching to warfarin is unnecessary.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-upper-gi-endoscopy-indications-q2`,stem:`On OGD for melaena, a duodenal ulcer shows a raised, non-bleeding visible vessel. What is its Forrest class and the approximate risk of rebleeding without endoscopic therapy?`,options:[`Forrest Ib, about 10%`,`Forrest IIb, about 22%`,`Forrest III, about 5%`,`Forrest IIa, about 43%`],answer:3,explanation:`A non-bleeding visible vessel is Forrest IIa, with a rebleeding risk of around 43% without treatment, so it receives endoscopic haemostasis and high-dose PPI. Forrest Ib is active oozing, IIb is an adherent clot with about 22% risk, and III is a clean base with about 5% risk that needs no endoscopic therapy.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-upper-gi-endoscopy-indications-q3`,stem:`A 55-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes on metformin and empagliflozin is to have colonoscopy next week. Which instruction about her diabetes drugs is most important to prevent a serious complication?`,options:[`Stop empagliflozin 3 days before the procedure`,`Double the metformin dose on the day of bowel preparation`,`Continue empagliflozin and drink only water during prep`,`Stop metformin 2 weeks before the procedure`],answer:0,explanation:`SGLT2 inhibitors combined with fasting and bowel-prep dehydration can cause euglycaemic diabetic ketoacidosis, so empagliflozin is stopped 3 days before. Metformin is simply omitted on the clear-fluid and procedure days, never doubled and not stopped weeks ahead, and continuing the SGLT2 inhibitor is the dangerous choice.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-upper-gi-endoscopy-indications-q4`,stem:`A 74-year-old hypertensive on ramipril and a thiazide with eGFR 42 needs colonoscopy. Which bowel preparation is the safest choice?`,options:[`Oral sodium phosphate solution in two doses`,`Sodium picosulfate with magnesium citrate`,`Split-dose polyethylene glycol-electrolyte solution`,`Castor oil 30 mL the evening before`],answer:2,explanation:`PEG-electrolyte solution is iso-osmotic with no net fluid or electrolyte shift, so it is the safest in CKD, elderly and diuretic users. Sodium phosphate causes acute phosphate nephropathy, especially with ACE inhibitors and thiazides; picosulfate-magnesium risks hyponatraemia and magnesium retention; castor oil is not a validated colonoscopy preparation.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-upper-gi-endoscopy-indications-q5`,stem:`A colonoscopy report reads: 'BBPS right 1, transverse 2, left 3 (total 6); caecum reached; no polyps seen.' What should the family physician advise?`,options:[`Normal colonoscopy - repeat screening in 10 years`,`Adequate preparation - repeat in 5 years because of the borderline total`,`Proceed directly to CT colonography in 3 years`,`Inadequate preparation - repeat colonoscopy with better prep within 1 year`],answer:3,explanation:`Adequate preparation needs a total BBPS of 6 or more AND every segment scoring 2 or more; a right colon score of 1 makes this inadequate despite the total of 6, and right-sided flat lesions are easily missed, so the colonoscopy is repeated within a year. Calling it normal with a 10-year interval, or a 5-year interval, ignores the segment rule, and CT colonography is not the standard response to poor prep.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-upper-gi-endoscopy-indications-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Enumerate the indications for upper GI endoscopy and colonoscopy that a family physician should recognise. Describe how you will prepare a 65-year-old diabetic on clopidogrel and apixaban for colonoscopy with polypectomy, and outline post-procedure care.`,openingLines:[`GI endoscopy is the diagnostic and therapeutic look-and-biopsy test of the gut; the family physician decides the indication and urgency, prepares the patient safely and acts on the report.`,`Preparation rests on four pillars: fasting and bowel preparation, antithrombotic plan by procedure and thrombotic risk (BSG/ESGE 2021), diabetes drug adjustment, and sedation with informed consent.`],answer:[{heading:`Indications for OGD`,points:[`Dysphagia at any age; dyspepsia with alarm features or new onset over about 45 years in India.`,`Upper GI bleeding (within 24 h), unexplained iron deficiency anaemia (with colonoscopy), positive coeliac serology.`,`Variceal screening in cirrhosis not meeting Baveno criteria; Barrett and gastric precancer surveillance; foreign body and caustic ingestion.`]},{heading:`Indications for colonoscopy`,points:[`Rectal bleeding with change in bowel habit, positive FIT (10 micrograms/g or more), iron deficiency anaemia, mass.`,`Chronic diarrhoea, suspected IBD or intestinal TB, screening from 45 years, family history, polyp surveillance.`]},{heading:`Antithrombotic plan (BSG/ESGE 2021)`,points:[`Polypectomy is a high-risk procedure.`,`Apixaban: last dose 3 days before if CrCl 15 or more; no bridging; restart at 2-3 days.`,`Clopidogrel: if no recent stent, stop 7 days before; if stent within 12 months, discuss with cardiologist and continue aspirin.`]},{heading:`Diabetes and other drugs`,points:[`Stop SGLT2 inhibitor 3 days before; omit oral agents on prep and procedure day; reduce basal insulin by about 20-25%.`,`GLP-1 RA: liquid diet 24 hours before; stop oral iron 5-7 days before.`,`First on the list; capillary glucose on arrival.`]},{heading:`Bowel preparation and fasting`,points:[`Low-residue diet the day before; split-dose 4 L PEG-electrolyte (or 2 L low-volume).`,`Second dose starts within 5 h and finishes 2 h before; avoid sodium phosphate.`,`Clear fluids until 2 h, no solids for 6 h before sedation.`]},{heading:`Consent and sedation`,points:[`Consent with numbers: perforation about 1 in 1,000-3,000, post-polypectomy bleed about 1 in 100-300.`,`Midazolam 1-2 mg IV titrated plus fentanyl 25-50 micrograms; pulse oximetry; flumazenil and naloxone at hand.`]},{heading:`Post-procedure care`,points:[`Escort home; no driving for 24 hours.`,`Return for pain, fever, distension or fresh bleeding up to 14 days.`,`Chase histology; set surveillance interval by ESGE 2020 polyp rules.`]}],mustDraw:[`2 x 2 grid of procedure bleeding risk versus patient thrombotic risk with drug actions`,`Timeline of split-dose bowel preparation`],markSplit:[{part:`Indications for OGD and colonoscopy`,marks:3},{part:`Antithrombotic and diabetes drug plan`,marks:3},{part:`Bowel prep, consent and sedation`,marks:2},{part:`Post-procedure care and follow-up`,marks:2}],keywords:[`BSG/ESGE 2021`,`split-dose PEG`,`SGLT2 inhibitor`,`DOAC`,`clopidogrel`,`polypectomy`,`BBPS`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-upper-gi-endoscopy-indications-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on the Forrest classification of bleeding peptic ulcers and its clinical significance.`,openingLines:[`The Forrest classification grades the endoscopic stigmata of recent haemorrhage in a peptic ulcer and predicts the risk of rebleeding without endoscopic therapy.`,`It decides who gets endoscopic haemostasis and high-dose PPI and who can eat and go home early.`],answer:[{heading:`Classes and rebleeding risk`,points:[`Ia spurting and Ib oozing - active bleeding, about 55% rebleed.`,`IIa non-bleeding visible vessel - about 43%; IIb adherent clot - about 22%; IIc flat pigmented spot - about 10%.`,`III clean base - about 5%.`]},{heading:`Management by class`,points:[`Ia, Ib, IIa: endoscopic therapy (adrenaline plus clips or thermal) then high-dose PPI for 72 hours.`,`IIb: remove the clot and treat the underlying vessel if high-risk.`,`IIc and III: no endoscopic therapy; oral PPI, early feeding and discharge.`]},{heading:`Primary-care relevance`,points:[`Test and treat H. pylori; stop NSAIDs; restart needed aspirin within 3-5 days with a PPI.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table of Forrest classes with rebleeding percentages and action`],markSplit:[{part:`Classes with rebleeding risk`,marks:3},{part:`Management by class and follow-up`,marks:2}],keywords:[`Forrest`,`visible vessel`,`rebleeding`,`endoscopic haemostasis`,`high-dose PPI`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Gastrointestinal endoscopy chapter`,`Veitch AM et al. Endoscopy in patients on antiplatelet or anticoagulant therapy: BSG/ESGE guideline update, Gut and Endoscopy 2021`,`Hassan C et al. Bowel preparation for colonoscopy: ESGE Guideline Update, Endoscopy 2019`,`Hassan C et al. Post-polypectomy colonoscopy surveillance: ESGE Guideline Update, Endoscopy 2020; Gupta S et al. USMSTF recommendations 2020`,`Rex DK et al. Quality indicators for colonoscopy, ACG/ASGE 2024`,`Multisociety clinical practice guidance on peri-procedural management of GLP-1 receptor agonists (ASA, AGA, ASMBS, ISPCOP, SAGES), 2024`,`ESGE guideline on Barrett oesophagus, 2023; MAPS III management of epithelial precancerous conditions of the stomach, 2025`,`Indian Society of Gastroenterology consensus statements on dyspepsia and H. pylori, 2021`]}),c.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-nutrition-gi`,title:`Nutrition in GI and liver disease: malnutrition, enteral and parenteral nutrition`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Disease-related malnutrition** is a state of altered body composition and function from reduced intake, malabsorption or inflammation, **screened by NRS-2002 (score 3 or more = at risk)** and **diagnosed by GLIM (one phenotypic plus one aetiological criterion)**; it is treated by the gut-first ladder - **diet, oral supplements, enteral tube feeding, then parenteral nutrition** - while guarding against **refeeding syndrome**, and in cirrhosis by **35 kcal/kg and 1.2-1.5 g/kg protein a day with a late evening snack, never protein restriction** (ESPEN 2019).`,keywords:[`malnutrition`,`NRS-2002`,`MUST`,`GLIM criteria`,`SGA`,`RFH-NPT`,`sarcopenia`,`refeeding syndrome`,`hypophosphataemia`,`thiamine`,`enteral nutrition`,`nasogastric feeding`,`PEG`,`parenteral nutrition`,`TPN`,`late evening snack`,`BCAA`,`ESPEN 2019`,`oral nutritional supplements`,`short bowel syndrome`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Malnutrition (undernutrition)** is a state resulting from lack of uptake or intake of nutrition, leading to altered body composition (**reduced fat-free mass**) and body cell mass, with diminished physical and mental function and impaired outcome. [ESPEN 2017]`,`**Disease-related malnutrition** is subdivided into **with inflammation** (acute disease, chronic disease such as cirrhosis, IBD, cancer) and **without inflammation** (starvation, dysphagia, malabsorption, anorexia). [ESPEN 2017]`,`**Sarcopenia** is progressive loss of skeletal muscle mass and strength; **frailty** is reduced physiological reserve; **cachexia** is inflammation-driven muscle loss not fully reversed by feeding. [EWGSOP2 2019]`,`**Hospital malnutrition** affects about **30-50% of adult inpatients** worldwide and is unrecognised in most; it doubles complications, length of stay and cost. [ESPEN 2021]`,`**India**: NFHS-5 found **18.7% of women and 16.2% of men aged 15-49 underweight (BMI under 18.5)**, so many patients enter illness already depleted; Indian hospital studies report malnutrition in about **40-60%** of medical and surgical inpatients. [NFHS-5 2021]`,`**Cirrhosis**: malnutrition in **20-50%** and sarcopenia in **40-70%**, rising with Child-Pugh class; sarcopenia independently predicts mortality, HE, infections and transplant waiting-list death. [EASL 2019]`,`Other high-risk GI groups: **IBD (especially Crohn)** 20-85%, **chronic pancreatitis**, **short bowel syndrome**, **upper GI cancer (oesophagus, stomach, pancreas)**, alcoholic hepatitis and severe acute pancreatitis. [ESPEN 2023]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors in GI and liver disease`,points:[`**Reduced intake**: anorexia from cytokines and leptin, early satiety from **tense ascites** or gastric outlet obstruction, dysphagia, nausea, pain after meals (mesenteric ischaemia, Crohn stricture) and iatrogenic **'nil by mouth'** orders. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Malabsorption**: coeliac disease, pancreatic exocrine insufficiency, short bowel, bacterial overgrowth, cholestasis (fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, K) and bile-acid loss after ileal resection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Increased losses**: fistula and stoma output, diarrhoea, protein-losing enteropathy, repeated paracentesis (albumin and protein), GI bleeding (iron). [ESPEN 2023]`,`**Increased needs**: sepsis, surgery, burns, alcoholic hepatitis and active IBD raise resting energy expenditure; about **one-third of cirrhotic patients are hypermetabolic**. [EASL 2019]`,`**Unnecessary dietary restriction** is a major Indian cause: protein restriction in liver disease, multiple food taboos ('no milk, no egg' in jaundice) and very low-salt diets that make food unpalatable. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Alcohol use disorder** combines poor intake with deficiency of **thiamine, folate, zinc, magnesium and phosphate**. [ESPEN 2019]`,`**Drugs**: PPIs (B12, magnesium), metformin (B12), cholestyramine (fat-soluble vitamins), long-term diuretics (zinc, magnesium), lactulose overuse (dehydration). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology: starvation, cachexia, cirrhosis and refeeding`,points:[`**Simple starvation** uses glycogen in the first 24 hours, then **gluconeogenesis from muscle protein**, then switches to **ketone bodies** to spare protein - insulin falls and the body adapts. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Disease-related malnutrition** is different: **IL-1, IL-6 and TNF-alpha** drive muscle proteolysis (ubiquitin-proteasome pathway) and insulin resistance, so muscle is lost even with adequate calories - feeding slows but does not fully reverse it. [ESPEN 2017]`,`**Cirrhosis is a state of 'accelerated starvation'**: hepatic glycogen stores are small, so after an **overnight fast of 10-12 hours** the metabolic state equals **2-3 days of fasting** in a healthy person, and muscle is broken down for gluconeogenesis. [ESPEN 2019]`,`This is why a **late evening snack (about 50 g complex carbohydrate, 200 kcal)** and meals every 3-4 hours reduce overnight protein catabolism and improve nitrogen balance in cirrhosis. [EASL 2019]`,`**Hyperammonaemia** in cirrhosis enters muscle, upregulates **myostatin** and inhibits **mTOR** signalling, reducing muscle protein synthesis; muscle is also a key site of ammonia disposal by **glutamine synthetase**, so sarcopenia worsens hepatic encephalopathy - a vicious cycle. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Protein restriction in HE is harmful**: it accelerates muscle loss, which raises ammonia; normal-to-high protein is tolerated by nearly all patients. [ESPEN 2019]`,`**Refeeding syndrome**: after starvation, carbohydrate triggers **insulin release**, which drives **phosphate, potassium and magnesium into cells** and increases **thiamine** use; total body stores are already depleted, so serum levels crash. [ASPEN 2020]`,`Consequences of refeeding: **hypophosphataemia** (respiratory muscle weakness, rhabdomyolysis, haemolysis, heart failure, seizures), hypokalaemia and hypomagnesaemia (arrhythmia), **sodium and water retention** (oedema, heart failure) and **Wernicke encephalopathy** from thiamine deficiency. [NICE CG32]`,`**Bowel rest causes villous atrophy**, loss of gut barrier and bacterial translocation; **enteral nutrition maintains gut integrity and immunity**, which is why the gut is used whenever it works. [ESPEN 2023]`]},{heading:`Screening and diagnosis: NRS-2002, MUST and GLIM`,points:[`**Screen every inpatient within 24-48 hours of admission** and every at-risk outpatient (cirrhosis, IBD, cancer, elderly) with a validated tool. [ESPEN 2021]`,`**NRS-2002** (hospital): pre-screen (BMI under 20.5, weight loss in 3 months, reduced intake last week, severely ill); final score = **nutritional status (0-3) + disease severity (0-3) + 1 if age 70 or more**; **score 3 or more = at nutritional risk**, start a nutrition plan. [ESPEN 2003]`,`**MUST** (community, 'Malnutrition Universal Screening Tool'): BMI score + weight loss score + acute disease effect; **0 low, 1 medium, 2 or more high risk**. [BAPEN 2011]`,`**MNA-SF** is used in the elderly; **RFH-NPT (Royal Free Hospital Nutritional Prioritizing Tool)** is the liver-specific screen because fluid overload makes BMI misleading. [EASL 2019]`,`**GLIM diagnosis** needs **at least 1 phenotypic** (unintentional weight loss, low BMI, reduced muscle mass) **plus at least 1 aetiological** criterion (reduced intake or assimilation, or inflammation/disease burden). [GLIM 2019]`,`**GLIM phenotypic cut-offs**: weight loss **over 5% in 6 months or over 10% beyond 6 months**; BMI under 20 (under 70 years) or under 22 (70 or more); **Asian cut-offs BMI under 18.5 (under 70) or under 20 (70 or more)**; reduced muscle mass by validated method. [GLIM 2019]`,`**GLIM aetiological criteria**: intake **50% or less of requirement for over 1 week**, any reduction for over 2 weeks, or chronic GI condition impairing absorption; or acute/chronic **inflammation** related to disease. [GLIM 2019]`,`**GLIM severity**: stage 2 (severe) = weight loss **over 10% in 6 months or over 20% beyond 6 months**, or BMI under 18.5 (under 70 years) or under 20 (70 or more), or severe muscle deficit. [GLIM 2019]`]},{heading:`Clinical assessment - history`,points:[`**Weight history**: usual weight, current weight and percentage lost; in India patients often know their weight from the last illness or a pharmacy scale - document it. [ESPEN 2021]`,`**24-hour dietary recall** or a 3-day food record: number of meals, protein sources (dal, milk, curd, egg, paneer, meat), and whether the patient fasts for religious reasons or because of advice. [ICMR-NIN 2024]`,`Ask about **food restrictions given by doctors or family** - 'no protein for liver', 'no ghee or milk in jaundice' - which are unnecessary and common. [AASLD 2021]`,`**GI symptoms limiting intake**: anorexia, early satiety, dysphagia, vomiting, post-prandial pain, diarrhoea, steatorrhoea (pale, bulky, floating stools). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Functional status**: ability to climb stairs, rise from a chair without arms, walking speed - early markers of sarcopenia. [EWGSOP2 2019]`,`**Alcohol** quantity, **previous resections**, stoma output, number of paracenteses, and medications affecting nutrition. [ESPEN 2023]`,`**Social factors**: food insecurity, living alone, cost of supplements - link to PDS ration, Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana (TB) or community kitchens where relevant. [NFSA 2013]`]},{heading:`Examination and body composition`,points:[`**BMI** (weight/height squared) is the first measure, but in **ascites and oedema use dry weight**: subtract **5% for mild, 10% for moderate and 15% for tense ascites**, plus **5% for bilateral pedal oedema**. [EASL 2019]`,`**Mid-upper arm circumference (MUAC)** and **triceps skinfold** are not affected by ascites; MUAC under 23 cm in adults suggests undernutrition. [WHO 2013]`,`**Muscle wasting signs**: temporal hollowing, prominent clavicles and scapulae, squared shoulders, wasted interossei and quadriceps; **fat loss**: loose skin, orbital hollowing. [ASPEN 2012]`,`**Handgrip strength** (dynamometer) measures function: **under 27 kg in men and under 16 kg in women** indicates low strength (sarcopenia). [EWGSOP2 2019]`,`**Liver Frailty Index** (grip strength, chair stands, balance) **4.5 or more = frail**; **CT skeletal muscle index at L3** under 50 cm2/m2 (men) or 39 cm2/m2 (women) defines sarcopenia in cirrhosis. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Micronutrient signs**: angular stomatitis and glossitis (B2, B3, B12, iron), **koilonychia** (iron), perifollicular haemorrhage (vitamin C), night blindness and Bitot spots (vitamin A), **acrodermatitis and poor wound healing (zinc)**, ophthalmoplegia and ataxia (thiamine). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Serum albumin is not a marker of nutrition** - it is a negative acute-phase protein lowered by inflammation, liver disease and fluid overload; use it for prognosis, not to diagnose malnutrition. [ASPEN 2018]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Baseline**: CBC, sodium, **potassium, phosphate, magnesium**, calcium, glucose, urea, creatinine, LFT and INR before starting any feeding in a malnourished patient. [NICE CG32]`,`**Micronutrients when indicated**: ferritin with CRP, **vitamin B12 and folate**, **25-OH vitamin D**, zinc (interpret with CRP and albumin), and PT/INR as a marker of vitamin K in cholestasis. [ESPEN 2022]`,`**Malabsorption work-up**: faecal elastase (under 200 micrograms/g pancreatic insufficiency), tTG-IgA with total IgA, stool fat - see the Malabsorption topic. [ESPEN 2023]`,`**Body composition**: bioimpedance (BIA) phase angle, DEXA, ultrasound of quadriceps, or **CT at L3** if a CT is being done for other reasons. [GLIM 2022]`,`**Monitoring during refeeding**: phosphate, potassium, magnesium and glucose **daily for the first 3 days** (or more often if low), then as needed; fluid balance and weight daily. [ASPEN 2020]`,`**Nasogastric tube position**: aspirate **pH 5.5 or less** confirms gastric placement; if no aspirate or pH over 5.5, **chest X-ray**; never use the 'whoosh' test. [NHS Patient Safety 2016]`,`**PN monitoring**: glucose 4-6 hourly initially, electrolytes daily until stable, LFT and triglycerides weekly (stop or reduce lipid if triglycerides over 4.5 mmol/L, about 400 mg/dL). [ESPEN 2023]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD: requirements, diet and oral supplements`,points:[`**Energy**: most patients need **25-30 kcal/kg/day**; **cirrhosis (non-obese) 35 kcal/kg/day** of actual (dry) body weight. [ESPEN 2019]`,`**Protein**: **1.0-1.5 g/kg/day** in most illness; **cirrhosis 1.2 g/kg (non-malnourished) to 1.5 g/kg (malnourished or sarcopenic)**; the healthy Indian RDA is only about **0.8 g/kg/day**. [ESPEN 2019]`,`**Cirrhosis eating pattern**: **3 meals and 2-3 snacks**, no gap over **3-6 hours** in the day, plus a **late evening snack of about 50 g complex carbohydrate** (e.g. 2 chapatis with dal, or a milk-and-oats bowl). [EASL 2019]`,`**Obese cirrhosis (BMI over 30)**: aim for gradual weight loss of **5-10%** with a **500-800 kcal/day deficit**, keeping protein at **2.0 g/kg ideal body weight** and adding exercise, to avoid losing muscle. [ESPEN 2019]`,`**Salt in ascites**: moderate restriction to **about 5 g salt (2 g sodium) per day** - more severe restriction makes food unpalatable and worsens intake. [EASL 2018]`,`**Protein sources** in Indian diets: milk and curd, paneer, dal and legumes, soy, eggs, fish and chicken; **vegetable and dairy proteins** are better tolerated in HE-prone patients. [EASL 2019]`,`**Oral nutritional supplements (ONS)**: add **400-600 kcal and about 30 g protein/day** when diet alone does not meet 75% of needs; homemade options (milk with roasted sattu, peanut chikki, egg) work if commercial ONS is unaffordable. [ESPEN 2021]`,`**BCAA supplements** (about **0.25 g/kg/day**) are reserved for cirrhotic patients who **cannot tolerate dietary protein** or have recurrent HE despite therapy. [EASL 2019]`,`**Micronutrients**: correct proven deficiency - **vitamin D, zinc, B12, iron**; empirical **thiamine and folate** in alcohol use disorder; fat-soluble vitamins in cholestasis. [ESPEN 2022]`,`**Exercise**: resistance plus aerobic exercise (for example 150 minutes/week of moderate activity) is advised in compensated cirrhosis to build muscle; screen for varices before heavy straining. [AASLD 2021]`]},{heading:`Inpatient care: enteral and parenteral nutrition`,points:[`**Gut-first ladder**: diet and fortified food, then ONS, then **enteral tube feeding**, then **parenteral nutrition** only if the gut cannot be used or cannot meet needs. [ESPEN 2023]`,`**Enteral nutrition (EN)** is indicated when oral intake is expected to be **under 50-75% of needs for more than 5-7 days**; start within **24-48 hours** in the critically ill who cannot eat. [ESPEN ICU 2023]`,`**Route**: **nasogastric tube** for short term (up to about 4 weeks); **nasojejunal** for gastroparesis, high aspiration risk or intolerance; **PEG** or surgical gastrostomy if feeding is needed **beyond 4 weeks**. [ESPEN 2021]`,`**Varices are not a contraindication to a fine-bore nasogastric tube**; PEG is avoided in cirrhosis with ascites or large gastric varices because of bleeding and peritonitis risk. [ESPEN 2019]`,`**EN safety**: head end raised **30-45 degrees**, confirm tube position before each use, start at **10-30 mL/h** and step up, flush with 30 mL water every 4 hours; use **polymeric formula** first, peptide formula only for malabsorption. [ESPEN 2023]`,`**Parenteral nutrition (PN) indications**: intestinal failure (short bowel, high-output fistula, obstruction, prolonged ileus, severe mucositis), or EN failing to meet **60% of needs after 7 days** in a well-nourished patient (earlier if malnourished). [ESPEN ICU 2023]`,`**PN delivery**: **central line** (PICC or CVC) for standard hyperosmolar bags; **peripheral PN only if osmolarity under about 850-900 mOsm/L** and for short periods; use **all-in-one 3-chamber bags** where available. [ESPEN 2023]`,`**Critical illness**: avoid overfeeding early; reach **70-100% of energy needs by day 3-7** and **protein 1.3 g/kg/day** progressively. [ESPEN ICU 2023]`,`**Refeeding protocol** (high-risk): **thiamine 200-300 mg orally daily** (or IV) before feeding and for 10 days, start at **10 kcal/kg/day** (5 kcal/kg if BMI under 14 or no intake for over 15 days), increase slowly over **4-7 days**, and replace **phosphate 0.3-0.6 mmol/kg/day, potassium 2-4 mmol/kg/day, magnesium 0.2-0.4 mmol/kg/day**. [NICE CG32]`,`**ASPEN 2020** gives a slightly faster schedule: start **10-20 kcal/kg** (100-150 g dextrose) in the first 24 hours and advance by **33% of goal every 1-2 days**; **thiamine 100 mg** before feeding and daily for 5-7 days - both approaches are accepted. [ASPEN 2020]`]},{heading:`Disease-specific nutrition (special situations)`,points:[`**Acute pancreatitis**: start **oral feeding within 24-72 hours** in mild disease as pain allows, without waiting for lipase to normalise; in severe disease **enteral (NG or NJ) is preferred to PN**. [ACG 2024]`,`**Chronic pancreatitis**: no routine fat restriction; give **pancreatic enzyme replacement (PERT) 25,000-50,000 lipase units with each meal** (half with snacks), with a PPI if response is poor; see the Chronic pancreatitis topic. [ESPEN 2024]`,`**Crohn disease**: **exclusive enteral nutrition for 6-8 weeks** is first-line induction in children and an option in adults; check iron, **B12 after ileal resection**, vitamin D and zinc. [ESPEN 2023]`,`**Short bowel syndrome** (under 200 cm small bowel): high-output jejunostomy needs **restriction of hypotonic fluids to about 500 mL/day** and **oral rehydration solution with sodium 90-120 mmol/L**, plus loperamide and PPI; teduglutide in PN-dependent adults. [ESPEN 2023]`,`**Alcoholic hepatitis**: **35-40 kcal/kg and 1.2-1.5 g/kg protein**, enteral feeding if intake is poor; intake under 21.5 kcal/kg/day predicts higher mortality. [ESPEN 2019]`,`**Hepatic encephalopathy**: **do not restrict protein**; in grade 3-4 HE feed by nasogastric tube; spread intake through the day with a late evening snack. [ESPEN 2019]`,`**Pregnancy**: hyperemesis gravidarum causes **Wernicke encephalopathy** if dextrose is given without thiamine - always give thiamine first. [RCOG 2024]`,`**Elderly**: target **at least 1.0 g/kg protein** and 30 kcal/kg; texture-modified diets (IDDSI levels) for dysphagia; avoid restrictive 'diabetic' or 'low-salt' diets that cut intake. [ESPEN Geriatrics 2022]`,`**CKD with liver disease**: do not apply strict protein restriction in malnourished patients; dialysis patients need **1.0-1.2 g/kg/day**. [KDOQI 2020]`]},{heading:`Complications of malnutrition and of nutrition support`,points:[`**Malnutrition** causes poor wound healing, infections, pressure injury, falls, longer stay, reduced drug tolerance (chemotherapy) and higher mortality. [ESPEN 2021]`,`**Refeeding syndrome** usually appears in the **first 72 hours**; hypophosphataemia under **0.32 mmol/L (1 mg/dL)** is severe and needs IV replacement. [ASPEN 2020]`,`**EN complications**: **aspiration pneumonia**, tube misplacement into the lung (fatal if fed), diarrhoea (check for C. difficile and sorbitol-containing syrups before blaming the feed), blockage and sinusitis. [ESPEN 2023]`,`**PN complications**: **catheter-related bloodstream infection**, line thrombosis, hyperglycaemia, hypertriglyceridaemia, and **intestinal-failure-associated liver disease (IFALD)** with long-term PN. [ESPEN 2023]`,`**Re-feeding Wernicke**: confusion, ophthalmoplegia and ataxia after dextrose in an alcoholic - preventable with thiamine. [NICE CG32]`,`**Prognosis**: correcting sarcopenia in cirrhosis is slow; even with full nutrition, muscle gain takes months, and severe sarcopenia improves post-transplant survival only partly. [AASLD 2021]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer to a dietitian** for every patient with NRS-2002 3 or more, GLIM malnutrition, cirrhosis Child B-C, IBD flare, short bowel, or chronic pancreatitis. [ESPEN 2021]`,`**Refer to a nutrition support team or GI centre** for home PN, high-output stoma, intestinal failure or recurrent refeeding syndrome. [ESPEN 2023]`,`**Cirrhosis follow-up**: weight (dry), MUAC and **handgrip strength at every visit (3-6 monthly)**; Liver Frailty Index; repeat RFH-NPT. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Home enteral feeding**: review tube site, weight and electrolytes monthly; teach the family tube-position checks and feed hygiene. [ESPEN 2021]`,`**After discharge**: continue ONS for at least **1 month** after an admission with malnutrition - it reduces readmission and mortality. [EFFORT trial 2019]`,`**Bariatric surgery patients** need lifelong B12, iron, calcium, vitamin D and multivitamin; check levels yearly. [ASMBS 2019]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and national programme links`,points:[`**Counsel against harmful myths**: liver disease does not require protein restriction; milk, curd, eggs and dal are safe in jaundice and cirrhosis. [ICMR-NIN 2024]`,`**ICMR-NIN Dietary Guidelines for Indians 2024** recommend a diverse diet with pulses, milk and vegetables, salt under 5 g/day and less ultra-processed food - the baseline for counselling. [ICMR-NIN 2024]`,`**Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana** gives **Rs 1,000 per month** by direct benefit transfer to every TB patient during treatment (raised from Rs 500 in 2024) - relevant for abdominal TB. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Anaemia Mukt Bharat** and **POSHAN Abhiyaan** address iron deficiency and maternal-child undernutrition; link patients with IDA from GI blood loss or malabsorption. [MoHFW 2018]`,`**Ayushman Arogya Mandir** teams screen for NCDs and can measure BMI and MUAC; use them for follow-up weighing in rural areas. [NP-NCD 2023]`,`**Alcohol cessation** is the most important nutritional intervention in alcohol-related liver disease; offer brief intervention and refer to de-addiction services. [EASL 2018]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2019-2026)`,points:[`**ESPEN 2019 liver guideline and EASL 2019 CPG on nutrition in chronic liver disease** fixed cirrhosis targets at **35 kcal/kg and 1.2-1.5 g/kg protein**, late evening snack and no protein restriction in HE. [ESPEN 2019]`,`**AASLD 2021 practice guidance on malnutrition, frailty and sarcopenia in cirrhosis** introduced **Liver Frailty Index** and CT L3 SMI cut-offs into routine assessment. [AASLD 2021]`,`**GLIM 2019** criteria are now the global standard for diagnosis, with **GLIM guidance on muscle mass (2022) and on inflammation (2024)** clarifying how to apply them. [GLIM 2024]`,`**ASPEN 2020 consensus** redefined refeeding syndrome as a fall in phosphate, potassium or magnesium by **10-20% (mild), 20-30% (moderate) or over 30% or organ dysfunction (severe)** within 5 days of feeding. [ASPEN 2020]`,`**ESPEN ICU 2023 update** endorsed gradual feeding (no full feeding in the first 48 hours) and **protein 1.3 g/kg/day** progressively. [ESPEN ICU 2023]`,`**ESPEN 2023 IBD guideline** reaffirmed exclusive enteral nutrition in paediatric Crohn and screening for malnutrition at every IBD visit. [ESPEN 2023]`,`**ACG 2024 acute pancreatitis** guideline supports **early oral feeding within 24-72 hours** and enteral over parenteral nutrition in severe disease. [ACG 2024]`,`**ICMR-NIN Dietary Guidelines for Indians (2024)** replaced the 2011 edition. [ICMR-NIN 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic viva**: 'Protein requirement in cirrhosis with HE?' - **1.2-1.5 g/kg/day, no restriction**; restriction is an outdated, harmful answer. [ESPEN 2019]`,`**Classic viva**: 'Hallmark of refeeding syndrome?' - **hypophosphataemia**; prevention is thiamine before feeding and starting at 10 kcal/kg. [NICE CG32]`,`'**Why a late evening snack?**' - cirrhosis is accelerated starvation; an overnight fast equals 2-3 days of fasting, so muscle is used for glucose. [EASL 2019]`,`**Common mistake**: using serum albumin to diagnose malnutrition, or BMI without correcting for ascites. [ASPEN 2018]`,`**Common mistake**: starting PN when the gut works - EN is cheaper, safer and protects the gut barrier. [ESPEN 2023]`,`**Theory favourites**: 'Refeeding syndrome', 'Nutrition in cirrhosis', 'Enteral versus parenteral nutrition', 'GLIM criteria'. [DNB FM papers]`,`**Mnemonic for refeeding risk ('NICE 1-2')**: one major (BMI under 16, weight loss over 15%, no intake over 10 days, low K/PO4/Mg) or two minor (BMI under 18.5, loss over 10%, no intake over 5 days, alcohol or drugs). [NICE CG32]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs and supplements in nutrition support`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Thiamine`,`200-300 mg/day orally (or IV) in refeeding risk; 100 mg IV before dextrose in alcoholics`,`Before feeding and for 10 days; higher IV doses for Wernicke`,`Very safe; rare anaphylaxis with IV`],[`Potassium phosphate / oral phosphate`,`0.3-0.6 mmol/kg/day oral or IV`,`Daily during refeeding until stable`,`Diarrhoea (oral), hypocalcaemia with rapid IV`],[`Magnesium sulphate`,`0.2 mmol/kg/day IV or 0.4 mmol/kg/day oral`,`Until level normal`,`Diarrhoea orally; caution in CKD`],[`Oral nutritional supplement`,`400-600 kcal and about 30 g protein/day orally`,`Until intake meets 75% of needs; at least 1 month after discharge`,`Bloating, diabetes - glucose rise; cost`],[`BCAA granules`,`About 0.25 g/kg/day orally`,`Long-term in protein-intolerant cirrhosis`,`Costly; limited evidence outside HE`],[`Zinc sulphate`,`220 mg orally once or twice daily (50 mg elemental)`,`8-12 weeks, then recheck`,`Nausea; copper deficiency with long use`],[`Cholecalciferol`,`60,000 IU orally weekly`,`8 weeks, then maintenance 1,000-2,000 IU/day`,`Hypercalcaemia if overused`],[`Pancreatic enzymes (pancrelipase)`,`25,000-50,000 lipase units orally with meals, half with snacks`,`Long-term, titrate to stools and weight`,`Abdominal discomfort; fibrosing colonopathy at very high doses`],[`Parenteral nutrition 3-chamber bag`,`Central line, 20-30 kcal/kg/day with amino acids 1.2-1.5 g/kg`,`Build up over 2-3 days; reassess daily`,`Line sepsis, hyperglycaemia, IFALD, hypertriglyceridaemia`]]},{heading:`Screening and assessment tools`,columns:[`Tool`,`Setting`,`Components`,`Action threshold`],rows:[[`NRS-2002`,`Hospital`,`Nutritional status 0-3 + disease severity 0-3 + age 70 or more (1)`,`3 or more - at risk, start nutrition plan`],[`MUST`,`Community and hospital`,`BMI + weight loss + acute disease effect`,`2 or more - high risk, treat and refer`],[`MNA-SF`,`Elderly`,`Intake, weight loss, mobility, stress, neuropsychological, BMI or calf circumference`,`0-7 malnourished, 8-11 at risk`],[`RFH-NPT`,`Cirrhosis`,`Alcoholic hepatitis/tube feeding, fluid overload, BMI, weight loss, intake`,`2 or more - high risk`],[`SGA`,`Any`,`History plus examination of fat, muscle and oedema`,`B moderate, C severe malnutrition`],[`Liver Frailty Index`,`Cirrhosis`,`Grip strength, chair stands, balance`,`4.5 or more - frail`]]},{heading:`GLIM criteria for diagnosing malnutrition`,columns:[`Criterion type`,`Criterion`,`Stage 1 (moderate)`,`Stage 2 (severe)`],rows:[[`Phenotypic`,`Weight loss`,`5-10% in 6 months or 10-20% beyond 6 months`,`Over 10% in 6 months or over 20% beyond 6 months`],[`Phenotypic`,`Low BMI (Asian)`,`Under 18.5 if under 70 years; under 20 if 70 or more (both stages)`,`Under 18.5 if under 70 years; under 20 if 70 or more`],[`Phenotypic`,`Reduced muscle mass`,`Mild to moderate deficit`,`Severe deficit`],[`Aetiological`,`Reduced intake or assimilation`,`50% or less of needs for over 1 week, any reduction over 2 weeks, or malabsorption`,`Same - aetiology does not grade severity`],[`Aetiological`,`Inflammation`,`Acute disease or chronic disease-related`,`Same - aetiology does not grade severity`]]},{heading:`Refeeding syndrome: risk and management`,columns:[`Step`,`Detail`],rows:[[`High risk - one of`,`BMI under 16; weight loss over 15% in 3-6 months; little or no intake over 10 days; low K, PO4 or Mg before feeding`],[`High risk - two of`,`BMI under 18.5; weight loss over 10% in 3-6 months; little intake over 5 days; alcohol misuse or drugs (insulin, chemotherapy, antacids, diuretics)`],[`Before feeding`,`Check electrolytes; thiamine 200-300 mg/day and vitamin B complex; correct severe deficits but do not delay feeding`],[`Starting rate`,`10 kcal/kg/day (5 kcal/kg if BMI under 14 or no intake over 15 days); increase over 4-7 days`],[`Electrolytes`,`Phosphate 0.3-0.6, potassium 2-4, magnesium 0.2-0.4 mmol/kg/day`],[`Monitoring`,`Electrolytes daily for 3 days then as needed; fluid balance, weight, cardiac rhythm, oedema`],[`If phosphate falls under 0.32 mmol/L`,`IV phosphate, slow the feed, cardiac monitoring`]]},{heading:`Enteral versus parenteral nutrition`,columns:[`Feature`,`Enteral nutrition`,`Parenteral nutrition`],rows:[[`Indication`,`Gut works but oral intake inadequate`,`Gut not usable or insufficient (intestinal failure, obstruction, fistula, ileus)`],[`Access`,`NG, NJ, PEG, surgical jejunostomy`,`PICC or central venous catheter; peripheral only if under 850-900 mOsm/L`],[`Gut barrier`,`Maintained`,`Villous atrophy, bacterial translocation`],[`Main complications`,`Aspiration, tube misplacement, diarrhoea`,`Line sepsis, thrombosis, hyperglycaemia, IFALD`],[`Cost`,`Low`,`High (5-10 times)`],[`Timing in ICU`,`Within 24-48 hours if cannot eat`,`If EN gives under 60% after 7 days (earlier if malnourished)`]]},{heading:`Daily requirements by condition`,columns:[`Condition`,`Energy (kcal/kg/day)`,`Protein (g/kg/day)`,`Key point`],rows:[[`Healthy Indian adult`,`About 30`,`About 0.8 (RDA)`,`ICMR-NIN 2020 RDA`],[`General illness / elderly`,`25-30`,`1.0-1.5`,`At least 1.0 g/kg in elderly`],[`Cirrhosis, non-obese`,`35`,`1.2 (1.5 if malnourished)`,`Late evening snack; no gap over 3-6 h`],[`Cirrhosis, obese`,`Deficit of 500-800 kcal`,`2.0 per kg ideal weight`,`Lose 5-10% weight, keep muscle`],[`Alcoholic hepatitis`,`35-40`,`1.2-1.5`,`Tube feed if intake poor`],[`Critical illness`,`Build to 20-25 by day 3-7`,`1.3, progressive`,`Avoid early overfeeding`],[`Refeeding risk`,`Start 10 (or 5)`,`Build over 4-7 days`,`Thiamine and electrolytes first`]]}],redFlags:[`**BMI under 16, or no intake for over 10 days** - high refeeding risk, admit for supervised feeding.`,`**Serum phosphate under 0.32 mmol/L, or falling potassium or magnesium** after feeding starts - refeeding syndrome.`,`**Confusion, ophthalmoplegia or ataxia** in a malnourished or alcoholic patient - Wernicke; give IV thiamine now.`,`**Weight loss over 10% in 6 months** with GI symptoms - investigate for malignancy or malabsorption.`,`**High-output stoma (over 1.5-2 L/day)** with dehydration or rising creatinine.`,`**Fever or rigors in a patient on PN** - catheter-related bloodstream infection.`,`**Cough or desaturation during tube feeding** - aspiration or misplaced tube; stop the feed.`],pearls:[`**If the gut works, use it** - enteral before parenteral.`,`Cirrhosis: **'35 and 1.5'** - 35 kcal/kg, up to 1.5 g/kg protein, plus a late evening snack.`,`**Never restrict protein in hepatic encephalopathy.**`,`GLIM = **one phenotypic plus one aetiological** criterion.`,`**NRS-2002 of 3 or more** means at nutritional risk.`,`Refeeding: **thiamine first, start low (10 kcal/kg), go slow, replace phosphate**.`,`**Albumin is an inflammation marker, not a nutrition marker.**`,`Correct BMI for ascites: **minus 5, 10 or 15%** by ascites grade, minus 5% more for pedal oedema.`,`**Fine-bore NG tubes are safe in oesophageal varices**; PEG is not advised with ascites.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-nutrition-gi-q1`,stem:`A 52-year-old man with Child-Pugh B alcohol-related cirrhosis and a recent episode of grade 2 hepatic encephalopathy (now resolved on lactulose) weighs 60 kg (dry weight). What daily protein intake should be advised?`,options:[`20-30 g/day until encephalopathy has been absent for 3 months`,`About 0.6 g/kg/day using only vegetable protein`,`About 72-90 g/day, spread through the day with a late evening snack`,`Over 150 g/day as branched-chain amino acid supplements`],answer:2,explanation:`ESPEN 2019 and EASL 2019 advise 1.2-1.5 g/kg/day protein in cirrhosis even with encephalopathy (72-90 g for 60 kg), with frequent meals and a late evening snack to counter accelerated starvation. Restricting to 20-30 g or 0.6 g/kg worsens sarcopenia and, by reducing muscle ammonia clearance, encephalopathy; BCAA supplements are for protein-intolerant patients and are not used in such amounts.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-nutrition-gi-q2`,stem:`A 30-year-old woman with Crohn disease and BMI 14.5 who has eaten almost nothing for 3 weeks is admitted. On day 2 of nasogastric feeding at full calories she becomes weak, breathless and confused. Which abnormality is the hallmark of her condition?`,options:[`Hypophosphataemia`,`Hypercalcaemia`,`Hyperkalaemia`,`Hypernatraemia`],answer:0,explanation:`This is refeeding syndrome: feeding triggers insulin release that drives phosphate (plus potassium and magnesium) into cells, and hypophosphataemia causes respiratory muscle weakness, heart failure and confusion. Potassium falls rather than rises, calcium is not raised, and sodium and water are retained, so hypernatraemia is not expected. She should have started at 5 kcal/kg/day with thiamine first.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-nutrition-gi-q3`,stem:`Under GLIM criteria, which combination is sufficient to diagnose malnutrition in a 50-year-old Indian man?`,options:[`Serum albumin 2.8 g/dL and NRS-2002 score of 3`,`BMI 19.5 and a family history of gastric cancer`,`Low handgrip strength and serum prealbumin below normal`,`Unintentional 7% weight loss over 4 months and intake under half of needs for 2 weeks`],answer:3,explanation:`GLIM needs at least one phenotypic criterion (weight loss over 5% in 6 months qualifies) plus one aetiological criterion (intake 50% or less of requirement for over a week). Albumin and prealbumin are inflammation-sensitive and not GLIM criteria, NRS-2002 is a screening tool not a diagnosis, and BMI 19.5 is above the Asian cut-off of 18.5 with no aetiological criterion present.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-nutrition-gi-q4`,stem:`Why is a late evening snack of about 50 g complex carbohydrate recommended for patients with cirrhosis?`,options:[`It lowers portal pressure overnight`,`It shortens the overnight fast and reduces muscle protein breakdown for gluconeogenesis`,`It prevents nocturnal hypoglycaemia caused by lactulose`,`It increases urinary sodium excretion and helps ascites`],answer:1,explanation:`Cirrhosis is a state of accelerated starvation with small glycogen stores, so an overnight fast equals 2-3 days of fasting in a healthy person and muscle protein is broken down for glucose; a late evening snack improves nitrogen balance and muscle mass. It has no effect on portal pressure or natriuresis, and lactulose does not cause hypoglycaemia.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-nutrition-gi-q5`,stem:`A 60-year-old with severe acute pancreatitis is in the ICU on day 3, haemodynamically stable, with no ileus, but cannot eat because of pain. What is the preferred nutrition strategy?`,options:[`Keep nil by mouth until serum lipase normalises, then start oral feeds`,`Start total parenteral nutrition through a central line`,`Start enteral feeding through a nasogastric or nasojejunal tube`,`Give intravenous 5% dextrose only for 7 days`],answer:2,explanation:`In severe acute pancreatitis enteral nutrition (nasogastric or nasojejunal) is preferred over parenteral because it maintains the gut barrier and reduces infected necrosis, organ failure and mortality. Waiting for lipase to normalise is unnecessary and prolongs starvation, PN is reserved for when EN fails or is not tolerated, and dextrose alone does not meet protein needs.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-nutrition-gi-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`How will you screen for and diagnose malnutrition in a patient with chronic liver disease? Describe the nutritional management of cirrhosis, and add a note on refeeding syndrome.`,openingLines:[`Malnutrition and sarcopenia affect up to half of patients with cirrhosis and independently predict encephalopathy, infection and death, so every cirrhotic must be screened at each visit.`,`Cirrhosis is a state of accelerated starvation; treatment is 35 kcal/kg and 1.2-1.5 g/kg protein a day with frequent meals and a late evening snack, never protein restriction (ESPEN 2019).`],answer:[{heading:`Magnitude and pathophysiology`,points:[`Malnutrition 20-50%, sarcopenia 40-70% in cirrhosis.`,`Accelerated starvation: overnight fast equals 2-3 days of fasting; muscle used for gluconeogenesis.`,`Hyperammonaemia raises myostatin; sarcopenia reduces muscle ammonia clearance - vicious cycle with HE.`]},{heading:`Screening`,points:[`RFH-NPT (liver-specific) or NRS-2002 at every visit.`,`Dry weight: subtract 5/10/15% for ascites grade and 5% for pedal oedema.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis and assessment`,points:[`GLIM: one phenotypic (weight loss, low BMI, low muscle) plus one aetiological criterion.`,`MUAC, triceps skinfold, handgrip (under 27 kg men, 16 kg women), Liver Frailty Index 4.5 or more, CT L3 SMI.`,`Albumin is not a nutritional marker.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Energy 35 kcal/kg/day; protein 1.2-1.5 g/kg/day; no restriction in HE.`,`3 meals and 2-3 snacks; late evening snack about 50 g complex carbohydrate.`,`Salt about 5 g/day; ONS 400-600 kcal; BCAA 0.25 g/kg if protein intolerant.`,`Obese cirrhosis: 500-800 kcal deficit with protein 2 g/kg ideal weight plus exercise.`,`Correct zinc, vitamin D, thiamine; tube feeding if oral intake poor (NG safe in varices).`]},{heading:`Refeeding syndrome`,points:[`Insulin surge drives phosphate, potassium, magnesium into cells; thiamine consumed.`,`Identify risk by NICE criteria; thiamine 200-300 mg/day first; start 10 kcal/kg (5 if extreme); build over 4-7 days.`,`Replace phosphate, potassium, magnesium; monitor daily for 3 days.`]},{heading:`Follow-up`,points:[`Weight, MUAC and grip every 3-6 months; dietitian referral; counsel against food myths.`]}],mustDraw:[`Vicious cycle: cirrhosis, accelerated starvation, sarcopenia, hyperammonaemia and encephalopathy`,`Nutrition support ladder: diet, ONS, enteral, parenteral`],markSplit:[{part:`Screening and diagnosis (GLIM, tools, dry weight)`,marks:3},{part:`Nutritional management of cirrhosis`,marks:4},{part:`Refeeding syndrome`,marks:3}],keywords:[`RFH-NPT`,`GLIM`,`35 kcal/kg`,`1.2-1.5 g/kg`,`late evening snack`,`refeeding`,`thiamine`,`sarcopenia`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-nutrition-gi-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate enteral from parenteral nutrition: indications, routes, complications and monitoring.`,openingLines:[`Enteral nutrition delivers feed into the gut by mouth or tube; parenteral nutrition delivers nutrients intravenously, bypassing the gut.`,`The rule is 'if the gut works, use it' - enteral nutrition is cheaper, safer and preserves the gut barrier.`],answer:[{heading:`Indications`,points:[`EN: functioning gut with oral intake under 50-75% of needs for over 5-7 days (stroke, HE, pancreatitis, ICU).`,`PN: intestinal failure, obstruction, high-output fistula, prolonged ileus, or EN under 60% of needs after 7 days.`]},{heading:`Routes`,points:[`EN: NG under 4 weeks, NJ for gastroparesis or aspiration risk, PEG beyond 4 weeks.`,`PN: central line or PICC; peripheral only if osmolarity under 850-900 mOsm/L.`]},{heading:`Complications`,points:[`EN: aspiration, tube misplacement, diarrhoea, blockage.`,`PN: line sepsis, thrombosis, hyperglycaemia, hypertriglyceridaemia, IFALD; both cause refeeding syndrome.`]},{heading:`Monitoring`,points:[`EN: tube position (aspirate pH 5.5 or less or X-ray), head up 30-45 degrees, electrolytes.`,`PN: glucose 4-6 hourly initially, electrolytes daily, LFT and triglycerides weekly, line site daily.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column table comparing enteral and parenteral nutrition`],markSplit:[{part:`Indications`,marks:2},{part:`Routes and complications`,marks:2},{part:`Monitoring`,marks:1}],keywords:[`enteral`,`parenteral`,`PEG`,`PICC`,`IFALD`,`refeeding`,`aspiration`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Malnutrition and nutritional assessment; Enteral and parenteral nutrition chapters`,`Plauth M et al. ESPEN guideline on clinical nutrition in liver disease, Clinical Nutrition 2019`,`EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines on nutrition in chronic liver disease, Journal of Hepatology 2019`,`Lai JC et al. AASLD practice guidance: malnutrition, frailty and sarcopenia in patients with cirrhosis, Hepatology 2021`,`Cederholm T et al. GLIM criteria for the diagnosis of malnutrition, Clinical Nutrition 2019 (with GLIM guidance on muscle mass 2022 and inflammation 2024)`,`da Silva JSV et al. ASPEN consensus recommendations for refeeding syndrome, Nutrition in Clinical Practice 2020; NICE CG32 Nutrition support for adults (updated 2017)`,`Singer P et al. ESPEN practical and partially revised guideline: clinical nutrition in the ICU, 2023; Bischoff SC et al. ESPEN guideline on clinical nutrition in IBD, 2023`,`ICMR-National Institute of Nutrition. Dietary Guidelines for Indians, 2024`]}),c.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-tb-abdominal`,title:`Abdominal tuberculosis`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Abdominal tuberculosis** is infection of the **peritoneum, gut (mostly ileocaecal), abdominal lymph nodes or solid viscera** by *Mycobacterium tuberculosis*, presenting as chronic pain, fever, weight loss, **exudative lymphocytic ascites (SAAG under 1.1, ADA 39 IU/L or more)** or **subacute intestinal obstruction**; diagnosis rests on imaging plus tissue (**histology, CBNAAT/Truenat, culture**), it must be told apart from **Crohn disease**, and it is cured by the **NTEP 6-month regimen (2HRZE/4HRE)** with surgery only for complications.`,keywords:[`abdominal TB`,`peritoneal tuberculosis`,`intestinal tuberculosis`,`ileocaecal TB`,`tubercular abdomen`,`ADA`,`adenosine deaminase`,`CBNAAT`,`Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra`,`Truenat`,`Crohn disease`,`subacute intestinal obstruction`,`SAIO`,`Fleischner sign`,`Stierlin sign`,`pulled-up caecum`,`necrotic lymph nodes`,`2HRZE/4HRE`,`NTEP`,`INDEX-TB`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Abdominal tuberculosis** includes TB of the **gastrointestinal tract, peritoneum, abdominal lymph nodes and solid organs (liver, spleen, pancreas)**; genitourinary TB is usually classed separately. [Harrison 22e]`,`India carries about **26-27% of the world's TB burden**, the largest of any country; WHO estimated a **17.7% fall in India's TB incidence between 2015 and 2023**. [WHO Global TB Report 2024]`,`**Extrapulmonary TB (EPTB)** makes up about **20-25% of notified TB in India**, and abdominal TB is roughly **the sixth commonest EPTB site (about 10-12% of EPTB)** after lymph node, pleural and others. [India TB Report 2024]`,`Abdominal TB is a disease of **young adults (20-40 years)** in India, with a slight female preponderance for peritoneal disease; **concurrent pulmonary TB is seen in only about 15-25%**, so a normal chest X-ray does not exclude it. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`In high-income countries it is seen mainly in migrants, people with HIV, peritoneal dialysis and cirrhosis; **M. bovis** from unpasteurised milk was historically important and is now uncommon. [Harrison 22e]`,`The **ileocaecal region is involved in about 75%** of intestinal TB, followed by the jejunum and ileum, colon (segmental), and rarely the oesophagus, stomach and duodenum. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Intestinal TB and Crohn disease** overlap clinically, endoscopically and histologically; India has a rising Crohn incidence, so misdiagnosis in both directions is common. [ISG Crohn consensus 2015]`]},{heading:`Aetiology, routes of spread and risk factors`,points:[`**Swallowing infected sputum** from active pulmonary TB seeds the gut mucosa; this is why intestinal TB is commoner with cavitary lung disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Haematogenous spread** from a primary (often healed) lung focus reactivates later in peritoneum, nodes and solid organs - the usual route for **peritoneal and nodal TB**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lymphatic spread** from infected mesenteric nodes, and **contiguous spread** from the fallopian tubes (genital TB) or psoas abscess, also occur. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Ingestion of M. bovis** in unpasteurised milk causes primary intestinal TB, with mesenteric nodal involvement. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Risk factors**: HIV, diabetes, **cirrhosis**, peritoneal dialysis (CAPD), malnutrition, alcohol use, TNF-alpha inhibitors and steroids, chronic kidney disease and close contact with active TB. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Anti-TNF therapy started for presumed Crohn disease** can unmask or disseminate unrecognised intestinal TB - screen with IGRA/Mantoux and chest X-ray first. [ECCO 2021]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology and pathology`,points:[`The **ileocaecal region is favoured** because of **abundant lymphoid tissue (Peyer patches)**, physiological **stasis**, increased fluid and electrolyte absorption, and close contact of contents with mucosa. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`Bacilli enter **Peyer patches**, form **epithelioid granulomas** in the submucosa, undergo **caseation**, and the overlying mucosa sloughs to form ulcers. [Robbins 10e]`,`**Ulcers are transverse (circumferential)** because they follow the circumferential lymphatics of the bowel wall; healing by fibrosis produces **short 'napkin-ring' strictures** - the cause of subacute obstruction. [Robbins 10e]`,`Three gross types: **ulcerative** (about 60%, multiple superficial ulcers, more systemic illness), **hypertrophic** (about 10%, fibrosis and inflammatory mass mimicking cancer) and **ulcero-hypertrophic** (about 30%, mixed). [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Fibrosis of the ileocaecal region** pulls the caecum up out of the iliac fossa and makes the ileocaecal valve **gaping (patulous)** with an obtuse ileocaecal angle - signs seen on barium and colonoscopy. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Histology hallmark**: **large (over 400 micrometres), confluent, caseating granulomas**, often in the submucosa and nodes, with **ulcers lined by epithelioid histiocytes**; Crohn granulomas are small, few, non-caseating and discrete. [Robbins 10e]`,`**Peritoneal TB** studs the peritoneum with **whitish tubercles** that exude protein-rich, lymphocyte-rich fluid; activated T cells release **adenosine deaminase (ADA)** and interferon-gamma - the basis of the ascitic ADA test. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nodal TB** causes matted mesenteric and retroperitoneal nodes with **central caseous necrosis** (rim-enhancing on CT); they may compress the bile duct or form cold abscesses. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`**Peritoneal TB - wet (ascitic) type**: commonest form, with free or loculated exudative ascites. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Peritoneal TB - fibrotic-fixed type**: omental and mesenteric thickening with matted bowel loops forming masses; **dry (plastic) type**: dense adhesions and the classic **'doughy abdomen'**. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Intestinal TB**: ulcerative, hypertrophic, ulcero-hypertrophic and **stricturous** forms; by site - ileocaecal, small bowel, colonic, anorectal, gastroduodenal, oesophageal. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Nodal TB**: mesenteric, peripancreatic, periportal and retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy, sometimes the only abdominal finding. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Visceral TB**: hepatic (miliary granulomas or tuberculoma/abscess), splenic micro-abscesses, pancreatic TB mimicking cancer. [Harrison 22e]`,`For programme purposes all these are **extrapulmonary TB**, classified as **microbiologically confirmed** (CBNAAT/Truenat, culture or smear positive) or **clinically diagnosed**. [NTEP 2021]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Chronic abdominal pain** (about 80-90%) - vague, diffuse, or colicky in the right iliac fossa - lasting weeks to months. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Constitutional symptoms**: low-grade evening fever, **night sweats, anorexia and weight loss** in most patients, but they may be absent in up to a third. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Subacute intestinal obstruction (SAIO)**: recurrent colicky periumbilical pain, distension, borborygmi and vomiting, relieved by passing flatus or vomiting - the hallmark of **ileocaecal strictures**. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Abdominal distension** from ascites (peritoneal TB) - painless or mildly tender, developing over weeks. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Altered bowel habit**: diarrhoea (ulcerative type, bacterial overgrowth above strictures) or alternating constipation; overt rectal bleeding is uncommon and suggests colonic TB or Crohn disease. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Atypical presentations**: acute abdomen from **perforation** (often ileal, may occur during ATT), RIF mass mistaken for appendicular mass or caecal cancer, obstructive jaundice from nodes, infertility or menstrual disturbance in women, and **fever of unknown origin**. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`Ask about **TB contact, previous ATT and its duration, HIV, diabetes, cirrhosis, steroid or biologic use**, and alcohol. [NTEP 2021]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`General: **pallor, wasting, low BMI**, lymphadenopathy (cervical nodes are a biopsy target), and signs of pulmonary TB. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Doughy abdomen** - a classical but non-specific 'rubbery' feel from peritoneal thickening and adhesions in plastic peritoneal TB. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Ascites**: shifting dullness or fluid thrill; **no stigmata of chronic liver disease** and no raised JVP point away from portal hypertension. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Right iliac fossa mass** - firm, mildly tender, fixed, often with **visible peristalsis and loud borborygmi** from obstruction; differential includes appendicular mass, caecal carcinoma, Crohn and amoeboma. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Matted bowel loops or rolled-up omentum ('omental cake')** may be palpable as a transverse mass in the upper abdomen. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Per-rectal examination**: fistula or fissure (anorectal TB or Crohn); **pelvic examination** in women for adnexal masses (genital TB). [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Peritonitis signs** (guarding, rigidity, absent bowel sounds) mean perforation and need emergency surgery. [Bailey and Love 28e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Crohn disease** - the key differential for ileocaecal TB; longitudinal ulcers, skip lesions, perianal disease and **comb sign** favour Crohn (see the table and the Crohn disease topic). [ISG Crohn consensus 2015]`,`**Peritoneal carcinomatosis** (ovarian, gastric, colon) - also high-protein, low-SAAG ascites; **positive cytology, nodular peritoneal thickening and low ADA** distinguish it; **CA-125 is raised in both**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ileocaecal carcinoma and lymphoma** - older age, anaemia, irregular mass, no fever; lymphoma has bulky nodes with homogeneous (non-necrotic) enhancement. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Amoeboma** - caecal mass in an endemic area, responds to metronidazole; **Yersinia ileitis** - acute, self-limiting. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Appendicular mass** - short history, recent acute pain, fever; resolves with antibiotics. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Cirrhotic ascites** - SAAG 1.1 or more, protein under 2.5 g/dL; but **TB in cirrhosis** gives mixed results with lower ADA, so keep a low threshold for laparoscopy. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Mesenteric lymphadenitis** from other causes (lymphoma, Whipple disease, histoplasmosis) - biopsy settles it. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line in OPD and district hospital`,points:[`**Blood**: anaemia, raised ESR and CRP, low albumin; **HIV test and blood glucose/HbA1c are mandatory** in every TB patient under NTEP. [NTEP 2021]`,`**Chest X-ray**: evidence of active or healed TB in only about 15-25%; if sputum is produced, send it for **CBNAAT/Truenat** - a positive sputum makes the diagnosis. [NTEP 2021]`,`**Ultrasound abdomen**: ascites with **fine septations and debris**, thickened omentum, **mesenteric nodes with hypoechoic necrotic centres**, ileocaecal wall thickening (**pseudokidney sign**) and the **'club sandwich' or 'sliced bread' sign** of fluid between radially arranged bowel loops. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Diagnostic paracentesis**: **SAAG under 1.1 g/dL, total protein over 2.5 g/dL, cell count over 500/mm3 with lymphocytes over 70%**, low glucose; and **ascitic ADA**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ascitic ADA 39 IU/L or more** (various cut-offs 30-40 IU/L) has about **100% sensitivity and 97% specificity** for TB peritonitis; it is falsely low in cirrhosis and HIV and falsely high in lymphoma and some malignancy. [Riquelme meta-analysis 2006]`,`**Ascitic AFB smear** is positive in under 3% and culture in 20-35% (higher if 1 L is centrifuged); **Xpert MTB/RIF on ascitic fluid** has low sensitivity (about 30-60%) but high specificity - **a negative test never excludes peritoneal TB**. [WHO 2021]`,`**Mantoux/IGRA** show infection, not disease; in India a positive test adds little, and a negative test does not exclude TB. [INDEX-TB 2016]`]},{heading:`Investigations - advanced and tertiary`,points:[`**CECT abdomen** (or CT enterography): high-density ascites (20-45 HU), **smooth peritoneal thickening with enhancement** (nodular favours carcinomatosis), omental cake, **necrotic rim-enhancing nodes** (highly suggestive), concentric ileocaecal thickening and **pulled-up conical caecum**. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Colonoscopy with ileoscopy**: transverse ulcers, nodularity, **patulous ileocaecal valve**, strictures and pseudopolyps; take **at least 6-8 biopsies from ulcer edges** for histology, AFB stain, **CBNAAT/Truenat and liquid culture (MGIT)**. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Biopsy yield**: granulomas in about 50-80%, caseation in about 20-40%, AFB smear about 20-30%, culture about 30-40%, Xpert about 20-40% sensitivity with about 99% specificity - **combine tests to raise yield**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Laparoscopy with peritoneal biopsy** is the gold standard for peritoneal TB: **multiple whitish tubercles, adhesions and thickened peritoneum** with over 90% diagnostic accuracy; histology shows caseating granulomas. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**EUS-guided FNA** of mesenteric or retroperitoneal nodes, or image-guided core biopsy, when there is no ascites or mucosal lesion. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Barium studies** (now largely replaced) show the **Stierlin sign** (rapid emptying of the inflamed caecum), **Fleischner (inverted umbrella) sign** (thick, gaping valve with narrow terminal ileum), **string sign** and pulled-up caecum. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Universal drug susceptibility testing (UDST)**: every microbiologically confirmed TB needs rifampicin resistance testing (CBNAAT/Truenat) and, if rifampicin-sensitive, **isoniazid resistance by line probe assay**. [NTEP 2021]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`**Notify every TB case** (mandatory for public and private practitioners since 2018) and register on **Ni-kshay**; treatment drugs are free under NTEP. [NTEP 2021]`,`**Regimen for drug-sensitive abdominal TB**: **2 months HRZE (intensive) then 4 months HRE (continuation)** daily, as weight-band fixed-dose combinations (FDC HRZE 75/150/400/275 mg). [NTEP 2021]`,`**Adult weight bands (FDC tablets daily)**: **25-34 kg: 2; 35-49 kg: 3; 50-64 kg: 4; 65-75 kg: 5; over 75 kg: 6**. [NTEP 2021]`,`**Six months is enough** for abdominal TB - Indian RCTs found **6 months as effective as 9 months** with no more relapse; prolonging treatment for persistent mild symptoms or scars is not needed. [Makharia 2015]`,`**Pyridoxine 10 mg/day** (higher in pregnancy, diabetes, HIV, alcohol, malnutrition) prevents isoniazid neuropathy. [NTEP 2021]`,`**Corticosteroids are not routinely recommended** for TB peritonitis or intestinal TB - they do not reliably prevent adhesions or strictures. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Baseline LFT** before ATT, especially in the elderly, alcohol users, chronic liver disease, HIV and malnutrition; repeat if symptoms - see the Drug-induced liver injury topic. [NTEP 2021]`,`**Nutrition**: enrol in **Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana (Rs 1,000/month by DBT)** and Ni-kshay Mitra food baskets; protein-energy support improves treatment success. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Therapeutic ATT trial** where tissue diagnosis fails and Crohn is still possible: a **clinical response by 8 weeks plus mucosal healing at 2-6 months** supports TB; lack of response should prompt re-evaluation for Crohn, not extension of ATT. [ISG Crohn consensus 2015]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergencies`,points:[`**Admission criteria**: SAIO not settling, complete obstruction, perforation, GI bleeding, severe malnutrition, DILI, or diagnostic laparoscopy/colonoscopy needs. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**SAIO**: 'drip and suck' - nil by mouth, IV fluids, nasogastric decompression, correct electrolytes - and start ATT; most strictures settle with ATT alone. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Strictures**: about **70-90% of short tubercular strictures resolve** with ATT; persistent symptomatic strictures after ATT need **endoscopic balloon dilatation** or **strictureplasty / limited ileocaecal resection**. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Surgical indications**: **perforation, complete obstruction not resolving, massive bleeding, abscess or fistula, and diagnostic doubt with suspected malignancy**. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Perforation** (usually ileal, may be multiple, sometimes during ATT as a paradoxical reaction): resuscitation, broad-spectrum antibiotics and laparotomy with **resection and anastomosis or stoma**; simple closure of TB perforation often leaks. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Drug-induced liver injury**: stop hepatotoxic drugs if **ALT over 3 times ULN with symptoms or jaundice, or over 5 times ULN without symptoms**; give a non-hepatotoxic bridge (e.g. ethambutol, levofloxacin, streptomycin/amikacin) if needed and **reintroduce rifampicin, then isoniazid**, one at a time, once ALT is below 2 times ULN. [NTEP 2021]`,`**Paradoxical reaction** (new ascites, nodes or obstruction weeks into ATT, especially in HIV after ART) is not treatment failure; confirm adherence and drug sensitivity, continue ATT and consider steroids. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: HRZE is safe and should be started promptly; add **pyridoxine**; avoid streptomycin and other aminoglycosides (fetal ototoxicity). [NTEP 2021]`,`**HIV**: start **ART within 2 weeks** of ATT in most patients; rifampicin lowers dolutegravir levels, so give **dolutegravir 50 mg twice daily** during and 2 weeks after rifampicin; give co-trimoxazole prophylaxis. [NACO 2021]`,`**Diabetes**: screen all; diabetes delays culture conversion and raises relapse; rifampicin lowers sulfonylurea levels - insulin may be needed. [NTEP 2021]`,`**Chronic liver disease or cirrhosis**: modified regimens with fewer hepatotoxic drugs are used depending on Child-Pugh class, under specialist care; peritoneal TB in cirrhosis has high mortality. [NTEP 2021]`,`**CKD (CrCl under 30)**: give **pyrazinamide and ethambutol three times weekly** after dialysis; isoniazid and rifampicin are unchanged. [ATS/CDC/IDSA 2016]`,`**Children**: abdominal TB is often nodal or peritoneal; use paediatric FDC by weight band (HRZ 50/75/150 dispersible plus ethambutol 100 mg); the **WHO 4-month regimen for non-severe TB does not apply to abdominal TB**. [WHO 2022]`,`**Elderly**: more DILI, more misdiagnosis as malignancy - keep a low threshold for tissue diagnosis and monitor LFT. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drug-resistant TB**: rifampicin-resistant abdominal TB is treated per NTEP with the **6-month BPaLM regimen (bedaquiline, pretomanid, linezolid, moxifloxacin)** where eligible, or longer oral regimens under specialist care. [NTEP 2024]`]},{heading:`Complications, prognosis and follow-up`,points:[`**Intestinal obstruction** from strictures, adhesions or matted loops - the commonest complication. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Perforation** (free or contained), **fistulae** (enterocutaneous, entero-enteric), **abscess** and **haemorrhage**. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Malabsorption** from bacterial overgrowth above strictures (blind loop), bile-salt loss with ileal disease, and lymphatic obstruction - B12 deficiency, steatorrhoea. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ATT-related**: DILI in about **5-10%** of Indians on first-line ATT, peripheral neuropathy, optic neuritis (ethambutol), gout (pyrazinamide). [NTEP 2021]`,`**Prognosis**: with timely ATT, cure exceeds **90%**; mortality rises with **delayed diagnosis, cirrhosis, HIV, old age and surgical complications**. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Follow-up monthly** during ATT: weight (revise FDC dose by weight band), symptoms, adherence (99DOTS, family DOT, Ni-kshay app), side effects. [NTEP 2021]`,`**Clinical response** (fever, appetite, weight) is expected within **4-8 weeks**; failure needs drug-resistance testing and re-evaluation for Crohn disease, lymphoma or cancer. [ISG Crohn consensus 2015]`,`**Repeat colonoscopy at 2-6 months** in intestinal TB (or when Crohn was a differential) to document **mucosal healing**, which confirms the diagnosis. [ISG Crohn consensus 2015]`,`**Ascites** usually resolves in weeks; imaging residual nodes or mild thickening at 6 months **does not mean treatment failure** and does not justify extending ATT. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Post-treatment follow-up** at 6, 12, 18 and 24 months for relapse symptoms, as per NTEP. [NTEP 2021]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and NTEP links`,points:[`**TB Mukt Bharat**: NTEP (National TB Elimination Programme, 2020) aims to end TB in India; detection, treatment, prevention and building blocks ('Detect-Treat-Prevent-Build'). [NSP 2017-25]`,`**TB preventive treatment (TPT)** for household contacts and people with HIV: **3HP (weekly isoniazid-rifapentine for 3 months)**, 6H or 4R after ruling out active TB. [NTEP TPT 2021]`,`**Contact screening** of household members of every TB patient, and **pasteurisation or boiling of milk** to prevent M. bovis infection. [NTEP 2021]`,`**Counselling**: complete 6 months even when symptoms resolve; report jaundice, vomiting, rash, visual blurring; rifampicin makes urine orange and **reduces efficacy of oral contraceptives** (use barrier or IUCD). [NTEP 2021]`,`**Social support**: Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana, Ni-kshay Mitra (Pradhan Mantri TB Mukt Bharat Abhiyaan), free diagnostics and drugs in the private sector through patient provider support agencies. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Screen for diabetes, HIV, alcohol and tobacco use** and link to NP-NCD and de-addiction services. [NTEP 2021]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2021-2026)`,points:[`**NTEP Guidelines for Programmatic Management of TB (2021)** and training modules (2023) adopted **daily FDC weight-band dosing**, universal drug susceptibility testing and 4-weekly follow-up for all TB including EPTB. [NTEP 2021]`,`**Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana** support was **raised from Rs 500 to Rs 1,000 per month** in November 2024, with an energy-dense nutrition supplement for underweight patients. [NTEP 2024]`,`**NTEP 2024** introduced the **6-month BPaLM regimen** for MDR/RR-TB, replacing longer regimens for eligible patients. [NTEP 2024]`,`**WHO 2021-2024** endorsed **Truenat and Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra** as initial tests for EPTB samples; Ultra has better sensitivity in paucibacillary fluids. [WHO 2024]`,`**WHO 2022** 4-month regimen for non-severe childhood TB excludes abdominal TB; the **4-month HPMZ regimen** for adult pulmonary TB is not used for EPTB. [WHO 2022]`,`The **100-day intensified TB Mukt Bharat campaign (Dec 2024 - Mar 2025)** expanded active case finding in vulnerable groups using handheld X-ray and NAAT. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**India TB Report 2024** reported record notification of about **25.5 lakh cases in 2023**, with growing private-sector notification. [India TB Report 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Viva**: 'Why are TB ulcers transverse?' - they follow the **circumferential lymphatics**; Crohn ulcers are longitudinal. [Robbins 10e]`,`**Viva**: 'Commonest site of intestinal TB?' - **ileocaecal region (about 75%)**, because of lymphoid tissue, stasis and absorption. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Viva**: 'Ascitic ADA cut-off?' - **39 IU/L or more** (30-40 used); falsely low in cirrhosis and HIV. [Riquelme meta-analysis 2006]`,`**Common mistake**: extending ATT to 9-12 months for abdominal TB - **6 months is adequate**. [Makharia 2015]`,`**Common mistake**: starting a 'therapeutic ATT trial' without trying for tissue diagnosis, or starting steroids/anti-TNF for Crohn without excluding TB. [ISG Crohn consensus 2015]`,`**Common mistake**: excluding TB because the chest X-ray, Mantoux or ascitic CBNAAT is negative. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Theory favourites**: 'Ileocaecal TB', 'Differentiate intestinal TB from Crohn disease', 'Approach to tubercular ascites'. [DNB FM papers]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for drug-sensitive abdominal TB (NTEP)`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Isoniazid (H)`,`5 mg/kg (max 300 mg) orally daily, in FDC`,`6 months (2 intensive + 4 continuation)`,`Hepatitis, peripheral neuropathy (give pyridoxine), lupus-like syndrome`],[`Rifampicin (R)`,`10 mg/kg (max 600 mg) orally daily, in FDC`,`6 months`,`Hepatitis, orange body fluids, flu-like illness, thrombocytopenia; many interactions (OCP, warfarin, DTG, sulfonylureas)`],[`Pyrazinamide (Z)`,`25 mg/kg orally daily, in FDC`,`First 2 months only`,`Hepatitis, hyperuricaemia and gout, arthralgia; thrice weekly if CrCl under 30`],[`Ethambutol (E)`,`15 mg/kg orally daily, in FDC`,`6 months (continued in NTEP continuation phase)`,`Optic neuritis (red-green colour vision) - test vision; thrice weekly if CrCl under 30`],[`FDC HRZE 75/150/400/275 mg`,`Weight band: 25-34 kg 2, 35-49 kg 3, 50-64 kg 4, 65-75 kg 5, over 75 kg 6 tablets daily`,`2 months, then HRE 75/150/275 FDC same band for 4 months`,`Re-weigh monthly and change band`],[`Pyridoxine`,`10 mg orally daily (up to 25-50 mg in high-risk)`,`Throughout isoniazid`,`None at these doses`],[`Dolutegravir (in HIV on rifampicin)`,`50 mg orally twice daily`,`During and 2 weeks after rifampicin`,`Insomnia, headache`]]},{heading:`Forms of abdominal TB`,columns:[`Form`,`Subtypes`,`Typical presentation`,`Key finding`],rows:[[`Peritoneal`,`Wet (ascitic), fibrotic-fixed, dry (plastic)`,`Distension, pain, fever, doughy abdomen`,`Lymphocytic exudate, high ADA, tubercles at laparoscopy`],[`Intestinal`,`Ulcerative, hypertrophic, ulcero-hypertrophic, stricturous`,`SAIO, RIF mass, diarrhoea, perforation`,`Transverse ulcers, patulous IC valve, strictures`],[`Nodal`,`Mesenteric, peripancreatic, periportal, retroperitoneal`,`Pain, fever, mass, obstructive jaundice`,`Necrotic rim-enhancing nodes on CT`],[`Visceral`,`Hepatic, splenic, pancreatic`,`Fever of unknown origin, hepatosplenomegaly, mass`,`Micro-abscesses, tuberculoma; biopsy`]]},{heading:`Intestinal TB versus Crohn disease`,columns:[`Feature`,`Intestinal TB`,`Crohn disease`],rows:[[`Symptoms`,`Fever, night sweats, ascites, shorter history`,`Chronic diarrhoea, blood in stool, perianal disease, longer history`],[`Pulmonary TB`,`Present in about 15-25%`,`Absent`],[`Ulcers`,`Transverse, circumferential`,`Longitudinal, aphthous, cobblestoning`],[`Distribution`,`Ileocaecal, under 4 segments, patulous valve`,`Skip lesions, 4 or more segments, rectum may be involved`],[`CT`,`Necrotic nodes over 1 cm, short-segment concentric thickening, ascites`,`Comb sign, fibrofatty proliferation, long-segment asymmetric thickening`],[`Granulomas`,`Large (over 400 micrometres), confluent, caseating, submucosal`,`Small, discrete, non-caseating, few`],[`Microbiology`,`AFB, CBNAAT, culture positive in a minority`,`Negative`],[`Response to ATT`,`Clinical by 8 weeks, mucosal healing at 2-6 months`,`No mucosal healing`],[`Perianal disease and extraintestinal features`,`Rare`,`Common`]]},{heading:`Ascitic fluid: TB versus other causes`,columns:[`Parameter`,`TB peritonitis`,`Cirrhosis`,`Malignant ascites`],rows:[[`SAAG (g/dL)`,`Under 1.1`,`1.1 or more`,`Under 1.1 (unless liver metastases)`],[`Total protein (g/dL)`,`Over 2.5`,`Under 2.5`,`Over 2.5`],[`Cells`,`Over 500/mm3, lymphocytes over 70%`,`Under 250 PMN unless SBP`,`Variable, lymphocytes; malignant cells`],[`ADA (IU/L)`,`39 or more`,`Low`,`Usually low (high in lymphoma)`],[`Glucose`,`Low`,`Normal`,`Low or normal`],[`Cytology`,`Negative`,`Negative`,`Positive in about 60-90% with peritoneal carcinomatosis`]]},{heading:`Diagnostic tests and their yield`,columns:[`Test`,`Sample`,`Approximate sensitivity`,`Comment`],rows:[[`Ascitic ADA (39 IU/L or more)`,`Ascitic fluid`,`About 100% (specificity about 97%)`,`False negative in cirrhosis, HIV`],[`AFB smear`,`Ascitic fluid`,`Under 3%`,`Not useful alone`],[`Culture (MGIT)`,`Ascitic fluid or biopsy`,`20-40%`,`Takes 2-6 weeks; gives drug susceptibility`],[`CBNAAT/Truenat`,`Fluid or biopsy`,`20-60%`,`Specificity about 99%; detects rifampicin resistance`],[`Granuloma on histology`,`Colonic biopsy`,`50-80%`,`Caseation in 20-40%`],[`Laparoscopic peritoneal biopsy`,`Peritoneum`,`Over 90%`,`Gold standard for peritoneal TB`]]},{heading:`Imaging signs of abdominal TB`,columns:[`Sign`,`Modality`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`Club sandwich / sliced bread`,`Ultrasound`,`Fluid between radially arranged bowel loops - peritoneal TB`],[`Pseudokidney sign`,`Ultrasound`,`Ileocaecal wall thickening`],[`Necrotic rim-enhancing nodes`,`CECT`,`Highly suggestive of TB over lymphoma or Crohn`],[`Smooth peritoneal thickening, high-density ascites`,`CECT`,`TB; nodular thickening suggests carcinomatosis`],[`Stierlin sign`,`Barium`,`Rapid emptying of inflamed caecum`],[`Fleischner (inverted umbrella) sign`,`Barium`,`Thickened gaping IC valve with narrow terminal ileum`],[`Pulled-up conical caecum, goose-neck deformity`,`Barium or CT`,`Fibrotic ileocaecal TB`]]}],redFlags:[`**Guarding, rigidity or free gas under the diaphragm** - perforation, emergency surgery.`,`**Absolute constipation with vomiting and distension** not settling on drip and suck - complete obstruction.`,`**Jaundice, vomiting or ALT over 3 times ULN with symptoms on ATT** - stop hepatotoxic drugs, assess for DILI.`,`**Massive rectal bleeding** - colonic TB or Crohn; resuscitate and refer.`,`**No clinical response after 8 weeks of ATT** - suspect drug resistance, Crohn, lymphoma or cancer.`,`**HIV with CD4 under 100 and abdominal TB** - disseminated disease, high mortality.`,`**Visual blurring or colour vision change** on ethambutol - stop it and refer.`],pearls:[`TB ulcers are **transverse** (lymphatics), Crohn ulcers are **longitudinal**.`,`Ileocaecal TB is commonest because of **'Lymphoid tissue, Stasis, Absorption'**.`,`TB ascites: **'low SAAG, high protein, high lymphocytes, high ADA'**.`,`**Ascitic ADA 39 or more** - but low in cirrhosis and HIV.`,`**Six months of ATT is enough** for abdominal TB (2HRZE/4HRE).`,`**Negative CBNAAT does not exclude** paucibacillary abdominal TB.`,`**CA-125 is raised in TB peritonitis** - do not label ovarian cancer without tissue.`,`**Mucosal healing at 2-6 months** on ATT confirms TB over Crohn.`,`**Never start anti-TNF** without excluding latent and active TB.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-tb-abdominal-q1`,stem:`A 28-year-old woman has 2 months of fever, weight loss and abdominal distension. Ascitic fluid: SAAG 0.8 g/dL, protein 4.2 g/dL, cells 1,200/mm3 with 85% lymphocytes, cytology negative. Which single ascitic test best supports the likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Adenosine deaminase 52 IU/L`,`CA-125 elevated to 380 U/mL`,`Ascitic amylase three times serum`,`Triglycerides 320 mg/dL`],answer:0,explanation:`Low-SAAG, high-protein, lymphocyte-rich ascites with negative cytology in a young Indian woman is TB peritonitis until proven otherwise, and an ascitic ADA of 39 IU/L or more has very high sensitivity and specificity for it. CA-125 rises in any peritoneal inflammation including TB, so it does not discriminate; high amylase points to pancreatic ascites and high triglycerides to chylous ascites.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-tb-abdominal-q2`,stem:`On colonoscopy of a 32-year-old with right iliac fossa pain, which feature most favours intestinal tuberculosis over Crohn disease?`,options:[`Longitudinal ulcers with cobblestoning`,`Perianal fistula`,`Transverse ulcers with a patulous ileocaecal valve`,`Aphthous ulcers in skip segments of the colon`],answer:2,explanation:`Transverse ulcers follow the circumferential lymphatics and, with a gaping patulous ileocaecal valve, favour intestinal TB. Longitudinal ulcers, cobblestoning, aphthous skip lesions and perianal fistulae are the classic Crohn features and argue against TB.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-tb-abdominal-q3`,stem:`A 40-year-old man weighing 58 kg has biopsy-proven, rifampicin-sensitive ileocaecal TB with a short stricture causing intermittent colic. What is the appropriate initial treatment under NTEP?`,options:[`HRZE for 2 months then HRE for 7 months because of the stricture`,`HRZE 4 FDC tablets daily for 2 months then HRE for 4 months`,`Immediate right hemicolectomy followed by 6 months of ATT`,`HRZE plus prednisolone 40 mg daily to prevent stricture fibrosis`],answer:1,explanation:`Drug-sensitive abdominal TB is treated for 6 months (2HRZE/4HRE), and at 50-64 kg the dose is 4 FDC tablets daily; Indian trials showed 6 months equals 9 months. Most short strictures respond to ATT, so surgery is reserved for obstruction that fails ATT, perforation or bleeding, and steroids are not recommended to prevent strictures.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-tb-abdominal-q4`,stem:`In a patient with alcohol-related cirrhosis and new ascites with SAAG 1.3 g/dL, fever and lymphocytic fluid, the ascitic ADA is 22 IU/L. What is the best interpretation?`,options:[`TB peritonitis is excluded because ADA is below 39 IU/L`,`The high SAAG proves the ascites is purely cirrhotic`,`Start empirical ATT without further tests`,`ADA is falsely low in cirrhosis, so TB is not excluded and laparoscopic biopsy or culture is needed`],answer:3,explanation:`In cirrhosis, ADA is falsely low and SAAG stays high from portal hypertension even when TB coexists, so lymphocytic ascites with fever needs culture, CBNAAT and ideally peritoneal biopsy. Calling TB excluded or the ascites purely cirrhotic ignores these pitfalls, and empirical ATT in cirrhosis risks serious hepatotoxicity without a diagnosis.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-tb-abdominal-q5`,stem:`Which barium sign describes a thickened, gaping ileocaecal valve with a narrowed terminal ileum in ileocaecal tuberculosis?`,options:[`Stierlin sign`,`Fleischner (inverted umbrella) sign`,`Apple-core sign`,`Bird's beak sign`],answer:1,explanation:`The Fleischner or inverted-umbrella sign is the thickened, patulous ileocaecal valve with narrowed terminal ileum in ileocaecal TB. The Stierlin sign is rapid emptying of the inflamed caecum, the apple-core sign is an annular colorectal carcinoma, and the bird's beak sign is achalasia or volvulus.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-tb-abdominal-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Describe the pathology, clinical features, diagnosis and management of ileocaecal tuberculosis. How will you differentiate it from Crohn disease?`,openingLines:[`Ileocaecal tuberculosis is the commonest site (about 75%) of intestinal TB, caused by M. tuberculosis reaching the gut by swallowed sputum, haematogenous or lymphatic spread, and presenting with pain, fever, weight loss, a right iliac fossa mass or subacute obstruction.`,`Diagnosis needs tissue (histology, CBNAAT, culture) because Crohn disease mimics it; treatment is the NTEP 6-month regimen 2HRZE/4HRE, with surgery only for complications.`],answer:[{heading:`Pathology`,points:[`Predilection: lymphoid tissue, stasis, absorption.`,`Types: ulcerative (60%), hypertrophic (10%), ulcero-hypertrophic (30%).`,`Transverse ulcers along lymphatics; caseating confluent granulomas; napkin-ring strictures; pulled-up caecum, patulous valve.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Chronic pain, fever, night sweats, weight loss; SAIO with colic and borborygmi.`,`RIF mass, visible peristalsis; ascites; complications - perforation, fistula, bleeding.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`CBC, ESR, albumin, HIV, glucose; chest X-ray and sputum CBNAAT.`,`USG and CECT: concentric thickening, necrotic nodes, ascites.`,`Colonoscopy with 6-8 biopsies: histology, AFB, CBNAAT/Truenat, MGIT culture.`]},{heading:`ITB versus Crohn`,points:[`Transverse vs longitudinal ulcers; under 4 vs 4 or more segments; necrotic nodes vs comb sign.`,`Caseating confluent vs small non-caseating granulomas; perianal disease favours Crohn.`,`Mucosal healing on ATT at 2-6 months confirms TB.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Notify; 2HRZE/4HRE weight-band FDC for 6 months; pyridoxine; baseline LFT.`,`SAIO: drip and suck plus ATT; dilatation or strictureplasty for persistent strictures.`,`Surgery for perforation, complete obstruction, bleeding, abscess or fistula.`]},{heading:`Follow-up and programme`,points:[`Monthly review, adherence, DILI watch; Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana; contact screening and TPT.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of ileocaecal region showing transverse ulcers, stricture, pulled-up caecum and patulous valve`,`Comparison table: intestinal TB versus Crohn disease`],markSplit:[{part:`Pathology`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features and investigations`,marks:3},{part:`Differentiation from Crohn disease`,marks:2},{part:`Management including surgery`,marks:3}],keywords:[`ileocaecal TB`,`transverse ulcers`,`Crohn`,`CBNAAT`,`2HRZE/4HRE`,`SAIO`,`stricture`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-tb-abdominal-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on the diagnosis of tubercular peritonitis.`,openingLines:[`Tubercular peritonitis is the commonest form of abdominal TB, usually from haematogenous reactivation, presenting with fever, weight loss and exudative ascites.`,`Diagnosis combines ascitic fluid analysis (low SAAG, high protein, lymphocytes, ADA 39 IU/L or more), imaging and, when doubt remains, laparoscopic peritoneal biopsy.`],answer:[{heading:`Ascitic fluid`,points:[`SAAG under 1.1, protein over 2.5 g/dL, cells over 500 with lymphocytes over 70%, low glucose.`,`ADA 39 IU/L or more - false low in cirrhosis and HIV.`,`AFB smear under 3%, culture 20-35%, CBNAAT low sensitivity - negative does not exclude.`]},{heading:`Imaging`,points:[`USG: septated ascites, club sandwich sign, necrotic nodes.`,`CECT: high-density ascites, smooth peritoneal enhancement, omental thickening.`]},{heading:`Tissue`,points:[`Laparoscopy: tubercles, adhesions; biopsy shows caseating granulomas - over 90% accurate.`,`CA-125 is raised in TB too - do not misdiagnose ovarian cancer.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table of ascitic fluid findings in TB, cirrhosis and malignancy`],markSplit:[{part:`Ascitic fluid analysis`,marks:2},{part:`Imaging`,marks:1},{part:`Laparoscopy and pitfalls`,marks:2}],keywords:[`ADA`,`SAAG`,`laparoscopy`,`CBNAAT`,`CA-125`,`lymphocytic ascites`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Tuberculosis chapter`,`Bailey and Love's Short Practice of Surgery, 28th edition, 2023 - Abdominal tuberculosis`,`Index-TB Guidelines: Guidelines on extra-pulmonary tuberculosis for India, ICMR and Central TB Division, 2016`,`National TB Elimination Programme. Guidelines for Programmatic Management of Tuberculosis in India, 2021, and NTEP training modules 2023`,`India TB Report 2024, Central TB Division, MoHFW; WHO Global Tuberculosis Report 2024`,`Makharia GK et al. Six months versus nine months of anti-tubercular therapy for abdominal tuberculosis: a randomized controlled trial. Gastroenterology 2015`,`Indian Society of Gastroenterology consensus statements on Crohn's disease in India, Indian Journal of Gastroenterology 2015`,`Riquelme A et al. Value of adenosine deaminase in ascitic fluid for the diagnosis of tuberculous peritonitis: a meta-analysis. J Clin Gastroenterol 2006`]}),c.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-ascites-approach`,title:`Approach to ascites (non-cirrhotic causes)`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Ascites** is pathological accumulation of fluid in the peritoneal cavity; every new ascites needs a **diagnostic tap**, and the **serum-ascites albumin gradient (SAAG 1.1 g/dL or more = portal hypertension)** plus **ascitic total protein (2.5 g/dL cut-off)** sort it into **cirrhotic, cardiac/post-sinusoidal (Budd-Chiari), and non-portal-hypertensive causes (malignancy, TB, pancreatic, nephrotic, chylous)**, with targeted tests (**ADA, cytology, amylase, triglycerides, BNP, Doppler**) to confirm the cause and treat it.`,keywords:[`ascites`,`SAAG`,`serum-ascites albumin gradient`,`ascitic total protein`,`diagnostic paracentesis`,`malignant ascites`,`peritoneal carcinomatosis`,`tubercular ascites`,`ADA`,`cardiac ascites`,`constrictive pericarditis`,`nephrotic ascites`,`chylous ascites`,`pancreatic ascites`,`Budd-Chiari syndrome`,`hepatic venous outflow obstruction`,`Meigs syndrome`,`pseudomyxoma peritonei`,`NT-proBNP`,`PSVD`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Ascites** is a pathological collection of fluid in the peritoneal cavity; clinical detection by **flank dullness needs about 1,500 mL**, whereas **ultrasound detects as little as 100 mL**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cirrhosis causes about 80-85%** of ascites in Western series; the rest are **malignancy (about 10%), heart failure (about 3%), tuberculosis (about 2%)**, and pancreatic, nephrotic, chylous and other causes. [AASLD 2021]`,`**In India, TB and malignancy are the commonest non-cirrhotic causes**, and TB contributes a larger share than in the West; Budd-Chiari syndrome is also relatively more frequent in Asia. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`About **5% of patients with ascites have two causes** ('mixed ascites') - typically cirrhosis plus TB, malignancy or heart failure - so one abnormal result does not end the work-up. [AASLD 2021]`,`**International Ascites Club grading**: grade 1 (mild, ultrasound only), grade 2 (moderate, symmetrical distension), grade 3 (large or tense, marked distension). [EASL 2018]`,`New-onset ascites carries a poor prognosis whatever the cause: in cirrhosis, **about 15% die within 1 year and 44% within 5 years** of its onset; malignant ascites from peritoneal carcinomatosis has a median survival of weeks to months (longer for ovarian cancer). [EASL 2018]`]},{heading:`Aetiology: causes grouped by mechanism`,points:[`**Portal hypertension, sinusoidal**: cirrhosis, alcoholic hepatitis, massive liver metastases, acute liver failure, acute fatty liver of pregnancy - see the Chronic liver disease topic for cirrhotic ascites. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Portal hypertension, post-sinusoidal**: **heart failure, constrictive pericarditis, severe tricuspid regurgitation, Budd-Chiari syndrome and sinusoidal obstruction syndrome (SOS/VOD)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pre-sinusoidal (portal vein thrombosis, EHPVO, PSVD)** rarely causes ascites unless the thrombosis is acute, there is coexisting liver disease, or the patient has an intercurrent illness. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Peritoneal disease (low SAAG)**: **peritoneal carcinomatosis** (ovary, stomach, pancreas, colon, breast), **mesothelioma**, **TB peritonitis**, fungal peritonitis, SLE serositis, eosinophilic gastroenteritis, pseudomyxoma peritonei. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypoalbuminaemia**: **nephrotic syndrome**, protein-losing enteropathy, severe malnutrition (kwashiorkor). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Leak of a body fluid**: **pancreatic** (duct disruption), **biliary** (bile leak after cholecystectomy), **chylous** (lymphatic obstruction or injury), **urinary** (bladder rupture). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Miscellaneous**: **myxoedema**, **Meigs syndrome** (benign ovarian fibroma with ascites and right hydrothorax), ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome, peritoneal dialysis, filariasis (chylous). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology: why fluid collects and what SAAG measures`,points:[`**Starling forces** govern peritoneal fluid: high **hydrostatic pressure** in sinusoids and splanchnic capillaries pushes fluid out, while plasma **oncotic pressure** (mainly albumin) holds it in. [Harrison 22e]`,`**SAAG = serum albumin minus ascitic albumin** (same day); it is a surrogate of the **oncotic-hydrostatic balance**, so a high gradient (**1.1 g/dL or more**) means raised portal (sinusoidal) pressure with about **97% accuracy**. [Runyon 1992]`,`**Low SAAG (under 1.1 g/dL)** means the fluid is not from portal hypertension but from **leaky inflamed or malignant peritoneum, or a leaking duct** - so albumin-rich fluid sits at nearly serum concentration. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Ascitic total protein** reflects sinusoidal permeability: normal **hepatic sinusoids are leaky** (fenestrated), so post-sinusoidal congestion (heart failure, early Budd-Chiari) gives **high-protein (2.5 g/dL or more)** fluid, while **cirrhotic sinusoids are 'capillarised'** and give low-protein fluid. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cardiac ascites**: raised right atrial pressure is transmitted to hepatic veins and sinusoids, giving **high SAAG plus high protein** - the same pattern as Budd-Chiari. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Constrictive pericarditis** produces **ascites out of proportion to leg oedema ('ascites praecox')** because hepatic congestion appears early and lymphatic drainage of the liver is overwhelmed. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Budd-Chiari**: obstruction of hepatic veins or the suprahepatic IVC causes sinusoidal congestion, centrilobular necrosis and portal hypertension; the **caudate lobe hypertrophies** because it drains directly into the IVC through its own veins. [EASL 2016]`,`**Malignant ascites** forms through **tumour-induced capillary leak (VEGF)** and lymphatic obstruction by peritoneal deposits - which is why diuretics rarely help unless liver metastases cause portal hypertension. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chylous ascites** follows disruption or obstruction of lymphatics carrying chylomicron-rich lymph (lymphoma, surgery, filariasis); **pancreatic ascites** follows a **pancreatic duct leak** into the peritoneum. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification by SAAG and protein (the one examiners ask)`,points:[`**High SAAG (1.1 or more) + low protein (under 2.5 g/dL)**: **cirrhosis**, late Budd-Chiari, massive liver metastases, acute liver failure. [AASLD 2021]`,`**High SAAG + high protein (2.5 g/dL or more)**: **heart failure, constrictive pericarditis, early Budd-Chiari, SOS**, myxoedema. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Low SAAG (under 1.1) + high protein**: **peritoneal carcinomatosis, TB peritonitis, pancreatic ascites, biliary ascites, serositis**, secondary bacterial peritonitis. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Low SAAG + low protein**: **nephrotic syndrome**, protein-losing enteropathy, severe malnutrition. [AASLD 2021]`,`**SAAG has replaced the old transudate-exudate (protein 2.5 g/dL) classification**, which was right only about 55-60% of the time; protein is still useful as a second axis. [Runyon 1992]`,`**Caveats**: SAAG may be falsely low if serum albumin is under 1.1 g/dL, and slightly falsely high in hyperglobulinaemia (over 5 g/dL) or with chylous fluid; take serum and ascitic samples on the same day. [AASLD 2021]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Abdominal distension and weight gain** with tight clothes; breathlessness and early satiety in tense ascites. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Liver clues**: alcohol, hepatitis B/C risk, jaundice, haematemesis, confusion - pointing to cirrhosis. [EASL 2018]`,`**Cardiac clues**: exertional breathlessness, orthopnoea, PND, past rheumatic fever, prior pericarditis or TB (constrictive), known heart failure. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Malignancy clues**: weight loss, anorexia, change in bowel habit, early satiety, **post-menopausal bleeding or pelvic pain**, breast lump, previous cancer surgery. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TB clues**: evening fever, night sweats, weight loss, TB contact, HIV, diabetes - see the Abdominal TB topic. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Nephrotic clues**: **frothy urine, periorbital puffiness on waking**, generalised oedema. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Budd-Chiari clues**: **abdominal pain, hepatomegaly and ascites** (the triad), especially in young women on **oral contraceptives, in pregnancy or post-partum**, or with a known prothrombotic state. [EASL 2016]`,`**Other clues**: recent **abdominal surgery or trauma** (chylous, biliary, urinary), alcohol with recurrent pancreatitis (pancreatic ascites), residence in a **filarial-endemic area** (chylous), cold intolerance (myxoedema). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings and their meaning`,points:[`**Confirm ascites**: **flank dullness** (most sensitive, over 80%), **shifting dullness** (most useful, about 83% sensitive), fluid thrill only in tense ascites, and the **puddle sign** is obsolete. [JAMA Rational Clinical Exam 1992]`,`**Raised JVP is the single most discriminating sign** - it means cardiac or pericardial ascites; look for **Kussmaul sign and pericardial knock** (constrictive), and **pulsatile liver with giant v waves** (tricuspid regurgitation). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Stigmata of chronic liver disease** (spider naevi, palmar erythema, gynaecomastia, splenomegaly) suggest cirrhosis; their absence with ascites should prompt a search for other causes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tender smooth hepatomegaly with ascites** suggests Budd-Chiari or congestive hepatopathy; **dilated veins over the back and flanks with upward flow below the umbilicus** suggest IVC obstruction. [EASL 2016]`,`**Malignancy signs**: **Virchow node (left supraclavicular)**, **Sister Mary Joseph nodule (umbilical)**, **Blumer shelf on rectal examination**, Krukenberg ovarian masses, breast lump, hard nodular liver, omental cake. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Anasarca with facial puffiness** suggests nephrotic syndrome; **pleural effusion on the right with a pelvic mass** suggests Meigs syndrome. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Signs of infection**: fever, abdominal tenderness and encephalopathy suggest SBP (cirrhosis) or secondary peritonitis. [AASLD 2021]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis - discriminating features`,points:[`**Cirrhosis vs cardiac ascites**: both have high SAAG; **cardiac has protein 2.5 g/dL or more, raised JVP and raised BNP** - serum BNP over about **364 pg/mL** strongly favours heart failure. [Farias 2014]`,`**TB vs malignancy**: both low SAAG, high protein, lymphocytic; **ADA 39 IU/L or more** favours TB, **positive cytology and nodular peritoneum** favour malignancy; **CA-125 is raised in both** and does not discriminate. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Pancreatic ascites**: ascitic **amylase over 1,000 IU/L** (and much higher than serum), high protein, history of chronic alcohol-related pancreatitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chylous ascites**: milky fluid with **triglycerides over 200 mg/dL**; **pseudochylous** (turbid from cells or cholesterol) has low triglycerides. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Biliary ascites**: green-brown fluid with **ascitic bilirubin greater than serum** (typically over 6 mg/dL); **urinary ascites**: ascitic creatinine greater than serum. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Secondary bacterial peritonitis vs SBP**: suspect secondary (perforation or abscess) if **2 of 3 Runyon criteria** - protein over 1 g/dL, glucose under 50 mg/dL, LDH above the serum upper limit - or polymicrobial culture; ascitic **CEA over 5 ng/mL or ALP over 240 U/L** suggests perforation. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Mimics of ascites**: obesity, massive ovarian cyst (central dullness, resonant flanks), distended bladder, pregnancy, gaseous distension - ultrasound settles it. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line in OPD or district hospital`,points:[`**Diagnostic paracentesis in every new ascites** and every admitted patient with ascites; it is safe even with raised INR or low platelets - **routine FFP or platelets are not needed**. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Site**: left lower quadrant, about **3 cm (2 finger-breadths) medial and cephalad to the anterior superior iliac spine**, avoiding the inferior epigastric vessels and scars; ultrasound guidance if available. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Routine tests on fluid**: **cell count with differential, albumin (for SAAG) and total protein**; inoculate **10 mL into each of 2 blood-culture bottles at the bedside** if infection is possible. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Optional tests by suspicion**: glucose, LDH, amylase, Gram stain; **unusual tests**: ADA, AFB/CBNAAT and culture, **cytology (send 50 mL or more, fresh)**, triglycerides, bilirubin, creatinine. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Interpreting cells**: **neutrophils 250/mm3 or more** = SBP (in cirrhosis) or secondary peritonitis; **lymphocyte-predominant** high counts = TB, malignancy or lymphoma; blood-stained fluid suggests malignancy, trauma or TB. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Blood tests**: LFT, INR, albumin, creatinine, electrolytes, **urine protein (dipstick and ACR/PCR)**, TSH, HBsAg and anti-HCV, CBC, glucose, HIV. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Ultrasound with Doppler**: liver size and texture, spleen, **hepatic veins, IVC and portal vein patency**, peritoneal or omental nodules, ovaries, nodes; **ECG and chest X-ray** for cardiac or pleural clues. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Cytology sensitivity**: about **96% for peritoneal carcinomatosis when 3 samples are processed promptly**, but much lower overall (about 58-75%) because liver metastases and HCC without peritoneal deposits are cytology-negative. [Runyon 1988]`]},{heading:`Investigations - advanced and tertiary`,points:[`**Echocardiography** for suspected cardiac ascites: RV function, TR, pericardial thickening, **septal bounce and respiratory variation** (constriction); cardiac MRI or CT for pericardial thickness and catheter study if needed. [ESC 2025]`,`**CECT abdomen and pelvis**: peritoneal nodules, omental cake, primary tumour, necrotic nodes (TB), pancreatic duct disruption, lymphatic mass; **CA-125** is useful only with an adnexal mass or for ovarian monitoring. [ESMO 2023]`,`**Doppler, CT or MR venography for Budd-Chiari**: absent or reversed hepatic venous flow, **spider-web collaterals**, IVC web, caudate hypertrophy; then a **thrombophilia work-up** - JAK2 V617F (and CALR if negative), PNH flow cytometry, antiphospholipid antibodies, protein C/S, antithrombin, factor V Leiden, homocysteine. [EASL 2016]`,`**Upper GI endoscopy** for varices if SAAG is high; **mammography and gynaecology review** in women with malignant-pattern fluid. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Laparoscopy with peritoneal biopsy** for unexplained low-SAAG ascites after fluid tests - the diagnostic gold standard for TB and peritoneal carcinomatosis. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Liver biopsy (transjugular, with HVPG)** when the cause of high-SAAG ascites is unclear (non-cirrhotic portal hypertension, PSVD, SOS); **transient elastography is unreliable in ascites** and falsely high in congestion. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**Lymphangiography or lymphoscintigraphy** localises chylous leaks; **ERCP or MRCP** shows pancreatic duct disruption or bile leak, and can treat it. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - by cause`,points:[`**General rule**: diuretics and salt restriction work **only for high-SAAG (portal hypertensive) ascites**; low-SAAG ascites is treated by treating the cause and by therapeutic paracentesis. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Cardiac ascites**: treat heart failure - **loop diuretic (furosemide 40-80 mg/day or torsemide 10-20 mg/day orally, titrated)**, guideline-directed medical therapy, and **pericardiectomy for chronic constrictive pericarditis**. [ESC 2023]`,`**Nephrotic ascites**: treat the glomerular disease (steroids in minimal change), **ACE inhibitor or ARB** for proteinuria, loop diuretic with salt restriction, and consider anticoagulation if albumin is very low (membranous). [KDIGO 2021]`,`**TB peritonitis**: **NTEP 6-month regimen (2HRZE/4HRE)**; ascites usually resolves within weeks; see the Abdominal TB topic. [NTEP 2021]`,`**Malignant ascites**: treat the primary where possible (**ovarian cancer - cytoreduction and chemotherapy**); for palliation, **therapeutic paracentesis** as needed and an **indwelling tunnelled peritoneal catheter** for frequent recurrence; diuretics help only if liver metastases raise SAAG. [ESMO 2023]`,`**Chylous ascites**: **low-fat, high-protein diet with medium-chain triglycerides** (absorbed directly into the portal vein, bypassing lymphatics), **octreotide 100 micrograms SC three times daily** for refractory cases, treat cause (lymphoma, TB); **filarial chylous ascites - diethylcarbamazine 6 mg/kg/day for 12 days**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Myxoedema ascites** resolves with **levothyroxine**; **Meigs syndrome** resolves after removal of the ovarian tumour. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Budd-Chiari (outpatient continuation)**: **lifelong anticoagulation** - LMWH then warfarin (INR 2-3); DOACs are increasingly used but are avoided in antiphospholipid syndrome; treat the underlying myeloproliferative neoplasm (aspirin, hydroxyurea) with haematology. [AASLD 2020]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency`,points:[`**Admit**: tense ascites with breathlessness, fever or abdominal pain (possible infected ascites), encephalopathy, AKI, hyponatraemia under 125 mmol/L, GI bleeding, suspected Budd-Chiari or new ascites with weight loss for urgent work-up. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Therapeutic paracentesis**: remove ascites to relieve symptoms; in cirrhosis, give **albumin 6-8 g per litre removed when over 5 L** is drained - in malignant ascites albumin is not routinely needed. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Acute Budd-Chiari stepwise therapy**: **anticoagulation immediately**, then **angioplasty or stenting of short-segment hepatic vein or IVC stenosis** (common in Indian patients with membranous IVC obstruction), then **TIPS** if no improvement, then **liver transplantation** for fulminant or failed cases. [EASL 2016]`,`**Pancreatic ascites**: bowel rest with **nasojejunal feeding or PN**, **octreotide**, repeated paracentesis, and **ERCP with pancreatic duct stenting** to bridge the leak; surgery if it fails. [ACG 2024]`,`**Biliary ascites**: drain, antibiotics, **ERCP with biliary stent or sphincterotomy** to divert bile; surgery for major duct injury. [ESGE 2019]`,`**Secondary bacterial peritonitis**: broad-spectrum antibiotics covering anaerobes and **urgent CT and surgical review** - unlike SBP, it needs source control. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Constrictive pericarditis or cardiac tamponade**: urgent cardiology; paracentesis alone will reaccumulate. [ESC 2025]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: ascites suggests **acute fatty liver of pregnancy, pre-eclampsia/HELLP, Budd-Chiari (prothrombotic state)** or ovarian hyperstimulation after IVF; LMWH is the anticoagulant of choice - warfarin is avoided. [EASL 2023]`,`**Children**: commonest causes are **nephrotic syndrome**, TB, chylous (congenital lymphatic) and urinary ascites in neonates with posterior urethral valves. [Nelson 22e]`,`**Elderly**: malignancy (ovarian, gastric, colorectal, pancreatic) and heart failure dominate - cytology, CT and echocardiography early. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CKD and dialysis**: 'nephrogenic ascites' in haemodialysis patients and peritoneal dialysis fluid; exclude PD peritonitis (cloudy effluent, over 100 white cells/mm3 with over 50% neutrophils). [ISPD 2022]`,`**HIV**: TB, lymphoma, Kaposi sarcoma and fungal peritonitis; ADA may be falsely low with low CD4 counts. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: a bedside tap with **total protein, albumin (SAAG) and cell count** plus ultrasound and ADA answers most questions; refer for laparoscopy only when these fail. [INDEX-TB 2016]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Tense ascites**: dyspnoea, umbilical hernia (rupture risk), pleural effusion (hepatic hydrothorax in cirrhosis), malnutrition from early satiety. [EASL 2018]`,`**Infection**: SBP in cirrhosis, secondary peritonitis after perforation, and PD peritonitis. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Paracentesis complications** are rare (under 1%): abdominal wall haematoma, persistent leak, bowel perforation, and **post-paracentesis circulatory dysfunction** after large volumes in cirrhosis. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Budd-Chiari**: with stepwise therapy, **5-year survival is about 80-90%**; complications include variceal bleeding, HCC (especially with IVC obstruction) and progression to cirrhosis. [EASL 2016]`,`**Chylous ascites** leads to malnutrition, lymphopenia and immunodeficiency if the leak continues. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Malignant ascites** signals advanced disease: median survival is about **1-4 months** for most GI primaries, longer for ovarian cancer and lymphoma. [ESMO 2023]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and prevention`,points:[`**Refer to gastroenterology/hepatology** for unexplained ascites after the first tap, suspected Budd-Chiari or PSVD, and any high-SAAG ascites without obvious cirrhosis. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Refer to cardiology** for raised JVP, high-protein high-SAAG fluid or raised BNP; **to gynaecological oncology** for malignant cytology with adnexal mass or raised CA-125. [ESMO 2023]`,`**Monitor on diuretics**: weight (target loss **0.5 kg/day without oedema, 1 kg/day with oedema**), sodium, potassium and creatinine at 1 week then monthly. [EASL 2018]`,`**Budd-Chiari**: INR monthly on warfarin, Doppler every 6 months for stent patency, and **HCC surveillance with ultrasound and AFP every 6 months**. [AASLD 2020]`,`**Re-tap** if ascites recurs unexpectedly, or with fever, pain or encephalopathy; repeat cytology up to 3 times if malignancy is suspected. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Palliative care involvement** early for malignant ascites - drainage plans, symptom control and home catheter care. [ESMO 2023]`,`**Counsel on salt** (about 5 g/day) only for high-SAAG ascites; avoid NSAIDs, which blunt diuretic response and cause AKI. [EASL 2018]`,`**Women on combined oral contraceptives** with prothrombotic risk factors should be switched to non-oestrogen methods; Budd-Chiari survivors must avoid oestrogen. [EASL 2016]`,`**NP-NCD screening** for oral, breast and cervical cancer and for hypertension and diabetes at **Ayushman Arogya Mandir** helps catch cancers and heart disease that cause ascites. [NP-NCD 2023]`,`**TB detection** under NTEP, and **Mass Drug Administration** under the National Filaria Elimination programme to prevent filarial lymphatic disease. [NCVBDC 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2020-2026)`,points:[`**AASLD 2021 guidance on ascites, SBP and HRS** reaffirmed SAAG 1.1 g/dL, diagnostic tap in all admitted patients, no routine FFP before paracentesis, and albumin 6-8 g/L for taps over 5 L in cirrhosis. [AASLD 2021]`,`**BSG 2021 (Aithal et al.) guideline on ascites in cirrhosis** recommends tunnelled peritoneal catheters only in palliative settings and long-term albumin is not routinely advised. [BSG 2021]`,`**AASLD 2020 practice guidance on vascular liver disorders** endorses the **stepwise anticoagulation-angioplasty-TIPS-transplant** approach for Budd-Chiari and allows DOACs in selected patients without APS. [AASLD 2020]`,`**Baveno VII (2022)** formalised **porto-sinusoidal vascular disorder (PSVD)**, replacing 'idiopathic non-cirrhotic portal hypertension', as a cause of high-SAAG ascites without cirrhosis. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**ESC 2025 myocarditis and pericarditis guideline** updated the work-up of constrictive pericarditis with multimodality imaging. [ESC 2025]`,`**ESC 2023 heart failure focused update** and **ACG 2024 acute pancreatitis guideline** shape management of cardiac and pancreatic ascites respectively. [ESC 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Viva**: 'What is SAAG and why is it better than protein?' - serum minus ascitic albumin; **1.1 g/dL or more = portal hypertension with about 97% accuracy**; protein (exudate/transudate) misclassifies cardiac ascites and SBP. [Runyon 1992]`,`**Viva**: 'High SAAG with high protein?' - **cardiac ascites, constrictive pericarditis, early Budd-Chiari**; look at the JVP and get BNP and echocardiography. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Viva**: 'Why is the caudate lobe enlarged in Budd-Chiari?' - it drains directly into the IVC and escapes the hepatic vein block. [EASL 2016]`,`**Viva**: 'Milky ascites?' - check **triglycerides over 200 mg/dL**; causes - lymphoma, TB, filariasis, surgery, cirrhosis. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Common mistake**: giving spironolactone to a patient with malignant (low-SAAG) ascites and wondering why it fails. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Common mistake**: not examining the JVP, missing constrictive pericarditis labelled as 'cryptogenic cirrhosis' - an Indian classic with TB pericarditis. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Theory favourites**: 'Approach to a patient with ascites', 'SAAG', 'Budd-Chiari syndrome', 'Chylous ascites'. [DNB FM papers]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs used in non-cirrhotic ascites`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Spironolactone (high-SAAG only)`,`100 mg orally once daily`,`Increase every 3-5 days to max 400 mg`,`Hyperkalaemia, gynaecomastia; avoid if K over 5.5 or severe CKD`],[`Furosemide`,`40 mg orally once daily (cardiac: 40-80 mg)`,`Titrate to 160 mg; IV in acute heart failure`,`Hypokalaemia, hyponatraemia, AKI, ototoxicity with rapid IV`],[`Torsemide`,`10-20 mg orally once daily`,`Titrate by weight and symptoms`,`Electrolyte depletion; better oral bioavailability than furosemide`],[`Enoxaparin (Budd-Chiari)`,`1 mg/kg SC twice daily`,`Bridge to warfarin; in pregnancy throughout`,`Bleeding, HIT; adjust in CrCl under 30`],[`Warfarin (Budd-Chiari)`,`Start 2.5-5 mg orally daily`,`Target INR 2-3, lifelong`,`Bleeding; teratogenic; many interactions`],[`Octreotide (chylous, pancreatic)`,`100 micrograms SC three times daily`,`Until leak closes, usually 2-4 weeks`,`Gallstones, hyperglycaemia, diarrhoea`],[`Diethylcarbamazine (filarial chylous)`,`6 mg/kg/day orally in divided doses`,`12 days`,`Fever, headache, Mazzotti-like reaction; avoid in onchocerciasis`],[`Human albumin 20%`,`6-8 g IV per litre drained (cirrhosis, over 5 L)`,`With each large-volume paracentesis`,`Fluid overload; not routine in malignant ascites`],[`Levothyroxine (myxoedema)`,`25-50 micrograms orally daily (elderly/cardiac: 12.5-25)`,`Increase every 4-6 weeks to TSH target`,`Angina, AF if started too high`]]},{heading:`SAAG and protein classification of ascites`,columns:[`Pattern`,`Total protein under 2.5 g/dL`,`Total protein 2.5 g/dL or more`],rows:[[`SAAG 1.1 g/dL or more (portal hypertension)`,`Cirrhosis, late Budd-Chiari, massive liver metastases, acute liver failure`,`Heart failure, constrictive pericarditis, early Budd-Chiari, SOS, myxoedema`],[`SAAG under 1.1 g/dL (no portal hypertension)`,`Nephrotic syndrome, protein-losing enteropathy, malnutrition`,`Peritoneal carcinomatosis, TB, pancreatic, biliary, serositis, secondary peritonitis`]]},{heading:`Ascitic fluid tests and cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`Cut-off`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`SAAG`,`1.1 g/dL or more`,`Portal hypertension (about 97% accurate)`],[`Total protein`,`2.5 g/dL or more`,`Post-sinusoidal (cardiac, Budd-Chiari) or peritoneal disease`],[`Neutrophils`,`250/mm3 or more`,`SBP or secondary peritonitis`],[`ADA`,`39 IU/L or more`,`TB peritonitis (false low in cirrhosis and HIV)`],[`Triglycerides`,`Over 200 mg/dL`,`Chylous ascites`],[`Amylase`,`Over 1,000 IU/L, well above serum`,`Pancreatic ascites`],[`Bilirubin`,`Over 6 mg/dL and above serum`,`Biliary leak`],[`Creatinine`,`Above serum`,`Urinary leak (bladder rupture)`],[`Glucose / LDH`,`Glucose under 50 mg/dL, LDH above serum ULN, protein over 1 g/dL (2 of 3)`,`Secondary bacterial peritonitis`],[`Cytology`,`Malignant cells`,`Peritoneal carcinomatosis (about 96% with 3 samples)`],[`Serum BNP`,`Over about 364 pg/mL`,`Cardiac ascites`]]},{heading:`Non-cirrhotic causes: clinical clues and confirmation`,columns:[`Cause`,`Clinical clue`,`Fluid pattern`,`Confirming test`],rows:[[`Heart failure / constrictive pericarditis`,`Raised JVP, Kussmaul sign, pericardial knock, ascites out of proportion to oedema`,`High SAAG, high protein`,`BNP, echocardiography, cardiac MRI`],[`Budd-Chiari syndrome`,`Pain, tender hepatomegaly, ascites; OCP, pregnancy, MPN; back veins`,`High SAAG, high protein early`,`Doppler, CT/MR venography; JAK2 and thrombophilia screen`],[`Peritoneal carcinomatosis`,`Weight loss, Virchow node, Sister Mary Joseph nodule, pelvic mass`,`Low SAAG, high protein, lymphocytes, malignant cells`,`Cytology x3, CECT, laparoscopy`],[`TB peritonitis`,`Fever, night sweats, doughy abdomen, young`,`Low SAAG, high protein, lymphocytes, ADA 39+`,`ADA, CBNAAT, culture, laparoscopic biopsy`],[`Nephrotic syndrome`,`Facial puffiness, frothy urine, anasarca`,`Low SAAG, low protein`,`Urine PCR over 3.5 g/day equivalent, serum albumin`],[`Pancreatic ascites`,`Alcohol, chronic pancreatitis, little pain`,`Low SAAG, high protein, amylase over 1,000`,`CECT, MRCP or ERCP`],[`Chylous ascites`,`Milky fluid, lymphoma, surgery, filarial area`,`Triglycerides over 200 mg/dL`,`CT, lymphangiography, filarial antigen test`],[`Myxoedema`,`Cold intolerance, bradycardia, dry skin`,`High protein, variable SAAG`,`TSH, free T4`]]},{heading:`Budd-Chiari syndrome at a glance`,columns:[`Aspect`,`Key points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Hepatic venous outflow obstruction from small hepatic veins to IVC-right atrial junction, excluding cardiac causes and SOS`],[`Asian pattern`,`IVC membranous obstruction (obliterative hepatocavopathy), chronic course, less often MPN`],[`Western pattern`,`Hepatic vein thrombosis with MPN (JAK2 V617F), PNH, APS, OCP, pregnancy`],[`Presentation`,`Pain, hepatomegaly, ascites; acute, subacute or chronic; fulminant rare`],[`Imaging`,`Doppler first; spider-web collaterals, caudate hypertrophy, IVC web`],[`Treatment ladder`,`Anticoagulation, then angioplasty/stent, then TIPS, then liver transplant`],[`Follow-up`,`Lifelong anticoagulation; HCC surveillance 6-monthly; avoid oestrogen`]]}],redFlags:[`**Fever, abdominal pain or new confusion with ascites** - infected ascites (SBP or secondary peritonitis); tap today.`,`**Tense ascites with breathlessness** or umbilical hernia skin breakdown - admit for therapeutic paracentesis.`,`**Acute pain, tender hepatomegaly and rapidly accumulating ascites** in a young woman, pregnant or on OCP - suspect acute Budd-Chiari.`,`**Raised JVP with ascites and hypotension** - constrictive pericarditis or tamponade.`,`**Weight loss with ascites and a pelvic or abdominal mass, Virchow node or umbilical nodule** - suspected malignancy, urgent work-up.`,`**Polymicrobial culture or ascitic glucose under 50 mg/dL with LDH above serum** - secondary peritonitis, needs CT and surgery.`,`**Sodium under 125 mmol/L or rising creatinine on diuretics** - stop diuretics, admit.`],pearls:[`**Tap every new ascites** - and never give FFP just to tap.`,`**SAAG 1.1 or more = portal hypertension**; then protein 2.5 sorts liver from heart.`,`**High SAAG + high protein** = 'Heart, Hepatic veins (Budd-Chiari), Hypothyroid'.`,`**Low SAAG + high protein** = 'TB, Tumour, Pancreas, Bile'.`,`**Low SAAG + low protein** = nephrotic syndrome.`,`**Look at the JVP** in every ascites - constrictive pericarditis hides as 'cirrhosis'.`,`**Caudate lobe hypertrophy** = Budd-Chiari (it drains straight into the IVC).`,`**Diuretics fail in low-SAAG ascites** - treat the cause.`,`**Milky fluid: measure triglycerides** - over 200 mg/dL is chylous.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-ascites-approach-q1`,stem:`A 45-year-old man has ascites and pedal oedema. JVP is raised to the angle of the jaw and rises on inspiration. Ascitic fluid: SAAG 1.6 g/dL, total protein 3.4 g/dL, cells 150/mm3. What is the most likely cause?`,options:[`Constrictive pericarditis`,`Decompensated alcohol-related cirrhosis`,`Tuberculous peritonitis`,`Nephrotic syndrome`],answer:0,explanation:`High SAAG with high protein means post-sinusoidal portal hypertension with intact leaky sinusoids, and a raised JVP with Kussmaul sign points to constrictive pericarditis. Cirrhosis gives high SAAG with low protein, TB peritonitis gives low SAAG with a lymphocytic high cell count, and nephrotic syndrome gives low SAAG with low protein.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-ascites-approach-q2`,stem:`A 26-year-old woman, 6 weeks post-partum, develops right upper quadrant pain, tender hepatomegaly and rapidly increasing ascites with high SAAG. CT shows an enlarged caudate lobe. Which first-line imaging test confirms the likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Upper GI endoscopy`,`Doppler ultrasound of the hepatic veins and IVC`,`Liver biopsy`,`HIDA scan`],answer:1,explanation:`Pain, tender hepatomegaly and ascites in a prothrombotic post-partum state with caudate hypertrophy suggest Budd-Chiari syndrome, and Doppler ultrasound of hepatic veins and IVC is the first-line confirmatory test. Endoscopy only looks for varices, liver biopsy is rarely needed once imaging is diagnostic, and a HIDA scan tests gallbladder function.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-ascites-approach-q3`,stem:`Ascitic fluid in a patient with ascites after a road traffic accident looks milky. Which result confirms chylous ascites?`,options:[`Ascitic amylase 2,500 IU/L`,`Ascitic creatinine higher than serum creatinine`,`Ascitic bilirubin 8 mg/dL`,`Ascitic triglycerides 450 mg/dL`],answer:3,explanation:`Chylous ascites is defined by ascitic triglycerides over 200 mg/dL from lymphatic disruption, which trauma can cause. A very high amylase indicates pancreatic duct leak, ascitic creatinine above serum indicates bladder rupture, and a high ascitic bilirubin indicates a bile leak - each is a different traumatic body-fluid ascites.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-ascites-approach-q4`,stem:`A cirrhotic patient's ascitic fluid shows 3,200 neutrophils/mm3, total protein 2.3 g/dL, glucose 32 mg/dL, LDH above the serum upper limit, and Gram stain with several organisms. What is the best next step?`,options:[`Cefotaxime alone and repeat tap at 48 hours`,`Intravenous albumin and norfloxacin prophylaxis`,`Urgent CT abdomen and surgical review with broad-spectrum antibiotics including anaerobic cover`,`Start spironolactone and furosemide`],answer:2,explanation:`Two or more Runyon criteria (protein over 1 g/dL, glucose under 50 mg/dL, LDH above serum ULN) and polymicrobial Gram stain suggest secondary bacterial peritonitis from perforation or abscess, which needs CT and surgical source control with anaerobic cover. Cefotaxime alone is treatment for spontaneous bacterial peritonitis, norfloxacin is prophylaxis, and diuretics do nothing for infection.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-ascites-approach-q5`,stem:`A 60-year-old woman has ascites with SAAG 0.7 g/dL, protein 4.0 g/dL, lymphocytic fluid, ADA 18 IU/L and a raised CA-125. She has been on spironolactone 200 mg daily for 3 weeks with no response. What is the best explanation and next step?`,options:[`Diuretic resistance from cirrhosis - add furosemide`,`Low-SAAG ascites does not respond to diuretics; send repeat cytology and arrange CECT with gynaecological review`,`TB peritonitis is confirmed by the raised CA-125 - start ATT`,`Nephrotic syndrome - start prednisolone`],answer:1,explanation:`Low SAAG means no portal hypertension, so diuretics will not work; low ADA with lymphocytic high-protein fluid in an older woman points to peritoneal carcinomatosis, often ovarian, so repeat cytology, CECT and gynaecological review are needed. Cirrhosis gives high SAAG, CA-125 is raised in both TB and malignancy and confirms neither, and nephrotic ascites has low protein.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-ascites-approach-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Describe the approach to a patient presenting with ascites. Discuss the role of the serum-ascites albumin gradient and outline the evaluation of non-cirrhotic causes.`,openingLines:[`Ascites is pathological accumulation of fluid in the peritoneal cavity; cirrhosis causes about 80% but malignancy, TB, heart failure, nephrotic syndrome and Budd-Chiari make up the rest, and in India TB is a major cause.`,`Every new ascites needs a diagnostic tap; SAAG 1.1 g/dL or more indicates portal hypertension, and ascitic protein 2.5 g/dL separates hepatic from cardiac and peritoneal causes.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes by SAAG and protein`,points:[`High SAAG, low protein: cirrhosis, liver metastases, late Budd-Chiari.`,`High SAAG, high protein: heart failure, constrictive pericarditis, early Budd-Chiari.`,`Low SAAG, high protein: TB, malignancy, pancreatic, biliary; low SAAG, low protein: nephrotic.`]},{heading:`History and examination`,points:[`Alcohol, hepatitis risk, fever, weight loss, orthopnoea, frothy urine, OCP or pregnancy, surgery, filarial area.`,`JVP and Kussmaul sign, CLD stigmata, tender hepatomegaly, back veins, Virchow node, Sister Mary Joseph nodule, pelvic mass.`]},{heading:`Diagnostic paracentesis`,points:[`Left lower quadrant; no routine FFP; cell count, albumin, protein, culture in blood culture bottles.`,`By suspicion: ADA, CBNAAT, cytology (3 samples), amylase, triglycerides, bilirubin, creatinine, glucose, LDH.`]},{heading:`SAAG`,points:[`Serum albumin minus ascitic albumin, same day; 1.1 g/dL or more is about 97% accurate for portal hypertension.`,`Better than exudate/transudate; caveats - very low serum albumin, hyperglobulinaemia, mixed ascites.`]},{heading:`Evaluation of non-cirrhotic causes`,points:[`Cardiac: BNP, echocardiography; Budd-Chiari: Doppler, CT venography, JAK2 and thrombophilia screen.`,`Malignancy: cytology, CECT, CA-125 with mass, laparoscopy; TB: ADA, CBNAAT, laparoscopic biopsy.`,`Nephrotic: urine PCR, albumin; pancreatic: amylase, MRCP; chylous: triglycerides, CT, lymphangiography.`]},{heading:`Principles of management`,points:[`Diuretics and salt only for high SAAG; treat the cause otherwise.`,`Therapeutic paracentesis for tense ascites; indwelling catheter in malignant ascites.`,`Budd-Chiari: anticoagulation, angioplasty/stent, TIPS, transplant.`]}],mustDraw:[`2 x 2 grid of SAAG (high/low) against ascitic protein (high/low) with causes`,`Flow chart: new ascites, tap, SAAG, protein, targeted tests`],markSplit:[{part:`Causes and classification`,marks:2},{part:`History, examination and paracentesis`,marks:3},{part:`SAAG explained`,marks:2},{part:`Evaluation of non-cirrhotic causes and management`,marks:3}],keywords:[`SAAG`,`ascitic protein`,`paracentesis`,`ADA`,`cytology`,`Budd-Chiari`,`cardiac ascites`,`chylous`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-ascites-approach-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on Budd-Chiari syndrome.`,openingLines:[`Budd-Chiari syndrome is hepatic venous outflow obstruction anywhere from the small hepatic veins to the junction of the IVC with the right atrium, excluding cardiac causes and sinusoidal obstruction syndrome.`,`It presents with the triad of abdominal pain, hepatomegaly and high-SAAG high-protein ascites and is treated by a stepwise ladder: anticoagulation, angioplasty or stent, TIPS, transplantation.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes`,points:[`Asia: membranous IVC obstruction; West: hepatic vein thrombosis with MPN (JAK2), PNH, APS, OCP, pregnancy, factor V Leiden.`]},{heading:`Features and diagnosis`,points:[`Pain, tender hepatomegaly, ascites; back and flank veins with IVC block; caudate hypertrophy.`,`Doppler first, then CT or MR venography; thrombophilia and JAK2 screen.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`LMWH then warfarin (INR 2-3) lifelong; treat cause.`,`Angioplasty or stent for short stenosis; TIPS if no response; transplant for fulminant or failed cases.`]},{heading:`Follow-up`,points:[`Stent patency Doppler; HCC surveillance 6-monthly; avoid oestrogen; 5-year survival about 80-90%.`]}],mustDraw:[`Stepwise treatment ladder for Budd-Chiari syndrome`,`Diagram of hepatic veins, IVC and caudate lobe drainage`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and causes`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical features and diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment and follow-up`,marks:2}],keywords:[`Budd-Chiari`,`hepatic venous outflow`,`caudate lobe`,`JAK2`,`IVC web`,`TIPS`,`anticoagulation`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Abdominal swelling and ascites chapter`,`Biggins SW et al. Diagnosis, evaluation, and management of ascites, spontaneous bacterial peritonitis and hepatorenal syndrome: 2021 practice guidance by AASLD, Hepatology 2021`,`EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines for the management of patients with decompensated cirrhosis, Journal of Hepatology 2018`,`Northup PG et al. Vascular liver disorders, portal vein thrombosis and procedural bleeding in patients with liver disease: 2020 practice guidance by AASLD, Hepatology 2021`,`EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines: Vascular diseases of the liver, Journal of Hepatology 2016`,`de Franchis R et al. Baveno VII - Renewing consensus in portal hypertension, Journal of Hepatology 2022`,`Runyon BA et al. The serum-ascites albumin gradient is superior to the exudate-transudate concept in the differential diagnosis of ascites, Annals of Internal Medicine 1992`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022 - Pericardial diseases; Index-TB Guidelines, ICMR 2016`]});var l=[];l.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-drugs-ppi-safety`,title:`Prescribing in gastroenterology: PPI deprescribing, laxatives, antiemetics, antispasmodics`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`Rational GI prescribing means giving a **proton pump inhibitor (PPI) only for a named indication at the lowest effective dose and reviewing it for deprescribing** (AGA 2022), choosing a **laxative by class and mechanism** (PEG, stimulants, secretagogues, prucalopride; PAMORA for opioid-induced constipation), matching an **antiemetic to the vomiting pathway** with respect for QT and extrapyramidal limits, and using **antispasmodics as short-term symptom relief** - avoiding irrational fixed-dose combinations.`,keywords:[`proton pump inhibitor`,`PPI deprescribing`,`rebound acid hypersecretion`,`vonoprazan`,`potassium-competitive acid blocker`,`P-CAB`,`polyethylene glycol`,`lactulose`,`bisacodyl`,`senna`,`prucalopride`,`linaclotide`,`opioid-induced constipation`,`naldemedine`,`naloxegol`,`ondansetron`,`domperidone`,`metoclopramide`,`drotaverine`,`dicyclomine`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Proton pump inhibitors** are among the most prescribed drugs worldwide; in high-income countries **25-70% of PPI prescriptions have no appropriate indication**, and many continue for years after a short course was intended. [AGA 2022]`,`**Deprescribing** is the planned, supervised process of **dose reduction, on-demand use or stopping** a drug whose harm or burden may outweigh benefit - it is not simply stopping. [Canadian PPI deprescribing guideline 2017]`,`In India PPIs are sold **over the counter** and as **fixed-dose combinations (FDCs)** with domperidone, levosulpiride or antacids; pantoprazole and rabeprazole are among the top-selling molecules in the Indian pharmaceutical market. [Indian Pharmaceutical Market data]`,`Indian hospital prescription audits repeatedly find **inappropriate stress-ulcer or 'cover' PPI** in a large share of admitted non-ICU patients, often continued at discharge. [AGA 2022]`,`**Chronic constipation** affects about **14% of adults globally**; Indian community surveys (Indian Motility and Functional Diseases Association) report a similar burden, higher in women and the elderly, with straining and hard stools the commonest complaints. [AGA-ACG 2023, IMFDA]`,`**Opioid-induced constipation (OIC)** occurs in **40-80% of patients on long-term opioids**; unlike sedation and nausea, **tolerance does not develop** to it. [AGA OIC 2019]`,`**Antispasmodics** (drotaverine, dicyclomine, hyoscine butylbromide, mebeverine) and **antiemetics** (ondansetron, domperidone) are among the most frequently prescribed OPD drugs in Indian primary care, and many such prescriptions carry no working diagnosis. [WHO Guide to Good Prescribing]`]},{heading:`Indications and risk factors for inappropriate use`,points:[`**Definite long-term PPI indications**: **Barrett oesophagus**, **severe erosive oesophagitis (LA grade C/D)**, **peptic stricture**, **eosinophilic oesophagitis responding to PPI**, **Zollinger-Ellison syndrome**, and **gastroprotection in high-risk NSAID or antiplatelet users**. [AGA 2022]`,`**Time-limited indications**: uncomplicated GERD or LA A/B oesophagitis (4-8 weeks), peptic ulcer healing (4-8 weeks), **H. pylori eradication (14 days)** and functional dyspepsia (4-8 week trial). [ACG GERD 2022]`,`**Commonest inappropriate uses**: 'gastric cover' with every antibiotic or steroid course, stress-ulcer prophylaxis outside the ICU, continued after discharge without review, and indefinite use for vague dyspepsia. [AGA 2022]`,`**Elderly patients** are most exposed: Beers Criteria advise **avoiding scheduled PPI beyond 8 weeks** unless high risk (oral corticosteroids or chronic NSAID, erosive oesophagitis, Barrett, hypersecretory states or failed step-down). [AGS Beers 2023]`,`**Constipation risk factors**: female sex, age over 65, low fibre and fluid intake, immobility, hypothyroidism, hypercalcaemia, diabetes, Parkinson disease and **constipating drugs** (opioids, anticholinergics, calcium channel blockers especially verapamil, iron, calcium, aluminium antacids, clozapine, ondansetron). [Harrison 22e]`,`**OIC risk** is highest with **oral morphine, oxycodone and codeine**, lower with transdermal fentanyl and buprenorphine, and is dose-related; **tapentadol** causes less constipation. [AGA OIC 2019]`,`Antiemetic and antispasmodic overuse is driven by **symptomatic prescribing without a diagnosis**, so appendicitis, obstruction, ectopic pregnancy and myocardial infarction can be masked. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pharmacology and mechanism - the why behind the drugs`,points:[`**PPIs** are **prodrugs** activated in the acidic parietal-cell canaliculus to a sulphenamide that binds **H+/K+ ATPase irreversibly**; they work only on **actively secreting pumps**, so they must be taken **30-60 minutes before the first meal**. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`A single PPI dose inhibits about 70% of pumps; **steady state takes 3-5 days**, explaining why on-demand PPI is slower than antacids; **CYP2C19 polymorphism** (rapid metabolisers) reduces omeprazole and lansoprazole effect, less so rabeprazole and esomeprazole. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Rebound acid hypersecretion**: chronic acid suppression raises **gastrin**, causing **ECL-cell hyperplasia**; on abrupt stopping, acid output overshoots for **2-4 weeks**, producing dyspepsia even in healthy volunteers - the main reason deprescribing fails. [AGA 2022]`,`**Vonoprazan** is a **potassium-competitive acid blocker (P-CAB)** that binds the pump **reversibly**, needs no acid activation, works from the **first dose**, is **acid-stable** and is **not dependent on CYP2C19**, giving more potent and longer acid suppression than PPIs. [AGA P-CAB update 2024]`,`**Osmotic laxatives** (PEG, lactulose, magnesium) retain water in the lumen; **lactulose** is fermented by colonic bacteria to short-chain acids, causing **bloating and flatulence** and lowering colonic pH (the basis for its use in hepatic encephalopathy). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Stimulant laxatives** (bisacodyl, sodium picosulfate, senna) act on the enteric nervous system to increase **high-amplitude propagated contractions** and secretion; **prucalopride** is a highly selective **5-HT4 agonist** without the cardiac hERG effects of cisapride and tegaserod. [AGA-ACG 2023]`,`**Secretagogues**: **linaclotide and plecanatide** activate **guanylate cyclase-C**, raising cGMP and CFTR chloride secretion; **lubiprostone** opens **ClC-2 chloride channels**; **PAMORAs** (naldemedine, naloxegol, methylnaltrexone) block gut mu-receptors without crossing the blood-brain barrier, so analgesia is preserved. [AGA OIC 2019]`,`**Antiemetics act on four inputs**: the **chemoreceptor trigger zone** (D2, 5-HT3, NK1), the **vagal afferents from the gut** (5-HT3), the **vestibular system** (H1, muscarinic) and the **cortex** (benzodiazepines, steroids) - pick the drug for the pathway. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Domperidone** is a peripheral D2 antagonist (does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so few extrapyramidal effects) but blocks **hERG potassium channels**, prolonging QT; **metoclopramide** crosses the barrier, causing **dystonia and tardive dyskinesia**. [EMA 2013-2014]`,`**Antispasmodics**: **dicyclomine and hyoscine butylbromide** are antimuscarinics; **drotaverine** is a **phosphodiesterase-4 inhibitor** without anticholinergic effects; **mebeverine** is a direct smooth-muscle relaxant; **peppermint oil** blocks smooth-muscle calcium channels. [BSG IBS 2021]`]},{heading:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Acid suppressants**: antacids and alginates (minutes, on demand), **H2-receptor antagonists** (famotidine; tachyphylaxis within 2 weeks), **PPIs** (omeprazole, pantoprazole, rabeprazole, esomeprazole, lansoprazole) and **P-CABs** (vonoprazan, tegoprazan, fexuprazan). [Harrison 22e]`,`**PPI dose equivalence (standard dose)**: omeprazole 20 mg = pantoprazole 40 mg = rabeprazole 20 mg = esomeprazole 20-40 mg = lansoprazole 30 mg; 'double dose' means the standard dose twice daily. [ACG GERD 2022]`,`**Laxative classes**: **bulk-forming** (ispaghula/psyllium), **osmotic** (PEG 3350, lactulose, magnesium salts), **stimulant** (bisacodyl, sodium picosulfate, senna), **softener/lubricant** (docusate, liquid paraffin), **secretagogue** (lubiprostone, linaclotide, plecanatide), **prokinetic** (prucalopride), **PAMORA**, and **rectal** agents (glycerine, bisacodyl suppository, enemas). [AGA-ACG 2023]`,`**Antiemetic classes**: 5-HT3 antagonists (ondansetron, granisetron, palonosetron), D2 antagonists (domperidone, metoclopramide, prochlorperazine, haloperidol), antihistamines (promethazine, cyclizine, doxylamine), antimuscarinic (hyoscine hydrobromide), NK1 antagonists (aprepitant), corticosteroids (dexamethasone) and atypical antipsychotics (olanzapine). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Antispasmodic classes**: antimuscarinic (dicyclomine, hyoscine butylbromide), direct smooth-muscle relaxants (mebeverine, drotaverine, alverine), calcium-channel blocking (otilonium, pinaverium, peppermint oil). [BSG IBS 2021]`,`**Bristol Stool Form Scale** types 1-2 indicate slow transit and types 6-7 diarrhoea; it is the bedside tool to titrate any laxative. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Rome IV opioid-induced constipation**: new or worsening constipation on starting, changing or increasing opioids, with 2 or more constipation features in over 25% of defaecations. [Rome IV 2016]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - what to ask before prescribing or deprescribing`,points:[`**PPI review history**: original indication, who started it, duration, current dose, previous attempts to stop, whether symptoms are **typical reflux or dyspepsia**, and any **upper GI bleed** history. [AGA 2022]`,`Ask about **alarm features** before any symptomatic GI prescription: dysphagia, weight loss, GI bleeding or anaemia, persistent vomiting, a mass, and new symptoms over **age 45-50**. [ACG-CAG Dyspepsia 2017]`,`**Constipation history**: stool frequency, **Bristol type**, straining, sensation of incomplete evacuation, **need for digital manoeuvres** (suggests dyssynergia), drug list, red flags, and duration. [AGA-ACG 2023]`,`**OIC history**: opioid, dose and route; bowel function before the opioid; the **Bowel Function Index** (score of 30 or more out of 100 means clinically significant OIC). [AGA OIC 2019]`,`**Nausea and vomiting history** should identify the pathway: motion or vertigo (vestibular), drug or metabolic (CTZ), gastroenteritis or chemotherapy (gut 5-HT3), raised intracranial pressure (morning vomiting with headache), pregnancy, and anticipation. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **cardiac history and QT-prolonging co-medication** (macrolides, fluoroquinolones, antipsychotics, azoles, hydroxychloroquine) before ondansetron or domperidone. [EMA 2014]`,`Antispasmodics are used for **colicky abdominal pain**: always ask about fever, persistent localised pain, vomiting, missed period and cardiac risk before treating pain symptomatically. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings that change the prescription`,points:[`**Pallor, koilonychia and weight loss** on a PPI prescription demand endoscopy, not a higher dose - PPIs can **heal and mask gastric cancer**. [BSG 2021]`,`**Abdominal distension, visible peristalsis and tinkling bowel sounds** suggest obstruction - **stimulant laxatives, prokinetics and PAMORAs are contraindicated**. [AGA OIC 2019]`,`**Digital rectal examination** is mandatory in constipation: a loaded rectum (impaction), anal fissure, paradoxical sphincter contraction on bearing down (dyssynergia) or a rectal mass change the plan. [AGA-ACG 2023]`,`**Postural hypotension, dry mucosa and reduced skin turgor** in a vomiting patient mean IV fluids and admission rather than an oral antiemetic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bradycardia, a long QT on ECG, or hypokalaemia/hypomagnesaemia** preclude ondansetron or domperidone until corrected. [EMA 2014]`,`**Extrapyramidal signs** (oculogyric crisis, torticollis, trismus) after metoclopramide or prochlorperazine are an **acute dystonic reaction**, treated with IV/IM **promethazine or diphenhydramine** (or benztropine). [BNF 2025]`,`**Anticholinergic signs** after dicyclomine or hyoscine (dry mouth, tachycardia, urinary retention, mydriasis, confusion) are commonest in the elderly; check for **angle-closure glaucoma and prostatism** before prescribing. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Adverse effects and differential considerations`,points:[`**PPI associations** reported in observational studies: **C. difficile infection** (about 1.5-2-fold), enteric infections, **hypomagnesaemia** (FDA 2011), **vitamin B12 deficiency** (over 2 years), hip fracture, community-acquired pneumonia, CKD, dementia and gastric cancer - most have **low-quality, likely confounded evidence**. [AGA 2022]`,`In **COMPASS**, a randomised trial of **17,598 patients on pantoprazole 40 mg vs placebo for 3 years**, there was **no excess of any adverse event except possibly enteric infections** - so fear of adverse effects alone should not drive deprescribing. [Gastroenterology 2019]`,`**Definite idiosyncratic PPI harms**: **acute interstitial nephritis**, **subacute cutaneous lupus**, **microscopic colitis** (especially lansoprazole), **fundic gland polyps** and rare **hyponatraemia**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PPI interactions**: **omeprazole/esomeprazole reduce clopidogrel activation** (prefer pantoprazole), reduce absorption of **atazanavir, rilpivirine, itraconazole, ketoconazole, iron, levothyroxine** and dasatinib, and raise **methotrexate** levels at high dose. [FDA label]`,`**Laxative harms**: **phosphate enemas** cause hyperphosphataemia and AKI in CKD and the elderly; **magnesium salts** cause hypermagnesaemia in CKD; **liquid paraffin** causes lipoid pneumonia (aspiration) and fat-soluble vitamin malabsorption; lactulose causes bloating. [BNF 2025]`,`**Stimulant laxatives do not cause 'cathartic colon'** or colonic nerve damage at therapeutic doses; melanosis coli from anthraquinones (senna) is benign and reversible. [AGA-ACG 2023]`,`**Antiemetic harms**: ondansetron - QT prolongation, constipation, headache, **serotonin syndrome** with SSRIs; domperidone - QT, sudden cardiac death, hyperprolactinaemia; metoclopramide - dystonia, **tardive dyskinesia** (FDA boxed warning beyond 12 weeks), neuroleptic malignant syndrome. [FDA, EMA]`,`**Differential for 'PPI-refractory' symptoms**: non-adherence or wrong timing, functional heartburn, reflux hypersensitivity, eosinophilic oesophagitis, achalasia, rumination, gastroparesis, bile reflux, and cardiac pain - not simply a dose issue. [ACG GERD 2022]`]},{heading:`Investigations - when and what`,points:[`Most GI prescribing needs **no tests**; investigate for **alarm features** or before committing to lifelong therapy. [AGA 2022]`,`Before labelling someone a **lifelong PPI user**, AGA suggests **endoscopy** (to look for Barrett or severe oesophagitis) if there is no clear indication, and **off-PPI ambulatory pH monitoring** if GERD has never been proven. [AGA 2022]`,`On long-term PPI: check **serum magnesium** before starting with digoxin or diuretics and periodically in long-term users; check **vitamin B12** in the elderly or with neuropathy or anaemia. [FDA 2011]`,`In constipation: **haemoglobin, TSH, calcium and glucose** are reasonable; **colonoscopy** only for alarm features or age-appropriate screening. [AGA-ACG 2023]`,`**Anorectal manometry with balloon expulsion test** (expel a 50 mL water-filled balloon within 1-2 minutes) for laxative-refractory constipation or suspected dyssynergia. [AGA-ACG 2023]`,`**Baseline ECG** before domperidone or IV ondansetron in patients with cardiac disease, electrolyte disturbance or QT drugs; QTc over **500 ms** or an increase over 60 ms means stop. [EMA 2014]`,`**Serum potassium, magnesium, urea and creatinine** in persistent vomiting; urine pregnancy test in any woman of reproductive age with nausea. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`OPD management - PPI deprescribing and P-CABs`,points:[`**Every patient on a PPI should have the indication reviewed and documented**, ideally at each prescription renewal. [AGA 2022]`,`**Uncomplicated GERD that responded to short-term PPI** should be offered a trial of deprescribing; **twice-daily users should first step down to once daily**. [AGA 2022]`,`**Do not deprescribe** in Barrett oesophagus, LA grade C/D oesophagitis, peptic stricture, or a **history of high-risk upper GI bleeding**. [AGA 2022]`,`**Two methods are acceptable**: **taper** (halve the dose or alternate days for 2-4 weeks then stop) or **abrupt stop**; then use **on-demand PPI or antacid/alginate** for breakthrough symptoms. [Canadian PPI deprescribing guideline 2017]`,`**Warn about rebound dyspepsia** for 2-4 weeks and treat it with antacid-alginate (e.g., sodium alginate with bicarbonate 10-20 mL after meals and at bedtime) rather than restarting full-dose PPI. [AGA 2022]`,`**Re-evaluate at 4-12 weeks**: symptom-free stays off; recurrent symptoms go back on the **lowest effective dose**, and symptoms that persist despite PPI need investigation, not escalation. [Canadian PPI deprescribing guideline 2017]`,`**Vonoprazan 20 mg once daily for 8 weeks** heals erosive oesophagitis, with **superior healing in LA grade C/D** in PHALCON-EE; maintenance is **10 mg once daily**. [AGA P-CAB update 2024]`,`P-CABs are reasonable for **PPI-refractory erosive oesophagitis** and **H. pylori regimens** (vonoprazan 20 mg twice daily + amoxicillin 1 g three times daily for 14 days), but are **not first-line for NERD** and long-term safety data (hypergastrinaemia) are still limited. [ACG H. pylori 2024]`,`Prefer **pantoprazole with clopidogrel**; prefer **famotidine (20 mg twice daily) or on-demand antacids** where acid suppression is mild or intermittent. [ACG GERD 2022]`]},{heading:`OPD management - laxatives, OIC, antiemetics and antispasmodics`,points:[`**Constipation ladder**: fluid, **fibre 25-30 g/day or ispaghula 3.5 g one to three times daily with 250 mL water** (works over 1-2 weeks; may worsen bloating), then **PEG 3350 17 g in 240 mL water once daily**, then add a stimulant, then secretagogue or prucalopride. [AGA-ACG 2023]`,`AGA-ACG 2023 gives **strong recommendations** for **PEG, bisacodyl, sodium picosulfate, prucalopride, linaclotide and plecanatide**, and **conditional** ones for fibre, lactulose, senna, magnesium oxide and lubiprostone. [AGA-ACG 2023]`,`**Bisacodyl 5-10 mg** or **senna (sennosides 7.5-15 mg)** at bedtime acts in **6-12 hours**; **bisacodyl suppository 10 mg** or **glycerine suppository** works in 15-60 minutes. [BNF 2025]`,`**Prucalopride 2 mg once daily** (1 mg in age over 65 or eGFR under 30) - stop if no benefit at **4 weeks**; **linaclotide 72-145 mcg once daily** for chronic idiopathic constipation (290 mcg in IBS-C) - diarrhoea is the main side effect. [AGA-ACG 2023]`,`**OIC**: start a **stimulant with or without osmotic laxative prophylactically with every opioid**; if refractory, use a **PAMORA** - **naldemedine 0.2 mg once daily** or **naloxegol 25 mg once daily** (strong), or **methylnaltrexone 12 mg SC** on alternate days (conditional). [AGA OIC 2019]`,`PAMORAs are **contraindicated in known or suspected bowel obstruction**; stop other maintenance laxatives when starting them and restart if response is inadequate after 3 days. [AGA OIC 2019]`,`**Antiemetic choice**: gastroenteritis or chemotherapy - **ondansetron 4-8 mg orally or IV 8-12 hourly**; vertigo/motion - **prochlorperazine 5-10 mg** or cinnarizine; drug/metabolic or palliative - **haloperidol 0.5-1.5 mg**; gastric stasis - **metoclopramide 10 mg up to three times daily for at most 5 days**. [EMA 2013, BNF 2025]`,`**Domperidone 10 mg up to three times daily (maximum 30 mg/day) for at most 1 week**; avoid in cardiac disease, QT drugs, potent CYP3A4 inhibitors and severe hepatic impairment. [EMA 2014]`,`**Antispasmodics for IBS or colic**: **drotaverine 40-80 mg three times daily**, **dicyclomine 10-20 mg three to four times daily**, **hyoscine butylbromide 10-20 mg four times daily orally (20 mg IM/IV for acute colic)**, **mebeverine 135 mg three times daily 20 minutes before meals**, or **peppermint oil 0.2 mL capsules three times daily**. [BSG IBS 2021]`]},{heading:`Inpatient and emergency prescribing`,points:[`**Stress-ulcer prophylaxis** is only for ICU patients at high risk - **mechanical ventilation over 48 hours** or **coagulopathy**; in **REVISE (2024)** pantoprazole 40 mg IV daily reduced clinically important upper GI bleeding in ventilated patients without changing mortality. [NEJM 2024]`,`**Stop in-hospital PPIs at discharge** unless a definite indication is written in the discharge summary - this is the commonest route to indefinite use. [AGA 2022]`,`**IV PPI for upper GI bleeding**: high-dose bolus and continuous or intermittent dosing after endoscopic therapy for high-risk stigmata - see the Upper and lower GI bleeding topic. [ACG 2021]`,`**Faecal impaction**: rectal disimpaction with **phosphate-free enemas** or manual removal, then **high-dose PEG (8 sachets in 1 L over 6 hours for up to 3 days)** for proximal impaction, avoiding PEG when obstruction is suspected. [BNF 2025]`,`**Acute dystonia** from metoclopramide: stop the drug, give **promethazine 25-50 mg IM** or procyclidine 5-10 mg IM/IV; reassure that it resolves within hours. [BNF 2025]`,`**Anticholinergic toxicity** in the elderly after hyoscine or dicyclomine (delirium, retention): stop, catheterise if in retention, supportive care. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Postoperative nausea**: ondansetron 4 mg IV plus dexamethasone 4-8 mg IV at induction for high-risk patients (Apfel score 3-4). [Fourth Consensus PONV 2020]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: antacids/alginates first, then **H2RA or omeprazole** (large cohorts show no teratogenicity); **doxylamine 10 mg with pyridoxine 10 mg at bedtime** is first-line for nausea and vomiting of pregnancy; ondansetron is second-line with a small, inconsistent oral cleft signal in the first trimester. [ACOG 2018, RCOG Green-top 69]`,`**Constipation in pregnancy**: fibre, **lactulose or PEG**; short courses of senna or bisacodyl are acceptable; avoid castor oil and liquid paraffin. [RCOG]`,`**Elderly**: avoid **liquid paraffin**, **anticholinergic antispasmodics** (dicyclomine, hyoscine) and **metoclopramide** (unless gastroparesis), and review PPIs beyond 8 weeks. [AGS Beers 2023]`,`**CKD**: avoid **magnesium and phosphate laxatives and enemas**, aluminium antacids (accumulation), and reduce prucalopride to 1 mg; PPIs need no dose change but watch for interstitial nephritis. [KDIGO, BNF 2025]`,`**Children**: PEG 3350 is first-line - **disimpaction 1-1.5 g/kg/day for 3-6 days**, then **maintenance 0.4-0.8 g/kg/day**; metoclopramide is avoided under 1 year and promethazine under 2 years. [ESPGHAN/NASPGHAN 2014]`,`**Liver disease**: lactulose is preferred (also treats encephalopathy); avoid constipating opioids and sedating antiemetics; PPIs in cirrhosis are linked to SBP and encephalopathy, so use only with a clear indication. [EASL 2018]`,`**Palliative care**: **haloperidol** for opioid or metabolic nausea, **metoclopramide** for gastric stasis (not if complete obstruction), **dexamethasone and hyoscine butylbromide** for malignant bowel obstruction, and laxatives alongside every opioid. [NICE palliative care]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: omeprazole, ondansetron, metoclopramide, domperidone, dicyclomine, hyoscine butylbromide, lactulose, bisacodyl and ispaghula are cheap generics available through **Jan Aushadhi Kendras** and state free-drug lists. [NLEM 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`About **half to two-thirds of long-term PPI users** without a firm indication can stay off or reduce PPI at 1 year after a structured deprescribing programme. [Canadian PPI deprescribing guideline 2017]`,`**Severe hypomagnesaemia** on PPIs (often with diuretics) can cause tetany, arrhythmias and refractory hypokalaemia and hypocalcaemia; it resolves on stopping the PPI. [FDA 2011]`,`**Masked gastric cancer** is the most important harm of empirical PPIs in an older patient with alarm features - delayed diagnosis worsens stage. [BSG 2021]`,`**Laxative misuse** (eating disorders) causes hypokalaemia, metabolic alkalosis or acidosis and pseudo-Bartter physiology; stimulant laxatives at therapeutic doses are safe long term. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Untreated OIC** leads to impaction, overflow diarrhoea, urinary retention and **opioid under-dosing** by patients who prefer pain to constipation. [AGA OIC 2019]`,`**Domperidone** at doses over 30 mg/day or age over 60 roughly doubles the risk of **ventricular arrhythmia and sudden cardiac death**. [EMA 2014]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and prevention`,points:[`**Review every long-term PPI** at least **annually**: indication, dose, attempt step-down, magnesium and B12 when relevant. [AGA 2022]`,`**Refer for endoscopy**: alarm features, symptoms persisting despite 8 weeks of correctly timed PPI, or uncertain indication before lifelong therapy. [ACG GERD 2022]`,`**Refer to a gastroenterologist** for constipation refractory to two classes of laxatives, suspected dyssynergia (for manometry and biofeedback), or red flags. [AGA-ACG 2023]`,`**Follow up a new laxative at 2-4 weeks**, titrating to a soft-formed stool (**Bristol 4**) without straining. [AGA-ACG 2023]`,`**Re-check ECG** if domperidone or ondansetron is continued in high-risk patients; **stop metoclopramide** after 5 days and never exceed 12 weeks. [EMA 2013]`,`**Lifestyle for reflux** (weight loss, head-end elevation, no meal within 3 hours of lying down) reduces the need for long-term PPI - see the Acid peptic disease, GERD and H. pylori topic. [ACG GERD 2022]`,`Counsel to **take the PPI 30-60 minutes before breakfast** - wrong timing is the commonest cause of apparent PPI failure. [ACG GERD 2022]`,`**Avoid irrational FDCs** (PPI plus domperidone or levosulpiride for GERD; antispasmodic plus analgesic combinations) - India's CDSCO has banned several hundred irrational FDCs in **2016-2018 and 2023-2024**. [CDSCO 2023]`,`**Antimicrobial stewardship** includes not adding a 'cover' PPI to antibiotics, since PPIs add to C. difficile risk. [ICMR AMSP 2023]`,`Counsel opioid users that **constipation is expected and preventable** and that laxatives must continue as long as the opioid. [AGA OIC 2019]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**AGA Clinical Practice Update on Deprescribing PPIs (2022)** remains the reference: review all users, step down twice-daily users, taper or stop, do not deprescribe Barrett, severe oesophagitis or high-risk bleeders. [AGA 2022]`,`**AGA-ACG Chronic Idiopathic Constipation guideline (2023)** for the first time gave **strong recommendations for bisacodyl and sodium picosulfate** alongside PEG, prucalopride, linaclotide and plecanatide. [AGA-ACG 2023]`,`**Vonoprazan approved by the US FDA (2023)** for erosive oesophagitis healing and maintenance, and the **AGA 2024 Clinical Practice Update on P-CABs** positioned it for LA C/D, PPI failure and H. pylori, not routine NERD. [AGA P-CAB update 2024]`,`**ACG H. pylori guideline (2024)** lists **vonoprazan-amoxicillin dual therapy** as an acceptable empirical first-line option where susceptibility is unknown, after optimised bismuth quadruple therapy. [ACG H. pylori 2024]`,`**REVISE trial (2024)**: pantoprazole stress-ulcer prophylaxis in invasively ventilated patients reduced bleeding with no mortality difference - supports ICU-only prophylaxis. [NEJM 2024]`,`**AGS Beers Criteria 2023** keep PPI over 8 weeks, metoclopramide, mineral oil and anticholinergic antispasmodics on the list to avoid in older adults. [AGS Beers 2023]`,`**NLEM 2022** removed **ranitidine** (NDMA contamination concerns), leaving omeprazole as the listed PPI and famotidine as the H2RA option. [NLEM 2022]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Viva: **'Why take a PPI before breakfast?'** - prodrug needs acid activation and binds only active pumps, which are switched on by the meal. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`Viva: **'Why do patients fail PPI withdrawal?'** - **rebound acid hypersecretion** from hypergastrinaemia and ECL hyperplasia; warn and cover with antacids. [AGA 2022]`,`Viva: **'Which PPI with clopidogrel?'** - **pantoprazole**, as omeprazole and esomeprazole inhibit CYP2C19. [FDA label]`,`Viva: **'How does vonoprazan differ from omeprazole?'** - reversible, potassium-competitive, acid-stable, first-dose effect, CYP2C19-independent. [AGA P-CAB update 2024]`,`Common mistake: **using lactulose as first-line for chronic constipation** - PEG is more effective and causes less bloating. [AGA-ACG 2023]`,`Common mistake: **domperidone beyond 1 week or above 30 mg/day**, and **metoclopramide beyond 5 days**. [EMA 2013-2014]`,`Common mistake: **opioid without a laxative** - every opioid prescription needs a co-prescribed stimulant laxative. [AGA OIC 2019]`,`Common mistake: **giving hyoscine to an elderly man with BPH or glaucoma**, or treating undiagnosed abdominal pain with antispasmodics. [AGS Beers 2023]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drug table - common GI drugs in OPD`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Omeprazole / pantoprazole`,`20 mg / 40 mg orally once daily, 30-60 min before breakfast; double dose = twice daily`,`4-8 weeks for GERD/ulcer; review for step-down`,`C. difficile, hypomagnesaemia, B12 deficiency, interstitial nephritis; omeprazole-clopidogrel interaction`],[`Vonoprazan`,`20 mg orally once daily (healing); 10 mg (maintenance); 20 mg twice daily in H. pylori regimens`,`8 weeks healing; 14 days for H. pylori`,`Hypergastrinaemia, diarrhoea; limited long-term data; avoid with atazanavir/rilpivirine`],[`Famotidine`,`20 mg orally twice daily or 40 mg at night`,`On demand or short course`,`Tachyphylaxis; reduce dose if eGFR under 50; confusion in elderly`],[`PEG 3350`,`17 g in 240 mL water once daily (adult)`,`Titrate to Bristol 4; long-term safe`,`Bloating, diarrhoea; avoid in obstruction`],[`Lactulose`,`15-30 mL orally once or twice daily`,`Effect in 1-2 days`,`Bloating, flatulence, cramps; sweet taste`],[`Bisacodyl / senna`,`Bisacodyl 5-10 mg or sennosides 7.5-15 mg orally at bedtime; bisacodyl 10 mg suppository`,`Acts in 6-12 h (oral), 15-60 min (rectal)`,`Cramps, diarrhoea, hypokalaemia with misuse; avoid in obstruction`],[`Prucalopride`,`2 mg orally once daily (1 mg if over 65 or eGFR under 30)`,`Stop if no benefit at 4 weeks`,`Headache, nausea, diarrhoea in first days`],[`Linaclotide`,`72-145 mcg (CIC) or 290 mcg (IBS-C) orally once daily before breakfast`,`Assess at 4-12 weeks`,`Diarrhoea; contraindicated under 2 years`],[`Naldemedine / naloxegol`,`0.2 mg / 25 mg orally once daily`,`As long as opioid continues`,`Abdominal pain, diarrhoea, opioid withdrawal rarely; contraindicated in obstruction`],[`Ondansetron`,`4-8 mg orally or IV every 8-12 h (max single IV dose 16 mg)`,`Short course`,`QT prolongation, constipation, headache, serotonin syndrome`],[`Domperidone`,`10 mg orally up to three times daily (max 30 mg/day)`,`Max 7 days`,`QT prolongation, sudden death; avoid in cardiac disease, CYP3A4 inhibitors`],[`Metoclopramide`,`10 mg orally/IV up to three times daily (max 0.5 mg/kg/day)`,`Max 5 days (never over 12 weeks)`,`Acute dystonia, tardive dyskinesia, NMS; avoid under 1 year, Parkinson disease, epilepsy`],[`Drotaverine`,`40-80 mg orally three times daily`,`Short course`,`Hypotension, dizziness; avoid in severe hepatic, renal or cardiac failure`],[`Dicyclomine / hyoscine butylbromide`,`Dicyclomine 10-20 mg three to four times daily; hyoscine 10-20 mg four times daily orally or 20 mg IM/IV`,`Short course`,`Anticholinergic: dry mouth, retention, tachycardia; avoid in glaucoma, BPH, myasthenia, elderly`]]},{heading:`PPI deprescribing - who and how (AGA 2022)`,columns:[`Patient group`,`Action`,`Rationale`],rows:[[`Uncomplicated GERD responsive to short-term PPI`,`Attempt deprescribing: step-down, on-demand or stop`,`Most stay well off or on low dose`],[`Twice-daily PPI users`,`Step down to once daily first`,`Twice daily rarely needed long term`],[`Barrett oesophagus`,`Continue long-term PPI`,`Reduces progression to dysplasia`],[`LA grade C/D oesophagitis or peptic stricture`,`Continue long-term PPI`,`High relapse and stricture risk`],[`History of high-risk upper GI bleed or on DAPT/anticoagulant with risk factors`,`Continue PPI`,`Rebleeding risk outweighs adverse effects`],[`No documented indication`,`Consider endoscopy or off-PPI pH study before lifelong therapy; else deprescribe`,`Avoids lifelong treatment of unproven GERD`],[`Patient worried only about adverse effects`,`Do not stop solely for this if indication is valid`,`Associations are low quality; COMPASS reassuring`]]},{heading:`Laxative classes compared`,columns:[`Class`,`Examples`,`Onset`,`Best for`,`Avoid when`],rows:[[`Bulk-forming`,`Ispaghula (psyllium) 3.5 g one to three times daily`,`2-3 days, full effect 2 weeks`,`Mild constipation, low-fibre diet, IBS-C`,`Opioid-induced or slow-transit constipation, immobile, poor fluid intake`],[`Osmotic`,`PEG 3350, lactulose, magnesium hydroxide`,`1-3 days`,`First-line drug for most chronic constipation; children; pregnancy`,`Magnesium in CKD; lactulose if bloating`],[`Stimulant`,`Bisacodyl, sodium picosulfate, senna`,`6-12 hours`,`Rescue, OIC, add-on to osmotic`,`Obstruction, acute abdomen`],[`Softener/lubricant`,`Docusate, liquid paraffin`,`1-3 days`,`Painful defaecation (fissure) - docusate`,`Liquid paraffin in elderly, dysphagia, children under 3`],[`Secretagogue`,`Linaclotide, plecanatide, lubiprostone`,`1 week`,`Refractory CIC, IBS-C`,`Obstruction, children under 2 (linaclotide)`],[`5-HT4 agonist`,`Prucalopride`,`1-3 days`,`Refractory CIC`,`Bowel perforation, obstruction, severe IBD`],[`PAMORA`,`Naldemedine, naloxegol, methylnaltrexone`,`Hours (SC) to 1 day`,`OIC refractory to laxatives`,`Known or suspected obstruction`],[`Rectal`,`Glycerine or bisacodyl suppository, enemas`,`15-60 minutes`,`Impaction, evacuation disorders`,`Phosphate enemas in CKD, elderly, heart failure`]]},{heading:`Choosing an antiemetic by mechanism`,columns:[`Cause of vomiting`,`Pathway`,`First choice`,`Alternative / caution`],rows:[[`Gastroenteritis, chemotherapy, post-operative`,`Gut vagal 5-HT3`,`Ondansetron 4-8 mg`,`Granisetron; add dexamethasone for chemotherapy`],[`Drugs, uraemia, hypercalcaemia, opioids`,`CTZ D2`,`Haloperidol 0.5-1.5 mg`,`Prochlorperazine, metoclopramide`],[`Gastric stasis, gastroparesis`,`Peripheral D2, 5-HT4`,`Metoclopramide 10 mg before meals`,`Domperidone 10 mg (QT), erythromycin short term`],[`Motion sickness, vertigo`,`Vestibular H1, muscarinic`,`Promethazine, cinnarizine, hyoscine hydrobromide`,`Prochlorperazine for acute vertigo`],[`Pregnancy`,`Multifactorial`,`Doxylamine-pyridoxine`,`Promethazine, metoclopramide, ondansetron second line`],[`Raised intracranial pressure`,`Meningeal and brainstem`,`Dexamethasone`,`Treat the cause; image urgently`],[`Anticipatory`,`Cortical`,`Lorazepam 0.5-1 mg`,`Behavioural therapy`]]},{heading:`Antispasmodics compared`,columns:[`Drug`,`Mechanism`,`Usual adult dose`,`Caution`],rows:[[`Drotaverine`,`PDE4 inhibitor, smooth-muscle relaxant`,`40-80 mg orally three times daily`,`Hypotension; no anticholinergic effects`],[`Dicyclomine`,`Antimuscarinic`,`10-20 mg orally three to four times daily`,`Glaucoma, BPH, elderly; contraindicated under 6 months`],[`Hyoscine butylbromide`,`Antimuscarinic, poorly absorbed orally`,`10-20 mg orally four times daily; 20 mg IM/IV`,`Tachycardia; avoid IV in cardiac disease`],[`Mebeverine`,`Direct smooth-muscle relaxant`,`135 mg three times daily or 200 mg MR twice daily`,`Well tolerated; rare rash`],[`Peppermint oil`,`Calcium-channel blockade`,`0.2 mL enteric-coated capsule three times daily`,`Heartburn; avoid with GERD`]]}],redFlags:[`Dysphagia, GI bleeding, iron deficiency anaemia, weight loss or a mass in a patient on long-term PPI - endoscopy, do not escalate the dose`,`Abdominal distension, absolute constipation and vomiting - suspect obstruction; stop stimulants, prokinetics and PAMORAs`,`Persistent vomiting with dehydration, postural hypotension or hypokalaemia - admit for IV fluids`,`Syncope, palpitations or QTc over 500 ms on ondansetron or domperidone - stop and monitor`,`Oculogyric crisis, torticollis or rigidity with fever after metoclopramide - acute dystonia or neuroleptic malignant syndrome`,`Severe hypomagnesaemia with tetany or arrhythmia on long-term PPI`,`Colicky pain with fever, guarding or a missed period - do not mask with antispasmodics; assess for surgical or gynaecological cause`,`Morning vomiting with headache or papilloedema - raised intracranial pressure`],pearls:[`**PPI: Prodrug, Pump-active, Pre-breakfast** - the three Ps of correct PPI use.`,`**Every PPI needs an indication and an end date** - write both on the prescription.`,`**Rebound acid hypersecretion** lasts 2-4 weeks after stopping a PPI; cover with antacid-alginate rather than restarting.`,`**Pantoprazole with clopidogrel**; avoid omeprazole and esomeprazole.`,`**COMPASS**: 3 years of pantoprazole vs placebo showed no harm except possibly enteric infection - indication, not fear, decides deprescribing.`,`**PEG over lactulose** for chronic constipation: more effective, less bloating.`,`**Every opioid gets a laxative**; refractory OIC gets a PAMORA (naldemedine or naloxegol).`,`**Domperidone 30 mg/day for 7 days; metoclopramide 30 mg/day for 5 days** - the EMA limits.`,`**Drotaverine** is the only commonly used antispasmodic without anticholinergic effects - safer in BPH and glaucoma.`,`**Vonoprazan** works from the first dose and does not need CYP2C19 - but it is not first-line for NERD.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-drugs-ppi-safety-q1`,stem:`A 58-year-old man has taken pantoprazole 40 mg twice daily for 3 years after an episode of heartburn that settled within 2 weeks. He has never had endoscopy and has no alarm features. Per the AGA 2022 clinical practice update, the best next step is:`,options:[`Stop the PPI immediately and prescribe nothing`,`Step down to pantoprazole 40 mg once daily, then attempt taper or on-demand use`,`Continue twice-daily dosing indefinitely because he is asymptomatic`,`Switch to vonoprazan 20 mg twice daily lifelong`],answer:1,explanation:`AGA 2022 advises that twice-daily users without a definite indication should first step down to once daily, then attempt taper or on-demand use with antacid cover for rebound. Continuing twice-daily dosing ignores the lack of indication, switching to lifelong vonoprazan replaces one unproven long-term therapy with another, and abrupt stopping with no rescue plan invites rebound acid hypersecretion and failure.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-drugs-ppi-safety-q2`,stem:`Which patient should NOT be offered PPI deprescribing?`,options:[`A 45-year-old with NERD whose symptoms resolved after 8 weeks of omeprazole`,`A 70-year-old started on PPI as stress-ulcer prophylaxis during a ward admission`,`A 60-year-old with long-segment Barrett oesophagus on omeprazole 20 mg daily`,`A 35-year-old given PPI with every antibiotic course`],answer:2,explanation:`Barrett oesophagus is a definite indication for long-term PPI, so deprescribing is not advised. NERD that has responded to a short course is the classic group to deprescribe; ward stress-ulcer prophylaxis outside the ICU and PPI 'cover' with antibiotics have no indication at all and should be stopped.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-drugs-ppi-safety-q3`,stem:`A 66-year-old woman on oral morphine 60 mg/day for bone metastases has had hard stools every 4-5 days despite senna and PEG for 3 weeks. Abdomen is soft, non-distended with normal bowel sounds. The most appropriate addition is:`,options:[`Liquid paraffin 10 mL at night`,`Switch morphine to codeine`,`Naldemedine 0.2 mg once daily`,`Metoclopramide 10 mg three times daily for 4 weeks`],answer:2,explanation:`Laxative-refractory opioid-induced constipation without obstruction is the indication for a PAMORA; naldemedine (and naloxegol) carry strong AGA recommendations and preserve analgesia. Liquid paraffin risks lipoid pneumonia in the elderly, codeine is itself highly constipating, and metoclopramide is not a laxative and must not exceed 5 days.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-drugs-ppi-safety-q4`,stem:`Which statement about vonoprazan is correct?`,options:[`It binds the H+/K+ ATPase reversibly and does not require acid activation`,`It is a prodrug that must be taken 30-60 minutes before meals to work`,`Its effect is markedly reduced in CYP2C19 rapid metabolisers`,`It is recommended as first-line therapy for all non-erosive reflux disease`],answer:0,explanation:`Vonoprazan is a potassium-competitive acid blocker that binds the pump reversibly, is acid-stable and acts from the first dose. The prodrug requiring pre-meal dosing and CYP2C19 dependence describe PPIs, and the AGA 2024 update does not position P-CABs as first-line for NERD.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-drugs-ppi-safety-q5`,stem:`A 72-year-old man with ischaemic heart disease on clarithromycin for pneumonia develops nausea. ECG shows QTc 490 ms. Which antiemetic plan is safest?`,options:[`Domperidone 20 mg three times daily`,`Ondansetron 16 mg IV stat`,`Correct potassium and magnesium and use a low dose of a non-QT antiemetic such as cyclizine or prochlorperazine with monitoring`,`Metoclopramide 10 mg three times daily for 3 weeks`],answer:2,explanation:`With a long QTc and a QT-prolonging macrolide, the priority is correcting electrolytes and avoiding further QT burden. Domperidone at 60 mg/day exceeds the 30 mg limit and is contraindicated in cardiac disease with QT drugs; high-dose IV ondansetron adds QT risk; metoclopramide beyond 5 days risks tardive dyskinesia in the elderly.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-drugs-ppi-safety-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Discuss the appropriate indications for long-term proton pump inhibitor therapy. Describe the adverse effects of long-term PPI use and outline a stepwise approach to PPI deprescribing in primary care. Add a note on vonoprazan.`,openingLines:[`Proton pump inhibitors are prodrugs that irreversibly block the parietal-cell H+/K+ ATPase; they are among the most over-prescribed drugs, with up to two-thirds of long-term use lacking a valid indication.`,`AGA 2022 advises that every PPI prescription carry a documented indication, that long-term users be reviewed regularly, and that those without a definite indication be offered structured deprescribing.`],answer:[{heading:`Mechanism in brief`,points:[`Prodrug activated in the acidic canaliculus; binds active pumps irreversibly; take 30-60 min before breakfast.`,`Steady state in 3-5 days; CYP2C19 metabolism; chronic use raises gastrin and ECL mass, causing rebound on stopping.`]},{heading:`Indications`,points:[`Long-term: Barrett, LA C/D oesophagitis, peptic stricture, PPI-responsive EoE, Zollinger-Ellison, high-risk NSAID/antiplatelet gastroprotection.`,`Short-term: uncomplicated GERD 4-8 weeks, ulcer healing, H. pylori 14 days, functional dyspepsia trial, ICU stress ulcer prophylaxis in ventilated or coagulopathic patients.`]},{heading:`Adverse effects`,points:[`Definite: interstitial nephritis, hypomagnesaemia, B12 deficiency with prolonged use, C. difficile, fundic gland polyps, microscopic colitis, drug interactions (clopidogrel, atazanavir, iron, levothyroxine).`,`Associated, low-quality evidence: fracture, pneumonia, CKD, dementia, gastric cancer; COMPASS trial reassuring.`,`Masking gastric cancer and rebound acid hypersecretion.`]},{heading:`Stepwise deprescribing`,points:[`Review indication and alarm features; do not deprescribe Barrett, LA C/D, stricture, high-risk bleeders.`,`Step down twice daily to once daily; then taper (halve dose or alternate days 2-4 weeks) or stop.`,`Counsel about rebound; on-demand PPI or antacid-alginate for breakthrough.`,`Review at 4-12 weeks; restart lowest effective dose if needed; investigate persistent symptoms.`]},{heading:`Vonoprazan`,points:[`Potassium-competitive acid blocker: reversible, acid-stable, first-dose effect, CYP2C19-independent.`,`20 mg daily for 8 weeks heals erosive oesophagitis (superior in LA C/D); 10 mg maintenance; dual therapy with amoxicillin for H. pylori.`,`Not first-line for NERD; long-term safety data limited.`]},{heading:`System measures`,points:[`Stop hospital PPIs at discharge unless indicated; pharmacist-led review at refill; avoid PPI-domperidone FDCs.`]}],mustDraw:[`PPI deprescribing flow chart: indication check, step-down, taper/stop, on-demand, review at 4-12 weeks`,`Parietal cell diagram showing H2, M3, CCK2 receptors and the proton pump with PPI and P-CAB sites`],markSplit:[{part:`Mechanism and indications`,marks:3},{part:`Adverse effects`,marks:2},{part:`Deprescribing algorithm with flow chart`,marks:3},{part:`Vonoprazan and system measures`,marks:2}],keywords:[`PPI`,`deprescribing`,`rebound acid hypersecretion`,`Barrett`,`COMPASS`,`vonoprazan`,`AGA 2022`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-drugs-ppi-safety-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on opioid-induced constipation and its management.`,openingLines:[`Opioid-induced constipation is new or worsening constipation on starting, changing or increasing opioids (Rome IV), affecting 40-80% of long-term opioid users, and tolerance does not develop to it.`,`It is caused by gut mu-receptor activation reducing propulsive motility and secretion, and is prevented by co-prescribing a laxative and treated, when refractory, with a peripherally acting mu-opioid receptor antagonist.`],answer:[{heading:`Mechanism and assessment`,points:[`Mu-receptors in the myenteric plexus: reduced peristalsis, increased segmental tone, reduced secretion, increased sphincter tone.`,`Assess with Bristol scale and Bowel Function Index (30 or more significant); exclude obstruction and impaction by examination and DRE.`]},{heading:`Prevention and first line`,points:[`Prophylactic stimulant (senna 15 mg or bisacodyl 10 mg at night) with or without PEG 17 g daily from the first opioid dose.`,`Avoid bulk-forming agents; ensure fluids and mobility; consider opioid switch to transdermal fentanyl or tapentadol.`]},{heading:`Refractory OIC`,points:[`PAMORA: naldemedine 0.2 mg or naloxegol 25 mg once daily (strong), methylnaltrexone 12 mg SC (conditional); lubiprostone conditional.`,`Contraindicated in obstruction; analgesia preserved because they do not cross the blood-brain barrier.`]}],mustDraw:[`OIC management ladder: prophylactic laxative, add osmotic, PAMORA, rectal measures`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and mechanism`,marks:1},{part:`Assessment`,marks:1},{part:`Prevention and first line`,marks:1.5},{part:`PAMORAs`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`Rome IV`,`Bowel Function Index`,`PAMORA`,`naldemedine`,`naloxegol`,`methylnaltrexone`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Peptic ulcer disease and related disorders; Disorders of gastrointestinal motility`,`Targownik LE et al. AGA Clinical Practice Update on De-Prescribing of Proton Pump Inhibitors: Expert Review. Gastroenterology, 2022`,`Chang L et al. AGA-ACG Clinical Practice Guideline: Pharmacological Management of Chronic Idiopathic Constipation, 2023`,`Crockett SD et al. AGA Institute Guideline on the Medical Management of Opioid-Induced Constipation, 2019`,`AGA Clinical Practice Update on Integrating Potassium-Competitive Acid Blockers into Clinical Gastroenterology Practice: Expert Review, 2024`,`Katz PO et al. ACG Clinical Guideline for the Diagnosis and Management of GERD, 2022; Chey WD et al. ACG Clinical Guideline: Treatment of Helicobacter pylori Infection, 2024`,`American Geriatrics Society 2023 Updated AGS Beers Criteria for Potentially Inappropriate Medication Use in Older Adults`,`National List of Essential Medicines (NLEM) 2022, Ministry of Health and Family Welfare, Government of India`]}),l.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-dili-herbal`,title:`Drug-induced liver injury: ATT, herbal and dietary supplements`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Drug-induced liver injury (DILI)** is liver damage caused by a prescription drug, over-the-counter medicine or **herbal and dietary supplement (HDS)**, diagnosed by **exclusion and causality scoring (RUCAM/RECAM)**, typed by the **R ratio** into hepatocellular, cholestatic or mixed, and graded by **Hy's law** (hepatocellular injury with jaundice carries about **10% mortality**); in India **anti-tuberculosis therapy** and **ayurvedic/complementary medicines** are the leading causes, and management is **stopping the culprit**, NAC for acute liver failure and **structured ATT reintroduction** per NTEP/ATS.`,keywords:[`DILI`,`drug-induced liver injury`,`hepatotoxicity`,`RUCAM`,`RECAM`,`R ratio`,`Hy's law`,`eDISH`,`anti-tubercular drug hepatotoxicity`,`ATT reintroduction`,`isoniazid hepatitis`,`herbal and dietary supplement`,`HILI`,`Tinospora cordifolia (giloy)`,`ashwagandha`,`complementary and alternative medicine`,`LiverTox`,`N-acetylcysteine`,`DRESS`,`INDILI`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**DILI** is injury to the liver from drugs, biologicals, or **herbal and dietary supplements (HDS)** after exclusion of other causes; HDS injury is also called **herb-induced liver injury (HILI)**. [ACG 2021]`,`**Biochemical case definition**: **ALT 5 x ULN or more**, or **ALP 2 x ULN or more** (with raised GGT and no bone cause), or **ALT 3 x ULN or more with bilirubin over 2 x ULN**. [International DILI Expert Working Group 2011]`,`Population incidence of idiosyncratic DILI is about **14-19 per 100,000 per year** (France, Iceland); DILI causes about **10-15% of acute liver failure in the West** after paracetamol. [Harrison 22e]`,`In the US DILI Network, **HDS cause about 16-20% of DILI**, rising over time, led by **bodybuilding anabolic steroids** and **multi-ingredient weight-loss products** (green tea extract, Garcinia). [AASLD 2023]`,`In India, the **Indian Network of DILI (INDILI)** registry found **anti-tuberculosis drugs the commonest cause (close to half of cases)**, followed by **complementary and alternative medicines**, antiepileptics and antiretrovirals; DILI mortality in Indian series exceeds 10%. [INDILI, Devarbhavi]`,`**ATT hepatotoxicity** occurs in **2-28%** of treated patients worldwide, at the upper end of this range in Indian cohorts; India has about **27% of the world's TB burden** (India TB Report 2024). [ATS 2006, India TB Report 2024]`,`**Ayurvedic, siddha, unani and homeopathic (AYUSH) products** are freely sold without prescription in India; INASL-associated series describe CAM-related acute hepatitis, AIH-like injury and **CAM-precipitated acute-on-chronic liver failure** with high mortality. [INASL, J Clin Exp Hepatol 2021]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Commonest culprits worldwide**: **amoxicillin-clavulanate** (commonest single drug in Western registries), isoniazid, nitrofurantoin, minocycline, fluoroquinolones, antiepileptics, NSAIDs, methotrexate, azathioprine, checkpoint inhibitors and HDS. [ACG 2021]`,`**Indian culprits**: **isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide**, phenytoin and carbamazepine (with DRESS), valproate, nevirapine and efavirenz, dapsone, **nimesulide and diclofenac**, methotrexate, ketoconazole, and ayurvedic preparations. [INDILI]`,`**Herbal culprits**: **Tinospora cordifolia (giloy/guduchi)** - AIH-like hepatitis reported in India after COVID-era over-use; **ashwagandha (Withania somnifera)** - cholestatic hepatitis; **turmeric/curcumin**, **green tea extract**, **Garcinia cambogia**, kava, and **pyrrolizidine alkaloid** plants causing sinusoidal obstruction syndrome. [LiverTox, AASLD 2023]`,`**Contamination and adulteration** of HDS matter as much as the herb: **heavy metals** (lead, mercury, arsenic in rasa-shastra 'bhasma' preparations), undeclared **steroids, NSAIDs or sildenafil analogues**, and mislabelled species. [AASLD 2023]`,`**ATT DILI risk factors**: **age over 35-60**, **alcohol use**, **malnutrition and hypoalbuminaemia**, female sex, **NAT2 slow acetylator** genotype (common in Indians), **HBV, HCV or HIV co-infection**, pre-existing liver disease, and concomitant hepatotoxic drugs. [ATS 2006]`,`**General DILI host factors**: older age (cholestatic phenotype), female sex (hepatocellular and AIH-like phenotype), **HLA alleles** (B*57:01 with flucloxacillin, DRB1*15:01 with amoxicillin-clavulanate, B*35:01 with green tea and turmeric), and daily dose **over 50-100 mg** of an oral drug with high hepatic metabolism. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Underlying chronic liver disease** does not greatly raise idiosyncratic DILI incidence but sharply **raises mortality when DILI occurs**, and CAM is a leading precipitant of **ACLF** in Indian registries. [APASL ACLF 2019]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**Intrinsic (direct) DILI** is dose-dependent, predictable and occurs within days - the model is **paracetamol**, whose CYP2E1 metabolite **NAPQI** depletes glutathione and causes zone 3 necrosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Idiosyncratic DILI** is rare (1 in 10,000-100,000 exposures), not clearly dose-related, appears after **5-90 days** latency, and is mostly **adaptive-immune mediated**: a reactive metabolite forms a hapten that is presented by specific **HLA molecules** to T cells. [ACG 2021]`,`**Indirect DILI** is the drug altering the liver or immunity rather than being toxic - **HBV reactivation** with rituximab or chemotherapy, **immune-mediated hepatitis with checkpoint inhibitors**, and fatty liver with steroids or tamoxifen. [AASLD 2023]`,`**Isoniazid** is acetylated by **NAT2** to acetylisoniazid, hydrolysed to **acetylhydrazine and hydrazine**, which are oxidised by **CYP2E1** to toxic intermediates; slow acetylators accumulate hydrazine, and **rifampicin induces** the toxic pathway. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rifampicin** causes **unconjugated and conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia** by competing for bilirubin uptake and the bile salt export pump (BSEP) - isolated early jaundice may be adaptive; **pyrazinamide** causes dose-related hepatocellular injury that is often severe and prolonged. [ATS 2006]`,`**Mitochondrial injury** (valproate, high-dose tetracycline, nucleoside analogues) produces **microvesicular steatosis** and lactic acidosis; **BSEP inhibition** (bosentan, rifampicin, anabolic steroids) produces bland cholestasis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pyrrolizidine alkaloids** injure sinusoidal endothelium causing **sinusoidal obstruction syndrome** - tender hepatomegaly, ascites and jaundice; **Tinospora** is thought to unmask autoimmunity, producing an **AIH-like** pattern with ANA and raised IgG. [J Clin Exp Hepatol 2021]`,`**Adaptation**: many patients on isoniazid have transient ALT rises under 3 x ULN that settle despite continuing the drug - injury occurs when adaptation fails. [ATS 2006]`]},{heading:`Classification, causality and severity`,points:[`**R ratio** = (ALT / ULN) divided by (ALP / ULN) at first presentation: **5 or more hepatocellular**, **2 or less cholestatic**, **between 2 and 5 mixed**; it guides the differential and expected recovery. [ACG 2021]`,`**RUCAM (updated 2016)** scores temporal relationship, course after stopping, risk factors, concomitant drugs, exclusion of other causes, known hepatotoxicity and rechallenge: **9 or more highly probable, 6-8 probable, 3-5 possible, 1-2 unlikely, 0 or less excluded**. [Danan 2016]`,`**RECAM (2022)** is a revised, electronic, evidence-based causality tool that tightens the exclusion of competing causes and performs better than RUCAM against expert opinion. [Hepatology 2022]`,`**Hy's law**: drug-induced **hepatocellular** injury (ALT over 3 x ULN) with **bilirubin over 2 x ULN**, without initial cholestasis and with no other explanation, carries about **10% risk of death or transplant**. [FDA 2009]`,`**Severity (International DILI Expert Working Group)**: mild (no jaundice), moderate (jaundice or symptomatic), severe (jaundice with **INR 1.5 or more**, ascites, encephalopathy or other organ failure), fatal or transplant. [International DILI Expert Working Group 2011]`,`**ATT hepatotoxicity (ATS)**: **ALT over 3 x ULN with symptoms** (nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, jaundice) **or over 5 x ULN without symptoms** - the threshold to stop ATT. [ATS 2006]`,`**Clinical phenotypes**: acute hepatitis, acute cholestasis, mixed, **AIH-like**, DRESS with hepatitis, steatosis/steatohepatitis, sinusoidal obstruction, nodular regenerative hyperplasia, vanishing bile duct syndrome and hepatic adenoma. [AASLD 2023]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Most DILI is **asymptomatic and found on LFTs**; symptomatic patients have **anorexia, nausea, fatigue, right upper quadrant discomfort, dark urine and jaundice**. [ACG 2021]`,`**ATT hepatitis** typically starts within the **first 2-8 weeks**, with **anorexia, nausea and vomiting** preceding jaundice - in a TB patient these must not be dismissed as 'gastritis'. [ATS 2006]`,`**Cholestatic DILI** presents with **pruritus** and jaundice; amoxicillin-clavulanate injury can appear **up to 6 weeks after the course ends**. [ACG 2021]`,`**Hypersensitivity features** - fever, rash, lymphadenopathy, eosinophilia, facial oedema - starting **2-8 weeks** after antiepileptics, dapsone, sulfonamides or allopurinol suggest **DRESS**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Take a drug history like a detective**: every prescription in the last **6 months**, OTC analgesics, **ayurvedic churnas, kadhas (decoctions) and bhasmas**, gym supplements, weight-loss powders, and the exact product label if available. [AASLD 2023]`,`Patients rarely volunteer HDS - **ask specifically** about immunity boosters (giloy, ashwagandha, kadha), 'liver tonics', and treatments for diabetes, arthritis, infertility or weight loss. [AASLD 2023]`,`Ask about **alcohol, risk factors for viral hepatitis**, recent hypotension, pregnancy and previous liver disease, which shape both differential and prognosis. [ACG 2021]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Jaundice** is the key prognostic sign - with hepatocellular injury it defines Hy's law. [FDA 2009]`,`**Tender hepatomegaly** occurs in acute hepatitis; **tender hepatomegaly with ascites** suggests **sinusoidal obstruction syndrome** (pyrrolizidine herbs). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Asterixis, confusion or drowsiness** with jaundice means **acute liver failure** - admit and contact a transplant centre. [AASLD ALF]`,`**Rash, fever, lymphadenopathy and facial oedema** point to DRESS; check for mucosal involvement (SJS/TEN overlap). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Stigmata of chronic liver disease** (spider naevi, palmar erythema, splenomegaly) mean DILI on cirrhosis or ACLF, with much higher mortality. [APASL ACLF 2019]`,`**Scratch marks and xanthelasma** suggest prolonged cholestasis or vanishing bile duct syndrome. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for features of the indication drug: **TB** (cachexia, lymph nodes), **HIV**, epilepsy, rheumatoid arthritis - they tell you which drug may need replacing. [ACG 2021]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Acute viral hepatitis** A, B, E (and C) - check IgM anti-HAV, HBsAg and IgM anti-HBc, IgM anti-HEV and HCV RNA; **hepatitis E** is the commonest DILI mimic in India and is often missed. [ACG 2021]`,`**Autoimmune hepatitis** - ANA, ASMA, IgG; separating idiopathic AIH from drug-induced AIH-like injury may need biopsy and observation after stopping steroids. [AASLD 2023]`,`**Alcohol-associated hepatitis** - AST:ALT over 2, AST rarely over 400, GGT high, heavy drinking history. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ischaemic hepatitis** - very high ALT and LDH after hypotension, falling rapidly within days. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Biliary obstruction** (stones, malignancy) for cholestatic patterns - **ultrasound** first; **MRCP** if ducts are equivocal. [ACG 2021]`,`**Wilson disease** in patients under 40, Budd-Chiari syndrome, and **TB itself** (hepatic or granulomatous TB, IRIS in HIV) for ATT-associated LFT rise. [EASL 2019]`,`**Sepsis-associated cholestasis** and **total parenteral nutrition** in inpatients. [EASL 2019]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**First line**: ALT, AST, ALP, GGT, bilirubin (fractionated), albumin, **INR**, CBC with **eosinophil count**, creatinine and glucose; calculate the **R ratio**. [ACG 2021]`,`**Exclude other causes**: IgM anti-HAV, **HBsAg and IgM anti-HBc**, **IgM anti-HEV**, anti-HCV with **HCV RNA**, and **abdominal ultrasound with Doppler** in every case. [ACG 2021]`,`**Second line** as indicated: ANA, ASMA, IgG (AIH); ceruloplasmin (under 40 years); CMV, EBV, HSV in the immunosuppressed or with very high ALT; MRCP for cholestasis. [EASL 2019]`,`**Product analysis**: keep the HDS container; send samples for **heavy metal or adulterant testing** where available; record the exact ingredients. [AASLD 2023]`,`**Liver biopsy** is not routine - consider if AIH is suspected or immunosuppression is being considered, or if **ALT fails to fall by 50% within 60 days** (hepatocellular) or **ALP fails to fall by 50% within 180 days** (cholestatic) after stopping. [EASL 2019]`,`**LiverTox** (NIH online database) provides the hepatotoxicity likelihood category and phenotype for each drug and herb. [LiverTox]`,`**ATT monitoring**: baseline LFT before ATT in **high-risk** patients (alcohol, chronic liver disease, HIV, age over 60, pregnancy/postpartum, malnutrition), then **2-4 weekly in the intensive phase**; check LFT in anyone with symptoms. [NTEP Technical Guidance 2021]`]},{heading:`OPD management`,points:[`**Stop the suspected drug or supplement** - the single most important step; stop all non-essential drugs and all HDS together. [ACG 2021]`,`**Mild asymptomatic ALT rise under 3 x ULN** on isoniazid or statins can be monitored without stopping (adaptation), with repeat LFT in 1-2 weeks. [ATS 2006]`,`**Stop ATT** when ALT is **over 3 x ULN with symptoms or jaundice, or over 5 x ULN without symptoms**; if bilirubin rises alone, look for haemolysis, Gilbert syndrome or rifampicin cholestasis. [ATS 2006]`,`**Do not rechallenge** a drug that caused severe or hypersensitivity DILI; rechallenge is reserved for indispensable drugs such as first-line ATT under monitoring. [ACG 2021]`,`**Pruritus**: cholestyramine 4 g up to four times daily (separated from other drugs by 4 hours), then rifampicin or sertraline if refractory; **ursodeoxycholic acid** is widely used but has **no proven benefit** in DILI. [EASL 2019]`,`**Corticosteroids** are only for **DRESS**, **AIH-like DILI** or checkpoint-inhibitor hepatitis - not for routine DILI. [AASLD 2023]`,`**Follow-up LFTs weekly** until improving, then 2-4 weekly until normal; most hepatocellular DILI resolves in **1-3 months**, cholestatic DILI may take **3-6 months**. [ACG 2021]`,`**Report every case** to the **Pharmacovigilance Programme of India (PvPI)** - suspected ADR form or the ADR helpline 1800-180-3024; AYUSH product reactions go to the AYUSH pharmacovigilance programme. [PvPI, IPC]`]},{heading:`ATT hepatotoxicity - stopping, bridging and reintroduction`,points:[`When ATT is stopped, if TB is **severe or infectious** (smear-positive pulmonary, TB meningitis, spinal TB) give a **non-hepatotoxic bridging regimen**: **ethambutol + levofloxacin + streptomycin or amikacin** until LFTs settle. [ATS 2016, NTEP Technical Guidance 2021]`,`**Restart when ALT is under 2 x ULN** (or back to near baseline) and bilirubin normal, with symptoms resolved. [ATS 2003]`,`**Sequential reintroduction (ATS 2003, followed by NTEP)**: **rifampicin first** (with ethambutol) at full dose; after **3-7 days** check ALT and add **isoniazid**; after a further 3-7 days, **pyrazinamide** may be added if the reaction was mild. [ATS 2003]`,`**Omit pyrazinamide** if the original injury was severe or prolonged, or jaundice occurred - then give **2HRE + 7HR (9 months)**. [ATS 2003]`,`If **isoniazid** is implicated: **6 months RZE** with levofloxacin as per programme guidance; if **rifampicin** is implicated: a longer non-rifampicin regimen (**H, E, levofloxacin for 12-18 months**, initially with an injectable). [ATS 2016]`,`An **Indian RCT (Sharma, Clin Infect Dis 2010)** found **no difference in recurrence** (about 10%) between **simultaneous full-dose reintroduction**, ATS sequential and BTS graded-dose regimens - so any structured method is acceptable. [Clin Infect Dis 2010]`,`The **BTS graded method** restarts isoniazid 50 mg, increasing to 300 mg over 2-3 days, then rifampicin 75 mg rising to full dose, then pyrazinamide 250 mg rising to full dose, with daily LFTs. [BTS 1998]`,`**Recurrence** during reintroduction identifies the culprit, which is then permanently omitted; record the reaction in the **Ni-kshay** patient record. [NTEP Technical Guidance 2021]`]},{heading:`IPD and emergency management`,points:[`**Admit**: **INR 1.5 or more**, encephalopathy, bilirubin rising with Hy's law pattern, ascites, persistent vomiting or poor oral intake, DRESS or SJS, or DILI on underlying cirrhosis. [ACG 2021]`,`**Acute liver failure** (INR 1.5 or more with encephalopathy, no prior liver disease): ICU, glucose monitoring, avoid sedatives, and **early referral to a transplant centre** using King's College criteria. [AASLD ALF]`,`**N-acetylcysteine in non-paracetamol ALF**: improves transplant-free survival in adults with **early (grade I-II) coma** - 150 mg/kg over 1 h, 12.5 mg/kg/h for 4 h, then 6.25 mg/kg/h for 67 h; **not recommended in children**. [Lee, Gastroenterology 2009; ACG 2021]`,`**Paracetamol overdose**: NAC by the treatment nomogram or empirically when the time of ingestion is unknown or LFTs are already abnormal. [AASLD ALF]`,`**DRESS**: stop the drug, give **prednisolone 0.5-1 mg/kg/day** tapered over weeks, and monitor for multi-organ involvement. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Herbal ACLF**: treat as ACLF (APASL), consider **steroids only with AIH-like features**, avoid further CAM, and assess early for transplant; plasma exchange is used in some Indian centres as a bridge. [APASL ACLF 2019]`,`**Monitor** INR, glucose, sodium, encephalopathy grade and urine output at least daily; INR rising beyond 1.5 or new encephalopathy triggers transplant-centre discussion. [AASLD ALF]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy and postpartum**: first-line ATT (H, R, Z, E) is safe in pregnancy but **isoniazid hepatitis risk is higher postpartum**; give **pyridoxine** and monitor LFTs monthly. [WHO TB 2022]`,`**HIV-TB**: highest DILI risk - nevirapine, efavirenz and cotrimoxazole add hepatotoxicity; **dolutegravir-based ART** has lower risk; distinguish DILI from **IRIS** and HBV flares. [WHO TB 2022]`,`**Chronic liver disease with TB**: compensated - regimen with fewer hepatotoxic drugs (for example omit pyrazinamide: 2HRE + 7HR); decompensated - **non-hepatotoxic regimens** (ethambutol, levofloxacin, aminoglycoside, linezolid) under specialist care. [NTEP Technical Guidance 2021]`,`**HBV carriers** starting ATT, chemotherapy or rituximab: check **HBsAg and anti-HBc**; give **tenofovir or entecavir prophylaxis** before rituximab or high-risk chemotherapy. [AASLD 2018]`,`**Elderly**: more cholestatic and prolonged DILI, more polypharmacy; amoxicillin-clavulanate DILI is commonest in men over 55. [ACG 2021]`,`**Children**: lower ATT DILI rates; **valproate under 2 years** and with **POLG mutations** carries fatal mitochondrial hepatotoxicity risk; aspirin causes Reye syndrome. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diabetes and MASLD**: baseline raised ALT is not a contraindication to statins or metformin; define DILI against the patient's baseline (ALT 3 x baseline rather than 3 x ULN). [AASLD 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Most DILI resolves completely after stopping the drug; **overall mortality or transplant is about 5-10%**, higher with **Hy's law** (about 10%) and **ALF** (about 40% transplant-free survival without NAC/transplant). [ACG 2021]`,`**Hepatocellular DILI** carries higher short-term mortality; **cholestatic DILI** resolves more slowly and more often becomes chronic. [ACG 2021]`,`**Chronic DILI** - abnormal tests or imaging persisting beyond **6 months** (DILIN) or **1 year** (EASL) - occurs in about **10-20%**; outcomes include vanishing bile duct syndrome and cirrhosis. [EASL 2019]`,`**ATT DILI** has higher mortality when jaundice develops, in the elderly, with hypoalbuminaemia and with underlying liver disease; it also leads to **treatment interruption, default and drug resistance**. [ATS 2006]`,`**CAM-related ACLF** in Indian series carries very high short-term mortality - among the worst of all ACLF precipitants. [APASL ACLF 2019]`,`**Rechallenge** with the culprit in non-ATT DILI can cause a faster, more severe reaction - often fatal with halothane-like immune injury. [ACG 2021]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and prevention`,points:[`**Refer urgently** to a hepatologist or transplant centre: INR 1.5 or more, encephalopathy, rising bilirubin after stopping, DILI in cirrhosis, or herbal ACLF. [AASLD ALF]`,`**Refer** for suspected AIH-like DILI, DRESS, or failure of ALT to halve within 60 days of stopping. [EASL 2019]`,`**ATT patients**: high-risk patients have LFTs **every 2-4 weeks** in the intensive phase and monthly thereafter; everyone is told to stop and report on nausea, vomiting or jaundice. [NTEP Technical Guidance 2021]`,`After recovery, **document the culprit drug** prominently in the record and give the patient a written **drug-allergy note**. [ACG 2021]`,`Check LFTs at **6 months** to identify chronic DILI. [EASL 2019]`,`**NTEP / TB Mukt Bharat**: patient education card - stop ATT and attend if nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain or yellow eyes; **Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana** nutrition support lowers malnutrition-related risk. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Avoid alcohol** and other hepatotoxic drugs (paracetamol over 2 g/day in high risk, NSAIDs, azoles) during ATT. [ATS 2006]`,`Counsel patients that **'natural' does not mean safe**, and to **tell every doctor** about herbal products; avoid multi-ingredient weight-loss and bodybuilding supplements. [AASLD 2023]`,`Advise people with chronic liver disease to **avoid all non-prescribed herbal medicines**, as CAM is a leading ACLF precipitant in India. [INASL]`,`**Vaccinate** against hepatitis A and B in chronic liver disease to prevent superimposed injury. [ACIP]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2021-2026)`,points:[`**ACG Clinical Guideline: Diagnosis and Management of Idiosyncratic DILI (2021)**: R ratio, exclusion work-up including HEV, RUCAM as a structured adjunct, NAC for adult ALF, steroids only for selected phenotypes, avoid rechallenge. [ACG 2021]`,`**AASLD Practice Guidance on Drug, Herbal and Dietary Supplement-Induced Liver Injury (2023)**: highlights rising HDS injury, product adulteration, indirect DILI (checkpoint inhibitors) and HLA associations. [AASLD 2023]`,`**RECAM (2022)**: revised electronic causality assessment, replacing RUCAM in research and increasingly in practice. [Hepatology 2022]`,`**CIOMS DILI consensus (2020)**: harmonised definitions, baseline-adjusted thresholds in patients with abnormal baseline LFTs, and drug-development monitoring rules. [CIOMS 2020]`,`**WHO consolidated TB guidelines (2022 update)** and NTEP adoption of shorter regimens (4-month HPMZ, 6-month BPaLM for drug-resistant TB) - new drugs bring their own hepatotoxicity monitoring (bedaquiline, pretomanid). [WHO TB 2022]`,`**Indian giloy hepatotoxicity reports (2021-2022)** led INASL to caution against unsupervised Tinospora use; the Ministry of AYUSH disputed causality, illustrating the need for causality scoring and product testing. [J Clin Exp Hepatol 2021]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Viva: **'Define Hy's law'** - ALT over 3 x ULN plus bilirubin over 2 x ULN, ALP not raised, no other cause; about 10% mortality. [FDA 2009]`,`Viva: **'How do you calculate R?'** - (ALT/ULN) divided by (ALP/ULN); 5 or more hepatocellular, 2 or less cholestatic. [ACG 2021]`,`Viva: **'When do you stop ATT?'** - ALT over 3 x ULN with symptoms or jaundice, or over 5 x ULN without symptoms. [ATS 2006]`,`Viva: **'Which ATT drug do you restart first and why?'** - rifampicin, as it is the most important sterilising drug and least often the cause of hepatocellular DILI. [ATS 2003]`,`Common mistake: **not testing for hepatitis E** before labelling DILI in India. [ACG 2021]`,`Common mistake: **forgetting the herbal history** - ask about kadha, giloy, ashwagandha and gym supplements in every unexplained hepatitis. [AASLD 2023]`,`Common mistake: **giving steroids or UDCA routinely** - neither is evidence-based for ordinary DILI. [ACG 2021]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drug table - DILI and ATT hepatotoxicity`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`N-acetylcysteine (non-paracetamol ALF)`,`150 mg/kg IV over 1 h, then 12.5 mg/kg/h for 4 h, then 6.25 mg/kg/h`,`Total 72 h`,`Anaphylactoid reaction, vomiting; not recommended in children with non-paracetamol ALF`],[`Prednisolone (DRESS, AIH-like DILI)`,`0.5-1 mg/kg/day orally`,`Taper over 4-12 weeks`,`Infection, hyperglycaemia; only for selected phenotypes`],[`Cholestyramine (pruritus)`,`4 g orally up to four times daily`,`Until pruritus settles`,`Bloating, binds other drugs (separate by 4 h), fat-soluble vitamin deficiency`],[`Rifampicin (reintroduction)`,`10 mg/kg (450 mg under 50 kg, 600 mg 50 kg or more) orally daily`,`Restart first, full dose; ALT check at 3-7 days`,`Cholestasis, orange secretions, CYP induction`],[`Isoniazid (reintroduction)`,`5 mg/kg (300 mg) orally daily with pyridoxine 10-25 mg`,`Add after rifampicin tolerated; ALT check at 3-7 days`,`Hepatitis (NAT2 slow acetylators), neuropathy`],[`Pyrazinamide`,`25 mg/kg orally daily`,`Reintroduce last, only if injury was mild; omit if severe`,`Dose-related hepatotoxicity, hyperuricaemia, arthralgia`],[`Bridging regimen`,`Ethambutol 15-20 mg/kg + levofloxacin 750-1000 mg orally + streptomycin 15 mg/kg IM or amikacin`,`Until LFT recovery`,`Optic neuritis, tendinopathy and QT, ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity`],[`Tenofovir disoproxil (HBV prophylaxis)`,`300 mg orally daily`,`During and 12-18 months after rituximab/chemotherapy`,`Renal tubular injury, bone loss`]]},{heading:`Patterns of DILI by R ratio`,columns:[`Pattern`,`R ratio`,`Typical drugs/herbs`,`Expected recovery`],rows:[[`Hepatocellular`,`5 or more`,`Isoniazid, pyrazinamide, paracetamol, nevirapine, diclofenac, nimesulide, green tea extract, giloy`,`1-3 months; Hy's law risk`],[`Cholestatic`,`2 or less`,`Amoxicillin-clavulanate, anabolic steroids, chlorpromazine, azathioprine, ashwagandha, rifampicin`,`3-6 months; more chronicity`],[`Mixed`,`Between 2 and 5`,`Phenytoin, carbamazepine, sulfonamides, cotrimoxazole`,`Intermediate`],[`AIH-like`,`Usually hepatocellular`,`Nitrofurantoin, minocycline, statins, methyldopa, Tinospora`,`Often needs steroids; watch for relapse`],[`Sinusoidal obstruction`,`Variable`,`Pyrrolizidine alkaloids, oxaliplatin, busulfan`,`Variable; may need TIPS or transplant`],[`Microvesicular steatosis`,`Variable`,`Valproate, tetracycline, nucleoside analogues`,`Severe; lactic acidosis`]]},{heading:`ATT hepatotoxicity - stop, bridge, restart`,columns:[`Step`,`Criterion or action`,`Source`],rows:[[`Stop`,`ALT over 3 x ULN with symptoms or jaundice; over 5 x ULN asymptomatic`,`ATS 2006`],[`Investigate`,`HAV, HBV, HEV, HCV, ultrasound, alcohol, other drugs`,`ACG 2021`],[`Bridge`,`Ethambutol + levofloxacin + streptomycin/amikacin if TB severe`,`ATS 2016, NTEP`],[`Restart threshold`,`ALT under 2 x ULN, normal bilirubin, symptoms resolved`,`ATS 2003`],[`Step 1`,`Rifampicin (+ ethambutol); ALT at 3-7 days`,`ATS 2003`],[`Step 2`,`Add isoniazid; ALT at 3-7 days`,`ATS 2003`],[`Step 3`,`Add pyrazinamide only if injury mild; else 2HRE + 7HR`,`ATS 2003`],[`Recurrence`,`Stop the last drug added; permanently omit and record`,`NTEP`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of suspected DILI`,columns:[`Condition`,`Clue`,`Test that separates`],rows:[[`Hepatitis E`,`Endemic, faeco-oral, may coexist with ATT`,`IgM anti-HEV (HEV RNA if immunosuppressed)`],[`Hepatitis A, B, C`,`Exposure history`,`IgM anti-HAV, HBsAg + IgM anti-HBc, HCV RNA`],[`Autoimmune hepatitis`,`Woman, other autoimmunity`,`ANA, ASMA, IgG; biopsy`],[`Alcohol-associated hepatitis`,`Heavy drinking, AST:ALT over 2`,`History, GGT, AST under 400`],[`Ischaemic hepatitis`,`Recent hypotension, very high LDH`,`Rapid fall of ALT within days`],[`Biliary obstruction`,`Colic, dilated ducts`,`Ultrasound, MRCP`],[`Wilson disease`,`Under 40, haemolysis, low ALP:bilirubin`,`Ceruloplasmin, urinary copper, KF rings`]]},{heading:`RUCAM causality categories`,columns:[`Total score`,`Causality`],rows:[[`9 or more`,`Highly probable`],[`6-8`,`Probable`],[`3-5`,`Possible`],[`1-2`,`Unlikely`],[`0 or less`,`Excluded`]]}],redFlags:[`INR 1.5 or more, or any confusion or drowsiness with jaundice - acute liver failure; admit and call a transplant centre`,`Hy's law pattern: ALT over 3 x ULN with bilirubin over 2 x ULN`,`Jaundice, vomiting or abdominal pain in a patient on ATT - stop ATT and check LFT the same day`,`Fever, rash, eosinophilia and facial oedema after an antiepileptic or sulfonamide - DRESS`,`Ascites or tender hepatomegaly after a herbal product - sinusoidal obstruction syndrome or ACLF`,`DILI in a patient with known cirrhosis or chronic hepatitis B`,`Bilirubin still rising 2 weeks after stopping the drug`],pearls:[`**Hy's law = hepatocellular injury + jaundice = 10% mortality.**`,`**R = (ALT/ULN) / (ALP/ULN)**: 5 or more hepatocellular, 2 or less cholestatic.`,`**ATT stop rule: 3 with symptoms, 5 without.**`,`**Restart order R-H-Z** ('Rifampicin Returns first, Pyrazinamide Possibly never').`,`**Always test for hepatitis E** before labelling DILI in India.`,`**Ask for the bottle**: herbal products may contain heavy metals, steroids or NSAIDs.`,`**NAC helps non-paracetamol ALF in adults with early coma**, not in children.`,`**Giloy causes AIH-like injury; ashwagandha causes cholestasis; pyrrolizidines cause SOS.**`,`**Amoxicillin-clavulanate** DILI can begin weeks after the course ends.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-dili-herbal-q1`,stem:`A 45-year-old man in the third week of first-line ATT has nausea and vomiting. ALT is 190 U/L (ULN 40), bilirubin 1.1 mg/dL. The correct action is:`,options:[`Continue ATT and add an antiemetic`,`Stop isoniazid, rifampicin and pyrazinamide and investigate`,`Reduce all drug doses by half`,`Stop only pyrazinamide and continue the rest`],answer:1,explanation:`ALT of 190 is over 3 x ULN and he is symptomatic, which meets the ATS threshold to stop all hepatotoxic ATT and look for viral and other causes. Continuing with an antiemetic risks fulminant hepatitis; dose reduction is not a recognised strategy; and selectively stopping pyrazinamide guesses the culprit before LFTs settle and sequential reintroduction identifies it.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-dili-herbal-q2`,stem:`A woman has ALT 600 U/L (ULN 40) and ALP 150 U/L (ULN 120). What is the R ratio and injury pattern?`,options:[`R = 4, mixed`,`R = 1.25, cholestatic`,`R = 15, cholestatic`,`R = 12, hepatocellular`],answer:3,explanation:`ALT/ULN is 15 and ALP/ULN is 1.25, so R = 15/1.25 = 12, which is 5 or more and therefore hepatocellular. A ratio of 1.25 is simply the ALP multiple, not R; calling 15 cholestatic inverts the cut-offs; and 4 would be mixed but is arithmetically wrong.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-dili-herbal-q3`,stem:`Hy's law, used to predict severe drug-induced liver injury, requires:`,options:[`ALT over 3 x ULN with bilirubin over 2 x ULN and no initial ALP elevation`,`ALP over 2 x ULN with bilirubin over 3 x ULN`,`ALT over 10 x ULN regardless of bilirubin`,`INR over 1.5 with encephalopathy`],answer:0,explanation:`Hy's law is hepatocellular injury (ALT over 3 x ULN) with jaundice (bilirubin over 2 x ULN), without significant initial cholestasis and no other cause; it predicts about 10% death or transplant. A raised ALP pattern is cholestatic injury, which has a better short-term prognosis; isolated very high ALT without jaundice is not Hy's law; INR with encephalopathy defines acute liver failure.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-dili-herbal-q4`,stem:`A 38-year-old woman took a giloy (Tinospora cordifolia) decoction daily for 4 months. She now has jaundice, ALT 900 U/L, ANA positive and raised IgG. Viral markers are negative. The most appropriate statement is:`,options:[`Herbal products cannot cause liver injury, so this is idiopathic AIH`,`Continue giloy as it is an immunomodulator that will reverse the hepatitis`,`Stop giloy; this is likely herb-induced AIH-like injury, and steroids may be needed if it does not settle`,`Start ursodeoxycholic acid alone and continue the decoction at half strength`],answer:2,explanation:`Tinospora has been linked in Indian series to AIH-like liver injury with ANA and raised IgG; the herb must be stopped and corticosteroids considered for persistent or severe injury, with biopsy if needed. Dismissing herbs as safe misses the diagnosis; continuing the product perpetuates injury; and UDCA has no proven role.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-dili-herbal-q5`,stem:`After ATT-induced hepatitis, ALT has fallen to 55 U/L (ULN 40) and bilirubin is normal. The initial episode was mild and anicteric. According to the ATS sequential approach, what is reintroduced first?`,options:[`Pyrazinamide`,`Isoniazid`,`All three drugs together at half dose`,`Rifampicin with ethambutol`],answer:3,explanation:`ATS 2003 restarts rifampicin (with ethambutol) first once ALT is under 2 x ULN, then adds isoniazid after 3-7 days, then pyrazinamide only if the injury was mild. Pyrazinamide is the most hepatotoxic and is added last or omitted; isoniazid is added second; half-dose triple reintroduction is not a recommended method.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-dili-herbal-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Define drug-induced liver injury. Discuss the risk factors, diagnosis and management of anti-tuberculosis drug-induced hepatotoxicity, including the reintroduction of drugs.`,openingLines:[`Drug-induced liver injury is liver damage caused by a drug, biological or herbal/dietary supplement after exclusion of other causes, defined biochemically as ALT 5 x ULN or more, ALP 2 x ULN or more, or ALT 3 x ULN with bilirubin over 2 x ULN.`,`Anti-tuberculosis drugs are the commonest cause of DILI in India, occurring in 2-28% of treated patients, and the ATS defines ATT hepatotoxicity as ALT over 3 x ULN with symptoms or over 5 x ULN without symptoms.`],answer:[{heading:`Classification and mechanism`,points:[`Intrinsic vs idiosyncratic vs indirect; R ratio hepatocellular, cholestatic, mixed.`,`Isoniazid: NAT2 acetylation to hydrazine, CYP2E1 activation, rifampicin induction; pyrazinamide dose-related; rifampicin cholestasis.`]},{heading:`Risk factors`,points:[`Age over 35-60, alcohol, malnutrition, female, NAT2 slow acetylator, HBV/HCV/HIV, chronic liver disease, pregnancy/postpartum, concomitant hepatotoxic drugs.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Symptoms in first 2-8 weeks; LFT, INR; exclude HAV, HBV, HEV, HCV, alcohol, biliary obstruction by ultrasound.`,`Causality by RUCAM/RECAM; severity by Hy's law and INR.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Stop H, R, Z at threshold; admit if INR 1.5 or more, encephalopathy, jaundice with vomiting.`,`Bridge with ethambutol, levofloxacin and streptomycin/amikacin in severe TB.`,`NAC in adult non-paracetamol ALF; transplant referral.`]},{heading:`Reintroduction`,points:[`When ALT under 2 x ULN: rifampicin + E, then isoniazid at 3-7 days, then pyrazinamide if mild.`,`Omit pyrazinamide after severe injury: 2HRE + 7HR; alternatives if H or R implicated.`,`Indian RCT: simultaneous, sequential and graded methods have similar recurrence (about 10%).`]},{heading:`Prevention and monitoring`,points:[`Baseline LFT in high-risk patients, 2-4 weekly LFT in intensive phase, patient education, avoid alcohol, report to PvPI, record in Ni-kshay.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: ATT hepatotoxicity - stop, investigate, bridge, sequential reintroduction R-H-Z`,`Table of R ratio patterns`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and mechanism`,marks:2},{part:`Risk factors and diagnosis`,marks:3},{part:`Management and reintroduction with flow chart`,marks:4},{part:`Prevention`,marks:1}],keywords:[`DILI`,`ATS threshold`,`R ratio`,`Hy's law`,`NAT2`,`sequential reintroduction`,`NTEP`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-dili-herbal-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on herbal and dietary supplement-induced liver injury in India.`,openingLines:[`Herb- and dietary supplement-induced liver injury is DILI caused by herbal, ayurvedic or other complementary products or by their contaminants, and is the second commonest cause of DILI in Indian registries.`,`It ranges from asymptomatic hepatitis to AIH-like injury, sinusoidal obstruction and acute-on-chronic liver failure with high mortality.`],answer:[{heading:`Culprits`,points:[`Tinospora cordifolia (AIH-like), ashwagandha (cholestatic), turmeric, green tea extract, Garcinia, pyrrolizidine plants (SOS), anabolic steroids.`,`Heavy metals in bhasmas, undeclared steroids and NSAIDs, mislabelled species.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Specific herbal history, product sample, exclusion work-up including HEV and AIH serology, RUCAM/RECAM, LiverTox.`]},{heading:`Management and prevention`,points:[`Stop all products; steroids only for AIH-like injury; NAC and transplant referral for ALF; ACLF care in cirrhosis.`,`Counselling that natural is not safe; avoid CAM in liver disease; report to PvPI and AYUSH pharmacovigilance.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: herb, pattern of injury, key feature`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and burden`,marks:1},{part:`Culprits and mechanisms`,marks:1.5},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:1},{part:`Management and prevention`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`HILI`,`giloy`,`ashwagandha`,`pyrrolizidine`,`heavy metals`,`ACLF`,`PvPI`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Toxic and drug-induced hepatitis`,`Chalasani NP et al. ACG Clinical Guideline: Diagnosis and Management of Idiosyncratic Drug-Induced Liver Injury. Am J Gastroenterol, 2021`,`Fontana RJ et al. AASLD Practice Guidance on Drug, Herbal, and Dietary Supplement-Induced Liver Injury. Hepatology, 2023`,`EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines: Drug-induced liver injury, 2019`,`Saukkonen JJ et al. An Official ATS Statement: Hepatotoxicity of Antituberculosis Therapy, 2006; ATS/CDC/IDSA Treatment of Drug-Susceptible Tuberculosis, 2016`,`Technical Guidance for TB Care and Control in India, National TB Elimination Programme, MoHFW, 2021; India TB Report 2024`,`Hayashi PH et al. RECAM: a revised electronic causality assessment method for DILI. Hepatology, 2022`,`Sharma SK et al. Safety of 3 different reintroduction regimens of antituberculosis drugs after development of antituberculosis treatment-induced hepatotoxicity. Clin Infect Dis, 2010`]}),l.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-anorectal-medical`,title:`Medical management of anorectal disorders and faecal incontinence`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`**Benign anorectal disorders** - haemorrhoids, anal fissure, pruritus ani, dyssynergic defaecation and faecal incontinence - are diagnosed by **history, inspection, digital rectal examination and anoscopy**, managed first with **fibre, stool normalisation and topical therapy** (diltiazem or GTN for fissure; office banding for grade I-III haemorrhoids), with **biofeedback** as the treatment of choice for **dyssynergic defaecation** and refractory **faecal incontinence** (ACG 2021), while **rectal bleeding is never attributed to piles without excluding colorectal cancer**.`,keywords:[`haemorrhoids`,`piles`,`Goligher grading`,`rubber band ligation`,`flavonoids`,`anal fissure`,`fissure-in-ano`,`diltiazem gel`,`glyceryl trinitrate ointment`,`botulinum toxin`,`pruritus ani`,`dyssynergic defaecation`,`anismus`,`anorectal manometry`,`balloon expulsion test`,`biofeedback`,`faecal incontinence`,`St Mark's score`,`levator ani syndrome`,`proctalgia fugax`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Haemorrhoids** are normal vascular cushions of the anal canal; **haemorrhoidal disease** is when they become symptomatic by **bleeding, prolapse, pain or itching** - internal arise above, external below the **dentate line**. [ACG 2021]`,`**Anal fissure** is a longitudinal tear in the anoderm distal to the dentate line; **acute** if under **6-8 weeks**, **chronic** if longer or with chronic features. [ASCRS 2023]`,`**Pruritus ani** is itching of the perianal skin; it is **secondary** to an identifiable cause in most cases, and **idiopathic** when none is found. [ACG 2021]`,`**Dyssynergic defaecation** is failure of coordinated **abdominal push and pelvic floor/anal relaxation** during defaecation - it causes about **one-third of chronic constipation** referred to tertiary centres. [ACG 2021]`,`**Faecal incontinence (FI)** is recurrent uncontrolled passage of faecal material for at least **3 months**; it affects about **8% of community-dwelling adults** (NHANES) and over 40% of nursing-home residents, yet most never report it. [ACG 2021]`,`In India, **'piles' is a lay term for any anal symptom**, and patients often present late after treatment by unqualified 'piles clinics'; **young-onset colorectal cancer** is relatively common in India, so bleeding needs proper evaluation. [Indian Journal of Gastroenterology]`,`**Functional anorectal pain** (levator ani syndrome, proctalgia fugax) affects about **6-8%** of adults in population surveys, women more than men. [Rome IV 2016]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Haemorrhoids**: **straining and prolonged sitting on the toilet** (including mobile-phone use), **constipation or chronic diarrhoea**, low-fibre diet, **pregnancy**, obesity, ageing of supporting tissue, and raised intra-abdominal pressure. [ASCRS 2024]`,`**Fissure**: passage of **hard stool** (or explosive diarrhoea) with **internal anal sphincter hypertonia**; postpartum fissures are often anterior. [ASCRS 2023]`,`**Secondary fissure** (lateral, multiple, painless or non-healing) suggests **Crohn disease, tuberculosis, HIV, syphilis, herpes or anal cancer**. [ASCRS 2023]`,`**Pruritus ani causes**: faecal soiling and moisture, **over-cleaning with soaps or wipes**, dietary triggers (coffee, tea, citrus, tomato, chocolate, spices, beer), haemorrhoids, fissure, fistula, **threadworm (Enterobius)**, candida, dermatophyte, scabies, **contact dermatitis, psoriasis, lichen sclerosus**, diabetes, cholestasis and **anal intraepithelial neoplasia**. [ACG 2021]`,`**Dyssynergia risk factors**: learned behaviour from childhood withholding, painful defaecation (fissure), **history of sexual or physical abuse**, and post-surgical or obstetric pelvic floor change. [ACG 2021]`,`**Faecal incontinence causes**: **diarrhoea** (the commonest reversible factor), **obstetric anal sphincter injury** (third/fourth-degree tears, forceps), anorectal surgery (sphincterotomy, fistulotomy, haemorrhoidectomy), **diabetes and spinal or neurological disease**, rectal prolapse, radiation proctitis, and **faecal impaction with overflow** in the elderly. [ACG 2021]`,`**Functional anorectal pain** is linked to **pelvic floor muscle hypertonicity**, anxiety and stress. [Rome IV 2016]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - anatomy that explains the treatment`,points:[`Above the dentate line the mucosa is **viscerally innervated** (painless), so **internal haemorrhoids bleed but do not hurt**; below it the anoderm is **somatically innervated** (pudendal nerve), so fissures and thrombosed external haemorrhoids are **intensely painful**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Haemorrhoidal disease** results from **degeneration of the supporting Treitz muscle and connective tissue**, allowing cushions to slide down and engorge; bright red bleeding is **arterial** (presinusoidal arteriovenous communications). [ASCRS 2024]`,`**Fissure cycle**: hard stool tears anoderm, pain causes **internal anal sphincter spasm**, raised resting pressure reduces flow through the **poorly perfused posterior commissure**, producing **ischaemia and non-healing** - hence drugs that relax the sphincter (nitrates, calcium-channel blockers, botulinum) heal fissures. [ASCRS 2023]`,`**Continence** depends on the **internal anal sphincter** (smooth muscle, about 70-85% of resting tone - passive leakage when weak), **external anal sphincter and puborectalis** (striated, voluntary squeeze - urge incontinence when weak), rectal compliance and sensation, stool consistency and cognition. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Normal defaecation** requires a rise in **intrarectal pressure** with **relaxation of puborectalis and the external sphincter**, straightening the anorectal angle; in **dyssynergia** the sphincter **paradoxically contracts** or fails to relax. [ACG 2021]`,`**Biofeedback** works by **operant conditioning**: real-time manometric or EMG display lets patients learn to relax the pelvic floor while pushing, and to retrain rectal sensation and sphincter strength in incontinence. [ANMS-ESNM 2015]`,`**Pruritus ani** is perpetuated by the **itch-scratch cycle**: moisture and faecal proteases irritate skin, scratching causes lichenification and further itch. [ACG 2021]`]},{heading:`Classification and grading`,points:[`**Goligher grading of internal haemorrhoids**: **I** bleed, no prolapse; **II** prolapse, reduce spontaneously; **III** prolapse, need **manual reduction**; **IV** permanently prolapsed, irreducible. [ASCRS 2024]`,`**Chronic fissure features**: **sentinel skin tag**, **hypertrophied anal papilla**, exposed **internal sphincter fibres** at the base, and indurated edges. [ASCRS 2023]`,`**Washington classification of pruritus ani**: **stage 0** normal skin, **stage 1** red and inflamed, **stage 2** white lichenified skin, **stage 3** lichenification with coarse ridges and often ulceration. [ASCRS]`,`**Rome IV functional defaecation disorder**: functional constipation or IBS-C plus **2 of 3 abnormal tests** - anorectal manometry/EMG, **balloon expulsion** and defaecography - subtyped as **inadequate propulsion** or **dyssynergic defaecation**. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Rao types of dyssynergia (manometry)**: type I adequate push with paradoxical anal contraction; type II inadequate push with paradoxical contraction; type III adequate push with absent or incomplete relaxation; type IV inadequate push with absent relaxation. [ACG 2021]`,`**Faecal incontinence types**: **passive** (unaware - internal sphincter), **urge** (aware but cannot defer - external sphincter or reduced rectal capacity), and **seepage/soiling** after normal evacuation; severity by **St Mark's (Vaizey) score 0-24** or **Wexner score 0-20**. [ACG 2021]`,`**Functional anorectal pain (Rome IV)**: **levator ani syndrome** (pain 30 minutes or longer with puborectalis tenderness), **unspecified** (no tenderness), and **proctalgia fugax** (sudden pain lasting **seconds to 30 minutes**, symptom-free between). [Rome IV 2016]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Internal haemorrhoids**: **painless bright red bleeding** on the toilet paper, dripping in the pan, or streaking the stool, with or without prolapse, mucus and itching. [ASCRS 2024]`,`**Thrombosed external haemorrhoid**: sudden **painful perianal lump**, worst in the first **48-72 hours**. [ASCRS 2024]`,`**Fissure**: **sharp, tearing pain during defaecation lasting minutes to hours afterwards**, with small amounts of bright blood; patients fear and avoid defaecation, worsening constipation. [ASCRS 2023]`,`**Dyssynergia**: excessive straining, **sensation of incomplete evacuation or blockage**, prolonged toilet time, and **digital assistance** (vaginal splinting or perineal pressure) despite soft stool. [ACG 2021]`,`**Faecal incontinence**: ask directly and sensitively - frequency, stool type (Bristol), **urge vs passive**, pad use, impact on life, obstetric history (tears, forceps), surgery, diabetes, neurological symptoms and drugs (laxatives, metformin, magnesium, orlistat). [ACG 2021]`,`**Pruritus ani**: nocturnal itch (threadworm in children and families), hygiene practices, wipes and creams used, diet triggers, and itching at other sites (psoriasis, lichen sclerosus). [ACG 2021]`,`**Alarm features**: age over 45-50 with new bleeding, **change in bowel habit**, iron deficiency anaemia, weight loss, family history of colorectal cancer, dark or mixed blood, tenesmus or a mass. [BSG 2021]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Examine in **left lateral position** with a chaperone: inspection with gentle buttock separation, then **digital rectal examination (DRE)**, then **anoscopy/proctoscopy**. [ACG 2021]`,`**Fissure**: seen on parting the buttocks, usually **posterior midline (about 90%)**; anterior in **10-25% of women**; **avoid DRE and anoscopy if acutely painful** - the history and inspection suffice. [ASCRS 2023]`,`**Internal haemorrhoids** are not palpable on DRE unless thrombosed; they are seen on anoscopy at the **3, 7 and 11 o'clock** positions (left lateral, right anterior, right posterior). [ASCRS 2024]`,`**DRE for dyssynergia**: on asking the patient to bear down, **paradoxical anal contraction or failure of perineal descent** - DRE has about **75% sensitivity and 87% specificity** against manometry. [ACG 2021]`,`**DRE in faecal incontinence**: low **resting tone** (internal sphincter), weak **squeeze** (external sphincter), a **gaping anus**, scars, perineal body thinning, **impacted stool**, and the **anal wink reflex** (S2-4). [ACG 2021]`,`**Pruritus ani skin**: erythema, maceration, lichenification (Washington stage), **satellite lesions** (candida), **porcelain-white atrophic plaques** (lichen sclerosus), and threadworms seen at night or on sticky-tape test. [ACG 2021]`,`**Levator ani syndrome**: **tenderness on posterior traction of the puborectalis**, more on the left. [Rome IV 2016]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Colorectal cancer** - dark or mixed blood, change in bowel habit, anaemia, weight loss, age over 45; colonoscopy required. [BSG 2021]`,`**Anal cancer** - non-healing ulcer, induration, inguinal nodes, HIV or HPV risk; biopsy any atypical fissure. [ASCRS 2023]`,`**Inflammatory bowel disease** - lateral or multiple fissures, fistulae, skin tags with oedema, diarrhoea; see Crohn disease topic. [ACG 2021]`,`**Perianal abscess or fistula** - throbbing constant pain, fever, swelling, discharge; needs surgical drainage. [ASCRS 2022]`,`**Rectal prolapse** - concentric mucosal rings vs radial folds of haemorrhoids; examine while straining on a commode. [ASCRS]`,`**Solitary rectal ulcer syndrome** - straining, mucus, bleeding, anterior rectal ulcer; associated with dyssynergia. [ACG 2021]`,`**Slow-transit constipation and IBS-C** - normal balloon expulsion; dyssynergia must be excluded first because it is treatable by biofeedback. [ACG 2021]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Anoscopy/proctoscopy** in OPD confirms haemorrhoids and fissure; **flexible sigmoidoscopy** for under-45s with typical bleeding not settling, and **colonoscopy** for alarm features or age over 45-50. [ASCRS 2024]`,`**Haemoglobin and ferritin** in any patient with bleeding; **iron deficiency must not be blamed on haemorrhoids** until the colon is examined. [BSG 2021]`,`**Anorectal manometry (ARM)** measures resting and squeeze pressures, recto-anal inhibitory reflex, sensation and push dynamics. [ANMS-ESNM 2015]`,`**Balloon expulsion test**: inability to expel a **50 mL water-filled balloon within 1-2 minutes** suggests an evacuation disorder - a simple, cheap screening test. [ACG 2021]`,`**Defaecography** (barium or MR) shows inadequate evacuation, rectocele, intussusception and perineal descent when ARM and BET are discordant. [ACG 2021]`,`**Endoanal ultrasound** detects **sphincter defects** in faecal incontinence (obstetric injury); **stool studies, TSH, glucose and coeliac serology** when diarrhoea drives incontinence. [ACG 2021]`,`**Pruritus ani**: sticky-tape test for threadworm, skin scraping for fungus, **biopsy** of persistent or atypical skin, and glucose. [ACG 2021]`]},{heading:`OPD management - haemorrhoids and fissure`,points:[`**Fibre for all**: **psyllium (ispaghula) 3.5 g one to three times daily** with adequate water reduces bleeding by about **50%**; avoid straining and limit toilet time to **3-5 minutes** (no phone). [ASCRS 2024]`,`**Flavonoids** (micronised purified flavonoid fraction, diosmin-hesperidin) - **1000 mg three times daily for 4 days, then twice daily for 3 days** for acute symptoms - reduce bleeding and pain in short courses. [ESCP 2020]`,`**Topical agents** (lidocaine, short-term hydrocortisone for **no more than 7 days**) give symptom relief only; avoid prolonged steroids (skin atrophy). [ASCRS 2024]`,`**Office procedures for grade I-III internal haemorrhoids** failing conservative care: **rubber band ligation** is most effective; sclerotherapy and infrared coagulation for grade I-II; **haemorrhoidectomy** for grade III-IV or mixed disease. [ASCRS 2024]`,`**Thrombosed external haemorrhoid**: **excision under local anaesthetic within 72 hours** of onset; later, conservative care with topical **nifedipine 0.3% with lidocaine 1.5%**, sitz baths and analgesia. [ASCRS 2024]`,`**Acute fissure**: fibre, **warm sitz baths** for 10-15 minutes two to three times daily, topical lidocaine 2-5% - about **50% heal** with these alone. [ASCRS 2023]`,`**Chronic fissure first line**: topical **diltiazem 2% twice daily for 6-8 weeks** (as effective as GTN, fewer headaches) or **GTN 0.2-0.4% twice daily** (healing about 50-60%; **headache in 20-30%**). [ASCRS 2023]`,`**Second line**: **botulinum toxin injection (20-100 units)** into the internal sphincter - healing about 60-80%, transient minor incontinence; **lateral internal sphincterotomy** is the surgical gold standard (over **90% healing**, small risk of incontinence). [ASCRS 2023]`]},{heading:`OPD management - pruritus ani, dyssynergia, incontinence and anorectal pain`,points:[`**Pruritus ani**: treat the cause; **wash with water only, pat dry, no wipes or perfumed soap**, loose cotton underwear, remove dietary triggers, **zinc oxide barrier cream**, and **1% hydrocortisone for no more than 2 weeks**. [ACG 2021]`,`**Refractory idiopathic pruritus ani**: topical **capsaicin 0.006%**, **tacrolimus 0.03-0.1%**, or intradermal **methylene blue** injection by a specialist; threadworm: **albendazole 400 mg once, repeated at 2 weeks**, treating the whole household. [ACG 2021]`,`**Dyssynergic defaecation**: **biofeedback therapy** is first-line (ACG 2021 strong) - typically **4-6 sessions** over 2-3 months, with **70-80% improvement**, superior to laxatives and sham in RCTs. [ACG 2021]`,`Alongside biofeedback: **adequate fibre and osmotic laxative**, **toilet posture** (knees above hips with a footstool, leaning forward), and timed toileting after meals (gastrocolic reflex). [ACG 2021]`,`**Faecal incontinence first line**: **normalise stool** - fibre supplement (psyllium) for loose stools, **loperamide 2 mg before meals, titrated up to 16 mg/day**, cholestyramine for bile acid diarrhoea, stop offending drugs. [ACG 2021]`,`**Next**: **pelvic floor muscle training with biofeedback** for patients not responding to conservative care; **anal plugs**, **transanal irrigation** and skin care; then specialist options - **sacral nerve stimulation**, perianal bulking agents, sphincteroplasty for acute obstetric defects. [ACG 2021]`,`**Overflow incontinence from impaction** (elderly, bedridden): **disimpaction** with enemas, then regular osmotic laxative and scheduled toileting. [ACG 2021]`,`**Levator ani syndrome**: reassurance, sitz baths, **biofeedback** (ACG preferred), digital massage; **proctalgia fugax**: reassurance and **inhaled salbutamol** for prolonged attacks. [ACG 2021]`]},{heading:`IPD and emergency`,points:[`**Massive haemorrhoidal bleeding** is uncommon; if haemodynamically unstable, treat as **lower GI bleeding** and exclude an upper source - see the Upper and lower GI bleeding topic. [ACG 2023]`,`**Strangulated or gangrenous grade IV haemorrhoids**: admit, analgesia, ice, elevation, and urgent surgical review. [ASCRS 2024]`,`**Perianal abscess**: **incision and drainage** is the treatment; antibiotics are added for cellulitis, diabetes, immunosuppression or sepsis. [ASCRS 2022]`,`**Post-banding sepsis** (pain, fever, urinary retention after RBL) is rare but life-threatening - admit for IV antibiotics and imaging. [ASCRS 2024]`,`**Faecal impaction** with abdominal distension or vomiting: admit, disimpact, exclude obstruction and stercoral perforation (CT). [ACG 2021]`,`**Incontinence-associated dermatitis** in inpatients: no-rinse cleanser, barrier film or zinc cream, and treat diarrhoea or C. difficile. [ACG 2021]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: haemorrhoids peak in the **third trimester and postpartum**; use fibre, bulk/osmotic laxatives, sitz baths and short topical therapy; defer banding and surgery unless complicated. [ASCRS 2024]`,`**Postpartum fissure** often anterior; **diltiazem** topical is preferred in breastfeeding women; GTN is acceptable. [ASCRS 2023]`,`**Obstetric anal sphincter injury**: recognise and repair at delivery; **physiotherapy and pelvic floor training** afterwards; offer endoanal ultrasound if symptomatic and discuss mode of next delivery. [RCOG Green-top 29]`,`**Elderly**: impaction with overflow is the commonest cause of incontinence in care homes; check for dementia, immobility and drugs; avoid excessive laxatives. [ACG 2021]`,`**Children**: fissure is commonly due to constipation - treat with **PEG** and toilet training; **threadworm** causes nocturnal pruritus; rectal prolapse suggests malnutrition or cystic fibrosis. [ESPGHAN/NASPGHAN 2014]`,`**Anticoagulated or cirrhotic patients**: bleeding needs full evaluation; **anorectal varices** differ from haemorrhoids and must not be banded casually. [ASCRS 2024]`,`**HIV and immunosuppressed**: atypical fissures and ulcers may be **CMV, HSV, syphilis, TB or anal cancer** - biopsy early; avoid office banding in uncontrolled HIV or severe neutropenia. [ASCRS 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Haemorrhoids**: anaemia (rare and a diagnosis of exclusion), thrombosis, strangulation; RBL complications include pain, delayed bleeding at **7-10 days** (higher on antithrombotics) and rare pelvic sepsis. [ASCRS 2024]`,`**Haemorrhoid recurrence** after banding is about **20-30%** at 2 years but repeat banding is effective; haemorrhoidectomy has the lowest recurrence but most pain. [ASCRS 2024]`,`**Chronic fissure**: about **25-50% recur** after topical therapy; sphincterotomy recurrence is under 10% with a small risk of **flatus or minor faecal incontinence**. [ASCRS 2023]`,`**Untreated dyssynergia** leads to laxative escalation, rectocele, solitary rectal ulcer, and poor quality of life; biofeedback gains persist for **over 1 year** in most. [ACG 2021]`,`**Faecal incontinence** causes social isolation, depression, skin breakdown, and is a leading reason for **nursing-home admission**. [ACG 2021]`,`**Missed colorectal or anal cancer** is the most serious complication of labelling bleeding as 'piles'. [BSG 2021]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and prevention`,points:[`**Refer for colonoscopy**: bleeding with age over 45-50, change in bowel habit, IDA, weight loss, family history, or bleeding not settling with treatment. [BSG 2021]`,`**Refer to surgeon**: grade III-IV or symptomatic refractory haemorrhoids, chronic fissure failing 8 weeks of topical therapy, abscess, fistula, prolapse, or atypical fissure. [ASCRS 2023]`,`**Refer for anorectal physiology and biofeedback**: chronic constipation not responding to laxatives with features of an evacuation disorder, and FI not responding to stool normalisation. [ACG 2021]`,`**Review fissure at 6-8 weeks** of topical therapy; **review FI with a bowel diary** at 4-6 weeks after stool modification. [ASCRS 2023]`,`**Biopsy and dermatology referral** for pruritus ani not responding to 4-6 weeks of measures, or with atypical skin. [ACG 2021]`,`**Dietary fibre 25-30 g/day** from whole grains, pulses, fruit and vegetables, with adequate water - the foundation for haemorrhoids, fissure and constipation. [ICMR-NIN Dietary Guidelines 2024]`,`**Bowel habit counselling**: respond to the urge, avoid straining, **squatting or footstool posture**, no reading or phone on the toilet. [ACG 2021]`,`**Obstetric prevention**: perineal protection and **recognition and repair of sphincter tears** reduce lifelong incontinence; pelvic floor exercises antenatally and postnatally. [RCOG Green-top 29]`,`**Destigmatise incontinence** - ask routinely in elderly, diabetic and postpartum women at Ayushman Arogya Mandir and NP-NCD clinics; most will not volunteer it. [ACG 2021]`,`Counsel against **untrained 'piles clinics'** that inject or apply caustic agents; **Ksharasutra (ayurvedic medicated seton)** has trial evidence for fistula-in-ano and should be given by trained practitioners. [ICMR multicentric trial]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2021-2026)`,points:[`**ACG Clinical Guideline: Management of Benign Anorectal Disorders (2021)** - strong recommendations for **biofeedback in dyssynergic defaecation** and for FI not responding to conservative measures; DRE plus ARM and BET to diagnose evacuation disorders. [ACG 2021]`,`**ASCRS Clinical Practice Guideline for Anal Fissures (2023)** - topical **calcium-channel blockers as first line** (fewer side effects than nitrates), botulinum toxin or sphincterotomy for refractory chronic fissure. [ASCRS 2023]`,`**ASCRS Clinical Practice Guideline for Haemorrhoids (2024)** - fibre for all, **RBL as the preferred office procedure** for grade I-III, early excision of thrombosed external haemorrhoids within 72 hours. [ASCRS 2024]`,`**ASCRS Clinical Practice Guideline for Faecal Incontinence (2023)** - stool bulking and antidiarrhoeals first, biofeedback and sacral neuromodulation for refractory cases, sphincteroplasty reserved for selected defects. [ASCRS 2023]`,`**ESCP haemorrhoid guideline (2020)** supports flavonoids for short-term symptom control and tailoring procedures to symptoms rather than grade alone. [ESCP 2020]`,`**London classification (2020)** of anorectal manometry findings standardises reporting of dyssynergia and hyposensitivity. [International Anorectal Physiology Working Group 2020]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Viva: **'Why are internal haemorrhoids painless?'** - above the dentate line, visceral innervation. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: **'Why does a fissure fail to heal?'** - sphincter spasm causing posterior commissure ischaemia; hence chemical sphincterotomy. [ASCRS 2023]`,`Viva: **'What does a lateral fissure suggest?'** - Crohn disease, TB, HIV, syphilis or malignancy. [ASCRS 2023]`,`Viva: **'Goligher grade III?'** - prolapse requiring manual reduction. [ASCRS 2024]`,`Viva: **'Treatment of dyssynergic defaecation?'** - biofeedback, not more laxatives. [ACG 2021]`,`Common mistake: **doing DRE or proctoscopy in an acute painful fissure** - inspect only. [ASCRS 2023]`,`Common mistake: **attributing bleeding or anaemia to piles** in an older patient without colonoscopy. [BSG 2021]`,`Common mistake: **forgetting to ask about incontinence** - it is under-reported in over half of sufferers. [ACG 2021]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drug table - anorectal disorders`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Psyllium (ispaghula)`,`3.5 g orally one to three times daily with 250 mL water`,`Long term`,`Bloating; avoid in obstruction or dysphagia`],[`Micronised purified flavonoid fraction`,`1000 mg orally three times daily x 4 days, then twice daily x 3 days`,`Short courses`,`GI upset, headache`],[`Diltiazem 2% ointment`,`Pea-sized amount to anal margin twice daily`,`6-8 weeks`,`Perianal itch, dermatitis; few headaches`],[`Glyceryl trinitrate 0.2-0.4% ointment`,`Apply to anal margin twice daily`,`6-8 weeks`,`Headache (20-30%), hypotension; avoid with PDE5 inhibitors`],[`Botulinum toxin A`,`20-100 units injected into internal sphincter`,`Single, may repeat`,`Transient flatus or faecal incontinence, perianal haematoma`],[`Lidocaine 2-5% / hydrocortisone 1%`,`Topically two to three times daily`,`Hydrocortisone no more than 7-14 days`,`Contact sensitisation, skin atrophy with prolonged steroid`],[`Loperamide`,`2 mg orally before meals; titrate up to 16 mg/day`,`Long term, titrate to stool`,`Constipation, bloating; avoid in C. difficile or IBD flare`],[`Albendazole (threadworm)`,`400 mg orally once, repeat at 2 weeks`,`Treat household`,`Avoid in first trimester`],[`Salbutamol inhaler (proctalgia fugax)`,`200 mcg inhaled at onset`,`As needed`,`Tremor, tachycardia`]]},{heading:`Goligher grading and treatment of internal haemorrhoids`,columns:[`Grade`,`Description`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`I`,`Bleeding, no prolapse`,`Fibre, toilet habits, flavonoids; sclerotherapy, IRC or RBL if persistent`],[`II`,`Prolapse reducing spontaneously`,`Conservative, then rubber band ligation`],[`III`,`Prolapse needing manual reduction`,`RBL; haemorrhoidectomy or haemorrhoidopexy if fails`],[`IV`,`Irreducible prolapse`,`Excisional haemorrhoidectomy`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of perianal pain and bleeding`,columns:[`Condition`,`Pain`,`Bleeding`,`Discriminating feature`],rows:[[`Internal haemorrhoids`,`Painless`,`Bright red, on paper or dripping`,`Anoscopy at 3, 7, 11 o'clock`],[`Anal fissure`,`Sharp, during and after defaecation`,`Small, bright red`,`Posterior midline tear on inspection`],[`Thrombosed external haemorrhoid`,`Acute constant, 48-72 h peak`,`Only if ulcerated`,`Tender blue perianal lump`],[`Perianal abscess`,`Throbbing, constant, fever`,`Pus rather than blood`,`Fluctuant tender swelling`],[`Levator ani syndrome`,`Dull ache over 30 min`,`None`,`Puborectalis tenderness`],[`Proctalgia fugax`,`Sudden, seconds to 30 min`,`None`,`Normal examination between attacks`],[`Colorectal or anal cancer`,`Often painless; tenesmus`,`Dark or mixed, with mucus`,`Mass, anaemia, change in bowel habit`]]},{heading:`Tests for evacuation disorders and incontinence`,columns:[`Test`,`What it shows`,`Abnormal finding`],rows:[[`DRE with push`,`Sphincter relaxation and descent`,`Paradoxical contraction; poor perineal descent`],[`Balloon expulsion test`,`Ability to evacuate`,`Fails to expel 50 mL balloon within 1-2 minutes`],[`Anorectal manometry`,`Resting/squeeze pressures, RAIR, push pattern`,`Dyssynergia types I-IV; low resting (IAS) or squeeze (EAS) pressure`],[`Defaecography`,`Structural and functional evacuation`,`Rectocele, intussusception, poor emptying`],[`Endoanal ultrasound`,`Sphincter integrity`,`IAS or EAS defect (obstetric injury)`]]},{heading:`Faecal incontinence - type and mechanism`,columns:[`Type`,`Patient experience`,`Mechanism`,`Mainstay`],rows:[[`Passive`,`Unaware of leakage`,`Internal anal sphincter weakness or neuropathy`,`Stool bulking, loperamide, plugs`],[`Urge`,`Aware but cannot reach toilet`,`External sphincter weakness, low rectal capacity`,`Biofeedback, loperamide, timed toileting`],[`Seepage`,`Soiling after normal stool`,`Incomplete evacuation, haemorrhoids, dyssynergia`,`Evacuation retraining, hygiene`],[`Overflow`,`Liquid leakage around impaction`,`Faecal impaction (elderly)`,`Disimpaction, regular laxatives`]]}],redFlags:[`Rectal bleeding with change in bowel habit, weight loss or iron deficiency anaemia, especially age over 45`,`Palpable rectal or anal mass, or a non-healing, indurated or lateral fissure`,`Fever with throbbing perianal pain and swelling - perianal abscess`,`Severe pain, fever and urinary retention after haemorrhoid banding - pelvic sepsis`,`Irreducible, strangulated or gangrenous haemorrhoids`,`New faecal incontinence with leg weakness, saddle anaesthesia or urinary retention - cauda equina`,`Haemodynamic instability from rectal bleeding`],pearls:[`**Above the dentate line - painless; below - painful.**`,`**Haemorrhoids at 3, 7, 11 o'clock** in lithotomy.`,`**Posterior midline fissure is primary; lateral fissure is secondary** until proven otherwise.`,`**Fissure triad**: sentinel tag, hypertrophied papilla, fissure - chronic.`,`**Diltiazem first** - as good as GTN with fewer headaches.`,`**Dyssynergia needs biofeedback, not more laxatives.**`,`**Balloon expulsion test: 50 mL in under 1-2 minutes.**`,`**Passive leakage = internal sphincter; urge leakage = external sphincter.**`,`**Never blame anaemia on piles** without a colonoscopy.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-anorectal-medical-q1`,stem:`A 30-year-old woman has severe tearing pain with each bowel movement for 3 months and a small posterior midline tear with a sentinel tag. The preferred first-line pharmacological treatment is:`,options:[`Topical diltiazem 2% twice daily for 6-8 weeks`,`Oral metronidazole for 2 weeks`,`Topical hydrocortisone for 3 months`,`Immediate lateral internal sphincterotomy`],answer:0,explanation:`A chronic posterior fissure is treated first with chemical sphincterotomy; topical diltiazem heals as well as GTN with fewer headaches and is favoured by ASCRS 2023. Metronidazole has no role without abscess or Crohn disease, prolonged topical steroids cause atrophy and do not heal fissures, and sphincterotomy is reserved for failure of medical therapy.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-anorectal-medical-q2`,stem:`Internal haemorrhoids that prolapse on straining and need to be pushed back manually are Goligher grade:`,options:[`I`,`II`,`IV`,`III`],answer:3,explanation:`Grade III haemorrhoids prolapse and require manual reduction. Grade I bleed without prolapse, grade II prolapse but reduce spontaneously, and grade IV are permanently prolapsed and irreducible - the distinction drives treatment from banding to haemorrhoidectomy.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-anorectal-medical-q3`,stem:`A 40-year-old woman has chronic constipation with excessive straining and needs vaginal splinting despite soft stools on PEG. On DRE, the anal sphincter contracts when she bears down. She cannot expel a 50 mL balloon in 3 minutes. Best treatment:`,options:[`Increase PEG and add bisacodyl`,`Biofeedback therapy`,`Subtotal colectomy`,`Lateral internal sphincterotomy`],answer:1,explanation:`Paradoxical sphincter contraction with failed balloon expulsion indicates dyssynergic defaecation, for which ACG 2021 strongly recommends biofeedback (70-80% respond). Escalating laxatives does not fix the coordination problem, colectomy is for proven refractory slow transit without dyssynergia, and sphincterotomy treats fissure and would risk incontinence.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-anorectal-medical-q4`,stem:`A 62-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes reports leakage of loose stool without awareness. Resting anal tone is reduced, squeeze is normal. The most appropriate first step is:`,options:[`Referral for sacral nerve stimulation`,`Overlapping sphincteroplasty`,`Review drugs such as metformin, normalise stool with fibre and loperamide, and keep a bowel diary`,`Daily phosphate enema`],answer:2,explanation:`Passive incontinence with loose stools is first managed by stool normalisation - fibre, loperamide titration and removing culprits such as metformin - as recommended by ACG 2021. Sacral nerve stimulation and sphincteroplasty are later options for refractory cases or defined defects, and phosphate enemas are hazardous in diabetics with possible CKD.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-anorectal-medical-q5`,stem:`A 35-year-old man has an anal fissure at the 3 o'clock position, along with recurrent perianal discharge and weight loss. The most important next step is:`,options:[`Topical GTN and review in 8 weeks`,`Botulinum toxin injection`,`Ispaghula and sitz baths only`,`Evaluate for secondary causes such as Crohn disease, TB, HIV and malignancy, including colonoscopy and biopsy`],answer:3,explanation:`A lateral fissure with discharge and weight loss is atypical and suggests Crohn disease, TB, HIV, syphilis or cancer; it needs evaluation and biopsy before treatment. GTN, botulinum toxin or fibre alone treat a primary posterior fissure and would delay diagnosis of the underlying disease.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-anorectal-medical-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Discuss the evaluation and medical management of faecal incontinence in adults. Add a note on dyssynergic defaecation and biofeedback.`,openingLines:[`Faecal incontinence is the recurrent uncontrolled passage of faecal material for at least 3 months; it affects about 8% of community adults and is under-reported because patients rarely volunteer it.`,`Continence depends on stool consistency, the internal and external anal sphincters, puborectalis, rectal compliance and sensation, and cognition - treatment starts with normalising stool and progresses to biofeedback and specialist procedures (ACG 2021).`],answer:[{heading:`Mechanisms and causes`,points:[`Internal sphincter (passive leak), external sphincter and puborectalis (urge leak), rectal reservoir and sensation.`,`Causes: diarrhoea, obstetric injury, anorectal surgery, diabetes, neurological disease, prolapse, radiation, impaction with overflow.`]},{heading:`Evaluation`,points:[`History: type (passive, urge, seepage), frequency, stool form, obstetric, surgical, drug history; St Mark's or Wexner score; bowel diary.`,`Examination: perineum, DRE resting tone and squeeze, push, anal wink, impaction, neurological exam.`,`Tests: stool studies and colonoscopy if diarrhoea or alarm features; anorectal manometry, endoanal ultrasound.`]},{heading:`Management ladder`,points:[`Stool normalisation: fibre, loperamide 2 mg titrated to 16 mg/day, cholestyramine; stop culprits.`,`Skin care, scheduled toileting, anal plugs, transanal irrigation.`,`Pelvic floor training with biofeedback for non-responders.`,`Specialist: sacral nerve stimulation, bulking agents, sphincteroplasty for defects, stoma as last resort.`]},{heading:`Dyssynergic defaecation`,points:[`Paradoxical contraction or failed relaxation of the anal sphincter on pushing; Rome IV needs 2 of 3 abnormal tests (ARM, BET, defaecography).`,`Rao types I-IV by push and relaxation pattern.`]},{heading:`Biofeedback`,points:[`Operant conditioning using manometry or EMG display; 4-6 sessions; 70-80% respond; ACG 2021 strong recommendation for dyssynergia and refractory incontinence.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of the anal sphincter complex: IAS, EAS, puborectalis and anorectal angle`,`Faecal incontinence management ladder`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and mechanisms`,marks:2},{part:`Evaluation`,marks:3},{part:`Management ladder`,marks:3},{part:`Dyssynergia and biofeedback`,marks:2}],keywords:[`faecal incontinence`,`IAS`,`EAS`,`loperamide`,`biofeedback`,`sacral nerve stimulation`,`dyssynergia`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-anorectal-medical-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate between internal haemorrhoids and anal fissure, and outline the medical management of each.`,openingLines:[`Internal haemorrhoids arise above the dentate line and cause painless bright red bleeding, whereas an anal fissure is a tear below the dentate line causing severe pain on defaecation.`,`Both are first treated with fibre and stool softening; fissures need chemical sphincterotomy, haemorrhoids office banding when symptomatic.`],answer:[{heading:`Differences`,points:[`Site: above vs below dentate line; innervation visceral vs somatic.`,`Pain: absent vs severe during and after defaecation; bleeding: dripping vs streak.`,`Examination: anoscopy at 3, 7, 11 o'clock vs posterior midline tear on inspection, DRE avoided.`,`Pathophysiology: cushion slide vs sphincter spasm and ischaemia.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Haemorrhoids: fibre, toilet habits, flavonoids, short topical agents; RBL grade I-III; surgery III-IV.`,`Fissure: fibre, sitz baths, lidocaine; diltiazem 2% or GTN 0.2-0.4% for 6-8 weeks; botulinum toxin or sphincterotomy if refractory.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column comparison table`,`Sketch of anal canal showing dentate line, haemorrhoid positions and posterior fissure`],markSplit:[{part:`Differentiating features`,marks:3},{part:`Management of each`,marks:2}],keywords:[`dentate line`,`Goligher`,`RBL`,`diltiazem`,`GTN`,`sentinel tag`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Diseases of the anorectum`,`Wald A et al. ACG Clinical Guidelines: Management of Benign Anorectal Disorders. Am J Gastroenterol, 2021`,`ASCRS Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Management of Anal Fissures. Dis Colon Rectum, 2023`,`ASCRS Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Management of Hemorrhoids. Dis Colon Rectum, 2024`,`ASCRS Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Management of Fecal Incontinence. Dis Colon Rectum, 2023`,`van Tol RR et al. European Society of Coloproctology: guideline for haemorrhoidal disease, 2020`,`Rao SSC et al. ANMS-ESNM position paper and consensus guidelines on biofeedback therapy for anorectal disorders, 2015; Rome IV Functional Anorectal Disorders, 2016`]}),l.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-occult-gi-bleed-ida`,title:`Occult GI bleeding and iron deficiency anaemia work-up`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Iron deficiency anaemia (IDA)** - low haemoglobin with **ferritin under 45 ug/L** (or under 100 with inflammation) - in a **man or postmenopausal woman** is **occult GI blood loss or malabsorption until proven otherwise** and needs **coeliac serology, urinalysis and bidirectional endoscopy** (BSG 2021, AGA 2020); when OGD and colonoscopy are negative, persistent or recurrent bleeding is **suspected small bowel bleeding** investigated by **capsule endoscopy** and enteroscopy, while iron is replaced orally (**one tablet daily or alternate days**) or **intravenously** when oral iron fails or is unsuitable.`,keywords:[`iron deficiency anaemia`,`IDA`,`occult GI bleeding`,`obscure GI bleeding`,`small bowel bleeding`,`ferritin`,`transferrin saturation`,`bidirectional endoscopy`,`capsule endoscopy`,`double-balloon enteroscopy`,`angioectasia`,`Heyde syndrome`,`Cameron lesion`,`FIT`,`ferric carboxymaltose`,`iron sucrose`,`ferric derisomaltose`,`Ganzoni formula`,`Anemia Mukt Bharat`,`hookworm`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Anaemia** (WHO 2024): haemoglobin under **130 g/L in men** and under **120 g/L in non-pregnant women**; **iron deficiency** is best shown by a **low serum ferritin** - under **15 ug/L is diagnostic**, and **under 45 ug/L** is the practical cut-off recommended for GI work-up. [WHO 2024, AGA 2020]`,`**Occult GI bleeding** is bleeding not visible to the patient, detected by **iron deficiency anaemia or a positive faecal occult blood test**; **overt bleeding** is visible melaena or haematochezia. [ACG 2015]`,`**Small bowel bleeding** (replacing the old term 'obscure GI bleeding') is bleeding from between the **ampulla of Vater and the ileocaecal valve**, suspected when **OGD and colonoscopy are negative**; it accounts for about **5-10% of GI bleeding**. [ACG 2015]`,`**Obscure GI bleeding** is now reserved for bleeding with **no source found after small bowel evaluation** (capsule and/or enteroscopy and imaging) as well. [ACG 2015]`,`**India (NFHS-5, 2019-21)**: anaemia in **57% of women 15-49 years**, **52% of pregnant women**, **25% of men** and **67% of children 6-59 months** - most is iron deficiency. [NFHS-5]`,`In **men and postmenopausal women with IDA**, GI investigation finds a cause in about **two-thirds**; **colorectal cancer in about 5-10%** and **upper GI cancer in 1-2%**; **dual pathology** (upper and lower) in **1-10%**. [BSG 2021]`,`Globally, IDA is the **commonest nutritional deficiency** and the leading cause of years lived with disability from anaemia (Global Burden of Disease). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Blood loss - upper GI**: peptic ulcer and **NSAID/aspirin erosions**, oesophagitis, **gastric cancer**, **Cameron lesions** in a large hiatus hernia, **gastric antral vascular ectasia (GAVE)**, portal hypertensive gastropathy. [BSG 2021]`,`**Blood loss - lower GI**: **colorectal cancer** (especially **right-sided**, which bleeds occultly), large polyps, angioectasias, IBD, and in India **intestinal TB**. [BSG 2021]`,`**Blood loss - small bowel**: **angioectasias** (commonest over 40 years), **NSAID enteropathy**, Crohn disease, small bowel tumours (GIST, neuroendocrine, lymphoma, adenocarcinoma), **Meckel diverticulum** and Dieulafoy lesion in the young. [ACG 2015]`,`**Hookworm** (Ancylostoma duodenale, Necator americanus) causes about **0.03-0.2 mL blood loss per worm per day** - a major cause of IDA in rural India, especially in barefoot agricultural workers. [WHO]`,`**Malabsorption**: **coeliac disease** (about 3-5% of IDA; higher in north India), **atrophic/autoimmune gastritis**, **H. pylori**, post-gastrectomy or **bariatric surgery**, and drugs (long-term PPI). [BSG 2021]`,`**Non-GI causes**: **menstrual loss** (commonest in premenopausal women), pregnancy, **haematuria** (renal and bladder cancer), blood donation, epistaxis, haemoptysis, and dietary deficiency (vegetarian diets, tea with meals). [BSG 2021]`,`**Risk amplifiers**: **antiplatelets and anticoagulants** unmask lesions but do not explain IDA by themselves - investigate as for anyone else. [BSG 2021]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - iron balance and why the gut matters`,points:[`The body holds about **3-4 g of iron**; daily absorption is only **1-2 mg** in the **duodenum and upper jejunum**, matching obligatory losses - so **chronic blood loss of only 5-10 mL/day** outstrips absorption and depletes stores. [Harrison 22e]`,`Dietary **non-haem iron (Fe3+)** is reduced to Fe2+ by **duodenal cytochrome b**, needs **gastric acid and vitamin C**, and enters enterocytes via **DMT1**; **ferroportin** exports it to transferrin. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hepcidin** from the liver degrades ferroportin; it rises with **inflammation and after each oral iron dose** for about 24 hours - the basis for **once-daily or alternate-day dosing** and for **anaemia of inflammation**. [AGA CPU 2024]`,`**Stages of iron deficiency**: depleted stores (low ferritin), then **iron-deficient erythropoiesis** (low transferrin saturation, raised zinc protoporphyrin), then **microcytic hypochromic anaemia**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ferritin** is an **acute-phase reactant**, so it may be falsely normal in inflammation, CKD, liver disease and malignancy - use **ferritin under 100 ug/L with TSAT under 20%** in these settings. [BSG 2021]`,`**Angioectasias** are ectatic mucosal vessels from chronic low-grade venous obstruction and **VEGF-driven angiogenesis**; in **aortic stenosis (Heyde syndrome)** shear stress cleaves **von Willebrand multimers** (acquired type 2A vWD), making them bleed. [Harrison 22e]`,`Right-sided colon cancer bleeds **occultly into liquid stool**, so it presents with anaemia rather than visible blood or obstruction. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**GI bleeding by site**: **upper** (proximal to ampulla), **small bowel / mid-gut** (ampulla to ileocaecal valve), **lower** (colon and rectum). [ACG 2015]`,`**By visibility**: **overt** (melaena, haematochezia, haematemesis) vs **occult** (IDA or positive FOBT/FIT only). [ACG 2015]`,`**Small bowel bleeding causes by age**: **under 40** - Crohn disease, small bowel tumours, Meckel diverticulum, Dieulafoy lesion, polyposis syndromes; **over 40** - **angioectasias**, NSAID enteropathy, tumours. [ACG 2015]`,`**Severity of anaemia (WHO, adults)**: mild 110-119 (women) or 110-129 (men) g/L, **moderate 80-109 g/L**, **severe under 80 g/L**. [WHO 2024]`,`**Absolute vs functional iron deficiency**: absolute - depleted stores (low ferritin); functional - adequate stores but iron locked away by hepcidin (inflammation, CKD), with **TSAT under 20%** and normal or high ferritin. [KDIGO, ESC 2023]`,`**Saurin classification of capsule findings**: P0 no bleeding potential, P1 uncertain (red spots, small erosions), **P2 high bleeding potential** (angioectasias, large ulcers, tumours, varices). [ESGE 2022]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Symptoms of anaemia: **fatigue, breathlessness on exertion, palpitations, reduced exercise tolerance, headache**; chronic anaemia is often well tolerated until Hb is very low. [Harrison 22e]`,`Specific iron-deficiency symptoms: **pica** (especially **pagophagia - ice craving**, geophagia - eating mud or clay), **restless legs**, hair loss, and dysphagia (Plummer-Vinson syndrome). [Harrison 22e]`,`**GI history**: dyspepsia, change in bowel habit, abdominal pain, weight loss, **episodes of melaena** or dark stools, diarrhoea or bloating (coeliac), and previous GI surgery. [BSG 2021]`,`**Drug history**: **NSAIDs, aspirin, antiplatelets, anticoagulants**, PPIs, and iron already taken (and how). [BSG 2021]`,`**Women**: menstrual history (pad counts, clots, flooding), pregnancies, breastfeeding; **heavy menstrual bleeding does not exclude a GI cause**, especially over 50. [BSG 2021]`,`**Diet and exposure**: vegetarian diet, tea with meals, barefoot walking or farm work (hookworm), blood donation, and **family history of colorectal cancer, coeliac disease, or hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia**. [BSG 2021]`,`**Comorbidities**: aortic stenosis (Heyde), CKD, heart failure, cirrhosis, bleeding disorders. [ACG 2015]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Pallor** of conjunctiva, palms and nail beds; tachycardia and flow murmur in severe anaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Koilonychia** (spoon nails), **angular cheilitis**, **atrophic glossitis** - signs of chronic iron deficiency. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Abdominal mass** (right iliac fossa caecal cancer), hepatomegaly (metastases), **Virchow node** (gastric cancer), and signs of chronic liver disease. [BSG 2021]`,`**Digital rectal examination** for a rectal mass and stool colour - mandatory in every IDA work-up. [BSG 2021]`,`**Telangiectasias** on lips, tongue and fingertips suggest **hereditary haemorrhagic telangiectasia** (Osler-Weber-Rendu); **ejection systolic murmur** suggests aortic stenosis (Heyde). [ACG 2015]`,`**Dermatitis herpetiformis** (itchy vesicles on elbows, knees, buttocks) points to coeliac disease; **Peutz-Jeghers freckles** on the lips point to small bowel hamartomas. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Signs of heart failure** in severe anaemia (oedema, raised JVP) indicate need for admission and cautious transfusion. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of microcytic anaemia`,points:[`**Iron deficiency**: low ferritin, low TSAT, high TIBC, high RDW, **Mentzer index (MCV/RBC count) over 13**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Thalassaemia trait**: microcytosis out of proportion to anaemia, **normal ferritin**, high RBC count, **Mentzer index under 13**, raised HbA2 on HPLC (beta trait) - very common in India; **iron and thalassaemia can coexist**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anaemia of chronic disease/inflammation**: normal or high ferritin, low TSAT, **low TIBC**, raised CRP. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sideroblastic anaemia**: ring sideroblasts on marrow, raised iron; causes include alcohol, lead, isoniazid, copper deficiency. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lead poisoning**: basophilic stippling, raised blood lead; consider with traditional medicines or occupational exposure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mixed deficiency** (iron plus B12/folate, e.g., coeliac or post-gastrectomy) can produce a **normal MCV with high RDW** - check ferritin even when MCV is normal. [BSG 2021]`]},{heading:`Investigations - OPD and district hospital`,points:[`**Confirm IDA**: CBC with indices and peripheral smear, **serum ferritin**, and **TSAT** if inflammation is present; a **therapeutic response to iron** is supportive. [BSG 2021]`,`**Every adult with IDA**: **coeliac serology (tissue transglutaminase IgA with total IgA)** and **urinalysis for haematuria**. [BSG 2021]`,`**Stool microscopy for hookworm ova** in rural and high-prevalence settings, with empirical albendazole where prevalent. [Anemia Mukt Bharat]`,`**Faecal occult blood (guaiac) testing is not useful in IDA** - a negative test does not exclude cancer and all patients with IDA still need endoscopy; **FIT** can help **prioritise** urgency but does not replace colonic investigation. [BSG 2021]`,`**Non-invasive H. pylori testing** (stool antigen or urea breath test) where gastric biopsies are not taken, and **treat if positive**. [AGA 2020]`,`**Renal and liver function, CRP, B12 and folate** complete the baseline. [BSG 2021]`,`**Haemoglobin rise of about 20 g/L in 4 weeks** confirms response to oral iron; failure means non-adherence, malabsorption, ongoing loss or wrong diagnosis. [BSG 2021]`]},{heading:`Investigations - endoscopic and small bowel work-up`,points:[`**Bidirectional endoscopy (OGD plus colonoscopy)** for **men and postmenopausal women** with IDA, done at the same sitting where possible; **CT colonography** is an alternative in frail patients. [BSG 2021, AGA 2020]`,`**Premenopausal women**: bidirectional endoscopy if **age 50 or over**, GI symptoms, a **strong family history of colorectal cancer**, or **IDA persisting despite iron and correction of menstrual loss**. [BSG 2021]`,`**At OGD**: take **duodenal biopsies** if coeliac serology is positive or was not done, and consider gastric biopsies for **atrophic gastritis** and H. pylori. [BSG 2021]`,`**Do not stop at an upper GI finding** such as oesophagitis or gastritis - colonoscopy is still needed because of **dual pathology**; only a cancer or a large ulcer is a convincing upper source. [BSG 2021]`,`**Negative bidirectional endoscopy**: if Hb responds and stays normal, no further tests; **capsule endoscopy** for **recurrent or refractory IDA**, transfusion dependence, or overt suspected small bowel bleeding. [BSG 2021, ESGE 2022]`,`**Consider second-look OGD or colonoscopy** before capsule if the first examination was poor quality - missed lesions within reach (Cameron lesions, angioectasias, GAVE) are common. [ACG 2015]`,`**Capsule endoscopy** has a diagnostic yield of about **40-60%**, highest when done **within 48 hours of overt bleeding**; use a **patency capsule or CT/MR enterography first** if a stricture is suspected (Crohn, **TB**, NSAIDs). [ESGE 2022]`,`**Device-assisted enteroscopy** (single- or double-balloon) for biopsy and therapy of capsule-detected lesions; **CT enterography** for suspected tumours in the young; **CT angiography** for active overt bleeding (detects about **0.3-0.5 mL/min**); **Tc-99m pertechnetate scan** for Meckel diverticulum. [ACG 2015, ESGE 2022]`]},{heading:`OPD management - iron replacement`,points:[`**Treat the cause and replace iron simultaneously** - do not delay iron for investigation once bloods are taken. [BSG 2021]`,`**Oral iron: one tablet daily** (ferrous sulphate 200 mg = **65 mg elemental iron**, or ferrous fumarate 210 mg = 69 mg); **alternate-day dosing** improves fractional absorption and tolerance because of the hepcidin surge. [BSG 2021, AGA CPU 2024]`,`**Take on an empty stomach with vitamin C or orange juice**, and avoid **tea, coffee, calcium, antacids and PPIs** within 1-2 hours. [BSG 2021]`,`**Continue for 3 months after Hb normalises** to replenish stores; check Hb at **2-4 weeks** (expect about 20 g/L rise by 4 weeks). [BSG 2021]`,`**India**: **IFA tablets** (60 mg elemental iron + 500 mcg folic acid) under **Anemia Mukt Bharat**; treatment dose is **two tablets daily for 3 months** in non-pregnant adults with anaemia, as per programme protocol. [Anemia Mukt Bharat 2018]`,`**Intravenous iron** when oral iron is **not tolerated or ineffective**, in **malabsorption** (coeliac, active IBD, bariatric surgery), **CKD**, **heart failure**, ongoing losses exceeding absorption, or when **rapid repletion** is needed (late pregnancy, before surgery). [BSG 2021]`,`**IV options**: **ferric carboxymaltose** up to **1000 mg (20 mg/kg) per infusion over 15 minutes**; **ferric derisomaltose** up to 20 mg/kg in one infusion; **iron sucrose 200 mg** per dose up to three times weekly; calculate need by **Ganzoni**: weight (kg) x (target - actual Hb, g/dL) x 2.4 + 500 mg stores. [BNF 2025]`,`**H. pylori eradication, coeliac gluten-free diet, deworming (albendazole 400 mg single dose)** and **stopping NSAIDs** treat the cause. [BSG 2021]`]},{heading:`IPD and emergency management`,points:[`**Admit**: haemodynamic instability, **Hb under 70 g/L** with symptoms, chest pain or heart failure, overt bleeding (melaena or haematochezia), or syncope. [BSG 2021]`,`**Restrictive transfusion**: transfuse red cells at **Hb under 70 g/L** (under **80 g/L with cardiovascular disease**), one unit at a time with reassessment; follow with iron to replenish stores. [AABB 2023]`,`**Overt suspected small bowel bleeding**: resuscitate, **CT angiography** if active bleeding and unstable (then embolisation), or **capsule within 48 hours** if stable, followed by **enteroscopy** for therapy. [ESGE 2022]`,`**Angioectasias**: endoscopic **argon plasma coagulation**; for recurrent bleeding despite therapy, **octreotide** (100 mcg SC twice daily or long-acting 20 mg IM monthly) or **thalidomide** (teratogenic - specialist use). [ESGE 2022]`,`**Heyde syndrome**: **aortic valve replacement (TAVI or surgical)** corrects the acquired von Willebrand defect and usually stops the bleeding. [ESC 2021 valve guideline]`,`**IV iron safety**: observe for 30 minutes; **hypersensitivity is rare (under 1 in 250,000 serious reactions)** with modern formulations; **ferric carboxymaltose causes hypophosphataemia** (check phosphate if repeated doses); avoid in active bacteraemia. [EMA 2013, BNF 2025]`,`**Anticoagulants and antiplatelets**: review need, use the minimum effective regimen, and coordinate with cardiology; do not stop secondary-prevention aspirin without discussion. [ACG 2022 periendoscopic]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: oral iron first; **IV iron (ferric carboxymaltose or iron sucrose) in the second and third trimesters** for moderate-severe anaemia or oral failure - Anemia Mukt Bharat supports FCM for pregnant women; **avoid IV iron in the first trimester**. [Anemia Mukt Bharat, RCOG]`,`**CKD**: IV iron for most on dialysis; target **ferritin over 100-200 ug/L and TSAT over 20%** before ESA; GI work-up still needed if iron deficiency is absolute and unexplained. [KDIGO 2012]`,`**Heart failure**: **IV ferric carboxymaltose or derisomaltose** in symptomatic HFrEF/HFmrEF with iron deficiency (**ferritin under 100, or 100-299 with TSAT under 20%**) improves symptoms and reduces HF hospitalisation. [ESC 2023]`,`**IBD**: IV iron preferred when active disease or Hb under 100 g/L; oral iron at low dose in quiescent disease. [ECCO 2015]`,`**Elderly and frail**: weigh endoscopy risk; **CT colonography** and OGD, or CT abdomen if colonoscopy unsuitable; correct anaemia even if investigation is declined. [BSG 2021]`,`**Children and adolescents**: dietary deficiency and worms dominate; **coeliac disease** and **Meckel diverticulum** (young male with painless bleeding) are the GI causes to consider. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Post-bariatric surgery**: lifelong iron monitoring; oral iron often fails, so IV iron is frequently needed. [BSG 2021]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Untreated IDA** causes fatigue, reduced work capacity, cognitive impairment in children, **adverse pregnancy outcomes** (preterm birth, low birth weight, maternal death) and worsens heart failure. [WHO 2024]`,`**Missed colorectal or gastric cancer** is the main danger of treating IDA with iron alone in an older adult. [BSG 2021]`,`After **negative bidirectional endoscopy with good iron response**, long-term outcome is good - **serious pathology is found later in only a small minority**. [BSG 2021]`,`**Angioectasias** often recur after APC (rebleeding in **about 40%** over 1-2 years), so many patients need repeated therapy or medical treatment. [ESGE 2022]`,`**Oral iron side effects** (constipation, nausea, epigastric pain, black stools) cause non-adherence in up to **half** of patients. [AGA CPU 2024]`,`**Severe anaemia** in the elderly precipitates angina, heart failure and falls. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and prevention`,points:[`**Refer urgently (2-week pathway)** for bidirectional endoscopy: IDA in men and postmenopausal women, especially with Hb under 100 g/L, age over 60, or alarm features. [NICE NG12]`,`**Monitor Hb and indices every 3 months for 1 year**, then at 2 years; give further iron if Hb or MCV falls. [BSG 2021]`,`**Re-investigate** (capsule, repeat endoscopy) if IDA **recurs** despite correction of causes and adequate iron. [BSG 2021]`,`**Refer to haematology** if iron studies are discordant, thalassaemia or myelodysplasia is suspected, or there is no response to IV iron. [BSG 2021]`,`**Refer to cardiology** for a murmur suggesting aortic stenosis in a patient with angioectasias. [ESC 2021 valve guideline]`,`**Anemia Mukt Bharat (2018)** - the **6x6x6 strategy**: six target groups, six interventions (prophylactic IFA, deworming, behaviour change, testing and treatment with digital Hb meters, fortified foods, addressing non-nutritional causes) and six institutional mechanisms. [Anemia Mukt Bharat 2018]`,`**Prophylaxis**: **weekly IFA** for adolescents (WIFS), **daily IFA for 180 days** in pregnancy and lactation; **biannual albendazole** through **National Deworming Day**. [Anemia Mukt Bharat 2018]`,`**Food fortification**: **fortified rice** distributed through PDS, ICDS and PM POSHAN, and **double-fortified salt**. [MoHFW]`,`**Diet counselling**: green leafy vegetables, pulses, jaggery, meat if acceptable; **vitamin C with meals; tea and coffee 1 hour away** from meals. [ICMR-NIN Dietary Guidelines 2024]`,`**Footwear and sanitation (Swachh Bharat)** reduce hookworm transmission. [WHO]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2020-2026)`,points:[`**BSG guidelines for the management of iron deficiency anaemia in adults (2021)**: ferritin as the key test, **bidirectional endoscopy** for men and postmenopausal women, coeliac serology and urinalysis for all, capsule for recurrent IDA, **one tablet daily or alternate-day oral iron**. [BSG 2021]`,`**AGA guideline on GI evaluation of IDA (2020)**: **ferritin cut-off 45 ng/mL**, bidirectional endoscopy for asymptomatic men and postmenopausal women, H. pylori and coeliac testing. [AGA 2020]`,`**AGA Clinical Practice Update on management of IDA (2024)**: alternate-day or once-daily oral iron, earlier **IV iron** when oral fails or in malabsorption, and monitoring for hypophosphataemia with ferric carboxymaltose. [AGA CPU 2024]`,`**WHO guideline on haemoglobin cut-offs (2024)**: updated anaemia thresholds by age, sex and pregnancy trimester, with altitude and smoking adjustments. [WHO 2024]`,`**ESGE small bowel guideline update (2022)**: capsule first-line for suspected small bowel bleeding, **ideally within 48 hours of overt bleeding**; device-assisted enteroscopy for therapy. [ESGE 2022]`,`**ESC heart failure focused update (2023)**: IV iron recommended in symptomatic HFrEF/HFmrEF with iron deficiency. [ESC 2023]`,`**NICE DG56 (2023)**: FIT (f-Hb 10 ug/g or more) to guide referral in symptomatic patients; IDA itself remains an indication for colonic investigation. [NICE 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Viva: **'A 65-year-old man has IDA and oesophagitis on OGD. Next step?'** - colonoscopy; dual pathology and oesophagitis rarely explain IDA. [BSG 2021]`,`Viva: **'Why not FOBT in IDA?'** - a negative test does not exclude cancer; IDA itself mandates endoscopy. [BSG 2021]`,`Viva: **'What is Heyde syndrome?'** - aortic stenosis with bleeding angioectasias due to acquired von Willebrand type 2A; cured by valve replacement. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: **'Ferritin is 80 ug/L in rheumatoid arthritis - is she iron deficient?'** - possibly; use TSAT under 20% and ferritin under 100 in inflammation. [BSG 2021]`,`Viva: **'Why alternate-day iron?'** - each dose raises hepcidin for 24 hours, blocking absorption of the next. [AGA CPU 2024]`,`Common mistake: **treating IDA with iron alone in a man** without investigation. [BSG 2021]`,`Common mistake: **calling microcytosis iron deficiency without ferritin** - thalassaemia trait is common in India. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: **capsule endoscopy before a good-quality colonoscopy**, or in a patient with a suspected TB or Crohn stricture without a patency test. [ESGE 2022]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drug table - iron replacement and small bowel bleeding`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Ferrous sulphate`,`200 mg (65 mg elemental) orally once daily or alternate days, empty stomach with vitamin C`,`Until Hb normal, then 3 more months`,`Constipation, nausea, black stools; poor absorption with tea, PPIs`],[`IFA tablet (Anemia Mukt Bharat)`,`60 mg elemental iron + 500 mcg folic acid; treatment two tablets daily per protocol`,`3 months for anaemia; prophylaxis daily (pregnancy) or weekly (adolescents)`,`GI upset`],[`Ferric carboxymaltose`,`Up to 1000 mg (20 mg/kg) IV over 15 min per dose`,`Total dose by Ganzoni; may repeat after 1 week`,`Hypophosphataemia, hypersensitivity (rare), skin staining; avoid first trimester`],[`Ferric derisomaltose`,`Up to 20 mg/kg IV single infusion over at least 15-20 min`,`Total dose by Ganzoni`,`Hypersensitivity (rare), less hypophosphataemia than FCM`],[`Iron sucrose`,`200 mg IV over 10-30 min, up to three times weekly`,`Until calculated deficit replaced`,`Hypotension, metallic taste; multiple visits`],[`Albendazole`,`400 mg orally single dose`,`Repeat 6-monthly in endemic areas`,`Avoid first trimester`],[`Octreotide (angioectasias)`,`100 mcg SC twice daily or LAR 20 mg IM monthly`,`Long term if recurrent bleeding`,`Gallstones, diarrhoea, hyperglycaemia`],[`Thalidomide (refractory angioectasias)`,`100 mg orally daily (specialist)`,`Under specialist supervision`,`Teratogenic - strict contraception; neuropathy, thrombosis`]]},{heading:`Iron studies - interpretation`,columns:[`Condition`,`Ferritin`,`TSAT`,`TIBC`,`Other`],rows:[[`Iron deficiency`,`Low (under 15 diagnostic; under 45 practical)`,`Low (under 16-20%)`,`High`,`High RDW, Mentzer over 13`],[`Anaemia of inflammation`,`Normal or high`,`Low`,`Low or normal`,`Raised CRP`],[`Iron deficiency with inflammation`,`Under 100`,`Under 20%`,`Normal`,`Response to iron`],[`Thalassaemia trait`,`Normal`,`Normal`,`Normal`,`Mentzer under 13, raised HbA2`],[`Sideroblastic anaemia`,`High`,`High`,`Normal`,`Ring sideroblasts`]]},{heading:`Who needs what work-up (BSG 2021, AGA 2020)`,columns:[`Patient group`,`Investigation`],rows:[[`All adults with IDA`,`Coeliac serology (tTG IgA + total IgA), urinalysis, H. pylori test if no biopsy`],[`Men and postmenopausal women`,`Bidirectional endoscopy (OGD + colonoscopy) at the same sitting`],[`Premenopausal women, no GI symptoms`,`Coeliac serology; iron; endoscopy if 50 or older, family history or persistent IDA`],[`Frail or unfit for colonoscopy`,`OGD + CT colonography or CT abdomen`],[`Negative endoscopy, good iron response`,`No further tests; monitor Hb 3-monthly for 1 year`],[`Negative endoscopy, recurrent or refractory IDA`,`Capsule endoscopy; then enteroscopy or CT enterography`],[`Rural, high hookworm prevalence`,`Stool ova and empirical albendazole alongside work-up`]]},{heading:`Causes of small bowel bleeding by age`,columns:[`Age group`,`Common causes`,`Best test`],rows:[[`Under 40 years`,`Crohn disease, small bowel tumours, Meckel diverticulum, Dieulafoy lesion, polyposis`,`CT/MR enterography, Meckel scan, capsule`],[`Over 40 years`,`Angioectasias, NSAID enteropathy, tumours (GIST, NET, adenocarcinoma, lymphoma)`,`Capsule endoscopy, then enteroscopy`],[`Any age in India`,`Intestinal TB, hookworm, Crohn disease`,`CT enterography, stool microscopy, biopsy at enteroscopy`],[`Active overt bleeding`,`Any of the above`,`CT angiography (0.3-0.5 mL/min), then embolisation or enteroscopy`]]},{heading:`Oral versus intravenous iron`,columns:[`Feature`,`Oral iron`,`Intravenous iron`],rows:[[`First choice`,`Most patients`,`Oral failure, intolerance, malabsorption, CKD, HF, late pregnancy`],[`Speed`,`Hb rise about 20 g/L in 4 weeks`,`Faster; full replacement in 1-2 visits`],[`Cost and access`,`Cheap; free IFA under programmes`,`Costlier; needs monitored setting`],[`Adverse effects`,`GI upset in up to half`,`Hypophosphataemia (FCM), rare hypersensitivity`],[`Avoid`,`Active IBD with poor absorption`,`First trimester, active bacteraemia`]]}],redFlags:[`Iron deficiency anaemia in a man or postmenopausal woman - urgent bidirectional endoscopy`,`Hb under 70 g/L with chest pain, breathlessness at rest, heart failure or syncope - admit`,`Overt melaena or haematochezia with haemodynamic instability`,`Weight loss, abdominal or rectal mass, dysphagia or change in bowel habit with IDA`,`IDA recurring despite adequate iron and negative endoscopy - small bowel evaluation`,`Family history of colorectal cancer or HHT with IDA`,`Microcytic anaemia not responding to iron after 4 weeks of adherence`],pearls:[`**IDA in a man or postmenopausal woman is GI cancer until proven otherwise.**`,`**Ferritin under 15 diagnostic, under 45 practical, under 100 with inflammation.**`,`**Scope both ends** - an upper finding does not excuse a colonoscopy.`,`**FOBT has no role in IDA** - IDA itself is the indication for endoscopy.`,`**Coeliac serology and urinalysis in every adult with IDA.**`,`**One tablet daily or alternate days** - more iron means more side effects, not more absorption (hepcidin).`,`**Hb should rise 20 g/L in 4 weeks**; if not, think adherence, malabsorption, ongoing loss or wrong diagnosis.`,`**Heyde syndrome**: aortic stenosis + angioectasias + acquired vWD type 2A.`,`**Mentzer index under 13 suggests thalassaemia trait**, over 13 iron deficiency.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-occult-gi-bleed-ida-q1`,stem:`A 62-year-old man has Hb 95 g/L, MCV 72 fL and ferritin 8 ug/L. OGD shows LA grade A oesophagitis. Coeliac serology is negative. The next step is:`,options:[`Start PPI and oral iron with no further investigation`,`Faecal occult blood test and colonoscopy only if positive`,`Capsule endoscopy`,`Colonoscopy`],answer:3,explanation:`Mild oesophagitis does not explain iron deficiency, and dual pathology occurs in up to 10%, so BSG 2021 requires colonoscopy to complete bidirectional endoscopy. FOBT is not recommended in IDA because a negative result cannot exclude cancer; capsule endoscopy is for recurrent IDA after a negative OGD and colonoscopy; treating without colonoscopy risks missing right-sided colon cancer.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-occult-gi-bleed-ida-q2`,stem:`Which test should be done in every adult with iron deficiency anaemia according to BSG 2021?`,options:[`Coeliac serology (tissue transglutaminase IgA)`,`Bone marrow aspiration`,`Serum gastrin`,`Tc-99m pertechnetate Meckel scan`],answer:0,explanation:`Coeliac disease is found in about 3-5% of IDA and serology (with urinalysis) is recommended for all adults with IDA. Bone marrow examination is rarely needed when ferritin is low, serum gastrin is for suspected Zollinger-Ellison or atrophic gastritis, and a Meckel scan is only for young patients with suspected Meckel bleeding.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-occult-gi-bleed-ida-q3`,stem:`A 75-year-old woman with recurrent IDA despite iron has a harsh ejection systolic murmur. Bidirectional endoscopy is normal. Capsule endoscopy shows multiple jejunal angioectasias. The underlying mechanism linking her murmur and bleeding is:`,options:[`Hepcidin excess from chronic inflammation`,`Acquired von Willebrand factor deficiency from shear-induced cleavage of large multimers`,`Portal hypertension from cardiac cirrhosis`,`Thrombocytopenia from valve-induced haemolysis`],answer:1,explanation:`Heyde syndrome is aortic stenosis with bleeding angioectasias; high shear across the valve cleaves high-molecular-weight von Willebrand multimers (acquired type 2A vWD), and valve replacement usually stops bleeding. Hepcidin excess causes anaemia of inflammation, not angioectasia bleeding; cardiac cirrhosis and haemolytic thrombocytopenia are not the recognised mechanism.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-occult-gi-bleed-ida-q4`,stem:`The best regimen for oral iron in adult IDA, according to current guidance, is:`,options:[`Ferrous sulphate 200 mg three times daily with meals and tea`,`Iron tablets only after Hb falls below 80 g/L`,`One ferrous sulphate 200 mg tablet once daily or on alternate days on an empty stomach, continued for 3 months after Hb normalises`,`Iron for 2 weeks then stop once symptoms improve`],answer:2,explanation:`Each oral dose raises hepcidin for about 24 hours, so once-daily or alternate-day dosing absorbs as well with fewer side effects; iron is continued 3 months after normal Hb to refill stores. Three-times-daily dosing with tea increases side effects and reduces absorption, waiting for Hb under 80 delays treatment, and a 2-week course leaves stores empty.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-occult-gi-bleed-ida-q5`,stem:`A 30-year-old man from rural Bihar with known ileocaecal tuberculosis on treatment has recurrent IDA. OGD and colonoscopy were normal apart from healing caecal ulcers. Before capsule endoscopy, the most important step is:`,options:[`Proceed directly to capsule endoscopy`,`Start thalidomide`,`Perform a Tc-99m RBC scan`,`Assess small bowel patency with a patency capsule or CT/MR enterography`],answer:3,explanation:`Intestinal TB causes strictures, so capsule retention is a real risk; a patency capsule or CT/MR enterography should come first (ESGE 2022). Going straight to capsule risks retention and obstruction, thalidomide is for refractory angioectasias, and an RBC scan needs active bleeding of about 0.1 mL/min and is not a test for occult IDA.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-occult-gi-bleed-ida-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Outline the approach to a 60-year-old man with iron deficiency anaemia in primary care. Discuss investigations, including evaluation of suspected small bowel bleeding, and the principles of iron replacement.`,openingLines:[`Iron deficiency anaemia - haemoglobin under 130 g/L in a man with low ferritin (under 45 ug/L) - in a man or postmenopausal woman indicates occult GI blood loss or malabsorption until proven otherwise.`,`BSG 2021 and AGA 2020 recommend coeliac serology, urinalysis and bidirectional endoscopy, with capsule endoscopy for recurrent or refractory IDA, alongside iron replacement.`],answer:[{heading:`Confirm iron deficiency`,points:[`CBC, smear, ferritin (under 45; under 100 with TSAT under 20% in inflammation); Mentzer index; exclude thalassaemia trait.`]},{heading:`History and examination`,points:[`GI symptoms, weight loss, NSAIDs, anticoagulants, diet, hookworm exposure, family history, aortic stenosis, CKD.`,`Pallor, koilonychia, mass, DRE, telangiectasia, murmur.`]},{heading:`First-line tests`,points:[`Coeliac serology, urinalysis, renal and liver function, CRP; stool for hookworm ova in endemic areas; no role for FOBT.`]},{heading:`Endoscopic work-up`,points:[`Bidirectional endoscopy at one sitting; duodenal biopsy if coeliac suspected; H. pylori testing; CT colonography if frail.`,`Upper lesion other than cancer or large ulcer does not end the work-up.`]},{heading:`Suspected small bowel bleeding`,points:[`Negative OGD and colonoscopy with recurrent IDA: second-look if poor quality, capsule endoscopy (patency first if stricture risk), then device-assisted enteroscopy for therapy.`,`Under 40: tumours, Crohn, Meckel; over 40: angioectasias, NSAID enteropathy; India: TB, hookworm.`,`CT angiography for active overt bleeding; Meckel scan in young.`]},{heading:`Iron replacement and follow-up`,points:[`Oral one tablet daily or alternate days, with vitamin C, for 3 months after normal Hb; expect 20 g/L rise at 4 weeks.`,`IV iron (FCM, FDI, iron sucrose) for intolerance, malabsorption, CKD, HF, ongoing loss; Ganzoni formula.`,`Hb 3-monthly for 1 year; re-investigate if recurrence.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: IDA confirmed - coeliac serology and urinalysis - bidirectional endoscopy - capsule - enteroscopy`,`Table: iron studies in IDA vs anaemia of inflammation vs thalassaemia trait`],markSplit:[{part:`Confirming IDA and differential`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical assessment`,marks:1},{part:`Investigations including small bowel with flow chart`,marks:4},{part:`Iron replacement and follow-up`,marks:3}],keywords:[`ferritin`,`bidirectional endoscopy`,`coeliac serology`,`capsule endoscopy`,`angioectasia`,`IV iron`,`BSG 2021`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-occult-gi-bleed-ida-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on intravenous iron therapy.`,openingLines:[`Intravenous iron replaces iron stores rapidly in one or two visits and is indicated when oral iron is not tolerated or ineffective, in malabsorption, CKD, heart failure, ongoing losses, or when rapid repletion is needed.`,`Modern formulations - ferric carboxymaltose, ferric derisomaltose and iron sucrose - have a very low risk of serious hypersensitivity.`],answer:[{heading:`Indications`,points:[`Oral intolerance or failure, coeliac, active IBD, bariatric surgery, CKD, HFrEF with iron deficiency, second and third trimester with moderate-severe anaemia, preoperative anaemia.`]},{heading:`Preparations and dose`,points:[`FCM up to 1000 mg (20 mg/kg) over 15 min; FDI up to 20 mg/kg; iron sucrose 200 mg up to three times weekly.`,`Ganzoni: weight x (target - actual Hb in g/dL) x 2.4 + 500 mg.`]},{heading:`Safety`,points:[`Observe 30 minutes with resuscitation available; hypophosphataemia with FCM; avoid first trimester and active infection; skin staining with extravasation.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table comparing oral and IV iron`],markSplit:[{part:`Indications`,marks:2},{part:`Preparations and dosing`,marks:2},{part:`Safety`,marks:1}],keywords:[`ferric carboxymaltose`,`ferric derisomaltose`,`iron sucrose`,`Ganzoni`,`hypophosphataemia`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Iron deficiency and other hypoproliferative anemias; Gastrointestinal bleeding`,`Snook J et al. British Society of Gastroenterology guidelines for the management of iron deficiency anaemia in adults. Gut, 2021`,`Ko CW et al. AGA Clinical Practice Guidelines on the Gastrointestinal Evaluation of Iron Deficiency Anemia. Gastroenterology, 2020`,`Gerson LB et al. ACG Clinical Guideline: Diagnosis and Management of Small Bowel Bleeding, 2015`,`Pennazio M et al. Small-bowel capsule endoscopy and device-assisted enteroscopy for diagnosis and treatment of small-bowel disorders: ESGE Guideline - Update 2022`,`WHO guideline on haemoglobin cutoffs to define anaemia in individuals and populations, 2024`,`Anemia Mukt Bharat Intensified National Iron Plus Initiative operational guidelines, MoHFW, 2018; National Family Health Survey (NFHS-5) 2019-21`,`McDonagh TA et al. 2023 Focused Update of the 2021 ESC Guidelines for the diagnosis and treatment of acute and chronic heart failure`]});var u=[];u.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-functional-other`,title:`Functional abdominal bloating, belching and centrally mediated abdominal pain`,oneLiner:`**Functional bloating/distension, belching disorders and centrally mediated abdominal pain syndrome (CAPS)** are **Rome IV disorders of gut-brain interaction (DGBI)** - symptoms from **visceral hypersensitivity, abnormal viscerosomatic reflexes and altered central pain processing** without structural disease - managed by **positive diagnosis, explanation, diet, gut-brain behavioural therapy and central neuromodulators**, not by repeated tests.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`functional bloating`,`abdominal distension`,`DGBI`,`disorder of gut-brain interaction`,`Rome IV`,`supragastric belching`,`aerophagia`,`rumination syndrome`,`CAPS`,`functional abdominal pain`,`narcotic bowel syndrome`,`opioid-induced hyperalgesia`,`abdominophrenic dyssynergia`,`SIBO`,`hydrogen breath test`,`amitriptyline`,`duloxetine`,`gut-directed hypnotherapy`,`CBT`,`baclofen`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Disorders of gut-brain interaction (DGBI)** is the Rome IV name for functional GI disorders: symptoms arising from any combination of **motility disturbance, visceral hypersensitivity, altered mucosal and immune function, altered microbiota and altered CNS processing**. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Functional abdominal bloating/distension (Rome IV F3)**: recurrent bloating (a sensation of trapped gas or pressure) and/or visible distension, **on average at least 1 day a week**, that **predominates over other symptoms**, without criteria for IBS, functional dyspepsia or functional constipation; symptoms for **3 months with onset at least 6 months** before diagnosis. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Belching disorders (Rome IV B3)**: **bothersome belching more than 3 days a week**, split into **excessive supragastric belching** (air sucked into the oesophagus and expelled before reaching the stomach) and **excessive gastric belching** (venting of swallowed air from the stomach). [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Centrally mediated abdominal pain syndrome (CAPS, Rome IV D1)**: **continuous or nearly continuous** abdominal pain, with **no or only occasional relation to eating, defecation or menses**, that **limits daily functioning**, is not feigned and is not explained by another disorder. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Narcotic bowel syndrome / opioid-induced GI hyperalgesia (Rome IV D2)**: chronic abdominal pain on high-dose or chronic opioids that **paradoxically worsens or fails to settle with escalating doses**, worsens as each dose wears off ('soar and crash') and progresses in frequency and intensity. [Rome IV 2016]`,`The **Rome Foundation Global Epidemiology Study** (33 countries, India included) found at least one DGBI in **about 40% of adults on internet survey and about 21% on household survey**; functional bloating/distension was about **3.5%** on internet survey. [Rome Foundation GES 2021]`,`**Bloating as a symptom** is reported by **15-30% of the general population** and by up to **90% of IBS patients**; it is more common and more severe in **women**, often worse premenstrually. [AGA CPU Belching and Bloating 2023]`,`In Indian practice these patients present as **'gas', 'acidity' or 'wind'** complaints and are a large share of general OPD visits; CAPS is uncommon but consumes disproportionate investigations, admissions and opioid prescriptions. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Visceral hypersensitivity** - a normal gas volume is perceived as painful bloating; patients with bloating usually have **normal total intestinal gas volume**. [AGA CPU Belching and Bloating 2023]`,`**Abnormal gas handling**: impaired transit and clearance of gas from the small bowel (shown by gas-infusion studies) causes retention and bloating. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Carbohydrate maldigestion** - lactose, fructose, fructans, polyols (FODMAPs) and sucrase-isomaltase deficiency - increases colonic fermentation; see the Food intolerance and FODMAPs topic. [AGA CPU Belching and Bloating 2023]`,`**Small intestinal bacterial overgrowth (SIBO)** risk factors: **PPIs, opioids, diabetes, scleroderma, previous gastric or ileocaecal surgery, strictures, diverticula, blind loops, pancreatic insufficiency and cirrhosis**. [ACG SIBO 2020]`,`**Constipation and pelvic floor dyssynergia** - slow transit and incomplete evacuation are among the commonest treatable causes of bloating and distension. [AGA CPU Belching and Bloating 2023]`,`**Psychological comorbidity** - anxiety, depression, somatisation, and **early-life adversity or trauma** - increases risk of all three disorders, especially CAPS. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Post-infection** (after acute gastroenteritis) and **after abdominal surgery or repeated laparoscopies** - often precede CAPS; repeated procedures reinforce the pain cycle. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Chronic opioid use**, including tramadol and codeine-containing cough syrups commonly used in India, is the cause of narcotic bowel syndrome and worsens all DGBI. [Rome IV 2016]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**Bloating versus distension** are separate: bloating is a sensation (hypersensitivity); distension is a measurable increase in girth, worst by evening and settled overnight. [AGA CPU Belching and Bloating 2023]`,`**Abdominophrenic dyssynergia**: in response to normal gas, the **diaphragm contracts and descends and the anterior abdominal wall relaxes** - a paradoxical viscerosomatic reflex that pushes contents forward and produces visible distension **without extra gas**. [AGA CPU Belching and Bloating 2023]`,`**Supragastric belching** is a learned behaviour: the patient **relaxes the upper oesophageal sphincter and sucks or pushes air into the oesophagus** and expels it within seconds, up to **20 times a minute**; it stops during speech, sleep and distraction. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Gastric belching** is physiological venting of swallowed air via **transient lower oesophageal sphincter relaxations (TLESRs)** - the same vagal reflex that drives reflux, which is why **baclofen** (a GABA-B agonist that reduces TLESRs) helps. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**CAPS is a central pain disorder**: functional imaging shows **reduced descending pain inhibition** (periaqueductal grey and anterior cingulate) and **central sensitisation**, so pain persists without peripheral input - a gut-level equivalent of fibromyalgia. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Opioids cause hyperalgesia** by activating **glial (toll-like receptor 4) pathways and NMDA-mediated central sensitisation**, explaining narcotic bowel syndrome and why the cure is opioid withdrawal, not dose escalation. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Neuromodulators work centrally**, not on the gut: TCAs and SNRIs boost **descending noradrenergic and serotonergic inhibition**, lowering visceral pain perception at doses below antidepressant range. [Rome Foundation Neuromodulators 2018]`,`**Gut-brain behavioural therapies** reduce hypervigilance, catastrophising and symptom-specific anxiety, which amplify visceral signals through the **anterior cingulate and insula**. [AGA CPU Gut-Brain Behavioural Therapy 2022]`]},{heading:`Classification - Rome IV categories that matter`,points:[`**Rome IV section B (gastroduodenal)**: functional dyspepsia, **belching disorders (excessive supragastric, excessive gastric)**, nausea and vomiting disorders, and **rumination syndrome**. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Rome IV section C (bowel)**: IBS, functional constipation, functional diarrhoea, **functional abdominal bloating/distension (C3)**, unspecified functional bowel disorder and opioid-induced constipation. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Rome IV section D (centrally mediated GI pain)**: **CAPS (D1)** and **narcotic bowel syndrome/opioid-induced GI hyperalgesia (D2)**. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Hierarchy rule**: functional bloating is diagnosed only if IBS, functional dyspepsia and functional constipation criteria are not met - bloating with pain linked to defecation is **IBS**, not functional bloating. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Rumination syndrome**: **effortless regurgitation of recently ingested food** into the mouth within minutes of eating, re-chewed or spat out, **not preceded by retching** - distinct from vomiting and from belching. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Aerophagia** (excessive air swallowing with bloating) was merged into the belching disorders in Rome IV; it is seen with anxiety, gum chewing, carbonated drinks and CPAP. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Rome V** revision is due in 2026; the categories above remain the ones Indian examiners expect until it is adopted. [Rome Foundation 2026]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Bloating**: pressure, fullness or trapped gas; ask about **diurnal pattern** - flat in the morning, progressive distension through the day ('look pregnant by evening') is typical of functional disease. [AGA CPU Belching and Bloating 2023]`,`Ask **bowel habit by the Bristol stool chart**, straining, incomplete evacuation and digital manoeuvres - constipation and dyssynergia are the commonest hidden drivers. [AGA CPU Belching and Bloating 2023]`,`Ask **food triggers** (milk, wheat, onion, garlic, legumes, fruit, sugar-free sweets), carbonated drinks, chewing gum and eating fast. [AGA CPU Belching and Bloating 2023]`,`**Belching**: count frequency; **frequent audible belches during the consultation that stop while talking or distracted** are characteristic of supragastric belching; ask about co-existing heartburn. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**CAPS history**: continuous pain for years, **multiple consultations, scans, endoscopies and often surgeries (appendicectomy, cholecystectomy, adhesiolysis)**, pain described in emotional terms, high disability and requests for opioids. [Rome IV 2016]`,`Ask an explicit **opioid history** - tramadol, tapentadol, codeine syrup, pentazocine and morphine; escalating dose with worsening pain suggests narcotic bowel syndrome. [Rome IV 2016]`,`Screen mood with **PHQ-9 and GAD-7**, ask about sleep, trauma and abuse history, and the patient's own fears (cancer, 'gas going to the heart'). [AGA CPU Gut-Brain Behavioural Therapy 2022]`,`Always ask **alarm features**: age over 45-50 at onset, weight loss, GI bleeding, anaemia, nocturnal symptoms waking the patient, progressive dysphagia, persistent vomiting, family history of GI cancer or coeliac disease, and new ascites. [AGA CPU Belching and Bloating 2023]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Examination is normal or shows only mild tenderness** in uncomplicated DGBI; any objective finding (mass, organomegaly, ascites, lymphadenopathy) needs explanation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Measure girth** or ask for morning and evening photographs - documented distension that settles overnight supports functional distension or abdominophrenic dyssynergia. [AGA CPU Belching and Bloating 2023]`,`**Shifting dullness and fluid thrill** distinguish ascites from gaseous distension; **succussion splash** suggests gastric outlet obstruction or gastroparesis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Carnett sign**: pain that persists or worsens when the patient tenses the abdominal wall (head and shoulders raised) indicates **abdominal wall pain** such as anterior cutaneous nerve entrapment, not visceral pain. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Closed-eyes sign** and pain behaviour out of proportion to findings are described in CAPS; they suggest central sensitisation but never exclude organic disease alone. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Digital rectal examination** with push manoeuvre assesses **dyssynergic defecation** (paradoxical anal contraction on straining) - essential in every bloated, constipated patient. [AGA CPU Belching and Bloating 2023]`,`Look for clues to secondary disease: **pallor, weight loss, sclerodactyly (scleroderma SIBO), surgical scars, signs of thyroid disease or diabetes**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**IBS**: pain linked to defecation and change in stool form or frequency - bloating is common but pain-defecation link decides the label; see the IBS and chronic constipation topic. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Coeliac disease**: bloating, diarrhoea, iron-deficiency anaemia; test **tTG-IgA with total IgA** on a gluten-containing diet before labelling functional bloating. [ACG Coeliac 2023]`,`**SIBO and carbohydrate maldigestion**: bloating 30-90 minutes after meals, diarrhoea, flatus; risk factors as above; breath test if a trial of therapy would change care. [ACG SIBO 2020]`,`**Ovarian cancer and ascites**: new persistent bloating in a woman over 50 needs **CA-125 and pelvic ultrasound** - bloating is the commonest early symptom. [NICE NG12 2015, updated 2023]`,`**Gastroparesis and gastric outlet obstruction**: early satiety, nausea, vomiting of food eaten hours earlier and succussion splash. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction**: distension with **dilated bowel loops and air-fluid levels on X-ray**, vomiting and weight loss - not functional bloating. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Supragastric belching versus GERD versus rumination**: belching with air only, regurgitation of acid, and effortless regurgitation of food respectively; all three can coexist. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**CAPS mimics**: abdominal wall pain (Carnett positive), **acute intermittent porphyria**, abdominal migraine, lead poisoning, familial Mediterranean fever, mesenteric ischaemia, chronic pancreatitis and endometriosis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Rome IV is a positive diagnosis**: in a young patient without alarm features, minimal tests are enough - **CBC, CRP, tTG-IgA with total IgA** and, where relevant, **TSH and fasting glucose**. [AGA CPU Belching and Bloating 2023]`,`**Stool tests** where diarrhoea coexists: stool microscopy for Giardia (common in India) and **faecal calprotectin** (under 50 mcg/g makes IBD unlikely). [BSG IBS 2021]`,`**Plain abdominal X-ray** during symptoms can show faecal loading or dilated loops; **ultrasound** for organomegaly, ascites and ovarian masses. [AGA CPU Belching and Bloating 2023]`,`**Endoscopy and CT are not routine** - only for alarm features, age-appropriate screening or new findings; AGA 2023 advises against repeat imaging in established DGBI. [AGA CPU Belching and Bloating 2023]`,`**Glucose hydrogen breath test** (75 g glucose; preferred for SIBO): rise in **hydrogen of 20 ppm or more over baseline within 90 minutes** is positive. [ACG SIBO 2020]`,`**Methane of 10 ppm or more at any time** defines **intestinal methanogen overgrowth (IMO)**, associated with constipation and bloating. [ACG SIBO 2020]`,`**Lactulose breath test** (10 g) has more false positives because rapid transit gives an early colonic peak; **jejunal aspirate with 10^3 CFU/mL or more** is the reference standard but invasive. [ACG SIBO 2020]`,`**Breath test preparation**: no antibiotics for 4 weeks, stop laxatives and prokinetics about 1 week before, low-fermentation dinner, **fast 8-12 hours**, no smoking or exercise on test day. [North American Breath Testing Consensus 2017]`,`**High-resolution impedance manometry** separates supragastric from gastric belching and confirms rumination (**abdominal pressure spike over 30 mmHg** preceding regurgitation); **anorectal manometry and balloon expulsion** detect dyssynergia. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Gastric emptying scintigraphy** only if nausea and vomiting dominate; **abdominal CT** in CAPS only once, if never done, to reassure doctor and patient. [AGA CPU Belching and Bloating 2023]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Make a confident positive diagnosis and explain the mechanism** (sensitive gut, gut-brain loop, normal gas volume) - the therapeutic relationship itself reduces symptoms and repeat visits. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Diet**: avoid carbonated drinks, gum and sugar-free sweets, eat slowly; a **dietitian-led low-FODMAP diet** for 2-6 weeks then reintroduction helps bloating with IBS overlap. [AGA CPU Belching and Bloating 2023]`,`**Treat constipation first** when present: **PEG 3350 17 g/day**, or **prucalopride 2 mg once daily** (1 mg over 65) or **linaclotide 290 mcg** where available; refer for **biofeedback** in dyssynergia. [AGA CPU Belching and Bloating 2023]`,`**SIBO (breath test positive)**: **rifaximin 550 mg three times daily for 14 days** (India often uses 400 mg three times daily); alternatives are **amoxicillin-clavulanate, metronidazole, ciprofloxacin or doxycycline** for 7-14 days. [ACG SIBO 2020]`,`**Supragastric belching**: **diaphragmatic breathing, speech therapy-led techniques and CBT** - show the patient the air-sucking behaviour; no drug works reliably. [AGA CPU Belching and Bloating 2023]`,`**Excessive gastric belching**: reduce air swallowing; **baclofen 5-10 mg three times daily** reduces TLESRs (sedation, dizziness; taper to stop). [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Bloating and distension neuromodulators**: **amitriptyline 10 mg at night**, titrate every 2-3 weeks to **25-50 mg**; or **duloxetine 30-60 mg/day**; counsel that they act on pain processing, not depression. [Rome Foundation Neuromodulators 2018]`,`**CAPS first line**: **TCA (amitriptyline or nortriptyline 25-75 mg at night) or SNRI (duloxetine 60 mg/day)**; augment with **low-dose quetiapine 25-100 mg** or **pregabalin 75-150 mg twice daily** if partial response. [Rome Foundation Neuromodulators 2018]`,`**Rumination**: **diaphragmatic breathing** taught at meals is the treatment of choice; baclofen 10 mg three times daily if refractory. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Avoid**: opioids, repeat imaging, empirical surgery, routine probiotics (AGA 2023 does not recommend them for bloating), and anticholinergic antispasmodics in constipation-predominant bloating. [AGA CPU Belching and Bloating 2023]`]},{heading:`Gut-brain behavioural therapy`,points:[`**Brain-gut behavioural therapies (BGBT)** have evidence across DGBI: **CBT, gut-directed hypnotherapy, mindfulness-based therapy, psychodynamic-interpersonal therapy and acceptance and commitment therapy**. [AGA CPU Gut-Brain Behavioural Therapy 2022]`,`**CBT** targets catastrophising, symptom-specific anxiety and avoidance; 4-12 sessions, including validated **digital and telehealth formats**. [AGA CPU Gut-Brain Behavioural Therapy 2022]`,`**Gut-directed hypnotherapy** (e.g. 7-12 sessions) gives durable responses in about half of refractory patients and helps bloating and pain. [BSG IBS 2021]`,`**Diaphragmatic breathing** is the specific behavioural therapy for **rumination, supragastric belching and abdominophrenic dyssynergia**. [AGA CPU Belching and Bloating 2023]`,`**Refer early** - BGBT works best combined with medical care, not as a last resort; present it as treatment of the gut-brain axis, not 'psychiatric referral'. [AGA CPU Gut-Brain Behavioural Therapy 2022]`,`In India, use **District Mental Health Programme clinical psychologists, Tele-MANAS (14416)** and app- or video-based CBT where trained psychogastroenterologists are not available. [MoHFW Tele-MANAS 2022]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Admission is rarely needed** for DGBI; admit only for a new red flag, intractable vomiting with dehydration, suspected obstruction, or for supervised opioid withdrawal. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Acute pain in a known CAPS patient**: examine fully each time, check **lactate, lipase, CBC, urine and erect X-ray** if the pain pattern has changed - new organic disease does occur. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Narcotic bowel detoxification**: start a **TCA or SNRI first**, then **taper opioids by 10-20% every 2-3 days** (faster in hospital) with **clonidine 0.1 mg two to three times daily** for withdrawal and laxatives for constipation. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Avoid IV opioids and pentazocine** for CAPS flares in emergency; use paracetamol, heat, reassurance and the patient's agreed neuromodulator plan. [Rome IV 2016]`,`Suspected **acute intermittent porphyria** (pain, dark urine, hyponatraemia, neuropathy): check urine **porphobilinogen**; avoid porphyrinogenic drugs and give haem arginate/glucose. [Harrison 22e]`,`Pseudo-obstruction or massive distension with dilated colon over **9 cm caecum** is not functional - manage as Ogilvie syndrome with surgical input. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Elderly**: new bloating over 50 is organic until proved otherwise - colorectal and ovarian cancer, medications (calcium-channel blockers, opioids, anticholinergics) and constipation. [AGA CPU Belching and Bloating 2023]`,`**Pregnancy**: physiological bloating from progesterone and constipation; use diet, fibre, **lactulose or PEG**; avoid rifaximin data-poor use and neuromodulator initiation unless necessary. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diabetes**: bloating may be autonomic gastroparesis, SIBO, metformin or **GLP-1 receptor agonist** effect - review drugs before labelling functional. [AGA CPU Belching and Bloating 2023]`,`**Children and adolescents**: Rome IV paediatric criteria include **functional abdominal pain - not otherwise specified** and abdominal migraine; avoid TCA in the young without specialist input. [Rome IV Paediatric 2016]`,`**Cardiac disease**: before a TCA in patients over 50 or with cardiac history, do an **ECG (QTc, conduction block)**; prefer duloxetine or low-dose nortriptyline. [Rome Foundation Neuromodulators 2018]`,`**CKD and liver disease**: reduce duloxetine (avoid if eGFR under 30) and pregabalin (renal dosing); avoid duloxetine in hepatic impairment. [BNF 2025]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: Rome IV diagnosis, CBC, tTG, ultrasound, a trial of diet, laxative and low-dose amitriptyline (NLEM, Jan Aushadhi) cover most patients without breath testing. [ISG IBS Consensus 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Quality of life** is impaired as much as in organic GI disease; CAPS carries high disability, work absence and healthcare cost. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Iatrogenic harm** is the main complication: unnecessary surgery, radiation from repeated CT, opioid dependence and narcotic bowel syndrome. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Nutritional restriction**: prolonged self-imposed exclusion diets risk **avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder (ARFID)** and micronutrient deficiency. [AGA CPU Diet in IBS 2022]`,`**Supragastric belching** may coexist with GERD and drive reflux episodes; treating the behaviour reduces reflux symptoms. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Prognosis**: symptoms fluctuate but are not life-shortening; about half improve meaningfully with combined neuromodulator and behavioural therapy. [Rome Foundation Neuromodulators 2018]`,`**SIBO recurs** in up to **40-45% within 9 months** after successful antibiotic treatment if the predisposing cause persists. [ACG SIBO 2020]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer to gastroenterology**: any alarm feature, diagnostic doubt, need for manometry or breath testing, refractory symptoms after 3-6 months of first-line care, or suspected pseudo-obstruction. [AGA CPU Belching and Bloating 2023]`,`**Refer to pain/psychiatry/psychology services**: CAPS, narcotic bowel syndrome, severe depression or suicidality, and eating-disorder features. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Review neuromodulators at 4-6 weeks** for response and side effects (dry mouth, constipation, sedation, weight gain); continue **6-12 months** after response, then taper. [Rome Foundation Neuromodulators 2018]`,`**One named clinician and scheduled visits** (not symptom-driven visits) reduce emergency attendance and investigation cycling in CAPS. [Rome IV 2016]`,`Use a **symptom and food diary** and **IBS-SSS or bloating severity scale** to measure response objectively. [AGA CPU Belching and Bloating 2023]`,`Recheck **weight and CBC** at follow-up; new weight loss or anaemia restarts the work-up. [AGA CPU Belching and Bloating 2023]`]},{heading:`Prevention and counselling`,points:[`**Rational prescribing**: limit PPIs to clear indications (SIBO risk), and avoid opioids for chronic non-cancer abdominal pain. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Counsel**: eat slowly, avoid carbonated drinks, gum and straws, regular meals, daily physical activity and adequate sleep; walking after meals helps gas transit. [AGA CPU Belching and Bloating 2023]`,`**Explain the gut-brain axis** in plain language and that the disorder is real but not dangerous; give written information. [AGA CPU Gut-Brain Behavioural Therapy 2022]`,`Screen and treat **anxiety and depression** at the Ayushman Arogya Mandir/primary care level under the NMHP/DMHP and Tele-MANAS. [MoHFW Tele-MANAS 2022]`,`Link **diabetes and thyroid** screening under **NP-NCD** when the work-up suggests a metabolic contributor. [NP-NCD 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**AGA Clinical Practice Update on belching, abdominal bloating and distension (2023)**: Rome IV positive diagnosis, breath tests and gastric emptying only in selected patients, **biofeedback for abdominophrenic dyssynergia**, **central neuromodulators** and BGBT; probiotics not recommended. [AGA CPU Belching and Bloating 2023]`,`**AGA Clinical Practice Update on gut-brain behavioural therapies (2022)**: BGBT recommended across DGBI, including digital delivery; clinicians should screen for and refer to psychogastroenterology. [AGA CPU Gut-Brain Behavioural Therapy 2022]`,`**Rome Foundation Global Epidemiology Study (2021)** established DGBI prevalence of about **40%** worldwide and included India in household surveys. [Rome Foundation GES 2021]`,`**European guideline on hydrogen and methane breath tests (2022)** standardised glucose and lactose test doses and cut-offs; it does not support routine fructose or sorbitol testing. [ESNM-UEG Breath Test Guideline 2022]`,`**ACG SIBO guideline (2020)** introduced the term **intestinal methanogen overgrowth** (methane 10 ppm or more) and the 20 ppm hydrogen rise within 90 minutes. [ACG SIBO 2020]`,`**Rome V** is being finalised in 2026; expect refinements of bloating and CAPS criteria - quote Rome IV unless asked otherwise. [Rome Foundation 2026]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **'Define functional bloating and distension and outline its management'** - write Rome IV criteria, bloating versus distension, abdominophrenic dyssynergia and the stepwise plan. [Rome IV 2016]`,`Viva: **supragastric versus gastric belching** - supragastric is behavioural, stops on talking, very frequent, treated with diaphragmatic breathing; gastric is TLESR-mediated venting, treated with air-swallowing reduction and baclofen. [Rome IV 2016]`,`Viva: **SIBO breath test cut-offs** - hydrogen rise 20 ppm or more within 90 minutes; methane 10 ppm or more = IMO. [ACG SIBO 2020]`,`**Common mistake**: labelling CAPS without excluding Carnett-positive abdominal wall pain, porphyria or a new organic problem when the pain pattern changes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake**: escalating opioids for chronic abdominal pain - the answer is opioid withdrawal with neuromodulator cover. [Rome IV 2016]`,`Remember the **neuromodulator rule**: start low, go slow, explain why an 'antidepressant' is used for pain, and review at 4-6 weeks. [Rome Foundation Neuromodulators 2018]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Rome IV criteria at a glance (3 months, onset 6 months or more earlier)`,columns:[`Disorder`,`Core criterion`,`Key exclusion or qualifier`,`First-line treatment`],rows:[[`Functional abdominal bloating/distension (C3)`,`Recurrent bloating and/or distension at least 1 day a week, predominant`,`IBS, functional dyspepsia, functional constipation criteria not met; mild pain allowed`,`Explanation, diet, treat constipation, neuromodulator, biofeedback`],[`Excessive supragastric belching (B3a)`,`Bothersome belching more than 3 days a week; air expelled from oesophagus`,`Stops during speech and sleep; impedance confirms`,`Diaphragmatic breathing, speech therapy, CBT`],[`Excessive gastric belching (B3b)`,`Bothersome belching more than 3 days a week; air vented from stomach`,`Exclude GERD, gastroparesis`,`Reduce air swallowing; baclofen`],[`Rumination syndrome (B4)`,`Effortless regurgitation of recently eaten food, re-chewed or spat`,`Not preceded by retching`,`Diaphragmatic breathing at meals`],[`Centrally mediated abdominal pain syndrome (D1)`,`Continuous or near-continuous pain limiting function`,`Little relation to eating, defecation, menses; not feigned`,`TCA or SNRI, BGBT, one clinician`],[`Narcotic bowel syndrome (D2)`,`Pain worsens or persists with escalating opioid doses`,`Soar and crash pattern, progression`,`Opioid taper under neuromodulator cover`]]},{heading:`Drugs used in bloating, belching and CAPS`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Amitriptyline (TCA)`,`10 mg orally at night`,`Increase by 10-25 mg every 2-3 weeks to 25-75 mg; continue 6-12 months after response`,`Dry mouth, constipation, sedation, weight gain, QT prolongation; avoid after recent MI, in arrhythmia, glaucoma, urinary retention`],[`Nortriptyline (TCA)`,`10-25 mg orally at night`,`Titrate to 50-75 mg`,`Fewer anticholinergic effects than amitriptyline; ECG over 50 years`],[`Duloxetine (SNRI)`,`30 mg orally daily`,`Increase to 60 mg after 1-2 weeks`,`Nausea, sweating, hypertension; avoid if eGFR under 30 or hepatic disease`],[`Pregabalin (augmentation)`,`75 mg orally twice daily`,`Up to 150 mg twice daily`,`Dizziness, sedation, oedema, misuse potential; reduce in CKD`],[`Quetiapine (augmentation)`,`25 mg orally at night`,`Up to 100 mg`,`Sedation, metabolic effects, QT; specialist use`],[`Baclofen`,`5 mg orally three times daily`,`Up to 10 mg three times daily; taper to stop`,`Sedation, dizziness, withdrawal seizures if stopped abruptly; reduce in CKD`],[`Rifaximin (SIBO)`,`550 mg orally three times daily (400 mg three times daily in Indian practice)`,`14 days; may repeat on recurrence`,`Minimal absorption; nausea; C. difficile rare`],[`Prucalopride (constipation-related bloating)`,`2 mg orally once daily (1 mg if over 65 or eGFR under 30)`,`Review at 4 weeks`,`Headache, diarrhoea, abdominal pain; mood changes rare`]]},{heading:`Breath testing - indications and cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`Substrate and dose`,`Positive result`,`Pitfalls`],rows:[[`Glucose hydrogen breath test (SIBO)`,`75 g glucose in water`,`Hydrogen rise 20 ppm or more over baseline within 90 minutes`,`Misses distal SIBO; glucose absorbed proximally`],[`Lactulose hydrogen breath test (SIBO)`,`10 g lactulose`,`Hydrogen rise 20 ppm or more within 90 minutes`,`Rapid transit gives false positive colonic peak`],[`Methane measurement`,`With either test`,`Methane 10 ppm or more at any time = intestinal methanogen overgrowth`,`Associated with constipation; does not need a rise`],[`Lactose breath test`,`25 g lactose (or 50 g)`,`Hydrogen rise 20 ppm or more within 3 hours plus symptoms`,`Non-hydrogen producers give false negatives`],[`Jejunal aspirate culture`,`Endoscopic aspirate`,`10^3 CFU/mL or more`,`Invasive, contamination, not available widely`]]},{heading:`Differentiating causes of chronic bloating and abdominal pain`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating feature`,`Test that settles it`],rows:[[`Functional bloating/distension`,`Normal by morning, worse by evening; no defecation-linked pain`,`Clinical (Rome IV) plus normal basic tests`],[`IBS`,`Pain linked to defecation and stool change`,`Rome IV clinical criteria`],[`Coeliac disease`,`Diarrhoea, iron deficiency, short stature, family history`,`tTG-IgA with total IgA, duodenal biopsy`],[`SIBO`,`Risk factor (PPI, diabetes, scleroderma, surgery), diarrhoea, B12 deficiency`,`Glucose breath test`],[`Ovarian cancer`,`New persistent bloating, woman over 50`,`CA-125 and pelvic ultrasound`],[`Ascites`,`Shifting dullness, fluid thrill`,`Ultrasound and paracentesis (SAAG)`],[`Abdominal wall pain`,`Localised point tenderness, Carnett sign positive`,`Clinical; trigger-point injection response`],[`Acute intermittent porphyria`,`Episodic pain, dark urine, neuropathy, hyponatraemia`,`Urine porphobilinogen during attack`]]}],redFlags:[`Onset of bloating or abdominal pain after age 45-50, or new persistent bloating in a woman over 50 (ovarian cancer)`,`Unintentional weight loss, GI bleeding, iron-deficiency anaemia or a palpable mass`,`Nocturnal symptoms that wake the patient, or progressive dysphagia`,`Persistent vomiting, dilated bowel loops on X-ray or absolutely no flatus (obstruction or pseudo-obstruction)`,`Ascites, organomegaly or lymphadenopathy`,`Family history of colorectal cancer, IBD or coeliac disease`,`Escalating opioid use with worsening pain (narcotic bowel syndrome) or suicidal ideation`],pearls:[`**Bloating is a sensation; distension is a sign** - and most patients with distension have normal gas volume (abdominophrenic dyssynergia).`,`**Supragastric belching stops when the patient talks** - observe it in the consultation room.`,`**Glucose breath test**: hydrogen rise 20 ppm in 90 minutes; **methane 10 ppm** at any time = IMO.`,`**CAPS = continuous pain, little link to gut function, high disability** - treat the brain end with TCA/SNRI and BGBT.`,`**Narcotic bowel**: more opioid, more pain - taper by 10-20% every few days under neuromodulator cover.`,`**Carnett sign positive** points to abdominal wall pain, not visceral disease.`,`Neuromodulator rule: **start low, go slow, explain, review at 4-6 weeks, continue 6-12 months**.`,`AGA 2023: **no routine probiotics, no repeat imaging** in established functional bloating.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-functional-other-q1`,stem:`A 34-year-old woman complains that her abdomen is flat each morning but becomes visibly distended by evening, 'like a 5-month pregnancy'. Stool habit is normal and pain is mild. CBC, tTG-IgA and ultrasound are normal. Which mechanism best explains the visible distension?`,options:[`Excess colonic gas production from fermentation`,`Paradoxical diaphragmatic descent with anterior abdominal wall relaxation`,`Small-volume ascites missed on ultrasound`,`Delayed gastric emptying with gastric dilatation`],answer:1,explanation:`Abdominophrenic dyssynergia - the diaphragm contracts and descends while the anterior wall relaxes in response to normal gas - explains visible distension in functional bloating; intestinal gas volume is usually normal, so excess fermentation is not the main cause. A normal ultrasound excludes meaningful ascites, and gastroparesis causes nausea and early satiety rather than evening distension. EMG-guided biofeedback treats it.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-functional-other-q2`,stem:`In a glucose hydrogen breath test for small intestinal bacterial overgrowth, which result is considered positive according to the ACG 2020 guideline?`,options:[`Hydrogen rise of 10 ppm over baseline within 180 minutes`,`Methane of 3 ppm at any time`,`Hydrogen rise of 20 ppm or more over baseline within 90 minutes`,`Any hydrogen peak occurring after 120 minutes`],answer:2,explanation:`The ACG 2020 and North American consensus cut-off is a hydrogen rise of 20 ppm or more over baseline within 90 minutes; methane of 10 ppm or more (not 3 ppm) defines intestinal methanogen overgrowth. A late peak after 120 minutes reflects colonic fermentation, not small-bowel overgrowth, and a 10 ppm rise is below threshold.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-functional-other-q3`,stem:`A 28-year-old man belches audibly 15-20 times a minute during history-taking; the belching stops completely while he answers questions continuously. Upper endoscopy is normal. The most appropriate first-line treatment is:`,options:[`Diaphragmatic breathing and behavioural therapy`,`High-dose PPI twice daily for 8 weeks`,`Laparoscopic fundoplication`,`Domperidone 10 mg three times daily`],answer:0,explanation:`Very frequent belching that stops during speech is typical of supragastric belching, a learned behaviour in which air is sucked into the oesophagus and expelled; diaphragmatic breathing, speech-therapy techniques and CBT are first line. PPIs help only coexisting reflux, prokinetics do not change the behaviour, and fundoplication may make belching impossible and cause gas-bloat.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-functional-other-q4`,stem:`A 45-year-old woman with continuous abdominal pain for 4 years has had a cholecystectomy and two diagnostic laparoscopies. She now takes tramadol 400 mg/day with worsening pain between doses. Investigations are normal. Which is the best management?`,options:[`Switch to tapentadol and add pregabalin`,`Start duloxetine, then taper tramadol gradually with clonidine for withdrawal`,`Repeat CT abdomen and refer for adhesiolysis`,`Increase tramadol and add hyoscine butylbromide`],answer:1,explanation:`Escalating opioid use with worsening pain and soar-and-crash pattern fits narcotic bowel syndrome superimposed on CAPS; the treatment is a neuromodulator such as duloxetine or a TCA, then gradual opioid taper with clonidine and laxatives. Switching opioids or increasing the dose perpetuates hyperalgesia, and further imaging and adhesiolysis add iatrogenic harm without benefit.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-functional-other-q5`,stem:`According to the AGA 2023 Clinical Practice Update on belching and bloating, which of the following is NOT recommended for routine treatment of functional bloating?`,options:[`Central neuromodulators to reduce visceral hypersensitivity`,`Biofeedback for abdominophrenic dyssynergia`,`Treating coexisting constipation with secretagogues or laxatives`,`Probiotics`],answer:3,explanation:`The AGA 2023 update states that probiotics should not be used for abdominal bloating and distension because evidence is insufficient. It supports central neuromodulators for visceral hypersensitivity, EMG-guided biofeedback for abdominophrenic dyssynergia, and treatment of constipation, which is a common hidden driver of bloating.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-functional-other-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Define disorders of gut-brain interaction. Describe the Rome IV criteria, pathophysiology, evaluation and management of functional abdominal bloating/distension and centrally mediated abdominal pain syndrome.`,openingLines:[`Disorders of gut-brain interaction (Rome IV, 2016) are GI symptoms arising from altered motility, visceral hypersensitivity, mucosal-immune and microbiota changes and altered central processing, without structural disease.`,`Functional bloating/distension and centrally mediated abdominal pain syndrome sit at two ends - one peripheral reflex disorder, one central pain disorder - and both are diagnosed positively, not by exclusion alone.`],answer:[{heading:`Rome IV criteria`,points:[`Functional bloating/distension: recurrent bloating or distension at least 1 day a week, predominant, IBS/FD/FC criteria not met; 3 months, onset 6 months earlier.`,`CAPS: continuous or near-continuous pain, little relation to physiological events, limits function, not feigned, not explained by another disorder.`,`Narcotic bowel syndrome: worsening pain with escalating opioids, soar and crash.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Visceral hypersensitivity; impaired gas transit; carbohydrate maldigestion and SIBO; constipation and dyssynergia.`,`Abdominophrenic dyssynergia: diaphragm descends, wall relaxes - distension without extra gas.`,`CAPS: loss of descending inhibition and central sensitisation; opioid-induced glial activation.`]},{heading:`Evaluation`,points:[`History with Bristol chart, diet, opioid and psychological history; alarm features.`,`Examination: girth, ascites, Carnett sign, DRE for dyssynergia.`,`Minimal tests: CBC, CRP, tTG-IgA, TSH, glucose; CA-125 and pelvic USG in women over 50; breath test or manometry only if it changes care.`]},{heading:`Management of bloating`,points:[`Explanation; avoid carbonated drinks and gum; low-FODMAP diet with dietitian.`,`Treat constipation (PEG, prucalopride), biofeedback for dyssynergia; rifaximin if SIBO proven.`,`Amitriptyline 10-50 mg at night or duloxetine; no routine probiotics.`]},{heading:`Management of CAPS`,points:[`One clinician, scheduled visits, avoid repeat tests and surgery.`,`TCA or SNRI first; augment with quetiapine or pregabalin.`,`Brain-gut behavioural therapy: CBT, hypnotherapy; taper opioids under neuromodulator cover.`]},{heading:`Prognosis and referral`,points:[`Not life-shortening; iatrogenic harm is the main risk.`,`Refer for alarm features, refractory symptoms, narcotic bowel or psychiatric comorbidity.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: chronic bloating - alarm features - minimal tests - treat constipation/diet - breath test if indicated - neuromodulator and BGBT`,`Diagram of abdominophrenic dyssynergia (diaphragm descent, wall protrusion)`],markSplit:[{part:`Definitions and Rome IV criteria`,marks:2},{part:`Pathophysiology`,marks:2},{part:`Evaluation and red flags`,marks:2},{part:`Management of bloating`,marks:2},{part:`Management of CAPS and narcotic bowel`,marks:2}],keywords:[`Rome IV`,`DGBI`,`abdominophrenic dyssynergia`,`CAPS`,`narcotic bowel`,`neuromodulator`,`BGBT`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-functional-other-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Differentiate supragastric belching, gastric belching and rumination syndrome.`,openingLines:[`Rome IV groups belching disorders and rumination under gastroduodenal DGBI; all three bring gas or food up but by different mechanisms.`,`Supragastric belching is behavioural, gastric belching is TLESR-mediated venting, and rumination is a learned abdominal wall contraction regurgitating food.`],answer:[{heading:`Mechanism`,points:[`Supragastric: air sucked into the oesophagus and expelled within seconds; never reaches stomach.`,`Gastric: swallowed air vented from the stomach through TLESRs.`,`Rumination: abdominal pressure rise over 30 mmHg pushes food up after meals.`]},{heading:`Clinical clues`,points:[`Supragastric: up to 20 belches a minute, stops on talking and in sleep.`,`Gastric: fewer belches, after meals and carbonated drinks.`,`Rumination: effortless regurgitation of undigested food within minutes of eating, re-chewed or spat, no retching.`]},{heading:`Confirmation and treatment`,points:[`Impedance-pH or high-resolution impedance manometry separates all three.`,`Supragastric: diaphragmatic breathing, speech therapy, CBT.`,`Gastric: reduce air swallowing, baclofen.`,`Rumination: diaphragmatic breathing at meals; baclofen if refractory.`]}],mustDraw:[`Three-column comparison table: mechanism, clinical clue, test, treatment`],markSplit:[{part:`Mechanism`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:1.5},{part:`Investigations and treatment`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`supragastric belching`,`TLESR`,`rumination`,`diaphragmatic breathing`,`baclofen`,`impedance manometry`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`Drossman DA, et al. Rome IV: Functional Gastrointestinal Disorders - Disorders of Gut-Brain Interaction, 4th edition, Rome Foundation, 2016`,`Moshiree B, et al. AGA Clinical Practice Update on Evaluation and Management of Belching, Abdominal Bloating, and Distention: Expert Review. Gastroenterology, 2023`,`Keefer L, et al. AGA Clinical Practice Update on the Role of Gut-Brain Behavioral Therapies (Psychogastroenterology) in DGBI: Expert Review. Gastroenterology, 2022`,`Pimentel M, et al. ACG Clinical Guideline: Small Intestinal Bacterial Overgrowth. American Journal of Gastroenterology, 2020`,`Drossman DA, et al. Neuromodulators for Functional GI Disorders (DGBI): A Rome Foundation Working Team Report. Gastroenterology, 2018`,`Sperber AD, et al. Worldwide Prevalence and Burden of Functional Gastrointestinal Disorders: Rome Foundation Global Study. Gastroenterology, 2021`,`Hammer HF, et al. European guideline on indications, performance and clinical impact of hydrogen and methane breath tests. United European Gastroenterology Journal, 2022`]}),u.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-food-allergy-intolerance`,title:`Food intolerance, lactose intolerance and FODMAPs`,oneLiner:`**Food intolerance** is a **non-immune, dose-dependent** reaction to food (enzymatic, pharmacological or fermentative), unlike **IgE- or cell-mediated food allergy**; its commonest form is **lactose intolerance from adult-type lactase non-persistence** (two-thirds to four-fifths of Indians), diagnosed by the **lactose hydrogen breath test** and managed by **lactose reduction, not elimination**, while **FODMAP** intolerance in IBS is managed by a **three-phase low-FODMAP diet** (restrict, reintroduce, personalise).`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`lactose intolerance`,`lactase non-persistence`,`hypolactasia`,`LCT -13910`,`hydrogen breath test`,`lactose tolerance test`,`FODMAP`,`low-FODMAP diet`,`fructans`,`polyols`,`fructose malabsorption`,`non-coeliac gluten sensitivity`,`NCGS`,`Salerno criteria`,`wheat allergy`,`sucrase-isomaltase deficiency`,`food allergy`,`IgG food test`,`lactase enzyme`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Adverse food reactions** are either **immune-mediated (food allergy: IgE-mediated, non-IgE or mixed, and coeliac disease)** or **non-immune (food intolerance)**; the distinction decides risk - allergy can kill, intolerance does not. [EAACI Food Allergy Guideline 2023]`,`**Lactose malabsorption** is incomplete digestion of lactose from low lactase; **lactose intolerance** is malabsorption that **causes symptoms** - many malabsorbers are asymptomatic at usual intakes. [ESNM-UEG Breath Test Guideline 2022]`,`**Lactase non-persistence** (genetically programmed fall in lactase after weaning) affects about **two-thirds of the world population**; persistence is a recent mutation common in north-west Europe and pastoralist populations. [Harrison 22e]`,`**India shows a north-south gradient**: lactose malabsorption in about **two-thirds of north Indians and over 80% of south Indians**, mirroring the frequency of the persistence allele. [Babu et al, Am J Clin Nutr 2010]`,`Only a minority of malabsorbers have symptoms with **up to 12 g of lactose (about 250 mL milk) taken with food**; most Indian patients therefore tolerate tea with milk and curd. [NIH Consensus Lactose 2010]`,`**FODMAP sensitivity** contributes to symptoms in **50-75% of IBS patients**, who respond to a low-FODMAP diet in randomised trials. [AGA CPU Diet in IBS 2022]`,`**Non-coeliac gluten (wheat) sensitivity (NCGS)**: self-reported in up to **6-10%** of populations, but confirmed by blinded challenge in a minority - many 'gluten-sensitive' patients actually react to **fructans**. [Salerno Experts' Criteria 2015]`,`**IgE-mediated food allergy** affects about **2-6% of children and 1-3% of adults**; in India, sensitisation to shrimp, peanut, egg, milk and chickpea is reported, but challenge-proven allergy is less common than in the West. [EAACI Food Allergy Guideline 2023]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and types of food intolerance`,points:[`**Primary (adult-type) hypolactasia**: genetic lactase non-persistence; symptoms usually begin in late childhood or adolescence and progress slowly. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Secondary lactase deficiency**: brush-border injury from **acute gastroenteritis (rotavirus), giardiasis, coeliac disease, tropical sprue, Crohn disease, SIBO, HIV enteropathy, chemotherapy and radiation**; it is reversible when the mucosa heals. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Congenital lactase deficiency** (autosomal recessive, LCT gene) causes watery diarrhoea from the first feeds - very rare, mostly reported from Finland. [Nelson 22e]`,`**Developmental lactase deficiency**: preterm infants under 34 weeks have low lactase, which matures with feeding. [Nelson 22e]`,`**Congenital sucrase-isomaltase deficiency**: diarrhoea starting when sucrose and starch enter the diet; heterozygote variants are now linked to IBS-like symptoms. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fructose malabsorption**: limited GLUT5 transport when fructose exceeds glucose (honey, mango, apple, high-fructose drinks); distinct from **hereditary fructose intolerance** (aldolase B deficiency, hypoglycaemia and liver injury). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pharmacological intolerance**: **caffeine, histamine (fermented food, fish), tyramine, capsaicin, alcohol and monosodium glutamate**; **sulphites** can trigger asthma. [EAACI Food Allergy Guideline 2023]`,`**Polyol and sweetener intolerance**: sorbitol, mannitol, xylitol in sugar-free sweets, chewing gum, diabetic foods and some syrups cause osmotic diarrhoea and bloating. [Monash FODMAP Program 2024]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**Lactase-phlorizin hydrolase** on the jejunal brush border splits lactose into glucose and galactose; in non-persistence, transcription falls after weaning under control of a regulatory site in the **MCM6 gene upstream of LCT**. [Harrison 22e]`,`The persistence variant **LCT -13910 C>T** (rs4988235) keeps lactase on; the **C/C genotype** predicts non-persistence and is commoner in south than north India. [Babu et al, Am J Clin Nutr 2010]`,`Unabsorbed lactose is **osmotically active**, drawing water into the small bowel (borborygmi, loose stools), then reaches the colon where bacteria ferment it to **short-chain fatty acids, hydrogen, CO2 and methane** (gas, bloating, flatus). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Stool pH falls below 5.5** and stool contains **reducing substances** from fermented sugars - the basis of paediatric bedside tests; the **stool osmotic gap exceeds 125 mOsm/kg**. [Nelson 22e]`,`**FODMAPs** (Fermentable Oligosaccharides - fructans, galacto-oligosaccharides; Disaccharides - lactose; Monosaccharides - excess fructose; And Polyols) are short-chain carbohydrates that are **poorly absorbed, osmotic and rapidly fermented**. [Monash FODMAP Program 2024]`,`**MRI studies** show FODMAPs increase **small-bowel water and colonic gas** in everyone; symptoms arise only in people with **visceral hypersensitivity** (IBS), which is why FODMAPs are not 'bad foods'. [AGA CPU Diet in IBS 2022]`,`**NCGS** may be triggered by **gluten, amylase-trypsin inhibitors (ATIs, innate immune activation via TLR4) or fructans**; there are no villous atrophy, no coeliac antibodies and no wheat IgE. [Salerno Experts' Criteria 2015]`,`**Food allergy** differs fundamentally: **IgE on mast cells** (minutes to 2 hours, urticaria, angioedema, anaphylaxis) or **T-cell mediated** (FPIES, eosinophilic oesophagitis, food protein-induced proctocolitis). [EAACI Food Allergy Guideline 2023]`]},{heading:`Classification of adverse food reactions`,points:[`**Immune IgE-mediated**: urticaria, angioedema, oral allergy syndrome, anaphylaxis; tests are skin prick and specific IgE. [EAACI Food Allergy Guideline 2023]`,`**Immune non-IgE**: food protein-induced enterocolitis (FPIES), allergic proctocolitis, cow's milk protein enteropathy and eosinophilic oesophagitis. [EAACI Food Allergy Guideline 2023]`,`**Immune autoimmune**: **coeliac disease** - gluten-triggered, tTG antibodies, villous atrophy; see the Malabsorption and coeliac disease topic. [ACG Coeliac 2023]`,`**Non-immune enzymatic**: lactase deficiency, sucrase-isomaltase deficiency, fructose malabsorption, pancreatic insufficiency. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Non-immune pharmacological**: caffeine, histamine, tyramine, MSG, alcohol. [EAACI Food Allergy Guideline 2023]`,`**Non-immune fermentative**: FODMAP intolerance in IBS and functional bloating. [AGA CPU Diet in IBS 2022]`,`**Toxic**: scombroid fish poisoning (histamine), contaminated food, natural toxins such as lathyrism from khesari dal - occur in anyone with sufficient dose. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cow's milk protein allergy (CMPA)** in infants is an immune reaction to milk **protein**; **lactose intolerance** is a reaction to milk **sugar** - the commonest viva confusion. [IAP Guidelines 2023]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Lactose intolerance**: bloating, borborygmi, cramps, flatus and loose stools **30 minutes to 2 hours after milk**; symptoms are **dose-dependent** and less with curd, paneer or milk taken with meals. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Food allergy history**: reaction **within minutes**, involves skin, airway or circulation, occurs with **trace amounts** and is reproducible every time. [EAACI Food Allergy Guideline 2023]`,`**FODMAP pattern**: symptoms after **wheat (atta, maida), onion, garlic, rajma, chana, milk, mango, apple, watermelon, cauliflower and honey**; usually cumulative over the day. [Monash FODMAP Program 2024]`,`**NCGS**: bloating, abdominal pain, fatigue, 'brain fog', headache, joint pain within hours to a day of wheat; symptoms settle on gluten-free diet. [Salerno Experts' Criteria 2015]`,`**Secondary lactose intolerance clues**: onset after an episode of gastroenteritis, weight loss, iron deficiency or diarrhoea persisting despite milk withdrawal - investigate the underlying enteropathy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Children**: watery, frothy, acidic stools with perianal excoriation after diarrhoea suggest secondary lactose intolerance; failure to thrive points to coeliac disease or CMPA. [Nelson 22e]`,`Take a **diet history and 7-14 day food-symptom diary**, including hidden lactose (sweets, whey, medicines with lactose excipient) and sugar-free products. [AGA CPU Diet in IBS 2022]`,`Ask about **restrictive eating, weight loss and anxiety around food** before advising any exclusion diet - screen for **ARFID** and eating disorders. [AGA CPU Diet in IBS 2022]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Examination is usually normal** in primary lactose intolerance and FODMAP sensitivity. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for **weight loss, pallor, glossitis, angular stomatitis, oedema and clubbing** - they suggest malabsorption (coeliac, tropical sprue, Crohn) rather than simple intolerance. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dermatitis herpetiformis** (itchy vesicles on extensor surfaces) points to coeliac disease. [ACG Coeliac 2023]`,`**Urticaria, angioedema, wheeze or hypotension** after food indicate **IgE-mediated allergy**, not intolerance. [EAACI Food Allergy Guideline 2023]`,`In children, plot **growth on WHO/IAP charts**; growth faltering excludes simple primary lactose intolerance. [IAP Guidelines 2023]`,`**Perianal excoriation** in infants with diarrhoea suggests acidic stools from carbohydrate malabsorption. [Nelson 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Coeliac disease**: diarrhoea, iron deficiency, short stature, positive tTG-IgA - must be excluded **before** a gluten-free trial, because a gluten-free diet makes serology falsely negative. [ACG Coeliac 2023]`,`**Wheat allergy**: IgE-mediated, urticaria or anaphylaxis (including wheat-dependent exercise-induced anaphylaxis); positive skin prick or specific IgE. [EAACI Food Allergy Guideline 2023]`,`**IBS**: recurrent pain linked to defecation; food triggers are common but the disorder is broader than one food. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Giardiasis**: bloating, sulphurous belching, greasy stools and secondary lactose intolerance - very common in India; stool antigen or microscopy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tropical sprue**: chronic diarrhoea, megaloblastic anaemia after residence in tropics; responds to folic acid and tetracycline. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Microscopic colitis, bile acid diarrhoea, IBD and pancreatic insufficiency** in older patients with persistent watery or fatty diarrhoea; see the Approach to chronic diarrhoea topic. [BSG Chronic Diarrhoea 2018]`,`**Cow's milk protein allergy** in infants: blood in stools, eczema, vomiting; treated with extensively hydrolysed formula, not lactose-free formula. [IAP Guidelines 2023]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**First step in primary care**: a **2-week lactose-free trial followed by rechallenge** with 250-500 mL milk - resolution and recurrence support the diagnosis. [AGA CPU Diet in IBS 2022]`,`**Lactose hydrogen breath test**: **25 g lactose** (some labs use 50 g, which over-diagnoses), breath every 15-30 minutes for **3 hours**; **hydrogen rise of 20 ppm or more over baseline** = malabsorption; symptoms during the test = intolerance. [ESNM-UEG Breath Test Guideline 2022]`,`**False negatives** occur in **non-hydrogen producers (about 10-15%, methane producers)**, after recent antibiotics or colonoscopy prep; measuring methane improves yield. [ESNM-UEG Breath Test Guideline 2022]`,`**Lactose tolerance test**: 50 g lactose; **blood glucose rise under 20 mg/dL (1.1 mmol/L)** suggests malabsorption - cheaper but less accurate; affected by diabetes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Genetic test** for LCT -13910 C/C (and other variants) identifies non-persistence but **cannot detect secondary deficiency** and does not predict symptoms. [ESNM-UEG Breath Test Guideline 2022]`,`**Children**: **stool pH under 5.5 and reducing substances 0.5% or more** (Clinitest/Benedict) support carbohydrate malabsorption. [Nelson 22e]`,`**Exclude coeliac disease**: **tTG-IgA with total IgA** while eating gluten (at least 3 g/day for 2-6 weeks if already restricted), with duodenal biopsy if positive. [ACG Coeliac 2023]`,`**Fructose and sorbitol breath tests** are **not recommended** routinely because positive results do not predict response to restriction. [ESNM-UEG Breath Test Guideline 2022]`,`**IgG or IgG4 food antibody panels, hair analysis, kinesiology and cytotoxic tests have no diagnostic value** - IgG reflects tolerance and exposure; do not order them. [EAACI Position Paper 2008, reaffirmed 2023]`,`**NCGS** has no biomarker: diagnosis requires exclusion of coeliac disease and wheat allergy, then response to gluten withdrawal and recurrence on **double-blind placebo-controlled challenge** (Salerno). [Salerno Experts' Criteria 2015]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Lactose reduction, not elimination**: most tolerate **12 g lactose (one cup of milk) with meals**, and up to 24 g spread over the day; take milk with food, in small amounts, several times a day. [NIH Consensus Lactose 2010]`,`**Use low-lactose dairy**: **curd/yoghurt** (live bacterial beta-galactosidase), **hard cheese and paneer** (lactose drained in whey), buttermilk, lactose-free milk; ghee is virtually lactose-free. [ICMR-NIN Dietary Guidelines 2024]`,`**Lactase enzyme supplements** (tablets or drops) taken with the first sip of dairy reduce symptoms in some patients; colonic adaptation with regular small doses also helps. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Maintain calcium**: adults need about **1000 mg/day** (ICMR-NIN); if dairy is restricted, use curd, ragi, sesame, green leafy vegetables, small fish and calcium supplements with vitamin D. [ICMR-NIN RDA 2020]`,`**Secondary lactose intolerance**: treat the cause (giardiasis - **tinidazole 2 g single dose** or **metronidazole 400 mg three times daily for 5-7 days**, coeliac disease, sprue); temporary lactose reduction for 2-4 weeks while the mucosa heals. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Low-FODMAP diet - phase 1 restriction** for **2-6 weeks** with a dietitian, replacing high-FODMAP foods with low ones (rice, millets, potato, firm banana, papaya, orange, carrot, spinach, lactose-free dairy, paneer, eggs, meat). [AGA CPU Diet in IBS 2022]`,`**Phase 2 reintroduction** over **6-8 weeks**: reintroduce one FODMAP group at a time over 3 days in increasing amounts (fructans, GOS, lactose, fructose, sorbitol, mannitol) to identify triggers and thresholds. [Monash FODMAP Program 2024]`,`**Phase 3 personalisation**: long-term diet that avoids only proven triggers at the threshold dose - **lifelong strict low-FODMAP diet is wrong**, because it reduces bifidobacteria and nutritional diversity. [AGA CPU Diet in IBS 2022]`,`**Simpler 'FODMAP-gentle' or bottom-up approach** (restrict only a few high-yield groups such as wheat, onion-garlic and legumes) suits patients without dietitian access or at risk of disordered eating. [AGA CPU Diet in IBS 2022]`,`**NCGS**: after coeliac and wheat allergy are excluded, a trial of **gluten reduction or low-FODMAP diet** (fructan reduction) is reasonable; review need for strictness periodically. [Salerno Experts' Criteria 2015]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Food intolerance itself rarely needs admission**; admit for dehydration from severe diarrhoea, or when the work-up reveals malabsorption with weight loss, hypoalbuminaemia or electrolyte deficits. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Food-induced anaphylaxis** is the emergency: **IM adrenaline 0.5 mg (0.5 mL of 1 mg/mL) in adults, 0.01 mg/kg in children** into the anterolateral thigh, repeat in 5 minutes if needed; then refer to allergy and prescribe an auto-injector where available. [EAACI Anaphylaxis 2021]`,`**Hospitalised children with persistent diarrhoea**: WHO/IAP advise **continuing feeds**; lactose-free or reduced-lactose diet is used for **persistent diarrhoea** (14 days or more), not routinely in acute diarrhoea. [WHO/IAP Diarrhoea Guidelines 2023]`,`**Enteral nutrition intolerance**: high-osmolar or lactose-containing feeds can cause diarrhoea; most hospital formulas are lactose-free - check for C. difficile and sorbitol-containing liquid medicines before blaming the feed. [ESPEN 2023]`,`**Hereditary fructose intolerance** (vomiting, hypoglycaemia, liver failure after fructose/sucrose in infants) is an emergency: stop all fructose, sucrose and sorbitol, including IV solutions and syrups. [Nelson 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Infants**: breastfed babies almost never need lactose avoidance - 'lactose overload' from fore-milk is managed by complete emptying of one breast; congenital deficiency is very rare. [IAP Guidelines 2023]`,`**Children after acute gastroenteritis**: continue breast milk and normal diet with ORS and zinc; transient lactose intolerance is usually mild. [WHO/IAP Diarrhoea Guidelines 2023]`,`**Pregnancy and lactation**: lactase activity may rise in pregnancy; ensure **calcium 1000 mg/day** and avoid unnecessary dairy restriction. [ICMR-NIN RDA 2020]`,`**Elderly**: dairy avoidance worsens **osteoporosis and sarcopenia**; prefer curd and lactase rather than exclusion; new 'intolerance' in old age needs work-up for organic disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diabetes**: lactose tolerance test is invalid; sugar-free products contain **polyols** that cause osmotic diarrhoea - review them. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Medicines with lactose excipient**: quantity (under 0.5 g per tablet) is too small to cause symptoms in nearly all patients; do not switch essential drugs for this reason. [BNF 2025]`,`**Vegetarian Indian diet**: restricting dairy, wheat and legumes together can cause **protein, calcium and B12 deficiency** - dietitian input essential. [ICMR-NIN Dietary Guidelines 2024]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Unnecessary dietary restriction** from self-diagnosis or invalid IgG tests causes **calcium, vitamin D and protein deficiency** and lowers quality of life. [EAACI Food Allergy Guideline 2023]`,`**Low bone mineral density and fractures** are reported in people avoiding dairy without calcium replacement. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ARFID and orthorexia** can be triggered by prolonged restrictive diets in anxious patients. [AGA CPU Diet in IBS 2022]`,`**Microbiome change**: long low-FODMAP restriction reduces **bifidobacteria**; reintroduction restores it. [AGA CPU Diet in IBS 2022]`,`**Missed diagnosis**: labelling coeliac disease, giardiasis or Crohn disease as 'lactose intolerance' delays treatment. [ACG Coeliac 2023]`,`**Prognosis**: primary lactose intolerance is lifelong but benign; secondary forms resolve once the mucosa heals (usually 2-4 weeks after infection). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer to a dietitian** for any low-FODMAP diet, multiple exclusions, children, pregnancy and the elderly. [AGA CPU Diet in IBS 2022]`,`**Refer to gastroenterology** for alarm features, positive coeliac serology, persistent diarrhoea despite dietary change, weight loss or anaemia. [BSG Chronic Diarrhoea 2018]`,`**Refer to allergy** for any reaction with urticaria, angioedema, wheeze or hypotension, and for suspected FPIES or CMPA. [EAACI Food Allergy Guideline 2023]`,`**Review at 4-6 weeks** after starting a low-FODMAP diet: if no response, stop restriction rather than tighten it. [AGA CPU Diet in IBS 2022]`,`Monitor **weight, dietary adequacy (calcium, protein, fibre)** and quality of life at follow-up; consider vitamin D and BMD where dairy has long been avoided. [ICMR-NIN Dietary Guidelines 2024]`]},{heading:`Prevention and counselling`,points:[`**Explain** that lactose intolerance is common and harmless, that small amounts are fine and curd is usually tolerated - this prevents needless dairy exclusion. [NIH Consensus Lactose 2010]`,`**Discourage commercial IgG 'food intolerance' panels** and self-directed elimination diets. [EAACI Position Paper 2008, reaffirmed 2023]`,`**Read labels** for whey, milk solids, sorbitol, mannitol, xylitol and inulin (chicory root). [Monash FODMAP Program 2024]`,`Promote **WASH and safe drinking water** to prevent giardiasis and post-infectious lactase deficiency, especially in children. [MoHFW Swachh Bharat 2024]`,`Use **ICMR-NIN Dietary Guidelines for Indians 2024** to plan balanced vegetarian substitutions (millets, pulses in tolerated amounts, curd). [ICMR-NIN Dietary Guidelines 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**AGA Clinical Practice Update on diet in IBS (2022)**: low-FODMAP diet is the best-evidenced diet, delivered in **three phases** with a GI dietitian; screen for eating disorders; simpler diets acceptable. [AGA CPU Diet in IBS 2022]`,`**European breath test guideline (2022)**: lactose test with **25 g**, hydrogen rise 20 ppm, measure symptoms during test; **fructose and sorbitol tests not recommended routinely**. [ESNM-UEG Breath Test Guideline 2022]`,`**ACG Coeliac guideline (2023)**: tTG-IgA with total IgA on a gluten-containing diet before any gluten-free trial; no-biopsy diagnosis in selected adults remains debated. [ACG Coeliac 2023]`,`**EAACI Food Allergy Guideline (2023)**: diagnosis by history plus skin prick/specific IgE and oral food challenge; component testing; **IgG testing not recommended**. [EAACI Food Allergy Guideline 2023]`,`**ICMR-NIN Dietary Guidelines for Indians (2024)** updated RDA-based plate recommendations, relevant to dairy-restricted vegetarians. [ICMR-NIN Dietary Guidelines 2024]`,`**ACG IBS guideline (2021)** recommends a **limited trial of low-FODMAP diet** to improve global IBS symptoms. [ACG IBS 2021]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Viva: **'Difference between lactose intolerance and cow's milk protein allergy'** - sugar versus protein, enzymatic versus immune, lactose-free formula versus extensively hydrolysed formula. [IAP Guidelines 2023]`,`Viva: **'Expand FODMAP and name three Indian foods in each group'** - fructans (wheat, onion, garlic), GOS (rajma, chana), lactose (milk), fructose (mango, honey), polyols (mushroom, cauliflower, sugar-free sweets). [Monash FODMAP Program 2024]`,`Viva: **lactose breath test cut-off** - hydrogen rise 20 ppm above baseline after 25 g lactose over 3 hours. [ESNM-UEG Breath Test Guideline 2022]`,`**Common mistake**: starting a gluten-free diet before coeliac serology. [ACG Coeliac 2023]`,`**Common mistake**: advising lifelong strict low-FODMAP diet - phase 1 is only 2-6 weeks. [AGA CPU Diet in IBS 2022]`,`**Common mistake**: ordering IgG food panels, which measure exposure, not intolerance. [EAACI Food Allergy Guideline 2023]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Food allergy vs food intolerance vs coeliac disease vs NCGS`,columns:[`Feature`,`IgE food allergy`,`Lactose/FODMAP intolerance`,`Coeliac disease`,`NCGS`],rows:[[`Mechanism`,`IgE on mast cells`,`Enzymatic or fermentative, non-immune`,`Autoimmune, HLA-DQ2/DQ8`,`Unclear; gluten, ATIs, fructans`],[`Onset`,`Minutes to 2 hours`,`30 minutes to hours`,`Weeks to years`,`Hours to a day`],[`Dose`,`Trace amounts`,`Dose-dependent threshold`,`Small amounts damage mucosa`,`Variable`],[`Features`,`Urticaria, angioedema, wheeze, anaphylaxis`,`Bloating, flatus, loose stools`,`Diarrhoea, anaemia, short stature, osteoporosis`,`Bloating, fatigue, brain fog`],[`Test`,`Skin prick, specific IgE, oral challenge`,`Breath test, dietary trial`,`tTG-IgA plus biopsy`,`Exclusion, then blinded challenge`],[`Treatment`,`Strict avoidance, adrenaline plan`,`Reduce to threshold`,`Lifelong strict gluten-free diet`,`Gluten or fructan reduction`]]},{heading:`FODMAP groups with Indian examples`,columns:[`FODMAP group`,`High-FODMAP Indian foods`,`Low-FODMAP swaps`],rows:[[`Fructans (oligosaccharide)`,`Wheat atta, maida, onion, garlic, cashew`,`Rice, millets, oats in moderate amounts, spring onion green tops, asafoetida (hing, gluten-free)`],[`GOS (oligosaccharide)`,`Rajma, chana, chole, soya beans, cashew`,`Small portions of canned/rinsed chickpea, moong dal in limited amounts`],[`Lactose (disaccharide)`,`Milk, kheer, ice cream, soft cheese, khoa sweets`,`Curd, paneer, hard cheese, lactose-free milk, ghee`],[`Excess fructose (monosaccharide)`,`Mango, apple, pear, watermelon, honey`,`Papaya, orange, firm banana, guava in small amounts, pineapple`],[`Polyols`,`Mushroom, cauliflower, sugar-free sweets, chewing gum, stone fruits`,`Carrot, potato, tomato, cucumber, spinach, brinjal`]]},{heading:`Investigations for suspected carbohydrate intolerance`,columns:[`Test`,`How it is done`,`Positive result`,`Limitations`],rows:[[`Dietary exclusion and rechallenge`,`Lactose-free 2 weeks, then 250-500 mL milk`,`Symptom resolution then recurrence`,`Placebo effect; unblinded`],[`Lactose hydrogen breath test`,`25 g lactose; breath every 15-30 min for 3 hours`,`H2 rise 20 ppm or more; symptoms during test`,`Non-H2 producers, recent antibiotics`],[`Lactose tolerance test`,`50 g lactose; blood glucose at 0, 30, 60, 120 min`,`Glucose rise under 20 mg/dL`,`Invalid in diabetes, gastroparesis`],[`LCT genotyping`,`Blood or buccal swab`,`-13910 C/C = non-persistence`,`Misses secondary deficiency`],[`Stool pH and reducing substances`,`Fresh liquid stool (children)`,`pH under 5.5; reducing substances 0.5% or more`,`Needs fresh sample; not for adults`],[`tTG-IgA with total IgA`,`Serum on gluten diet`,`Above lab cut-off; biopsy to confirm`,`False negative on gluten-free diet or IgA deficiency`]]},{heading:`Drugs and supplements in food intolerance`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Lactase enzyme (tablets/drops)`,`One to two tablets (about 3000-9000 FCC units) with first bite of dairy`,`As needed with dairy-containing meals`,`Very safe; variable efficacy`],[`Calcium carbonate with vitamin D3`,`500 mg elemental calcium with 250-500 IU D3 orally once or twice daily`,`Long term if dairy restricted`,`Constipation, renal stones; separate from levothyroxine and iron`],[`Tinidazole (giardiasis)`,`2 g orally single dose (children 50 mg/kg)`,`Single dose`,`Metallic taste, disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol; avoid in first trimester`],[`Metronidazole (giardiasis)`,`400 mg orally three times daily`,`5-7 days`,`Metallic taste, nausea, neuropathy with long use; no alcohol`],[`Adrenaline (anaphylaxis to food)`,`0.5 mg IM (0.01 mg/kg children) anterolateral thigh`,`Repeat every 5 minutes if needed`,`Tachycardia, tremor; no absolute contraindication in anaphylaxis`]]}],redFlags:[`Urticaria, angioedema, wheeze, stridor or hypotension after food (anaphylaxis - give IM adrenaline)`,`Weight loss, iron-deficiency anaemia or growth faltering in a child`,`Blood in stools or nocturnal diarrhoea`,`Onset of new food 'intolerance' after age 50`,`Positive coeliac serology or family history of coeliac disease`,`Persistent diarrhoea despite lactose and FODMAP restriction`,`Restrictive eating with weight loss (ARFID or eating disorder)`],pearls:[`**Malabsorption is not intolerance** - only symptomatic malabsorbers are lactose intolerant.`,`**12 g of lactose (one cup of milk) with meals** is tolerated by most lactose malabsorbers.`,`**Curd is the Indian solution** - its bacterial lactase digests lactose.`,`**North Indians two-thirds, south Indians over 80%** lactase non-persistent.`,`**Lactose breath test**: 25 g, 3 hours, hydrogen rise 20 ppm.`,`Low-FODMAP is **Restrict, Reintroduce, Personalise** - never lifelong strict restriction.`,`**Serology before gluten-free** - always check tTG-IgA first.`,`**IgG food panels are useless** - they measure exposure, not disease.`,`**Sugar vs protein**: lactose intolerance vs cow's milk protein allergy.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-food-allergy-intolerance-q1`,stem:`A 26-year-old man from Chennai has bloating and loose stools within an hour of drinking two glasses of milk but tolerates curd rice daily. Which is the best initial approach?`,options:[`Lifelong complete exclusion of all dairy products`,`Serum IgG food antibody panel`,`Reduce milk to small amounts with meals, continue curd, and ensure calcium intake`,`Upper GI endoscopy with duodenal disaccharidase assay`],answer:2,explanation:`This is typical primary lactose intolerance, very common in south India; most patients tolerate up to 12 g lactose with meals and curd contains bacterial lactase, so lactose reduction with adequate calcium is correct. Complete dairy exclusion risks calcium deficiency, IgG food panels measure exposure and have no diagnostic value, and endoscopic disaccharidase assay is unnecessary in a young patient without alarm features.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-food-allergy-intolerance-q2`,stem:`In the lactose hydrogen breath test as standardised by the European 2022 guideline, which finding indicates lactose malabsorption?`,options:[`Rise in breath hydrogen of 20 ppm or more over baseline within 3 hours after 25 g lactose`,`Rise in blood glucose of more than 20 mg/dL after 50 g lactose`,`Breath methane below 3 ppm throughout the test`,`Early hydrogen peak within 15 minutes of 10 g lactulose`],answer:0,explanation:`The European guideline uses 25 g lactose with breath sampling for 3 hours; a hydrogen rise of 20 ppm or more over baseline indicates malabsorption, and symptoms during the test define intolerance. A blood glucose rise above 20 mg/dL is a normal lactose tolerance test (it argues against malabsorption), low methane is not a criterion, and a lactulose test is used for SIBO, not lactase deficiency.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-food-allergy-intolerance-q3`,stem:`A 32-year-old woman reports bloating, fatigue and 'brain fog' after eating chapatis and wants to start a gluten-free diet. She has not been tested. What should be done first?`,options:[`Start a strict gluten-free diet for 6 months and review`,`Order tTG-IgA with total IgA while she continues eating gluten`,`Order a serum IgG antibody panel to wheat`,`Advise a low-FODMAP diet permanently`],answer:1,explanation:`Coeliac disease must be excluded with tTG-IgA plus total IgA on a gluten-containing diet before any gluten-free trial, because gluten withdrawal makes serology and biopsy falsely negative. Only after coeliac disease and wheat allergy are excluded can non-coeliac gluten sensitivity or fructan intolerance be considered. IgG panels have no diagnostic value and a low-FODMAP diet should never be permanent.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-food-allergy-intolerance-q4`,stem:`Regarding the low-FODMAP diet in irritable bowel syndrome, which statement is correct as per the AGA 2022 Clinical Practice Update?`,options:[`Restriction of all FODMAPs should continue lifelong once symptoms improve`,`It should be started without dietitian support to improve adherence`,`It is the first choice in patients with a history of eating disorders`,`It is delivered in three phases: restriction for 2-6 weeks, structured reintroduction, then personalisation`],answer:3,explanation:`The AGA 2022 update describes three phases - short restriction for 2-6 weeks, reintroduction of each FODMAP group to identify triggers, and a personalised long-term diet - ideally with a GI dietitian. Lifelong strict restriction harms the microbiome and nutrition, and restrictive diets are avoided or modified in patients with eating disorders or ARFID risk.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-food-allergy-intolerance-q5`,stem:`A 3-year-old child develops watery, frothy stools with perianal excoriation that persist for 3 weeks after an episode of rotavirus gastroenteritis. Stool pH is 5.0 with reducing substances. Growth is normal. The most likely diagnosis is:`,options:[`Secondary lactase deficiency`,`Congenital lactase deficiency`,`Cow's milk protein allergy`,`Coeliac disease`],answer:0,explanation:`Acidic stools with reducing substances after viral gastroenteritis indicate carbohydrate malabsorption from brush-border injury - secondary lactase deficiency, which resolves as the mucosa heals. Congenital lactase deficiency presents from the first feeds in infancy, cow's milk protein allergy is immune and usually presents with blood in stools, eczema or vomiting, and coeliac disease causes growth faltering rather than a post-infective course.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-food-allergy-intolerance-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Classify adverse reactions to food. Discuss the pathophysiology, diagnosis and management of lactose intolerance in Indian adults, and outline the low-FODMAP diet.`,openingLines:[`Adverse food reactions are immune-mediated (IgE, non-IgE and coeliac disease) or non-immune food intolerances (enzymatic, pharmacological, fermentative and toxic).`,`Lactose intolerance - symptoms from lactose malabsorption due to lactase non-persistence - affects two-thirds of north and over four-fifths of south Indians and is managed by reduction, not exclusion.`],answer:[{heading:`Classification`,points:[`Immune: IgE-mediated allergy, non-IgE (FPIES, proctocolitis, EoE), coeliac disease.`,`Non-immune: enzymatic (lactase, sucrase-isomaltase), pharmacological (caffeine, histamine), fermentative (FODMAP), toxic.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Lactase falls after weaning; LCT -13910 C/C genotype; north-south gradient in India.`,`Unabsorbed lactose is osmotic and fermented to SCFA, H2, CO2, methane.`,`Secondary deficiency: gastroenteritis, giardiasis, coeliac, sprue, Crohn.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Bloating, borborygmi, cramps, flatus, loose stools 30 minutes to 2 hours after milk, dose-dependent.`,`Red flags: weight loss, anaemia, blood, nocturnal symptoms, onset over 50.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Exclusion and rechallenge trial.`,`Lactose hydrogen breath test: 25 g, 3 hours, rise 20 ppm.`,`Lactose tolerance test: glucose rise under 20 mg/dL; genotyping; stool pH in children.`,`Exclude coeliac disease and giardiasis.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Reduce to threshold (12 g with meals); curd, paneer, lactose-free milk; lactase enzyme.`,`Calcium 1000 mg/day and vitamin D; treat secondary cause.`]},{heading:`Low-FODMAP diet`,points:[`FODMAP expansion with Indian examples.`,`Restrict 2-6 weeks; reintroduce over 6-8 weeks; personalise.`,`Dietitian-led; screen for eating disorders; never lifelong strict.`]}],mustDraw:[`Classification tree of adverse food reactions`,`Flow chart of the three phases of the low-FODMAP diet`],markSplit:[{part:`Classification`,marks:2},{part:`Pathophysiology and Indian epidemiology`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Management of lactose intolerance`,marks:2},{part:`Low-FODMAP diet`,marks:2}],keywords:[`lactase non-persistence`,`hydrogen breath test`,`FODMAP`,`curd`,`calcium`,`coeliac exclusion`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-food-allergy-intolerance-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Differentiate coeliac disease, wheat allergy and non-coeliac gluten sensitivity.`,openingLines:[`Wheat can harm by three routes: autoimmune (coeliac disease), allergic (IgE-mediated wheat allergy) and neither (non-coeliac gluten or wheat sensitivity).`,`Coeliac disease and wheat allergy must be excluded before NCGS is diagnosed by the Salerno criteria.`],answer:[{heading:`Mechanism and onset`,points:[`Coeliac: HLA-DQ2/DQ8, tTG autoantibodies, villous atrophy; weeks to years.`,`Wheat allergy: IgE, minutes to 2 hours; includes exercise-induced anaphylaxis.`,`NCGS: innate response to gluten, ATIs or fructans; hours to a day.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Coeliac: tTG-IgA with total IgA on gluten, duodenal biopsy (Marsh).`,`Wheat allergy: skin prick, specific IgE (omega-5 gliadin), oral challenge.`,`NCGS: negative both, response to withdrawal, blinded placebo-controlled challenge.`]},{heading:`Treatment and prognosis`,points:[`Coeliac: lifelong strict gluten-free diet; risk of lymphoma and osteoporosis.`,`Wheat allergy: avoidance, adrenaline action plan.`,`NCGS: gluten or fructan reduction to tolerance; benign.`]}],mustDraw:[`Three-column table: mechanism, onset, test, treatment`],markSplit:[{part:`Mechanism`,marks:1.5},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`coeliac`,`wheat allergy`,`NCGS`,`Salerno criteria`,`tTG-IgA`,`omega-5 gliadin`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`Chey WD, Hashash JG, Manning L, Chang L. AGA Clinical Practice Update on the Role of Diet in Irritable Bowel Syndrome: Expert Review. Gastroenterology, 2022`,`Hammer HF, et al. European guideline on indications, performance and clinical impact of hydrogen and methane breath tests. United European Gastroenterology Journal, 2022`,`Rubio-Tapia A, et al. ACG Clinical Guideline: Diagnosis and Management of Celiac Disease. American Journal of Gastroenterology, 2023`,`Santos AF, et al. EAACI guidelines on the diagnosis of IgE-mediated food allergy. Allergy, 2023`,`Catassi C, et al. Diagnosis of Non-Celiac Gluten Sensitivity: The Salerno Experts' Criteria. Nutrients, 2015`,`Babu J, et al. Frequency of lactose malabsorption among healthy southern and northern Indian populations. American Journal of Clinical Nutrition, 2010`,`ICMR-National Institute of Nutrition. Dietary Guidelines for Indians, 2024`]}),u.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-in-systemic-disease`,title:`GI and liver manifestations of systemic disease`,oneLiner:`Many systemic diseases present first to the gut or liver: **diabetes (gastroparesis, diarrhoea, MASLD), thyroid disease (motility, LFT changes, antithyroid drug hepatotoxicity), systemic sclerosis (oesophageal aperistalsis, SIBO, GAVE), amyloidosis (hepatomegaly, pseudo-obstruction), HIV (opportunistic oesophagitis, diarrhoea, cholangiopathy), heart failure (congestive hepatopathy, ischaemic hepatitis), sickle cell disease (pigment stones, hepatic crises) and parenteral nutrition (IFALD)** - recognising the pattern avoids needless GI work-up and directs treatment to the underlying disease.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`diabetic gastroparesis`,`diabetic diarrhoea`,`thyrotoxicosis LFT`,`myxoedema ascites`,`propylthiouracil hepatotoxicity`,`systemic sclerosis oesophagus`,`GAVE`,`watermelon stomach`,`amyloidosis liver`,`AIDS cholangiopathy`,`CMV colitis`,`congestive hepatopathy`,`nutmeg liver`,`ischaemic hepatitis`,`shock liver`,`Heyde syndrome`,`sickle cell intrahepatic cholestasis`,`IFALD`,`PNALD`,`Fontan-associated liver disease`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**GI and liver manifestations of systemic disease** are gut, liver, biliary or pancreatic abnormalities caused by a primary disease of another system, its complications or its treatment. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diabetes**: India has about **101 million adults with diabetes (11.4%)**; GI symptoms (constipation, bloating, diarrhoea, early satiety) are reported in up to **75%** of diabetics attending clinics. [ICMR-INDIAB 2023]`,`**MASLD** affects about **65-70% of people with type 2 diabetes**, and diabetes doubles the risk of cirrhosis and HCC in MASLD. [AASLD MASLD Guidance 2023]`,`**Systemic sclerosis**: GI tract involved in up to **90%**, oesophagus most often; GI disease causes about 10% of scleroderma deaths. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Heart failure**: abnormal LFTs in up to **one-third to two-thirds** of acute decompensated HF; ischaemic hepatitis occurs in about **2.5% of ICU admissions**, mostly cardiogenic. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**HIV**: India has about **2.5 million people living with HIV**; GI symptoms affect most patients at some stage, now more often drug-related and from HBV/HCV coinfection than opportunistic infection. [NACO Sankalak 2023]`,`**Sickle cell disease** is concentrated in tribal belts of **central and western India (Chhattisgarh, Madhya Pradesh, Maharashtra, Odisha, Gujarat)**; gallstones develop in up to **50-70% by adulthood**. [National Sickle Cell Anaemia Elimination Mission 2023]`,`**Intestinal failure-associated liver disease (IFALD)** develops in **40-60% of children** and a smaller proportion of adults on long-term parenteral nutrition. [ESPEN Chronic Intestinal Failure 2023]`]},{heading:`Aetiology - which disease, which organ`,points:[`**Diabetes**: autonomic neuropathy (oesophagus, stomach, small bowel, colon, anorectum), hyperglycaemia slowing emptying, drugs (**metformin** diarrhoea, **GLP-1 RA** nausea and delayed emptying, **SGLT2i** euglycaemic DKA pain), MASLD, pancreatic exocrine insufficiency and **glycogenic hepatopathy**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Thyroid**: thyrotoxicosis accelerates transit and raises ALP and transaminases; hypothyroidism slows transit (constipation, ileus, pseudo-obstruction) and can cause **myxoedema ascites**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Antithyroid drugs**: **propylthiouracil causes hepatocellular injury and acute liver failure** (boxed warning); **carbimazole/methimazole causes cholestatic injury**; both cause agranulocytosis. [ATA Hyperthyroidism 2016]`,`**Systemic sclerosis**: smooth muscle atrophy and fibrosis, preceded by **neural dysfunction from vasculopathy** - affecting oesophagus, stomach, small bowel, colon and anal sphincter. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Amyloidosis**: **AL** (plasma cell dyscrasia) and **AA** (chronic inflammation - in India TB, bronchiectasis, rheumatoid arthritis, leprosy) deposit in vessel walls, muscularis and nerves. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HIV**: opportunistic infection (Candida, CMV, HSV, Cryptosporidium, Cystoisospora, Microsporidia, MAC, TB), neoplasm (Kaposi sarcoma, lymphoma), drug toxicity and viral hepatitis coinfection. [NACO HIV Care Guidelines 2021]`,`**Heart failure and shock**: venous congestion (right HF, tricuspid regurgitation, constrictive pericarditis, Fontan) and low flow (cardiogenic, septic shock) injure the liver and gut. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Sickle cell disease**: chronic haemolysis (pigment stones), vaso-occlusion in hepatic sinusoids and mesentery, transfusion (iron overload, viral hepatitis) and functional asplenia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Parenteral nutrition**: lack of enteral stimulation, **soybean lipid phytosterols and omega-6 load**, overfeeding with dextrose, sepsis, short bowel and bacterial overgrowth. [ESPEN Chronic Intestinal Failure 2023]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - the why behind the patterns`,points:[`**Diabetic diarrhoea**: loss of **alpha-2 adrenergic enterocyte tone** (reduced fluid absorption), rapid or disordered transit, SIBO, bile acid malabsorption and anal sphincter neuropathy - typically **nocturnal, painless and alternating with constipation**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute hyperglycaemia above about 250 mg/dL slows gastric emptying** and blunts prokinetic action - so glycaemic control is part of gastroparesis treatment; see the Nausea, vomiting and gastroparesis topic. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**Glycogenic hepatopathy**: excess glucose with insulin drives hepatic glycogen storage in poorly controlled type 1 diabetes - **tender hepatomegaly with very high transaminases, reversible with glycaemic control** (Mauriac syndrome when with growth failure). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Scleroderma oesophagus**: the smooth-muscle **distal two-thirds becomes aperistaltic and the LES hypotensive**, so reflux is severe and cleared poorly - causing **erosive oesophagitis, stricture and Barrett oesophagus**; the striated upper third is spared. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Congestive hepatopathy**: raised right atrial pressure is transmitted to hepatic veins, causing **centrilobular (zone 3) sinusoidal congestion, haemorrhage and atrophy** - the **'nutmeg liver'** - and later bridging fibrosis (cardiac cirrhosis). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Ischaemic hepatitis**: zone 3 hepatocytes have the lowest oxygen tension, so low flow plus venous congestion causes **centrilobular necrosis** - transaminases rise within 24-48 hours to **often over 1000-5000 IU/L** and fall by half within 72 hours once perfusion returns. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Cardiac ascites is high-protein**: sinusoids leak protein-rich lymph because sinusoidal walls are still normal - so **SAAG 1.1 g/dL or more with ascitic protein 2.5 g/dL or more**, unlike cirrhosis (protein usually under 2.5). [AASLD Ascites Guidance 2021]`,`**Heyde syndrome**: severe aortic stenosis shears **high-molecular-weight von Willebrand multimers (acquired vWD type 2A)**, so GI angiodysplasias bleed; valve replacement often stops the bleeding. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Sickle intrahepatic cholestasis**: widespread sinusoidal sickling causes ischaemia and **massive conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia (often over 13 mg/dL), coagulopathy and renal failure** - a life-threatening crisis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**IFALD**: phytosterols in soybean lipids antagonise the **farnesoid X receptor (FXR)**, reducing bile acid export, while absent enteral feeding stops cholecystokinin-driven gallbladder emptying - causing **cholestasis, steatosis and sludge**. [ESPEN Chronic Intestinal Failure 2023]`]},{heading:`Classification - organ patterns that examiners ask`,points:[`**Hepatocellular pattern (ALT over 1000)**: ischaemic hepatitis, glycogenic hepatopathy, PTU hepatotoxicity, acute sickle hepatic crisis (modest), drug-induced liver injury. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cholestatic pattern (ALP/GGT dominant)**: congestive hepatopathy, amyloid infiltration, granulomatous disease (TB, sarcoid), AIDS cholangiopathy, carbimazole injury, IFALD, sickle intrahepatic cholestasis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hepatomegaly with near-normal LFTs**: amyloidosis (firm, ALP may be high), congestion, glycogen storage, lymphoma infiltration, extramedullary haematopoiesis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Motility pattern**: diabetes, scleroderma, amyloidosis, hypothyroidism, Parkinson disease, paraneoplastic syndromes - causing gastroparesis, SIBO and pseudo-obstruction. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bleeding pattern**: GAVE (scleroderma, cirrhosis, CKD), angiodysplasia (Heyde, CKD, LVAD), amyloid vascular fragility, Kaposi sarcoma, CMV ulcers. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ascites pattern by SAAG and protein**: high SAAG + high protein = **cardiac, Budd-Chiari, early myxoedema**; high SAAG + low protein = cirrhosis; low SAAG = TB, malignancy, nephrotic syndrome, pancreatic. [AASLD Ascites Guidance 2021]`,`**HIV GI disease by CD4 count**: any CD4 - bacterial enteritis, TB, HBV/HCV; **under 200 - candidiasis, Cryptosporidium, Cystoisospora, Microsporidia**; **under 50 - CMV, MAC**. [NACO HIV Care Guidelines 2021]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Diabetic**: early satiety, vomiting of old food, **nocturnal painless diarrhoea**, faecal incontinence, erratic glucose and hypoglycaemia after insulin (food delivered late); review metformin and GLP-1 RA timing. [ADA Standards of Care 2026]`,`**Thyroid**: weight loss with hyperdefecation, heat intolerance and tremor; or constipation, cold intolerance, weight gain, and new ascites in hypothyroidism. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Scleroderma**: **heartburn and dysphagia to solids and liquids** in a patient with Raynaud phenomenon and skin thickening; bloating, diarrhoea and weight loss from SIBO; faecal incontinence. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Amyloidosis**: weight loss, **macroglossia** (AL), easy bruising (periorbital purpura), diarrhoea or constipation, pseudo-obstruction, nephrotic syndrome, cardiomyopathy and neuropathy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HIV**: **odynophagia** (Candida, CMV, HSV, aphthous ulcers), chronic watery diarrhoea and wasting, bloody diarrhoea (CMV colitis), RUQ pain with cholestasis (cholangiopathy). [NACO HIV Care Guidelines 2021]`,`**Heart failure**: RUQ heaviness on exertion, anorexia, early satiety, bloating, ascites; jaundice in severe right HF or after a hypotensive episode. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Sickle cell**: RUQ pain with fever and jaundice (crisis vs cholecystitis vs cholangitis), recurrent biliary colic, abdominal crises, priapism and leg ulcers as clues to disease severity. [Harrison 22e]`,`**On parenteral nutrition**: rising bilirubin, RUQ discomfort, catheter sepsis episodes, and duration and composition of lipid. [ESPEN Chronic Intestinal Failure 2023]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Raised JVP with hepatojugular reflux and pulsatile liver** (tricuspid regurgitation) distinguish congestive hepatopathy from cirrhosis; **Kussmaul sign and pericardial knock** suggest constrictive pericarditis. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Absence of stigmata of chronic liver disease** with ascites and tender firm hepatomegaly points to a cardiac or Budd-Chiari cause. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Sclerodactyly, digital pits, telangiectasia, calcinosis and Raynaud** (CREST) with reflux point to scleroderma; anti-centromere CREST also associates with **PBC**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Macroglossia, periorbital purpura ('raccoon eyes'), shoulder-pad sign and firm hepatomegaly** suggest AL amyloidosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Oral thrush, oral hairy leukoplakia, Kaposi sarcoma lesions, generalised lymphadenopathy and wasting** suggest advanced HIV. [NACO HIV Care Guidelines 2021]`,`**Goitre, tremor, lid lag, or delayed relaxation of ankle jerks and non-pitting oedema** indicate the thyroid cause. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Postural hypotension without heart-rate rise, absent sweating and peripheral neuropathy** indicate diabetic autonomic neuropathy as the cause of gut dysmotility. [ADA Standards of Care 2026]`,`**Pallor, icterus, frontal bossing, splenomegaly in children (absent in adults from autosplenectomy)** and leg ulcers in sickle cell disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ejection systolic murmur of aortic stenosis** in an elderly patient with recurrent occult GI bleeding suggests Heyde syndrome. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Congestive hepatopathy vs cirrhosis**: raised JVP, high-protein ascites (2.5 g/dL or more), BNP over 364 pg/mL in ascitic fluid or serum and echo findings favour cardiac ascites. [AASLD Ascites Guidance 2021]`,`**Ischaemic hepatitis vs viral or drug hepatitis**: ischaemic has a hypotensive trigger, **early very high LDH (ALT/LDH ratio under 1.5)**, rising creatinine and a rapid fall of transaminases within days. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Sickle hepatic crisis vs acute cholecystitis vs choledocholithiasis vs viral hepatitis**: ultrasound for gallbladder wall and CBD, viral markers, and the trend of ALT and bilirubin. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Thyrotoxic LFT rise vs antithyroid drug injury vs coexistent autoimmune hepatitis**: check LFT before starting antithyroid drugs - baseline abnormality is common in thyrotoxicosis itself. [ATA Hyperthyroidism 2016]`,`**Scleroderma oesophagus vs achalasia**: both aperistaltic, but scleroderma has **low LES pressure and severe reflux**, achalasia has **high LES pressure with failed relaxation**; see the Achalasia topic. [Chicago Classification v4.0 2021]`,`**HIV diarrhoea vs ART side effect**: protease inhibitors (lopinavir/ritonavir) and some NRTIs cause diarrhoea; dolutegravir is better tolerated. [NACO HIV Care Guidelines 2021]`,`**IFALD vs sepsis cholestasis vs drug injury vs biliary obstruction**: ultrasound for biliary dilatation and sludge, blood cultures for catheter infection. [ESPEN Chronic Intestinal Failure 2023]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**LFT pattern first**: hepatocellular vs cholestatic vs isolated bilirubin, plus **INR, albumin, LDH and creatinine**; trend over 48-72 hours separates ischaemic from viral hepatitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Heart failure**: **NT-proBNP, echocardiography** (RV function, TR, pericardium), Doppler of hepatic veins; ascitic fluid SAAG and total protein. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Diabetes**: HbA1c, **gastric emptying scintigraphy (4-hour solid meal, retention over 10% at 4 hours)** after excluding obstruction by endoscopy; glucose breath test for SIBO; faecal elastase for pancreatic insufficiency. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**MASLD in diabetes**: **FIB-4** in every adult with type 2 diabetes; if 1.3 or more, do **transient elastography (LSM 8 kPa or more)**. [ADA Standards of Care 2026]`,`**Thyroid**: TSH and free T4 in unexplained diarrhoea, constipation, LFT rise or ascites; LFT and CBC before antithyroid drugs. [ATA Hyperthyroidism 2016]`,`**Scleroderma**: ANA, **anti-Scl-70 (diffuse), anti-centromere (limited), anti-RNA polymerase III**; high-resolution oesophageal manometry; endoscopy for oesophagitis, Barrett and GAVE. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Amyloidosis**: **abdominal fat pad aspirate** (sensitivity about 70-80% in AL) or **rectal or GI biopsy** stained with **Congo red - apple-green birefringence under polarised light**; serum free light chains and immunofixation for AL. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Avoid percutaneous liver biopsy in amyloidosis** when possible - risk of haemorrhage; if needed, use transjugular route. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HIV**: CD4 count, viral load, HBsAg, anti-HCV; **stool modified acid-fast stain** (Cryptosporidium, Cystoisospora, Cyclospora); colonoscopy with biopsy for CMV (owl's eye inclusions); MRCP or ERCP for cholangiopathy. [NACO HIV Care Guidelines 2021]`,`**Sickle cell**: Hb, reticulocytes, bilirubin fractions, LDH, INR; **ultrasound for stones and CBD**; serum ferritin and **MRI T2* liver iron** in multiply transfused patients. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Parenteral nutrition**: LFT every 1-2 weeks initially then monthly; **conjugated bilirubin over 2 mg/dL** defines PN-associated cholestasis in children; ultrasound for sludge; triglycerides and phytosterol load. [ESPEN Chronic Intestinal Failure 2023]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Diabetic gastroparesis**: small low-fat low-fibre meals, glucose control, stop GLP-1 RA if implicated, **metoclopramide 5-10 mg three times daily before meals for up to 12 weeks** (tardive dyskinesia), or **domperidone 10 mg three times daily** (QT; ECG). [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**Diabetic diarrhoea**: exclude metformin effect (switch to extended release), SIBO, coeliac and bile acid diarrhoea; **loperamide 2-4 mg up to 16 mg/day**; **clonidine 0.1 mg twice daily** restores alpha-2 tone; **rifaximin** if SIBO. [Harrison 22e]`,`**MASLD with diabetes**: weight loss of **7-10%**, and prefer **GLP-1 RA (semaglutide) or pioglitazone** for glucose control; **resmetirom** and **semaglutide** have regulatory approval for MASH with F2-F3 fibrosis; see the MASLD topic. [AASLD MASLD Guidance 2023]`,`**Scleroderma reflux**: **high-dose PPI (pantoprazole 40 mg twice daily)** long term, head-end elevation, small meals; **prucalopride 2 mg** or **domperidone** for dysmotility; avoid fundoplication (worsens dysphagia). [EULAR SSc 2023]`,`**Scleroderma SIBO**: **rotating antibiotics** (rifaximin, amoxicillin-clavulanate, metronidazole, doxycycline) for 7-14 days each month as needed; **octreotide 50 mcg subcutaneously at night** for pseudo-obstruction and SIBO. [EULAR SSc 2023]`,`**Thyrotoxicosis**: LFT changes settle with euthyroidism; prefer **carbimazole** except in **first trimester and thyroid storm (PTU)**; stop drug and check LFT if jaundice, dark urine or pruritus. [ATA Hyperthyroidism 2016]`,`**HIV**: start **ART (TLD - tenofovir + lamivudine + dolutegravir)** early - it treats Cryptosporidium and Microsporidia by immune reconstitution and covers HBV; **cotrimoxazole prophylaxis** prevents Cystoisospora. [NACO HIV Care Guidelines 2021]`,`**HIV oesophageal candidiasis**: **fluconazole 200-400 mg/day for 14-21 days**; CMV: **valganciclovir 900 mg twice daily or IV ganciclovir 5 mg/kg twice daily for 3-6 weeks**; HSV: aciclovir. [NACO HIV Care Guidelines 2021]`,`**Congestive hepatopathy**: treat the heart - **decongestion with loop diuretics** and guideline-directed HF therapy; LFTs improve as right atrial pressure falls; avoid hepatotoxic and sedative drugs. [ESC Heart Failure 2023 Focused Update]`,`**Sickle cell gallstones**: **elective laparoscopic cholecystectomy for symptomatic stones**, with **preoperative transfusion to Hb about 10 g/dL**; **hydroxyurea 15-35 mg/kg/day** reduces crises. [National Sickle Cell Anaemia Elimination Mission 2023]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Ischaemic hepatitis**: restore cardiac output and perfusion (inotropes, treat arrhythmia or sepsis); no liver-specific therapy; avoid hepatotoxins; monitor INR, glucose and creatinine - mortality reflects the underlying shock. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Acute sickle hepatic crisis**: IV fluids, analgesia (opioids acceptable), oxygen, antibiotics if febrile; **sickle intrahepatic cholestasis needs urgent exchange transfusion** and correction of coagulopathy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hepatic sequestration** (rapid liver enlargement with falling Hb and rising reticulocytes): cautious simple transfusion - beware **hyperviscosity when sequestered cells are released**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PTU-induced acute liver failure**: stop drug, ICU care, **NAC IV**, early liver transplant referral; control thyrotoxicosis with beta-blockers, steroids, iodine and plasmapheresis. [ATA Hyperthyroidism 2016]`,`**Scleroderma pseudo-obstruction**: bowel rest, NG decompression, correct electrolytes, **octreotide**, antibiotics for SIBO; avoid surgery where possible; long-term **home parenteral nutrition** for intestinal failure. [EULAR SSc 2023]`,`**IFALD**: **reduce soybean lipid to 1 g/kg/day or less**, switch to **mixed lipid (SMOFlipid) or fish-oil emulsion**, **cycle PN over 12-16 hours**, avoid overfeeding (dextrose under about 5 mg/kg/min), start any enteral feeding, treat catheter sepsis, consider ursodeoxycholic acid. [ESPEN Chronic Intestinal Failure 2023]`,`**Diabetic gastroparesis crisis**: IV fluids, insulin infusion, IV antiemetics (metoclopramide 10 mg IV, ondansetron), correct potassium; **erythromycin 3 mg/kg IV** for short-term use; NJ feeding if malnourished. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**GLP-1 RA and procedures**: follow the **2024 multisociety guidance** - assess GI symptoms on the day; for high-risk patients consider a **24-hour clear-liquid diet** or holding the drug, rather than routine withholding for 1 week. [ASA-AGA Multisociety Guidance 2024]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: hyperthyroidism in the first trimester uses **PTU**, then switch to carbimazole; sickle cell crises and cholestasis worsen in pregnancy; see the Liver disease in pregnancy topic. [ATA Thyroid in Pregnancy 2017]`,`**Children with sickle cell**: splenic sequestration (falling Hb, enlarging spleen) is a killer under 5 years; teach parents to palpate the spleen; **penicillin prophylaxis** and vaccination. [National Sickle Cell Anaemia Elimination Mission 2023]`,`**Elderly**: occult GI bleeding with aortic stenosis (Heyde), amyloidosis (AL and wild-type ATTR) and drug-induced dysmotility are under-recognised. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**CKD**: uraemic gastritis, angiodysplasia bleeding, constipation from binders, and AA amyloid; avoid metoclopramide accumulation (halve dose). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fontan circulation**: **Fontan-associated liver disease (FALD)** - fibrosis in nearly all after 10 years, risk of **HCC**; surveillance with ultrasound and AFP every 6-12 months. [AHA FALD Statement 2019]`,`**Diabetes and coeliac disease**: screen children with type 1 diabetes for coeliac disease at diagnosis and periodically (tTG-IgA). [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: LFT pattern, ascitic SAAG and protein, ECG and bedside echo, CD4 and stool modified AFB answer most questions without advanced imaging. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Cardiac cirrhosis** develops after years of uncontrolled right HF or constriction; **MELD-XI** (bilirubin and creatinine, excludes INR) predicts outcome in anticoagulated HF patients. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Ischaemic hepatitis** in-hospital mortality is about **50%**, driven by the cause of shock rather than the liver itself. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Scleroderma**: aspiration pneumonia, Barrett oesophagus, strictures, malnutrition and intestinal failure; GAVE causes iron-deficiency anaemia (treated with argon plasma coagulation). [EULAR SSc 2023]`,`**AL amyloidosis with liver involvement** and raised bilirubin has a poor prognosis; spontaneous splenic and hepatic rupture can occur. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HIV**: AIDS cholangiopathy (papillary stenosis responds to ERCP sphincterotomy), IRIS flares of TB or MAC after starting ART, HBV flares if tenofovir is stopped. [NACO HIV Care Guidelines 2021]`,`**Sickle cell**: transfusional iron overload leads to cirrhosis; viral hepatitis from transfusions; intrahepatic cholestasis carries high mortality without exchange. [Harrison 22e]`,`**IFALD in children** can progress to biliary cirrhosis and need **intestine-liver transplantation**; fish-oil lipid has markedly reduced this. [ESPEN Chronic Intestinal Failure 2023]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer** unexplained jaundice, transaminases over 10 times ULN, INR rise, ascites with diagnostic doubt, GI bleeding and suspected amyloidosis or pseudo-obstruction. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diabetes**: annual **FIB-4** and LFT, enquiry about GI autonomic symptoms, foot and neuropathy screening under NP-NCD. [ADA Standards of Care 2026]`,`**Antithyroid drugs**: baseline LFT and CBC; educate the patient to report sore throat, fever, jaundice or dark urine immediately. [ATA Hyperthyroidism 2016]`,`**Scleroderma**: endoscopy for Barrett surveillance where present, annual Hb and ferritin (GAVE), weight and nutritional screening (MUST). [EULAR SSc 2023]`,`**HIV**: LFT at baseline, 1 month and 3-6 monthly on ART; HBsAg-positive patients must never have tenofovir stopped without an alternative. [NACO HIV Care Guidelines 2021]`,`**Sickle cell**: annual ultrasound for gallstones in symptomatic patients, ferritin every 3-6 months if transfused, hydroxyurea monitoring with CBC. [National Sickle Cell Anaemia Elimination Mission 2023]`,`**Home PN**: LFT monthly, ultrasound and liver elastography periodically; refer to an intestinal rehabilitation unit for rising bilirubin. [ESPEN Chronic Intestinal Failure 2023]`]},{heading:`Prevention and national programme links`,points:[`**NP-NCD** population screening of adults over 30 for diabetes and hypertension at **Ayushman Arogya Mandirs** - the entry point to detect MASLD and diabetic GI disease. [NP-NCD 2023]`,`**National Sickle Cell Anaemia Elimination Mission (launched July 2023)**: screening of **7 crore people aged 0-40 years** in tribal areas, genetic counselling cards, hydroxyurea and elimination by **2047**. [National Sickle Cell Anaemia Elimination Mission 2023]`,`**NACO / National AIDS and STD Control Programme Phase V**: test and treat with TLD, cotrimoxazole prophylaxis, and HBV vaccination of HIV-negative contacts. [NACO NACP-V 2021]`,`**Hepatitis B vaccination** for sickle cell patients and anyone needing repeated transfusions, HIV patients and haemodialysis patients. [MoHFW NVHCP 2023]`,`**Rational antithyroid prescribing**: avoid PTU except where specifically indicated; counsel on agranulocytosis and hepatotoxicity. [ATA Hyperthyroidism 2016]`,`**Heart failure care**: guideline-directed therapy and diuretic optimisation prevent congestive and ischaemic liver injury. [ESC Heart Failure 2023 Focused Update]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**AASLD MASLD Practice Guidance (2023) and EASL-EASD-EASO MASLD CPG (2024)**: new MASLD nomenclature, FIB-4 then elastography in all with type 2 diabetes; **resmetirom (FDA 2024)** and **semaglutide (FDA 2025)** for MASH F2-F3. [EASL-EASD-EASO MASLD 2024]`,`**ADA Standards of Care 2026**: screen adults with type 2 diabetes or prediabetes with cardiometabolic risk for MASLD fibrosis using FIB-4. [ADA Standards of Care 2026]`,`**ACG Gastroparesis guideline (2022)**: 4-hour solid scintigraphy, metoclopramide first-line, domperidone via restricted access, G-POEM in selected refractory cases. [ACG Gastroparesis 2022]`,`**Multisociety guidance on GLP-1 RA before procedures (2024)**: individualised risk assessment and liquid diet for 24 hours instead of routine 1-week hold. [ASA-AGA Multisociety Guidance 2024]`,`**EULAR recommendations for systemic sclerosis (2023 update)**: PPI for reflux, prokinetics for dysmotility, rotating antibiotics for SIBO. [EULAR SSc 2023]`,`**ESPEN guideline on chronic intestinal failure in adults (2023 update)**: lipid limits, mixed or fish-oil emulsions, cycling and IFALD monitoring. [ESPEN Chronic Intestinal Failure 2023]`,`**National Sickle Cell Anaemia Elimination Mission (2023)** launched in India with universal screening in tribal districts. [National Sickle Cell Anaemia Elimination Mission 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Viva: **'How do you tell cardiac ascites from cirrhotic ascites?'** - both SAAG 1.1 or more; cardiac has **protein 2.5 g/dL or more, raised JVP and high BNP**. [AASLD Ascites Guidance 2021]`,`Viva: **'Transaminases 4000 IU/L in a patient after cardiac arrest'** - ischaemic hepatitis, very high LDH, falls by half in 72 hours. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Viva: **'Which antithyroid drug is hepatotoxic?'** - PTU hepatocellular (ALF); carbimazole cholestatic. [ATA Hyperthyroidism 2016]`,`Viva: **'Oesophagus in scleroderma'** - aperistalsis of distal two-thirds, hypotensive LES, severe reflux - contrast with achalasia. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: **'Heyde syndrome'** - aortic stenosis, angiodysplasia bleeding, acquired vWD type 2A. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Common mistake**: diagnosing cirrhosis in a patient with ascites and hepatomegaly without looking at the JVP. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Common mistake**: stopping tenofovir in an HIV-HBV coinfected patient - causes a hepatitis flare. [NACO HIV Care Guidelines 2021]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Systemic disease and its GI/liver manifestations`,columns:[`Systemic disease`,`Gut manifestations`,`Liver/biliary manifestations`,`Key test`],rows:[[`Diabetes mellitus`,`Gastroparesis, nocturnal diarrhoea, SIBO, incontinence, candidiasis`,`MASLD, glycogenic hepatopathy, emphysematous cholecystitis`,`Gastric emptying scan, FIB-4, elastography`],[`Hyperthyroidism`,`Hyperdefecation, weight loss`,`Raised ALP/ALT; PTU hepatocellular injury`,`TSH, LFT before antithyroid drugs`],[`Hypothyroidism`,`Constipation, ileus, pseudo-obstruction`,`Myxoedema ascites (high protein), raised CK, dyslipidaemia`,`TSH, ascitic protein`],[`Systemic sclerosis`,`Aperistaltic oesophagus, severe GERD, SIBO, GAVE, wide-mouth diverticula, incontinence`,`PBC overlap (limited cutaneous)`,`Manometry, endoscopy, breath test, AMA`],[`Amyloidosis`,`Macroglossia, pseudo-obstruction, bleeding, malabsorption`,`Firm hepatomegaly, high ALP, splenic rupture`,`Fat pad or rectal biopsy, Congo red`],[`HIV`,`Candida/CMV/HSV oesophagitis, Cryptosporidium, Cystoisospora, MAC, CMV colitis, Kaposi`,`HBV/HCV coinfection, ART hepatotoxicity, AIDS cholangiopathy`,`CD4, stool modified AFB, biopsy, MRCP`],[`Heart failure / shock`,`Bowel oedema, non-occlusive mesenteric ischaemia, Heyde bleeding`,`Congestive hepatopathy, ischaemic hepatitis, cardiac cirrhosis`,`Echo, NT-proBNP, ascitic protein, LDH`],[`Sickle cell disease`,`Abdominal vaso-occlusive crisis, mesenteric sickling`,`Pigment stones, hepatic crisis, intrahepatic cholestasis, sequestration, iron overload`,`USG, bilirubin fractions, ferritin, MRI T2*`],[`Parenteral nutrition`,`Mucosal atrophy, SIBO`,`IFALD: cholestasis (children), steatosis (adults), sludge`,`LFT trend, conjugated bilirubin, USG`]]},{heading:`Liver injury patterns in cardiac disease`,columns:[`Feature`,`Congestive hepatopathy`,`Ischaemic hepatitis (shock liver)`,`Cirrhosis (comparison)`],rows:[[`Mechanism`,`Raised right atrial pressure, zone 3 congestion`,`Low flow plus congestion, zone 3 necrosis`,`Chronic injury with regenerative nodules`],[`Transaminases`,`Normal or mildly raised`,`Over 1000, often over 5000; fall by half in 72 h`,`Mildly raised or normal`],[`Bilirubin and ALP`,`Mild rise, mixed bilirubin, ALP/GGT up`,`Bilirubin rises later`,`Variable`],[`LDH`,`Mildly raised`,`Very high early; ALT/LDH under 1.5`,`Normal`],[`Ascites`,`SAAG 1.1 or more, protein 2.5 g/dL or more`,`Usually absent`,`SAAG 1.1 or more, protein under 2.5 g/dL`],[`Clinical clue`,`Raised JVP, pulsatile liver, TR murmur`,`Recent hypotension, arrest, sepsis`,`Stigmata of chronic liver disease`],[`Treatment`,`Diuretics, treat heart`,`Restore perfusion`,`Cirrhosis care`]]},{heading:`Drugs used for GI manifestations of systemic disease`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Metoclopramide (gastroparesis)`,`5-10 mg orally three times daily before meals`,`Up to 12 weeks`,`Tardive dyskinesia, dystonia; halve dose in CKD`],[`Domperidone (gastroparesis, scleroderma)`,`10 mg orally three times daily`,`Lowest effective dose; review`,`QT prolongation; avoid with QT-prolonging drugs, cardiac disease`],[`Clonidine (diabetic diarrhoea)`,`0.1 mg orally twice daily`,`Titrate to 0.3 mg twice daily`,`Postural hypotension, rebound hypertension on stopping`],[`Pantoprazole (scleroderma reflux)`,`40 mg orally twice daily before meals`,`Long term`,`Hypomagnesaemia, C. difficile, fractures with long use`],[`Octreotide (scleroderma pseudo-obstruction)`,`50 mcg subcutaneously at night`,`Long term if effective`,`Gallstones, glucose changes, abdominal pain`],[`Fluconazole (HIV oesophageal candidiasis)`,`200-400 mg orally daily`,`14-21 days`,`Hepatotoxicity, QT, interactions`],[`Valganciclovir (CMV oesophagitis/colitis)`,`900 mg orally twice daily (or ganciclovir 5 mg/kg IV twice daily)`,`3-6 weeks`,`Neutropenia, thrombocytopenia; renal dosing`],[`Hydroxyurea (sickle cell)`,`15 mg/kg/day orally, up to 35 mg/kg/day`,`Lifelong; CBC monitoring`,`Myelosuppression; teratogenic`]]},{heading:`HIV GI disease by CD4 count`,columns:[`CD4 count (cells/microL)`,`GI pathogens or conditions`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`Any`,`Salmonella, Shigella, Campylobacter, TB, HBV, HCV, ART side effects`,`Standard antibiotics, ATT, TLD covers HBV`],[`Under 200`,`Oral/oesophageal Candida, Cryptosporidium, Cystoisospora, Microsporidia, Kaposi sarcoma`,`Fluconazole; ART; cotrimoxazole for Cystoisospora; albendazole for some Microsporidia`],[`Under 100`,`AIDS cholangiopathy, lymphoma`,`ERCP sphincterotomy; oncology`],[`Under 50`,`CMV oesophagitis/colitis, MAC`,`Ganciclovir or valganciclovir; clarithromycin plus ethambutol`]]}],redFlags:[`Transaminases over 1000 IU/L with hypotension, rising INR or encephalopathy`,`Jaundice with fever and RUQ pain in sickle cell disease (cholangitis or intrahepatic cholestasis)`,`Jaundice, dark urine or pruritus on propylthiouracil or carbimazole`,`Odynophagia or bloody diarrhoea in HIV with CD4 under 200`,`Abdominal distension with vomiting and dilated loops in scleroderma or amyloidosis (pseudo-obstruction)`,`Recurrent GI bleeding with aortic stenosis or GAVE causing transfusion-dependent anaemia`,`Rising conjugated bilirubin over 2 mg/dL on parenteral nutrition`,`Rapid liver or spleen enlargement with falling Hb in a sickle cell patient (sequestration)`],pearls:[`**Cardiac ascites = high SAAG, high protein** - look at the JVP before calling it cirrhosis.`,`**Shock liver**: ALT in thousands, LDH very high, halves in 72 hours.`,`**PTU hits hepatocytes, carbimazole blocks bile** - PTU only in first trimester and storm.`,`**Scleroderma oesophagus**: weak LES, no peristalsis, bad reflux; **achalasia**: tight LES.`,`**Heyde triad**: aortic stenosis, angiodysplasia, acquired vWD 2A.`,`**Congo red, apple-green** - fat pad first, avoid percutaneous liver biopsy in amyloid.`,`**HIV CD4 ladder**: under 200 Candida and Cryptosporidium; under 50 CMV and MAC.`,`**Sickle cell RUQ pain**: stones, crisis, cholestasis - ultrasound and bilirubin trend decide.`,`**IFALD prevention**: soybean lipid 1 g/kg/day or less, mixed or fish-oil lipid, cycle PN, feed the gut.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-in-systemic-disease-q1`,stem:`A 62-year-old man with rheumatic tricuspid regurgitation has ascites and tender hepatomegaly. Ascitic fluid: SAAG 1.6 g/dL, total protein 3.4 g/dL. The most likely cause of ascites is:`,options:[`Alcohol-related cirrhosis`,`Tuberculous peritonitis`,`Congestive hepatopathy`,`Peritoneal carcinomatosis`],answer:2,explanation:`A high SAAG (1.1 g/dL or more) means portal or sinusoidal hypertension, and a high ascitic protein (2.5 g/dL or more) means the sinusoids are leaking protein-rich lymph through normal walls - typical of cardiac ascites. Cirrhosis gives a high SAAG with low protein, while tuberculous peritonitis and carcinomatosis give a low SAAG (under 1.1) exudate.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-in-systemic-disease-q2`,stem:`A 70-year-old woman resuscitated from cardiogenic shock has ALT 4800 IU/L and LDH 6200 IU/L on day 2, with ALT falling to 2100 IU/L by day 4. Bilirubin is 2.1 mg/dL. The best explanation is:`,options:[`Acute hepatitis E`,`Ischaemic hepatitis`,`Paracetamol hepatotoxicity`,`Acute Budd-Chiari syndrome`],answer:1,explanation:`Ischaemic hepatitis follows a hypotensive event with very high transaminases, an even higher LDH (ALT/LDH ratio under 1.5), and a rapid fall by about half within 72 hours once perfusion is restored. Viral hepatitis has a slower rise and fall with a lower LDH, paracetamol injury needs an ingestion history, and Budd-Chiari causes ascites and hepatomegaly with persistent injury.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-in-systemic-disease-q3`,stem:`A 40-year-old woman with Raynaud phenomenon, sclerodactyly and severe heartburn has dysphagia to both solids and liquids. High-resolution manometry is most likely to show:`,options:[`Absent peristalsis in the distal oesophagus with a hypotensive lower oesophageal sphincter`,`Elevated integrated relaxation pressure with panesophageal pressurisation`,`Hypercontractile (jackhammer) peristalsis`,`Normal peristalsis with a hypertensive upper oesophageal sphincter`],answer:0,explanation:`Systemic sclerosis causes smooth-muscle atrophy and fibrosis of the distal two-thirds of the oesophagus, giving absent peristalsis with a weak LES and severe reflux. Raised integrated relaxation pressure with pressurisation is type II achalasia, jackhammer oesophagus is a hypercontractile disorder, and the striated upper oesophagus is spared in scleroderma.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-in-systemic-disease-q4`,stem:`A 28-year-old woman in her 8th week of pregnancy has Graves disease and is started on propylthiouracil. Which adverse effect is this drug particularly associated with, compared with carbimazole?`,options:[`Cholestatic jaundice`,`Aplasia cutis in the newborn`,`Hepatocellular injury and acute liver failure`,`Hypoglycaemia from insulin autoantibodies`],answer:2,explanation:`Propylthiouracil carries a boxed warning for severe hepatocellular injury and acute liver failure, so it is reserved for the first trimester and thyroid storm. Cholestatic jaundice and aplasia cutis (with choanal or oesophageal atresia embryopathy) are associated with carbimazole or methimazole, and insulin autoimmune syndrome is a rare methimazole effect.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-in-systemic-disease-q5`,stem:`A 6-year-old child with short bowel syndrome on parenteral nutrition for 4 months develops rising conjugated bilirubin (3.2 mg/dL). The PN uses a soybean oil lipid emulsion at 3 g/kg/day. The most appropriate change is:`,options:[`Increase dextrose to compensate for lipid calories`,`Stop all enteral feeds to rest the gut`,`Start a course of antifungal therapy empirically`,`Reduce soybean lipid and switch to a fish-oil-containing emulsion, cycle PN and maximise enteral feeding`],answer:3,explanation:`This is intestinal failure-associated liver disease; phytosterols and omega-6 load in soybean lipids drive cholestasis, so lipid is reduced to 1 g/kg/day or less and switched to fish-oil or mixed emulsions, PN is cycled and enteral feeding increased. Adding dextrose causes overfeeding and steatosis, stopping enteral feeds worsens cholestasis, and empirical antifungals are not indicated without evidence of infection.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-in-systemic-disease-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Describe the gastrointestinal and hepatic manifestations of diabetes mellitus, systemic sclerosis and heart failure, with their evaluation and management.`,openingLines:[`Systemic diseases reach the gut through neuropathy, smooth-muscle fibrosis, vascular congestion or low flow, infiltration and the drugs used to treat them.`,`Diabetes, systemic sclerosis and heart failure illustrate the three main mechanisms - autonomic neuropathy, myopathy-fibrosis and haemodynamic liver injury.`],answer:[{heading:`Diabetes mellitus`,points:[`Gastroparesis, nocturnal diarrhoea, SIBO, faecal incontinence, oesophageal candidiasis.`,`MASLD in 65-70% of type 2; FIB-4 then elastography; glycogenic hepatopathy in poorly controlled type 1.`,`Drug effects: metformin, GLP-1 RA, SGLT2i euglycaemic DKA.`,`Treatment: glucose control, small meals, metoclopramide or domperidone, clonidine and loperamide for diarrhoea, GLP-1 RA or pioglitazone for MASLD.`]},{heading:`Systemic sclerosis`,points:[`Oesophagus: distal aperistalsis, hypotensive LES, severe GERD, stricture, Barrett.`,`Stomach: GAVE, gastroparesis; small bowel: SIBO, pseudo-obstruction, pneumatosis; colon: wide-mouth diverticula; anorectum: incontinence.`,`PBC overlap in limited cutaneous disease.`,`Treatment: high-dose PPI, prokinetics, rotating antibiotics, octreotide, APC for GAVE, nutrition support.`]},{heading:`Heart failure`,points:[`Congestive hepatopathy: zone 3 congestion, nutmeg liver, cholestatic LFTs, cardiac cirrhosis.`,`Ischaemic hepatitis: ALT over 1000, high LDH, rapid fall.`,`Cardiac ascites: SAAG 1.1 or more, protein 2.5 or more.`,`Heyde syndrome; non-occlusive mesenteric ischaemia; protein-losing enteropathy in constriction or Fontan.`,`Treatment: treat the heart - decongestion, restore perfusion.`]},{heading:`Evaluation principles`,points:[`LFT pattern, ascitic SAAG and protein, echo and NT-proBNP, gastric emptying scan, manometry, breath test.`,`Exclude primary GI disease before attributing symptoms to the systemic disorder.`]},{heading:`Referral`,points:[`Refer for jaundice, rising INR, bleeding, pseudo-obstruction or diagnostic doubt.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table comparing congestive hepatopathy, ischaemic hepatitis and cirrhosis`,`Diagram of hepatic lobule zones showing zone 3 involvement`],markSplit:[{part:`Diabetes`,marks:3},{part:`Systemic sclerosis`,marks:3},{part:`Heart failure`,marks:3},{part:`Evaluation and referral`,marks:1}],keywords:[`gastroparesis`,`MASLD`,`scleroderma oesophagus`,`GAVE`,`congestive hepatopathy`,`ischaemic hepatitis`,`SAAG`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-in-systemic-disease-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on hepatobiliary complications of sickle cell disease.`,openingLines:[`Sickle cell disease injures the liver and biliary tree through chronic haemolysis, sinusoidal vaso-occlusion and repeated transfusion.`,`In India it clusters in tribal belts, and the National Sickle Cell Anaemia Elimination Mission (2023) targets elimination by 2047.`],answer:[{heading:`Biliary`,points:[`Pigment gallstones in up to 50-70% by adulthood; cholecystitis, choledocholithiasis, cholangitis.`,`Elective laparoscopic cholecystectomy for symptomatic stones with preoperative transfusion to Hb about 10 g/dL.`]},{heading:`Hepatic crises`,points:[`Acute sickle hepatic crisis: RUQ pain, fever, tender hepatomegaly, modest ALT rise; supportive care.`,`Hepatic sequestration: enlarging liver, falling Hb; cautious transfusion.`,`Sickle intrahepatic cholestasis: bilirubin often over 13 mg/dL, coagulopathy, renal failure; urgent exchange transfusion.`]},{heading:`Chronic`,points:[`Transfusional iron overload (ferritin, MRI T2*, chelation).`,`Transfusion-transmitted hepatitis B and C; HBV vaccination.`,`Hydroxyurea reduces crises; screen and counsel under the national mission.`]}],mustDraw:[`Branch diagram: haemolysis, vaso-occlusion, transfusion and their liver complications`],markSplit:[{part:`Biliary complications`,marks:1.5},{part:`Hepatic crises`,marks:2},{part:`Chronic complications and prevention`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`pigment stones`,`sickle hepatic crisis`,`intrahepatic cholestasis`,`exchange transfusion`,`iron overload`,`hydroxyurea`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022`,`Rinella ME, et al. AASLD Practice Guidance on the clinical assessment and management of nonalcoholic fatty liver disease (MASLD). Hepatology, 2023`,`American Diabetes Association. Standards of Care in Diabetes - 2026. Diabetes Care, 2026`,`Camilleri M, et al. ACG Clinical Guideline: Gastroparesis. American Journal of Gastroenterology, 2022`,`Pironi L, et al. ESPEN guideline on chronic intestinal failure in adults - Update 2023. Clinical Nutrition, 2023`,`National Guidelines for HIV Care and Treatment, NACO, MoHFW, 2021`,`National Sickle Cell Anaemia Elimination Mission, Ministry of Health and Family Welfare / Ministry of Tribal Affairs, 2023`]}),u.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatic-encephalopathy-detail`,title:`Hepatic encephalopathy: covert to overt, precipitants and long-term care`,oneLiner:`**Hepatic encephalopathy (HE)** is **brain dysfunction caused by liver insufficiency and/or portosystemic shunting**, ranging from **covert HE (minimal HE and West Haven grade 1, detected only by psychometric tests such as PHES or Stroop)** to **overt HE (grade 2-4, disorientation, asterixis, coma)**; it is driven by **gut-derived ammonia and systemic inflammation**, treated by **finding the precipitant plus lactulose**, with **rifaximin added to prevent recurrence**, and every overt episode is a **trigger for liver transplant evaluation**.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`hepatic encephalopathy`,`portosystemic encephalopathy`,`minimal hepatic encephalopathy`,`covert hepatic encephalopathy`,`overt hepatic encephalopathy`,`West Haven criteria`,`ISHEN`,`PHES`,`Stroop EncephalApp`,`critical flicker frequency`,`animal naming test`,`asterixis`,`ammonia`,`lactulose`,`rifaximin`,`LOLA`,`L-ornithine L-aspartate`,`BCAA`,`PEG 3350`,`TIPS`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**HE** is a brain dysfunction caused by **liver insufficiency and/or portosystemic shunting**, manifesting as a wide spectrum of neurological or psychiatric abnormalities from subclinical alterations to coma. [AASLD/EASL HE Guideline 2014]`,`**Overt HE (OHE)** occurs in **30-40% of patients with cirrhosis** at some time in their course and is present in **10-14% at diagnosis of cirrhosis**; it is a **decompensating event**. [AASLD/EASL HE Guideline 2014]`,`**Covert HE (minimal HE plus grade 1)** is found in **20-80% of patients with cirrhosis** depending on the test and population; Indian studies using PHES report it in about **half of outpatients with cirrhosis**. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`After a first OHE episode, **recurrence is about 40% within 1 year** without prophylaxis, and patients on lactulose still recur in about 40% within 6 months - the basis for adding rifaximin. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Survival after the first OHE episode** is about **42% at 1 year and 23% at 3 years** without transplantation. [Bustamante, J Hepatol 1999]`,`**Post-TIPS OHE** occurs in **35-50%** of patients, mostly in the first 3 months. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`In India, **alcohol, MASLD and hepatitis B** are the leading causes of cirrhosis; HE is among the commonest reasons for admission to Indian hepatology and medical wards. [INASL Consensus 2019]`,`**HE accounts for a large share of cirrhosis readmissions** within 30 days; covert HE predicts the development of OHE, falls, road traffic accidents, poor work performance and death. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`]},{heading:`Aetiology - types and precipitants`,points:[`**Type A**: HE with **acute liver failure**; **Type B**: HE from **portosystemic bypass/shunt without intrinsic liver disease** (e.g. congenital shunt, extrahepatic portal vein obstruction); **Type C**: HE with **cirrhosis** (the common type). [AASLD/EASL HE Guideline 2014]`,`**Precipitants are identified in most episodes**: infections (SBP, UTI, pneumonia, cellulitis), **GI bleeding**, **constipation**, **dehydration and diuretic overuse**, **hypokalaemia and metabolic alkalosis**, **hyponatraemia**, **AKI**, sedatives and opioids. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Drugs**: **benzodiazepines**, opioids, antihistamines, antipsychotics, **excessive diuretics** and **PPIs** (associated with HE via SIBO and dysbiosis) are common iatrogenic triggers. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Alcohol relapse and alcoholic hepatitis**, non-adherence to lactulose, and large-volume paracentesis without albumin are other triggers. [AASLD/EASL HE Guideline 2014]`,`**TIPS, surgical shunts and large spontaneous portosystemic shunts (splenorenal)** cause recurrent or persistent HE despite relatively preserved liver function. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Sarcopenia** worsens HE because skeletal muscle is a key site of **ammonia detoxification by glutamine synthetase**. [AASLD Malnutrition and Sarcopenia Guidance 2021]`,`**Hepatocellular carcinoma, portal vein thrombosis and progression of liver failure** can present as new or refractory HE without an external trigger. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Diabetes, hyponatraemia, previous OHE and high MELD** are risk factors for developing OHE. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**Ammonia comes mainly from the gut**: colonic bacteria with **urease** split urea and protein, and **enterocyte glutaminase** deaminates glutamine; portal blood carries it to the liver. [Harrison 22e]`,`The healthy liver clears ammonia by the **periportal urea cycle** (high capacity) and **perivenous glutamine synthetase** (high affinity); in cirrhosis, reduced hepatocyte mass and **portosystemic shunting** let ammonia reach the systemic circulation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Astrocytes are the brain's only ammonia sink**: they combine ammonia with glutamate to form **glutamine**, an osmolyte that causes **astrocyte swelling and low-grade cerebral oedema** - and glutamine transported into mitochondria (the **'Trojan horse'**) releases ammonia and causes oxidative stress. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Alzheimer type II astrocytes** are the histological hallmark; neurons are largely spared, explaining why HE is mostly **reversible**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Systemic inflammation and infection act synergistically with ammonia** - cytokines cross the blood-brain barrier and amplify astrocyte swelling, which is why sepsis precipitates HE even at modest ammonia levels. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Increased GABAergic tone** from endogenous benzodiazepine-like neurosteroids, plus **manganese deposition in the basal ganglia** (T1 pallidal hyperintensity on MRI), contribute to motor features. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypokalaemia and alkalosis** increase **renal ammoniagenesis** and shift NH4+ to NH3, which diffuses freely into the brain - so correcting potassium and avoiding over-diuresis is a direct treatment. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hyponatraemia** lowers extracellular osmolality and worsens astrocyte swelling; it potentiates HE and must be corrected slowly. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**How drugs map on**: lactulose acidifies the colon (NH3 to non-absorbable NH4+) and is cathartic; rifaximin reduces ammonia-producing bacteria; LOLA stimulates urea cycle and muscle glutamine synthesis; BCAA supply muscle for ammonia uptake. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`]},{heading:`Classification and grading`,points:[`Classify HE on **four axes**: **underlying disease (type A, B, C)**, **severity (West Haven grade)**, **time course (episodic, recurrent, persistent)** and **precipitated or not**. [AASLD/EASL HE Guideline 2014]`,`**West Haven grade 0 / minimal HE**: no clinical signs; abnormal psychometric or neurophysiological tests. [AASLD/EASL HE Guideline 2014]`,`**Grade 1**: trivial lack of awareness, **euphoria or anxiety, shortened attention span, impaired addition or subtraction, altered sleep rhythm**; oriented in time and space. [AASLD/EASL HE Guideline 2014]`,`**Grade 2**: **lethargy or apathy, disorientation for time, obvious personality change, inappropriate behaviour, dyspraxia, asterixis**. [AASLD/EASL HE Guideline 2014]`,`**Grade 3**: **somnolence to semi-stupor** but responsive to stimuli, confusion, **gross disorientation (time and space)**, bizarre behaviour; asterixis may be lost as consciousness falls. [AASLD/EASL HE Guideline 2014]`,`**Grade 4**: **coma** - unresponsive to pain. [AASLD/EASL HE Guideline 2014]`,`**ISHEN consensus**: **covert HE = minimal HE + grade 1** (both oriented, no asterixis); **overt HE = grades 2-4** (disorientation or asterixis) - grade 1 is unreliable between observers. [AASLD/EASL HE Guideline 2014]`,`**Time course**: **episodic**; **recurrent** (episodes within 6 months or less); **persistent** (behavioural alterations always present, interspersed with relapses). [AASLD/EASL HE Guideline 2014]`,`Use the **Glasgow Coma Scale** for grades 3-4 and to decide airway protection (**GCS 8 or less = intubate**). [EASL HE CPG 2022]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Covert HE**: subtle forgetfulness, poor concentration, slow work, **reversal of sleep-wake cycle (daytime sleepiness, night insomnia)**, mistakes in handling money, falls and minor accidents - often noticed by family, not patient. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Overt HE**: confusion, disorientation to time, inappropriate behaviour, slurred speech, drowsiness, and progression to stupor and coma. [AASLD/EASL HE Guideline 2014]`,`**Ask for each precipitant**: fever, dysuria, cough, abdominal pain (SBP), black stools or haematemesis, constipation (last stool), vomiting or diarrhoea, diuretic dose changes, new sedatives, alcohol, missed lactulose. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Count stools**: patients on lactulose should pass **2-3 soft stools per day**; fewer means under-dosing, frequent watery stools mean dehydration risk. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Ask the caregiver** about the timeline: abrupt onset suggests a precipitant; slow decline over months suggests persistent HE, shunt or alternative diagnosis (dementia, Wernicke). [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Head injury and falls** - anticoagulated or coagulopathic cirrhotic patients may have a **subdural haematoma** mimicking HE. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Occupation and driving**: does the patient drive, operate machinery or handle money at work - covert HE testing is most important here. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Asterixis (negative myoclonus, 'flapping tremor')**: brief lapses of postural tone when wrists are dorsiflexed with arms outstretched and fingers spread; present in grade 2-3, lost in coma; also seen in uraemia, CO2 retention and drug toxicity. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fetor hepaticus** - sweet, musty breath from **dimethyl sulphide** - indicates severe liver dysfunction or portosystemic shunting. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hyperreflexia, hypertonia and extensor plantars** occur in grade 3; **decerebrate posturing** in grade 4; focal deficits are **not** typical and need CT. [AASLD/EASL HE Guideline 2014]`,`Look for **stigmata of chronic liver disease**: jaundice, spider naevi, palmar erythema, gynaecomastia, ascites, splenomegaly, caput medusae. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Search for precipitants**: fever, hypotension, tachycardia, melaena on **per-rectal examination**, abdominal tenderness (SBP), chest signs, dehydration, injury marks. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Bedside cognitive tests**: orientation, **serial 7s or counting backwards**, and the **animal naming test** (fewer than 15 animals in 60 seconds suggests covert HE). [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Hepatic myelopathy** (spastic paraparesis) and **acquired hepatocerebral degeneration** (parkinsonism, ataxia) are chronic, poorly reversible variants after long-standing shunting. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Hypoglycaemia** - check capillary glucose immediately in every confused cirrhotic. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Wernicke encephalopathy** in alcohol users - ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, confusion; give **thiamine 200-500 mg IV three times daily** before glucose. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Alcohol intoxication or withdrawal / delirium tremens** - tremor, sweating, hallucinations, autonomic overactivity; benzodiazepines may still be needed but worsen HE. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Intracranial haemorrhage (subdural)** - focal signs, falls, coagulopathy; **non-contrast CT head** if any doubt. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Sepsis-associated encephalopathy, CNS infection** (meningitis, TB meningitis in India) - fever, neck stiffness; lumbar puncture if indicated. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Electrolyte and metabolic**: hyponatraemia, hypercalcaemia, uraemia, **hypercapnia**, hypoxia, hypothyroidism. [AASLD/EASL HE Guideline 2014]`,`**Drug toxicity** (benzodiazepines, opioids, antiepileptics) and **non-convulsive status epilepticus** (EEG). [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Dementia, depression and psychiatric illness** - persistent cognitive decline without response to HE therapy suggests another diagnosis. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**HE is a clinical diagnosis of exclusion**: first-line tests are **glucose, sodium, potassium, creatinine, CBC, INR, bilirubin, albumin, blood and urine cultures, chest X-ray and diagnostic paracentesis (PMN 250/mm3 or more = SBP)**. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Ammonia**: a **normal venous ammonia makes OHE unlikely** and should prompt re-evaluation of the diagnosis; a high level does not by itself confirm HE or grade it. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Ammonia sampling**: venous or arterial sample **without prolonged tourniquet or fist clenching**, in EDTA on ice, analysed **within 30-60 minutes** - poor handling gives false high values. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**CT or MRI brain** for first episode, focal signs, head injury, seizures or poor response to treatment; MRI may show pallidal T1 hyperintensity (manganese). [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Psychometric Hepatic Encephalopathy Score (PHES)**: 5 paper-pencil tests (**number connection tests A and B, digit symbol, serial dotting, line tracing**); a total below **-4** against **population norms (Indian norms available)** = minimal HE. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Stroop test (EncephalApp)**: a smartphone app timing colour-naming with congruent and incongruent stimuli; validated screening tool for covert HE. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Critical flicker frequency** under **39 Hz** and **continuous reaction time** tests are alternatives; **EEG slowing** (triphasic waves) supports OHE in difficult cases. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Animal naming test**: fewer than **15 animals in 60 seconds** (adjust for age and education) is a simple bedside screen recommended by EASL 2022. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Look for spontaneous portosystemic shunts** on contrast CT in recurrent or persistent HE with preserved liver function. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`Calculate **MELD-Na or MELD 3.0 and Child-Pugh** (encephalopathy is one of the five Child-Pugh variables) to guide transplant referral. [AASLD Transplant Evaluation 2025]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Secondary prophylaxis after the first OHE episode**: **lactulose 15-30 mL (10-20 g) two to three times daily, titrated to 2-3 soft stools per day**, continued indefinitely unless the precipitant was fully reversible and liver function improves. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**After a second (recurrent) episode**: add **rifaximin 550 mg twice daily** (or 400 mg three times daily as used in India) to lactulose - it reduces recurrence by about **58% and HE hospitalisation by about 50%**. [Bass, NEJM 2010]`,`**Covert HE** treatment is individualised: lactulose or rifaximin improves psychometric performance, quality of life and driving simulator errors; treat if it impairs work, driving or daily function. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Nutrition**: **energy 35 kcal/kg/day** and **protein 1.2-1.5 g/kg/day** - **never restrict protein**; small frequent meals (4-6), and a **late-evening snack with complex carbohydrate and protein**. [EASL Nutrition in CLD 2019]`,`**Vegetable and dairy protein** are better tolerated than meat in protein-intolerant patients; **oral BCAA (about 0.25 g/kg/day)** helps those who cannot reach protein targets. [EASL Nutrition in CLD 2019]`,`**Avoid** benzodiazepines, opioids, sedating antihistamines and unnecessary PPIs; for insomnia, **hydroxyzine 25 mg at night** has been studied in covert HE but use cautiously. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Diuretics and electrolytes**: keep **potassium in the normal range and sodium above about 130 mmol/L**; reduce diuretics if hyponatraemic or dehydrated. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Constipation**: lactulose doubles as laxative; add **PEG 3350** if stool frequency falls; ensure fluid intake. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Zinc supplementation** (zinc sulphate 220 mg twice daily) if zinc deficient - a cofactor of urea cycle enzymes; evidence modest. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Alcohol abstinence** and treatment of the underlying liver disease (HBV antivirals, HCV DAAs) improve liver function and HE. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Admit all overt HE grade 2 or more**; grade 3-4 needs **HDU/ICU**, airway protection (**intubate if GCS 8 or less**), head-end elevation 30 degrees and aspiration precautions. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Step 1 - identify and treat the precipitant**: cultures and **empirical antibiotics** if infection suspected (third-generation cephalosporin; consider local resistance), control GI bleeding, correct fluids, potassium and sodium, stop sedatives and diuretics. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Step 2 - lactulose**: **25 mL every 1-2 hours until at least two soft bowel movements**, then titrate to **2-3 soft stools per day**; via **nasogastric tube** if the patient cannot swallow safely. [AASLD/EASL HE Guideline 2014]`,`**Lactulose enema**: **300 mL lactulose in 700 mL water** (1 L), retained for 30-60 minutes, every 4-6 hours, when oral route is impossible. [AASLD/EASL HE Guideline 2014]`,`**PEG 3350 (4 L over 4 hours)** resolved HE faster than lactulose in the HELP trial and is an alternative to lactulose in acute OHE. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Rifaximin** can be added in hospital in OHE (lactulose plus rifaximin improved recovery and reduced mortality in an Indian RCT). [Sharma, Am J Gastroenterol 2013]`,`**IV LOLA (L-ornithine L-aspartate) 20-30 g/day** as infusion can be used in patients not responding to standard therapy; Indian RCT data show faster improvement in grade 3-4 OHE. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Flumazenil** only if benzodiazepine exposure is suspected; **metronidazole or neomycin** are second-line alternatives (neuro- and nephrotoxicity limit use). [AASLD/EASL HE Guideline 2014]`,`**Monitor**: hourly GCS, glucose 4-6 hourly, stool count, electrolytes daily, urine output; avoid hypokalaemia from diarrhoea and dehydration from over-purging (hypernatraemia). [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Feed early**: start oral or **nasogastric feeding within 24-48 hours** once airway is safe; avoid prolonged fasting. [EASL Nutrition in CLD 2019]`,`**Persistent HE despite therapy**: look for **large spontaneous portosystemic shunts** for embolisation (if MELD is low), TIPS-related HE (stent reduction), and refer for **liver transplant**. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Post-TIPS**: use smaller covered stents (**8 mm**); **rifaximin started before TIPS** reduced post-TIPS OHE in an RCT; avoid TIPS in patients with recurrent OHE. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Acute liver failure (type A)**: encephalopathy is with **cerebral oedema and intracranial hypertension**; ammonia over 150-200 micromol/L predicts herniation - lactulose is not useful; manage in ICU with transplant referral; see the Acute liver failure topic. [EASL ALF CPG 2017]`,`**Type B (shunt without cirrhosis)**: e.g. **EHPVO and congenital shunts in children**; shunt closure or embolisation can cure it. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Elderly**: HE overlaps with dementia, delirium and polypharmacy; prioritise deprescribing sedatives and anticholinergics. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**CKD and AKI**: urea and ammonia rise; avoid neomycin and metformin accumulation; lactulose and rifaximin are safe. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Diabetes**: lactulose is safe in diabetes (minimal absorption); avoid hypoglycaemia from insulin and sulphonylureas in poor intake; prefer insulin in decompensated cirrhosis. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: diagnose clinically, check glucose and electrolytes, treat infection and constipation, give lactulose by NG or enema - this alone resolves most episodes. [INASL Consensus 2019]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Aspiration pneumonia**, falls and head injury, pressure sores, malnutrition, and **dehydration, hypernatraemia and hypokalaemia from over-purging** with lactulose. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Covert HE** doubles the risk of OHE and is associated with **traffic violations and accidents, falls, unemployment and poor quality of life**. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Persistent cognitive deficits** may remain after resolved OHE and after transplant, especially with repeated episodes. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**OHE is a marker of poor prognosis** - 1-year survival about 42% without transplant; HE complicating ACLF has higher short-term mortality. [Bustamante, J Hepatol 1999]`,`**Caregiver burden** is high - financial loss, sleep disruption and depression in families. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and long-term care`,points:[`**Refer for liver transplant evaluation after the first OHE episode** (with MELD 15 or more, or any recurrent/persistent HE) - HE alone is an accepted indication. [AASLD Transplant Evaluation 2025]`,`**Follow-up every 1-3 months**: stool frequency, adherence, cognition (animal naming, Stroop), sodium, potassium, creatinine, weight and muscle mass, and HCC surveillance (USG with or without AFP every 6 months). [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Educate the caregiver**: early signs (sleep reversal, irritability, small errors), how to titrate lactulose to 2-3 stools, when to come to hospital, and avoiding sedatives and herbal remedies. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Written HE action plan** - increase lactulose if fewer than 2 stools, seek care for fever, black stools, vomiting or confusion. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Stopping prophylaxis**: consider only if the precipitant was clearly reversible, liver function has improved (e.g. after abstinence or antiviral therapy) and nutritional status is good. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Deprescribe PPIs** without a clear indication, and review benzodiazepines and opioids at every visit. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Vaccinate** against hepatitis A and B, pneumococcus and influenza; screen for sarcopenia (grip strength, CT skeletal muscle index) and refer for nutrition and supervised exercise. [AASLD Malnutrition and Sarcopenia Guidance 2021]`]},{heading:`Driving, work and counselling`,points:[`**Covert HE impairs driving**: slower reaction time, poor attention and lane deviation; patients have more crashes and traffic violations. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**After an overt episode**, advise the patient **not to drive** until HE has resolved and cognition has been assessed; document the advice in the notes. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Covert HE**: discuss driving risk; treatment with rifaximin or lactulose improves simulator performance; consider psychometric testing before return to driving or hazardous work. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`In India, a driving licence applicant must self-declare medical fitness (**Form 1 under the Central Motor Vehicles Rules**); counsel the patient to declare a disqualifying impairment. [Central Motor Vehicles Rules 1989, amended]`,`**Work**: avoid operating machinery or heights, financial decision-making may need a family member's oversight; certify sick leave during episodes. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Alcohol abstinence** with relapse-prevention support (baclofen or acamprosate; avoid disulfiram and naltrexone in decompensated disease) is part of HE prevention. [EASL Alcohol CPG 2018]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines on HE (2022)**: a **normal ammonia should prompt re-evaluation** of an OHE diagnosis; **simple screening tests (animal naming) followed by PHES or Stroop** for covert HE. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**EASL 2022**: lactulose for first episode and secondary prophylaxis, **rifaximin add-on for recurrence**; **PEG** as an alternative in acute OHE; **IV LOLA** and **oral BCAA** as adjuncts in selected patients. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**EASL 2022**: consider **rifaximin before TIPS** in selected patients; **embolise large spontaneous shunts** in recurrent HE with preserved liver function. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**EASL 2022**: assess **driving ability and fitness to work** in patients with covert HE and document counselling. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**AASLD/EASL 2014 joint guideline** remains the source of West Haven and ISHEN definitions and lactulose dosing. [AASLD/EASL HE Guideline 2014]`,`**Baveno VII (2022)**: OHE defines decompensation; **recompensation** possible after removal of the cause; consider transplant at first decompensation. [Baveno VII 2022]`,`**MELD 3.0** (adds sex and albumin) replaced MELD-Na for US transplant allocation in 2023; Indian centres use MELD-Na or MELD 3.0 locally. [OPTN MELD 3.0 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **'Precipitating factors and management of hepatic encephalopathy'** - list precipitants, West Haven grades, two-step management (precipitant then lactulose), rifaximin for recurrence, nutrition, transplant referral. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`Viva: **'Covert vs overt HE'** - covert = minimal + grade 1 (oriented, no asterixis); overt = grade 2-4 (disorientation or asterixis). [AASLD/EASL HE Guideline 2014]`,`Viva: **'Mechanism of lactulose'** - colonic acidification traps NH4+, osmotic catharsis, shift of bacterial flora to non-urease producers and increased bacterial ammonia uptake. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: **'Does protein restriction help?'** - No; give 1.2-1.5 g/kg/day; restriction worsens sarcopenia and HE. [EASL Nutrition in CLD 2019]`,`**Common mistake**: giving benzodiazepines for agitation in HE, or fluid-restricting a patient whose HE was precipitated by over-diuresis. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Common mistake**: ordering ammonia to grade or follow HE - it neither grades nor monitors; its value is its high negative predictive value. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`,`**Common mistake**: missing hypoglycaemia, Wernicke or subdural haematoma by labelling every confused cirrhotic as HE. [EASL HE CPG 2022]`]}],tables:[{heading:`West Haven criteria with ISHEN grouping`,columns:[`Grade`,`Clinical description`,`Asterixis`,`ISHEN group`],rows:[[`Minimal HE`,`No clinical signs; abnormal psychometric or neurophysiological tests`,`Absent`,`Covert`],[`Grade 1`,`Trivial lack of awareness, euphoria or anxiety, short attention, impaired arithmetic, sleep reversal`,`Absent (may be subtle)`,`Covert`],[`Grade 2`,`Lethargy, apathy, disorientation for time, personality change, inappropriate behaviour, dyspraxia`,`Present`,`Overt`],[`Grade 3`,`Somnolence to semi-stupor, responsive to stimuli, confusion, gross disorientation, bizarre behaviour`,`Usually present`,`Overt`],[`Grade 4`,`Coma, unresponsive to pain`,`Absent`,`Overt`]]},{heading:`Drugs used in hepatic encephalopathy`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Lactulose (acute OHE)`,`25 mL orally or by NG every 1-2 hours`,`Until 2 soft stools, then titrate to 2-3 soft stools/day`,`Bloating, diarrhoea, dehydration, hypernatraemia, hypokalaemia; avoid in bowel obstruction`],[`Lactulose (prophylaxis)`,`15-30 mL orally two to three times daily`,`Indefinite after first OHE`,`As above; adherence suffers from taste and urgency`],[`Lactulose enema`,`300 mL lactulose in 700 mL water per rectum`,`Every 4-6 hours, retained 30-60 minutes`,`Aspiration risk in unprotected airway; electrolyte loss`],[`Rifaximin`,`550 mg orally twice daily (or 400 mg three times daily)`,`Long term with lactulose after recurrence`,`Well tolerated; nausea; C. difficile rare; cost`],[`Polyethylene glycol 3350`,`4 L orally or by NG over 4 hours (acute OHE)`,`Single course; then lactulose`,`Diarrhoea, electrolyte shifts, aspiration`],[`L-ornithine L-aspartate (LOLA)`,`IV 20-30 g/day infusion; oral 6 g three times daily`,`Until improvement; oral weeks`,`Nausea, vomiting; avoid in severe renal failure (creatinine over 3 mg/dL)`],[`Branched-chain amino acids`,`Oral about 0.25 g/kg/day (granules)`,`Long term in protein-intolerant patients`,`Bloating, cost; not for acute OHE alone`],[`Metronidazole (alternative)`,`250 mg orally two to three times daily`,`Short term only`,`Peripheral neuropathy, ototoxicity, disulfiram-like reaction`],[`Thiamine (if alcohol use)`,`200-500 mg IV three times daily`,`3-5 days, then oral`,`Rare anaphylaxis; give before glucose`]]},{heading:`Tests for covert hepatic encephalopathy`,columns:[`Test`,`What it measures`,`Abnormal result`,`Practical point`],rows:[[`PHES (5 paper-pencil tests)`,`Attention, psychomotor speed, visuomotor coordination`,`Total score below -4 (age- and education-adjusted)`,`Gold standard; Indian norms available; 15-20 min`],[`Stroop test (EncephalApp)`,`Psychomotor speed and cognitive flexibility`,`Time above age-adjusted cut-off`,`Smartphone app; quick screen`],[`Animal naming test`,`Verbal fluency`,`Fewer than 15 animals in 60 seconds`,`Bedside screen; adjust for age and education`],[`Critical flicker frequency`,`Visual discrimination threshold`,`Below 39 Hz`,`Needs device; independent of literacy`],[`Continuous reaction time`,`Motor reaction to sound`,`Prolonged or variable reaction times`,`Needs equipment`],[`EEG`,`Background slowing, triphasic waves`,`Slowing of mean dominant frequency`,`Useful in OHE grading; operator-dependent`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of confusion in cirrhosis`,columns:[`Condition`,`Clue`,`Test`,`Immediate action`],rows:[[`Hepatic encephalopathy`,`Asterixis, precipitant present, fluctuating`,`Clinical; normal ammonia argues against`,`Treat precipitant, lactulose`],[`Hypoglycaemia`,`Sweating, rapid fall in consciousness`,`Capillary glucose`,`IV dextrose`],[`Wernicke encephalopathy`,`Alcohol use, ophthalmoplegia, ataxia`,`Clinical`,`IV thiamine before glucose`],[`Subdural haematoma`,`Falls, coagulopathy, focal signs`,`Non-contrast CT head`,`Neurosurgery, correct coagulopathy`],[`Alcohol withdrawal`,`Tremor, hallucinations, autonomic overactivity 6-72 h after last drink`,`Clinical (CIWA-Ar)`,`Short-acting benzodiazepine (lorazepam) cautiously`],[`Hyponatraemia`,`Sodium under 125 mmol/L, diuretics`,`Serum sodium`,`Stop diuretics; correct slowly (under 8 mmol/L per 24 h)`],[`Sepsis or meningitis`,`Fever, neck stiffness`,`Cultures, LP`,`Antibiotics`],[`Drug toxicity`,`Benzodiazepine or opioid use`,`History, drug levels`,`Flumazenil or naloxone cautiously`]]}],redFlags:[`West Haven grade 3-4 or GCS 8 or less - ICU and airway protection`,`Fever, abdominal pain or tenderness (SBP) or hypotension (sepsis)`,`Haematemesis or melaena (variceal bleed precipitating HE)`,`Focal neurological signs, seizures or head injury (CT head)`,`Hypoglycaemia, sodium under 125 mmol/L or rising creatinine (AKI/HRS)`,`No improvement within 24-48 hours of lactulose and precipitant correction`,`Recurrent or persistent HE - transplant referral`],pearls:[`**Covert = minimal + grade 1; overt = grade 2-4** (disorientation or asterixis).`,`**Find the precipitant, then give lactulose** - two steps, in that order of importance.`,`Lactulose target: **2-3 soft stools a day** - not diarrhoea.`,`**First episode lactulose, second episode add rifaximin** (Bass 2010: recurrence down by 58%).`,`**Normal ammonia = rethink the diagnosis** (EASL 2022); ammonia never grades HE.`,`**Never restrict protein** - 1.2-1.5 g/kg/day plus late-evening snack.`,`**Animal naming under 15 in 60 seconds** = screen positive for covert HE.`,`**First OHE = transplant referral**; 1-year survival only about 42%.`,`Precipitant mnemonic **HEPATICS**: Haemorrhage, Electrolytes (low K, low Na), Protein/constipation, Alcohol and alkalosis, Tranquillisers, Infection, Constipation, Shunts/surgery.`],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatic-encephalopathy-detail-q1`,stem:`A 55-year-old man with alcohol-related cirrhosis is oriented to time and place but his wife reports daytime sleepiness, insomnia at night and errors handling money. There is no asterixis. According to ISHEN, this is best classified as:`,options:[`Overt HE, West Haven grade 2`,`Covert HE`,`Persistent overt HE`,`Type B hepatic encephalopathy`],answer:1,explanation:`ISHEN groups minimal HE and West Haven grade 1 together as covert HE: the patient is oriented and has no asterixis, but has subtle changes such as sleep reversal and impaired arithmetic. Grade 2 (overt) requires disorientation for time or asterixis, persistent HE describes continuous behavioural alteration, and type B refers to shunting without intrinsic liver disease.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatic-encephalopathy-detail-q2`,stem:`A 60-year-old woman with cirrhosis is admitted with grade 2 hepatic encephalopathy. After lactulose and treatment of a UTI she recovers. This is her second episode in 4 months; she has been taking lactulose regularly. The best next step for prevention is:`,options:[`Restrict dietary protein to 0.6 g/kg/day`,`Replace lactulose with metronidazole long term`,`Add rifaximin 550 mg twice daily to lactulose`,`Start a daily oral benzodiazepine for sleep`],answer:2,explanation:`Recurrent OHE despite lactulose is the indication to add rifaximin, which reduced recurrence and HE hospitalisation by about half in the Bass 2010 trial. Protein restriction worsens sarcopenia and is not recommended (1.2-1.5 g/kg/day is advised), long-term metronidazole causes neuropathy, and benzodiazepines precipitate HE.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatic-encephalopathy-detail-q3`,stem:`A patient with cirrhosis presents confused. A correctly handled venous ammonia sample is normal. According to EASL 2022, the correct interpretation is:`,options:[`A normal ammonia should prompt re-evaluation of the diagnosis of overt HE`,`Normal ammonia confirms grade 1 HE`,`Ammonia level is used to grade HE severity`,`Normal ammonia means lactulose should be doubled`],answer:0,explanation:`EASL 2022 states that a normal ammonia level in a patient with suspected overt HE should prompt re-evaluation, because ammonia has a high negative predictive value - look for hypoglycaemia, Wernicke, subdural haematoma, sepsis or drugs. Ammonia does not grade HE, does not confirm grade 1 and does not guide lactulose dosing, which is titrated to stool frequency.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatic-encephalopathy-detail-q4`,stem:`Which nutritional advice is correct for a patient with cirrhosis and recurrent hepatic encephalopathy?`,options:[`Protein under 0.8 g/kg/day during maintenance`,`Avoid all dairy and vegetable protein`,`Fast overnight for at least 12 hours to rest the liver`,`Protein 1.2-1.5 g/kg/day with a late-evening carbohydrate-protein snack`],answer:3,explanation:`EASL nutrition guidance advises energy 35 kcal/kg/day and protein 1.2-1.5 g/kg/day with small frequent meals and a late-evening snack to shorten overnight fasting, which in cirrhosis behaves like starvation. Protein restriction worsens sarcopenia and therefore ammonia clearance, vegetable and dairy proteins are actually better tolerated, and prolonged fasting drives muscle catabolism.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatic-encephalopathy-detail-q5`,stem:`A 48-year-old man with alcohol-related cirrhosis on anticoagulation for portal vein thrombosis presents drowsy with right-sided weakness after a fall. Asterixis is absent. What is the most important immediate investigation?`,options:[`Serum ammonia`,`Non-contrast CT head`,`PHES battery`,`Electroencephalogram`],answer:1,explanation:`Focal weakness after a fall in an anticoagulated cirrhotic patient suggests subdural or intracerebral haemorrhage, so a non-contrast CT head is the priority; HE does not usually cause focal deficits. Ammonia neither confirms nor excludes a bleed, PHES is for covert HE in stable outpatients, and EEG does not replace imaging when focal signs are present.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatic-encephalopathy-detail-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Define hepatic encephalopathy. Discuss its classification, pathogenesis, precipitating factors and management, including long-term prevention.`,openingLines:[`Hepatic encephalopathy is brain dysfunction caused by liver insufficiency and/or portosystemic shunting, ranging from subclinical alterations to coma (AASLD/EASL 2014).`,`It is classified by underlying disease, West Haven severity, time course and precipitants, and managed by treating the precipitant plus lactulose, adding rifaximin for recurrence (EASL 2022).`],answer:[{heading:`Classification`,points:[`Type A (ALF), B (bypass), C (cirrhosis).`,`West Haven grades 0-4; ISHEN covert (minimal + grade 1) vs overt (2-4).`,`Episodic, recurrent (within 6 months), persistent; precipitated or not.`]},{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Gut ammonia (urease bacteria, glutaminase) bypasses failing liver and shunts.`,`Astrocyte glutamine accumulation: swelling, Trojan horse mitochondrial injury.`,`Inflammation synergy, GABAergic tone, manganese; hypokalaemia and alkalosis raise NH3; muscle as ammonia sink.`]},{heading:`Precipitating factors`,points:[`Infection, GI bleed, constipation, dehydration and diuretics, hypokalaemia, hyponatraemia, AKI.`,`Sedatives, alcohol, TIPS, non-adherence, HCC or portal vein thrombosis.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Clinical; exclude hypoglycaemia, Wernicke, subdural, sepsis, drugs.`,`Ammonia: normal value questions diagnosis; CT head for first episode or focal signs.`,`Covert HE: animal naming, PHES, Stroop, CFF.`]},{heading:`Acute management`,points:[`Admit grade 2+; ICU and airway for grade 3-4.`,`Treat precipitant; lactulose 25 mL every 1-2 h until 2 soft stools, then titrate; enema or NG.`,`PEG 3350, IV LOLA, rifaximin as adjuncts; feed early.`]},{heading:`Long-term prevention`,points:[`Lactulose after first episode; add rifaximin 550 mg twice daily after recurrence.`,`Protein 1.2-1.5 g/kg/day, late-evening snack, BCAA; avoid sedatives and PPIs.`,`Caregiver education, driving advice, shunt embolisation, transplant referral.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: ammonia from gut to liver to astrocyte, with sites of action of lactulose, rifaximin and LOLA`,`West Haven table with ISHEN covert/overt grouping`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and classification`,marks:2},{part:`Pathogenesis`,marks:2},{part:`Precipitants and diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Acute management`,marks:2},{part:`Long-term prevention and transplant`,marks:2}],keywords:[`West Haven`,`ISHEN`,`ammonia`,`astrocyte`,`lactulose`,`rifaximin`,`LOLA`,`transplant`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-hepatic-encephalopathy-detail-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on minimal (covert) hepatic encephalopathy.`,openingLines:[`Minimal HE is the mildest form of HE: no clinical signs, but abnormal psychometric or neurophysiological tests; with grade 1 it forms covert HE (ISHEN).`,`It affects up to half of Indian outpatients with cirrhosis and impairs driving, work and quality of life.`],answer:[{heading:`Significance`,points:[`Predicts overt HE, falls, traffic accidents, unemployment and death.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Screen: animal naming under 15 in 60 seconds, Stroop EncephalApp.`,`Confirm: PHES total below -4 with Indian norms; CFF under 39 Hz.`,`Test when results change management (driving, work, cognitive complaints).`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Lactulose or rifaximin improves test scores, quality of life and driving simulator performance.`,`Nutrition, avoid sedatives, treat underlying liver disease.`]},{heading:`Counselling`,points:[`Driving and hazardous work risk; document advice; caregiver education.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table of covert HE tests with cut-offs`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and significance`,marks:1},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment and counselling`,marks:2}],keywords:[`minimal HE`,`covert HE`,`PHES`,`Stroop`,`animal naming`,`driving`,`lactulose`,`rifaximin`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`European Association for the Study of the Liver. EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines on the management of hepatic encephalopathy. Journal of Hepatology, 2022`,`Vilstrup H, et al. Hepatic encephalopathy in chronic liver disease: 2014 Practice Guideline by AASLD and EASL. Hepatology, 2014`,`European Association for the Study of the Liver. EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines on nutrition in chronic liver disease. Journal of Hepatology, 2019`,`Bass NM, et al. Rifaximin treatment in hepatic encephalopathy. New England Journal of Medicine, 2010`,`de Franchis R, et al. Baveno VII - Renewing consensus in portal hypertension. Journal of Hepatology, 2022`,`Lai JC, et al. AASLD Practice Guidance: Malnutrition, frailty and sarcopenia in patients with cirrhosis. Hepatology, 2021`,`Indian National Association for Study of the Liver (INASL) consensus documents on hepatic encephalopathy and minimal hepatic encephalopathy`]});var d=[];d.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-emergencies-ipd`,title:`GI emergencies in the ward: foreign body, caustic ingestion, toxic megacolon, obstruction`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`Ward GI emergencies are **time-critical conditions in which the timing of endoscopy or decompression decides the outcome**: an **oesophageal foreign body, button battery or complete food bolus obstruction needs endoscopy within 2-6 hours (ESGE 2016)**, a **caustic burn is graded by Zargar endoscopy at 12-48 hours**, **toxic megacolon is colonic dilatation over 6 cm with systemic toxicity (Jalan criteria)**, and **acute colonic pseudo-obstruction with a caecum over 10-12 cm responds to IV neostigmine 2 mg** once mechanical obstruction is excluded.`,keywords:[`foreign body ingestion`,`food bolus impaction`,`button battery`,`disc battery`,`caustic ingestion`,`corrosive acid ingestion`,`Zargar grading`,`toxic megacolon`,`Jalan criteria`,`intestinal obstruction`,`adhesive small bowel obstruction`,`sigmoid volvulus`,`Ogilvie syndrome`,`acute colonic pseudo-obstruction`,`neostigmine`,`Gastrografin challenge`,`ESGE 2016`,`WSES`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Foreign body ingestion** covers true foreign objects (coins, batteries, magnets, dentures, bones, pins) and **food bolus impaction**; **80-90% pass spontaneously**, 10-20% need endoscopy and **under 1% need surgery**. [ASGE 2011]`,`Children (peak **6 months to 6 years**) swallow coins and toys; adults present with **meat or fish/chicken bone bolus**, dentures, and deliberate ingestion in psychiatric illness, prisoners and **body packers**. [ESGE 2016]`,`In adults **food bolus impaction usually unmasks an underlying oesophageal disease** - eosinophilic oesophagitis, Schatzki ring, peptic or caustic stricture, or cancer - so the event is a diagnostic opportunity. [ESGE 2016]`,`**Caustic ingestion** in India is mostly **acid** (toilet cleaners with hydrochloric acid, battery sulphuric acid, 'tezaab'), largely **deliberate in adults** and accidental in children, whereas in the West alkali (lye, drain cleaners) predominates. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Toxic megacolon** is total or segmental **non-obstructive colonic dilatation of at least 6 cm with systemic toxicity**; it complicates about **1-5% of severe ulcerative colitis** admissions and an increasing share of fulminant **C. difficile colitis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Intestinal obstruction** is one of the commonest surgical emergencies; in India **obstructed hernia, adhesions after laparotomy, abdominal tuberculosis and sigmoid volvulus** are the leading causes, with northern India in the **volvulus belt**. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Acute colonic pseudo-obstruction (Ogilvie syndrome)** is massive colonic dilatation **without a mechanical cause**, seen in elderly, post-operative (orthopaedic, caesarean, cardiac) and septic inpatients. [ASGE 2020]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Anatomical narrowings** where objects lodge: upper oesophageal sphincter (cricopharyngeus, the commonest site in children), aortic arch, left main bronchus crossing, and the **lower oesophageal sphincter**; beyond these, the pylorus, duodenal sweep and ileocaecal valve. [ESGE 2016]`,`Objects **over 6 cm long or over 2.5 cm wide** rarely negotiate the pylorus and duodenal C-loop and should be retrieved from the stomach. [ESGE 2016]`,`**Caustic severity** rises with pH below 2 or above 12, volume (**over 150 mL** in deliberate ingestion), concentration, viscosity and duration of contact; solid alkali granules stick to the pharynx and upper oesophagus. [WSES 2019]`,`Toxic megacolon precipitants: **antimotility agents (loperamide, opioids, anticholinergics)**, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, barium enema, colonoscopy in active colitis and abrupt steroid withdrawal. [Harrison 22e]`,`Infective causes of toxic megacolon: **C. difficile**, Shigella, Salmonella (including typhoid), Campylobacter, **amoebic colitis** and **CMV colitis** in steroid-treated or HIV-positive patients. [Harrison 22e]`,`Pseudo-obstruction risk: **opioids, anticholinergics, antipsychotics, calcium channel blockers**, hypokalaemia, hypocalcaemia, hypothyroidism, sepsis, recent surgery, spinal injury and Parkinson disease. [ASGE 2020]`,`**Sigmoid volvulus** is favoured by a long redundant sigmoid with a narrow mesentery - high-fibre diet, chronic constipation, high altitude, Chagas disease, institutionalised elderly and neuropsychiatric patients on anticholinergics. [WSES 2023]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why each one is dangerous`,points:[`A **button (disc) battery** lodged in the oesophagus generates **hydroxide at the negative pole** by electrolysis of tissue fluid, causing **liquefactive necrosis within 2 hours** - perforation, tracheo-oesophageal or **aorto-oesophageal fistula** can follow days later. [ESPGHAN 2021]`,`Two or more **magnets** (or a magnet and metal) attract across loops of bowel and cause **pressure necrosis, fistula and perforation**, so multiple magnets are never left to pass. [ESGE 2016]`,`**Alkali causes liquefactive necrosis** - saponification of fat and protein dissolution let it penetrate deep into the **oesophageal wall**; **acid causes coagulative necrosis** with an eschar that limits depth but injures the **stomach (antrum and pylorus)** more. [Harrison 22e]`,`After a caustic burn, **sloughing at days 4-7** and granulation with **minimal collagen at days 5-15** make the wall weakest, which is why endoscopy and dilatation are avoided then; **collagen contraction from week 3** forms strictures. [WSES 2019]`,`In **toxic megacolon**, transmural inflammation reaches the **muscularis propria and myenteric plexus**; local **nitric oxide** release paralyses smooth muscle, so the colon dilates and thins and perforates. [Harrison 22e]`,`In **mechanical obstruction**, gas and fluid accumulate proximally, raising intraluminal pressure; when it exceeds venous then arterial pressure the loop becomes **ischaemic (strangulation)** with bacterial translocation and sepsis. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Laplace law** (wall tension proportional to pressure times radius) explains why the **caecum, the widest part, perforates first** in colonic obstruction and pseudo-obstruction. [ASGE 2020]`,`Ogilvie syndrome reflects **autonomic imbalance - excess sympathetic tone or loss of sacral parasympathetic drive** to the distal colon; this is why the **acetylcholinesterase inhibitor neostigmine** decompresses it. [ASGE 2020]`]},{heading:`Classification and grading that examiners ask`,points:[`**ESGE 2016 timing for foreign bodies**: emergent endoscopy (ideally **within 2 hours, at the latest 6 hours**) for complete oesophageal obstruction, **batteries** and **sharp-pointed objects in the oesophagus**; urgent (**within 24 hours**) for other oesophageal foreign bodies. [ESGE 2016]`,`In the stomach, ESGE advises **urgent removal (within 24 hours)** of sharp-pointed objects, magnets, batteries and large objects, and **non-urgent removal (within 72 hours)** of medium-sized blunt objects; small blunt objects can be observed. [ESGE 2016]`,`**Zargar endoscopic grading of caustic injury**: **0** normal; **1** oedema and hyperaemia; **2a** friability, erosions, blisters, exudates, superficial ulcers; **2b** plus deep discrete or circumferential ulcers; **3a** scattered small areas of necrosis; **3b** extensive necrosis. [Zargar 1991]`,`Zargar grade predicts outcome: **grades 0-2a heal without stricture**, **grade 2b and 3a stricture in about 70-100%**, and **grade 3b carries high early mortality** and need for oesophagectomy or gastrectomy. [WSES 2019]`,`**Jalan criteria for toxic megacolon**: radiological colonic dilatation **over 6 cm**, plus **3 of** fever over 38.6 C, heart rate over 120/min, leucocytes over 10.5 x 10^9/L, anaemia; plus **1 of** dehydration, altered sensorium, electrolyte disturbance, hypotension. [Jalan 1969]`,`**Obstruction** is classified as small vs large bowel, complete vs partial, simple vs **closed-loop**, and **simple vs strangulated** - the last two decide immediate surgery. [WSES Bologna 2017]`,`**Rule of 3-6-9**: small bowel over **3 cm**, colon over **6 cm** and caecum over **9 cm** are dilated; in pseudo-obstruction a caecum over **12 cm** for more than **6 days** has a high perforation risk. [ASGE 2020]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Oesophageal foreign body: **sudden dysphagia, odynophagia, drooling and inability to swallow saliva** (complete obstruction), retrosternal pain and regurgitation; a child may only refuse feeds or have stridor or cough. [ESGE 2016]`,`Patients localise a **pharyngeal or upper oesophageal** object reasonably well but **distal sensation is unreliable**, so symptom site does not exclude a lower lesion. [ESGE 2016]`,`Caustic history must capture **agent (bottle label), acid or alkali, volume, intent, time** and associated drugs or alcohol; **absence of oral burns does not exclude severe oesophageal injury**. [WSES 2019]`,`**Stridor, hoarseness and dyspnoea** after caustic ingestion mean laryngeal or epiglottic injury; **severe chest or back pain, haematemesis and abdominal pain** suggest deep or transmural necrosis. [WSES 2019]`,`Toxic megacolon: a patient with known or new colitis whose **bloody diarrhoea paradoxically decreases** while **abdominal distension, pain, fever and tachycardia worsen**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Obstruction: **colicky pain, vomiting, distension and absolute constipation**; **early copious vomiting** suggests high small bowel, while **early distension and late faeculent vomiting** suggest large bowel. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`Pain changing from colicky to **constant and severe**, out of proportion to signs, suggests **strangulation or mesenteric ischaemia**. [WSES Bologna 2017]`,`Ogilvie syndrome: **painless or mildly painful progressive distension over 3-7 days** in a bedbound inpatient, often **still passing some flatus or stool**. [ASGE 2020]`]},{heading:`Examination - bedside signs and their meaning`,points:[`Check **airway first** - stridor, drooling, voice change; a foreign body or caustic oedema at the larynx is the immediate killer. [WSES 2019]`,`**Subcutaneous emphysema in the neck or crepitus** and fever after an ingestion mean **oesophageal perforation**; Hamman crunch suggests pneumomediastinum. [ESGE 2016]`,`Look at **lips, tongue and oropharynx** for caustic burns, but treat the oral exam as a poor guide to oesophageal depth. [WSES 2019]`,`Toxic megacolon: **tachycardia, fever, hypotension, tympanitic distension and reduced bowel sounds**; steroids can **mask peritonism**, so tenderness may be minimal even with perforation. [Harrison 22e]`,`Obstruction: **examine all hernial orifices** (femoral in elderly women), look for laparotomy scars, visible peristalsis, and do a **per-rectal examination** (empty ballooned rectum, mass, impacted faeces). [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Fever, tachycardia, guarding, rebound and leucocytosis** in obstruction indicate strangulation; absence of these signs **does not reliably exclude it**. [WSES Bologna 2017]`,`Sigmoid volvulus: **gross asymmetrical distension** in an elderly man with chronic constipation, with an **empty rectum**. [WSES 2023]`,`Ogilvie syndrome: **massively distended, tympanitic but relatively non-tender** abdomen with bowel sounds often present; tenderness or peritonism suggests **ischaemia or impending perforation**. [ASGE 2020]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`Foreign body sensation after a meal vs **mucosal abrasion** - an abrasion from a passed fish bone gives persisting sensation with a normal X-ray and endoscopy. [ESGE 2016]`,`Coin in oesophagus vs trachea: an oesophageal coin lies **en face (a full circle) on AP view**, a tracheal one lies edge-on, because tracheal rings are open posteriorly. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Button battery vs coin**: the battery shows a **double-ring (halo) on AP** and a **step-off on lateral** view; misreading it as a coin delays a 2-hour emergency. [ESPGHAN 2021]`,`Toxic megacolon in colitis vs **mechanical obstruction or pseudo-obstruction**: megacolon has **systemic toxicity and known colitis**; pseudo-obstruction is non-toxic with no colitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Small bowel obstruction vs **paralytic ileus**: ileus is painless, silent, follows surgery, sepsis or hypokalaemia, and shows **gas throughout small and large bowel** including rectum. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`Pseudo-obstruction vs **distal colonic cancer or sigmoid volvulus**: CT or water-soluble contrast enema shows a **transition point** only in mechanical obstruction. [ASGE 2020]`,`Abdominal distension with vomiting in India: consider **abdominal tuberculosis** (doughy abdomen, ileocaecal stricture) - see the Abdominal tuberculosis topic. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line and advanced`,points:[`**Plain X-ray neck, chest and abdomen (AP and lateral)** for suspected radio-opaque objects, batteries and coins; it misses most fish bones, wood, plastic, glass and food boluses. [ESGE 2016]`,`**Do not give barium** for a suspected foreign body - it risks aspiration and coats the mucosa, impairing endoscopy. [ESGE 2016]`,`**CT** is recommended when perforation or another complication is suspected, or when surgery is anticipated; it detects fish and chicken bones that X-ray misses. [ESGE 2016]`,`In caustic ingestion, **CT chest and abdomen with contrast** identifies **transmural necrosis** (absent wall enhancement, periesophageal fat stranding, free air) and predicts need for surgery better than endoscopy. [WSES 2019]`,`**Endoscopy for caustic injury within 12-48 hours** (ideally within 24 hours) grades the burn; it is avoided **between days 5 and 15** and contraindicated with perforation or airway compromise. [WSES 2019]`,`Toxic megacolon work-up: **supine abdominal X-ray** (transverse colon over 6 cm, loss of haustra, mucosal islands), CBC, **potassium, magnesium, albumin, CRP, lactate**, blood cultures, **stool C. difficile toxin/PCR** and **CMV on flexible sigmoidoscopy biopsy** if steroid-refractory. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Contrast-enhanced CT abdomen** is the test of choice for obstruction - it locates the **transition point**, cause, closed loop and signs of strangulation (reduced wall enhancement, mesenteric oedema, free fluid, whirl sign, pneumatosis). [WSES Bologna 2017]`,`**Serum lactate** rises late in strangulation and is not reliable on its own; a normal lactate does not exclude ischaemia. [WSES Bologna 2017]`,`In sigmoid volvulus the X-ray shows the **coffee-bean (bent inner-tube) sign** pointing to the right upper quadrant; caecal volvulus shows a dilated caecum displaced to the **left upper quadrant**. [WSES 2023]`]},{heading:`Initial management in casualty or the ward (all four)`,points:[`**ABC first**: secure the airway (early intubation by an experienced operator for caustic stridor), oxygen, two wide-bore cannulas, crystalloid resuscitation, **nil by mouth**. [WSES 2019]`,`Caustic ingestion: **do not induce vomiting, do not neutralise** with acid or alkali, **no activated charcoal**, and **no blind nasogastric tube**; small sips of water are allowed only in the first minutes if the airway is safe and the patient can swallow. [WSES 2019]`,`Give **IV PPI** (pantoprazole 40 mg IV twice daily) after caustic ingestion; evidence for stricture prevention is weak but it limits acid reflux onto injured mucosa. [WSES 2019]`,`**Systemic steroids are not recommended** to prevent caustic strictures (no benefit in the NEJM 1990 paediatric RCT); a small trial suggested benefit of high-dose dexamethasone in grade 2b injury, so practice varies. [WSES 2019]`,`Obstruction: **drip and suck** - nasogastric decompression, IV fluids with potassium, urinary catheter with **hourly urine output**, analgesia and serial abdominal examination. [WSES Bologna 2017]`,`Toxic megacolon: **stop opioids, loperamide and anticholinergics**, correct **potassium (target over 4 mmol/L) and magnesium**, give **VTE prophylaxis (LMWH)** despite bleeding, and involve the surgeon from day 1. [ECCO 2022]`,`Ogilvie syndrome: **nil by mouth, nasogastric and rectal tube, stop offending drugs, correct electrolytes**, mobilise, and use **knee-chest or prone positioning**; this resolves many cases within 24-48 hours. [ASGE 2020]`]},{heading:`Inpatient protocol - foreign body and food bolus`,points:[`Endoscopic retrieval is done with an **overtube or a latex protector hood** for sharp objects, under **airway protection (intubation)** when objects are multiple, large or in the upper oesophagus. [ESGE 2016]`,`**Food bolus**: gentle **push into the stomach** with the endoscope is acceptable once the path is seen; otherwise retrieve en bloc or piecemeal with a net or snare. [ESGE 2016]`,`**Take oesophageal biopsies** at the index endoscopy for food bolus (at least 6 biopsies from proximal and distal oesophagus) to diagnose **eosinophilic oesophagitis**. [ESGE 2016]`,`**Glucagon** (1 mg IV) relaxes the LOS but has poor evidence and causes vomiting; it must **never delay endoscopy**. [ESGE 2016]`,`**Button battery in the oesophagus**: remove immediately; in a child presenting **within 12 hours** of a witnessed ingestion, give **honey 10 mL every 10 minutes (up to 6 doses)** before arrival and **sucralfate** in hospital to coat the battery (not under 1 year for honey). [ESPGHAN 2021]`,`After battery removal, inspect the injury; a deep injury needs **CT or MRI for vascular proximity**, NBM and close watch for delayed fistula or aorto-oesophageal bleeding. [ESPGHAN 2021]`,`**Body packers (drug packets)**: **never endoscopically retrieve** - rupture can be fatal; manage conservatively with laxatives and imaging, and operate for obstruction, failure to progress or signs of packet rupture. [ESGE 2016]`,`Blunt objects that have passed the stomach are followed with **stool check and weekly X-ray**; a non-progressing object for **over 1 week** or any symptom needs removal. [ESGE 2016]`]},{heading:`Inpatient protocol - caustic ingestion by grade`,points:[`**Zargar 0-1**: start oral liquids within 24-48 hours and discharge; no follow-up endoscopy is needed. [WSES 2019]`,`**Zargar 2a**: soft diet as tolerated, PPI, short admission; strictures are uncommon. [WSES 2019]`,`**Zargar 2b and 3a**: admit, **nasogastric or nasojejunal feeding passed under endoscopic vision** or a **feeding jejunostomy**; total parenteral nutrition only if enteral access fails. [WSES 2019]`,`**Zargar 3b or CT transmural necrosis**: emergency surgery - **oesophagogastrectomy with cervical oesophagostomy and feeding jejunostomy**, with reconstruction (colon interposition) after 6-12 months. [WSES 2019]`,`**Antibiotics** are not routine; give them for aspiration pneumonia, perforation or when steroids are used. [WSES 2019]`,`**Barium or water-soluble contrast swallow at 3 weeks** maps strictures; **endoscopic dilatation starts 3-6 weeks** after injury with bougies or balloon. [WSES 2019]`,`**Psychiatric assessment** is mandatory before discharge in every deliberate ingestion. [WSES 2019]`]},{heading:`Inpatient protocol - toxic megacolon and obstruction`,points:[`**IBD-related toxic megacolon**: IV **methylprednisolone 60 mg/day or hydrocortisone 100 mg 6-hourly**, broad-spectrum antibiotics, and **surgery if no improvement in 24-72 hours**. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Fulminant C. difficile with ileus**: **oral or nasogastric vancomycin 500 mg 6-hourly plus IV metronidazole 500 mg 8-hourly**, with **rectal vancomycin 500 mg in 100 mL saline 6-hourly** if ileus. [IDSA/SHEA 2021]`,`Monitor with **4-6-hourly clinical review**, daily bloods and **12-24-hourly abdominal X-ray**; rolling into the prone or knee-elbow position redistributes gas and may decompress the colon. [Harrison 22e]`,`Surgery for toxic megacolon is **subtotal colectomy with end ileostomy**; indications are perforation, massive bleeding, worsening dilatation or toxicity, or no response in 24-72 hours. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Adhesive small bowel obstruction without strangulation**: non-operative management for **up to 72 hours**, with a **water-soluble contrast (Gastrografin 100 mL) challenge** by NG tube. [WSES Bologna 2017]`,`Contrast reaching the **colon on an X-ray within 24 hours** predicts resolution without surgery and also shortens stay; failure to reach the colon predicts need for laparotomy. [WSES Bologna 2017]`,`**Immediate surgery** for strangulation, peritonitis, closed-loop obstruction, **obstructed or strangulated hernia** and a virgin abdomen without an obvious cause. [WSES Bologna 2017]`,`**Sigmoid volvulus without ischaemia**: **endoscopic detorsion** with a decompression tube, then **elective sigmoid colectomy in the same admission** because recurrence after detorsion alone is high; gangrene or perforation needs emergency resection (Hartmann procedure). [WSES 2023]`]},{heading:`Inpatient protocol - acute colonic pseudo-obstruction and neostigmine`,points:[`First **exclude mechanical obstruction** with **CT abdomen** or a water-soluble contrast enema; never give neostigmine to an unexcluded mechanical obstruction. [ASGE 2020]`,`Conservative care for **24-72 hours** is appropriate when the caecum is **under 12 cm** and there is no tenderness, peritonism or ischaemia. [ASGE 2020]`,`**Neostigmine 2 mg IV slowly over 3-5 minutes** is given when conservative care fails; response (flatus, stool, reduced girth) usually comes **within 30 minutes**, with success in **about 80-90%** (Ponec NEJM 1999). [ASGE 2020]`,`Give it **supine, with cardiac monitoring, for 15-30 minutes** and **atropine (0.6 mg IV) at the bedside** for symptomatic bradycardia; a second dose can be given if the first fails or dilatation recurs. [ASGE 2020]`,`Contraindications: **mechanical obstruction, perforation or ischaemia, heart rate under 60/min, active bronchospasm**, recent MI, uncontrolled arrhythmia; **reduce the dose in renal failure**. [ASGE 2020]`,`Side effects are cholinergic: **bradycardia, salivation, abdominal cramps, vomiting, bronchospasm and sweating**. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`If neostigmine fails or is contraindicated: **colonoscopic decompression** with placement of a decompression tube; recurrence after colonoscopy alone is up to 40%. [ASGE 2020]`,`**Daily oral polyethylene glycol** after decompression reduces recurrence; **surgery (caecostomy or resection)** is reserved for perforation, ischaemia or failure of all measures. [ASGE 2020]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Children**: battery ingestion in a child **under 5 years with a battery 20 mm or larger** is the highest-risk scenario; a gastric battery in an asymptomatic child can be observed with X-ray, but symptomatic children or those with magnets need endoscopy. [ESPGHAN 2021]`,`**Elderly with dentures** lose palatal sensation and swallow bridgework; partial dentures with wire clasps are sharp objects needing **emergent retrieval**. [ESGE 2016]`,`**Pregnancy**: shield the fetus during X-ray; prefer **ultrasound or MRI** for obstruction; neostigmine is used only after obstetric review because it can stimulate uterine contraction. [ASGE 2020]`,`Toxic megacolon in **pregnancy with UC** carries high fetal loss; early colectomy with obstetric support improves maternal outcome. [ECCO 2022]`,`**Elderly and post-operative orthopaedic patients** are the classic Ogilvie group; review the drug chart for opioids, anticholinergics and antipsychotics daily. [ASGE 2020]`,`**Psychiatric illness and prisoners**: repeated deliberate ingestion of sharp objects; manage with the psychiatrist and plan airway-protected endoscopy. [ESGE 2016]`,`**Resource-limited district hospital**: if endoscopy is not available, stabilise, keep NBM and refer immediately for any **battery, sharp object or complete obstruction** - do not wait for spontaneous passage. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications, prognosis, referral and follow-up`,points:[`Foreign body complications: **perforation, mediastinitis, retropharyngeal abscess, aorto-oesophageal fistula** (batteries, fish bones) and bleeding; mortality is low but battery injuries in children are often severe. [ESGE 2016]`,`**Caustic strictures** develop in the first 2 months; refractory strictures need serial dilatation, intralesional triamcinolone or surgery. [WSES 2019]`,`**Caustic oesophagus carries a 1000-3000-fold risk of squamous cell carcinoma** after a latency of 15-40 years; **surveillance endoscopy from about 15-20 years** after ingestion is advised. [ASGE 2019]`,`**Acid ingestion often causes antral or pyloric stricture with gastric outlet obstruction** at 4-8 weeks, needing balloon dilatation or gastrojejunostomy. [Harrison 22e]`,`Toxic megacolon mortality is low with early surgery but **rises steeply after perforation**, which is why serial X-rays and a low threshold for colectomy matter. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ogilvie syndrome with **caecal perforation** has mortality around **40%**; overall mortality is driven by comorbidity. [ASGE 2020]`,`After discharge: review caustic patients at **3 weeks** for dysphagia, follow food bolus patients with the biopsy result (**EoE treatment**), and refer volvulus survivors for elective sigmoid colectomy if not done. [WSES 2023]`]},{heading:`Prevention, latest updates and the exam angle`,points:[`**Prevention**: keep button batteries and magnets away from children, secure battery compartments, and store acids in labelled original bottles; the **Supreme Court (Laxmi v Union of India, 2013)** led to rules restricting over-the-counter acid sale. [MoHFW 2013]`,`Deliberate caustic ingestion is a **suicide attempt** - link to the **National Suicide Prevention Strategy (2022)** and **Tele-MANAS (14416)**. [MoHFW 2022]`,`**ESGE 2016 foreign body guideline** remains the current reference for timing; the **ESPGHAN 2021 position paper** added honey and sucralfate first aid for button batteries. [ESPGHAN 2021]`,`**ASGE 2020** guideline on acute colonic pseudo-obstruction and sigmoid volvulus confirms neostigmine 2 mg IV as first pharmacological therapy and endoscopic detorsion followed by elective resection. [ASGE 2020]`,`**WSES 2023 sigmoid volvulus update** advises endoscopic detorsion when there is no ischaemia and sigmoidectomy in the same admission in fit patients. [WSES 2023]`,`**IDSA/SHEA 2021 focused update** prefers fidaxomicin for an initial C. difficile episode but keeps **high-dose vancomycin plus IV metronidazole** for fulminant colitis with ileus. [IDSA/SHEA 2021]`,`**ACG 2025 and BSG 2025** ulcerative colitis guidance keep IV steroids with day-3 assessment and **infliximab or ciclosporin rescue** for acute severe colitis, with early colectomy if toxic megacolon. [ACG UC 2025]`,`Viva traps: calling a **button battery a coin**, giving **barium** for a foreign body, scoping a caustic burn **in week 2**, prescribing **loperamide** in colitis, and giving **neostigmine before excluding mechanical obstruction**. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs used in ward GI emergencies`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Neostigmine (Ogilvie syndrome)`,`2 mg IV slowly over 3-5 minutes, supine with cardiac monitoring`,`Single dose; may repeat once if no response or recurrence`,`Bradycardia, bronchospasm, salivation, cramps; contraindicated in mechanical obstruction, perforation, HR under 60, active asthma; keep atropine 0.6 mg ready`],[`Polyethylene glycol 3350`,`17 g orally once daily (maintenance after decompression)`,`Weeks, then review`,`Bloating; avoid until obstruction excluded`],[`Hydrocortisone / methylprednisolone (IBD toxic megacolon)`,`Hydrocortisone 100 mg IV 6-hourly or methylprednisolone 60 mg IV daily`,`Assess at day 3; colectomy if no response in 24-72 h`,`Masks peritonism, hyperglycaemia, infection; exclude C. difficile and CMV`],[`Vancomycin (fulminant C. difficile)`,`500 mg orally or by NG 6-hourly; rectal 500 mg in 100 mL saline 6-hourly if ileus`,`10-14 days`,`Minimal absorption orally; do not use IV vancomycin for C. difficile`],[`Metronidazole (fulminant C. difficile)`,`500 mg IV 8-hourly, added to oral vancomycin`,`10-14 days`,`Metallic taste, neuropathy with long use, disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol`],[`Pantoprazole (caustic ingestion)`,`40 mg IV twice daily, then oral 40 mg daily`,`Until mucosal healing (2-4 weeks)`,`Headache, hypomagnesaemia with long use`],[`Sucralfate (button battery, in hospital)`,`10 mL (1 g/10 mL) orally every 10 minutes, 3 doses, before endoscopy`,`Only before removal, within 12 h of ingestion`,`Do not give if perforation suspected or sepsis; do not delay endoscopy`],[`Glucagon (food bolus, adjunct)`,`1 mg IV`,`Single dose`,`Vomiting, hyperglycaemia; contraindicated in phaeochromocytoma and insulinoma; never delays endoscopy`]]},{heading:`Zargar endoscopic grading of caustic injury`,columns:[`Grade`,`Endoscopic finding`,`Outcome`,`Management`],rows:[[`0`,`Normal mucosa`,`Heals`,`Oral diet, discharge`],[`1`,`Oedema and hyperaemia`,`Heals without stricture`,`Oral liquids within 24-48 h`],[`2a`,`Friability, erosions, blisters, exudates, superficial ulcers`,`Stricture uncommon`,`Soft diet, PPI, short admission`],[`2b`,`2a plus deep discrete or circumferential ulcers`,`Stricture in about 70-100%`,`Endoscopic-guided nasojejunal feeding, dilatation from 3-6 weeks`],[`3a`,`Small scattered areas of necrosis`,`Stricture very likely`,`Admit, enteral feeding or jejunostomy, watch for perforation`],[`3b`,`Extensive necrosis`,`High mortality, perforation`,`Emergency oesophagogastrectomy if transmural necrosis on CT`]]},{heading:`ESGE 2016 timing of endoscopy for ingested foreign bodies`,columns:[`Situation`,`Timing`,`Reason`,`Note`],rows:[[`Complete oesophageal obstruction (cannot swallow saliva)`,`Emergent - within 2 h, at most 6 h`,`Aspiration risk`,`Food bolus is the commonest cause`],[`Button battery in the oesophagus`,`Emergent - within 2 h`,`Liquefactive necrosis within 2 h`,`Double-halo sign on AP X-ray`],[`Sharp-pointed object in the oesophagus`,`Emergent - within 2 h, at most 6 h`,`Perforation`,`Use overtube or protector hood`],[`Other oesophageal foreign body, incomplete obstruction`,`Urgent - within 24 h`,`Risk rises after 24 h`,`Coins in children often pass if distal`],[`Gastric: sharp objects, magnets, batteries, objects over 6 cm long or 2.5 cm wide`,`Urgent - within 24 h`,`Will not pass pylorus or will injure bowel`,`Multiple magnets never left to pass`],[`Gastric medium-sized blunt object`,`Non-urgent - within 72 h`,`May not pass`,`Small blunt objects can be observed`],[`Drug packets (body packing)`,`No endoscopy`,`Rupture can be fatal`,`Conservative; surgery for obstruction or rupture`]]},{heading:`Differentiating causes of acute colonic distension`,columns:[`Feature`,`Toxic megacolon`,`Acute colonic pseudo-obstruction`,`Mechanical large bowel obstruction`],rows:[[`Setting`,`Severe UC, C. difficile or infective colitis`,`Elderly post-operative or septic inpatient on opioids`,`Colorectal cancer, sigmoid volvulus, hernia`],[`Systemic toxicity`,`Present - fever, tachycardia, leucocytosis`,`Usually absent`,`Absent until strangulation or perforation`],[`Stool`,`Bloody diarrhoea that decreases`,`Some flatus or stool may pass`,`Absolute constipation`],[`Imaging`,`Transverse colon over 6 cm, mucosal islands, lost haustra`,`Diffuse colonic dilatation, no transition point`,`Transition point, coffee-bean sign in volvulus`],[`First treatment`,`Steroids or anti-C. difficile therapy, NBM, early surgical review`,`Conservative care, then neostigmine 2 mg IV`,`Drip and suck, detorsion or surgery`],[`Contraindicated`,`Antimotility drugs, colonoscopy`,`Neostigmine before excluding obstruction`,`Neostigmine, laxatives`]]},{heading:`Imaging clues and cut-offs`,columns:[`Finding`,`Cut-off or sign`,`Meaning`,`Action`],rows:[[`Small bowel diameter`,`Over 3 cm`,`Small bowel obstruction or ileus`,`CT to find transition point`],[`Colon diameter`,`Over 6 cm`,`Dilated colon; with toxicity, toxic megacolon`,`Serial X-ray 12-24 hourly`],[`Caecal diameter`,`Over 9 cm dilated; over 12 cm for over 6 days high perforation risk`,`Impending caecal perforation`,`Neostigmine or colonoscopic decompression`],[`Gastrografin challenge`,`Contrast in colon within 24 h`,`Adhesive SBO likely to resolve`,`Continue non-operative care`],[`CT strangulation signs`,`Reduced wall enhancement, mesenteric fluid, whirl, pneumatosis`,`Ischaemic bowel`,`Emergency laparotomy`],[`Caustic CT`,`Absent oesophageal wall enhancement, periesophageal fat blurring`,`Transmural necrosis`,`Emergency surgery`]]}],redFlags:[`Drooling and inability to swallow saliva after a meal or ingestion - complete oesophageal obstruction needing endoscopy within 6 hours`,`Any suspected button battery in the oesophagus or a double-halo on X-ray - endoscopy within 2 hours`,`Stridor, hoarseness or respiratory distress after caustic ingestion - secure airway`,`Neck crepitus, chest pain, fever or pneumomediastinum after an ingestion - oesophageal perforation`,`Colitis with colonic diameter over 6 cm and tachycardia or fever - toxic megacolon, surgical review now`,`Obstruction with constant pain, fever, tachycardia, peritonism or an irreducible hernia - strangulation`,`Caecal diameter over 12 cm or new tenderness in pseudo-obstruction - impending perforation`],pearls:[`**Battery = 2 hours**: electrolysis, not the swallowing, burns through the oesophagus.`,`**Honey for batteries** (10 mL every 10 minutes, up to 6 doses, child over 1 year, within 12 hours) - the one home first aid that helps.`,`**Alkali liquefies and goes deep in the oesophagus; acid coagulates and hits the antrum.**`,`**Scope the caustic burn at 12-48 hours, never at days 5-15.**`,`**Zargar 2b is the stricture threshold** - grades 0-2a heal, 2b onward stricture.`,`**Jalan: 6 cm plus 3 of fever, HR, WBC, anaemia plus 1 of dehydration, sensorium, electrolytes, BP.**`,`**Rule of 3-6-9** for small bowel, colon and caecum; caecum over 12 cm in Ogilvie means act.`,`**Neostigmine 2 mg IV with atropine at the bedside** - but only after CT excludes a mechanical block.`,`**Every adult food bolus deserves oesophageal biopsies** - EoE is the commonest hidden cause in young men.`],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-emergencies-ipd-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`A 24-year-old woman is brought 2 hours after deliberately drinking a toilet cleaner. Discuss the pathophysiology of corrosive injury, the immediate management, endoscopic grading and the long-term complications.`,openingLines:[`Corrosive (caustic) ingestion is tissue injury from a strong acid (pH under 2) or alkali (pH over 12); in India it is usually acid from toilet cleaners, often deliberate.`,`Outcome depends on the depth of necrosis, which is graded by Zargar endoscopy at 12-48 hours or by contrast CT, and decides feeding, surgery and follow-up for stricture and cancer.`],answer:[{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Acid: **coagulative necrosis** with eschar; injures the **stomach (antrum, pylorus)** more.`,`Alkali: **liquefactive necrosis**, deep penetration, **oesophagus**; perforation more likely.`,`Phases: necrosis (days 1-4), sloughing and granulation with **weakest wall at days 5-15**, scar contraction from week 3 forming strictures.`]},{heading:`Immediate management`,points:[`**Airway** - stridor or hoarseness needs early intubation; oxygen, IV access, fluids.`,`**Do not induce vomiting, neutralise, give charcoal or pass a blind NG tube**.`,`NBM, **IV pantoprazole 40 mg twice daily**, analgesia; no routine steroids or antibiotics.`,`Bloods including ABG, lactate; **chest X-ray and contrast CT chest-abdomen** for perforation and transmural necrosis.`]},{heading:`Endoscopic grading (Zargar) and management by grade`,points:[`0-1: oral liquids within 24-48 h, discharge.`,`2a: soft diet, PPI, short admission.`,`2b-3a: nasojejunal feeding under vision or feeding jejunostomy, watch for perforation.`,`3b or CT transmural necrosis: **emergency oesophagogastrectomy**.`]},{heading:`Long-term complications`,points:[`**Oesophageal stricture** (2b onward) - dilatation from 3-6 weeks.`,`**Gastric outlet obstruction** after acid at 4-8 weeks.`,`**Squamous cell carcinoma** risk 1000-3000-fold after 15-40 years - surveillance endoscopy.`,`Malnutrition, aspiration and psychological sequelae.`]},{heading:`Prevention and psychiatric care`,points:[`Psychiatric assessment before discharge; Tele-MANAS 14416.`,`Restriction on acid sale following Laxmi v Union of India 2013; child-safe storage.`]}],mustDraw:[`Zargar grading table with outcome and management`,`Flow chart: caustic ingestion from casualty to grade-based management`],markSplit:[{part:`Pathophysiology acid vs alkali`,marks:2},{part:`Immediate management with do-nots`,marks:3},{part:`Zargar grading and grade-wise plan`,marks:3},{part:`Long-term complications and prevention`,marks:2}],keywords:[`caustic ingestion`,`Zargar`,`corrosive`,`stricture`,`oesophagectomy`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-emergencies-ipd-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Differentiate toxic megacolon from acute colonic pseudo-obstruction (Ogilvie syndrome), including the role of neostigmine.`,openingLines:[`Toxic megacolon is non-obstructive colonic dilatation over 6 cm with systemic toxicity complicating colitis, whereas Ogilvie syndrome is massive colonic dilatation without mechanical cause or colitis in a sick inpatient.`,`The distinction matters because neostigmine helps pseudo-obstruction but has no role in toxic megacolon, which needs treatment of the colitis and early colectomy.`],answer:[{heading:`Cause and setting`,points:[`Toxic megacolon: colitis - UC, C. difficile, infective or CMV colitis; precipitated by opioids, loperamide, hypokalaemia.`,`Ogilvie: autonomic imbalance in elderly post-operative, septic or bedbound inpatients on opioids or anticholinergics.`]},{heading:`Clinical picture`,points:[`Toxic megacolon: Jalan criteria - fever, tachycardia, leucocytosis, anaemia plus dehydration, sensorium, electrolytes or hypotension.`,`Ogilvie: non-toxic, painless progressive distension, often still passing flatus.`]},{heading:`Imaging`,points:[`Toxic megacolon: transverse colon over 6 cm, loss of haustra, mucosal islands.`,`Ogilvie: diffuse colonic dilatation, caecum often over 10 cm, no transition point on CT.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Toxic megacolon: NBM, stop antimotility drugs, IV steroids or anti-C. difficile therapy, colectomy if no response in 24-72 h.`,`Ogilvie: conservative 24-72 h, then neostigmine, then colonoscopic decompression, PEG maintenance.`]},{heading:`Neostigmine in Ogilvie syndrome`,points:[`Exclude obstruction by CT first; **2 mg IV over 3-5 minutes**, supine, cardiac monitor, atropine ready.`,`Response in about 80-90% within 30 minutes.`,`Contraindicated with obstruction, perforation, bradycardia, bronchospasm.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column comparison table`],markSplit:[{part:`Comparison table`,marks:3},{part:`Neostigmine protocol and contraindications`,marks:2}],keywords:[`toxic megacolon`,`Ogilvie`,`neostigmine`,`Jalan`]}],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-emergencies-ipd-q1`,stem:`A 3-year-old child is brought 1 hour after swallowing a round metallic object. AP chest X-ray shows a circular radio-opaque object at the level of the aortic arch with a double-ring contour. What is the most appropriate next step?`,options:[`Observe and repeat the X-ray after 24 hours`,`Barium swallow to confirm the level`,`Emergency endoscopic removal within 2 hours`,`Give a laxative and check stools`],answer:2,explanation:`A double-ring (halo) contour identifies a button battery, which causes liquefactive necrosis within 2 hours by generating hydroxide; ESGE 2016 and ESPGHAN 2021 require emergent removal. Observation suits a distal coin, not a battery, a barium swallow risks aspiration and impairs endoscopy, and laxatives are irrelevant to an oesophageal object.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-emergencies-ipd-q2`,stem:`Endoscopy 24 hours after acid ingestion shows deep circumferential ulceration of the oesophagus without necrosis. What is the Zargar grade and its main implication?`,options:[`Grade 1, which heals without stricture`,`Grade 2b, with a high risk of stricture`,`Grade 3b, which needs emergency oesophagectomy`,`Grade 2a, which allows immediate discharge`],answer:1,explanation:`Deep discrete or circumferential ulcers without necrosis are Zargar 2b, the threshold at which stricture develops in most patients, so enteral feeding under vision and later dilatation are planned. Grade 1 is only oedema and hyperaemia, 2a has superficial ulcers and erosions, and 3b means extensive necrosis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-emergencies-ipd-q3`,stem:`A 72-year-old man 4 days after hip replacement has painless progressive abdominal distension. CT shows diffuse colonic dilatation with a caecum of 11 cm and no transition point. After 48 hours of conservative care he is unchanged. Heart rate is 84/min. What is the best next step?`,options:[`Neostigmine 2 mg IV over 3-5 minutes with cardiac monitoring`,`Emergency right hemicolectomy`,`Loperamide to reduce colonic secretions`,`Barium enema to decompress the colon`],answer:0,explanation:`This is acute colonic pseudo-obstruction with mechanical obstruction excluded and failure of conservative care, so ASGE 2020 recommends neostigmine 2 mg IV with monitoring and atropine ready, which decompresses about 80-90%. Surgery is reserved for perforation or ischaemia, loperamide worsens dilatation, and barium is contraindicated in a dilated colon.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-emergencies-ipd-q4`,stem:`Which set of findings fulfils the Jalan criteria for toxic megacolon in a patient with ulcerative colitis?`,options:[`Colon 5 cm, fever 39 C, heart rate 130, leucocytes 15,000/microlitre`,`Colon 7 cm, normal temperature, heart rate 90, normal blood count, hypotension`,`Colon 8 cm with bloody diarrhoea 10 times a day and no systemic features`,`Colon 7 cm, fever 39 C, heart rate 125, anaemia, and hypokalaemia`],answer:3,explanation:`Jalan criteria need colonic dilatation over 6 cm plus three of fever over 38.6 C, heart rate over 120, leucocytosis or anaemia, plus one of dehydration, altered sensorium, electrolyte disturbance or hypotension - the 7 cm colon with fever, tachycardia, anaemia and hypokalaemia fits. A 5 cm colon is not dilated enough, and dilatation without three toxicity features or without the systemic criterion does not qualify.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-emergencies-ipd-q5`,stem:`A 35-year-old man has a meat bolus impaction that is pushed into the stomach endoscopically. He reports two earlier episodes of food sticking and has asthma. What should be done at the same endoscopy?`,options:[`Start a 6-week course of oral prednisolone`,`Take multiple oesophageal biopsies from proximal and distal oesophagus`,`Dilate the oesophagus to 20 mm immediately`,`No further action, as the bolus has cleared`],answer:1,explanation:`Recurrent food bolus in a young atopic man strongly suggests eosinophilic oesophagitis, and ESGE 2016 advises biopsies at the index endoscopy to make the diagnosis. Systemic steroids are not first-line for EoE, aggressive dilatation of an acutely injured oesophagus risks perforation, and doing nothing misses the underlying disease that causes recurrence.`,difficulty:`easy`}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`Birk M et al. Removal of foreign bodies in the upper gastrointestinal tract in adults: European Society of Gastrointestinal Endoscopy (ESGE) Clinical Guideline, 2016`,`Mubarak A et al. Diagnosis, management and prevention of button battery ingestion in childhood: ESPGHAN position paper, 2021`,`Chirica M et al. Esophageal emergencies: WSES guidelines (caustic ingestion, foreign bodies, perforation), World Journal of Emergency Surgery, 2019`,`ASGE guideline on the management of acute colonic pseudo-obstruction and colonic volvulus, Gastrointestinal Endoscopy, 2020`,`Ten Broek RPG et al. Bologna guidelines for diagnosis and management of adhesive small bowel obstruction (WSES), 2017; WSES sigmoid volvulus guidelines, 2023`,`Johnson S et al. IDSA/SHEA 2021 focused update guidelines on management of Clostridioides difficile infection`,`ECCO guidelines on therapeutics in ulcerative colitis, 2022; ACG clinical guideline: ulcerative colitis in adults, 2025`]}),d.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-parasitic-gi`,title:`Intestinal parasites and hepatobiliary worms`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`Intestinal parasites are **soil-transmitted helminths (Ascaris, hookworm, Trichuris, Strongyloides), tapeworms, protozoa such as Giardia and hepatobiliary worms (hydatid, liver flukes)**; India carries the **world's largest soil-transmitted helminth burden**, controlled through **National Deworming Day with albendazole 400 mg twice a year**, while the clinician must recognise **Strongyloides hyperinfection before steroids** and stage **hydatid cysts by the WHO-IWGE ultrasound classification** to choose albendazole, **PAIR** or surgery.`,keywords:[`soil-transmitted helminths`,`STH`,`ascariasis`,`biliary ascariasis`,`hookworm`,`Ancylostoma duodenale`,`Necator americanus`,`Strongyloides hyperinfection`,`Trichuris`,`Enterobius`,`Taenia solium`,`Hymenolepis nana`,`giardiasis`,`hydatid cyst`,`Echinococcus granulosus`,`PAIR`,`Clonorchis`,`albendazole`,`ivermectin`,`praziquantel`,`National Deworming Day`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Soil-transmitted helminths (STH)** - Ascaris lumbricoides, Trichuris trichiura and the hookworms - infect about **1.5 billion people (24% of the world)**, mostly children in South Asia and sub-Saharan Africa. [WHO 2023]`,`**India has the largest number of children needing preventive chemotherapy for STH** of any country; state prevalence surveys show wide variation, highest in humid states with poor sanitation. [MoHFW NDD 2024]`,`**National Deworming Day (NDD)**, launched in **2015**, gives **albendazole** to all children and adolescents aged **1-19 years** through schools and anganwadis on **10 February and 10 August**, with a mop-up day for those missed. [MoHFW NDD 2024]`,`**Strongyloides stercoralis** infects an estimated **300-600 million** people and is under-diagnosed because stool microscopy is insensitive; it is common in rural India, the North-East and among farm workers. [WHO 2023]`,`**Giardia lamblia (intestinalis)** is the commonest intestinal protozoan worldwide and a frequent cause of persistent diarrhoea in Indian children and travellers. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cystic echinococcosis (hydatid disease)** is endemic in sheep- and cattle-rearing regions - Andhra Pradesh, Telangana, Tamil Nadu, Karnataka, Jammu and Kashmir, Himachal - with the **liver involved in about 70%** and lung in 20%. [WHO 2024]`,`**Liver flukes** (Clonorchis sinensis, Opisthorchis viverrini) are endemic in East and South-East Asia and are seen in India mainly in migrants; the intestinal fluke **Fasciolopsis buski** occurs in Assam, Bihar and eastern India. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Taenia solium** taeniasis matters in India mainly because its eggs cause **neurocysticercosis**, the commonest cause of acquired epilepsy in many Indian districts. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Aetiology, transmission and risk factors`,points:[`**Faeco-oral egg ingestion**: Ascaris and Trichuris (embryonated eggs in soil, vegetables), Enterobius (eggs under fingernails, autoinfection), Taenia solium eggs (cysticercosis), Echinococcus eggs from **dog faeces**, Giardia cysts in water. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Skin penetration by filariform larvae** from faecally contaminated soil: hookworm and Strongyloides - barefoot walking and **open defecation** are the key risks. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Eating undercooked meat or fish**: **Taenia solium (pork)**, **Taenia saginata (beef)**, Diphyllobothrium (freshwater fish), **Clonorchis and Opisthorchis (raw freshwater fish)**, Fasciolopsis (water caltrop and other aquatic plants). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hydatid life cycle**: dog is the **definitive host** (adult worm), sheep and cattle are **intermediate hosts**; humans are accidental dead-end intermediate hosts after ingesting eggs from dog faeces. [WHO 2024]`,`**Strongyloides autoinfection** lets the worm persist for **decades** in an untreated host; corticosteroids, **HTLV-1 infection**, transplant, haematological malignancy and alcoholism trigger **hyperinfection**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Giardia risk rises with **IgA deficiency, common variable immunodeficiency**, achlorhydria, day-care settings and unboiled water; only **10-100 cysts** are needed for infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hymenolepis nana (dwarf tapeworm)** completes its cycle in one host and spreads person to person, so it is common in **children in institutions** and causes heavy autoinfection. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - the why behind the signs`,points:[`Ascaris larvae hatch in the gut, penetrate the wall and migrate through **liver and lungs** before being swallowed; the lung passage at **9-12 days** gives **Loeffler syndrome** (cough, wheeze, eosinophilic infiltrates). [Harrison 22e]`,`Adult Ascaris (**15-35 cm**) migrate when irritated (fever, anaesthesia, partial treatment) into the **ampulla, bile duct and pancreatic duct**, causing biliary colic, cholangitis and pancreatitis; a worm bolus obstructs the **terminal ileum** in children. [Harrison 22e]`,`Hookworms attach to the **jejunal mucosa** with cutting plates or teeth and secrete anticoagulants; each **Ancylostoma** causes about **0.15-0.2 mL**, each **Necator** about **0.03 mL** of blood loss a day, giving **iron-deficiency anaemia and hypoalbuminaemia**. [Harrison 22e]`,`In **Strongyloides hyperinfection**, steroids increase larval maturation; massive numbers of larvae penetrate the gut and carry **enteric Gram-negative bacteria**, causing **E. coli or Klebsiella sepsis and meningitis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Trichuris** threads its head into the caecal and rectal mucosa; heavy loads cause **dysentery, rectal prolapse, growth retardation and anaemia** (Trichuris dysentery syndrome). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Giardia trophozoites attach by a ventral sucking disc** to duodenal and jejunal epithelium, causing villous blunting, disaccharidase loss and **malabsorption with steatorrhoea and secondary lactose intolerance** without invasion or blood. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hydatid cyst** has three layers - outer host **pericyst** (fibrous), parasite **laminated ectocyst**, and the germinal **endocyst** that produces brood capsules, protoscolices and **daughter cysts**; rupture releases antigen causing **anaphylaxis** and seeding. [WHO 2024]`,`**Liver flukes** live in the bile ducts causing chronic inflammation, periductal fibrosis, **pigment stones, recurrent pyogenic cholangitis** and **cholangiocarcinoma** - Clonorchis and Opisthorchis are **IARC group 1 carcinogens**. [IARC 2012]`,`**Diphyllobothrium latum** competes for **vitamin B12** in the jejunum and can cause megaloblastic anaemia. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and staging examiners ask`,points:[`Helminths are classified as **nematodes (roundworms)**: Ascaris, hookworm, Trichuris, Strongyloides, Enterobius; **cestodes (tapeworms)**: Taenia, Hymenolepis, Diphyllobothrium, Echinococcus; and **trematodes (flukes)**: Clonorchis, Opisthorchis, Fasciola, Fasciolopsis, Schistosoma. [Harrison 22e]`,`**WHO intensity of STH infection** by Kato-Katz eggs per gram (epg): **heavy Ascaris 50,000 epg or more, heavy Trichuris 10,000 or more, heavy hookworm 4,000 or more**; elimination as a public-health problem means **under 2% moderate-to-heavy** infections. [WHO 2030 roadmap]`,`**WHO preventive chemotherapy thresholds**: STH prevalence **20% or more** in children - annual mass deworming; **50% or more** - twice a year (India chose biannual nationally). [WHO 2017]`,`**WHO-IWGE ultrasound classification of hydatid cyst**: **CL** undifferentiated cyst; **CE1** unilocular with **double-line sign** (active); **CE2** multivesicular with daughter cysts, honeycomb (active); **CE3a** detached endocyst, **water-lily sign**; **CE3b** daughter cysts in a solid matrix (transitional); **CE4** heterogeneous solid, **ball of wool** (inactive); **CE5** calcified (inactive). [WHO-IWGE 2010]`,`Hydatid stage-specific treatment: **CE1 and CE3a under 5 cm**: albendazole alone; **CE1 and CE3a 5-10 cm**: **PAIR plus albendazole**; **CE2 and CE3b**: surgery or non-PAIR percutaneous techniques plus albendazole; **CE4 and CE5**: **watch and wait**. [WHO-IWGE 2010]`,`Strongyloides spectrum: **asymptomatic chronic infection**, **hyperinfection** (accelerated autoinfection confined to gut and lungs) and **disseminated strongyloidiasis** (larvae in other organs - brain, liver, kidney); the last two carry **mortality up to 60-85%**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Most STH infections are asymptomatic**; the burden is chronic - **iron-deficiency anaemia, stunting, poor school performance** and vitamin A deficiency in children. [WHO 2023]`,`Ascaris: vague abdominal pain, **passage of a worm per rectum or vomiting of a worm**, and in heavy infection colicky pain and distension of **worm bolus obstruction**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Biliary ascariasis**: recurrent **biliary colic, jaundice, fever or pancreatitis** in a young adult or post-cholecystectomy patient; very common in Kashmir valley series. [Harrison 22e]`,`Hookworm: **ground itch** at entry site, then fatigue, breathlessness, **pica (geophagia)** and pedal oedema from anaemia and protein loss. [Harrison 22e]`,`Strongyloides chronic: intermittent diarrhoea, epigastric pain, urticaria and **larva currens** - a fast-moving (up to 10 cm/hour) serpiginous rash over buttocks and trunk; hyperinfection presents with **fever, ileus, GI bleeding, cough and Gram-negative sepsis** in a steroid-treated patient. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Enterobius**: **nocturnal perianal itch**, disturbed sleep, vulvovaginitis in girls; the whole household is often affected. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Giardia**: **foul-smelling, greasy, non-bloody diarrhoea**, bloating, sulphurous belching, weight loss and post-giardial lactose intolerance lasting weeks. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hydatid liver cyst**: often an **incidental ultrasound finding**; large cysts cause RUQ heaviness or a mass; complications present as **jaundice or cholangitis (intrabiliary rupture), fever (infection) or anaphylaxis (rupture)**. [WHO 2024]`,`Liver flukes: usually silent; heavy chronic infection gives **recurrent cholangitis, hepatomegaly and obstructive jaundice** - ask about raw fish eating and travel. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Pallor, koilonychia, glossitis, pedal oedema** - chronic hookworm anaemia; in children also **stunting, wasting and pot belly**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rectal prolapse with worms seen on the mucosa** in a malnourished child - heavy Trichuris infection; finger clubbing may accompany it. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Larva currens** on buttocks, thighs or abdomen is pathognomonic of Strongyloides; a **purpuric periumbilical (thumbprint) rash** indicates dissemination. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Smooth, non-tender, cystic hepatomegaly** in a dog owner from an endemic district - hydatid cyst; hydatid thrill is a classic but rarely elicited sign. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Doughy mass in the right iliac fossa or a palpable mass of worms** in a distended child - Ascaris bolus obstruction. [Harrison 22e]`,`Perianal excoriation from scratching - Enterobius; examine at night or early morning for visible white threadworms. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis and the eosinophilia clue`,points:[`**Eosinophilia** points to **tissue-invasive helminths** (larval migration of Ascaris, hookworm, Strongyloides, Toxocara, Fasciola, schistosomes, filariae); **Giardia, Enterobius and adult luminal worms cause little or none**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Eosinophilia with cough and wheeze in India** - consider **tropical pulmonary eosinophilia (filarial)**, Loeffler syndrome and allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis; TPE has eosinophils usually over 3,000/microlitre and responds to DEC. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Giardiasis vs coeliac disease and tropical sprue**: all cause malabsorption with villous blunting; Giardia antigen or PCR positive and cure with nitroimidazole separate it - see the Malabsorption topic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hydatid cyst vs simple hepatic cyst**: simple cysts have a thin imperceptible wall, no double line, no daughter cysts and no serology; CE1 can look identical, so **serology and a follow-up scan** are needed. [WHO-IWGE 2010]`,`**Hydatid vs amoebic or pyogenic abscess**: abscess has fever, toxicity, tenderness and an irregular wall with debris; hydatid is usually afebrile and well defined - see the Liver abscess topic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Biliary ascariasis vs choledocholithiasis**: ultrasound shows **a long echogenic tubular structure with a central sonolucent line (strip or tube sign)**, sometimes moving. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Strongyloides hyperinfection vs Gram-negative sepsis of other cause**: think of it in any steroid-treated patient from an endemic area with unexplained enteric sepsis or meningitis - sputum shows larvae. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line and advanced`,points:[`**Stool microscopy (saline and iodine wet mount)** on **3 samples on different days** with a **formol-ether concentration** detects eggs, cysts and trophozoites; Kato-Katz gives egg counts for intensity. [WHO 2023]`,`**CBC with absolute eosinophil count**, peripheral smear (microcytic hypochromic anaemia) and **serum ferritin**; **albumin** in heavy hookworm. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Strongyloides**: single stool microscopy misses up to **70%**; use **serology (IgG ELISA)**, **agar plate culture** or Baermann concentration, or PCR; in hyperinfection, **larvae appear in stool, sputum and BAL**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Giardia stool antigen (ELISA or rapid test) or multiplex PCR** is more sensitive than microscopy; duodenal aspirate or biopsy only when stool tests are negative and suspicion stays high. [IDSA 2017]`,`**Enterobius**: **adhesive (Scotch) tape test** on the perianal skin in the early morning before bathing, repeated on 3 mornings; stool examination is usually negative. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hydatid**: **ultrasound is the cornerstone** and stages the cyst (WHO-IWGE); **CT or MRI** for deep, complicated or extrahepatic cysts and before surgery; **MRCP** for biliary communication. [WHO-IWGE 2010]`,`**Hydatid serology (IgG ELISA, confirmed by immunoblot)** supports diagnosis; it is **less sensitive for lung, CE1, CE4-5 and calcified cysts**, so a negative test does not exclude it. [WHO-IWGE 2010]`,`**Diagnostic aspiration of a suspected hydatid cyst is avoided** outside a PAIR set-up with albendazole cover, because of anaphylaxis and spillage. [WHO-IWGE 2010]`,`**Liver flukes**: eggs in stool or bile; ultrasound or MRCP shows **dilated intrahepatic ducts, periductal thickening**, and flukes as non-shadowing echogenic foci. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - drugs and doses`,points:[`**Ascaris and hookworm**: **albendazole 400 mg orally single dose** (200 mg for children 12-24 months); alternatives **mebendazole 500 mg single dose** or 100 mg twice daily for 3 days, or **ivermectin 150-200 microgram/kg** single dose (Ascaris). [WHO 2017]`,`**Single-dose albendazole cures over 90% of Ascaris but less hookworm and only about 30% of Trichuris**; Trichuris needs **albendazole 400 mg daily for 3 days** or albendazole plus ivermectin. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Enterobius**: **albendazole 400 mg single dose, repeated after 2 weeks**, treating **all household members**; trim nails, wash bedding in hot water, and handwashing to stop autoinfection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Uncomplicated Strongyloides**: **ivermectin 200 microgram/kg orally once daily for 2 days** (cure over 80%); albendazole 400 mg twice daily for 7 days is less effective. [CDC 2024]`,`**Taenia saginata or T. solium intestinal infection**: **praziquantel 5-10 mg/kg single dose** or **niclosamide 2 g chewed single dose**; niclosamide is preferred for T. solium if neurocysticercosis has not been excluded, because praziquantel can provoke seizures from occult cysts. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hymenolepis nana**: **praziquantel 25 mg/kg single dose**, repeated after 10 days because of autoinfection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Giardia**: **tinidazole 2 g single dose** (children 50 mg/kg, max 2 g), or **metronidazole 250-500 mg three times daily for 5-7 days**, or **nitazoxanide 500 mg twice daily for 3 days** (children 1-3 years 100 mg, 4-11 years 200 mg twice daily). [IDSA 2017]`,`**Clonorchis and Opisthorchis**: **praziquantel 25 mg/kg three times daily for 2 days**; albendazole 10 mg/kg/day for 7 days is an alternative. **Fasciola hepatica**: **triclabendazole 10 mg/kg once or twice 12 hours apart** - it does not respond to praziquantel. [CDC 2024]`,`**Iron supplementation** (elemental iron 2-3 mg/kg/day in children, 60-120 mg/day in adults for 3 months after normal haemoglobin) and nutrition counselling accompany deworming in anaemic patients. [Anemia Mukt Bharat 2018]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD - complications`,points:[`**Ascaris intestinal obstruction**: NBM, NG decompression, IV fluids; **avoid anthelmintic until colic settles** in partial obstruction (worm bolus can tighten); **hypertonic saline or Gastrografin enema** and surgery (milking or enterotomy) for failure or ischaemia. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Biliary ascariasis**: conservative (NBM, analgesia, antispasmodic, IV fluids, antibiotics if cholangitis) - most worms return to the duodenum in days; give **albendazole** after the worm leaves the duct; **ERCP extraction** if persisting beyond 3-7 days, cholangitis or pancreatitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Strongyloides hyperinfection**: **ivermectin 200 microgram/kg daily until stool and sputum are negative for 2 weeks**, plus broad-spectrum antibiotics for enteric Gram-negatives; **reduce or stop immunosuppression**; subcutaneous (veterinary) ivermectin is used in ileus where oral absorption fails. [CDC 2024]`,`**PAIR for hydatid**: **P**uncture under ultrasound, **A**spiration of fluid, **I**njection of scolicidal **hypertonic (20%) saline or 95% ethanol**, **R**e-aspiration after **15-20 minutes**; check aspirate bilirubin first - bile means biliary communication and contraindicates scolicide. [WHO-IWGE 2010]`,`**Albendazole cover for PAIR or surgery**: **10-15 mg/kg/day in two divided doses (usually 400 mg twice daily) with a fatty meal**, starting **1 day to 1 week before** and continuing **1 month after PAIR and 1-3 months after surgery**. [WHO-IWGE 2010]`,`**PAIR is contraindicated** in CE2, CE3b, CE4, CE5, lung cysts, inaccessible cysts and cysts communicating with the biliary tree. [WHO-IWGE 2010]`,`**Surgery (open or laparoscopic)** - pericystectomy or partial cystectomy with **scolicidal packing** - for complicated cysts (rupture, biliary fistula, infection, compression), large CE2/CE3b cysts, and superficial cysts at risk of rupture; keep **adrenaline ready** for anaphylaxis. [WHO-IWGE 2010]`,`**Intrabiliary hydatid rupture** with cholangitis needs **ERCP with sphincterotomy and clearance of membranes**, followed by definitive treatment of the cyst. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: avoid albendazole and mebendazole in the **first trimester**; **Anemia Mukt Bharat gives albendazole 400 mg single dose in the second trimester**; ivermectin is avoided; for Giardia use **paromomycin** or defer nitroimidazoles to after the first trimester. [Anemia Mukt Bharat 2018]`,`**Children under 1 year** are not dewormed under NDD; **12-24 months get albendazole 200 mg (half tablet)**, older children 400 mg chewed under supervision; sick children are deferred to mop-up day. [MoHFW NDD 2024]`,`**Before high-dose steroids or immunosuppression** (transplant, chemotherapy, rituximab, COVID dexamethasone), patients from endemic areas should be **screened by serology or presumptively given ivermectin** to prevent hyperinfection. [CDC 2024]`,`**HTLV-1 co-infection** blunts Th2 responses and predisposes to recurrent Strongyloides hyperinfection; treat longer and follow closely. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypogammaglobulinaemia or IgA deficiency** with recurrent giardiasis needs immunology work-up and sometimes prolonged therapy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Albendazole in hydatid patients** needs **LFT and CBC every 2 weeks in the first month, then monthly**, because of hepatotoxicity, neutropenia and alopecia; avoid in pregnancy and bone marrow suppression. [WHO-IWGE 2010]`,`**Elderly patients** with chronic Strongyloides may present only with unexplained eosinophilia, recurrent Gram-negative bacteraemia or pruritus. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Ascaris: **intestinal obstruction, volvulus, intussusception, appendicitis, biliary colic, cholangitis, pancreatitis, liver abscess** and aspiration of worms during anaesthesia. [Harrison 22e]`,`Hookworm: **chronic iron-deficiency anaemia, heart failure, hypoproteinaemia**, impaired cognition and adverse pregnancy outcomes. [WHO 2023]`,`Strongyloides hyperinfection or dissemination: **mortality 60-85%**, largely from Gram-negative sepsis, meningitis and respiratory failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`Taenia solium: **neurocysticercosis** with seizures, hydrocephalus; see the neurology topic for its treatment. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`Hydatid: **rupture (anaphylaxis, secondary echinococcosis), intrabiliary rupture with cholangitis, infection, portal hypertension and Budd-Chiari** from compression; recurrence after surgery is about **2-25%** depending on technique. [WHO 2024]`,`Liver flukes: **recurrent pyogenic cholangitis, intrahepatic stones and cholangiocarcinoma**. [IARC 2012]`,`**Post-giardial lactose intolerance and irritable bowel symptoms** can persist for months after cure. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Refer urgently: **worm bolus obstruction, biliary ascariasis with cholangitis or pancreatitis, suspected Strongyloides hyperinfection, complicated or symptomatic hydatid cyst**. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`Hydatid: **ultrasound every 3-6 months in the first year then yearly for at least 5 years** after treatment to detect reactivation (cyst moving back from CE3a/CE4 to an active stage). [WHO-IWGE 2010]`,`**Check haemoglobin 4-8 weeks** after deworming plus iron to confirm a rise of about 1 g/dL. [Anemia Mukt Bharat 2018]`,`**Test of cure** for Strongyloides: repeat serology at 6-12 months (titre falls) or stool culture; for Giardia only if symptoms persist. [CDC 2024]`,`**Persistent diarrhoea after Giardia treatment**: consider resistance, reinfection, lactose intolerance or another cause before repeating a different drug class. [IDSA 2017]`,`Refer to a hepatobiliary surgeon for **CE2 and CE3b cysts, cysts over 10 cm, or any biliary communication**. [WHO-IWGE 2010]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and national programme link`,points:[`**National Deworming Day (2015)**: albendazole 400 mg (200 mg for 1-2 years) to all aged **1-19 years twice a year**, with ASHA mobilisation and adverse event reporting (nausea, abdominal pain mostly in heavily infected children). [MoHFW NDD 2024]`,`**Anemia Mukt Bharat (2018)** adds **biannual deworming for women of reproductive age** and second-trimester deworming in pregnancy, alongside IFA supplementation. [Anemia Mukt Bharat 2018]`,`**Swachh Bharat Mission** (toilets, ending open defecation) and **Jal Jeevan Mission** (safe piped water) address the root cause - deworming alone is followed by reinfection within months. [WHO 2030 roadmap]`,`Counsel **handwashing with soap, wearing footwear, washing vegetables, boiling or filtering water, and cooking pork, beef and fish thoroughly**. [WHO 2023]`,`**Hydatid prevention**: deworm dogs with praziquantel, prevent dogs eating offal at slaughter, and wash hands after handling dogs. [WHO 2024]`,`**School health under RBSK** screens for anaemia and growth faltering, and is the natural platform for deworming and WASH education. [MoHFW RBSK]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**WHO NTD road map 2021-2030** targets elimination of STH as a public-health problem (under 2% moderate-to-heavy infections) in 96 countries by 2030, with India among the largest contributors. [WHO 2030 roadmap]`,`**NDD rounds continue twice yearly (2023-2025)**; the programme now stresses integration with WASH and Anemia Mukt Bharat. [MoHFW NDD 2024]`,`**Co-administration and fixed-dose albendazole-ivermectin** (ALIVE trial, 2025) improves cure of Trichuris and hookworm over albendazole alone and is being considered for mass drug administration. [ALIVE 2025]`,`**Moxidectin**, a long-acting macrocyclic lactone, has shown efficacy in Strongyloides trials and is an emerging alternative to ivermectin. [Harrison 22e]`,`CDC and expert guidance since the COVID era recommend **empirical ivermectin before high-dose steroids** in people with epidemiological risk, now extended to biologic and transplant protocols. [CDC 2024]`,`The **WHO-IWGE 2010 expert consensus** remains the reference for stage-based hydatid treatment; recent reviews (2023-2025) confirm PAIR for CE1 and CE3a and watch-and-wait for CE4-5. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic short notes: **hydatid cyst of liver (WHO classification, PAIR)**, **Strongyloides hyperinfection**, **National Deworming Day**, **biliary ascariasis**, **giardiasis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: writing **albendazole single dose for Trichuris** - it needs 3 days; and **single dose for Enterobius** without the 2-week repeat and family treatment. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: giving **praziquantel for T. solium taeniasis without excluding neurocysticercosis** - niclosamide is safer. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: **aspirating a liver cyst for diagnosis** without albendazole cover - risks anaphylaxis and seeding. [WHO-IWGE 2010]`,`Viva: 'Which worm can complete its life cycle in man?' - **Strongyloides (autoinfection)**, also Enterobius and Hymenolepis nana. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: 'Which worm causes B12 deficiency?' - **Diphyllobothrium latum**; 'Which is a group 1 carcinogen?' - **Clonorchis and Opisthorchis**. [IARC 2012]`,`Viva: 'Why give albendazole with a fatty meal for hydatid but on an empty stomach for gut worms?' - fat increases absorption for tissue action, whereas luminal worms need gut concentration. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Anti-parasitic drugs: doses and cautions`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Albendazole (Ascaris, hookworm, NDD)`,`400 mg orally (200 mg for 12-24 months)`,`Single dose; Trichuris 3 days; Enterobius repeat at 2 weeks`,`Abdominal pain, nausea; avoid first trimester`],[`Albendazole (hydatid)`,`10-15 mg/kg/day in 2 doses (400 mg twice daily) with fatty meal`,`1 day-1 week before and 1 month after PAIR; 1-3 months after surgery; months if medical alone`,`Hepatotoxicity, neutropenia, alopecia; LFT and CBC 2-weekly then monthly; not in pregnancy`],[`Mebendazole`,`500 mg single dose or 100 mg twice daily`,`Single dose or 3 days`,`Poorly absorbed; avoid first trimester`],[`Ivermectin (Strongyloides)`,`200 microgram/kg orally`,`Once daily for 2 days; hyperinfection until stool and sputum negative 2 weeks`,`Dizziness, pruritus; avoid in pregnancy and children under 15 kg; Loa loa encephalopathy risk`],[`Praziquantel`,`Taenia 5-10 mg/kg single; H. nana 25 mg/kg single; Clonorchis 25 mg/kg three times daily`,`Single dose (H. nana repeat at 10 days); flukes 2 days`,`Dizziness, abdominal pain; can provoke seizures in occult neurocysticercosis; contraindicated in ocular cysticercosis`],[`Niclosamide`,`2 g chewed, single dose (children 50 mg/kg)`,`Single dose`,`Not absorbed; preferred for T. solium taeniasis`],[`Tinidazole (Giardia)`,`2 g orally single dose (child 50 mg/kg, max 2 g)`,`Single dose`,`Metallic taste, disulfiram-like reaction; avoid first trimester`],[`Metronidazole (Giardia)`,`250-500 mg orally three times daily (child 5 mg/kg three times daily)`,`5-7 days`,`Metallic taste, nausea, alcohol intolerance`],[`Nitazoxanide (Giardia)`,`500 mg twice daily (children 1-3 years 100 mg, 4-11 years 200 mg)`,`3 days`,`Abdominal pain, yellow urine`],[`Triclabendazole (Fasciola)`,`10 mg/kg orally`,`Once, or twice 12 h apart`,`Biliary colic as flukes die`]]},{heading:`WHO-IWGE ultrasound classification of hydatid cyst and treatment`,columns:[`Stage`,`Ultrasound appearance`,`Activity`,`Preferred treatment`],rows:[[`CL`,`Unilocular anechoic cyst without visible wall`,`Undifferentiated`,`Serology and follow-up scan`],[`CE1`,`Unilocular cyst with double-line sign, hydatid sand`,`Active`,`Under 5 cm albendazole; 5-10 cm PAIR plus albendazole`],[`CE2`,`Multivesicular, honeycomb, daughter cysts`,`Active`,`Surgery or modified catheterisation plus albendazole`],[`CE3a`,`Detached endocyst - water-lily sign`,`Transitional`,`As CE1 (albendazole or PAIR)`],[`CE3b`,`Daughter cysts within a solid matrix`,`Transitional`,`Surgery or non-PAIR percutaneous plus albendazole`],[`CE4`,`Heterogeneous solid, ball-of-wool`,`Inactive`,`Watch and wait`],[`CE5`,`Thick calcified wall`,`Inactive`,`Watch and wait`]]},{heading:`Key intestinal parasites at a glance`,columns:[`Parasite`,`Transmission`,`Key clinical clue`,`Diagnosis`],rows:[[`Ascaris lumbricoides`,`Eggs in soil, food`,`Worm passed, bolus obstruction, biliary colic, Loeffler`,`Stool eggs; worm on ultrasound in CBD`],[`Hookworm`,`Larvae penetrate skin`,`Ground itch, iron-deficiency anaemia, pica`,`Stool eggs; low ferritin`],[`Trichuris trichiura`,`Eggs in soil`,`Rectal prolapse, dysentery in child`,`Barrel-shaped eggs`],[`Strongyloides stercoralis`,`Larvae penetrate skin; autoinfection`,`Larva currens; sepsis on steroids`,`Serology, agar plate culture; larvae in sputum`],[`Enterobius vermicularis`,`Eggs from fingers, autoinfection`,`Nocturnal perianal itch`,`Scotch tape test`],[`Taenia solium / saginata`,`Undercooked pork / beef`,`Proglottids in stool; NCC (solium)`,`Stool eggs, proglottid morphology`],[`Giardia lamblia`,`Cysts in water`,`Greasy non-bloody diarrhoea, bloating`,`Stool antigen or PCR`],[`Clonorchis / Opisthorchis`,`Raw freshwater fish`,`Cholangitis, cholangiocarcinoma`,`Stool eggs; dilated ducts on MRCP`]]},{heading:`Differentiating a cystic liver lesion`,columns:[`Feature`,`Hydatid cyst`,`Simple cyst`,`Amoebic abscess`],rows:[[`Exposure`,`Dogs, sheep, endemic district`,`None`,`Recent dysentery, alcohol`],[`Symptoms`,`Often none; mass, jaundice if rupture`,`None`,`Fever, RUQ pain, tenderness`],[`Ultrasound`,`Double line, daughter cysts, water lily`,`Thin wall, anechoic, no septa`,`Hypoechoic with low-level echoes, irregular wall`],[`Serology`,`Echinococcus IgG ELISA often positive`,`Negative`,`Amoebic IgG positive`],[`Treatment`,`Albendazole, PAIR or surgery by stage`,`None unless symptomatic`,`Metronidazole then luminal agent`]]}],redFlags:[`Fever, ileus, GI bleeding or Gram-negative sepsis in a patient on steroids from an endemic area - Strongyloides hyperinfection`,`Colicky pain, vomiting and distension with a mass of worms in a child - Ascaris bolus obstruction`,`Jaundice, fever and pancreatitis with a worm in the bile duct on ultrasound - biliary ascariasis with cholangitis`,`Sudden pain, urticaria, hypotension in a patient with a liver cyst - hydatid rupture and anaphylaxis`,`Jaundice with membranes in the bile duct - intrabiliary hydatid rupture`,`Haemoglobin under 7 g/dL or heart failure from hookworm anaemia`,`Seizures in a patient with taeniasis - neurocysticercosis`],pearls:[`**NDD: 10 February and 10 August, ages 1-19, albendazole 400 mg (200 mg under 2 years).**`,`**Albendazole for 3 days for Trichuris, repeat at 2 weeks for Enterobius** - single dose fails both.`,`**Ivermectin before steroids** in anyone from an endemic area - Strongyloides is the worm that kills on steroids.`,`**Larva currens moves fast (up to 10 cm/hour); cutaneous larva migrans moves slowly.**`,`**PAIR = Puncture, Aspiration, Injection (20% saline or 95% ethanol), Re-aspiration** - only for CE1 and CE3a.`,`**Water-lily sign = CE3a; ball of wool = CE4; double line = CE1.**`,`**Niclosamide for T. solium** if NCC not excluded; praziquantel can unmask seizures.`,`**Fasciola is the only fluke that needs triclabendazole** - praziquantel fails.`,`**Clonorchis and Opisthorchis are group 1 carcinogens** for cholangiocarcinoma.`],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-parasitic-gi-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the aetiopathogenesis, ultrasound classification, diagnosis and management of hydatid cyst of the liver. Add a note on PAIR.`,openingLines:[`Hydatid cyst (cystic echinococcosis) is the larval stage of Echinococcus granulosus in a human accidental intermediate host, acquired by ingesting eggs from dog faeces; the liver is involved in about 70% of cases.`,`Treatment is stage-specific by the WHO-IWGE ultrasound classification - albendazole, PAIR, surgery, or watch-and-wait.`],answer:[{heading:`Aetiopathogenesis`,points:[`Dog definitive host; sheep and cattle intermediate; humans accidental after eggs in dog faeces.`,`Oncosphere crosses the gut, reaches the liver by portal vein (first filter), lung second.`,`Cyst wall: host pericyst, laminated ectocyst, germinal endocyst with brood capsules and daughter cysts.`]},{heading:`Clinical features and complications`,points:[`Often incidental; RUQ heaviness, hepatomegaly.`,`Complications: rupture with anaphylaxis, intrabiliary rupture with cholangitis, infection, compression.`]},{heading:`WHO-IWGE classification`,points:[`CL, CE1 (double line), CE2 (daughter cysts) - active.`,`CE3a (water lily), CE3b (daughter cysts in solid matrix) - transitional.`,`CE4 (ball of wool), CE5 (calcified) - inactive.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Ultrasound stages the cyst; CT or MRI for complex or extrahepatic disease; MRCP for biliary communication.`,`IgG ELISA with immunoblot confirmation; less sensitive in CE1, CE4-5, lung.`,`No diagnostic aspiration without albendazole cover.`]},{heading:`Management by stage`,points:[`CE1/CE3a under 5 cm: albendazole 400 mg twice daily with fatty meal.`,`CE1/CE3a 5-10 cm: PAIR plus albendazole.`,`CE2/CE3b and complicated: surgery (pericystectomy) or modified catheterisation plus albendazole.`,`CE4/CE5: watch and wait with yearly ultrasound.`,`LFT and CBC 2-weekly then monthly on albendazole.`]},{heading:`PAIR`,points:[`Puncture under ultrasound, Aspiration, Injection of 20% saline or 95% ethanol, Re-aspiration after 15-20 minutes.`,`Check aspirate bilirubin; bile contraindicates scolicide.`,`Albendazole from 1 day-1 week before to 1 month after; adrenaline ready.`,`Contraindicated: CE2, CE3b, CE4-5, lung cysts, biliary communication.`]}],mustDraw:[`Life cycle of Echinococcus granulosus`,`WHO-IWGE classification table with stage-wise treatment`],markSplit:[{part:`Aetiopathogenesis and life cycle`,marks:2},{part:`WHO-IWGE classification`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Stage-wise management and PAIR`,marks:4}],keywords:[`hydatid`,`Echinococcus`,`PAIR`,`WHO-IWGE`,`albendazole`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-parasitic-gi-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on Strongyloides hyperinfection syndrome.`,openingLines:[`Strongyloides hyperinfection is accelerated autoinfection by Strongyloides stercoralis, usually triggered by corticosteroids or HTLV-1, in which massive larval migration carries enteric bacteria into the blood.`,`It carries mortality of 60-85% and is preventable by screening or empirical ivermectin before immunosuppression.`],answer:[{heading:`Pathogenesis and risk`,points:[`Autoinfection allows decades-long persistence.`,`Triggers: steroids, HTLV-1, transplant, haematological malignancy, alcoholism.`,`Larvae penetrate gut wall carrying Gram-negative bacteria.`]},{heading:`Features and diagnosis`,points:[`Fever, ileus, GI bleeding, cough, ARDS, Gram-negative sepsis or meningitis.`,`Larvae in stool, sputum, BAL; eosinophilia may be absent on steroids.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Ivermectin 200 microgram/kg daily until stool and sputum negative for 2 weeks; subcutaneous if ileus.`,`Antibiotics for enteric Gram-negatives; reduce immunosuppression.`]},{heading:`Prevention`,points:[`Screen by serology or give empirical ivermectin before steroids, transplant or chemotherapy in at-risk people.`,`Test for HTLV-1 in recurrent or severe cases.`]}],mustDraw:[`Strongyloides autoinfection cycle`],markSplit:[{part:`Pathogenesis and risk factors`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features and diagnosis`,marks:1},{part:`Treatment and prevention`,marks:2}],keywords:[`Strongyloides`,`hyperinfection`,`ivermectin`,`HTLV-1`,`steroids`]}],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-parasitic-gi-q1`,stem:`A 55-year-old farmer from Assam is on prednisolone 60 mg/day for 3 weeks for autoimmune haemolytic anaemia. He develops fever, abdominal distension, cough and Klebsiella bacteraemia. Sputum shows motile larvae. What is the drug of choice?`,options:[`Albendazole 400 mg single dose`,`Praziquantel 25 mg/kg three times daily`,`Mebendazole 100 mg twice daily for 3 days`,`Ivermectin 200 microgram/kg daily until stool and sputum are negative for 2 weeks`],answer:3,explanation:`Larvae in sputum with Gram-negative bacteraemia in a steroid-treated patient from an endemic area is Strongyloides hyperinfection, treated with daily ivermectin until clearance plus antibiotics and reduced steroids. Single-dose albendazole and short mebendazole are inadequate for Strongyloides, and praziquantel is for tapeworms and flukes.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-parasitic-gi-q2`,stem:`Ultrasound of a 30-year-old shepherd shows a 7 cm liver cyst with a floating detached membrane inside it. Which WHO-IWGE stage is this and what is the preferred treatment?`,options:[`CE3a; PAIR with albendazole cover`,`CE4; watch and wait`,`CE2; albendazole alone`,`CE5; surgery`],answer:0,explanation:`A detached floating endocyst is the water-lily sign of CE3a, and for CE1 or CE3a cysts of 5-10 cm WHO-IWGE recommends PAIR with albendazole. CE4 is a solid ball-of-wool inactive cyst left alone, CE2 has daughter cysts and needs surgery or catheterisation rather than drugs alone, and CE5 is calcified and inactive.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-parasitic-gi-q3`,stem:`Under National Deworming Day, what is given to a 20-month-old child?`,options:[`Albendazole 400 mg single dose`,`Mebendazole 500 mg single dose`,`Albendazole 200 mg (half tablet) single dose`,`No drug - children under 2 years are excluded`],answer:2,explanation:`NDD covers ages 1-19 years, and children aged 12-24 months receive half a tablet (200 mg) of albendazole, crushed and given under supervision. The full 400 mg dose is for ages 2-19 years, mebendazole is not the NDD drug, and only infants under 1 year are excluded.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-parasitic-gi-q4`,stem:`A 6-year-old has nocturnal perianal itching and disturbed sleep; siblings have similar symptoms. Which approach is most appropriate?`,options:[`Stool microscopy for 3 days before treating`,`Albendazole 400 mg single dose to the child and all household members, repeated after 2 weeks`,`Metronidazole for 7 days to the child only`,`Ivermectin 200 microgram/kg for 2 days`],answer:1,explanation:`This is enterobiasis; eggs are laid on perianal skin so stool microscopy is usually negative (the Scotch tape test is used), and treatment is albendazole repeated after 2 weeks to kill newly hatched worms, for the whole household. Metronidazole is anti-protozoal and ivermectin is not the first choice for pinworm.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-parasitic-gi-q5`,stem:`A man found to pass tapeworm segments in stool has a history of a single seizure 2 years ago and has not had brain imaging. Which drug is preferred for his intestinal taeniasis?`,options:[`Praziquantel 10 mg/kg single dose`,`Albendazole 15 mg/kg/day for 28 days`,`Triclabendazole 10 mg/kg`,`Niclosamide 2 g chewed as a single dose`],answer:3,explanation:`Niclosamide is not absorbed and kills adult tapeworms in the gut without affecting tissue cysts, so it is preferred when neurocysticercosis has not been excluded. Praziquantel is absorbed and can inflame occult brain cysts and provoke seizures, prolonged albendazole is a neurocysticercosis regimen, and triclabendazole is for Fasciola.`,difficulty:`hard`}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 (chapters on intestinal nematodes, cestodes, trematodes and protozoa)`,`World Health Organization. Soil-transmitted helminth infections fact sheet, 2023; Guideline: preventive chemotherapy to control soil-transmitted helminth infections, 2017`,`WHO. Ending the neglect to attain the SDGs: road map for neglected tropical diseases 2021-2030`,`Brunetti E, Kern P, Vuitton DA; WHO-IWGE. Expert consensus for the diagnosis and treatment of cystic and alveolar echinococcosis in humans, Acta Tropica, 2010; WHO echinococcosis fact sheet, 2024`,`Ministry of Health and Family Welfare, Government of India. National Deworming Day operational guidelines, 2024; Anemia Mukt Bharat operational guidelines, 2018`,`Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. Clinical care of strongyloidiasis and parasitic disease treatment guidance, 2024`,`Shane AL et al. IDSA clinical practice guidelines for the diagnosis and management of infectious diarrhea, 2017`,`ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows of India, 2023`]}),d.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-liver-mass-approach`,title:`Approach to a liver mass/space-occupying lesion`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`A **focal liver lesion (space-occupying lesion)** is any solid or cystic mass distinct from normal parenchyma on imaging; the approach turns on **two questions - is the liver cirrhotic (or is there a known cancer)?** - because in cirrhosis a lesion of **1 cm or more is hepatocellular carcinoma until proven otherwise**, whereas in a normal liver most incidental lesions are **benign cysts, haemangiomas or FNH**, characterised by **contrast-enhanced MRI, CT or CEUS** rather than biopsy (ACG 2014, updated 2024; EASL 2016).`,keywords:[`focal liver lesion`,`space-occupying lesion liver`,`SOL liver`,`hepatic haemangioma`,`focal nodular hyperplasia`,`FNH`,`hepatocellular adenoma`,`simple hepatic cyst`,`mucinous cystic neoplasm`,`polycystic liver disease`,`liver metastases`,`hepatocellular carcinoma`,`LI-RADS`,`CEUS`,`gadoxetate MRI`,`Kasabach-Merritt`,`incidentaloma`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`A **focal liver lesion (FLL)** is a solid or cystic abnormality distinct from surrounding parenchyma; most are **incidental findings on ultrasound** done for unrelated reasons. [ACG 2014]`,`Incidental FLLs are found in up to **15-30%** of adults on cross-sectional imaging, and in a patient without cirrhosis or cancer **the vast majority are benign**. [ACG 2024]`,`**Simple hepatic cysts** (prevalence **2.5-18%**) and **haemangiomas** (up to **5-20%** in autopsy series) are the commonest benign lesions; **FNH** is second commonest solid benign lesion (about 0.3-3%). [EASL 2016]`,`**Hepatocellular adenoma** is rare (about **1-4 per 100,000** in long-term oral contraceptive users) but matters because of **bleeding and malignant transformation**. [EASL 2016]`,`**Metastases are the commonest malignant liver tumours** in a non-cirrhotic liver, far outnumbering primary tumours in the West; colorectal, pancreatic, breast, lung, gastric and neuroendocrine primaries dominate. [Harrison 22e]`,`In India the commonest malignant liver mass is **HBV-related HCC**, and in the Gangetic belt **gallbladder cancer invading segments IV and V** is an important cause of a liver mass. [ICMR Cancer Registry 2020]`,`**Infective SOLs** - amoebic and pyogenic abscess and hydatid cyst - are far commoner in India than in Western series and must be in every Indian differential; see the Liver abscess and Intestinal parasites topics. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors - the context decides`,points:[`**Cirrhosis or chronic HBV** (even without cirrhosis) raises HCC risk; HCV, alcohol, MASLD, haemochromatosis and aflatoxin are other drivers - see the HCC topic. [AASLD 2023]`,`**Oral contraceptives, anabolic steroids, obesity and metabolic syndrome, glycogen storage disease type I and III and MODY3 (HNF1A)** predispose to hepatocellular adenoma. [EASL 2016]`,`**Female sex and reproductive age** - haemangioma, FNH and adenoma are all commoner in women; FNH is not caused by OCPs. [EASL 2016]`,`**Known extrahepatic malignancy**, weight loss or a change in bowel habit points to metastases; neuroendocrine tumours, colorectal cancer and melanoma metastasise to liver commonly. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fever, recent dysentery, alcohol use, diabetes or biliary disease** suggests an abscess; **contact with dogs and sheep in an endemic district** suggests hydatid. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**ADPKD and autosomal dominant polycystic liver disease (PRKCSH, SEC63 genes)** cause multiple cysts; female sex, oestrogen and pregnancy enlarge them. [EASL 2022]`,`**Primary sclerosing cholangitis, liver flukes, hepatolithiasis and choledochal cyst** predispose to intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why lesions look the way they do`,points:[`**Normal liver has dual supply** (75% portal vein, 25% hepatic artery), whereas tumours are fed mainly by the **hepatic artery**; multiphasic imaging exploits this difference. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HCC** develops new unpaired arteries, so it enhances brightly in the **late arterial phase (arterial phase hyperenhancement)** and loses contrast faster than liver in the portal/delayed phase (**washout**) with an enhancing **capsule**. [AASLD 2023]`,`**Haemangioma** is a mesenchymal lesion of large vascular spaces with slow flow, so contrast pools as **peripheral nodular discontinuous enhancement with centripetal fill-in** that matches the blood pool. [EASL 2016]`,`**FNH** is a polyclonal hyperplastic response of hepatocytes to an **arterial malformation**, with a **central stellate scar** containing a dystrophic artery that gives the **spoke-wheel** pattern; abnormal ductules retain hepatobiliary contrast. [EASL 2016]`,`**Hepatocellular adenoma** is a monoclonal hepatocyte neoplasm without portal tracts or bile ducts; its subtypes are defined by molecular change (**HNF1A, inflammatory, beta-catenin, sonic hedgehog**), and each has different bleeding and cancer risk. [EASL 2016]`,`**Most metastases (colorectal, pancreatic, gastric)** are **hypovascular** with rim enhancement and a **target or bull's-eye** look; **neuroendocrine, renal, thyroid, melanoma and choriocarcinoma** metastases are **hypervascular**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma** is rich in fibrous stroma, so contrast enters slowly - **progressive delayed enhancement** with **capsular retraction** and peripheral duct dilatation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Simple cysts** arise from aberrant bile duct microhamartomas lined by cuboidal epithelium that secretes fluid - hence thin-walled, anechoic and non-communicating. [EASL 2022]`]},{heading:`Classification of focal liver lesions`,points:[`**Benign solid**: haemangioma, FNH, hepatocellular adenoma, focal fat or fatty sparing, nodular regenerative hyperplasia, angiomyolipoma, regenerative and dysplastic nodules in cirrhosis. [ACG 2024]`,`**Benign cystic**: simple cyst, polycystic liver disease, biliary hamartomas (von Meyenburg complexes), Caroli disease, **hydatid cyst**, abscess (amoebic, pyogenic). [EASL 2022]`,`**Malignant primary**: **HCC** (commonest), intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma, fibrolamellar HCC (young, non-cirrhotic), angiosarcoma (vinyl chloride, arsenic), hepatoblastoma (children), epithelioid haemangioendothelioma. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Malignant secondary**: metastases (commonest overall), lymphoma, and **direct invasion from gallbladder cancer**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hepatocellular adenoma subtypes (Bordeaux)**: HNF1A-inactivated (**30-40%**, steatotic, lowest risk), inflammatory (**40-50%**, obesity and alcohol, bleeding), **beta-catenin exon 3-activated (10-15%, men, highest malignant risk)**, sonic hedgehog (about 5%, bleeding) and unclassified. [EASL 2016]`,`**LI-RADS (ACR, CT/MRI v2018)** grades observations in at-risk livers from **LR-1 (definitely benign) to LR-5 (definitely HCC)**, plus **LR-M** (probably malignant, not HCC-specific) and **LR-TIV** (tumour in vein). [AASLD 2023]`,`**Cyst classification (EASL 2022)**: simple cyst, polycystic liver disease, **mucinous cystic neoplasm** (septations, mural nodules, ovarian-type stroma), and infective cysts. [EASL 2022]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history to take`,points:[`**Most benign lesions are asymptomatic** and found incidentally; symptoms (pain, fullness) occur mainly in lesions **over 5-10 cm** or from complications. [ACG 2024]`,`Ask about **cirrhosis risk**: HBV, HCV, alcohol quantity, diabetes and obesity, blood transfusion, family history of liver disease or HCC. [AASLD 2023]`,`Ask about **hormones**: oral contraceptive duration, anabolic steroids in gym users, pregnancy and HRT. [EASL 2016]`,`Ask about **cancer**: weight loss, bowel change, rectal bleeding, breast lump, smoking, previous cancer, flushing and diarrhoea (carcinoid). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sudden severe RUQ pain with shock** in a young woman on OCPs suggests **ruptured adenoma**; fever and rigors suggest abscess or infected cyst. [EASL 2016]`,`Ask about **dogs and sheep, travel, dysentery** (hydatid, amoebic abscess), and **raw fish** (liver flukes). [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**Constitutional symptoms with decompensation** (new ascites, jaundice, encephalopathy) in a cirrhotic suggest HCC, often with **portal vein tumour thrombus**. [AASLD 2023]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Look for **stigmata of chronic liver disease** - spider naevi, palmar erythema, gynaecomastia, splenomegaly, ascites - which shift the probability towards HCC. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hard, irregular, nodular hepatomegaly** suggests metastases or HCC; a **smooth, tender liver with intercostal tenderness** suggests abscess. [Harrison 22e]`,`A **hepatic arterial bruit or friction rub** over the liver suggests **HCC** (bruit) or a superficial tumour or abscess (rub). [Harrison 22e]`,`Examine for a primary: **breast, thyroid, lymph nodes (Virchow node), per-rectal examination**, skin for melanoma, and **Sister Mary Joseph nodule**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Smooth cystic non-tender hepatomegaly** with a family history of renal cysts suggests polycystic disease; palpable kidneys confirm ADPKD. [EASL 2022]`,`**Petechiae and bruising** with a giant haemangioma suggest **Kasabach-Merritt syndrome** (consumptive coagulopathy). [EASL 2016]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis - imaging characteristics`,points:[`**Haemangioma**: hyperechoic, well-defined on ultrasound; CT/MRI **peripheral nodular discontinuous enhancement with centripetal fill-in**; **markedly bright on T2 (light-bulb sign)**. [EASL 2016]`,`**FNH**: iso-attenuating lesion with **homogeneous intense arterial enhancement**, becoming iso in portal phase, **central scar bright on T2 and late-enhancing**, and **iso- or hyperintense in the gadoxetate hepatobiliary phase**. [EASL 2016]`,`**Adenoma**: arterial enhancement without consistent washout, **hypointense in the hepatobiliary phase**; HNF1A adenoma **loses signal on out-of-phase MRI** (intralesional fat); inflammatory adenoma shows a T2-bright **atoll sign**. [EASL 2016]`,`**HCC in cirrhosis**: **arterial phase hyperenhancement plus washout** (and capsule) in a lesion of **10 mm or more** is diagnostic (LR-5) without biopsy. [AASLD 2023]`,`**Metastasis**: multiple lesions of varied size, **rim enhancement**, target sign; on CEUS **early and marked washout (within 60 seconds)**, while HCC shows **late mild washout (after 60 seconds)**. [EFSUMB 2020]`,`**Focal fat or focal sparing**: geographic, near the gallbladder fossa, falciform ligament or porta, **no mass effect, vessels pass through undisplaced**; confirmed on chemical-shift MRI. [ACG 2024]`,`**Simple cyst vs mucinous cystic neoplasm vs hydatid**: simple cyst is anechoic and thin-walled; MCN has **thick septations, mural nodules or calcification**; hydatid has a **double wall, daughter cysts or water-lily sign**. [EASL 2022]`,`**Abscess vs necrotic tumour**: abscess has fever, raised CRP, an irregular wall with **double-target sign** and rapid change in days; a necrotic metastasis enhances at the rim and is stable over days. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line and advanced`,points:[`**Ultrasound** is the first test in the OPD and district hospital - cystic vs solid, number, size, background liver echotexture, splenomegaly, portal vein patency. [ACG 2014]`,`**Bloods**: LFT, **INR, platelets**, HBsAg, anti-HCV, **AFP**, and CEA or CA 19-9 if metastasis or cholangiocarcinoma is suspected; tumour markers support but never make the diagnosis alone. [ACG 2024]`,`**Contrast-enhanced multiphasic MRI** (preferably with a hepatobiliary agent such as gadoxetate) is the **best single test to characterise a solid lesion**; multiphasic CT is the alternative when MRI is unavailable. [ACG 2024]`,`**Contrast-enhanced ultrasound (CEUS)** with microbubbles characterises haemangioma, FNH and metastasis in real time, is safe in renal failure, and is increasingly used in Indian tertiary centres. [EFSUMB 2020]`,`**Lesions under 1 cm in a non-cirrhotic liver without cancer history** are usually benign and too small to characterise; a repeat ultrasound or MRI in **3-6 months** is reasonable. [ACG 2024]`,`**Biopsy (ultrasound or CT-guided core)** is reserved for lesions that remain **indeterminate after optimal imaging** or when histology will change treatment (suspected metastasis of unknown primary, LR-M, atypical adenoma). [ACG 2024]`,`**Do not biopsy a likely haemangioma** (bleeding), **a suspected hydatid cyst** (anaphylaxis, seeding) or a **resectable typical HCC** (needle-tract seeding about 2-3%). [ACG 2014]`,`**Search for a primary** in suspected metastases: CT chest-abdomen-pelvis, colonoscopy, mammography, and **Ga-68 DOTATATE PET-CT** for neuroendocrine tumours; FDG PET-CT for staging selected cancers. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Serology** - amoebic IgG and Echinococcus IgG ELISA - when imaging suggests abscess or hydatid. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - benign lesions`,points:[`**Typical haemangioma** of any size: **reassure, no follow-up imaging and no treatment**; OCPs and pregnancy are **not contraindicated**. [EASL 2016]`,`Treat haemangioma only for **persistent symptoms, rapid growth, Kasabach-Merritt syndrome or diagnostic doubt** - enucleation or resection, or transarterial embolisation in poor surgical candidates. [EASL 2016]`,`**Typical FNH**: **no follow-up and no treatment**, no need to stop OCPs; follow up only if imaging is atypical. [EASL 2016]`,`**Hepatocellular adenoma**: **stop oral contraceptives and advise weight loss**, then repeat **MRI after 6 months**; most shrink. [EASL 2016]`,`**Resect adenoma** if it remains **5 cm or more** after 6 months of lifestyle change, in **any man regardless of size**, if **beta-catenin activated**, or if it is growing; lesions under 5 cm are followed with yearly imaging. [EASL 2016]`,`**Simple cyst**: **no follow-up if asymptomatic**; for symptomatic large cysts, **laparoscopic deroofing (fenestration)** or **aspiration with sclerotherapy (ethanol)** - aspiration alone almost always recurs. [EASL 2022]`,`**Polycystic liver disease** with symptoms: **somatostatin analogues (octreotide LAR or lanreotide)** for 6-12 months reduce liver volume modestly; avoid oestrogen; refer for fenestration, resection or transplant in severe cases. [EASL 2022]`,`**Infective lesions**: amoebic abscess - metronidazole then a luminal agent; hydatid - stage-based albendazole, PAIR or surgery; see the Liver abscess and Intestinal parasites topics. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergencies`,points:[`**Ruptured or bleeding adenoma**: resuscitate, contrast CT, and **transarterial embolisation** as first-line haemostasis, with delayed resection of the residual lesion. [EASL 2016]`,`**Kasabach-Merritt syndrome**: correct coagulopathy (fibrinogen, platelets), then urgent embolisation or resection of the haemangioma. [EASL 2016]`,`**Spontaneous HCC rupture** in cirrhosis presents as haemoperitoneum and shock; **transarterial embolisation** is first-line, with high early mortality. [AASLD 2023]`,`**Infected or haemorrhagic cyst**: antibiotics and percutaneous drainage; cyst haemorrhage alone settles with analgesia. [EASL 2022]`,`**Obstructive jaundice from a hilar mass or metastases**: ERCP or PTBD drainage before chemotherapy; avoid unnecessary stenting of potentially resectable lesions before surgical review. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hydatid rupture with anaphylaxis**: adrenaline 0.5 mg IM, fluids, steroids and antihistamines, then surgery. [WHO-IWGE 2010]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: haemangioma and FNH need no change; **adenoma under 5 cm** is not a contraindication to pregnancy but needs **ultrasound surveillance each trimester**; adenoma 5 cm or more should be treated before conception. [EASL 2016]`,`**Imaging in pregnancy**: ultrasound first; non-contrast MRI if needed; **gadolinium is avoided**; CEUS microbubbles are generally avoided. [EASL 2016]`,`**Men with adenoma** have a much higher malignant transformation rate, so resection is advised **regardless of size**. [EASL 2016]`,`**Children**: infantile haemangioma (propranolol for large or symptomatic), mesenchymal hamartoma and **hepatoblastoma (AFP very high)** - refer to paediatric hepatology. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CKD**: avoid iodinated contrast where possible; **CEUS** has no nephrotoxicity, and **group II gadolinium agents** carry very low nephrogenic systemic fibrosis risk. [ACR 2024]`,`**Elderly patients** with a solid lesion are more likely to have malignancy; a new lesion in a person **over 50 without cirrhosis** needs metastasis work-up. [ACG 2024]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: ultrasound plus HBsAg, anti-HCV, AFP and LFT allow triage; refer cirrhotic patients with any lesion of 1 cm or more, and all indeterminate solid lesions, for multiphasic imaging. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Haemangioma**: rupture is rare even in giant lesions; Kasabach-Merritt syndrome and compression symptoms are uncommon. [EASL 2016]`,`**FNH** has **no malignant potential** and does not bleed meaningfully. [EASL 2016]`,`**Adenoma**: **bleeding in about 15-20%** (mostly lesions over 5 cm) and **malignant transformation in about 5%**, higher in men and beta-catenin subtype. [EASL 2016]`,`**Simple cysts** rarely bleed or become infected; **MCN** carries a risk of cystadenocarcinoma and needs complete resection. [EASL 2022]`,`**Colorectal liver metastases** confined to the liver can be **resected with 5-year survival of 40-50%**, so early referral to a hepatobiliary team matters. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HCC prognosis** depends on BCLC stage and liver function; surveillance-detected lesions are far more often curable - see the HCC topic. [AASLD 2023]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer urgently**: any lesion **1 cm or more in cirrhosis or chronic HBV**, suspected malignancy, ruptured lesion, adenoma in a man, symptomatic cyst or Kasabach-Merritt syndrome. [ACG 2024]`,`**Cirrhotic patient with a lesion under 1 cm**: repeat ultrasound in **3-6 months**; if stable for 2 years, return to routine **6-monthly surveillance**. [AASLD 2023]`,`**Adenoma under 5 cm in a woman**: MRI at 6 months after stopping OCP, then **yearly**; once stable or regressing, imaging can be spaced further. [EASL 2016]`,`**Typical haemangioma, FNH and simple cyst**: **discharge without follow-up** - over-surveillance causes anxiety and cost. [EASL 2016]`,`**Indeterminate lesion not biopsied**: repeat MRI at **3-6 months**; growth or new features trigger biopsy or resection. [ACG 2024]`,`**Multidisciplinary tumour board** (hepatologist, radiologist, surgeon, oncologist) decides malignant and atypical lesions. [AASLD 2023]`]},{heading:`Prevention and counselling`,points:[`**HBV vaccination** (universal infant immunisation under UIP, and adults at risk) is the single most effective prevention of HCC in India. [NVHCP 2018]`,`**Hepatitis B and C testing and treatment under NVHCP** reduces cirrhosis and HCC; offer testing to every patient with a liver lesion. [NVHCP 2018]`,`**Six-monthly ultrasound with or without AFP** for cirrhotics and high-risk HBV carriers detects HCC at a curable stage. [AASLD 2023]`,`Counsel women with adenoma to **avoid oestrogen-containing contraception** (use copper IUD or progestogen-only methods) and lose weight. [EASL 2016]`,`Counsel patients with haemangioma, FNH or simple cyst clearly that the lesion is **benign and needs no follow-up**, to prevent repeated scans. [EASL 2016]`,`**Colorectal cancer screening and follow-up** with CEA and CT detects resectable liver metastases early. [NCCN 2025]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**ACG 2024 focal liver lesion guideline** (updating ACG 2014) prefers **contrast-enhanced MRI** for characterisation, reserves biopsy for indeterminate lesions, and supports no follow-up for typical haemangioma and FNH. [ACG 2024]`,`**AASLD 2023 HCC guidance** keeps non-invasive diagnosis by **LI-RADS LR-5 (arterial hyperenhancement plus washout, 10 mm or more)** in at-risk patients and recommends biopsy for LR-M. [AASLD 2023]`,`**EASL 2025 HCC guideline** similarly allows imaging diagnosis only in cirrhosis and endorses abbreviated MRI and biomarker panels as evolving surveillance tools. [EASL 2025]`,`**EASL 2022 cystic liver disease guideline** advises no follow-up for simple cysts, MRI to identify MCN, and somatostatin analogues for symptomatic polycystic liver disease. [EASL 2022]`,`**EFSUMB 2020 CEUS guidelines** standardised CEUS washout timing (early and marked for metastasis, late and mild for HCC), and CEUS LI-RADS is now widely used where MRI is limited. [EFSUMB 2020]`,`**EASL 2016 benign liver tumour guideline** remains the reference for adenoma: resect 5 cm or more after 6 months, any size in men. [EASL 2016]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Opening line for any liver SOL answer: **'Is the liver cirrhotic, and is there a known primary?'** - examiners reward this triage. [ACG 2024]`,`Classic viva: **'How do you differentiate haemangioma, FNH and adenoma on imaging?'** - peripheral nodular fill-in vs central scar and spoke-wheel vs fat and hepatobiliary-phase hypointensity. [EASL 2016]`,`Classic viva: **'Which lesion should never be biopsied?'** - haemangioma, hydatid and typical resectable HCC. [ACG 2014]`,`Common mistake: **following up typical haemangioma or FNH with serial scans**, or stopping OCPs for FNH. [EASL 2016]`,`Common mistake: forgetting **men and beta-catenin adenomas** need resection at any size. [EASL 2016]`,`Common mistake: missing **amoebic abscess and hydatid** in an Indian differential of a liver SOL. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**'Light-bulb' bright T2 = haemangioma; 'central scar' = FNH (and fibrolamellar HCC); 'water-lily' = hydatid; 'target' = metastasis.** [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs and procedures used for focal liver lesions`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Octreotide LAR (polycystic liver disease)`,`20-40 mg IM every 4 weeks`,`6-12 months, continue if volume falls`,`Gallstones, diarrhoea, glucose change; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Lanreotide (polycystic liver disease)`,`90-120 mg deep subcutaneous every 4 weeks`,`6-12 months, reassess`,`Gallstones, injection-site reactions, abdominal pain`],[`Ethanol sclerotherapy (symptomatic simple cyst)`,`95-99% ethanol instilled after aspiration, about 10-25% of cyst volume`,`Single session, repeat if recurrence`,`Pain, intoxication; contraindicated if cyst communicates with bile ducts`],[`Albendazole (hydatid cyst)`,`400 mg twice daily (10-15 mg/kg/day) with a fatty meal`,`Around PAIR or surgery; months if medical alone`,`Hepatotoxicity, neutropenia, alopecia; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Metronidazole (amoebic abscess)`,`750 mg orally three times daily (child 35-50 mg/kg/day)`,`7-10 days, then a luminal agent`,`Metallic taste, neuropathy, alcohol reaction`],[`Propranolol (infantile hepatic haemangioma)`,`1-3 mg/kg/day orally in divided doses`,`Months, tapered`,`Hypoglycaemia, bradycardia, bronchospasm`],[`Stopping oestrogen (adenoma)`,`Stop combined OCP; use copper IUD or progestogen-only method`,`Reassess with MRI at 6 months`,`Unplanned pregnancy if contraception not replaced`]]},{heading:`Imaging characteristics of common focal liver lesions`,columns:[`Lesion`,`Ultrasound`,`CT/MRI enhancement`,`Key sign`],rows:[[`Haemangioma`,`Hyperechoic, well defined`,`Peripheral nodular discontinuous, centripetal fill-in`,`Very bright on T2 (light bulb)`],[`FNH`,`Iso, subtle; Doppler spoke-wheel`,`Intense homogeneous arterial, iso in portal phase`,`Central scar; iso or bright in hepatobiliary phase`],[`Hepatocellular adenoma`,`Variable, may be hyperechoic (fat)`,`Arterial enhancement, no consistent washout`,`Fat drop-out (HNF1A), atoll sign (inflammatory), dark in hepatobiliary phase`],[`HCC (in cirrhosis)`,`Hypoechoic nodule`,`Arterial hyperenhancement plus washout, capsule`,`LR-5 if 10 mm or more`],[`Metastasis`,`Target or bull's-eye, multiple`,`Rim enhancement (hypovascular) or arterial (NET, RCC, melanoma)`,`CEUS early marked washout under 60 s`],[`Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma`,`Ill-defined mass, duct dilatation`,`Progressive delayed enhancement`,`Capsular retraction`],[`Simple cyst`,`Anechoic, thin wall, posterior enhancement`,`No enhancement`,`No septa or nodules`],[`Hydatid cyst`,`Double line, daughter cysts`,`Wall enhancement only`,`Water-lily sign`]]},{heading:`Benign solid lesions: haemangioma vs FNH vs adenoma`,columns:[`Feature`,`Haemangioma`,`FNH`,`Hepatocellular adenoma`],rows:[[`Nature`,`Vascular malformation`,`Hyperplastic response to arterial malformation`,`Monoclonal neoplasm`],[`OCP link`,`None meaningful`,`None (OCP can continue)`,`Strong - stop OCP`],[`Bleeding risk`,`Very low`,`Negligible`,`15-20%, mainly over 5 cm`],[`Malignant potential`,`None`,`None`,`About 5%; higher in men and beta-catenin`],[`Follow-up`,`None if typical`,`None if typical`,`MRI at 6 months, then yearly`],[`Treatment`,`Only if symptomatic, growing or KMS`,`Rarely needed`,`Resect if 5 cm or more after 6 months, any man, beta-catenin`]]},{heading:`Investigations and decision cut-offs`,columns:[`Situation`,`Test`,`Cut-off or finding`,`Action`],rows:[[`Cirrhosis, lesion under 1 cm`,`Ultrasound`,`Under 10 mm`,`Repeat ultrasound in 3-6 months`],[`Cirrhosis, lesion 1 cm or more`,`Multiphasic CT or MRI`,`Arterial hyperenhancement plus washout (LR-5)`,`Diagnose HCC without biopsy; refer`],[`Cirrhosis, LR-M or LR-4`,`Biopsy or MDT review`,`Atypical features`,`Biopsy or repeat imaging in 3 months`],[`Non-cirrhotic, incidental under 1 cm`,`Ultrasound or MRI`,`Too small to characterise`,`Repeat in 3-6 months or discharge if typical`],[`Non-cirrhotic, solid lesion`,`MRI with hepatobiliary agent or CEUS`,`Typical haemangioma or FNH`,`Discharge`],[`Adenoma`,`MRI after 6 months off OCP`,`5 cm or more`,`Resect`],[`Suspected metastasis`,`CT chest-abdomen-pelvis, CEA, CA 19-9`,`Primary not found`,`Biopsy the liver lesion`]]}],redFlags:[`Any new liver lesion of 1 cm or more in a patient with cirrhosis or chronic hepatitis B - presume HCC`,`Sudden RUQ pain with hypotension in a young woman on OCPs - ruptured adenoma`,`Liver lesion with weight loss, anaemia or change in bowel habit - metastasis work-up`,`Fever, rigors and tender hepatomegaly - abscess needing same-day treatment`,`Bruising and low platelets with a giant haemangioma - Kasabach-Merritt syndrome`,`Obstructive jaundice with a hilar or liver mass - cholangiocarcinoma or gallbladder cancer`,`Portal vein thrombus with an arterially enhancing mass - advanced HCC`],pearls:[`**Two triage questions: cirrhosis? known cancer?** - they decide everything.`,`**In cirrhosis, 1 cm or more with arterial enhancement plus washout = HCC; no biopsy needed.**`,`**Haemangioma fills in from the edge (centripetal), FNH lights up from the centre (spoke-wheel).**`,`**FNH keeps hepatobiliary contrast (bright); adenoma does not (dark)** on gadoxetate MRI.`,`**Adenoma: stop the pill, wait 6 months, resect if still 5 cm or more; men - resect all.**`,`**Never needle a haemangioma or a hydatid.**`,`**CEUS: metastasis washes out early and marked; HCC washes out late and mild.**`,`**Typical haemangioma, FNH and simple cyst need no follow-up** - discharge and reassure.`],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-liver-mass-approach-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`A 35-year-old woman has a 4 cm solid liver lesion found incidentally on ultrasound. Discuss the differential diagnosis, the approach to investigation and the management of focal liver lesions in a non-cirrhotic liver.`,openingLines:[`A focal liver lesion is a solid or cystic abnormality distinct from normal parenchyma; in a woman with a non-cirrhotic liver and no cancer history, a solid incidental lesion is most likely a haemangioma, FNH or hepatocellular adenoma.`,`The approach is to confirm the absence of cirrhosis and malignancy, characterise the lesion with contrast-enhanced MRI or CEUS, and reserve biopsy for lesions that stay indeterminate (ACG 2024, EASL 2016).`],answer:[{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`Benign solid: haemangioma, FNH, adenoma, focal fat.`,`Malignant: metastasis, fibrolamellar HCC, HCC in HBV carrier.`,`Indian infective: amoebic abscess, hydatid (cystic).`]},{heading:`History and examination`,points:[`OCP and steroid use, HBV/HCV/alcohol risk, cancer symptoms, fever, dogs.`,`Stigmata of chronic liver disease, hepatomegaly character, search for a primary.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`LFT, INR, platelets, HBsAg, anti-HCV, AFP; CEA/CA 19-9 if indicated.`,`Contrast MRI with hepatobiliary agent (best) or CEUS or multiphasic CT.`,`Biopsy only if indeterminate; never for haemangioma or hydatid.`]},{heading:`Imaging characteristics`,points:[`Haemangioma: peripheral nodular fill-in, T2 light bulb.`,`FNH: arterial enhancement, central scar, bright in hepatobiliary phase.`,`Adenoma: fat drop-out or atoll sign, dark in hepatobiliary phase.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Haemangioma and FNH: reassure, no follow-up, OCP can continue.`,`Adenoma: stop OCP, weight loss, MRI at 6 months; resect if 5 cm or more, men, beta-catenin.`,`Bleeding adenoma: embolisation then resection.`,`Pregnancy: adenoma under 5 cm - ultrasound each trimester.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: incidental liver lesion - cirrhosis yes/no - imaging - biopsy or discharge`,`Table comparing haemangioma, FNH and adenoma`],markSplit:[{part:`Differential diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Investigation approach and imaging features`,marks:4},{part:`Management of each lesion`,marks:3},{part:`Flow chart`,marks:1}],keywords:[`focal liver lesion`,`haemangioma`,`FNH`,`adenoma`,`MRI`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-liver-mass-approach-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Differentiate focal nodular hyperplasia from hepatocellular adenoma.`,openingLines:[`FNH is a polyclonal hyperplastic response to an arterial malformation with no malignant potential, while hepatocellular adenoma is a monoclonal oestrogen-related neoplasm that can bleed or turn malignant.`,`The distinction decides between reassurance and surveillance or resection, and is made by contrast MRI with a hepatobiliary agent.`],answer:[{heading:`Pathology and risk factors`,points:[`FNH: polyclonal hyperplasia around an arterial malformation, central scar with ductules; HCA: monoclonal hepatocytes without portal tracts or bile ducts.`,`FNH: no OCP link, OCP can continue; HCA: strong link with OCP, anabolic steroids, obesity, GSD and MODY3.`,`Both commoner in women of reproductive age; HCA in men is more often beta-catenin activated.`]},{heading:`Imaging`,points:[`FNH: intense homogeneous arterial enhancement, iso in portal phase, T2-bright late-enhancing central scar, spoke-wheel on Doppler or CEUS.`,`FNH: iso- or hyperintense in the gadoxetate hepatobiliary phase.`,`HCA: fat drop-out on out-of-phase MRI (HNF1A) or T2-bright atoll sign (inflammatory); hypointense in the hepatobiliary phase.`]},{heading:`Complications`,points:[`FNH: no bleeding of note and no malignant potential.`,`HCA: bleeding in 15-20% (mostly over 5 cm) and malignant transformation in about 5%, higher in men and beta-catenin subtype.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`FNH: reassure, no follow-up if typical.`,`HCA: stop OCP, weight loss, MRI at 6 months; resect if 5 cm or more, any man, or beta-catenin; embolise if bleeding.`,`Pregnancy: FNH no change; HCA under 5 cm needs ultrasound each trimester.`]}],mustDraw:[`Comparison table`],markSplit:[{part:`Pathology and risk factors`,marks:1},{part:`Imaging`,marks:2},{part:`Complications and management`,marks:2}],keywords:[`FNH`,`adenoma`,`central scar`,`gadoxetate`]}],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-liver-mass-approach-q1`,stem:`A 52-year-old man with HBV-related cirrhosis has a new 2.2 cm liver nodule on surveillance ultrasound. Multiphasic MRI shows arterial phase hyperenhancement with washout in the portal venous phase. What is the next step?`,options:[`Diagnose HCC and refer for staging and treatment without biopsy`,`Ultrasound-guided biopsy to confirm before any treatment`,`Repeat ultrasound in 6 months`,`Serum AFP and treat only if over 400 ng/mL`],answer:0,explanation:`In a cirrhotic liver a lesion of 10 mm or more with arterial hyperenhancement and washout is LI-RADS LR-5 and is diagnostic of HCC under AASLD 2023 without biopsy. Biopsy is for LR-M or indeterminate lesions and risks seeding, repeat ultrasound wastes curable time, and a normal AFP does not exclude HCC.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-liver-mass-approach-q2`,stem:`A 30-year-old woman has a 4 cm lesion with peripheral nodular discontinuous enhancement and progressive centripetal fill-in on CT, and very bright signal on T2 MRI. What is the correct management?`,options:[`Stop the oral contraceptive pill and repeat MRI in 6 months`,`Percutaneous biopsy to exclude malignancy`,`Reassure; no follow-up imaging is needed`,`Elective laparoscopic resection`],answer:2,explanation:`This is a typical haemangioma, which needs no treatment or follow-up and does not require stopping contraception (EASL 2016). Stopping the pill and repeat MRI is the plan for adenoma, biopsy of a haemangioma risks bleeding, and resection is only for symptomatic, growing or Kasabach-Merritt lesions.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-liver-mass-approach-q3`,stem:`On gadoxetate-enhanced MRI, a lesion shows intense homogeneous arterial enhancement, a central T2-bright scar and is iso- to hyperintense in the hepatobiliary phase. What is the most likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Hepatocellular adenoma`,`Hepatocellular carcinoma`,`Hypovascular metastasis`,`Focal nodular hyperplasia`],answer:3,explanation:`Retention of hepatobiliary contrast with a central scar and homogeneous arterial enhancement is typical of FNH, whose abnormal ductules take up gadoxetate. Adenoma lacks bile ducts and is usually dark in the hepatobiliary phase, HCC washes out and is dark, and hypovascular metastases show rim enhancement rather than intense homogeneous enhancement.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-liver-mass-approach-q4`,stem:`A 28-year-old male bodybuilder using anabolic steroids has a 3 cm hepatocellular adenoma confirmed on MRI. What is the recommended management?`,options:[`Stop steroids and repeat MRI yearly; resect only if it exceeds 5 cm`,`Resection regardless of size`,`No action, as adenomas under 5 cm never bleed`,`Radiofrequency ablation only if AFP rises`],answer:1,explanation:`EASL 2016 recommends resection of adenomas in men regardless of size because malignant transformation is much higher in men, often with the beta-catenin subtype. The 5 cm threshold after 6 months of lifestyle change applies to women, adenomas under 5 cm can still bleed, and AFP is not a reliable marker of transformation.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-liver-mass-approach-q5`,stem:`Which liver lesion should NOT be routinely sampled with a percutaneous needle?`,options:[`An indeterminate solid lesion in a patient with no cirrhosis`,`A suspected hydatid cyst`,`Multiple liver lesions with an unknown primary`,`An LR-M lesion in a cirrhotic liver`],answer:1,explanation:`Puncturing a hydatid cyst outside a PAIR protocol with albendazole cover can cause anaphylaxis and peritoneal seeding, so diagnosis rests on imaging and serology. Indeterminate solid lesions, metastases of unknown primary and LR-M observations are exactly the lesions where biopsy is recommended.`,difficulty:`easy`}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`Marrero JA, Ahn J, Reddy KR. ACG clinical guideline: the diagnosis and management of focal liver lesions, American Journal of Gastroenterology, 2014; ACG focal liver lesion guideline update, 2024`,`European Association for the Study of the Liver. EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines on the management of benign liver tumours, Journal of Hepatology, 2016`,`EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines on the management of cystic liver diseases, Journal of Hepatology, 2022`,`Singal AG et al. AASLD practice guidance on prevention, diagnosis and treatment of hepatocellular carcinoma, Hepatology, 2023; ACR LI-RADS CT/MRI v2018`,`Dietrich CF et al. EFSUMB guidelines and recommendations on contrast-enhanced ultrasound of the liver, 2020`,`EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines on the management of hepatocellular carcinoma, 2025`,`ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows of India, 2023`]}),d.push({id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-elderly-palliative`,title:`GI problems in the elderly and palliative GI care`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`GI care of the older and dying patient rests on **age-related physiology, polypharmacy and goals of care**: **oropharyngeal dysphagia is a geriatric syndrome** managed by screening, IDDSI texture modification and careful hand feeding (**not PEG in advanced dementia**), **every opioid prescription carries a laxative** with **PAMORAs for refractory opioid-induced constipation**, **malignant bowel obstruction is palliated with antisecretory drugs (hyoscine butylbromide, octreotide), haloperidol and dexamethasone** when surgery is not appropriate, and **recurrent malignant ascites is drained by paracentesis or a tunnelled indwelling catheter**.`,keywords:[`geriatric gastroenterology`,`presbyphagia`,`oropharyngeal dysphagia`,`IDDSI`,`aspiration pneumonia`,`Zenker diverticulum`,`opioid-induced constipation`,`PAMORA`,`methylnaltrexone`,`naloxegol`,`malignant bowel obstruction`,`octreotide`,`hyoscine butylbromide`,`venting gastrostomy`,`PEG in dementia`,`malignant ascites`,`indwelling peritoneal catheter`,`Beers criteria`,`palliative care`,`NPPC`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**India's population aged 60 and over** was about **10.5% (149 million) in 2022** and is projected to reach **about 20.8% by 2050**, so GI problems of ageing are now everyday OPD work. [UNFPA India Ageing Report 2023]`,`The **Longitudinal Ageing Study in India (LASI wave 1, 2017-18)** found that about **three-quarters of older Indians have at least one chronic disease**, with high polypharmacy and functional limitation. [LASI 2020]`,`**Oropharyngeal dysphagia** affects about **15-30% of community-dwelling older people**, **up to half of nursing-home residents**, and about **half of patients with acute stroke**; it is recognised as a **geriatric syndrome**. [ESSD-EUGMS 2016]`,`**Constipation** affects about **25-50% of older adults** and most patients on opioids; **opioid-induced constipation (OIC)** occurs in about **40-80%** of those on long-term opioids and does not develop tolerance. [MASCC 2020]`,`**Malignant bowel obstruction (MBO)** complicates about **20-50% of advanced ovarian** and **10-28% of advanced colorectal cancers**, and is a common reason for hospice admission. [MASCC 2021]`,`**Malignant ascites** accounts for about 10% of all ascites and is commonest in **ovarian, gastric, pancreatic, colorectal and breast cancer**; median survival after it appears is often only a few months. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Palliative care access in India is very low** - only a small minority of those in need receive it; the **National Programme for Palliative Care (NPPC, 2012)**, now under NP-NCD, and the **NDPS Amendment Act 2014** aim to expand services and oral morphine access. [MoHFW NPPC 2017]`]},{heading:`Ageing GI physiology - why older patients present differently`,points:[`**Presbyphagia** is the healthy ageing of swallowing - slower oral transit, delayed swallow reflex, reduced tongue strength and pharyngeal sensation - which **decompensates into dysphagia** with stroke, dementia, delirium, sedatives or illness. [ESSD-EUGMS 2016]`,`**Reduced saliva** (drugs, dehydration) impairs bolus formation and oral clearance, and **poor oral hygiene** colonises the mouth with pathogens that cause aspiration pneumonia. [ESPEN 2022]`,`**Atrophic gastritis and PPI use** reduce acid, impairing absorption of **vitamin B12, iron and calcium** and increasing small bowel bacterial overgrowth and C. difficile risk. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Colonic transit slows** with loss of enteric neurons and interstitial cells of Cajal, **rectal sensation falls** and pelvic floor coordination weakens, favouring **faecal impaction with overflow**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hepatic blood flow and mass fall by about 30-40%**, reducing first-pass metabolism, and **renal clearance declines** - so opioids, metoclopramide and anticholinergics accumulate. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Blunted inflammatory and pain responses** mean an older patient with perforation, cholecystitis or appendicitis may have **no fever, little guarding and delirium as the only sign**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Opioids act on enteric mu-receptors** to reduce propulsive peristalsis, increase fluid absorption and raise anal sphincter tone - the basis for **peripherally acting mu-opioid receptor antagonists (PAMORAs)** that reverse gut effects without blocking analgesia. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`In **MBO**, distension stimulates **secretion of water and electrolytes** into the lumen, which increases distension further - a **distension-secretion cycle** broken by antisecretory drugs (hyoscine butylbromide, octreotide). [MASCC 2021]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`Oropharyngeal dysphagia causes: **stroke, Parkinson disease, dementia, motor neurone disease, myasthenia, head and neck cancer and radiotherapy, sarcopenia**, and drugs (sedatives, antipsychotics). [ESSD-EUGMS 2016]`,`Oesophageal dysphagia in the elderly: **cancer, peptic stricture, Zenker diverticulum, achalasia, pill oesophagitis, dysphagia aortica**; see the Dysphagia topic for the full approach. [Harrison 22e]`,`Constipation drivers in the elderly: **opioids, anticholinergics, calcium channel blockers, iron, calcium, aluminium antacids, diuretics**, immobility, low fluid intake, hypothyroidism, hypercalcaemia and depression. [AGS Beers 2023]`,`**MBO causes**: peritoneal carcinomatosis (commonest, often multiple levels), a single intraluminal tumour, adhesions or radiation enteritis, and **benign causes in 3-48%** - adhesions, hernia, faecal impaction - which must not be missed. [MASCC 2021]`,`**Malignant ascites mechanisms**: peritoneal carcinomatosis (high protein, low SAAG), **massive liver metastases or portal vein thrombosis (high SAAG)**, chylous ascites from lymphatic obstruction. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Malnutrition risk** rises with dysphagia, dentition problems, depression, dementia, poverty and living alone; under-nutrition is common among hospitalised older Indians. [ESPEN 2022]`]},{heading:`Classification and staging tools`,points:[`**Dysphagia type**: **oropharyngeal** (difficulty initiating the swallow, coughing, nasal regurgitation) vs **oesophageal** (food sticking seconds after swallowing) - the first decision. [Harrison 22e]`,`**IDDSI framework (International Dysphagia Diet Standardisation Initiative)**: drinks **levels 0-4** (thin, slightly thick, mildly thick, moderately thick, extremely thick) and foods **levels 3-7** (liquidised, pureed, minced and moist, soft and bite-sized, regular). [IDDSI 2019]`,`**MBO consensus definition**: clinical evidence of obstruction, **obstruction beyond the ligament of Treitz**, an intra-abdominal primary with incurable disease or an extra-abdominal primary with clear intraperitoneal disease. [MASCC 2021]`,`**Constipation in palliative care** is classed as **primary (immobility, low intake), secondary (hypercalcaemia, spinal cord compression, obstruction)** and **iatrogenic (opioids, antiemetics, anticholinergics)**. [ESMO 2018]`,`**Rome IV OIC**: new or worsening constipation on starting or escalating opioids, with fewer than 3 spontaneous bowel movements a week or straining, hard stools, incomplete evacuation. [Rome IV 2016]`,`**Performance status (ECOG or Karnofsky) and prognosis** decide palliative interventions; **Palliative Performance Scale (PPS)** of 30% or less suggests weeks of life. [MASCC 2021]`,`**Mini Nutritional Assessment - short form (MNA-SF)**: **12-14 normal, 8-11 at risk, 0-7 malnourished**; GLIM criteria then confirm malnutrition. [ESPEN 2022]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Oropharyngeal dysphagia: **coughing or choking during meals, wet or gurgly voice after swallowing, nasal regurgitation, prolonged meals, weight loss and recurrent chest infections**; many patients do not complain - ask the carer. [ESSD-EUGMS 2016]`,`**Silent aspiration** (aspiration without cough) occurs in a substantial proportion of stroke and dementia patients, so a **normal cough does not mean a safe swallow**. [ESSD-EUGMS 2016]`,`**Zenker diverticulum**: regurgitation of **undigested food hours after eating, halitosis, gurgling in the neck** and aspiration in an elderly man. [Harrison 22e]`,`Constipation history: stool frequency and form (**Bristol stool chart**), straining, digital evacuation, **overflow diarrhoea or new faecal incontinence** (impaction), and every drug including over-the-counter and Ayurvedic preparations. [ESMO 2018]`,`**MBO**: intermittent colicky pain, **vomiting (early and large volume in proximal obstruction; late and faeculent in distal)**, distension, and variable passage of flatus as obstruction comes and goes. [MASCC 2021]`,`**Malignant ascites**: rising girth, breathlessness, early satiety, and discomfort; ask how often paracentesis is needed and how quickly fluid returns. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Atypical acute abdomen in the elderly**: delirium, falls, reduced intake or unexplained tachycardia may be the only signs of perforation, ischaemia or obstruction. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Bedside water swallow test** (for example 90 mL in sequential sips) - coughing, wet voice or desaturation over 2% suggests aspiration; **EAT-10 score of 3 or more** is abnormal. [ESSD-EUGMS 2016]`,`Examine **cranial nerves, tongue fasciculations (MND), parkinsonian signs, dentition, oral candidiasis and oral hygiene**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Record **weight, BMI and calf circumference**; look for temporal wasting and sarcopenia (grip strength). [ESPEN 2022]`,`**Per-rectal examination** in constipation detects **impaction, an empty ballooned rectum, a mass, anal fissure** and dyssynergia; an empty rectum with distension suggests higher obstruction. [ESMO 2018]`,`In MBO, a **distended tympanitic abdomen with tinkling or absent bowel sounds** and palpable tumour masses; examine hernial orifices for a benign, treatable cause. [MASCC 2021]`,`In ascites, **shifting dullness and fluid thrill**; look for **Sister Mary Joseph nodule**, supraclavicular nodes and leg oedema; check spinal tenderness and leg power to exclude **cord compression** as a cause of constipation. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Oropharyngeal vs oesophageal dysphagia**: coughing, nasal regurgitation and neurological signs vs food sticking retrosternally; the latter needs endoscopy first. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Faecal impaction with overflow vs infective diarrhoea** in a bedbound elderly patient - the rectal examination distinguishes them; antidiarrhoeals worsen impaction. [ESMO 2018]`,`**MBO vs constipation vs opioid-induced ileus vs pseudo-obstruction**: CT shows a transition point in MBO; PAMORAs and stimulant laxatives are contraindicated in mechanical obstruction. [MASCC 2021]`,`**Malignant vs cirrhotic ascites**: **SAAG under 1.1 g/dL with high protein** suggests peritoneal carcinomatosis; SAAG of 1.1 or more suggests portal hypertension (cirrhosis or massive liver metastases); cytology confirms malignancy in many peritoneal cases. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Nausea in palliative care**: separate **chemical (opioids, hypercalcaemia, uraemia), gastric stasis, bowel obstruction, raised intracranial pressure and vestibular** causes, because each needs a different antiemetic. [ESMO 2018]`,`**Anorexia and weight loss in the elderly**: depression, dementia, dentition, drugs, malignancy, hyperthyroidism and social isolation - not just 'old age'. [ESPEN 2022]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Videofluoroscopic swallow study (VFSS)** or **fibreoptic endoscopic evaluation of swallowing (FEES)** defines aspiration, the safe texture and posture; FEES can be done at the bedside. [ESSD-EUGMS 2016]`,`**Upper GI endoscopy** for oesophageal-type dysphagia; **barium swallow** shows a Zenker diverticulum and is safer than blind endoscopy when it is suspected. [Harrison 22e]`,`Constipation bloods in the elderly: **CBC, calcium, TSH, glucose, creatinine**; **colonoscopy** for alarm features (bleeding, anaemia, weight loss, recent change). [ESMO 2018]`,`**Plain abdominal X-ray** shows faecal loading and dilated loops but **CT abdomen is the test for MBO** - level, number of sites, carcinomatosis and benign causes. [MASCC 2021]`,`**Diagnostic paracentesis**: SAAG, total protein, cell count, **cytology** and culture; **ultrasound** guides drainage of loculated fluid. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Nutrition screening (MNA-SF)** at admission and **albumin, prealbumin only as illness markers** - they are not measures of nutritional status. [ESPEN 2022]`,`Before any intervention, ask **'will the result change management?'** - in the last weeks of life many tests add burden without benefit. [MASCC 2021]`]},{heading:`OPD management - dysphagia and nutrition in the elderly`,points:[`**Texture modification by IDDSI level** recommended by a speech and language therapist, with **upright posture (90 degrees), small bolus, slow pace and chin-tuck**; thickened fluids reduce aspiration but lower fluid intake, so monitor hydration. [ESPEN 2022]`,`**Oral hygiene** (brushing teeth or dentures twice daily, chlorhexidine where appropriate) reduces aspiration pneumonia in care-home residents. [ESSD-EUGMS 2016]`,`**Review drugs** that impair swallowing or saliva - antipsychotics, benzodiazepines, anticholinergics - and change tablets to liquids or dispersible forms where safe. [AGS Beers 2023]`,`**Oral nutritional supplements** providing at least **400 kcal and 30 g protein a day** for malnourished or at-risk older people; target **30 kcal/kg/day and 1 g protein/kg/day**. [ESPEN 2022]`,`**Zenker diverticulum** in symptomatic patients: **flexible endoscopic septotomy** or rigid stapler diverticulotomy; refer. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Restorative swallowing therapy** (exercises, neuromuscular stimulation) helps after stroke and in Parkinson disease, alongside treating the cause. [ESSD-EUGMS 2016]`]},{heading:`OPD management - constipation and opioid-induced constipation`,points:[`**Prescribe a laxative with every regular opioid** - stimulant plus softener or osmotic (for example **senna 7.5-15 mg at night plus PEG 3350 17 g daily**) - and titrate to a soft stool every 1-3 days. [ESMO 2018]`,`**Osmotic options**: **PEG 3350 17 g (one sachet) once or twice daily** (preferred in elderly), **lactulose 15-30 mL once or twice daily** (more bloating); **stimulants**: senna 7.5-15 mg or **bisacodyl 5-10 mg at night**. [AGA 2019]`,`**Rectal measures** for impaction or a loaded rectum: **glycerine suppository, bisacodyl 10 mg suppository**, or a **sodium citrate micro-enema**; manual disimpaction with analgesia if needed. [ESMO 2018]`,`**Avoid in the elderly**: **liquid paraffin (lipoid aspiration pneumonia)**, **phosphate enemas in CKD or dehydration** (hyperphosphataemia), **magnesium salts in CKD**, and **bulk fibre in immobile or fluid-restricted patients** (worsens impaction). [AGS Beers 2023]`,`**Refractory OIC despite two laxatives**: a **PAMORA** - **naloxegol 25 mg once daily** (12.5 mg if poorly tolerated or CrCl under 60), **naldemedine 0.2 mg once daily**, or **methylnaltrexone SC 8 mg (38-62 kg) or 12 mg (62-114 kg) on alternate days** in advanced illness. [AGA 2019]`,`**PAMORAs are contraindicated in known or suspected bowel obstruction** and used cautiously in colon cancer, diverticulitis or peritoneal disease because of **perforation risk**. [MASCC 2020]`,`**Opioid switching** (for example to transdermal fentanyl or tapentadol) causes somewhat less constipation in some patients; rule out hypercalcaemia and cord compression. [ESMO 2018]`]},{heading:`Malignant bowel obstruction - inpatient and palliative management`,points:[`Decide **goals first**: surgery helps selected patients - **good performance status, a single site of obstruction, no massive ascites, and expected survival over 2-3 months**; peritoneal carcinomatosis with multiple levels usually favours medical care. [MASCC 2021]`,`**Endoscopic stents**: **colonic self-expanding metal stents** for left-sided colorectal obstruction and **duodenal stents** for gastric outlet obstruction offer quick relief without laparotomy. [ESGE 2020]`,`**Antisecretory therapy**: **hyoscine butylbromide 60-120 mg/24 h by SC infusion** (also relieves colic) and/or **octreotide 300-600 microgram/24 h SC infusion**; ranitidine or a PPI reduces gastric secretion. [MASCC 2021]`,`**Antiemetics**: **haloperidol 1.5-5 mg/24 h SC** is first-line; **cyclizine 100-150 mg/24 h SC** is an alternative; **metoclopramide only in partial obstruction without colic** - it is contraindicated in complete obstruction. [MASCC 2021]`,`**Dexamethasone 6-16 mg/day SC or IV for 5 days** may reduce peritumoural oedema and help resolution; **stop if no benefit after 5 days**. [MASCC 2021]`,`**Analgesia**: **SC morphine or other opioid infusion** via a syringe driver - opioids are not withheld because of obstruction; stimulant laxatives and PAMORAs are stopped in complete obstruction. [MASCC 2021]`,`**Nasogastric tube** only for short-term relief; for persistent vomiting, a **venting gastrostomy (PEG)** relieves symptoms and allows the patient to sip and taste. [MASCC 2021]`,`**Hydration**: **SC fluids (hypodermoclysis) about 1 L/day** relieve thirst and delirium; **parenteral nutrition** only for patients with expected survival over 2-3 months and good performance status. [ESPEN 2021]`]},{heading:`PEG and artificial nutrition decisions`,points:[`**Indication**: enteral feeding expected for **more than 4 weeks** in a patient with a functioning gut who cannot swallow safely - stroke with persistent dysphagia, **motor neurone disease**, head and neck cancer. [ESGE 2021]`,`**After stroke**, use a **nasogastric tube first** and consider PEG if dysphagia persists **beyond about 4 weeks**; early PEG (within the first week) did not improve outcomes in the FOOD trial. [ESPEN 2018]`,`**Motor neurone disease**: place PEG or RIG **early, before forced vital capacity falls below 50%**, to reduce procedural risk. [ESPEN 2018]`,`**Advanced dementia**: **tube feeding is not recommended** - it does not prolong survival, prevent aspiration, heal pressure ulcers or improve comfort; **careful hand feeding** is preferred. [ESPEN 2024]`,`**Contraindications**: uncorrected coagulopathy, **massive ascites**, peritonitis, gastric outlet obstruction (for feeding), and **very short life expectancy** - 30-day mortality after PEG in frail elderly inpatients is high. [ESGE 2021]`,`Give **single-dose IV antibiotic prophylaxis (for example cefazolin)** before PEG to reduce peristomal infection; start feeding **within 4 hours** in uncomplicated cases. [ESGE 2021]`,`**Ethics and law in India**: artificial nutrition is a medical treatment that can be withheld or withdrawn when it no longer serves the patient; **Common Cause v Union of India (2018)**, simplified by the Supreme Court in **2023**, recognises **advance directives** and withdrawal of life-sustaining treatment. [Supreme Court of India 2023]`,`Make the decision with the **patient (or surrogate), family and team**, documenting prognosis, goals, a **time-limited trial** where uncertain, and the plan if it fails. [ESPEN 2024]`]},{heading:`Malignant ascites and other palliative GI symptoms`,points:[`**Therapeutic paracentesis** is the mainstay for malignant ascites - relief is immediate; albumin replacement is not routinely needed unless the patient has cirrhosis or high-volume drainage with haemodynamic risk. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diuretics (spironolactone 100-400 mg/day with or without furosemide)** help mainly when **SAAG is 1.1 or more** (massive liver metastases, portal vein obstruction) and rarely in peritoneal carcinomatosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tunnelled indwelling peritoneal catheters** allow drainage at home by family or nurses when **paracentesis is needed more often than every 1-2 weeks**; risks are infection, blockage and leak. [Harrison 22e]`,`In **palliative refractory cirrhotic ascites** (not a transplant or TIPS candidate), a **long-term abdominal drain** is an option to avoid repeated hospital paracentesis, as studied in the UK REDUCe 2 trial. [BSG 2020]`,`**Nausea by mechanism**: **haloperidol 0.5-1.5 mg at night** for chemical causes, **metoclopramide 10 mg three times daily** for gastric stasis, **cyclizine** for raised ICP or vestibular causes, **dexamethasone** for raised ICP and bowel oedema, **ondansetron** for chemotherapy. [ESMO 2018]`,`**Anorexia-cachexia**: short courses of **dexamethasone 3-4 mg/day** or **progestins** improve appetite; **low-dose olanzapine 2.5 mg daily** is now recommended for appetite and weight in advanced cancer. [ASCO 2023]`,`**Mouth care** - oral candidiasis (**fluconazole 50-100 mg daily for 7-14 days** or nystatin), dry mouth (sips, saliva substitutes) - is the most neglected GI symptom at the end of life. [ESMO 2018]`,`**Hiccups** from gastric distension: **baclofen 5-10 mg three times daily** or **metoclopramide**; **chlorpromazine** reserved for refractory cases. [ESMO 2018]`]},{heading:`Special situations, complications and prognosis`,points:[`**CKD**: avoid magnesium laxatives and phosphate enemas; use PEG 3350; reduce **metoclopramide, haloperidol and morphine** doses; prefer fentanyl or buprenorphine. [ESMO 2018]`,`**Parkinson disease**: avoid **metoclopramide, haloperidol and prochlorperazine** (dopamine blockers worsen rigidity); use **domperidone** (QT caution) or ondansetron. [AGS Beers 2023]`,`**Beers 2023 GI drugs to avoid** in the elderly: **metoclopramide** (except gastroparesis), **PPIs beyond 8 weeks** without a clear indication, **mineral oil orally**, **anticholinergic antispasmodics** (dicyclomine, hyoscyamine), **prochlorperazine and promethazine**. [AGS Beers 2023]`,`**Home-based palliative care**: SC syringe drivers, butterfly cannulas for SC medication and hypodermoclysis allow MBO care at home with trained nurses (Kerala model). [MoHFW NPPC 2017]`,`**Aspiration pneumonia** is the main complication of oropharyngeal dysphagia and a leading cause of death in dementia and Parkinson disease. [ESSD-EUGMS 2016]`,`**MBO prognosis**: median survival in inoperable MBO is often **weeks to a few months**; recurrent obstruction after surgery is common. [MASCC 2021]`,`**PEG complications**: peristomal infection, leak, **buried bumper syndrome**, tube dislodgement, aspiration and **refeeding syndrome** in severely malnourished patients - start feeds slowly and replace phosphate. [ESGE 2021]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up, prevention and national programmes`,points:[`Refer for **speech and language therapy assessment** any older patient with coughing on swallowing, recurrent chest infections or weight loss; refer oesophageal-type dysphagia for endoscopy. [ESSD-EUGMS 2016]`,`Refer to **palliative care early** - at diagnosis of advanced cancer, not only in the last days; early palliative care improves symptom control and quality of life. [ASCO 2017]`,`**Review bowel function at every visit** in patients on opioids, using the Bristol stool chart and bowel diary. [ESMO 2018]`,`**National Programme for Health Care of the Elderly (NPHCE, 2010)** provides geriatric clinics at district hospitals and CHCs, and home care through **Ayushman Arogya Mandir** teams. [MoHFW NPHCE]`,`**National Programme for Palliative Care (NPPC)**, now integrated with NP-NCD, provides palliative care units in district hospitals and community follow-up; **oral morphine** is on the **NLEM** and its licensing is simplified under the **NDPS Amendment Act 2014**. [MoHFW NPPC 2017]`,`**Advance care planning** - discussing preferences on tube feeding, hospitalisation and place of death before a crisis - prevents unwanted PEG and ICU care. [Supreme Court of India 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates and exam angle`,points:[`**AGS Beers Criteria 2023** keeps metoclopramide, long-term PPIs without indication, oral mineral oil and anticholinergic antispasmodics as potentially inappropriate in the elderly. [AGS Beers 2023]`,`**ESPEN 2024 guideline on nutrition in dementia** reaffirms **no tube feeding in severe dementia** and supports careful hand feeding, oral supplements and eating assistance. [ESPEN 2024]`,`**ESPEN 2022 geriatric nutrition guideline** sets energy (about 30 kcal/kg) and protein (about 1 g/kg) targets and supports texture modification for dysphagia. [ESPEN 2022]`,`**ASCO 2023 cancer cachexia update** adds **low-dose olanzapine 2.5 mg daily** for appetite and weight gain in advanced cancer. [ASCO 2023]`,`**MASCC 2021 MBO recommendations** support antisecretory drugs, haloperidol, short dexamethasone trials and patient-centred decisions on surgery and venting gastrostomy. [MASCC 2021]`,`**Supreme Court of India 2023** simplified the advance directive procedure from Common Cause 2018, making advance care planning practical in Indian family practice. [Supreme Court of India 2023]`,`Viva traps: giving **metoclopramide in complete MBO**, a **PAMORA in suspected obstruction**, **liquid paraffin to an elderly dysphagic patient**, and recommending **PEG in advanced dementia**. [MASCC 2021]`,`Classic question: **'A 78-year-old with advanced dementia has stopped eating - the family asks for a feeding tube'** - answer with evidence, ethics, Indian law and careful hand feeding. [ESPEN 2024]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in palliative and geriatric GI care`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Polyethylene glycol 3350`,`17 g orally in 200 mL water once or twice daily`,`Titrate to soft stool every 1-3 days`,`Bloating; preferred in elderly and CKD`],[`Senna`,`7.5-15 mg orally at night (up to 30 mg)`,`Ongoing with opioids`,`Colic; avoid in obstruction`],[`Bisacodyl`,`5-10 mg orally at night or 10 mg suppository`,`As needed`,`Cramps; avoid in obstruction`],[`Lactulose`,`15-30 mL orally once or twice daily`,`Titrate`,`Bloating, flatulence; needs fluid intake`],[`Naloxegol`,`25 mg orally once daily (12.5 mg if CrCl under 60)`,`While on opioids`,`Abdominal pain, diarrhoea; contraindicated in bowel obstruction, strong CYP3A4 inhibitors`],[`Methylnaltrexone`,`SC 8 mg (38-62 kg) or 12 mg (62-114 kg), else 0.15 mg/kg, alternate days`,`Advanced illness OIC`,`Colic, perforation risk; contraindicated in obstruction`],[`Hyoscine butylbromide`,`60-120 mg/24 h by SC infusion`,`Days to weeks in MBO`,`Dry mouth, tachycardia; does not cross blood-brain barrier`],[`Octreotide`,`300-600 microgram/24 h by SC infusion`,`Review at 3-5 days`,`Injection pain, hyperglycaemia; costly`],[`Haloperidol`,`1.5-5 mg/24 h SC (0.5-1.5 mg at night orally for chemical nausea)`,`Ongoing`,`Extrapyramidal effects; avoid in Parkinson disease`],[`Dexamethasone`,`6-16 mg/day SC or IV in MBO; 3-4 mg/day for appetite`,`Stop at 5 days if no benefit (MBO); short courses for appetite`,`Hyperglycaemia, insomnia, candidiasis, proximal myopathy`],[`Olanzapine (cachexia)`,`2.5 mg orally at night`,`Weeks, review`,`Sedation, metabolic effects`],[`Spironolactone (SAAG 1.1 or more ascites)`,`100-400 mg orally daily`,`Titrate to weight loss`,`Hyperkalaemia, gynaecomastia, renal impairment`]]},{heading:`Malignant bowel obstruction: options by situation`,columns:[`Situation`,`Preferred option`,`Avoid`,`Note`],rows:[[`Good PS, single site, survival over 2-3 months`,`Surgery (resection, bypass, stoma)`,`Delaying surgical review`,`Discuss goals first`],[`Left colonic obstruction`,`Colonic self-expanding metal stent`,`Long-term NG tube`,`Also bridge to surgery`],[`Gastric outlet obstruction`,`Duodenal stent or gastrojejunostomy`,`Prokinetics in complete obstruction`,`Allows oral intake`],[`Peritoneal carcinomatosis, multiple levels`,`Medical: hyoscine butylbromide or octreotide, haloperidol, dexamethasone trial, SC opioid`,`Metoclopramide, stimulant laxatives, PAMORAs`,`Syringe driver at home`],[`Persistent vomiting despite medical care`,`Venting gastrostomy`,`Repeated NG tubes`,`Patient can sip and taste`],[`Partial obstruction without colic`,`Metoclopramide may be tried, softener`,`Stimulant laxatives`,`Stop if colic appears`]]},{heading:`Oropharyngeal vs oesophageal dysphagia in the elderly`,columns:[`Feature`,`Oropharyngeal`,`Oesophageal`],rows:[[`Main complaint`,`Difficulty starting the swallow, coughing, choking`,`Food sticking behind the sternum`],[`Timing`,`Within 1 second of swallowing`,`Several seconds after`],[`Associated`,`Nasal regurgitation, wet voice, neurological signs`,`Heartburn, weight loss, regurgitation`],[`Common causes`,`Stroke, Parkinson, dementia, MND, sarcopenia`,`Cancer, stricture, achalasia, pill injury`],[`First test`,`Bedside screen, then VFSS or FEES`,`Endoscopy (barium if Zenker suspected)`],[`Management`,`IDDSI texture, posture, SLT, oral care`,`Treat cause, dilate, stent`]]},{heading:`Antiemetic choice by mechanism in palliative care`,columns:[`Mechanism`,`Clues`,`First choice`,`Avoid / caution`],rows:[[`Chemical (opioids, hypercalcaemia, uraemia)`,`Constant nausea`,`Haloperidol 0.5-1.5 mg at night`,`Parkinson disease`],[`Gastric stasis`,`Fullness, large-volume vomits relieving nausea`,`Metoclopramide 10 mg three times daily`,`Complete obstruction, Parkinson disease`],[`Bowel obstruction`,`Colic, distension, faeculent vomit`,`Haloperidol, cyclizine, hyoscine butylbromide`,`Metoclopramide in complete obstruction`],[`Raised intracranial pressure`,`Morning headache, vomiting`,`Dexamethasone plus cyclizine`,`Hyperglycaemia`],[`Vestibular or motion`,`Worse on moving`,`Cyclizine or prochlorperazine`,`Anticholinergic load in elderly`],[`Chemotherapy`,`Timing with cycles`,`Ondansetron, dexamethasone, olanzapine`,`Constipation, QT with ondansetron`]]}],redFlags:[`Coughing on swallowing with recurrent chest infections or weight loss - aspiration risk, needs swallow assessment`,`Progressive oesophageal dysphagia with weight loss in an older adult - urgent endoscopy for cancer`,`Delirium, tachycardia or falls with abdominal distension in an elderly patient - occult surgical abdomen`,`Constipation with back pain and leg weakness in a cancer patient - spinal cord compression`,`Colicky pain, vomiting and distension in a patient with ovarian or colorectal cancer - malignant bowel obstruction`,`New constipation with confusion, thirst and polyuria in a cancer patient - hypercalcaemia`,`Fever and abdominal pain with an indwelling peritoneal catheter or after PEG - peritonitis`],pearls:[`**No opioid without a laxative** - tolerance never develops to opioid constipation.`,`**PAMORA = peripheral block, analgesia kept** - but never in obstruction.`,`**MBO trio: hyoscine butylbromide or octreotide to dry, haloperidol to settle, dexamethasone 5-day trial.**`,`**Metoclopramide only for partial obstruction without colic.**`,`**Advanced dementia: careful hand feeding, not PEG.**`,`**PEG when feeding is needed beyond 4 weeks; in MND, before FVC falls below 50%.**`,`**Liquid paraffin in the elderly risks lipoid pneumonia** - avoid.`,`**SAAG 1.1 or more in malignant ascites means diuretics may work** (liver metastases, portal vein obstruction).`,`**A normal cough does not mean a safe swallow** - silent aspiration is common after stroke.`],theory:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-elderly-palliative-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`A 58-year-old woman with recurrent ovarian cancer presents with colicky abdominal pain, vomiting and distension. Discuss the assessment and palliative management of malignant bowel obstruction.`,openingLines:[`Malignant bowel obstruction is clinical obstruction beyond the ligament of Treitz in a patient with incurable intra-abdominal cancer or clear intraperitoneal disease; it complicates 20-50% of advanced ovarian cancers.`,`Management is guided by goals of care, performance status and prognosis - surgery or stents for selected patients, and antisecretory drugs, antiemetics, analgesia and venting gastrostomy for the rest (MASCC 2021).`],answer:[{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Peritoneal carcinomatosis, single tumour, adhesions or radiation; benign causes in a significant minority.`,`Distension-secretion cycle: distension increases secretion, which increases distension.`]},{heading:`Assessment`,points:[`Symptoms: colic, continuous pain, vomiting pattern, flatus.`,`Examine for hernia, faecal impaction, ascites; PS and prognosis.`,`CT abdomen: level, number of sites, carcinomatosis.`,`Bloods: electrolytes, calcium, renal function.`,`Goals-of-care discussion with patient and family.`]},{heading:`Interventional options`,points:[`Surgery if good PS, single site, survival over 2-3 months.`,`Colonic or duodenal self-expanding metal stent.`,`Venting gastrostomy for persistent vomiting.`]},{heading:`Medical management`,points:[`SC opioid infusion for pain; hyoscine butylbromide 60-120 mg/24 h for colic and secretion.`,`Octreotide 300-600 microgram/24 h SC if vomiting persists.`,`Haloperidol 1.5-5 mg/24 h SC; avoid metoclopramide in complete obstruction.`,`Dexamethasone 6-16 mg/day for 5 days, stop if no response.`,`SC fluids about 1 L/day; mouth care; PN only if prognosis over 2-3 months.`]},{heading:`Holistic care`,points:[`Psychological, spiritual and family support; plan place of care; syringe driver at home.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: MBO - goals and prognosis - surgery/stent vs medical ladder`,`Table of drugs with SC doses`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and pathophysiology`,marks:2},{part:`Assessment`,marks:2},{part:`Interventional options`,marks:2},{part:`Medical management with doses`,marks:3},{part:`Holistic care`,marks:1}],keywords:[`malignant bowel obstruction`,`octreotide`,`hyoscine butylbromide`,`haloperidol`,`venting gastrostomy`]},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-elderly-palliative-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on opioid-induced constipation.`,openingLines:[`Opioid-induced constipation is new or worsening constipation on starting or escalating opioids (Rome IV), caused by enteric mu-receptor effects that slow transit, dry stool and tighten the sphincter.`,`It affects 40-80% of patients on long-term opioids, does not develop tolerance, and is prevented by co-prescribing laxatives with every opioid.`],answer:[{heading:`Mechanism`,points:[`Enteric mu-receptors: reduced propulsion, increased fluid absorption, raised anal sphincter tone.`,`No tolerance develops, unlike nausea and sedation.`]},{heading:`Assessment`,points:[`Bristol chart, bowel diary, PR examination for impaction.`,`Exclude obstruction, hypercalcaemia, hypothyroidism and spinal cord compression.`]},{heading:`Management ladder`,points:[`Prophylactic stimulant plus osmotic: senna 7.5-15 mg at night plus PEG 17 g daily.`,`Rectal measures for impaction: suppositories, enema.`,`PAMORA if two laxatives fail: naloxegol 25 mg daily, naldemedine 0.2 mg, methylnaltrexone SC alternate days.`,`Consider opioid switch.`]},{heading:`Cautions`,points:[`PAMORAs contraindicated in known or suspected bowel obstruction; caution with peritoneal disease.`,`Avoid liquid paraffin in the elderly and phosphate enemas in CKD.`]}],mustDraw:[`OIC management ladder`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and mechanism`,marks:2},{part:`Management ladder with doses`,marks:3}],keywords:[`opioid-induced constipation`,`PAMORA`,`naloxegol`,`methylnaltrexone`]}],mcqs:[{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-elderly-palliative-q1`,stem:`An 82-year-old woman with advanced Alzheimer disease (bedbound, non-verbal) is eating less and has had one aspiration pneumonia. Her son asks for a PEG tube. What is the most appropriate advice?`,options:[`Insert a PEG to prevent further aspiration and prolong survival`,`Recommend careful hand feeding with texture modification and discuss goals of care`,`Place a long-term nasogastric tube instead`,`Start total parenteral nutrition`],answer:1,explanation:`ESPEN and AGS guidance advise against tube feeding in advanced dementia because it does not prolong survival, prevent aspiration of saliva or improve comfort; careful hand feeding with a goals-of-care discussion is preferred. A long-term nasogastric tube carries the same lack of benefit with more distress, and parenteral nutrition adds risk without benefit.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-elderly-palliative-q2`,stem:`A patient with peritoneal carcinomatosis has complete bowel obstruction with colic and vomiting and is not fit for surgery. Which antiemetic should be avoided?`,options:[`Haloperidol`,`Cyclizine`,`Metoclopramide`,`Hyoscine butylbromide`],answer:2,explanation:`Metoclopramide is a prokinetic that increases colic and risks perforation in complete obstruction, so it is used only in partial obstruction without colic. Haloperidol is the first-line antiemetic in MBO, cyclizine is a reasonable alternative, and hyoscine butylbromide reduces both colic and secretions.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-elderly-palliative-q3`,stem:`A 70-year-old man on morphine for bone metastases remains constipated despite senna and PEG 3350 for 2 weeks. Abdominal X-ray shows no obstruction. What is the most appropriate next drug?`,options:[`Liquid paraffin 15 mL at night`,`Loperamide to regulate the bowel`,`Stop morphine completely`,`Naloxegol 25 mg once daily`],answer:3,explanation:`Opioid-induced constipation refractory to two laxatives is treated with a PAMORA such as naloxegol, which blocks gut mu-receptors without reversing analgesia, once obstruction is excluded. Liquid paraffin risks lipoid aspiration pneumonia in the elderly, loperamide worsens constipation, and stopping morphine leaves his cancer pain untreated.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-elderly-palliative-q4`,stem:`In a patient with motor neurone disease and progressive bulbar dysphagia, when should gastrostomy ideally be placed?`,options:[`Before forced vital capacity falls below 50% of predicted`,`Only after the first aspiration pneumonia`,`When body weight has fallen by 25%`,`Only when oral intake stops completely`],answer:0,explanation:`Gastrostomy in MND should be placed early, before FVC falls below 50%, because procedural risk with sedation rises steeply with respiratory weakness. Waiting for aspiration, 25% weight loss or complete oral failure exposes the patient to malnutrition and higher procedural mortality.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`gastro-hepatology-gi-elderly-palliative-q5`,stem:`A woman with metastatic gastric cancer needs paracentesis every 7-10 days for symptomatic ascites. Ascitic fluid SAAG is 0.8 g/dL with positive cytology. What is the best option to reduce hospital visits?`,options:[`High-dose spironolactone and furosemide`,`TIPS insertion`,`Tunnelled indwelling peritoneal catheter for home drainage`,`Weekly albumin infusion without drainage`],answer:2,explanation:`Low-SAAG ascites from peritoneal carcinomatosis responds poorly to diuretics, so frequent recurrence is best palliated with a tunnelled indwelling peritoneal catheter drained at home. Diuretics help mainly when SAAG is 1.1 or more, TIPS treats portal hypertensive ascites and is inappropriate here, and albumin alone does not remove fluid.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`Davis M et al. MASCC recommendations: management of malignant bowel obstruction, Supportive Care in Cancer, 2021; Davies A et al. MASCC recommendations on opioid-induced constipation, 2020`,`Crockett SD et al. AGA guideline on the medical management of opioid-induced constipation, Gastroenterology, 2019; Larkin PJ et al. ESMO Clinical Practice Guidelines: diagnosis, assessment and management of constipation in advanced cancer, 2018`,`Volkert D et al. ESPEN practical guideline: clinical nutrition and hydration in geriatrics, 2022; ESPEN guideline on nutrition in dementia, 2024`,`Gkolfakis P et al. Endoscopic management of enteral tubes in adult patients: ESGE Guideline, 2021`,`American Geriatrics Society 2023 Updated AGS Beers Criteria for potentially inappropriate medication use in older adults`,`Roeland EJ et al. Management of cancer cachexia: ASCO guideline rapid recommendation update, 2023`,`Ministry of Health and Family Welfare. National Programme for Palliative Care guidelines, 2017; UNFPA India Ageing Report, 2023; Common Cause v Union of India, Supreme Court of India, 2018 (modified 2023)`]});var f=[...e,...t,...n,...r,...i,...a,...o,...s,...c,...l,...u,...d];export{f as default};